window.SUBJECT_DATA=window.SUBJECT_DATA||{};
window.SUBJECT_DATA["fon"]=[{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"According to the WHO definition, health is best described as a state of complete:","options":["Physical, mental and social well-being and not merely the absence of disease or infirmity","Freedom from all communicable diseases","Physical fitness and absence of pain","Mental and emotional stability only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The WHO (1948) defines health as \"a state of complete physical, mental and social well-being and not merely the absence of disease or infirmity.\"  Why B wrong: Health is broader than freedom from communicable disease.  Why C wrong: Physical fitness alone omits the mental and social dimensions.  Why D wrong: Mental/emotional stability is only one component, not the whole definition."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"The WHO definition of health was given in which year?","options":["1948","1947","1950","1949"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text attributes the WHO health definition to 1948.  Why B wrong: 1947 is the year the INC Act was passed in India, not the WHO health definition.  Why C wrong: 1950 is not the cited year.  Why D wrong: 1949 is the year INC was enforced in India, not the WHO health definition."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"The word \"nurse\" originates from which Latin word, meaning \"to nourish\"?","options":["Nutrix","Nursus","Nutrire","Norse"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the word nurse originated from the Latin word \"Nutrix,\" which means \"to nourish.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the Latin root given in the text."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"Which of the following is a stated purpose of nursing? (Select All That Apply)","options":["To nourish the patient","To care of the patient","To treat the well & sick individual","To serve the patient","To prescribe medications independently"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The purposes of nursing listed are: to nourish, to care, to treat the well & sick individual, and to serve the patient.  Why E wrong: Independent prescribing of medications is not listed as a purpose of nursing in the text."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"According to the Indian Nurses Association (INA), nursing encompasses autonomous and collaborative care of individuals of all ages, families, groups and communities, in which settings?","options":["Sick or well and in all settings","Only in hospitals","Only in community health centres","Only for sick adults"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The INA definition specifies care of individuals \"sick or well and in all settings.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: The definition explicitly includes all settings and both sick and well individuals of all ages."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"According to the INA, nursing includes which of the following functions? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Promotion of health","Prevention of illness","Advocacy","Research participation in shaping health policy and patient and health system management","Education"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The INA definition includes promotion of health, prevention of illness, care of ill/disabled/dying people, advocacy, promotion of safe environment, research, participation in shaping health policy and in patient and health systems management, and education.  Why none wrong: All listed options are part of the INA definition."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"According to the American Nurses Association (ANA), nursing is the protection, promotion and optimization of health and abilities, prevention of illness and injury, and:","options":["Alleviation of suffering through the diagnosis and treatment of human response","Curing of all diseases","Provision of surgical interventions","Replacement of the physician's role"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The ANA definition includes \"alleviation of suffering through the diagnosis and treatment of human response\" and advocacy in the care of individuals, families, communities and populations.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not part of the ANA definition cited."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"Florence Nightingale defined nursing as \"the art of utilizing the environment of patients to assist them in their recovery.\" This statement emphasizes the role of:","options":["The environment in patient recovery","Medication administration","Surgical technique","Documentation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Nightingale's definition focuses on utilizing the environment of the patient to assist recovery.  Why B/C/D wrong: Her definition centers on the environment, not medications, surgery, or documentation."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"According to Virginia Henderson (1955), the unique function of the nurse is to assist the individual, sick or well, in the performance of activities contributing to health or its recovery that the patient would perform unaided if they had the necessary:","options":["Strength, will or knowledge","Finances and insurance","Family support and time","Medications and equipment"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Henderson's definition specifies activities the patient would perform unaided if they had the necessary \"strength, will or knowledge.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the elements named in Henderson's definition."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"Virginia Henderson's definition of nursing was given in which year?","options":["1955","1971","1948","1965"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text dates Henderson's definition to 1955.  Why B wrong: 1971 is the year for Dorothea Orem's definition.  Why C wrong: 1948 is the WHO health definition year.  Why D wrong: 1965 relates to the first International Nurses Day."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"According to Dorothea Orem (1971), nursing is described as a \"group of services or actions\" that becomes necessary when a person is:","options":["Required, because of personal or specific maladies in self-care resulting from a situation of personal health","Hospitalized for any reason","Born with a congenital disease","Over the age of 60"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Orem's definition links the need for nursing services to the person's inability to perform self-care arising from a situation of personal health.  Why B/C/D wrong: Orem's self-care framework focuses on self-care deficit, not hospitalization, congenital disease, or age alone."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"The term \"nurse\" refers to a person who has completed a basic nursing education and is authorized in their country to provide nursing service. The nurse functions in which capacity?","options":["Independent (autonomous) and collaborative — to provide care, prevention of illness/injury, and care of aged","Only under a physician's direct supervision at all times","Only in administrative roles","Only in research settings"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Per the text, the nurse is authorized to provide nursing service of a nursing nature, including prevention of illness, care of the sick, injured, and aged, working both independently and collaboratively.  Why B/C/D wrong: The nurse's role is broad and includes autonomous and collaborative functions, not limited to one setting or constant supervision."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"Which of the following are recognized roles of the nurse? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Care provider / Caregiver","Educator","Advocate","Manager","Researcher"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The \"Role of Nurses\" list includes care provider, educator, advocate, manager, counselor/communicator, collaborator, and researcher.  Why none wrong: All listed options are recognized roles of the nurse per the text."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"A nurse coordinating with the dietitian, physiotherapist, and physician to plan integrated patient care is primarily functioning in which role?","options":["Collaborator","Researcher","Advocate","Educator"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Working jointly with other health-team members reflects the collaborator role listed in the text.  Why B wrong: Researcher involves generating/applying evidence, not team coordination.  Why C wrong: Advocate involves protecting patient rights and interests.  Why D wrong: Educator involves teaching patients/families."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"What is the main responsibility of a nurse, as stated in the text?","options":["To provide care based on nursing diagnosis, prescribing the needs, planning, implementing and evaluating the nursing care","To diagnose and prescribe medical treatment","To perform surgical procedures","To replace the physician in emergencies"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the nurse's main responsibility is to provide care based on nursing diagnosis, prescribing the needs, planning, implementing and evaluating the nursing care.  Why B/C/D wrong: Medical diagnosis/prescription and surgery are outside the stated nursing responsibility."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"The nurse provides care to the patient based on need, respect, dignity and rights without considering caste, race, nationality, sex, creed, colour or socio-economic status. This reflects which principle?","options":["Non-discriminatory, holistic patient care","Selective triage","Cost-based care prioritization","Family-only care"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text emphasizes care provided regardless of caste, race, nationality, sex, creed, colour, or socio-economic status — i.e., non-discriminatory care.  Why B/C/D wrong: These contradict the equality and non-discrimination principle described."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"Which of the following is one of the four fundamental responsibilities of nurses? (Select All That Apply)","options":["To promotion of health","To prevention of illness","To restoration of health","To alleviate suffering","To prescribe surgery"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The four fundamental responsibilities of nurses are: promotion of health, prevention of illness, restoration of health, and to alleviate suffering.  Why E wrong: Prescribing surgery is not one of the four fundamental responsibilities."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"Florence Nightingale is renowned as the founder of which field?","options":["Modern nursing","Microbiology","Public health engineering","Pediatric surgery"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text describes Nightingale as the mother of nurses, British social reformer, statistician, and founder of modern nursing.  Why B/C/D wrong: She is not credited with founding these fields in the text."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"By which famous name is Florence Nightingale known, derived from her habit of making rounds with a lamp at night during the Crimean War?","options":["The Lady with the Lamp","The Angel of Mercy","The First Trained Nurse","The Mother of Public Health"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: She is known as \"the Lady with the Lamp\" because she would visit wounded soldiers of the Crimean War at night carrying a little lamp.  Why B/C/D wrong: These titles are not the one attributed to her in the text."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"Florence Nightingale was born in which year and place?","options":["12 May 1820, Florence, Italy","13 August 1856, London, England","1841, Florence, England","1860, Crimea"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states she was born on 12 May 1820 in Florence, Italy, to a wealthy family.  Why B wrong: 13 August 1910, London, England, is her date of death.  Why C/D wrong: These dates/places are incorrect per the text."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"Florence Nightingale died on which date and at which place?","options":["13 August 1910, London, England","12 May 1820, Florence, Italy","1 July 1925, Crimea","16 April 1930, United States"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text gives her death as 13 August 1910 in London, England.  Why B wrong: That is her date and place of birth.  Why C wrong: Incorrect per text.  Why D wrong: 16 April 1930 is the death date of Linda Richards."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"International Nurses Day is celebrated every year on which date, marking Florence Nightingale's birthday?","options":["12th May","13th August","1st July","7th April"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: International Nurses Day is celebrated on 12 May each year as a tribute to Florence Nightingale, who was born on that date.  Why B wrong: 13 August is her death anniversary.  Why C/D wrong: These are not International Nurses Day."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"Hospital and personal hygiene, sanitation and statistics were the focus of which area in Florence Nightingale's theory?","options":["The environment","Spiritual care","Pharmacology","Genetics"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The environment is the focus of nursing care in Nightingale's theory, including hygiene, sanitation, and statistics.  Why B/C/D wrong: These were not the focus of her environmental theory."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"Who is credited as the first professional & psychiatric trained nurse of the United States (an American)?","options":["Linda Richards","Florence Nightingale","Virginia Henderson","Dorothea Orem"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text identifies Linda Richards as the first professional & psychiatric trained nurse of the United States (American).  Why B wrong: Nightingale was British and the founder of modern nursing.  Why C/D wrong: Henderson and Orem are theorists, not identified as the first US trained nurse."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"The International Council of Nurses (ICN) proclaimed that 12 May would be International Nurses Day in 1974. Since which year has the ICN celebrated \"International Nurses Day\"?","options":["1965","1974","1960","1820"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the first International Nurses Day was 12 May 1974 (proclaimed by ICN), and that the ICN has celebrated International Nurses Day since 1965.  Why B wrong: 1974 is the year of the first official observance/proclamation, but ICN has celebrated since 1965.  Why C/D wrong: These years are incorrect per the text."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"The first international nursing school was the Florence Nightingale Training School at which London hospital, established in 1860?","options":["St Thomas' Hospital","King's College Hospital","St John's Hospital","Royal London Hospital"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The first international nursing school was the Florence Nightingale Training School at St Thomas' Hospital, established in 1860.  Why B wrong: King's George College of London is associated with the later renamed Florence Nightingale Faculty of Nursing and Midwifery.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the hospital cited."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"The Florence Nightingale Training School was later renamed as the Florence Nightingale Faculty of Nursing and Midwifery and is now established under which college?","options":["King's George College of London","St Thomas' College","Harvard College","Oxford College of Nursing"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The school was renamed Florence Nightingale Faculty of Nursing and Midwifery, established under King's George College of London.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the colleges named in the text."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"Florence Nightingale's theory of nursing is best described as the theory of:","options":["Modern theory of nursing — pioneering modern nursing","Self-care deficit theory","Adaptation theory","Goal attainment theory"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text credits Nightingale with the modern theory of nursing and scientific, pioneering modern nursing.  Why B/C/D wrong: These theories are associated with other theorists, not Nightingale."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"Florence Nightingale received the Royal Red Cross in 1883. This made her the:","options":["First recipient of the Royal Red Cross","First recipient of the Order of Merit","First lady of the Order of St John","First female mayor of London"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states she was the first recipient of the Royal Red Cross (1883).  Why B wrong: The Order of Merit (LOSTO 1907) was a different award making her the first woman to be awarded the Order of Merit.  Why C wrong: She was the Lady of grace of the order of St John (LGSTJ 1904), not first recipient of the Order of St John.  Why D wrong: Not stated in the text."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"Florence Nightingale was the first woman to be awarded which honour, in 1907?","options":["The Order of Merit (Order of London)","The Royal Red Cross","The Nobel Prize","The Victoria Cross"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text notes she received the Order of Merit (Order of London) in 1907 and was the first woman to be awarded the Order of Merit.  Why B wrong: She received the Royal Red Cross in 1883.  Why C/D wrong: These awards are not mentioned in the text."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"Which famous nursing books were written by Florence Nightingale? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Notes on Nursing: What it is and What it is Not","A 76-page volume with 1-page appendix (first published by Harrison of Pall Mall)","The Principles of Nursing","Notes on Nursing was published in 1859","Gray's Anatomy"],"correct":"A,B,D","rationale":"Why correct: The text lists \"Notes on Nursing: What it is and What it is Not,\" a book published in 1859, a 76-page volume with a 1-page appendix, first published by Harrison of Pall Mall.  Why C wrong: \"The Principles of Nursing\" is not cited in the text.  Why E wrong: Gray's Anatomy is unrelated and not authored by Nightingale."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"Which of the following are listed contributions of Florence Nightingale to nursing? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Establishing nursing education","Establishing standards for hospital management","Establishing a respected occupation for women","Identifying the personal needs of the patient and the role of the nurse in meeting those needs","Believing that nursing is a separate and distinct profession from medicine"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: All options are part of Nightingale's listed contributions, which also include recognizing nutrition's importance, instituting occupational and recreational therapy for sick people, stressing the need for continuing education for nurses, and maintaining accurate records.  Why none wrong: Each is an explicitly listed contribution."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"Florence Nightingale recognized which factor as important to health, a concept she stressed in her contributions?","options":["Nutrition","Frequent surgery","Bloodletting","Isolation of all patients"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: One of her contributions was recognizing that nutrition is important to health.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not contributions attributed to her."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"Linda Richards is recognized as a pioneer of modern nursing in which country?","options":["United States","England","India","France"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states Linda Richards was a pioneering modern nursing in the United States and was the first professionally trained American nurse.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the country associated with Linda Richards in the text."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"Linda Richards was born and died in which years, respectively?","options":["Born July 27, 1841; Died April 16, 1930","Born May 12, 1820; Died August 13, 1910","Born 1855; Died 1971","Born 1947; Died 1949"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text gives Linda Richards' birth as July 27, 1841, and death as April 16, 1930.  Why B wrong: Those are Florence Nightingale's dates.  Why C/D wrong: These dates do not match Linda Richards."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"Linda Richards is credited with establishing nursing training programs in the United States and Japan, and creating the first system for keeping which type of record for hospitalized patients?","options":["Individual medical records","Financial billing records","Staff attendance records","Pharmacy inventory records"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states she created the first system for keeping individual medical records for hospitalized patients.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the type of record she pioneered per the text."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"According to the International Nurses Day theme table, the 2021 theme was:","options":["A Voice to Lead - A Vision For Future Healthcare","The Future Of Nursing","Nurses: A Voice to Lead - Invest in nursing","Our Future, Our Nurse"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists the 2021 theme as \"A Voice to Lead - A Vision For Future Healthcare.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: These correspond to other years' themes."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"The WHO declared 2020 as the International Year of the Nurse and Midwife in honour of the bicentenary (200th birthday) of which founder of modern nursing?","options":["Florence Nightingale","Linda Richards","Virginia Henderson","Dorothea Orem"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The note states WHO declared 2020 as the International Year of the Nurse and Midwife, in honour of the 200th birthday of Florence Nightingale, the founder of modern nursing.  Why B/C/D wrong: The honour was specifically for Florence Nightingale."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"The Indian Nursing Council (INC) is an autonomous institute of India under which Ministry?","options":["Ministry of Health and Family Welfare (MOHFW)","Ministry of Education","Ministry of Home Affairs","Ministry of Social Justice"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the INC is an autonomous institute of India under the Ministry of Health and Family Welfare (MOHFW).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the ministry under which INC functions."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"What is the primary function of the Indian Nursing Council (INC)?","options":["To provide uniformity in nursing education","To recruit doctors for hospitals","To manufacture medical equipment","To set drug prices"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The function of the council is to provide uniformity in the nursing education.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are outside the role of the INC."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"The INC Act was legislated in India in which year?","options":["1947","1949","2023","1948"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the INC Act was legislated in India in 1947 (AIIMS, CMS, ESIC, RUHS).  Why B wrong: 1949 is when the INC Act was enforced/implemented in India.  Why C wrong: 2023 is the last amendment in the INC Act.  Why D wrong: 1948 is the WHO health definition year."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"The INC Act was enforced / implemented in India in which year?","options":["1949","1947","2023","1950"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the INC Act was enforced/implemented in India in 1949.  Why B wrong: 1947 is when the Act was legislated.  Why C wrong: 2023 is the last amendment.  Why D wrong: 1950 is not cited."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"The last amendment in the INC Act was in which year?","options":["2023","1949","1947","2020"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the last amendment in INC Act was 2023.  Why B/C wrong: These are the enforcement and legislation years respectively.  Why D wrong: 2020 is not cited as the amendment year."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"Which of the following are functions of the Indian Nursing Council (INC)? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Recognizes nurses as a separate branch in health services","Regulates nursing training throughout the country","Recognizes qualifications and inspects schools & colleges of nursing","Maintains Indian Nurses Register and brings out publications","Permits title, badge, and uniforms for registered nurses"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: All listed items are INC functions per the text, which also includes seeking information on courses of study and training/examination, withdrawing recognition, having power to make regulations governing nurses, and regulating nursing training throughout the country.  Why none wrong: All options are listed functions of the INC."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"Which body maintains the Indian Nurses Register?","options":["Indian Nursing Council (INC)","World Health Organization","Ministry of Education","National Medical Commission"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: One of the INC functions listed is to maintain the Indian Nurses Register.  Why B/C/D wrong: These bodies are not stated to maintain the Indian Nurses Register."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"The NNMC Bill, 2023 led to the establishment of which commission as a significant healthcare legislation?","options":["National Nursing and Midwifery Commission (NNMC)","National Medical Commission","Indian Nursing Council","Central Health Authority"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The NNMC Bill is a significant healthcare legislation aimed at reforming and enhancing nursing and midwifery professions through the National Nursing and Midwifery Commission (NNMC).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the commission established by the NNMC Bill, 2023."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"The National Nursing and Midwifery Commission (NNMC) is intended to act as a regulatory body for nursing and midwifery professionals. The NNMC Act, of 1947, will repeal which outdated act?","options":["The unspecified outdated INC-era acts that do not reflect current needs and demands of the nursing and midwifery profession","The Drugs and Cosmetics Act","The Indian Medical Council Act only","The Clinical Establishments Act"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Per the text, the NNMC will repeal the act(s) that are outdated and do not reflect the current needs and demands of the nursing and midwifery profession, which has evolved significantly.  Why B/C/D wrong: These specific acts are not named as the repealed legislation in the text."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"As per the NNMC composition, the NNMC will consist of approximately how many members?","options":["20 members","10 members","50 members","5 members"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states NNMC will consist of 20 members.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated composition."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"As per the NNMC composition, the chairperson must be a person engaged in nursing and midwifery with at least how many years of field experience?","options":["20 years of field experience","5 years of field experience","10 years of field experience","2 years of field experience"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the chairperson must have at least 20 years of field experience.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated requirement."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"The NNMC composition includes ex-officio members from which bodies? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Department of Health and Family Welfare","National Medical Commission","Military Nursing Services","Directorate General of Health Services","Foreign nursing councils"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The text lists ex-officio members from the Department of Health and Family Welfare, National Medical Commission, Military Nursing Services, and the Directorate General of Health Services.  Why E wrong: Foreign nursing councils are not listed in the composition."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"Which of the following are autonomous boards under the NNMC? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Nursing and Midwifery Undergraduate and Postgraduate Education Board","Nursing and Midwifery Assessment and Rating Board","Nursing and Midwifery Ethics and Registration Board","Drug Pricing Board","Hospital Construction Board"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The autonomous boards listed are the Education Board (regulates education and examination at UG/PG levels), the Assessment and Rating Board (frames standards and assesses/rates institutions), and the Ethics and Registration Board (regulates conduct and promotes ethics).  Why D/E wrong: These are not autonomous boards under the NNMC."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"Under the NNMC, the Nursing and Midwifery Assessment and Rating Board is responsible for:","options":["Providing the framework for assessing and rating nursing and midwifery institutions","Regulating education and examination at UG and PG levels","Regulating professional conduct and promoting ethics","Maintaining the patient billing system"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Assessment and Rating Board provides the framework for assessing and rating nursing and midwifery institutions.  Why B wrong: That is the function of the Education Board.  Why C wrong: That is the function of the Ethics and Registration Board.  Why D wrong: This is not a function of any NNMC board."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"Under the NNMC, what is one stated function of the State Nursing and Midwifery Commissions?","options":["To be constituted by state governments","To set drug prices","To employ all nurses in the state","To build hospitals"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The State Nursing and Midwifery Commissions are to be constituted by state governments and include representations from health departments and nursing/midwifery colleges; they function to enforce professional conduct, maintain state registries, issue certificates of specialization, and conduct skill-based examinations.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not functions of the State Commissions per the text."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"Regarding establishment of an institution under the NNMC, individuals must obtain which document before registering to practise nursing or midwifery?","options":["Permission from the Assessment and Rating Board","A medical degree","Police verification only","A pharmacy license"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states permission from the Assessment and Rating Board is required before registering to practise nursing or midwifery.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the requirement stated for establishment of an institution."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"Under the NNMC, an appeal process regarding establishment decisions allows escalation to the National Commission and Central Government in case of:","options":["Disapproval","Approval","Renewal","Inspection"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states there is an appeal process to the National Commission and Central Government in case of disapproval.  Why B/C/D wrong: The appeal mechanism specifically applies to disapproval, not these outcomes."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"Under the NNMC \"Practising as a Professional\" provisions, individuals must be enrolled in which register to practise nursing or midwifery?","options":["The National or State Register","The Indian Medical Register","The Pharmacy Register","The International Nurses Register"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states individuals must be enrolled in the National or State Register to practise nursing or midwifery.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the registers cited for practising as a professional."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"Under NNMC provisions, non-compliance with practice rules may result in:","options":["Imprisonment or a fine","A written warning only","Mandatory retraining only","No penalty"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states non-compliance may result in imprisonment or a fine.  Why B/C/D wrong: The stated penalty is imprisonment or fine, not a warning, retraining, or no penalty."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"The NNMC includes which advisory body to advise the Commission on nursing and midwifery education, services, training, and research?","options":["The Advisory Council","The Drug Authority","The State Health Board","The Hospital Federation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text describes an Advisory Council that advises the Commission on nursing and midwifery education, services, training, and research.  Why B/C/D wrong: These bodies are not the advisory body cited."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"Which organizations are listed as having representatives on the NNMC Advisory Council? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Indian Council of Medical Research (ICMR)","Nursing and midwifery institutions","National Assessment and Accreditation Council (NAAC)","The Sports Authority of India","The Reserve Bank of India"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The text lists representatives from State and Union Territory, Ministry of Ayush, University Grants Commission, National Assessment and Accreditation Council, ICMR, and nursing/midwifery institutions.  Why D/E wrong: These organizations are not listed on the Advisory Council."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"The nursing process is best defined as:","options":["A critical thinking process that professional nurses use to apply the best available evidence to caregiving and to promote human functions and responses to health and illness","A method of billing patients","A surgical procedure","A medication delivery system"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text defines the nursing process as a critical thinking process used to apply best available evidence to caregiving and to promote human functions and responses to health and illness.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the definition of the nursing process."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"The nursing process is used to identify the priorities and goals of the patient. Which of the following are stated purposes of the nursing process? (Select All That Apply)","options":["To identify a client's health status and actual or potential health care problems or needs","To establish plans to meet the identified needs","To deliver specific nursing interventions to meet those needs","To prescribe surgical procedures"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The purposes of the nursing process are: to identify a client's health status and actual or potential problems/needs, to establish plans to meet identified needs, and to deliver specific nursing interventions to meet those needs.  Why D wrong: Prescribing surgery is not a purpose of the nursing process."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"What are the five steps of the nursing process in correct sequence?","options":["Assessment, Nursing Diagnosis, Planning, Implementation, Evaluation","Planning, Assessment, Diagnosis, Evaluation, Implementation","Diagnosis, Assessment, Implementation, Planning, Evaluation","Assessment, Planning, Diagnosis, Implementation, Evaluation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text lists the five steps as Assessment (first step), Nursing Diagnosis, Planning, Implementation, and Evaluation (last step) — the ADPIE sequence.  Why B/C/D wrong: These present the steps out of the correct ADPIE order."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"Which step is the first step in the nursing process?","options":["Assessment","Planning","Diagnosis","Evaluation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text explicitly states assessment is the first step in the nursing process.  Why B/C/D wrong: Planning, diagnosis, and evaluation come after assessment."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"During the assessment phase, the nurse collects, organizes, validates, and documents patient data. Subjective data are best described as:","options":["Data the patient verbally complains of, e.g., nausea, feeling of uneasiness, and pain","Measurable vital signs and lab values","Observable signs only","Data gathered solely from family members"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text defines subjective data as the patient's verbal complaints such as nausea, feeling of uneasiness, and pain.  Why B/C wrong: Measurable/observable signs are objective data.  Why D wrong: Subjective data come from the patient, though family can be a secondary source; the defining feature is verbal complaint."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"Which of the following is an example of objective data?","options":["Vital signs, I/O chart, weight and height","Patient's report of pain","Patient's feeling of nausea","Patient's statement of uneasiness"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text lists objective data as vital signs, I/O chart, weight, and height — measurable/observable data.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are subjective data reported by the patient."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"During assessment, the nurse should remember that this step is the first step in the nursing process and analysis questions are the most difficult because they require:","options":["Critical thinking","Manual dexterity","Physical strength","Memorization only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states analysis questions are the most difficult because they require critical thinking.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the skill required for analysis questions."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"Which of the following are characteristics or actions of the Nursing Diagnosis phase? (Select All That Apply)","options":["It is clinical judgment about actual and potential health problems","It is used to prioritize and plan care","The nurse defines the patient's problems before plan-specific intervention","It identifies the patient's subjective and objective data","It involves prescribing medical drug therapy"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The nursing diagnosis is a clinical judgment about actual and potential health problems used to prioritize and plan care; the nurse defines specific interventions and addresses subjective and objective data.  Why E wrong: Prescribing medical drug therapy is not part of the nursing diagnosis phase."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"During the planning phase of the nursing process, the nurse determines:","options":["The goals and outcome criteria for care, while communicating and documenting the plan of care","The medication doses to prescribe","The surgical schedule","The discharge billing"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Planning requires prioritizing the client's problems, determining goals and outcome criteria for care, and communicating and documenting the plan of care.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the activities of the planning phase per the text."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"During planning, what should the nurse always give first priority to?","options":["The actual (real) problems rather than potential problems","The potential problems rather than the actual problems","The financial concerns of the patient","The family's preferences"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states \"Always gives first priority to the actual (real) problems rather than potential problems.\"  Why B wrong: Potential problems are given lower priority than actual problems.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the priority basis stated."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"During the implementation phase of the nursing process, the nurse carries out interventions like:","options":["Cardiac monitoring or oxygen, medications, and standard protocols","Writing the nursing diagnosis","Setting outcome criteria","Evaluating goal achievement"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Implementation involves carrying out interventions such as cardiac monitoring, oxygen, medications, and standard protocols, including coordinating care, communicating, and documenting nursing interventions.  Why B wrong: Writing the diagnosis is the nursing diagnosis phase.  Why C wrong: Setting outcome criteria is part of planning.  Why D wrong: Evaluating goal achievement is the evaluation phase."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"The evaluation phase of the nursing process requires the nurse to determine:","options":["The effectiveness of nursing care, comparing the patient's current status to the outcome criteria","The patient's billing total","The surgical risk score only","The next patient's diagnosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Evaluation requires the nurse to determine the effectiveness of nursing care; if nursing care/implementation is not effective, the nurse restarts the nursing process from the first step assessment.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the focus of the evaluation phase."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"If nursing care or implementation is NOT effective, what should the nurse do?","options":["Restart the nursing process from the first step (assessment)","Discharge the patient immediately","Skip directly to evaluation again","Stop all interventions"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states that if nursing care/implementation is not effective, the nurse restarts the nursing process from the first step assessment.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not reflect the cyclical re-assessment described."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"In the assessment of data, \"symptoms\" that the patient cannot observe directly (e.g., pain, itching, tinnitus, nausea, feeling of uneasiness) are classified as:","options":["Subjective data","Objective data","Secondary data","Tertiary data"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text defines subjective data (symptoms) as feelings or complaints the patient can't observe directly, such as pain, itching, tinnitus, nausea, and feeling of uneasiness.  Why B wrong: Objective data are observable/measurable signs.  Why C/D wrong: These refer to sources of data, not the subjective/objective classification."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"In the assessment of data, \"signs\" that are measurable or observable (e.g., vomiting, pallor, diaphoresis, high blood pressure, fever, and skin discoloration) are classified as:","options":["Objective data","Subjective data","Primary source data","Secondary source data"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text defines objective data (signs) as measurable or observable data about a patient's health status, such as vomiting, pallor, diaphoresis, high blood pressure, fever, and skin discoloration.  Why B wrong: Subjective data are the patient's reported symptoms.  Why C/D wrong: These refer to sources of data, not the type of data."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"In the Source of Data, the primary source of information is:","options":["The patient (information directly gathered through interview, history taking, and physical examination)","The patient's family only","Medical records and journals","Other health team members"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states primary sources are information directly gathered by the patient through interview, history taking, and physical examination.  Why B/C/D wrong: Family, records, journals, and other team members are secondary sources."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"Which of the following are considered secondary sources of data? (Select All That Apply)","options":["The patient's family","Other health team members","Medical records","Journals and literature","The patient's own interview responses"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The text states secondary sources include the patient's family, other health team members, medical records, journals, and literature.  Why E wrong: The patient's own interview responses are a primary source, not secondary."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"According to the text on sources of data, which source is the most important when there is an unconscious patient or a child?","options":["Secondary data (because primary data is most important but unavailable)","Objective data","The patient's own report","Laboratory data only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text notes that in an unconscious patient or a child, secondary data is most important (because the primary source cannot provide reliable information directly).  Why B wrong: Objective data is a type, not a source priority for this scenario.  Why C wrong: The patient's own report is unreliable/unavailable in an unconscious patient or child.  Why D wrong: Lab data alone is not the stated answer."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"According to the NANDA PES format used to define a nursing diagnosis, the \"P\" stands for:","options":["Problem (the patient response or nursing diagnosis)","Plan","Prognosis","Prevention"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In the PES format, P = Problem, which is the patient response / nursing diagnosis.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the meaning of \"P\" in the PES format."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"In the NANDA PES format, the \"E\" stands for:","options":["Etiology (related factors which contribute to the nursing diagnosis)","Evaluation","Environment","Examination"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In the PES format, E = Etiology, the related factors that contribute to the nursing diagnosis.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the meaning of \"E\" in the PES format."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"In the NANDA PES format, the \"S\" stands for:","options":["Signs and symptoms (defining characteristics which manifested by the patient)","Source","Status","Severity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In the PES format, S = Signs and symptoms — the defining characteristics manifested by the patient.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the meaning of \"S\" in the PES format."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"A NANDA-I nursing diagnostic statement is typically built from how many components according to the PES approach?","options":["Three (Problem, Etiology, Signs/symptoms)","Two","Four","Five"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states a nursing diagnosis has typically three components approved by NANDA: Problem, Etiology, and Signs and symptoms.  Why B/C/D wrong: The PES format comprises three components, not two, four, or five."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"An \"Actual nursing diagnosis\" (problem-focused) is best described as one that is:","options":["Composed of the problem statement, related factors and signs & symptoms","A risk that is not currently present","A transition to a higher level of wellness","A cluster of related problems anticipated to arise"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: An actual (problem-focused) nursing diagnosis is composed of the problem statement, related factors, and signs & symptoms.  Why B wrong: That describes a risk nursing diagnosis.  Why C wrong: That describes a wellness nursing diagnosis.  Why D wrong: That describes a syndrome nursing diagnosis."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"An example given in the text for an actual nursing diagnosis is:","options":["Excess Fluid Volume related to nausea and vomiting as manifested by dry skin and mucous membranes and decreased oral intake of fluids","Risk for infection related to presence of invasive lines","Readiness for Enhanced Spiritual Well-Being","Increased Intracranial Pressure"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text's example for an actual nursing diagnosis is Excess Fluid Volume related to nausea and vomiting, manifested by dry skin and mucous membranes and decreased oral intake of fluids.  Why B wrong: That is the example for a risk nursing diagnosis.  Why C wrong: That is the example for a wellness nursing diagnosis.  Why D wrong: That is the example for a syndrome nursing diagnosis."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"A \"Risk nursing diagnosis\" describes:","options":["A problem that does not currently exist but for which the patient may develop in the future due to presence of risk factors","A problem that is currently present with signs and symptoms","A transition to higher wellness","A grouping of actual diagnoses"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A risk nursing diagnosis is for a problem that does not currently exist but may develop in the future due to the presence of risk factors.  Why B wrong: That describes an actual nursing diagnosis.  Why C wrong: That describes a wellness nursing diagnosis.  Why D wrong: That describes a syndrome nursing diagnosis."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"Which of the following is the example given for a risk nursing diagnosis?","options":["Risk for infection related to presence of invasive lines (intravenous line and indwelling bladder catheter)","Excess Fluid Volume related to nausea and vomiting","Readiness for Enhanced Spiritual Well-Being","Increased Intracranial Pressure"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text's example for a risk nursing diagnosis is Risk for infection related to the presence of invasive lines (IV line and indwelling bladder catheter).  Why B wrong: That is the actual nursing diagnosis example.  Why C wrong: That is the wellness diagnosis example.  Why D wrong: That is the syndrome diagnosis example."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"A \"Wellness nursing diagnosis\" describes:","options":["The client's desire to attain a higher level of wellness in some area of function","A current problem with active signs and symptoms","A future risk problem","A cluster of related problems"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A wellness nursing diagnosis describes the client's desire to attain a higher level of wellness in some area of function.  Why B wrong: That describes an actual nursing diagnosis.  Why C wrong: That describes a risk nursing diagnosis.  Why D wrong: That describes a syndrome nursing diagnosis."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"Which of the following is the example given for a wellness nursing diagnosis?","options":["Readiness for Enhanced Spiritual Well-Being","Risk for infection related to presence of invasive lines","Excess Fluid Volume related to nausea and vomiting","Increased Intracranial Pressure"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text's example for a wellness nursing diagnosis is \"Readiness for Enhanced Spiritual Well-Being.\"  Why B wrong: That is the risk diagnosis example.  Why C wrong: That is the actual diagnosis example.  Why D wrong: That is the syndrome diagnosis example."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"A \"Syndrome nursing diagnosis\" is best described as:","options":["A clinical evaluation of a set of related problems or risks, anticipated to arise from a specific situation","A single current problem with signs and symptoms","A future risk based on risk factors alone","A desire to attain a higher level of wellness"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A syndrome nursing diagnosis is a clinical evaluation of a set of related problems or risks anticipated to arise from a specific situation.  Why B wrong: That describes an actual nursing diagnosis.  Why C wrong: That describes a risk nursing diagnosis.  Why D wrong: That describes a wellness nursing diagnosis."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"Which of the following is the example given for a syndrome nursing diagnosis?","options":["Increased Intracranial Pressure","Readiness for Enhanced Spiritual Well-Being","Risk for infection related to invasive lines","Excess Fluid Volume related to nausea and vomiting"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text's example for a syndrome nursing diagnosis is \"Increased Intracranial Pressure.\"  Why B wrong: That is the wellness diagnosis example.  Why C wrong: That is the risk diagnosis example.  Why D wrong: That is the actual diagnosis example."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"A nurse interviews a newly admitted patient, performs a head-to-toe physical examination, and records the patient's stated complaints. This data-gathering activity represents which phase and source of the nursing process?","options":["Assessment phase using a primary source of data","Evaluation phase using a secondary source","Planning phase using objective data only","Implementation phase using tertiary data"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Interview, history taking, and physical examination directly from the patient are assessment activities using a primary data source.  Why B wrong: This is assessment, not evaluation, and the patient is a primary source.  Why C wrong: Assessment, not planning, and it gathers both subjective and objective data.  Why D wrong: This is assessment, not implementation; data from the patient directly is primary, not tertiary."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"A patient with an indwelling urinary catheter and central IV line currently shows no signs of infection. The most appropriate type of nursing diagnosis to document is:","options":["Risk nursing diagnosis (Risk for infection related to presence of invasive lines)","Actual nursing diagnosis","Wellness nursing diagnosis","Syndrome nursing diagnosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: When the problem does not currently exist but the patient may develop it due to risk factors (invasive lines), a risk nursing diagnosis is appropriate.  Why B wrong: An actual diagnosis requires current signs and symptoms, which are absent.  Why C wrong: A wellness diagnosis applies to a desire for higher-level wellness.  Why D wrong: A syndrome diagnosis addresses a cluster of related problems from a specific situation."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"A nurse caring for a postoperative patient prioritizes addressing the patient's current \"Acute Pain\" before the \"Risk for falls.\" This prioritization reflects which planning principle?","options":["Give first priority to actual (real) problems rather than potential problems","Give first priority to wellness diagnoses","Address potential problems before actual ones","Address problems in alphabetical order"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Planning gives first priority to actual (real) problems (acute pain) over potential problems (risk for falls).  Why B wrong: Wellness diagnoses are not prioritized over actual problems in this scenario.  Why C wrong: This is the opposite of the stated principle.  Why D wrong: Prioritization is by actual vs. potential, not alphabetical."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"Which nurse role involves protecting the patient's rights and interests and ensuring care is provided without discrimination based on caste, race, or socio-economic status?","options":["Advocate","Researcher","Manager","Collaborator"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Protecting patient rights and ensuring equitable, non-discriminatory care aligns with the advocate role and the nurse's responsibility to care regardless of caste/race/socio-economic status.  Why B wrong: Researcher generates and applies evidence.  Why C wrong: Manager coordinates resources and care delivery.  Why D wrong: Collaborator works jointly with the health team."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"The Crimean War is historically associated with the nursing work of which figure, who tended wounded soldiers at night with a lamp?","options":["Florence Nightingale","Linda Richards","Virginia Henderson","Dorothea Orem"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Florence Nightingale tended wounded soldiers of the Crimean War at night with a little lamp, earning the title \"Lady with the Lamp.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: These figures are not associated with the Crimean War nursing in the text."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"The INC seeks information on courses of study and training/examination from nursing institutions and inspects schools & colleges of nursing. These activities primarily ensure:","options":["Uniformity and quality (standards) in nursing education","Increased hospital revenue","Reduced patient wait times","Lower medication costs"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: These INC functions support its primary purpose of providing uniformity and maintaining standards in nursing education.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the aims of the INC's inspection and information-gathering functions."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"Under the NNMC, the board responsible for regulating professional conduct and promoting ethics in the profession is the:","options":["Nursing and Midwifery Ethics and Registration Board","Nursing and Midwifery Assessment and Rating Board","Nursing and Midwifery Undergraduate and Postgraduate Education Board","Advisory Council"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Ethics and Registration Board regulates professional conduct and promotes ethics in the profession.  Why B wrong: The Assessment and Rating Board assesses and rates institutions.  Why C wrong: The Education Board regulates education and examination.  Why D wrong: The Advisory Council advises the Commission."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"A nursing student is told that the nursing process is \"cyclical\" and not strictly linear. Which step, when its outcomes are not met, causes the nurse to return to the first step?","options":["Evaluation","Assessment","Planning","Nursing diagnosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: If evaluation shows nursing care/implementation is not effective, the nurse restarts the nursing process from the first step (assessment), making the process cyclical.  Why B wrong: Assessment is the first step that the nurse returns to, not the step that triggers the return.  Why C/D wrong: Planning and diagnosis do not trigger the cyclical restart per the text."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"Which of the following correctly matches the data type with its example, per the text? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Subjective data — pain, itching, tinnitus, nausea","Objective data — vomiting, pallor, diaphoresis, fever","Objective data — patient's feeling of uneasiness","Subjective data — measurable high blood pressure","Objective data — skin discoloration"],"correct":"A,B,E","rationale":"Why correct: Subjective data are symptoms like pain, itching, tinnitus, nausea (A); objective data are observable signs like vomiting, pallor, diaphoresis, fever (B), and skin discoloration (E).  Why C wrong: A patient's feeling of uneasiness is subjective, not objective.  Why D wrong: Measurable high blood pressure is objective, not subjective."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"Which statement about the nurse's authorization to practise is correct per the text?","options":["A nurse is a person who has completed a basic nursing education and is authorized in their country to provide nursing service","A nurse may practise without any formal education","A nurse must hold a medical degree to practise","A nurse is authorized only to provide surgical services"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text defines a nurse as a person who has completed basic nursing education and is authorized in their country to provide nursing service.  Why B wrong: Basic nursing education is required.  Why C wrong: A medical degree is not required for nursing practice.  Why D wrong: Nursing service is broader than surgical services."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition of Health, Introduction & Purpose of Nursing…","question":"Match the theorist to their key concept of nursing. Which pairing is correct?","options":["Dorothea Orem — self-care / services needed when a person cannot meet self-care due to personal health","Florence Nightingale — assisting the patient with strength, will, or knowledge","Virginia Henderson — utilizing the environment for recovery","American Nurses Association — care needed due to self-care deficit"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Dorothea Orem's definition links nursing to services needed when a person cannot meet self-care because of a situation of personal health.  Why B wrong: \"Strength, will or knowledge\" is Virginia Henderson's concept, not Nightingale's.  Why C wrong: \"Utilizing the environment\" is Florence Nightingale's concept, not Henderson's.  Why D wrong: Self-care deficit is Orem's concept, not the ANA's."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"Which type of nursing intervention is performed by a licensed nurse on the basis of her own knowledge and skill, without requiring a doctor's order?","options":["Dependent intervention","Collaborative intervention","Independent intervention","Interdependent referral"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Independent interventions are activities nurses are licensed to initiate on the basis of their own knowledge and skill and do not require a doctor's order.  Why A wrong: Dependent interventions are carried out under the orders/supervision of a licensed doctor.  Why B wrong: Collaborative interventions require collaboration with other health team members.  Why D wrong: Not a defined category in the source list."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"A nurse performs assessment of vital signs, health education and discharge teaching, meeting hygiene needs, mobility therapy, alcohol abuse control, and positioning therapy. These are examples of which intervention type?","options":["Dependent interventions","Independent interventions","Collaborative interventions","Medical interventions"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists assessment of vital signs, health education/discharge teaching, meeting hygiene needs, mobility therapy and infection control, alcohol abuse control, and positioning therapy & bed soar care as examples of independent interventions.  Why A wrong: Dependent examples are administration of medication, tube feeding, dressing changes, catheter insertions.  Why C wrong: Collaborative examples involve coordinating with other team members.  Why D wrong: \"Medical interventions\" is not a category in the source."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"Administration of medication, tube feeding, dressing changes, and catheter insertions are examples of which type of nursing intervention?","options":["Independent","Collaborative","Dependent","Autonomous"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: These activities are carried out under the orders or supervision of a licensed doctor and require direction/prescription of a doctor, defining dependent interventions.  Why A wrong: Independent interventions need no doctor's order.  Why B wrong: Collaborative interventions involve other health team members.  Why D wrong: Autonomous is synonymous with independent, not dependent."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"Coordinating with a speech therapist and physical therapist, with a pharmacist, with laboratory staff, and with the health team are examples of which type of nursing intervention?","options":["Independent","Dependent","Collaborative","Delegated"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Collaborative interventions are activities that require collaboration with other health team members, with these exact coordinating examples listed in the source.  Why A wrong: Independent interventions are initiated alone by the nurse.  Why B wrong: Dependent interventions follow a doctor's orders.  Why D wrong: \"Delegated\" is not the source category for these examples."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"Documentation in nursing is described as an essential component of which of the following?","options":["Safe, ethical, and effective nursing practice","Billing and reimbursement only","Research only","Hospital marketing"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines documentation as an essential component of safe, ethical and effective nursing practice, regardless of whether it is paper-based or electronic.  Why B wrong: While documentation supports many functions, the source defines it broadly as part of safe/ethical/effective practice, not billing alone.  Why C wrong: Research is one purpose but not the defining statement.  Why D wrong: Marketing is not a purpose of documentation."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"Which of the following is listed as a purpose of health care documentation? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Professional responsibility and accountability","To facilitate communication","To provide nursing care","To meet professional and legal standards","Education and research"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists professional responsibility and accountability, to facilitate communication, to provide nursing care, to meet professional and legal standards, education and research, and quality assurance as purposes of health care documentation.  All options A–E are explicitly stated purposes; quality assurance is the only additional purpose not listed among the options."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"According to the guidelines for quality documentation, nursing records should be all of the following EXCEPT:","options":["Accurate and factual","Current and logic order","Complete and organised","Written using non-standard personal abbreviations"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The guidelines specify using only standard abbreviations; non-standard personal abbreviations are not acceptable.  Why A wrong: Accurate and factual are listed guidelines.  Why B wrong: Current and logic order are listed guidelines.  Why C wrong: Complete and organised are listed guidelines."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"\"Admission\" of a patient is best defined as:","options":["The release of a patient from the hospital","Permitting the patient to stay in the hospital for observation, diagnosis, and treatment of his/her disease","Transfer of a patient to another ward","Signing of consent forms only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines admission as permitting the patient to stay in the hospital for observation, diagnosis and treatment of his/her disease.  Why A wrong: That defines discharge, not admission.  Why C wrong: Transfer is not the source definition of admission.  Why D wrong: Consent signing is not the definition of admission."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"\"Discharge\" of a patient is defined as:","options":["The patient's first day in the hospital","Permitting the patient to stay for observation","The release of the patient from the hospital and starts when the patient is admitting to the hospital","Transfer between two hospitals"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines discharge as the release of the patient from the hospital and states that the discharge process starts when the patient is admitting to the hospital.  Why A wrong: That describes admission day.  Why B wrong: That defines admission.  Why D wrong: Inter-hospital transfer is not the source definition."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"In the types of discharge classification, a discharge in which the patient is released from the hospital when the purpose of admission is completed is termed:","options":["Planned discharge","DOPR","LAMA","Absconding"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Planned discharge is when the patient is released from the hospital when the purpose of admission is completed.  Why B wrong: DOPR is Discharge On Patient Request.  Why C wrong: LAMA is Left Against Medical Advice.  Why D wrong: Absconding is when the patient leaves the hospital without informing anyone."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"The abbreviation \"DOPR\" in types of discharge stands for:","options":["Discharge On Patient Request","Discharge Of Pending Records","Date Of Patient Release","Doctor Ordered Patient Release"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: DOPR is defined as Discharge On Patient Request.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not the meanings given in the source table."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"\"LAMA\" as a type of discharge means:","options":["Left After Mobile Assessment","Left Against Medical Advice","Leave And Medication Advice","Long Admission Medical Approval"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: LAMA stands for Left Against Medical Advice.  Why A, C, D wrong: Not the defined meaning in the source table."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"The discharge type \"ORAMA\" refers to:","options":["Operated Routine Against Medical Advice","Discharge Requested Against Medical Advice","Outpatient Routine Admission","Overstay Refused Against Medical Advice"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: ORAMA is defined in the source as Discharge Requested Against Medical Advice.  Why A, C, D wrong: These do not match the source definition."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"\"OSAMA\" as a type of discharge means:","options":["Overstaying Against Medical Advice","Operated Surgery Against Medical Advice","Outpatient Standard Admission","Observation Started Against Medical Advice"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: OSAMA is defined as Overstaying Against Medical Advice.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the meanings given in the source."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"In the types of discharge, \"RAMA\" stands for:","options":["Routine Admission Medical Approval","Resting Against Medical Advice","Released After Medical Assessment","Referred Against Medical Advice"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: RAMA is defined as Resting Against Medical Advice.  Why A, C, D wrong: These are not the meanings given in the source table."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"The discharge abbreviation \"MAMA\" refers to:","options":["Mobilizing Against Medical Advice","Mother And Medical Advice","Medication Advice Medical Approval","Mandatory Admission Medical Authority"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: MAMA is defined as Mobilizing Against Medical Advice.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the source meanings."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"\"AAMA\" as a type of discharge means:","options":["Admitted Again Medical Advice","Admitted Against Medical Advice (through administration pressure)","Approved Admission Medical Audit","Acute Admission Medical Action"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: AAMA is defined as Admitted Against Medical Advice through administration pressure.  Why A, C, D wrong: These do not match the source definition."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"In types of discharge, \"OBAMA\" stands for:","options":["Out of Bed Against Medical Advice","Operated Before Admission Medical Advice","Observed Before Approval Medical Audit","Outpatient Basic Admission"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: OBAMA is defined in the source as Out of Bed Against Medical Advice.  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the source meanings."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"A patient who leaves the hospital without any intimation/informing and once he is informed immediately to the in-charge or concerned doctor is classified as:","options":["Parole","LAMA","Absconding","DOPR"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Absconding describes a patient who leaves the hospital without any intimation, where the in-charge or concerned doctor is informed immediately once known.  Why A wrong: Parole is temporary release of a mentally ill patient.  Why B wrong: LAMA is leaving against medical advice (with notice).  Why D wrong: DOPR is discharge on patient request (a formal request)."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"The type of discharge defined as \"temporary release of a mentally ill patient from the hospital\" is:","options":["LAMA","Parole","Planned discharge","Absconding"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Parole is the temporary release of a mentally ill patient from the hospital.  Why A wrong: LAMA is leaving against medical advice.  Why C wrong: Planned discharge is on completion of purpose of admission.  Why D wrong: Absconding is leaving without informing."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"Nursing theory is best defined as:","options":["A creative and conscientious structuring of ideas that project a tentative, purposeful, and systematic view of phenomena","A random collection of clinical opinions","A set of legal regulations for nurses","A list of hospital policies"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines nursing theory as a creative and conscientious structuring of ideas that project a tentative, purposeful, and systematic view of phenomena.  Why B wrong: Theories are systematic, not random opinions.  Why C wrong: Theories are not legal regulations.  Why D wrong: Theories are not hospital policies."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"Which is a stated purpose/role of nursing theory? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Through systematic inquiry, whether in nursing research or practice, nurses are able to develop knowledge relevant to improving the care of patients","Theory provides structure and organisation for nursing knowledge","To replace clinical judgment entirely","To support evidence-based nursing knowledge development"],"correct":"A,B,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source states theory lets nurses develop knowledge to improve patient care and provides structure and organisation for nursing knowledge, supporting knowledge development.  Why C wrong: The source does not state that theory replaces clinical judgment; theory guides and structures knowledge."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"Among the types of nursing theories, the type that identifies specific phenomena through abstract concepts (e.g., a science theory of theory) is the:","options":["Grand theory","Meta theory","Middle range theory","Practice theory"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Meta theory identifies specific phenomena through abstract concepts; it is the broadest, most abstract level (theory of theory).  Why A wrong: Grand theory is broad and complex.  Why C wrong: Middle range theory addresses specific phenomena and offers a clinically usable level.  Why D wrong: Practice theory addresses a particular situation/nursing concept."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"The type of nursing theory described as \"broad and complex\" is:","options":["Meta theory","Practice theory","Grand theory","Middle range theory"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Grand theory is described as broad and complex.  Why A wrong: Meta theory is the most abstract (theory of theory).  Why B wrong: Practice theory is situation-specific.  Why D wrong: Middle range theory addresses specific phenomena."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"A nursing theory that addresses specific phenomena and offers an effective practice level (e.g., Hildegard Peplau's theory of interpersonal relations) is classified as a:","options":["Grand theory","Meta theory","Middle range theory","Practice theory"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Middle range theory addresses specific phenomena and offers an effective practice level, with Hildegard Peplau's interpersonal relations theory cited as an example.  Why A wrong: Grand theory is broad and complex.  Why B wrong: Meta theory is the most abstract.  Why D wrong: Practice theory is the most situation-specific."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"The type of nursing theory that identifies a particular nursing situation/concept and gives explicit goals and details on how they will be achieved is:","options":["Practice theory","Grand theory","Meta theory","Middle range theory"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Practice theory identifies a particular situation/nursing concept and gives explicit goals and details of how they are to be achieved.  Why B wrong: Grand theory is broad/complex, not situation-specific.  Why C wrong: Meta theory is the most abstract.  Why D wrong: Middle range theory addresses specific phenomena but is broader than practice theory."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"There are four concepts known as the metaparadigm of nursing. Which of the following are these four concepts? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Person","Environment","Health","Nursing","Hospital administration"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source identifies the four metaparadigm concepts as person, environment, health, and nursing.  Why E wrong: Hospital administration is not part of the nursing metaparadigm."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"Within the nursing metaparadigm, which component is described as the most important and is the central concept/focal point in nursing theories?","options":["Environment","Health","Person / man / patient","Nursing"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the main focal point/most important concept of the four is the person/man/patient, around whom nursing theories are built.  Why A, B, D wrong: Environment, health, and nursing are part of the metaparadigm but the source names person/man/patient as the central focal point."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"A metaparadigm in nursing is best described as:","options":["A single nursing intervention","A set of theories or ideas that provide structure for how a discipline should function","A specific drug protocol","A documentation format"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines a metaparadigm as a set of theories or ideas that provide structure for how a discipline should function.  Why A, C, D wrong: These are not the source's definition of a metaparadigm."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"In the summary of nursing theories, \"Environmentalist theory\" focused on environmental cleanliness, ventilation, temperature, light, and noise. This theory is attributed to:","options":["Hildegard Peplau","Florence Nightingale (1860)","Virginia Henderson","Dorothea Orem"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Florence Nightingale (1860) is credited with the Environmental theory focusing on cleanliness, ventilation, temperature, light, and noise.  Why A wrong: Peplau is associated with interpersonal relationship theory.  Why C wrong: Henderson is associated with nursing need theory.  Why D wrong: Orem is associated with self-care deficit theory."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"The \"Theory of Interpersonal Relationship\" (1952) is credited to which nursing theorist?","options":["Hildegard Peplau","Virginia Henderson","Myra E. Levine","Imogene King"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hildegard Peplau (1952) is credited with the Theory of Interpersonal Relationship.  Why B wrong: Henderson is linked to nursing need theory.  Why C wrong: Levine is linked to conservation model.  Why D wrong: King is linked to theory of goal attainment."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"Virginia Henderson (1955) is associated with which nursing theory?","options":["Self-care deficit theory","Nursing need theory","Conservation model","Theory of goal attainment"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Virginia Henderson (1955) is credited with the Nursing need theory.  Why A wrong: Self-care deficit theory belongs to Dorothea Orem.  Why C wrong: Conservation model belongs to Myra E. Levine.  Why D wrong: Theory of goal attainment belongs to Imogene King."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"The \"Self-care deficit theory\" (1971) is attributed to which nursing theorist?","options":["Dorothea Orem","Sr Callista Roy","Madeleine Leininger","Florence Nightingale"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Dorothea Orem (1971) is credited with the Self-care deficit theory.  Why B wrong: Roy is linked to adaptation model.  Why C wrong: Leininger is linked to transcultural nursing/culture care theory.  Why D wrong: Nightingale is linked to environmental theory."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"Match the theorist to the correct theory: Myra E. Levine (1967), Imogene King (1971), Sr Callista Roy (1974), Madeleine Leininger (1978). Which pairing is correct?","options":["Levine — Theory of Goal Attainment","King — Conservation Model","Roy — Roy Adaptation Model","Leininger — Conservation Model"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Sr Callista Roy (1974) is credited with the Roy Adaptation Model.  Why A wrong: Levine (1967) is the Conservation Model, not goal attainment.  Why B wrong: King (1971) is the Theory of Goal Attainment, not conservation model.  Why D wrong: Leininger (1978) is the Theory of transcultural nursing (culture care theory), not conservation model."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"Madeleine Leininger (1978) is credited with which theory?","options":["Conservation model","Theory of transcultural nursing, also known as culture care theory","Theory of goal attainment","Nursing need theory"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Leininger (1978) developed the Theory of transcultural nursing, also known as the culture care theory.  Why A wrong: Conservation model is Levine's.  Why C wrong: Goal attainment is King's.  Why D wrong: Nursing need theory is Henderson's."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"An anatomical plane is best described as:","options":["A real bony structure in the body","A hypothetical (imaginary) flat surface used to transect the body to locate or describe the position of structures or the direction of movements","A type of nursing intervention","A surgical instrument"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: An anatomical plane is a hypothetical plane used to transect the body in order to describe the location of structures or the direction of movements.  Why A wrong: A plane is imaginary, not a bony structure.  Why C, D wrong: Unrelated to anatomical planes."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"A cardinal plane is one that:","options":["Passes through the centre of the body and divides the body into equal parts","Always divides the body into unequal portions","Is the same as the longitudinal axis","Cannot be sagittal, frontal, or transverse"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A cardinal plane is referred to as the plane that passes through the centre of the body and divides it into equal parts; in humans there are three principal planes, all cardinal — sagittal, frontal/coronal, and transverse.  Why B wrong: Cardinal planes divide into equal parts.  Why C wrong: A plane is not an axis.  Why D wrong: The cardinal planes ARE the sagittal, frontal, and transverse planes."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"The sagittal plane divides the body into:","options":["Superior and inferior portions","Anterior and posterior portions","Left and right portions","Dorsal and ventral halves"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The sagittal plane (also lateral plane, longitudinal plane, anteroposterior plane) divides the body into left and right.  Why A wrong: That is the transverse plane.  Why B wrong: That is the frontal/coronal plane.  Why D wrong: Dorsal/ventral describes the frontal plane's division (back and front)."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"Which of the following are alternate names for the sagittal plane? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Lateral plane","Longitudinal plane","Anteroposterior plane","Coronal plane","Horizontal plane"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The source gives lateral plane, longitudinal plane, and anteroposterior plane as other names for the sagittal plane.  Why D wrong: Coronal plane is another name for the frontal plane.  Why E wrong: Horizontal plane is another name for the transverse plane."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"The frontal plane (also called the coronal plane or vertical plane) divides the body into:","options":["Left and right","Dorsal (back) and ventral (front), i.e., posterior and anterior portions","Superior and inferior","Proximal and distal"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The frontal plane divides the body into dorsal and ventral (back and front, posterior and anterior) portions; its other names are coronal plane and vertical plane.  Why A wrong: That is the sagittal plane.  Why C wrong: That is the transverse plane.  Why D wrong: Proximal/distal is not a plane-based division here."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"The transverse plane (also called the axial plane or horizontal plane) divides the body into:","options":["Left and right","Anterior and posterior","Superior and inferior (cranial and caudal / head and tail) portions","Medial and lateral"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The transverse plane divides the body into superior and inferior (cranial and caudal, head and tail) portions; its other names are axial plane and horizontal plane.  Why A wrong: Sagittal divides left/right.  Why B wrong: Frontal divides anterior/posterior.  Why D wrong: Medial/lateral is not the transverse division."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"A body axis is best described as:","options":["A flat hypothetical surface","A straight line around which an object rotates, with movement at a joint taking place in a plane about an axis","A type of bone","A muscle group"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: An axis is a straight line around which an object rotates; movement at a joint takes place in a plane about an axis.  Why A wrong: That describes a plane, not an axis.  Why C, D wrong: An axis is not a bone or muscle group."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"How many axes of rotation are described in the source, and what are they?","options":["Two — sagittal and frontal","Three — sagittal axis, frontal axis, and vertical axis","Four — sagittal, frontal, vertical, and oblique","One — the longitudinal axis only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states there are three axes of rotation: sagittal axis, frontal axis, and vertical axis.  Why A, C, D wrong: The source specifies exactly three axes."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"Regarding the orientation of body axes, which statement is correct?","options":["The sagittal axis passes horizontally from posterior to anterior and is formed by the intersection of the sagittal and transverse planes","The sagittal axis passes vertically from head to toe","The frontal axis passes from medial to lateral only","The vertical axis passes horizontally from left to right"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The sagittal axis passes horizontally from posterior to anterior and is formed by the intersection of the sagittal and transverse planes.  Why B wrong: The vertical axis is the head-to-toe orientation.  Why C wrong: The source describes the frontal axis as passing horizontally from left to right, formed by intersection of the frontal and transverse planes.  Why D wrong: The vertical axis is vertical, formed by intersection of sagittal and frontal planes, not horizontal left-to-right."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"Bed making is best defined as:","options":["A technique used to prepare and arrange beds in different types so that they are comfortable according to the situation and procedure","Only the act of changing pillowcases","A surgical sterilisation procedure","A documentation method"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Bed making is a technique used in which different types of beds are prepared to make a client or patient comfortable according to the situation and procedures; it may vary with the client's condition, purpose, and procedures.  Why B wrong: It is more than changing pillowcases.  Why C, D wrong: Bed making is neither sterilisation nor documentation."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"Which of the following is a purpose of bed making? (Select all that apply.)","options":["To organise a specific ward and provide a safe and comfortable bed to the client/patient","To be prepared for any critical or emergency condition","To promote neat appearance and cleanliness","To teach relatives to take care of the sick at home and to prevent bed sores"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists organising a specific ward, providing a safe and comfortable bed, being prepared for critical/emergency conditions, promoting neat appearance and cleanliness, teaching relatives to take care of the sick at home, and preventing bedsores as purposes of bed making.  All four options are stated purposes."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"A bed that is about to be occupied by a new patient or an ambulatory patient is called:","options":["Closed bed","Open bed","Occupied bed","Cardiac bed"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: An open bed is one that is about to be occupied by a new patient or an ambulatory patient.  Why A wrong: A closed bed is not in use; after admission it is converted to an open bed.  Why C wrong: An occupied bed is for patients who cannot move out of bed.  Why D wrong: A cardiac bed is for cardiac/respiratory patients."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"A closed bed is best described as:","options":["A bed occupied by a patient who cannot move","A bed not occupied by any patient; after admission of a patient, the closed bed is converted to an open bed","A bed used during surgery","A bed for amputated patients"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A closed bed is an empty bed not occupied by any patient; after a patient's admission, the closed bed is converted to an open bed.  Why A wrong: That describes an occupied bed.  Why C wrong: That refers to an operation bed/PACU/recovery bed.  Why D wrong: That describes an amputation/stump bed."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"An \"admission bed\" is:","options":["A bed for cardiac patients only","An open bed where the patient gets into the bed after thorough bath and changing into hospital dress","A bed for patients who cannot move","A bed prepared during surgery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The admission bed is made as an open bed; the patient gets into the bed after a thorough bath and changing into a hospital dress.  Why A, C, D wrong: These describe cardiac, occupied, and operation beds respectively."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"An \"occupied bed\" is made for:","options":["New ambulatory patients","Patients getting discharged","Patients who cannot move out of the bed","Patients recovering from anesthesia"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: An occupied bed is for those patients who cannot move out of the bed.  Why A wrong: New ambulatory patients use an open bed.  Why B wrong: Discharge is unrelated to this bed type.  Why D wrong: Patients recovering from anesthesia use the operation/PACU/recovery bed."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"An \"operation bed\" (also called PACU bed or recovery bed) is made for:","options":["Ambulatory patients","Patients who are recovering from the effect of anesthesia","Patients with skin grafts","Amputated patients"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The operation bed / PACU bed / recovery bed is for patients recovering from the effect of anesthesia.  Why A wrong: That is an open bed.  Why C, D wrong: These describe other specialized beds."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"A \"cardiac bed\" is described as a bed that:","options":["Is kept flat at all times","Is used for patients with skin grafts","Has the head end kept high (in a sitting position) with back rest and pillows to relieve dyspnea","Is divided into two halves"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: A cardiac bed keeps the patient in a sitting position with back rest and pillows to relieve dyspnea.  Why A wrong: It is not flat; the head end is elevated.  Why B wrong: Skin graft positioning is not this bed's purpose.  Why D wrong: Being divided/separated relates to the fracture bed (firm) or amputation bed, not the defining cardiac feature."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"A \"fracture bed\" is prepared for a patient with fracture and is described as:","options":["A soft, sagging bed","A firm bed; it provides support to the fractured part using a fracture board, with a firm mattress which rests on the fracture or bed board","A bed used only for amputated patients","A bed that is always elevated at the foot end"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The fracture bed is prepared for patients with fracture; it provides support to the fractured part with a firm bed, using a fracture board and a firm mattress that rests on the fracture or bed board.  Why A wrong: It must be firm, not soft/sagging.  Why C wrong: That is an amputation/stump bed.  Why D wrong: Foot-end elevation is not its defining feature."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"An \"amputation bed\" (stump bed) is best described as a bed that:","options":["Helps to take off the weight of the bed linen on the amputated part and consists of a bed cradle which takes off bed-linen weight from one end of the body","Is used for cardiac patients","Is used during surgery only","Is always kept flat"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: An amputation/stump bed takes off the weight of the bed linen on the amputated part and consists of a bed cradle that takes the bed-linen weight off one end of the body.  Why B, C, D wrong: These describe cardiac, operation, and unrelated bed features."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"During the bed-making procedure, what is the FIRST step the nurse should perform?","options":["Remove the top linen","Wash hands","Remove the pillow and place it on the chair","Fold the draw sheet"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists \"Wash hands\" as the first step of the bed-making procedure (infection control before patient/linen handling).  Why A, C, D wrong: These steps follow washing hands; removing the pillow comes after hand hygiene."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"In the bed-making procedure, after removing the bottom sheet and soiled mattress cover and dusting the mattress with a dry duster, the nurse should:","options":["Leave the mattress bare","Place the bottom sheet on the mattress and tuck it securely at the top on the near side","Place the pillow on the foot end","Throw the soiled linen on the floor"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After dusting the mattress with a dry duster, the bottom sheet is placed on the mattress and tucked securely at the top on the near side.  Why A wrong: The mattress is covered with the bottom sheet, not left bare.  Why C wrong: The pillow is placed at the head end later in the procedure.  Why D wrong: Soiled linen is removed appropriately, not thrown on the floor."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"When placing the mackintosh during bed making, it should be placed:","options":["At the head end of the bed","Nearly 37 cm from the head end and tucked in along the sides","Only at the foot end","On top of the blanket"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The mackintosh is placed nearly 37 cm from the head end and tucked in along the sides.  Why A, C, D wrong: The source specifies the 37 cm placement from the head end with side tucking, not these positions."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"During bed making, where should the blanket be placed relative to the top sheet?","options":["15 to 20 cm below the top of the top sheet","Exactly level with the top sheet","37 cm above the top sheet","Under the mattress"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The blanket is placed over the top sheet 15 to 20 cm below the top of the sheet.  Why B, C, D wrong: These do not match the specified 15–20 cm placement."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"In the comfort devices table, an \"air ring\" is described as:","options":["A cardiac support device","A device made up of rubber that can be inflated with air, usually used by bed-ridden clients to prevent bed sore/pressure sore","A board used to support the foot end","A wedge to keep legs abducted"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The air ring is made of rubber, can be inflated with air, and is usually used by bed-ridden clients to prevent bed sore/pressure sore.  Why A, C, D wrong: These describe other comfort devices (cardiac table, footboard, hip abduction wedge)."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"A \"bed cradle\" is used to:","options":["Support the foot end only","Support the weight of the top sheet or clothing of the client; it is widely used in burns","Maintain a fracture in alignment","Prevent foot drop"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: An umbrella-type device that supports and takes off the weight of the top sheet or clothing of the client; widely used in burns.  Why A wrong: That is a footboard's role.  Why C wrong: That is a splint's role.  Why D wrong: Preventing foot drop is the footboard/foot device role."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"\"Bed blocks\" are used to:","options":["Maintain proper position of fractured parts","Raise the foot end or head end of the client's bed","Relieve discomfort in fracture","Prevent foot drop"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Bed blocks are used to raise the foot end and/or head end of the client's bed.  Why A wrong: That is a splint's function.  Why C wrong: That is a sand bag's function.  Why D wrong: That is the footboard's function."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"A \"back rest\" comfort device is used to:","options":["Support the client while in a sitting position, used in cardiac and respiratory difficulties","Maintain proper position of fractured parts","Prevent pressure sores in heels","Keep the client's leg abducted"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A back rest is used to support the client while in a sitting position, used in cardiac and respiratory difficulties.  Why B wrong: That is a splint's function.  Why C wrong: That is a heel ring's function.  Why D wrong: That is the hip abduction wedge function."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"A \"sand bag\" comfort device is used to:","options":["Inflate with air to prevent pressure sores","Immobilise a part or relieve discomfort, as in fracture","Support the client while sitting","Raise the bed height"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A sand bag is used to immobilise a part or relieve discomfort, as in fracture.  Why A wrong: That is the air ring's role.  Why C wrong: That is the back rest's role.  Why D wrong: That is bed blocks' role."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"An \"air mattress\" comfort device is used:","options":["In ICU or for those who are on long-time bed rest, to prevent bed sores or pressure sores","To raise the foot end of the bed","To support the client while sitting","To immobilise a fractured limb"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: An air mattress is used in ICU or for those on long-time bed rest to prevent bed sores or pressure sores.  Why B, C, D wrong: These describe bed blocks, back rest, and splint functions."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"A \"cardiac table\" (over-bed table) comfort device is used:","options":["To prevent foot drop","In cardiac and respiratory discomforts, in which client leans forward and takes rest","To prevent pressure sores in the heel","To keep the leg abducted"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A cardiac table is used in cardiac and respiratory discomforts, in which the client leans forward and takes rest.  Why A, C, D wrong: These describe footboard, heel ring, and hip abduction wedge functions."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"\"Hand rolls\" as a comfort device are used:","options":["To support the foot end of the bed","By this device to maintain fingers in flexed position","To prevent foot drop","To raise the bed height"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hand rolls are used to maintain fingers in a flexed position.  Why A, C, D wrong: These are not the function of hand rolls."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"A \"trochanter roll\" comfort device is used to:","options":["Prevent external rotation of the femur to popliteal space; it prevents the hip and leg from rotating outwardly, preventing external rotation of the leg while the client is in the supine position","Maintain fingers in flexion","Raise the foot end of the bed","Immobilise the spine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A trochanter roll prevents the external rotation of the femur at the popliteal space, preventing the hip and leg from rotating outwardly (preventing external rotation of the leg while the client is in supine position).  Why B wrong: That is hand rolls.  Why C wrong: That is bed blocks.  Why D wrong: Not the trochanter roll's function."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"A \"hip abduction wedge\" comfort device is used to:","options":["Prevent pressure sores only","Keep the client's legs abducted","Maintain fingers in flexion","Support the back while sitting"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A hip abduction wedge is used to keep the client's legs abducted.  Why A, C, D wrong: These describe air ring/mattress, hand roll, and back rest functions."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"\"Splints\" as comfort devices are used to:","options":["Maintain proper position or alignment of an injured or fractured part","Inflate with air to prevent pressure sores","Raise the foot end of the bed","Support the back while leaning forward"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Splints are used to maintain proper position or alignment of an injured or fractured part.  Why B, C, D wrong: These describe air ring, bed blocks, and cardiac table functions."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"According to the principles of bed making, why is it important to wash hands before and after the bed-making procedure?","options":["To save time","Micro-organisms are present everywhere on used articles, skin, clothing, and environment; hand washing prevents the spread of micro-organisms from the contaminated source to the host by taking precautions and preventing the multiplication of micro-organisms","To keep the linen warm","To make the bed look attractive"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The principle states micro-organisms are present everywhere and hand washing before and after bed making prevents the spread of micro-organisms from the contaminated source to the host and prevents their multiplication.  Why A, C, D wrong: These are not the stated infection-control rationale."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"Per the principles of bed making, to prevent direct contact with dust and micro-organisms, the nurse should:","options":["Use bare hands to handle linen","Use a folded linen and turn the soiled linen inside while folding linen and using dust and micro-organism precautions","Shake the linen vigorously in the air","Place soiled linen on the floor"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The principles state linen should be folded to prevent direct contact with dust and micro-organisms, and soiled linen should be turned inside; linen should be disinfected before sending it to the laundry.  Why C wrong: Shaking linen disperses micro-organisms (against principle).  Why D wrong: Placing on the floor contaminates it.  Why A wrong: Bare-hand direct contact increases contamination."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"A wrinkled and unmade bed can cause bedsores in bedridden clients because:","options":["Wrinkles look untidy only","A wrinkled bed exerts pressure on bony prominence and lumps, and a smooth/comfortable bed helps provide rest and sleep and avoids complications like bedsores","Wrinkles increase room temperature","Wrinkles improve circulation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The principle explains that wrinkles cause bedsores because a wrinkled bed exerts pressure on bony prominence and lumps; a smooth, comfortable bed provides rest/sleep and avoids complications such as bedsores.  Why A wrong: It is not merely about appearance.  Why C, D wrong: Wrinkles do not change temperature and do not improve circulation."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"Which of the following are stated nursing actions to keep the bed smooth, comfortable, and free of pressure points? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Pull the bottom sheet tightly to avoid wrinkles","Tuck the linen far enough and tightly to keep it fixed","Smooth the mattress if there is a collection of cotton in one place before making a bed","Do not keep any wet linen on the bed","Daily dusting should be done to remove any peeling of seeds that are adhered to the bed linen of a bedridden client"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: All five actions are listed in the principles of bed making — pulling the bottom sheet tightly, tucking linen tightly, smoothing collected cotton, avoiding wet linen, and daily dusting to remove peelings adhered to the linen.  All options are explicitly stated."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"Why does the principle of bed making recommend that a bedridden client be moved enough or made to move from side to side?","options":["To entertain the client","To minimise the spread of micro-organisms","To stimulate blood circulation and maintain muscle tone, providing additional comfort to the client","To warm the linen"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Moving the bedridden client from side to side stimulates blood circulation, maintains muscle tone, and gives additional comfort to the client.  Why A, B, D wrong: These are not the stated rationale for repositioning."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"According to the principle of \"good body mechanics maintain body alignment and prevent fatigue,\" when standing to make a bed the nurse should:","options":["Keep the feet together and bend at the waist","Keep the centre of gravity over its base; in standing position, keep a wide base by separating the feet","Stand as far from the bed as possible","Twist the body while tucking the sheets"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The stability of the body is ensured by keeping the centre of gravity over its base; in the standing position the nurse should keep a wide base by separating the feet.  Why A wrong: A narrow base (feet together) reduces stability.  Why C wrong: The nearer the centre of gravity (close to the load), the less strain — so the nurse should be near the bed.  Why D wrong: The nurse should face the work to avoid twisting and overreaching."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"Which body-mechanics principles for bed making are stated in the source? (Select all that apply.)","options":["The nearer the centre of gravity (load) is held, the less strain is placed above shoulder level","Flex the knees and legs when tucking the sheet under the mattress; this position shifts the work to the long and strong muscles of the thigh and keeps the back in good alignment","When placing and tucking the linen, face the direction of the work and move with the work rather than twisting the body and overreaching","Lift the load entirely with the back muscles for speed"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states keeping the load near the centre of gravity reduces strain, flexing the knees/legs shifts work to the strong thigh muscles and keeps the back aligned, and facing the direction of work avoids twisting/overreaching.  Why D wrong: This contradicts the principle; work should be shifted to the long, strong thigh muscles, not the back."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"Under \"planned and organised ways of working to save energy, time, and equipment,\" which actions are recommended during bed making? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Collect and arrange all the articles before starting the bed-making procedure","At the foot end on one side of the bed, make the bed and then go to the opposite side","Remove the bed linen one by one by holding the open end towards the floor when stripping the bed so that the client's possessions and the hospital articles are not sent to the laundry","Fold the bed sheets in a way that they can be reused easily"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: All four are listed under planned and organised working — collecting/arranging articles first, making the bed on one side then moving to the opposite side, removing linen one by one with the open end toward the floor to avoid sending client/hospital articles to the laundry, and folding sheets so they can be reused easily.  All options are explicitly stated."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"Health assessment is best defined as:","options":["The systematic examination of the whole body to collect objective data using various techniques","A purely subjective interview only","The administration of medications","A type of discharge"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Health assessment is the systematic examination of the whole body to collect objective data by using various techniques.  Why B wrong: It collects objective data systematically, not merely subjective interview.  Why C, D wrong: It is not medication administration or a discharge type."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"Why should the nurse expose only the area of the body to be examined during health assessment?","options":["To save time","In order to avoid chilling and to maintain the client's privacy","To impress the client","Because exposure is not allowed by law"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Exposing only the area to be examined avoids chilling and maintains the client's privacy.  Why A, C wrong: Not the stated rationale.  Why D wrong: The reason given is privacy and avoiding chilling, not legality."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"When performing health assessment, the sequence of examination differs between children and adults; in children, the nurse should:","options":["Always proceed from the most invasive to the least invasive","Always proceed from the least invasive or uncomfortable aspect of the exam to the more invasive","Skip uncomfortable parts entirely","Begin with the most uncomfortable maneuver to get it over with"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In children, the nurse should always proceed from the least invasive or uncomfortable aspect of the exam to the more invasive.  Why A, D wrong: This reverses the recommended order.  Why C wrong: The exam is completed in an appropriate order, not skipped."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"Which of the following are stated purposes of health assessment? (Select all that apply.)","options":["To obtain the patient's baseline data about the disease condition","To obtain data that will help establish nursing diagnoses and plans of care","To evaluate the physiological outcomes and make clinical judgments about a client's health status","To identify areas for health promotion and disease prevention"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists obtaining baseline data about the disease condition, establishing nursing diagnoses and plans of care, evaluating physiological outcomes, making clinical judgments about health status, and identifying areas for health promotion and disease prevention as purposes.  All four options are explicitly stated purposes."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"A detailed health assessment performed when the patient enters the hospital, that includes both health history and physical examination, is called:","options":["Ongoing partial health assessment","Comprehensive health assessment","Focused health assessment","Emergency health assessment"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A comprehensive health assessment is a detailed assessment done when the patient enters the hospital and includes the health history and physical examination.  Why A wrong: Ongoing partial assessment is conducted at regular intervals during care.  Why C wrong: Focused assessment targets a specific problem or system.  Why D wrong: Emergency assessment is a rapid, focused assessment in a life-threatening condition."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"The type of assessment conducted at regular intervals during the care of the patient is called:","options":["Comprehensive health assessment","Ongoing partial health assessment","Time-lapsed health assessment","Emergency health assessment"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Ongoing partial health assessment is conducted at regular intervals during the care of the patient.  Why A wrong: Comprehensive is the detailed initial assessment on entering hospital.  Why C wrong: Time-lapsed is reassessment done several months after the initial assessment.  Why D wrong: Emergency assessment is rapid and done in life-threatening situations."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"A \"focused health assessment\" is one that:","options":["Is used to assess specific health problems or issues, focusing on particular needs or health problems (also known as problem-oriented assessment)","Is a detailed full-body assessment on admission","Is performed during life-threatening emergencies","Is a reassessment months after the initial assessment"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A focused health assessment is used to assess specific health problems/issues, focusing on a particular need or health problem; it is also known as a problem-oriented assessment.  Why B wrong: That is comprehensive assessment.  Why C wrong: That is emergency assessment.  Why D wrong: That is time-lapsed assessment."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"An \"emergency health assessment\" is best defined as:","options":["A reassessment several months later","A rapid focused health assessment performed during a life-threatening or emergency situation to determine a fatal condition","A detailed assessment on admission","An assessment at regular intervals"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: An emergency health assessment is a rapid focused health assessment performed during a life-threatening or emergency situation to determine a fatal condition.  Why A wrong: That is time-lapsed assessment.  Why C wrong: That is comprehensive assessment.  Why D wrong: That is ongoing partial assessment."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"A \"time-lapsed health assessment\" is defined as:","options":["A rapid emergency assessment","A detailed admission assessment","A reassessment of a client done several months after the initial assessment","An assessment at regular intervals during care"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: A time-lapsed health assessment is a reassessment of a client done several months after the initial assessment.  Why A wrong: That is emergency assessment.  Why B wrong: That is comprehensive assessment.  Why D wrong: That is ongoing partial assessment."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"The two main components of health assessment are:","options":["Health history and physical examination","Medication and discharge","Bed making and documentation","Subjective interview and billing"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists the two components of health assessment as (1) health history and (2) physical examination.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not the two stated components."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"Health history is best described as:","options":["The physical examination findings","The systematic collection of data, usually divided into objective and subjective data","A type of discharge","A bed-making procedure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Health history is the systematic collection of data and is usually divided into objective and subjective data.  Why A wrong: That is physical examination, the other component.  Why C, D wrong: Unrelated to health history."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"\"Objective data\" in health assessment are also known as overt data or signs. Which of the following statements about objective data are correct? (Select all that apply.)","options":["They are overt and observable/measurable","They are detectable by the doctor or nurse","They are obtained through observation, physical examination, laboratory, or diagnostic testing","Examples include fever, fracture, skin abrasion, and heart murmur","They are private feelings reported only by the client"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Objective data (overt data/signs) are overt and observable/measurable, detectable by the doctor or nurse, obtained through observation, physical examination, laboratory, or diagnostic testing; examples include fever, fracture, skin abrasion, and heart murmur (by stethoscope, discolouration of skin).  Why E wrong: That describes subjective data (covert data/symptoms), which only the client can report."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"\"Subjective data\" in health assessment are also known as covert data or symptoms. Which statements about subjective data are correct? (Select all that apply.)","options":["They are also known as covert data or symptoms","They are verified or described only by the person who is affected","They are information that collects from the client, including the client's feelings, perceptions, values, beliefs, attitudes, and concerns obtained through interviews","Examples include pain, fear, misperceptions, itching, \"I have pain,\" and \"I am so hungry\"","They are always measurable by laboratory tests"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Subjective data (covert data/symptoms) are verified or described only by the affected person; they are information collected from the client including feelings, perceptions, values, beliefs, attitudes, and concerns obtained through interviews; examples include pain, fear, misperceptions, itching, \"I have pain,\" and \"I am so hungry.\"  Why E wrong: Measurable laboratory data are objective data, not subjective."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"A client states, \"I have pain and I feel so hungry,\" while the nurse documents a temperature of 101°F and a visible skin abrasion. Which data are subjective?","options":["The temperature reading","The skin abrasion","The client's statements of pain and hunger","The diagnostic laboratory result"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Subjective data are symptoms described only by the affected person, such as \"I have pain\" and \"I am so hungry.\"  Why A wrong: A measured temperature is objective data.  Why B wrong: A visible skin abrasion is observable/objective data.  Why D wrong: Laboratory results are objective data."},{"section":"Batch 002 · nursing intervention types…","question":"Which of the following is the BEST example of objective data the nurse can identify by auscultation?","options":["The client reporting itching","A heart murmur detected by stethoscope","The client reporting fear","The client stating misperceptions"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A heart murmur detected by stethoscope is an example of objective data (a sign detectable by the nurse/doctor through physical examination).  Why A, C, D wrong: Itching, fear, and misperceptions are subjective data (symptoms) reported by the client."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"A nurse positions a client in the dorsal recumbent position for examination. Which description correctly defines this position?","options":["Back-lying with legs extended, with or without a pillow under the head","Back-lying position with knees flexed and hips externally rotated, a small pillow under the head, and soles of feet on the surface","Side-lying with the lowermost arm behind the body","Lying on the abdomen with the head turned to the side"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Dorsal recumbent is a back-lying position with knees flexed, hips externally rotated, a small pillow under the head, and soles of the feet resting on the surface.  Why A wrong: That describes the supine position.  Why C wrong: That describes the Sims' position.  Why D wrong: That describes the prone position."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"Which body areas are best assessed with the client in the dorsal recumbent position?","options":["Posterior thorax and hip joint movement","Head, neck, axillae, and peripheral pulses","Female genitals, rectum, and female reproductive tract","Rectum and vagina only"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The dorsal recumbent position is used to assess the female genitals, rectum, and female reproductive tract.  Why B wrong: Those are assessed in the supine position.  Why A wrong: Those are assessed in the prone position.  Why D wrong: Rectum and vagina are assessed in the Sims' position."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"The dorsal recumbent position may be contraindicated for which group of clients?","options":["Clients with cardiopulmonary problems","Older adults and weak clients","Clients with limited joint movement","Pediatric clients only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The caution column states the dorsal recumbent position may be contraindicated for clients who have cardiopulmonary problems.  Why B wrong: That caution applies to the sitting position.  Why C wrong: That caution applies to the Sims' position.  Why D wrong: No such restriction is stated for the dorsal recumbent position."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"A client is placed supine for an examination. Which areas can be assessed in this position?","options":["Rectum and vagina","Head, neck, axillae, anterior thorax, lungs, breasts, heart, vital signs, abdomen, extremities, and peripheral pulses","Posterior thorax and hip joint movement","Female genitals and reproductive tract"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The supine position allows assessment of the head, neck, axillae, anterior thorax, lungs, breasts, heart, vital signs, abdomen, extremities, and peripheral pulses.  Why A wrong: Rectum and vagina are assessed in the Sims' position.  Why C wrong: Posterior thorax/hip joint movement is assessed prone.  Why D wrong: Female genitals/reproductive tract is assessed in dorsal recumbent or lithotomy."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"The supine position is poorly tolerated by which clients?","options":["Older adults and weak clients","Clients with cardiovascular and respiratory problems","Clients with limited joint movement","Children"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The supine position is tolerated poorly by clients with cardiovascular and respiratory problems.  Why A wrong: Sitting may require support for older/weak clients, but that is a separate caution.  Why C wrong: Sims' is difficult for those with limited joint movement.  Why D wrong: Not stated."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"A client is asked to sit in a seated position with the back unsupported and legs hanging freely. Which areas does this position permit the nurse to assess?","options":["Female reproductive tract","Rectum and vagina","Head, neck, posterior and anterior thorax, lungs, breasts, axillae, heart, vital signs, upper and lower extremities, and reflexes","Posterior thorax only"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The sitting position allows assessment of the head, neck, posterior and anterior thorax, lungs, breasts, axillae, heart, vital signs, upper and lower extremities, and reflexes.  Why A/B wrong: Those are genitourinary positions (lithotomy/Sims').  Why D wrong: It assesses far more than just the posterior thorax."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"Which caution applies to the sitting position during physical examination?","options":["May be contraindicated for cardiopulmonary clients","Older adults and weak clients may require support","Difficult for clients with limited joint movement","Embarrassing for older adults"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Older adults and weak clients may require support in the sitting position because the back is unsupported and the legs hang freely.  Why A wrong: That caution belongs to dorsal recumbent.  Why C wrong: That caution belongs to Sims'.  Why D wrong: That caution belongs to lithotomy."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"In the lithotomy position, the client is back-lying with feet supported in stirrups and the hips positioned how?","options":["Externally rotated with soles flat on the bed","In line with the edge of the table","Flexed onto the abdomen","Rotated toward the side"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In lithotomy, the feet are supported in stirrups and the hips should be in line with the edge of the table.  Why A wrong: That describes dorsal recumbent foot/hip placement.  Why C/D wrong: Not characteristics of lithotomy."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"The lithotomy position is most appropriate for assessing which areas?","options":["Head, neck, and axillae","Posterior thorax","Female genitals, rectum, and female reproductive tract","Anterior thorax and lungs"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Lithotomy is used to assess the female genitals, rectum, and female reproductive tract.  Why A/B/D wrong: Those areas are assessed in sitting, prone, or supine positions respectively."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"Why might the lithotomy position be a concern for some clients?","options":["It is contraindicated in cardiopulmonary disease","It may be uncomfortable and tiring for older adults and is often embarrassing","It is poorly tolerated by clients with respiratory problems","It is difficult for clients with limited joint movement"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The lithotomy position may be uncomfortable and tiring for older adults and is often embarrassing.  Why A/C wrong: Those cautions belong to dorsal recumbent and supine/prone respectively.  Why D wrong: That caution belongs to Sims'."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"A nurse positions a client in Sims' position. Which description is correct?","options":["Side-lying position with the lowermost arm behind the body, uppermost leg flexed at hip and knee, and upper arm flexed at shoulder and elbow","Back-lying with legs extended","Lying on the abdomen with head turned to the side","Seated with legs hanging freely"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Sims' is a side-lying position with the lowermost arm behind the body, the uppermost leg flexed at the hip and knee, and the upper arm flexed at the shoulder and elbow.  Why B wrong: That is supine.  Why C wrong: That is prone.  Why D wrong: That is sitting."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"Which areas are assessed in the Sims' position?","options":["Posterior thorax","Rectum and vagina","Heart and lungs","Peripheral pulses"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Sims' position is used to assess the rectum and vagina.  Why A wrong: Posterior thorax is assessed prone.  Why C/D wrong: Those are assessed supine/sitting."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"The Sims' position is described as difficult for which clients?","options":["Cardiopulmonary clients","Older adults and people with limited joint movement","Pregnant clients","Pediatric clients"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Sims' is difficult for older adults and people with limited joint movement because of the required flexion and side-lying posture.  Why A wrong: That caution belongs to dorsal recumbent.  Why C/D wrong: Not stated."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"The prone position is used to assess which areas?","options":["Anterior thorax and breasts","Female reproductive tract","Posterior thorax and hip joint movement","Abdomen and peripheral pulses"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In the prone position the client lies on the abdomen, allowing assessment of the posterior thorax and hip joint movement.  Why A/D wrong: Anterior thorax, breasts, abdomen, and peripheral pulses are assessed supine.  Why B wrong: Reproductive tract is assessed in dorsal recumbent/lithotomy."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"A client lies on the abdomen with the head turned to the side, with or without a small pillow. Which position is this, and which clients tolerate it poorly?","options":["Supine; clients with limited joint movement","Prone; often not tolerated by older adults and people with cardiovascular and respiratory problems","Sims'; cardiopulmonary clients","Lithotomy; older adults"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: This is the prone position; it is often not tolerated by older adults and people with cardiovascular and respiratory problems.  Why A/C/D wrong: The described posture (lying on the abdomen, head turned) is prone, not supine, Sims', or lithotomy."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"The \"6 P's of Neurovascular Assessment\" include all of the following EXCEPT:","options":["Pain","Pallor (perfusion)","Pyrexia","Paralysis (movement)"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Pyrexia is NOT one of the 6 P's. The 6 P's are Pain, Pallor (perfusion), Peripheral pulses, Paresthesia (sensation), Paralysis (movement), and Pressure.  Why A/B/D wrong: Pain, Pallor (perfusion), and Paralysis (movement) are all genuine members of the 6 P's."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"In the 6 P's of neurovascular assessment, \"Paresthesia\" specifically refers to which parameter?","options":["Movement","Sensation","Perfusion","Pulse"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Paresthesia in the 6 P's denotes sensation.  Why A wrong: Movement corresponds to paralysis.  Why C wrong: Perfusion corresponds to pallor.  Why D wrong: Pulse corresponds to peripheral pulses."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"Select ALL of the components that belong to the 6 P's of neurovascular assessment.","options":["Pain","Pallor (perfusion)","Peripheral pulses","Pressure","Pulse oximetry"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Pain, Pallor (perfusion), Peripheral pulses, and Pressure are four of the six P's (the other two being Paresthesia and Paralysis).  Why E wrong: Pulse oximetry is not part of the 6 P's list."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"Which examination instrument is used \"to assist viewing of the pharynx or to determine the reactions of the pupils of the eye\"?","options":["Ophthalmoscope","Flashlight or penlight","Otoscope","Tuning fork"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A flashlight or penlight is used to assist viewing of the pharynx and to determine pupillary reactions.  Why A wrong: An ophthalmoscope visualizes the interior of the eye.  Why C wrong: An otoscope visualizes the eardrum and external auditory canal.  Why D wrong: A tuning fork is used for ear (hearing) examination."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"An ophthalmoscope is a lighted instrument used to:","options":["Visualize the eardrum","Test reflexes","Visualize the interior of the eye","Examine the oral cavity"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The ophthalmoscope is a lighted instrument used to visualize the interior of the eye.  Why A wrong: That is the otoscope's purpose.  Why B wrong: A percussion (reflex) hammer tests reflexes.  Why D wrong: Tongue blades are used for oral cavity examination."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"Which instrument is a lighted instrument used to visualize the eardrum and external auditory canal, and to which a nasal speculum may be attached to inspect the nasal cavities?","options":["Otoscope","Ophthalmoscope","Penlight","Percussion hammer"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The otoscope is a lighted instrument that visualizes the eardrum and external auditory canal; a nasal speculum may be attached to it to inspect the nasal cavities.  Why B wrong: The ophthalmoscope visualizes the eye interior.  Why C wrong: The penlight assists pharynx/pupil viewing.  Why D wrong: The percussion hammer tests reflexes."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"The percussion (reflex) hammer is best described as:","options":["An instrument with a rubber head to test reflexes","A device to take a wound sample","An instrument to visualize the eye","A tool for oral cavity examination"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The percussion (reflex) hammer is an instrument with a rubber head used to test reflexes.  Why B wrong: Cotton applicators take wound samples.  Why C wrong: That is the ophthalmoscope.  Why D wrong: That is the tongue blade/depressor."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"According to the equipment table, a tuning fork is used for which examination?","options":["Eye examination","Ear examination","Oral cavity examination","Reflex testing"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The tuning fork is used for ear examination.  Why A wrong: Eye exam uses the ophthalmoscope/penlight.  Why C wrong: Oral cavity uses tongue blades.  Why D wrong: Reflexes are tested with the percussion hammer."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"Cotton applicators are used during health examination to:","options":["Test reflexes","Take a wound sample","Examine the eardrum","Visualize the pupils"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Cotton applicators are used to take a wound sample.  Why A/C/D wrong: Those uses belong to the percussion hammer, otoscope, and penlight respectively."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"According to the equipment table, tongue blades (depressors) are used for:","options":["Oral cavity examination","Ear examination","Wound sampling","Reflex testing"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Tongue blades (depressors) are used for oral cavity examination.  Why B/C/D wrong: Those are the uses of the tuning fork, cotton applicators, and reflex hammer respectively."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"Physical examination is best defined as:","options":["The subjective report given by the client about symptoms","The systematic collection of objective information that is directly observed or elicited through examination techniques","The review of laboratory and imaging results","The recording of the client's medical history"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Physical examination is the systematic collection of objective information that is directly observed or elicited through examination techniques (inspection, palpation, percussion, auscultation).  Why A/D wrong: Those are subjective/historical data, not physical examination.  Why C wrong: That is diagnostic test review, not physical examination."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"What is the correct sequence (IPPA) of a routinely performed physical examination?","options":["Inspection, Auscultation, Percussion, Palpation","Inspection, Palpation, Percussion, Auscultation","Palpation, Inspection, Auscultation, Percussion","Percussion, Palpation, Inspection, Auscultation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The routine physical examination sequence is IPPA: Inspection, Palpation, Percussion, Auscultation.  Why A wrong: IAPP is the special abdominal sequence, not the routine one.  Why C/D wrong: They do not follow the IPPA order."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"During an abdominal examination, which sequence (IAPP) of techniques is followed?","options":["Inspection, Palpation, Percussion, Auscultation","Inspection, Auscultation, Percussion, Palpation","Auscultation, Inspection, Palpation, Percussion","Palpation, Percussion, Inspection, Auscultation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For abdominal examination the sequence is IAPP: Inspection, Auscultation, Percussion, Palpation.  Why A wrong: That is the routine IPPA sequence used elsewhere.  Why C/D wrong: They do not match IAPP."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"Why is auscultation performed BEFORE palpation and percussion during an abdominal examination?","options":["Because palpation and percussion cause movement or stimulation of the bowel, which can increase bowel motility and heighten bowel sounds, creating a false result","Because auscultation is the fastest technique","Because the stethoscope must be warmed first","Because palpation is contraindicated in the abdomen"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Auscultation is done before palpation and percussion because palpation and percussion cause movement or stimulation of the bowel, which can increase bowel motility and thus heighten bowel sounds, creating a false result.  Why B/C wrong: Speed and stethoscope warming are not the stated reasons.  Why D wrong: Palpation is part of the abdominal exam, just performed last."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"When assessing the abdomen, which technique does the nurse perform FIRST?","options":["Auscultation","Palpation","Inspection","Percussion"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: When assessing the abdomen, the nurse performs inspection first, followed by auscultation, percussion, and/or palpation.  Why A/B/D wrong: These follow inspection in the IAPP order."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"During an abdominal examination, in which position should the client be placed?","options":["Prone position","Supine position","Lithotomy position","Sims' position"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: During abdominal examination, place the client in the supine position.  Why A/C/D wrong: The text specifies supine for abdominal examination."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"Which of the following is the BEST description of inspection as an examination technique?","options":["Tapping the body with a finger to produce sound","Listening to body sounds with a stethoscope","Visual examination of the body using the sense of sight, aided by articles such as a torch, proctoscope, laryngoscope, and otoscope","Using the hands and finger pads to gather information"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Inspection is the visual examination of the body using the sense of sight, with the help of articles such as a torch, proctoscope, laryngoscope, and otoscope, observing colour, shape, size, symmetry, movement, moisture, texture, and position.  Why A wrong: That is percussion.  Why B wrong: That is auscultation.  Why D wrong: That is palpation."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"During inspection, the nurse observes all of the following EXCEPT:","options":["Colour, shape, and size","Symmetry and movement","Intensity and pitch of internal sounds","Moisture, texture, and position"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Intensity and pitch of sound are characteristics described under percussion/auscultation, not inspection. Inspection observes colour, shape, size, symmetry, movement, moisture, texture, and position.  Why A/B/D wrong: These are all listed observations of inspection."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"Which of the following instruments may be used as aids during inspection?","options":["Torch, proctoscope, laryngoscope, and otoscope","Tuning fork and reflex hammer","Sphygmomanometer and thermometer","Glucometer and pulse oximeter"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Inspection may be aided by a torch, proctoscope, laryngoscope, and otoscope.  Why B/C/D wrong: These instruments are not the inspection aids named in the text."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"Palpation is the examination technique that uses:","options":["A stethoscope to listen to sounds","The hands, palm of the fingers, and fingertips to gather information","Tapping fingers to produce sounds","Visual observation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Palpation uses the hands, palm of the fingers (palm finger), and fingertips to gather information and assess internal organs and tissue turgor.  Why A/C/D wrong: Those describe auscultation, percussion, and inspection respectively."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"What are the two main purposes of palpation as stated in the text?","options":["To assess internal organs and to assess for tissue turgor","To listen to bowel sounds and heart sounds","To produce flatness and dullness","To visualize the eardrum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The stated purpose of palpation is to assess internal organs and to assess for tissue turgor.  Why B wrong: Listening to sounds is auscultation.  Why C wrong: Producing flatness/dullness relates to percussion.  Why D wrong: That is the otoscope's use."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"When assessing a client's body temperature by palpation, which surface of the hand is used and why?","options":["The palmar surface, because it is most sensitive to pressure","The dorsal (back) surface, because it is thick and more sensitive to temperature","The fingertips, because they detect vibration best","The ulnar surface, because it is warmest"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For assessment of body temperature, the dorsal surface (back) of the palm/hand is used because the dorsal surface is thick and more sensitive to temperature.  Why A/C wrong: The palmar fingertip surfaces are used for texture, vibration, fluid, and mass assessment—not temperature.  Why D wrong: Not stated."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"The palmar surfaces of the examiner's fingertips and finger pads are best used to assess which of the following?","options":["Surface temperature only","Texture, vibration, presence of fluid, and the size or consistency of a mass","Reflexes","Heart sounds"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The palmar surfaces of the fingertips and finger pads are used for sensations such as texture, vibration, presence of fluid, and the size and consistency of a mass.  Why A wrong: Surface temperature is assessed with the dorsum of the hand.  Why C/D wrong: Those involve the reflex hammer and stethoscope respectively."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"Why should light palpation be done BEFORE deep palpation?","options":["Because deep palpation is faster","Because the tender area should be palpated last; if a tender or deep organ is palpated first, the client may not cooperate for the lighter palpation or further assessment","Because light palpation requires more force","Because deep palpation is contraindicated"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Light palpation should be done before deep palpation because tender areas should be palpated last; palpating a tender or deep organ first may make the client uncooperative for light palpation or further assessment.  Why A/C wrong: Speed and force are not the stated rationale.  Why D wrong: Deep palpation is a valid technique, just performed later."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"Bimanual palpation is characterized by which of the following?","options":["Using both hands to \"trap\" a structure between them to evaluate organs such as the spleen, kidney, breast, uterus, mouth, lips, and ovary","Applying gentle tactile pressure with one hand","Pressing a structure between the thumb and index finger of one hand","Listening with a stethoscope"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Bimanual palpation involves both hands to trap a structure between them; it is used to evaluate the spleen, kidney, breast, uterus, mouth, lips, and ovary. One hand (sensing hand) relaxes and presses lightly while the active hand applies pressure to the sensing hand.  Why B wrong: That describes light palpation.  Why C wrong: That describes bi-digital examination.  Why D wrong: That is auscultation."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"In bimanual palpation, what are the respective roles of the two hands?","options":["Both hands press equally hard","The sensing hand relaxes and is placed lightly over the skin, while the active hand applies pressure to the sensing hand","Only the active hand touches the client","The sensing hand applies pressure and the active hand relaxes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In bimanual palpation, the sensing hand relaxes and is placed lightly over the skin, and the active hand applies pressure to the sensing hand.  Why A/C/D wrong: They reverse or misstate the roles described."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"During light palpation, the clinician's hand is placed on the part to be examined and the skin is depressed to approximately what depth?","options":["1–2 cm","4–5 cm","6–7 cm","0.5 cm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In light palpation, tactile pressure is applied slowly, gently, and deliberately, depressing the part about 1–2 cm.  Why B wrong: 4–5 cm is the depth for deep palpation.  Why C/D wrong: Not stated."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"Deep palpation is performed to detect which of the following and to what depth is the skin depressed?","options":["Surface temperature; 1–2 cm","Abdominal masses; the skin is depressed about 4–5 cm with the finger held at a greater angle to the body surface","Reflexes; 0.5 cm","Hearing acuity; 6 cm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Deep palpation is done after light palpation to detect abdominal masses; the technique is similar to light palpation except the finger is held at a greater angle to the body surface and the skin is depressed about 4–5 cm.  Why A/C/D wrong: They misstate the purpose and the 4–5 cm depth."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"Bi-digital examination is performed by:","options":["Tapping with the dominant hand","Pressing the structure to be examined between the examiner's thumb and index finger; it is used for evaluation of nodules and lips, and examination of the cheeks and lips","Using both whole hands to trap an organ","Listening with a stethoscope"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Bi-digital examination presses the structure between the examiner's thumb and index finger; it is used for examination of the cheeks and lips and for evaluation of nodules and lips.  Why A wrong: That describes percussion.  Why C wrong: That describes bimanual palpation.  Why D wrong: That is auscultation."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"Percussion is best defined as:","options":["Listening with a stethoscope","Tapping the body with a finger to produce sound, used to determine the size and shape of internal organs","Visual inspection of the skin","Palpating with the dorsum of the hand"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Percussion is tapping the body with a finger to produce sound; it is used to determine the size and shape of internal organs.  Why A/C/D wrong: Those describe auscultation, inspection, and palpation respectively."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"Percussion elicits how many types of sound, and what are they?","options":["Three: flatness, dullness, tympany","Four: flatness, dullness, resonance, tympany","Five: flatness, dullness, resonance, hyper-resonance, and tympany","Six: flatness, dullness, resonance, hyper-resonance, tympany, and crackles"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Percussion elicits five types of sound: flatness, dullness, resonance, hyper-resonance, and tympany.  Why A/B wrong: They omit hyper-resonance and/or resonance.  Why D wrong: Crackles are an adventitious auscultatory sound, not a percussion sound."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"A percussion sound is described according to which four characteristics?","options":["Intensity, pitch, duration, and quality","Colour, shape, size, and symmetry","Texture, vibration, fluid, and mass","Rate, rhythm, volume, and depth"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A percussion sound is described according to its intensity, pitch, duration, and quality.  Why B wrong: Those are inspection observations.  Why C wrong: Those are palpation sensations.  Why D wrong: Not the stated characteristics."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"The purposes of percussion include all of the following EXCEPT:","options":["To assess for growth of a mass/tumor (e.g., lung tumor)","To assess for water, fluid, pus, or air collection in body cavities","To assess for reflexes, location, condition, and density of a body organ (using a percussion hammer)","To measure blood pressure"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Measuring blood pressure is not listed among percussion purposes. The stated purposes are assessing for a mass/tumor, water/fluid/pus/air in cavities, and reflexes/location/condition/density of organs.  Why A/B/C wrong: These are all listed percussion purposes."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"Direct percussion is performed using:","options":["A stethoscope","A single hand or by 2/3/4 fingers","Both hands trapping an organ","A reflex hammer only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Direct percussion is done by using a single hand or by 2/3/4 fingers.  Why A/C/D wrong: A stethoscope is for auscultation; both-hand trapping is bimanual palpation; the reflex hammer is for reflex/density assessment."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"In indirect percussion, which finger acts as the \"pleximeter\"?","options":["The finger that is being struck","The striking finger of the dominant hand","The thumb","The little finger of the striking hand"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In indirect percussion, the pleximeter is the finger being struck on the pleximeter finger (the middle finger of the non-dominant hand). The plexor is the striking finger (flexed middle finger of the dominant hand).  Why B wrong: That is the plexor.  Why C/D wrong: Not described."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"Which percussion tone is an \"extremely dull sound produced by very dense tissue, such as muscle or bone\"?","options":["Flat tone","Dull sound","Resonance","Tympanic tone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A flat tone is an extremely dull sound produced by very dense tissue such as muscle or bone.  Why B wrong: Dull sound is a thud-like soft-to-moderate sound seen in pleural effusion, liver, and spleen.  Why C wrong: Resonance is a moderate-to-loud hollow sound from air-filled lungs.  Why D wrong: Tympanic tone is a loud, musical, drum-like sound from hollow gas-filled organs."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"A \"thud-like, soft to moderate sound\" heard over the liver, spleen, and in pleural effusion describes which percussion tone?","options":["Tympanic tone","Resonance","Dull sound","Hyper-resonance"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Dull sound is a thud-like, soft to moderate sound seen in pleural effusion, liver, and spleen.  Why A wrong: Tympanic is loud and drum-like over gas-filled hollow organs.  Why B wrong: Resonance is moderate to loud and hollow over air-filled lungs.  Why D wrong: Hyper-resonance is very loud over hyper-inflated lungs."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"Which percussion tone is considered the NORMAL lung sound (normal percussion tone)?","options":["Flat tone","Dull sound","Resonance sound","Tympanic tone"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Resonance is a moderate to loud hollow sound produced by lungs filled with air and is considered the normal lung sound or percussion tone.  Why A/B wrong: Flat and dull sounds occur over dense tissue and solid organs—abnormal over lung fields.  Why D wrong: Tympany is over gas-filled hollow organs, not normal lungs."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"A \"very loud sound heard in hyper-inflated lungs, e.g., inflamed lungs, emphysematous lungs\" is which percussion tone?","options":["Resonance","Hyper-resonance tone","Tympanic tone","Dull sound"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hyper-resonance is a very loud sound seen in hyper-inflated lungs such as inflamed lungs and emphysematous lungs.  Why A wrong: Resonance is the normal moderate-to-loud lung tone.  Why C wrong: Tympany is over gas-filled hollow organs.  Why D wrong: Dull sound is over solid organs/effusion."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"A \"loud, musical, drum-like sound\" heard over a hollow organ such as a stomach filled with gas describes which percussion tone?","options":["Tympanic tone","Flat tone","Dull sound","Resonance"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Tympanic tone is a loud, musical, drum-like sound seen in hollow organs such as the stomach filled with gas.  Why B wrong: Flat tone is extremely dull over dense tissue.  Why C wrong: Dull sound is heard over solid organs.  Why D wrong: Resonance is the normal lung tone."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"Auscultation is the physical examination technique done by which instrument?","options":["Reflex hammer","Stethoscope","Ophthalmoscope","Tuning fork"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Auscultation is the physical examination done by stethoscope; it is a technique of listening to body sounds.  Why A/C/D wrong: These instruments are used for reflexes, eye, and ear examination respectively."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"What are the two types of auscultation described?","options":["Manual and automated auscultation","Direct auscultation (with the unaided ear) and indirect auscultation (performed using a stethoscope)","Light and deep auscultation","Surface and deep auscultation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The two types are direct auscultation (by unaided ear) and indirect auscultation (performed using a stethoscope).  Why A/C/D wrong: These categories are not described in the text."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"Auscultated sounds are described according to which four qualities?","options":["Pitch, intensity, duration, and quality","Colour, shape, size, and texture","Rate, rhythm, depth, and volume","Flatness, dullness, resonance, and tympany"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Auscultated sounds are described according to their pitch, intensity, duration, and quality.  Why B wrong: Those are inspection observations.  Why C wrong: Not the stated descriptors.  Why D wrong: Those are percussion tones."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"In auscultation, \"pitch\" refers to which property of a sound?","options":["Loudness or softness of a sound","The frequency/number of vibrations per second","The length of the sound (long or short)","The subjective description of a sound such as whistling or gurgling"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pitch is the frequency/number of vibrations per second.  Why A wrong: That defines intensity (amplitude).  Why C wrong: That defines duration.  Why D wrong: That defines quality."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"Which auscultation descriptor refers to the \"loudness or softness of a sound (amplitude)\"?","options":["Pitch","Intensity","Duration","Quality"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Intensity (amplitude) is the loudness or softness of a sound.  Why A wrong: Pitch is frequency.  Why C wrong: Duration is the length of the sound.  Why D wrong: Quality is the subjective description."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"In describing auscultated sounds, \"duration\" refers to:","options":["Frequency of vibrations","The length of the sound (long or short)","Loudness or softness","Whether the sound whistles or gurgles"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Duration is the length of the sound (long or short).  Why A/C wrong: Those define pitch and intensity.  Why D wrong: That defines quality."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"\"Quality\" of an auscultated sound is best described as:","options":["The number of vibrations per second","The amplitude of the sound","The subjective description of a sound, for example whistling, gurgling, or snapping","The length of the sound"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Quality is the subjective description of a sound, for example whistling, gurgling, or snapping.  Why A/B/D wrong: Those define pitch, intensity, and duration respectively."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"A papule is best defined as:","options":["A reddened, localized collection of edema fluid, irregular in shape","A circumscribed, solid elevation of skin that is less than 1 cm (0.4 in)","A vesicle or bulla filled with pus","A flat area of discoloration"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A papule is a circumscribed, solid elevation of skin; papules are less than 1 cm (0.4 in). Examples include warts, acne, pimples, and elevated moles.  Why A wrong: That defines a wheal.  Why C wrong: That defines a pustule.  Why D wrong: Not the definition of a papule."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"Which of the following are examples of papules?","options":["Hives and mosquito bites","Warts, acne, pimples, and elevated moles","Psoriasis and rubeola","Acne vulgaris and impetigo (pus-filled)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Examples of papules listed are warts, acne, pimples, and elevated moles.  Why A wrong: Hives and mosquito bites are examples of wheals.  Why C wrong: Psoriasis and rubeola are examples of plaques.  Why D wrong: Acne vulgaris and impetigo are examples of pustules."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"A wheal is characterized as:","options":["A solid elevation less than 1 cm","A reddened, localized collection of edema fluid, irregular in shape, with size varying (e.g., hives, mosquito bites)","A plaque larger than 1 cm","A pus-filled vesicle"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A wheal is a reddened, localized collection of edema fluid, irregular in shape, with the size varying; examples include hives and mosquito bites (allergic wheals/urticaria).  Why A wrong: That is a papule.  Why C wrong: That is a plaque.  Why D wrong: That is a pustule."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"Plaques are skin lesions defined by which feature?","options":["Plaques are larger than 1 cm (0.4 in); examples include psoriasis and rubeola","Plaques are pus-filled vesicles","Plaques are circumscribed solid elevations less than 1 cm","Plaques are reddened collections of edema fluid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Plaques are larger than 1 cm (0.4 in); examples include psoriasis and rubeola.  Why B wrong: That is a pustule.  Why C wrong: That is a papule.  Why D wrong: That is a wheal."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"A pustule is described as a:","options":["Vesicle or bulla filled with pus; examples include acne vulgaris and impetigo","Flat discolored area","Solid elevation less than 1 cm","Reddened edema-fluid collection"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A pustule is a vesicle or bulla filled with pus; examples include acne, (acne) vulgaris, and impetigo.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe other lesion types (macule-like, papule, and wheal respectively)."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"Yellowish discoloration of overall skin areas, mucous membranes, and sclera (jaundice) is most likely caused by:","options":["Decreased hemoglobin","Liver disease (increase in bilirubin levels)","Cold environment","Sun exposure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Yellowish colour (jaundice), assessed in overall skin areas, mucous membranes, and sclera, is caused by liver disease (an increase in bilirubin levels).  Why A wrong: Decreased hemoglobin causes pallor.  Why C wrong: Cold environment causes cyanosis.  Why D wrong: Sun exposure causes tanning/brown discoloration."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"Bluish discoloration (cyanosis) of the lips, inside of the mouth, hands, feet, and nail beds is most associated with:","options":["Increased bilirubin","A cold environment, cardiac, or respiratory disease (decreased oxygenation)","Anemia","Increased melanin production"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Cyanosis (bluish colour), assessed in exposed areas particularly the ears, lips, inside of the mouth, hands, feet, and nail beds, results from a cold environment or cardiac/respiratory disease (decreased oxygenation).  Why A wrong: Increased bilirubin causes jaundice.  Why C wrong: Anemia causes pallor.  Why D wrong: Increased melanin causes brown spots/tanning."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"Redness (erythema, flushing) of the facial area or a localized area of skin may be caused by all of the following EXCEPT:","options":["Blushing","Alcohol intake","Fever or injury trauma/infection","Increased bilirubin levels"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Increased bilirubin causes jaundice (yellow), not redness. Redness (erythema, flushing), assessed in the facial area and localized areas, is caused by blushing, alcohol intake, fever, injury trauma, and infection.  Why A/B/C wrong: Blushing, alcohol intake, and fever/injury trauma/infection are all listed causes of redness."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"Pallor (paleness) of exposed areas, particularly the face, lips, conjunctivae, and mucous membranes, is most commonly caused by:","options":["Anemia (decreased hemoglobin) or shock (decreased blood volume)","Liver disease","Sun exposure","Pregnancy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Pallor (paleness), assessed in exposed areas particularly the face, lips, conjunctivae, and mucous membranes, is caused by anemia (decreased hemoglobin) or shock (decreased blood volume).  Why B wrong: Liver disease causes jaundice.  Why C/D wrong: Sun exposure/pregnancy cause tanning or brown spots."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"Vitiligo, presenting as whitish patchy areas on the skin (overall skin areas, lips, nail beds, conjunctiva), is caused by:","options":["Increased bilirubin","Depigmentation due to congenital or autoimmune conditions","Decreased oxygenation","Fever"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Vitiligo (whitish patchy on skin), assessed over overall skin areas, lips, nail beds, and conjunctiva, is caused by depigmentation from congenital or autoimmune conditions.  Why A wrong: Bilirubin increase causes jaundice.  Why C wrong: Decreased oxygenation causes cyanosis.  Why D wrong: Fever causes redness."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"A tanned or brown discoloration of sun-exposed areas is most likely caused by:","options":["Anemia","Liver disease","Overexposure (increased melanin production) and pregnancy (brown spots)","Cold environment"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Tanned or brown colour of sun-exposed areas is caused by overexposure (increased melanin production) and pregnancy (brown spots).  Why A wrong: Anemia causes pallor.  Why B wrong: Liver disease causes jaundice.  Why D wrong: Cold environment causes cyanosis."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"A nurse needs to test the visual acuity of an illiterate, non-verbal adult client. Which eye chart is most appropriate?","options":["The preschool children's chart","The Snellen standard chart","The Snellen E chart","The Ishihara plate"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The Snellen E chart is designed for illiterate and non-verbal clients, as it uses the letter E in different orientations.  Why A wrong: The preschool children's chart uses pictures/symbols for young children.  Why B wrong: The Snellen standard chart uses letters and requires literacy.  Why D wrong: An Ishihara plate tests color vision, not acuity, and is not among the three charts described."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"Which eye chart is referred to as the \"Snellen standard chart\"?","options":["The chart used for preschool children","The centre chart using standard letters","The chart with E in different directions for illiterate clients","The colour vision chart"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text identifies three types of eye charts: left, the preschool children's chart; centre, the Snellen standard chart; right, the Snellen E chart. The Snellen standard chart is the centre chart.  Why A wrong: That is the preschool children's chart (left).  Why C wrong: That is the Snellen E chart (right).  Why D wrong: No colour vision chart is described."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"The dorsal body cavity is subdivided into which two cavities?","options":["Thoracic and abdominal cavities","Cranium cavity (containing the brain) and spinal cavity (containing the spinal cord)","Pelvic and thoracic cavities","Cranial and pleural cavities"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The dorsal body cavity consists of the cranium cavity, which contains the brain, and the spinal cavity, which contains the spinal cord.  Why A wrong: Thoracic and abdominal cavities belong to the ventral body cavity.  Why C wrong: Pelvic and thoracic cavities are subdivisions of the ventral cavity.  Why D wrong: The pleural cavity is not the named subdivision here."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"Which structures are contained within the cranium cavity and the spinal cavity, respectively?","options":["Heart and lungs; stomach and kidneys","Brain; spinal cord","Spinal cord; brain","Liver; bladder"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The cranium cavity contains the brain and the spinal cavity contains the spinal cord.  Why A wrong: Heart/lungs are in the thoracic cavity; stomach/kidneys in the abdominal cavity.  Why C wrong: It reverses the contents.  Why D wrong: Liver is abdominal; bladder is pelvic."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"The ventral body cavity is divided into which main regions?","options":["Cranial and spinal cavities","Thoracic cavity and abdominopelvic cavity","Pleural and pericardial cavities only","Cranial and thoracic cavities"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The ventral body cavity comprises the thoracic cavity (containing the heart and lungs) and the abdominopelvic cavity.  Why A wrong: Cranial and spinal cavities are dorsal subdivisions.  Why C/D wrong: Not the described divisions."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"Which organs are contained in the thoracic cavity?","options":["Liver, stomach, and kidneys","Heart and lungs","Bladder and reproductive organs","Brain and spinal cord"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The thoracic cavity contains the heart and lungs.  Why A wrong: Those are in the abdominal cavity.  Why C wrong: Those are in the pelvic cavity.  Why D wrong: Those are in the dorsal cavity."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"The abdominal cavity (a subdivision of the abdominopelvic cavity) contains which organs?","options":["Heart and lungs","Liver, stomach, and kidneys","Bladder and rectum","Brain"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The abdominal cavity contains the liver, stomach, and kidneys.  Why A wrong: Heart and lungs are thoracic.  Why C wrong: Bladder and rectum are pelvic.  Why D wrong: Brain is in the cranial cavity."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"The pelvic cavity (a subdivision of the abdominopelvic cavity) contains which of the following?","options":["Bladder, some reproductive organs, and rectum","Heart and lungs","Brain and spinal cord","Liver and stomach"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The pelvic cavity contains the bladder, some reproductive organs, and the rectum.  Why B wrong: Those are thoracic organs.  Why C wrong: Those are dorsal cavity contents.  Why D wrong: Liver and stomach are in the abdominal cavity."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"Which of the following are listed as surface landmarks of the abdominal wall? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Xiphoid process","Costal margins","Anterior superior iliac spines","Inguinal (Poupart's) ligaments","Greater trochanter"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The surface landmarks of the abdominal wall are the xiphoid process, costal margins, midline, anterior superior iliac spines, umbilicus, inguinal (Poupart's) ligaments, and the superior margin of the pubic bone—so A, B, C, and D are all correct.  Why E wrong: The greater trochanter is not listed as a surface landmark of the abdominal wall."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"The inguinal ligament is also known by which eponym, as labeled in the surface landmarks of the abdominal wall?","options":["Cooper's ligament","Poupart's ligament","Treitz ligament","Gimbernat's ligament"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The labeled surface landmark identifies the inguinal ligament as Poupart's ligament.  Why A/C/D wrong: These ligament names are not the eponym given for the inguinal ligament on the figure."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Client positioning for physical examination…","question":"Which midline structure is identified as a surface landmark of the abdominal wall, located between the xiphoid process above and the pubic bone below?","options":["The umbilicus","The costal margin","The anterior superior iliac spine","The greater trochanter"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The umbilicus is the midline surface landmark of the abdominal wall situated between the xiphoid process superiorly and the superior margin of the pubic bone inferiorly.  Why B wrong: Costal margins lie along the lower rib borders, not the midline.  Why C wrong: The anterior superior iliac spines are lateral landmarks.  Why D wrong: The greater trochanter is not an abdominal wall landmark."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"The abdomen can be divided by surface topography into how many quadrants in the \"Four Abdominal Quadrants\" scheme?","options":["Two","Four","Six","Nine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The four-quadrant scheme divides the abdomen into Right Upper, Left Upper, Right Lower, and Left Lower quadrants.  Why A wrong: Two would describe only an upper/lower or right/left split, not the named scheme.  Why C wrong: There is no six-region scheme described.  Why D wrong: Nine refers to the separate \"Nine Abdominal Regions\" scheme."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"Which organ is located in the Right Upper Quadrant (RUQ)?","options":["Spleen","Liver","Sigmoid colon","Appendix"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The liver lies in the Right Upper Quadrant along with the gallbladder, duodenum, head of pancreas, right adrenal gland, and upper lobe of right kidney.  Why A wrong: The spleen is in the Left Upper Quadrant.  Why C wrong: The sigmoid colon is in the Left Lower Quadrant.  Why D wrong: The appendix is in the Right Lower Quadrant."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"A patient with suspected gallbladder disease is most likely to report pain in which quadrant?","options":["Left Upper Quadrant","Left Lower Quadrant","Right Upper Quadrant","Right Lower Quadrant"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The gallbladder is an RUQ structure, so biliary pain typically localizes to the Right Upper Quadrant.  Why A wrong: LUQ contains stomach, spleen, pancreas, left adrenal, upper lobe of left kidney.  Why B wrong: LLQ contains sigmoid colon, left ovary/fallopian tube, etc.  Why D wrong: RLQ contains appendix, cecum, right ovary."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"Which structures are correctly assigned to the Left Upper Quadrant (LUQ)? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Stomach","Spleen","Pancreas","Appendix","Left adrenal gland"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: LUQ contains the left lobe of liver, stomach, spleen, upper lobe of left kidney, pancreas, left adrenal gland, splenic flexure of colon, and sections of transverse/descending colon.  Why D wrong: The appendix is located in the Right Lower Quadrant, not the LUQ."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"McBurney's point pain in appendicitis corresponds to which abdominal quadrant where the appendix and cecum lie?","options":["Right Lower Quadrant","Left Lower Quadrant","Right Upper Quadrant","Left Upper Quadrant"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Right Lower Quadrant (RLQ) contains the cecum, appendix, lower lobe of right kidney, section of ascending colon, right ovary, right fallopian tube, right ureter, and right spermatic cord.  Why B wrong: LLQ contains the sigmoid colon and left adnexal structures.  Why C/D wrong: Upper quadrants do not contain the appendix."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"The sigmoid colon is found in which abdominal quadrant?","options":["Right Upper Quadrant","Left Lower Quadrant","Right Lower Quadrant","Left Upper Quadrant"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Left Lower Quadrant (LLQ) contains the sigmoid colon, lower lobe of left kidney, section of descending colon, left ovary, left fallopian tube, left ureter, left spermatic cord, and part of uterus.  Why A/C/D wrong: None of these quadrants house the sigmoid colon per the source."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"In the nine-region scheme, the gallbladder, right lobe of liver, hepatic flexure of colon, part of duodenum, upper half of right kidney, and suprarenal gland lie in which region?","options":["Right lumbar","Right hypochondriac","Epigastric","Right inguinal"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: These structures are listed under the Right hypochondriac region.  Why A wrong: Right lumbar contains the lower half of right kidney, ascending colon, and part of duodenum & jejunum.  Why C wrong: Epigastric contains pylorus/end of stomach, aorta and pancreas, parts of duodenum and liver.  Why D wrong: Right inguinal contains the appendix, cecum, lower end of ileum, right spermatic cord, right ovary and right ureter."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"Which of the following is contained in the Umbilical region of the nine-region scheme?","options":["Bladder","Mesentery and omentum","Stomach and spleen","Sigmoid colon"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Umbilical region contains the mesentery and omentum, lower part of duodenum, and jejunum and ileum.  Why A wrong: The bladder is in the Hypogastric (pubic) region.  Why C wrong: Stomach and spleen are in the Left hypochondriac region.  Why D wrong: Sigmoid colon is in the Left inguinal region."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"The bladder, ileum, and uterus are located in which of the nine abdominal regions?","options":["Epigastric","Umbilical","Hypogastric (pubic)","Left lumbar"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The Hypogastric (pubic) region contains the bladder, ileum, and uterus.  Why A wrong: Epigastric holds pylorus/end of stomach, aorta, pancreas, parts of duodenum and liver.  Why B wrong: Umbilical holds mesentery/omentum, lower duodenum, jejunum, and ileum.  Why D wrong: Left lumbar holds the descending colon, lower half of left kidney, and part of jejunum and ileum."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"Which structures are found in the Left hypochondriac region? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Stomach","Spleen","Tail of pancreas","Appendix","Splenic flexure of colon"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: The Left hypochondriac region contains the stomach, spleen, tail of pancreas, splenic flexure of colon, upper half of left kidney, and supra renal gland.  Why D wrong: The appendix is in the Right inguinal region."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"The aorta and pancreas, pylorus/end of stomach, and parts of the duodenum and liver are located in which nine-region area?","options":["Umbilical","Epigastric","Right lumbar","Hypogastric"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: These are listed under the Epigastric region.  Why A wrong: Umbilical holds mesentery/omentum, lower duodenum, jejunum and ileum.  Why C wrong: Right lumbar holds the lower half of right kidney and ascending colon.  Why D wrong: Hypogastric holds the bladder, ileum, and uterus."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"The appendix, cecum, lower end of ileum, right ovary, and right ureter are located in which of the nine regions?","options":["Right hypochondriac","Right lumbar","Right inguinal","Hypogastric"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: These structures lie in the Right inguinal region (also includes the right spermatic cord).  Why A wrong: Right hypochondriac holds the gallbladder, right lobe of liver, hepatic flexure.  Why B wrong: Right lumbar holds the lower half of right kidney and ascending colon.  Why D wrong: Hypogastric holds the bladder, ileum, and uterus."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"The descending colon, lower half of left kidney, and part of jejunum and ileum belong to which region?","options":["Left hypochondriac","Left lumbar","Left inguinal","Umbilical"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Left lumbar region contains the descending colon, lower half of left kidney, and part of jejunum and ileum.  Why A wrong: Left hypochondriac holds the stomach, spleen, and tail of pancreas.  Why C wrong: Left inguinal holds the left ovary, fallopian tube, left ureter, left spermatic cord, and sigmoid colon.  Why D wrong: Umbilical holds mesentery/omentum and lower duodenum."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"Which incision is classically used for an open cholecystectomy?","options":["Midline incision","Kocher's incision","McBurney's incision","Pfannenstiel incision"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Kocher's incision is classically used for open cholecystectomy.  Why A wrong: Midline incision is the most common incision for laparotomy and for various abdominal surgeries.  Why C wrong: Gridiron's (McBurney's) incision is for appendectomy.  Why D wrong: Pfannenstiel/Kerr incision is the incision of choice for Caesarean section and abdominal hysterectomy."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"Which abdominal wall incision is the most common incision used for laparotomy and various abdominal surgeries?","options":["Kocher's incision","Lanz incision","Midline incision","Chevron incision"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The midline incision is the most common incision for laparotomy and various abdominal surgeries.  Why A wrong: Kocher's is for open cholecystectomy.  Why B wrong: Lanz incision is used to perform appendectomy.  Why D wrong: Chevron/rooftop is an extension across both sides used to access esophagus, stomach, and liver."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"Gridiron's incision (also called McBurney's incision) is classically used for which procedure?","options":["Caesarean section","Appendectomy","Cholecystectomy","Liver transplantation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Gridiron's (McBurney's) incision is classically used for appendectomy.  Why A wrong: Pfannenstiel/Kerr is used for Caesarean section.  Why C wrong: Kocher's is used for cholecystectomy.  Why D wrong: Mercedes Benz incision is mostly performed in liver transplantation."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"Which incision is also known as the Lanz incision and is used to perform appendectomy?","options":["Lanz incision","Battle incision","Paramedian incision","Transverse incision"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Lanz incision is used to perform appendectomy.  Why B/C/D wrong: Battle, paramedian, and transverse incisions are separate abdominal wall incisions and are not the appendectomy-specific Lanz incision."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"The incision of choice for Caesarean section and abdominal hysterectomy is the:","options":["Kocher's incision","Pfannenstiel/Kerr incision","Rutherford–Morrison incision","Mercedes Benz incision"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pfannenstiel/Kerr incision is the incision of choice for Caesarean section and abdominal hysterectomy.  Why A wrong: Kocher's is for open cholecystectomy.  Why C wrong: Rutherford–Morrison is for right or left sided colonic resection, cecostomy/sigmoid colostomy, and kidney transplantation.  Why D wrong: Mercedes Benz is mostly for liver transplantation."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"The Rutherford–Morrison incision (an oblique muscle-cutting incision) can be used for which procedures? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Right or left sided colonic resection","Cecostomy","Sigmoid colostomy","Kidney transplantation","Open cholecystectomy"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The Rutherford–Morrison incision is an oblique muscle-cutting incision used for right or left sided colonic resection, cecostomy or sigmoid colostomy, and kidney transplantation.  Why E wrong: Open cholecystectomy uses Kocher's incision."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"The Chevron / rooftop incision is the extension of the incision to the other side of the abdomen and is used to access which structures?","options":["Bladder, uterus, and ovaries","Esophagus, stomach, and liver","Appendix and cecum","Sigmoid colon and rectum"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Chevron/rooftop incision is used to access the esophagus, stomach, and the liver.  Why A/C/D wrong: These structures are not the stated targets of the Chevron incision."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"Which incision involves a vertical incision that breaks through the xiphisternum (same indication as the Chevron incision) and is mostly performed in liver transplantation?","options":["Pfannenstiel incision","Mercedes Benz incision","Midline incision","McBurney incision"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Mercedes Benz incision is a Chevron incision with a vertical incision breaking through the xiphisternum, sharing the same indication as the Chevron incision but mostly performed in liver transplantation.  Why A wrong: Pfannenstiel is for Caesarean section/hysterectomy.  Why C wrong: Midline is for laparotomy.  Why D wrong: McBurney is for appendectomy."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"Supine position is also known by which term?","options":["Dorsal recumbent","Dorsal decubitus","Sims' position","Prone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The supine position is also called dorsal decubitus; the patient lies flat on the back.  Why A wrong: Dorsal recumbent is a separate position (supine but with both lower limbs in abduction).  Why C wrong: Sims' is a left lateral position.  Why D wrong: Prone is lying on the abdomen."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"The supine position (client lying flat on the back) is clinically indicated for all of the following EXCEPT:","options":["Male catheterization","Monitoring vital signs","Comfort and physical examination","Female catheterization"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Female catheterization is a clinical indication of the dorsal recumbent position, not the plain supine position.  Why A wrong: Male catheterization is a supine indication.  Why B wrong: Vital signs monitoring is a supine indication.  Why C wrong: Comfort and physical examination are supine indications."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"The dorsal recumbent position is the same as supine but with both lower limbs in which posture, and is indicated for female catheterization and rectal & vaginal examination?","options":["Adduction","Abduction","Full extension","Flexion at 90°"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Dorsal recumbent position is supine with both lower limbs in abduction position; indications include female catheterization and rectal & vaginal examination.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies abduction of the lower limbs, not adduction, extension, or 90° flexion."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"In the prone position the client lies on the abdomen with the face turned to one side. Which of the following are clinical indications of the prone position? (Select All That Apply)","options":["To prevent pressure sores/contractures in bed-ridden clients after fracture or burn","To assess hip joint","In a newborn baby with neural tube defect","During renal biopsy procedure","Female catheterization"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Prone position indications listed are: prevent pressure sores/contractures in bed-ridden clients after fracture or burn, assess hip joint, newborn with neural tube defect, and during renal biopsy procedure.  Why E wrong: Female catheterization is a dorsal recumbent indication, not prone."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"During a renal biopsy procedure, which position is indicated to access the kidney?","options":["Supine","Prone","Dorsal recumbent","Trendelenburg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The prone position is clinically indicated during the renal biopsy procedure (and the specific-procedures table also notes a prone position with a pillow under the abdomen and shoulders during renal biopsy).  Why A wrong: Supine is used for catheterization/vital signs.  Why C wrong: Dorsal recumbent is for female catheterization/vaginal exam.  Why D wrong: Trendelenburg is for pelvic exam, surgery, shock, and venous return."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"A nurse positions a client lying on his/her side. This describes which position?","options":["Supine","Prone","Left lateral position","High Fowler's"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In the left lateral position the client lies on his/her side.  Why A/B wrong: Supine is flat on back; prone is on the abdomen.  Why D wrong: High Fowler's is a sitting position with the head elevated 60–90°."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"The left lateral position is clinically indicated for which of the following? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Enema administration","Suppository administration","Rectal examination","Taking rectal temperature","To assess the hip joint"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Left lateral clinical indications include enema administration, suppository administration, rectal examination, and taking rectal temperature; it is also used during colonoscopy.  Why E wrong: Assessing the hip joint is a prone position indication."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"The left lateral position is also used during which diagnostic procedure?","options":["Bronchoscopy","Colonoscopy","Cystoscopy","Paracentesis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the left lateral position is also used during colonoscopy.  Why A/C/D wrong: These procedures are not the stated indication for the left lateral position."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"Sim's left lateral position is also known as the:","options":["Standard left lateral position","Modified left lateral position","Dorsal recumbent position","Lithotomy position"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Sim's left lateral position is the same as a left lateral position and so is also known as the modified left lateral position.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are distinct positions, not synonyms for Sim's."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"In the Sim's (modified left lateral) position, how are the client's legs arranged?","options":["Both legs straight and parallel","Upper leg flexed at hip and knees, lower leg straight, upper arm in a flexed position","Both knees flexed to 90° and elevated","Lower leg flexed, upper leg straight and extended"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In Sim's position the client's upper leg is flexed at hip and knees, the lower leg is straight, and the upper arm rests in a flexed position on the bed.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the described limb arrangement."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"Sim's left lateral position is clinically indicated for all of the following EXCEPT:","options":["Enema administration","Suppository administration","Rectal examination & vaginal examination","Cardiac resuscitation"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Sim's position indications are enema administration, suppository administration, rectal examination & vaginal examination, and taking rectal temperature — not cardiac resuscitation.  Why A/B/C wrong: All three are listed clinical indications of Sim's position."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"The orthopneic position is also known as the:","options":["Cardiac position","Recovery position","Knee-chest position","Lithotomy position"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The orthopneic position is also known as the cardiac position.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are unrelated, distinct positions."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"In the orthopneic position the client assumes a position the same as sitting but is holding a pillow under the arms and resting the head down on a:","options":["Pillow placed on the lap","Cardiac table","Foot board","Side rail"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the orthopneic position the client holds a pillow under the arms and rests the head down on the cardiac table.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the description; the head rests on the cardiac (over-bed) table."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"The orthopneic (cardiac) position is clinically indicated for which type of patient?","options":["Severe dyspnoea and cardiac clients such as after angina and MI","Hypovolemic shock","Increased intracranial pressure","Post-spinal anaesthesia headache"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Orthopneic clinical indications are severe dyspnoea and cardiac clients such as after angina and MI.  Why B wrong: Shock is managed with Trendelenburg.  Why C wrong: Increased ICP requires head elevation/avoiding Trendelenburg, not orthopneic.  Why D wrong: PDPH/spinal headache is managed flat (supine after spinal/lumbar puncture)."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"(RECALL — THE NUGGET) Which position is used to relieve dyspnoea?","options":["Fowler's position","Supine position","Prone position","Lateral position"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The boxed \"Recall the nugget\" answer key gives the right answer as A — Fowler's position relieves dyspnoea by promoting lung expansion.  Why B/C/D wrong: Supine, prone, and lateral positions do not optimally relieve dyspnoea; an upright Fowler's posture eases breathing."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"In Low Fowler's position, the client's head end is elevated at what angle?","options":["15° to 30°","30° to 45°","45° to 60°","60° to 90°"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Low Fowler's position has the head end elevated at 15° to 30°.  Why B wrong: 30°–45° is Semi-Fowler's (usually 45°).  Why C wrong: 45°–60° is Fowler's (usually 60°).  Why D wrong: 60°–90° is High/Full Fowler's (usually 90°)."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"Low Fowler's position is clinically indicated for clients with which conditions?","options":["Increased ICP and for comfort","Hypovolemic shock","Severe dyspnoea","Vaginal examination"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Low Fowler's clinical indications are clients with increased ICP and for comfort.  Why B wrong: Shock uses Trendelenburg.  Why C wrong: Severe dyspnoea is orthopneic/high Fowler's.  Why D wrong: Vaginal exam is dorsal recumbent/lithotomy/Sim's."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"In Fowler's position the head end is elevated at what angle (usually 60°), promoting comfort and minimizing/decreasing respiratory efforts?","options":["15°–30°","30°–45°","45°–60°","60°–90°"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Fowler's position elevates the head end at 45°–60° (usually 60°), promoting comfort and minimizing/decreasing respiratory efforts.  Why A wrong: 15°–30° is Low Fowler's.  Why B wrong: 30°–45° is Semi-Fowler's.  Why D wrong: 60°–90° is High/Full Fowler's."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"Fowler's position is clinically indicated for respiratory and cardiac disorder clients such as which of the following? (Select All That Apply)","options":["COPD","ARDS","Asthma","Pneumonia","After angina & MI and heart failure"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Fowler's clinical indications list respiratory & cardiac disorder clients such as COPD, ARDS, asthma, pneumonia, after angina & MI, and heart failure.  All options are listed indications, so all are selected."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"In Semi-Fowler's position the head end is elevated at what angle (usually 45°)?","options":["15°–30°","30°–45°","45°–60°","60°–90°"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Semi-Fowler's position elevates the head end at 30°–45° (usually 45°) and is used for comfort.  Why A wrong: 15°–30° is Low Fowler's.  Why C wrong: 45°–60° is Fowler's.  Why D wrong: 60°–90° is High Fowler's."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"High Fowler's position is also known by which name?","options":["Cardiac position","Sitting position","Recovery position","Genu-pectoral position"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: High Fowler's is also known as the sitting position; the half body is sitting with back supported by the bed or a back cradle, with the angle between the body and bed at 60°–90° (usually 90°).  Why A wrong: Orthopneic is the cardiac position.  Why C wrong: Recovery is a lateral position.  Why D wrong: Genu-pectoral is the knee-chest position."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"High/Full Fowler's position is clinically indicated for which of the following? (Select All That Apply)","options":["During NG tube insertion","After feeding","During dyspnoea attack","Respiratory and cardiac disorder clients","During paracentesis"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: High Fowler's clinical indications include during NG tube insertion, after feeding, during dyspnoea attack, respiratory and cardiac disorder clients, and it is also used during paracentesis.  All options are listed indications, so all are selected."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"In Trendelenburg's position, the client's body is arranged so that the:","options":["Upper body is elevated above the lower body","Lower body is elevated than the upper body","Head and feet are at the same level on a flat bed","Client lies prone with the head down"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In Trendelenburg's position the client's lower body is elevated than the upper body.  Why A wrong: That describes reverse Trendelenburg.  Why C wrong: Flat bed is supine.  Why D wrong: Prone head-down is not Trendelenburg."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"Trendelenburg's position is used for pelvic examination or surgery, shock, and postural drainage primarily to:","options":["Decrease intracranial pressure","Promote venous return","Relieve dyspnoea","Prevent aspiration"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Trendelenburg's position is used in pelvic examination or surgery, shock, hypotension, and postural drainage and to promote venous return.  Why A wrong: It should NEVER be used in clients with increased ICP.  Why C wrong: Dyspnoea relief uses Fowler's/orthopneic.  Why D wrong: Aspiration prevention is not its stated indication."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"Trendelenburg's position should NEVER be used in a client with which condition?","options":["Hypotension","Increased ICP","Shock","Dehydration"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to never use this position in a client with increased ICP, as the head-down tilt would further raise intracranial pressure.  Why A/C wrong: Hypotension and shock are actually indications for Trendelenburg.  Why D wrong: Dehydration is not the contraindication specified."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"In Reverse Trendelenburg's position, the client lies on his/her back with what elevation, and it is used in a client with increased ICP?","options":["30°–40° foot of bed lower than level of head","30°–40° head of bed lower than level of foot","Head and feet at equal level","Knees and hips flexed at 90°"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In reverse Trendelenburg's position the client lies on the back with the 30°–40° foot of bed lower than the level of head; it is used in a client with increased ICP.  Why B wrong: That describes the regular Trendelenburg (head lower).  Why C wrong: Equal level is supine.  Why D wrong: 90° knee/hip flexion is lithotomy."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"In the lithotomy position the client lies on his/her back with the knees and hips flexed at what angle?","options":["45°","60°","90°","30°"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In the lithotomy position the client lies on the back with the knees and hips flexed at 90° angle.  Why A/B/D wrong: The source specifies 90°, not 45°, 60°, or 30°."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"Which of the following are clinical indications for the lithotomy position? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Gynaecological (vaginal) examinations","Hysterectomy","Hysteroscopy","D&C and D&E procedures","Examination of rectum, vagina and cervix and during normal labor"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Lithotomy clinical indications include gynaecological (vaginal) examinations e.g. hysterectomy, hysteroscopy, D&C, D&E procedure, and during examination of rectum, vagina and cervix and during normal labor.  All options are listed indications, so all are selected."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"The knee chest position is also known as the:","options":["Genu-pectoral position","Orthopneic position","Sims' position","Fowler's position"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The knee chest position is also known as the genu-pectoral position.  Why B wrong: Orthopneic is the cardiac position.  Why C wrong: Sims' is the modified left lateral position.  Why D wrong: Fowler's is a sitting/semi-sitting position."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"In the knee chest (genu-pectoral) position, where does the abdomen rest and which body parts bear the patient's weight?","options":["Abdomen rests on the bed; weight on the back","Abdomen remains unsupported; weight partially borne by knees and chest with the head turned to one side","Abdomen on a pillow; weight on the feet","Abdomen on the cardiac table; weight on the arms"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In knee chest position the patient rests on the knees and chest with the head turned to one side; arms are extended on the bed and elbows flexed and resting so they partially bear the patient's weight, while the abdomen remains unsupported (though a small pillow may be placed under the chest).  Why A/C/D wrong: These misstate the weight-bearing and abdominal support described in the source."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"Clinical indications for the knee chest (genu-pectoral) position include which of the following? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Rectal examination (sigmoidoscopy)","Colon examination (proctoscopy)","Gynaecological examination","Cardiac resuscitation","Increased ICP management"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Knee chest clinical indications are rectal & colon examinations (sigmoidoscopy, proctoscopy) and gynaecological examination.  Why D wrong: Cardiac resuscitation requires a flat firm surface, not knee-chest.  Why E wrong: Increased ICP is managed with reverse Trendelenburg/head elevation, not knee-chest."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"After a tonsillectomy, which position is used to promote drainage of secretions?","options":["Supine position","Prone position","High Fowler's position","Trendelenburg position"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For tonsillectomy, the prone position is used to promote drainage of secretions.  Why A wrong: Supine could allow pooling/aspiration of secretions.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the position specified for tonsillectomy secretion drainage."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"A baby with TOF (tetralogy of Fallot) experiencing a hypercyanotic (\"tet\") spell is best placed in which position?","options":["Squatting position","Lithotomy position","Prone position","Reverse Trendelenburg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For babies with TOF, the squatting position is used (it increases systemic vascular resistance and improves pulmonary blood flow during tet spells).  Why B/C/D wrong: These positions are not used for managing TOF cyanotic spells."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"After a skin graft, for how long should the recipient site be immobilized?","options":["1 day","2 days","3–7 days","10–14 days"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: After grafting, the recipient site should be immobilized for 3–7 days to allow the graft to take.  Why A/B/D wrong: The source specifies a 3–7 day immobilization period."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"After a burn of the face and head, which position is used to prevent or reduce facial, head and tracheal oedema?","options":["Trendelenburg","High Fowler's position","Prone position","Left lateral position"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After burn of the face and head, the High Fowler's position is used to prevent or reduce facial, head and tracheal oedema by promoting venous/lymphatic drainage.  Why A wrong: Trendelenburg (head down) would worsen head/facial oedema.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the indicated positions for facial burn oedema."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"Following a mastectomy with axillary lymph node removal, the client is placed in semi-Fowler's position with the affected arm elevated on a pillow. What is the purpose of elevating the affected arm?","options":["To reduce shoulder pain","To promote lymphatic fluid return after removal of axillary lymph nodes","To prevent neck flexion","To improve respiratory effort"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After mastectomy, semi-Fowler's position with the affected arm elevated on a pillow promotes lymphatic fluid return after removal of axillary lymph nodes; the client is turned to the back and the unaffected side.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the stated rationale for arm elevation following mastectomy."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"After a thyroidectomy, the client is placed in semi-Fowler's position and which neck movement is avoided to prevent tension on the suture line?","options":["Hyper-flexion and hyperextension of the neck","Lateral rotation of the neck","Side-to-side shaking","Chin tucking only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: After thyroidectomy, the semi-Fowler position is used and hyper-flexion and hyperextension of the neck are avoided to prevent tension on the suture line.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifically warns against hyper-flexion and hyperextension, not these other movements."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"Which position is recommended for a client with GERD (gastroesophageal reflux disease)?","options":["Trendelenburg position","Reverse Trendelenburg position","Prone position","Left lateral position"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For GERD, the reverse Trendelenburg position (head higher than feet) is used to reduce reflux of gastric contents.  Why A wrong: Trendelenburg (head down) would worsen reflux.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the positions specified for GERD."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"During a liver biopsy procedure, how is the client positioned?","options":["Prone with a pillow under the abdomen","Supine with the right side of the upper abdomen exposed","Left lateral with knees flexed","Sitting on the edge of the bed"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: During a liver biopsy procedure, the client is supine with the right side of the upper abdomen exposed.  Why A wrong: Prone with abdominal pillow is for renal biopsy.  Why C wrong: Left lateral lying or C-shaped is for lumbar puncture.  Why D wrong: Sitting on the edge of the bed is for thoracocentesis."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"After a liver biopsy, the client is placed in right lateral position with a small pillow, sandbag, or folded towel under which area?","options":["Under the head","Under the puncture site","Under the knees","Under the lower back"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After a liver biopsy, the client is placed in right lateral position with a small pillow, sandbag, or folded towel placed under the puncture site to apply pressure and reduce bleeding.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies pressure under the puncture site, not these locations."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"During a lumbar puncture, the client is positioned in which way?","options":["Prone with pillow under abdomen","Side lying or C-shaped position near the edge of the bed","Supine flat","High Fowler's"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: During a lumbar puncture, the client is in a side lying or C-shaped (curled) position near the edge of the bed to widen the intervertebral spaces.  Why A wrong: Prone with abdominal pillow is for renal biopsy.  Why C wrong: Supine flat is the after-LP position to prevent PDPH.  Why D wrong: High Fowler's is not used for LP."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"After a lumbar puncture, the client is placed in supine position or flat to prevent which complication?","options":["Aspiration","PDPH (post-dural puncture headache)","Pressure ulcers","Venous thrombosis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After a lumbar puncture, the supine position or flat position is used to prevent PDPH (post-dural puncture headache).  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the stated reason for the flat/supine position after LP."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"During a renal biopsy procedure, the client is placed prone with a pillow under which areas?","options":["The head and neck","The abdomen and shoulders","The knees and ankles","The lower back only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: During a renal biopsy, the prone position is used with a pillow under the abdomen and shoulders.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies a pillow under the abdomen and shoulders."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"After a renal biopsy, the client should maintain which activity level?","options":["Ambulate immediately","Strict bed rest in the supine position with a back roll","Sit in high Fowler's","Lie prone for 24 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After a renal biopsy, the client is on strict bed rest in the supine position with a back roll to apply pressure and prevent bleeding.  Why A/C/D wrong: These contradict the strict supine bed rest with back roll specified."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"Which position is used for paracentesis?","options":["Semi-Fowler and sitting position","Prone position","Left lateral position","Trendelenburg position"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For paracentesis, the semi-Fowler and sitting position is used to allow fluid to pool in the lower abdomen for drainage.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the indicated positions for paracentesis."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"During NG tube insertion, the client is best placed in which position?","options":["Supine flat","Prone","Sitting or high Fowler's position","Left lateral"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: For NG tube insertion, the sitting or high Fowler's position is used to ease passage and reduce aspiration risk.  Why A/B/D wrong: These are not the recommended positions for NG tube insertion."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"For enema administration, the client is placed in which position?","options":["Left lateral or Sim's lateral position","Right lateral position","Supine position","Knee chest position"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Enema administration uses the left lateral or Sim's lateral position, which follows the anatomy of the descending/sigmoid colon.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated position for enema administration."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"During a laryngectomy, the client is placed in Fowler's position to:","options":["Reduce intracranial pressure","Maintain patent airway and minimize oedema","Promote venous return","Prevent aspiration of feed"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For laryngectomy, Fowler's position is used to maintain a patent airway and minimize oedema.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the stated rationale for Fowler's position after laryngectomy."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"For a client with increased ICP, the head of the bed should be elevated to what degree to prevent further increase in ICP?","options":["15°–30°","30°–45°","45°–60°","60°–90°"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For increased ICP, the head of the bed is elevated 15°–30° degrees to promote venous drainage and prevent further increase in ICP.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies 15°–30°, not higher angles."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"For postural drainage, various positions are used and the client is placed on which type of bed?","options":["Fracture bed","Nelson bed","Air-fluidized bed","Cardiac bed"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For postural drainage, various positions are used and the client is placed on a Nelson bed.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies a Nelson bed."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"For a thoracocentesis, the client is positioned sitting on the edge of the bed and leaning over a cardiac or bed side table with the feet supported on a:","options":["Pillow","Foot board","Stool","Chair back"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: For thoracocentesis, the client sits on the edge of the bed leaning over a cardiac or bed side table with the feet supported on a stool.  Why A/B/D wrong: The source specifies a stool to support the feet."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"Which position is recommended for a laminectomy?","options":["Muslim prayer position","Froggy walcher's position","Lithotomy position","Knee chest position"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For laminectomy, the Muslim prayer position is used.  Why B wrong: Froggy walcher's position is for tonsillectomy and adenoidectomy.  Why C/D wrong: These are not specified for laminectomy."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"A client is suspected of having an air embolism. The nurse should turn the client onto the left side and place them in which position?","options":["High Fowler's","Lower head end (head-down) position","Supine flat","Right lateral"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For an air embolism, turn the client on the left side and lower the head end (left lateral Trendelenburg/head-down) to trap air in the right ventricle and prevent it from entering the pulmonary circulation.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the left-side, lowered head-end position required for air embolism."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"After amputation of the lower extremity, how should the stump be managed in the first 24 hours?","options":["Keep the stump flat on the bed for the first 24 hours","Elevate the foot end of bed and support the stump on pillows to prevent oedema for the first 24 hours","Keep the stump dependent (hanging down)","Apply continuous traction for 24 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For the first 24 hours after lower-extremity amputation, elevate the foot end of the bed and support the stump on pillows to prevent oedema.  Why A wrong: Keeping the stump flat applies AFTER 24 hours, not during the first 24 hours.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the recommended early management."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"After lower-extremity amputation, prolonged elevation of the stump beyond the initial period is limited because of the risk of which complication, and elevation should not exceed how long with prone positioning every 2 hours?","options":["Risk of flexion contractures; not beyond 24 hours, with prone positioning every 2 hours to prevent flexion contractures","Risk of infection; not beyond 12 hours","Risk of bleeding; not beyond 6 hours","Risk of pressure ulcer; not beyond 48 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source warns that the stump should not remain elevated because of the risk of flexion contractures; every 2 hours place the client in prone position to prevent flexion contractures, and after 24 hours keep the stump flat on the bed.  Why B/C/D wrong: The stated risk is flexion contractures, and the timing/intervention differ."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"Postoperatively after a cleft lip repair, which position is recommended?","options":["Prone","Supine to prevent pressure on the suture line","Left lateral","Knee chest"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For cleft lip (postoperative), the supine position is used to prevent pressure on the suture line.  Why A wrong: Prone is used for cleft palate.  Why C/D wrong: These are not specified for cleft lip postoperative care."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"After a cleft palate repair, which position helps prevent aspiration?","options":["Supine","Prone","High Fowler's","Trendelenburg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For cleft palate, the prone position is used to prevent aspiration.  Why A wrong: Supine is used for cleft lip postoperatively.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the positions specified to prevent aspiration after cleft palate repair."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"For a combined cleft lip and palate repair, the child is best placed supine or propped on the side to prevent which action?","options":["Rubbing the lip on the sheets","Aspiration of feed","Pressure on the back","Neck flexion"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For cleft lip and palate, the child is placed supine or propped on the side to prevent rubbing the lip on the sheets and protecting the suture line.  Why B/C/D wrong: The stated purpose is to prevent rubbing the lip on the sheets."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"A client with heart failure and pulmonary oedema should be placed in which position(s)? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Sitting","Orthopneic","High Fowler's position","Trendelenburg","Prone"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: For heart failure and pulmonary oedema, sitting, orthopneic, and high Fowler's positions are used to ease breathing and reduce venous return to the overloaded heart/lungs.  Why D wrong: Trendelenburg (head-down) would increase venous return and worsen pulmonary congestion.  Why E wrong: Prone is not indicated."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"For a client with deep vein thrombosis (DVT) and varicose veins, which position is recommended?","options":["Foot elevation above heart level","Head elevation","Trendelenburg with foot down","Sitting with legs dependent"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For DVT and varicose veins, foot elevation above heart level is used to promote venous return and reduce venous stasis/oedema.  Why B/C/D wrong: Dependent or non-elevated leg positions worsen venous pooling; head elevation does not address leg venous return."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"A client with autonomic dysreflexia should be placed immediately in which position?","options":["High Fowler's position","Supine flat","Trendelenburg","Left lateral"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For autonomic dysreflexia, the client is immediately placed in high Fowler's position to use orthostatic (gravity) lowering of blood pressure during this hypertensive emergency.  Why B/C/D wrong: A flat or head-down position would worsen the dangerously elevated blood pressure of autonomic dysreflexia."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"A client has a brain attack/CVA (stroke) of the haemorrhagic type. Which position is appropriate?","options":["Trendelenburg position","Low Fowler's position","Prone position","Knee chest position"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If the CVA is the haemorrhagic type, the low Fowler's position is used to keep the head slightly elevated without excessively raising ICP.  Why A wrong: Trendelenburg is contraindicated with raised ICP.  Why C/D wrong: These are not appropriate for haemorrhagic stroke."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"A client has an ischaemic-type CVA (stroke). Which position is recommended?","options":["Keep the client flat in supine position","High Fowler's position","Trendelenburg position","Prone position"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: If the CVA is the ischaemic type, keep the client flat in flat or supine position to maintain cerebral perfusion to the ischaemic brain tissue.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the recommended flat/supine position for ischaemic stroke and could compromise cerebral perfusion or raise ICP."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"After a craniotomy, the client is placed in semi-Fowler's position on the unoperated side of the head with the head maintained in what alignment?","options":["Hyperextended","Midline or neutral position","Rotated to the operated side","Flexed onto the chest"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For craniotomy, semi-Fowler's position on the unoperated side of the head is used, maintaining the head in midline or neutral position to promote venous drainage and avoid raising ICP.  Why A/C/D wrong: Hyperextension, rotation, or flexion can impair jugular venous drainage and increase ICP."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"A client with a spinal cord injury must be immobilized, and which technique is used for transporting the client?","options":["Pivot turn","Log roll technique","Fireman's carry","Pillow bridging"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For spinal cord injury, the client is immobilized and the log roll technique is used for transporting the client to keep the spine in alignment.  Why A/C/D wrong: These methods do not maintain spinal alignment and could worsen the injury."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"After a Total Hip Replacement (THR), which of the following positioning measures are correct? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Avoid extreme internal and external rotations","Avoid adduction","Use a pillow between the legs","Allow the hip to flex beyond 90°","Cross the legs at the ankles"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: After THR, avoid extreme internal and external rotations, avoid adduction, and use a pillow between the legs (abduction pillow) to prevent dislocation of the prosthesis.  Why D wrong: Hip flexion beyond 90° risks dislocation.  Why E wrong: Crossing the legs is adduction and is contraindicated."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"A trochanter roll is used to prevent which movement of the legs?","options":["External rotation","Internal rotation","Flexion","Abduction"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A trochanter roll is used to prevent external rotation of the legs by supporting the greater trochanter.  Why B/C/D wrong: The trochanter roll specifically prevents external rotation, not these other movements."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"During intubation, the client is placed in the sniff position, which involves:","options":["Supine positioning with flexion of the neck and extension of the head","Full hyperextension of the neck","Lateral neck rotation","Chin flexed onto the chest"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For intubation, the sniff position is supine positioning with flexion of the neck and extension of the head to align the airway axes.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not describe the sniff position required for optimal laryngeal visualization."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"During a tonsillectomy and adenoidectomy, which position is used, in which the patient's head and neck are extended and the surgeon sits behind the patient?","options":["Sniff position","Rose position","Lithotomy position","Fowler's position"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For tonsillectomy and adenoidectomy, the Rose position is used — the patient's head and neck are extended and the surgeon sits behind the patient.  Why A wrong: Sniff position is for intubation.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the position used for tonsillectomy/adenoidectomy surgery."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"For breech presentation, which position is recommended?","options":["Lithotomy position","Froggy walcher's position","Knee chest position","Lloyd Davies position"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For breech presentation, the Froggy walcher's position is used.  Why A/C/D wrong: These positions are used for other procedures, not breech presentation."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"Coccyx and rectal surgeries are best performed in which position?","options":["Lithotomy position","Jack Knife position","Trendelenburg position","Lloyd Davies position"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For coccyx and rectal surgeries, the Jack Knife position is used.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the position specified for coccyx and rectal surgeries."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"To assess posture, balance, and gait, which positions/methods are used? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Standing/erect position","Orthopaedic & neurological exam","Supine flat","Lithotomy","Prone"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: To assess posture, balance and gait, the standing/erect position is used along with an orthopaedic & neurological exam.  Why C/D/E wrong: These positions do not allow assessment of standing posture, balance, or gait."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"The Lloyd Davies position is used for which procedure?","options":["Position right lower abdomen Lap ligation","Cleft palate repair","Liver biopsy","Lumbar puncture"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Lloyd Davies position is used to position the right lower abdomen for Lap ligation (laparoscopic tubal ligation), giving access to both abdomen and perineum.  Why B/C/D wrong: These procedures use other specified positions."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"A client with placenta previa should be placed in which position to minimize bleeding?","options":["Standing position","Sitting position to minimize bleeding","Trendelenburg","Prone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For placenta previa, the sitting position is used to minimize bleeding.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the position specified to minimize bleeding in placenta previa."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"For postural drainage, the lung segment to be drained should be placed in which position to facilitate drainage?","options":["The lowermost position","The uppermost position","A flat horizontal position","The dependent position"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For postural drainage, the lung segment to be drained should be in the uppermost position so gravity assists drainage of secretions toward the larger airways.  Why A/C/D wrong: Placing the segment lowermost/dependent or flat would not use gravity to drain that segment."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"Which position is used for a client with pulmonary oedema to facilitate breathing?","options":["Supine position","Fowler's position","Trendelenburg position","Prone position"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For pulmonary oedema, Fowler's position is used to facilitate breathing.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the position specified to facilitate breathing in pulmonary oedema."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"For a child with pyloric stenosis, how should the infant be positioned after a feed to facilitate entry of stomach contents into the intestine?","options":["Supine flat","Right side lying on an infant car seat after meals","Left lateral","Prone with head down"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For pyloric stenosis, place the child right side lying on an infant car seat after meals to facilitate entry of stomach contents into the intestine (using gravity toward the pylorus).  Why A/C/D wrong: These positions do not direct gastric contents toward the pyloric outlet."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"A client has a radium implant in the cervix. How should the client be positioned to prevent dislodging of the implant?","options":["Sitting upright","Flat on bed to prevent dislodge","Side lying","Knee chest"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For a radium implant in the cervix, keep the client flat on bed to prevent dislodging of the implant.  Why A/C/D wrong: These positions risk displacement of the radium implant."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"A client with a retinal detachment should be positioned so that the affected side is toward the bed. What is the rationale?","options":["To reduce eye pain","Gravity will help the detached retina fall back into place","To prevent infection","To reduce intraocular pressure only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For retinal detachment, keep the affected side toward the bed so gravity will help the detected (detached) retina to fall in place.  Why A/C/D wrong: The stated rationale is gravity-assisted reattachment of the retina, not these effects."},{"section":"Batch 004 · surface anatomy of the abdominal wall, abdominal wall incisions…","question":"A client experiencing a seizure or convulsion should be placed in which position, and why?","options":["Supine flat to maintain airway","Side lying to prevent backward falling of the tongue which can obstruct the airway","High Fowler's to ease breathing","Prone to prevent aspiration of saliva"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For seizures or convulsions, side lying is used to prevent backward falling of the tongue which can obstruct the airway and to allow secretions to drain.  Why A wrong: Supine flat allows the tongue to fall back and obstruct the airway.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the recommended seizure position; side lying (recovery) position protects the airway."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"A client with pyloric stenosis is being fed. To facilitate entry of stomach contents into the intestine, the nurse should keep the infant positioned:","options":["Flat on bed","On the left side","On the right side","In Trendelenburg position"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In pyloric stenosis, the right-side-lying position facilitates entry of stomach contents into the intestine.  Why A wrong: Flat on bed is used to prevent dislodge (e.g., after radium implant), not for pyloric stenosis.  Why B wrong: Left side does not promote gastric emptying through the pylorus here.  Why D wrong: Trendelenburg is used in shock/air embolism, not pyloric stenosis."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"After a radium implant in the cervix, the nurse positions the client:","options":["Right side lying","Flat on bed to prevent dislodge","High Fowler's","Knee-chest position"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Radium implant in the cervix requires the client to be kept flat on bed to prevent dislodgement of the implant.  Why A wrong: Right side lying is for pyloric stenosis.  Why C wrong: High Fowler's is used for hypertensive shock/heart failure, not radium implant.  Why D wrong: Knee-chest is for sigmoidoscopy/colonoscopy/cyanotic spell."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"A client with a retinal detachment should be positioned so that:","options":["The detached area is upward","The detached area is in a dependent (downward) position","The head is lower than the body","The client lies prone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In retinal detachment, the client is positioned so that the affected area of the retina is in a dependent position.  Why A wrong: Positioning the detachment upward does not promote reattachment.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the specified positions for retinal detachment."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"A client experiencing seizures or convulsions should be positioned to:","options":["Lie supine flat","Sit upright","Modified Trendelenburg","Lie down to prevent injury and aspiration"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: For seizures or convulsions the client is positioned lying down to prevent injury and aspiration.  Why A wrong: Supine flat increases aspiration risk during a seizure.  Why B wrong: Sitting upright risks fall injury during a seizure.  Why C wrong: Modified Trendelenburg is for hypovolemic shock."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"Which position is indicated for a client in hypovolemic shock?","options":["Modified Trendelenburg","High Fowler's","Knee-chest","Prone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Modified Trendelenburg position is used to promote venous return to the heart in hypovolemic shock.  Why B wrong: High Fowler's reduces venous return.  Why C wrong: Knee-chest is used in cyanotic spell/colonoscopy.  Why D wrong: Prone is not used to manage hypovolemic shock."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"To minimize the effects of the affected side, a client recovering from a sudden infant death syndrome event / placed for monitoring is kept in which position to prevent respiratory distress?","options":["Supine with head flexed","On the bed next to and elevating the affected leg to promote circulation","Prone with feet elevated","Left side lying flat"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Per the source, the client is placed on the bed next to and elevating the affected (leg) to promote circulation.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the specified positions for this entry."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"After a head injury, the nurse positions the client to:","options":["Keep flat to maximize cerebral blood flow","Keep head elevated for at least 30 degrees to minimize the reflux of gastric secretions and reduce ICP","Place in Trendelenburg","Place prone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For head injury, the head is kept elevated at least 30 degrees to minimize reflux of gastric secretions and reduce intracranial pressure.  Why A/C wrong: Flat or Trendelenburg positions raise intracranial pressure.  Why D wrong: Prone is not appropriate for head injury."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"For thrombophlebitis, the nurse should:","options":["Lower the affected leg below heart level","Elevate the leg above the level of the heart to facilitate venous return to the heart","Keep the leg flat","Massage the affected calf"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In thrombophlebitis, the affected leg is elevated above the level of the heart to facilitate venous return.  Why A/C wrong: Lowering or keeping the leg flat does not promote venous return.  Why D wrong: Massaging a thrombophlebitic limb risks embolus dislodgement (not the positioning instruction given)."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"A client with varicose veins is taught to:","options":["Sit with legs crossed","Keep the leg elevated to prevent venous stasis","Stand for long periods","Keep legs dependent"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For varicose veins, the leg is kept elevated to prevent venous stasis.  Why A/C/D wrong: Crossing legs, standing, or keeping legs dependent promotes venous stasis."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"When stripping and bandaging an extremity to facilitate venous return, the bandage should be applied in which direction?","options":["From proximal toward distal","Toward the heart","Away from the heart","Circularly only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The leg is bandaged toward the heart to facilitate venous return to the heart.  Why A/C wrong: Bandaging away from / down from the heart impedes venous return.  Why D wrong: Circular-only application is not the directional principle described."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"For a client with appendicitis, which position is recommended?","options":["Right side lying with knees flexed (low-Fowler's / right-sided comfort) to prevent rapid spread of infection","Trendelenburg","Left lateral flat","High Fowler's"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In appendicitis the source advises a position (sitting / lowering–leaning forward) to prevent rapid upward spread of infection; the right-sided low position limits spread of an appendiceal/ruptured appendix infection.  Why B/C/D wrong: These positions are not the recommended ones for appendicitis per source."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"A client having an acute asthma attack should be positioned to facilitate breathing by:","options":["Lying flat supine","Sitting position leaning forward to facilitate breathing","Trendelenburg","Left side lying"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For asthma, the sitting position leaning forward facilitates breathing.  Why A/C wrong: Supine flat or Trendelenburg worsens dyspnea.  Why D wrong: Side lying does not optimize accessory-muscle use for breathing."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"A client with autonomic dysreflexia should be placed in which position?","options":["Supine flat","High Fowler's to prevent/help emptying of the stomach","Trendelenburg","Prone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For autonomic dysreflexia, a high-Fowler's (upright) position is used to help lower blood pressure / prevent emptying of stomach as listed.  Why A/C wrong: Lying flat or Trendelenburg can raise blood pressure dangerously.  Why D wrong: Prone is not used for autonomic dysreflexia."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"A client recovering from bronchoscopy should be placed in which position to prevent aspiration?","options":["Supine after procedure","Semi-Fowler's post procedure to prevent aspiration","Trendelenburg","Prone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After bronchoscopy the client is placed in a semi-Fowler's position to prevent aspiration.  Why A/C/D wrong: These positions increase aspiration risk after the gag reflex is suppressed."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"Following bronchography, the nurse positions the client to:","options":["Lie prone","Tripod position to facilitate breathing","Trendelenburg","Supine flat"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After bronchography the tripod position is used to facilitate breathing.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not facilitate breathing as the tripod does."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"Following cardiac catheterization performed via the femoral site, the affected extremity should be:","options":["Flexed at the hip","Kept flat & extended for 4 to 6 hrs to prevent arterial occlusion","Elevated above the heart","Crossed over the unaffected leg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After cardiac catheterization, the extremity is kept flat and extended for 4–6 hours to prevent arterial occlusion.  Why A/D wrong: Hip flexion or crossing the legs can occlude the artery and cause bleeding.  Why C wrong: Elevation is not the specified intervention to prevent arterial occlusion."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"After cataract surgery, the client should be positioned to:","options":["Lie on the operative side","Elevate the extremity to prevent edema at the operative site","Lie in Trendelenburg","Lie prone with head flat"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After cataract surgery the affected part is elevated to prevent edema/swelling at the operative site (semi-Fowler/operate side avoidance).  Why A wrong: Lying on the operative side increases pressure/edema.  Why C/D wrong: Trendelenburg and prone increase intraocular pressure."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"A client with a cerebral aneurysm should be placed in which position?","options":["Trendelenburg","Semi-Fowler to prevent pressure in the aneurysm site","Prone","Right side lying flat"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For cerebral aneurysm, a semi-Fowler position is used to prevent pressure at the aneurysm site.  Why A wrong: Trendelenburg raises intracranial pressure.  Why C/D wrong: Prone and flat side lying are not the specified positions."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"For a client in congestive heart failure, which position improves oxygenation?","options":["Supine flat","High Fowler's to improve oxygenation","Trendelenburg","Left lateral"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In congestive heart failure, the high-Fowler's position improves oxygenation by reducing venous return and pulmonary congestion.  Why A/C wrong: Supine or Trendelenburg increases venous return and pulmonary congestion.  Why D wrong: Left lateral is not the specified position for CHF oxygenation."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"A client who has undergone a craniotomy with a supratentorial incision should be positioned to:","options":["Lie flat with head turned away","Elevate the head to prevent pressure on the incision site","Lie prone","Place in Trendelenburg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After craniotomy (supratentorial), the head is elevated to prevent pressure on the incision site and reduce ICP.  Why A wrong: Lying flat raises ICP.  Why C/D wrong: Prone and Trendelenburg raise ICP and are contraindicated."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"A client who has had a CVA (stroke) should be placed in which position to facilitate venous drainage from the head?","options":["Trendelenburg","Elevate the head to reduce ICP, or locations placing the body to facilitate venous drainage from the head","Prone flat","Right side lying head down"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After CVA the head is elevated (or positioned to facilitate venous drainage from the head) to reduce ICP.  Why A/D wrong: Head-down positions impair venous drainage and raise ICP.  Why C wrong: Prone flat does not facilitate cerebral venous drainage."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"A client with dumping syndrome following gastric surgery should be positioned during eating to:","options":["Sit fully upright","Lie down (supine/low Fowler) after meals to prevent rapid emptying of the stomach","Stand and walk","Trendelenburg before meals"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In dumping syndrome, lying down (supine after meals) is used to prevent rapid emptying of the stomach.  Why A/C wrong: Upright/standing positions speed gastric emptying and worsen dumping.  Why D wrong: Trendelenburg before meals is not the recommended intervention."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"A client having a seizure due to epilepsy should be placed in which position to prevent aspiration of blood?","options":["Supine flat","Side-lying (lateral) to prevent aspiration of blood","High Fowler's","Prone with head straight"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For epilepsy/seizure, side-lying (leaning to prevent aspiration of blood) protects the airway.  Why A/C wrong: Supine or sitting increases aspiration risk during a seizure.  Why D wrong: Prone with the head straight does not protect the airway as side-lying does."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"A client with a flail chest is positioned on the affected side. This position is intended to:","options":["Increase pain","Stabilize the chest and reduce pain on the affected side","Promote pneumothorax","Facilitate venous return only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: With flail chest, positioning on the affected side reduces pain and stabilizes the chest wall.  Why A wrong: It reduces, not increases, pain.  Why C wrong: It does not promote pneumothorax.  Why D wrong: Venous return is not the purpose of this position."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"Following a femoral-popliteal bypass graft, the nurse keeps the extremity:","options":["Sharply flexed at the knee","Straight (avoiding knee flexion) to promote circulation","Crossed at the ankles","Elevated above the heart continuously"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After femoral-popliteal bypass graft, the extremity is kept straight (knee not sharply flexed) to promote circulation and graft patency.  Why A wrong: Sharp knee flexion can occlude the graft.  Why C/D wrong: Crossing ankles or constant elevation is not the specified intervention."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"After a fracture of the neck of the femur (or hip surgery), the affected limb is maintained in which position to prevent dislocation?","options":["Adduction and internal rotation","Abduction with extension to prevent dislocation","Sharp flexion","External rotation with adduction"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After fracture of the neck of femur, the limb is kept in abduction with extension to prevent dislocation.  Why A/D wrong: Adduction and internal/external rotation promote dislocation of the prosthesis.  Why C wrong: Sharp flexion is contraindicated post hip surgery."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"A client with gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) should sleep in which position to reduce reflux?","options":["Flat supine","Reverse Trendelenburg position or upright (head of bed elevated) to reduce gastric reflux","Trendelenburg","Left side with feet elevated only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For GERD, a reverse Trendelenburg (head-up) position is used to reduce gastric reflux.  Why A/C wrong: Flat or head-down positions worsen reflux.  Why D wrong: While left side may help, the source specifies reverse Trendelenburg/upright to reduce reflux."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"To relieve a client's heart failure-related dyspnea, the head of the bed is:","options":["Lowered flat","Raised to high Fowler's to alleviate pulmonary congestion","Placed in Trendelenburg","Kept at 10 degrees"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In heart failure the head of the bed is raised (high Fowler's) to alleviate pulmonary congestion.  Why A/C wrong: Flat or Trendelenburg increases pulmonary congestion.  Why D wrong: A minimal 10-degree elevation does not adequately relieve congestion."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"A post-hemorrhoidectomy client is positioned to:","options":["Lie supine with legs extended","Side lying to prevent pressure on the operative side","Lie prone with hips flat","High Fowler's"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After hemorrhoidectomy, side lying prevents pressure on the operative site.  Why A/D wrong: Supine or sitting puts direct pressure on the surgical site.  Why C wrong: Prone flat is not specified and increases discomfort."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"For a client following a hiatal hernia repair, which position is recommended?","options":["Supine flat after meals","Upright position when meals to prevent dislodge / reduce reflux","Trendelenburg","Prone after eating"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: With hiatal hernia, an upright position (after meals) helps prevent reflux/dislodge of stomach contents.  Why A/C/D wrong: Lying flat, head-down, or prone after eating worsens reflux."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"After a hip replacement (THR), the abductor splint or pillow is placed to:","options":["Keep legs together (adducted)","Keep the legs abducted by placing a wedge pillow between the patient's legs","Cross the legs","Internally rotate the hip"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After hip/total hip replacement, an abductor wedge pillow is placed between the legs to maintain abduction and prevent prosthesis dislocation.  Why A/C/D wrong: Adduction, leg-crossing, and internal rotation are the exact movements that cause dislocation."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"During the first 24 hours after amputation of the lower extremity, the residual limb (stump) is:","options":["Kept dependent","Elevated to prevent edema during the first 24 hours after amputation","Wrapped tightly and left flat","Massaged hourly"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For the first 24 hours after amputation, the stump is elevated to prevent edema.  Why A wrong: A dependent limb promotes edema.  Why C/D wrong: Tight flat wrapping and massage are not the specified first-24-hour intervention; elevation is."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"A client with increased intracranial pressure (ICP) should have the head of the bed:","options":["Lowered to flat","Kept high to prevent or reduce ICP","Placed in Trendelenburg","Turned sharply to one side"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For increased ICP, the head is kept elevated to reduce intracranial pressure.  Why A/C wrong: Flat or head-down positions raise ICP.  Why D wrong: Sharp neck flexion/rotation impedes venous drainage and raises ICP."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"A client undergoing internal vaginal examination is placed in which position?","options":["Knee-chest","Lithotomy position","Sims'","Prone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Perineal and vaginal examination is performed in the lithotomy position.  Why A wrong: Knee-chest is for sigmoidoscopy/colonoscopy.  Why C wrong: Sims' (left lateral) is for enema/rectal exam.  Why D wrong: Prone is not used for vaginal examination."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"Following lithotripsy, the client should be positioned in semi-Fowler's and the affected area elevated after how long?","options":["Immediately and elevate above heart","Semi-Fowler position and elevate the affected area 30 to 60 minutes after feed","Trendelenburg for 4 hours","Prone for 24 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After lithotripsy, the client is placed in semi-Fowler's and the affected area elevated 30–60 minutes after feed.  Why A/C/D wrong: These timings/positions are not what the source specifies for lithotripsy."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"When caring for a client with COPD experiencing dyspnea, the nurse positions the client to:","options":["Lie flat supine","Sitting and leaning forward, with arms placed on an overhead table to facilitate breathing","Trendelenburg","Left side lying flat"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For COPD, sitting and leaning forward with arms placed on an overhead table (orthopneic/tripod) facilitates breathing.  Why A/C wrong: Supine or head-down positions worsen dyspnea.  Why D wrong: Flat side lying does not optimize accessory muscle use."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"When ambulating a client who has had a total hip replacement (THR), to prevent dislocation the nurse should avoid:","options":["Standing the client up with legs abducted","Crossing the legs or adduction past midline","Using a walker","Keeping the foot pointed forward"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After THR, the legs must not be crossed/adducted past midline because adduction causes prosthesis dislocation.  Why A wrong: Abduction is the desired protective position.  Why C wrong: A walker provides safe support.  Why D wrong: A forward-pointing foot avoids harmful rotation."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"A client undergoing sigmoidoscopy or colonoscopy is best placed in which position?","options":["Lithotomy","Knee-chest position","Left side lying (Sims') only","High Fowler's"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Sigmoidoscopy/colonoscopy is performed in the knee-chest position.  Why A wrong: Lithotomy is for vaginal/perineal exam.  Why C wrong: Left side/Sims' is for cyanotic spell or rectal procedures here; the source lists knee-chest for sigmoidoscopy/colonoscopy.  Why D wrong: High Fowler's is not used for endoscopy of the lower bowel."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"A child experiencing a cyanotic (\"tet\") spell should be placed in which position to improve oxygenation?","options":["Supine flat","Knee-chest position","Trendelenburg","Prone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: During a cyanotic spell, the knee-chest position improves systemic vascular resistance and oxygenation.  Why A/C/D wrong: These positions do not relieve a cyanotic spell as knee-chest does."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"Vital signs are best defined as:","options":["A measurement of body weight only","An evaluation of the vital signs of the patient — measurable indicators of living organisms","A laboratory blood test","A psychological assessment"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Vital signs are the first set of clinical examinations and an evaluation of the vital signs/parameters of the patient; they are objective, measurable measurements of living organisms.  Why A/C/D wrong: Vital signs are not weight alone, lab tests, or psychological assessments."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"Vital signs are called \"cardinal signs\" or \"vital parameters\" because they:","options":["Are difficult to measure","Act as governors of body function (regulate it/regulate body control)","Require laboratory equipment","Change only during illness"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: They are called cardinal signs/vital parameters because these are working as governors of body function (regulate/control the body).  Why A/C/D wrong: They are bedside-measurable, do not require labs, and reflect ongoing physiology, not only illness."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"Which of the following is included in the traditional list of vital signs? (SATA)","options":["Temperature","Pulse","Respiration","Blood pressure","Pain (5th vital sign)"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Vital signs include Temperature, Pulse, Respiration, Blood pressure/BP, and Pain (known as the 5th vital sign).  All listed options are explicitly part of the source's \"Vital sign Includes\" list."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"In emergency conditions, in addition to the standard vital signs, which parameters are considered the 5th vital sign? (SATA)","options":["Oxygen saturation","Pupillary response","Blood glucose level","Hair color","Body weight"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The note states that in emergency conditions oxygen saturation, pupillary response, and blood glucose level are considered as the 5th vital sign.  Why D/E wrong: Hair color and body weight are not listed as emergency 5th vital sign parameters."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"In a newborn, the sequence for taking vital signs prioritizes least intrusive first because a crying baby's results may be false. Which is correct?","options":["Begin with temperature, then pulse, then respiration","Respiration → Pulse → Temperature (in neonates)","Blood pressure first, then respiration","Pain assessment before respiration"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In newborns, the least intrusive measurement is done first; the sequence of respiration is taken first followed by pulse and lastly temperature (Respiration → Pulse → Temperature), because crying may give a false reading.  Why A wrong: Temperature is the most intrusive and is done last in neonates.  Why C/D wrong: This is not the neonatal sequence described."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"In adults and children, the standard sequence of vital sign measurement is:","options":["TPR & BP (Temperature, Pulse, Respiration & Blood pressure)","Respiration → Pulse → Temperature","BP → Temperature → Pulse","Pain → BP → Pulse"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In adults and children the sequence is TPR & BP (Temperature, Pulse, Respiration, then Blood Pressure).  Why B wrong: Respiration-first is the neonatal sequence.  Why C/D wrong: These orders are not the standard adult/child sequence."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"In an emergency situation, the sequence of vital sign assessment is:","options":["Temperature → Pulse → Respiration","Respiration → BP → Temperature","BP → Pulse → Respiration → Temperature","Pain → Temperature → Pulse"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In emergency, the source's sequence is BP → Pulse → Respiration → Temperature.  Why A/B/D wrong: These orderings do not match the emergency sequence given."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"What is the normal range for body temperature according to the Summary of Vital Signs table?","options":["95.4°F or 35.2°C","98.6°F or 37°C","99.8°F or 37.6°C","96°F or 35.5°C"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The summary table lists normal temperature as 98.6°F or 37°C.  Why A wrong: 95.4°F / 35.2°C is the listed \"low\" value.  Why C wrong: 99.8°F / 37.6°C is the listed \"high\" value.  Why D wrong: 96°F is not the normal value in the table."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"According to the Summary of Vital Signs table, a high temperature is recorded as:","options":["98.6°F / 37°C","99.8°F / 37.6°C","95.4°F / 35.2°C","100 bpm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists the high temperature as 99.8°F or 37.6°C.  Why A wrong: That is normal.  Why C wrong: That is the low value.  Why D wrong: 100 bpm is a pulse value, not temperature."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"According to the Summary of Vital Signs table, the normal pulse rate is:","options":["12 to 20 bpm","60–100 bpm","110/80 mmHg","100 or higher"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists normal pulse rate as 60–100 bpm.  Why A wrong: 12–20 is the respiratory rate.  Why C wrong: 110/80 mmHg is blood pressure.  Why D wrong: 100 or higher is listed as high pulse."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"According to the table, a pulse rate considered \"high\" is:","options":["60 bpm or lower","100 bpm or higher","12 bpm","80 bpm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists high pulse as 100 bpm or higher.  Why A wrong: 60 bpm or lower is the low value.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the \"high\" pulse cutoff."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"According to the table, the normal respiratory rate is:","options":["60–100 bpm","12 to 20 bpm","20 bpm or higher","12 bpm or lower"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists normal respiratory rate as 12–20 bpm.  Why A wrong: 60–100 is the pulse range.  Why C wrong: 20 or higher is the high value.  Why D wrong: 12 or lower is the low value."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"According to the table, the normal blood pressure is:","options":["60–100 mmHg","110/80 mmHg","100/60 mmHg or lower","120/80 mmHg or higher"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists normal blood pressure as 110/80 mmHg.  Why C wrong: 100/60 mmHg or lower is the low value.  Why D wrong: 120/80 mmHg or higher is the high value.  Why A wrong: 60–100 is a pulse range, not BP."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"Body temperature is best defined as the balance between:","options":["Oxygen intake and output","Heat production and heat loss","Systolic and diastolic pressure","Inspiration and expiration"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Body temperature is the balance between heat production and heat loss.  Why A/C/D wrong: These describe respiration or blood pressure, not temperature balance."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"Body temperature represents the degree of heat that is:","options":["Lost by the body only","Maintained by our body","Produced by digestion only","Equal to environmental temperature"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Temperature is the degree of heat that is maintained by our body.  Why A/C/D wrong: It is not solely loss, only digestion-related, or equal to ambient temperature."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"The temperature-regulating centre of the body is located in the:","options":["Cerebellum","Hypothalamus (in the brain)","Medulla oblongata","Pituitary gland"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The hypothalamus is the temperature regulating centre / thermoregulation centre in the brain.  Why A/C/D wrong: The cerebellum, medulla, and pituitary do not serve as the primary thermoregulation centre."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"Heat loss and heat production are controlled by which part of the hypothalamus, respectively?","options":["Heat loss: posterior hypothalamus; Heat production: anterior hypothalamus","Heat loss: pre-optic region of anterior hypothalamus; Heat production: posterior hypothalamus","Both by the anterior hypothalamus","Both by the posterior hypothalamus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Heat loss is controlled by the pre-optic region of the anterior hypothalamus; heat production is controlled by the posterior hypothalamus.  Why A wrong: The regions are reversed.  Why C/D wrong: The two functions are controlled by different regions, not the same one."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"Thermogenesis is best described as:","options":["Heat loss through the skin","Heat production by oxidation of food & chemical action","Conversion of glucose to glycogen","The cooling of core organs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Thermogenesis is heat production by oxidation of food & chemical action.  Why A wrong: That describes heat loss, not thermogenesis.  Why C/D wrong: These do not define thermogenesis."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"Core temperature is best described as the temperature of:","options":["The skin surface","Deep tissues like heart, lungs, brain, and abdominal viscera","The extremities only","The forehead"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Core temperature is the internal body or deep tissue's temperature like heart, lungs, brain, and abdominal viscera.  Why A/D wrong: Skin/forehead reflect surface (shell) temperature.  Why C wrong: Extremities are part of the shell, not the core."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"Core body temperature remains relatively constant unless exposed to:","options":["Mild fever","Severe extremes in environmental temperature","Eating food","Minor exercise"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Core temperature remains relatively constant (98.6°F) unless exposed to severe extremes in environmental temperature.  Why A/C/D wrong: These cause minor fluctuations, not the constant-disrupting severe extremes described."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"Which sites are used to monitor core body temperature? (SATA)","options":["Tympanic membrane","Nasopharynx","Distal part of esophagus","Pulmonary artery","Forehead"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Core temperature monitoring sites include the tympanic membrane, nasopharynx, distal esophagus, and pulmonary artery.  Why E wrong: The forehead (temporal artery) is a surface/shell monitoring site, not a core site."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"Intermediate sites near to core body temperature include which of the following? (SATA)","options":["Oral","Rectum","Urinary bladder","Axilla","Forehead"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Intermediate sites near to core body temperature are oral, rectum, and urinary bladder. These are reliable but less reliable than core sites.  Why D/E wrong: Axilla and forehead/temporal are surface (skin) sites considered less reliable than core."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"Surface (shell) temperature is best characterized by which statement?","options":["It is higher than the core temperature","It is lower than the core body temperature, closer to skin, and rises and falls with the surrounding environment","It is the most accurate temperature","It is unaffected by ambient temperature"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Surface/shell temperature is closer to the skin, lower than the core, and rises and falls with the temperature of the surrounding environment; it is less reliable and less accurate.  Why A wrong: It is lower, not higher, than core.  Why C wrong: It is less reliable, not the most accurate.  Why D wrong: It is affected by the surrounding environment."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"Which sites are used to monitor surface (shell) temperature? (SATA)","options":["Axilla (armpit)","Forehead (temporal artery)","Pulmonary artery","Distal esophagus","Nasopharynx"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: Surface/shell temperature monitoring sites are the axilla (armpit) and forehead (temporal artery).  Why C/D/E wrong: Pulmonary artery, distal esophagus, and nasopharynx are core monitoring sites."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"What is Specific Dynamic Action (SDA)?","options":["The amount of heat lost through the skin","The amount of energy expenditure above the basal metabolic rate due to the cost of processing food for use and storage","The action of the hypothalamus on heat loss","The conversion of glycogen to glucose"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: SDA is the amount of energy expenditure above the basal metabolic rate due to the cost of processing food for use and storage.  Why A/C/D wrong: These describe heat loss, thermoregulation, or glycogenolysis, not SDA."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"Which macronutrient has the highest Specific Dynamic Action (SDA)?","options":["Carbohydrate","Fat (lipids)","Protein","Mixed diet"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Proteins have the highest SDA, while carbohydrates and fats have the lowest SDA.  Why A/B wrong: Carbohydrate and fat have the lowest SDA.  Why D wrong: A mixed diet falls between, not the highest."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"According to the Specific Dynamic Action table, what is the SDA value for protein?","options":["5%","10%","30%","50%"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The SDA table lists protein as 30%.  Why A wrong: 5% is carbohydrate.  Why B wrong: 10% is fat (lipids).  Why D wrong: 50% is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"According to the SDA of food table, the values for carbohydrate and fat respectively are:","options":["Carbohydrate 5%, Fat 10%","Carbohydrate 30%, Fat 5%","Carbohydrate 10%, Fat 30%","Carbohydrate 15%, Fat 15%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists carbohydrate 5% and fat/lipids 10%.  Why B/C/D wrong: These values do not match the table."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"SDA is the result of which type of metabolic processing?","options":["Heat loss through evaporation","Use and storage of food","Renal filtration","Pulmonary gas exchange"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: SDA reflects the cost of processing food for use and storage.  Why A/C/D wrong: SDA is not about evaporative heat loss, renal filtration, or gas exchange."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"Which of the following are listed mechanisms of heat production in the body? (SATA)","options":["Oxidation of food","Exercise","Hormonal effect","Change in posture","Shivering"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Listed heat-producing factors include oxidation of food, exercise, hormonal effect, change in posture, shivering, and starvation (during which heat production is decreased).  All options A–E are explicitly listed as heat production mechanisms."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"During starvation, heat production in the body is:","options":["Increased","Decreased","Unchanged","Doubled"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In starvation, heat production is decreased.  Why A/C/D wrong: Starvation lowers metabolic heat production, it does not increase, double, or keep it unchanged."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"Which of the following are mechanisms of heat loss from the body? (SATA)","options":["Radiation","Evaporation","Conduction","Convection","Oxidation"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Heat loss occurs through radiation, evaporation, conduction, and convection.  Why E wrong: Oxidation is a heat-production mechanism, not heat loss."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"Radiation, as a heat loss mechanism, is defined as the:","options":["Transfer of heat via moving air","Transfer of heat via infrared rays / most common heat-loss method","Transfer through direct contact","Loss of heat as perspiration evaporates"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Radiation is the transfer of heat via infrared rays.  Why A wrong: Moving air describes convection.  Why C wrong: Direct contact describes conduction.  Why D wrong: Perspiration evaporating describes evaporation."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"Evaporation as a heat loss mechanism occurs as:","options":["Heat lost via infrared rays","Heat loss as perspiration & insensible loss (e.g., as sweat evaporates from the skin)","Heat transferred via moving air","Heat transferred via direct contact"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Evaporation is heat loss as perspiration and insensible loss (water evaporating from the skin/respiratory tract).  Why A/C/D wrong: These describe radiation, convection, and conduction respectively."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"Convection as a heat loss mechanism is best described as heat loss by:","options":["Direct contact with another surface","Air currents / moving air over the skin","Infrared radiation","Sweat evaporation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Convection is heat loss by air currents (moving air carrying heat away from the skin).  Why A wrong: Direct contact is conduction.  Why C wrong: Infrared radiation is radiation.  Why D wrong: Sweat evaporation is evaporation."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"Conduction as a heat loss mechanism is defined as:","options":["Heat loss by direct contact with another surface, such as by an ice pack (usually about 3%)","Heat transfer through moving air","Heat loss via infrared rays","Heat lost as sweat evaporates"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Conduction is heat loss by direct contact with another surface, such as an ice pack, usually about 3%.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe convection, radiation, and evaporation."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"According to the Normal Body Temperature table, the normal oral temperature is:","options":["37.0°C / 98.6°F","37.5°C / 99.5°F","36.5°C / 97.6°F","35.0°C / 95°F"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists oral normal temperature as 37.0°C / 98.6°F.  Why B wrong: 37.5°C/99.5°F is the rectal value.  Why C wrong: 36.5°C/97.6°F is the axillary value.  Why D wrong: 35.0°C/95°F is not a listed normal value."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"According to the Normal Body Temperature table, the normal rectal temperature is:","options":["37.0°C / 98.6°F","37.5°C / 99.5°F","36.5°C / 97.6°F","37.0°C / 97.6°F"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Rectal normal temperature is 37.5°C / 99.5°F (slightly higher than oral).  Why A wrong: That is oral.  Why C wrong: That is axillary.  Why D wrong: This combination is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"According to the Normal Body Temperature table, the normal axillary temperature is:","options":["37.0°C / 98.6°F","37.5°C / 99.5°F","36.5°C / 97.6°F","38.0°C / 100°F"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Axillary normal temperature is 36.5°C / 97.6°F (lowest of the sites).  Why A wrong: That is oral.  Why B wrong: That is rectal.  Why D wrong: This is not a listed normal value."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"According to the table, the normal tympanic and temporal-artery temperatures are approximately:","options":["Tympanic 37.5°C / 99.5°F; Temporal 37.0°C / 98.6°F","Tympanic 36.5°C / 97.6°F; Temporal 35°C","Tympanic 35°C; Temporal 36°C","Both 39°C"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists tympanic 37.5°C/99.5°F and temporal artery 37.0°C/98.6°F as normal values.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the table values."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"A nurse documents an axillary (or skin) temperature. Which range should the nurse expect for a normal oral-route reading?","options":["95.4°F – 96°C","97.6°F – 99.6°F (about 36.5°C – 37.5°C)","100°F – 102°F","Below 95°F"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The \"Should Remember\" box notes axillary–oral–rectum 97.6°F–98.6°F–99.6°F (≈36.5°C–37.0°C–37.5°C) <37.5°C as the normal spread.  Why A/C/D wrong: These ranges fall outside the documented normal spread."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"When measuring temperature with a client who has just had a hot bath or a hot or cold beverage, the nurse should:","options":["Measure immediately for accuracy","Wait at least 1 hour after these activities before measuring body temperature","Use only the rectal route","Add 1°F to the reading"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source's \"Should Remember\" point states to wait at least 1 hour after a hot bath or hot/cold beverage before measuring body temperature, as it alters the reading.  Why A wrong: Immediate measurement gives a falsely altered value.  Why C wrong: Route change does not correct the timing problem.  Why D wrong: Arbitrarily adjusting the reading is not appropriate."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"If a client drinks cold or hot fluids, the nurse should wait for how long before taking an oral temperature measurement?","options":["5 minutes","20–30 minutes for temperature measurement","2 hours","No wait needed"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If a client drinks cold or hot fluids, wait 20–30 minutes for accurate oral temperature measurement.  Why A wrong: 5 minutes is insufficient.  Why C wrong: 2 hours is longer than required.  Why D wrong: A wait is required to avoid a false reading."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"After a client has smoked, eaten, or had a hot/cold drink, the nurse must wait how long before measuring oral body temperature?","options":["5 minutes","30 minutes after smoking, eating, or drinking a hot or cold liquid (per the \"Lastly\" note)","2 hours","No wait required for smoking"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The \"Lastly\" point notes that mouth-route oral temperature measurement should be delayed about 30 minutes after smoking, eating, or drinking a hot or cold liquid.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the source's wait time."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"According to WHO classification, mild hypothermia in a newborn is defined by which temperature range?","options":["32.0–35.9°C","36.0–36.4°C","<32°C","36.5–37.5°C"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The WHO classification lists mild hypothermia (cold stress) as 36.0–36.4°C.  Why A wrong: 32.0–35.9°C is moderate hypothermia.  Why C wrong: <32°C is severe hypothermia.  Why D wrong: 36.5–37.5°C is the normal range."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"According to WHO classification, moderate hypothermia in a newborn is defined as:","options":["36.0–36.4°C","32.0–35.9°C","<32°C","36.5–37.5°C"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Moderate hypothermia (cause of concern) is 32.0–35.9°C.  Why A wrong: That is mild.  Why C wrong: That is severe.  Why D wrong: That is normal."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"According to WHO classification, severe hypothermia in a newborn is defined as:","options":["<32°C — dangerous, requiring immediate warming of the newborn (outside given, intensive and skilled care)","36.0–36.4°C","32.0–35.9°C","36.5–37.5°C"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Severe hypothermia is <32.0°C, classified as dangerous and requiring immediate warming of the newborn (outside given/intensive skilled care).  Why B wrong: That is mild.  Why C wrong: That is moderate.  Why D wrong: That is normal."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"According to the WHO classification table, the normal temperature range for a newborn is:","options":["36.0–36.4°C","36.5–37.5°C","32.0–35.9°C","<32°C"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The normal newborn temperature range is 36.5–37.5°C.  Why A wrong: That is mild hypothermia.  Why C wrong: That is moderate hypothermia.  Why D wrong: That is severe hypothermia."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"(RECALL THE NCLEX) According to WHO, mild hypothermia is considered in a newborn based on axillary temperature. Which value matches mild hypothermia (recorded in Centigrade)?","options":["36.5° Centigrade","35.5° Centigrade","36.7° Centigrade","38.6° Centigrade"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: A newborn is considered to have mild hypothermia when the axillary temperature is less than 36.4°C; among the options, 36.7°C is closest within the mild-stress framing the source's answer flags (mild range 36.0–36.4°C). Per source, the right answer is C.  Why A/B/D wrong: The source designates option C as the right answer for this recall item."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"(Recall the NCLEX) Which route is used as a core body temperature assessment site, EXCEPT one option? Identify the EXCEPTION.","options":["Temporal artery","Rectum","Oral","Urinary bladder"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Temporal artery is a surface/shell site, not a core/intermediate-core site, so it is the EXCEPTION; the source marks the right answer as A.  Why B/C/D wrong: Rectum, oral, and urinary bladder are intermediate sites near core body temperature."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"When measuring temperature, the nurse should remember that body temperature varies during the day. The general pattern is:","options":["Lowest in the early morning and highest in the evening","Highest in the morning and lowest in the evening","Constant throughout the day","Highest at midnight"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Body temperature varies, being lowest in the early morning and highest in the evening (circadian variation).  Why B/C/D wrong: This contradicts the documented diurnal pattern."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"When reporting temperature to a health care provider, the nurse should be sure to:","options":["Report only the number","Say which method was used (route) along with the value","Round to the nearest whole degree","Report only abnormal values"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: When reporting temperature, be sure to say which method/route was used, because normal values differ by route.  Why A wrong: A number without the route can be misinterpreted.  Why C/D wrong: Rounding or selectively reporting can mislead the provider."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"What is the recommended frequency of temperature measurement for a patient with a high fever?","options":["Once a day","If patient is afebrile then 2 times daily; if patient has a high fever, record every 1 to 2 hourly","Only on admission","Every 8 hours regardless of fever"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If a patient is afebrile, take temperature about 2 times in morning and evening; if the patient has a high fever, record the temperature every 1–2 hourly.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the documented frequency."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"Which of the following are advantages of the oral route of temperature measurement? (SATA)","options":["Most common route","Convenient","Good blood supply under the tongue","No need for privacy","Gives accurate reading"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Listed oral-route advantages include: most common route, convenient, good blood supply under the tongue, no need of privacy, and gives accurate reading.  All listed options are explicitly stated advantages of the oral route."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"What is the normal oral temperature range and duration of placement for oral temperature measurement?","options":["96.4°F (35.9°C), 1 minute","98.6°F (37±0.7) and held in place under the tongue","99.5°F (37.5°C), 10 minutes","95°F, 30 seconds"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The normal oral temperature is 98.6°F (37±0.7°F/0.5°C) and the thermometer is held in place under the tongue.  Why A/C/D wrong: These values/positions do not match the oral-route data."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"Which of the following are contraindications to oral temperature measurement? (SATA)","options":["Client having convulsions/seizures","Mouth breather client","Frequent attacks of cough","Children under 6 years of age","Mentally ill patients"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Contraindications to the oral route include clients having convulsions, mouth-breathers, frequent attacks of cough, children under 6 years of age, and mentally ill patients (plus uncooperative clients and after oral surgery/oral route in mouth surgery).  All options listed are documented contraindications."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"Which of the following are nursing considerations during oral temperature measurement? (SATA)","options":["Wait 10–30 minutes after drinking or eating any hot or cold liquid to measure oral temperature","Turn the thermometer on","Put the thermometer in the mouth and under the tongue at the back, close to the lips around the tongue","Listen for the signal or beep","Remove the thermometer and put it in a freezer immediately"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Documented oral nursing considerations include waiting 10–30 minutes after hot/cold intake, turning the thermometer on, placing it in the mouth under the tongue at the back close to the lips, and listening for the signal/beep, then reading at the eye level and removing it in front of a light source.  Why E wrong: Freezing the thermometer is not a documented step."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"When taking an oral temperature reading, the nurse should listen for the beep/signal and then:","options":["Read the result while inserting deeper","Read the reading at eye level / in front of a light source","Shake the thermometer first","Re-insert without cleaning"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After the beep, the reading is taken at eye level/in front of a light source for accuracy.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not part of the documented reading technique."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"The axillary route of temperature measurement is described as which type of method?","options":["Most accurate, intrusive method","Most common method of temperature measurement; least intrusive (used in children)","Most invasive, core route","Reserved for unconscious adults only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The axillary route is the most common method of temperature measurement and is the least intrusive; it is commonly used in children.  Why A/C wrong: It is not the most accurate or a core/invasive route.  Why D wrong: It is broadly applicable, not reserved only for unconscious adults."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"What is the normal axillary temperature according to the axillary-route section?","options":["98.6°F","97.6°F (36.5°C)","99.5°F","100°F"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The normal axillary temperature is 97.6°F (36.5°C), the lowest of the routine routes.  Why A wrong: 98.6°F is oral.  Why C wrong: 99.5°F is rectal.  Why D wrong: 100°F is febrile."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"For axillary temperature measurement, the thermometer should be held in place for approximately:","options":["30 seconds","3–5 minutes (held in position until the beep/result)","10 minutes","1 hour"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For axillary measurement, hold the mercury thermometer for 3–5 minutes in position until beep/result.  Why A/C/D wrong: These durations do not match the axillary-route instruction."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"Which of the following are indications/advantages for the axillary route? (SATA)","options":["Most accurate (oral route is contraindicated)","Cough","Used when oral route is contraindicated","Unconscious and dehydrated patient","History of convulsion & mental illness"],"correct":"B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The axillary route is indicated when the oral route is contraindicated, including with cough, unconscious and dehydrated patients, and history of convulsion/mental illness.  Why A wrong: The axillary route is the least accurate (surface site), not the most accurate."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"Which of the following are contraindications to axillary temperature measurement? (SATA)","options":["Axilla is moist/sweaty (perspiration), which is not necessary to dry — wipe and dry the axilla first","Inflammation of the axilla","Emaciated patient","Patient in shock with poor peripheral perfusion","Healthy adult"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Axillary contraindications/cautions include a moist (perspiring) axilla that must be wiped/dried first, inflammation of the axilla, emaciated patients, and patients in shock/poor perfusion that make a surface reading unreliable.  Why E wrong: A healthy adult is not a contraindication."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"During axillary temperature measurement, the nurse should position the thermometer so that:","options":["It floats freely in the armpit","It is held across and over the chest (arm folded) so the bulb rests in the axilla","It points outward away from the body","The client raises the arm fully"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The axilla thermometer is folded/held across and over the chest so the bulb stays in the axilla to obtain an accurate reading.  Why A/C/D wrong: A freely floating, outward-pointing, or raised-arm position prevents proper bulb contact."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"The rectal route of temperature measurement is best described as:","options":["Least accurate route","Most reliable, known as core body temperature, with normal temperature by rectum 99.6°F","A surface temperature site","Used only in adults over 60"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The rectal route is the most reliable, known as core body temperature; the normal temperature by rectum is 99.6°F.  Why A/C wrong: It is the most reliable core route, not least accurate or surface.  Why D wrong: It is not restricted to adults over 60."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"When using a rectal thermometer for a child for 1 minute, how does its reading compare to other routes?","options":["Lower than oral","Held the mercury thermometer for 1 minute (short time compared to other routes); reflects core temperature","Same as axillary","Unrelated to core temperature"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The rectal mercury thermometer is held for about 1 minute (a short time compared to other routes) and reflects core body temperature.  Why A/C/D wrong: The rectal reading is higher (core) and not equivalent to axillary or unrelated to core."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"Which statements about the advantages of the rectal route are correct? (SATA)","options":["More reliable or accurate compared to other routes due to vascularity of the rectum","It is not influenced by the external environment (air)","It is the least accurate site","It is affected by mouth breathing","It reflects core/deep body temperature"],"correct":"A,B,E","rationale":"Why correct: Rectal advantages: more reliable/accurate compared to other routes due to vascularity of the rectum, not influenced by external air/environment, and reflects core body temperature.  Why C wrong: It is the most, not least, accurate.  Why D wrong: Mouth breathing affects the oral route, not the rectal route."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"Which of the following are contraindications to rectal temperature measurement? (SATA)","options":["Rectal surgery","Diarrhea & constipation","Hemorrhoids & appendicitis","Blood cancer/hemophilia, thrombocytopenia","A healthy febrile adult who can cooperate"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Rectal contraindications include rectal surgery, diarrhea & constipation, hemorrhoids & appendicitis, and blood cancer/hemophilia/thrombocytopenia (bleeding risk). Cardiac clients are also cautioned because rectal stimulation can stimulate the vagus nerve.  Why E wrong: A healthy cooperative adult is not a contraindication."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"Why is the rectal route cautioned in cardiac clients?","options":["It causes hyperthermia","Inserting the thermometer/taking a rectal temperature can stimulate the vagus nerve, resulting in bradycardia","It raises blood pressure permanently","It causes hemorrhoids"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In cardiac clients, taking a rectal temperature can stimulate the vagus nerve, resulting in bradycardia/cardiac effects, so caution is needed.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the documented reason for cardiac caution."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"Which of the following are correct nursing considerations for rectal temperature measurement? (SATA)","options":["Explain the procedure to the client","Reassure and provide privacy to the patient","Place the client in Sims' lateral or left lateral position","Lubricate the catheter/thermometer with water-soluble jelly","Insert the thermometer with no lubrication"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Rectal nursing considerations include explaining the procedure, reassuring/providing privacy, placing the client in Sims' lateral / left lateral position, lubricating with water-soluble jelly, and separating the buttocks until the anal sphincter is clearly visible.  Why E wrong: Inserting without lubrication is incorrect; water-soluble lubricant must be used."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"In what direction should the rectal thermometer be inserted?","options":["Anterior and upward","Posterior & downward direction","Laterally toward the hip","Straight horizontally"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The rectal thermometer is inserted in a posterior and downward direction to follow the rectal contour safely.  Why A/C/D wrong: These directions risk mucosal injury and are not the documented technique."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"How far is a rectal thermometer inserted in an adult versus a child?","options":["1.5 inches in adults and 0.5–1 inches in a child","3 inches in adults and 2 inches in a child","0.5 inches in both","4 inches in adults"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The thermometer is inserted 1.5 inches in adults and 0.5–1 inch in a child.  Why B/C/D wrong: These depths do not match the documented insertion depths."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"To relax the anal sphincter and ease insertion of a rectal thermometer, the nurse should instruct the patient to:","options":["Hold their breath","Take a deep breath and exhale (deep inhale & exhale)","Bear down forcefully","Cough repeatedly"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Instruct the patient to take a deep breath / deep inhale and exhale to relax the sphincter while inserting the thermometer.  Why A/C/D wrong: Breath-holding, bearing down, or coughing does not relax the sphincter as instructed."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"After obtaining a rectal temperature, the nurse should hold the probe in place until:","options":["The client says to stop","It beeps, then read the temperature on the display","Exactly 10 minutes pass","The probe cools down"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hold the probe in place until you hear a beep, then read the temperature on the display.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the documented procedure for reading the rectal probe."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"After reading a rectal thermometer, infection control requires the nurse to:","options":["Reinsert it immediately","Disinfect it with antiseptic solution using a gauze of a disposable cover, dispose of the probe cover, and disinfect accordingly","Rinse with plain water only","Store it without cleaning"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Before holding the result, the nurse should clean the thermometer with antiseptic solution using a gauze or disposable cover, dispose of the probe cover, and disinfect accordingly to prevent cross-contamination.  Why A/C/D wrong: Reinserting, rinsing with plain water only, or not cleaning violates infection control."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"Using a toilet tissue, the nurse wipes off any feces or excess lubricant when:","options":["Before insertion only","After removing the thermometer","During insertion only","It is never done"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Using toilet tissue, the nurse wipes off any feces or excess lubricant after removing the thermometer.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the documented post-removal cleaning step."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"Which route is the most reliable route of temperature measurement, and which is the most common route?","options":["Most reliable: oral; most common: rectal","Most reliable: rectal route; most common: oral route","Most reliable: axillary; most common: tympanic","Most reliable: tympanic; most common: rectal"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the most reliable route of temperature measurement is the rectal route, and the most common route is the oral route.  Why A/C/D wrong: These reverse or mismatch the documented reliability/commonality."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"Tympanic membrane temperature measurement is described as:","options":["A surface temperature site","An internal body temperature, known as core body temperature","The least reliable route","A route contraindicated in all children"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The tympanic membrane reflects internal/core body temperature.  Why A/C wrong: It is a core measurement, not a surface or least-reliable route.  Why D wrong: It is used in children with age-appropriate ear-pull technique, not contraindicated in all children."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"What is the normal tympanic temperature, and how long is the method held to provide a quick result?","options":["97.6°F, held 5 minutes","99.6°F, held for 2–3 seconds so this method provides quick results","95°F, held 10 minutes","98.6°F, held 1 minute"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The normal tympanic temperature is 99.6°F and the probe is held for 2–3 seconds, so this method provides quick results.  Why A/C/D wrong: These values/timings do not match the tympanic-route data."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"When using a tympanic membrane thermometer, how should the ear be pulled for a child under 3 years of age?","options":["Upward and backward","Downward and backward","Straight outward","No pull needed"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: While measuring temperature from the tympanic membrane, for a child under 3 years the ear/pinna is pulled downward and backward.  Why A wrong: Upward and backward is for children over 3 years and adults.  Why C/D wrong: Straight outward or no pull does not straighten the canal correctly."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"When using a tympanic membrane thermometer for a child over 3 years of age or an adult, how should the ear be pulled?","options":["Downward and backward","Upward and backward","Straight down","No manipulation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For a child older than 3 years and adults, the ear is pulled upward and backward to straighten the canal.  Why A wrong: Downward and backward is for children under 3 years.  Why C/D wrong: These do not straighten the adult ear canal correctly."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"Which of the following are contraindications to tympanic membrane temperature measurement? (SATA)","options":["Ear drum perforation","Ear infection (otitis)","Otorrhoea (ear discharge)","Healthy intact ear canal","Routine vital signs in a well adult"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Tympanic contraindications include ear drum perforation, ear infection, and otorrhoea (ear discharge).  Why D/E wrong: A healthy intact ear canal and routine vitals in a well adult are not contraindications."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"Which of the following are correct nursing considerations for tympanic temperature measurement? (SATA)","options":["Use a tympanic membrane thermometer","Insert the ear probe snugly into the ear canal using gentle but firm pressure, angling toward the jaw","Push the button to take the temperature and hold until reading is obtained","Remove the thermometer after reading","Insert the probe deep into the perforated drum"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Documented tympanic nursing considerations include using a tympanic membrane thermometer, inserting the probe snugly with gentle firm pressure angled toward the jaw (especially if the person is younger than 3 years, also pulling the ear back), pushing the button and holding until the reading is obtained, then removing the thermometer.  Why E wrong: Inserting into a perforated drum is contraindicated and dangerous."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Patient positioning for conditions/surgeries…","question":"(RECALL THE NCLEX) In a patient, core body temperature would best be assessed by which of the following?","options":["Forehead and skin","Axilla","Oral","Tympanic membrane"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The tympanic membrane reflects core body temperature; the source marks the right answer as D.  Why A wrong: Forehead/skin is a surface site.  Why B wrong: Axilla is a surface site (least reliable).  Why C wrong: Oral is an intermediate site, less reliable than tympanic for core."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"A glass thermometer used to measure a patient's body temperature is also referred to as a:","options":["Tympanic thermometer","Clinical thermometer","Lotion thermometer","Temporal artery thermometer"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A glass thermometer is also known as a clinical thermometer.  Why A wrong: Tympanic thermometers measure ear temperature in 2–3 seconds, not glass.  Why C wrong: Lotion thermometers measure temperature of liquid solution/water.  Why D wrong: Temporal artery thermometers measure temporal artery temperature."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"The glass (clinical) thermometer was invented in 1866 by which physician?","options":["Gabriel Fahrenheit","Anders Celsius","Thomas Clifford Allbutt","Carl Wunderlich"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The glass thermometer was invented by Thomas Clifford Allbutt in 1866, a British physician.  Why A wrong: Fahrenheit devised the Fahrenheit temperature scale, not this thermometer.  Why B wrong: Celsius devised the Celsius scale.  Why D wrong: Wunderlich is associated with normal body temperature data, not invention of this thermometer."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"The part of a glass thermometer that is a cylindrical shape and gives results in centigrade (°C) and Fahrenheit (°F) is the:","options":["Bulb","Stem","Range","Probe"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The stem is cylindrical in shape and gives results in centigrade (°C) and Fahrenheit (°F).  Why A wrong: The bulb is the long & cylindrical-shaped part that holds the substance (mercury).  Why C wrong: The range refers to the scale 94°F–108°F or 35°C–42°C.  Why D wrong: A \"probe\" is a separate type of electronic thermometer component."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"The standard measuring range of a clinical glass thermometer is:","options":["90°F–100°F or 30°C–38°C","94°F–108°F or 35°C–42°C","96°F–110°F or 36°C–43°C","92°F–106°F or 34°C–41°C"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The range of a clinical thermometer is 94°F–108°F or 35°C–42°C.  Why A, C, D wrong: These do not match the documented clinical thermometer range."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"At what temperature point do the Celsius and Fahrenheit scales read equal on both scales?","options":["0° on both scales","32° on both scales","-40° on both scales","100° on both scales"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: -40°C or -40°F is the point at which the temperatures are equal on both scales.  Why A wrong: 0°C equals 32°F, not equal values.  Why B wrong: 32°F equals 0°C; they are not equal at 32.  Why D wrong: 100°C equals 212°F; not equal."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"Which advantage best describes an electronic (digital) thermometer?","options":["It contains mercury for accuracy","It is unbreakable, easy to use, fast reading and gives readings within 30–60 seconds","It can only be used in non-clinical areas","It must be sterilized after every use in glutaraldehyde"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A digital thermometer is unbreakable, easy to use, fast reading, and gives a reading within 30–60 seconds.  Why A wrong: Electronic thermometers are valued for being mercury-free, not for containing mercury.  Why C wrong: It is suitable for clinical use, not restricted to non-clinical areas.  Why D wrong: This relates to disinfection generally, not the defining advantage of digital thermometers."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"A lotion thermometer is primarily used to check the temperature of:","options":["The patient's oral cavity","Liquid solution or water","The infant's ear canal","The temporal artery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A lotion thermometer is used to check the temperature of a liquid solution or water.  Why A, C, D wrong: These are functions of clinical, tympanic, and temporal-artery thermometers respectively."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"The freezing point of water as registered on a lotion thermometer is:","options":["0°C / 32°F","0°C / 100°C","32°C / 0°F","4°C / 39°F"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The lotion thermometer reading shows the freezing point as 0°C/100°C... per source the freezing temperature is 0°C (32°F).  Why B wrong: 100°C is the boiling point of water, not freezing.  Why C wrong: Values are reversed/incorrect.  Why D wrong: This is not the freezing point."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"A tympanic thermometer gives a reading within how many seconds?","options":["30–60 seconds","2–3 seconds","30 seconds","5 minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A tympanic (ear) thermometer gives a reading in 2–3 seconds.  Why A wrong: 30–60 seconds applies to digital thermometers.  Why C wrong: A disposable thermometer registers in about 60 seconds.  Why D wrong: 5 minutes relates to disinfectant soak time for Dettol, not a reading."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"A tympanic thermometer is best suited for which population?","options":["Only critically ill ICU patients","Infants & younger children","Only elderly patients","Only adults over 60"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: It is mostly used in infants & younger children.  Why A, C, D wrong: Not the documented preferred population for the tympanic thermometer."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"A disposable paper thermometer registers a temperature within approximately:","options":["2–3 seconds","30 seconds","60 seconds","5 minutes"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: A disposable paper thermometer gives results within 60 seconds.  Why A wrong: 2–3 seconds is tympanic.  Why B, D wrong: Not the documented disposable thermometer time."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"A disposable paper thermometer is made of a paper strip that is chemically treated and contains \"Liquid crystal\" chemical DOTs. The main advantage of its disposable design is that it:","options":["Reads faster than a digital thermometer","Is used only once and discarded, helping prevent infection","Can be reused after sterilization","Measures temporal artery temperature"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: It is used once and then disposed to prevent infection.  Why A wrong: Speed is not its defining advantage.  Why C wrong: It is disposable (single-use), not reusable.  Why D wrong: That function belongs to the temporal artery thermometer."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"Placement of a temporal artery thermometer probe is on which quadrant of the head?","options":["Left lower quadrant","Right upper quadrant","Right lower quadrant","Left upper quadrant"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Placement of the probe is in the right upper quadrant region because the liver is located in this quadrant, so this region will give fixed temporal artery temperature.  Why A, C, D wrong: These do not correspond to the documented placement region."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"A temp probe used to measure temperature is usually used in which setting?","options":["Home setting only","ICU, usually in newborns","Outpatient clinics only","Ambulance transport only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A temp probe is most commonly used in ICU, usually in newborns.  Why A, C, D wrong: Not the documented usual setting for the temp probe."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"According to the disinfectant table, the required immersion/soak time for a thermometer placed in Dettol is:","options":["5 min","5 sec","15–20 seconds","60 seconds"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Dettol at 1:40 dilution requires a 5-minute soak time.  Why B wrong: 5 sec is the Savlon (1:40) time.  Why C wrong: 15–20 seconds is the Spirit time.  Why D wrong: 60 seconds is a disposable-thermometer reading time, not a disinfectant time."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"Per the disinfectant table, the dilution and soak time for Savlon used to disinfect a thermometer is:","options":["1:40 dilution, 5 min","1:40 dilution, 5 sec","Concentrated, 15–20 seconds","1:20 dilution, 10 min"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Savlon at 1:40 dilution requires a 5-second soak.  Why A wrong: 5 min is Dettol's time.  Why C wrong: Concentrated/15–20 seconds is Spirit.  Why D wrong: Not listed."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"Spirit, used as a thermometer disinfectant, is used in which form and for how long?","options":["1:40 dilution for 5 minutes","Concentrated form for 15–20 seconds","1:40 dilution for 5 seconds","Diluted 1:100 for 60 seconds"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Spirit is used concentrated for 15–20 seconds.  Why A wrong: That is Dettol.  Why C wrong: That is Savlon.  Why D wrong: Not documented."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"After use, a thermometer should first be washed with soap & water and then immersed in disinfectant. The correct disinfecting sequence is:","options":["Bulb to stem","Stem to bulb","Stem only","Bulb only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The thermometer should be washed and then disinfected by changing direction from stem to bulb (so contaminated end is treated last), and after taking temperature, change direction from stem to bulb (B85) to prevent contamination of the less-contaminated area.  Why A wrong: Source specifies stem-to-bulb direction.  Why C, D wrong: Both the stem and bulb are addressed; not a single part."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"When taking an oral temperature with a clinical thermometer, the thermometer should be placed under the tongue. Which value is documented as the normal body temperature in °C (oral)?","options":["35°C","37°C (98.6°F)","42°C","40°C"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 37°C (or 98.6°F) is considered normal body temperature (Stem to Bulb / B85 reference).  Why A wrong: 35°C is the lower end of the thermometer range, not normal body temperature.  Why C wrong: 42°C is the upper range limit.  Why D wrong: 40°C indicates fever/hyperpyrexia range."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"According to WHO guidelines, mercury is best described as a/an:","options":["Harmless organic compound","Neurotoxin that can damage the brain, spinal cord, kidneys, eyes, and liver","Antiseptic agent","Essential trace mineral"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Mercury is a neurotoxin; exposure to mercury vapor (via central and peripheral nervous system) can damage the brain, spinal cord, kidneys, eyes, and liver.  Why A wrong: It is hazardous, not harmless.  Why C wrong: It is not used as an antiseptic.  Why D wrong: It is toxic, not an essential nutrient."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"Which of the following is a recommended FIRST step in WHO mercury spill management? (SATA)","options":["Close all the doors","Switch off all the fans and put caution boards","Wear all PPE such as gown, mask, gloves and eye protection","Vacuum the spill immediately to remove mercury","Take a soft card-board type of paper/use an X-ray film"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: Steps of mercury spill management include closing all doors; switching off fans; wearing PPE (gown, mask, gloves, eye protection); and using a soft cardboard/X-ray film to scoop, plus picking up beads with a syringe and sealing in a bag/bottle.  Why D wrong: A regular vacuum cleaner must NOT be used as it will spread mercury vapors and contaminate the vacuum cleaner."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"After cleaning a mercury spill, the contaminated area should be mopped using:","options":["A regular vacuum cleaner","A broom and dustpan","Sodium hypochlorite solution","Plain tap water only"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: After cleaning the mercury, mop the area with Sodium hypochlorite solution and send it separately to the BMW agency.  Why A wrong: A vacuum cleaner spreads mercury vapor.  Why B wrong: A broom is not the documented method; the spill is scooped/collected.  Why D wrong: Plain water is not the documented decontaminant."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"Which actions should be AVOIDED when cleaning up a mercury spill? (SATA)","options":["Use a regular vacuum cleaner","Use a broom to handle mercury, as it will break it up into smaller droplets","Place mercury or contaminated clothing/fabrics in a washing machine","Pour mercury beads into a syringe for collection","Use a large garbage bag/X-ray film to scoop"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Do NOT use a regular vacuum cleaner (spreads vapors), do NOT handle mercury with a broom (breaks it into smaller droplets), and do NOT wash contaminated clothing/fabrics in a washing machine (contaminates the machine and wastewater).  Why D wrong: Picking up mercury beads with a syringe is a correct step.  Why E wrong: Using a cardboard/X-ray film to scoop is a correct collection method."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"Using the conversion formula, convert 23°F to Celsius. (Formula: °C = 5/9 × (°F − 32))","options":["-5°C","-3°C","0°C","5°C"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: °C = 5/9 × (23 − 32) = 5/9 × (−9) = −5°C, matching the worked example.  Why B, C, D wrong: They do not result from correct application of the formula."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"Convert 15°C to Fahrenheit. (Formula: °F = 9/5 × °C + 32)","options":["49°F","59°F","60°F","27°F"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: °F = (9/5 × 15) + 32 = 27 + 32 = 59°F, matching the worked example.  Why A, C, D wrong: Incorrect arithmetic results."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"The formula used to convert Celsius to Fahrenheit is:","options":["°C = 5/9 × (°F − 32)","°F = 9/5 × °C + 32","°F = 5/9 × °C − 32","°C = 9/5 × °F + 32"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: To convert Celsius to Fahrenheit: °F = 9/5 × °C + 32.  Why A wrong: That is the Fahrenheit-to-Celsius formula.  Why C, D wrong: Incorrect coefficients/operations."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"Fever (pyrexia) is defined as an elevation of body temperature at or more than:","options":["36°C / 96.8°F","38°C / 100.4°F","40°C / 104°F","37°C / 98.6°F"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Fever is elevation of body temperature at or more than 38°C or 100.4°F.  Why A wrong: This is around normal/low range.  Why C wrong: 40°C indicates high/hyperpyrexia, not the threshold.  Why D wrong: 37°C is normal body temperature."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"Match the grade of pyrexia with its temperature: A \"Low or mild pyrexia\" corresponds to which range?","options":["99°F to 100°F","100°F to 101°F","101°F to 103°F","Above 105°F"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Low or mild pyrexia is 99°F to 100°F.  Why B wrong: 100°F to 101°F is moderate pyrexia.  Why C wrong: 101°F to 103°F is high pyrexia.  Why D wrong: Above 105°F is hyperpyrexia."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"Hyperpyrexia is defined as a body temperature:","options":["99°F to 100°F","Above 105°F (>105°F)","100°F to 101°F","Exactly 98.6°F"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hyperpyrexia is when body temperature reaches >105°F.  Why A wrong: That is low/mild pyrexia.  Why C wrong: That is moderate pyrexia.  Why D wrong: 98.6°F is normal temperature."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"Hypothermia is defined as a state in which the body temperature reaches:","options":[">105°F","<95°F","100.4°F","99°F–100°F"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hypothermia is when body temperature reaches <95°F.  Why A wrong: That is hyperpyrexia.  Why C wrong: That is the fever threshold.  Why D wrong: That is low/mild pyrexia."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"Euthermic state refers to a normal body temperature of:","options":["100.4°F (38°C)","98.6°F (37°C)","95°F (35°C)","105°F (40.5°C)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Euthermic is a state of normal body temperature, i.e., 98.6°F (37°C).  Why A wrong: 38°C is the fever threshold.  Why C wrong: 95°F borders hypothermia.  Why D wrong: 105°F is hyperpyrexia."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"\"Crisis of temperature\" is best described as:","options":["Gradual fall in temperature with improvement","Rapid return to normal temperature with improvement in client's health status","Rise in temperature above 105°F","Reduction in body temperature without improvement in client's health status"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Crisis is a rapid return to normal temperature with improvement in the client's health status.  Why A wrong: A gradual fall describes lysis.  Why C wrong: That describes hyperpyrexia.  Why D wrong: That describes false crisis."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"A \"false crisis\" is defined as:","options":["Reduction in body temperature WITHOUT improvement in client's health status","Rapid return to normal with improvement","A gradual decline in temperature","A sudden spike in temperature"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: False crisis is reduction in body temperature without improvement in the client's health status.  Why B wrong: That is true crisis.  Why C wrong: That is lysis.  Why D wrong: That is not the definition."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"\"Lysis of temperature\" refers to:","options":["A rapid return to normal temperature","A gradual return to normal temperature with improvement in the client's health status","A reduction without improvement","A rise above 105°F"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Lysis is the gradual return of temperature to normal with improvement in the client's health status.  Why A wrong: A rapid return is crisis.  Why C wrong: That is false crisis.  Why D wrong: That describes hyperpyrexia."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"A continuous fever is characterized by:","options":["Daily temperature returning to normal then rising again","Body temperature remaining constant in a 1° (between morning and evening) variation","Temperature returning to baseline at least once in a 24-hour period","Two peaks of fever in a 24-hour cycle"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Continuous fever shows body temperature variation in 1° between morning and evening (it stays elevated without major fluctuation).  Why A wrong: That describes intermittent fever.  Why C wrong: Returning to baseline once a day is intermittent.  Why D wrong: Two peaks describes biphasic fever."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"A remittent fever is best described as:","options":["Body temperature variation more than 1°C between morning and evening, but not returning to normal","Temperature returning to normal at least once in 24 hours","A double-peaked fever","Step-by-step rise over several days"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Remittent fever shows variation of >1°C between morning and evening but never reaches normal.  Why B wrong: That is intermittent fever.  Why C wrong: Double-peak is biphasic.  Why D wrong: Step-ladder rise is step-ladder/typhoid pattern fever."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"Which type of fever is characterized by the temperature being elevated but returning to normal (baseline) at least once in 24 hours, and is the classic pattern of malaria?","options":["Continuous fever","Remittent fever","Intermittent fever","Step-ladder fever"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Intermittent fever returns to normal at least once in 24 hours; example is malaria.  Why A wrong: Continuous fever stays elevated.  Why B wrong: Remittent fever never returns to normal.  Why D wrong: Step-ladder (typhoid) fever rises in steps."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"A biphasic fever (e.g., dengue fever) is characterized by:","options":["A single sustained plateau","A \"saddleback\" pattern — fever is high, breaks, then returns for one or more days","A step-by-step daily rise","Variation within 1° between morning and evening"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Biphasic fever is classically high then breaks (\"saddleback\") and returns for one or more days; example is dengue fever.  Why A wrong: Sustained plateau is continuous fever.  Why C wrong: Step rise is step-ladder fever.  Why D wrong: 1° variation is continuous fever."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"A step-ladder fever, which rises day by day with subsequent morning remissions, then continues to rise with shallow remissions, is the classic pattern of which disease?","options":["Malaria","Dengue fever","Typhoid fever","Brucellosis"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Step-ladder fever example is typhoid fever (rises day by day with shallow morning remissions).  Why A wrong: Malaria shows intermittent fever.  Why B wrong: Dengue shows biphasic fever.  Why D wrong: Brucellosis shows undulant/relapsing patterns, not step-ladder."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"\"Inverse fever,\" in which morning temperature is higher than evening temperature, is seen in: (SATA)","options":["Sepsis","Tuberculosis","Brucellosis","Malaria only","Dengue only"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Inverse fever (morning higher than evening) is seen in sepsis, tuberculosis, and brucellosis.  Why D wrong: Malaria typically shows intermittent fever, not specifically inverse type.  Why E wrong: Dengue shows biphasic fever, not inverse."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"A \"relapsing fever,\" in which febrile little disease is caused by Borrelia and transmitted by lice or ticks, is most associated with:","options":["Borrelia infection","Plasmodium infection","Staphylococcus infection","Influenza virus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Relapsing fever — recurring febrile disease caused by several species of the spirochete Borrelia, transmitted by lice or ticks.  Why B wrong: Plasmodium causes malaria (intermittent pattern).  Why C wrong: Staphylococcus is not the documented cause.  Why D wrong: Influenza is not the documented cause."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"\"Undulant/swinging fever,\" in which there is ≥1°C fluctuation in temperature throughout the day, is associated with:","options":["Brucellosis","Typhoid only","Hypothermia","Tetanus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Undulant/swinging fever, with ≥1°C fluctuation throughout the day, is documented in brucellosis (per the source list).  Why B, C, D wrong: Not the documented associations for undulant/swinging fever."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"A patient presents with body temperature ≥41°C (≥106.4°F) and manifests giddiness, confusion, coma, muscle cramps, and visual disturbances. This is most consistent with:","options":["Hypothermia","Heat stroke","Hypoglycemia","False crisis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Heat stroke occurs when body temperature ≥41°C (≥106.4°F), manifested by giddiness, confusion, coma, muscle cramps, visual disturbances, and hypotension.  Why A wrong: Hypothermia is low temperature (<95°F).  Why C wrong: Hypoglycemia is a glucose disorder, not defined by ≥41°C temperature.  Why D wrong: False crisis is a fall in temperature without health improvement."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"Which is an appropriate management measure for heat stroke? (SATA)","options":["Body temperature reduction rapidly","Move the patient to a cool environment","Remove excess clothes and administer cool IV fluids","Apply external warming blankets immediately","Withhold all fluids"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Heat-stroke management includes rapid body temperature reduction, moving the patient to a cool environment, removing excess clothes, and administering cool IV fluids.  Why D wrong: Warming is contraindicated in heat stroke; the patient is overheated.  Why E wrong: Cool IV fluids are administered, not withheld."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"A \"zig-zag pattern\" of temperature return to normal indicates:","options":["Crisis with measurement error","Improvement in the client's health status as temperature returns to normal in a fluctuating manner","Hyperpyrexia","Hypothermia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: When temperature returns to normal in a zig-zag pattern, it reflects improvement in the client's health status (Also Remember note).  Why A wrong: It is a recognized recovery pattern, not an error.  Why C, D wrong: It does not indicate hyperpyrexia or hypothermia."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"For every 1°C rise in body temperature, the metabolic effects include: (SATA)","options":["Pulse rate increases (about 7 to 8 beats per minute)","Respiration rate increases","Oxygen consumption increases","Blood pressure always falls to zero","Metabolism stops entirely"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: If temperature is increased by 1°C, pulse rate increases (about 7–8 beats/min), respiration rate increases, and oxygen consumption increases.  Why D wrong: Hypotension may occur in heat stroke, but BP does not fall to zero.  Why E wrong: Metabolism actually increases with temperature; it does not stop."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"The pulse is defined as the alteration in rise (expansion) and fall (recoiling) of an artery by the wave of blood passing through it during contraction of which heart chamber?","options":["Right atrium","Left ventricle","Right ventricle","Left atrium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The pulse is the alteration in rise and fall of an artery produced by the wave of blood passing through during contraction by the left ventricle.  Why A, C, D wrong: The pulse wave is generated by left ventricular contraction, not by these chambers."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"A pulse is also known as:","options":["Heart sound","Heart rate","Cardiac output","Stroke volume"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A pulse is also known as a heart rate.  Why A wrong: Heart sounds are auscultatory phenomena, not the pulse.  Why C wrong: Cardiac output is volume of blood pumped per minute.  Why D wrong: Stroke volume is volume ejected per beat."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"Which three fingers are used to palpate a pulse and should be used while manually assessing the pulse by palpation?","options":["Thumb, index, and middle fingers","Index, middle, and ring fingers (3 fingers: first, middle & index finger)","Middle, ring, and little fingers","Thumb and index only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 3 fingers (first, middle & index finger) should be used while manually assessing the pulse by palpation.  Why A wrong: The thumb should NOT be used because it has its own pulse, which can be felt and mistaken for the patient's pulse.  Why C, D wrong: Not the documented finger technique."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"The study of the pulse is known as:","options":["Sphygmology","Cardiology","Hematology","Angiology"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The study of the pulse is known as sphygmology.  Why B wrong: Cardiology is the study of the heart.  Why C wrong: Hematology is the study of blood.  Why D wrong: Angiology is the study of vessels broadly."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"\"Pulse deficit\" is defined as:","options":["The difference between systolic and diastolic pressure","The difference between apical pulse and radial pulse count","The absence of any pulse","The number of pulses felt per minute"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pulse deficit is the difference between apical pulse and radial pulse count (PD = AP − RP).  Why A wrong: That is pulse pressure.  Why C wrong: That describes absent pulse.  Why D wrong: That is the pulse rate."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"The formula for pulse deficit (PD) is:","options":["PD = RP − AP","PD = AP − RP","PD = AP + RP","PD = AP × RP"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: PD = AP − RP (apical pulse minus radial pulse).  Why A wrong: The order is reversed.  Why C, D wrong: Pulse deficit is a difference, not a sum or product."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"A pulse should be taken where an artery across or crosses a bone close to the surface of the skin. The four characteristics of a pulse are:","options":["Rate, rhythm, volume","Rate, depth, symmetry","Volume, depth, rhythm","Rate, temperature, pressure"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Pulse has three characteristics: rate, rhythm, and volume.  Why B, C, D wrong: These mix in characteristics (depth/symmetry/temperature) that belong to respiration or are not pulse characteristics."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"A pulse is best assessed by which method?","options":["Auscultation","Palpation","Percussion","Inspection"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pulse is assessed by the palpation method.  Why A wrong: Auscultation is used for the apical pulse (with a stethoscope), but the peripheral pulse is palpated.  Why C wrong: Percussion is not used to assess pulse.  Why D wrong: Inspection is used to assess respiration."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"On the pulse grading scale, a grade of \"0\" indicates:","options":["Bounding pulse","Absent pulse (no pulsation felt despite extreme pressure)","Normal pulse","Weak/thready pulse"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Grade 0 = absent pulse — no pulsation felt despite extreme pressure.  Why A wrong: Bounding pulse is grade 3+.  Why C wrong: Normal expected pulse is grade 2+.  Why D wrong: Weak/thready pulse is grade 1+."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"On the pulse grading scale, which grade represents a \"normal, easily palpable, expected pulse\"?","options":["0","1+","2+","3+"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Grade 2+ = normal, easily palpable, expected pulse.  Why A wrong: 0 is absent.  Why B wrong: 1+ is weak/thready (not easily palpable, easy to obliterate).  Why D wrong: 3+ is bounding."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"A grade \"3+\" pulse on the pulse grading scale is described as:","options":["Absent","Weak/thready, easily obliterated","Normal expected pulse","Bounding — strong pulse that does not disappear with moderate pressure"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Grade 3+ = bounding pulse, which is strong and does not disappear with moderate pressure.  Why A wrong: Absent is grade 0.  Why B wrong: Weak/thready is 1+.  Why C wrong: Normal expected is 2+."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"The normal pulse rate in adults is:","options":["40–60 beats/min, average 50 bpm","60–100 beats/min, average 72 bpm","100–120 beats/min, average 110 bpm","120–160 beats/min, average 140 bpm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Normal pulse rate in adults is 60–100 beats per minute, with an average of 72 bpm.  Why A wrong: <60 bpm is bradycardia.  Why C wrong: >100 bpm is tachycardia.  Why D wrong: That range applies to newborns, not adults."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"Tachycardia is defined as a pulse rate of:","options":["Less than 60 bpm","More than 100 bpm (≥120–160 in newborns context, >100 in adults)","Exactly 72 bpm","80–90 bpm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Tachycardia is a pulse rate more than 100 bpm.  Why A wrong: <60 bpm is bradycardia.  Why C wrong: 72 bpm is the normal adult average.  Why D wrong: This is within the normal adult range."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"Bradycardia is defined as a pulse rate:","options":["Less than 60 bpm","More than 100 bpm","60–100 bpm","Exactly 100 bpm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Bradycardia is a pulse rate of less than 60 bpm.  Why B wrong: That is tachycardia.  Why C wrong: That is the normal adult range.  Why D wrong: 100 bpm is the upper normal limit, not bradycardia."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"To accurately assess an abnormal pulse rhythm, the nurse should:","options":["Place the client in a standing position then count","Place the client in a supine position; if the pulse is taken in standing position, it may give a higher value","Count for 15 seconds and multiply by 4","Only assess the radial pulse"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: To assess pulse rate, place the client in a supine position; if the pulse is taken in standing position, then it may give a higher value.  Why A wrong: Standing falsely elevates the reading.  Why C wrong: For abnormal/irregular pulse, count for a full 1 minute (60 seconds), not 15 seconds.  Why D wrong: Apical pulse is more reliable for abnormal rhythms."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"Pulse volume refers to:","options":["The number of beats per minute","The fullness of the artery or force of the blood that felt during each beat","The interval between two beats","The regularity of beats"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Volume is the fullness of the artery or the force of the blood felt during each beat.  Why A wrong: That is the rate.  Why C wrong: That describes rhythm intervals.  Why D wrong: That describes rhythm."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"Stroke volume is the amount of blood ejected by the left ventricle with each beat; usually normal stroke volume is approximately:","options":["50 mL","70 mL","100 mL","120 mL"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Stroke volume is the amount of blood ejected by the left ventricle in each contraction; usually normal stroke volume is 70 mL.  Why A, C, D wrong: Do not match the documented normal stroke volume."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"Which of the following are abnormal pulse volumes seen in various conditions? (SATA)","options":["Bounding pulse","Water hammer pulse / Corrigan's pulse / collapsing pulse","Bigeminal pulse","Paradoxical pulse","Normal expected pulse"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Abnormal pulse volumes/types listed include bounding pulse, water hammer (Corrigan's/collapsing) pulse, bigeminal pulse, paradoxical pulse, and weak/thready pulse.  Why E wrong: A normal expected pulse is not an abnormal volume."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"While taking the radial pulse, the rhythm refers to:","options":["The strength of each beat","The regular pattern in a specific time frame, or regularity of the heartbeat","The number of beats per minute only","The blood pressure reading"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Rhythm is the regular pattern in a specific time frame, i.e., regularity of the heartbeat.  Why A wrong: That describes volume.  Why C wrong: That describes rate.  Why D wrong: BP is unrelated to pulse rhythm definition."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"A normal cardiac cycle duration is:","options":["0.4 seconds","0.8 seconds","1.2 seconds","2.0 seconds"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Normal cardiac cycle duration is 0.8 seconds.  Why A, C, D wrong: Do not match the documented cardiac cycle duration."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"Which of the following are abnormal rhythms seen in various conditions? (SATA)","options":["Arrhythmia and intermittent pulse or pulse deficit","Dicrotic pulse","Atrial fibrillation, ventricular fibrillation","Sinus arrhythmias","A perfectly regular 72-bpm pulse"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Abnormal rhythms in various conditions include arrhythmia/intermittent pulse (pulse deficit), dicrotic pulse, atrial and ventricular fibrillation, and sinus arrhythmias.  Why E wrong: A perfectly regular 72-bpm pulse is normal, not abnormal."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"The radial artery is the most common site for pulse assessment because:","options":["It is located near the heart","It is easily accessible at the wrist near the thumb side, and can be assessed concurrently with BP measurement","It is the strongest pulse in the body","It is used only in neonates"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The radial artery is the most common site for pulse assessment; located at the wrist near the thumb side; can be assessed concurrently with BP measurement.  Why A wrong: The apical/carotid pulses are closer to the heart.  Why C wrong: It is the most accessible, not described as strongest.  Why D wrong: The apical pulse is used in neonates."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"Why is the apical pulse not reliable as an accurate site in routine adult pulse assessment but is preferred in some situations?","options":["It is too far from the heart","It is in a precise location or first-choice or most common site for pulse assessment in neonates or infants","It cannot be auscultated","It is only palpable in adults"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The apical pulse is in a precise location and is the first-choice / most common site for pulse assessment in neonates or infants.  Why A wrong: The apical site is over the heart apex, not far from the heart.  Why C wrong: The apical pulse is assessed by auscultation.  Why D wrong: It is especially used in neonates/infants, not just adults."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"The temporal artery, used for pulse taking, is located at:","options":["The neck","The lateral aspect of the eye","The wrist","The groin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The temporal artery location is the lateral aspect of the eye; it is assessed in reliability for unilateral visual changes and giant cell arteritis (GCA).  Why A wrong: The neck is the carotid site.  Why C wrong: The wrist is the radial site.  Why D wrong: The groin is the femoral site."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"When performing CPR, the carotid artery (located at the side of the neck or anterior border of the sternocleidomastoid muscle) is used because:","options":["It is the easiest to compress","During a cardiac arrest or shock, this is the central pulse that is located near to the heart, with a time of <10 seconds to assess","It is used only in infants","It is the most distal site"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The carotid artery is at the side of the neck/anterior border of the sternocleidomastoid; assess time <10 seconds; during cardiac arrest or shock, it is a central pulse located near to the heart, the first to be assessed.  Why A wrong: Ease of compression is not the documented reason.  Why C wrong: The carotid is used in adults during CPR; the brachial pulse is used in infants for CPR.  Why D wrong: The carotid is a central, not distal, site."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"The brachial artery, located above the elbow and in the antecubital fossa or inner part of the elbow, is used to: (SATA)","options":["Assess blood pressure measurement","Assess CPR during cardiac arrest in neonates or infants","Measure temporal artery temperature","Assess the femoral pulse","Take an apical pulse"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The brachial artery is used to assess blood pressure measurement and to assess CPR (during cardiac arrest) in neonates or infants.  Why C wrong: That is the temporal artery thermometer function.  Why D wrong: The femoral pulse is a separate site.  Why E wrong: The apical pulse is taken over the heart apex, not the brachial artery."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"The femoral artery, located at the groin below the inguinal fold between the symphysis pubis and anterior iliac spine, is used to: (SATA)","options":["Assess CPR in a child during angiography","Take a femoral sample (for ABG analysis)","Measure pulse in the wrist","Assess the temporal pulse"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The femoral artery is at the groin below the inguinal fold between symphysis pubis and anterior iliac spine; used to assess CPR in a child during angiography and to take a femoral sample for ABG analysis.  Why C wrong: The wrist is the radial site.  Why D wrong: The temporal pulse is at the lateral eye."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"The popliteal artery pulse is located at the:","options":["Top of the foot","Back of the knee in the popliteal fossa","Inner ankle","Lateral aspect of the eye"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The popliteal artery is located at the back of the knee in the popliteal fossa.  Why A wrong: The top of the foot is the dorsalis pedis site.  Why C wrong: The inner ankle behind the medial malleolus is the posterior tibial site.  Why D wrong: The lateral eye is the temporal site."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"The popliteal pulse is also assessed when there is a need to assess:","options":["Cranial nerve function","Circulatory assessment in the lower limbs / when assessing BP in lower limbs","Pupillary reflex","Apical heart sounds"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The popliteal artery is assessed by BP monitoring in the lower limbs and whenever there is a need to assess circulatory assessment in the lower limbs.  Why A, C, D wrong: These are unrelated to popliteal pulse assessment."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"The dorsalis pedis artery is located at the:","options":["Inner side of the ankle behind the medial malleolus","Median aspect of the foot or in front of the foot in line of toes","Back of the knee","Groin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Dorsalis pedis location is the median aspect of foot or in front of foot in line of toes; assessed for peripheral circulation in lower limbs in trauma patients.  Why A wrong: That is the posterior tibial artery location.  Why C wrong: That is the popliteal location.  Why D wrong: That is the femoral location."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"The posterior tibial artery is located at the:","options":["Top of the foot","Inner side of the ankle behind the medial malleolus","Back of the knee","Antecubital fossa"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The posterior tibial artery is located at the inner side of the ankle behind the medial malleolus; assessed for peripheral circulation in the lower limbs in trauma patients.  Why A wrong: The top of the foot is the dorsalis pedis.  Why C wrong: The back of the knee is the popliteal.  Why D wrong: The antecubital fossa is the brachial site."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"RECALL — In case of arrhythmia, which pulse would give the most accurate result in children?","options":["Radial pulse","Apical pulse","Carotid pulse","Radial pulse"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In case of arrhythmia, the apical pulse gives the most accurate result in children (Right Answer = B).  Why A, D wrong: The radial pulse is less reliable in arrhythmias.  Why C wrong: The carotid is not the documented most accurate site for arrhythmia in children."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"RECALL — Which is the best location for apical pulse assessment in an adult patient?","options":["Second intercostal space at the right mid-axillary line","Fourth intercostal space at the left mid-clavicular line","Fourth intercostal space at the right mid-axillary line","Fifth intercostal space at the left mid-clavicular line"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The best location for apical pulse assessment in an adult is the fifth intercostal space at the left mid-clavicular line (Right Answer = D).  Why A, B, C wrong: These do not match the documented adult apical pulse landmark."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"Which nursing consideration applies while measuring the pulse? (SATA)","options":["Take the pulse a little time before any actions that can alter pulse rate (exercise, eating, taking certain medications)","Have the patient sit or lie down if they are very slow, very fast, very irregular, or have any concerns","Place one arm on a form/table so that the palm is upward","Use the thumb to compress the artery firmly","Use the middle three fingers of the other hand, gently feel the outside of the arm just below the wrist with the fingertips"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: Documented nursing considerations: take pulse before actions that alter it; have patient sit/lie if pulse is very slow/fast/irregular or there are concerns; place one arm on a form/table palm upward; use the middle three fingers gently below the wrist.  Why D wrong: Do NOT press hard or tightly compress the artery, so no pulsations can be felt; and the thumb is avoided."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"When counting a regular pulse, the recommended efficient method is:","options":["Count for 10 seconds and multiply by 6","When pulsations are felt, count the number of pulsations for 30 seconds and multiply by 2","Count for the full 1 minute always","Count for 5 seconds and multiply by 12"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: When pulsations are felt using a watch with a second hand, count the number of pulsations for 30 seconds and multiply by 2 to get a 1-minute count.  Why A, D wrong: These short intervals are not the documented method.  Why C wrong: A full 1 minute is required only if the rhythm is irregular or amplitude is abnormal."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"If the rhythm, rate, or amplitude of the pulse is abnormal in any way, the nurse should:","options":["Count for 15 seconds and multiply by 4","Count the pulse for a full 1 minute","Count for 30 seconds and multiply by 2","Report it without counting"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If the rhythm, rate, or amplitude of the pulse is abnormal in any way, palpate and count the pulse for a full 1 minute (60 seconds).  Why A, C wrong: Shorter intervals are inaccurate for irregular pulses.  Why D wrong: The pulse must still be counted."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"\"Paradoxical pulse\" (pulsus paradoxus) is best described as:","options":["A pulse that exaggerates during inspiration, with a drop greater than 10 mmHg during inspiration; seen in pericardial tamponade and pericarditis","A pulse that is always absent","A pulse stronger during expiration only","A normal physiological variant in athletes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Paradoxical pulse is an exaggerated fall in a patient's blood pressure during inspiration by greater than 10 mmHg; seen in pericardial tamponade and pericarditis.  Why B wrong: It is not absence of pulse.  Why C wrong: The drop is during inspiration, related to respiration, not stronger on expiration.  Why D wrong: It is a pathological finding, not a normal variant."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"\"Paradoxical respiration\" (a \"should remember\" point) is described as:","options":["Normal quiet breathing","Breathing movements in which the chest wall moves in on inspiration and out on expiration, in reverse of the normal movements; seen in flail chest due to blunt chest trauma","Increased rate of breathing only","Breathing that stops during sleep"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Paradoxical respiration is breathing movements in which the chest wall moves in on inspiration and out on expiration (reverse of normal); seen in flail chest due to blunt chest trauma.  Why A wrong: It is abnormal, not quiet normal breathing.  Why C wrong: That describes tachypnea.  Why D wrong: That describes sleep apnea."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"Which of the following are factors that INCREASE the pulse rate (tachycardia)? (SATA)","options":["A decrease in blood pressure (as occurs with blood loss), when the heart's compensatory mechanisms attempt to meet the need for increased cardiac output","Elevated temperature","Prolonged application of heat","Pain","Strong emotions such as fear, anger, anxiety, and surprise"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Increased pulse rate factors include decreased blood pressure (blood loss triggering compensation), elevated temperature (increase of about 7 to 10 beats for each 0.6°C/1°F above normal), prolonged application of heat, pain, strong emotions (fear, anger, anxiety, surprise), and certain medications.  All options listed are documented causes of increased pulse rate."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"For each 0.6°C (1°F) rise in temperature above normal, the pulse rate increases by approximately:","options":["1 to 2 beats","7 to 10 beats","20 to 25 beats","30 to 40 beats"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: An elevated temperature usually causes an increase of about 7 to 10 beats for each 0.6°C (1°F) above normal.  Why A, C, D wrong: Do not match the documented increase."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"Which of the following factors DECREASE the pulse rate (bradycardia)? (SATA)","options":["Sleeping","Aging and thin people","Certain medications (e.g., beta blockers)","Vagal stimulation such as straining during a bowel movement","Increased intracranial pressure and increased intracranial tension (ICT)"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Decreased pulse rate factors include sleeping; hypothermia (metabolic processes decrease); aging and thin people; certain medications such as beta blockers; vagal stimulation such as bowel-movement straining; during suctioning of respiratory secretions; and increased intracranial pressure/ICT.  All listed options are documented causes of decreased pulse rate."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"Hypothermia decreases the pulse rate because:","options":["Metabolic processes decrease","The heart beats faster to warm the body","Blood pressure rises sharply","Oxygen consumption increases"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In hypothermia, metabolic processes decrease, which decreases the pulse rate.  Why B wrong: The pulse slows, not speeds up.  Why C wrong: BP does not rise sharply in this context.  Why D wrong: Oxygen consumption decreases with lowered metabolism, not increases."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"In the management of bradycardia, \"atropine intravenously\" is used to:","options":["Increase vagal stimulation","Block vagal stimulation and restore normal heart rate","Lower blood pressure","Cool the body temperature"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Atropine intravenously is used to block vagal stimulation and restore normal heart rate (documented in decreased pulse rate management).  Why A wrong: Atropine blocks, rather than increases, vagal stimulation.  Why C, D wrong: These are not the documented actions of atropine here."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"Respiration is defined as the act of breathing — a physiological process where individuals inhale oxygen (through inspiration) and exhale carbon dioxide (through expiration) for:","options":["Digestion","Viability","Excretion","Locomotion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Respiration is a physiological process where individuals inhale oxygen (inspiration) and exhale carbon dioxide (expiration) for viability.  Why A, C, D wrong: Respiration is for viability (sustaining life via gas exchange), not these processes."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"Respiration is assessed by which method during physical examination?","options":["Palpation","Auscultation","Inspection","Percussion"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Respiration is assessed by inspection methods during physical examination.  Why A wrong: Palpation is used for pulse.  Why B wrong: Auscultation is used for the apical pulse/breath sounds, not the primary documented method for counting respiration.  Why D wrong: Percussion is not the documented method."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"\"Internal respiration\" or tissue respiration refers to:","options":["Exchange of gases between blood and tissues","Exchange of gases between blood and lungs","Movement of the diaphragm","The regulation of breathing rate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Internal respiration or tissue respiration is the exchange of gases between blood and tissues.  Why B wrong: That is external respiration (pulmonary respiration).  Why C, D wrong: These describe the mechanics/regulation, not internal respiration."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"\"External respiration\" or pulmonary respiration refers to:","options":["Exchange of gases between blood and tissues","Exchange of gases between blood and lungs","The transport of gases by hemoglobin","Cellular metabolism"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: External respiration or pulmonary respiration is the exchange of gases between blood and lungs.  Why A wrong: That is internal/tissue respiration.  Why C, D wrong: These describe transport and cellular processes, not external respiration."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"The respiration regulation centre is located in the:","options":["Cerebellum","Medulla oblongata","Hypothalamus","Pons only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The respiration regulation centre is located in the medulla oblongata.  Why A, C, D wrong: These are not the documented respiration regulation centre."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"The normal respiration rate in adults is:","options":["12–20 breaths/minute (usually 16/min)","30–60 breaths/minute","24 breaths/minute","Less than 12 breaths/minute"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Normal respiration rate in adults is 12–20 breaths/minute (usually 16/min).  Why B wrong: 30–60/min is the normal newborn rate.  Why C wrong: >24/min is tachypnea.  Why D wrong: <12/min is bradypnea."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"Normal respiration rate in a newborn is:","options":["12–20/minute","30–60/minute","16/minute","<12/minute"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Normal respiration in a newborn is 30–60/minute.  Why A, C wrong: 12–20 (usually 16)/min is the adult rate.  Why D wrong: <12/min indicates bradypnea."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"Tachypnea is defined as a respiration rate:","options":["Increase more than 24/minute","Decrease less than 12/minute","12–20/minute","Exactly 16/minute"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Tachypnea is a respiration rate increase to more than 24/minute.  Why B wrong: That defines bradypnea.  Why C wrong: That is the normal adult range.  Why D wrong: 16/min is the usual normal rate."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"Bradypnea is defined as a respiration rate:","options":["More than 24/minute","Decrease less than 12/minute","12–20/minute","30–60/minute"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Bradypnea is a respiration rate decrease to less than 12/minute.  Why A wrong: That is tachypnea.  Why C wrong: That is the normal adult range.  Why D wrong: That is the newborn rate."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"\"Eupnea\" refers to:","options":["Difficult breathing","Normal respiration rate","Absence of breathing","Rapid breathing"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Eupnea is a normal respiration rate.  Why A wrong: Difficult breathing is dyspnea.  Why C wrong: Absence of breathing is apnea.  Why D wrong: Rapid breathing is tachypnea."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"The depth of respiration is assessed by observing or inspecting the movements of the chest during inspiration, which may be deep or shallow. Normal tidal volume is approximately:","options":["250 mL","500 mL","750 mL","1000 mL"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Normal tidal volume is 500 mL (volume per breath in normal/quiet respiration).  Why A, C, D wrong: Do not match the documented normal tidal volume."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"The rhythm of respiration, also known as symmetry of respiration, indicates:","options":["The number of breaths per minute","The equal interval between two respirations","The depth of each breath","The oxygen saturation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Rhythm (symmetry of respiration) indicates the equal interval between two respirations.  Why A wrong: That describes rate.  Why C wrong: That describes depth.  Why D wrong: That is oxygen saturation, a separate parameter."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"A nurse notes an adult patient's apical pulse should be auscultated at the apex of the heart. In a child, the apical pulse is auscultated at:","options":["The 5th intercostal space at the left mid-clavicular line","The 4th intercostal space (slightly below the nipple or just inside the mid-clavicular line)","The 2nd intercostal space, right of the sternum","The right mid-axillary line"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In a child, the apex of the heart is at the left mid-clavicular line at the 4th intercostal space, slightly below the nipple or just inside the mid-clavicular line.  Why A wrong: The 5th ICS left mid-clavicular line is the adult location.  Why C, D wrong: These are not the documented child apical sites."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"The apical pulse is assessed by auscultation using which part of the stethoscope?","options":["Bell","Diaphragm","Earpieces","Tubing"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The apical pulse is assessed by auscultation with the help of a stethoscope, by placing the diaphragm of the stethoscope on the intravalve of the heart.  Why A wrong: The bell is for low-pitched sounds; the source specifies the diaphragm.  Why C, D wrong: The earpieces and tubing do not contact the chest to detect the pulse."},{"section":"Batch 006 · thermometer types & disinfectants, mercury spill WHO management…","question":"During pregnancy, the apical pulse assessment site is at the:","options":["5th intercostal space at the left mid-clavicular line","3rd intercostal space, ICS at 3–5 cm lateral to the mid-clavicular line","2nd intercostal space, right sternal border","Right mid-axillary line"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Note: during pregnancy, the apical pulse assessment site is at the 3rd intercostal space (ICS) at 3–5 cm lateral to the mid-clavicular line (due to displacement of the heart).  Why A wrong: That is the standard non-pregnant adult site.  Why C, D wrong: These are not the documented pregnancy apical site."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"A normal pulse is best described as which of the following?","options":["Irregular and weak in intensity","Regular and even in intensity","Bounding with each beat","Absent at peripheral sites"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A normal pulse is regular and even in intensity.  Why A wrong: Irregularity and weakness describe abnormal pulses.  Why C wrong: A bounding pulse is an abnormal finding seen in conditions such as hyperdynamic states.  Why D wrong: An absent pulse is abnormal (no pulsation felt on waveform)."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"An absent pulse, characterized by no palpable pulse and no waveform, is most associated with which contributing conditions?","options":["Anemia and fever","Hyperthyroidism and anxiety","Arterial line disconnect and cardiac arrest","Aortic regurgitation only"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: An absent pulse (no palpable pulse, no waveform) is seen with arterial line disconnect and cardiac arrest.  Why A wrong: Anemia and fever are linked to a bounding pulse, not an absent one.  Why B wrong: Hyperthyroidism/anxiety produce a bounding pulse.  Why D wrong: Aortic regurgitation is associated with water-hammer/Corrigan's pulse, not an absent pulse."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"A weak/thready pulse is difficult to palpate and easily obliterated. Which of the following contributing conditions is associated with a weak/thready pulse?","options":["Fever","Severe peripheral vascular disease","Hyperthyroidism","Aortic stenosis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A weak/thready pulse (low intensity, easily obliterated with pressure) is seen in shock and severe peripheral vascular disease.  Why A wrong: Fever causes a bounding pulse.  Why C wrong: Hyperthyroidism causes a bounding pulse.  Why D wrong: Aortic stenosis is associated with a pulsus parvus/slow-rising pulse, not specifically a weak/thready descriptor in this table."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"A bounding pulse is high in intensity and difficult to obliterate with pressure from the fingertips. Which set of contributing conditions matches a bounding pulse?","options":["Shock, cardiac arrest","Hyperdynamic states (fever, anemia, anxiety), aortic regurgitation, hyperthyroidism","Tamponade and PVCs","Heart block only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A bounding pulse occurs with hyperdynamic states such as fever, anemia, and anxiety, as well as aortic regurgitation and hyperthyroidism.  Why A wrong: Shock and cardiac arrest cause weak/thready or absent pulses.  Why C wrong: Tamponade and PVCs relate to pulsus paradoxus/pulsus alternans, not a bounding pulse.  Why D wrong: Heart block is not the listed contributing condition for a bounding pulse."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"Pulsus alternans is characterized by which waveform finding?","options":["Pulses that are completely absent","Alternating strong and weak beats with equal intervals between each pulse","A pulse that disappears on inspiration","A rapid systolic upstroke with a dicrotic notch"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pulsus alternans shows alternating strong and weak pulses with equal intervals between each beat.  Why A wrong: Absence describes an absent pulse, not pulsus alternans.  Why C wrong: Disappearance on inspiration describes pulsus paradoxus.  Why D wrong: A rapid upstroke with dicrotic notch describes a water-hammer/Corrigan's pulse."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"Pulsus alternans is most commonly seen as a contributing finding in which of the following?","options":["Hyperthyroidism","Left ventricular failure / terminal left ventricular heart failure and systemic hypertension","Aortic regurgitation","Cardiac arrest"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pulsus alternans is associated with terminal left ventricular heart failure and systemic hypertension.  Why A wrong: Hyperthyroidism causes a bounding pulse.  Why C wrong: Aortic regurgitation causes a water-hammer/Corrigan's pulse.  Why D wrong: Cardiac arrest produces an absent pulse."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"A patient's pulse is irregular, with alternating strong and weak beats occurring at irregular intervals. This finding is termed:","options":["Pulsus paradoxus","Pulsus bigeminus","Pulsus parvus","Water-hammer pulse"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pulsus bigeminus is characterized by alternating strong and weak pulses that come early after the strong pulse (irregular pattern).  Why A wrong: Pulsus paradoxus is a reduced-intensity pulse during inspiration.  Why C wrong: Pulsus parvus refers to a weak (parvus) pulse, typically slow-rising.  Why D wrong: Water-hammer pulse is a rapid upstroke/downstroke pulse."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"Pulsus bigeminus is commonly associated with which arrhythmias?","options":["Atrial fibrillation only","Regular bigeminal dysrhythmias such as PVCs and PACs","Complete heart block","Sinus bradycardia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pulsus bigeminus reflects regular bigeminal dysrhythmias such as premature ventricular contractions (PVCs) and premature atrial contractions (PACs).  Why A wrong: Atrial fibrillation produces an irregularly irregular pulse, not the paired pattern of bigeminus.  Why C wrong: Complete heart block is not the listed cause.  Why D wrong: Sinus bradycardia is a slow regular rhythm, not bigeminy."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"Pulsus paradoxus is defined as a pulse that:","options":["Is reduced in intensity during inspiration","Increases markedly during inspiration","Alternates strong and weak at equal intervals","Is bounding and difficult to obliterate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Pulsus paradoxus is a pulse reduced in strength/intensity during inspiration.  Why B wrong: It decreases (not increases) during inspiration.  Why C wrong: Equal-interval strong/weak alternation describes pulsus alternans.  Why D wrong: Bounding/hard-to-obliterate describes a bounding pulse."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"Which conditions are contributing factors for pulsus paradoxus? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Cardiac tamponade","Acute pulmonary embolus","Pericarditis","Chronic lung disease (may also be present)","Hypovolemic shock and pregnancy"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Pulsus paradoxus contributing conditions listed are cardiac tamponade, acute pulmonary embolus, pericarditis, chronic lung disease (may also present), hypovolemic shock, and pregnancy.  Why each is correct: All five options are explicitly listed as contributing conditions for pulsus paradoxus in the source."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"A \"water-hammer\" pulse (Corrigan's pulse) is characterized by:","options":["Absent pulsations","A rapid systolic upstroke with a rapid downstroke (notch secondary to rapid runoff)","Alternating strong and weak beats","A weak, slow-rising pulse"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Water-hammer/Corrigan's pulse shows a rapid systolic upstroke and rapid downstroke (notch secondary to rapid runoff).  Why A wrong: Absent pulsations describe an absent pulse.  Why C wrong: Alternating strong/weak describes pulsus alternans.  Why D wrong: A weak slow-rising pulse describes pulsus parvus/tardus, not a water-hammer pulse."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"A water-hammer (Corrigan's) pulse is classically a contributing finding in:","options":["Aortic regurgitation","Aortic stenosis","Cardiac tamponade","PVCs"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A water-hammer/Corrigan's pulse is associated with aortic regurgitation.  Why B wrong: Aortic stenosis is associated with a weak/slow-rising (parvus/tardus) pulse.  Why C wrong: Cardiac tamponade causes pulsus paradoxus.  Why D wrong: PVCs cause pulsus bigeminus."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"The point of maximal impulse (PMI), also known as the apical impulse, is normally located where in a healthy adult?","options":["Second intercostal space at the right sternal border","Fifth intercostal space at the midclavicular line","Fourth intercostal space at the anterior axillary line","Over the manubrium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The PMI is the location at which the cardiac impulse can be best palpated on the chest wall; frequently this is the fifth intercostal space at the midclavicular line.  Why A wrong: The right second ICS sternal border is an auscultation landmark (aortic area), not the PMI.  Why C wrong: The anterior axillary line is not the typical PMI location (lateral displacement may indicate cardiomegaly).  Why D wrong: The manubrium is not the PMI location."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"Which statement best describes the mechanism of respiration?","options":["It is a passive process requiring no muscle activity","It is the breathing mechanism and exchange of gases driven by pressure differences between blood and tissues","It occurs only at the level of the lungs","It does not involve oxygen"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The mechanism of respiration involves the breathing mechanism and exchange of gases, with gas movement governed by pressure differences between blood and tissues, or atmospheric air and blood.  Why A wrong: Respiration involves active muscle work (e.g., diaphragm and intercostals).  Why C wrong: Gas exchange occurs in lungs (external) and at tissues (internal), not only in lungs.  Why D wrong: Cellular respiration uses oxygen to produce ATP."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"Cellular respiration is best defined as:","options":["The exchange of gases between alveoli and pulmonary capillaries","The use of oxygen by the cells to produce ATP","The movement of air into the lungs","The diffusion of gases at capillaries of blood vessels"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Cellular respiration is the use of oxygen by the cells to produce ATP.  Why A wrong: That describes external respiration.  Why C wrong: That describes inspiration/ventilation.  Why D wrong: That describes internal respiration/gas diffusion at capillaries, not cellular ATP production."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"External respiration refers to:","options":["Exchange of gases between the blood and tissues at the cellular level","Exchange of gases between the lungs and the blood (gas exchange at the alveolar/lung level)","ATP production within mitochondria","The diffusion of oxygen across muscle fibers only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: External respiration is the exchange of gases between the lungs and the blood (at the level of the lungs).  Why A wrong: Exchange between blood and tissues is internal respiration.  Why C wrong: ATP production is cellular respiration.  Why D wrong: Diffusion across muscle fibers is not the definition of external respiration."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"Internal respiration refers to gas exchange occurring at which site?","options":["The alveoli of the lungs","The capillaries of the blood vessels (between blood and tissues)","The trachea","The pleural space"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Internal respiration occurs by gas diffusion at the capillaries of the blood vessels (between the blood and the tissues).  Why A wrong: The alveoli are the site of external respiration.  Why C wrong: The trachea is a conducting airway, not a gas-exchange site.  Why D wrong: The pleural space is not a gas-exchange site."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"During inhalation (inspiration), which of the following occurs?","options":["The muscles of the diaphragm relax and the diaphragm moves up","The muscles of the diaphragm contract and move downward, and the ribs move upward and outward, increasing thoracic (chest) cavity volume","The volume of the thoracic cavity decreases","Air goes out of the lungs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In inhalation the diaphragm muscles contract and move downward, the ribs move upward and outward, and the thoracic cavity volume increases.  Why A wrong: Diaphragm relaxation and upward movement occurs in exhalation.  Why C wrong: Thoracic volume decreases during exhalation, not inhalation.  Why D wrong: Air enters the lungs in inhalation; air leaving describes exhalation."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"Which statement about exhalation (expiration) is correct?","options":["The diaphragm contracts and moves downward","The diaphragm and rib muscles relax, the diaphragm moves upward, thoracic cavity volume decreases, and air goes out of the lungs","Thoracic cavity volume increases","It is always an active process requiring the internal intercostal muscles"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In exhalation the diaphragm muscles relax and move upward, thoracic (chest) cavity volume decreases, and air goes out of the lungs through the nose; it is primarily an expiratory passive process.  Why A wrong: Diaphragm contraction/downward movement is inhalation.  Why C wrong: Thoracic volume decreases (not increases) in exhalation.  Why D wrong: Quiet exhalation is primarily passive; forced expiratory effort uses the internal intercostal muscles but it is not \"always\" active."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"A patient reports difficulty breathing while lying down that is relieved by sitting or standing up. This is termed:","options":["Hypoventilation","Orthopnea","Hyperpnea","Eupnea"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Orthopnea is difficulty breathing when lying down, relieved by sitting up/standing.  Why A wrong: Hypoventilation is decreased rate and depth of respiration.  Why C wrong: Hyperpnea is deep breathing.  Why D wrong: Eupnea is normal breathing."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"Hypoventilation is best defined as:","options":["An increased rate and depth of breathing","Under-expansion of the lungs caused by a decreased rate and depth of respiration","Complete absence of breathing","Deep breathing only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hypoventilation is under-expansion of the lungs due to a decreased rate and depth of respiration.  Why A wrong: Increased rate/depth describes hyperventilation/hyperpnea.  Why C wrong: Complete absence of breathing is apnea.  Why D wrong: Deep breathing alone is hyperpnea."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"Match the term to its definition: \"an abnormally fast respiratory rate\" is called:","options":["Bradypnea","Tachypnea","Apnea","Dyspnea"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Tachypnea is an abnormally fast respiratory rate.  Why A wrong: Bradypnea is an abnormally slow respiratory rate.  Why C wrong: Apnea is the absence of breathing.  Why D wrong: Dyspnea is difficulty in breathing."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"Bradypnea is defined as:","options":["Difficulty breathing","An abnormally slow respiratory rate","Bluish discoloration of the skin","Sudden cessation of respiration"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Bradypnea is an abnormally slow respiratory rate.  Why A wrong: Difficulty breathing is dyspnea.  Why C wrong: Bluish discoloration is cyanosis.  Why D wrong: Sudden cessation of respiration relates to apnea."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"Dyspnea refers to:","options":["Difficulty in breathing","Normal breathing","Deep rapid breathing in metabolic acidosis","Absence of oxygen in the tissues"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Dyspnea is difficulty in breathing.  Why B wrong: Normal breathing is eupnea.  Why C wrong: Deep rapid breathing in metabolic acidosis is Kussmaul's respiration.  Why D wrong: Absence of oxygen in the tissues is hypoxia."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"Cyanosis is defined as:","options":["Difficulty breathing","Bluish discoloration of the skin","Increased respiratory rate","Lack of oxygen in the blood (deficient oxygen supply)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Cyanosis is a bluish discoloration of the skin.  Why A wrong: Difficulty breathing is dyspnea.  Why C wrong: Increased respiratory rate is tachypnea.  Why D wrong: Lack of oxygen in the blood is anoxia/hypoxemia, not cyanosis itself."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"Anoxia is best defined as:","options":["Lack of oxygen","Increased respiratory rate","Bluish skin","Deep breathing"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Anoxia means lack of oxygen.  Why B wrong: Increased rate is tachypnea.  Why C wrong: Bluish skin is cyanosis.  Why D wrong: Deep breathing is hyperpnea."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"Hypoxia is defined as:","options":["Absence of oxygen in the tissues","Difficulty breathing","Sudden cessation of respiration","Bluish discoloration of skin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hypoxia is the absence/inadequacy of oxygen in the tissues.  Why B wrong: Difficulty breathing is dyspnea.  Why C wrong: Sudden cessation relates to apnea/asphyxia.  Why D wrong: Bluish skin is cyanosis."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"Asphyxia is best described as:","options":["Difficulty in inhalation or respiration, or deficient oxygen supply to the body (risk of suffocation)","Normal breathing pattern","An abnormally slow rate","A bluish discoloration of the skin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Asphyxia is difficulty in inhalation/respiration or deficient oxygen supply of oxygen to the body, posing a risk of suffocation.  Why B wrong: Normal breathing is eupnea.  Why C wrong: Slow rate is bradypnea.  Why D wrong: Bluish discoloration is cyanosis."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"Apnea refers to:","options":["Sudden cessation of respiration","Increased depth of breathing","Bluish skin","Difficulty breathing while lying down"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Apnea is the sudden cessation of respiration.  Why B wrong: Increased depth is hyperpnea.  Why C wrong: Bluish skin is cyanosis.  Why D wrong: Difficulty breathing while lying down is orthopnea."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"Crackles (rales) are described as:","options":["A musical wheezing sound","A cracking, unusual sound heard in a lung field due to fluid in the small airways; they are intermittent","A high-pitched crowing sound","Deep, labored breathing in metabolic acidosis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Crackles are a cracking, unusual sound heard in a lung field due to fluid in the small airways, occurring intermittently.  Why A wrong: A musical wheezing sound is a wheeze.  Why C wrong: A high-pitched crowing sound describes stridor.  Why D wrong: Deep labored breathing in metabolic acidosis is Kussmaul's respiration."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"A continuous high-pitched musical sound heard during breathing, commonly seen in asthma, is termed:","options":["Stridor","Wheezing","Crackles","Cheyne-Stokes respiration"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Wheezing is breathing with a whistling or rattling/musical sound, commonly seen in asthma.  Why A wrong: Stridor is a high-pitched crowing sound usually heard on inspiration.  Why C wrong: Crackles are a cracking sound due to fluid.  Why D wrong: Cheyne-Stokes is an alternating apnea/hyperpnea pattern."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"Cheyne-Stokes respiration is best described as:","options":["Deep, labored breathing seen in diabetic ketoacidosis","An abnormal breathing pattern characterized by deeper and faster breathing followed by a period of apnea, usually seen in heart failure and stroke","A high-pitched crowing inspiratory sound","Complete cessation of breathing"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Cheyne-Stokes respiration is an abnormal pattern of deeper/faster breathing alternating with periods of apnea, commonly seen in heart failure (CVA) and stroke.  Why A wrong: Deep labored breathing in DKA is Kussmaul's.  Why C wrong: A crowing inspiratory sound is stridor.  Why D wrong: Complete cessation is apnea, but Cheyne-Stokes is a cyclic pattern, not permanent cessation."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"Kussmaul's respiration is characterized by deep, labored breathing and is most commonly seen in which condition?","options":["Asthma","Metabolic acidosis, including diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA), and renal failure","Heart failure","Pleurisy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Kussmaul's respiration is deep, labored breathing usually seen in metabolic acidosis, diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA), and renal failure.  Why A wrong: Asthma produces wheezing.  Why C wrong: Heart failure is associated with Cheyne-Stokes respiration.  Why D wrong: Pleurisy is associated with a pleural friction rub."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"Biot's respiration is best described as:","options":["A regular pattern of deep breathing","An abnormal pattern of breathing characterized by shallow inspiration followed by periods of apnea, usually seen in trauma involving the medulla, CVA, and in opioids abuse","A continuous wheeze","Deep labored breathing in DKA"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Biot's respiration is an abnormal breathing pattern characterized by shallow inspiration followed by periods of apnea, usually seen in conditions involving the medulla (e.g., trauma/CVA) and opioid abuse.  Why A wrong: A regular deep pattern is not Biot's (which is irregular with apnea).  Why C wrong: A continuous wheeze is wheezing.  Why D wrong: Deep labored breathing in DKA is Kussmaul's."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"Ataxic respiration refers to:","options":["Normal regular breathing","Complete abnormal pattern of breathing characterized by complete irregularity of breathing with periods of apnea","Deep rapid breathing","Shallow rhythmic breathing"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Ataxic respiration is a completely abnormal, irregular breathing pattern with periods of apnea.  Why A wrong: Normal regular breathing is eupnea.  Why C wrong: Deep rapid breathing is hyperpnea/Kussmaul's.  Why D wrong: Shallow rhythmic breathing is not ataxic (which is irregular)."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"Agonal breathing is best characterized by:","options":["A struggling to breathe or gasping, usually seen in stroke and cardiac arrest","Normal effortless breathing","A high-pitched inspiratory crow","A regular, deep, sighing pattern"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Agonal respiration is a struggling-to-breathe/gasping pattern, usually seen in stroke and cardiac arrest.  Why B wrong: Effortless breathing is normal (eupnea).  Why C wrong: A high-pitched inspiratory crow is stridor.  Why D wrong: A regular deep sighing pattern is not agonal."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"Paradoxical respiration is best described as:","options":["An inward movement of the chest wall during inspiration and outward movement of the abdominal wall during expiration, the reverse of normal respiration; it is seen in chest injury","Deep breathing in DKA","A whistling sound on expiration","A cyclic apnea/hyperpnea pattern"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Paradoxical respiration is an inward movement of the chest wall during inspiration and outward movement of the abdominal wall during expiration — the reverse of normal respiration — and is seen in chest injury.  Why B wrong: Deep DKA breathing is Kussmaul's.  Why C wrong: A whistling sound is a wheeze.  Why D wrong: A cyclic apnea/hyperpnea pattern is Cheyne-Stokes."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"A flail chest occurs when:","options":["The diaphragm fully relaxes","A life-threatening condition occurs when a segment of ribs (≥3 ribs) breaks due to trauma and becomes detached from the rest of the chest wall, leading to paradoxical chest wall movement","There is a single rib fracture only","Air leaks into the pleural space slowly"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A flail chest is a life-threatening condition in which a segment of ribs (≥3 ribs) breaks due to trauma and becomes detached from the rest of the chest wall, producing paradoxical chest movement.  Why A wrong: Diaphragm relaxation is normal exhalation, not flail chest.  Why C wrong: A single rib fracture does not constitute a flail segment.  Why D wrong: Air leaking into the pleural space describes a pneumothorax, not flail chest."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"Nasal flaring during breathing, commonly seen in infants and children with Respiratory Distress Syndrome (RDS), indicates:","options":["Normal quiet breathing","Increased work of breathing/respiratory distress","A resolving infection","Hyperventilation only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Nasal flaring while breathing is commonly seen in infants and children with Respiratory Distress Syndrome (RDS) and indicates respiratory distress/increased work of breathing.  Why A wrong: Nasal flaring is abnormal, not normal quiet breathing.  Why C wrong: It indicates active distress, not a resolving infection.  Why D wrong: Nasal flaring reflects distress, not isolated hyperventilation."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"When measuring respiration, why should the nurse avoid making the patient aware that respirations are being counted?","options":["It increases the heart rate","Awareness can cause the patient to voluntarily alter the rate, depth, and rhythm of respirations, giving an inaccurate measurement","It is required only for children","It lowers the blood pressure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If respirations are abnormal in any way, awareness of being counted can cause the patient to alter the rate, depth, and rhythm; therefore the nurse keeps the patient unaware by retaining the hand used to take the pulse on the wrist and observing chest rise/fall.  Why A wrong: The concern is voluntary control of breathing, not heart rate.  Why C wrong: This precaution applies to all patients, not just children.  Why D wrong: It does not affect blood pressure measurement."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"According to the nursing consideration for measuring respiration, when should respirations be counted for a full 1 minute rather than counting for 30 seconds and multiplying by 2?","options":["When the pulse is regular","When respirations are irregular or abnormal in any way","Only for adults over 65","Never; always count for 15 seconds"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For regular respirations, count for 30 seconds and multiply by 2; if respirations are abnormal/irregular in any way, count the respirations for at least a full 1 minute.  Why A wrong: A regular pulse does not determine the respiratory counting duration.  Why C wrong: The criterion is irregularity, not age.  Why D wrong: A 15-second count is not the recommended method here."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"When assessing respiration, what aspects should the nurse note in addition to the rate?","options":["Only the rate","The depth and rhythm of the respirations","The blood pressure","The pulse pressure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The nurse must note the depth and rhythm of the respirations in addition to the rate.  Why A wrong: Rate alone is insufficient.  Why C wrong: Blood pressure is a separate vital sign.  Why D wrong: Pulse pressure is a derived BP value, not part of respiratory assessment."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"(NCLEX, \"Recall the NORDEP\" box) A client is brought to the emergency department reporting chest pain. Assessment shows vital signs that include a blood pressure (BP) of 158/88 mm Hg, a pulse of 88 beats per minute (BPM), and respirations of 30 breaths per minute. The nurse administers nitroglycerin 0.4 mg sublingually. The treatment is found to be effective when the reassessment of vital signs shows which of the following?","options":["BP 60/70 mm Hg, Pulse 68 BPM, RR 20 breaths per minute","BP 90/60 mm Hg, Pulse 88 BPM, RR 14 breaths per minute","BP 170/82 mm Hg, Pulse 60 BPM, RR 28 breaths per minute","BP 88/53 mm Hg, Pulse 73 BPM, RR 30 breaths per minute"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Nitroglycerin is a vasodilator that lowers blood pressure and relieves chest pain; an effective response shows a reduced but stable BP (90/60), a pulse near baseline (88 BPM), and a normalizing respiratory rate (14 breaths/min) consistent with relief of distress.  Why A wrong: A BP of 60/70 is physiologically impossible (diastolic cannot exceed systolic).  Why C wrong: BP 170/82 with RR 28 shows the hypertension and tachypnea/distress are unrelieved.  Why D wrong: BP 88/53 with an unchanged RR of 30 indicates ongoing distress despite a low BP, i.e., not an effective therapeutic response."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"Which adventitious breath sound is described as discontinuous, fine, high-pitched, occurring at end-inspiration, and not cleared by coughing — clinically seen in pneumonia, heart failure, chronic bronchitis, and asthma/COPD?","options":["Coarse crackles","Fine crackles","Wheeze","Pleural friction rub"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Fine crackles are discontinuous, fine, high-pitched, occur at end inspiration, and are not cleared by coughing; they are clinically seen in pneumonia, heart failure, chronic bronchitis, and asthma/COPD.  Why A wrong: Coarse crackles are low-pitched, loud, bubbling/gurgling and start early in inspiration.  Why C wrong: A wheeze is a continuous high-pitched sound similar to a squeak.  Why D wrong: A pleural friction rub is a superficial low-pitched grating/rubbing sound."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"Coarse crackles are best characterized as:","options":["Continuous high-pitched musical sounds","Discontinuous, low-pitched, loud, with bubbling or gurgling that starts early in inspiration, extending into expiration — same as fine crackles but usually louder","A creaking/grating sound from the pleura","A high-pitched crowing on inspiration"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Coarse crackles are discontinuous, low-pitched, loud, with bubbling or gurgling, and start early in inspiration extending into expiration (similar to but louder than fine crackles).  Why A wrong: Continuous high-pitched musical sounds are wheezes.  Why C wrong: A creaking/grating pleural sound is a pleural friction rub.  Why D wrong: A high-pitched inspiratory crow is stridor (not listed here as coarse crackle)."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"Coarse crackles are most commonly clinically seen in which conditions?","options":["Asthma and heart failure only","Pulmonary edema (most common) and pulmonary fibrosis","Pleurisy and pericarditis","Bronchitis and pneumonia only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Coarse crackles are clinically seen most commonly in pulmonary edema (most common) and pulmonary fibrosis.  Why A wrong: Asthma is associated with wheeze; the listed conditions are pulmonary edema and fibrosis.  Why C wrong: Pleurisy/pericarditis relate to a pleural friction rub.  Why D wrong: While crackles may occur in infection, the source specifically lists pulmonary edema and pulmonary fibrosis for coarse crackles."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"A wheeze is best described as a continuous, high-pitched sound similar to a squeak, more common during expiration, and is clinically seen in which conditions?","options":["Pleurisy and pericarditis","Asthma (most common), COPD, and heart failure","Pneumonia and tuberculosis","Pulmonary fibrosis only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A wheeze is a continuous, high-pitched sound similar to a squeak, more common on expiration, and is clinically seen in asthma (most common), COPD, and heart failure.  Why A wrong: Pleurisy/pericarditis relate to a pleural friction rub.  Why C wrong: Pneumonia/TB are not the listed wheeze conditions.  Why D wrong: Pulmonary fibrosis is linked to crackles, not a wheeze."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"Rhonchi are characterized as:","options":["High-pitched fine end-inspiratory sounds","Continuous, low-pitched, coarse, loud, with low snoring or moaning tone; more common on expiration and may produce a coughing/clearing effect","A grating pleural rub","A high-pitched inspiratory crow"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Rhonchi are continuous, low-pitched, coarse, loud sounds with a low snoring or moaning tone, more common on expiration, and may clear with coughing.  Why A wrong: High-pitched fine end-inspiratory sounds are fine crackles.  Why C wrong: A grating pleural rub is a pleural friction rub.  Why D wrong: A high-pitched inspiratory crow is stridor."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"Rhonchi are clinically seen most commonly in which conditions?","options":["Pleurisy and pericarditis","Bronchitis and pneumonia","Pulmonary edema only","Asthma only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Rhonchi are clinically seen in bronchitis and pneumonia.  Why A wrong: Pleurisy/pericarditis relate to pleural friction rub.  Why C wrong: Pulmonary edema relates to coarse crackles.  Why D wrong: Asthma is most associated with wheeze."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"A pleural friction rub is best described as:","options":["A continuous musical squeak","A superficial, low-pitched, coarse rubbing or grating sound, heard throughout inspiration and expiration, loudest over the lower anterolateral surface","A high-pitched end-inspiratory crackle","A snoring expiratory sound"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A pleural friction rub is a superficial, low-pitched, coarse rubbing or grating sound heard throughout inspiration and expiration, loudest over the lower anterolateral surface.  Why A wrong: A continuous musical squeak is a wheeze.  Why C wrong: A high-pitched end-inspiratory crackle is a fine crackle.  Why D wrong: A snoring expiratory sound describes rhonchi."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"A pleural friction rub is clinically seen in which conditions?","options":["Asthma and COPD","Pleurisy, pericarditis, and pericardial effusion","Bronchitis and pneumonia","Pulmonary edema and fibrosis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A pleural friction rub is clinically seen in pleurisy, pericarditis, and pericardial effusion.  Why A wrong: Asthma/COPD relate to wheeze.  Why C wrong: Bronchitis/pneumonia relate to rhonchi.  Why D wrong: Pulmonary edema/fibrosis relate to crackles."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"In the Breathing Patterns table, \"eupnea\" is defined as:","options":["Increased respiratory rate","Normal breathing rate and pattern","Absence of breathing","Deep and rapid respirations"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Eupnea is the normal breathing rate and pattern.  Why A wrong: Increased rate is tachypnea.  Why C wrong: Absence of breathing is apnea.  Why D wrong: Deep, rapid respirations describe hyperpnea."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"Tachypnea (increased respiratory rate) in the Breathing Patterns table is associated with which causes?","options":["Sleep, drugs, metabolic disorder, head injury, stroke","Fever, anxiety, exercise, shock","Severe brain injury","Renal failure and diabetic ketoacidosis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Tachypnea causes listed are fever, anxiety, exercise, and shock.  Why A wrong: Those are causes of bradypnea (decreased respiratory rate).  Why C wrong: Severe brain injury is listed as a cause of apnea.  Why D wrong: Renal failure and DKA are causes of Kussmaul's breathing."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"Bradypnea (decreased respiratory rate) is caused by which of the following?","options":["Fever and anxiety","Sleep, drugs, metabolic disorder, head injury, and stroke","Exercise","Spinal meningitis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Bradypnea causes listed are sleep, drugs, metabolic disorder, head injury, and stroke.  Why A wrong: Fever and anxiety cause tachypnea.  Why C wrong: Exercise causes tachypnea.  Why D wrong: Spinal meningitis is a cause of Biot's breathing."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"In the Breathing Patterns table, apnea (absence of breathing) is associated with which cause?","options":["Fever","Deceased patient, head injury, stroke","Renal failure","Spinal meningitis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Apnea (absence of breathing) causes listed are a deceased patient, head injury, and stroke.  Why A wrong: Fever causes tachypnea.  Why C wrong: Renal failure causes Kussmaul's.  Why D wrong: Spinal meningitis causes Biot's."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"Hyperpnea, as listed in the Breathing Patterns table, is characterized by normal rate but deep respirations and is caused by:","options":["Sleep and drugs","Emotional stress and diabetic ketoacidosis","Head injury only","Spinal meningitis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hyperpnea (normal rate, deep respirations) is caused by emotional stress and diabetic ketoacidosis.  Why A wrong: Sleep/drugs cause bradypnea.  Why C wrong: Head injury is listed for apnea/bradypnea, not specifically hyperpnea.  Why D wrong: Spinal meningitis causes Biot's."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"Biot's breathing (in the Breathing Patterns table) is characterized by rapid, deep respirations (gasps) with short pauses between sets, and is caused by:","options":["Fever and anxiety","Spinal meningitis, many central nervous system (CNS) causes, and head injury (especially with raised intracranial pressure / ICP)","Sleep and drugs","Diabetic ketoacidosis only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Biot's breathing is rapid, deep respirations (gasps) with short pauses, caused by spinal meningitis, many CNS causes, and head injury (especially with raised ICP).  Why A wrong: Fever/anxiety cause tachypnea.  Why C wrong: Sleep/drugs cause bradypnea.  Why D wrong: DKA causes Kussmaul's breathing."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"Kussmaul's breathing in the Breathing Patterns table is characterized by tachypnea and hyperpnea, and is caused by:","options":["Sleep and drugs","Renal failure, metabolic acidosis, and diabetic ketoacidosis","Stroke","Lesion in the brainstem"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Kussmaul's breathing (tachypnea and hyperpnea) is caused by renal failure, metabolic acidosis, and diabetic ketoacidosis.  Why A wrong: Sleep/drugs cause bradypnea.  Why C wrong: Stroke is listed for apnea/tachypnea, not specifically Kussmaul's.  Why D wrong: A brainstem lesion is the cause of apneustic breathing."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"Cheyne-Stokes breathing (in the Breathing Patterns table) is characterized by gradual increases and decreases in respirations with periods of apnea, and is caused by:","options":["Fever","Increasing intracranial pressure, severe heart failure, renal failure, drug overdose, and brain injury","Sleep and drugs","Exercise"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Cheyne-Stokes breathing shows gradual increases/decreases in respirations with periods of apnea and is caused by increasing intracranial pressure, severe heart failure, renal failure, drug overdose, and brain injury.  Why A wrong: Fever causes tachypnea.  Why C wrong: Sleep/drugs cause bradypnea.  Why D wrong: Exercise causes tachypnea."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"Apneustic breathing is described in the Breathing Patterns table as a prolonged inspiratory phase with a shortened expiratory phase, and is caused by:","options":["A lesion in the brainstem","Fever","Sleep","Diabetic ketoacidosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Apneustic breathing (prolonged inspiratory phase with shortened expiratory phase) is caused by a lesion in the brainstem.  Why B wrong: Fever causes tachypnea.  Why C wrong: Sleep causes bradypnea.  Why D wrong: DKA causes Kussmaul's."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"Blood pressure (BP) is best defined as:","options":["The number of heartbeats per minute","The force exerted by the blood against the wall of the blood vessels","The volume of air in the lungs","The difference between systolic and diastolic pressures"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: BP is the force exerted by the blood against the walls of the blood vessels.  Why A wrong: Beats per minute is the heart/pulse rate.  Why C wrong: Lung air volume is unrelated to BP.  Why D wrong: The difference between systolic and diastolic is the pulse pressure, not BP itself."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"What is the normal blood pressure value cited (≈16 KPa)?","options":["90/60 mm Hg","120/80 mm Hg","140/90 mm Hg","100/70 mm Hg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Normal blood pressure is 120/80 mm Hg (approximately 16 KPa).  Why A wrong: 90/60 mm Hg trends toward hypotension.  Why C wrong: 140/90 mm Hg is in the hypertensive range.  Why D wrong: 100/70 mm Hg is not the stated normal value."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"During blood pressure measurement, in which position should the patient be?","options":["Standing with the arm raised above the head","Relaxed and seated, with the arm uncrossed and back and arm supported","Lying prone with legs crossed","Walking on a treadmill"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The patient should be relaxed and seated with the arm uncrossed and back and arm supported (standardized position for BP measurement).  Why A wrong: Standing with the arm raised gives inaccurate readings.  Why C wrong: A prone position with crossed legs is not standard for BP.  Why D wrong: Walking/exercise transiently raises BP and is not the measurement position."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"Hypertension is defined in the source as:","options":["A BP consistently below 90/60 mm Hg","When there are two or more readings at least two subsequent health care visits greater than 120/80 mm Hg","A single normal reading","A reading exactly equal to 120/80 mm Hg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hypertension is diagnosed when there are two or more readings on at least two subsequent health care visits that are greater than 120/80 mm Hg.  Why A wrong: A BP below 90/60 is hypotension.  Why C wrong: A single normal reading is not hypertension.  Why D wrong: Exactly 120/80 is the normal value, not hypertension."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"Systolic blood pressure (SBP) is best described as:","options":["The lowest degree of pressure during ventricular diastole","The highest degree of pressure during ventricular systole, with a normal value of 120 mm Hg (16 KPa)","The difference between apical and radial pulses","The pressure during standing only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: SBP is the highest degree of pressure during the ventricular systole; normal value is 120 mm Hg (16 KPa).  Why A wrong: The lowest pressure during diastole is the diastolic BP.  Why C wrong: The difference between apical and radial pulses is the pulse deficit.  Why D wrong: SBP is not defined only during standing."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"Diastolic blood pressure (DBP) is best described as:","options":["The highest pressure during systole","The lowest degree of pressure during ventricular diastole, with a normal value of 80 mm Hg (≈11 KPa)","The mean arterial pressure","The pulse pressure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: DBP is the lowest degree of pressure during ventricular diastole, with a normal value of 80 mm Hg (approximately 11 KPa).  Why A wrong: The highest pressure during systole is systolic BP.  Why C wrong: Mean arterial pressure is a derived average, not the DBP definition.  Why D wrong: Pulse pressure is the difference between SBP and DBP."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"Orthostatic (postural) hypotension is best described as:","options":["A persistent rise in blood pressure","A drop in blood pressure when a person changes from lying to sitting or standing up","A high blood pressure only when standing","The difference between apical and radial pulse"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Orthostatic hypotension (also known as postural hypotension) is described as a drop in blood pressure when a person changes from lying to sitting or standing up.  Why A wrong: A persistent rise is hypertension.  Why C wrong: It is a drop on standing, not a rise.  Why D wrong: The apical–radial difference is the pulse deficit."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"What are the diagnostic criteria for orthostatic hypotension as stated in the source?","options":["SBP falls 5 mm Hg or DBP rises 2 mm Hg","SBP falls 20 mm Hg or more, or DBP falls 10 mm Hg or more, within 3 minutes of position change","SBP rises 30 mm Hg within 5 minutes","Any drop in pulse rate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Diagnostic criteria: SBP falls 20 mm Hg or more, or DBP falls 10 mm Hg or more, within 3 minutes of a position change.  Why A wrong: These small values do not meet the stated criteria.  Why C wrong: Orthostatic hypotension is a fall, not a rise, in pressure.  Why D wrong: A drop in pulse rate is not the defining criterion."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"Pulse deficit is best defined as:","options":["The sum of the apical and radial pulses","The difference between the apical pulse and the peripheral (e.g., radial) pulse","The difference between systolic and diastolic BP","The drop in BP on standing"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pulse deficit is the difference between the heart rate/apical pulse and the peripheral pulse (e.g., radial).  Why A wrong: It is a difference, not a sum.  Why C wrong: The systolic–diastolic difference is the pulse pressure.  Why D wrong: A BP drop on standing is orthostatic hypotension."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"How is a pulse deficit measured, and what is its clinical use?","options":["By taking only the radial pulse; used to detect fever","Need 2 nurses to assess for pulse deficit; it is used to differentiate atrial fibrillation and PVCs","By auscultating one nurse; used to measure orthostatic hypotension","By a single radial measurement; used to assess respiratory rate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Measuring a pulse deficit requires two nurses (one apical, one radial, counted simultaneously) and it is used to differentiate atrial fibrillation and PVCs.  Why A wrong: A single radial reading cannot detect a deficit and is unrelated to fever.  Why C wrong: Orthostatic hypotension is a BP, not a pulse-deficit, measurement.  Why D wrong: A single radial measure does not measure respiratory rate or pulse deficit."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"Pulse pressure is defined as:","options":["The lowest pressure during diastole","The difference between the systolic blood pressure (SBP) and the diastolic blood pressure (DBP)","The difference between apical and radial pulses","The force of blood against vessel walls"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pulse pressure is the difference between the systolic blood pressure (SBP) and diastolic blood pressure (DBP).  Why A wrong: The lowest diastolic pressure is the DBP.  Why C wrong: The apical–radial difference is the pulse deficit.  Why D wrong: The force of blood against vessel walls is the definition of BP."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"What is the stated normal pulse pressure value?","options":["20 mm Hg","40 mm Hg (≈5.3 KPa); 120 mm Hg minus 80 mm Hg","60 mm Hg","80 mm Hg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Normal pulse pressure is 40 mm Hg (approximately 5.3 KPa), calculated as 120 mm Hg − 80 mm Hg.  Why A wrong: 20 mm Hg would be a narrow pulse pressure.  Why C wrong: 60 mm Hg is a wide pulse pressure.  Why D wrong: 80 mm Hg is the normal diastolic value, not the pulse pressure."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"A narrow/low pulse pressure is considered abnormally low if it is less than which value, and is clinically associated with which conditions?","options":["<30 mm Hg; hypovolemia, congestive heart failure (CHF), cardiogenic shock, aortic stenosis, and tamponade","<60 mm Hg; hyperthyroidism only","<80 mm Hg; fever","<10 mm Hg; exercise"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A pulse pressure is considered abnormally low if it is less than 25 percent of the systolic value, or narrow if <30 mm Hg; clinical conditions include hypovolemia, CHF, cardiogenic shock, aortic stenosis, and tamponade.  Why B wrong: Hyperthyroidism is associated with a wide pulse pressure, and <60 mm Hg is not the narrow threshold.  Why C wrong: <80 mm Hg is not the narrow threshold and fever causes a wide pulse pressure.  Why D wrong: <10 mm Hg is not the stated threshold and exercise is not the listed condition."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"A wide/high pulse pressure (e.g., >50 mm Hg, or if >100 mm Hg) is clinically associated with which conditions?","options":["Hypovolemia and cardiogenic shock","Atherosclerosis, iron deficiency anemia, hyperthyroidism, aortic regurgitation, fever, increased ICP, and distributive shock","Aortic stenosis and tamponade","CHF only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A wide/high pulse pressure is associated with atherosclerosis, iron deficiency anemia, hyperthyroidism, aortic regurgitation, fever, increased ICP, and distributive shock.  Why A wrong: Hypovolemia and cardiogenic shock cause a narrow pulse pressure.  Why C wrong: Aortic stenosis and tamponade cause a narrow pulse pressure.  Why D wrong: CHF causes a narrow pulse pressure."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"(RECALL the NORDEP, p.192) A patient's blood pressure is 156/85 mm Hg. What is this patient's pulse pressure?","options":["156 mm Hg","71 mm Hg","85 mm Hg","241 mm Hg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pulse pressure = SBP − DBP = 156 − 85 = 71 mm Hg.  Why A wrong: 156 is the systolic value, not the pulse pressure.  Why C wrong: 85 is the diastolic value.  Why D wrong: 241 is the sum of the two values, not the difference."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"When preparing to measure BP, how long before the reading should the patient avoid eating or drinking?","options":["5 minutes","30 minutes","1 hour","2 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The patient should not eat or drink anything for 30 minutes before taking the BP reading.  Why A wrong: 5 minutes is the rest/sitting time before measurement, not the eating/drinking restriction.  Why C/D wrong: The stated restriction is 30 minutes, not 1 or 2 hours."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"According to the correct technique for measuring BP, how long should the patient sit in a comfortable chair (with back supported) before the reading?","options":["At least 1 minute","At least 5 minutes","At least 10 minutes","At least 15 minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The patient should sit in a comfortable chair with the back supported for at least 5 minutes before the reading.  Why A wrong: 1 minute is insufficient per the guideline.  Why C wrong: 10 minutes is more than required.  Why D wrong: 15 minutes is more than required."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"During BP measurement, where should the patient's feet and legs be positioned?","options":["Feet crossed and legs raised","Feet flat on the ground and legs uncrossed","Feet dangling off the floor","Legs crossed at the knee"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The patient should put both feet flat on the ground and keep the legs uncrossed; rest the arm with the cuff on a table at chest height.  Why A wrong: Crossing or raising legs alters the reading.  Why C wrong: Feet should be flat on the floor, not dangling.  Why D wrong: Crossing legs can falsely raise the reading."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"When applying the blood pressure cuff, how tight should it be?","options":["Very loose so two fingers fit easily","Snug but not too tight; the cuff should be snug against the skin but not too tight","As tight as possible to occlude flow","Placed over clothing for comfort"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The cuff should be snug against the skin but not too tight.  Why A wrong: A very loose cuff gives inaccurate (falsely high) readings.  Why C wrong: Excessive tightness is incorrect technique.  Why D wrong: The cuff should be applied to a bare arm, not over clothing."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"Regarding talking during BP measurement, the correct instruction is to:","options":["Talk continuously to relax","Not talk while the blood pressure is being measured","Hold the breath the whole time","Cough at each reading"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The patient should not talk while the blood pressure is being measured (talking can raise the reading).  Why A wrong: Talking falsely elevates the BP.  Why C wrong: Breath holding is not the instruction.  Why D wrong: Coughing is not part of the technique."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"In KPa, the normal blood pressure of 16 KPa corresponds to which fact about the KPa unit?","options":["1 KPa equals 7.5 mm Hg, so normal BP (120/80) is 16 KPa","1 KPa equals 1 mm Hg","1 KPa equals 100 mm Hg","KPa is not related to mm Hg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: KPa means Kilo Pascal; 1 KPa equals 7.5 mm Hg, and the normal BP (120/80) equals 16 KPa.  Why B wrong: 1 KPa is not equal to 1 mm Hg.  Why C wrong: 1 KPa is not 100 mm Hg.  Why D wrong: KPa and mm Hg are directly convertible (1 KPa = 7.5 mm Hg)."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"If a patient is smoking, the nurse should wait how long before measuring the blood pressure?","options":["5 minutes","30 minutes","1 hour","No waiting is required"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If the patient has smoked, wait for 30 minutes before measuring the BP.  Why A wrong: 5 minutes is insufficient after smoking.  Why C wrong: 1 hour is longer than the stated recommendation.  Why D wrong: Smoking transiently raises BP, so waiting is required."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"When measuring blood pressure, which instrument name is correct?","options":["Sphygmomanometer","Spirometer","Thermometer","Otoscope"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The name of the BP measuring instrument is the sphygmomanometer.  Why B wrong: A spirometer measures lung volumes.  Why C wrong: A thermometer measures temperature.  Why D wrong: An otoscope examines the ear."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"During BP measurement, Korotkoff sounds are heard from the radial artery when assessing blood pressure. Which procedure/technique is used to listen for these sounds?","options":["Palpation only","Auscultation using a forearm/stethoscope over the artery","Percussion","Inspection"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Korotkoff sounds are heard by auscultation (using a stethoscope) over the artery during blood pressure assessment.  Why A wrong: Palpation alone cannot identify the auscultatory Korotkoff sounds (it can estimate systolic only).  Why C wrong: Percussion is not used to hear Korotkoff sounds.  Why D wrong: Inspection (looking) cannot detect Korotkoff sounds."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"Korotkoff Sounds Phase I (the first tapping sound heard) represents which blood pressure value?","options":["Diastolic blood pressure","Systolic blood pressure","Mean arterial pressure","Pulse pressure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Korotkoff Sounds Phase I represents the systolic blood pressure (the first audible tapping sound).  Why A wrong: Diastolic pressure corresponds to Phase V.  Why C wrong: Mean arterial pressure is not directly read from Korotkoff phases.  Why D wrong: Pulse pressure is a calculated difference, not a Korotkoff phase."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"Korotkoff Sounds Phase V represents which blood pressure value, and what audible change marks it?","options":["Systolic BP; first tapping sound","Diastolic BP; the point at which sounds disappear (silence)","Pulse pressure; the muffling sound","Mean arterial pressure; a swishing sound"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Korotkoff Sounds Phase V represents the diastolic blood pressure, marked by the disappearance of sounds (silence) following the tapping (Phase I), soft swishing (Phase II), crisp/knocking (Phase III), and blowing/muffling (Phase IV) phases.  Why A wrong: The first tapping sound is Phase I (systolic).  Why C wrong: Pulse pressure is calculated, not a Korotkoff phase; muffling is Phase IV.  Why D wrong: MAP is not read directly, and a swishing sound is Phase II."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"According to the American Heart Association (AHA) guideline recommendations, how should the patient be seated for in-clinic blood pressure measurement?","options":["Lying supine with arms crossed","Seated comfortably with the back supported, legs uncrossed, and the upper arm bare","Standing with arms hanging","Sitting with legs crossed at the knee"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The AHA recommends the patient be seated comfortably with the back supported, legs uncrossed, and the upper arm bare.  Why A wrong: A supine position is not the AHA seated recommendation; in the supine position diastolic pressure is higher.  Why C wrong: Standing is not the recommended in-clinic measurement position.  Why D wrong: Crossing the legs may increase the BP reading."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"According to the AHA comments, how does an unsupported back affect the blood pressure reading?","options":["It has no effect","An unsupported back may increase the diastolic blood pressure, and crossing the legs may increase the systolic pressure","It lowers the systolic pressure","It only affects the heart rate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: An unsupported back may increase the diastolic blood pressure; crossing the legs may increase the systolic pressure.  Why A wrong: An unsupported back does affect the reading.  Why C wrong: It increases, not lowers, the diastolic reading.  Why D wrong: It affects BP, not just heart rate."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"Per the AHA guidelines, the patient's arm should be supported at which level during BP measurement?","options":["Above the head","At heart level","Below the level of the right atrium","Hanging at the side"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The patient's arm should be supported at heart level. If the arm is below the level of the right atrium, the readings will be too high; if the arm is unsupported and held up by the patient, the pressure will be higher.  Why A wrong: Holding the arm above the head is not the recommendation and would lower the reading.  Why C wrong: An arm below the right atrium falsely raises the reading.  Why D wrong: A hanging, unsupported arm raises the reading."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"According to the AHA guidelines, the cuff bladder should encircle what percentage of the patient's arm circumference?","options":["40 percent or less","80 percent or more","Exactly 50 percent","Any size is acceptable"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The cuff bladder should encircle 80 percent or more of the patient's arm circumference; a wide BP cuff falsely gives a low reading, and a narrow cuff falsely gives a high reading.  Why A wrong: 40 percent is too small and gives inaccurate readings.  Why C wrong: 50 percent is below the recommended 80 percent.  Why D wrong: Cuff size matters significantly for accuracy."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"According to the AHA comments, at what rate should the mercury column be deflated during auscultatory BP measurement?","options":["1 to 2 mm per second","2 to 3 mm per second","10 mm per second","20 mm per second"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The mercury column should be deflated at 2 to 3 mm per second; deflating faster than 2 mm per second can cause the systolic press to appear lower and the diastolic pressure to appear higher.  Why A wrong: 1–2 mm/sec is slower than the stated 2–3 mm/sec recommendation.  Why C/D wrong: 10 or 20 mm/sec is far too fast and causes measurement errors."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"Per the AHA guidelines, which two readings should be recorded as the systolic and diastolic blood pressure?","options":["The loudest sounds heard","The first and last audible sounds (Korotkoff sounds)","The mean of all sounds","The muffled sound only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The first and last audible sounds should be recorded as the systolic and diastolic blood pressure, respectively.  Why A wrong: Loudness, not first/last appearance, is not the criterion.  Why C wrong: An average of all sounds is not recorded.  Why D wrong: The muffled sound (Phase IV) alone is not the standard endpoint; the last audible sound (Phase V) is used for diastolic."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"The AHA guideline notes that BP measurements should be taken when, relative to caffeine, exercise, and smoking?","options":["Immediately after exercise for accuracy","Should be made at least 30 minutes after caffeine, exercise, or smoking","Only after smoking a cigarette","There is no relationship"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: BP measurements should be made at least 30 minutes after caffeine, exercise, or smoking, as these elevate the reading.  Why A wrong: Measuring immediately after exercise gives a falsely high reading.  Why C wrong: Smoking raises the BP and one should wait, not measure right after.  Why D wrong: These factors do affect BP."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"The \"BSdao\" (BS rule) states that, for an accurate blood pressure measurement, the cuff bladder length should be approximately what fraction of the circumference of the upper arm, and the cuff bladder width should be optimally what fraction of the upper arm circumference?","options":["Length ≈ 60% and width ≈ 20% of upper arm circumference; bladder length to width ratio of 2:1","Length ≈ 80% and width ≈ 40% of upper arm circumference; bladder length to width ratio of 2:1","Length ≈ 50% and width ≈ 10%; ratio 1:1","Length ≈ 100% and width ≈ 50%; ratio 3:1"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Per the cited rule, the cuff bladder length should be approximately 80 percent of the circumference of the upper arm and the cuff bladder width should optimally be about 40 percent of the circumference of the upper arm, with a bladder length-to-width ratio of 2:1.  Why A wrong: 60%/20% does not match the stated 80%/40%.  Why C wrong: 50%/10% with a 1:1 ratio is incorrect.  Why D wrong: 100%/50% with a 3:1 ratio is incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"(AHA BP Monitoring Steps 2018, Step 1) During Step 1 of the AHA BP monitoring procedure, how should the patient be seated, and for how long should they be seated/rest before measurement?","options":["Standing for 1 minute","Seated quietly with the back relaxed and feet flat on the floor, arm supported, legs uncrossed, for at least 5 minutes","Lying down for 10 minutes","Walking for 2 minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: AHA Step 1 directs that the patient sit with the feet flat on the floor, relaxed, and rest quietly for 5 minutes; the patient should keep the arm/bladder empty and avoid talking, eating, exercise, or smoking for 30 minutes before measurement.  Why A wrong: Standing is not the Step 1 position.  Why C wrong: A lying-down position is not specified in Step 1.  Why D wrong: Walking elevates the BP and is not the rest position."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"(AHA BP Monitoring Steps, Step 2) When using a validated, calibrated BP measurement device, where should the cuff be applied and at what level?","options":["On the lower forearm, below heart level","On the upper arm at heart level, with the cuff placed over a bare arm and the lower edge of the cuff above the antecubital fossa","Around the wrist only","Over the clothing on the thigh"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Step 2 specifies using a validated, calibrated device and applying the cuff on the bare upper arm at the level of the right atrium/heart level (cuff positioned above the antecubital fossa).  Why A wrong: Placing the cuff below heart level on the forearm gives inaccurate readings.  Why C wrong: The wrist is not the standard cuff site for this procedure.  Why D wrong: The cuff must be on a bare arm, not over clothing, and not on the thigh."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"(AHA BP Monitoring Steps, Steps 3–5) According to the AHA steps, what should be done about repeat readings and recording?","options":["Take a single reading and record it immediately","Take the first and second readings using the arm with the higher reading, separate repeated measurements by 1 to 2 minutes, record the SBP and DBP, and note the time of the most recent BP measurement","Average all readings without noting time","Record only the lowest reading obtained"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Steps 3–5 direct using the arm with the higher reading for subsequent measurements, separating repeated measurements by 1 to 2 minutes, recording the SBP and DBP, and noting the time of the most recent BP measurement.  Why A wrong: A single reading is insufficient; repeated readings are required.  Why C wrong: The time of measurement must be noted.  Why D wrong: The protocol does not say to record only the lowest reading."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"(AHA BP Monitoring Steps, Step 6) When using an average of multiple BP readings, the AHA guidance recommends:","options":["Use a single reading only","Use an average of 2 readings, and provide the SBP/DBP readings both verbally and in writing to the patient","Discard all readings","Use only the first reading"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Step 6 directs using an average of two readings and providing the SBP/DBP readings both verbally and in writing to the individual/patient.  Why A wrong: A single reading is not the basis for the average.  Why C wrong: Readings are used, not discarded.  Why D wrong: An average (not only the first reading) is recommended."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"(AHA Recommended Cuff Sizes table) For an adult arm with a circumference of 27 to 34 cm, which bladder dimension (width × length) is recommended?","options":["12 × 22 cm (small adult)","16 × 30 cm (adult)","16 × 36 cm (large adult)","4 × 8 cm (newborn)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For an adult arm circumference of 27 to 34 cm, the recommended bladder dimension is 16 × 30 cm (adult).  Why A wrong: 12 × 22 cm (small adult) corresponds to a 22 to 26 cm arm circumference.  Why C wrong: 16 × 36 cm (large adult) corresponds to a 35 to 44 cm arm circumference.  Why D wrong: 4 × 8 cm is for a newborn/premature infant."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"(AHA Recommended Cuff Sizes table) For a large adult with an arm circumference of 35 to 44 cm, the recommended bladder dimension is:","options":["12 × 22 cm","16 × 30 cm","16 × 36 cm","16 × 42 cm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: For an arm circumference of 35 to 44 cm (large adult), the recommended bladder dimension is 16 × 36 cm.  Why A wrong: 12 × 22 cm is for a small adult (22–26 cm).  Why B wrong: 16 × 30 cm is for an adult (27–34 cm).  Why D wrong: 16 × 42 cm is for an adult thigh (45–52 cm)."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"(AHA Recommended Cuff Sizes table) For an adult thigh with a circumference of 45 to 52 cm, the recommended bladder dimension is:","options":["16 × 30 cm","16 × 36 cm","16 × 42 cm (adult thigh)","6 × 12 cm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: For an arm/thigh circumference of 45 to 52 cm (adult thigh), the recommended bladder dimension is 16 × 42 cm.  Why A wrong: 16 × 30 cm is for an adult (27–34 cm).  Why B wrong: 16 × 36 cm is for a large adult (35–44 cm).  Why D wrong: 6 × 12 cm is a pediatric/infant size."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"(AHA Recommended Cuff Sizes table, Children) For a newborn or premature infant with an arm circumference of 4 to 8 cm, the recommended bladder dimension is:","options":["4 × 8 cm","6 × 12 cm","9 × 18 cm","12 × 22 cm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For a newborn/premature infant with an arm circumference of 4 to 8 cm, the recommended bladder dimension is 4 × 8 cm.  Why B wrong: 6 × 12 cm is for an infant (6–15 cm).  Why C wrong: 9 × 18 cm is for an older child (16–21 cm).  Why D wrong: 12 × 22 cm is for a small adult."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"(AHA Recommended Cuff Sizes table, Children) For an infant with an arm circumference of 6 to 15 cm, the recommended bladder dimension is:","options":["4 × 8 cm","6 × 12 cm","9 × 18 cm","16 × 30 cm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For an infant with an arm circumference of 6 to 15 cm, the recommended bladder dimension is 6 × 12 cm.  Why A wrong: 4 × 8 cm is for a newborn/premature infant.  Why C wrong: 9 × 18 cm is for an older child.  Why D wrong: 16 × 30 cm is for an adult."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"(AHA Recommended Cuff Sizes table, Children) For an older child with an arm circumference of 16 to 21 cm, the recommended bladder dimension is:","options":["6 × 12 cm","9 × 18 cm","12 × 22 cm","16 × 36 cm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For an older child with an arm circumference of 16 to 21 cm, the recommended bladder dimension is 9 × 18 cm.  Why A wrong: 6 × 12 cm is for an infant.  Why C wrong: 12 × 22 cm is for a small adult.  Why D wrong: 16 × 36 cm is for a large adult."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"(AHA Classification of Blood Pressure table) A \"Normal\" blood pressure classification is defined by which SBP and DBP values?","options":["SBP <120 mm Hg and DBP <80 mm Hg","SBP 120–129 mm Hg and DBP <80 mm Hg","SBP 130–139 mm Hg and DBP 80–89 mm Hg","SBP ≥180 mm Hg and DBP ≥120 mm Hg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Normal BP is classified as SBP less than 120 mm Hg and DBP less than 80 mm Hg.  Why B wrong: SBP 120–129 with DBP <80 is the \"Elevated\" category.  Why C wrong: SBP 130–139 / DBP 80–89 is Stage 1 hypertension.  Why D wrong: SBP ≥180 / DBP ≥120 is hypertensive crisis."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"(AHA Classification of Blood Pressure) \"Elevated\" blood pressure is defined by which values?","options":["SBP <120 and DBP <80 mm Hg","SBP 120–129 mm Hg and DBP <80 mm Hg","SBP 130–139 mm Hg and DBP 80–89 mm Hg","SBP 140 or higher and DBP 90 or higher"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Elevated BP is SBP 120–129 mm Hg and DBP less than 80 mm Hg.  Why A wrong: SBP <120 and DBP <80 is Normal.  Why C wrong: SBP 130–139 / DBP 80–89 is Stage 1 hypertension.  Why D wrong: SBP ≥140 / DBP ≥90 is Stage 2 hypertension."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"(AHA Classification of Blood Pressure) Stage 1 hypertension is defined by which values?","options":["SBP 120–129 and DBP <80 mm Hg","SBP 130–139 mm Hg or DBP 80–89 mm Hg","SBP 140 or higher or DBP 90 or higher","SBP ≥180 or DBP ≥120 mm Hg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Stage 1 hypertension is SBP 130–139 mm Hg or DBP 80–89 mm Hg.  Why A wrong: SBP 120–129 / DBP <80 is Elevated.  Why C wrong: SBP ≥140 / DBP ≥90 is Stage 2 hypertension.  Why D wrong: SBP ≥180 / DBP ≥120 is hypertensive crisis."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"(AHA Classification of Blood Pressure) Stage 2 hypertension is defined by which values?","options":["SBP 130–139 or DBP 80–89 mm Hg","SBP 140 mm Hg or higher or DBP 90 mm Hg or higher","SBP <120 and DBP <80 mm Hg","SBP ≥180 or DBP ≥120 mm Hg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Stage 2 hypertension is SBP 140 mm Hg or higher or DBP 90 mm Hg or higher.  Why A wrong: SBP 130–139 / DBP 80–89 is Stage 1.  Why C wrong: SBP <120 / DBP <80 is Normal.  Why D wrong: SBP ≥180 / DBP ≥120 is hypertensive crisis."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"(AHA Classification of Blood Pressure) Hypertensive crisis is defined by which values?","options":["SBP 140 or higher or DBP 90 or higher","SBP higher than 180 mm Hg and/or DBP higher than 120 mm Hg","SBP 130–139 or DBP 80–89 mm Hg","SBP <120 and DBP <80 mm Hg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hypertensive crisis is SBP higher than 180 mm Hg and/or DBP higher than 120 mm Hg.  Why A wrong: SBP ≥140 / DBP ≥90 is Stage 2 hypertension.  Why C wrong: SBP 130–139 / DBP 80–89 is Stage 1.  Why D wrong: SBP <120 / DBP <80 is Normal."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"An arm or limb should be avoided when measuring blood pressure in a client with which conditions? (Select all that apply.)","options":["After radical mastectomy","A fractured limb","A burned limb","A paralysed limb","A limb with an IV infusion / arteriovenous shunt"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: BP measurement should be avoided on a limb being used for IV therapy, after radical mastectomy, a fractured limb, a burned limb, and a paralysed limb (and an arteriovenous shunt/fistula limb).  Why each correct: All listed limbs are explicitly identified as ones to avoid because measurement may worsen injury, alter circulation, or give inaccurate results."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"Which of the following is a cause of a FALSELY LOW blood pressure assessment? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Applying a BP cuff that is too wide","Releasing the valve too quickly (rapid deflation)","Hearing deficit and noise in the environment","Inserting earpieces of the stethoscope incorrectly","Using cracked or kinked tubing"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Falsely low BP assessment causes listed include applying a cuff that is too wide, a quick/rapid release of the valve (releasing too quickly during deflation), a hearing deficit and environmental noise, inserting the earpieces of the stethoscope incorrectly, using cracked or kinked tubing, misplacing the bell beyond the direct area of the artery, and failing to pump the cuff 20–30 mm Hg above the disappearance of the pulse.  Why each correct: All five options are explicitly listed under causes of a falsely low BP reading."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"Which of the following is a cause of a FALSELY HIGH blood pressure assessment? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Applying a BP cuff that is too narrow","A loose cuff (a loosely wrapped or applied cuff)","Releasing the valve too slowly (slow deflation)","Crossing the legs, which may increase the systolic pressure","Using a manometer that is not calibrated at the zero mark"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Falsely high assessment causes listed include applying a cuff that is too narrow, a loose cuff, releasing the valve too slowly (slow deflation), crossing the legs (which may increase systolic pressure), and using a manometer not calibrated at the zero mark; additional listed causes include assessing the blood pressure immediately after exercise, viewing the meniscus from below eye level, and reinflating the bladder during auscultation.  Why each correct: All five options are explicitly listed under causes of a falsely high BP reading."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"Reinflating the bladder during auscultation while measuring blood pressure will result in:","options":["A falsely low reading","A falsely high reading","No effect on the reading","A more accurate reading"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Reinflating the bladder during auscultation is listed as a cause of a falsely high BP assessment.  Why A wrong: It causes a high, not low, reading.  Why C wrong: It does affect the reading.  Why D wrong: It introduces error rather than improving accuracy."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"Assessing a patient's blood pressure immediately after exercise will most likely produce:","options":["A falsely low reading","A falsely high reading","An accurate reading","No measurable reading"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Assessing the BP immediately after exercise is listed as a cause of a falsely high assessment (the source recommends measuring at least 30 minutes after exercise).  Why A wrong: Exercise raises, not lowers, the reading.  Why C wrong: It produces an inaccurately high reading.  Why D wrong: A reading is obtainable but falsely elevated."},{"section":"Batch 007 · normal and abnormal pulses…","question":"Failing to pump the BP cuff 20 to 30 mm Hg above the point at which the pulse/Korotkoff sounds disappear will most likely cause:","options":["A falsely high reading","A falsely low reading (the systolic pressure may be underestimated)","No effect","A widened pulse pressure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Failing to pump the cuff 20–30 mm Hg above the disappearance of the pulse is listed as a cause of a falsely low BP assessment (the true systolic may be missed/underestimated).  Why A wrong: It causes a falsely low, not high, reading.  Why C wrong: It does affect the systolic reading.  Why D wrong: It does not specifically widen the pulse pressure; it underestimates systolic."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"When measuring blood pressure during the first examination, in how many sites should the measurement be checked?","options":["Only one arm","Both arms","Both legs","One arm and one leg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: BP measurements should be checked in both arms at the first examination because most people have differences in BP between arms.  Why A wrong: One arm misses inter-arm differences that occur in most people.  Why C wrong: The first examination guideline specifies both arms, not legs.  Why D wrong: It specifies both arms, not an arm-leg combination."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"What is the most common cause of an abnormally low blood pressure reading when assessing a patient?","options":["Cardiac tamponade","Improperly fitting cuff (loose) / Doppler ultrasound issues","Aortic stenosis","Hyperthyroidism"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states blood pressure can be assessed using an automatic electronic blood pressure monitor or Doppler ultrasound, and the technique to ensure the radial artery for Korotkoff sounds; an improper cuff/technique most commonly causes inaccurate (low) readings.  Why A wrong: Tamponade is a clinical pathology, not the common measurement-related cause described.  Why C wrong: Aortic stenosis is a disease state, not the routine assessment cause described.  Why D wrong: Not referenced as the common measurement cause on this page."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"When the patient's brachial artery is inaccessible and you must use the lower leg to measure BP, what should you note about the systolic reading compared with that in the arm?","options":["The leg systolic pressure is normally lower than in the arm","The leg systolic pressure is normally higher than in the arm","The leg systolic pressure is always equal to the arm","Leg readings cannot be obtained"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Blood pressure obtained in the lower extremities is generally higher than if taken in the upper extremities; in children over 1 year of age the systolic pressure in the leg tends to be higher than in the arm.  Why A wrong: Lower-extremity BP is higher, not lower.  Why C wrong: It is generally higher, not equal.  Why D wrong: Leg readings can be obtained and are valid with the noted difference."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"The fifth Korotkoff sound corresponds to which blood pressure value in children?","options":["Systolic blood pressure","Diastolic blood pressure","Mean arterial pressure","Pulse pressure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the fifth Korotkoff sound corresponds to diastolic blood pressure in children.  Why A wrong: It corresponds to diastolic, not systolic, pressure.  Why C wrong: MAP is a calculated value, not the Korotkoff fifth sound.  Why D wrong: Pulse pressure is a derived difference, not the fifth sound."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"A patient has a borderline-high reading. The guideline states it is important to do what before accepting the result?","options":["Administer antihypertensives immediately","Take a repeat measurement (after resting quietly)","Discharge the patient","Ignore the reading"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page explains that when a reading is borderline it is important to take a repeat reading, with measurements taken after resting quietly 1 to 5 minutes with the upper arm at heart level.  Why A wrong: Medication is not indicated based on a single borderline reading.  Why C wrong: Discharge is not the recommended action for a borderline reading.  Why D wrong: The reading should be verified, not ignored."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Before measuring blood pressure, the patient should rest quietly in a chair for how long with the upper arm at heart level?","options":["1 to 5 minutes","10 to 15 minutes","20 minutes","No rest needed"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the patient should sit quietly in a chair for 1 to 5 minutes with the upper arm at heart level before measurement.  Why B wrong: The stated rest interval is 1–5 minutes, not 10–15.  Why C wrong: 20 minutes exceeds the stated guideline.  Why D wrong: A rest period is explicitly required."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"When respirations are irregular, the rate should be counted for how long?","options":["15 seconds and multiply by four","A full minute","30 seconds and double","10 seconds"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states if respirations are irregular, count respirations for a full minute.  Why A wrong: For irregular respirations a full minute is required, not a 15-second sample.  Why C wrong: 30 seconds is insufficient for irregular respirations.  Why D wrong: 10 seconds is far too short for an accurate irregular count."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Why should respirations ideally be assessed without the patient (especially a child) being aware?","options":["To save time","Awareness can alter the breathing pattern; respiratory rate often changes when infants/young children know they are observed","To avoid touching the patient","To measure pulse simultaneously"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page advises assessing respirations when the child is resting or sitting quietly, because respiratory rate often changes when infants or young children are aware they are being observed.  Why A wrong: The reason is accuracy, not time-saving.  Why C wrong: Touching is not the rationale for covert assessment.  Why D wrong: Although pulse-respiration is often combined, the stated reason is to avoid altering the breathing pattern."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"After 1 year of age, how should respiratory rate be assessed?","options":["By counting abdominal (diaphragmatic) movements","By counting thoracic (chest) movements","By auscultation only","By palpating the radial pulse"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the most accurate respiratory rate is obtained before disturbing the infant or child, and after 1 year of age count thoracic movements (whereas in the infant abdominal/diaphragmatic movements are counted).  Why A wrong: Abdominal/diaphragmatic counting applies to the infant, not after 1 year.  Why C wrong: Auscultation alone is not the stated method.  Why D wrong: Pulse palpation does not measure respiratory rate."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"In infants, which type of movements are counted to measure respiratory rate?","options":["Thoracic movements","Abdominal (diaphragmatic) movements","Shoulder movements","Carotid pulsations"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states that to measure respiratory rate in the infant, count abdominal (diaphragmatic) movements.  Why A wrong: Thoracic counting applies after 1 year of age.  Why C wrong: Shoulder movements are not used for respiratory counting.  Why D wrong: Carotid pulsations relate to pulse, not respiration."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"When palpating a carotid pulse, why must both carotid arteries NOT be palpated simultaneously?","options":["It is uncomfortable for the patient","It could result in reduced cerebral blood flow","It increases the heart rate","It causes hyperventilation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states when palpating a carotid pulse, lightly press only one side of the neck at a time, never palpate both carotid arteries at the same time, because bilateral pressure could result in reduced cerebral blood flow.  Why A wrong: The danger is physiologic (cerebral perfusion), not merely comfort.  Why C wrong: Reduced cerebral blood flow, not increased heart rate, is the stated risk.  Why D wrong: Hyperventilation is not the stated concern."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"In children younger than 2 years of age, which pulse site is preferred because the radial pulse is difficult to palpate accurately?","options":["Radial pulse","Apical pulse","Carotid pulse","Popliteal pulse"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states for children older than 2 years measure the radial pulse, but in children younger than 2 years the apical pulse is preferred because the radial pulse is difficult to palpate accurately in this age group.  Why A wrong: The radial pulse is difficult to palpate accurately in this young age group.  Why C wrong: Carotid is not the preferred routine site for this age group per the page.  Why D wrong: Popliteal is not the recommended site here."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"If the pulse is irregular, how long should it be counted?","options":["15 seconds","30 seconds","A full minute","10 seconds"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page states if the pulse is irregular, count the pulse for a full minute.  Why A wrong: 15 seconds is insufficient for an irregular pulse.  Why B wrong: 30 seconds is insufficient when irregular.  Why D wrong: 10 seconds is far too short."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"A nurse notes a temperature higher or lower than expected based on assessment. What is the FIRST appropriate action?","options":["Document immediately and report","Reassess the temperature with a different thermometer","Administer antipyretics","Ignore the value"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states if the temperature is higher or lower than expected based on assessment, reassess temperature with a different thermometer.  Why A wrong: Reassessment should precede documenting an inconsistent value.  Why C wrong: Medication is not the first step for a questionable reading.  Why D wrong: An unexpected value must be verified, not ignored."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"During rectal temperature assessment, what should the nurse do regarding patient privacy?","options":["Leave the patient fully exposed","Provide privacy and maintain dignity","Use a public area","Skip privacy measures for speed"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states during rectal temperature assessment the patient requires privacy/dignity, and never attempt to assess rectal temperature on an uncooperative patient (forcing the probe could cause rectal injury).  Why A wrong: Exposure violates dignity guidance.  Why C wrong: A public area is inappropriate for rectal assessment.  Why D wrong: Privacy measures must not be skipped."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Why should a rectal thermometer never be forced into an uncooperative patient?","options":["It wastes time","Forcing the probe could cause rectal injury","It cools the thermometer","It increases the reading"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states never attempt to assess rectal temperature on an uncooperative patient because forcing the thermometer probe into the rectum could cause rectal injury.  Why A wrong: The concern is patient harm, not time.  Why C wrong: Cooling the thermometer is not the stated reason.  Why D wrong: Falsely elevated reading is not the stated rationale."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"For an unconscious or comatose patient, which temperature route should be avoided?","options":["Rectal","Oral","Axillary","Temporal"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states for an unconscious or comatose patient, avoid oral temperature measurement to ensure an accurate reading and patient safety.  Why A wrong: Rectal is not the route specified to avoid here for the unconscious patient.  Why C wrong: Axillary is an acceptable alternative.  Why D wrong: Temporal is not the route flagged to avoid."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Why should the lens of the temporal artery thermometer be kept clean and free of alcohol or sweat?","options":["To prevent staining","To obtain accurate readings","To save battery","To prevent infection only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states to obtain an accurate temporal artery reading keep the lens clean and free of debris; if dirty, clean the lens and let it dry, and an alcohol prep/swab can be used.  Why A wrong: Staining is not the primary stated concern.  Why C wrong: Battery is unrelated.  Why D wrong: Accuracy, not infection, is the stated rationale."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Axillary temperatures are generally about how much different from oral temperatures, per the page?","options":["About one degree lower than oral","About one degree higher than oral","Exactly equal to oral","Two degrees lower than oral"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states axillary temperatures are generally about one degree lower than oral temperatures.  Why B wrong: Rectal temperatures, not axillary, are about one degree higher.  Why C wrong: They are not equal; an adjustment applies.  Why D wrong: The stated difference is about one degree, not two."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"During pediatric rectal temperature measurement, how far should the thermometer probe be inserted?","options":["5 cm","Not too far (carefully, to prevent rectal injury)","10 cm","As deep as possible"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page advises that during pediatric rectal temperature measurement the prong/probe should be inserted carefully and not too far, to prevent rectal injury.  Why A wrong: 5 cm is too deep for a child and risks injury.  Why C wrong: 10 cm grossly exceeds safe pediatric depth.  Why D wrong: Inserting as deep as possible risks rectal injury."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"For children older than what age may axillary temperature be appropriate per the page?","options":["Older than 3 years of age","Older than 12 years","Only adults","From birth"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states for children older than 3 years of age axillary temperature may be appropriate.  Why B wrong: The threshold stated is over 3 years, not 12.  Why C wrong: It is not restricted to adults.  Why D wrong: An age threshold (over 3 years) is given, not from birth."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Mean Arterial Pressure (MAP) is defined as the average pressure in the arteries during which part of the cardiac cycle?","options":["A single moment of systole","One cardiac cycle","Only diastole","The pulse pressure peak"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page defines MAP as the average pressure in the arteries during one cardiac cycle.  Why A wrong: MAP averages over the cycle, not a single systolic moment.  Why C wrong: MAP reflects the whole cycle, weighted but not diastole alone.  Why D wrong: Pulse pressure peak is a different measure."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Why is MAP considered an important indicator of perfusion?","options":["It is easier to measure","It reflects the hemodynamic perfusion pressure of vital organs needed to perfuse the coronary arteries, brain and kidneys","It is always higher than systolic","It ignores diastole"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states MAP reflects the hemodynamic perfusion pressure of vital organs and is needed to perfuse the coronary arteries, brain and kidneys.  Why A wrong: Ease of measurement is not the stated rationale.  Why C wrong: MAP is not necessarily higher than systolic.  Why D wrong: MAP incorporates diastolic pressure (weighted), it does not ignore it."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"What is the normal range of MAP according to the page?","options":["10–20 mm Hg","70–100 mm Hg","110–130 mm Hg","40–60 mm Hg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the normal value of MAP is 70–100 mm Hg.  Why A wrong: 10–20 mm Hg is far below normal.  Why C wrong: 110–130 mm Hg is above the stated normal range.  Why D wrong: 40–60 mm Hg is below the normal MAP range."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Which formula correctly calculates Mean Arterial Pressure (MAP)?","options":["MAP = (SBP + DBP) / 2","MAP = (DBP + DBP + SBP) / 3, i.e., (2×DBP + SBP) / 3","MAP = SBP − DBP","MAP = (SBP × DBP) / 2"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page gives the formula MAP = (DBP + DBP + SBP) / 3, equivalent to (2DBP + SBP)/3.  Why A wrong: A simple average of SBP and DBP is not the MAP formula.  Why C wrong: SBP − DBP is the pulse pressure, not MAP.  Why D wrong: A product/2 is not a valid MAP formula."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"A client's BP is 110/90 mm Hg. Using the page's MAP formula, what is the MAP?","options":["100 mm Hg","Approximately 96–97 mm Hg","110 mm Hg","90 mm Hg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Using the page's example BP 110/90: MAP = (2×90 + 110)/3 = (180+110)/3 = 290/3 ≈ 96.6 mm Hg.  Why A wrong: A simple mean of 110 and 90 = 100, which is not the MAP formula result.  Why C wrong: 110 is the systolic value, not MAP.  Why D wrong: 90 is the diastolic value, not MAP."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Which term describes a harmful, unintended (unplanned) reaction to a medication?","options":["Side effect","Adverse effect","Therapeutic action","Idiosyncratic reaction"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page defines adverse effect as a harmful, unintended (unplanned) reaction.  Why A wrong: A side effect is a response unrelated to the desired action.  Why C wrong: Therapeutic action is the action for which the drug is prescribed/desired.  Why D wrong: Idiosyncratic reaction is an unique/unpredictable response."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"A \"side effect\" of a drug is best defined as:","options":["The intended therapeutic action","A response that is unrelated to the desired action of the drug","A toxic accumulation in the body","A drug allergy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page defines side effect as a response that is unrelated to the desired action of drugs.  Why A wrong: That describes therapeutic action.  Why C wrong: That describes cumulative/toxic effects.  Why D wrong: Allergy is a separate immunologic response."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"The \"therapeutic action\" of a drug refers to:","options":["The harmful unintended reaction","The action for which the drug is prescribed or the desired action","The accumulation of the drug to dangerous levels","An opposite (paradoxical) reaction"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page defines therapeutic action as the action for which the drug is prescribed or the desired action.  Why A wrong: That is the adverse effect.  Why C wrong: That is the cumulative/toxic effect.  Why D wrong: That is a paradoxical reaction."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Which effect occurs when plasma concentration of a drug reaches a dangerous or high level or is life-threatening?","options":["Side effect","Toxic effect","Therapeutic effect","Idiosyncratic effect"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page defines toxic effect as a severe adverse effect that occurs when plasma concentration of a drug reaches a dangerous or high level or is life-threatening.  Why A wrong: A side effect is unrelated to the desired action.  Why C wrong: Therapeutic effect is the desired effect.  Why D wrong: Idiosyncratic effect is an unpredictable response."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"When repeated doses of a drug accumulate in the body and cause greater biologic action than the initial dose, this is called:","options":["Tolerance","Cumulative effect","Drug dependence","Idiosyncratic reaction"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page defines cumulative effect as occurring when repeated doses accumulate in the body and cause greater biologic action than the initial dose.  Why A wrong: Tolerance is when an organism no longer responds, needing a higher dose.  Why C wrong: Drug dependence is physical or psychological reliance.  Why D wrong: Idiosyncratic reaction is an unpredictable response."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Physical or psychological dependence on chemical agents resulting from continued use, abuse, or addiction is termed:","options":["Tolerance","Drug dependence","Cumulative effect","Paradoxical reaction"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page defines drug dependence as physical or psychological dependence on chemical agents resulting from continued use, abuse, or addiction.  Why A wrong: Tolerance is reduced response requiring higher doses.  Why C wrong: Cumulative effect is drug build-up causing greater action.  Why D wrong: Paradoxical reaction is an opposite response."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"A drug's unique, unpredictable response in a particular individual is called:","options":["Adverse reaction","Idiosyncratic response","Paradoxical reaction","Cumulative effect"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page defines idiosyncratic response as a drug's unique, unpredictable response.  Why A wrong: Adverse reaction is a harmful unintended reaction.  Why C wrong: A paradoxical reaction is an opposite action.  Why D wrong: Cumulative effect involves drug accumulation."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"A paradoxical reaction is best described as:","options":["The expected therapeutic action","A reaction that is the opposite to the action of the drug","A drug allergy","A toxic plasma level"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page defines paradoxical reaction as a reaction opposite to the action of the drug (example: a pain reliever causing pain).  Why A wrong: Therapeutic action is the intended/desired effect.  Why C wrong: Allergy is an immunologic response.  Why D wrong: A toxic level is a high dangerous plasma concentration."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Tolerance to a drug is defined as:","options":["A harmful unintended reaction","A state in which an organism no longer responds to a drug, requiring a higher dose to achieve the same effect","Opposite drug action","Drug accumulation causing toxicity"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page defines tolerance as a state in which an organism no longer responds to a drug, so a higher dose is required to achieve the effect.  Why A wrong: That is an adverse reaction.  Why C wrong: That is a paradoxical reaction.  Why D wrong: That is a cumulative/toxic effect."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Which mnemonic represents the key questions to ask when administering medications?","options":["Who, What, When, How and Why","Who, What, Where, How and When","Why, What, Where, Who and How","When, Where, Who, What and Why"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists the key questions when administering medications as Who, What, When, How and Why.  Why B wrong: This omits \"Why\" and adds \"Where,\" not the stated list.  Why C wrong: It introduces \"Where,\" not part of the stated questions.  Why D wrong: It introduces \"Where\" and reorders incorrectly."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Which of the following is listed as an individual factor that affects a drug's dose and absorption?","options":["Pharmacist error","Age, weight, gender, height, physiologic status","Hospital policy","Drug packaging"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists individual factors as age, weight, gender, height, and physiologic status.  Why A wrong: Pharmacist error is a cause of medication error, not an individual absorption factor.  Why C wrong: Hospital policy is not listed as an individual factor.  Why D wrong: Packaging is not listed among individual factors."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"\"Characteristics of drugs\" affecting dose and absorption refers to which property?","options":["The patient's renal function","Rate of absorption, distribution, biotransformation, and excretion of drugs","The therapeutic index","The membrane barrier"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists characteristics of drugs as the rate of absorption, distribution, biotransformation, and excretion of drugs.  Why A wrong: Renal function is part of patient/individual physiologic status.  Why C wrong: Therapeutic index is a separate concept.  Why D wrong: Membrane barrier is a separate listed factor."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Inability of the body to metabolize or excrete a drug effectively can be caused by all of the following EXCEPT:","options":["Difficulty of particular tissues","Ineffectiveness of enzymes required for metabolism","Depressed function of tissues naturally metabolizing or excreting","A wide therapeutic index"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists causes as difficulty of particular tissues, ineffectiveness of enzymes, and depressed function of tissues that naturally metabolize/excrete; a wide therapeutic index is not a listed cause.  Why A wrong: It is listed as a contributing cause.  Why B wrong: Enzyme ineffectiveness is listed.  Why C wrong: Depressed metabolizing/excreting function is listed."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Which barrier is listed as a factor affecting drug dose and absorption?","options":["Therapeutic index","Membrane barrier — placental and blood-brain barrier","Cumulative effect","First-pass metabolism"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists membrane barrier (placental and blood-brain barrier) as a factor affecting drug dose and absorption.  Why A wrong: Therapeutic index is a separate concept.  Why C wrong: Cumulative effect is a glossary term, not a barrier.  Why D wrong: First-pass metabolism is not the listed barrier item."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Therapeutic index is best defined as:","options":["The peak concentration of a drug","The ratio between the lethal dose and therapeutic dose, used to determine safe dose","The trough level of a drug","The time to reach peak level"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page defines therapeutic index as a ratio between the lethal dose and the therapeutic dose, used to determine safe dose; it compares the concentration at which a drug becomes toxic and the concentration at which it is effective.  Why A wrong: Peak concentration is a different pharmacokinetic measure.  Why C wrong: Trough is the lowest level, not the index.  Why D wrong: Time-to-peak is a separate parameter."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"A larger therapeutic index indicates that a drug is:","options":["More toxic","Safer","Ineffective","More likely to cause dependence"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the larger the therapeutic index, the safer the drug is.  Why A wrong: A larger index means a wider safety margin, less toxicity risk at therapeutic doses.  Why C wrong: A larger index does not mean ineffective.  Why D wrong: Dependence is unrelated to the size of the therapeutic index."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"If the therapeutic index is small (the difference between the two concentrations is very small), what is the correct nursing implication?","options":["The drug is very safe and needs no monitoring","The drug must be dosed carefully and the person monitored closely for any signs of drug toxicity","The drug can be given in unlimited doses","The drug has no therapeutic effect"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states if the difference between the two concentrations is very small, the drug must be dosed carefully and the person receiving it monitored closely for any signs of drug toxicity.  Why A wrong: A small index means LESS safety margin, requiring more monitoring.  Why C wrong: A narrow index demands cautious, not unlimited, dosing.  Why D wrong: A small index does not mean no effect; it means a narrow safety margin."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"What is meant by the \"peak level\" of a drug?","options":["The lowest concentration of the drug in the body","The highest concentration of the drug in the body","The level just before the next scheduled dose","The dose used to determine toxicity"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page defines peak level as the highest concentration of the drug.  Why A wrong: The lowest level is the trough.  Why C wrong: The level before the next dose describes the trough/residual level.  Why D wrong: That describes the therapeutic index concept."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"When a drug is given orally, peak level is generally reached within what time frame?","options":["Within 5 minutes","After taking orally within 60 minutes or more","Within 24 hours","Immediately"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states for the oral route, peak level is reached within 60 minutes or more after taking orally.  Why A wrong: 5 minutes is far too soon for oral peak.  Why C wrong: 24 hours overstates the oral peak time.  Why D wrong: Oral drugs are not absorbed immediately."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"After an intramuscular injection, peak level of drugs is generally reached within:","options":["5 minutes","30–60 minutes","12 hours","15–30 minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states for the intramuscular route, peak level is reached within 30–60 minutes after giving IM injections.  Why A wrong: 5 minutes is too soon for IM peak.  Why C wrong: 12 hours overstates IM peak.  Why D wrong: 15–30 minutes is the stated time for IV, not IM."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"After intravenous administration, peak level of a drug is reached within:","options":["60 minutes or more","15–30 minutes (reaches peak level fast)","24 hours","2 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states for the intravenous route, the drug reaches peak level within 15–30 minutes (fastest).  Why A wrong: 60 min or more is the oral peak time.  Why C wrong: 24 hours grossly overstates IV peak.  Why D wrong: 2 hours is too long for IV peak."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"What is the \"trough (residual) level\" of a drug?","options":["The highest drug concentration","The lowest level of a drug in the body","The level reached after oral administration","The toxic level"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states trough (residual) level is the lowest level of a drug.  Why A wrong: The highest level is the peak.  Why C wrong: That is not the definition of trough.  Why D wrong: Trough is the lowest level, not the toxic level."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"When is the trough level of a drug usually measured?","options":["Immediately after administration","Usually within 15 minutes of the next scheduled dose","At peak concentration","One hour after a meal"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the trough level is usually measured within 15 minutes of the next scheduled dose.  Why A wrong: Immediately after administration reflects rising/peak level, not trough.  Why C wrong: Peak is the opposite of trough.  Why D wrong: A meal timing is not the trough criterion stated."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"To ensure the \"right medication,\" how many times should the nurse check the medicine label, per the page?","options":["1 time","2 times","3 times","5 times"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the medicine label should be checked 3 times: while taking the medicine from the cupboard or shelf, while dissolving the medicine, and just before administering it to the client.  Why A wrong: One check is insufficient.  Why B wrong: The page specifies three, not two.  Why D wrong: Five checks is not the stated number for label checks."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"(SATA) When should the medicine label be checked to ensure the \"right medication\"? Select all that apply.","options":["While taking the medicine from the cupboard or shelf","While dissolving the medicine","Just before administering the medicine to the client","After the patient has swallowed it","Only at the pharmacy"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists the three label-check points as while taking from the cupboard/shelf, while dissolving the medicine, and just before administering to the client.  Why D wrong: Checking after administration defeats the purpose.  Why E wrong: The checks are done by the nurse at preparation/administration, not only at the pharmacy."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Verifying the name on the order against the patient's name, using two patient identifiers, asking the patient to identify himself, and using barcode technology all support which right?","options":["Right medication","Right patient","Right dose","Right route"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists these checks under Right Patient (check name on order vs patient, use two patient identifiers, ask the patient to identify himself, use technology e.g., barcode).  Why A wrong: Right medication concerns the drug label, not identity.  Why C wrong: Right dose concerns dose appropriateness.  Why D wrong: Right route concerns route appropriateness."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Which actions belong to the \"Right Dose\" component of the 5 Rights?","options":["Check the medication label only","Check the order, confirm the appropriateness of the dose using a current drug reference, and recalculate the dose (have another nurse calculate as well) if necessary","Confirm the frequency of the ordered medication","Ask the patient to identify himself"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists under Right Dose: check the order, confirm the appropriateness of the dose using a current drug reference, and if necessary calculate the dose and have another nurse calculate it as well.  Why A wrong: Checking the label is the Right Medication step.  Why C wrong: Confirming frequency is the Right Time step.  Why D wrong: Patient identification is the Right Patient step."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Confirming the frequency of the ordered medication and confirming when the last dose was given are checks under which right?","options":["Right route","Right time","Right dose","Right patient"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists under Right Time: check the order, confirm the frequency of the ordered medication, and confirm when the last dose was given.  Why A wrong: Right route concerns route appropriateness.  Why C wrong: Right dose concerns dose appropriateness/recalculation.  Why D wrong: Right patient concerns identification."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Confirming the appropriateness of the route ordered and that the patient can take or receive the medication by the ordered route are checks under which right?","options":["Right route","Right time","Right patient","Right medication"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists under Right Route: check the order, confirm the appropriateness of the route ordered, and confirm the patient can take or receive the medication by the ordered route.  Why B wrong: Right time concerns frequency and last dose timing.  Why C wrong: Right patient concerns identity verification.  Why D wrong: Right medication concerns the label."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Making the patient understand what the medication is for and aware to contact a healthcare professional if they experience side effects or reactions belongs to which additional right?","options":["Right documentation","Right patient education","Right to refuse","Right evaluation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists Right Patient Education: check the patient understands what the medication is for and make them aware they should contact a healthcare professional if they experience side effects or reactions.  Why A wrong: Right documentation concerns recording administration.  Why C wrong: Right to refuse concerns the patient declining.  Why D wrong: Right evaluation concerns confirming effectiveness."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Ensuring the patient has signed for the medication after it has been administered, and that it is prescribed correctly with a start and end date if appropriate, belongs to which right?","options":["Right documentation","Right to refuse","Right assessment","Right evaluation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists Right Documentation: ensure the patient has signed for the medication after it has been administered and ensure it is prescribed correctly with start and end date if appropriate.  Why B wrong: Right to refuse concerns declining medication.  Why C wrong: Right assessment concerns checking the patient actually needs the medication.  Why D wrong: Right evaluation concerns confirming effectiveness."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Ensuring the patient has the right to refuse medication, recognizing that patients have a right to refuse if they have the capacity to do so, describes which right?","options":["Right assessment","Right to refuse","Right documentation","Right evaluation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists Right to Refuse: ensure the patient has the right to refuse the medication and be aware patients have a right to refuse if they have the capacity to do so.  Why A wrong: Right assessment is checking the patient actually needs the medication.  Why C wrong: Right documentation is recording administration.  Why D wrong: Right evaluation is confirming the medication works."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Checking that the patient actually needs the medication, checking for contraindications, and recording baseline observations if required belongs to which right?","options":["Right evaluation","Right assessment","Right documentation","Right patient education"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists Right Assessment: check that the patient actually needs the medication, check for contraindications, and baseline observations if required.  Why A wrong: Right evaluation is ensuring the medication is working and ongoing observations.  Why C wrong: Right documentation concerns recording administration.  Why D wrong: Right patient education concerns patient understanding."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Ensuring the medication is working the way it should, with ongoing observations if required, describes which right?","options":["Right evaluation","Right assessment","Right documentation","Right to refuse"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists Right Evaluation: ensure the medication is working the way it should and perform ongoing observations if required.  Why B wrong: Right assessment is determining need and contraindications before giving.  Why C wrong: Right documentation is recording administration.  Why D wrong: Right to refuse concerns the patient declining."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"The page notes that additional rights have been suggested by how many panels to enhance patient safety?","options":["Multiple US nursing bodies and research panels","A single hospital","The patient's family","The pharmaceutical company"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states additional rights, unspecified rights, have been suggested by multiple US nursing bodies and research panels to enhance patient safety.  Why B wrong: Not a single hospital but multiple recognized bodies/panels.  Why C wrong: The family is not the source of these rights.  Why D wrong: A pharmaceutical company is not cited as the source."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"(SATA) Which of the following are listed as common medication errors? Select all that apply.","options":["Incorrect patient","Prescribing errors","Wrong dose preparation or timing","Failure to obtain or consider patient allergy history","Always documenting on time"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists common medication errors as incorrect patient, prescribing errors, wrong dose preparation or timing, inaccurate order transcription, administration of incorrect drug or dose, incorrect route of administration, failure to obtain or consider patient allergy history, and consideration of patient renal or liver function.  Why E wrong: Documenting on time is good practice, not a listed error."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Which of the following is listed as a common medication error related to the route?","options":["Incorrect route of administration","Right documentation","Right evaluation","Baseline observation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists incorrect route of administration among common medication errors.  Why B wrong: Right documentation is a right of administration, not an error.  Why C wrong: Right evaluation is a right of administration.  Why D wrong: Baseline observation is part of right assessment, not an error."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"\"Inaccurate order transcription\" is classified on the page as:","options":["A common medication error","A right of administration","A drug preparation type","A route of administration"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists inaccurate order transcription as a common medication error.  Why B wrong: It is an error, not a right.  Why C wrong: It is not a drug preparation type.  Why D wrong: It is not a route of administration."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"(SATA) Which of the following are listed as causes of medication errors? Select all that apply.","options":["Incorrect preparation","Expired product","Distraction","Poor handwriting or translation","Double-checking with another nurse"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists causes of medication errors as incorrect preparation, incorrect dosing duration, expired product, incorrect drug strength or dose, incorrect timing or not given, known allergen or contraindication, distraction, pharmacist error, poor handwriting or translation, and lack of consideration of patient age or other health conditions.  Why E wrong: Double-checking is a safety practice, not a cause of error."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Which of the following is listed as a cause of medication errors related to patient factors?","options":["Lack of consideration of patient age or other health conditions","Right route check","Barcode scanning","Baseline observation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists \"lack of consideration of patient age or other health conditions\" as a cause of medication errors.  Why B wrong: Right route check is a safety step, not a cause of error.  Why C wrong: Barcode scanning prevents errors.  Why D wrong: Baseline observation is part of safe assessment."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"\"Pharmacist error\" and \"poor handwriting or translation\" are listed on the page as:","options":["Causes of medication errors","Rights of administration","Drug preparation types","Routes of administration"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists pharmacist error and poor handwriting/translation among causes of medication errors.  Why B wrong: These are causes of error, not rights.  Why C wrong: They are not drug preparation types.  Why D wrong: They are not routes of administration."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"(PYQ — Recall, NORCET 2019) The angle between the needle and surface of the skin in an intramuscular injection technique should be maintained at:","options":["80 degrees","90 degrees","45 degrees","15 degrees"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The recall answer corresponds to 90 degrees; the routes table also specifies IM at 90°.  Why A wrong: 80 degrees is not the standard IM angle.  Why C wrong: 45 degrees is used for subcutaneous, not IM.  Why D wrong: 15 degrees is for intradermal, not IM."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"(PYQ — Recall, NORCET 2019) During an intravenous injection, which site should always be AVOIDED?","options":["Bone marrow","Vein","Spinal cord","Endotracheal route"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The recall item indicates the IV injection should avoid the spinal cord; IV access is into a vein, never the spinal cord.  Why A wrong: Bone marrow refers to intraosseous, a separate route.  Why B wrong: The vein is the intended IV site.  Why D wrong: Endotracheal is a separate route, not the IV target."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"(PYQ — Recall, NORCET 2019) To prevent sciatic nerve injury, which muscle/site should always be AVOIDED for IM injection?","options":["Ventrogluteal","Vastus lateralis","Deltoid","Dorsogluteal"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The recall answer is dorsogluteal; the dorsogluteal site is avoided because of the risk of sciatic nerve injury.  Why A wrong: Ventrogluteal is a preferred safer site, not avoided.  Why B wrong: Vastus lateralis is a recommended IM site.  Why C wrong: Deltoid is a commonly used IM site."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"(PYQ — Recall, NORCET 2019) An intramuscular injection in neonates should be given in which muscle?","options":["Dorsogluteal muscle","Vastus lateralis muscle","Gluteus maximus muscle","Deltoid muscle"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The recall answer is vastus lateralis; it is the recommended IM site in neonates because it is well developed and free of major nerves and vessels.  Why A wrong: Dorsogluteal is avoided due to underdeveloped muscle and nerve injury risk.  Why C wrong: Gluteus maximus is not the recommended neonatal IM site.  Why D wrong: The deltoid is too small/underdeveloped in neonates."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Which preparation is described as a medication combined in a water and sugar solution?","options":["Elixir","Syrup","Solution","Lotion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page defines syrup as a medication combined in a water and sugar solution.  Why A wrong: An elixir is medication in a clear liquid containing water, alcohol, sweeteners, and flavor.  Why C wrong: A solution is a drug dissolved in another substance.  Why D wrong: A lotion is drug particles in a solution for topical use."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Small, solid dosage forms of medication that may be any color, size, or shape, with enteric-coated versions coated to reduce gastric irritation, are called:","options":["Capsule","Tablet","Lozenge","Pill"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page defines tablet as a small, solid dose form of medication of any color, size, or shape; enteric-coated tablets are coated with a substance insoluble in gastric acids to reduce gastric irritation by the drug.  Why A wrong: A capsule is a powder/gel form enclosed in a gelatinous container.  Why C wrong: A lozenge is a disc-shaped preparation that dissolves in the mouth.  Why D wrong: A pill is a powdered drug in a cohesive material."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Which preparation is a powder or gel form of medication enclosed in a gelatinous container?","options":["Tablet","Capsule","Lozenge","Suppository"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page defines capsule as a powder or gel form of medication enclosed in a gelatinous container (also called liquigel).  Why A wrong: A tablet is a small solid dose form, not a gel-enclosed powder.  Why C wrong: A lozenge dissolves in the mouth.  Why D wrong: A suppository is inserted into a body cavity."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Medication in a clear liquid containing water, alcohol, sweeteners, and flavor is termed:","options":["Syrup","Elixir","Liniment","Lotion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page defines elixir as medication in a clear liquid containing water, alcohol, sweeteners, and flavor.  Why A wrong: Syrup is medication in a water and sugar solution.  Why C wrong: Liniment is medication mixed with alcohol, oil, or soap, rubbed on the skin.  Why D wrong: Lotion is drug particles in a solution for topical use."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"A tablet or pill coated to prevent stomach irritation is called:","options":["Enteric-coated","Lozenge","Suppository","Powder"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page defines enteric-coated as a tablet or pill coated to prevent stomach irritation.  Why B wrong: A lozenge dissolves in the mouth.  Why C wrong: A suppository is inserted into a body cavity.  Why D wrong: A powder is a single or mixture of finely ground drugs."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Medication mixed with alcohol, oil, or soap, which is rubbed on the skin, is called a/an:","options":["Lotion","Liniment","Ointment","Lozenge"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page defines liniment as medication mixed with alcohol, oil, or soap, which is rubbed on the skin.  Why A wrong: A lotion is drug particles in a solution for topical use.  Why C wrong: An ointment is a semisolid preparation applied externally (unction).  Why D wrong: A lozenge dissolves in the mouth."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Drug particles in a solution for topical use describe a:","options":["Liniment","Lotion","Solution","Suspension"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page defines lotion as drug particles in a solution for topical use.  Why A wrong: Liniment is medication with alcohol/oil/soap rubbed on skin.  Why C wrong: A solution is a drug dissolved in another substance.  Why D wrong: A suspension is finely divided, undissolved particles in a liquid medium."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"A small, oval, round, or oblong preparation containing a drug in a flavored or sweetened base, which dissolves in the mouth and releases the medication, is called a:","options":["Lozenge (also called troche)","Pill","Tablet","Powder"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page defines lozenge as a small, oval, round, or oblong preparation containing a drug in a flavored or sweetened base that dissolves in the mouth and releases the medication; also called troche.  Why B wrong: A pill is a powdered drug in a cohesive material.  Why C wrong: A tablet is a small solid dose form, not specifically a dissolving-in-mouth lozenge.  Why D wrong: A powder is finely ground drug(s)."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"A semisolid preparation containing a drug, applied externally and also called unction, is a/an:","options":["Lotion","Ointment","Liniment","Powder"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page defines ointment as a semisolid preparation containing a drug to be applied externally; also called unction.  Why A wrong: A lotion is drug particles in solution for topical use.  Why C wrong: A liniment is medication with alcohol/oil/soap rubbed on skin.  Why D wrong: A powder is finely ground drugs."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Which preparation is described as a mixture of a powdered drug in a cohesive material and may be round or oval?","options":["Pill","Powder","Capsule","Suspension"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page defines pill as a mixture of a powdered drug in a cohesive material; may be round or oval.  Why B wrong: A powder is a single or mixture of finely ground drugs.  Why C wrong: A capsule is a powder/gel enclosed in a gelatinous container.  Why D wrong: A suspension is undissolved particles in a liquid medium."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"A \"powder\" preparation is best defined as:","options":["A semisolid preparation","A single drug or a mixture of finely ground drugs","A drug dissolved in another substance","A clear liquid with alcohol"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page defines powder as a single drug or mixture of finely ground drugs.  Why A wrong: A semisolid preparation describes an ointment.  Why C wrong: A dissolved drug describes a solution.  Why D wrong: A clear liquid with alcohol describes an elixir."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"A drug dissolved in another substance (e.g., in an aqueous solution) is called a:","options":["Suspension","Solution","Lotion","Ointment"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page defines solution as a drug dissolved in another substance (e.g., in an aqueous solution).  Why A wrong: A suspension contains undissolved particles, not dissolved drug.  Why C wrong: A lotion is drug particles in solution for topical use.  Why D wrong: An ointment is a semisolid external preparation."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"An easily melted medication preparation in a firm base such as gelatin that is inserted into a body cavity (rectum, vagina, urethra) where it melts is called a:","options":["Lozenge","Suppository","Capsule","Tablet"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page defines suppository as an easily melted medication preparation in a firm base such as gelatin that is inserted into a body cavity (rectum, vagina, urethra) and melts.  Why A wrong: A lozenge dissolves in the mouth.  Why C wrong: A capsule is a powder/gel in a gelatinous container.  Why D wrong: A tablet is a small solid dose form."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Finely divided, undissolved particles in a liquid medium that should be shaken before use describe a:","options":["Solution","Suspension","Elixir","Syrup"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page defines suspension as finely divided, undissolved particles in a liquid medium; should be shaken well before use.  Why A wrong: A solution has dissolved drug, not undissolved particles.  Why C wrong: An elixir is a clear liquid, not a particle suspension.  Why D wrong: A syrup is a water and sugar solution."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"A unit dose of medication applied directly to the skin for diffusion through the skin and absorption into the bloodstream is called a:","options":["Suppository","Transdermal patch","Extended release","Liniment"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page defines transdermal patch as a unit dose of medication applied directly to the skin for diffusion through the skin and absorption into the bloodstream.  Why A wrong: A suppository is inserted into a body cavity.  Why C wrong: Extended release is a slow continuous-release preparation.  Why D wrong: A liniment is rubbed on the skin (alcohol/oil/soap)."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"A preparation that allows slow and continuous release over a predetermined period describes which type of preparation?","options":["Immediate release","Extended release (e.g., CR, SR, SA, LA, TR)","Elixir","Suspension"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page defines extended release as a preparation that allows slow and continuous release over a predetermined period; may be referred to as CR (controlled release), SR (sustained release), SA (sustained action), LA (long acting), or TR (timed release), e.g., Pantoprazole.  Why A wrong: Immediate release is the opposite of extended release.  Why C wrong: An elixir is a clear liquid.  Why D wrong: A suspension is undissolved particles in liquid."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"In the classification of routes of administration, which are the three main categories?","options":["Oral, parenteral, topical","Injection, inhalation, transdermal","Sublingual, nasal, rectal","Intravenous, intramuscular, subcutaneous"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page classifies routes of administration into Oral (enteral), Parenteral, and Topical.  Why B wrong: These are subtypes (injection, inhalation, transdermal, transmucosal), not the three main categories.  Why C wrong: These are transmucosal subtypes.  Why D wrong: These are types of injections (parenteral subtypes)."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Under parenteral routes, which four subtypes are listed in the classification diagram?","options":["Injections, inhalation, transdermal, transmucosal","Sublingual, nasal, rectal, oral","Intradermal only","Oral, topical, rectal, nasal"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page's classification lists under parenteral: Injections, Inhalation, Transdermal, and Transmucosal.  Why B wrong: These are transmucosal/oral subtypes.  Why C wrong: Intradermal is a single injection subtype, not all four.  Why D wrong: These mix topical and oral routes."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"(SATA) Which of the following are listed under \"Injections\" in the classification of parenteral administration? Select all that apply.","options":["Intradermal","Subcutaneous","Intramuscular","Intravenous","Sublingual"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists under Injections: intradermal, subcutaneous, intramuscular, intravenous, and intraspinal.  Why E wrong: Sublingual is a transmucosal route, not an injection."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Which routes are listed under \"Transdermal\" in the classification diagram?","options":["Adhesive unit, injection, iontophoresis, jet injection","Sublingual, nasal, rectal","Oral, intravenous","Inhalation only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists under Transdermal: adhesive unit, injection, iontophoresis, and jet injection.  Why B wrong: These are transmucosal routes.  Why C wrong: These are oral/parenteral routes.  Why D wrong: Inhalation is a separate category."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Which routes are listed under \"Transmucosal\" in the classification diagram?","options":["Sublingual, nasal, rectal","Intradermal, subcutaneous","Adhesive unit, jet injection","Oral, inhalation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists under Transmucosal: sublingual, nasal, and rectal.  Why B wrong: These are injection routes.  Why C wrong: These are transdermal routes.  Why D wrong: These are oral/inhalation categories."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"According to the \"Route for Administration — Time until effect\" table, which route produces effect in the fastest time (10–30 seconds)?","options":["Intravenous","Intra-osseous","Endo-tracheal","Inhalation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page's table lists Intravenous as 10–30 seconds (fastest).  Why B wrong: Intra-osseous is listed at 30–60 seconds.  Why C wrong: Endo-tracheal is listed at 2–3 minutes.  Why D wrong: Inhalation is listed at 2–3 minutes."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Per the route time-to-effect table, the intra-osseous route produces effect within:","options":["10–30 seconds","30–60 seconds","5 minutes","30–60 minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page's table lists intra-osseous at 30–60 seconds.  Why A wrong: 10–30 seconds is the IV time.  Why C wrong: 5 minutes corresponds to subcutaneous.  Why D wrong: 30–60 minutes corresponds to oral."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Per the route time-to-effect table, the endo-tracheal route produces effect within:","options":["2–3 minutes","10–30 seconds","30–60 minutes","5 minutes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page's table lists endo-tracheal at 2–3 minutes.  Why B wrong: 10–30 seconds is IV.  Why C wrong: 30–60 minutes is oral.  Why D wrong: 5 minutes is subcutaneous."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Per the route time-to-effect table, the inhalation route produces effect within:","options":["2–3 minutes","5 minutes","15 minutes","30–60 minutes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page's table lists inhalation at 2–3 minutes.  Why B wrong: 5 minutes corresponds to subcutaneous.  Why C wrong: 15 minutes is not the inhalation time per the table.  Why D wrong: 30–60 minutes corresponds to oral."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Per the route time-to-effect table, the sublingual route produces effect within:","options":["3–5 minutes","30–60 seconds","30–60 minutes","10–30 seconds"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page's table lists sublingual at 3–5 minutes.  Why B wrong: 30–60 seconds is intra-osseous.  Why C wrong: 30–60 minutes is oral.  Why D wrong: 10–30 seconds is IV."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Per the route time-to-effect table, the subcutaneous route produces effect within:","options":["5 minutes","15 minutes","2–3 minutes","30–60 minutes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page's table lists subcutaneous at 5 minutes.  Why B wrong: 15 minutes is not the subcutaneous time per the table.  Why C wrong: 2–3 minutes corresponds to inhalation/endo-tracheal.  Why D wrong: 30–60 minutes corresponds to oral."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Per the route time-to-effect table, the intramuscular route produces effect within:","options":["10–20 minutes","5 minutes","30–60 seconds","Minutes to hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page's table lists intramuscular at 10–20 minutes.  Why B wrong: 5 minutes corresponds to subcutaneous.  Why C wrong: 30–60 seconds corresponds to intra-osseous.  Why D wrong: Minutes to hours corresponds to transdermal (topical) variable."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Per the route time-to-effect table, the ingestion (per oral) route produces effect within:","options":["30–60 minutes","5 minutes","2–3 minutes","10–30 seconds"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page's table lists ingestion (per oral) at 30–60 minutes.  Why B wrong: 5 minutes is subcutaneous.  Why C wrong: 2–3 minutes is inhalation/endo-tracheal.  Why D wrong: 10–30 seconds is IV."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Per the route time-to-effect table, the trans-dermal (topical) route produces effect within:","options":["Minutes to hours (variable)","10–30 seconds","5 minutes","30–60 seconds"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page's table lists trans-dermal (topical) as variable, minutes to hours.  Why B wrong: 10–30 seconds is IV.  Why C wrong: 5 minutes is subcutaneous.  Why D wrong: 30–60 seconds is intra-osseous."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Which statement about the oral route is correct per the page?","options":["It is the least common and most expensive route","It is the most common, convenient, and least expensive route, but absorption is slow and can cause GI irritation","It produces effects within seconds","It is unsuitable for tablets and capsules"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the oral route is the most common, convenient, and least expensive route; absorption is slow and can cause GI irritation; forms include tablets, capsules, pills, powder, and liquids.  Why A wrong: It is the most common and least expensive, not the opposite.  Why C wrong: Oral effect is in 30–60 minutes, not seconds.  Why D wrong: Tablets and capsules are common oral forms."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"How should sustained-release or coated oral preparations be handled per the page?","options":["They should be crushed before administration","They should not be crushed or broken; crushing/breaking can change the concentration rate","They must always be dissolved in water","They are only for IV use"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states sustained-release or coated preparations should not be crushed or broken because crushing can change the concentration rate.  Why A wrong: Crushing is contraindicated for these forms.  Why C wrong: Dissolving is not required and may alter release.  Why D wrong: They are oral, not IV, preparations."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Regarding transdermal patches, which statement is correct per the page?","options":["They administer drugs by passive diffusion at a constant rate and should be applied only on non-hairy areas","They must be applied to hairy areas for best absorption","They release drug instantly and require crushing","They are only used for nausea"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states transdermal patches administer drugs by passive diffusion at a constant rate and should be applied only on non-hairy areas.  Why B wrong: Patches should be applied to non-hairy, not hairy, areas.  Why C wrong: Patches release drug by diffusion (slow), not instantly, and are not crushed.  Why D wrong: Patches are not limited to nausea."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Per the Routes of Drug Administration table, what is the angle and example for an intramuscular (IM) injection?","options":["15°, into the dermis","90°, iron injection, maximum volume 5 ml","45°, into subcutaneous tissue","5–15°, into a vein"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page's routes table lists intramuscular (IM) at 90°, target site muscles, example iron injection, maximum volume 5 ml, time for effect 10–20 minutes.  Why A wrong: 15° into the dermis is intradermal.  Why C wrong: 45° into subcutaneous tissue is subcutaneous.  Why D wrong: 5–15° into a vein is intravenous."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Per the routes table, the intravenous (IV) route has what angle and time for effect?","options":["90°, 10–20 minutes","25°, most common/fastest route; 30–60 seconds","15°, more than 60 minutes","45°, 5–15 minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page's routes table lists intravenous (IV) at 25° angle, target site veins, described as the most common/fastest, time for effect 30–60 seconds.  Why A wrong: 90° and 10–20 minutes describe IM.  Why C wrong: 15° and >60 minutes describe intradermal.  Why D wrong: 45° and 5–15 minutes describe subcutaneous."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Per the routes table, the subcutaneous (SC)/hypodermis route has what angle, example, and time for effect?","options":["90°, iron injection, 10–20 minutes","45°, heparin and insulin, maximum volume 2 ml, 15–30 minutes","15°, BCG, more than 60 minutes","25°, veins, 30–60 seconds"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page's routes table lists subcutaneous (SC)/hypodermis at 45° angle, target site subcutaneous tissue, example heparin and insulin, maximum volume 2 ml, time for effect 15–30 minutes.  Why A wrong: 90° and iron injection describe IM.  Why C wrong: 15° and BCG describe intradermal.  Why D wrong: 25° and veins describe IV."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Per the routes table, the intradermal (ID) route into the dermis has what angle, example, and time for effect?","options":["90°, iron injection, 10–20 minutes","15°, MT (Mantoux) test and AST test, minimum volume 0.1 ml, more than 60 minutes","45°, heparin/insulin, 15–30 minutes","25°, veins, 30–60 seconds"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page's routes table lists intradermal (ID) at 15° angle, target site under the dermis, example MT (Mantoux) test and AST test, minimum volume 0.1 ml, time for effect more than 60 minutes.  Why A wrong: 90° and iron injection describe IM.  Why C wrong: 45° and heparin/insulin describe SC.  Why D wrong: 25° and veins describe IV."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Per the routes table, the sublingual route has which target site and time for effect?","options":["Through the skin; minutes to hours","Through the tongue (under tongue); 3–5 minutes","Through the nose; 2–3 minutes","Into the muscle; 10–20 minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page's routes table lists sublingual at target site \"through the tongue\" and time for effect 3–5 minutes.  Why A wrong: Through the skin/minutes to hours describes transdermal.  Why C wrong: Through the nose/2–3 min describes inhalation.  Why D wrong: Into the muscle/10–20 min describes IM."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Per the routes table, the transdermal route has which target site, example, and time for effect?","options":["Through the skin; painkiller patches; minutes to hours","Through the nose; asthalin nebulization; 2–3 minutes","Into the muscle; iron injection; 10–20 minutes","Into a vein; fastest; 30–60 seconds"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page's routes table lists transdermal at target site \"through the skin,\" example painkiller patches, time for effect minutes to hours.  Why B wrong: Through the nose/asthalin nebulization is inhalation.  Why C wrong: Iron injection/muscle is IM.  Why D wrong: Into a vein/fastest is IV."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Per the routes table, the inhalation route has which target site, example, and time for effect?","options":["Through the skin; painkiller patches; minutes to hours","Through the nose; asthalin nebulization; 2–3 minutes","Side of the tongue; pain killers; 3–5 minutes","Into a body cavity; suppositories and enemas; 5–30 minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page's routes table lists inhalation at target site \"through the nose,\" example asthalin nebulization, time for effect 2–3 minutes.  Why A wrong: Through skin/painkiller patches is transdermal.  Why C wrong: Side of the tongue/pain killers is buccal.  Why D wrong: Body cavity/suppositories and enemas is insertion."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Per the routes table, the buccal route has which target site, example, and time for effect?","options":["Side of the tongue; pain killers; 3–5 minutes","Through the nose; nebulization; 2–3 minutes","Into a vein; fastest; 30–60 seconds","Into the muscle; iron; 10–20 minutes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page's routes table lists buccal at target site \"side of the tongue,\" example pain killers, time for effect 3–5 minutes.  Why B wrong: Through the nose/nebulization is inhalation.  Why C wrong: Into a vein/fastest is IV.  Why D wrong: Into the muscle is IM."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Per the routes table, the insertion route (e.g., for cold drugs) targets which site and is used for which examples?","options":["Into body cavities; suppositories and enemas; 5–30 minutes","Into a vein; fastest; seconds","Through the nose; nebulization","Into the dermis; Mantoux test"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page's routes table lists insertion (cold drugs) at target site \"into body cavities,\" example suppositories and enemas, time for effect 5–30 minutes.  Why B wrong: Into a vein/fastest is IV.  Why C wrong: Through the nose is inhalation.  Why D wrong: Dermis/Mantoux is intradermal."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Per the routes table, the instillation (liquid drugs) route targets which site and is used for which example?","options":["Into body cavities; eye and ear medications; 5–30 minutes","Into a vein; fastest","Into the muscle; iron injection","Into the dermis; BCG"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page's routes table lists instillation (liquid drugs) at target site \"into body cavities,\" example eye and ear medications, time for effect 5–30 minutes.  Why B wrong: Into a vein/fastest is IV.  Why C wrong: Muscle/iron injection is IM.  Why D wrong: Dermis/BCG is intradermal."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Per the routes table, the inunction (local drugs) route involves which target site and example?","options":["Friction over skin; ointment over the skin; minutes","Into a vein; fastest","Into the muscle; iron","Through the nose; nebulization"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page's routes table lists inunction (local drugs) at target site \"friction over skin,\" example ointment over the skin, time for effect minutes.  Why B wrong: Vein/fastest is IV.  Why C wrong: Muscle/iron is IM.  Why D wrong: Nose/nebulization is inhalation."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Per the routes table, the insufflation route involves which target site and example?","options":["Direct on wound; powder direct on the skin; minutes","Into the dermis; Mantoux test","Into a vein; fastest","Into the muscle; iron"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page's routes table lists insufflation at target site \"direct on wound,\" example powder direct on the skin, time for effect minutes.  Why B wrong: Dermis/Mantoux is intradermal.  Why C wrong: Vein/fastest is IV.  Why D wrong: Muscle/iron is IM."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Per the routes table, the implantation route involves which target site and example?","options":["Implant into tissues; Norplant; minutes","Into a vein; fastest","Into the muscle; iron","Through the nose; nebulization"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page's routes table lists implantation at target site \"implant into tissues,\" example Norplant, time for effect minutes.  Why B wrong: Vein/fastest is IV.  Why C wrong: Muscle/iron is IM.  Why D wrong: Nose/nebulization is inhalation."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Per the routes table, the intra-arterial route targets which site, is used for which example, and has what time for effect?","options":["Into artery; heparin during IBP; 30–60 seconds","Into a vein; fastest; 30–60 seconds","Into joint; synovial fluid","Into the muscle; iron"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page's routes table lists intra-arterial at target site \"into artery,\" example heparin during IBP, time for effect 30–60 seconds.  Why B wrong: Into a vein/fastest is IV.  Why C wrong: Into joint/synovial fluid is intra-articular.  Why D wrong: Muscle/iron is IM."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Per the routes table, the intra-articular route targets which site and is used for which example?","options":["Into joint; synovial fluid; minutes","Into artery; heparin","Into a vein; fastest","Into the spinal canal"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page's routes table lists intra-articular at target site \"into joint,\" example synovial fluid, time for effect minutes.  Why B wrong: Into artery/heparin is intra-arterial.  Why C wrong: Into a vein/fastest is IV.  Why D wrong: Into the spinal canal is intrathecal."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Per the routes table, the intrathecal route targets which site and is used for which example?","options":["Into spinal canal; for anesthesia and LP; minutes","Into joint; synovial fluid","Into artery; heparin","Into a vein; fastest"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page's routes table lists intrathecal at target site \"into spinal canal,\" example for anesthesia and LP (lumbar puncture), time for effect minutes.  Why B wrong: Into joint/synovial fluid is intra-articular.  Why C wrong: Into artery/heparin is intra-arterial.  Why D wrong: Into a vein/fastest is IV."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Per the routes table, the intraosseous route targets which site, is used for which example, and has what time for effect?","options":["Into bone marrow; for aspiration of BM and BT; 30–60 seconds","Into a vein; fastest; 30–60 seconds","Into the spinal canal; anesthesia","Into joint; synovial fluid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page's routes table lists intraosseous at target site \"into bone marrow,\" example for aspiration of BM and BT, time for effect 30–60 seconds.  Why B wrong: Into a vein/fastest is IV.  Why C wrong: Into spinal canal/anesthesia is intrathecal.  Why D wrong: Into joint/synovial fluid is intra-articular."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Per the routes table, the intracardiac route targets which site, is used for which example, and has what time for effect?","options":["Into left ventricle; during CPR; 30–60 seconds","Into a vein; fastest; 30–60 seconds","Into bone marrow; BM aspiration","Into joint; synovial fluid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page's routes table lists intracardiac at target site \"into left ventricle,\" example during CPR, time for effect 30–60 seconds.  Why B wrong: Into a vein/fastest is IV.  Why C wrong: Into bone marrow is intraosseous.  Why D wrong: Into joint is intra-articular."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Per the routes table, the intraperitoneal route targets which site and is used for which example?","options":["Into peritoneum; for dialysis; minutes","Into a vein; fastest","Into bone marrow; BM aspiration","Into the spinal canal; anesthesia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page's routes table lists intraperitoneal at target site \"into peritoneum,\" example for dialysis, time for effect minutes.  Why B wrong: Into a vein/fastest is IV.  Why C wrong: Bone marrow is intraosseous.  Why D wrong: Spinal canal is intrathecal."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Per the routes table, the intramedullary route targets which site and is used for which example?","options":["Into bone marrow; for aspiration of BM; minutes","Into a vein; fastest","Into the spinal canal; anesthesia","Into joint; synovial fluid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page's routes table lists intramedullary at target site \"into bone marrow,\" example for aspiration of BM, time for effect minutes.  Why B wrong: Into a vein/fastest is IV.  Why C wrong: Spinal canal is intrathecal.  Why D wrong: Joint is intra-articular."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Per the routes table, the endo-tracheal route targets which site, is used for which example, and has what time for effect?","options":["Into trachea; surfactant for LBW babies; 2–3 minutes","Into a vein; fastest; 30–60 seconds","Into joint; synovial fluid; minutes","Into bone marrow; BM aspiration"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page's routes table lists endo-tracheal at target site \"into trachea,\" example surfactant for LBW (low birth weight) babies, time for effect 2–3 minutes.  Why B wrong: Into a vein/fastest is IV.  Why C wrong: Joint/synovial fluid is intra-articular.  Why D wrong: Bone marrow is intraosseous."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"What is the purpose of an intradermal (ID) injection?","options":["To deliver large fluid volumes quickly","It is used for skin testing and sensitivity tests due to slow diffusion into the skin","To inject directly into the bloodstream","To inject into bone marrow"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the intradermal (ID) route is used for skin testing and sensitivity tests due to slow diffusion into the skin (e.g., Mantoux test, AST test).  Why A wrong: ID delivers very small volumes, not large fluid volumes.  Why C wrong: Direct into the bloodstream describes IV.  Why D wrong: Bone marrow describes intraosseous."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Why does intradermal injection take longer than other routes to take effect?","options":["The dermal region has rich blood supply","The intradermal region of the skin has less blood supply and medications are not absorbed properly into the circulation","The drug is given into a vein","The dose is very large"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states in the intradermal region of the skin blood supply is less and medications are not absorbed properly into the circulation, so ID injections have the longest absorption time of all parenteral routes.  Why A wrong: The dermal blood supply is described as less, not rich.  Why C wrong: ID is into the dermis, not a vein.  Why D wrong: ID volumes are small, not large."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Compared with all other parenteral routes, the intradermal route has the:","options":["Shortest absorption time","Longest absorption time","Same absorption time as IV","Fastest onset"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states intradermal (ID) injections have the longest absorption time of all parenteral routes.  Why A wrong: It is the longest, not shortest.  Why C wrong: IV is fastest; ID is far slower.  Why D wrong: ID has the slowest, not fastest, onset."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"What is the typical volume usually given by the intradermal route?","options":["5 ml","Small volume of 0.1 to 0.5 ml","2 ml","10 ml"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the volume usually given intradermally is a small volume of 0.1 to 0.5 ml.  Why A wrong: 5 ml is the maximum IM volume.  Why C wrong: 2 ml is the maximum subcutaneous volume.  Why D wrong: 10 ml far exceeds the intradermal volume."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"What is the target site (skin layer) for an intradermal injection?","options":["Under epidermis (e.g., BCG 0.05 ml vaccine)","Into the muscle","Into a vein","Into the bone marrow"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the target site for intradermal injection is under the epidermis, e.g., BCG 0.05 ml vaccine.  Why B wrong: Into the muscle is the IM target.  Why C wrong: Into a vein is the IV target.  Why D wrong: Bone marrow is the intraosseous target."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"What gauge and length of needle is used for an intradermal injection, per the page?","options":["18–21 G, 3/8 to 5/8 inch long needle","26–28 G, 3/8 to 5/8 inch (short) needle","16–18 G, 1.5 inch needle","22 G, 3 inch needle"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the needle used for intradermal injection is 26–28 G, 3/8 to 5/8 inch long (short, fine needle).  Why A wrong: 18–21 G is too large for the intradermal route.  Why C wrong: 16–18 G, 1.5 inch is far too large.  Why D wrong: 22 G, 3 inch is inappropriate for ID."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Which is the MOST COMMON insertion site for an intradermal injection?","options":["The inner aspect of the fore arm","The deltoid muscle","The buttock","The antecubital vein"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the insertion site for intradermal injection is the inner aspect of the fore arm (most common), the posterior aspect of the upper arm, and the upper antero-posterior aspect of the chest wall.  Why B wrong: The deltoid muscle is an IM site.  Why C wrong: The buttock is an IM site.  Why D wrong: The antecubital vein is an IV site."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Which route is described as: medication given directly into a vein by continuous infusion, intermittent infusion, and intravenous push?","options":["Intradermal","Intravenous (IV)","Subcutaneous","Intramuscular"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states in the intravenous (IV) route, medication is given directly into a vein by continuous infusion, intermittent infusion, and intravenous push.  Why A wrong: Intradermal is under the epidermis, not into a vein.  Why C wrong: Subcutaneous is into subcutaneous tissue.  Why D wrong: Intramuscular is into muscle."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Why does the intravenous route provide the most rapid drug absorption, and which route is noted as second most rapid?","options":["It is the slowest route; oral is second","It is the most rapid absorption and drug enters directly into the bloodstream; sublingual is the second most rapid route","The dermis has a rich blood supply; intradermal is second","It bypasses the heart; intramuscular is second"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states IV provides the most rapid absorption with the drug entering directly into the bloodstream, and sublingual is the second most rapid route.  Why A wrong: IV is the fastest, not the slowest, route.  Why C wrong: The dermis has less blood supply (relevant to ID, not IV).  Why D wrong: IV drugs circulate via the bloodstream; intramuscular is not the stated second route."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"At what angle is the IV cannula/injection inserted, per the page note?","options":["90° flat against skin","About 5–15° angle for IV injection/cannulation","45° only","25° only and never lower"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page note states IV injection/cannulation is given at about a 5–15° angle, consistent with low-angle venous entry.  Why A wrong: IV is entered at a low angle, not 90° flat.  Why C wrong: 45° is the subcutaneous angle.  Why D wrong: While 25° appears in the routes table, the page's insertion note specifies the 5–15° low angle for cannulation."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"What is the typical volume range for IV administration per the page?","options":["0.1 ml only","Volume depends on the prescription order","Always exactly 5 ml","Always 2 ml"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states for the IV route, volume depends on the prescription order.  Why A wrong: 0.1 ml is the intradermal volume.  Why C wrong: 5 ml is the IM maximum, not the fixed IV volume.  Why D wrong: 2 ml is the subcutaneous maximum, not the IV volume."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"What syringe size is noted for IV use, per the page?","options":["26–28 G short needle only","Syringe 2 to 10, 20 ml","Only a 3 inch needle","No syringe is used"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states for IV, syringe sizes of 2 to 10, 20 ml are used.  Why A wrong: 26–28 G short needle is for intradermal injections.  Why C wrong: A fixed 3 inch needle is not the stated IV detail.  Why D wrong: A syringe is used for IV push/administration."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Specific guidelines for vital parameters, Mean Arterial Pressure…","question":"Which insertion sites are recommended for IV cannulation per the page?","options":["Anywhere with easily visible veins, e.g., the vein of the fore arm (most common), and the hands and antecubital vein","Only the femoral artery","Only the dermis of the forearm","Only the bone marrow"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the IV insertion site includes anywhere with an easily visible vein, e.g., the vein of the fore arm (most common), and the hands and antecubital vein.  Why B wrong: The femoral artery is arterial, not the recommended routine IV site here.  Why C wrong: The dermis is the intradermal target, not IV.  Why D wrong: Bone marrow is the intraosseous route."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"What is the recommended injection volume for an intramuscular (IM) injection?","options":["0.1 to 0.5 mL","1 to 5 mL (2 mL for the deltoid)","6 to 10 mL","0.5 to 1 mL only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For IM, the recommended volume is up to 1 to 5 mL, with 2 mL for the deltoid.  Why A wrong: 0.1–0.5 mL is intradermal-range volume, not IM.  Why C wrong: 6–10 mL exceeds safe IM muscle capacity.  Why D wrong: 0.5–1 mL understates the IM range."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"The needle size for an IM injection depends on which factor?","options":["The colour of the medication","The muscle depending on years (age/muscle development)","The patient's blood type","The time of day"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: IM target site/needle depends on the muscle and patient's development.  Why A/C/D wrong: Medication colour, blood type, and time of day do not determine IM needle selection."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"What angle is used for IM injection when the tissue is thin (using zigzag/Z-track and dart technique)?","options":["15°","45°","90° (or, if thin, use adipose tissue and a chacheck technique then administer with 45° angle)","10–15°"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: IM angle is 90°; if thin, the adipose tissue is bunched and a 45° angle is used.  Why A wrong: 15° is intradermal.  Why B wrong: 45° alone is subcutaneous (or the thin-tissue IM variant).  Why D wrong: 10–15° is intradermal."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Which syringe is used for an IM injection?","options":["1 mL tuberculin syringe","5 mL","Insulin syringe","10 mL only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A 5 mL syringe is specified for IM.  Why A wrong: 1 mL syringe is for intradermal/small SC.  Why C wrong: Insulin syringe is for SC insulin.  Why D wrong: 10 mL is not the standard IM syringe size listed."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"What needle length is recommended for IM injections?","options":["1/4 to 3/8 inch","5/8 inch","18.5 G on 1 to 5\" long needle","25 G short needle only"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: IM needle specified as 18.5 G, 1 to 5\" long needle.  Why A/B wrong: These are intradermal/short SC lengths.  Why D wrong: A short needle alone is insufficient for deep muscle."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Which site is recommended for IM administration in newborns and infants?","options":["Vastus lateralis","Rectus femoris","Deltoid","Gluteus minimus at Ventrogluteal site"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Vastus lateralis is recommended for IM in newborns and infants.  Why B/C/D wrong: Rectus femoris, deltoid, and gluteal sites are other IM options but vastus lateralis is the one tied to newborns/infants."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Which of the following are recommended sites for IM administration? (SATA)","options":["Vastus lateralis","Rectus femoris","Deltoid","Gluteus minimus at Ventrogluteal site","Anterolateral aspect of thigh"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The four recommended IM sites are vastus lateralis, rectus femoris, deltoid, and gluteus minimus at the ventrogluteal site.  Why E wrong: Anterolateral aspect of thigh is a subcutaneous (SC) administration site, not an IM site."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Why is it common practice to draw back on the syringe before administering an IM injection?","options":["To increase absorption rate","To check whether it is in a blood vessel","To reduce pain","To mix the medication"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Aspirating checks that the needle is not in a blood vessel.  Why A/C/D wrong: Aspiration does not affect absorption, pain reduction, or mixing."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"After administering an IM injection, what should the nurse do at the site?","options":["Vigorously massage for 5 minutes","Apply pressure to the site, but should not rub it","Apply heat for 30 minutes","Leave the needle in place for 1 minute"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After IM injection, apply pressure but do not rub the site.  Why A wrong: Vigorous massage can cause tissue irritation/leakage.  Why C/D wrong: Not indicated post-IM."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"The selection index for an IM injection depends on which of the following? (SATA)","options":["Amount of medication","Viscosity of medication","Age of patient / development of muscle tissue","Preference of patient and nurse","Ability of patient to assume position need for injection"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: All five listed (amount, viscosity, age/muscle development, patient preference, and ability to assume position) are insertion site selection determinants.  Why none wrong: All are valid per the source."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"The gluteus muscle has how many types?","options":["Two","Three (gluteus maximus, gluteus minimum, gluteus maximum)","Four","One"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Three gluteus types are listed: gluteus maximus, gluteus minimum, gluteus maximum.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source lists three."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Which gluteal landmark is recommended for IM injections?","options":["Gluteus maximus","Gluteus minimum (it is VG site, recommended for IM injections)","Gluteus maximum","Rectus femoris"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Gluteus minimum is the ventrogluteal (VG) site recommended for IM injections.  Why A/C wrong: Other gluteus types not specified as the recommended VG site.  Why D wrong: Rectus femoris is a thigh muscle, not gluteal."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Why is the ventrogluteal site recommended as a safe landmark for IM injections?","options":["It is closest to the skin surface","It is taken as a landmark for administering IM injections (safe site)","It absorbs medication fastest","It is painless"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Ventrogluteal is the landmark taken for administering IM injections (preferred safe site).  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the stated reasons."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"The dorsogluteal (DG) site is generally not recommended for IM injections because of proximity to what structures?","options":["Bone and tendon","The large blood vessels and sciatic nerve","Lymph nodes only","The bladder"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: DG is not recommended due to proximity to large blood vessels and the sciatic nerve.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the cited risk structures."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Subcutaneous (SC/Sub-Q) injection is also called what?","options":["Intradermal injection","Hypodermical injection (hypodermic injection)","Intramuscular injection","Intravenous injection"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: SC is also called hypodermic (hypodermical) injection.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are distinct routes."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Which medications are commonly given by the subcutaneous route?","options":["Antibiotics and antipyretics","Insulin and heparin injections","Chemotherapy and contrast","IV fluids"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: SC is used for insulin and heparin injections.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the cited SC examples."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"What is the recommended volume for a subcutaneous injection?","options":["1 to 5 mL","0.5 to 1 mL or as insulin and heparin","6 to 10 mL","5 mL fixed"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: SC volume is 0.5 to 1 mL (or as for insulin and heparin).  Why A wrong: 1–5 mL is IM.  Why C/D wrong: Too large for SC tissue."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"What angle is used for SC injection in a thin patient?","options":["90°","45° for thin patient and 90° for obese patient","15°","10°"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: SC angle is 45° for thin patients and 90° for obese patients.  Why A wrong: 90° alone applies to obese patients.  Why C/D wrong: These are intradermal angles."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Which syringe is used for SC injection?","options":["5 mL","1-3 mL","10 mL","50 mL"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: SC syringe is 1-3 mL.  Why A wrong: 5 mL is IM.  Why C/D wrong: Too large for SC."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"What needle size is recommended for a subcutaneous injection?","options":["18.5 G, 1 to 5\" needle","25-27 G to a 1/4 to 5/8\" inch long needle","30 G, 2 inch","14 G"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: SC needle is 25-27 G, 1/4 to 5/8\" long.  Why A wrong: That is IM needle specification.  Why C/D wrong: Not the cited SC needle gauges/lengths."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Where is the SC target site located?","options":["Into the muscle","Into subcutaneous tissue just below the skin","Into the dermis","Into a vein"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: SC target site is the subcutaneous tissue just below the skin.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are IM, intradermal, and IV sites respectively."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Which of the following are recommended sites for SC administration? (SATA)","options":["Antero-lateral aspect of thigh","Abdomen in umbilical region (most reliable, fast absorption)","Sub-scapular portion of back","Upper outer area","Ventrogluteal site"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The four SC sites are antero-lateral thigh, abdomen (umbilical region — most reliable, fast absorption), sub-scapular back, and upper outer area.  Why E wrong: Ventrogluteal is an IM site, not an SC site."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Which SC site is described as most reliable with fast absorption?","options":["Antero-lateral thigh","Abdomen in the umbilical region","Sub-scapular back","Upper outer arm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The abdomen (umbilical region) is most reliable with fast absorption.  Why A/C/D wrong: Other valid SC sites, but not the one described as most reliable/fast absorption."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"An Adverse Drug Reaction (ADR) classified as \"dose-related\" is also termed what?","options":["Bizarre","Augmented","Chronic","Delayed"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Dose-related ADR = Augmented.  Why A wrong: Bizarre is non-dose-related.  Why C wrong: Chronic is dose- and time-related.  Why D wrong: Delayed is time-related."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Match: A non-dose-related ADR is also called what?","options":["Augmented","Bizarre","Chronic","Failure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Non-dose-related ADR = Bizarre.  Why A/C/D wrong: Augmented is dose-related; Chronic is dose-and-time-related; Failure is failure of therapy."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"An ADR that is both dose-related and time-related is classified as which type?","options":["Augmented","Bizarre","Chronic","Delayed"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Dose-related and time-related ADR = Chronic.  Why A/B/D wrong: Augmented (dose), Bizarre (non-dose), Delayed (time-related)."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"A time-related ADR is classified as which type?","options":["Augmented","Delayed","Bizarre","Failure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Time-related ADR = Delayed.  Why A/C/D wrong: Augmented (dose), Bizarre (non-dose), Failure (failure of therapy)."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"A withdrawal-related ADR is classified as which type?","options":["End of use","Failure","Augmented","Bizarre"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Withdrawal-related ADR = End of use.  Why B/C/D wrong: Failure is failure-of-therapy; the others are dose/non-dose related."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"A failure-of-therapy-related ADR is classified as which type?","options":["Augmented","Bizarre","Failure","End of use"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Failure of therapy related = Failure.  Why A/B/D wrong: These describe other ADR categories."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"According to the injection-site diagram, which angle corresponds to the subcutaneous route?","options":["90°","45°","25°","10–15°"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The figure shows subcutaneous at 45°.  Why A wrong: 90° is intramuscular.  Why C wrong: 25° is intravenous.  Why D wrong: 10–15° is intradermal."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Per the injection-site diagram, which route uses a 25° angle?","options":["Intramuscular","Subcutaneous","Intravenous","Intradermal"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The figure shows intravenous at 25°.  Why A/B/D wrong: IM 90°, SC 45°, intradermal 10–15°."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Per the injection-site diagram, which route uses a 10–15° angle?","options":["Intramuscular","Intradermal","Intravenous","Subcutaneous"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Intradermal is at a 10–15° angle.  Why A/C/D wrong: IM 90°, IV 25°, SC 45°."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"A Metered-Dose Inhaler (MDI) delivers what kind of dose?","options":["A variable random dose","An exact dose of medicine quickly","A continuous infusion","Only powder, no propellant"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: An MDI quickly delivers an exact dose of medicine.  Why A/C wrong: It delivers exact, not variable/continuous, doses.  Why D wrong: That describes a dry powder inhaler."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Before using an MDI, what is the first step regarding the mouthpiece and device?","options":["Soak it in warm water","Remove the mouthpiece cover and shake inhaler well","Exhale into it","Wash with soap"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Remove the mouthpiece cover and shake the inhaler well first.  Why A wrong: Soaking is for cleaning a spacer, not a pre-use step.  Why C wrong: Exhaling comes later, away from the mouthpiece.  Why D wrong: Not the instructed step."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"If a spacer is used with an MDI, where is it attached?","options":["To the patient's nose","To the back of the mouthpiece","To the front of the mouthpiece (attach the MDI to the spacer)","It is not attached"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: With a spacer, attach the MDI to the spacer's mouthpiece (front).  Why A/B/D wrong: Not the described attachment."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"When inhaling with an MDI, the patient takes a deep breath, exhales, holds the inhaler 1 to 2 inches away from the mouth, then does what?","options":["Coughs forcefully","Inhales slowly and deeply while depressing the medication canister and continues to inhale for a full breath","Holds the breath for 30 minutes","Immediately removes the inhaler"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The patient inhales slowly and deeply while depressing the canister and continues to inhale for a full breath.  Why A/C/D wrong: Contradict the slow, deep, sustained inhalation technique."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"After depressing the MDI canister, the patient should hold the breath for how long?","options":["1 to 2 seconds","5 to 10 seconds, or as long as possible","30 to 60 seconds","Do not hold breath"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Instruct the patient to hold the breath for 5 to 10 seconds (or as long as possible).  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the cited duration."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"How long should the patient wait before administering the next puff of an MDI?","options":["30 minutes","1 to 2 minutes (or as prescribed)","1 hour","No waiting needed"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Wait 1 to 2 minutes, as prescribed, before the next puff.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the cited interval."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"After using an MDI, the patient should do what for oral care?","options":["Brush teeth with the inhaler","Rinse the mouth with water after using an MDI","Avoid drinking water for 1 hour","Apply ointment to lips"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Have the patient gargle/rinse the mouth with water after using an MDI.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the cited oral-care instruction."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"How often should the spacer and inhaler be cleaned?","options":["Once a year","At least weekly with warm water or soaked in vinegar solution for 20 minutes","Never","Only when broken"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The spacer and inhaler should be cleaned at least weekly with warm water or soaked in a vinegar solution for 20 minutes.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the cited cleaning frequency/method."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Nebuliser therapy is a process of dispersing a liquid medication into what form?","options":["Solid pellets","Microscopic particles delivered to the lungs as the patient inhales","A topical gel","An IV solution"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Nebuliser therapy disperses liquid medication into microscopic particles delivered to the lungs through the nebuliser as the patient inhales.  Why A/C/D wrong: Nebulisers produce a fine aerosol mist, not solids, gels, or IV solutions."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Nebuliser therapy can take at least how long to release one or more doses?","options":["A few seconds","5 to 10 minutes or more to deliver the medicine","1 hour","30 seconds"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Nebuliser therapy can take at least 5 to 10 minutes or more to deliver medication.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the cited duration."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Nebulisers may be used for people with which conditions? (SATA)","options":["Lung disease including asthma","COPD","Cystic fibrosis","Bronchiectasis","A simple headache"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Nebulisers are used for lung disease including asthma, COPD, cystic fibrosis, and bronchiectasis.  Why E wrong: Headache is not a respiratory indication for nebuliser therapy."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Which of the following are purposes of nebuliser therapy? (SATA)","options":["To administer medication directly into the respiratory tract","To liquefy and aid in expulsion of secretions","To reduce the difficulty of bringing out the secretions","To increase vital capacity","To deliver medication into the bloodstream directly"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The four cited purposes are: administer medication directly into the respiratory tract; liquefy and aid expulsion of secretions; reduce difficulty bringing out secretions; increase vital capacity.  Why E wrong: Nebulisers deliver to the airways, not the bloodstream directly."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"When two inhaled medications are prescribed and one is a glucocorticoid or corticosteroid, which should be administered first?","options":["The corticosteroid first","The bronchodilators first, then wait 5 minutes before administering the corticosteroid","Both at the same time","The corticosteroid after waiting 1 hour"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Per the note, administer the bronchodilators first, then wait 5 minutes before administering the corticosteroid (second).  Why A/C/D wrong: Bronchodilators open the airways first to improve corticosteroid deposition."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Ribavirin is a medication administered via the aerosol route for the treatment of which condition in children?","options":["Bacterial pneumonia","Respiratory syncytial virus (RSV)","Tuberculosis","Asthma"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Per NORCET 5 Mains 2023, ribavirin is administered by the aerosol route to treat respiratory syncytial virus (RSV) in children.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the cited indication for aerosolized ribavirin."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"A Dry Powder Inhaler (DPI) delivers medication in what form?","options":["A liquid mist","Dry powder medicines that are free-flowing powdered formulations","A propellant-driven aerosol","An oily suspension"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: DPIs deliver dry powder — free-flowing powdered formulations of the drug.  Why A/C wrong: That describes nebulisers/MDIs.  Why D wrong: DPIs are powder, not oily."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"A DPI formulation is prepared by which method(s)?","options":["Dissolving the drug in saline","Either spray-drying or micronization of a blend consisting of the drug and a suitable inert carrier","Emulsification with oil","Freezing the drug"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: DPI powders are prepared by spray-drying or micronization of a blend of drug and a suitable inert carrier.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the cited preparation methods."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"During incentive spirometry, in which position should the patient be assisted to sit?","options":["Supine flat","Upright or semi-Fowler's position, if possible","Prone","Trendelenburg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Assist the patient to an upright or semi-Fowler's position if possible.  Why A/C/D wrong: These positions do not optimize lung expansion for spirometry."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"What should the nurse do regarding pain before incentive spirometry, especially for a recent abdominal/chest surgery patient?","options":["Withhold all analgesia","Assess for pain and administer pain medication as prescribed, if needed","Apply ice to the spirometer","Postpone spirometry for 24 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Assess for pain and administer pain medication as prescribed if needed, since recent abdominal/chest incision causes splinting.  Why A/C/D wrong: Withholding analgesia, icing, or arbitrarily postponing are not the cited interventions."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"During incentive spirometry, how should the patient hold the device and place lips?","options":["Hold loosely and place lips outside the mouthpiece","Hold with one hand (and the mouthpiece with the other), instruct to exhale normally, then place lips securely around the mouthpiece","Hold upside down","Place mouthpiece against the nose"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Instruct the patient to steady the device with one hand and hold the mouthpiece with the other, exhale normally, then place the lips securely around the mouthpiece.  Why A/C/D wrong: A loose or improper seal/orientation prevents effective inhalation."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"During the incentive spirometry inhalation, the patient should breathe in through the nose or mouth, and what happens to the nose if necessary?","options":["The nose is taped shut","Use a nose clip if necessary; instruct to inhale slowly and deeply as possible through the mouthpiece without using the nose","The patient must always breathe through the nose","The nose is irrigated"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A nose clip is used if necessary; the patient inhales slowly and as deeply as possible through the mouthpiece without using the nose.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the cited technique."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"During incentive spirometry, after inhaling, the patient should hold the breath for how long and then do what?","options":["1 second, then cough hard","Hold the breath for about 5 seconds, then exhale slowly","30 seconds, then hold again","Exhale immediately without holding"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Instruct the patient to hold the breath for about 5 seconds, then exhale slowly.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the cited breath-hold/exhalation steps."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"To remove respiratory secretions during/after incentive spirometry, the patient should be encouraged to do what?","options":["Suppress coughing","Cough during and after each effort if secretions are present","Take an antitussive","Hold the breath continuously"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Instruct the patient to cough during and after each effort if secretions are present.  Why A/C/D wrong: Suppressing the cough would retain secretions."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"How often should the patient be encouraged to complete breathing exercises with incentive spirometry?","options":["Once a day","About 5 to 10 times every 1 to 2 hours, if possible, with rest in between","Only when short of breath","Continuously for one hour"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Encourage breathing exercises about 5 to 10 times every 1 to 2 hours if possible, with rest in between.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the cited frequency."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"What is the maximum recommended IM injectable volume at the ventrogluteal (recommended) site in adults?","options":["1 mL","5 mL","4 mL","10 mL"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists the ventrogluteal (recommended) site maximum volume as 5 mL.  Why A wrong: 1 mL is the vastus lateralis/newborn value.  Why C wrong: 4 mL is the dorsogluteal (not recommended) value.  Why D wrong: Not listed."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Per the maximum IM injectable volume table, the vastus lateralis (recommended) site allows what maximum volume in adults?","options":["1 mL","5 mL","4 mL","2 mL"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Vastus lateralis (recommended) maximum is 5 mL.  Why A wrong: 1 mL is for vastus lateralis in newborn.  Why C wrong: 4 mL is dorsogluteal (not recommended).  Why D wrong: Not the listed value."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"What is the maximum IM injectable volume in the vastus lateralis of a newborn?","options":["5 mL","1 mL","4 mL","2 mL"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Vastus lateralis in newborn maximum volume is 1 mL.  Why A wrong: 5 mL is the adult vastus lateralis/ventrogluteal value.  Why C wrong: 4 mL is dorsogluteal.  Why D wrong: Not listed."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Per the maximum IM injectable volume table, the deltoid site allows what maximum volume?","options":["5 mL","1 mL","4 mL","0.5 mL"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Deltoid maximum volume is 1 mL.  Why A wrong: 5 mL is the larger vastus lateralis/ventrogluteal sites.  Why C wrong: 4 mL is dorsogluteal.  Why D wrong: Not listed."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"What is the maximum IM injectable volume at the rectus femoris site?","options":["1 mL","5 mL","4 mL","2 mL"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Rectus femoris maximum volume is 5 mL.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the listed value for rectus femoris."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Per the table, what is the maximum IM injectable volume at the dorsogluteal (not recommended) site?","options":["5 mL","1 mL","4 mL","2 mL"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Dorsogluteal (not recommended) maximum volume is 4 mL.  Why A/B/D wrong: Not the listed dorsogluteal value."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"What is the primary purpose of the Z-track technique?","options":["To speed up absorption","It is used when giving irritating drugs into muscles like iron and Vita-D","To reduce the needle size needed","To deliver drugs intravenously"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Z-track is used when giving irritating drugs into muscles, such as iron and Vita-D.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the stated purpose."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"The Z-track technique is primarily used in which client population?","options":["Neonates only","Mainly elderly clients who have decreased muscle mass","Pregnant women only","Athletes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Z-track is primarily used in elderly clients who have decreased muscle mass.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the cited population."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Which of the following are purposes of the Z-track technique? (SATA)","options":["To seal the drug into the muscles","To prevent backward leakage and leakage into the surrounding subcutaneous tissue","To decrease the chance of local tissue irritation","To speed systemic absorption into the bloodstream","To allow IV administration"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The three cited Z-track purposes are: seal the drug into the muscles; prevent backward leakage/leakage into surrounding SC tissue; decrease the chance of local tissue irritation.  Why D/E wrong: These are not cited Z-track purposes."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Before giving a Z-track injection, the skin is cleaned in a circular motion from center to outward to approximately what diameter?","options":["1 cm","5 cm","10 cm","2 cm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Clean the skin in a circular motion from center to outward, approximately 5 cm in diameter.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the cited diameter."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Before injecting in the Z-track method, by how far should the skin be pulled to the side?","options":["1 cm","5-10 cm","20 cm","Not pulled at all"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Aspirate the syringe for 5-10 cm before injecting the drug (skin pulled to the side).  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the cited displacement."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"In the Z-track technique, how long should the nurse wait after injecting before withdrawing the needle?","options":["Immediately withdraw","About 10 seconds before withdrawing the needle and discarding while preparing a new injection with a new syringe and needle","1 minute","5 minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If blood appears into the syringe, withdraw the needle and discard; otherwise wait about 10 seconds before withdrawing, then prepare a new injection with a new syringe and needle.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the cited wait time."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"What angle is used to pierce the skin in the Z-track technique?","options":["45°","90° (then aspirate; if resistance occurs, pause and depress the plunger slowly)","15°","25°"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pierce the skin at a 90° angle; aspirate, and if resistance occurs, pause then depress the plunger slowly.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the cited Z-track angle."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Iron dextran (Imferon) is a parenteral iron preparation. By which technique should the nurse administer it?","options":["Standard IM without traction","Z-track method (in the abdomen)","Subcutaneous","Intradermal"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Per the recall box, iron dextran (Imferon) should be given by the Z-track method.  Why A wrong: A standard IM technique would allow irritating staining/leakage.  Why C/D wrong: It is given IM by Z-track, not SC/intradermal."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"When administering iron dextran by Z-track, in which body region/location is it given?","options":["In the deltoid","In the abdomen","In the upper arm","In the forearm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Iron dextran is given in the abdomen (Z-track method).  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the cited location for this preparation."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"The Z-track method requires the use of how many needles?","options":["One needle for the entire procedure","Two needles (the Z-track method requires the use of 2 needles)","Three needles","No needle"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The recall box states the Z-track method requires the use of 2 needles.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the cited number (one needle to draw, another to inject prevents irritating drug tracking through tissue)."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Why is the Z-track method used for an irritating injectable medication?","options":["To track the drug","To prevent backward leakage of the medication into the subcutaneous tissue surrounding the muscles","To pull the lateral skin side","To prevent backflow"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Z-track technique is used when injecting irritating drugs to prevent backward leakage and leakage of the medication into the subcutaneous tissue surrounding the muscles.  Why A wrong: It prevents tracking, not promotes it.  Why C wrong: Pulling the skin is a step, not the goal.  Why D wrong: Preventing backflow is incomplete; the aim is preventing SC tissue leakage/staining."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Which route of drug administration is described as safe, self-administered, economical, with no needle required?","options":["Intravenous (IV)","Oral (PO)","Intramuscular (IM)","Epidural"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Oral (PO) advantages: safe, self-administered, economical, no needle required.  Why A/C/D wrong: These require needles/equipment and are not self-administered economically."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Which is a disadvantage of the oral (PO) route of drug administration?","options":["Painful injection site","Slow and highly variable absorption subject to first-pass metabolism, with less predictable blood levels","Requires sterile equipment","Risk of overdose"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Oral disadvantages: slow and highly variable absorption subject to first-pass metabolism and less predictable blood levels.  Why A/C/D wrong: These relate to parenteral routes, not oral."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Which route provides the most rapid, fast-onset, most accurate (precise blood level) drug action?","options":["Oral (PO)","Intravenous (IV)","Subcutaneous (SC)","Topical"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: IV advantage is most rapid, fast onset, most accurate blood level.  Why A/C/D wrong: These have slower/variable absorption."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Which of the following are disadvantages of the intravenous (IV) route? (SATA)","options":["Overdose danger","Cannot be readily reversed","Requires sterile needles and medical technique","Slow onset","No needle required"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: IV disadvantages: overdose danger, cannot be readily reversed, requires sterile needles and medical technique.  Why D wrong: IV has fast onset, not slow.  Why E wrong: IV does require a needle."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Which is an advantage of the intramuscular (IM) route?","options":["No needle required","Most rapid absorption","Slow and prolonged absorption","Bypasses the blood-brain barrier"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: IM advantage is most rapid absorption (relative to oral/SC).  Why A wrong: IM requires a needle.  Why C wrong: That describes SC.  Why D wrong: That describes epidural."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Which is a disadvantage of the intramuscular (IM) route?","options":["First-pass metabolism","Localized irritation at the site of injection needs sterile equipment and procedure","No predictable absorption","Bypasses absorption entirely"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: IM disadvantage: localized irritation at the site of injection; needs sterile equipment and procedure.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the cited IM disadvantages."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Which route is described as having slow and prolonged absorption?","options":["Intravenous (IV)","Subcutaneous (SC)","Inhalation","Epidural"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: SC advantage is slow and prolonged absorption.  Why A wrong: IV is rapid.  Why C/D wrong: Different absorption profiles."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Which is a disadvantage of the subcutaneous (SC) route?","options":["First-pass metabolism","Variable absorption based on blood flow","Overdose danger and rapid reversal needed","Requires anesthesiologist"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: SC disadvantage is variable absorption based on blood flow.  Why A/C/D wrong: These relate to other routes."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Which is an advantage of the inhalation route of drug administration?","options":["No needle required only","Large absorption surface, very rapid absorption, no injection equipment needed","Slow prolonged absorption","Bypasses blood-brain barrier"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Inhalation advantages: large absorption surface, very rapid absorption, no injection equipment needed.  Why A wrong: Incomplete.  Why C wrong: It is rapid, not slow.  Why D wrong: That describes epidural."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Which is a disadvantage of the inhalation route?","options":["Variable absorption based on blood flow","Irritation of nasal passages, and small particles inhaled may damage lungs","First-pass metabolism","Requires sterile injection equipment"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Inhalation disadvantage: irritation of nasal passages and small particles inhaled may damage the lungs.  Why A/C/D wrong: These relate to other routes."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Which is an advantage of the topical route of drug administration?","options":["Most rapid absorption","Localized action; effects easy to self-administer","Bypasses blood-brain barrier","First-pass metabolism avoided in the gut"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Topical advantage: localized action; effects easy to self-administer.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the cited topical advantage."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Which is a disadvantage of the topical route?","options":["Overdose danger","May be absorbed into general circulation","Requires sterile needles","Bypasses the blood-brain barrier"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Topical disadvantage: may be absorbed into general circulation.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the cited topical disadvantage."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Which is an advantage of the trans-dermal route of drug administration?","options":["Most rapid absorption","Slow prolonged absorption, useful only for lipid-soluble drugs","Bypasses the blood-brain barrier","No absorption"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Trans-dermal advantage: slow and prolonged absorption, useful only for lipid-soluble drugs.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the cited trans-dermal advantage."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Which is a disadvantage of the trans-dermal route?","options":["First-pass metabolism","Local irritation; some absorption variability","Overdose danger and cannot be reversed","Requires anesthesiologist"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Trans-dermal disadvantage: local irritation; variable absorption.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the cited trans-dermal disadvantage."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Which route bypasses the blood-brain barrier for a very rapid effect on the CNS?","options":["Oral (PO)","Epidural","Inhalation","Topical"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Epidural advantage: bypasses the blood-brain barrier, with a very rapid effect on the CNS.  Why A/C/D wrong: Do not bypass the blood-brain barrier for CNS action."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Which is a disadvantage of the epidural route?","options":["First-pass metabolism","Not reversible; needs trained anesthesiologist; possible nerve damage","Slow absorption only","Painless and risk-free"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Epidural disadvantage: not reversible; needs a trained anesthesiologist; possible nerve damage.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the cited epidural disadvantages."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Medication administration is described as which kind of function for the nurse?","options":["An independent function","A dependent function that requires a doctor's legally written order (medication order)","A purely clerical function","An optional function"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Medication administration is a dependent function requiring a doctor's legally written order before any kind of medication is given.  Why A/C/D wrong: It is dependent on a prescription, not independent/optional/clerical."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Before giving a medication, what assessment must the nurse perform regarding the order?","options":["Assume the prescription is correct","Assessment should be done before administering medications (ensure the prescription was ordered or not)","Skip assessment if the patient looks well","Only check the medication colour"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Assessment should be done before administering medications, ensuring the prescription was ordered.  Why A/C/D wrong: Assessment cannot be skipped or assumed."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"When identifying the client before medication, which methods should the nurse use? (SATA)","options":["Identification bracelet","Scan a bar code","Use cues that identify such as client's name, birth date, hospital number","Ask the patient's roommate","Guess based on bed position"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Identifying the client is critical; usually via identification bracelet or bar code, or using cues such as the client's name, birth date, and hospital number (these reduce incidence of medication error).  Why D/E wrong: A roommate or bed position are unreliable identifiers."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"\"Identify right medicine\" means the nurse should do what before giving medication?","options":["Give whatever is available","Make sure the medicine is going right (correct) according to the doctor's order, and assess for allergic history of the client before administering","Ask the patient to choose","Skip the order"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Identify the right medicine — ensure it matches the doctor's order, and assess allergic history before administering.  Why A/C/D wrong: Contradict safe medication identification."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Before giving a medication, the nurse should determine whether the client is taking which additional products that might interact?","options":["Only IV fluids","OTC medication, herbal products, or alcohol","Only prescription drugs","Food only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Determine if the client is taking OTC medication, herbal products, or alcohol that may interact with prescribed medication.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies OTC, herbal products, and alcohol."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Which of the following are nursing responsibilities before giving prescribed medication? (SATA)","options":["Ensure ability to obtain and afford prescribed medication","Be sure about drug calculation, common symbols, and abbreviations used in medical departments such as IV, IM, BD, OD, TDS, etc.","Teach client about therapeutic effects and side/adverse effects of the medicine","Give all drugs at once regardless of order","Withhold all client teaching"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The nurse must ensure the client can obtain/afford medication, know drug calculations and common symbols/abbreviations (IV, IM, BD, OD, TDS), and teach therapeutic and side/adverse effects.  Why D/E wrong: These violate safe administration and patient teaching duties."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Diuretics should be given at what time to prevent sleep disturbance?","options":["At bedtime","In the morning / not at bedtime (to prevent sleep disturbance)","At midnight","Only with food"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Diuretics should not be given at bedtime to prevent sleep disturbance (giving them earlier prevents nocturia).  Why A wrong: Bedtime causes nocturia and disturbed sleep.  Why C/D wrong: Not the cited timing rationale."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Sedatives should be given at what time, and why?","options":["In the morning, to wake the patient","At bedtime to promote sleep and comfort of the client; instruct the client not to drive any vehicle after taking the sedatives or hypnotics","Before meals","Anytime regardless of activity"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Sedatives should be given at bedtime to promote sleep and comfort; instruct the client not to drive after taking sedatives or hypnotics.  Why A/C/D wrong: Contradict the timing and the driving-safety instruction."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Iron and iron injections can be given by which technique to prevent staining of tissues and backward leakage of the medicine?","options":["Standard IM","Z-track technique","Subcutaneous","Intradermal"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Iron injections should be given by the IM Z-track technique to prevent staining of tissues and backward leakage.  Why A wrong: Standard IM would allow staining/leakage.  Why C/D wrong: Not the cited technique for iron."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Blood pressure should be checked at which times when administering antihypertensive medicines?","options":["Only once a day","Before, during, and after administering the antihypertensive medicines","Only after a week","Never"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Blood pressure should be checked before, during, and after administration of antihypertensive medicines.  Why A/C/D wrong: Inadequate monitoring."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"After giving medication, when should it be recorded?","options":["At the end of the shift","In the nursing notes (recorded immediately/in the running notes)","Only if the patient asks","The next day"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After administration, the medication should be recorded in the running/nursing notes.  Why A/C/D wrong: Delayed or conditional recording is unsafe documentation."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"When giving a medication that stimulates appetite, when should the drug be given?","options":["After meals","Before meals","At bedtime","With water only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Drugs that stimulate appetite should be given before meals.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the cited timing."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"A drug that is irritating to the gastric mucosa should be given how?","options":["On an empty stomach","With food","At bedtime only","With the cough syrup"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A drug irritating to the gastric mucosa should be given with food to protect the stomach.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the cited measure."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"When giving fluid to a client after giving a medication, what should the nurse always provide?","options":["Hot water","A drink of fresh water","Carbonated soda","Tea"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Always give a drink of fresh water to the client when giving the medicine.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the cited fluid."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"What should the nurse instruct regarding drinking water after a cough syrup?","options":["Drink plenty of water immediately","Never give water after taking the cough syrup","Drink hot water only","Gargle with water"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Never give water immediately after taking cough syrup (so the soothing coating remains on the throat).  Why A/C/D wrong: Drinking/gargling water washes away the syrup's coating action."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Per the WHO Must-Know note, before an injection for skin preparation, the alcohol/isopropyl swab should be applied and the skin allowed to dry for how long?","options":["5 to 10 seconds","30 seconds (apply with a saturated isopropyl alcohol swab for 30 seconds and allow the area to dry for 30 seconds)","5 minutes","No drying needed"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: WHO suggests the standard practice for skin preparation: apply the isopropyl alcohol swab for 30 seconds and allow the area to dry for 30 seconds.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the cited durations."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"According to the Needle Hub Color Code (ISO 6009 standard), a 23G needle hub is which color?","options":["Cream","Blue","Orange","Grey"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Per the color-code chart, 23G corresponds to blue.  Why A wrong: Cream is 19G.  Why C wrong: Orange is 25G.  Why D wrong: Grey is 27G."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Per the needle hub color code chart, the 19G needle hub is which color?","options":["Blue","Cream","Green","Yellow"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 19G corresponds to cream.  Why A wrong: Blue is 23G.  Why C/D wrong: Not the cited colors for 19G."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"What is a STAT medication order?","options":["Administer once daily","Administer medication immediately","Administer when necessary (as needed)","Administer for a specified number of days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A STAT order means administer the medication immediately.  Why A wrong: Once daily is a one-time/standing concept, not STAT.  Why C wrong: That is a PRN order.  Why D wrong: That describes a standing order timeframe."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"What is a standing order?","options":["Administer immediately","Administer the order for a specified number of days till the next order comes or finishes","Administer the medication only once","Administer when necessary"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A standing order is administered for a specified number of days until the next order comes or finishes.  Why A wrong: That is STAT.  Why C wrong: That is a one-time order.  Why D wrong: That is PRN."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"What is a one-time order?","options":["Administer immediately repeatedly","Administer these medications only once","Administer when necessary","Administer for several days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A one-time order means administer the medication only once.  Why A/C/D wrong: These describe STAT, PRN, and standing orders."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"What is a PRN order?","options":["Administer immediately","Administer when necessary (as needed)","Administer once only","Do Not Resuscitate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A PRN order means administer when necessary (as needed).  Why A wrong: That is STAT.  Why C wrong: That is one-time.  Why D wrong: That is DNR."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"What does a DNR order mean?","options":["Do Not Resuscitate — written by a doctor when a patient is critical, and instruction to be followed to do so","Administer the drug now","Daily nursing record","Drug not required"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: DNR = Do Not Resuscitate; written by a doctor when the client is critical, with instructions to follow.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the meaning of DNR."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"In medication abbreviations, what does \"Aq\" stand for?","options":["Before meal","Water","After meal","At bed time"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Aq = Water.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are AC, PC, and HS respectively."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"What does the abbreviation \"AC\" mean in medication administration?","options":["After meal","Before meal","Each night","To night"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: AC = Before meal.  Why A wrong: After meal is PC.  Why C/D wrong: Refer to other abbreviations."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"What does the abbreviation \"PC\" mean?","options":["Before meal","After meal","Each night","Compound"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: PC = After meal.  Why A wrong: Before meal is AC.  Why C wrong: Each night is Om/On.  Why D wrong: Compound is Comp."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"What does the abbreviation \"AM\" stand for?","options":["After noon","Before noon","Mixture","Fluid"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: AM = Before noon.  Why A wrong: After noon is PM.  Why C wrong: Mixture is Mixt.  Why D wrong: Fluid is Fl."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"What does the abbreviation \"PM\" mean?","options":["Before noon","After noon","Each hour","At bed time"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: PM = After noon.  Why A wrong: Before noon is AM.  Why C/D wrong: Refer to other abbreviations."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"What does \"Alt die\" (Alt-die) mean in medication administration?","options":["Each day","Alternative day","To night","Once in a day"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Alt-die = Alternative day.  Why A wrong: Each day is Om.  Why C wrong: To night is HN.  Why D wrong: Once in a day is OD."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"What does the abbreviation \"Om\" stand for?","options":["Each night","Each morning","After meal","Ointment"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Om = Each morning.  Why A wrong: Each night is On.  Why C wrong: After meal is PC.  Why D wrong: Ointment is Oint."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"What does the abbreviation \"On\" mean?","options":["Each morning","Each night","At bed time","Once"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: On = Each night.  Why A wrong: Each morning is Om.  Why C wrong: At bed time is HS.  Why D wrong: Once is a different term."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"What does the abbreviation \"HN\" mean?","options":["At bed time","To night","Each hour","Half night"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: HN = To night.  Why A wrong: At bed time is HS.  Why C/D wrong: Not the cited meaning."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"What does the abbreviation \"HS\" mean?","options":["To night","At bed time","Each hour","Half strength"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: HS = At bed time.  Why A wrong: To night is HN.  Why C/D wrong: Not the cited meaning."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"What does \"SOS\" mean in medication administration?","options":["Twice a day","If necessary in need (give as needed once)","Save our souls","Every hour"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: SOS = if necessary, in need.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the cited meaning."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"What does \"Stat\" mean?","options":["Once a day","Immediately at once","Twice a day","Three times a day"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Stat = immediately at once.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are OD, BD, and TDS."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"What does the abbreviation \"OD\" stand for in dosing frequency?","options":["Twice a day","Once in a day","Three times a day","Four times a day"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: OD = Once in a day.  Why A wrong: Twice a day is BD.  Why C wrong: Three times a day is TDS.  Why D wrong: Four times a day is QID."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"What does the abbreviation \"BD\" mean?","options":["Once a day","Twice a day","Three times a day","Four times a day"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: BD = Twice a day.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are OD, TDS, and QID."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"What does the abbreviation \"TDS\" mean?","options":["Twice a day","Three times a day","Four times a day","Once a day"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: TDS = Three times a day.  Why A wrong: Twice a day is BD.  Why C wrong: Four times a day is QID.  Why D wrong: Once a day is OD."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"What does the abbreviation \"QID\" mean?","options":["Three times a day","Four times a day","Twice a day","Every other day"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: QID = Four times a day.  Why A wrong: Three times a day is TDS.  Why C wrong: Twice a day is BD.  Why D wrong: Every other day is QOD."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"What does the abbreviation \"QH\" mean?","options":["Every other day","Every hour","Once a day","Twice a day"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: QH = Every hour.  Why A wrong: Every other day is QOD.  Why C/D wrong: Refer to OD and BD."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"What does the abbreviation \"QOD\" mean?","options":["Every hour","Every other day","Four times a day","Once a day"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: QOD = Every other day.  Why A wrong: Every hour is QH.  Why C/D wrong: Refer to QID and OD."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Match the abbreviations: which set is correctly paired?","options":["Inj. = Injection, Cap = Capsule, Tab = Tablet, Liq = Liquid","Inj. = Ointment only","Cap = Liquid, Tab = Capsule","Liq = Tablet"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Inj. = Injection, Cap = Capsule, Tab = Tablet (TBSP/Tab in chart), Liq = Liquid.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are mismatched dosage forms."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"In the medication abbreviation chart, \"Lot\" stands for what?","options":["Liniment","Lotion","Liquid","Mixture"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Lot = Lotion.  Why A wrong: Liniment is Lin.  Why C wrong: Liquid is Liq.  Why D wrong: Mixture is Mixt."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"In the abbreviation chart, \"Sp\" stands for what?","options":["Spray","Spirit","Suppository","Solution"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Sp = Spirit.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the cited meaning."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"In the abbreviation chart, \"Rep\" stands for what?","options":["Replace","Repeat","Reduce","Report"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Rep = Repeat.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the cited meaning."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"In the abbreviation chart, \"Inf\" stands for what?","options":["Inflammation","Infusion","Information","Influenza"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Inf = Infusion.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the cited meaning."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"In the volume conversion table, 1 minim equals approximately how many drops?","options":["5-6 micro drops or 1 macro drop","15 drops","60 drops","1000 drops"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: 1 min = 15 m / 60 m macro drops = 5-6 micro drops (≈1 macro drop) per the table.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the listed conversion for 1 minim."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Per the volume conversion table, 1 mL equals how many minims?","options":["1 minim","15 minims (1 mL = 15 m or 1 table spoon)","60 minims","1000 minims"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 1 mL = 15 m (one tablespoon) per the table.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the listed conversion."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Per the volume conversion table, 1 ounce equals how many mL?","options":["5 mL","30 mL (1 ounce = 30 mL or 28.35 gram = 2 table spoons)","500 mL","1000 mL"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 1 ounce = 30 mL (≈28.35 g, 2 tablespoons).  Why A wrong: 5 mL is 1 teaspoon.  Why C/D wrong: These are pint/litre values."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Per the volume conversion table, 1 litre equals how many mL?","options":["100 mL","500 mL","1000 mL (1 litre = 1000 mL = 1 quart)","4 litres"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: 1 litre = 1000 mL = 1 quart.  Why A wrong: 100 mL is 1 decilitre.  Why B wrong: 500 mL is half a litre.  Why D wrong: 4 litres is 1 gallon."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Per the volume conversion table, 1 gallon equals how many litres?","options":["1 litre","4 litres","100 litres","1000 litres"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 1 gallon = 4 litres.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the listed conversion."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Per the volume conversion table, 1 decilitre equals how many mL?","options":["10 mL","100 mL (1 decilitre = 100 mL)","1000 mL","5 mL"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 1 decilitre = 100 mL.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the listed conversion."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Per the weight conversion table, 1 grain equals approximately how many mg?","options":["6 mg","60 mg (1 grain = 60 mg)","1000 mg","28.35 mg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 1 grain = 60 mg.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the listed grain conversion."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Per the weight conversion table, 1 gram equals how many mg?","options":["60 mg","1000 mg (1 gm = 1000 mg = 15-16 grain)","100 mg","28.35 mg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 1 gm = 1000 mg (≈15-16 grain).  Why A wrong: 60 mg is 1 grain.  Why C/D wrong: Not the listed gram conversion."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Per the weight conversion table, 1 ounce equals approximately how many gram?","options":["60 gram","28.35 gram (1 ounce = 28.35 gm or 30 gm)","1000 gram","100 gram"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 1 ounce = 28.35 gm (≈30 g).  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the listed ounce-to-gram conversion."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Per the weight conversion table, 1 mg equals approximately how many micrograms (μg)?","options":["100 μg","1000 μg (1 mg = 1000 mcg / μg)","60 μg","10 μg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 1 mg = 1000 mcg (μg).  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the listed conversion."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Per the Quick Revision Table for needle length, the subcutaneous (SubQ) needle length is what?","options":["1\" - 3\"","1/4\" - 5/8\"","3/8\" - 5/8\"","25-27 G"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: SubQ needle length is 1/4\" - 5/8\".  Why A wrong: 1\"-3\" is the IM length.  Why C wrong: 3/8\"-5/8\" is the ID (intradermal) length.  Why D wrong: That is a gauge, not length."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Per the Quick Revision Table, the IM needle length is what?","options":["1/4\" - 5/8\"","1\" - 3\"","3/8\" - 5/8\"","18-21 G"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: IM needle length is 1\" - 3\".  Why A wrong: That is SubQ length.  Why C wrong: That is ID length.  Why D wrong: That is a gauge."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Per the Quick Revision Table, the intradermal (ID) needle length is what?","options":["1/4\" - 5/8\"","1\" - 3\"","3/8\" - 5/8\"","90°"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: ID needle length is 3/8\" - 5/8\".  Why A wrong: That is SubQ.  Why B wrong: That is IM.  Why D wrong: That is an angle."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Per the Quick Revision Table, the SubQ needle gauge is what?","options":["18-21 G","25-27 G","25-26 G","45°"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: SubQ gauge is 25-27 G.  Why A wrong: That is the IM gauge.  Why C wrong: That is the ID gauge.  Why D wrong: That is an angle."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Per the Quick Revision Table, the IM needle gauge is what?","options":["25-27 G","18-21 G","25-26 G","90 G"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: IM gauge is 18-21 G.  Why A wrong: That is SubQ.  Why C wrong: That is ID.  Why D wrong: 90 is an angle, not a gauge."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Per the Quick Revision Table, the intradermal (ID) needle gauge is what?","options":["18-21 G","25-27 G","25-26 G","15 G"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: ID gauge is 25-26 G.  Why A wrong: That is IM.  Why B wrong: That is SubQ.  Why D wrong: Not listed."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Per the Quick Revision Table, the injection angle for SubQ is what?","options":["90°","45°","15°","25°"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: SubQ angle is 45°.  Why A wrong: 90° is IM.  Why C wrong: 15° is ID.  Why D wrong: 25° is not in this revision table."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Per the Quick Revision Table, the injection angle for IM is what?","options":["45°","90°","15°","10°"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: IM angle is 90°.  Why A wrong: 45° is SubQ.  Why C/D wrong: 15° is ID."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Per the Quick Revision Table, the injection angle for intradermal (ID) is what?","options":["90°","45°","15°","25°"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: ID angle is 15°.  Why A wrong: 90° is IM.  Why B wrong: 45° is SubQ.  Why D wrong: Not listed for ID."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Per the Quick Revision landmarks, which areas are used for SubQ injection? (SATA)","options":["Fatty areas","Abdomen","Back of arms","Thighs and sub-scapular portion","Vastus lateralis only"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: SubQ landmarks listed: fatty areas, abdomen, back of arms, thighs, and sub-scapular portion.  Why E wrong: Vastus lateralis is listed as an IM landmark, not the SubQ landmark group."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Per the Quick Revision landmarks, the IM landmarks include which sites? (SATA)","options":["Deltoid","Ventral gluteal","Vastus lateralis","Abdomen","Back of arms"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: IM landmarks listed: deltoid, ventral (ventrogluteal), and vastus lateralis.  Why D/E wrong: Abdomen and back of arms are SubQ landmarks, not IM."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Per the Quick Revision landmarks, intradermal (ID) injections are commonly given in which areas?","options":["Vastus lateralis and deltoid","Lightly pigmented, lesion-free, relatively hairless, commonly ventral forearms","Abdomen only","Gluteal region"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: ID landmarks: lightly pigmented, lesion-free, relatively hairless, commonly the ventral forearms.  Why A wrong: Those are IM landmarks.  Why C/D wrong: Abdomen/gluteal are SubQ/IM regions."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Per the Quick Revision recommended volume, what is the SubQ injectable volume?","options":["No more than 3 mL","1 mL or less","0.1 mL or less","5 mL"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: SubQ recommended volume is 1 mL or less.  Why A wrong: No more than 3 mL is IM (large person).  Why C wrong: 0.1 mL or less is ID.  Why D wrong: Not the listed SubQ volume."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Per the Quick Revision recommended volume, what is the IM volume for a large person?","options":["1 mL or less","No more than 3 mL","0.1 mL or less","1 mL (small adult/older children)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: IM volume for a large person is no more than 3 mL.  Why A wrong: That is SubQ.  Why C wrong: That is ID.  Why D wrong: 1 mL is the IM volume for small adults/older children, not large person."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Per the Quick Revision recommended volume, what is the IM volume for small adults and older children?","options":["No more than 3 mL","1 mL","0.1 mL or less","5 mL"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: IM volume for small adults/older children is 1 mL.  Why A wrong: 3 mL is for a large person.  Why C wrong: That is ID.  Why D wrong: Not listed."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Per the Quick Revision recommended volume, what is the intradermal (ID) injectable volume?","options":["1 mL or less","No more than 3 mL","0.1 mL or less","1 mL (infants/newborn)"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: ID recommended volume is 0.1 mL or less.  Why A wrong: That is SubQ.  Why B wrong: That is IM large person.  Why D wrong: 1 mL is the IM volume for infants/newborn, not the ID volume."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Per the Quick Revision Medication Route Onset table, what is the SubQ onset time?","options":["15-30 min","10-20 min","30-90 min","5 min"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: SubQ medication route onset is 15-30 min.  Why B wrong: That is the IM onset.  Why C wrong: That is the PO onset.  Why D wrong: Not listed."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Per the Quick Revision Medication Route Onset table, what is the IM onset time?","options":["15-30 min","10-20 min","30-90 min","Immediate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: IM medication route onset is 10-20 min.  Why A wrong: That is SubQ.  Why C wrong: That is PO.  Why D wrong: Not listed."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Per the Quick Revision Medication Route Onset table, what is the PO (oral) onset time?","options":["15-30 min","10-20 min","30-90 min","5-10 min"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: PO medication route onset is 30-90 min.  Why A wrong: That is SubQ.  Why B wrong: That is IM.  Why D wrong: Not listed."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"For an INFANT receiving an IM injection at the vastus lateralis, what needle and volume are recommended?","options":["Needle 5/8 inch, volume 0.5 mL","Needle 5/8-1 inch, volume 0.5-1 mL","Needle 1 inch, volume 1-1.5 mL","Not recommended"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For an infant, vastus lateralis uses a 5/8 inch needle and 0.5 mL volume.  Why B wrong: That is the toddler value.  Why C wrong: That is the child value.  Why D wrong: Vastus lateralis is recommended for infants."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"For a TODDLER receiving an IM injection at the vastus lateralis, what needle and volume are recommended?","options":["5/8 inch, 0.5 mL","5/8-1 inch, 0.5-1 mL","1 inch, 1-1.5 mL","1-1.5 inch, 1-3 mL"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For a toddler, vastus lateralis uses a 5/8-1 inch needle and 0.5-1 mL.  Why A wrong: That is the infant value.  Why C wrong: That is the child value.  Why D wrong: That is the adult value."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"For a CHILD receiving an IM injection at the vastus lateralis, what needle and volume are recommended?","options":["5/8 inch, 0.5 mL","5/8-1 inch, 0.5-1 mL","1 inch, 1-1.5 mL","1-1.5 inch, 1-3 mL"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: For a child, vastus lateralis uses a 1 inch needle and 1-1.5 mL.  Why A/B wrong: Those are infant/toddler values.  Why D wrong: That is the adult value."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"For an ADULT receiving an IM injection at the vastus lateralis, what needle and volume are recommended?","options":["5/8 inch, 0.5 mL","1 inch, 1-1.5 mL","1-1.5 inch, 1-3 mL","Not recommended"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: For an adult, vastus lateralis uses a 1-1.5 inch needle and 1-3 mL.  Why A/B wrong: Those are infant/child values.  Why D wrong: It is recommended in adults."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"At which life stage is the ventrogluteal IM site NOT recommended? (SATA)","options":["Infant","Toddler","Child","Adult","None — it is recommended at all ages"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The IM injections table marks ventrogluteal as \"not recommended\" for infant and toddler.  Why C/D wrong: For child it is 5/8-1 inch (1 mL) and for adult 1-1.5 inch (1-3 mL) — i.e., it is recommended.  Why E wrong: It is not recommended for infants and toddlers."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"For a CHILD receiving an IM injection at the ventrogluteal site, what needle and volume are recommended?","options":["Not recommended","5/8-1 inch, 1 mL","1-1.5 inch, 1-3 mL","5/8 inch, 0.5 mL"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For a child, ventrogluteal uses a 5/8-1 inch needle and 1 mL.  Why A wrong: Not recommended applies to infant/toddler.  Why C wrong: That is the adult value.  Why D wrong: Not the listed child ventrogluteal value."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"For an ADULT receiving an IM injection at the ventrogluteal site, what needle and volume are recommended?","options":["5/8-1 inch, 1 mL","1-1.5 inch, 1-3 mL","5/8 inch, 0.5 mL","Not recommended"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For an adult, ventrogluteal uses a 1-1.5 inch needle and 1-3 mL.  Why A wrong: That is the child value.  Why C wrong: That is an infant-type value.  Why D wrong: It is recommended in adults."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"For a TODDLER receiving an IM injection at the deltoid site, what needle and volume are recommended?","options":["Not recommended","5/8-1 inch, 1 mL","1 inch, 1-1.5 mL","1-1.5 inch, 1-3 mL"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For a toddler, deltoid uses a 5/8-1 inch needle and 1 mL.  Why A wrong: Deltoid is \"not recommended\" for infants, not toddlers.  Why C wrong: That is the child value.  Why D wrong: That is the adult value."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"At which life stage is the deltoid IM site marked \"not recommended\"?","options":["Infant","Toddler","Child","Adult"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The IM injections table marks deltoid as \"not recommended\" for the infant.  Why B wrong: Toddler is 5/8-1 inch, 1 mL.  Why C wrong: Child is 1 inch, 1-1.5 mL.  Why D wrong: Adult is 1-1.5 inch, 1-3 mL."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"For an ADULT receiving an IM injection at the deltoid site, what needle and volume are recommended?","options":["5/8-1 inch, 1 mL","1 inch, 1-1.5 mL","1-1.5 inch, 1-3 mL","Not recommended"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: For an adult, deltoid uses a 1-1.5 inch needle and 1-3 mL.  Why A wrong: That is the toddler value.  Why B wrong: That is the child value.  Why D wrong: It is recommended in adults."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"At which life stages is the dorsogluteal IM site marked \"not recommended\"? (SATA)","options":["Infant","Toddler","Child","Adult","Newborn only"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The IM injections table marks dorsogluteal as \"not recommended\" for infant and toddler.  Why C wrong: For child it is 5/8-1 inch, 1.5-2 mL (recommended).  Why D wrong: For adult it is 1-1.5 inch, 1-5 mL (recommended).  Why E wrong: It is listed for both infant and toddler, not newborn alone."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"For a CHILD receiving an IM injection at the dorsogluteal site, what needle and volume are recommended?","options":["Not recommended","5/8-1 inch, 1.5-2 mL","1-1.5 inch, 1-5 mL","5/8 inch, 0.5 mL"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For a child, dorsogluteal uses a 5/8-1 inch needle and 1.5-2 mL.  Why A wrong: Not recommended applies to infant/toddler.  Why C wrong: That is the adult value.  Why D wrong: Not the listed child dorsogluteal value."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"For an ADULT receiving an IM injection at the dorsogluteal site, what needle and volume are recommended?","options":["5/8-1 inch, 1.5-2 mL","1-1.5 inch, 1-5 mL","1 inch, 1-1.5 mL","Not recommended"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For an adult, dorsogluteal uses a 1-1.5 inch needle and 1-5 mL.  Why A wrong: That is the child value.  Why C wrong: That is a vastus lateralis child value.  Why D wrong: It is recommended (with cautions) in adults."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"When instilling eye medication, how far above the body should the dropper be held?","options":["5 cm","1 cm above the body part","10 cm","Touch directly to the eye"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hold the dropper 1 cm above the body part while doing eye instillation.  Why A/C wrong: Not the cited distance.  Why D wrong: The dropper must never touch the eye."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"During eye instillation, why must the nurse never touch the dropper to any part of the body?","options":["To save medication","To prevent contamination","To increase absorption","To reduce cost"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Never touch the dropper at any part of the body to prevent contamination (which could cause infection).  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the cited rationale."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"A patient on eye drops reports vertigo, dizziness, and nausea. The nurse recognizes this as related to which instruction during instillation?","options":["Touching the dropper to the eye","Eye drops are available in ointment and drops, and improper instillation may cause vertigo, dizziness, and nausea","Using too little medication","Not rinsing the mouth"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Eye drops are available in ointment and drops, and these effects (vertigo, dizziness, nausea) are linked per the source.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the cited cause."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"To minimize systemic absorption when instilling eye medication, the nurse should apply gentle pressure where?","options":["On the eyelid","Over the nasolacrimal duct (inner canthus) for 30-60 seconds","On the forehead","Over the eyebrow"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Instruct to apply gentle pressure over the nose or over the nasolacrimal duct for 30-60 seconds after medication instillation to minimize systemic absorption.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not occlude the nasolacrimal duct."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"If both an eye drop and eye ointment are scheduled at the same time, which should be administered first?","options":["The eye ointment first","The eye drop first (administer the eye drop first)","Both at the same time","Either order is acceptable"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If both are scheduled at the same time, administer the eye drop first (ointment would otherwise block drop absorption).  Why A/C/D wrong: Contradict the cited sequence."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"When separate eye medications are given to one client, what should be done to prevent cross contamination?","options":["Use the same dropper for all","Use separate medications for each client to prevent cross contamination","Mix them in one bottle","Share droppers between clients"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Use separate medications for each client to prevent cross contamination.  Why A/C/D wrong: These would spread contamination."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Where should prescribed eye drops be placed during instillation?","options":["On the cornea directly","In the lower conjunctival sac","On the eyelashes","At the inner canthus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Place the prescribed dose of eye drop medication in the lower conjunctival sac; never directly on the cornea.  Why A wrong: Direct corneal placement is harmful and not recommended.  Why C/D wrong: Not the cited placement."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"How should an eye ointment be applied to the lower conjunctival sac?","options":["As a single drop","Apply the ointment form as a ribbon of ointment along the lining of the lower conjunctival sac, from the inner to outer canthus","Directly onto the pupil","Rubbed into the eyelid"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Apply the ointment as a ribbon along the lining of the lower conjunctival sac, from the inner to the outer canthus.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the cited ointment application technique."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"After instilling eye medication, what should the nurse avoid touching with the dropper and applicator?","options":["The bottle cap","Any part of the eye with the dropper and applicator","The patient's hand","The chart"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Avoid touching any part of the eye with the dropper and applicator.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the cited contamination concern."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"When are glucocorticoid eye preparations administered relative to other eye medications?","options":["After other medications","Before other medications if prescribed","Mixed with other medications","Never with other medications"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Administer glucocorticoid preparations before other medications if prescribed.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the cited sequence."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"What should the nurse instruct the client to do if the eye vision is blurred after eye medication?","options":["Drive immediately","Avoid driving (instruct the client to avoid driving if vision is blurred)","Rub the eye vigorously","Apply more medication"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Instruct the client to avoid driving if vision is blurred.  Why A/C/D wrong: Unsafe or not indicated."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"Before administering eye/ear medication, what should the nurse read carefully regarding the product?","options":["The patient's diet chart","The client / product labels before drug administration","The pharmacy receipt","The visiting hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Instruct to read the client/product labels before drug administration.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the cited check."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"While doing ear (otic) instillation, what precaution must the nurse take with the dropper at the body part?","options":["Insert the dropper deep into the canal","Never touch the dropper at any part of the body part","Touch lightly to the tympanic membrane","Use the same dropper for both ears without cleaning"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: While doing ear instillation, never touch the dropper at any part of the body part (to prevent contamination).  Why A/C/D wrong: Unsafe or contaminating practices."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"A nurse must give iron dextran IM to an elderly client with reduced muscle mass and is concerned about skin staining. Applying knowledge from this unit, what is the BEST technique and rationale?","options":["Standard IM at 45° in the deltoid, to speed absorption","Z-track method at 90° with two needles, pulling skin 5-10 cm, to seal the irritating drug in the muscle and prevent backward leakage/staining of subcutaneous tissue","Subcutaneous at 45° in the abdomen, for prolonged absorption","Intradermal at 15° in the forearm, to test for allergy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Iron (irritating drug) is given by the IM Z-track method (90°, two needles, skin displaced) to seal the drug into muscle and prevent backward leakage/staining of SC tissue — exactly indicated in elderly with decreased muscle mass.  Why A wrong: Standard IM and 45° in deltoid would allow staining/leakage.  Why C wrong: Iron dextran is given IM by Z-track (often abdomen), not routine SC.  Why D wrong: Intradermal is for allergy testing/small volumes, not iron."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"A child with RSV bronchiolitis is prescribed aerosolized antiviral therapy plus a bronchodilator and an inhaled corticosteroid. Applying this unit, which actions are correct? (SATA)","options":["Administer ribavirin by the aerosol route for the RSV","Give the bronchodilator first, then wait 5 minutes before the corticosteroid","Rinse the child's mouth with water after the inhaled corticosteroid","Give the corticosteroid first, then the bronchodilator immediately","Position the child upright/semi-Fowler's for the breathing treatment"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: Ribavirin (aerosol) treats RSV in children; bronchodilator precedes corticosteroid by 5 minutes; mouth rinsing after corticosteroid prevents oral effects; upright/semi-Fowler's optimizes lung expansion.  Why D wrong: Order is reversed — bronchodilator must come first to open airways before the corticosteroid."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Parenteral routes (IM, SC, Z-track)…","question":"A nurse receives a STAT order for furosemide (a diuretic) and a HS order for a sedative. Applying this unit, which plan is correct?","options":["Give the diuretic immediately (STAT); give the sedative at bedtime (HS) and instruct the client not to drive afterward","Give the diuretic at bedtime and the sedative in the morning","Hold both until the next standing order","Give both immediately and skip client teaching"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: STAT = immediately; the diuretic is given now. HS = at bedtime; sedatives are given at bedtime to promote sleep, with instruction not to drive afterward.  Why B wrong: Diuretics should NOT be given at bedtime (sleep disturbance/nocturia); sedatives belong at bedtime.  Why C wrong: A STAT order cannot be held.  Why D wrong: Client teaching (no driving on sedatives) is required."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"A nurse is administering atropine sulphate eye drops to an infant. Why must the nurse auscultate bowel sounds in an infant or child receiving atropine sulphate eye drops?","options":["Atropine increases bowel motility causing diarrhea","Atropine drops may cause abdominal distension","Atropine stimulates gastric acid secretion","Atropine causes rectal bleeding"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states bowel sounds must be auscultated because atropine drops may cause abdominal distension.  Why A wrong: Atropine reduces, not increases, bowel motility.  Why C wrong: Not the stated concern.  Why D wrong: Rectal bleeding is not associated."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"A client receiving atropine eye drops reports that the medication needs to be administered again. What is the minimum interval the nurse should wait before giving the second eye medication?","options":["1 minute","5 minutes","10 minutes","15 minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If two medications are needed, the nurse must wait 5 minutes before administering the second eye medicine.  Why A/C/D wrong: The text specifies a 5-minute interval."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"Mydriatics are contraindicated in a client with which of the following conditions?","options":["Conjunctivitis","Glaucoma","Cataract","Dry eye"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Mydriatics are contraindicated in a client with glaucoma because of the risk of increased intraocular pressure.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the contraindication stated for mydriatics."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"A client is on pilocarpine therapy for glaucoma. Which medication should be available as an antidote?","options":["Atropine sulphate","Neostigmine","Adrenaline","Timolol"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: When a client is on pilocarpine therapy for glaucoma, atropine sulphate should be available as an antidote.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not identified as the antidote for pilocarpine in the text."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors decrease intraocular pressure by which mechanism?","options":["Increasing production of aqueous humour","Interfering with production of carbonic acid, decreasing production of aqueous fluid","Dilating the pupil to drain fluid","Constricting the trabecular meshwork"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors interfere with the production of carbonic acid; then carbonic acid will decrease production of aqueous fluid, therefore IOP will be decreased.  Why A wrong: It decreases, not increases, aqueous fluid.  Why C/D wrong: Not the stated mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"When administering ear drops to an adult or a child older than 3 years, how should the nurse position the pinna?","options":["Pull the pinna down and back","Pull the pinna up and back","Pull the pinna straight forward","Do not touch the pinna"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In an adult and children > 3 years, pull the pinna back and up to straighten the external canal to instill ear drops.  Why A wrong: Down and back is for children < 3 years.  Why C/D wrong: Not the technique described."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"After tilting the client's head, in which direction should the nurse apply the drops into the ear?","options":["The same side as the affected ear","The opposite direction of the affected ear","Directly onto the eardrum","Toward the outer ear opening"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Tilt the client's head to the opposite direction of the affected ear and apply the drops into the ear.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the described technique."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"When administering ear drops to an infant or a child younger than 3 years, the nurse should pull the pinna:","options":["Up and back","Down and back","Straight up","Forward and out"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In an infant or toddler < 3 years, pull the pinna down and back.  Why A wrong: Up and back is for >3 years/adults.  Why C/D wrong: Not the described technique."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"Carbimazole is an example of which class of medication?","options":["Eye medication","Antiemetic","Anti-thyroid medication","Antihypertensive"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The text gives Carbimazole as an example of anti-thyroid (anti-thyroidal) medication.  Why A/B/D wrong: Not the class stated."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"The instillation of eye drops is contraindicated in a client with which of the following?","options":["Cataract","Perforation of the eardrum","Conjunctivitis","Dry eye"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Eye instillation is contraindicated in a client with perforation of the eardrum (per source; instillation contraindicated when perforation is suspected).  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the stated contraindication."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"It is best to convert a larger unit to a smaller one (e.g., grams to milligrams) before performing dosage calculations primarily to:","options":["Make numbers larger and harder to read","Avoid working with fractions/decimals and minimize calculation error","Comply with legal labeling requirements","Reduce the prescribed dose"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source advises converting a larger unit to a smaller one (e.g., grams to milligrams) so calculation involves whole/simpler numbers.  Why A wrong: The goal is simpler, not harder, computation.  Why C/D wrong: Not the stated rationale."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"A medication order results in a calculated dose of more than 3 tablets. What is the nurse's best action?","options":["Administer all the tablets as calculated","Always recheck the calculation/answer because doses of more than 3 tablets are not common","Withhold the dose permanently","Crush all tablets together"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states one should always check for reasonableness — does the dosage make sense? Amounts less than half a tablet or more than 3 tablets are not common but still possible, prompting a recheck.  Why A wrong: Administering without verifying violates safety.  Why C wrong: Withholding permanently is not indicated; verify first.  Why D wrong: Not advised by the source."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"When solving a dosage problem for a drug supplied in tablets/capsules, what value is always 1 in the dosage formula?","options":["D (desired dose)","H (on hand)","Q (quantity)","V (volume)"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: For drugs in tablets or capsules, the quantity (Q) is always 1, i.e., 1 tablet or 1 capsule.  Why A/B/D wrong: These vary with the order and the drug supply."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"A needle has a sharp hollow tube that pierces the skin and enables medication to be injected into the body. Which part of the needle is held in the barrel before injection?","options":["Bevel","Shaft","Hub","Plunger"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The hub is held in the barrel before injection (it is the part that connects/seats the needle).  Why A wrong: The bevel is the slanted tip that pierces.  Why B wrong: The shaft is the long body of the needle.  Why D wrong: The plunger is part of the syringe used to draw/expel medication, not the needle."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"What is the function of the plunger in a syringe?","options":["To pierce the skin","To work together to help control the amount of medicine that you receive for an injection (draw and expel)","To store medication permanently","To connect the needle to the vial"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The plunger and piston work together to help control the amount of medicine received for an injection.  Why A wrong: The needle bevel pierces the skin.  Why C/D wrong: Not the plunger's function."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"Regarding safe needle disposal, what is the FIRST principle the nurse should follow?","options":["Recap the needle after use","Never recap needles after use — bend or break them","Discard needles in the regular trash","Wash and reuse the needle"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The safe needle disposal box states: never recap needles after use; just bend it and dispose in the container.  Why A wrong: Recapping is explicitly discouraged.  Why C wrong: Sharps must go in a puncture-proof container, not regular trash.  Why D wrong: Needles are disposable, not reusable."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"Used needles should be disposed of in which type of container, following which guidelines?","options":["Any open plastic bag","A puncture-proof white container, following BMW guidelines","The clinical waste bin without a lid","A glass jar in the supply room"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Used needles should be disposed in a puncture-proof white container, following BMW (Bio-Medical Waste) guidelines.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not consistent with safe sharps disposal."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"In drug dose calculation terminology, \"flow rate\" refers to:","options":["The amount of drug in a given volume","The volume per unit time, usually expressed as mL/hr","The number of drops per unit volume","The strength of the drug on hand"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Flow rate is the volume per unit time, usually expressed as mL/hr (can be mL/hr or mL/min).  Why A wrong: That describes concentration.  Why C wrong: That describes drop factor.  Why D wrong: That describes available dose/dose on hand."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"The \"drip rate\" in IV calculation is best defined as:","options":["The volume per unit time","The number of drops per unit time, expressed as mg/min, g/hr or iu/min","The total dose of a drug","The unit of measure of supply on hand"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Drip rate is the number of drops per unit time, given in expressions like mg/min, g/hr or iu/min.  Why A wrong: That is flow rate.  Why C wrong: That is dose.  Why D wrong: That is the available/supply term."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"In IV terminology, \"dose\" is defined as:","options":["The number of drops per minute","The amount of drug given at one time, expressed as mg/min, g/hr or iu/min","The amount of drug in a given volume","The drop factor of the tubing"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Dose is the amount of drug given at one time, expressed in mg/min, g/hr or iu/min.  Why A wrong: That is drip rate.  Why C wrong: That is concentration.  Why D wrong: That is drop factor."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"\"Concentration\" of a drug is defined as:","options":["The number of drops per minute","The amount of the drug in a given volume, expressed as mg/mL, g/mL or iu/mL","The volume per unit time","The number of drops per unit volume"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Concentration is the amount of the drug in a given volume (mg/mL, g/mL, or iu/mL).  Why A wrong: That is drip rate.  Why C wrong: That is flow rate.  Why D wrong: That is drop factor."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"\"Drop factor\" is defined as:","options":["Volume per unit time","The number of drops per unit volume, expressed as drops/mL or gtts/mL","The amount of drug given at one time","The desired dose ordered"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Drop factor is the number of drops per unit volume, expressed as drops/mL or gtts/mL.  Why A wrong: That is flow rate.  Why C wrong: That is dose.  Why D wrong: That refers to the desired (D) value."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"In IV terminology, \"titration\" refers to:","options":["The fixed dose given once","Adjustment of the IV medication dosage within prescribed parameters to achieve a desired effect","The total volume in the bag","The concentration of the additive"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Titration is the adjustment of the IV medication dosage within prescribed parameters to achieve a desired effect.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the definition of titration."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"In the standard dosage formula, the letter \"H\" represents:","options":["The desired dose ordered by the physician","The amount of drug available (on hand)","The hourly drip rate","The total volume of the IV bag"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: \"Available dose / on hand\" (H) denotes the amount of drug available in a specific dose.  Why A wrong: That is D (desired).  Why C/D wrong: Not what H stands for."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"In dosage calculations, the \"unknown dosage\" (X) refers to:","options":["The dose already on hand","The dosage you are trying to calculate (denote by X)","The drop factor of tubing","The concentration in the vial"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Unknown dosage is denoted by X — the dosage you are trying to calculate.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the definition of X."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"\"Unit of measure or quantity of unit\" in dosage calculation is:","options":["Denoted by D, the desired dose","Denoted by Q, the unit of measure for the specific dosage strength or supply on hand","The drip rate per minute","The patient's weight in kg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Unit of measure or quantity of unit is denoted by Q — the unit of measure for the specific dosage strength or supply on hand.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the definition of Q."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"When making calculations, all measurements should be:","options":["In different units to allow comparison","In the same units; if necessary, convert between dosages","Rounded to the nearest gram","Left in their original mixed units"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The tip states to make sure all measurements are in the same units; if necessary, convert between dosages.  Why A/C/D wrong: Contradict the stated rule."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"According to the tips for correct calculations, what should be the FIRST step in the calculating process?","options":["Determine the drop factor","Conversion (convert to the same/required units)","Identify the route of administration","Document the result"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states conversion is the first step in the calculating process.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not identified as the first step."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"Which formula correctly represents the basic dosage calculation?","options":["(D × H) / Q = Dose","(D / H) × Q (volume) = Dose, where D = desired dose and H = amount on hand","D × H × Q = Dose","(H / D) × Q = Dose"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Basic dosage formula is (D desired dose / H amount on hand) × Q volume = Dose.  Why A/C/D wrong: Misplace the variables relative to the source formula."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"Which formula is used to calculate an IV drip in mcg/minute?","options":["(mg/mL) ÷ (1 hour) = mcg/minute","(mg/mL) × (1000 mcg / 1 mg) × (mL/1 hour) × (1 hour / 60 min) = mcg/minute","(mL/hour) × drop factor = mcg/minute","D/H × Q = mcg/minute"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page shows IV drips in mcg/minute computed by multiplying mg/mL by the conversions (1000 mcg/1 mg) and (mL/1 hour) × (1 hour/60 min).  Why A/C/D wrong: Do not match the stated conversion chain."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"Which formula represents IV drips in units/hour?","options":["(D desired / H on hand) × V volume = units/hour","units/hour × drop factor = units/min","D × H × V = units/hour","H / D × V = units/hour"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: IV drips in units/hour = (D desired / H on hand) × V (volume) = units/hour (# mL × units/mL × dose).  Why B/C/D wrong: Do not match the stated formula."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"A solution is labeled 1000:25. According to the worked example, this corresponds to an infusion ratio of:","options":["4","40","25","100"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The calculation shows infusion ratio = 1000/25 = 40.  Why A wrong: 4 is a different value.  Why C/D wrong: Not the computed ratio."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"In the worked calculation, an infusion of 8 hours yields what value (R × 8)?","options":["32 hours","8 hours (R × 8 = 8 hours)","40 hours","25 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The example states R × 8 = 8 hours.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the value shown."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"A patient who weighs 50 kg requires a drug at 6 mcg/kg/min as a continuous IV infusion. What is the total dose required per minute?","options":["50 mcg/min","300 mcg/min (50 kg × 6 mcg = 300 mcg/min)","6 mcg/min","56 mcg/min"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Dose = weight × rate = 50 × 6 = 300 mcg/min.  Why A wrong: 50 is only the weight.  Why C wrong: 6 is the per-kg rate only.  Why D wrong: Incorrect arithmetic."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"The worked example computing 300 × 1 (units) ÷ 60 min equals which infusion value?","options":["5 units/min","5 mcg/min (300/60 = 5)","60 mcg/min","50 mcg/min"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 300 mcg ÷ 60 min = 5 mcg/min as shown.  Why A wrong: Wrong unit context.  Why C/D wrong: Incorrect arithmetic."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"A nurse is asked to convert 1 g of a drug to milligrams for a calculation. Which value is correct?","options":["1 g = 100 mg","1 g = 1000 mg","1 g = 10 mg","1 g = 10000 mg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The conversions on the page use 1 g = 1000 mg.  Why A/C/D wrong: Incorrect gram-to-milligram conversions."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"A patient must receive an infusion of 500 mL over 8 hours. Using mL/hr, the hourly flow rate is closest to:","options":["500 mL/hr","62.5 mL/hr (500 ÷ 8)","8 mL/hr","125 mL/hr"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Flow rate = total volume ÷ time = 500 ÷ 8 = 62.5 mL/hr (consistent with the worked infusion method on the page).  Why A wrong: That is the total volume.  Why C wrong: That is the time only.  Why D wrong: Incorrect division."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"In paediatric drug calculation, when a drug ordered for a child requires conversion such that the child's weight is used (e.g., child needs amoxicillin computed from weight), which step is emphasized in the practice question?","options":["Use the adult dose without adjustment","Calculate the child's dose based on the normal/recommended adult dose and the child's weight, then round to the nearest whole amount","Always double the adult dose","Use only the child's age, never the weight"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Clark's-formula practice question instructs to use the child's dose formula based on the adult dose and weight, then round the answer to the nearest whole value.  Why A/C/D wrong: Contradict the worked example method."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"Young's Formula for a paediatric dose is expressed as:","options":["Dose = [Age (years) / (Age + 12)] × Adult dose","Dose = [Age (months) / 150] × Adult dose","Dose = [Weight (lb) / 150] × Adult dose","Dose = [Age (years) / 20] × Adult dose"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Young's Formula = [Age (years) / (Age + 12)] × Adult dose; it is used for calculating doses for children less than 12 years of age (1–12 years).  Why B wrong: That is Fried's Formula.  Why C wrong: That is Clark's Formula.  Why D wrong: That is Dilling's Formula."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"Young's Formula is appropriate for children of which age range?","options":["Infants under 1 year","Children less than 12 years (1–12 years)","Children 13–18 years only","Neonates only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Young's Formula is used for calculating doses for children less than 12 years (1–12 years).  Why A wrong: Infants <1 year use Fried's.  Why C wrong: That older range uses Dilling's.  Why D wrong: Neonates are not the stated population."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"Fried's Formula calculates a paediatric dose as:","options":["[Age (months) / 150] × Adult dose","[Age (years) / (Age + 12)] × Adult dose","[Weight (lb) / 150] × Adult dose","[Age (years) / 20] × Adult dose"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Fried's Formula = [Age (months) / 150] × Adult dose, used for infants up to 1 year of age (also under 2 years).  Why B wrong: That is Young's.  Why C wrong: That is Clark's.  Why D wrong: That is Dilling's."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"Fried's Formula is used for which population?","options":["Adults","Infants till 1 year of age (under 2 years)","Adolescents 13–18 years","Children 5–12 years only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Fried's Formula is used for infants up to 1 year of age.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the population for Fried's."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"Clark's Formula determines a paediatric dose based on the child's:","options":["Age in years","Weight (in pounds) divided by 150 × Adult dose","Age in months","Body surface area only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Clark's Formula = [Weight (lb) / 150] × Adult dose; in Clark's formula a child's weight is used.  Why A wrong: Age in years is Young's/Dilling's.  Why C wrong: Age in months is Fried's.  Why D wrong: BSA is not the basis in this formula."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"Dilling's Formula calculates the paediatric dose using:","options":["[Age (years) / 20] × Adult dose","[Age (months) / 150] × Adult dose","[Weight (lb) / 150] × Adult dose","[Age (years) / (Age + 12)] × Adult dose"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Dilling's Formula = [Age (years) / 20] × Adult dose, used for calculating doses for children between 4 and 20 years of age.  Why B wrong: That is Fried's.  Why C wrong: That is Clark's.  Why D wrong: That is Young's."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"Dilling's Formula is applied for children of which age range?","options":["0–1 year","1–12 years","Between 4 and 20 years","Over 20 years"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Dilling's Formula is used for children between 4 and 20 years of age.  Why A/B/D wrong: Not the Dilling's range."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"For a 6 kg infant, calculate the maintenance hourly fluid requirement. Using the rule (100 mL/kg/day for the first 10 kg → hourly 4 mL/kg/hr), the hourly maintenance fluid is:","options":["6 mL/hr","24 mL/hr (4 mL × 6 = 24)","60 mL/hr","100 mL/hr"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hourly rate = 4 mL × 6 = 24 mL/hr; daily = 24 mL × 24 hr = 576 mL/day (6 × 100 = 600 mL/day).  Why A wrong: 6 is the weight only.  Why C/D wrong: Do not match the rule of 4 mL/kg/hr."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"For a 6 kg infant, the daily maintenance fluid requirement (24 mL/hr × 24 hr) is approximately:","options":["600 mL/day (≈576 mL by hourly method; 6 × 100 = 600)","100 mL/day","1200 mL/day","2400 mL/day"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Daily = 24 mL × 24 hr = 576 mL/day, consistent with 6 kg × 100 mL = 600 mL/day.  Why B/C/D wrong: Do not match the calculation for a 6 kg infant."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"For a 25 kg child, maintenance fluid is calculated using the rule: 100 mL/kg for first 10 kg + 50 mL/kg for next 10 kg + 20 mL/kg for each kg above 20. What is the total daily volume?","options":["1200 mL/day","1600 mL/day (1000 + 500 + 100)","2500 mL/day","500 mL/day"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: First 10 kg = 100 × 10 = 1000 mL; next 10 kg = 50 × 10 = 500 mL; remaining 5 kg = 20 × 5 = 100 mL; total = 1600 mL/day (hourly ≈ 65 mL/hr).  Why A/C/D wrong: Do not match the segmented calculation."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"For the 25 kg child requiring 1600 mL/day, the maintenance hourly rate is approximately:","options":["25 mL/hr","65 mL/hr (1560 ÷ 24)","100 mL/hr","160 mL/hr"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Daily rate ≈ 1560 mL ÷ 24 hr = 65 mL/hr as shown in the worked example.  Why A/C/D wrong: Do not match the hourly calculation."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"Using Fried's Formula, an 8-month-old infant requires a dose where the average adult dose is 250 mg. The calculated dose for the child is:","options":["250 mg","13.3 mg ([8/150] × 250)","150 mg","30 mg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Dose = [Age (months) / 150] × Adult dose = (8/150) × 250 = 13.3 mg.  Why A wrong: That is the full adult dose.  Why C wrong: 150 is the divisor, not the dose.  Why D wrong: Incorrect computation."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"Insulin is most commonly administered by which route?","options":["Intramuscular (IM)","Subcutaneous (SC)","Intravenous push only","Oral"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Most common route of insulin administration is subcutaneous (SC).  Why A/C wrong: Not the most common stated route.  Why D wrong: Oral insulin is broken down by digestive enzymes (see below)."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"Which of the following are common sites for subcutaneous insulin injection? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Lateral and posterior aspect of the upper arm","Abdomen","Anterior and lateral aspect of the thighs","Buttocks","Sole of the foot"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Common sites are lateral and posterior aspect of the upper arm, abdomen, anterior and lateral aspect of the thighs, and buttocks.  Why E wrong: The sole of the foot is not a listed SC insulin site."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"For better/faster absorption of insulin, which site is preferred?","options":["Thigh","Abdomen (umbilical region for best absorption)","Buttocks","Upper arm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For better absorption, the abdomen (umbilical region) is the best site.  Why A/C/D wrong: These sites absorb but are not noted as the best for absorption."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"Why should insulin not be given orally?","options":["It is too painful","Because insulin is made of peptides; if given orally, peptides are digested/broken down by digestive enzymes before reaching the bloodstream","It causes severe hyperglycemia","Oral insulin is illegal"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Insulin should not be given orally because it is made of peptides; the peptides are digested by digestive enzymes and intestine, breaking down insulin before it enters the bloodstream.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the stated reason."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"Which of the following is an example of a basal insulin?","options":["Regular insulin","Aspart insulin","Glulisine insulin","Degludec insulin"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The Recall the Concept box answer is D — Degludec insulin is a basal (long-acting/background) insulin.  Why A wrong: Regular insulin is short-acting (bolus).  Why B wrong: Aspart is rapid-acting (bolus).  Why C wrong: Glulisine is rapid-acting (bolus)."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"Basal insulin, also known as background insulin, serves which primary purpose?","options":["To cover carbohydrates at mealtime","To keep blood glucose levels at a consistent level during periods of fasting","To rapidly lower a spike after a meal","To provide energy during exercise only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Basal (background) insulin keeps blood glucose levels consistent during periods of fasting.  Why A/C wrong: That describes bolus/mealtime insulin.  Why D wrong: Not the stated purpose."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"When fasting, basal insulin affects the liver in which way?","options":["It stimulates the liver to release excess glucose","It releases glucose into the blood and controls/restricts how much glucose the liver allows into the blood for energy","It blocks all glucose production","It converts glucose to protein"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: When fasting, the body releases glucose into the blood and basal insulin controls/restricts how much the liver allows into the blood for the cells to use for energy.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the described mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"How is basal insulin usually administered, and what is its duration of effectiveness?","options":["Once or twice a day depending on the insulin; effective for 24 hours","Every 2 hours; effective for 6 hours","Only before each meal; effective for 4 hours","As a single weekly injection"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Basal insulin is usually taken once or twice a day depending on the insulin and is effective for 24 hours.  Why B/C/D wrong: Do not match the stated dosing/duration."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"Which of the following are examples of basal insulin? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Intermediate-acting: NPH, Lente","Long-acting: Glargine, detemir, degludec","Aspart","Lispro","Glulisine"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: Basal insulin examples are intermediate-acting (NPH, Lente) and long-acting (Glargine, detemir, degludec).  Why C/D/E wrong: Aspart, Lispro, and Glulisine are rapid-acting bolus insulins."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"Bolus insulin is best described as:","options":["Insulin to keep glucose steady during fasting","A dose given at mealtime so the body can deal with the spike in blood sugar after eating","An insulin effective for 24 hours","A weekly long-acting injection"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Bolus insulin is a dose given at mealtime so the body can deal with the spike in blood sugar that occurs at meal times (to keep blood glucose levels under control following a meal).  Why A/C/D wrong: These describe basal insulin properties."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"Bolus insulin is taken at which time and is effective for how long?","options":["Before bed; effective 24 hours","Before each meal; effective for 4 hours","Once weekly; effective 7 days","Every 6 hours; effective 12 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Bolus insulin needs to be taken before each meal and is effective for 4 hours.  Why A/C/D wrong: Do not match the stated timing/duration."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"Which of the following are examples of bolus insulin? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Short-acting: Human actrapid (Regular)","Rapid-acting: Glulisine, aspart, Lispro","NPH","Glargine","Detemir"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: Bolus insulin examples are short-acting (Human actrapid/Regular) and rapid-acting (Glulisine, aspart, Lispro).  Why C/D/E wrong: NPH, Glargine, and Detemir are basal insulins."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"What is the color code of a U-40 insulin syringe?","options":["Orange","Red","Blue","Green"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The U-40 insulin vial/syringe color code is Red.  Why A wrong: Orange is the U-100 color code.  Why C/D wrong: Not the stated color codes."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"Only U-40 insulin (marked with U-40 / Unit-40) should be used with which syringe?","options":["A U-100 marked syringe","A U-40 marked syringe","Any tuberculin syringe","A 5 mL standard syringe"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Only U-40 insulin (with markings unit 40) should be used with a U-40 marked syringe.  Why A/C/D wrong: Mismatched syringes would deliver an incorrect dose."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"In India, 40 units of U-40 insulin is equal to what volume?","options":["0.5 mL (40 × U/40)","1 mL (1 mL = 40 units; i.e., 40 × U/40)","0.4 mL","4 mL"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In India, 1 mL of U-40 insulin contains 40 units (40 units = 1 mL; needle size for U-40 is 8 mm, 30 G, 5/16\").  Why A/C/D wrong: Do not match the stated equivalence."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"What is the needle size specified for a U-40 insulin syringe?","options":["12.7 mm, 12.5 mm, 1/2\"","8 mm, 30 G, 5/16\"","5 mm, 25 G, 1\"","10 mm, 22 G, 3/8\""],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In India, needle size for U-40 is 8 mm, 30 G, 5/16\".  Why A wrong: That is the U-100 needle size.  Why C/D wrong: Not the stated sizes."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"What is the color code of a U-100 insulin syringe/vial?","options":["Red","Orange","Yellow","Green"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The U-100 insulin color code is Orange.  Why A wrong: Red is the U-40 color code.  Why C/D wrong: Not the stated codes."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"Only U-100 insulin (marked with U-100) should be used with which syringe?","options":["A U-40 marked syringe","A U-100 marked syringe","Any blood-draw syringe","A glass barrel syringe only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Only U-100 insulin (with marking U-100) should be used with a U-100 marked syringe.  Why A/C/D wrong: Mismatching delivers a wrong dose."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"In India, what is the needle size for a U-100 insulin syringe?","options":["8 mm, 30 G, 5/16\"","12.7 mm, 12.5 mm, 1/2\"","5 mm, 25 G","4 mm, 32 G"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In India, the needle size for U-100 insulin is 12.7 mm, 12.5 mm, 1/2\".  Why A wrong: That is the U-40 size.  Why C/D wrong: Not the stated sizes."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"When converting from a U-40 syringe to a U-100 syringe, the result in U-40 insulin units must be multiplied by which factor?","options":["2.5 + 40 × 2.5","(2.5 × U40 units) + 40 + 2.5, i.e., U40 result × 2.5","60 × U40","divided by 2.5"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: To convert, the U-40 result in units (i.e., insulin in any U-40 line) is multiplied by 2.5 (formula shown: 2.5 + 40 × 2.5 conversion chain), since each U-40 line equals 2.5 units on a U-100 line.  Why A wrong: Misstates the multiplier placement.  Why C wrong: 60 is not the conversion factor.  Why D wrong: It is multiplied, not divided, by 2.5."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"Per the worked example, if a doctor ordered 16 units of U-40 insulin and you have a U-100 syringe, how many units must be drawn up in the U-100 syringe?","options":["16 units","40 units (16 × 2.5)","25 units","6.4 units"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Multiply by 2.5: 16 × 2.5 = 40 units to draw in the U-100 syringe.  Why A wrong: 16 is the ordered U-40 amount.  Why C/D wrong: Incorrect conversions."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"Per the example, if you must draw 25 units of U-40 insulin in a U-100 syringe, how many units should be drawn?","options":["25 units","62.5 units (25 × 2.5)","40 units","10 units"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 25 × 2.5 = 62.5 units to draw in the U-100 syringe.  Why A wrong: 25 is the U-40 amount.  Why C/D wrong: Incorrect conversions."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"A BCG vaccine is administered by which route, as per WHO recommendations?","options":["Subcutaneous","Intradermal","Intramuscular","Intravenous"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: BCG vaccine is administered to newborns by the intradermal route as per WHO recommendations.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the recommended route for BCG."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"Which syringe is used for BCG vaccination and what is its capacity?","options":["A U-40 insulin syringe used to draw 0.5 mL","A U-100 tuberculin-style syringe used to draw 0.1 mL (10 units)","A 5 mL syringe used to draw 1 mL","A glass syringe used to draw 2 mL"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A U-100 tuberculin syringe is used; 10 units (0.1 mL) is used to draw the BCG vaccine dose.  Why A/C/D wrong: Do not match the stated syringe/volume."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"What is the needle length specified for the BCG syringe?","options":["8 mm","26 G, needle length 5/16\" in length","12.7 mm","1 inch"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The BCG syringe uses a 26 G needle, 5/16\" in length.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the stated BCG needle specification."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"A nurse is mixing 10 units of Regular insulin and 20 units of NPH insulin in one syringe. What is the FIRST step?","options":["Draw up the regular insulin","Wash hands","Inject air into the NPH vial","Inject air into the regular vial"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The first step listed in mixing insulins is to wash hands.  Why A/C/D wrong: These steps come later in the sequence."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"When mixing Regular and NPH insulin, after washing hands and inspecting the insulin for clarity, the next step is to:","options":["Inject air into the regular vial","Gently rotate NPH insulin to mix it well","Draw up the regular insulin","Administer the insulin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After inspecting for clarity, gently rotate the NPH insulin to mix it well.  Why A/C/D wrong: These occur later in the procedure."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"While mixing insulins, after rotating the NPH vial, the nurse should wipe both vials with what?","options":["Plain water","Alcohol pad","Povidone-iodine","Dry gauze"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The procedure states to gently rotate NPH insulin and wipe both vials with an alcohol pad.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the stated antiseptic step."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"When mixing Regular (clear) and NPH (cloudy) insulin in one syringe, into which vial is air injected FIRST?","options":["The regular insulin vial","The NPH insulin vial (Figure A — 20 units air into NPH/cloudy first), then withdraw the needle","Both vials simultaneously","Neither vial"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The procedure injects 20 units of air into the NPH (cloudy) vial first, then withdraws the needle (without withdrawing insulin), per Figure A.  Why A wrong: Air into the regular vial comes next (Figure B), and regular is drawn before NPH.  Why C/D wrong: Air is injected sequentially, not simultaneously."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"After injecting air into the NPH vial and withdrawing the needle, the next steps are to invert the regular (clear) vial, inject air, and then:","options":["Inject NPH first","Withdraw 10 units of regular insulin (Figure B), then withdraw the needle","Discard the syringe","Add a third insulin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Invert the regular vial, inject air (10 units), withdraw 10 units of regular insulin (clear), then withdraw the needle — regular (clear) is drawn before NPH (cloudy).  Why A wrong: NPH is drawn last.  Why C/D wrong: Not part of the procedure."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"In the final drawing steps of mixing insulins (10 U regular + 20 U NPH), after drawing the regular insulin the nurse inserts the needle into the NPH vial and carefully withdraws how many units, to make what total?","options":["10 units NPH; total 20 units","20 units of NPH insulin; total 30 units mixed insulin","30 units NPH; total 40 units","5 units NPH; total 15 units"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Insert the needle into the NPH vial and carefully withdraw 20 units of NPH insulin (Figure D); total = 30 units of mixed insulin dosage.  Why A/C/D wrong: Do not match the 10 + 20 = 30 unit total."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"What is the FINAL step after withdrawing the mixed insulin?","options":["Recap and store the vials","Administer the insulin","Wash hands and properly dispose of the syringe","Re-rotate the NPH vial"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: After administering the insulin, the final step is to wash hands and properly dispose of the syringe.  Why A/B/D wrong: Administration precedes the final disposal step; the last action is hand hygiene and disposal."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"Trauma is best defined as:","options":["A chronic disease process","A bodily wound or shock produced by sudden physical injury, as from violence or an accident","A psychiatric disorder only","A type of infection"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Trauma is a bodily wound or shock produced by sudden physical injury, as from violence or an accident.  Why A/C/D wrong: Do not match the definition."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"\"Blunt trauma\" is defined as:","options":["An injury caused by an explosion","Trauma that comes from being hit by a blunt object or road traffic accident (RTA)","A psychological response to witnessing harm","Multisystem trauma"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Blunt trauma comes from being hit by a blunt object or road traffic accident.  Why A wrong: That is blast injury.  Why C wrong: That describes psychological trauma/PTSD.  Why D wrong: That is polytrauma."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"\"Blast injury\" refers to:","options":["Trauma from a blunt object","Physical trauma caused by an explosion","Injury from a penetrating object","A psychiatric condition"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Blast injury is physical trauma caused by an explosion.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the definition of blast injury."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"Psychological trauma (PTSD) is best described as:","options":["A wound caused by a sharp object","Something that the witnesses to a harmful situation are unable to cope with on their own; such trauma can occur after cases of sexual abuse","A road traffic accident","An explosion injury"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Psychological trauma (PTSD) results if someone witnesses something they cannot cope with on their own; it can occur after cases such as sexual abuse.  Why A/C/D wrong: Describe physical trauma types."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"A \"trauma center\" is defined as:","options":["A unit that only treats minor injuries","A hospital that provides emergency medical services to trauma victims","A rehabilitation clinic","An outpatient pharmacy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A trauma center is a hospital that provides emergency medical services to trauma victims.  Why A/C/D wrong: Do not match the definition."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"A \"mass casualty\" situation is defined as:","options":["Enough resources are available to manage all patients","Enough resources are NOT available to manage the patients coming to the casualty/hospital","A single critically injured patient","A psychiatric emergency"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Mass casualty = enough resources are not available to manage the patients coming to the casualty.  Why A wrong: That is the opposite of a mass casualty.  Why C/D wrong: Not the definition."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"\"Multiple casualties\" is best described as:","options":["Resources are not available to manage the patients","Enough resources are available to manage the patients","A single patient with multiple injuries","A mass disaster scenario"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Multiple casualties means enough resources are available to manage the patients.  Why A wrong: That defines mass casualty.  Why C wrong: A single multi-injury patient is polytrauma.  Why D wrong: Not the definition."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"\"Injury\" in trauma terminology results from:","options":["A psychiatric stressor","A harmful event that arises from the release of specific forms of energy","Only a road traffic accident","A surgical procedure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Injury is the result of a harmful event that arises from the release of specific forms of energy.  Why A/C/D wrong: Do not match the definition."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"\"Polytrauma\" is defined as:","options":["Injury to one body system","Multisystem trauma — injury to two or more body systems, or system with deranged vital signs","A psychological response","A blunt object injury only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Polytrauma is multisystem trauma — injury of two or more systems, or a system with deranged vital signs.  Why A/C/D wrong: Do not match the definition."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"\"Blunt trauma\" as the most common closed injury causes indirect injury to underlying structures by what mechanism?","options":["Penetration of the skin","Indirect injury to underlying structures (e.g., spleen rupture), transmission of energy into the body — for example, tearing of the muscles, vessels, and bone, or rupture of solid organs","Burns to the skin","Chemical exposure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Blunt trauma, the most common closed injury, causes indirect injury to underlying structures (e.g., spleen rupture) by transmission of energy into the body, for example tearing of the muscles, vessels and bone, or rupture of solid organs.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the described mechanism of blunt trauma."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"In Fyre's ABCDE acronym for prioritizing care, the letter \"A\" stands for:","options":["Airway","Bleeding","Circulation","Disease process"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In Fyre's ABCDE acronym, A → Airway.  Why B/C/D wrong: B → Bleeding, C → Circulation, D → Disease process."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"In Fyre's ABCDE acronym, \"B\" stands for:","options":["Breathing","Bleeding","Bone","Burns"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In Fyre's ABCDE acronym, B → Bleeding.  Why A wrong: Breathing is the \"B\" in ATLS ABCDE, not Fyre's.  Why C/D wrong: Not part of Fyre's acronym."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"In Fyre's ABCDE acronym, \"C\" stands for:","options":["Circulation","Confusion","Convulsion","Cyanosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In Fyre's ABCDE acronym, C → Circulation.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not part of Fyre's acronym."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"In Fyre's ABCDE acronym, \"D\" stands for:","options":["Disability","Disease process","Dehydration","Disorientation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In Fyre's ABCDE acronym, D → Disease process.  Why A wrong: Disability is the \"D\" in ATLS ABCDE.  Why C wrong: Dehydration is the \"C\" in WHO ETAT ABCD.  Why D wrong: Not part of Fyre's acronym."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"In Fyre's ABCDE acronym, \"E\" stands for:","options":["Exposure","Everything else","Environment","Emergency treatment"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In Fyre's ABCDE acronym, E → Everything else.  Why A wrong: Exposure is the \"E\" in ATLS ABCDE.  Why C/D wrong: Not part of Fyre's acronym."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"According to the source, what is the most common cause of death in a traumatised patient?","options":["Aspiration","Hypoxia","Hypovolemia","Hemorrhage"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The list ranks the most common cause of death in a traumatised patient as: 1. Hypoxia, 2. Aspiration, 3. Hypovolemia — hypoxia is the most common.  Why A wrong: Aspiration is listed second.  Why C wrong: Hypovolemia is listed third.  Why D wrong: Not the top-listed cause."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"ATLS stands for:","options":["Acute Trauma Life Support","Advanced Trauma Life Support","Advanced Triage and Life Saving","Acute Triage Life System"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: ATLS = Advanced Trauma Life Support, a training program providing a method for immediate management of injured patients.  Why A/C/D wrong: Incorrect expansions."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"The ATLS priority algorithm uses which sequence for managing injured patients?","options":["ABCD (Airway, Breathing, Circulation, Dehydration)","ABCDE — Airway, Breathing, Circulation, Disability, Exposure & Environment control","ABCDE — Airway, Bleeding, Circulation, Disease process, Everything else","CAB — Circulation, Airway, Breathing"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: ATLS ABCDE = Airway, Breathing, Circulation, Disability, Exposure & Environment control.  Why A wrong: That is the WHO ETAT ABCD.  Why C wrong: That is Fyre's ABCDE.  Why D wrong: Not the ATLS sequence."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"In the ATLS ABCDE assessment, \"A\" (Airway) involves:","options":["Checking the HR and capillary refill time","Checking for airway obstruction","Checking the pattern alertness and response to painful stimuli","Checking for bleeding, temperature, and other skin signs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A = Airway → checking for airway obstruction.  Why A wrong: That is C (Circulation).  Why C wrong: That is D (Disability).  Why D wrong: That is E (Exposure)."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"In the ATLS ABCDE assessment, \"B\" (Breathing) involves:","options":["Checking for airway obstruction","Checking if the pattern is breathing and if the breathing is normal","Checking the HR and capillary refill time","Checking pattern alertness and response to painful stimuli"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: B = Breathing → checking if the patient is breathing and if the breathing is normal.  Why A wrong: That is A (Airway).  Why C wrong: That is C (Circulation).  Why D wrong: That is D (Disability)."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"In the ATLS ABCDE assessment, \"C\" (Circulation) involves:","options":["Checking for airway obstruction","Checking the HR (heart rate) and capillary refill time","Checking pattern alertness and response to painful stimuli","Checking for bleeding and skin temperature"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: C = Circulation → checking the HR and capillary refill time.  Why A wrong: That is A (Airway).  Why C wrong: That is D (Disability).  Why D wrong: That is E (Exposure)."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"In the ATLS ABCDE assessment, \"D\" (Disability) involves:","options":["Checking for airway obstruction","Checking the pattern/alertness and response to painful stimuli","Checking the HR and capillary refill time","Checking bleeding, temperature, and skin signs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: D = Disability → checking the pattern alertness and response to painful stimuli.  Why A/C/D wrong: Correspond to A, C, and E respectively."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"In the ATLS ABCDE assessment, \"E\" (Exposure) involves:","options":["Checking for airway obstruction","Checking the patient for trauma, bleeding, temperature, and other skin signs","Checking the HR and capillary refill time","Checking alertness and response to pain"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: E = Exposure → checking the patient for trauma, bleeding, temperature, and other skin signs.  Why A/C/D wrong: Correspond to A, C, and D respectively."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"The ATLS mnemonic \"AND NOT: Accuse, Blame, Criticise, Defend, Escape\" reminds the responder to:","options":["Document legal liability immediately","Avoid accusing, blaming, criticising, defending, or escaping during trauma management","Discharge the patient quickly","Withhold treatment until investigation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The \"AND NOT\" reminder lists Accuse, Blame, Criticise, Defend, Escape — behaviors to avoid in trauma response.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the intent of the mnemonic."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"ATLS divides the management of a trauma patient into how many surveys, and what are they?","options":["Two — primary and secondary survey","Three — primary, secondary and tertiary survey","One — primary survey only","Four — primary, secondary, tertiary, and quaternary"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: ATLS includes three surveys for management of trauma patients: primary, secondary, and tertiary survey.  Why A/C/D wrong: Do not match the three surveys stated."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"In the WHO ETAT \"ABCD\" course guide for prioritizing care, \"A\" stands for:","options":["Airway","Alertness","Additional","Admission"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In the WHO ETAT ABCD guide, A → Airway.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not part of the ETAT ABCD core letters (additional items follow separately)."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"In the WHO ETAT \"ABCD\" course guide, \"B\" stands for:","options":["Bleeding","Breathing","Blood pressure","Burns"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the WHO ETAT ABCD guide, B → Breathing.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the stated meaning in ETAT."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"In the WHO ETAT \"ABCD\" course guide, \"C\" stands for:","options":["Circulation, Coma, Convulsion","Capillary refill only","Cyanosis","Cardiac arrest"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In the WHO ETAT ABCD guide, C → Circulation, Coma, Convulsion.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the full stated meaning."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"In the WHO ETAT \"ABCD\" course guide, \"D\" stands for:","options":["Disability","Dehydration (severe)","Disease process","Death"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the WHO ETAT ABCD guide, D → Dehydration (severe).  Why A wrong: Disability is the \"D\" in ATLS ABCDE.  Why C wrong: Disease process is the \"D\" in Fyre's ABCDE.  Why D wrong: Not the meaning."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"In the WHO ETAT guide, beyond ABCD, \"E,\" \"P,\" and \"Q\" respectively stand for:","options":["E → Exposure; P → Pressure; Q → Quarantine","E → Initiate Emergency treatment; P → Put on priority; Q → Non-urgent cases / wait in the Queue (non-urgent)","E → Everything else; P → Patient; Q → Quick discharge","E → Environment; P → Pain; Q → Question"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The ETAT guide adds E → Initiate Emergency treatment; P → Put on priority; Q → Non-urgent (wait in the Queue).  Why A/C/D wrong: Do not match the stated meanings."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"The \"trauma triangle of death\" (also called the trauma triad of death) is described by which three components?","options":["Hypoxia, hypovolemia, hyperthermia","Hypothermia, acidosis, and coagulopathy","Hypotension, tachycardia, bradypnea","Hemorrhage, infection, shock"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The trauma triangle of death includes hypothermia, acidosis, and coagulopathy.  Why A/C/D wrong: Do not match the stated triad of death."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"Regarding the mydriatic instillation reminder, the nurse should maintain the eye drop technique knowing mydriatics dilate the pupil. Which complication is the basis for their contraindication in glaucoma?","options":["Excessive tearing","Increased intraocular pressure (IOP)","Corneal abrasion","Photophobia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Mydriatics are contraindicated in glaucoma due to the risk of increased intraocular pressure.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the stated rationale for the contraindication."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Atropine/pilocarpine/neostigmine medication safety…","question":"When converting a U-40 line to a U-100 syringe, why must the multiplication-by-2.5 conversion be done carefully?","options":["Because U-40 and U-100 are the same concentration","Because U-40 (40 units/mL) and U-100 (100 units/mL) have different concentrations, so 1 unit on U-40 equals 2.5 units on U-100","Because U-100 is weaker than U-40","Because the syringes cannot be interchanged at all"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: U-40 has 40 units/mL and U-100 has 100 units/mL; the ratio 100/40 = 2.5, so each U-40 unit corresponds to 2.5 units on a U-100 syringe — hence multiply by 2.5.  Why A wrong: They are different concentrations.  Why C wrong: U-100 is more concentrated, not weaker.  Why D wrong: Conversion is possible using the 2.5 factor."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"Triage is best defined as the:","options":["Process of admitting all emergency patients to the ICU","Process of determining priority of a patient's treatment in the emergency situation","Method of documenting injuries after death","System of discharging stable patients first"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Triage is the process of determining priority of a patient's treatment based on the severity of their condition in an emergency situation.  Why A wrong: Triage prioritizes treatment, it does not admit everyone to ICU.  Why C wrong: Documenting injuries after death is not the definition of triage.  Why D wrong: Triage prioritizes the sickest needing immediate care, not discharge of the stable first (that is reverse triage in a disaster)."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"In triage, patients are classified according to which two main factors?","options":["Age and gender","Insurance status and address","The type and urgency of their conditions","Family preference and religion"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Triage classifies patients according to the type and urgency of their conditions to get the right patient to the right place at the right time with the right provider.  Why A/B/D wrong: Age, gender, insurance, address, family preference and religion are not the triage classification criteria."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"The guiding principle of triage—getting the right patient to the right place at the right time with the right provider—reflects the \"4 Rights\" of triage. This principle ensures that the determination of priority depends on:","options":["The order in which patients arrive","The severity of the patient's condition","The patient's ability to pay","The shift timing of the staff"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Determination of priority in triage depends on the severity (type and urgency) of the patient's condition.  Why A wrong: Priority is not based on arrival order; the most severe are seen first.  Why C wrong: Ability to pay is not a triage criterion.  Why D wrong: Staff shift timing is irrelevant to clinical priority."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"The START triage system stands for:","options":["Standard Triage And Routine Transport","Simple Triage And Rapid Transport","Severe Trauma And Resuscitation Team","Systematic Trauma Assessment Response Tool"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: START is the Simple Triage And Rapid Transport system.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the correct expansion of START."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"JumpSTART triage is specifically designed for triaging which population in a disaster setting?","options":["Geriatric patients","Pregnant patients","Children","Trauma patients with burns"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: JumpSTART is designed specifically for triaging children in a disaster setting.  Why A/B/D wrong: JumpSTART is pediatric-specific, not for geriatric, pregnant, or burn patients in particular."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"Triage categories are first divided into two broad groups. They are:","options":["START and Advanced","Adult and Pediatric","Trauma and Medical","Red and Green"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The two types of triage are START and Advanced; advanced patients are further classified into categories.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the two broad triage types stated; the colour categories (Red/Green) are subcategories under advanced triage."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"In the colour-coded triage system, which colour code indicates clients who require immediate care, are the first priority, and have life-threatening injuries?","options":["Yellow","Green","Red","Black"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Red indicates clients who require immediate care, the first priority, with life-threatening injuries; it is also known as resuscitation.  Why A wrong: Yellow is urgent/second priority.  Why B wrong: Green is non-urgent/minimal.  Why D wrong: Black is expectant (dead or severely injured)."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"The Red triage category is also known as:","options":["Reverse triage","Resuscitation","Expectant","Minimal"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Red (Emergent/Immediate) category is also known as resuscitation.  Why A/C/D wrong: Reverse triage, expectant (Black), and minimal (Green) are distinct concepts/categories."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"Within what time frame should Red (immediate priority) clients be assessed and continue evaluation?","options":["Every 5–15 minutes","Every 30 minutes","Every 60 minutes or later","Only once on arrival"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Red clients must be assessed and continue evaluation in every 5–15 minutes.  Why B wrong: 30 minutes corresponds to Yellow (urgent) clients.  Why C wrong: 60 minutes or later corresponds to Green (non-urgent) clients.  Why D wrong: Red clients require frequent, repeated assessment."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"Which of the following are listed as first-priority Red victims? (SATA)","options":["Cardiac arrest (MI)","Severe hypertension, gunshot, stab wound","Suspected sexual assault","RTA (road traffic accident)","Visible neck swelling"],"correct":"A,B,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Cardiac arrest (MI); severe hypertension, gunshot, stab wound; and RTA with visible neck swelling are all listed as first-priority Red victims.  Why C wrong: Suspected sexual assault is listed under Yellow (urgent/second priority), not Red."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"Which findings are listed as Red second-priority victims? (SATA)","options":["Suicide attempt via poisoning, hanging, or self-inflicted trauma","Drowning","20% TBSA active seizure","Chest wounds","Simple fracture of limb"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Suicide attempt (poisoning, hanging, self-inflicted trauma), drowning, 20% TBSA active seizures, and chest wounds (chest pain and PDS, asthma attack, breathlessness, active bleeding/hemoptysis, epistaxis, hematuria) are listed as Red second-priority victims.  Why E wrong: Simple fracture & dislocation of a limb is a Yellow (urgent) second-priority finding, not Red."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"In triage, which colour code indicates urgent clients who are the second priority and require care within 30 minutes?","options":["Red","Yellow","Green","Black"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Yellow indicates urgent clients, second priority, who require care within 30 minutes.  Why A wrong: Red is immediate (5–15 min reassessment).  Why C wrong: Green is non-urgent (60 minutes or later).  Why D wrong: Black is expectant."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"A Yellow-coded patient whose condition is stable at present but is at risk of deteriorating to an immediate danger requires care within what time frame?","options":["5–15 minutes","30 minutes","60 minutes or later","No care needed"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: An imminent life-threatening victim who is stable but in danger requires immediate attention within 30 minutes and regular observation.  Why A wrong: 5–15 min is the Red reassessment interval.  Why C wrong: 60 minutes is the Green category.  Why D wrong: All Yellow patients still need care."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"Which conditions are listed as Yellow second-priority victims? (SATA)","options":["Simple fracture & dislocation","Asthma without respiratory distress","Fever with headache","Cardiac arrest","Severe hypertension"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Simple fracture & dislocation, asthma without respiratory distress, and fever with headache are Yellow second-priority victims.  Why D/E wrong: Cardiac arrest and severe hypertension are Red (immediate) findings, not Yellow."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"Which colour code indicates non-urgent clients who can ambulate and care for their own injuries, requiring evaluation within 60 minutes or later?","options":["Red","Yellow","Green","Black"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Green indicates non-urgent clients who can ambulate and care for their own injuries; assessment requires 60 minutes or later, and they evaluate 1 to 2 hours or every shift.  Why A/B wrong: Red and Yellow are higher priority with shorter time frames.  Why D wrong: Black is the expectant category."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"Which of the following are listed as Green (third-priority/minimal) victims? (SATA)","options":["Lacerations, sprain, contusions and bruises","Cold symptoms, fever","Open or closed stable fractures","Passive suicidal ideation, command hallucinations","Hypertension (pre-hypertension, type 1 hypertension)"],"correct":"A,B,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Green victims include lacerations/sprain/contusions/bruises; cold symptoms/fever; passive suicidal ideation/command hallucinations; hypertension (pre-hypertension, type 1 hypertension); abdominal pain; and renal stone.  Why C wrong: Open or closed stable fractures are not specifically listed under the Green minimal category in this source."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"A non-urgent (Green) victim with minimal injuries whose treatment may be delayed may have their care delayed until how long, and may walk or ambulate to care for their own injuries?","options":["6 hours","The \"1° & 2°\" priority victims have been treated","12 hours","24 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Per source, victims with minor injuries whose treatment may be delayed until the 1° & 2° priority victims have been treated; these victims who do require ICU admission can have provision of life support; or victims who can ambulate and care for their own injuries.  Why A/C/D wrong: Specific hour values are not the stated criterion; delay is relative to higher-priority victim treatment."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"In triage, which colour code is assigned to victims in the None/Expectant category—those who are already deceased, dead, or so severely injured that they are not expected to survive?","options":["Green","Yellow","Black","Red"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Black colour codes the None/Expectant category—those who are already deceased, dead, or severely injured and are not expected to survive given limited resources.  Why A/B/D wrong: Green, Yellow, and Red indicate survivable categories requiring care."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"Why are Black (expectant) category victims typically not given active care first?","options":["They have minor injuries","They will most likely not benefit/recover from any care or treatment because of the severity of the injuries","They can care for themselves","They are non-urgent and can wait 60 minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: These victims most likely will not benefit/recover from any care and treatment given the severity of injuries.  Why A/C wrong: Those describe Green/minimal victims.  Why D wrong: The 60-minute window describes Green, not Black."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"According to the Summary of Triage Categories table, match the priority. Which priority/colour/care combination is correct?","options":["Priority 1 = Emergent/Immediate = Red = within 5–15 minutes","Priority 2 = Urgent = Green = 60 minutes","Priority 3 = Non-Urgent = Yellow = 30 minutes","Priority 4 = Expectant = White = no care"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Priority 1 = Emergent/Immediate (Red) requires care within 5–15 minutes.  Why B wrong: Priority 2 Urgent is Yellow, within 30 minutes.  Why C wrong: Priority 3 Non-Urgent is Green, 60 minutes or later.  Why D wrong: Priority 4 Expectant is Black, no care required."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"Reverse triage is a process that:","options":["Prioritizes the most critically injured for ICU admission","Can be applied to discharge patients early when the medical system is strained","Assigns Black codes to all walking patients","Reverses the colour-code order permanently"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Reverse triage can be applied to discharge patients early when the medical system is strained (e.g., during a surge in demand such as a natural disaster), so hospital beds can be occupied by regular non-critical patients.  Why A wrong: That describes standard triage.  Why C/D wrong: Reverse triage does not blanket-assign Black codes or permanently reverse colour codes."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"During a \"surge\" in demand on the medical system, what is the goal of reverse triage?","options":["To admit more patients to ICU","To accommodate a greater number of new critical patients by triaging and discharging those who will not need immediate care","To stop all admissions","To declare a mass-casualty incident"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: During a surge, the goal is to accommodate a greater number of new critical patients; existing patients who will not need immediate care may be triaged and those who will not need immediate care can be discharged.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the stated reverse-triage objective."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"A patient presenting with myocardial infarction and severe HTN would be classified under which triage colour example?","options":["Red","Yellow","Green","Black"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Myocardial infarction with severe HTN is listed as a Red triage example.  Why B/C/D wrong: These colours correspond to less acute or non-survivable examples."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"Which of the following are listed as Red triage examples? (SATA)","options":["Road traffic accident with multiple injuries, open fractures of hand and feet, major vascular injury, two or more long bone fractures, pelvic fractures, chest trauma and traumatic amputation","Gunshot and stab wounds","Visible neck swelling and suspected sexual assault","Post-suture stage","Active bleeding (hematemesis, hemoptysis, epistaxis, hematuria)"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: RTA with multiple injuries/open fractures/major vascular injury/long-bone fractures/pelvic fractures/chest trauma/traumatic amputation; gunshot and stab wounds; visible neck swelling and suspected sexual assault; and active bleeding (hematemesis, hemoptysis, epistaxis, hematuria) are all Red examples.  Why D wrong: Post-suture stage is a Yellow example."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"Which additional findings are listed as Red triage examples? (SATA)","options":["Fever with delirium","Poisoning with unstable vital sign","Snake/scorpion bite","Burn ≥20% RSA (TBSA)","Minor head injury"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Fever with delirium; poisoning with unstable vital sign; snake/scorpion bite; and burn ≥20% RSA are Red examples; also active seizures.  Why E wrong: Minor head injury is listed as a Yellow example."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"Which conditions are listed as Yellow triage examples? (SATA)","options":["Post-suture stage","Pain abdomen / loose motions (3 episodes), painful bleeding per rectum and IUD bleeding","Patient known anaemia for transfusion","Fever with headache / chest pain / jaundice","Active seizures"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Post-suture stage; pain abdomen/loose motions/painful bleeding per rectum and IUD bleeding; known anaemia for transfusion; and fever with headache/chest pain/jaundice are Yellow examples.  Why E wrong: Active seizures are listed as a Red example."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"Which of the following are Yellow triage examples? (SATA)","options":["Fever with chemotherapy/HIV patients/diabetic patients","Drug overdose","Poisoning with stable vital signs","Painful swelling/wound, headache, dizziness","Minor symptoms of existing illness"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Fever in chemotherapy/HIV/diabetic patients; drug overdose; poisoning with stable vital signs; and painful swelling/wound, headache, dizziness are Yellow examples.  Why E wrong: Minor symptoms of existing illness is a Green example."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"Which conditions are listed among Yellow triage examples? (SATA)","options":["Unable to pass stool and urine","Minor head injury","Open or closed fractures of hand and feet","Isolated long bone fracture","Active seizures"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Unable to pass stool and urine; minor head injury; open or closed fractures of hand and feet; and isolated long bone fracture are Yellow examples.  Why E wrong: Active seizures are Red, not Yellow."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"A patient who comes with alcohol, anticoagulant, LOC and vomiting, nasal & ENT bleed, limb head injury would be triaged as which colour example?","options":["Green","Yellow","Red","Black"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: \"G/c-G with alcohol, anticoagulant, LOC and vomiting, nasal & ENT bleed, limb head injury\" is listed as a Yellow example.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match this stated example placement."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"Which of the following are listed as Green triage examples? (SATA)","options":["Minor symptoms of existing illness; fitness, urticaria, skin rash","Fever; for medico-legal examination","Minor conditions and low-risk conditions (cough, cold, etc.)","Abrasions and lacerations; isolated fracture of small bones of hand and foot; contusions and bruises","Drug overdose"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: All listed Green examples include minor symptoms of existing illness, fitness/urticaria/skin rash, fever, medico-legal examination, minor/low-risk conditions (cough, cold), abrasions/lacerations, isolated small-bone fractures of hand/foot, and contusions/bruises.  Why E wrong: Drug overdose is a Yellow example."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"According to the Black triage example note, the Black colour code is used for the deceased and for those whose injuries are so endless (severe) that:","options":["They would benefit from full resuscitation","They would not be able to survive","They should be transferred to ICU","They require 30-minute observation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Black code is used for the deceased and those whose injuries are so severe that they would not be able to survive.  Why A/C/D wrong: Black-coded victims are not expected to survive and are not prioritized for active resuscitation, ICU transfer, or scheduled observation."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"Damage Control Resuscitation (DCR) is best described as a:","options":["Surgical technique to repair fractures permanently","Systematic approach to managing the trauma patient with severe injuries that starts in the emergency room and continues into the operating room and intensive care unit (ICU)","Method of triaging children in disasters","Routine fluid maintenance protocol for stable patients"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: DCR is a systematic approach to the management of the trauma patient with severe injuries that starts in the emergency room and continues into the operating room and the intensive care unit.  Why A/C/D wrong: DCR is a resuscitation strategy for severe trauma, not a fracture repair, a pediatric triage tool, or routine maintenance fluids."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"DCR involves which three core components? (SATA)","options":["Hemostatic resuscitation","Permissive hypotension (where appropriate)","Damage control surgery","Aggressive crystalloid bolus loading","Early full anticoagulation"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: DCR involves hemostatic resuscitation, permissive hypotension (where appropriate), and damage control surgery.  Why D wrong: DCR minimizes crystalloids rather than aggressive bolus loading.  Why E wrong: Early full anticoagulation is not a DCR component; coagulopathy is being treated, not induced."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"According to the components diagram, the DCR strategy pairs permissive hypotension and early hemorrhage control with which approach to fluids?","options":["Maximizing crystalloids","Minimizing of crystalloids","No fluids at all","Colloid-only resuscitation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The components figure lists permissive hypotension, minimizing of crystalloids, 1:1:1 products ratio, and early hemorrhage control.  Why A wrong: DCR minimizes crystalloids.  Why C wrong: Fluids/blood products are still given, just judiciously.  Why D wrong: The source specifies a 1:1:1 product ratio, not colloid-only resuscitation."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"DCR aims to maintain circulating volume, prevent or correct the \"lethal triad\" of coagulopathy, acidosis and hypothermia, until definitive intervention is appropriate. The lethal triad/trauma triad of death consists of:","options":["Coagulopathy, acidosis, hypothermia","Hypertension, bradycardia, hyperthermia","Hyperglycemia, alkalosis, fever","Tachypnea, leukocytosis, edema"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: DCR aims to correct the lethal triad of coagulopathy, acidosis and hypothermia (the trauma triad of death).  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not represent the lethal/trauma triad of death."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"According to the trauma triad of death figure, severe blood loss leads to decreased oxygen delivery and increased lactic acid, which causes:","options":["Acidosis","Alkalosis","Hyperthermia","Hypertension"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Severe blood loss → decreased oxygen delivery/increased lactic acid → acidosis, which along with hypothermia and coagulopathy forms the lethal triad.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not depicted as the result of increased lactic acid in the trauma triad figure."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"Damage control surgery involves performing only the necessary amounts of surgery to control bleeding, remove nonviable tissue, stabilize fractures, and:","options":["Achieve cosmetic closure","Restore extremity perfusion","Perform elective reconstruction","Complete all definitive repairs at once"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Damage control surgery performs only necessary surgery to control bleeding, remove nonviable tissue, stabilize fractures, and restore extremity perfusion.  Why A/C/D wrong: Cosmetic closure, elective reconstruction, and complete definitive repair are deferred in damage control surgery."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"Which of the following are listed as Anatomic Parameters of damage control surgery? (SATA)","options":["Estimated ISS >35","Penetrating abdominal injuries","Penetrating chest injuries","Open pelvic fracture","Lactate ≤2.5 mmol/L"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Anatomic parameters include estimated ISS >35, penetrating abdominal injuries, penetrating chest injuries, open pelvic fracture, long bone with head injury, long bone with vascular injury, and truncal hemorrhage & amputation.  Why E wrong: Lactate ≤2.5 mmol/L is a Lab parameter, not an anatomic parameter."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"Which of the following are listed as Physiologic Parameters of damage control surgery? (SATA)","options":["Weak or absent radial pulse","Core body temperature <35°C","Systolic BP <100 mmHg","Heart rate >110/min","Platelet count <90,000/ml"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Physiologic parameters include weak or absent radial pulse, core body temperature <35°C, systolic BP <100 mmHg, heart rate >110/min, PaO2/FiO2 <250, and urinary output <50 ml/hour.  Why E wrong: Platelet count <90,000/ml is a Lab parameter, not a physiologic parameter."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"Which of the following are listed as Lab Parameters of damage control surgery? (SATA)","options":["Lactate ≤2.5 mmol/L","Platelet count <90,000/ml","Fibrinogen >1 g/dl","PT >16 sec, INR >1.5, Hb <11","pH <7.2"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Lab parameters include lactate ≤2.5 mmol/L, platelet count <90,000/ml, fibrinogen >1 g/dl, PT >16 sec, INR >1.5, Hb <11, pH <7.2, and base deficit >6.  Why (none) wrong: All listed are lab parameters per the source."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"In the Clinical Practice Guideline, when there is hemorrhage and a rapidly recognized need for early DCR and initial early hemorrhage control and source transfixation as close to time-of-injury as possible, the triage/rapid assessment guideline recommends initiating:","options":["Maintenance crystalloids only","Rapid DCR with blood products and assessing for signs of hemorrhagic shock","Discharge planning","Oral rehydration"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The guideline directs initiating rapid DCR, maintaining a target systolic BP (SBP) for DCR at 100 mmHg (100 mmHg with resuscitating if SBP presumed when resuscitating with blood products), and assessing for signs of hemorrhagic shock.  Why A/C/D wrong: Maintenance crystalloids alone, discharge, or oral rehydration are inappropriate for active hemorrhage requiring DCR."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"Per the Clinical Practice Guideline, during DCR resuscitation the target systolic blood pressure (SBP) should be maintained at approximately:","options":["80 mmHg","100 mmHg","120 mmHg","140 mmHg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The updated guideline is to maintain a target SBP at 100 mmHg (100 mmHg) when resuscitating with blood products during DCR.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated DCR target SBP of 100 mmHg."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"When assessing for signs of hemorrhagic shock during DCR, which assessment findings/actions are listed? (SATA)","options":["SBP <90 mmHg","Pulse >100 bpm","Physiologic signs of shock","Hematocrit <32%","pH ≥7.45"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Signs of hemorrhagic shock include SBP <90 mmHg, pulse >100 bpm, physiologic signs of shock, hematocrit <32%, pH <7.35, and base deficit ≥6 mEq/L.  Why E wrong: pH ≥7.45 is alkalosis; the guideline lists pH <7.35 (acidosis), not ≥7.45."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"According to the resuscitation guideline, when bleeding shock occurs the practitioner should prioritize transfusion using which fluid choice to control or reverse hemorrhage as close to time-of-injury as possible?","options":["Normal saline only","Whole blood (the fluid of choice for DCR)","Dextrose 5%","Albumin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The guideline prioritizes low titre Group O whole blood as the fluid of choice for DCR; whole blood is preferred to treat and reverse hemorrhage as close to time-of-injury as possible.  Why A/C/D wrong: Normal saline, dextrose, and albumin are not the preferred DCR fluid; crystalloids are minimized in DCR."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"The guideline states to administer DCR fluids from least to most preferred. Which order reflects the most-to-least preferred resuscitation products? (SATA — select the products listed)","options":["Whole blood (low titre Group O whole blood preferred)","Plasma, platelets, red blood cells (RBCs) in a 1:1:1 ratio","Whole blood in a recently revived donor","Plasma and RBCs in a 1:1 ratio","Plasma or RBCs alone"],"correct":"A,B,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The ordered resuscitation choices are: 1. whole blood (low titre Group O preferred); 2. plasma, platelets, and RBCs (1:1:1); 3. whole blood in a recently revived donor; 4. plasma and RBCs (1:1); 5. plasma or RBCs alone.  Why C wrong as a standalone \"best\" choice: While \"whole blood in a recently revived donor\" is listed, it is item #3 (not the top preference); the question asks which products are listed — C is listed but is included for completeness; (Note: C is part of the list. Among A,B,D,E the products are unambiguously listed; option C describes the same #3 item and is also acceptable, but the principal preferred-order products are A, B, D, E.)"},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"Per the pharmacologic guideline, tranexamic acid (TXA) should be administered to treat hyperfibrinolysis. The recommended dosing is:","options":["1 g/100 ml over 10 seconds, then nothing further","1 g/100 ml over 1 minute within 3 hours of injury, then a second 1 g/100 ml over 8 hours","5 g IV push immediately","Oral only, once daily"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Administer TXA 1 g/100 ml over 1 minute within 3 hours of injury, then a second 1 g/100 ml over 8 hours.  Why A wrong: A 10-second push and no follow-up dose is incorrect.  Why C wrong: 5 g IV push is not the recommended regimen.  Why D wrong: TXA in trauma is given IV per the guideline, not oral once daily."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"To prevent or treat hypocalcemia related to massive transfusion (because banked blood with citrate binds calcium), the guideline directs the nurse to:","options":["Withhold all calcium","Provide IV/IO calcium for all hemorrhagic shock patients receiving uncrossmatched blood transfusions/citrate, or after transfusion of the first unit of blood","Administer potassium instead","Give oral calcium tablets only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: To treat hypocalcemia related to massive transfusion, provide IV/IO calcium to all hemorrhagic shock patients receiving uncrossmatched blood transfusions or citrate, or immediately after the first unit of blood.  Why A wrong: Calcium should be provided, not withheld.  Why C wrong: Potassium does not correct citrate-induced hypocalcemia.  Why D wrong: IV/IO calcium is indicated in massive transfusion, not oral-only."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"Which agents are listed as adjuncts that should be discontinued or used carefully for peripheral IV administration in this guideline note? (SATA)","options":["Hydroxyethyl starch (Hextend, Hespan)","Recombinant human activated factor VII (rFVIIa)","Normal saline","Lactated Ringer's"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The note states calcium should be preferred for peripheral IV administration and to DISCONTINUE use for DCR of hydroxyethyl starch (Hextend, Hespan) and recombinant human activated factor VII (rFVIIa).  Why C/D wrong: Normal saline and lactated Ringer's are not named in this discontinue note."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"(\"Recall the Memory\" box) Which of the following colour codes is used for emergent cases in the triage system of emergency?","options":["Red","Green","Yellow","Black"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The \"Recall the Memory\" box answer is A. Red — Red is used for emergent (immediate) cases.  Why B/C/D wrong: Green = non-urgent, Yellow = urgent, Black = expectant."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"ATLS primary survey uses the CABCDE acronym to assess an injured patient. The \"C\" placed first (before A) refers to:","options":["Circulation only","Catastrophic haemorrhage control","Cervical spine","Consciousness"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the CABCDE primary survey, the leading C stands for Catastrophic haemorrhage (control), assessed before Airway in trauma.  Why A wrong: General Circulation is the later \"C\" (step 4) in CABCDE.  Why C/D wrong: Cervical spine and consciousness are not the leading C in CABCDE."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"What is the primary survey designed to do in the injured patient?","options":["Provide definitive surgical repair","Rapidly assess patients and address life-threatening injuries, while not perfect, addressing the most common and immediate causes of death within 5 areas","Document the patient's full medical history","Plan discharge"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The primary survey rapidly assesses patients and addresses life-threatening injuries, addressing the most common and immediate causes of death within 5 areas.  Why A/C/D wrong: Definitive repair, full history, and discharge planning are not the role of the rapid primary survey."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"The 5 causes/areas addressed in the primary survey include which of the following? (SATA)","options":["Airway obstruction","Pneumothorax","Massive hemorrhage (both internal and external)","Fluid (chest)","Cardiac tamponade and stroke"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The 5 causes include airway obstruction, pneumothorax, massive hemorrhage (both internal and external), fluid (chest), and cardiac tamponade and stroke.  Why (none) wrong: All five are listed in the source."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"In the primary survey, the CABCDE acronym is used to assess victims. Which mnemonic order is correct?","options":["C-Catastrophic haemorrhage, A-Airway, B-Breathing, C-Circulation, D-Disability with neurological injury, E-Exposure/everything else","A-Airway, B-Breathing, C-Circulation only","D-Disability, E-Exposure, A-Airway, B-Breathing, C-Circulation","C-Cervical, A-Alertness, B-Bleeding, C-CPR"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: CABCDE = 1. Catastrophic haemorrhage, 2. Airway, 3. Breathing, 4. Circulation, 5. Disability with neurological injury, 6. Exposure/everything else.  Why B/C/D wrong: These misorder or misname the CABCDE components."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"Per the highlighted NOTE, when catastrophic bleeding (massive external bleeding) is present, why is CABCDE used instead of ABCDE?","options":["Because airway is unimportant in trauma","Because the primary survey needs to stop major vessels bleeding (catastrophic haemorrhage) first before ensuring a viable airway","Because breathing is assessed last","Because disability is the priority"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note states some guidelines prefer CABCDE so that in catastrophic bleeding the priority is to stop major-vessel (massive) bleeding first before ensuring a viable airway.  Why A wrong: Airway remains critical; it is simply preceded by stopping exsanguinating hemorrhage.  Why C/D wrong: The reordering is about catastrophic hemorrhage, not breathing-last or disability-priority."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"During Airway and Cervical Spine Control, the first action is to:","options":["Intubate immediately","Assess ABC","Apply a hard cervical collar before any assessment","Place all patients prone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Airway and Cervical Spine Control begins with \"Assess ABC.\"  Why A wrong: Intubation is initiated only if GCS score <8.  Why C wrong: A hard cervical collar is applied when spinal injury is suspected, after assessment.  Why D wrong: Prone is not the recovery/airway-protective position; the recovery position is used for unconscious-but-breathing patients."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"If a patient is unconscious but is breathing and has no other life-threatening conditions, they should be placed in which position?","options":["Supine flat","Recovery position","Trendelenburg","Prone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If a patient is unconscious but breathing and has no life-threatening conditions, they should be placed in the recovery position; this keeps the airway open, prevents choking, and ensures fluids drain from the mouth.  Why A/C/D wrong: These positions do not protect the airway of an unconscious breathing patient as the recovery position does."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"Placing a patient in the recovery position helps maintain an open airway, prevents choking, and ensures that:","options":["Blood pressure rises","Fluids drain from the mouth","The cervical spine is immobilized","The patient remains conscious"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The recovery position ensures fluids drain from the mouth, keeping the airway clear.  Why A/C/D wrong: It does not specifically raise BP, immobilize C-spine, or restore consciousness."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"If breathing is abnormal or there is no breathing, cardiac arrest, or suspicion of spinal injury, the patient SHOULD NOT be placed in the recovery position. Instead, the patient should be:","options":["Left in recovery position regardless","Placed flat for resuscitation and that patient should be placed flat in a supine position (for CPR)","Made to sit upright","Discharged"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If breathing is abnormal/absent, cardiac arrest, or spinal injury is suspected, the patient should NOT be placed in recovery position and instead should be placed in a supine/flat position for resuscitation.  Why A wrong: Recovery position is contraindicated in these scenarios.  Why C/D wrong: Upright sitting or discharge is inappropriate for an arrest/spinal-injury patient."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"Endotracheal (ET) intubation is initiated when the GCS score is:","options":["<15","<12","<8","<5"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Initiate intubation if the GCS score is <8.  Why A/B/D wrong: The stated threshold is GCS <8."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"In suspicion of spinal injury, the patient should be managed with which protective measures? (SATA)","options":["Put a hard cervical collar","Place the patient on a hard board (log roll technique)","Use airway manoeuvres to remove tongue falling back","Place the patient prone immediately","Vigorously flex the neck to check range"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: In suspected spinal injury, apply a hard cervical collar, place the patient on a hard board (log roll), and use airway manoeuvres to remove the tongue from falling back.  Why D wrong: Prone positioning is not indicated; spinal precautions/log roll are used.  Why E wrong: The neck should be immobilized, not flexed."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"A minimum of how many rescuers is required for the log roll procedure?","options":["2","3","4","6"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: A minimum of 4 rescuers is required for the log roll procedure.  Why A/B/D wrong: The stated minimum is 4 rescuers."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"Which airway manoeuvres are listed to remove the tongue falling back / open the airway? (SATA)","options":["Head thrust and head tilt chin lift","Guedel airway (oropharyngeal airway insertion)","Definitive airway: oropharyngeal, nasopharyngeal airway insertion, then endotracheal intubation","MILS (manual in-line stabilization)","Suprapubic pressure"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Airway manoeuvres include head thrust and head tilt chin lift; Guedel airway; definitive airway (oropharyngeal, nasopharyngeal insertion, endotracheal intubation); and MILS (Manual In-line Stabilization), similar to jaw thrust, strongly recommended in ATLS.  Why E wrong: Suprapubic pressure is not an airway manoeuvre."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"MILS (Manual In-line Stabilization) is described as a manoeuvre that is similar to which technique and is strongly recommended in ATLS guidelines?","options":["Head tilt-chin lift","Jaw thrust technique","Heimlich manoeuvre","Recovery position"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: MILS is similar to the jaw thrust technique and is strongly recommended in ATLS guidelines (used to stabilize the cervical spine).  Why A wrong: Head tilt-chin lift extends the neck and is avoided in suspected C-spine injury.  Why C/D wrong: Heimlich and recovery position are unrelated techniques."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"Orotracheal intubation refers to ET tube intubation through the:","options":["Nose","Mouth","Cricothyroid membrane","Tracheostomy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Orotracheal intubation is ET tube intubation through the mouth.  Why A wrong: Nasotracheal intubation is through the nose.  Why C/D wrong: Cricothyroid/tracheostomy routes are surgical airways."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"Regarding nasotracheal intubation, which statements are correct? (SATA)","options":["ET tube intubation through the nose","Less commonly used","Contraindicated in case of skull fractures","Contraindicated in maxillo-facial fractures, CSF rhinorrhea, and maxillo-facial fractures","It is the most common intubation method"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Nasotracheal intubation is ET tube intubation through the nose, less commonly used, and contraindicated in skull fractures, CSF rhinorrhea, and maxillo-facial fractures.  Why E wrong: Orotracheal (oral) intubation is the most common method, not nasotracheal."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"Which intubation route is identified as the most common intubation method?","options":["Nasotracheal","Orotracheal (through mouth)","Cricothyroidotomy","Tracheostomy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Orotracheal intubation through the mouth is the most common intubation method.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are less common or are surgical/alternative airways."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"In the ABCDE approach, the \"C\" (Catastrophic haemorrhage control) step uses which measure?","options":["Use of a haemostatic tourniquet","Endotracheal intubation","Needle decompression","Log roll"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Under the ABCDE approach, C – Catastrophic haemorrhage control uses a haemostatic tourniquet.  Why B/C/D wrong: Intubation (Airway), needle decompression (Breathing/Circulation), and log roll (spinal precaution) belong to other steps."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"In the ABCDE approach, the \"A\" (Airway + C-spine control + oxygen) includes which interventions? (SATA)","options":["Assess for patency","Remove foreign body/anterior fluids","Maintain airway using basic manoeuvres and airway adjuncts","Protect airway with definitive airway (cuffed tube, either endotracheal tube or surgical airway)","Use of haemostatic tourniquet"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Airway step: assess for patency; remove foreign body/anterior fluids; maintain airway with basic manoeuvres and adjuncts; protect airway with a definitive airway (cuffed tube — endotracheal or surgical).  Why E wrong: The haemostatic tourniquet belongs to Catastrophic haemorrhage control (C), not Airway."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"In the ABCDE approach, the \"B\" (Breathing) step includes which actions? (SATA)","options":["Avoid hypoxia","Ventilate/oxygenate","Identify and decompress the chest","Assess for life-threatening chest trauma","Assess pupils"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Breathing step: avoid hypoxia; ventilate/oxygenate; identify and decompress the chest; assess for life-threatening chest trauma.  Why E wrong: Assessing pupils belongs to the Deficit/Disability (D) neurological step."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"In the ABCDE approach, the \"C\" (Circulation) step includes which assessments/interventions? (SATA)","options":["Assess for shock (agitated/decreased consciousness, poor LOC, tachycardia, hypotension)","Assess for and stop external haemorrhage (external, internal — external; internal — splinting)","Urgent transfer for damage control surgery","Gain circulatory access (peripheral cannula intravenous or IV insertion); correct hypotension/permissive hypotension","Apply a full cervical collar"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Circulation step: assess for shock (agitated/decreased consciousness, poor LOC, tachycardia, hypotension); assess and stop external/internal haemorrhage (splinting); urgent transfer for damage control surgery; gain circulatory access (peripheral IV) and correct hypotension/permissive hypotension; correct hypovolemia with rapid blood transfusion and long periods of permissive hypotension; alleviate obstructive shock.  Why E wrong: Applying a cervical collar is part of Airway/C-spine control, not Circulation."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"In the ABCDE approach, the \"D\" (Deficit) step includes which assessments? (SATA)","options":["Assess global (alertness) level","GCS <8 requires intubation","Motor score may identify spinal cord injury","Assess pupils; manage raised intracranial pressure; correct hypoglycemia","Apply warming blankets and remove all clothing"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Deficit step: assess global (alertness) level; GCS <8 requires intubation; motor score may identify spinal cord injury; assess pupils; manage raised intracranial pressure; correct hypoglycemia.  Why E wrong: Warming/removing clothing belongs to the Exposure/environmental control (E) step."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"In the ABCDE approach, the \"E\" (Exposure/environmental control) step includes which actions? (SATA)","options":["Full exposure","Correct hypothermia (space blankets, bair hugger)","Assess pupils","Apply warming measures to prevent heat loss","Stop catastrophic haemorrhage with a tourniquet"],"correct":"A,B,D","rationale":"Why correct: Exposure step: full exposure, correct hypothermia using space blankets and a bair hugger, and apply warming measures to prevent heat loss.  Why C wrong: Assessing pupils belongs to Deficit (D).  Why E wrong: Tourniquet use belongs to Catastrophic haemorrhage control (C)."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"The Log Roll method depicted is performed in how many steps as shown in the figure?","options":["2 steps","3 steps","4 steps","5 steps"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The Log Roll method figure shows Step 1, Step 2, Step 3, and Step 4 — four steps.  Why A/B/D wrong: The figure depicts 4 steps."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"In the Head Tilt – Chin Lift technique, the head is positioned by tilting the forehead posteriorly (pharyngeal wall and epiglottis away from the laryngeal inlet). This manoeuvre lifts the mandible and tongue forward. When is this technique avoided?","options":["When the patient is fully conscious","If neck injury is suspected — do not do the head tilt; jaw thrust is used","When the airway is patent","During CPR"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Per the note, if neck injury is suspected, do not do the head tilt; the jaw thrust is used instead.  Why A/C/D wrong: The contraindication is suspected neck (cervical spine) injury, not consciousness, patency, or CPR per se."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"The Jaw Thrust is described as an alternative to head tilt – chin lift. It is the technique of choice in which situation?","options":["When there is a strong suspicion of cervical spine injury (e.g., RTA, falls, drowning, diving accidents)","When the patient is awake and alert","Only in pediatric patients","Only after intubation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Jaw Thrust is the technique of choice when there is a strong suspicion of cervical spine injury (e.g., RTA, falls, drowning, diving accidents), placing fingers posterior to the mandible (if jaw and apply upward and forward pressure) and pushing slightly open using thumbs to deploy chin inferiorly.  Why B/C/D wrong: It is specifically preferred when C-spine injury is suspected, not limited to awake patients, pediatrics, or post-intubation."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"Under Breathing and Ventilation, oxygen must be administered to all trauma patients via which device?","options":["Nasal cannula at 2 L/min","Reservoir mask in high flow","Venturi mask 24%","Room air"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Oxygen must be administered to all trauma patients via reservoir mask in high flow.  Why A/C/D wrong: Low-flow nasal cannula, Venturi 24%, and room air are inadequate for the high-flow oxygen requirement in trauma."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"Ventilation must be assessed by:","options":["Auscultating the abdomen","Seeing chest walls/lungs","Checking pupils","Palpating the pulse only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Ventilation must be assessed by seeing chest walls and lungs movement.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not assess ventilation directly."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"Patients requiring respiratory support are usually intubated and:","options":["Discharged","Mechanically ventilated","Placed prone","Given oral fluids"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Patients requiring respiratory support are usually intubated and mechanically ventilated.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not provide the needed respiratory support."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"In Breathing and Ventilation, needle thoracostomy, finger thoracostomy, or intercostal catheter insertion may be required urgently for which conditions? (SATA)","options":["Hemothorax","Pneumothorax","Hypoglycemia","Tension pneumothorax requiring needle decompression","Fever with headache"],"correct":"A,B,D","rationale":"Why correct: Hemothorax and pneumothorax are the most common presentations of traumatized patients; in the case of tension pneumothorax, immediate needle decompression (initial) through 2nd intercostal space in mid-clavicular line is performed.  Why C/E wrong: Hypoglycemia and fever with headache are not indications for thoracostomy/needle decompression."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"Hemothorax and pneumothorax are described as the:","options":["Least common presentation of traumatized patients","Most common presentations of traumatized patients","Findings only in burns","Signs of hypoglycemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hemothorax and pneumothorax are the most common presentations of traumatized patients.  Why A/C/D wrong: They are common in trauma, not least common, burn-specific, or hypoglycemia-related."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"In tension pneumothorax, immediate needle decompression is performed through which anatomical site?","options":["5th intercostal space, anterior axillary line","2nd intercostal space in the mid-clavicular line","4th intercostal space, mid-axillary line","Suprasternal notch"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Immediate needle decompression for tension pneumothorax is performed through the 2nd intercostal space in the mid-clavicular line.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the stated needle decompression site in this source."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"\"Total white-out\" on chest X-ray is described as an important radiological sign of:","options":["Massive hemothorax (when found, immediate chest tube/large-bore IV with crystalloid infusion and blood transfusion, and chest decompression with chest tube insertion)","Pneumonia only","Hypoglycemia","Cardiac tamponade"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Total white-out is an important X-ray sign of massive hemothorax; when found, immediate management includes large-bore IVs with crystalloid infusion and blood transfusion, and chest decompression with chest tube insertion.  Why B/C/D wrong: While other causes of white-out exist, the source links it specifically to massive hemothorax."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"Management of massive hemothorax includes which interventions? (SATA)","options":["Insert 2 large-bore IVs with crystalloid infusion","Blood transfusion","Chest decompression with chest tube insertion","Withhold all fluids","Oral rehydration only"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Management of massive hemothorax includes 2 large-bore IVs with crystalloid infusion, blood transfusion, and chest decompression with chest tube insertion.  Why D/E wrong: Withholding fluids or oral rehydration only is inadequate for massive hemothorax."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"Under Circulation, which finding is the most reliable and warning sign for blood pressure status?","options":["Skin temperature","Rapid thready pulse","Pupil size","Respiratory rate alone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A rapid thready pulse is the most reliable and warning sign on/for BP.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not described as the most reliable warning sign for BP in this section."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"In circulation assessment, \"one on floor and four more\" represents what?","options":["The number of rescuers needed","The blood loss (estimation of where blood may be lost)","The IV fluid volume","The GCS score"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: \"One on floor and four more\" represents the blood loss — estimating sites of significant hemorrhage.  Why A/C/D wrong: It is a blood-loss assessment mnemonic, not rescuers, fluids, or GCS."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"Per the Circulation section, what size and number of peripheral IV catheters should be obtained for trauma resuscitation?","options":["Two single 24 G cannulas","Two large bore, short, peripheral IV catheters (Green 16 G, 2 cannulas)","One 22 G catheter","A central line only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Obtain two large-bore, short, peripheral IV catheters (Green 16 G, 2 cannulas).  Why A/C/D wrong: Small-gauge or single/central-only access does not meet the large-bore resuscitation requirement."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"When peripheral IV access cannot be obtained, which alternative access options are listed for circulation? (SATA)","options":["Cut down into saphenous vein or IV","Intraosseous transfusion in children <6 years","Subcutaneous infusion in adults","Suprapubic line","Intramuscular injection of fluids"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: Other options are cut down into the saphenous vein or IV; in children <6 years, intraosseous transfusion is used.  Why C/D/E wrong: Subcutaneous, suprapubic, and intramuscular routes are not listed for trauma fluid resuscitation access."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"Regarding tranexamic acid in the Circulation section, the recommended dose is:","options":["1 g over 1 minute, then no further dose","Acid 1 g IV stat over 10 minutes, followed by 1 g over 8 hours","5 g over 8 hours","Oral 500 mg once"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Tranexamic acid 1 g IV stat over 10 minutes, followed by 1 g over 8 hours.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated regimen of 1 g over 10 min then 1 g over 8 hours."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"In fluid resuscitation for trauma patients, adults consider how much/which fluid initially?","options":["Up to 2 litres of crystalloid fluids if a patient is hypotensive and cardiac arrest (until blood available)","5 litres of dextrose rapidly","No fluids until surgery","Colloids only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For adults, consider up to 2 litres of crystalloid fluids if a patient is hypotensive and in cardiac arrest (until blood is available).  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not reflect the stated initial crystalloid approach."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"For children in fluid resuscitation, the recommended bolus is:","options":["10 cc/kg of body weight","20 cc/kg of body weight","40 cc/kg of body weight","5 cc/kg of body weight"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Children should receive 20 cc/kg of body weight.  Why A/C/D wrong: The stated pediatric bolus is 20 cc/kg."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"Per the Circulation section, when should hemorrhage be suspected as significant in adults?","options":["PR <60/min","PR >110/min or BP <90 systolic","Temperature >38°C","SpO2 <95%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: It should be considered to expect a significant hemorrhage with PR >110/min or BP <90 systolic.  Why A/C/D wrong: Bradycardia, fever, and mild desaturation are not the stated thresholds for significant hemorrhage."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"Why is it advised not to exceed 2 litres of crystalloid in trauma resuscitation?","options":["It causes hyperkalemia","It will cause dilution of both RBC and platelets (dilutional coagulopathy)","It improves clotting","It raises hematocrit"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Exceeding 2 litres of crystalloid causes dilution of both RBC and platelets (dilutional coagulopathy).  Why A/C/D wrong: Excess crystalloid worsens, not improves, clotting, and dilutes (lowers) hematocrit."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"The 1:1:1 method of transfusion refers to a fixed-ratio (1:1:1) transfusion strategy that promotes which components in equal ratio?","options":["Crystalloid, colloid, and saline","Packed red blood cells (RBC), plasma, and platelets (PLT) in a 1:1:1 ratio, minimizing crystalloid infusion","Whole blood, dextrose, and albumin","Plasma, calcium, and potassium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The 1:1:1 transfusion strategy is a resuscitation strategy for trauma patients that promotes transfusion of packed RBCs, plasma, and platelets (PLT) in a 1:1:1 ratio while minimizing crystalloid infusion.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the RBC:plasma:platelet 1:1:1 fixed-ratio strategy."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"Tachycardia in a traumatized patient most reliably indicates:","options":["Hypovolemia / shock","Hyperthermia","Hypoglycemia","Normal finding"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Tachycardia in a traumatized patient indicates shock (hypovolemia); hypovolemic shock is the most common type of shock in trauma.  Why B/C/D wrong: Tachycardia in trauma is a shock/hypovolemia indicator, not a benign or unrelated finding."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"What is the most common type of shock in a trauma patient?","options":["Cardiogenic shock","Hypovolemic shock","Septic shock","Neurogenic shock"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hypovolemic shock is the most common type of shock in a trauma patient.  Why A/C/D wrong: While these may occur, hypovolemic shock is the most common in trauma."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"Per the Circulation section, pelvic and femur fractures cause the most blood loss after:","options":["A burn injury","A trauma","A myocardial infarction","A stroke"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pelvic and femur fractures cause the most blood loss after a trauma.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source attributes major blood loss from pelvic/femur fractures to trauma."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"Open book fracture is described as:","options":["Rare and one of the most dangerous pelvic fractures, usually caused by traumatic injury","A common, low-risk minor injury","A type of skull fracture","A spinal injury"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Open book fracture is rare and one of the most dangerous pelvic fractures, which usually is caused by traumatic injury.  Why B/C/D wrong: It is a dangerous pelvic fracture from trauma, not a minor injury, skull, or spinal fracture."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"Per the transfusion guidance in Circulation, transfusion occurs/blood is given over what schedule for tranexamic acid administration in trauma?","options":["1 g IV stat over 10 minutes followed by 1 g over 8 hours","2 g over 1 hour","1 g over 24 hours","500 mg every 4 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Tranexamic acid is given 1 g IV stat over 10 minutes, followed by 1 g over 8 hours.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated TXA schedule in the Circulation section."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"The recovery position figure describes a sequence. Which of the following are listed steps for placing a patient in the recovery position? (SATA)","options":["Tilt head backwards, ensure clear airway and straighten head and neck","Bring far knee up to a 90° angle","Place arm at side and other arm across chest with hand against cheek","Roll person over towards you with knee at angle and ensure head is supported","Apply chest compressions at 100/min"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The recovery position figure lists: tilt head backwards/ensure clear airway and straighten head and neck; place arm at side and other arm across chest with hand against cheek; bring far knee up to a 90° angle; and roll the person over towards you with knee at angle, ensuring the head is supported.  Why E wrong: Chest compressions are part of CPR, not the recovery position; recovery position is for the unconscious patient who IS breathing."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"A patient with a strong suspicion of cervical spine injury from a diving accident requires airway opening. Which technique is the safest choice?","options":["Head tilt-chin lift","Jaw thrust","Vigorous neck hyperextension","Sniffing position with head tilt"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: With suspected cervical spine injury (e.g., diving accident), the jaw thrust is the technique of choice because it opens the airway without moving the neck.  Why A/D wrong: Head tilt-chin lift and sniffing position extend the neck and are avoided when neck injury is suspected.  Why C wrong: Neck hyperextension can worsen a spinal cord injury."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"A multi-trauma victim arrives with massive external arterial bleeding from a limb, a patent airway, and is breathing. Using the prioritized trauma survey, the FIRST intervention should be:","options":["Endotracheal intubation","Apply a haemostatic tourniquet to control the catastrophic haemorrhage","Needle decompression of the chest","Begin warming with a bair hugger"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: When catastrophic (massive external) bleeding is present, CABCDE is used and the priority is to stop major-vessel bleeding first using a haemostatic tourniquet — before securing the (already patent) airway.  Why A wrong: The airway is patent and the patient is breathing; intubation is not the immediate priority.  Why C wrong: No tension pneumothorax/chest finding is indicated here.  Why D wrong: Exposure/warming (E) is the last step, not first."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"A trauma patient has a GCS of 7 and noisy, abnormal breathing with no suspected spinal injury. The priority airway action is to:","options":["Place in the recovery position","Initiate endotracheal intubation (definitive airway) because GCS <8","Wait and reassess in 30 minutes","Give oral fluids"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: GCS <8 mandates intubation to secure a definitive airway; the patient cannot protect their own airway.  Why A wrong: Recovery position is for an unconscious patient who is breathing normally without airway compromise; abnormal breathing with GCS 7 needs a definitive airway.  Why C wrong: A GCS of 7 is a life-threatening emergency requiring immediate intervention.  Why D wrong: Oral fluids are contraindicated/dangerous in an obtunded patient."},{"section":"Batch 011 · Triage system of emergency…","question":"A trauma patient receiving multiple units of uncrossmatched (citrated) banked blood develops signs of hypocalcemia. The appropriate nursing action is to:","options":["Withhold all calcium","Administer IV/IO calcium (e.g., after the first unit of blood) to correct citrate-induced hypocalcemia","Give potassium chloride","Increase crystalloid infusion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Massive transfusion with citrated blood binds calcium; the guideline directs administering IV/IO calcium to hemorrhagic shock patients receiving uncrossmatched transfusions/citrate or after the first unit of blood.  Why A wrong: Withholding calcium worsens hypocalcemia.  Why C wrong: Potassium does not correct hypocalcemia.  Why D wrong: Extra crystalloid causes dilutional coagulopathy and does not correct calcium."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"A client sustains an isolated femur fracture in a road accident. Approximately how much internal blood loss should the nurse anticipate?","options":["175 ml","250–500 ml","1000–2000 ml","500–1000 ml"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Per the estimated blood loss table, a femur fracture causes approximately 1000–2000 ml of internal blood loss.  Why A wrong: 175 ml corresponds to a rib fracture.  Why B wrong: 250–500 ml corresponds to radius or ulna fracture.  Why D wrong: 500–1000 ml corresponds to the tibia or fibula."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"Match the bone to its approximate internal blood loss: a rib fracture results in how much blood loss?","options":["175 ml","250–500 ml","500–1000 ml","1000–massive"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists rib fracture as approximately 175 ml of internal blood loss.  Why B wrong: 250–500 ml is for radius/ulna.  Why C wrong: 500–1000 ml is for humerus and tibia/fibula.  Why D wrong: 1000–massive applies to the pelvis."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"A humerus fracture is associated with approximately how much internal blood loss?","options":["175 ml","250–500 ml","500–1000 ml","1000–2000 ml"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists humerus fracture as 500–1000 ml internal blood loss.  Why A wrong: 175 ml is for a rib fracture.  Why B wrong: 250–500 ml is for radius/ulna.  Why D wrong: 1000–2000 ml is for femur."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"Which fracture is associated with the GREATEST potential internal blood loss (\"1000–massive\")?","options":["Femur","Pelvis","Humerus","Tibia or fibula"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists pelvis fracture as \"1000–massive,\" the largest range, reflecting the high risk of hemorrhagic shock.  Why A wrong: Femur is 1000–2000 ml, not \"massive.\"  Why C wrong: Humerus is only 500–1000 ml.  Why D wrong: Tibia/fibula is 500–1000 ml."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"A radius or ulna fracture is estimated to cause what amount of internal blood loss?","options":["175 ml","250–500 ml","500–1000 ml","1000–2000 ml"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists radius or ulna fracture as 250–500 ml.  Why A wrong: 175 ml is for ribs.  Why C wrong: 500–1000 ml is for humerus/tibia/fibula.  Why D wrong: 1000–2000 ml is for femur."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"Under the \"Disability/Dysfunction\" component of trauma assessment, which method is recommended for a quick assessment of level of consciousness?","options":["Glasgow Coma Scale only","AVPU method","Mini-Mental State Examination","Ramsay Sedation Scale"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states to assess level of consciousness using the AVPU method; GCS is used where time permits.  Why A wrong: GCS is used \"where time permits,\" not the quick primary method.  Why C/D wrong: These are not mentioned for the disability component of trauma assessment."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"In the AVPU method of LOC assessment, what does the \"A\" represent?","options":["Patient responds to a pain stimulus","Patient is awake / alert","Patient responds to a verbal stimulus","Patient is unresponsive to all stimuli"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In AVPU, \"Awake\" (alert) means the patient is awake.  Why C wrong: That describes \"Verbal.\"  Why A wrong: That describes \"Pain.\"  Why D wrong: That describes \"Unresponsive.\""},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"Using the AVPU scale, a patient who only responds when a painful stimulus is applied is classified as:","options":["Awake","Verbal","Pain","Unresponsive"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: \"Pain\" indicates the patient responds to a pain stimulus.  Why A wrong: Awake means the patient is awake.  Why B wrong: Verbal means responds to a verbal stimulus.  Why D wrong: Unresponsive means no response to any stimulus."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"On the AVPU scale, \"U\" indicates that the patient:","options":["Is awake","Responds to verbal stimulus","Responds to pain stimulus","Is unresponsive to any stimulus"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: \"Unresponsive\" means the patient is unresponsive to any stimulus.  Why A/B/C wrong: These describe the Awake, Verbal, and Pain categories respectively."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"The cranium is described as a rigid structure that contains three main components. Which set correctly lists them?","options":["Blood, CSF, and lymph","Brain, CSF, and blood","Brain, blood, and air","CSF, plasma, and brain"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The head injury section states the cranium contains brain, CSF, and blood.  Why A/C/D wrong: Lymph, air, and plasma are not listed as the three intracranial components."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"According to the text, ischemia in head injury occurs when cerebral blood flow falls below what value?","options":["< 50 ml/minute","< 35 ml/minute","< 20 ml/minute","< 10 ml/minute"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The text states ischemia happens if blood flow is < 20 ml/minute.  Why A/B wrong: Normal cerebral blood flow is 55 ml/minute (not the ischemia threshold).  Why D wrong: This value is not given for ischemia."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"What is the normal cerebral blood flow value cited in the head injury section?","options":["35 ml/minute","50 ml/minute","55 ml/minute","70 ml/minute"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Normal cerebral blood flow is listed as 55 ml/minute.  Why A wrong: 35 ml/min is the perfusion pressure threshold (CPP-75 mmHg / ischemia per text values).  Why B/D wrong: These values are not given."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"According to the text, normal cerebral perfusion pressure is approximately:","options":["35 mmHg","50 mmHg","70 mmHg","85 mmHg"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The text states normal CPP is 70 mmHg.  Why A wrong: CPP-75 mmHg leads to ischemia per the text values.  Why B/D wrong: Not the cited normal value."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"Which formula correctly defines Cerebral Perfusion Pressure (CPP)?","options":["CPP = MAP + ICP","CPP = MAP - ICP","CPP = ICP - MAP","CPP = MAP × ICP"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The calculation box states CPP = MAP - ICP.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are incorrect manipulations not given in the text."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"Which formula correctly defines Mean Arterial Pressure (MAP) as given in the text?","options":["MAP = (2 × DBP + SBP) / 3","MAP = (SBP + DBP) / 2","MAP = (SBP + 2DBP) / 3","MAP = SBP - DBP"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The text gives MAP = (SBP + 2DBP) / 3.  Why A wrong: Reverses the weighting of SBP and DBP.  Why B/D wrong: These are not the formula given."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"A client has a systolic BP of 120 and diastolic BP of 84. Using MAP = (SBP + 2DBP)/3, what is the MAP?","options":["84","96","102","120"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: (120 + 2×84)/3 = (120 + 168)/3 = 288/3 = 96 mmHg, matching the worked example.  Why A/C/D wrong: These result from incorrect calculation."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"Using the example values (ICP = 10 mmHg, MAP = 96), what is the calculated CPP?","options":["106 mmHg","96 mmHg","86 mmHg","70 mmHg"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: CPP = MAP - ICP = 96 - 10 = 86 mmHg, as shown in the example.  Why A wrong: That would be MAP + ICP.  Why B wrong: That is the MAP value.  Why D wrong: 70 is the normal reference, not the calculated value."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"The Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS) is used primarily to assess what aspect of a client's condition?","options":["Cardiac output","Neurological condition / level of consciousness","Renal perfusion","Respiratory effort"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The GCS overview states it is a scale that assesses the neurological condition of the client.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not what the GCS measures."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"What is the range of total scores on the Glasgow Coma Scale?","options":["1 to 10","0 to 15","3 to 15","1 to 15"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the scoring system is based on a scale of 3 to 15 points (lowest possible score is 3).  Why A/B/D wrong: The lowest possible score is 3, not 0 or 1, and maximum is 15."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"A GCS score of 15 indicates the client is:","options":["In a deep coma","Fully awake / healthy","In a severe brain injury state","Comatose"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The maximum score of 15 indicates a fully awake or healthy state.  Why A/D wrong: Lower scores indicate coma.  Why C wrong: Severe brain injury is a score of 3–8."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"A score lower than what value on the GCS indicates a coma is present and requires intubation and other intervention?","options":["12","10","8","6"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The text states a score lower than 8 indicates coma is present, needing intubation and intervention.  Why A/B/D wrong: 8 is the cutoff cited for coma/intubation."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"According to the GCS classification, a \"mild brain injury\" corresponds to which score range?","options":["13–15","9–13","3–8","Score of 3"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Mild brain injury = 13–15.  Why B wrong: 9–13 is moderate brain injury.  Why C wrong: 3–8 is severe brain injury.  Why D wrong: Score of 3 is deep coma or death."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"A client with a GCS classification of \"moderate brain injury\" would have a score in which range?","options":["13–15","9–13","3–8","1–3"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Moderate brain injury = 9–13.  Why A wrong: 13–15 is mild.  Why C wrong: 3–8 is severe.  Why D wrong: Score of 3 is deep coma/death."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"A \"severe brain injury (comatose client)\" is classified by which GCS score range?","options":["13–15","9–13","3–8","Score of 3"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Severe brain injury (comatose) = 3–8.  Why A/B wrong: These are mild and moderate respectively.  Why D wrong: A score of exactly 3 = deep coma or death."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"A GCS score of exactly 3 is classified as:","options":["Mild brain injury","Moderate brain injury","Severe brain injury","Deep coma or death"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: A score of 3 = deep coma or death.  Why A/B/C wrong: These correspond to higher score ranges."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"On the GCS, what is the maximum (best) score possible for the \"Eye\" opening response?","options":["1","4","5","6"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The GCS eye component is scored 1–4 (4 = opens spontaneously).  Why A wrong: 1 = no response (worst).  Why C wrong: 5 is the best verbal score.  Why D wrong: 6 is the best motor score."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"On the GCS, an eye-opening score of 1 indicates:","options":["Opens spontaneously","Opens to voice","Opens to pain","No response"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Eye score 1 = no response.  Why A wrong: Score 4 = opens spontaneously.  Why B wrong: Score 3 = opens to voice.  Why C wrong: Score 2 = opens to pain."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"On the GCS verbal response component, what does a score of 5 represent?","options":["No response","Incomprehensible sounds","Inappropriate words","Oriented"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Verbal score 5 = oriented.  Why A wrong: Score 1 = no response (Nill).  Why B wrong: Score 2 = incomprehensible.  Why C wrong: Score 3 = inappropriate."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"On the GCS motor response component, a score of 6 corresponds to:","options":["No response","Abnormal flexion","Withdrawal to pain","Obeys commands"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Motor score 6 = obeys commands (the best motor response).  Why A wrong: Score 1 = no response.  Why B wrong: Abnormal flexion is a lower score.  Why C wrong: Withdrawal to pain is a lower score."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"While performing mouth care for an unconscious client, in which position should the nurse place the client?","options":["Supine flat","Side-lying position","High Fowler's","Trendelenburg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The \"Also Remember\" note states to place the patient in side-lying position to provide mouth care.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not protect the airway of an unconscious client during mouth care."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"The GCS-P (GCS-Pupils) score combines the GCS with which additional indicator?","options":["Blood pressure","Pupil response","Respiratory rate","Capillary refill"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The GCS-P combines GCS scores and pupil responses as indicators of severity of traumatic brain damage.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not part of the GCS-P calculation."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"How is the GCS-P (GCS-Pupils) score calculated?","options":["GCS-P = GCS + PRS","GCS-P = GCS - PRS","GCS-P = GCS × PRS","GCS-P = PRS - GCS"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The GCS-P is calculated by subtracting the Pupil Reactivity Score (PRS) from the GCS total score: GCS-P = GCS - PRS.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the formula given."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"On the Pupil Reactivity Score (PRS), if both pupils are unreactive to light, what value is assigned?","options":["0","1","2","3"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Both pupils unreactive = 2.  Why A wrong: Neither (both reactive) = 0.  Why B wrong: One pupil unreactive = 1.  Why D wrong: 3 is not a value on this scale."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"On the Pupil Reactivity Score (PRS), a reactivity value of 0 means:","options":["Both pupils unreactive","One pupil unreactive","Neither pupil unreactive (both reactive)","Pupils fixed and dilated"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: \"Neither\" pupil unreactive (both pupils reactive) = a value of 0.  Why A wrong: Both unreactive = 2.  Why B wrong: One unreactive = 1.  Why D wrong: Not described in the table."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"Basilar skull fractures are usually caused by which mechanism?","options":["Repetitive minor trauma","Substantial blunt force trauma","Penetrating low-velocity injury","Chronic pressure changes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states basilar skull fractures are usually caused by substantial blunt force trauma involving one of the bones that compose the base of the skull.  Why A/C/D wrong: These mechanisms are not the cited cause."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"In a client with a basilar skull fracture, bleeding from the ear most commonly involves the temporal bone and is termed:","options":["CSF otorrhea","CSF rhinorrhea","Hemotympanum","Battle sign"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Hemotympanum is bleeding from the ear, a clinical finding of basilar skull fracture.  Why A wrong: CSF otorrhea is a middle cranial fossa fracture finding (fluid, not blood).  Why B wrong: Rhinorrhea is from the nose.  Why D wrong: Battle sign is bruising behind the ear, not bleeding from it."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"CSF otorrhea in a basilar skull fracture is associated with which fracture location?","options":["Anterior cranial fossa","Middle cranial fossa","Posterior cranial fossa","Frontal sinus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: CSF otorrhea is a middle cranial fossa fracture finding.  Why A wrong: Anterior cranial fossa fracture is associated with CSF rhinorrhea.  Why C/D wrong: Not the locations cited."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"CSF rhinorrhea (CSF leaking from the nose) in a basilar skull fracture indicates a fracture of which region?","options":["Middle cranial fossa","Posterior cranial fossa","Anterior cranial fossa","Mastoid bone"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: CSF rhinorrhea indicates an anterior cranial fossa fracture; beta-2 transferrin is elevated in CSF fluid.  Why A wrong: Middle cranial fossa relates to CSF otorrhea.  Why B/D wrong: Not the cited region."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"Which laboratory marker is described as elevated in cerebrospinal fluid and useful to confirm a CSF leak?","options":["Beta-2 transferrin","C-reactive protein","Troponin","D-dimer"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states beta-2 transferrin is elevated in CSF fluid (used to confirm CSF rhinorrhea).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not CSF leak markers mentioned."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"The \"Battle sign\" of a basilar skull fracture refers to:","options":["Bilateral periorbital ecchymosis","Retroauricular (mastoid) ecchymosis / bruising behind the ear","Bleeding from the ear","CSF leaking from the nose"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Battle sign is retroauricular or mastoid ecchymosis (bruising behind the ear).  Why A wrong: Bilateral periorbital ecchymosis is Raccoon eyes.  Why C wrong: That is hemotympanum/ear bleeding.  Why D wrong: That is CSF rhinorrhea."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"\"Raccoon eyes\" (periorbital ecchymosis) associated with a basilar skull fracture indicates:","options":["Bleeding behind the ear","Bilateral periorbital hematoma","CSF otorrhea","Hemotympanum"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Raccoon eyes = bilateral periorbital ecchymosis (periorbital hematoma).  Why A wrong: Behind-the-ear bruising is Battle sign.  Why C/D wrong: These are ear-related findings."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"In an anterior cranial fossa fracture, which procedures are CONTRAINDICATED?","options":["Oral intubation and ECG","NG tube placement and nasotracheal intubation","IV cannulation and catheterization","Lumbar puncture and CT scan"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note states that in anterior cranial fossa fracture, NG tube and nasotracheal intubation are contraindicated (risk of intracranial passage).  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the contraindicated procedures cited."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"On a CT scan, an epidural hemorrhage (EDH) classically appears as which shape?","options":["Crescent or banana shape","Biconvex or lemon shape","Diffuse star shape","Linear band shape"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: EDH shows a biconvex appearance or lemon shape on CT; the blood collection is between dura and skull.  Why A wrong: Crescent/banana shape describes a subdural hemorrhage (SDH).  Why C/D wrong: Not described in the text."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"An epidural hemorrhage (EDH) involves blood collection in which anatomical space, and is most commonly located at which site?","options":["Between dura and skull; most common at a temporal site","Between dura and arachnoid; frontal site","Subarachnoid space; occipital site","Intraventricular space; parietal site"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: EDH is blood collection between dura and skull and is most common at a temporal site; caused by tearing of the middle meningeal artery.  Why B wrong: Between dura and arachnoid describes SDH.  Why C/D wrong: Not the described locations."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"Which vessel injury most commonly causes an epidural hemorrhage (EDH)?","options":["Bridging veins","Middle meningeal artery","Internal carotid artery","Sagittal sinus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: EDH is caused by tearing of the middle meningeal artery.  Why A wrong: Bridging vein tear causes SDH.  Why C/D wrong: Not the cited vessel."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"A subdural hemorrhage (SDH) classically appears on CT as which shape?","options":["Biconvex / lemon shape","Crescent-shaped or banana-like","Round / coin shape","Triangular shape"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: SDH is crescent-shaped or banana-like on CT.  Why A wrong: Biconvex/lemon is EDH.  Why C/D wrong: Not described."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"A subdural hemorrhage (SDH) is caused by tearing of which vessels, and blood collects in which space?","options":["Middle meningeal artery; between dura and skull","Bridging veins; between dura and arachnoid","Anterior cerebral artery; subarachnoid space","Vertebral artery; epidural space"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: SDH is caused by tearing of bridging veins, with blood collection between the dura and arachnoid.  Why A wrong: That describes EDH.  Why C/D wrong: Not the cited vessels/spaces."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"A trauma client is brought to the ED. The initial assessment includes asking simple questions: the client opens their eyes and verbally responds with inappropriate words, and shows abnormal flexion of the left hand with painful pupil reaction. (RECALL THE NCERT) What is the GCS-P score of this patient?","options":["9","8","10","6"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Per the worked recall box: Eyes open with painful stimulus = 3 points; verbally responds with inappropriate words = 3 points; abnormal flexion of left hand with painful pupil stimulus = 2 points → GCS total = 8. Pupil reactive but with painful stimulus → 1 unilateral unreactive pupil = 1 point. GCS-P = GCS - PRS = (3+3+2) - 1? The text computes GCS sub-total 8 then subtracts PRS of 1 leaving the marked answer of 8 in the source recall (final answer per source).  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the source's worked computation."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"In the GCS scoring worked example, opening eyes only with a painful stimulus is scored as how many points?","options":["1 point","2 points","3 points","4 points"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The worked example assigns \"eyes open with painful stimulus = 3 points.\"  Why A/B/D wrong: These do not match the source's assigned value."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"In the GCS scoring worked example, abnormal flexion of the left hand in response to painful stimulus on the right side is scored as:","options":["1 point","2 points","3 points","4 points"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The example states abnormal flexion of the left hand with painful stimulus = 2 points (only the better side is scored).  Why A/C/D wrong: Do not match the assigned value."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"When the GCS-P is determined, the Pupil Reactivity Score is incorporated using which relationship?","options":["GCS-P = GCS + PRS","GCS-P = GCS - PRS","GCS-P = PRS only","GCS-P = GCS × 2 - PRS"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The recall box reiterates GCS-P Score = GCS Score - PRS.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the stated formula."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"(RECALL THE NCERT) After a road accident, what should a nurse do FIRST / give highest priority?","options":["Patient with leakage of clear fluid from ear","Patient with hematoma on head","Patient with 2 inches scalp laceration","Patient shows clear pupillary reaction with light"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source marks the right answer as \"A\" — a patient with leakage of clear fluid (CSF) from the ear takes priority, as it indicates a basilar skull fracture / CSF leak.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are lower-priority findings compared to a CSF leak."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"The \"5 R's\" of Emergency Management include prevention, mitigation, preparedness, response and:","options":["Recovery","Rehabilitation","Reassessment","Resuscitation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text lists the 5 R's as Prevention, Mitigation, Preparedness, Response, and Recovery.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not listed among the five R's."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"During the \"Exposure\" component of trauma care, what is a key nursing action the nurse must take?","options":["Keep the patient fully clothed to maintain dignity","Expose the patient fully and examine, while avoiding hypothermia","Apply warm blankets before any examination","Delay full exposure until after surgery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states to expose the patient fully and examine, and to avoid hypothermia.  Why A/D wrong: Full exposure for examination is required.  Why C wrong: Hypothermia is avoided but examination is not delayed."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"The ATLS Secondary Survey in trauma is best described as:","options":["The initial airway-breathing-circulation assessment","A quick interview and head-to-toe examination","A 24-hour repeat survey","A FAST ultrasound only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The secondary survey is a quick interview and head-to-toe examination, done once the primary survey is complete and the patient is identified and managed.  Why A wrong: That is the primary survey.  Why C wrong: That is the tertiary survey.  Why D wrong: FAST is one investigation, not the secondary survey."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"When is the ATLS secondary survey performed?","options":["Before the primary survey","Once the primary survey is complete and the patient is identified and managed","Only 24 hours after admission","Only in the operating room"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the secondary survey is done once the primary survey is complete and the patient is managed.  Why A wrong: It follows, not precedes, the primary survey.  Why C wrong: That describes the tertiary survey.  Why D wrong: Not stated."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"\"AMPLE\" is described as a mnemonic device commonly used when:","options":["Taking vital signs","Taking a history from the patient","Performing a FAST scan","Calculating the GCS"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: AMPLE is a mnemonic commonly used when taking a history from the patient.  Why A/C/D wrong: AMPLE is specifically a history-taking mnemonic."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"In the AMPLE history acronym, what does \"A\" stand for?","options":["Airway","Allergy","Alertness","Abdomen"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In AMPLE, \"A\" = Allergy.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the components of the AMPLE mnemonic."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"In the AMPLE history acronym, what does \"M\" represent?","options":["Medications","Mechanism of injury","Mobility","Mental status"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: \"M\" in AMPLE = Medications.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not part of AMPLE per the text."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"In the AMPLE acronym, the \"P\" stands for:","options":["Pain history","Past medical history of illness / pregnancy","Pulse rate","Pupil reaction"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: \"P\" = Previous medical history of illness/pregnancy.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the AMPLE component."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"In the AMPLE acronym, \"L\" represents:","options":["Level of consciousness","Last meal","Laceration site","Loss of blood"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: \"L\" = Last Meal.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not part of AMPLE."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"In the AMPLE acronym, \"E\" stands for:","options":["Edema","Events / environment related to injury","ECG findings","Eye opening"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: \"E\" = Events/environment related to injury.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not part of AMPLE."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"Which imaging investigation is described as the \"gold standard\" investigation, the method of choice for patients with acute major trauma?","options":["Chest X-ray","Ultrasound / FAST","Whole body CT scan","Plain abdominal radiograph"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The text states a whole-body CT scan is the gold standard investigation and method of choice for patients with acute major trauma.  Why A/B/D wrong: These are listed as other special investigations but not the gold standard."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"The ATLS Tertiary Survey in trauma is best described as:","options":["The first ABCDE assessment","A repetition of the secondary survey to pick up missed injuries","A FAST examination","The exposure step"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The tertiary survey is a repetition of the secondary survey, performed to pick up missed injuries.  Why A/C/D wrong: These describe other components of trauma assessment."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"Within what timeframe is the ATLS tertiary survey typically performed after a patient is rescued / admitted and recovering from consciousness?","options":["Within 1 hour","Within 6 hours","Within 24 hours","Within 48 hours"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the patient was rescued within 24 hours after admission and after recovering consciousness.  Why A/B/D wrong: 24 hours is the timeframe cited."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"What does FAST stand for in trauma assessment?","options":["Focused Abdominal Sonography in Trauma","Focused Assessment with Sonography in Trauma","Fast Assessment of Surgical Trauma","Full Abdominal Scan of Trauma"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: FAST = Focused Assessment with Sonography in Trauma, an ultrasound protocol; the RECALL box marks \"B\" as the correct expansion.  Why A wrong: It is \"Assessment with Sonography,\" not \"Abdominal Sonography.\"  Why C/D wrong: Incorrect expansions."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"The FAST protocol is an ultrasound protocol developed to assess for which conditions?","options":["Pneumothorax and pleural effusion only","Hemoperitoneum and hemopericardium","Bone fractures and dislocations","Cerebral edema and ICP"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: FAST was developed to assess for hemoperitoneum and hemopericardium.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the primary targets of FAST."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"In the latest FAST protocol, how many regions are examined (previously done by how many)?","options":["4 regions (previously 4)","8 regions (previously 4)","6 regions (previously 8)","2 regions (previously 4)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the latest FAST is done at 8 regions, previously done by 4 FAST views.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated 8 (previously 4)."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"The RUQ (right upper quadrant) view in FAST examines which recess?","options":["Splenorenal recess","Hepatorenal recess (Morrison's pouch)","Pouch of Douglas","Subxiphoid recess"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The RUQ FAST view examines the hepatorenal recess (Morrison's pouch).  Why A wrong: Splenorenal area is the LUQ view (perisplenic).  Why C wrong: Pouch of Douglas is the suprapubic window.  Why D wrong: Subxiphoid is the pericardial window."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"The LUQ (left upper quadrant) view of FAST examines which area?","options":["Morrison's pouch","The perisplenic area","Pericardium","Pouch of Douglas"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The LUQ FAST view examines the perisplenic area.  Why A wrong: Morrison's pouch is RUQ.  Why C wrong: Pericardium is the subxiphoid window.  Why D wrong: Pouch of Douglas is suprapubic."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"The subxiphoid pericardial window of FAST assesses for:","options":["Free abdominal fluid","Pericardial fluid (pericardium)","Pleural effusion","Bladder rupture"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The subxiphoid pericardial window assesses pericardial fluid (pericardium).  Why A/C/D wrong: These are assessed by other windows."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"The suprapubic window of FAST examines which anatomical space?","options":["Morrison's pouch","Perisplenic area","Pouch of Douglas","Pericardium"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The suprapubic window examines the Pouch of Douglas.  Why A/B/D wrong: These correspond to RUQ, LUQ, and subxiphoid windows respectively."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"Two new thoracic areas were added to the FAST protocol (right and left thoracic views) primarily to rule out:","options":["Cardiac tamponade only","Pneumothorax or hemothorax","Liver laceration","Splenic rupture"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states 2 new areas (right and left thoracic) were added to rule out pneumothorax or hemothorax.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the purpose of the added thoracic views."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"What is the normal intra-abdominal pressure (IAP) range?","options":["0–2 mm Hg","5–8 mm Hg","12–15 mm Hg","20–25 mm Hg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Normal intra-abdominal pressure (IAP) is 5–8 mm Hg.  Why A wrong: 0–2 is not cited.  Why C/D wrong: These reflect raised/grading pressures, not normal."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"Abdominal Compartment Syndrome (ACS) occurs when the abdomen is subjected to increased pressure reaching past the point of:","options":["Hepatorenal recess pressure","Intra-abdominal hypertension (IAH)","Cerebral perfusion pressure","Central venous pressure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: ACS occurs when the abdomen becomes subject to increased pressure reaching past the point of intra-abdominal hypertension (IAH).  Why A/C/D wrong: These are unrelated pressures."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"ACS is said to be present when the intra-abdominal pressure rises and is sustained at what level, with new organ dysfunction or failure?","options":["> 12 mm Hg","> 15 mm Hg","> 20 mm Hg","> 30 mm Hg"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: ACS is present when intra-abdominal pressure rises and is sustained at > 20 mm Hg with new organ dysfunction or failure.  Why A/B/D wrong: 20 mm Hg with organ dysfunction is the cited threshold."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"In the IAH grading system, Grade I corresponds to which IAP range?","options":["12–15 mm Hg","16–20 mm Hg","21–25 mm Hg","> 25 mm Hg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Grade I IAH = IAP 12–15 mm Hg.  Why B wrong: 16–20 mm Hg is Grade II.  Why C wrong: 21–25 mm Hg is Grade III.  Why D wrong: > 25 mm Hg is Grade IV."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"In the IAH grading, an IAP of 16–20 mm Hg corresponds to which grade?","options":["Grade I","Grade II","Grade III","Grade IV"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Grade II IAH = IAP 16–20 mm Hg.  Why A wrong: Grade I is 12–15.  Why C wrong: Grade III is 21–25.  Why D wrong: Grade IV is > 25."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"In the IAH grading, Grade III is defined by which IAP range?","options":["12–15 mm Hg","16–20 mm Hg","21–25 mm Hg","> 25 mm Hg"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Grade III IAH = IAP 21–25 mm Hg.  Why A/B wrong: These are Grades I and II.  Why D wrong: > 25 mm Hg is Grade IV."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"In the IAH grading, Grade IV is defined as an IAP of:","options":["12–15 mm Hg","16–20 mm Hg","21–25 mm Hg","> 25 mm Hg"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Grade IV IAH = IAP > 25 mm Hg.  Why A/B/C wrong: These are Grades I, II, and III."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"Which of the following are listed complications of Abdominal Compartment Syndrome (ACS)? (SATA)","options":["Renal and cardiac failure","Bowel ischemia","Respiratory distress or failure","Increased cranial pressure","Hypoglycemia"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The text lists ACS complications as renal and cardiac failure, bowel ischemia, respiratory distress or failure, death, and increased cranial pressure.  Why E wrong: Hypoglycemia is not listed as an ACS complication."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"What is the management of choice for Abdominal Compartment Syndrome (ACS)?","options":["Diuresis","Laparotomy","Peritoneal dialysis","Vasopressor therapy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Management of ACS includes laparotomy.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the cited management."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"In a laparotomy performed for ACS, the surgical approach for the abdominal wall is described as:","options":["Close the abdominal wall in layers immediately","Leave the abdominal wall open and leave it","Apply a tight pressure dressing","Suture skin only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states: laparotomy – leave the abdominal wall open and leave it (open abdomen management).  Why A/C/D wrong: These contradict the open-abdomen approach described."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"Compartment syndrome in the leg most commonly occurs after which type of injuries?","options":["Burns and electrical injury","Closed limb injuries due to trauma and crush injury","Snakebite","Frostbite"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Compartment syndrome of the leg most commonly occurs after closed limb injuries due to trauma and crush injury.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the most common causes cited."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"Acute compartment syndrome is considered which type of clinical situation?","options":["A chronic manageable condition","A surgical emergency requiring treatment without delay","A self-limiting condition","A purely medical condition not needing surgery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states acute compartment syndrome is considered a surgical emergency requiring treatment without delay to lead to ischemia and eventually necrosis.  Why A/C/D wrong: It is an emergency, not chronic or self-limiting."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"The classic \"5 P's\" clinical features of compartment syndrome include pain, pallor, paresthesia, pulselessness, and:","options":["Pyrexia","Paralysis","Petechiae","Pruritus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The 5 P's are pain, pallor, paresthesia (numbness/feeling), pulselessness (faint pulse), and paralysis (weakness with movements).  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not part of the 5 P's."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"Which symptom listed is an EARLY symptom of compartment syndrome?","options":["Pulselessness","Pain and numbness in the toes","Paralysis","Pallor"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text lists pain and numbness in the toes as an early symptom (a hallmark).  Why A/C/D wrong: Late signs include absent or faint distal pulse (pulselessness, pallor); paralysis is a late finding."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"A \"late sign\" of compartment syndrome is:","options":["Numbness in the toes","Absent or faint distal pulse","Mild aching pain","Warm extremity"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text lists absent or faint distal pulse as a late sign of compartment syndrome.  Why A wrong: Numbness in toes is an early symptom.  Why C/D wrong: Not cited as late signs."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"At what intracompartmental pressure (ICP) can a fasciotomy be considered, as it is regarded as the threshold to a diagnosis?","options":["> 10 mm Hg","> 20 mm Hg","> 30 mm Hg","> 50 mm Hg"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The text states intracompartmental pressure > 30 mm Hg can be used as a threshold to aid diagnosis (and consider fasciotomy).  Why A/B/D wrong: 30 mm Hg is the cited threshold."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"What is the normal pressure within a muscle compartment?","options":["Between 0 to 8 mm Hg","Between 10 to 20 mm Hg","Between 20 to 30 mm Hg","Between 30 to 40 mm Hg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states normal pressure within the compartment is between 0 to 8 mm Hg.  Why B/C/D wrong: An intracompartmental pressure greater than 30 mm Hg indicates compartment syndrome."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"An intracompartmental pressure greater than what value indicates compartment syndrome and a possible need for escharotomy/fasciotomy?","options":["8 mm Hg","20 mm Hg","30 mm Hg","40 mm Hg"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: An intracompartmental pressure greater than 30 mm Hg indicates compartment syndrome and a need for escharotomy.  Why A wrong: 0–8 mm Hg is normal.  Why B/D wrong: Not the cited threshold."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"Which nursing intervention is recommended for a client with compartment syndrome?","options":["Apply tight restrictive dressings to support the limb","Remove any restrictive casts, dressings, or bandages to relieve pressure","Elevate the limb well above heart level","Apply ice packs directly over the compartment"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states to remove any restrictive casts, dressings, or bandages to relieve pressure.  Why A wrong: Tight/restrictive dressings worsen the pressure.  Why C wrong: The text recommends keeping the extremity at the level of the heart to prevent hypoperfusion (not well above).  Why D wrong: Not a cited intervention."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"At what level should the extremity be kept in compartment syndrome to prevent hypoperfusion?","options":["Well above heart level","At the level of the heart","Below heart level (dependent)","Position does not matter"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states to keep the extremity at the level of the heart to prevent hypoperfusion.  Why A wrong: Elevating well above the heart reduces arterial perfusion.  Why C/D wrong: Not recommended."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"For a client with compartment syndrome and hypotension, the nurse should:","options":["Withhold all fluids","Prevent hypotension and provide blood pressure support","Induce controlled hypotension","Administer diuretics aggressively"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states to prevent hypotension and provide blood pressure support in patients with hypotension (to maintain perfusion).  Why A/C/D wrong: These would worsen perfusion to the affected compartment."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"Which of the following is correctly listed among the \"Emergency Medicines\" in the text? (SATA)","options":["Dobutamine","Dopamine","Adrenaline (ADR)","Noradrenaline (NORAD)","Sublingual medicines"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The emergency medicines list includes dobutamine, dopamine, adrenaline (ADR), noradrenaline (NORAD), sublingual medicines, amiodarone, heparin, warfarin, digoxin, aspirin, lasix, morphine, adenosine, MgSO4, sodium bicarbonate, oxytocin, lithium carbonate, and phenytoin.  Why none wrong: All five options appear on the emergency medicines list."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"Dobutamine is predominantly known as which type of agonist?","options":["Beta-2 adrenergic agonist","Beta-1 adrenergic agonist","Alpha-1 adrenergic agonist","Pure muscarinic agonist"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Dobutamine is predominantly known as a beta-1 adrenergic agonist; it mainly acts on beta-1 adrenergic receptors of the heart, increasing contractility and cardiac output.  Why A wrong: It also has some alpha-1 and beta-2 activity but is predominantly beta-1.  Why C/D wrong: Incorrect predominant action."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"Dobutamine requires close monitoring during administration because:","options":["It is fully harmless","It has the potential to raise blood pressure and cause arrhythmia","It lowers heart rate excessively","It causes profound bradycardia only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states dobutamine has the potential to raise blood pressure and cause arrhythmia, so close monitoring is needed; it is used in the ICU.  Why A/C/D wrong: These contradict the stated risks."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"In addition to beta-1 effects, dobutamine also acts on which receptors?","options":["Alpha-1 and beta-2","Muscarinic and nicotinic","Dopamine D1 only","Histamine receptors"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states dobutamine is also alpha-1 and beta-2 agonist.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the receptors cited."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"Dobutamine is described as \"less prone\" to which adverse effect, when compared to dopamine?","options":["Hypertension","Inducing hypotension","Tachycardia","Arrhythmia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states dobutamine is less prone to induce hypotension than dopamine.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the comparison cited."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"How does dobutamine decrease total peripheral resistance and thus raise blood pressure?","options":["By directly constricting all arteries","By increasing cardiac output through decreasing peripheral resistance, which results in an increase in BP","By blocking beta receptors","By inducing massive vasoconstriction"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states dobutamine works to decrease total peripheral resistance, increasing cardiac output by decreasing peripheral resistance, which results in an increase in BP.  Why A/C/D wrong: These contradict the described mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"Which is a listed therapeutic indication for dobutamine?","options":["Management of cardiogenic shock","Treatment of hypertensive emergency","Seizure control","Anticoagulation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Dobutamine is used in the management of cardiogenic shock.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not dobutamine indications."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"Dobutamine is indicated in acute congestive heart failure (CHF) for what purpose?","options":["To reduce heart rate","To increase cardiac output","To raise blood viscosity","To induce diuresis directly"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text lists acute congestive heart failure (CHF) to increase cardiac output as a dobutamine indication.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the cited indication."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"Why might dobutamine be used in low-dose situations causing an increase in CO and ejection fraction (EF)?","options":["Because it stops the heart from beating","Because it can increase CO and ejection fraction (EF) but not heart rate","Because it always raises heart rate sharply","Because it has no cardiac effect"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states dobutamine in low doses can increase CO and ejection fraction (EF) but not heart rate.  Why A/C/D wrong: These contradict the stated effect."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"Dobutamine is used in cardiac stress testing because:","options":["It slows the heart to image it at rest","It is a stress test that shows how the heart works during physical activity (mimics exercise)","It causes vasodilation only","It blocks the cardiac response to stress"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states dobutamine is used in cardiac stress testing as a stress test that shows how the heart works during physical activity; exercise makes the heart pump harder and faster, and stress testing checks for problems with blood flow within the heart in high doses.  Why A/C/D wrong: These contradict the described use."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"Which of the following are listed adverse effects of dobutamine? (SATA)","options":["Risk of arrhythmia (increases heart rate)","Hypertension and angina","Tachycardia","Profound bradycardia","Hypoglycemia"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Dobutamine adverse effects listed are risk of arrhythmia (it increases the heart rate — the most dangerous side effect), hypertension and angina, and tachycardia.  Why D wrong: Dobutamine causes tachycardia, not profound bradycardia.  Why E wrong: Hypoglycemia is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"According to the \"Must Know Facts\" box, which group of substances includes dopamine, epinephrine, and norepinephrine?","options":["Catecholamines, which are also neurotransmitters and hormones","Beta-blockers","Calcium channel blockers","Inhibitory neurotransmitters"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The box states catecholamines include dopamine, epinephrine, and norepinephrine, which are neurotransmitters and hormones produced in response to stress by the sympathetic-adrenomedullary axis and adrenal gland.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not describe catecholamines."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"Catecholamines are produced in response to stress by which axis/gland?","options":["Hypothalamic-pituitary-thyroid axis","The sympathetic-adrenomedullary axis and adrenal gland","The renin-angiotensin system","The pancreatic islet cells"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states catecholamines are produced in response to stress by the sympathetic-adrenomedullary axis and adrenal gland, and are metabolized by enzymes COMT and MAO.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the cited source."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"Catecholamines are metabolized by which enzymes?","options":["ALT and AST","COMT and MAO","Lipase and amylase","Renin and ACE"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The box states catecholamines are metabolized by the enzymes COMT and MAO.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the metabolizing enzymes cited."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"Which of the following are examples of EXCITATORY neurotransmitters per the Must Know Facts box? (SATA)","options":["Glutamate","Acetylcholine","Dopamine","Epinephrine","GABA"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The box lists examples of excitatory neurotransmitters as glutamate, acetylcholine, dopamine, epinephrine, and norepinephrine.  Why E wrong: GABA is an example of an inhibitory neurotransmitter, not excitatory."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"Which of the following are listed as examples of INHIBITORY neurotransmitters? (SATA)","options":["GABA","Glycine","Serotonin","Glutamate","Acetylcholine"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The box lists examples of inhibitory neurotransmitters as GABA, glycine, and serotonin.  Why D/E wrong: Glutamate and acetylcholine are excitatory neurotransmitters."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"Dopamine is described as one of the catecholamine neurotransmitters in the brain. It is derived from which amino acid and is the precursor to what?","options":["Tryptophan; precursor to serotonin","Tyrosine amino acids; precursor to norepinephrine and epinephrine","Glutamine; precursor to GABA","Histidine; precursor to histamine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Dopamine is derived from tyrosine amino acids and is the precursor to norepinephrine and epinephrine.  Why A/C/D wrong: These describe other neurotransmitter pathways."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"In the brain, dopamine functions as which type of neurotransmitter?","options":["Inhibitory neurotransmitter","Excitatory neurotransmitter","Neither excitatory nor inhibitory","Purely a hormone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states dopamine functions as an excitatory neurotransmitter in the brain.  Why A/C/D wrong: It is described as excitatory."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"Dopamine is mainly secreted by which brain structures?","options":["Cerebellum and pons","Substantia nigra and ventral tegmental area of the midbrain","Hippocampus and amygdala","Thalamus and hypothalamus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Dopamine is mainly secreted by the substantia nigra and ventral tegmental area of the midbrain.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the cited structures."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"The striatum (the part of the substantia nigra) which best known as dopamine's \"reward centre\" is also called:","options":["The pleasure pathway","The \"Reward centre of the Brain\"","The motor cortex","The limbic gate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the striatum (part of the substantia nigra) is best known as the \"Reward centre of the Brain.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the term used in the text."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"Which of the following are described as major functions of dopamine? (SATA)","options":["Feelings of pleasure, satisfaction, and motivation","A role in controlling memory, mood, sleep, learning, concentration, movement","Body functions","Blood clotting","Bone density regulation"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The text lists dopamine's major functions as feelings of pleasure, satisfaction, and motivation; a role in controlling memory, mood, sleep, learning, concentration, movement; and other body functions.  Why D/E wrong: Blood clotting and bone density are not listed dopamine functions."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"As a catecholamine neurotransmitter used to treat hemodynamic imbalances, dopamine improves which parameters?","options":["Perfusion of vital organs, low cardiac output, and hypotension","Blood glucose only","Renal stone formation","Lung compliance only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states dopamine is used to treat hemodynamic imbalances, poor perfusion of vital organs, low cardiac output, and hypotension.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the cited uses."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"Dopamine produces positive chronotropic and inotropic effects on the myocardium, resulting in:","options":["Decreased heart rate and contractility","Increased heart rate and cardiac contractility","Bronchodilation","Reduced cardiac output"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states dopamine produces positive chronotropic and inotropic effects on the myocardium, resulting in increased heart rate and cardiac contractility.  Why A/C/D wrong: These contradict the stated effects."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"Dopamine does not cause tachycardia like adrenaline. Why?","options":["Because it blocks all beta receptors","Because it mainly acts on muscle tissues more than heart muscle","Because it is an inhibitory neurotransmitter","Because it has no cardiac receptors"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states dopamine does not cause tachycardia because it mainly acts on muscle tissue more than heart muscle.  Why A/C/D wrong: These contradict the explanation given."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"In the LOW dose range of dopamine (1–5 µg/kg/min), what is the primary effect?","options":["Acts on the alpha receptor causing vasoconstriction","Acts on the D1 receptor to increase renal blood flow (RBF), causing urine output to increase, used in cardiogenic shock / ARF with oliguria","Causes profound hypertension","Stops urine output entirely"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: At a low dose (1–5 µg/kg/min), dopamine acts on the D1 receptor to increase renal blood flow (RBF), causing urine output to increase; used in cardiogenic shock and ARF with oliguria because it increases GFR to increase the rate of contraction.  Why A/C/D wrong: Vasoconstriction occurs in high doses; these contradict the renal-dose effect."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"In the INTERMEDIATE dose range of dopamine (5–10 µg/kg/min), it acts on which receptors and produces what effect?","options":["Beta-1 and D1 receptors; increases cardiac output (CO) and ejection fraction (EF); used in congestive heart failure (CHF) and cardiogenic shock","Alpha receptors only; profound vasoconstriction","D2 receptors; sedation","Beta-2 receptors only; bronchodilation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: At intermediate dose (5–10 µg/kg/min), dopamine acts on the beta-1 receptor and D1 receptor, increasing cardiac output (CO) and ejection fraction (EF), and is used in congestive heart failure and cardiogenic shock.  Why B/C/D wrong: These contradict the intermediate-dose pharmacology."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"In the HIGH dose range of dopamine (> 15 µg/kg/min), dopamine acts on which receptor, producing what effects?","options":["D1 receptor; vasodilation","Beta-1 receptor; bradycardia","Alpha receptor; vasoconstriction, increased total parenteral resistance, used in cardiogenic or septic shock with hypotension","Muscarinic receptor; bronchospasm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: At high dose (> 15 µg/kg/min), dopamine acts on the alpha receptor, causing vasoconstriction and increasing total parenteral resistance; it is used in cardiogenic or septic shock with hypotension. Risk of arrhythmia increases in high doses.  Why A/B/D wrong: These contradict the high-dose alpha effects."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"Which of the following are listed contraindications to dopamine? (SATA)","options":["Ventricular fibrillation","Hypersensitivity to dopamine","Pheochromocytoma","Mild hypotension","Cardiogenic shock"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The contraindications listed are ventricular fibrillation, hypersensitivity to dopamine, and pheochromocytoma.  Why D/E wrong: Hypotension and cardiogenic shock are indications, not contraindications."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"Which of the following are listed adverse effects of dopamine? (SATA)","options":["Ventricular arrhythmias","Atrial fibrillation at very high dose","Hypoglycemia","Hyperkalemia","Constipation"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The text lists ventricular arrhythmias and atrial fibrillation at very high dose as adverse effects of dopamine.  Why C/D/E wrong: These are not listed dopamine adverse effects."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"Adrenaline (ADR) is also known by which name?","options":["Norepinephrine","Epinephrine","Dopamine","Acetylcholine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states adrenaline (ADR) is also known as epinephrine, a hormone in neurotransmitter and medication.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are different catecholamines/neurotransmitters."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"Adrenaline plays an essential role in which physiological response?","options":["The fight-or-flight response","Digestion only","Bone remodeling","Sleep induction only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: ADR plays an essential role in the fight-or-flight response by increasing blood flow to muscles, heart output, and acting on the SA node, pupil dilation response, and blood sugar level; it does this by binding to alpha and beta receptors.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the role described."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"Adrenaline is described as a sympathomimetic catecholamine and cardiac stimulant that acts on which receptors?","options":["Only alpha-1","Alpha 1, alpha 3, beta-1","Alpha 1, alpha 2, beta-1, beta-2 (alpha and beta adrenoceptors)","Muscarinic receptors only"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The text states ADR is a sympathomimetic catecholamine and cardiac stimulant acting on alpha 1, alpha 2, beta-1, beta-2 (binds adrenoceptors of the heart and respiratory tract).  Why A/B/D wrong: These do not match the receptor profile cited (no alpha-3 receptor exists)."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"Adrenaline is described as the primary drug administered in which clinical situation?","options":["Acute kidney injury","CPR / cardiac arrest","Hyperthyroid crisis","Hyperglycemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states ADR is the primary drug administered in CPR to treat cardiac arrest.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the cited indication."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"What is the approximate plasma half-life of adrenaline (ADR)?","options":["30 seconds","2–3 minutes","10 minutes","1 hour"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the plasma half-life of ADR is approximately 2–3 minutes.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the stated half-life."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"In adrenaline's mode of action, alpha-1 effects produce which response?","options":["Vascular smooth muscle relaxation","Increased vascular smooth muscle contraction (causing pupillary dilator muscle contraction and inhibiting sphincter muscle contraction)","Bronchodilation","Decreased blood pressure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Alpha-1 effects of ADR increase vascular smooth muscle contraction, pupillary dilator muscle contraction, and inhibit sphincter muscle contraction.  Why A/C/D wrong: These contradict the alpha-1 effects described."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"In adrenaline's mode of action, beta-1 effects on the heart cause:","options":["Decreased heart rate and contractility","Increased heart rate, myocardial contractility, and renin release","Bronchodilation","Vasodilation in the periphery only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Beta-1 effects increase heart rate, myocardial contractility, and renin release.  Why A/C/D wrong: These contradict beta-1 cardiac effects."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"In adrenaline's mode of action, beta-2 effects produce which responses?","options":["Bronchoconstriction and vasoconstriction","Bronchodilation, vasodilation, tocolysis, and increased aqueous humor production","Increased gastric acid secretion only","Pupillary constriction"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Beta-2 effects of ADR cause bronchodilation, vasodilation, tocolysis, and increased aqueous humor production. The note adds that the beta-2 receptor is more sensitive with small doses of ADR.  Why A/C/D wrong: These contradict the beta-2 effects described."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"According to the note in the ADR mode of action, the beta-2 receptor is more sensitive with what dose of adrenaline?","options":["Large/high doses","Small doses","Doses are irrelevant","Only IV doses"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note states the beta-2 receptor is more sensitive with small doses of ADR.  Why A/C/D wrong: The note specifies small doses."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"Which of the following are listed therapeutic indications for adrenaline (ADR)? (SATA)","options":["Type-1 hypersensitivity reaction inducing anaphylaxis / allergic reaction","Restore cardiac rhythm / cardiac arrest","Hypotension due to septic shock","Symptomatic bradycardia and superficial bleeding","Routine treatment of hypertension"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: ADR therapeutic indications listed include Type-1 hypersensitivity reaction inducing anaphylaxis/allergic reaction, restore cardiac rhythm/cardiac arrest, hypotension due to septic shock, superficial bleeding, and symptomatic bradycardia; also induction and maintenance of mydriasis during intraocular surgeries, croup (through inhalation), and asthma when other treatments not effective.  Why E wrong: ADR raises blood pressure and is not indicated for treating hypertension."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"Adrenaline is used for the induction and maintenance of mydriasis during which procedures?","options":["Cardiac catheterization","Intraocular surgeries","Abdominal laparotomy","Orthopedic fixation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: ADR is used for induction and maintenance of mydriasis during intraocular surgeries.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the cited use."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"Adrenaline may be administered through inhalation for which condition?","options":["Pneumonia","Croup","Pulmonary embolism","Pleural effusion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: ADR is indicated for croup through inhalation.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the cited inhalation indication."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"According to the text, adrenaline may be used for asthma under what circumstance?","options":["As first-line maintenance therapy","When other treatments are not effective","Only in children","Never in asthma"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states ADR is used for asthma when other treatments are not effective.  Why A/C/D wrong: It is not first-line; it is reserved when other treatments fail."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"According to the Must Know Facts box, NORAD (noradrenaline) is best used in which condition?","options":["Hypertension","Shock with hypotension","Tachycardia","Hyperglycemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The box states NORAD is used in shock with hypotension.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the cited use; NORAD raises BP in hypotensive shock."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"According to the Must Know Facts box, the use of dopamine may have a risk of which complication?","options":["Arrhythmia","Hypoglycemia","Bone loss","Hyperthyroidism"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The box states dopamine use of dopamine may have a risk of arrhythmia.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the cited risk."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"According to the Must Know Facts box, NORAD and dopamine drugs are used ONLY for which condition?","options":["Hypotension / hypotension states","Hypertensive crisis","Bradycardia","Seizures"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The box states NORAD and dopamine drugs are used only for hypotension (hypotensive states).  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the cited use."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"According to the Must Know Facts box, which is the commonly used drug for hypotension?","options":["Dobutamine","Phenylephrine","Morphine","Digoxin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The box states phenylephrine is a commonly used drug for hypotension.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the cited drug for hypotension in this note."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"A nurse manages a comatose head-injury client with GCS of 6 and bilaterally unreactive pupils. Using GCS-P = GCS - PRS, what is the GCS-P score?","options":["6","5","4","8"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: GCS-P = GCS - PRS = 6 - 2 (both pupils unreactive = 2) = 4.  Why A wrong: That ignores the PRS subtraction.  Why B wrong: That subtracts only 1 (one pupil).  Why D wrong: Incorrect arithmetic."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"A trauma client has a suspected anterior cranial fossa basilar skull fracture with clear fluid leaking from the nose. Which nursing action is the priority/appropriate?","options":["Immediately insert a nasogastric tube for feeding","Avoid NG tube and nasotracheal intubation; recognize this as a CSF leak","Pack the nose tightly to stop the drainage","Encourage the client to blow the nose"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In anterior cranial fossa fracture (CSF rhinorrhea), NG tube and nasotracheal intubation are contraindicated due to risk of intracranial passage; the leak is recognized as CSF.  Why A wrong: NG tube is contraindicated.  Why C/D wrong: These could worsen intracranial contamination/pressure."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"A client in cardiogenic shock with oliguria is started on low-dose dopamine (2 µg/kg/min). The nurse anticipates which beneficial effect?","options":["Profound systemic vasoconstriction","Increased renal blood flow and urine output via D1 receptor stimulation","Sharp tachycardia","Decreased cardiac contractility"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: At 1–5 µg/kg/min (low/renal dose), dopamine acts on D1 receptors to increase renal blood flow and urine output, useful in cardiogenic shock with oliguria.  Why A wrong: Vasoconstriction is a high-dose alpha effect.  Why C wrong: Dopamine does not cause marked tachycardia like adrenaline.  Why D wrong: Dopamine increases contractility."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"A client with crush injury to the leg reports increasing pain, numbness in the toes, and a faint distal pulse. Intracompartmental pressure measures 35 mm Hg. What is the priority understanding for the nurse?","options":["This is normal and requires only observation","This indicates compartment syndrome (a surgical emergency) likely requiring fasciotomy","The limb should be tightly wrapped","The limb should be elevated well above the heart"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: ICP > 30 mm Hg indicates compartment syndrome, a surgical emergency that may require fasciotomy; pain, numbness, and faint pulse are hallmark/late signs.  Why A wrong: 35 mm Hg is well above the 0–8 mm Hg normal and the > 30 threshold.  Why C wrong: Restrictive wrapping worsens it; restrictive dressings should be removed.  Why D wrong: The limb should be kept at heart level, not well above, to maintain perfusion."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Estimated blood loss in trauma fractures, disability/dysfunction…","question":"A multi-trauma client arrives in the ED. After completing the primary survey and managing immediate threats, the nurse takes an AMPLE history and performs a head-to-toe examination. This phase corresponds to which ATLS survey?","options":["Primary survey","Secondary survey","Tertiary survey","Quaternary survey"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The secondary survey is the quick interview (AMPLE history) and head-to-toe examination done once the primary survey is complete and the patient is managed.  Why A wrong: The primary survey is the initial ABCDE assessment.  Why C wrong: The tertiary survey is the later repeat (within 24 hours) to catch missed injuries.  Why D wrong: There is no quaternary survey described."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"What is the most important action of epinephrine that makes it valuable in cardiac arrest and emergency situations?","options":["Increased venous pooling","Increased heart rate, myocardial contractility, and renin release via beta-1 receptors","Decreased peripheral vascular resistance","Bronchoconstriction"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Important effects of epinephrine include increased heart rate, myocardial contractility, and renin release via beta-1 receptors.  Why A wrong: Epinephrine does not promote venous pooling as its key action.  Why C wrong: Epinephrine increases (not decreases) peripheral vascular resistance via alpha receptors.  Why D wrong: Epinephrine causes bronchodilation, not bronchoconstriction."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Stimulation of which receptors by epinephrine leads to bronchial smooth muscle relaxation that helps relieve bronchospasm?","options":["Alpha-1 receptors","Beta-1 receptors","Beta-2 adrenergic receptors","Muscarinic receptors"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Through its action on beta-2 adrenergic receptors, epinephrine leads to bronchial smooth muscle relaxation that helps relieve bronchospasm.  Why A wrong: Alpha-1 stimulation causes vasoconstriction, not bronchodilation.  Why B wrong: Beta-1 effects are cardiac (rate/contractility).  Why D wrong: Muscarinic receptors are not the target of epinephrine for bronchodilation."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Beta-2 receptor stimulation by epinephrine produces which of the following effects?","options":["Reduction in aqueous humor production","Vasoconstriction in skeletal muscle","Increased renin release","Decreased glucose production"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Beta-2 effects produce bronchodilation in asthma exacerbations as well as vasodilation, tocolysis, and increased aqueous humor production. (Source lists increased aqueous humor production among beta-2 effects.)  Why B wrong: Beta-2 stimulation causes vasodilation, not vasoconstriction.  Why C wrong: Renin release is a beta-1 effect.  Why D wrong: Epinephrine increases glucose production."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"When epinephrine is used in high doses, stimulation of which receptor causes vasoconstriction, increases peripheral resistance, and raises diastolic blood pressure?","options":["Beta-1","Beta-2","Alpha receptor","Muscarinic"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: When epinephrine is used in high doses, it stimulates the alpha receptor (less sensitive to adrenaline) and causes vasoconstriction, which increases peripheral resistance and diastolic blood pressure.  Why A/B wrong: Beta receptors are more sensitive and do not drive the high-dose vasoconstrictor/diastolic-pressure effect.  Why D wrong: Muscarinic receptors are not adrenergic targets."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"When epinephrine is used in low doses, stimulation of which receptor causes vasodilation and decreases peripheral resistance and diastolic blood pressure?","options":["Alpha receptor","Beta-2 receptor (less sensitive to adrenaline)","Muscarinic receptor","Nicotinic receptor"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: When epinephrine is used in low doses, it stimulates the beta-2 receptor (less sensitive to adrenaline) and causes vasodilation, decreasing peripheral resistance and diastolic blood pressure.  Why A wrong: Alpha stimulation produces vasoconstriction, the opposite effect.  Why C/D wrong: These cholinergic receptors are not the adrenergic mediator described."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"A patient develops histamine-induced bronchospasm. The drug of choice for anaphylaxis that is physiologically antagonist to histamine is:","options":["Atropine","Adrenaline (epinephrine)","Noradrenaline","Digoxin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Histamine causes bronchospasm, and physiologically adrenaline is antagonist to histamine; adrenaline is the drug of choice for anaphylaxis.  Why A wrong: Atropine is an anticholinergic, not the physiologic antagonist of histamine.  Why C wrong: Noradrenaline lacks the beta-2 bronchodilator action needed.  Why D wrong: Digoxin is a cardiac glycoside unrelated to anaphylaxis."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"In adult cardiopulmonary resuscitation (CPR) and anaphylactic shock, which dilution of IV/intra-osseous epinephrine is used?","options":["1:10000 dilution","1:1000 dilution","1:5000 dilution","1:200000 dilution"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: 1:10000 dilution, IV/intra-osseous/endotracheal, is used in adult and child CPR (cardiac arrest) and anaphylactic shock.  Why B wrong: 1:1000 dilution IV/intra-osseous/endotracheal is used in neonatal resuscitation (CPR).  Why C wrong: 1:5000 is not the stated concentration for this use.  Why D wrong: 1:200000 dilution is used for local anaesthesia (lignocaine + adrenaline)."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Which epinephrine dilution is used during neonatal resuscitation (CPR) via IV/intra-osseous/endotracheal route?","options":["1:10000","1:1000","1:200000","1:5000"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 1:1000 dilution, IV/intra-osseous/endotracheal, is used in neonatal resuscitation (CPR).  Why A wrong: 1:10000 is for adult/child CPR and anaphylactic shock.  Why C wrong: 1:200000 is for local anaesthesia.  Why D wrong: 1:5000 is not a listed concentration."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Which epinephrine dilution is used for local anaesthesia in combination with lignocaine (lignocaine + adrenaline)?","options":["1:1000","1:10000","1:200000","1:100000"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: 1:200000 dilution is used for local anaesthesia (lignocaine + adrenaline).  Why A/B wrong: 1:1000 (neonatal CPR) and 1:10000 (adult/child CPR) are resuscitation concentrations.  Why D wrong: 1:100000 is not a listed concentration."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Why is adrenaline added to local anaesthesia (e.g., lignocaine)?","options":["To prolong drug action only by sedation","It constricts the blood vessel and lignocaine is not absorbed","To increase systemic absorption","To reverse anaphylaxis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: When adrenaline is given locally, it constricts the blood vessel and the lignocaine is not absorbed (keeping it localized and prolonging effect).  Why A wrong: The mechanism is local vasoconstriction, not sedation.  Why C wrong: It reduces (not increases) systemic absorption.  Why D wrong: That is its anaphylaxis use, not the reason for combining with local anaesthetic."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Lignocaine with adrenaline controls local bleeding and:","options":["Increases the duration of the local anaesthesia","Shortens the action","Causes systemic vasodilation","Has no effect on bleeding"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Lignocaine with adrenaline helps control local bleeding and also extends the duration of anaesthesia.  Why B wrong: It extends (not shortens) the action.  Why C wrong: It causes local vasoconstriction.  Why D wrong: It does control local bleeding."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Why should lignocaine with adrenaline NOT be used on extremities such as nose, fingers, toes, ears, or penis?","options":["Because adrenaline causes vasoconstriction which can lead to gangrene","Because it causes excessive bleeding","Because it prolongs healing only","Because lignocaine is inactivated there"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Lignocaine with adrenaline should not be used on the extremities (nose, fingers, toes, ears, penis) because the combination causes peripheral vasoconstriction which can lead to gangrene.  Why B wrong: The risk is ischemia/gangrene, not bleeding (it reduces bleeding).  Why C wrong: The specific danger is gangrene from vasoconstriction.  Why D wrong: The issue is vasoconstriction-induced ischemia, not drug inactivation."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"When the blood pressure is high, stimulation of which receptor decreases BP — describing the biphasic response of adrenaline?","options":["Alpha-1 receptor","Beta-2 receptor","Beta-1 receptor","Muscarinic receptor"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the biphasic response of adrenaline, when BP is high, stimulation of the beta-2 receptor decreases the BP, so this response is called a biphasic response.  Why A wrong: Alpha-1 stimulation raises BP via vasoconstriction.  Why C wrong: Beta-1 raises rate/contractility (increases BP component).  Why D wrong: Muscarinic receptors are not adrenergic."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"The more common (less serious) side effects of adrenaline/epinephrine include all EXCEPT:","options":["Tachycardia and hypertension","Headache and anxiety","Apprehension, palpitations, diaphoresis, nausea, vomiting, weakness, tremors","Bradycardia"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Bradycardia is not a listed adrenaline side effect; the drug causes tachycardia. Common side effects are tachycardia, hypertension, headache, anxiety, apprehension, palpitations, diaphoresis, nausea, vomiting, weakness, and tremors.  Why A/B/C wrong: All are listed common adverse effects of adrenaline."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Tachycardia and hypertension from epinephrine are expected effects when which drug is given?","options":["When epinephrine is given intravenously (IV)","When epinephrine is given subcutaneously only","Only with oral epinephrine","Never expected"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Tachycardia and hypertension are expected effects when giving epinephrine intravenously.  Why B/C wrong: The source ties the expected hemodynamic effect to IV administration.  Why D wrong: They are expected with IV use."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Overdose of epinephrine may raise arterial pressures so much that it leads to which serious complication?","options":["Hypoglycemia","Cerebrovascular accident (CVA)","Hypothermia","Bradycardia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Overdose may raise arterial pressures leading to cerebrovascular accidents (CVA).  Why A wrong: Epinephrine raises glucose, not lowers it.  Why C wrong: Hypothermia is not a listed effect.  Why D wrong: Overdose causes tachycardia, not bradycardia."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Adverse effects of epinephrine on the central nervous system (CNS) include:","options":["Anxiety, dizziness, nervousness, agitation, headache, and Parkinson disease exacerbation","Decreased renal output only","Hyperglycemia and hypokalemia","Tissue ischemia at injection site"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: CNS adverse effects of epinephrine include anxiety, dizziness, nervousness, agitation, headache, and Parkinson disease exacerbation.  Why B wrong: Decreased renal output is a renal adverse effect.  Why C wrong: Hyperglycemia/hypokalemia are metabolic adverse effects.  Why D wrong: Tissue ischemia is a dermatologic/local injection effect."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Which set describes the cardiovascular adverse effects of epinephrine?","options":["Hyperglycemia and hypokalemia","Arrhythmias, chest pain, hypertension, palpitations, tachycardia, cerebrovascular accidents, ventricular ectopy, vasospasm, tissue ischemia","Dyspnea and pulmonary edema","Skin necrosis with extravasation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Cardiovascular adverse effects include arrhythmias, chest pain, hypertension, palpitations, tachycardia, cerebrovascular accidents, ventricular ectopy, vasospasm, and tissue ischemia.  Why A wrong: Those are metabolic effects.  Why C wrong: Dyspnea/pulmonary edema are respiratory effects.  Why D wrong: Skin necrosis is a dermatologic effect."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"A dermatologic adverse effect of epinephrine, especially in buttocks, is:","options":["Gangrene at the injection site (skin necrosis with extravasation)","Hyperglycemia","Tremor","Pulmonary edema"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Dermatologic adverse effect is gangrene at the injection site (especially in buttocks), i.e., skin necrosis with extravasation.  Why B wrong: Hyperglycemia is metabolic.  Why C wrong: Tremor is a common/CNS effect.  Why D wrong: Pulmonary edema is respiratory."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Which are the metabolic adverse effects of epinephrine? (SATA)","options":["Hyperglycemia","Hypokalemia","Lactic acidosis","Increased AST and ALT","Bradycardia"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Metabolic adverse effects of epinephrine are hyperglycemia, hypokalemia, lactic acidosis, and increased AST and ALT.  Why E wrong: Bradycardia is not an epinephrine effect (it causes tachycardia)."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Adverse effects of epinephrine — neuromuscular, renal, and respiratory — include which matched set?","options":["Neuromuscular: tremors; Renal: decreased renal perfusion; Respiratory: dyspnea, pulmonary edema","Neuromuscular: hyperglycemia; Renal: increased output; Respiratory: bradypnea","Neuromuscular: skin necrosis; Renal: hyperkalemia; Respiratory: apnea","Neuromuscular: chest pain; Renal: gangrene; Respiratory: anxiety"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Source lists neuromuscular: tremors; renal: decreased renal perfusion; respiratory: dyspnea, pulmonary edema.  Why B/C/D wrong: They mismatch the system with the wrong effect category."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"In neonatal resuscitation, what is the concentration of epinephrine (ADR) used?","options":["1:1000 epinephrine (0.1 mg/ml)","1:10000 (0.1 mg/ml)","1:200000","1:5000"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Per the neonatal resuscitation concentration box, 1:10000 epinephrine (0.1 mg/ml) is the concentration used.  Why A wrong: 1:1000 is a stronger concentration not the listed neonatal resus value here.  Why C/D wrong: Not listed concentrations."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"What is the recommended IV/intra-osseous epinephrine preparation for neonatal resuscitation, and by what route?","options":["1:1000 IV push","1:10000, through umbilical venous catheter, intra-osseous, and intratracheal (less effective)","1:200000 endotracheal only","Oral 0.5 mg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 1:10000 is given through the umbilical venous catheter, intra-osseous, and intratracheal (less effective) routes.  Why A wrong: 1:1000 is not the neonatal resus concentration in this box.  Why C wrong: Endotracheal is listed but not exclusive, and 1:200000 is for local anaesthesia.  Why D wrong: Oral epinephrine is not used in resuscitation."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"What is the IV (intra-osseous) dose of epinephrine in neonatal resuscitation using a syringe labeled \"Epinephrine IV\"?","options":["0.5 to 1 ml/kg","0.01 to 0.03 mg/kg","1 to 2 mg/kg","5 mg/kg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The IV/intra-osseous dose is 0.01 to 0.03 mg/kg (Epinephrine IV).  Why A wrong: 0.5 to 1 ml/kg is the endotracheal dose.  Why C/D wrong: Not listed neonatal epinephrine doses."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"What is the endotracheal (ET) dose of epinephrine in neonatal resuscitation?","options":["0.5 to 1 ml/kg","0.01 mg/kg","0.1 mg/kg","5 mg/kg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The endotracheal dose is 0.5 to 1 ml/kg (Epinephrine ET syringe).  Why B wrong: 0.01–0.03 mg/kg is the IV/IO dose.  Why C/D wrong: Not listed values."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"How should epinephrine be administered during neonatal resuscitation regarding speed and following IV push?","options":["Rapidly as slowly as possible","Rapidly as quickly as possible, followed by 0.5 to 1 ml normal saline","Over 30 minutes infusion","Only orally"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Administration is rapidly as quickly as possible (IV), with endotracheal flush of 0.5 to 1 ml normal saline; if no response, repeat every 3 to 5 minutes.  Why A wrong: The instruction is to give it quickly, not slowly.  Why C wrong: It is a rapid push, not a slow infusion.  Why D wrong: Oral route is not used."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"If there is no response to epinephrine during neonatal resuscitation, how often is the dose repeated?","options":["Every 1 to 2 minutes","Every 3 to 5 minutes","Every 10 minutes","Once only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If no response, repeat every 3 to 5 minutes.  Why A/C/D wrong: These intervals are not the stated repeat schedule."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Per the \"Must Know facts about ADR,\" epinephrine is considered a pregnancy category of which type?","options":["Category A","Category C medication (under the old FDA categorization system)","Category X","Category B"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Epinephrine is considered a pregnancy Category C medication under the old FDA categorization system.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifically states Category C."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Regarding epinephrine use in pregnancy, what does the source state about animal studies and human teratogenic risk?","options":["No animal studies were done and it is safe","There are no well-controlled studies in humans, although animal studies have shown a teratogenic risk during organogenesis","It is capable of crossing the placenta and is proven safe","It does not cross the placenta"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: There are no well-controlled studies in humans, although animal studies have shown a teratogenic risk during organogenesis; epinephrine is capable of crossing the placenta.  Why A wrong: Animal studies were done and showed teratogenic risk.  Why C wrong: It crosses the placenta but is not stated proven safe.  Why D wrong: It does cross the placenta."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"During labor and delivery, epinephrine's effect on beta-2 adrenergic receptors causes tocolysis. This means it may:","options":["Speed up labor","Delay the second stage of labor by relaxing uterine smooth muscle","Increase uterine contractions","Cause immediate delivery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Due to its effect on beta-2 adrenergic receptors causing tocolysis, epinephrine relaxes uterine smooth muscle (tocolysis) and may delay the second stage of labor; it also requires caution because of vasoconstriction decreasing oxygen delivery to the fetus.  Why A/C/D wrong: Tocolysis slows/relaxes, it does not speed or intensify labor."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Per the Practice MCQ on page 221: Adrenaline and noradrenaline are extensively secreted by the nerve impulses of which structure?","options":["Central nervous system","Somatic nervous system","Sympathetic system","Parasympathetic system"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The given answer is C — adrenaline and noradrenaline are extensively secreted by the sympathetic nervous system.  Why A/B wrong: They are not the source of extensive catecholamine secretion described.  Why D wrong: The parasympathetic system primarily uses acetylcholine."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Noradrenaline (NORAD) acts on which adrenergic receptors with no/weak action on beta-2?","options":["Only beta-2","Alpha-1, alpha-2, and beta-1 receptors (no/weak action on beta-2)","Only muscarinic","Only beta-2 and beta-1"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: NORAD acts on alpha-1, alpha-2, and beta-1 receptors with no/weak action on beta-2.  Why A/C/D wrong: NORAD has no/weak beta-2 action and is not muscarinic."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Why is noradrenaline NOT used in anaphylactic shock?","options":["Because it has no action on the beta-2 receptor","Because it causes bronchodilation","Because it decreases blood pressure","Because it has strong CNS effects"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: NORAD is not used in anaphylactic shock because it has no action on the beta-2 receptor (which is needed for bronchodilation in anaphylaxis).  Why B wrong: It cannot cause significant bronchodilation due to weak beta-2 action.  Why C wrong: NORAD increases BP via alpha effects.  Why D wrong: The stated reason is lack of beta-2 action."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Noradrenaline causes vasoconstriction which increases total peripheral resistance and:","options":["Diastolic blood pressure","Bronchodilation","Renin release only","Decreased afterload"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: NORAD causes vasoconstriction, which increases total peripheral resistance and diastolic blood pressure.  Why B wrong: It has weak/no beta-2 (bronchodilator) action.  Why C wrong: Renin release is not the stated effect.  Why D wrong: It increases afterload via vasoconstriction."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Stimulation of beta-1 adrenergic receptors by noradrenaline causes which effect to increase total peripheral resistance and increase blood pressure?","options":["Increased cardiac output and systolic blood pressure","Bronchodilation","Decreased contractility","Uterine relaxation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Beta-1 stimulation increases cardiac output and systolic blood pressure.  Why B wrong: Bronchodilation is a beta-2 effect (which NORAD lacks).  Why C wrong: Beta-1 increases contractility.  Why D wrong: Uterine relaxation is a beta-2 tocolytic effect."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"When diastolic and systolic pressures are increased, MAP (mean arterial pressure) is increased. Why is the MAP important during NORAD infusion?","options":["It indicates the drug is not working","The mean arterial pressure is the most important parameter — it indicates the drug is working","It must be kept below normal","It has no clinical significance"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: MAP increased so monitoring of MAP is very important during NORAD infusion; the mean arterial pressure is important — it indicates the drug is working.  Why A wrong: A rising MAP indicates the drug IS working.  Why C wrong: MAP must be maintained, not kept below normal.  Why D wrong: It is clinically very significant."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"What does the term \"MAP indicates tissue perfusion\" mean in NORAD therapy?","options":["MAP reflects skin color only","MAP indicates tissue perfusion","MAP indicates only heart rate","MAP is unrelated to perfusion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states MAP indicates tissue perfusion.  Why A/C/D wrong: MAP is a measure of tissue perfusion, not skin color, heart rate alone, or unrelated."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"When BP is too high or MAP increases too much, the carotid and aortic bodies sense the change and transfer this signal to the brain. The vasomotor centre work into this signal and stimulate parasympathetic fibres, causing acetylcholine to act on M2 of the heart, resulting in:","options":["Increase in heart rate","Decrease in heart rate (this mechanism is called reflex bradycardia)","No change in heart rate","Cardiac arrest"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: This causes a decrease in heart rate; the mechanism is called reflex bradycardia.  Why A wrong: It decreases, not increases, the heart rate.  Why C wrong: There is a definite decrease.  Why D wrong: It is a reflex slowing, not arrest."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Noradrenaline is the drug of choice for which conditions? (Indications)","options":["Cardiogenic shock, neurogenic shock, and septic shock (vasopressor)","Anaphylactic shock only","Hypertension","Asthma"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: NORAD is the drug of choice in cardiogenic shock, neurogenic shock, and septic shock; it is a vasopressor and the drug of choice for shock with hypotension. It is also used in acute CHF.  Why B wrong: Anaphylaxis requires adrenaline (beta-2 action).  Why C wrong: NORAD raises BP; it is not used to treat hypertension.  Why D wrong: NORAD lacks bronchodilator action for asthma."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Noradrenaline is the drug of choice for shock with hypotension and is also used in:","options":["Acute CHF","Bradycardia","Atrial fibrillation","Anaphylaxis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: NORAD is also indicated in acute CHF.  Why B wrong: NORAD increases BP, not specifically used for bradycardia.  Why C wrong: Atrial fibrillation is not a listed NORAD indication.  Why D wrong: Anaphylaxis needs adrenaline."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"According to the NORAD \"Must Know Facts,\" stimulation of beta-1 receptors increases HR and CO, which causes an increase in which blood pressure (depends on blood pressure)?","options":["Systolic BP / diastolic BP (depends on blood pressure)","Only pulse pressure","Only mean arterial pressure","None"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Stimulation of beta-1 receptors increases HR and CO which increases in systolic BP / diastolic BP depends on blood pressure.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source ties beta-1 effect to systolic/diastolic BP."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Per NORAD Must Know Facts, stimulation of alpha-1 receptors in blood vessels causes vasoconstriction, leading to increased peripheral resistance, and causes which change?","options":["Increased diastolic BP","Decreased systolic BP","Bronchodilation","Decreased heart rate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Stimulation of alpha-1 receptors in blood vessels causes vasoconstriction, increasing peripheral resistance and increased diastolic BP.  Why B wrong: It raises BP via vasoconstriction.  Why C wrong: Bronchodilation is a beta-2 effect.  Why D wrong: Heart rate change is not the alpha-1 effect described here."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Per NORAD Must Know Facts, stimulation of beta-2 receptors causes vasodilation and decreases total peripheral resistance, which would decrease diastolic BP. When diastolic BP rises, there is activation of which nervous system releasing noradrenaline acting on beta-1 and increasing heart rate?","options":["Sympathetic system","Parasympathetic system","Somatic system","Enteric system"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: When diastolic BP rises, there is activation of the sympathetic system, releasing noradrenaline acting on beta-1 and increasing HR.  Why B/C/D wrong: The reflex described involves sympathetic activation, not parasympathetic, somatic, or enteric systems."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"A drug has a \"positive inotropic effect.\" This means it affects which of the following?","options":["Force and strength of cardiac muscles, act as ventricles","Rate of heart beat, act on SA node","Speed of conduction, act on AV node","Coronary blood flow"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Inotropic effect = force and strength of cardiac muscles (acts on ventricles).  Why B wrong: That defines chronotropic effect (rate, SA node).  Why C wrong: That defines dromotropic effect (conduction speed, AV node).  Why D wrong: Coronary blood flow is not the definition of inotropy."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"A \"chronotropic effect\" of a cardiac drug refers to:","options":["Rate of heart beat, act on SA node","Force of contraction, act on ventricles","Speed of conduction, act on AV node","Preload reduction"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Chronotropic effect = rate of heart beat (acts on SA node).  Why B wrong: That is inotropic.  Why C wrong: That is dromotropic.  Why D wrong: Preload reduction is unrelated to this definition."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"A \"dromotropic effect\" of a cardiac drug refers to:","options":["Speed of conduction, act on AV node","Force of contraction, act on ventricles","Rate of heart beat, act on SA node","Afterload increase"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Dromotropic effect = speed of conduction (acts on AV node).  Why B wrong: That is inotropic.  Why C wrong: That is chronotropic.  Why D wrong: Afterload is unrelated to this definition."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"According to the cardiac drug table, Amiodarone has which inotropic, chronotropic, and dromotropic effects?","options":["Positive, Positive, Positive","Positive, Negative, Negative","Negative, Negative, Negative","Negative, Positive, Positive"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Amiodarone is positive inotropic, negative chronotropic, negative dromotropic.  Why A/C/D wrong: They do not match the table values for amiodarone."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"According to the cardiac drug table, Beta blockers have which inotropic, chronotropic, and dromotropic effects?","options":["Positive, Positive, Positive","Negative, Negative, Negative","Positive, Negative, Negative","Negative, Positive, Negative"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Beta blockers are negative inotropic, negative chronotropic, negative dromotropic.  Why A/C/D wrong: They do not match the beta blocker row."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"According to the cardiac drug table, Calcium channel blockers (CCB) have which effects?","options":["Positive inotropic, positive chronotropic, positive dromotropic","Negative inotropic, negative chronotropic, negative dromotropic","Positive inotropic, negative chronotropic, negative dromotropic","Negative inotropic, positive chronotropic, positive dromotropic"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Calcium channel blockers (CCB) are negative inotropic, negative chronotropic, negative dromotropic.  Why A/C/D wrong: They do not match the CCB row."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"According to the cardiac drug table, cardiac glycosides (digoxin) have which inotropic and chronotropic effects?","options":["Positive inotropic, negative chronotropic","Negative inotropic, positive chronotropic","Positive inotropic, positive chronotropic","Negative inotropic, negative chronotropic"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cardiac glycosides (digoxin) are positive inotropic and negative chronotropic.  Why B/C/D wrong: They do not match the digoxin row."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"According to the cardiac drug table, Dobutamine has which inotropic and chronotropic effects?","options":["Positive inotropic, positive chronotropic","Negative inotropic, negative chronotropic","Positive inotropic, negative chronotropic","Negative inotropic, positive chronotropic"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Dobutamine is positive inotropic and positive chronotropic.  Why B/C/D wrong: They do not match the dobutamine row."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"According to the cardiac drug table, Dopamine has which inotropic and chronotropic effects?","options":["Positive inotropic, positive chronotropic","Negative inotropic, negative chronotropic","Positive inotropic, negative chronotropic","Negative inotropic, positive chronotropic"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Dopamine is positive inotropic and positive chronotropic.  Why B/C/D wrong: They do not match the dopamine row."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"According to the cardiac drug table, Adrenaline has which inotropic and chronotropic effects?","options":["Positive inotropic, positive chronotropic","Negative inotropic, negative chronotropic","Positive inotropic, negative chronotropic","Negative inotropic, positive chronotropic"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Adrenaline is positive inotropic and positive chronotropic.  Why B/C/D wrong: They do not match the adrenaline row."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Amiodarone belongs to which Vaughan Williams class of antiarrhythmic drug?","options":["Class I","Class II","Class III antiarrhythmic","Class IV"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Amiodarone is a class III antiarrhythmic.  Why A/B/D wrong: It is specifically class III."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Amiodarone is indicated for: (SATA)","options":["Unstable ventricular tachycardia","Ventricular fibrillation","Anaphylaxis","Hypotensive shock","Asthma"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: Indications are unstable ventricular tachycardia and ventricular fibrillation.  Why C/D/E wrong: Anaphylaxis, hypotensive shock, and asthma are not listed amiodarone indications."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"What is the mode of action of amiodarone?","options":["It reduces cardiac muscle cell excitability, relaxes smooth muscles in low vascular walls (decreasing peripheral vascular resistance/afterload), and increases the cardiac index by a small amount","It increases cardiac excitability and increases afterload","It only blocks beta receptors","It only acts on the SA node to increase rate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Amiodarone reduces cardiac muscle cell excitability, relaxes smooth muscles in low vascular walls (decreasing peripheral vascular resistance/afterload), and increases the cardiac index by a small amount.  Why B wrong: It reduces excitability, not increases.  Why C wrong: It is broader than just beta blockade.  Why D wrong: It does not increase rate; it is negative chronotropic."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Amiodarone is contraindicated in: (SATA)","options":["Sinus nodal bradycardia","AV block","Second or third-degree heart block","Severe hepatic impairment","Ventricular fibrillation"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Contraindications are sinus nodal bradycardia, AV block, second or third-degree heart block, and severe hepatic impairment.  Why E wrong: Ventricular fibrillation is an indication, not a contraindication."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Which of the following are listed side effects of amiodarone? (SATA)","options":["Nausea and vomiting","Bluish-gray skin discoloration and weight loss","Bradycardia and heart block, QT interval prolongation","Thyroid dysfunctions, liver dysfunctions, pulmonary toxicity, taste disturbances","Hyperkalemia"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Side effects include nausea/vomiting, bluish-gray skin discoloration, weight loss, bradycardia and heart block, QT interval prolongation, thyroid dysfunctions, liver dysfunctions, pulmonary toxicity, and taste disturbances.  Why E wrong: Hyperkalemia is not a listed amiodarone side effect."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Heparin sodium acts on thrombin. How does this explain its mechanism in clot prevention?","options":["It dissolves existing clots","By acting on thrombin, it prevents fibrinogen from being converted to fibrin","It increases platelet aggregation","It activates vitamin K"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Heparin acts on thrombin; that way fibrinogen is unable to convert into fibrin (preventing clot formation).  Why A wrong: Heparin prevents clot extension; it does not dissolve existing clots.  Why C wrong: Heparin does not promote platelet aggregation.  Why D wrong: It does not activate vitamin K (that is unrelated to its mechanism)."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Heparin prevents further thrombus formation and thromboembolism. Which key indication is highlighted?","options":["Acute kidney injury","Deep vein thrombosis (DVT) prevention/thromboembolism","Anaphylaxis","Asthma"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Heparin prevents further thrombus formation and thromboembolism (e.g., DVT).  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not heparin indications described."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"By which routes is heparin given?","options":["Oral only","IV and SubQ route","Intramuscular only","Sublingual only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Heparin is given by IV and SubQ (subcutaneous) route.  Why A wrong: Heparin is not given orally (it is destroyed in GI).  Why C wrong: IM is avoided due to hematoma risk.  Why D wrong: Sublingual is not a heparin route."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"What is the half-life of heparin?","options":["60–90 minutes or 1 to 1.5 hours","6 to 9 hours","1 to 2 days","30 minutes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Half-life of heparin is 60–90 minutes or 1 to 1.5 hours.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated half-life."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"What is the antidote of heparin therapy?","options":["Vitamin K","Protamine sulphate","Vitamin E","Naloxone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The antidote of heparin therapy is protamine sulphate.  Why A wrong: Vitamin K is the antidote for warfarin.  Why C wrong: Vitamin E is not an antidote.  Why D wrong: Naloxone is the antidote for opioids."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Why does heparin not dissolve existing clots but only prevent new clot formation?","options":["Because it dissolves fibrin","Heparin does not dissolve existing clots; it only prevents new clot formation","Because it activates plasmin","Because it is a thrombolytic"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Heparin does not dissolve existing clots; it only prevents new clot formation.  Why A/C/D wrong: Heparin is an anticoagulant, not a fibrinolytic/thrombolytic; it does not activate plasmin."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"During heparin therapy, which laboratory test (aPTT — Activated Partial Thromboplastin Time) level should be monitored?","options":["INR","aPTT (Activated Partial Thromboplastin Time)","Prothrombin time (PT)","CBC only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: During heparin therapy, aPTT (Activated Partial Thromboplastin Time) level should be monitored.  Why A wrong: INR monitors warfarin.  Why C wrong: PT monitors warfarin.  Why D wrong: CBC monitors complete blood counts/platelets, not the coagulation control of heparin."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"What is the normal aPTT level (per source)?","options":["9.5–11.5 seconds","20–36 seconds","60–90 seconds","80 seconds maximum"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Normal aPTT level is 20–36 seconds.  Why A wrong: 9.5–11.5 seconds is the normal PT.  Why C wrong: 60–90 seconds is the therapeutic heparin range.  Why D wrong: aPTT level should be maximum 80 seconds (upper safe limit, not normal)."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"During heparin therapy, what is the therapeutic (heparinizing) aPTT level the nurse should ensure is reached?","options":["1.5 to 2.5 times normal; maximum 80 seconds","60 seconds always","Less than 20 seconds","Exactly normal"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Normal aPTT in heparin therapy is 1.5 to 2.5 times normal, maximum 80 seconds.  Why B/C/D wrong: The therapeutic target is 1.5–2.5x normal (up to 80 seconds), not these values."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"If the aPTT level is more than 80 seconds during heparin therapy, the nurse should:","options":["Increase the dose","Immediately withhold the heparin therapy or dose, because there should be no overdose","Continue at the same rate","Give protamine sulphate prophylactically"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If aPTT level is more than 80 seconds, immediately withhold the heparin therapy/dose, because there should be no overdose.  Why A wrong: Increasing the dose would worsen the overdose.  Why C wrong: Continuing risks bleeding.  Why D wrong: The first action is to withhold, not pre-emptively give the antidote."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"If the aPTT value is too short (less than 60 seconds) during heparin therapy, the dose should:","options":["Be increased","Be discontinued","Be unchanged","Be halved"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: If the aPTT value is too short, less than 60 seconds, the dose should be increased.  Why B/C/D wrong: A subtherapeutic aPTT requires increasing the dose to reach therapeutic range."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"How often should the aPTT level be monitored during heparin therapy?","options":["Initially every 6 hours, then every day","Once a week","Every hour continuously","Only at the start"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Initially every 6 hours, then every day, the aPTT level should be monitored.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated monitoring frequency."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Per the \"Recall the Nurse\" box: When evaluating the therapeutic response for a client receiving a heparin infusion, which laboratory result should the nurse monitor?","options":["Activated partial thromboplastin time (aPTT)","Complete blood count (CBC)","International normalized ratio (INR)","Prothrombin time (PT)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The right answer is A — aPTT monitors the therapeutic response to heparin.  Why B wrong: CBC monitors blood counts, not heparin effect.  Why C/D wrong: INR and PT monitor warfarin."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Per the \"Recall the Nurse\" box: Which substance reverses the effect of heparin (antidote of heparin)?","options":["Vitamin K","Vitamin E","Heparinase","Protamine sulphate"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The right answer is D — protamine sulphate reverses heparin.  Why A wrong: Vitamin K reverses warfarin.  Why B wrong: Vitamin E is not an antidote.  Why C wrong: Heparinase is not the listed antidote."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Warfarin therapy is best described as:","options":["A short-acting IV anticoagulant","An oral anticoagulant therapy","A thrombolytic","An antiplatelet only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: It is an oral anticoagulant therapy.  Why A wrong: Warfarin is oral, not IV.  Why C wrong: It is not a thrombolytic.  Why D wrong: It is an anticoagulant (vitamin K antagonist), not solely antiplatelet."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Warfarin acts as an antagonist of vitamin K, which means warfarin suppresses the action of which vitamin?","options":["Vitamin K","Vitamin C","Vitamin D","Vitamin B12"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Warfarin acts as an antagonist of vitamin K, meaning warfarin suppresses the action of vitamin K.  Why B/C/D wrong: Warfarin specifically antagonizes vitamin K, which is necessary for clotting factor formation."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Vitamin K is necessary for clotting factor formation in the liver, specifically clotting factors 2, 7, 9, and 10 (each marked as one of these are vitamin K dependent factors). What is the antidote of warfarin therapy?","options":["Protamine sulphate","Vitamin K","Naloxone","Calcium gluconate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Vitamin K is necessary for clotting factor (2, 7, 9, 10) formation in liver; the antidote of warfarin therapy is vitamin K.  Why A wrong: Protamine sulphate is the heparin antidote.  Why C wrong: Naloxone is the opioid antidote.  Why D wrong: Calcium gluconate is not the warfarin antidote."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"During warfarin therapy, which laboratory tests are monitored to check for clotting?","options":["aPTT and CBC","Prothrombin time (PT) and INR","Bleeding time only","Platelet count only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: During warfarin therapy, prothrombin time (PT) and INR should be monitored.  Why A wrong: aPTT monitors heparin.  Why C/D wrong: PT and INR are the specific warfarin monitoring tests."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"What is the normal prothrombin time (PT) per source?","options":["9.5–11.5 seconds","20–36 seconds","60–90 seconds","1.5 to 2.5 seconds"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Normal PT is 9.5–11.5 seconds.  Why B wrong: 20–36 seconds is the normal aPTT.  Why C wrong: 60–90 seconds is therapeutic heparin aPTT.  Why D wrong: 1.5–2.5 is the INR therapeutic range."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"What is the therapeutic value of PT during warfarin therapy?","options":["PT 1.5 to 2 times normal","PT less than normal","PT 5 to 10 times normal","PT exactly normal"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Therapeutic value of PT is 1.5 to 2 times normal.  Why B/C/D wrong: The therapeutic target is 1.5–2x normal."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"What is the normal value of INR (per source)?","options":["2 to 3","9.5–11.5","20–36","1.5 to 2.5"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Normal value of INR is 2 to 3.  Why B wrong: 9.5–11.5 is normal PT.  Why C wrong: 20–36 is normal aPTT.  Why D wrong: 1.5–2.5 relates to heparin aPTT multiplier (not normal INR per this source)."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Per the \"Recall the Nurse\" box: Which substance reverses the effect of warfarin sodium (antidote of warfarin)?","options":["Vitamin K","Vitamin C","Warafarinex","Protamine sulphate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The right answer is A — vitamin K reverses warfarin.  Why B wrong: Vitamin C is not an antidote.  Why C wrong: \"Warafarinex\" is a distractor.  Why D wrong: Protamine sulphate reverses heparin."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Per the nursing care in anticoagulant therapy, which assessments/monitoring should the nurse perform? (SATA)","options":["Monitor aPTT level when patient is on heparin therapy","Monitor PT & INR level when patient is on warfarin therapy","Monitor platelets counts and monitor vital signs","Observe for bleeding gums, nose bleed, haematuria, hematemesis, and occult blood in the stool","Administer vitamin K routinely to all patients"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing care includes monitoring aPTT (heparin), PT & INR (warfarin), platelet counts, vital signs, and observing for bleeding gums, nose bleed, haematuria, hematemesis, and occult blood in stool.  Why E wrong: Vitamin K is the warfarin antidote given for bleeding/overdose, not routinely to all patients."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Digoxin is classified as which type of drug?","options":["Beta blocker","Cardiac glycoside","Calcium channel blocker","Thrombolytic"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Digoxin is a cardiac glycoside used to manage and treat heart failure and certain arrhythmias.  Why A/C/D wrong: It is not a beta blocker, CCB, or thrombolytic."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"What is the mechanism by which digoxin increases myocardial contractility (positive inotropic effect)?","options":["It increases the force of cardiac muscles, increasing stroke volume and blood pressure, reducing heart rate","It blocks beta-1 receptors","It dilates coronary arteries","It thins the blood"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Positive inotropic effect — increase force & strength of cardiac muscles, which increases stroke volume and blood pressure, reducing heart rate.  Why B wrong: Digoxin is not a beta blocker.  Why C wrong: It does not act as a coronary vasodilator (nitrate effect).  Why D wrong: It is not an anticoagulant."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Digoxin produces a negative chronotropic effect, which means it:","options":["Increases the heart rate","Decreases the heart rate (acts on SA node)","Increases conduction speed","Increases blood pressure only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Negative chronotropic effect — decrease heart rate; acts on SA node.  Why A wrong: It decreases rate.  Why C wrong: That is dromotropic.  Why D wrong: That is not the chronotropic definition."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Digoxin produces a negative dromotropic effect, which means it:","options":["Decreases speed of conduction (acts on AV node)","Increases speed of conduction","Increases force of contraction","Increases heart rate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Negative dromotropic effect — decrease speed of conduction; acts on AV node.  Why B wrong: It decreases conduction.  Why C wrong: That is inotropic.  Why D wrong: Rate increase is not dromotropy."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"How does digoxin increase the output of the heart?","options":["By increasing the effectiveness of heart pumping","By decreasing contractility","By increasing the heart rate","By dilating arteries"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: It increases the output of the heart by increasing the effectiveness of heart pumping.  Why B wrong: It increases (not decreases) contractility.  Why C wrong: It decreases the heart rate.  Why D wrong: It does not act by arterial dilation."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Because digoxin effectively treats heart failure and fibrillation, digoxin is described as the drug of choice for which condition?","options":["Heart failure","Hypertension","Asthma","Anaphylaxis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Digoxin is the drug of choice for heart failure (so that it is effectively treating heart failure and fibrillation).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated digoxin drug-of-choice indication."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Before administering digoxin, the nurse should assess the apical pulse for 1 minute. The nurse should withhold digoxin therapy if the apical pulse is:","options":["Below 100 bpm","Below 60 in adult and below 90 in child / below 70 in child","Below 30 bpm only","Above 100 bpm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Assess apical pulse for full 1 minute while client on digoxin therapy; withhold if below 60 adult, below 70 in child, below 90 in child (bradycardia thresholds per source).  Why A wrong: 100 bpm is not the withhold threshold.  Why C wrong: 30 bpm is not the listed cutoff.  Why D wrong: A high rate is not the digoxin withhold reason."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"What is the first nursing action the nurse should take when digoxin toxicity is detected?","options":["Increase the dose","First nursing action in digoxin toxicity is withholding the next scheduled dose and immediately notify to duty doctor","Give more potassium","Give vitamin K"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: First nursing action in digoxin toxicity is withholding the next scheduled dose and immediately notify the duty doctor.  Why A wrong: Increasing the dose would worsen toxicity.  Why C wrong: Indiscriminate potassium is not the first action.  Why D wrong: Vitamin K is the warfarin antidote."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Why must the nurse monitor serum potassium level closely during digoxin therapy, and what level should it be maintained at?","options":["3.5–5.3 mEq/L","1.5–2.5 mEq/L","8–10 mEq/L","0.5–1 mEq/L"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Monitor serum potassium level closely during digoxin therapy; it should be maintained at 3.5–5.3 mEq/L.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated serum potassium target range."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Hypokalemia increases the chance of digoxin toxicity. To prevent this, the nurse should:","options":["Encourage intake of potassium-rich diet","Restrict all potassium","Give a diuretic","Stop digoxin permanently"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hypokalemia increases the chance of digoxin toxicity, so encourage intake of potassium-rich diet for prevention of hypokalemia.  Why B wrong: Restricting potassium worsens hypokalemia and toxicity risk.  Why C wrong: Diuretics can worsen hypokalemia.  Why D wrong: The action is dietary prevention, not stopping the drug."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"What is the normal therapeutic digoxin serum level the nurse should maintain?","options":["0.5–2 ng/ml","3.5–5.3 ng/ml","5–10 ng/ml","0.1–0.3 ng/ml"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Monitor the therapeutic digoxin serum level; it should be maintained at 0.5–2 ng/ml.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated therapeutic digoxin range."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"If serum digoxin level is more than 2 ng/ml, what does it indicate and what should the nurse do?","options":["It indicates toxicity; withhold the next scheduled dose and inform the doctor","It indicates therapeutic effect; continue","It indicates subtherapeutic level; increase dose","It indicates hypokalemia; give potassium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: If serum digoxin level is more than 2 ng/ml, it indicates toxicity; withhold the next scheduled dose and inform the doctor.  Why B wrong: >2 ng/ml is toxic, not therapeutic.  Why C wrong: It is supratherapeutic, not subtherapeutic.  Why D wrong: While hypokalemia worsens toxicity, the level itself indicates toxicity."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Which of the following are signs of digoxin toxicity, especially due to stimulation of the chemoreceptor trigger zone or medulla oblongata? (SATA)","options":["Anorexia, nausea, vomiting","Premature ventricular complexes","Xanthopsia or yellow vision (due to effect on visual cone)","Blurred vision (due to CNS effect)","Muscle weakness (due to CNS effect)"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Digoxin toxicity signs include anorexia, nausea, vomiting (CTZ/medulla stimulation), premature ventricular complexes, xanthopsia/yellow vision (visual cone effect), blurred vision (CNS effect), and muscle weakness (CNS effect).  All options listed are stated digoxin toxicity signs."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Hypokalemia, due to its CNS effect, is associated with digoxin toxicity. What is the specific antidote for digoxin toxicity?","options":["Vitamin K","Protamine sulphate","Digoxin immune Fab","Naloxone"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Antidote for digoxin toxicity is digoxin immune Fab.  Why A wrong: Vitamin K is the warfarin antidote.  Why B wrong: Protamine sulphate is the heparin antidote.  Why D wrong: Naloxone is the opioid antidote."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Per the \"Recall the Nurse\" box: Which is NOT a side effect of nitroglycerin?","options":["Blurred vision","Dizziness","Headache","Weakness"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The right answer is C — Headache is the option flagged as the answer (i.e., the option chosen in the source's MCQ; reproduced faithfully).  Why A/B/D wrong: Per the reproduced PYQ, blurred vision, dizziness, and weakness are the contrasting options; the marked answer is C (Headache)."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Per the page-224 PYQ: When a patient develops digoxin toxicity, the nurse should monitor for which abnormality? (Anorexia, vomiting, complete heart block, ventricular dysrhythmias are options)","options":["Atrial fibrillation","Complete heart block","Bradycardia","Ventricular dysrhythmias"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Per the reproduced source MCQ, the right answer is D — ventricular dysrhythmias.  Why A wrong: Atrial fibrillation is not the marked answer.  Why B/C wrong: While bradycardia/heart block occur, the source-marked answer is ventricular dysrhythmias."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Per the page-224 PYQ \"the first sign of digoxin toxicity\": which is the first sign of digoxin toxicity?","options":["Muscular weakness","Anorexia","Headache","Nausea"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The right answer is B — Anorexia is the first sign of digoxin toxicity.  Why A wrong: Muscular weakness is a later/CNS effect.  Why C wrong: Headache is not the first sign.  Why D wrong: Nausea follows but anorexia is the earliest sign per source."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Give an example of a sublingual nitrate.","options":["SORBITRATE (isosorbide dinitrate)","Atropine","Heparin","Dobutamine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Example of sublingual nitrate is SORBITRATE (isosorbide dinitrate).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not nitrates."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"What is the indication for sublingual nitrates?","options":["Angina attack","Anaphylaxis","Asthma","Heart block"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Indication for sublingual nitrates is angina attack.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not nitrate indications."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"During nitrate therapy, what should the nurse monitor that may be especially affected, lowering blood pressure?","options":["Vital signs, especially blood pressure during the therapy","Only temperature","Only urine output","Only respiratory rate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Monitor all vital signs, especially blood pressure, during the therapy (nitrates lower BP).  Why B/C/D wrong: BP monitoring is the priority emphasized, not these alone."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Why should the nurse offer sips of water before giving a sublingual nitrate tablet?","options":["Because dryness may inhibit drug absorption","To dilute the drug","To prevent nausea","To improve taste"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Offer sips of water before giving because dryness may inhibit drug absorption.  Why B/C/D wrong: The stated reason is to facilitate sublingual absorption (dryness inhibits it)."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"When administering a sublingual nitroglycerin tablet, where should the tablet be placed and how should the nurse instruct the patient?","options":["Place under the tongue (sublingual) and leave it until it is fully dissolved; do not swallow the tablet","Chew the tablet quickly","Swallow with water","Place between cheek and gum and spit out"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Place the medicine under the tongue (sublingual) and leave it until fully dissolved; do not swallow the tablet.  Why B wrong: It should dissolve, not be chewed.  Why C wrong: Swallowing defeats sublingual absorption.  Why D wrong: The route is sublingual, not buccal/spit."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Before giving the second sublingual NTG tablet, what should be checked because each tablet can reduce BP?","options":["BP should be checked because NTG medicines can cause hypotension","Heart rate only","Respiratory rate only","Temperature"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Before giving the second tablet, BP should be checked because NTG medicines can cause hypotension.  Why B/C/D wrong: BP is the priority due to the hypotensive effect of nitrates."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"How long should the nurse wait between sublingual NTG tablets (reassess after the client is stable)?","options":["5 minutes (then reassess BP after 5 minutes)","30 seconds","1 hour","15 minutes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Then again reassess after 5 minutes (administer NTG tablet if pain is not resolved and BP is stable).  Why B/C/D wrong: The stated interval is 5 minutes."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"What is the maximum number of sublingual NTG/sorbitrate tablets to administer, and over what period?","options":["3 tablets within 15 minutes","5 tablets within 1 hour","10 tablets within 30 minutes","1 tablet only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Administer if pain not resolved and BP stable; maximum dose of nitroglycerin/sorbitrate is 3 tablets within 15 minutes.  Why B/C/D wrong: The stated maximum is 3 tablets within 15 minutes."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"If chest pain is not relieved after the maximum 3 sublingual NTG tablets within 15 minutes, what should the nurse do?","options":["Reassess the chest pain and contact the doctor if 3rd tablet also did not relieve the pain","Give a 4th tablet","Discharge the patient","Do nothing"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Reassess the chest pain, contact the doctor if 3rd tablet also did not relieve the pain.  Why B wrong: The maximum is 3 tablets; a 4th is not given.  Why C/D wrong: Unrelieved chest pain after maximum dose requires physician notification, not discharge or inaction."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"During nitrate therapy, the foot end of the bed should be raised. Why?","options":["To prevent hypertension","Because hypotension may occur, raising the foot end helps to elevate/correct hypotension","To prevent aspiration","To increase intracranial pressure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: During therapy, the foot end of the bed should be raised because hypotension may occur and elevation helps prevent/correct hypotension.  Why A wrong: Nitrates cause hypotension, not hypertension.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the stated rationale."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Per the \"Recall the Nurse\" box on page 224: What is the purpose of antianginal drug therapy?","options":["Increase blood flow to the peripheral arteries","Vasodilate the heart blood vessels","Vasoconstrict the heart blood vessels","Decrease blood flow to the peripheral arteries"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The right answer is B — the purpose of antianginal drug therapy is to vasodilate the heart blood vessels (improving coronary blood flow).  Why A wrong: The aim is coronary vasodilation, not peripheral arterial flow increase.  Why C wrong: It vasodilates, not vasoconstricts.  Why D wrong: It does not decrease peripheral blood flow as its purpose."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Morphine belongs to which class of analgesics?","options":["NSAIDs","Opioid analgesics (suppress pain impulses)","Corticosteroids","Local anaesthetics"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Morphine is an opioid analgesic which suppresses pain impulses.  Why A/C/D wrong: It is not an NSAID, corticosteroid, or local anaesthetic."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"What are the therapeutic indications for morphine?","options":["MI, chronic or severe pain, and cancer pain","Anaphylaxis","Hypertension","Asthma"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Therapeutic indications include MI, chronic or severe pain, and cancer pain.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not morphine indications."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Due to oxygen suppression side effects of morphine, opioids are used in: (per source)","options":["Only mild headache","Severe respiratory depression (paradoxically — note: source states opioids are used in only cough/specific contexts; see source) — i.e., morphine has cough suppression","As first-line for asthma","As an antiplatelet"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Due to oxygen suppression side effects, opioids are used per source, and morphine has cough suppression effects (antitussive).  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the stated morphine uses; it is not an antiplatelet or asthma first-line."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Which are recognized side effects of morphine (opioids)? (SATA)","options":["Respiratory depression (most severe), asthma, hypotension, constipation","Reduction in pupil size or miosis (pinpoint pupil), nausea and vomiting","Sedation, cough suppression, and urine retention","Increased ICP, hepatic or pulmonary diseases concerns; contraindicated in severe respiratory depression","Hypertension and mydriasis (dilated pupils)"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Morphine side effects include respiratory depression (most severe), hypotension, constipation, miosis (pinpoint pupil), nausea/vomiting, sedation, cough suppression, urine retention; it is contraindicated in severe respiratory depression, head injuries, increased ICP, severe renal/hepatic or pulmonary diseases.  Why E wrong: Morphine causes miosis (pinpoint pupils), not mydriasis, and causes hypotension, not hypertension."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Morphine is contraindicated in which conditions? (SATA)","options":["Severe respiratory depression","Head injuries / increased ICP","Severe renal, hepatic, or pulmonary diseases","Mild musculoskeletal pain","Cancer pain"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Morphine is contraindicated in severe respiratory depression, head injuries/increased ICP, and severe renal, hepatic, or pulmonary diseases.  Why D wrong: Mild pain is not a stated contraindication.  Why E wrong: Cancer pain is an indication, not a contraindication."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"During nursing care of a patient on morphine, what respiratory rate and heart rate thresholds require the nurse to withhold the medication and notify the doctor immediately?","options":["Monitor respiratory rate and if rate is less than 12 breaths/minute in an adult, withhold the medication and notify the doctor immediately","Withhold only if respiratory rate is below 30","Notify only if heart rate is below 100","No monitoring needed"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Monitor respiratory rate; if rate is less than 12 breaths/minute in an adult (and heart rate below threshold per source — <12 breaths/min), withhold the medication and notify the doctor immediately.  Why B wrong: The cutoff is below 12, not below 30.  Why C wrong: The priority concern is respiratory depression.  Why D wrong: Close respiratory monitoring is essential."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"During morphine therapy, the nurse should monitor blood pressure because:","options":["Morphine causes hypertension","If reading is lower side then should inform to doctor (morphine causes hypotension)","Morphine has no effect on BP","It raises BP and needs antihypertensives"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Monitor pulse and blood pressure; if reading is lower side then should inform to doctor (morphine can cause hypotension).  Why A/D wrong: Morphine causes hypotension, not hypertension.  Why C wrong: It does affect BP."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Why should the nurse monitor breath sounds in a patient receiving morphine?","options":["Because opioids suppress the cough reflex","Because morphine causes bronchospasm","Because morphine increases mucus","Because it causes pneumonia directly"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Monitor breath sounds because opioids suppress cough reflex.  Why B/C/D wrong: The stated rationale is cough reflex suppression, not bronchospasm, mucus, or direct pneumonia."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"What activities should the nurse encourage to prevent atelectasis and pneumonia in patients on morphine?","options":["Turning, deep breathing, and incentive spirometry","Bed rest only","Fluid restriction","Cough suppression"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Encourage activities such as turning, deep breathing, and incentive spirometry to prevent atelectasis and pneumonia.  Why B/C/D wrong: Bed rest, fluid restriction, and cough suppression do not prevent atelectasis."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"What is the antidote for opioid (morphine) overdose, indicated by pinpoint pupils?","options":["Naloxone","Vitamin K","Protamine sulphate","Digoxin immune Fab"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Antidote for opioid toxicity is Naloxone; monitor for pupil change because pinpoint pupils can indicate morphine overdose.  Why B wrong: Vitamin K reverses warfarin.  Why C wrong: Protamine sulphate reverses heparin.  Why D wrong: Digoxin immune Fab reverses digoxin toxicity."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Per the page-225 PYQ: A prescription reads morphine sulfate, 8 mg IM. The medication ampule reads morphine sulfate, 10 mg/ml. The nurse prepares how many milliliters (ml) to administer the correct dose?","options":["1.16 ml","0.8 ml","0.5 ml","0.6 ml"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The right answer is B — 0.8 ml. Calculation: (8 mg desired / 10 mg available) x 1 ml = 0.8 ml.  Why A wrong: 1.16 ml is incorrect for this calculation.  Why C/D wrong: 0.5 ml and 0.6 ml do not match the dose calculation."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Atropine sulphate belongs to which class of drugs?","options":["Anticholinergic drugs","Opioid analgesic","Cardiac glycoside","Beta blocker"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Atropine sulphate is an anticholinergic drug.  Why B/C/D wrong: It is not an opioid, glycoside, or beta blocker."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"What is the mechanism of action of atropine?","options":["It blocks the effects of acetylcholine at muscarinic receptors (parasympatholytic) — it is a muscarinic antagonist (antimuscarinic agent)","It mimics acetylcholine","It is a beta agonist","It blocks sodium channels"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Atropine blocks the effects of acetylcholine at muscarinic receptors (parasympatholytic); it is a muscarinic antagonist (antimuscarinic agent) and blocks the effects of acetylcholine at muscarinic receptors.  Why B wrong: It blocks, not mimics, acetylcholine.  Why C wrong: It is not a beta agonist.  Why D wrong: It is not a sodium channel blocker."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Atropine is also used in cardiac arrest. What is the most common source of atropine?","options":["Atropa belladonna plant","Foxglove plant","Willow bark","Synthetic only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common source of atropine is the Atropa belladonna plant.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated source of atropine."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Atropine is used in cardiac arrest in which scenario regarding ADR (adverse drug reaction)?","options":["It is generally contraindicated in people with closed-angle glaucoma, pyloric stenosis, or prostatic hypertrophy","It is safe in all patients","It is the first-line for hypertension","It is used for anaphylaxis only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Atropine is generally contraindicated in people with closed-angle glaucoma, pyloric stenosis, or prostatic hypertrophy.  Why B wrong: It has notable contraindications.  Why C/D wrong: It is not first-line for hypertension or anaphylaxis."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"What are the therapeutic indications for atropine? (SATA)","options":["Organophosphates (OPS) poisoning","Unstable bradycardia (adult dose 1 mg IV stat)","To reduce secretion before surgery and ECT (electroconvulsive therapy)","Severe respiratory depression","Heart failure"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Therapeutic indications are organophosphates (OPS) poisoning, unstable bradycardia (adult dose 1 mg IV stat), and to reduce secretion before surgery and ECT.  Why D wrong: Severe respiratory depression is a morphine concern, not an atropine indication.  Why E wrong: Heart failure is a digoxin indication, not atropine."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Common side effects of atropine include: (SATA)","options":["Blurry vision","Dry mouth","Photophobia","Constipation, urinary retention, and tachycardia (fast heart rate)","Bradycardia"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Common side effects include blurry vision, dry mouth, photophobia, constipation, urinary retention, and fast heart rate (tachycardia).  Why E wrong: Atropine causes tachycardia, not bradycardia (it is used to treat bradycardia)."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Why does atropine cause tachycardia (fast heart rate)?","options":["Because it acts on the heart to relax muscles","By blocking M2 muscarinic receptors / acting on the heart and topical atropine is used as a cycloplegic","Because it stimulates beta-2","Because it suppresses the SA node"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Atropine, used as a cycloplegic, temporarily paralyzes the accommodation reflex; on the heart it blocks muscarinic (M2) action causing tachycardia (it blocks vagal/parasympathetic input to the heart, raising rate).  Why A wrong: It does not relax cardiac muscle to slow it; it blocks vagal slowing.  Why C wrong: It is not a beta-2 stimulant.  Why D wrong: It increases rate by removing parasympathetic suppression, not by suppressing the SA node."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Atropine is not an actual antidote for organophosphate (OPS) poisoning but is used in conjunction with which agent (used in conjunction with the oxime pralidoxime/PAM injection)?","options":["Pralidoxime (PAM)","Naloxone","Vitamin K","Protamine sulphate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Atropine is not an actual antidote for OPS poisoning; it is used in conjunction with the oxime pralidoxime (PAM) injection.  Why B wrong: Naloxone is the opioid antidote.  Why C wrong: Vitamin K reverses warfarin.  Why D wrong: Protamine sulphate reverses heparin."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Atropine should be used with care in patients with closed-angle glaucoma. What disease (IBD) may it cause a transient sinus tachycardia in?","options":["Inflammatory bowel disease (IBD)","Diabetes","Hypertension","Asthma"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Atropine should be used with care in patients with closed-angle glaucoma; in inflammatory bowel disease (IBD) it may cause a transient sinus tachycardia.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies IBD, not these conditions."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Per the page-225 \"Recall the Nurse\" box: A muscarinic antagonist (antimuscarinic) such as atropine blocks the effects of acetylcholine at muscarinic receptors. This is described as which type of action?","options":["Parasympatholytic / antimuscarinic","Sympathomimetic","Cholinergic agonist","Beta-blockade"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Atropine is a muscarinic antagonist (antimuscarinic agent) that blocks acetylcholine effects at muscarinic receptors — a parasympatholytic/antimuscarinic action.  Why B wrong: It is not sympathomimetic.  Why C wrong: It is an antagonist, not an agonist.  Why D wrong: It does not act by beta blockade."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Frusemide (Lasix) belongs to which class of drug?","options":["Loop diuretic","Thiazide diuretic","Potassium-sparing diuretic","Osmotic diuretic"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Frusemide is a loop (basic) diuretic.  Why B/C/D wrong: It is not a thiazide, potassium-sparing, or osmotic diuretic."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"What are the indications for frusemide (Lasix)? (SATA)","options":["Fluid overload","Anasarca or generalized oedema","Hypertension","Hypotension","Bradycardia"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Indications for frusemide are fluid overload, anasarca/generalized oedema, and hypertension.  Why D wrong: Hypotension is not an indication (it lowers BP).  Why E wrong: Bradycardia is not a frusemide indication."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"What is the mode of action of frusemide?","options":["Decreasing sodium re-absorption at the loop of Henle","Blocking aldosterone","Increasing potassium reabsorption","Inhibiting carbonic anhydrase in the proximal tubule"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Frusemide acts by decreasing sodium re-absorption at the loop of Henle.  Why B wrong: That describes a potassium-sparing diuretic (spironolactone).  Why C wrong: It promotes potassium loss, not reabsorption.  Why D wrong: That describes acetazolamide, not frusemide."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Which are recognized side effects of frusemide? (SATA)","options":["Electrolyte imbalances","Fluid depletion","Hearing loss or tinnitus due to too rapid intravenous administration","Orthostatic hypotension","Hyperglycemia"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Side effects include electrolyte imbalances, fluid depletion, hearing loss/tinnitus (due to too rapid intravenous administration — ototoxicity), orthostatic hypotension, and hyperglycemia.  All options are stated frusemide side effects."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Hydrocortisone and dexamethasone (decadron) are examples of which class of hormones?","options":["Stress hormones (glucocorticoids)","Mineralocorticoids","Sex hormones","Thyroid hormones"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hydrocortisone and dexamethasone are stress hormones (glucocorticoids).  Why B/C/D wrong: They are glucocorticoids, not mineralocorticoids, sex, or thyroid hormones."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Dexamethasone (decadron) and betamethasone are examples of long-acting glucocorticoids with what duration of action?","options":["36–72 hours","8–12 hours","1–2 hours","6–9 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Dexamethasone (decadron) and betamethasone are examples of long-acting glucocorticoids, with duration of action of 36–72 hours.  Why B wrong: 8–12 hours is hydrocortisone's duration (short-acting).  Why C/D wrong: Not the stated durations."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Hydrocortisone is an example of which type of glucocorticoid, and what is its duration of action?","options":["Short-acting glucocorticoid; duration of action 8–12 hours","Long-acting; 36–72 hours","Intermediate; 24 hours","Ultra-long; 7 days"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hydrocortisone is an example of short-acting glucocorticoid, with duration of action 8–12 hours.  Why B wrong: 36–72 hours is the long-acting (dexamethasone/betamethasone) duration.  Why C/D wrong: Not the stated values."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"What are the actions of glucocorticoids? (SATA)","options":["Affects glucose, protein, and bone metabolism","Alter the normal immune function to decrease immunity","Act as anti-inflammatory or decrease inflammation","Increase clotting and platelet count","Cause bradycardia"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Glucocorticoids affect glucose, protein, and bone metabolism; alter normal immune function to decrease immunity; and act as anti-inflammatory/decrease inflammation.  Why D wrong: Increasing clotting/platelets is not a stated glucocorticoid action.  Why E wrong: Bradycardia is not a listed action."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"What are the therapeutic uses of glucocorticoids? (SATA)","options":["Allergy","Asthma","Adrenal insufficiency","Inflammatory and autoimmune diseases","Hypertension as first-line"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Therapeutic uses are allergy, asthma, adrenal insufficiency, and inflammatory and autoimmune diseases.  Why E wrong: Glucocorticoids are not first-line for hypertension (they can raise BP)."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"What is the specific nursing care for glucocorticoid therapy? (SATA)","options":["Regular monitoring of RBS and blood pressure","All glucocorticoids should not stop abruptly","Initiate general nursing care","Treat all side effects, monitor vitals, I/O & electrolytes level, and restrict sodium and offer high potassium diet","Stop the drug immediately on the first day if any side effect appears"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Specific nursing care includes regular monitoring of RBS and blood pressure; not stopping glucocorticoids abruptly; initiating general nursing care; treating side effects; monitoring vitals, I/O & electrolytes; and restricting sodium and offering high potassium diet.  Why E wrong: Glucocorticoids should NOT be stopped abruptly; sudden stop risks adrenal crisis."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Why should glucocorticoids not be stopped abruptly?","options":["Because of the risk of bleeding","Because abrupt withdrawal causes adrenal insufficiency (they suppress the body's own cortisol production)","Because it causes hyperglycemia","Because it causes hypertension"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: All glucocorticoids should not be stopped abruptly because of suppression of endogenous cortisol; abrupt withdrawal causes adrenal insufficiency (the source emphasizes not stopping abruptly).  Why A/C/D wrong: Bleeding, hyperglycemia, and hypertension are side effects but not the reason against abrupt cessation."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Per the specific side effects table for glucocorticoids: \"Hypertension\" is caused by which mechanism?","options":["Due to sodium & water retention","Due to increased carbohydrate catabolism and glucogenesis","Due to potassium excretion","Due to decreased absorption of calcium from intestine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hypertension is due to sodium & water retention.  Why B wrong: That mechanism explains hyperglycemia.  Why C wrong: That explains hypokalemia.  Why D wrong: That explains hypocalcemia."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Per the glucocorticoid side effects table: \"Hyperglycemia\" is due to which mechanism?","options":["Due to increased carbohydrate catabolism and glucogenesis","Due to sodium & water retention","Due to potassium excretion","Due to suppression of cortisol"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hyperglycemia is due to increased carbohydrate catabolism and glucogenesis.  Why B wrong: That mechanism explains hypertension.  Why C wrong: That explains hypokalemia.  Why D wrong: Not the listed mechanism for hyperglycemia."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Per the glucocorticoid side effects table: \"Hypokalemia\" is due to which mechanism?","options":["Due to potassium excretion","Due to sodium retention","Due to decreased absorption of calcium","Due to immune suppression"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hypokalemia is due to potassium excretion.  Why B wrong: That relates to hypertension.  Why C wrong: That relates to hypocalcemia.  Why D wrong: Immune suppression relates to \"prone to infection,\" not hypokalemia."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Per the glucocorticoid side effects table: \"Hypocalcemia\" is due to which mechanism?","options":["Due to decreased absorption of calcium from the intestine","Due to potassium excretion","Due to sodium & water retention","Due to glucogenesis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hypocalcemia is due to decreased absorption of calcium from intestine.  Why B/C/D wrong: These mechanisms explain hypokalemia, hypertension, and hyperglycemia respectively."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Per the glucocorticoid side effects table: \"Steroid-induced hyperglycemia\" is due to which mechanism?","options":["Due to low density & low calcium level","Due to potassium excretion","Due to sodium retention","Due to immune suppression"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Steroid-induced hyperglycemia (per the table) is due to low density & low calcium level (as listed in source).  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the listed mechanism in the table."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Per the glucocorticoid side effects table: \"Weight gain\" is due to which mechanism?","options":["Due to sodium & water retention, usually central obesity seen","Due to potassium excretion","Due to decreased absorption of calcium","Due to increased blood sugar level"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Weight gain is due to sodium & water retention, usually central obesity seen.  Why B/C/D wrong: These explain other side effects, not weight gain."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Per the glucocorticoid side effects table: \"Mood changes\" are due to which mechanism?","options":["Due to CNS deterioration","Due to sodium retention","Due to immune suppression","Due to glucogenesis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Mood changes are due to CNS deterioration (as listed).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are mechanisms for other side effects."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Per the glucocorticoid side effects table: \"Glaucoma and cataracts\" are due to which mechanism?","options":["Due to increased ocular pressure and long-time hyperglycemia","Due to potassium excretion","Due to sodium retention","Due to calcium loss"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Glaucoma and cataracts are due to increased ocular pressure and long-time hyperglycemia.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the listed ocular side effect mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Per the glucocorticoid side effects table: \"Decreased wound healing\" is due to which mechanism?","options":["Due to increased blood sugar level","Due to sodium retention","Due to potassium excretion","Due to calcium loss"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Decreased wound healing is due to increased blood sugar level.  Why B/C/D wrong: These mechanisms explain other side effects, not wound healing delay."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Per the glucocorticoid side effects table: \"Prone to infection\" is due to which mechanism?","options":["Due to immune suppression","Due to sodium retention","Due to potassium excretion","Due to increased blood sugar"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Prone to infection is due to immune suppression.  Why B/C wrong: These relate to hypertension/hypokalemia.  Why D wrong: While hyperglycemia contributes to infection, the table lists immune suppression as the mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Per the glucocorticoid side effects table: \"Hypertrichosis or excessive hairiness\" (Cushing clinical feature) is due to which mechanism?","options":["Due to prolonged and excessive use of exogenous glucocorticoids","Due to sodium retention","Due to potassium excretion","Due to calcium loss"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hypertrichosis/excessive hairiness is due to prolonged and excessive use of exogenous glucocorticoids.  Why B/C/D wrong: These explain other electrolyte/fluid side effects."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Per the glucocorticoid side effects table: \"Cushingoid clinical feature/finding\" includes which physical signs?","options":["Moon face, buffalo hump, truncal obesity","Pinpoint pupils","Bluish-gray skin","Yellow vision"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cushingoid clinical features include moon face, buffalo hump, and truncal obesity.  Why B wrong: Pinpoint pupils relate to morphine.  Why C wrong: Bluish-gray skin relates to amiodarone.  Why D wrong: Yellow vision (xanthopsia) relates to digoxin toxicity."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"What is the antidote for opioid (morphine) toxicity per the morphine overview note (page 224)?","options":["Naloxone","Atropine","Vitamin K","Pralidoxime"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Antidote for opioids toxicity is Naloxone.  Why B wrong: Atropine treats bradycardia/OPS poisoning.  Why C wrong: Vitamin K reverses warfarin.  Why D wrong: Pralidoxime is used in OPS poisoning."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Cardiovascular/emergency & autonomic pharmacology — Epinephrine/Adrenaline…","question":"Per the page-224 PYQ on antidysrhythmic drug indication: which condition reflects the use of an antidysrhythmic and care for digoxin toxicity? (Atrial fibrillation, complete heart block, headache, nausea options) The marked answer reproduced faithfully is:","options":["Atrial fibrillation","Complete heart block","Headache","Nausea"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Per the reproduced page-224 MCQ, the right answer is B — Complete heart block (faithful reproduction of the source's marked answer).  Why A wrong: Atrial fibrillation is not the marked answer.  Why C/D wrong: Headache and nausea are distractors; source marks B."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"Aspirin (acetylsalicylic acid, ASA) belongs to which class of drugs commonly used to treat fever and pain?","options":["NSAID (nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug)","Opioid analgesic","Corticosteroid","Antihistamine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: ASA is a commonly used NSAID that treats pain or fever due to various causes.  Why B wrong: Opioids act on CNS opioid receptors, not the prostaglandin pathway.  Why C wrong: Corticosteroids are steroidal anti-inflammatories.  Why D wrong: Antihistamines block histamine receptors, not used as primary antipyretics here."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"Low-dose aspirin is used in the prevention of which of the following conditions?","options":["Blood clots, stroke, and myocardial infarction (MI)","Peptic ulcer disease","Tinnitus","Idiosyncrasy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Aspirin is also used in low doses to help prevent blood clots, stroke, and myocardial infarction (MI).  Why B wrong: Peptic ulcer is a side effect of aspirin, not something it prevents.  Why C wrong: Tinnitus is a salicylate poisoning symptom/side effect.  Why D wrong: Idiosyncrasy is a listed side effect, not a prevention indication."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"Aspirin's antiplatelet effect (inhibition of platelet aggregation) is useful in which of the following clinical situations?","options":["Prevention of blood clots, stroke, and myocardial infarction","Promotion of platelet aggregation in bleeding disorders","Treatment of hypotension","Reversal of tinnitus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Aspirin inhibits platelet aggregation, which is used in the prevention of blood clots, stroke, and MI.  Why B wrong: Aspirin inhibits, not promotes, platelet aggregation.  Why C wrong: It has no role in raising blood pressure.  Why D wrong: Tinnitus is a toxicity effect, not treated by aspirin."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"What is the mechanism by which acetylsalicylic acid (aspirin) exerts its action throughout the body?","options":["Disrupting the synthesis of prostaglandins by targeting cyclooxygenase-1 (COX-1) and cyclooxygenase-2 (COX-2)","Blocking sodium channels in cardiac cells","Enhancing GABA activity in the CNS","Increasing reuptake of norepinephrine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: ASA disrupts the production of prostaglandins throughout the body by targeting COX-1 and COX-2 enzymes.  Why B wrong: Sodium channel blockade is the mechanism of phenytoin, not aspirin.  Why C wrong: GABA enhancement relates to lithium/other agents, not aspirin.  Why D wrong: NE reuptake change is the lithium-related mechanism described elsewhere."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"Which enzymes are responsible for prostaglandin synthesis and are inhibited by aspirin?","options":["COX-1 and COX-2","Lipoxygenase only","Cytochrome P-450 only","Acetylcholinesterase"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Prostaglandins are responsible for headache or pain, and aspirin targets COX-1 and COX-2.  Why B wrong: Lipoxygenase is not the enzyme targeted by aspirin per the source.  Why C wrong: P-450 relates to phenytoin interactions.  Why D wrong: Acetylcholinesterase is not involved in aspirin's mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"Using the mnemonic \"ASPIRIN,\" which of the following is a recognized side effect of aspirin? (SATA)","options":["Asthma","Salicylates intolerance","Peptic ulcer","Reye's syndrome (in kids)","Noise (tinnitus)"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The ASPIRIN mnemonic lists Asthma, Salicylates intolerance, Peptic ulcer, Intestinal/gastric bleeding, Reye's syndrome (in kids), Idiosyncrasy, and Noise (tinnitus) as side effects.  All options are direct components of the listed side-effect mnemonic."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"The \"I\" components of the ASPIRIN side-effect mnemonic include which of the following? (SATA)","options":["Intestinal/gastric bleeding","Idiosyncrasy","Hypotension","Hyperkalemia","Asthma"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The mnemonic includes Intestinal/gastric bleeding and Idiosyncrasy.  Why C wrong: Hypotension is not in the aspirin side-effect mnemonic.  Why D wrong: Hyperkalemia is not listed.  Why E wrong: Asthma is in the mnemonic but is the \"A,\" not an \"I\" component."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"A child recovering from a viral infection (e.g., influenza or chickenpox) should NOT receive aspirin because of the risk of:","options":["Reye's syndrome","Peptic ulcer","Asthma","Tinnitus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Reye's syndrome (in kids) is a listed serious side effect of aspirin, making aspirin contraindicated in children.  Why B wrong: Peptic ulcer is a side effect but not the age-specific contraindication.  Why C wrong: Asthma exacerbation is a risk but not the pediatric-specific catastrophic concern.  Why D wrong: Tinnitus is a toxicity sign, not the pediatric contraindication."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"Which of the following are recognized symptoms of salicylate (aspirin) poisoning? (SATA)","options":["Ringing in the ears and tinnitus","Nausea, vomiting, and dehydration","Increased respiratory rate (fever)","Drowsiness and confusion","Hypoventilation"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Listed salicylate poisoning symptoms include ringing in the ears/tinnitus, nausea/vomiting/dehydration, increased temperature/fever, drowsiness/confusion, and hyperventilation.  Why E wrong: The source lists hyperventilation (rapid breathing), not hypoventilation."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"Salicylate poisoning typically affects acid-base balance by causing which respiratory change?","options":["Hyperventilation","Hypoventilation","Apnea","No change in respiration"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hyperventilation is listed as a salicylate poisoning symptom.  Why B wrong: Hypoventilation is the opposite of the documented finding.  Why C wrong: Apnea is not described.  Why D wrong: Respiratory changes (hyperventilation) do occur."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"In late-stage salicylate poisoning, the nurse should monitor for which of the following metabolic derangements?","options":["Metabolic acidosis (bleeding in later stage)","Metabolic alkalosis only","Hypocalcemia only","Hyperglycemia only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Metabolic acidosis and bleeding occur in the later stage of salicylate poisoning.  Why B wrong: The source notes metabolic acidosis in the later stage.  Why C wrong: Hypocalcemia is not the documented late-stage finding.  Why D wrong: Hyperglycemia is not the listed late-stage feature."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"(PYQ — Recall the Concept) Which agent has antiplatelet properties?","options":["Aspirin","Enoxaparin","Urokinase","Heparin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Per the source, the right answer is Aspirin, which has antiplatelet properties.  Why B wrong: Enoxaparin is a low-molecular-weight heparin anticoagulant.  Why C wrong: Urokinase is a thrombolytic.  Why D wrong: Heparin is an anticoagulant, not an antiplatelet agent."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"(PYQ — Recall the Concept) Aspirin toxicity is most likely to occur in the presence of which clinical condition?","options":["Tinnitus","Bleeding","Dehydration","Hypothermia"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Per the source, the right answer is Dehydration, which predisposes to aspirin toxicity.  Why A wrong: Tinnitus is a manifestation of toxicity, not a predisposing condition.  Why B wrong: Bleeding is a complication, not a predisposing factor.  Why D wrong: Hypothermia is not the documented predisposing condition."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"Sodium bicarbonate is used to treat metabolic acidosis associated with which condition?","options":["Cardiac arrest","Diabetes insipidus","Hyperventilation syndrome","Hypocalcemic tetany"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Sodium bicarbonate is used to treat the metabolic acidosis associated with cardiac arrest.  Why B wrong: Diabetes insipidus is not the documented indication.  Why C wrong: Hyperventilation causes alkalosis, not treated with bicarbonate.  Why D wrong: Hypocalcemic tetany is treated with calcium, not bicarbonate."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"Sodium bicarbonate, commonly known as baking soda, has which chemical formula?","options":["NaHCO3","NaCl","Na2CO3","KHCO3"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Sodium bicarbonate (baking soda) is a calcium-... compound with the formula NaHCO3.  Why B wrong: NaCl is sodium chloride (table salt).  Why C wrong: Na2CO3 is sodium carbonate (washing soda).  Why D wrong: KHCO3 is potassium bicarbonate."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"Routine administration of sodium bicarbonate for cardiac arrest is recommended at what point in resuscitation?","options":["No longer recommended; it should be given only in prolonged cardiac arrests","As the first-line drug immediately at arrest","Before any chest compressions begin","Only in pediatric arrests"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Routine bicarbonate administration for cardiac arrest is no longer a recommendation; authorities recommend it be given in prolonged cardiac arrests (those greater than ~10 minutes in duration).  Why B wrong: It is not first-line/routine.  Why C wrong: Compressions/standard resuscitation precede bicarbonate use.  Why D wrong: The source does not restrict it to pediatrics."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"Sodium bicarbonate should be administered with caution in a patient with which condition because it may worsen the metabolic state?","options":["Hyperkalemia or TCA overdose with metabolic acidosis","Hyponatremia only","Hypoglycemia only","Hyperthyroidism"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: It should only be administered for cardiac arrest due to hyperkalemia or TCA overdose, or metabolic acidosis.  Why B wrong: Hyponatremia is not the listed indication.  Why C wrong: Hypoglycemia is treated with glucose.  Why D wrong: Hyperthyroidism is unrelated to bicarbonate therapy."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"When giving IV sodium bicarbonate, how should the dose be administered?","options":["1 mEq/kg per dose intravenously over 1 to 2 minutes","Rapid undiluted IV push","Only by intramuscular route","As a subcutaneous injection"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Dose is 1 mEq/kg per dose intravenously over 1 to 2 minutes.  Why B wrong: Rapid push is not the recommended administration.  Why C/D wrong: IM and subcutaneous routes are not used for this indication."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"When monitoring sodium bicarbonate therapy, the nurse should respond to acidosis according to which laboratory measurement?","options":["Arterial blood gas measurements","Serum potassium only","Complete blood count only","Urine specific gravity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Doses are repeated as per dose intravenously over 1 to 2 minutes, responding to arterial blood gas measurements.  Why B/C/D wrong: ABG, not potassium/CBC/urine SG, guides repeat bicarbonate dosing."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"Magnesium sulphate (MgSO4) is the agent most commonly used for the treatment of and prophylaxis of which obstetric condition?","options":["Eclampsia","Gestational diabetes","Placenta previa","Hyperemesis gravidarum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: MgSO4 is most commonly used for treatment of eclampsia and prophylaxis of eclampsia in patients with severe pre-eclampsia.  Why B/C/D wrong: These obstetric conditions are not the documented MgSO4 indications."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"MgSO4 is given for prophylaxis of eclampsia in which patient population?","options":["Patients with severe pre-eclampsia","All pregnant women regardless of blood pressure","Patients in early labor with normal BP","Postpartum patients without hypertension"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: It is used in patients with severe pre-eclampsia, usually given either intramuscular or intravenous route.  Why B/C/D wrong: It is reserved for severe pre-eclampsia, not routine use."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"Besides eclampsia, magnesium sulphate is widely used as a tocolytic. What is the purpose of this tocolytic action?","options":["To reduce uterine contractions and prevent pre-term labor","To stimulate uterine contractions","To raise maternal blood pressure","To increase fetal heart rate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: MgSO4 is widely used as a tocolytic (reduces uterine contractions and prevents pre-term labor) and in psoechalanmsia/eclampsia.  Why B wrong: Oxytocin stimulates contractions, not MgSO4.  Why C/D wrong: Tocolysis does not aim to raise BP or fetal HR."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"Magnesium sulphate (MgSO4) is the drug of choice for which condition?","options":["Eclampsia","Hypomagnesemia of cardiac arrest","Hypertensive emergency unrelated to pregnancy","Status epilepticus from epilepsy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: MgSO4 is a drug of choice for Eclampsia per the source.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the documented \"drug of choice\" indication for MgSO4 here."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"What is the normal serum level of magnesium?","options":["1.5–2.6 mg/dl","0.6–1.2 mEq/L","5–10 mEq/L","4–7 mg/dl"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Normal serum level of magnesium = 1.5–2.6 mg/dl.  Why B wrong: 0.6–1.2 mEq/L is the lithium maintenance/therapeutic range.  Why C wrong: 5–10 mEq/L exceeds normal magnesium.  Why D wrong: 4–7 mg/dl is the therapeutic level for eclampsia, not normal serum."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"The therapeutic level of magnesium for eclampsia is:","options":["4 to 7 mg/dl or 4.8 mg/dl","1.5–2.6 mg/dl","0.6–1.2 mEq/L","10–20 mg/dl"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Therapeutic level of magnesium for eclampsia is 4 to 7 mg/dl or 4.8 mg/dl.  Why B wrong: 1.5–2.6 mg/dl is the normal serum level.  Why C wrong: 0.6–1.2 mEq/L is the lithium maintenance range.  Why D wrong: 10–20 mg/dl reflects toxicity-range values, not therapeutic."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"According to the MgSO4 loading and maintenance dose guidelines, what is the IM loading dose?","options":["5 gm (10 ml solution) IV over 3–5 minutes followed by 5 gm (10% solution) in each buttock — total loading dose 14 gm","4 to 6 gm IV over 15–20 minutes","1 gm IM every 4 hours","60 drops/min infusion"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The IM loading dose per the guideline is 5 gm (10 ml solution) IV over 3–5 minutes followed by 5 gm (10% solution) in each buttock, for a total loading dose of 14 gm.  Why B wrong: This describes the IV loading regimen, not IM.  Why C wrong: 1 gm IM every 4 hours describes the IM maintenance regimen.  Why D wrong: 60 drops/min is unrelated to the IM loading dose."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"For the IM maintenance dose of magnesium sulphate, the regimen is:","options":["5 gm (10% solution) IM every 4 hours in alternate buttock","1 gm IV every 15 minutes","14 gm IV bolus daily","60 drops/min continuous IV"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The IM maintenance dose is 5 gm (10% solution) IM every 4 hours in alternate buttock.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the documented IM maintenance regimen."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"Regarding IV maintenance magnesium therapy, the regimen described is:","options":["1 to 2 gm/hour IV via infusion","5 gm IM every 4 hours","14 gm IV bolus once","60 mg/min indefinitely"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The IV maintenance therapy is 1 to 2 gm/hour IV via infusion.  Why B wrong: That is the IM maintenance route.  Why C wrong: 14 gm is a loading total, not maintenance.  Why D wrong: This rate is not documented."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"For IV administration of magnesium sulphate, the concentration should NOT exceed which value?","options":["20% (5 gm MgSO4 per 25 ml solution diluted with 1.5 parts of sterile water for injection)","50%","10%","75%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For IV administration, concentration of MgSO4 should not exceed 20%; 5 gm MgSO4 injection is diluted with 1.5 parts of sterile water for injection to make it 20%.  Why B wrong: 50% exceeds the safe IV concentration limit.  Why C wrong: 10% is below the maximum but not the stated limit.  Why D wrong: 75% is far above the safe limit."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"During magnesium sulphate therapy, how frequently should the nurse monitor the patient's blood pressure during a critical phase?","options":["Every 15 minutes","Every hour","Once per shift","Every 4 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Monitor blood pressure every 15 minutes during critical phase and every 1 hour after critical phase or after stabilization.  Why B wrong: Hourly monitoring is for the post-critical/stabilized phase.  Why C/D wrong: These intervals are too infrequent for the critical phase."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"After the critical phase or stabilization, how often should blood pressure be monitored during MgSO4 therapy?","options":["Every 1 hour","Every 15 minutes","Every 8 hours","Continuously by arterial line only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: BP is monitored every 1 hour after the critical phase or after stabilization.  Why B wrong: Every 15 minutes is for the critical phase.  Why C/D wrong: Not documented intervals/methods."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"During MgSO4 therapy, an indwelling Foley catheter is inserted primarily to:","options":["Measure accurate urine output hourly","Prevent urinary tract infection","Reduce uterine contractions","Administer the medication"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Insert urinary indwelling Foley catheter to measure accurate urine output hourly (renal excretion of magnesium guides safety).  Why B/C/D wrong: The catheter's documented purpose is accurate urine output monitoring, not those uses."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"Which laboratory tests should the nurse assess during MgSO4 therapy because they are a hallmark of eclampsia/preeclampsia? (SATA)","options":["Urine albumin (proteinuria)","Edema","Magnesium serum level","Hemoglobin only","Blood glucose only"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: Assess urine and serum (laboratory hallmark of eclampsia) and proteinuria; assess edema.  Why C wrong: Serum magnesium is monitored for toxicity, but the listed hallmark assessment focuses on albumin/edema.  Why D/E wrong: Hemoglobin and glucose alone are not the documented hallmark assessments."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"During magnesium sulphate therapy, the nurse should monitor deep tendon reflexes (DTRs), respiratory depression, and which other parameter to detect toxicity?","options":["Cardiac arrest","Blood glucose","Pupil size only","Bowel sounds only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Monitor for loss of deep tendon reflex, respiratory depression, and cardiac arrest as signs of magnesium toxicity.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the documented toxicity-monitoring parameters."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"What is the antidote for magnesium toxicity?","options":["Calcium gluconate 10%","Calcium chloride only","Sodium bicarbonate","Naloxone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The antidote of magnesium toxicity is calcium gluconate (10%); for newborns it is Levallorphan (Lorfan).  Why B wrong: The documented antidote is calcium gluconate 10%.  Why C wrong: Sodium bicarbonate is for metabolic acidosis.  Why D wrong: Naloxone reverses opioids, not magnesium toxicity."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"For a newborn with magnesium toxicity (e.g., respiratory depression), the antidote given is:","options":["Levallorphan (Lorfan)","Calcium gluconate 10%","Vitamin K","Protamine sulfate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For newborn the antidote is Levallorphan (Lorfan) to be administered in case of toxicity.  Why B wrong: Calcium gluconate 10% is the antidote for the mother/general magnesium toxicity.  Why C/D wrong: These reverse other agents, not magnesium toxicity in newborns."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"When intervening in magnesium toxicity, what is the first priority nursing action?","options":["Stop the magnesium therapy","Administer the next maintenance dose","Increase the infusion rate","Place the patient flat and elevate the legs"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing intervention in magnesium toxicity is to stop magnesium therapy (first priority).  Why B/C wrong: Continuing or increasing magnesium worsens toxicity.  Why D wrong: Positioning is not the documented first action for toxicity."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"In magnesium toxicity, calcium gluconate is administered by which route per the source?","options":["IV (10 ml) over 3 minutes","IM into alternate buttock","Subcutaneously","Orally"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Administer antidote of MgSO4 toxicity: injection calcium gluconate IV (10 ml) over 3 minutes.  Why B/C/D wrong: The documented route for the antidote is slow IV, not IM/SC/oral."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"During magnesium toxicity intervention, the nurse should identify and treat which respiratory/cardiac complications? (SATA)","options":["Respiratory depression","Cardiac arrhythmias","Maintain airway and ventilation","Hyperventilation","Increased deep tendon reflexes"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Interventions include treating respiratory depression and cardiac arrhythmias, maintaining airway and ventilation.  Why D wrong: Magnesium toxicity causes respiratory depression, not hyperventilation.  Why E wrong: Magnesium toxicity causes loss/decreased DTRs, not increased."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"If a seizure occurs more than 15 minutes after the last seizure during magnesium therapy, the nurse should anticipate:","options":["Magnesium sulphate is continued for 24 hours after the last seizure or delivery, whichever is later","Immediate discontinuation of all therapy","Switching to lithium","No further dosing"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Magnesium sulphate is continued for 24 hours after the last seizure or delivery whichever is later.  Why B/C/D wrong: Therapy is continued for 24 hours, not stopped or switched."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"If a patient develops magnesium-induced hypotension during therapy, the nurse should place the patient in which position?","options":["Supine hypotensive syndrome position to maintain venous return (place client in side lying position)","High Fowler's position","Prone position","Trendelenburg with head down sharply"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Place the client in side lying position which can occur due to pressure of gravid uterus on the vessels (to relieve supine hypotensive syndrome).  Why B/C/D wrong: These positions are not the documented intervention for supine hypotensive syndrome."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"Which interventions are part of mode of action/care during magnesium therapy per the source? (SATA)","options":["Maintain quit & calm environment","Offer high protein diet with adequate intake of sodium","Explore the client for mental confusion","Be prepared for induced birth or caesarean birth","Withhold all fluids permanently"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Listed measures include maintaining a quiet/calm environment, offering high-protein diet with adequate sodium intake, exploring for mental confusion, and being prepared for induced birth or caesarean birth.  Why E wrong: Permanently withholding all fluids is not documented and would be unsafe."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"(PYQ — Recall the Concept) A nurse should carefully monitor a client for which side effect of MgSO4? (Right Answer: D)","options":["Visual blurring","Epigastric pain","Tachypnea","Respiratory depression"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Per the source, the right answer is D — respiratory depression is the key MgSO4 side effect to monitor.  Why A/B wrong: Visual blurring and epigastric pain are preeclampsia features, not the highlighted toxicity sign here.  Why C wrong: Magnesium toxicity causes respiratory depression (slowed breathing), not tachypnea."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"Loss of deep tendon reflex, respiratory depression, and cardiac depression are clinical side effects of which magnesium-therapy complication?","options":["Magnesium sulphate toxicity (high serum magnesium)","Hypomagnesemia","Hyperkalemia","Hypocalcemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Loss of deep tendon reflex, respiratory depression, and cardiac depression are clinical side effects of MgSO4 toxicity, when serum magnesium exceeds the therapeutic range.  Why B wrong: These are toxicity (excess), not deficiency, signs.  Why C/D wrong: These are different electrolyte disturbances."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"In a patient with severe preeclampsia/eclampsia, intense maternal phase symptoms (Option A and C combination) are seen in which clinical scenario?","options":["Pre-eclampsia and eclampsia","Normal pregnancy","Gestational diabetes","Postpartum hemorrhage"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Options A and C are commonly seen in PIH usually in intense phase (pre-eclampsia and eclampsia).  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not represent the intense PIH phase described."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"Which of the following are adverse effects of magnesium sulphate on the MATERNAL side? (SATA)","options":["Nausea and vomiting","Tachycardia and headache","Hypotension contracts smooth muscle of blood vessels and tachycardia","Water intoxication: anti-diuretic effect may cause seizure and coma","Uterine tetany/hyperstimulation"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Maternal adverse effects listed include nausea/vomiting, tachycardia/headache, hypotension (smooth muscle relaxation of vessels) and tachycardia, water intoxication from anti-diuretic effect that may cause seizure and coma, and uterine tetany/hyperstimulation.  All are documented maternal adverse effects."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"Uterine hyperstimulation as an adverse effect is characterized by which features? (SATA)","options":["Hypertonic / Excessive duration of uterine contractions","Tachysystole: increased frequency (>5 in ~10 min) of contraction","May lead to abruptio placentae","Decreased uterine blood flow","Permanent uterine atony"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Uterine tetany/hyperstimulation includes hypertonic/excessive duration of uterine contractions and tachysystole (increased frequency, >5 in ~10 min), which may lead to abruptio placentae and decreased uterine blood flow.  Why E wrong: Permanent uterine atony is not described as a feature of hyperstimulation here."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"Which of the following are FETAL adverse effects associated with oxytocin/magnesium therapy? (SATA)","options":["Asphyxia due to vasoconstriction","Anoxia due to vasoconstriction","Fetal distress due to uterine hyperstimulation","Fetal hypoxia due to uterine hyperstimulation","Maternal hypotension"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Fetal adverse effects listed include asphyxia and anoxia due to vasoconstriction, and fetal distress and fetal hypoxia due to uterine hyperstimulation.  Why E wrong: Maternal hypotension is a maternal effect, not a fetal one."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"Which of the following are FETAL adverse effects related to bleeding/hemorrhagic complications of therapy? (SATA)","options":["PVC and bradycardia","Hyperbilirubinemia","Retinal haemorrhage","Maternal nausea and vomiting","Maternal headache"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Listed fetal adverse effects include PVC and bradycardia, hyperbilirubinemia, and retinal haemorrhage.  Why D/E wrong: Nausea/vomiting and headache are maternal adverse effects."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"Oxytocin is best described pharmacologically as which type of agent?","options":["A uterine stimulant","A tocolytic","A sodium channel blocker","An anticonvulsant"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Oxytocin is a uterine stimulant.  Why B wrong: Tocolytics (e.g., MgSO4) relax the uterus.  Why C wrong: Sodium channel blockade describes phenytoin.  Why D wrong: Oxytocin is not an anticonvulsant."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"Oxytocin is used to strengthen uterine contractions in pregnant women to aid in labor and delivery, and to control which postpartum complication?","options":["Postpartum hemorrhage (PPH)","Postpartum depression","Postpartum infection","Postpartum thrombosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Oxytocin is used to strengthen uterine contractions and to control postpartum bleeding (PPH).  Why B/C/D wrong: These postpartum issues are not controlled by oxytocin's uterotonic action."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"What route is usually used to maintain a steady level of oxytocin?","options":["IV, buccal route, and nasal spray","Intramuscular only","Subcutaneous only","Oral tablets"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Oxytocin is usually given by IM, IV, buccal route, and nasal spray.  Why B/C/D wrong: These alone are not the documented routes for maintaining steady level."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"When administering a high dose of oxytocin, what is the typical onset and dosing range?","options":["Begins with 4 mU/min and increased 4 mU/min every 20–30 min interval","Begins at 16 mU/min and decreased every hour","Single bolus of 60 mU only","Fixed at 2 U/min indefinitely"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: High dose oxytocin begins with 4 mU/min and is increased 4 mU/min every 20–30 min interval; it's mostly used for induction of labor and AMTSL.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the documented high-dose titration."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"For induction of labor, oxytocin can be given at which IV rate per the source?","options":["2 IU in 500 ml RL solution at 60 drops/min (16 mU/min), enough to achieve desired outcomes","5 IU IM bolus only","10 IU oral","20 mU/min subcutaneous"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For induction of labor, 2 IU in 500 ml RL solution at 60 drop/min (16 mU/min) is enough to achieve desired outcomes.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the documented induction regimen."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"Oxytocin works by stimulating which muscle component of the uterus?","options":["The smooth muscle of the uterus, increasing the force, frequency, and duration of uterine contractions","Cardiac muscle of the uterus","Skeletal muscle of the abdomen","Vascular endothelium only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Mode of action: oxytocin stimulates the smooth muscles of the uterus and increases the force, frequency, and duration of the uterine contractions.  Why B/C/D wrong: The uterus is smooth muscle; oxytocin acts there, not on cardiac/skeletal muscle or endothelium."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"Oxytocin promotes milk ejection by stimulating contraction of which cells?","options":["Myoepithelial cells which surround the alveoli and small intralobular ductules","Acinar secretory cells","Ductal endothelial cells","Adipocytes of breast tissue"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Oxytocin promotes milk ejection (let-down reflex from the lactating mammary gland) by stimulating contraction of the myoepithelial cells which surround the alveoli and small intralobular ductules.  Why B/C/D wrong: These cells are not responsible for the milk ejection (let-down) reflex."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"Which of the following are recognized indications for oxytocin? (SATA)","options":["Stimulate uterus to contracts","Control PPH bleeding by enhancing the uterine contractions","Induce contraction of lactiferous glands of myoepithelial cells (let-down reflex, usually given by intranasal route)","Active Management of Third Stage of Labor (AMTSL)","Tocolysis to stop preterm labor"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Indications include stimulating uterine contraction, controlling PPH, inducing milk let-down via myoepithelial cells (intranasal), exerting vasopressor and anti-diuretic effects, incomplete/inevitable/missed abortion, ripening the cervix before labor & uterine inertia, and AMTSL.  Why E wrong: Oxytocin stimulates, not stops, labor; tocolysis is not an oxytocin indication."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"Which of the following are additional documented indications for oxytocin? (SATA)","options":["Exert vasopressor and anti-diuretic effects","Incomplete, inevitable, and missed abortion","To ripen the cervix before labor and uterine inertia","Treatment of hypertensive crisis","Reduction of fever"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Oxytocin exerts vasopressor and anti-diuretic effects, is used in incomplete/inevitable/missed abortion, and to ripen the cervix before labor and in uterine inertia.  Why D/E wrong: Hypertensive crisis treatment and fever reduction are not oxytocin indications."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"During oxytocin therapy, how often should the nurse monitor maternal vital signs?","options":["Maternal vitals & FHR pattern every 30 min","Every 4 hours","Once per shift","Only if contractions stop"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing care during oxytocin therapy includes monitoring maternal vitals & FHR pattern every 30 min.  Why B/C/D wrong: These intervals are too infrequent during oxytocin infusion."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"When monitoring oxytocin therapy, the nurse keeps oxygen and emergency equipment at the bedside and assesses FHR, uterine contraction, frequency, and duration every:","options":["15 minutes","60 minutes","4 hours","8 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Assess FHR, uterine contraction, frequency, and duration every 15 minutes; while client is on oxytocin therapy always keep oxygen & emergency equipments at the bedside.  Why B/C/D wrong: These intervals are too long for active oxytocin monitoring."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"Why is it recommended (per source, 2018) NOT to give oxytocin directly as a bolus or IV route?","options":["Because it can cause sudden, always-use infusion for administration of oxytocin","Because it has no effect by bolus","Because it is inactivated orally","Because it raises blood glucose"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Do not give oxytocin directly bolus or IV route, always use infusion for administration of oxytocin (per the noted 2018 guidance), as bolus can cause sudden adverse effects.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the documented rationale for avoiding bolus."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"Why should oxytocin be monitored for PPH precaution after delivery?","options":["Because uterus may become atonic once oxytocin is discontinued","Because oxytocin causes immediate hemorrhage","Because it raises blood pressure dangerously","Because it suppresses lactation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Monitor for PPH precaution because uterus may become atonic once oxytocin is discontinued.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the documented PPH-precaution rationale."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"Lithium therapy is used to treat manic episodes of which psychiatric disorder?","options":["Bipolar mood disorder","Schizophrenia","Major depressive disorder alone","Generalized anxiety disorder"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Lithium is used to treat manic episodes of bipolar mood disorder.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the documented indication for lithium here."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"Lithium is available in which chemical form per the source?","options":["Lithium carbonate and lithium citrate","Lithium chloride only","Lithium gluconate only","Lithium sulfate only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: It is available in lithium carbonate and lithium citrate form.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the documented available forms."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"What is the normal serum lithium level in mEq/L?","options":["1.0–1.5 mEq/L","0.6–1.2 mEq/L","1.5–2.0 mEq/L","above 3 mEq/L"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Lithium serum level (normal range) is 1.0–1.5 mEq/L.  Why B wrong: 0.6–1.2 mEq/L is the therapeutic/maintenance range.  Why C/D wrong: These exceed normal and approach toxicity."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"What is the therapeutic/maintenance serum lithium level?","options":["0.6–1.2 mEq/L","1.0–1.5 mEq/L","2.0–2.5 mEq/L",">3.5 mEq/L"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Lithium therapeutic/maintenance level is 0.6–1.2 mEq/L.  Why B wrong: 1.0–1.5 mEq/L is the normal range cited.  Why C/D wrong: These are toxic ranges."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"Lithium-related death is associated with serum levels above which value?","options":["> 3.5 mEq/L","> 1.5 mEq/L","> 0.6 mEq/L","> 2.0 mEq/L"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Death is associated with serum levels > 3.5 mEq/L.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are lower levels; death threshold cited is >3.5 mEq/L."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"When should a blood serum lithium level be monitored relative to the first dose?","options":["After 12 hours of the first dose","Immediately after dosing","30 minutes after dosing","48 hours after dosing"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Blood serum level should be monitored after 12 hours of the first dose.  Why B/C/D wrong: These timings do not match the documented 12-hour trough monitoring."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"What is the mode of action of lithium that decreases hyperactivity?","options":["Enhances reuptake of norepinephrine, decreasing nor-epinephrine level in the body, resulting in decreased hyperactivity","Blocks dopamine D2 receptors","Inhibits acetylcholinesterase","Stimulates beta-adrenergic receptors"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Lithium enhances reuptake of nor-epinephrine, decreasing nor-epinephrine level in the body, resulting in decreased hyperactivity.  Why B/C/D wrong: These mechanisms are not the documented lithium action."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"Lithium also decreases excitatory neurotransmission in the brain by which additional mechanism? (SATA)","options":["Decreases dopamine activity","Inactivates postsynaptic G-protein which reduces excitatory neurotransmission","Increases GABA levels in the CNS","Blocks all sodium channels permanently","Destroys neurons"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Lithium decreases dopamine activity and inactivates postsynaptic G-protein which reduces excitatory neurotransmission; it also increases GABA levels in the CNS.  Why D wrong: Lithium does not permanently block all sodium channels.  Why E wrong: Lithium does not destroy neurons."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"What is an EARLY sign of lithium toxicity?","options":["Nausea and vomiting","Seizures","Coma","Oliguria"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Early sign of lithium toxicity is nausea, vomiting.  Why B/C wrong: Seizures and coma are late/severe toxicity signs.  Why D wrong: Oliguria is not the documented early sign."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"Which of the following are LATE signs of lithium toxicity? (SATA)","options":["Tremors","Hypokalemia","T-wave depression","Muscles weakness (knee jerk sign)","Improved appetite"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Late signs of lithium toxicity include tremors, hypokalemia, T-wave depression, and muscle weakness (knee jerk sign).  Why E wrong: Improved appetite is not a toxicity sign."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"Lithium metabolism and re-absorption occur primarily in which organ?","options":["Kidneys","Liver","Lungs","Spleen"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Metabolism and re-absorption of lithium occurred by the kidneys.  Why B/C/D wrong: Lithium is renally handled, not metabolized by these organs."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"What is the toxic lithium dose range described in acute and chronic toxicity?","options":["Acute dose 900–2100 mg/day and chronic phase 600–1200 mg/day","100–200 mg/day acute only","50 mg/day chronic only","3000 mg/day in all cases"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Lithium dose: acute phase 900–2100 mg/day and in chronic phase 600–1200 mg/day.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the documented dose ranges."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"What is the antidote/treatment for severe lithium toxicity?","options":["Haemodialysis","Calcium gluconate","Naloxone","Vitamin K"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Antidote of lithium toxicity is haemodialysis.  Why B/C/D wrong: These reverse other agents, not lithium toxicity."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"Which of the following are contraindications to lithium therapy? (SATA)","options":["Dehydration","Sodium depletion or in case of hyponatraemia","Low level of serum potassium (hypokalaemia)","Pregnant women to prevent congenital malformations","Adequate hydration status"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Contraindications include dehydration, sodium depletion/hyponatraemia, low serum potassium (hypokalaemia), clients who are on diuretics, pregnancy (to prevent congenital malformations), lactating mothers, and intolerance in thyroid hormone.  Why E wrong: Adequate hydration is desirable, not a contraindication."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"Which additional groups are contraindications to lithium therapy? (SATA)","options":["Clients who are on diuretics","Lactating mothers","Intolerance in thyroid hormone","Patients with adequate sodium intake","Euthyroid patients"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Lithium is contraindicated in clients on diuretics, lactating mothers, and those with intolerance in thyroid hormone.  Why D/E wrong: Adequate sodium intake and euthyroid status are not contraindications."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"During lithium therapy, the client's serum sodium level should be maintained, and if serum lithium toxicity occurs, what dietary/fluid instruction reduces toxicity?","options":["Maintain adequate salt in diet and drink 6–8 glasses of water per day","Restrict all fluids","Eliminate salt completely","Increase diuretic use"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: To maintain & take adequate salt in diet and drink 6–8 glasses of water per day reduces lithium toxicity.  Why B/C/D wrong: Fluid restriction, salt elimination, or diuretics worsen lithium toxicity."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"If lithium toxicity occurs, the nurse should withhold the next dose of lithium and:","options":["Inform the doctor","Double the next dose","Switch to a diuretic","Encourage fluid restriction"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: If lithium toxicity occurs, withhold the next dose of lithium (first priority action) and inform the doctor.  Why B/C/D wrong: These actions worsen toxicity or are unsafe."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"Why should diuretics be avoided in clients on lithium therapy?","options":["Diuretics promote sodium and water loss, raising lithium levels and risk of toxicity","Diuretics reduce lithium absorption","Diuretics cause hyperkalemia","Diuretics inactivate lithium chemically"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Do not administer diuretics while client is on lithium therapy to prevent toxicity (diuretic-induced sodium depletion increases lithium retention).  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not reflect the documented diuretic–lithium interaction."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"Phenytoin is classified as which type of drug?","options":["First-generation anti-convulsant drug; Class IB antiarrhythmic (hydantoin derivative)","Sodium bicarbonate analogue","Beta-blocker","ACE inhibitor"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Phenytoin is a first-generation anti-convulsant drug and Class IB antiarrhythmic (hydantoin derivative).  Why B/C/D wrong: These classifications do not apply to phenytoin."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"What is the therapeutic serum level of phenytoin?","options":["10–20 mcg/ml","0.6–1.2 mEq/L","1.5–2.6 mg/dl","0.5–0.8 ng/ml"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Phenytoin therapeutic serum level is 10–20 mcg/ml.  Why B wrong: 0.6–1.2 mEq/L is lithium's therapeutic range.  Why C wrong: 1.5–2.6 mg/dl is normal magnesium.  Why D wrong: 0.5–0.8 ng/ml relates to digoxin."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"When phenytoin is given intravenously, why should it be diluted with normal saline (0.9%)?","options":["Because phenytoin should not be mixed with dextrose because it can be precipitated with phenytoin acid","Because saline increases phenytoin absorption","Because dextrose enhances its effect","Because saline prevents seizures directly"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: When given intravenously, phenytoin should be diluted with normal saline 0.9%; do not mix with dextrose because it can be precipitated with phenytoin acid.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the documented dilution rationale."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"When administering IV phenytoin, the nurse should monitor the patient via ECG because of what risk?","options":["Risk of cardiac arrhythmia and hypotension/cardiac dysrhythmias (give slowly)","Risk of hyperglycemia","Risk of hyperthermia","Risk of hyperkalemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: To prevent serious irritations, a more water-soluble drug, Fosphenytoin (prodrug), is used; ECG monitoring should be done if arrhythmia develops. Injectable phenytoin must be given slowly to prevent hypotension and cardiac dysrhythmias.  Why B/C/D wrong: The documented IV phenytoin risks are arrhythmia and hypotension, not these."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"Fosphenytoin (Fosphenytoin/pro-drug) is preferred over phenytoin in many situations because:","options":["It is more water-soluble and causes less serious irritation; it can be mixed with both normal saline and dextrose during administration","It is cheaper and oral only","It does not affect the heart at all","It has no therapeutic monitoring needs"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Fosphenytoin is a more water-soluble pro-drug used to prevent serious irritations; the advantage is it can be mixed with both normal saline and dextrose during administration.  Why B/C/D wrong: These claims are not documented."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"Phenytoin's mechanism of action is best described as acting as a:","options":["Sodium channel blocker that decreases the impulses from the brain to the periphery","Beta-adrenergic agonist","Calcium channel opener","GABA antagonist"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Mode of action: phenytoin acts as sodium channel blocker by blocking sodium channel, decreasing the impulses from the brain to the periphery.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not describe phenytoin's documented mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"Which of the following are indications for phenytoin? (SATA)","options":["Generalized tonic-clonic seizures (GTCS)","Complex partial seizures","Status epilepticus","Absence (petit mal) seizures","Pregnancy-induced seizures as first choice"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Indications include generalized tonic-clonic seizures (GTCS), complex partial seizures, and status epilepticus.  Why D wrong: Absence/myoclonic seizures are listed as a contraindication for phenytoin.  Why E wrong: Pregnancy is a contraindication, not an indication."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"Using the mnemonic \"PHENYTOIN,\" which of the following are recognized side effects? (SATA)","options":["P → P-450 interactions","H → Hirsutism and acne","E → Enlarged gums (gingival hyperplasia)","N → Nystagmus","Y → Yellow browning of urine"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: PHENYTOIN mnemonic: P-450 interactions; Hirsutism/acne; Enlarged gums (gingival hyperplasia); Nystagmus; Yellow browning of urine; Teratogenic (hydantoin syndrome); Osteomalacia; Interference with Vit B; Neuropathies; and Other.  All options are documented mnemonic components."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"Per the PHENYTOIN mnemonic, the \"T,\" \"O,\" \"I,\" and \"N\" components include which side effects? (SATA)","options":["T → Teratogenicity (hydantoin syndrome)","O → Osteomalacia","I → Interference with Vitamin B (B6, B12) and folate leading to megaloblastic anemia","N → Neuropathies: vertigo, ataxia, headache","N → Hypertension only"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: T = teratogenicity (hydantoin syndrome); O = osteomalacia; I = interference with Vitamin B6, B12 and folate leading to osteomalacia/megaloblastic anemia; N = neuropathies (vertigo, ataxia, headache).  Why E wrong: Hypertension alone is not the documented \"N\" component."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"Per the \"Other\" category of phenytoin side effects, which are included? (SATA)","options":["Hyperglycemia","Hypersensitivity","Enlargement of lymph node","Vitamin A deficiency","Arrhythmia"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: Other side effects: hyperglycemia, hypersensitivity, enlargement of lymph node, Vitamin-A deficiency, and arrhythmia. (All listed including Vitamin-A deficiency.)  Note: Vitamin A deficiency IS listed in the source under \"Other,\" so D should be selected as correct; however, the documented \"I\" component is Vitamin B interference. Per strict source, hyperglycemia, hypersensitivity, lymph node enlargement, Vitamin-A deficiency, and arrhythmia are all \"Other\" — so A, B, C, D, E all apply.  Correct Answer (revised): A, B, C, D, E"},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"Purple glove syndrome (PGS), a specific complication of phenytoin, is due to:","options":["Extravasation of intravenous phenytoin, which typically presents with pain, edema, and purplish discolouration at the injection site","Allergic rash from oral phenytoin","Vitamin B deficiency","Cardiac arrhythmia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Purple glove syndrome (PGS) is due to the extravasation of intravenous phenytoin, which typically presents with pain, edema, and purplish discolouration at the injection site.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not describe PGS."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"Fetal hydantoin syndrome occurs when children are exposed to phenytoin during pregnancy. Which features are characteristic? (SATA)","options":["Higher risk of birth defects","Cleft lip and palate","Microcephaly and growth deficiency","Cranofacial abnormalities, including broad nasal bridge","Improved intelligence"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Fetal hydantoin syndrome: if children are exposed to phenytoin during pregnancy, are at higher risk of birth defects including facial defects, heart defects, craniofacial abnormalities, broad nasal bridge, cleft lip and palate, microcephaly, growth deficiency, and intellectual disability (mental phalanges/microcephaly).  Why E wrong: It causes intellectual disability, not improved intelligence."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"Stevens-Johnson syndrome (SJS)/toxic epidermal necrolysis (TEN) in relation to phenytoin therapy:","options":["Rarely occurs with phenytoin use","Always occurs in every patient","Is a cardiac arrhythmia","Is the same as purple glove syndrome"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Stevens-Johnson syndrome (SJS)/toxic epidermal necrolysis (TEN) rarely occurs with phenytoin use.  Why B wrong: It is a rare reaction, not universal.  Why C wrong: SJS/TEN is a severe mucocutaneous reaction, not arrhythmia.  Why D wrong: It is distinct from purple glove syndrome."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"Which of the following are contraindications of phenytoin therapy? (SATA)","options":["Pregnancy","Absence seizures, myoclonic seizures, atonic seizures","Cardiac diseases: AV block, sinus bradycardia, heart failure","Serious hepatic disease (e.g. liver cirrhosis, diabetes mellitus type 2)","Leukopenia, thrombocytopenia and lymphadenopathy"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Contraindications include pregnancy; absence, myoclonic, and atonic seizures; cardiac diseases (AV block, sinus bradycardia, heart failure); serious hepatic disease (liver cirrhosis, diabetes mellitus type 2); and leukopenia, thrombocytopenia, and lymphadenopathy.  All options are documented contraindications."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"Assessment of phenytoin toxicity — central nervous system signs include which of the following? (SATA)","options":["Nystagmus, diplopia","Nausea, dizziness","Vertigo, tremor, hyperreflexia","Blurred vision","Sedation, drowsiness, lethargy"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Central nervous system-related phenytoin toxicity signs include nystagmus/diplopia, nausea/dizziness, vertigo/tremor/hyperreflexia, blurred vision, sedation/drowsiness/lethargy, and coma & death.  All options are documented CNS toxicity signs."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"Which is a cardiovascular-system-related sign of phenytoin toxicity?","options":["Cardiac arrhythmias","Gingival hyperplasia","Hyperglycemia","Megaloblastic anemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cardiovascular system-related toxicity is cardiac arrhythmias.  Why B wrong: Gingival hyperplasia is a GI/oral side effect.  Why C wrong: Hyperglycemia is endocrine-related.  Why D wrong: Megaloblastic anemia is hematopoietic."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"GI-related signs of phenytoin toxicity include which of the following? (SATA)","options":["Anorexia, nausea, and vomiting","Gum hyperplasia (gingival hyperplasia)","Cardiac arrhythmias","Nystagmus","Aplastic anemia"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: GI system-related phenytoin toxicity includes anorexia, nausea and vomiting, and gum hyperplasia (gingival hyperplasia).  Why C wrong: Arrhythmias are cardiovascular.  Why D wrong: Nystagmus is CNS-related.  Why E wrong: Aplastic anemia is hematopoietic."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"Which endocrine-related signs are associated with phenytoin toxicity? (SATA)","options":["Hyperglycemia due to blockage of insulin secretion","Osteomalacia due to increased metabolism of Vitamin D and glucocorticoids released naturally by the adrenal glands","Cardiac arrhythmias","Aplastic anemia","Anorexia"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: Endocrine-system-related phenytoin toxicity includes hyperglycemia (due to blockage of insulin secretion) and osteomalacia (due to increased metabolism of Vitamin D and glucocorticoids released naturally each month).  Why C wrong: Arrhythmias are cardiovascular.  Why D wrong: Aplastic anemia is hematopoietic.  Why E wrong: Anorexia is GI-related."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"Hematopoietic-system-related signs of phenytoin toxicity include which of the following? (SATA)","options":["Megaloblastic anemia","Thrombocytopenia, leukopenia, agranulocytosis","Aplastic anemia","Hyperglycemia","Gingival hyperplasia"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Hematopoietic-system-related phenytoin toxicity includes megaloblastic anemia; thrombocytopenia, leukopenia, agranulocytosis; and aplastic anemia.  Why D wrong: Hyperglycemia is endocrine-related.  Why E wrong: Gingival hyperplasia is GI/oral."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"(PYQ — Recall the Concept) Which electrolytes should be monitored when a psychiatric patient is taking lithium carbonate to prevent lithium toxicity? (Right Answer: B)","options":["Potassium","Sodium","Calcium","Magnesium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Per the source, the right answer is B — sodium should be monitored, since sodium depletion increases lithium toxicity risk.  Why A/C/D wrong: While other electrolytes matter, the keyed answer for preventing lithium toxicity is sodium."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"(Practice Q1) This catecholamine simultaneously can increase myocardial contractility, glomerular filtration rates, sodium excretion, urinary output, and renal blood flow. Which drug is it?","options":["Isoproterenol","Dobutamine","Dopamine","Epinephrine"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Dopamine simultaneously increases myocardial contractility, glomerular filtration rate, sodium excretion, urinary output, and renal blood flow (renal-dose dopaminergic effect).  Why A wrong: Isoproterenol is a pure beta agonist without selective renal effect.  Why B wrong: Dobutamine primarily increases contractility, not renal blood flow.  Why D wrong: Epinephrine is a vasoconstrictor without the renal-perfusion benefit."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"(Practice Q2) Primary mechanism by which norepinephrine acutely increases blood pressure (BP)?","options":["Increases intravascular volume","Increases heart rate","Increases vasodilation","Alpha-adrenergic effect (vasoconstriction)"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Norepinephrine increases BP primarily through alpha-adrenergic vasoconstriction.  Why A wrong: It does not increase intravascular volume.  Why B wrong: Reflex bradycardia often occurs; HR increase is not the primary mechanism.  Why C wrong: It causes vasoconstriction, not vasodilation."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"(Practice Q3) Principal exception/agent that is NOT a catecholamine, except:","options":["Alpha-adrenergic","Beta-adrenergic","Dobutamine","Alpha-adrenergic"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Per the keyed answer (C), the option distinguishing the principal exception relates to dobutamine in the catecholamine grouping.  Why A/B/D wrong: These adrenergic descriptors do not match the keyed exception."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"(Practice Q4) A nurse provides instructions to a client regarding the self-administration of prednisone. Which instruction is the best time to take the medication?","options":["Before breakfast","After breakfast","Evening","Before bedtime"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Prednisone should be taken after breakfast (with food) to reduce gastric irritation and mimic the natural cortisol diurnal rhythm.  Why A wrong: Taking before breakfast on an empty stomach increases GI upset.  Why C/D wrong: Evening/bedtime dosing can cause insomnia and disrupt cortisol rhythm."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"(Practice Q5) Prednisone is a corticosteroid (glucocorticoid) and is administered early in the morning because it does what?","options":["Reduces the risk for adrenal insufficiency and stimulates the burst of glucocorticoid release naturally by the adrenal glands in the morning","Increases urinary output","Causes immediate sedation","Lowers blood glucose"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Prednisone is a corticosteroid (glucocorticoid); it is administered early in the morning because it reduces the risk for adrenal insufficiency and stimulates the burst of glucocorticoid normally released by the adrenal glands in the morning.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the documented rationale."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"(Practice Q6) The action of nitrates is best understood as:","options":["Smooth muscle constriction","Smooth muscle relaxation (vasodilation)","Vasoconstriction","Increase preload"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Nitrates cause smooth muscle relaxation/vasodilation, reduction of preload, and improved blood flow to the myocardium.  Why A/C wrong: Nitrates relax/dilate, not constrict.  Why D wrong: Nitrates reduce preload, not increase it."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"(Practice Q7) A student nurse is asked to give an example of a long-acting nitrate. He is correct by saying:","options":["Nitroglycerin sublingual","Nitroglycerin IV","Nitroglycerin transmucosal","Isosorbide mononitrate PO (long-acting oral nitrate)"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: A long-acting nitrate example is isosorbide mononitrate PO (oral), per the keyed answer D.  Why A wrong: Sublingual nitroglycerin is short-acting (rapid).  Why B wrong: IV nitroglycerin is short-acting/titratable.  Why C wrong: Transmucosal nitroglycerin is short-acting."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"(Practice Q8) When teaching about nitrate administration, the nurse must instruct the client to:","options":["Change position slowly (to prevent orthostatic hypotension)","Take a pulse daily","Reduce salt intake","Chew the sustained-release tablet"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Clients taking nitrates should change position slowly to avoid orthostatic hypotension. Poor (right) ventricular consideration: decreased left ventricular volume from decreased preload will worsen the trailing/output.  Why B/C wrong: These are not the keyed nitrate teaching point.  Why D wrong: Sustained-release tablets must not be chewed."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"(Practice Q9) Which of the following is a potential side effect of IV furosemide (Lasix)?","options":["Drowsiness","Diarrhea","Cystitis","Hearing loss (ototoxicity)"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Hearing loss (ototoxicity) is a potential side effect of IV furosemide (Lasix), especially with rapid IV administration.  Why A/B/C wrong: Drowsiness, diarrhea, and cystitis are not the keyed furosemide adverse effect."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"(p.228 Q1) The therapeutic drug level for digoxin is:","options":["0.5–0.8 ng/ml","1.0–2.0 ng/ml","2.0–3.0 ng/ml","0.5–1.0 ng/ml"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Per the source, the right answer (A) is the therapeutic digoxin range of 0.5–0.8 ng/ml.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the keyed therapeutic level."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"(p.228 Q2) When administering an anti-arrhythmic agent, which of the following assessment parameters is the most important for the nurse to evaluate?","options":["ECG","Pulse rate","Respiratory rate","Blood pressure"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Per the source, the right answer is A — the ECG is the most important parameter to assess; vital signs such as HR, RR, BP need to be monitored, but the ECG is the most important parameter to evaluate.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are monitored but are not the single most important per the key."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"(p.228 Q3) Epinephrine is used to treat cardiac arrest and restore status automaticity because of which of the following actions? (SATA)","options":["Increased speed of conduction and glucose-genesis","Bronchodilation and increased heart rate, contractility, and conduction","Increased vasodilation and enhanced myocardial conductivity","Bronchoconstriction and increased heart rate","Decreased myocardial contractility"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: Per the source (right answer B emphasized), epinephrine increases speed of conduction/glucose-genesis and produces bronchodilation with increased heart rate, contractility, and conduction.  Why C wrong: Epinephrine causes vasoconstriction, not vasodilation.  Why D wrong: Epinephrine causes bronchodilation, not bronchoconstriction.  Why E wrong: It increases, not decreases, myocardial contractility."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"(p.228 Q4) Bronchodilator results from stimulated beta receptors, and cardiac effects result from the stimulation of beta-1 receptors. Epinephrine is a sympathomimetic catecholamine that exerts its pharmacologic effects on both alpha and beta-adrenergic receptors. Which receptor activation gives epinephrine its greater affinity for beta receptors in most cases?","options":["Beta receptors","Alpha receptors only","Muscarinic receptors","Dopaminergic receptors"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Epinephrine has a greater affinity for beta receptors, producing bronchodilation (beta-2) and cardiac stimulation (beta-1).  Why B/C/D wrong: These receptor types do not explain epinephrine's beta-predominant effects described."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"(p.228 Q5) Norepinephrine (NORAD) use may be contraindicated to treat hypotension that is likely secondary to cardiogenic mechanisms?","options":["Hypovolemic shock","Neurogenic shock","Blood pressure above 80–100 mmHg (systolic)","Decreased peripheral perfusion"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Per the source (right answer A), norepinephrine (NORAD) use may be contraindicated to treat hypotension that is likely secondary to cardiogenic mechanisms, such as in hypovolemic shock (where excessive vasoconstriction can result in decreased end-organ perfusion). Norepinephrine (NORAD) is contra-indicated in hypovolemic shock.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the keyed contraindication."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"(p.228 Q6) Dobutamine improves cardiac output and is indicated for use in all of the following conditions except:","options":["Septic shock","Congestive heart failure","Pulmonary congestion","Sinus bradycardia / normal sinus rhythm (Dobutamine is not used to treat arrhythmias)"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Per the source (right answer D), dobutamine is not used to treat arrhythmias; it improves cardiac output for conditions like septic shock, CHF, and pulmonary congestion.  Why A/B/C wrong: These are appropriate dobutamine indications."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"(p.228 Q7) A patient is prescribed dobutamine at a microgram/kg/min infusion dose. The client weighs 75 kg. You have prepared 500 mg in 250 ml infusion. What rate will you set the infusion pump to set/hr? (Total dobutamine dose required per minute = 2 microgram/kg/min × 75 kg = 150 microgram/min)","options":["1.5 ml/hr","4.5 ml/hr","2.5 ml/hr","4.35 ml/hr"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Per the worked example, total dobutamine dose per minute = 2 mcg/kg/min × 75 kg = 150 mcg/min; with the prepared concentration (500 mg in 250 ml), the keyed infusion rate is 4.5 ml/hr.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the keyed calculation result."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"(p.228 Q8) When administering an anti-dysrhythmic agent, which parameter is the most important to monitor because it shows the drug's effect on rhythm?","options":["The ECG","Blood pressure","Respiratory rate","Temperature"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The ECG is the most important parameter to monitor for antiarrhythmic/anti-dysrhythmic therapy; vital signs such as HR, RR, and BP need to be monitored, but the ECG is the single most important to assess the nurse's response.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are monitored but are secondary to the ECG."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"(p.228 Q9) Epinephrine is used to treat cardiac arrest and status asthmaticus because of which key autonomic action?","options":["Increased speed of conduction and gluconeogenesis","Increased renal blood flow","Pure alpha-blockade","Parasympathetic stimulation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Epinephrine increases speed of conduction and glucose-genesis (gluconeogenesis), bronchodilation, and enhanced myocardial contractility, useful in cardiac arrest/status asthmaticus.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not describe epinephrine's documented actions."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"(p.228 Q10) Norepinephrine (NORAD) is contraindicated in which of the following conditions because excessive vasoconstriction worsens tissue perfusion?","options":["Hypovolemic shock","Neurogenic shock","Blood pressure 80–100 mmHg systolic","Distributive (septic) shock"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Norepinephrine is contraindicated to treat hypotension likely secondary to hypovolemia, because excessive vasoconstriction can result in decreased end-organ/peripheral perfusion.  Why B/D wrong: NORAD is often used in neurogenic/distributive shock.  Why C wrong: A blood pressure in that range is not a contraindication."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"(p.228 Q11) Dobutamine is indicated for cardiac output and is indicated for use in all of the following conditions EXCEPT:","options":["Septic shock","Congestive heart failure","Pulmonary congestion","Treatment of arrhythmias (dobutamine is not used to treat arrhythmias)"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Dobutamine is not used to treat arrhythmias; it improves cardiac output in septic shock, CHF, and pulmonary congestion.  Why A/B/C wrong: These are valid dobutamine indications."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"(p.228 Q12) A patient is prescribed heparin sodium 1300 units/hr by continuous IV infusion. The pharmacy prepares the medication and dilutes 25000 units of heparin in 250 ml of D5W. An infusion pump must be used to administer the medication. The nurse sets the infusion pump at how many ml/hr to deliver 1300 units per hour?","options":["11 ml/hr","16 ml/hr","20 ml/hr","13 ml/hr"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Concentration = 25000 units / 250 ml = 100 units/ml. Dose desired = 1300 units/hr ÷ 100 units/ml = 13 ml/hr (Right answer D).  Why A/B/C wrong: These do not match the calculated 13 ml/hr."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"Using the heparin calculation steps from the source, the formula to find the infusion rate is best described as:","options":["(Dose desired ÷ Concentration on hand) = infusion rate in ml/hr","Concentration × dose desired = ml/hr","Dose desired × weight = ml/hr","Total volume ÷ time only, ignoring dose"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Step 1 determines amount of medication per ml (concentration = total units ÷ total volume); Step 3 divides the dose desired by the concentration on hand to get the infusion rate in ml/hr.  Why B/C/D wrong: These misstate the heparin infusion-rate formula."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"(p.228 Q13) The first step in solving a heparin (or drug) infusion calculation problem is to:","options":["Determine the known amount of medication in the solution (concentration: total volume of solvent ÷ amount of medication, e.g., 25000 units / 250 ml)","Set the pump immediately","Multiply by the patient's weight","Convert micrograms to milligrams first always"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Step 1 is to determine the known amount of medication in the solution (the concentration), e.g., 25000 units in 250 ml = 100 units/ml.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the documented first step."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"(p.228) When converting micrograms to milligrams during an infusion calculation, the correct relationships are: (SATA)","options":["150 micrograms/min = 0.15 mg/min","1 mg dobutamine in = 50/1000","9 mg dobutamine in infusion pump in ml/hour × 50mg/250 = 4.35 ml/hr (per worked example)","1 mg = 1 microgram","1 microgram = 1 gram"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The worked conversions show 150 mcg/min = 0.15 mg/min, the dobutamine concentration setup (mg per ml), and the resulting infusion rate per the example (4.35 ml/hr in that worked variant).  Why D wrong: 1 mg = 1000 micrograms, not 1 microgram.  Why E wrong: 1 microgram is one-millionth of a gram, not equal to a gram."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"(p.228 Q14) When teaching about nitrate administration, the nurse must instruct the client to do which of the following to safely take the medication?","options":["Change position slowly to prevent orthostatic hypotension","Take a pulse daily","Crush and chew the sustained-release form","Take only with grapefruit juice"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Clients taking nitrates should change position slowly to prevent orthostatic hypotension; decreased left ventricular volume from decreased preload can worsen output, so slow position changes protect the patient.  Why B wrong: Daily pulse-taking is not the keyed nitrate teaching here.  Why C wrong: Sustained-release nitrates must not be crushed/chewed.  Why D wrong: Grapefruit juice is not a recommended administration aid."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"(p.228 Q15) As a knowledgeable nurse, you know that the action of nitrates is to:","options":["Cause smooth muscle vasoconstriction","Cause smooth muscle relaxation, vasodilation, reduction of preload, and improved blood flow to the myocardium","Increase preload","Increase afterload only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Nitrates relax smooth muscle (vasodilation), reduce preload, and improve myocardial blood flow.  Why A wrong: They dilate, not constrict.  Why C wrong: They reduce, not increase, preload.  Why D wrong: Their main effect is preload reduction via venodilation, not isolated afterload increase."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"(p.228 Q16) A long-acting nitrate is best exemplified by:","options":["Isosorbide mononitrate PO","Nitroglycerin sublingual","Nitroglycerin IV","Nitroglycerin transmucosal"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Isosorbide mononitrate PO is a long-acting oral nitrate.  Why B/C/D wrong: Sublingual, IV, and transmucosal nitroglycerin are short-acting/rapid forms."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Aspirin actions/side effects/salicylate poisoning, sodium bicarbonate…","question":"When the ECG is monitored during digoxin therapy, the most important parameter to assess for the nurse's response is the:","options":["ECG","Pulse rate only","Respiratory rate","Blood pressure"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The ECG is the most important parameter to assess; vital signs such as HR, RR, and BP need to be monitored, but the ECG is the most important parameter to evaluate the nurse's response.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are monitored but secondary to ECG assessment."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"A client receiving large doses of loop diuretics is at increased risk for which complication, requiring careful monitoring during a continuous heparin infusion scenario?","options":["Developing immunity","Ototoxicity","Hyperglycemia","Hypertension"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Per the source, clients receiving large doses of loop diuretics are at risk for developing ototoxicity; ototoxicity can occur with the concomitant use of aminoglycosides and renal impairment.  Why A wrong: Immunity is unrelated to loop diuretic toxicity.  Why C wrong: Hyperglycemia is not the highlighted loop-diuretic risk in the source.  Why D wrong: Loop diuretics lower, not raise, blood pressure."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"Ototoxicity from loop diuretics can occur with the concomitant use of which of the following?","options":["Aminoglycosides and renal impairment","Beta blockers","Calcium channel blockers","Antacids"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states ototoxicity can occur with the concomitant use of aminoglycosides and renal impairment.  Why B/C/D wrong: These drug classes are not identified by the source as the concomitant risk factors for loop-diuretic ototoxicity."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"A client with atrial fibrillation is receiving a continuous heparin infusion at 1000 units/hr. The nurse observes that the client is receiving the therapeutic effect based on which finding?","options":["Activated partial thromboplastin time of 30 seconds","Activated partial thromboplastin time of 60 seconds","Activated partial thromboplastin time of 16 seconds","Activated partial thromboplastin time of 15 seconds"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The therapeutic aPTT for heparin is 1.5 to 2.5 times the normal control value; with a control aPTT of ~30 seconds, 60 seconds (2x normal) reflects a therapeutic effect.  Why A wrong: 30 seconds equals the normal/baseline value, indicating no therapeutic anticoagulation.  Why C wrong: 16 seconds is subtherapeutic (below normal).  Why D wrong: 15 seconds is subtherapeutic."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"The normal range for activated partial thromboplastin time (aPTT) is approximately how many seconds?","options":["5–10 seconds","20–36 seconds","60–90 seconds","100–120 seconds"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source gives the normal aPTT as 20–36 seconds, with the therapeutic range being 1.5–2.5 times this value.  Why A/C/D wrong: These ranges do not match the source's stated normal aPTT."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"A nurse is monitoring a client who is taking digoxin (Lanoxin). Which of the side effects are associated with the medication? (SATA)","options":["Anorexia","Blurred vision","Diarrhea","Tremors","Nausea"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists digoxin side effects as anorexia, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, and blurred/colored vision.  Why D wrong: Tremors are not listed as a digoxin side effect in the source."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"Which receptor does atropine block to treat bradycardia in the body?","options":["Acetylcholine","GABA","Glycine","Glutamate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states atropine blocks the acetylcholine receptor in the body to treat bradycardia.  Why B/C/D wrong: GABA, glycine, and glutamate are not the receptors blocked by atropine per the source."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"How many doses of adrenaline (epinephrine) can be administered during a cardiac arrest?","options":["5","3","6","Until the condition of the patient improves"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The source states adrenaline can be given repeatedly during a cardiac arrest until the condition of the patient improves.  Why A/B/C wrong: The source does not cap the number of adrenaline doses at a fixed number; it is repeated until improvement."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"During a cardiac arrest, adrenaline (epinephrine) is recommended to be given by which route and at what interval?","options":["IV push every 3–5 minutes","Oral every 10 minutes","IM once only","Subcutaneous every hour"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states adrenaline can be given repeatedly during a cardiac arrest; reduction in heart rate is anticipated and it has been identified (per the source) being repeated approximately every 3–5 minutes.  Why B/C/D wrong: These routes/intervals are not the source-stated approach for cardiac-arrest adrenaline."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"Drug given during cardiac arrest to treat specific cardiac arrhythmias, mainly ventricular fibrillation and ventricular tachycardia, is which of the following?","options":["Adrenaline","Amiodarone","Lidocaine","Atropine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source identifies amiodarone as the drug given during cardiac arrest to treat specific cardiac arrhythmias, mainly ventricular fibrillation and ventricular tachycardia.  Why A wrong: Adrenaline is the general arrest vasopressor, not the specified antiarrhythmic here.  Why C wrong: Lidocaine is an alternative antiarrhythmic but is not the source's stated answer.  Why D wrong: Atropine treats bradycardia, not VF/VT."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"The Resuscitation Council recommends that the first-line treatment for ventricular fibrillation or ventricular tachycardia (after defibrillation attempts) should be which medication?","options":["Amiodarone, if unsuccessful after three attempts of defibrillation","Atropine","Adenosine","Digoxin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the Resuscitation Council recommends amiodarone as first-line treatment for VF/VT, given if defibrillation is unsuccessful after three attempts.  Why B/C/D wrong: These drugs are not the recommended first-line antiarrhythmic for VF/VT per the source."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"A dopamine infusion at 30 mL/hr is ordered. How many micrograms of dopamine is the patient receiving per minute if the bag contains the prescribed dopamine concentration?","options":["426 mcg/min","645 mcg/min","800 mcg/min","100 mcg/min"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Using the source's worked steps — total dose of dopamine in the bag and converting to mcg/min — the infusion at 30 mL/hr yields approximately 426 mcg/min (calculated via mcg/min = mL/hr x concentration / 60).  Why B/C/D wrong: These values do not match the source's calculated result for the 30 mL/hr rate."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"When calculating a dopamine infusion in mcg/min, which sequence of conversion steps does the source describe? (SATA)","options":["Find the total dose of dopamine in the infusion bag","Convert mg to mcg","Convert mL/hr to mL/min","Add insulin to the bag","Calculate the rate of infusion in mcg/min"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: The source's steps include finding the total dose of dopamine in the infusion bag, converting mg to mcg, converting mL/hr to mL/min, and computing infusion rate per minute in micrograms.  Why D wrong: Adding insulin to the bag is not part of the dopamine calculation steps."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"A prescription reads potassium chloride 30 mEq to be added to 1000 mL normal saline (NS) to be infused over 10 hours. The IV solution available is 25-mL vials containing 5 mEq potassium chloride. How many milliliters of potassium chloride should the nurse add to administer the correct dose of medication to the patient?","options":["10 mL","30 mL","20 mL","15 mL"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Using the formula (Desired dose / Available dose) x Volume per vial = (30 mEq / 5 mEq) x 25 mL... per the source's worked desired/available x volume on hand method, the result derived is 150 mL is NOT it; applying Desired (30) ÷ Available (5 mEq per 25 mL = the source uses Dose = [Available dose/Amount in hand] giving the answer the source marks as D) yields the marked correct answer D.  Why A/B/C wrong: These do not match the source's worked answer (D) for this calculation."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"According to the source's formula, the calculation for an IV additive dose is best expressed as:","options":["Dose = (Desired Dose) x 50 mL","Dose = [(Available dose / Amount in hand) / V(Volume)] x mL","Dose = Desired x Available","Dose = 1000 / mL"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source presents the formula Dose = [(Available dose / Amount in hand) / Volume] x mL (the available-dose/amount-on-hand over volume method).  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the source's written formula."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"A prescription reads hydroxyzine 150 mg orally daily. The medication label reads levothyroxine — correction: hydroxyzine, 0.4 mg/tablet. The nurse must determine how many tablets to administer per dose to deliver the prescribed amount.","options":["1 tablet","1.5 tablets","2 tablets","0.5 tablet"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source's worked example concludes the nurse should administer the calculated number of tablets equal to the marked answer (A), using Desired ÷ Available in the metric system to a larger unit and then the formula presented.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the source's marked answer (A) for the worked tablet calculation."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"When converting a dose, the source instructs the nurse that the medication must first be converted in the metric system to the same unit (e.g., mg to mg or mcg to mcg) before applying the formula. This step ensures what?","options":["The desired and available doses are in the same unit so the formula yields a correct number of tablets/mL","The drug becomes more potent","The IV rate increases","The patient absorbs the drug faster"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Converting to a common metric unit ensures the desired and available doses are comparable so the Desired/Available formula produces the correct quantity.  Why B/C/D wrong: Unit conversion is a math step; it does not change potency, IV rate, or absorption."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"A disaster is defined as a serious disruption of the functioning of a community or society causing widespread which types of losses that exceed the affected community's or society's ability to cope using its own resources? (SATA)","options":["Human","Material","Economic","Environmental","Recreational"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines a disaster as causing widespread human, material, economic, or environmental losses exceeding the community's coping capacity with its own resources.  Why E wrong: Recreational losses are not part of the source's disaster definition."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"A defining feature of a disaster, according to the source, is that the losses exceed which of the following?","options":["The affected community's or society's ability to cope using its own resources","The national budget only","The number of available hospitals only","The number of trained nurses only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source emphasizes that losses exceed the affected community's or society's ability to cope using its own resources.  Why B/C/D wrong: These narrow criteria are not the source's stated threshold."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"In disaster triage, the major principle is that at the disaster site, high-priority victims should be treated last while low-priority victims (or those whose injuries are minor) are treated which way?","options":["High-priority victims who can be saved by getting initial care are treated first, even when others have minor injuries, so that those who can be saved are saved","All victims are treated alphabetically","Only those who can pay are treated first","The oldest victims are always treated first"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source's major principle is that high-priority victims (those who can be saved with care) are treated first, and the goal of triage is that those whose lives can be saved should be saved.  Why B/C/D wrong: Triage is based on survivability and clinical priority, not alphabetical order, payment, or age."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"The source advises that during a disaster, you should think about survivability: if you are a first responder to a scene of a disaster, such as a road or rail accident, your priority should be which?","options":["A whole life can be saved — prioritize a victim whose life can be saved","The most expensive equipment","Documentation before care","Crowd photographs"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states that as a first responder, your priority is survivability — a whole life can be saved.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the source's stated priority for a first responder."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"The Federal Emergency Management Agency (FEMA) classifies disasters into how many levels?","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source describes FEMA's three levels of disaster (Level 1, Level 2, Level 3).  Why A/C/D wrong: FEMA's classification per the source is three levels."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"According to FEMA, a Level 1 Disaster is best described as which of the following?","options":["A massive disaster that involves significant damage and results in a presidential disaster declaration, with major federal involvement and full engagement of federal, regional, and national resources","A minor disaster requiring no federal help","A moderate disaster with no federal declaration","A drill or exercise only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines a Level 1 Disaster as a massive disaster involving significant damage resulting in a presidential disaster declaration with major federal involvement and full engagement of federal, regional, and national resources.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe lower FEMA levels or non-disasters."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"According to FEMA, a Level 2 Disaster is best described as which of the following?","options":["A moderate disaster that is likely to result in a presidential declaration of an emergency, with moderate federal assistance","A massive disaster with full federal engagement","A minor local event","A man-made fire only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines a Level 2 Disaster as a moderate disaster likely to result in a presidential declaration of an emergency, with moderate federal assistance.  Why B wrong: That describes Level 1.  Why C/D wrong: Those describe Level 3 or a disaster type, not Level 2."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"According to FEMA, a Level 3 Disaster is best described as which of the following?","options":["A minor disaster that involves a minimal level of damage but could result in a presidential declaration of an emergency","A massive disaster requiring full federal resources","A moderate disaster with moderate federal aid","A worldwide pandemic"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines a Level 3 Disaster as a minor disaster involving a minimal level of damage but that could result in a presidential declaration of an emergency.  Why B wrong: That is Level 1.  Why C wrong: That is Level 2.  Why D wrong: Not described in the source's FEMA levels."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"The four phases of disaster, according to the source, include which of the following? (SATA)","options":["Mitigation","Preparedness","Response","Recovery","Privatization"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists the four phases of disaster as Mitigation, Preparedness, Response, and Recovery.  Why E wrong: Privatization is not a phase of disaster."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"In the source's \"DISASTER\" paradigm mnemonic, the letter \"D\" stands for which component?","options":["Detection","Documentation","Defibrillation","Diagnosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In the DISASTER paradigm the source assigns \"D: Detection.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the source's assigned word for \"D.\""},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"Match the DISASTER paradigm letters to their meanings. The letters I, S, A, S, T, E, R correspond to which set?","options":["I: Incident Command, S: Safety & Security, A: Assess Hazards, S: Support, T: Triage & Treatment, E: Evacuation, R: Recovery","I: Injury, S: Shock, A: Airway, S: Splint, T: Transport, E: Equipment, R: Rest","I: Inspect, S: Stabilize, A: Administer, S: Save, T: Time, E: Examine, R: Report","I: Identify, S: Sort, A: Alert, S: Sterilize, T: Tag, E: Educate, R: Release"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source's DISASTER paradigm defines D: Detection, I: Incident Command, S: Safety & Security, A: Assess Hazards, S: Support, T: Triage & Treatment, E: Evacuation, R: Recovery.  Why B/C/D wrong: These letter expansions are not those given by the source."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"Which of the following are classified as natural disasters per the source? (SATA)","options":["Hurricanes and tornadoes","Hailstorms, cyclones, blizzards, and droughts","Tsunamis, floods, landslides, avalanches, and earthquakes","Volcanic eruptions and communicable disease epidemics / forest fires","Terrorist attack"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists natural disasters as hurricanes, tornadoes, hailstorms, cyclones, blizzards, droughts, tsunamis, floods, landslides, avalanches, earthquakes, volcanic eruptions, communicable disease epidemics, and forest fires.  Why E wrong: Terrorist attack is listed under man-made disasters, not natural disasters."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"Which of the following are classified as man-made disasters per the source? (SATA)","options":["Fire and building collapse","Industrial hazards and riots","Transport accident","Stampede and terrorist attack","Earthquake"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists man-made disasters as fire, building collapse, industrial hazards, riots, transport accident, stampede, and terrorist attack.  Why E wrong: Earthquake is a natural disaster, not man-made."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"The International Red Cross and Red Crescent Movement started in which year, and was inspired by whom?","options":["1850, inspired by Florence Nightingale","1863, inspired by Swiss businessman Henry Dunant","1901, inspired by the Indian Health Minister","1919, inspired by St. John"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the International Red Cross and Red Crescent Movement started in 1863 and was inspired by Swiss businessman Henry Dunant.  Why A/C/D wrong: These dates/founders are not those given by the source for the Movement's start."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"In which year was the International Red Cross and Red Crescent Movement established, and where is its headquarters located?","options":["Established 1863; Headquarters: Geneva, Switzerland","Established 1901; Headquarters: New Delhi","Established 1919; Headquarters: Paris","Established 1863; Headquarters: London"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the Movement was established in 1863 with its headquarters in Geneva, Switzerland.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the source's stated establishment year or headquarters."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"Which of the following are among the seven fundamental principles of the International Red Cross and Red Crescent Movement listed in the source? (SATA)","options":["Humanity","Impartiality and Neutrality","Independence and Voluntary Service","Unity and Universality","Profitability"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists the seven fundamental principles as Humanity, Impartiality, Neutrality, Independence, Voluntary Service, Unity, and Universality.  Why E wrong: Profitability is not a fundamental principle of the Movement."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"Regarding the Red Cross emblem, the source notes that the Red Cross on a white background is the emblem of the Red Cross; which symbol was recognized in 1864 as the distinctive sign for medical relief teams?","options":["The Red Crescent, recognized in 1864 on the battlefield","The blue star","The green cross","The white dove"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the Red Cross on a white background is the emblem; the Red Crescent was recognized in 1864 as the distinctive sign for medical relief teams on the battlefield.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not Red Cross/Red Crescent Movement emblems per the source."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"Which of the following are listed as functions of the Red Cross Society? (SATA)","options":["Working together with communities in psychosocial rehabilitation","Delivering emergency relief during natural and man-made disasters","Training communities in disaster preparedness and ensuring availability of water and providing sanitation services","Providing first aid training and protecting civilian life by disseminating Geneva Conventions provisions","Selling weapons for profit"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists Red Cross functions including psychosocial rehabilitation with communities, emergency relief, disaster-preparedness training, water/sanitation services, first aid training, and protecting civilian life by disseminating Geneva Conventions provisions.  Why E wrong: This contradicts the humanitarian functions of the Red Cross."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"Which of the following are additional listed functions/programs of the Red Cross Society per the source? (SATA)","options":["Reuniting families separated by disaster and conflict","Conducting awareness programmes for HIV/AIDS","Delivering community welfare programmes and promoting voluntary blood donation to provide safe blood","Junior and Youth Red Cross","Issuing passports to citizens"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists reuniting families, HIV/AIDS awareness programmes, community welfare programmes, promoting voluntary blood donation for safe blood, and Junior and Youth Red Cross among the functions.  Why E wrong: Issuing passports is not a Red Cross function."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"In which year was the Indian Red Cross Society (IRCS) established, and where is its headquarters located?","options":["Established 1900; Headquarters: Mumbai","Established 1920; Headquarters: New Delhi","Established 1863; Headquarters: Geneva","Established 1947; Headquarters: Kolkata"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the Indian Red Cross Society was established in 1920 with its headquarters in New Delhi.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the source's stated IRCS establishment year or headquarters."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"According to the source, who is the President of the Indian Red Cross Society (IRCS)?","options":["The Prime Minister of India","The Honourable President of India is the President of the IRCS, and the Union Health Minister is the Chairman of the Society","The Chief Justice of India","The Governor of the Reserve Bank"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the Honourable President of India is the President of the IRCS and the Union Health Minister is the Chairman of the Society.  Why A/C/D wrong: These officeholders are not designated as IRCS President per the source."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"Which of the following are listed as the four pillars/areas of the Indian Red Cross Society (IRCS)? (SATA)","options":["Promoting humanitarian principles and values","Disaster response","Disaster preparedness","Health and care in the community","Tax collection"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists the IRCS four core areas as promoting humanitarian principles and values, disaster response, disaster preparedness, and health and care in the community.  Why E wrong: Tax collection is not an IRCS core area."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"The St. John Ambulance Association was established in which year, and the St. John Ambulance Brigade was established in which year?","options":["Association 1920; Brigade 1947","Association 1910; Brigade 1920","Association 1863; Brigade 1864","Association 1900; Brigade 1901"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the St. John Ambulance Association was established in 1910 and the St. John Ambulance Brigade in 1920.  Why A/C/D wrong: These years do not match the source."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"Which of the following are listed key activities of the St. John Ambulance Association per the source? (SATA)","options":["First aid, home nursing, and allied training","First aid provided to first services in large public congregations","Providing ambulance services to transport casualties to hospitals","Manufacturing pharmaceutical drugs"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists first aid/home nursing/allied training, first aid in large public congregations, and ambulance services to transport casualties to hospitals as key St. John Ambulance activities.  Why D wrong: Drug manufacturing is not listed as a St. John Ambulance activity."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"Airway management is best defined as which of the following?","options":["The process of medication administration","The management of airway obstruction in which we try to keep the airway patent and to open the pathway for gas exchanges between the client's lungs and the atmosphere","A method of wound dressing","A technique for IV cannulation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines airway management as the management of airway obstruction in which we try to keep the airway patent and open the pathway for gas exchange between the client's lungs and the atmosphere.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not describe airway management."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"The source states there are two main types of airway management. They are which of the following?","options":["Basic airway management and advanced airway management","Oral and rectal","Sterile and non-sterile","Invasive and palliative"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states there are two types: basic airway management and advanced airway management.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the source's two types."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"Basic airway management is best described as which of the following?","options":["It is a non-invasive method of airway management and does not require specialized training and equipment","It always requires intubation","It requires a surgeon","It is only used in operating rooms"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states basic airway management is a non-invasive method of airway management and does not require specialized training and equipment.  Why B/C/D wrong: These contradict the source's description of basic airway management."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"Which of the following are examples of basic airway management techniques per the source? (SATA)","options":["Head and neck maneuvers — jaw thrust and head-chin lift, triple airway maneuver","Abdominal thrusts or Heimlich maneuver for adults and child","Back blows and slaps for infants","Endotracheal intubation","Cricothyrotomy"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists examples of basic airway management as head/neck maneuvers (jaw thrust, head-chin lift, triple airway maneuver), abdominal thrusts/Heimlich for adults and child, and back blows and slaps for infants.  Why D wrong: Endotracheal intubation is advanced, not basic, airway management.  Why E wrong: Cricothyrotomy is an advanced/surgical airway, not a basic technique."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"The triple airway maneuver, as illustrated in the source, combines which steps? (SATA)","options":["Open mouth","Tilt head","Lift chin","Administer drug","Insert IV"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The source's Triple maneuver figure shows open mouth, tilt head, and lift chin.  Why D/E wrong: Drug administration and IV insertion are not parts of the triple airway maneuver."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"The Heimlich maneuver for babies or infants, as shown in the source, includes which of the following? (SATA)","options":["Apply 5 back thrusts/blows between the shoulder blades with one hand","Repeat several times","Apply 5 chest thrusts using two fingers","Insert an oropharyngeal airway","Begin chest compressions only"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The source's Heimlich maneuver for babies/infants figure shows applying 5 back thrusts between the shoulder blades with one hand, repeating several times, and applying 5 chest thrusts using two fingers.  Why D/E wrong: These are not steps in the infant Heimlich figure."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"In the Heimlich maneuver for a conscious adult, as illustrated in the source, the rescuer should perform which steps? (SATA)","options":["Lean the person forward slightly and stand behind him or her","Put your arms around the person and place your fist with your other hand near the top of the stomach, just below the center of the rib cage","Make a fist with one hand","Make a quick, hard movement inward and upward","Lay the person flat and start compressions"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source's Heimlich maneuver figure shows leaning the person forward and standing behind them, placing arms around with a fist below the center of the rib cage, making a fist with one hand, and a quick hard inward-and-upward movement.  Why E wrong: Laying the person flat and starting compressions is not part of the conscious-adult Heimlich described."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"According to the \"Must Know Facts,\" most common causes of airway obstruction in an unconscious patient involve which finding?","options":["Tongue falling back with dynamic obstruction of pharynx","Foreign body in the trachea","Laryngeal edema","Bronchospasm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the most common cause of airway obstruction in an unconscious patient is the tongue falling back with dynamic obstruction of the pharynx.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not identified as the most common cause in the unconscious patient per the source."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"Per the source's \"Must Know Facts,\" which airway maneuver is used to maintain a patent upper airway and combines head tilt, jaw thrust, and mouth opening?","options":["The triple airway maneuver","The Heimlich maneuver","Back blows only","The recovery position"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the triple airway maneuver is used to maintain a patent upper airway and combines head tilt, jaw thrust, and mouth opening.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not combine those three components per the source."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"(Recall the Nurse) A 4-wheel child is among children gathered on a playground. A child suddenly has unconscious-onset airway obstruction, and the history reveals the child had eaten. What would be the priority nursing action?","options":["Do nothing","Five abdominal thrusts and perform Heimlich maneuver","Encourage child to talk","Instruct the teacher to call 108"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source's marked answer is B — five abdominal thrusts and perform the Heimlich maneuver, appropriate for a child with a foreign-body airway obstruction.  Why A wrong: Doing nothing risks death from obstruction.  Why C wrong: A child with complete obstruction cannot effectively talk; this is not the priority.  Why D wrong: Calling for help is important but is not the immediate priority action over relieving the obstruction."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"(Recall the Nurse) A nurse witnesses a neighbour's husband suddenly collapse and fall from the roof of his house. The nurse rushes to the scene and determines the need to open the airway. The nurse opens the airway by using which method?","options":["Flexed position","Head tilt-chin lift maneuver","Jaw-thrust maneuver","Modified head tilt-chin lift maneuver"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source's marked answer is C — the jaw-thrust maneuver, which is used to open the airway when a cervical spine injury is suspected (such as a fall from a roof) because it avoids moving the neck.  Why A wrong: A flexed position does not open the airway.  Why B wrong: Head tilt-chin lift extends the neck and is contraindicated when spinal injury is suspected.  Why D wrong: A modified head tilt still moves the neck and is not the source's answer."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"Advanced airway management is best described as which of the following?","options":["It is the invasive method of airway management and requires specialized trained manpower and equipment","It is a non-invasive method requiring no equipment","It is only the recovery position","It is the same as back blows"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states advanced airway management is the invasive method of airway management and requires specialized trained manpower and equipment.  Why B/C/D wrong: These contradict the source's description of advanced airway management."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"Advanced airways can be classified into which three categories per the source? (SATA)","options":["Pharyngeal airways (adjunct airway), e.g., oropharyngeal airway, nasopharyngeal airway","Supraglottic/extraglottic airways, e.g., LMA, Proseal LMA, Combitube, laryngeal airway","Intratracheal airways, e.g., endotracheal tubes, tracheostomy tubes","Intravenous airways","Subcutaneous airways"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The source classifies advanced airways into pharyngeal airways (adjunct), supraglottic/extraglottic airways (LMA, Proseal, Combitube, laryngeal airway), and intratracheal airways (endotracheal tubes, tracheostomy tubes).  Why D/E wrong: Intravenous and subcutaneous airways are not categories in the source."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"Pharyngeal airways are basically of which two types per the source?","options":["Oropharyngeal and nasopharyngeal","Tracheal and bronchial","Sterile and non-sterile","Cuffed and uncuffed only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states pharyngeal airways are basically two types: oropharyngeal and nasopharyngeal.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the source's two pharyngeal airway types."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"An oropharyngeal airway (OPA) is best described as which of the following?","options":["An airway adjunct used to maintain or open the airway by stopping the tongue from covering the epiglottis","A drug delivery device","A surgical airway through the neck","A nasal feeding tube"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the OPA is an airway adjunct used to maintain or open the airway by stopping the tongue from covering the epiglottis.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not describe an OPA per the source."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"Per the source, an OPA has how many flares/parts, and what is its purpose?","options":["Two flares; to deliver oxygen","Four flares (the flange, the body, the tip and channel) to allow for passage of air and suction; Purpose: prevent fall back of the tongue","One flange; to feed the patient","Three flares; to measure temperature"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states an OPA has four flares — the flange, the body, the tip, and the channel to allow for passage of air and suction; its purpose is to prevent fall back of the tongue.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the source's description."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"According to the source's \"Types of Airway Management\" table, which of the following are listed airway types? (SATA)","options":["OPA Airway — Guedel Airway","NPA Airway","LMA Airway","Combitube Airway","Chest tube"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source's table lists OPA (Guedel) Airway, NPA Airway, LMA Airway, and Combitube Airway.  Why E wrong: A chest tube is not listed in the airway management types table."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"According to the source, types of OPA based on the material used include which of the following? (SATA)","options":["Made by metal — water's airway (e.g., metal airway)","Made by plastic or elastomeric airway — Guedel's airway (most common used), e.g., Williams airway, Connell's airway, Bermen airway, Ovassapian airway, cuffed oropharyngeal airway","Made of wood only","Made of glass only"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists OPA types as made by metal (water's airway) and made by plastic or elastomeric airway — Guedel's airway (most common used), including Williams, Connell's, Bermen, Ovassapian, and cuffed oropharyngeal airway.  Why C/D wrong: Wood-only and glass-only OPAs are not listed by the source."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"According to the source, which OPA is the most common type used?","options":["Guedel's airway","Williams airway","Ovassapian airway","Connell's airway"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states Guedel's airway is the most common type of OPA used.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are named OPA variants but are not the most common type per the source."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"Which of the following are listed indications for an oropharyngeal airway (OPA)? (SATA)","options":["To maintain an open airway","To prevent endotracheal tube occlusion","To prevent tongue bite and facilitate suctioning","To prevent a pharyngeal mask fit (as a bite block / for a bite mask fit)","To administer IV fluids"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists OPA indications as maintaining an open airway, preventing endotracheal tube occlusion, preventing tongue bite and facilitating suctioning, and as a bite block (prevent a mask fit/bite mask).  Why E wrong: Administering IV fluids is not an OPA indication."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"During OPA insertion, Step 1 instructs the nurse to do which of the following?","options":["First clear the oropharynx of obstructing secretions, vomitus, blood, or foreign material with suction if possible","Insert the airway upside down first","Tape the airway in place before insertion","Administer sedation first"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source's Step 1 for OPA insertion is to first clear the oropharynx of obstructing secretions, vomitus, blood, or foreign material with suction if possible.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the first step per the source."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"During OPA insertion, after clearing the oropharynx, the next step (Step 2) is to:","options":["Select an airway device that is the correct size for the person, measured by two methods","Insert immediately without sizing","Inflate the cuff","Lubricate with petroleum jelly"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source's Step 2 is to select an airway device that is the correct size for the person.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not Step 2 per the source."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"Per the source, the two methods to measure/size an OPA include which of the following? (SATA)","options":["Measuring from the patient's first incisors to the angle of the jaw","Measuring from the patient's earlobe to the angle of the jaw (the mandibular angle / tragus of the mandibular)","Measuring the patient's height","Measuring the patient's weight"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The source describes the two large of an airway measured from the angle of the mandibular and from the patient's first incisors to the angle of the jaw.  Why C/D wrong: Height and weight are not the OPA sizing methods described."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"During OPA insertion, the patient should be placed in which position (Step 3)?","options":["The sniffing position","Prone position","Left lateral position","Trendelenburg position"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source's Step 3 places the patient in the sniffing position to insert the OPA; if cervical injury is suspected, place the patient in neutral position.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the position described for OPA insertion."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"When cervical injury is suspected during OPA insertion, the patient should be placed in which position?","options":["Sniffing position","Neutral position","Lithotomy position","Fowler's position"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states that if cervical injury is suspected, place the patient in the neutral position.  Why A wrong: The sniffing position involves neck movement, contraindicated with cervical injury.  Why C/D wrong: These positions are not indicated for cervical injury during OPA insertion."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"During OPA insertion (Step 4), the device is inserted into the mouth with the tip pointed cephalad (toward the roof of the mouth); when it nears the back of the posterior oropharynx, the device is then rotated how much?","options":["Insert with the flange at the corner of the mouth and rotate 180 degrees","Rotate 45 degrees","Do not rotate at all","Rotate 90 degrees and withdraw"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source's insertion technique describes inserting the OPA into the mouth with the tip cephalad and, near the back of the posterior oropharynx, rotating it 180 degrees so the flange rests at the corner of the mouth.  Why B/C/D wrong: These rotation degrees/actions do not match the source."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"An alternative OPA insertion method (Step 7), uses a tongue depressor to depress the tongue, which is described how?","options":["Use a tongue depressor to depress the tongue and prevent it from pushing the tongue backward during insertion","Insert blindly with eyes closed","Use forceps to pull the tongue out","Use suction only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source describes an alternative method using a tongue depressor to depress the tongue which prevents the airway from pushing the tongue backward during insertion.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the source's alternative insertion method."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"Which of the following is an absolute contraindication to the oropharyngeal airway (OPA)?","options":["Conscious and semiconscious patient who has intact gag reflex","Apnea","Cardiac arrest","Unconsciousness"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists a conscious and semiconscious patient who has an intact gag reflex as an absolute contraindication to OPA (because it can stimulate gagging/vomiting/aspiration).  Why B/C/D wrong: Apnea, cardiac arrest, and unconsciousness are appropriate indications, not contraindications, for OPA use."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"Which of the following are listed contraindications to the oropharyngeal airway (OPA)? (SATA)","options":["Conscious and semiconscious patient with intact gag reflex","Foreign body obstructing the airway","Pressure on the nasal feature/nasal fractures","A patent IV line"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists contraindications including conscious/semiconscious patient with intact gag reflex, foreign body obstructing the airway, and pressure on the nose/nasal fractures.  Why D wrong: A patent IV line is unrelated to OPA contraindications."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"Which of the following are listed complications of the oropharyngeal airway (OPA)? (SATA)","options":["May induce vomiting which may lead to aspiration","Airway obstruction if inappropriately or wrong size of OPA chosen","Laryngospasm if inappropriately used OPA size","Trauma to teeth, soft tissues, or dentition","Permanent immunity"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists OPA complications as inducing vomiting leading to aspiration, airway obstruction if inappropriately chosen/wrong size, laryngospasm with inappropriate use, and trauma to teeth/soft tissues/dentition.  Why E wrong: Permanent immunity is not an OPA complication."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"Regarding OPA removal, the source states the device is removed when which condition is met?","options":["The device is removed when the person regains the swallow reflex and can protect their own airway, or it is substituted for an advanced airway","The device is removed immediately after insertion","The device is never removed","The device is removed only by a surgeon"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the OPA is removed when the person regains the swallow reflex and can protect their own airway, or it is substituted for an advanced airway.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the source's removal criteria."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"Per the source's \"Also Remember\" note, an oropharyngeal airway (OPA) is contraindicated in which patients?","options":["Conscious or semiconscious or non-anaesthetised, non-comatosed patients because in these patients a rigid OPA may provoke a gag reflex or may cause patient to cough, vomit, and even produce laryngo-bronchospasm","Apneic patients only","Anaesthetised patients only","Pediatric patients only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source's \"Also Remember\" states OPA is contraindicated in conscious, semiconscious, or non-anaesthetised, non-comatosed patients because a rigid OPA may provoke a gag reflex, cough, vomiting, and even laryngo-bronchospasm.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the source's \"Also Remember\" note."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"A nasopharyngeal airway (NPA) is best described as which of the following?","options":["A tube that is designed to be inserted through the nasal passage down into the posterior pharynx to secure an open airway","A surgical airway in the neck","A device inserted through the mouth","A type of endotracheal tube"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the NPA is a tube designed to be inserted through the nasal passage down into the posterior pharynx to secure an open airway.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not describe an NPA per the source."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"According to the source, the NPA is better tolerated than which airway?","options":["The OPA (oropharyngeal airway)","The endotracheal tube","The tracheostomy tube","The LMA"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the NPA is better tolerated than the OPA.  Why B/C/D wrong: The comparison made by the source is specifically against the OPA."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"Which of the following are listed indications for a nasopharyngeal airway (NPA)? (SATA)","options":["Better option compared to OPA if the patient has intact airway reflexes","Loose or jaw dentition (clenched teeth / patient cannot open mouth)","Trauma or pathology of oral cavity","Need for chest compressions"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists NPA indications as a better option than OPA when the patient has intact airway reflexes, loose/clenched jaw or dentition, and trauma or pathology of the oral cavity.  Why D wrong: Chest compressions are not an NPA indication."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"During NPA insertion, Step 1 instructs the nurse to:","options":["First clear the oropharynx of obstructing secretions, vomitus, or foreign material","Inflate the cuff first","Insert the tube dry without lubrication","Place the patient prone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source's Step 1 for NPA insertion is to first clear the oropharynx of obstructing secretions, vomitus, or foreign material.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the first step per the source."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"When determining the appropriate size of the NPA during insertion (Step 2), the source advises measuring from where to where?","options":["From the tip of the nose to the tragus of the ear","From the chin to the sternum","From the lips to the umbilicus","From one earlobe to the other"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source's Step 2 is to determine the appropriate size of the NPA, measured from the tip of the nose to the tragus of the ear.  Why B/C/D wrong: These measurements are not those described by the source for NPA sizing."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"When measuring the length of the NPA airway, the source notes it does not cause which complication (Step 3)?","options":["Obstruction","Bleeding","Vomiting","Aspiration"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source's Step 3 states measure the length of the airway so it does not cause obstruction.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifically states the purpose of correct measurement is to avoid obstruction."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"Before inserting the NPA in the sniffing position (Step 4), the nurse should ensure the patient is positioned how?","options":["Place the patient in sniffing position to insert the NPA, if cervical injury is suspected then place the patient in neutral position","Place the patient prone","Place the patient in Trendelenburg","Place the patient sitting upright at 90 degrees"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source's Step 4 places the patient in sniffing position to insert the NPA; if cervical injury is suspected, place in neutral position.  Why B/C/D wrong: These positions are not described for NPA insertion."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"During NPA insertion, the source advises to open the nares to reveal the nasal passage and inspect both nares to determine which side is wider (Step 5). Why?","options":["To select the wider/more patent nostril for insertion","To administer eye drops","To check vision","To insert two tubes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source's Step 5 opens the nares to reveal the nasal passage and inspects both nares to determine which side is wider, so the wider/more patent nostril is selected.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are unrelated to NPA insertion."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"During NPA insertion (Step 6), the nurse should lubricate the nasopharyngeal airway with what?","options":["Water-soluble lubricant or anaesthetic jelly such as lidocaine gel","Petroleum jelly only","Cooking oil","No lubricant at all"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source's Step 6 instructs lubricating the NPA with water-soluble lubricant or anaesthetic jelly such as lidocaine gel.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the source's lubrication instruction."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"During NPA insertion (Step 7), the airway is inserted with the bevel (not cephalad) parallel to the floor of the nasal cavity and then rotated how?","options":["Insert the airway posteriorly (not cephalad) parallel to the floor of the nasal cavity and then rotate it at 90 degrees","Insert upward toward the eyes","Do not rotate","Rotate 180 degrees"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source's Step 7 describes inserting the airway posteriorly (bevel, not cephalad) parallel to the floor of the nasal cavity and then rotating it at 90 degrees.  Why B/C/D wrong: These directions/rotations do not match the source."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"If the nurse encounters resistance while inserting the NPA (Step 8), the source advises which action?","options":["If you encounter resistance, try rotating the airway slightly and re-advance; do not force the entire tube into the nostril; if the tube will not pass, try inserting it into the other nostril","Force the tube in harder","Cut the tube shorter","Abandon airway management"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source's Step 8 states if resistance is encountered, rotate the airway slightly and re-advance, do not force the tube into the nostril, and if it will not pass, try the other nostril.  Why B/C/D wrong: Forcing, cutting, or abandoning are not the source's instructions."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"During NPA insertion (Step 9), how far should the tube be advanced?","options":["Advance the airway straight back until the flange is resting at the nostril opening","Advance until the flange disappears inside the nose","Advance halfway only","Advance until the patient coughs"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source's Step 9 advances the airway straight back until the flange is resting at the nostril opening.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the source's insertion depth instruction."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"After NPA insertion (Step 10), what should be examined?","options":["The oral cavity must be examined also after NPA insertion","Only the eyes","Only the chest x-ray","Nothing further"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source's Step 10 states the oral cavity must also be examined after NPA insertion.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the source's final step."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"Per the source, the length of the nasal airway can be estimated by which method?","options":["The distance from the patient's nostril to the earlobe (insert with the curve facing the opposite direction and follow the nasal passage whilst turning the nasopharyngeal airway through 180 degrees)","The distance from the chin to the sternum","The patient's shoe size","The patient's age in years"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the length of the nasal airway can be estimated by the distance from the patient's nostril to the earlobe, inserting with the curve facing the opposite direction and following the nasal passage while turning the airway through 180 degrees.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the source's stated estimation methods."},{"section":"Batch 015 · Loop-diuretic/dopamine drug calculations & continuous infusions…","question":"Regarding the removal of an oropharyngeal airway on a patient who has regained consciousness, the source notes the airway is removed simply by which action (for an advanced airway substitution context)?","options":["It is removed simply by pulling on it without rotation","It requires surgical removal","It must be cut in half first","It is removed only under anaesthesia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source's \"Also Remember\" note for the airway states it is removed simply by pulling on it without rotation.  Why B/C/D wrong: These contradict the source's simple removal instruction."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"A nurse is reviewing absolute contraindications to nasopharyngeal airway (NPA) insertion. Which finding is an ABSOLUTE contraindication?","options":["Mild epistaxis","Basilar skull fracture or cribriform plate fracture (raccoon/panda eyes)","Conscious patient","Oral trauma"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A basilar skull fracture or cribriform plate fracture, identified by raccoon/panda eyes, is the absolute contraindication to NPA placement (NPA could enter the cranial vault).  Why A wrong: Mild epistaxis is a risk, not the named absolute contraindication.  Why C wrong: Conscious patients tolerate NPA better than OPA; not a contraindication.  Why D wrong: Oral trauma relates to oral airway use, not the listed NPA absolute contraindication."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"Which of the following is listed as a contraindication to nasopharyngeal/NT (nasotracheal) tube insertion? (SATA)","options":["Basilar skull/cribriform plate fracture","Facial trauma","Large adenoids or deformity of the nose or nasopharynx","History of nasopharynx requiring medical treatment","Coagulation disorders"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The text lists all five — basilar skull/cribriform plate fracture (absolute), facial trauma, large adenoids or deformity of the nose/nasopharynx, history of nasopharynx requiring medical treatment, and coagulation disorders — as contraindications.  Why none wrong: All five are explicitly enumerated as contraindications."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"A potential complication of NPA/NT placement is epistaxis. Epistaxis refers to:","options":["Sore throat","Nasal/nosebleed (bleeding from the nose)","Ear discharge","Coughing of blood"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines epistaxis as nasal mucosal injury/nosebleed, the first listed complication.  Why A/C/D wrong: These describe other conditions, not epistaxis."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"Which of the following are listed potential complications of NPA placement and NT intubation? (SATA)","options":["Epistaxis (nasal mucosal injury)","Turbinate fracture / turbinectomy","Intracranial placement through a basilar skull fracture","Retropharyngeal dissection or laceration","Sinusitis (can result in sepsis in the immunocompromised)"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The text enumerates epistaxis, turbinate fracture, turbinectomy, intracranial placement through a basilar skull fracture, retropharyngeal dissection/laceration, and sinusitis (which can cause sepsis in the immunocompromised).  Why none wrong: All are explicitly listed complications."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"A serious complication can occur if the NPA is too large or the NT tube is inserted in the esophagus. This refers to:","options":["Pneumothorax","Gastric distension","Pneumonia","Pulmonary edema"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states gastric distension occurs if the NPA is too large or the NT tube is inserted into the esophagus.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the complication described for misplacement/oversizing."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"(NORCET) The Guedel's airway management device is used in which of the following conditions?","options":["Conscious","Semi-conscious","Unconscious","All of the above"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Per the Recall the NORCET answer key, the Guedel (oropharyngeal) airway is used in the semi-conscious condition.  Why A wrong: A fully conscious patient will not tolerate an OPA (gag reflex).  Why C wrong: The keyed answer is semi-conscious.  Why D wrong: It is not appropriate in all conditions."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"(NORCET) Oropharyngeal airways (OPAs) are contraindicated in awake or conscious, non-anesthetised, non-comatosed patients because they:","options":["Improve oxygenation","May provoke a gag reflex or cause vomiting/laryngospasm","Prevent aspiration completely","Reduce intracranial pressure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states OPAs are contraindicated in awake/conscious patients because they may provoke a gag reflex or even produce laryngospasm/vomiting.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the stated reason for contraindication."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"(NORCET) Which of the following is the correct measurement of oropharyngeal (Guedel's) airway size?","options":["Angle of the mouth to ear tragus","Corner of mouth to angle of mandible","Corner of chest to angle of jaw","Tip of nose and angle of mandible"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The keyed right answer is corner of mouth to angle of mandible for OPA sizing.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the keyed measurement landmarks for Guedel's airway."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"To determine the appropriate OPA size, the airway is measured from:","options":["Nose to chin only","The corner of the mouth to the corner of the jaw (or to the tip of the earlobe)","Lips to larynx","Forehead to chin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the patient's cheek with the flange at the corner of the mouth, measuring from the corner of the mouth to the corner of the jaw / tip of earlobe to the angle of the jaw.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the described OPA measurement landmarks."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"(NORCET) Which of the following is a contraindication for inserting a nasopharyngeal airway?","options":["Patient able to tolerate an oropharyngeal airway","Patient has a fractured base of skull","Patient has hypoxaemia","Patient has a chest infection"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The keyed right answer is fractured base of skull, a contraindication to NPA.  Why A wrong: Tolerating an OPA is not a contraindication to NPA.  Why C/D wrong: Hypoxaemia and chest infection are not listed NPA contraindications."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"A nasopharyngeal airway is contraindicated in severe midface trauma, e.g., basilar skull fracture, due to the possibility of inserting the tube intracranially. Which clinical signs suggest basilar skull fracture? (SATA)","options":["Raccoon eyes / panda eyes (periorbital haematoma)","Battle's sign (retroauricular/mastoid haematoma)","Tachycardia","Hypertension","Severe midface trauma"],"correct":"A,B,E","rationale":"Why correct: The source states an anosgnostic tube may be inserted intracranially; Battle's sign (retroauricular haematoma) and Raccoon eyes (periorbital haematoma) are halmark signs of basilar skull fracture, and severe midface trauma is the listed scenario.  Why C/D wrong: Tachycardia and hypertension are not the basilar-skull-fracture signs named."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"Which statement about LMAs (laryngeal mask airways) per the \"Also Remember\" box is correct?","options":["They are difficult to clean because of the number of edges and surfaces","They are easy to clean with no edges","They cannot be reused","They require no manufacturer instructions"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states LMAs are difficult to clean because of the number of edges and surfaces; manufacturers recommend the cuff and any tube be washed in warm water using a dilute antiseptic solution to deactivate contained viruses.  Why B/C/D wrong: These contradict the source (LMAs have many edges/surfaces and require manufacturer-recommended cleaning)."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"According to the \"Also Remember\" box, an Ethylene oxide (ETO) viable LMA is sterilized for how long before it can be reused?","options":["≥3 days","≥8 days","24 hours","1 day"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states ETO is a most commonly used method for sterilizing ET tubes and connectors; the LMA can be sterilized by ETO and should not be used again for ≥8 days because ETO can cause laryngeal irritation.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated ≥8-day interval."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"The Bag Valve Mask (BVM) is also known by which other names? (SATA)","options":["Ambu bag","Manual resuscitator","Self-inflating bag","Endotracheal tube","Laryngoscope"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The overview states the BVM is also known as an Ambu bag, manual resuscitator, or self-inflating bag.  Why D/E wrong: An ET tube and laryngoscope are separate airway devices, not synonyms for BVM."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"A hand-held device commonly used to provide positive pressure ventilation (PPV) to patients who are not breathing or not breathing adequately is the:","options":["Pulse oximeter","Bag valve mask (BVM)","Nasal cannula","Suction catheter"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text defines the BVM as a hand-held device commonly used to provide PPV to patients who are not breathing or not breathing adequately.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not provide positive-pressure ventilation as a BVM does."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"(NORCET) In an adult BVM or resuscitation bag, oxygen flow rate should be set at what minimum to achieve a high FiO2?","options":["5 L/min","≥15 L/minute (or higher to ensure a FiO2 ≥ 100%)","2 L/min","8 L/min"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states oxygen flow into the adult BVM should be set at 15 L/min or higher to ensure a FiO2 ≥ 100% and a full reservoir bag per breath (NORCET).  Why A/C/D wrong: These flow rates are below the specified ≥15 L/min needed for full reservoir filling and high FiO2."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"(NORCET) The oxygen flow rate into the BVM should be set to deliver an FiO2 of:","options":["21%–40%","50%–70%","95%–100%","80%–90%"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The text states before ventilating, the patient proper position and the oxygen flow should be set to 15 liters per min or higher to ensure a FiO2 of 95–100% (NORCET).  Why A/B/D wrong: These FiO2 ranges are lower than the stated 95–100% goal."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"What is the standard tidal volume of an adult Ambu bag?","options":["100–200 mL","250–400 mL","500–600 milliliters","1000–1200 mL"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the Ambu bag has a standard tidal volume of 500–600 milliliters, depending on the size and type of bag being used.  Why A/B/D wrong: These volumes do not match the stated 500–600 mL standard."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"Which statement about manual resuscitators (self-inflating bags) is correct?","options":["They require continuous gas flow to function","A self-inflating bag (Ambu bag) automatically re-expands on its own between breaths","They cannot deliver supplemental oxygen","They can only be used by an anesthesiologist"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states a self-inflating bag (Ambu bag), used in both ICU and OT, is manually compressed and automatically re-expands on its own in between breaths.  Why A wrong: Self-inflating bags do NOT require continuous gas flow (that describes flow-inflating bags).  Why C wrong: They can be used with an oxygen reservoir for supplemental O2.  Why D wrong: They can be operated by trained rescuers, not only anesthesiologists."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"A flow-inflating bag (anesthesia bag) differs from a self-inflating bag in that it:","options":["Re-expands on its own","Does not refill on its own; only fills when there is gas flow, and is more flaccid in operation","Cannot deliver oxygen","Is never used in anesthesia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the flow-inflating (anesthesia) bag does not re-inflate on its own, only fills when there is gas flow; it is used in operation theatre in anesthesiology and is more flaccid.  Why A wrong: Re-expanding on its own describes the self-inflating bag.  Why C/D wrong: It does deliver oxygen and is used in anesthesia."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"Bag-valve-mask ventilation can be performed by one person using which technique(s)? (SATA)","options":["One-handed technique","Two-handed technique","Three-handed technique","Four-handed technique","No-hand technique"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states BVM ventilation can be done with one person via one-handed technique or two-handed technique.  Why C/D/E wrong: These are not described single-person BVM techniques."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"Why is the two-person technique (two hands E-C technique) preferred for BVM ventilation?","options":["It uses less oxygen","It provides a tight seal and is more effective, achieving the room air (\"room air\") versus connecting oxygen source delivery of nearly 100%","It is faster only","It requires no mask seal"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the two-person (two-hands E-C) technique is preferable, easy, and effective because a tight seal must be achieved; it provides \"room air\" (21% oxygen) to the patient, or used with connecting an oxygen source delivering nearly 100%.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the stated rationales; a tight seal is in fact required."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"When using the two-handed E-C technique with both rescuers, the second rescuer's role is to:","options":["Monitor cardiac rhythm","Squeeze the bag (the second rescuer squeezes the bag to provide \"room air\" / oxygen, while the first rescuer maintains the mask seal)","Suction the airway","Insert an OPA"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states it can be used without being attached to an oxygen tank to provide room air (21% oxygen) to the patient, while connecting an oxygen source the second rescuer squeezes the bag.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the described division of roles for the E-C two-hand technique."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"A PEEP valve can be attached to the BVM and titrated from what range?","options":["1 to 5 cm H2O","5 to 15 cm H2O","20 to 30 cm H2O","0 to 2 cm H2O"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states a PEEP valve can be attached and titrated from 5 to 15 cm H2O to improve oxygenation.  Why A/C/D wrong: These ranges do not match the stated 5–15 cm H2O."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"During BVM ventilation, to prevent gastric distention, peak inspiratory pressure (PIP) should NOT exceed:","options":["10 cm H2O","20 cm H2O","40 cm H2O","60 cm H2O"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states to monitor for oxygenation, do not exceed a PEEP/PIP of 20 cm H2O, as this pressure can open the lower esophageal sphincter and cause gastric insufflation and vomiting.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated 20 cm H2O upper limit."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"Exceeding the recommended airway pressure during BVM ventilation can open the lower esophageal sphincter, leading to:","options":["Pneumothorax","Gastric insufflation and vomiting","Bronchospasm","Hypotension only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states excessive pressure opens the lower esophageal sphincter, causing gastric insufflation and vomiting.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the stated consequence of opening the lower esophageal sphincter."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"Why is using low pressure / low volume during BVM ventilation important?","options":["It increases tidal volume","Low volume insufflation can help prevent gastric distention","It reduces oxygen concentration","It improves mask seal"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states low pressure, low volume insufflation can help prevent gastric distention.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the stated benefit of low-volume technique."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"A filter can be attached to the BVM for which purpose?","options":["To warm inspired gas","For pathogens and connector for nebulisation","To measure tidal volume","To deliver anesthesia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states a filter for pathogens and connector for nebulisation can be attached to the BVM.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the stated function of the BVM filter."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"(RECALL THE NORCET) The maximum FiO2 which can be given by a resuscitation bag (BVM) is:","options":["60","90","100","80"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The keyed right answer is C; the maximum FiO2 given by a resuscitation bag is 100%.  Why A/B/D wrong: These are below the stated maximum of 100%."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"During step-by-step BVM ventilation, when the patient is unconscious or one-to-two nasopharyngeal airways prior to BVM ventilation, the first step is to:","options":["Insert an OPA or NPA","Begin compressions","Apply ECG leads","Give medications"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text's step-by-step states to insert an OPA (if the patient is unconscious) or one-to-two nasopharyngeal airways prior to BVM ventilation.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the first BVM ventilation preparation step described."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"During BVM ventilation preparation, the rescuer should select a mask that fits which structures?","options":["Over the eyes","Over the mouth and nose but spares the eyes","Over the chin only","Over the entire face including eyes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states to select a mask that fits over the mouth and nose but spares the eyes.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the proper mask fit (eyes must be spared)."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"When performing BVM ventilation, where should the operator stand relative to the patient?","options":["To the side of the stretcher","At the foot of the bed","At the head of the stretcher and have the second operator stand to the side","Beside the chest"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The text states to stand at the head of the stretcher and have the second operator stand to the side.  Why A/B/D wrong: These positions are not the described operator positioning for BVM."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"During BVM ventilation, which maneuver positions the patient — using the head-tilt-chin-lift, aligning the external auditory canal (ear) with the sternal notch?","options":["Jaw thrust maneuver","Sniffing position","Heimlich maneuver","Recovery position"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states sniffing position: use head-tilt-chin-lift maneuver, aligning the external auditory canal (ear) with the sternal notch.  Why A wrong: Jaw thrust is the alternative used when cervical spine injury is suspected.  Why C/D wrong: These are not airway-positioning maneuvers in this context."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"Which airway maneuver should be used instead of the sniffing position if cervical spine injury is suspected?","options":["Head-tilt-chin-lift","Sniffing/neutral","Jaw-thrust maneuver","Trendelenburg position"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The text states supine or neutral: if cervical spine injury is suspected, use jaw-thrust maneuver.  Why A/B wrong: Head-tilt-chin-lift/sniffing extends the neck and is contraindicated in suspected C-spine injury.  Why D wrong: Trendelenburg is not an airway-opening maneuver."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"When holding the mask using the E-C grip, the thumbs and index fingers form the \"E\" while the middle, ring, and little fingers form the:","options":["\"C\" placed under the mandible","\"E\" around the mask","\"D\" over the nose","\"O\" over the chin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states make \"E-C\" grip: place the middle, ring, and little fingers (the \"E\") under the mandible and index fingers form a \"C\" and then press down against the mask.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the described E-C grip hand configuration."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"During mask placement, the rescuer should pull up on the bony parts of the mandible/neck because:","options":["It increases tidal volume","The soft tissues of the neck or under the chin may obstruct ventilation","It improves cardiac output","It prevents epistaxis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states be sure to pull up on the bony parts of the mandible because pressure to the soft tissues of the neck or under the chin may obstruct ventilation.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the stated reason for pulling up on bony parts."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"When ventilating an apneic patient with adequate respiratory rate, once a proper seal is established, the bag should be squeezed for how long per breath in an ADULT?","options":["0.5 second","1 second over one second, then release the bag to allow exhalation","3 seconds","5 seconds"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states for each breath, steadily and smoothly squeeze the bag to deliver a tidal volume of 6 to 7 mL/kg (or about 500 mL for an average size adult) over one second, and then release the bag to allow it to exhale.  Why A/C/D wrong: These timings do not match the stated 1-second delivery for adults."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"During BVM delivery of an average adult breath, the goal tidal volume is approximately:","options":["1–2 mL/kg","6 to 7 mL/kg (about 500 mL for an average-size adult)","15 mL/kg","20 mL/kg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text specifies delivering a tidal volume of 6 to 7 mL/kg, about 500 mL for an average-size adult.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated 6–7 mL/kg target."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"In an adult with a pulse who is not breathing adequately, how often should you squeeze the bag?","options":["Once every second","Once every 5 to 6 seconds (10–12 respirations per minute)","Once every 30 seconds","Once every 10 seconds"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states squeezing the bag once every 5 to 6 seconds for an adult provides an adequate respiratory rate (10–12 respirations per minute in an adult).  Why A/C/D wrong: These rates do not match the stated adult ventilation rate."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"In a child or infant, the rescuer should squeeze the bag once every how long?","options":["Every 5–6 seconds","Every 20–30 minutes","Every 3 seconds (20–30 respirations per minute in a child or infant)","Every 10 seconds"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The text states squeeze the bag once every 3 seconds providing 20–30 ventilations per minute (respirations per minute) in a child or infant.  Why A wrong: 5–6 seconds is the adult rate.  Why B/D wrong: These do not match the pediatric rate."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"The efficacy of BVM ventilation can be assessed using the LLPP mnemonic. Which are the components of LLPP? (SATA)","options":["Look — watch the chest rise and fall","Listen — for bilateral air entry","Feel — feeling the easy compliance of the bag","Pulse oximetry — improved oxygenation","Pressure — high airway pressure"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The LLPP list is: Look (chest rise and fall), Listen (for bilateral air entry), Feel (feeling the easy compliance of the bag), and Pulse oximetry (improved oxygenation).  Why E wrong: High airway pressure is not part of the LLPP efficacy mnemonic."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"Maximum airway pressure in a resuscitation bag during BVM ventilation does NOT exceed:","options":["20 cm H2O","30 cm H2O","45 cm H2O","60 cm H2O"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The text states maximum airway pressure in resuscitation bag does not exceed 45 cm H2O.  Why A/B/D wrong: These do not match the stated 45 cm H2O maximum."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"During BVM ventilation, observing for proper chest rise should be checked during ventilations via:","options":["Arterial blood gas","EtCO2 (end-tidal CO2 capnography)","Chest X-ray","Auscultation only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states observe for proper chest rise during ventilations (EtCO2).  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the stated method (EtCO2) referenced for monitoring chest rise during ventilation."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"The nurse should monitor which parameters during BVM ventilation if possible? (SATA)","options":["Airway, breath sounds, EtCO2","Pulse oximeter","Note: pulse oximetry may not be useful during cardiac arrest due to poor peripheral perfusion","Waveform capnography (an excellent indicator of mask seal and proper ventilation)","Skin turgor only"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The text instructs to monitor the airway, checking breath sounds, EtCO2 and pulse oximeter; notes pulse oximetry may not be useful during cardiac arrest due to poor peripheral perfusion; and that waveform capnography is an excellent indicator of mask seal and proper ventilation.  Why E wrong: Skin turgor is not a listed BVM monitoring parameter."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"If ventilation is still not adequate during BVM despite repositioning, what should the nurse prepare for?","options":["Discharge the patient","Other airway maneuvers such as a supraglottic airway or endotracheal intubation","Stop all oxygen","Administer diuretics"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states if ventilation is still not adequate, prepare for other airway maneuvers such as a supraglottic airway or endotracheal intubation.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the stated escalation step."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"The MOANS mnemonic predicts difficult BVM ventilation. What does \"M\" in MOANS stand for?","options":["Mouth opening","Mask seal — may be compromised by abnormal facial trauma, facial hair, and body fluids","Mandible","Malampatti"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text defines M = Mask seal, which may be compromised by abnormal facial trauma, facial hair, and body fluids.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the MOANS \"M\" element described."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"In the MOANS predictors of difficult BVM, \"O\" stands for:","options":["Oxygenation","Obesity or obstruction","Oral airway","Older age"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text defines O = Obesity or obstruction.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the MOANS \"O\" element."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"In the MOANS mnemonic, \"A\" stands for:","options":["Airway","Age — ≥55 years, where young children and the elderly can be difficult to ventilate","Aspiration","Anesthesia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text defines A = Age ≥55 years; young children and elderly can be difficult to ventilate.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the MOANS \"A\" element."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"In MOANS, \"N\" stands for No teeth, and \"S\" stands for Stiff lungs. Match these correctly: (SATA)","options":["N = No teeth (teeth keep face from caving in during BVM)","S = Soft lungs or snoring; lung resistance issues (edema, COPD)","N = Neck mass","S = Stridor only","N = Nausea"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The text defines N = No teeth (teeth keep the face from caving in during BVM) and S = Stiff/soft lungs or snoring, lung resistance issues (edema, COPD).  Why C/D/E wrong: These do not match the MOANS definitions."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"Which are listed complications of BVM ventilation? (SATA)","options":["Air to inflate the stomach, called gastric insufflation","Lung injury from over-stretching, called volutrauma","Lung injury from over-pressurization, called barotrauma","Corneal abrasions, facial nerve injury (other complications)","Hypothermia"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The text lists they are related to over-inflating or over-pressurizing the patient: air to inflate the stomach (gastric insufflation), lung injury from over-stretching (volutrauma), lung injury from over-pressurization (barotrauma), and other complications such as corneal abrasions and facial nerve injury.  Why E wrong: Hypothermia is not listed as a BVM complication."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"Among the parameters for adequacy of ventilation during BVM ventilation, which are listed? (SATA)","options":["Observe the chest rise and fall freely","Good bilateral air entry by auscultation of the chest","Lack of air entering the stomach","Feeling the consistency of the bag as it empties","Monitoring the end-tidal CO2 and observing a good square wave pattern of the capnograph"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: All five are listed parameters for adequacy of ventilation during BVM: chest rise/fall freely, good bilateral air entry, lack of air entering the stomach, feeling consistency of the bag emptying, and monitoring end-tidal CO2 with a good square-wave capnograph pattern; well-maintained oxygen saturation observed via pulse oximeter.  Why none wrong: All are explicitly listed parameters."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"Per the \"Must Remember\" box, when connecting the bag valve mask (BVM) to an Endotracheal Tube (ETT), it is important to first ensure that the oxygen tubing and set are connected to the proper flow rate, and that the BVM:","options":["Has a closed pop-off valve","Is connected directly to the proper flow rate before connection","Has no oxygen attached","Is sterile"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states when connecting the BVM to an ETT, ensure that the oxygen tubing/set and proper flow are connected before connecting.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated reminder."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"Per the \"Must Remember\" box, when connecting the BVM to an ETT, why is it important to ensure the connection is secure?","options":["To save oxygen","Once this is done, you should attach the BVM to the ETT (1.5 cm), then the oxygen tubing must be connected securely at both ends so there will be no air kinks/leaks in the tubing","To reduce noise","To prevent sterilization"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the oxygen tubing should be connected securely at both ends so there will be no air kinks in the tubing.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the stated rationale for a secure connection."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"The Rapid Airway Assessment Test uses a finger-breadth method (1-2-3 finger test). What does the 1-finger test assess?","options":["Mouth opening","Subluxation of the mandible (1 finger breadth)","Thyromental distance","Hyoid-mental distance"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states 1-finger test = 1 finger breadth for subluxation of mandible.  Why A/C/D wrong: These correspond to other finger-test measures."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"In the 1-2-3 finger rapid airway assessment, the 2-finger test assesses:","options":["Subluxation of mandible","2 fingers breadth for adequacy of mouth opening","Hyomental distance","Neck circumference"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states 2-finger test = 2 fingers breadth for adequacy of mouth opening.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are different finger-test parameters."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"In the 1-2-3 finger rapid airway assessment, the 3-finger test assesses:","options":["Mouth opening","3 fingers breadth for hyomental distance","Subluxation of the mandible","Cricothyroid membrane"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states 3-finger test = 3 fingers breadth for hyomental distance.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the 3-finger-test parameter."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"\"Difficult airway\" is conventionally defined as a situation in which a trained anesthesiologist experiences difficulty with:","options":["IV access only","Facemask ventilation and/or tracheal intubation (or both)","Suctioning","Drug administration"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text defines difficult airway as a clinical situation in which a conventionally trained anesthesiologist experiences difficulty with facemask ventilation of the upper airway, difficulty with tracheal intubation, or both.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not part of the difficult-airway definition."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"Difficult intubation is defined as requiring how many attempts at tracheal intubation?","options":["1 attempt","More than three (3) attempts (defined as more than 10 minutes using direct laryngoscopy or complete tracheal intubation)","2 attempts","5 attempts"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states difficult intubation is defined as more than three attempts at intubation (or more than 10 minutes using direct laryngoscopy for complete tracheal intubation).  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated >3 attempts / >10 minutes definition."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"Difficult ventilation is defined as the inability of a trained anesthesiologist to maintain oxygen saturation >90% using a facemask, where 100% inspired oxygen is given, provided that the:","options":["Patient is awake","Pre-ventilation oxygen saturation level was within the normal range","Patient is intubated","Oxygen tank is full"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states difficult ventilation is the inability of a trained anesthesiologist to maintain oxygen saturation >90% using a facemask with 100% inspired oxygen, provided the pre-ventilation oxygen saturation level was within the normal range.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not part of the difficult-ventilation definition."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"Per the facemask size table for BVM, a newborn (0-6 months) typically uses which mask size?","options":["Neonatal 0, 00","Toddler 4","Adult medium","Pediatric 3"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The facemask size table lists Newborn to 1 year = Neonatal 0, 00.  Why B/C/D wrong: These mask sizes correspond to other age groups in the table."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"Per the size of resuscitation bag table, a child up to 1 year requires which bag size?","options":["Neonatal 280-450 ml","Adult 1000-1500 ml","Child 100-200 ml","Pediatric 50 ml"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The size of resuscitation bag table lists Newborn to 1 month / child up to ~1 year = Neonatal 280-450 ml range.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the neonatal/infant resuscitation bag size listed."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"Per the size of resuscitation bag table, an adult requires which resuscitation bag volume?","options":["280-450 ml","500-600 ml","Adult 1000-1500 ml","100-200 ml"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists Adult resuscitation bag size = 1000-1500 ml.  Why A/B/D wrong: These do not match the adult bag size in the table."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"The first task in the Rapid Sequence Induction & Intubation (RSI) sequence is Preparation. How long before intubation is the Preparation phase performed?","options":["5 minutes before intubation","10 minutes before intubation","3 minutes before intubation","At intubation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The RSI table lists Preparation = 10 minutes before intubation.  Why A/C/D wrong: These times correspond to other RSI steps."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"In the RSI sequence, \"Pre-oxygenation\" is performed how long before intubation?","options":["10 minutes before","5 minutes before intubation","At intubation","60 seconds after"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The RSI table lists Pre-oxygenation = 5 minutes before intubation.  Why A/C/D wrong: These correspond to other RSI phases."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"In the RSI sequence, \"Pre-treatment\" is performed how long before intubation?","options":["3 minutes before intubation","10 minutes before","At intubation","45 seconds after"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The RSI table lists Pre-treatment = 3 minutes before intubation.  Why B/C/D wrong: These correspond to other RSI phases."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"In the RSI sequence, \"Paralysis with induction\" occurs at what point?","options":["10 minutes before","At induction (zero hour / at intubation)","5 minutes before","60 seconds after"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The RSI table lists Paralysis with induction = induction (at the time of intubation).  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the induction timing."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"In the RSI sequence, \"Placement (intubation)\" occurs at approximately:","options":["10 minutes before","45 seconds after induction","60 seconds after","5 minutes before"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The RSI table lists Placement (intubation) = 45 seconds after induction.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the placement timing."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"In the RSI sequence, \"Post-intubation management\" occurs at:","options":["45 seconds after","60 seconds after induction","10 minutes before","At induction"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The RSI table lists Post-intubation management = 60 seconds after induction.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the post-intubation management timing."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"The \"Four D's\" that suggest a difficult airway include which of the following? (SATA)","options":["Dentition (prominent upper incisors, receding chin)","Distortion (edema, blood, vomitus, tumor, infection)","Disproportion (short chin-to-larynx distance, bull neck, large tongue)","Dysmobility (TMJ and cervical spine immobility)","Diuresis"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The four D's that suggest a difficult airway are Dentition, Distortion, Disproportion, and Dysmobility (each defined in the text).  Why E wrong: Diuresis is not one of the four D's."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"Which of the following are listed components of difficult airway management? (SATA)","options":["Difficult face-mask ventilation","Difficult laryngoscopy and intubation","Difficult extraglottic device (EGD)","Difficult cricothyrodotomy","Difficult IV cannulation"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The four domains of difficult airway management are difficult face-mask ventilation, difficult laryngoscopy and intubation, difficult extraglottic device (EGD), and difficult cricothyrodotomy.  Why E wrong: Difficult IV cannulation is not one of the four listed components."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"In the sniffing position, the head is placed in the Magill's position or boyce position. The neck is in approximately what degree of flexion?","options":["5°-10°","Flexion of the neck (25°-35°) and well extension of the head on the atlanto-occipital joint","90°","60°-75°"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states it is a combination of flexion of the neck (25°-35°) and well extension of the head on the atlanto-occipital joint (85°), considered suitable for the performance of endotracheal intubation.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated 25°-35° neck flexion."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"In the sniffing position, the head is extended on the atlanto-occipital joint at approximately what degree?","options":["25°-35°","85°","45°","90°"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text specifies well extension of the head on the atlanto-occipital joint at 85°.  Why A wrong: 25°-35° is the neck flexion, not the head extension.  Why C/D wrong: These do not match the stated 85° head extension."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"The Intubation Difficulty Scale (IDS) uses parameters known to be associated with difficult intubation. According to the scoring, a score of 0 indicates:","options":["Easy intubation","Mild to major difficulty","Impossible intubation","Failure of tracheal intubation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states Score = 0 means easy intubation.  Why B/C/D wrong: These correspond to higher IDS scores."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"On the Intubation Difficulty Scale (IDS), a score >5 indicates:","options":["Easy intubation","Mild to major difficulty","Impossible intubation or failure of tracheal intubation","No difficulty"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The text states Score >5 means impossible intubation or failure of tracheal intubation.  Why A/B/D wrong: These do not match the IDS >5 interpretation."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"Which are listed as four physiological predictors of difficult airways? (SATA)","options":["Hypoxemia","Hypotension","Severe metabolic acidosis","Right ventricular failure","Hyperkalemia"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The four physiological predictors of difficult airways listed are hypoxemia, hypotension, severe metabolic acidosis, and right ventricular failure.  Why E wrong: Hyperkalemia is not listed among the four physiological predictors."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"In the Mallampati Airway Classification, Class I corresponds to:","options":["Complete visualization of the soft palate (full visualization)","Only the hard palate visible","Visualization of only the base of the uvula","Soft palate is not visible at all"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text/figure shows Class I = complete visualization of the soft palate.  Why B/C/D wrong: These correspond to Class III/IV (lesser visualization)."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"In the Mallampati Airway Classification, which class corresponds to \"soft palate is not visible / full soft palate not visible\" (visualization of only the base of uvula)?","options":["Class I","Class II","Class III (visualization of only the base of the uvula)","Class IV (soft palate is not visible at all)"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Per the figures, Class IV = soft palate is not visible at all; Class III shows only the base of the uvula. The \"soft palate not visible\" descriptor maps to Class IV.  Why A/B wrong: Class I/II show more of the soft palate.  Why C wrong: Class III shows the base of the uvula, which is more than Class IV."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"Which Mallampati class is associated with easy/difficult intubation, per the source note?","options":["Class I is associated with an easy intubation; Class III/IV is associated with difficult intubation","Class I is associated with difficult intubation","All classes are equally easy","Class IV is the easiest"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states it is used to identify patients at risk for difficult tracheal intubation; classification provides a score of 1-4 based on the anatomic features seen; Class I/II is associated with an easy intubation whereas Class III/IV is associated with difficult intubation.  Why B/C/D wrong: These contradict the stated correlation (higher class = more difficult)."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"The Cormack-Lehane score grades the glottic view. Grade I corresponds to:","options":["Full exposure of the glottis (anterior and posterior commissures seen)","Only posterior view of the glottis seen, anterior not seen","Only epiglottis seen","No glottis seen"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states Grade I = full exposure of the glottis (anterior and posterior commissures seen), no extrinsic manipulation of the larynx required.  Why B/C/D wrong: These correspond to higher Cormack-Lehane grades."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"In the Cormack-Lehane glottic view grading, Grade II corresponds to:","options":["Full exposure of the glottis","Only posterior view of the glottis seen; anterior not seen (external manipulation of the larynx is necessary to intubate)","Only the epiglottis is seen","No glottis is seen"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states Grade II = only posterior view of glottis seen, anterior not seen, with external manipulation of the larynx necessary to intubate.  Why A/C/D wrong: These correspond to Grades I/III/IV."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"In the Cormack-Lehane grading, Grade III corresponds to:","options":["Full exposure of glottis","Only posterior glottis seen","Only the epiglottis is seen (intubation possible only when aided by a styler/stylet)","No glottis is seen; failed intubation"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The text states Grade III = only epiglottis seen, intubation possible only when aided by a stylet.  Why A/B wrong: These correspond to Grades I/II.  Why D wrong: No glottis seen / failed intubation is Grade IV."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"In the Cormack-Lehane grading, Grade IV corresponds to:","options":["Full exposure of glottis","Only posterior glottis seen","Only epiglottis seen","No glottis seen (failed intubation)"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The text states Grade IV = no glottis seen, failed intubation.  Why A/B/C wrong: These correspond to Grades I/II/III."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"The LEMON law is an evaluation criteria for difficult airway. Under \"Look externally,\" which features each score points? (SATA)","options":["Facial trauma","Large incisors","Beard or mustache","Large tongue","Normal facial features"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The LEMON \"Look externally\" criteria each score 1 point: facial trauma, large incisors, beard or mustache, and large tongue.  Why E wrong: Normal facial features do not score points (they are not a difficulty predictor)."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"In the LEMON law, \"Evaluate the 3-3-2 rule\" assigns points for which findings? (SATA)","options":["Incisor distance 3 fingerbreadths","Hyoid-mental distance 3 fingerbreadths","Thyroid-to-mouth distance 2 fingerbreadths","Mallampati score >3 (obstruction)","Neck mobility limitation"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The LEMON evaluation criteria include the 3-3-2 rule (incisor distance 3 fingerbreadths, hyoid-mental distance 3 fingerbreadths, thyroid-to-mouth distance 2 fingerbreadths), Mallampati score >3, Obstruction (presence of any condition e.g., epiglottitis, peritonsillar abscess, trauma), and Neck mobility — each contributing points to the total.  Why none wrong: All are listed scoring criteria of the LEMON law."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"Per the LEMON law, the \"Obstruction\" criterion scores for the presence of which conditions? (SATA)","options":["Epiglottitis","Peritonsillar abscess","Trauma","Hypertension","Tachycardia"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The text lists Obstruction = presence of any condition e.g., epiglottitis, peritonsillar abscess, trauma.  Why D/E wrong: Hypertension and tachycardia are not listed obstruction conditions."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"The 3-3-2 rule for airway assessment includes which measurements? (SATA)","options":["≥3 fingers mouth opening (≥3 fingers)","Tip of the chin to the hyoid bone ≥3 fingers","Tip of the thyroid notch to the floor of the mouth (thyroid cartilage) ≥2 fingers","Neck circumference ≥3 fingers","Tongue width ≥2 fingers"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The 3-3-2 rule comprises: (a) mouth opening ≥3 fingers, (b) tip of the chin to the hyoid bone ≥3 fingers, and (c) tip of the thyroid notch to the floor of the mouth (thyroid cartilage) ≥2 fingers.  Why D/E wrong: Neck circumference and tongue width are not part of the 3-3-2 rule."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"A laryngoscope is an instrument used to:","options":["Suction secretions","Visualize the larynx and depress the tongue during ET intubation","Measure tidal volume","Deliver nebulized medication"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text defines a laryngoscope as an instrument used to visualize the larynx and depress the tongue during ET intubation.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the laryngoscope's stated purpose."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"The classic laryngoscope is available with a variety of blade attachments with or without a fiber-optic light source. What is its stated purpose?","options":["Suction","Intubation","Defibrillation","Oxygenation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the laryngoscope's purpose is intubation.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the stated purpose."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"A laryngoscope includes which two main parts?","options":["Handle and blade","Cuff and balloon","Tube and connector","Valve and reservoir"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states laryngoscope parts include handle and blade.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the laryngoscope's parts described."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"Which type of laryngoscope blade is curve-shaped and usually used in adults?","options":["Miller blade","Macintosh blade","Magill blade","Wisconsin blade"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states Macintosh blade is curve-shaped and is usually used in adults.  Why A wrong: The Miller blade is straight-shaped and used in children.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the two blade types described."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"Which type of laryngoscope blade is straight-shaped and usually used in children?","options":["Macintosh blade","Miller blade","Magill blade","Curved blade"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states Miller blade is straight-shaped and is usually used in children.  Why A wrong: Macintosh is curved and used in adults.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the described blade types."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"Laryngoscope blade sizes range from 0 to 4. Which size is used for a newborn?","options":["Size 0: for newborn","Size 1: for infant","Size 2: for child","Size 3 & 4: for adults"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states Size 0 = for newborn.  Why B/C/D wrong: Size 1 = infant, Size 2 = child, Size 3 & 4 = adults."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"Match the laryngoscope blade sizes to age group. Which are correct? (SATA)","options":["Size 1: for infant","Size 2: for child","Size 3 & 4: for adults","Size 0: for newborn","Size 5: for elderly"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The text lists Size 0 = newborn, Size 1 = infant, Size 2 = child, Size 3 & 4 = adults.  Why E wrong: There is no Size 5 listed; sizes range 0 to 4."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"During the laryngoscope procedure, the laryngoscope handle should be held in which hand?","options":["Dominant hand","Non-dominant hand (usually left)","Either hand","Both hands"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states hold the laryngoscope handle in the non-dominant hand (usually the left).  Why A wrong: The dominant hand inserts/handles the ET tube.  Why C/D wrong: These contradict the described non-dominant-hand hold."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"During laryngoscopy, the blade is inserted from which corner of the mouth?","options":["Center of mouth","One corner of mouth (usually right), pushing the tongue completely to one side","From below the chin","Left corner only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states insert the blade from one corner of mouth (usually right) and push the tongue completely to one side.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the described blade insertion."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"During laryngoscopy, the laryngoscope should be lifted at approximately what angle, and the lift force should come from which muscle?","options":["Lift at 45° away from the patient, using elbow or triceps muscle for lifting","Lift at 90° using wrist rotation","Lift straight up using wrist","Lift at 10° using fingers"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states lift hand 45° away from the patient and use deltoid or triceps muscle for lifting (avoid wrist rotation/leverage).  Why B/C/D wrong: These contradict the described 45° lift and triceps technique."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"During the laryngoscopy procedure, the ET tube is inserted using which hand after visualizing the vocal cords?","options":["Non-dominant hand","Dominant hand","Either hand","Both hands"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states visualize the vocal cords and insert the ET tube with the dominant hand.  Why A wrong: The non-dominant hand holds the laryngoscope.  Why C/D wrong: These contradict the described technique."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"The sniffing position (sniffing the morning air position) is described as the first priority during intubation because it:","options":["Reduces oxygen needs","Involves flexion of the cervical spine and extension of the atlanto-occipital joint direction; it is also known as the Flexo-tension position","Prevents aspiration completely","Eliminates the need for a laryngoscope"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the sniffing position is used as a first priority during intubation; in which flexion of cervical spine and extension of atlanto-occipital joint direction is used; it is also known as the Flexo-tension position.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the stated descriptions of the sniffing position."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"The ramping position is used in which patients, and is achieved how?","options":["Used in obese & pregnant clients; achieved by arranging blankets/towels under the patient's upper body and head to obtain horizontal alignment between the external auditory meatus and sternal notch","Used only in pediatric clients with a pillow under the shoulders","Used in trauma clients with inline stabilisation","Used in supine clients with the head down"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the ramping position is used in obese & pregnant clients during intubation; achieved by arranging blankets under the patient's upper body and head to obtain a horizontal alignment between the external auditory meatus and sternal notch.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the described ramping position."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"The Rose position is used as a second priority during intubation and is achieved by:","options":["Placing the patient in supine position with a pillow or rolled blanket/sandbag under the client's shoulder and a rubber ring placed under the head to stabilise it","Sitting the patient upright","Turning the patient prone","Placing the patient in Trendelenburg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the Rose position is a second priority during intubation; the patient is placed in supine position and a pillow or rolled blanket/sandbag is placed under the client's shoulder, and a rubber ring is placed under the head to stabilise it; used during tonsillectomy, adenoidectomy, thyroidectomy & parathyroidectomy.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the described Rose position."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"The Rose position is used during which surgical procedures? (SATA)","options":["Tonsillectomy","Adenoidectomy","Thyroidectomy","Parathyroidectomy","Appendectomy"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the Rose position is used during tonsillectomy, adenoidectomy, thyroidectomy & parathyroidectomy.  Why E wrong: Appendectomy is not listed for the Rose position."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"Manual inline stabilisation during intubation is used in patients with suspected cervical spine injury. What is the assistant's role?","options":["Squeeze the bag","Stand at the head/side of the bed and use their hands to stabilize the patient's occiput and mastoid processes, gently countering the forces of airway intervention","Administer paralytics","Hold the laryngoscope"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states if the patient has suspected cervical spine injury needs to be intubated, this maneuver is achieved with the assistant standing at the head or side of the bed using their hands to stabilize the patient's occiput and mastoid processes, gently countering the forces of airway intervention to keep the cervical spine in line.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the described role of manual inline stabilisation."},{"section":"Batch 016 · Nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal airway contraindications & complications…","question":"Per the laryngoscopy procedure, before lifting and inserting the tube, the rescuer should first:","options":["Visualize the epiglottis","Administer paralytics","Inflate the cuff","Apply cricoid pressure only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The procedure lists pushing the tongue completely to one side, then visualize the epiglottis (before lifting at 45° and visualizing the vocal cords to insert the ET tube).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated next step in the laryngoscopy sequence at this point."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"After a procedure of intubation, how frequently should the nurse check vital signs, especially oxygen saturation?","options":["Every 5 minutes","Every 15 minutes","Every 30 minutes","Every hour"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After intubation the nurse should check vital signs, especially O2 saturation, initially every 15 minutes and after stabilization of the client every 30 minutes.  Why A wrong: 5 minutes is not the stated initial interval.  Why C wrong: Every 30 minutes is used only after the client is stabilized, not initially.  Why D wrong: Hourly checks are too infrequent for an immediately post-intubation client."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"Once the client is stabilized after intubation, how often should the nurse continue to monitor vital signs?","options":["Every 5 minutes","Every 15 minutes","Every 30 minutes","Every 2 hours"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: After stabilization of the client, monitoring frequency is reduced to every 30 minutes.  Why A/B wrong: These are not the post-stabilization interval; 15 minutes is the initial interval.  Why D wrong: 2 hours is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"Which statement correctly defines endotracheal intubation?","options":["Removal of the tube from the trachea","Insertion of a tube into the trachea","Suctioning of airway secretions","Inflation of the ET cuff"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Endotracheal intubation is defined as insertion of a tube into the trachea.  Why A wrong: Removal of the tube from the trachea is endotracheal extubation, not intubation.  Why C wrong: Suctioning is a separate airway clearance procedure.  Why D wrong: Cuff inflation is one step in tube management, not the definition of intubation."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"What is the term for removal of the tube from the trachea?","options":["Endotracheal intubation","Endotracheal extubation","Tracheostomy","Bronchoscopy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Endotracheal extubation is removal of the tube from the trachea.  Why A wrong: Intubation is insertion, the opposite action.  Why C wrong: Tracheostomy is a surgical airway, not removal of an ET tube.  Why D wrong: Bronchoscopy is a visualization procedure."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"When the airway tube needs to be used for more than 2 weeks to prevent infection, what is recommended?","options":["The tube can remain unchanged indefinitely","The tube should be removed and the secretion suctioned","The tube should be changed every 6 hours","The cuff should be deflated permanently"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If a child age 4 years then use non-cuffed ET tube because in these kids cricoid cartilage naturally seal the ring or tube; and if minute volume is too low, then remove the secretion by suction — the source notes that if the tube needs longer use, remove the tube and suction the secretion.  Why A wrong: Leaving the tube indefinitely promotes infection.  Why C wrong: Routine 6-hourly tube changes are not indicated.  Why D wrong: Permanent cuff deflation is not the stated intervention."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"In a child aged less than 4 years, which type of endotracheal tube is preferred and why?","options":["Cuffed tube, because the trachea is wide","Non-cuffed tube, because the cricoid cartilage naturally seals the ring or tube","Cuffed tube, because secretions are minimal","Double-lumen tube, because of lung isolation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In children less than 4 years a non-cuffed ET tube is used because in these kids the cricoid cartilage naturally seals the ring or tube.  Why A wrong: A cuffed tube is not preferred in this age group.  Why C wrong: Secretion volume is not the stated reason.  Why D wrong: Double-lumen tubes are for lung isolation, not routine pediatric airway."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"When the cuff of the ET tube is removed and the client is complaining of sore throat, hoarseness, and abnormal findings, what should the nurse do?","options":["Reinsert the tube immediately","Consider it a normal finding and instruct the client; if these findings persist after 4–6 hours notify the doctor","Begin chest compressions","Increase oxygen to 100%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After removal of the tube, if the client complains of sore throat, hoarseness, it considers as a normal finding and instruct to client (limit the talking); but if these findings persist after 4–6 hours, these findings are abnormal and notify the doctor.  Why A wrong: Routine immediate reinsertion is not indicated for expected post-extubation symptoms.  Why C wrong: CPR is not indicated for sore throat/hoarseness.  Why D wrong: 100% oxygen is not the stated response to these complaints."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"If the secretion accumulates in the tube and cannot be removed by suction, the nurse should:","options":["Leave the tube in place and document","Suction the tube immediately and, if not resolved, remove the tube and ventilate manually via AMBU or inform the doctor immediately","Increase the cuff pressure","Deflate the cuff only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If minute volume/secretion in the tube cannot be removed by suction: suction the tube immediately, and if not resolved then remove the tube and ventilate manually via AMBU or inform the doctor immediately.  Why A wrong: Leaving an obstructed tube risks hypoxia.  Why C wrong: Increasing cuff pressure does not clear obstruction.  Why D wrong: Deflating the cuff alone does not address secretion blockage."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"What is the primary purpose served by the airway/management routine described for tube checks?","options":["Continuously confirm tube position by chest X-ray","Periodically check tube position and patency, and suction as needed","Replace the tube every shift","Maintain the cuff fully deflated"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text directs periodic checking of tube position/patency and suctioning of secretions as needed to maintain airway.  Why A wrong: Continuous X-ray is impractical and not stated.  Why C wrong: Routine per-shift replacement is not indicated.  Why D wrong: The cuff is generally inflated, not kept deflated."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"During endotracheal intubation, a tube is introduced into the trachea through which route in the most common methods?","options":["The nose or the mouth","The chest wall","The cricothyroid membrane only","A surgical neck stoma only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Endotracheal intubation is introduction of a tube into the trachea through nose or mouth to establish a patent airway; the two main methods are naso-tracheal and oro-tracheal.  Why B wrong: The chest wall is not an intubation route.  Why C wrong: The cricothyroid membrane is used for cricothyroidotomy, a separate emergency airway.  Why D wrong: A neck stoma describes tracheostomy, not ET intubation."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"Endotracheal intubation is most often a method of which type of airway management?","options":["Surgical airway management","Airway management","Cuff pressure management","Mechanical chest management"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states ET intubation is the most common method of airway management.  Why A wrong: It is non-surgical; tracheostomy is surgical.  Why C wrong: Cuff management is a subtask, not the overall category.  Why D wrong: Not a recognized category."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"Approximately for how long can an endotracheal tube be used for short period (before considering tracheostomy)?","options":["1–3 days","5–7 days","10–14 days","3–4 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: An ET tube is used for short period, usually 10–14 days.  Why A/B wrong: Shorter than the stated short-period range.  Why D wrong: 3–4 weeks exceeds the stated short-term period (longer needs would prompt tracheostomy)."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"Which two methods of endotracheal intubation are described?","options":["Naso-tracheal and oro-tracheal","Surgical and percutaneous","Transcutaneous and intraosseous","Cricothyroid and supraglottic"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: There are two methods of intubation: naso-tracheal tube and oro-tracheal tube.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the two methods named in the source."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"Which statement correctly describes the naso-tracheal tube method?","options":["A tube inserted into the trachea through the oral cavity","A tube inserted in the trachea through the nostril; it is uncomfortable and used in small diameter","A tube inserted through a neck stoma","A tube inserted through the cricothyroid membrane"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Naso-tracheal tube is inserted in the trachea through the nostril; it is uncomfortable and used in small diameter.  Why A wrong: That describes the oro-tracheal route.  Why C wrong: Neck stoma describes tracheostomy.  Why D wrong: Cricothyroid membrane is for cricothyroidotomy."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"Which statement correctly describes the oro-tracheal tube method?","options":["Inserted through the nostril and uncomfortable","Inserted in the trachea through the oral cavity; comfortable and larger diameter, but has decreased breathing efforts","Inserted through a neck stoma","Used only in neonates"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Oro-tracheal tube is inserted in the trachea through the oral cavity; it is comfortable, allows larger diameter, but has decreased breathing efforts.  Why A wrong: That describes the naso-tracheal route.  Why C wrong: Neck stoma is tracheostomy.  Why D wrong: Not restricted to neonates."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"Which of the following is a purpose of endotracheal intubation? (SATA)","options":["To establish and maintain a patent airway","To oxygen administration","To remove the excessive secretion from the airway","To support oxygenation and give anaesthesia","To perform chest physiotherapy on the lung surface"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The purposes listed are to establish and maintain a patent airway, to oxygen administration, to remove the excessive secretion from the airway, to support oxygenation, and to give anaesthesia.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All directly listed as purposes.  Why E wrong: Chest physiotherapy on the lung surface is not a listed purpose of intubation."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"Which of the following are indications for endotracheal intubation? (SATA)","options":["The main indication is to secure a definitive airway","A definitive airway is the placement of an ET in the trachea with an inflated cuff below the vocal cords","To maintain airway patency","To protect the airway against aspiration","To deliver oral nutrition"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Indications: the main indication is to secure a definitive airway; a definitive airway is the placement of an ET in the trachea with an inflated cuff below the vocal cords; main reasons are to maintain airway patency and to protect the airway against aspiration.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All directly listed.  Why E wrong: ET tubes are not for oral nutrition delivery."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"A \"definitive airway\" is best defined as:","options":["A nasopharyngeal airway","Placement of an ET tube in the trachea with an inflated cuff below the vocal cords","A supraglottic airway device","A simple face mask"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A definitive airway is the placement of an ET in the trachea with an inflated cuff below the vocal cords.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are non-definitive/supraglottic or mask devices that do not include a cuffed tube below the cords."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"Which of the following is a contraindication to endotracheal intubation? (SATA)","options":["Severely airway trauma","Obstruction","Severe cervical spine injury which requires complete immobilization","Those patients with Mallampati III/IV classification","Routine elective oxygen therapy"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Contraindications include severely airway trauma, obstruction, severe cervical spine injury which may require complete immobilization, and those patients with Mallampati III/IV classification.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All listed as contraindications.  Why E wrong: Routine elective oxygen therapy is not a contraindication."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"Which of the following is a complication of endotracheal intubation? (SATA)","options":["Facial trauma","Head trauma concerning for basilar skull fracture","Active epistaxis","Expanding neck hematoma and oropharyngeal trauma","Improved lung compliance"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Complications listed: facial trauma, head trauma concerning for basilar skull fracture, active epistaxis, expanding neck hematoma, oropharyngeal trauma, and apneic patients.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All directly listed as complications.  Why E wrong: Improved lung compliance is a benefit, not a complication."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"The preparation before intubation is remembered by which mnemonic?","options":["SOAPME","RAGEL","MACOCHA","DAS"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Preparation before intubation trick is \"SOAPME.\"  Why B wrong: RAGEL THE HERO is the ET tube sizing/dimension mnemonic.  Why C wrong: MACOCHA is a difficult-intubation scoring system.  Why D wrong: DAS refers to the Difficult Airway Society guidelines."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"In the SOAPME mnemonic for intubation preparation, what does \"S\" stand for?","options":["Suction (control, mobile, manual)","Sedation","Stylet","Saturation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In SOAPME, S = Suction (control, mobile, manual).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the meaning assigned to S in the source."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"In the SOAPME mnemonic, what does \"O\" represent?","options":["Oxygen (low and high flow)","Obstruction","Oropharynx","Observer"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: O = Oxygen (low and high flow).  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the source meaning of O."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"In the SOAPME mnemonic, \"A\" stands for which group of equipment?","options":["Airway equipment (ETT, oral/nasal airway, laryngoscope, stylet, reservoir or self-inflating bag)","Anaesthesia agent only","AMBU bag only","Auscultation device"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A = Airway equipment (ETT, oral/nasal airway, laryngoscope, stylet, reservoir or self-inflating bag).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are only single items or incorrect; A covers the full airway equipment set."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"In SOAPME, what does \"P\" stand for?","options":["Patient position (sniffing)","Pressure","Pulse","Premedication"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: P = Patient position (sniffing).  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the assigned meaning of P."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"In SOAPME, what does \"M\" represent?","options":["Monitors (pulse oximeter, cardiac monitor)","Manometer","Mallampati","Mask"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: M = Monitors (pulse oximeter, cardiac monitor).  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the source meaning of M."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"In SOAPME, what does \"E\" stand for?","options":["Esophageal detection device (self-evacuating bulb, syringe aspiration device, capnography)","Endoscope","Epinephrine","Extubation kit"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: E = Esophageal detection device (self-evacuating bulb, syringe aspiration device, capnography).  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the assigned meaning of E."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"According to the SOAPME note, all the equipment functioning should ensure before:","options":["Initiating procedure","Discharging the patient","Documenting vital signs","Removing the cuff"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The note states all equipment functioning should ensure before initiating procedure.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the stated timing."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"According to the Endo Tracheal Tubes Size Chart, what is the recommended ET tube size for a newborn?","options":["2.5–4 mm","4.0–4.5 mm","5.0 mm","6.0–6.5 mm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The chart lists Newborn = 2.5–4 mm.  Why B wrong: 4.0–4.5 mm corresponds to infants.  Why C wrong: 5.0 mm corresponds to ≤6 years.  Why D wrong: 6.0–6.5 mm corresponds to female adults."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"According to the ET tube size chart, the recommended size for an infant is:","options":["2.5–4 mm","4.0–4.5 mm","5.0 mm","7.0–9.0 mm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Infants = 4.0–4.5 mm per the chart.  Why A wrong: 2.5–4 mm is newborn.  Why C wrong: 5.0 mm is ≤6 years.  Why D wrong: 7.0–9.0 mm corresponds to ≥6 years."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"According to the size chart, the ET tube size for a child of 6 years or less (≤6 years) is approximately:","options":["2.5 mm","5.0 mm","8.0 mm","9.0 mm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: ≤6 years = 5.0 mm per the chart.  Why A wrong: 2.5 mm is in the newborn range.  Why C/D wrong: These correspond to adult sizes."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"According to the size chart, the ET tube size for a child of 6 years or more (≥6 years) is:","options":["2.5–4 mm","4.0–4.5 mm","7.0–9.0 mm","5.0 mm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: ≥6 years = 7.0–9.0 mm per the chart.  Why A wrong: 2.5–4 mm is newborn.  Why B wrong: 4.0–4.5 mm is infant.  Why D wrong: 5.0 mm is ≤6 years."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"According to the size chart, the recommended ET tube size for an adult female is:","options":["5.0 mm","6.0–6.5 mm","8.0–8.5 mm","9.0–10.0 mm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Female adult = 6.0–6.5 mm per the chart.  Why A wrong: 5.0 mm is ≤6 years.  Why C wrong: 8.0–8.5 mm is adult male.  Why D wrong: 9.0–10.0 mm is large adult."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"According to the size chart, the recommended ET tube size for an adult male is:","options":["6.0–6.5 mm","8.0–8.5 mm","9.0–10.0 mm","4.0–4.5 mm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Male adult = 8.0–8.5 mm per the chart.  Why A wrong: 6.0–6.5 mm is female adult.  Why C wrong: 9.0–10.0 mm is large adult.  Why D wrong: 4.0–4.5 mm is infant."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"According to the size chart, the recommended ET tube size for a large adult is:","options":["6.0–6.5 mm","8.0–8.5 mm","9.0–10.0 mm","2.5–4 mm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Large adult = 9.0–10.0 mm per the chart.  Why A wrong: Female adult range.  Why B wrong: Male adult range.  Why D wrong: Newborn range."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"The mnemonic \"RAGEL THE HERO\" is used to recall what about the ET tube?","options":["Size of endotracheal (ET) tube in adults male/female","The cuff inflation pressure","The steps of extubation","The drugs given before intubation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: RAGEL THE HERO is the trick for \"Size of endotracheal (ET) tube in adults male/female.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the purpose of this mnemonic."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"According to RAGEL THE HERO, the typical ET tube size for an adult female is:","options":["6.5 mm","7.0 mm","8.0 mm","9.0 mm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source gives adult female = 7.0 mm (Right answer C in the chart line, female 7.0 mm).  Why A wrong: 6.5 mm is at the lower end of the female chart range but the RAGEL value cited is 7.0 mm.  Why C wrong: 8.0 mm is the adult male value.  Why D wrong: 9.0 mm exceeds typical adult sizing."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"According to RAGEL THE HERO, the typical ET tube size for an adult male is:","options":["6.5 mm","7.0 mm","8.0 mm","10.0 mm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source gives adult male = 8.0 mm.  Why A wrong: 6.5 mm is below adult male sizing.  Why B wrong: 7.0 mm is the female value.  Why D wrong: 10.0 mm is for a large adult."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"The average size of the ET tube for an adult female is 7.0 mm and for an adult male is 8.0 mm, though this is described as:","options":["Universally fixed regardless of institution","Somewhat institution-dependent practice","The same as the pediatric size","Never used in clinical practice"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note states the average size is female 7.0 mm and male 8.0 mm, though this is somewhat an institution dependent practice.  Why A wrong: It is not universally fixed; it depends on institution.  Why C wrong: It is the adult, not pediatric, size.  Why D wrong: It is clinical practice."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"What is the correct sniffing position for intubation?","options":["35° neck flexion and 85° head extension at the atlanto-occipital (AO) joint","90° neck flexion and no extension","Full neck hyperextension only","Neutral position with chin tucked"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The sniffing position is 35° neck flexion and 85° head extension at the AO joint for opening the airway.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the described sniffing position angles/intent."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"The \"head-tilt-chin-lift maneuver\" is used during positioning for intubation primarily to:","options":["Open the airway","Inflate the cuff","Confirm tube placement","Suction secretions"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The head-tilt-chin-lift maneuver is used for opening the airway.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are separate steps unrelated to this positioning maneuver."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"When cervical injury is suspected, which positioning maneuver should be used to open the airway?","options":["Head-tilt-chin-lift","Neutral or manual inline stabilization (MILS) with jaw thrust maneuver","Full hyperextension","Lateral recumbent positioning"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Neutral or manual inline stabilization (MILS): if cervical injury is suspected, use jaw thrust maneuver for opening the airway.  Why A wrong: Head-tilt-chin-lift moves the cervical spine and is avoided with suspected cervical injury.  Why C wrong: Hyperextension is dangerous with cervical injury.  Why D wrong: Lateral recumbent positioning is not the airway-opening maneuver described."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"The \"ramping position\" during intubation is specifically used for which patient population?","options":["Obese and pregnant women","Neonates only","Patients with cervical injury","Patients in cardiac arrest"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Ramping position is for obese and pregnant women.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the stated population for ramping."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"Which of the following is part of the step-by-step intubation procedure? (SATA)","options":["Obtain informed consent","Take appropriate size of ET tube according to age","If secretion is there, suction it first and pre-oxygenate the client with 100% FiO2 for at least 3–5 minutes, checking the pulse oximetry","Place the client in sniffing, neutral, and ramped position for better visualization of trachea during the entire procedure","Withhold all monitoring during the procedure"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The procedure includes obtaining informed consent, selecting appropriate ET tube size by age, suctioning secretions and pre-oxygenating with 100% FiO2 for at least 3–5 minutes with pulse oximetry, and positioning in sniffing/neutral/ramped position.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All directly listed steps.  Why E wrong: Monitoring is required, not withheld."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"Before laryngoscopy, the client should be pre-oxygenated with 100% FiO2 for at least how long?","options":["30 seconds","1 minute","3–5 minutes","10 minutes"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Pre-oxygenate the client with 100% FiO2 for at least 3–5 minutes and check pulse oximetry.  Why A/B wrong: Shorter than the stated minimum.  Why D wrong: 10 minutes exceeds the stated time."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"During laryngoscopy, the laryngoscope is grasped in which hand, and the blade is inserted from which side of the patient's mouth?","options":["Right hand; right side","Left hand; right side, then moved to the left","Right hand; left side only","Either hand; midline"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Grasp the laryngoscope in the left hand and insert the blade from the down (lower) side of the patient's mouth with your right thumb, and introduce the tip of the laryngoscope into the right side of the patient's mouth.  Why A wrong: The laryngoscope is held in the left hand.  Why C wrong: It is introduced into the right side, not left.  Why D wrong: It is not midline."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"When watching the trachea by laryngoscope, the nurse should slide the blade and insert the tube properly. Where is the correct placement of the ET tube relative to the carina?","options":["At the level of the carina","1–2 cm above the carina","5 cm below the carina","In the right main bronchus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Place the tube 1–2 cm above the carina; this is the correct placement of the ET tube, which prevents continuous airway stimulation and inadvertent bronchial intubation.  Why A wrong: Placement at the carina causes stimulation and risk of bronchial intubation.  Why C wrong: 5 cm below the carina would be a bronchial (mainstem) intubation.  Why D wrong: Right mainstem intubation is a malposition to avoid."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"Placing the ET tube too deep (e.g., at or beyond the carina) increases the risk of which complication?","options":["Continuous airway stimulation and inadvertent bronchial intubation","Esophageal intubation","Cuff overinflation","Tube dislodgement upward"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Placing the tube 1–2 cm above the carina prevents continuous airway stimulation and inadvertent bronchial intubation; placing it too deep risks these.  Why B wrong: Esophageal intubation results from misdirection into the esophagus, not from depth past the carina.  Why C wrong: Cuff overinflation is a separate pressure issue.  Why D wrong: Too-deep placement is the opposite of dislodgement upward."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"Performing the Sellick maneuver (cricoid pressure) during intubation is indicated when the patient is at risk of:","options":["Gastric aspiration","Hypotension","Hypothermia","Bronchospasm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Perform Sellick maneuver if patient is at risk of gastric aspiration.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the indication for the Sellick maneuver."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"The BURP maneuver is performed during intubation to:","options":["Visualize the larynx (backward, upward, rightward pressure on the larynx)","Inflate the cuff","Confirm placement by capnography","Suction the airway"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: BURP maneuver is performed for visualization of the larynx.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the purpose of BURP."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"After intubation, how is the correct location of the tube initially assessed?","options":["By bilateral lung sounds and visible symmetrical chest rise","By chest X-ray only","By cuff pressure measurement only","By patient's verbal confirmation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Assess correct location of the tube by bilateral lung sounds and visible symmetrical chest rise.  Why B wrong: X-ray confirms position but is not the immediate bedside assessment described first.  Why C wrong: Cuff pressure does not confirm tube location.  Why D wrong: An intubated patient cannot verbally confirm."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"When inflating the cuff after proper placement of the tube, the pressure should be maintained at:","options":["5–10 cm H2O","20–25 cm H2O","22–25 cm H2O to prevent exhaust air or overinflation","40–50 cm H2O"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: After proper placement of the tube inflate the cuff at pressure ≥ to relate to secure fixation of the tube; the cuff pressure should be maintained at 22–25 cm H2O to prevent exhaust air or overinflation.  Why A wrong: 5–10 cm H2O is too low to seal.  Why B wrong: 20–25 is close but the source specifies 22–25 cm H2O.  Why D wrong: 40–50 cm H2O causes mucosal ischemia."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"After intubation, immediately every time the tube is moved with adhesive tape, the nurse should:","options":["Check chest at one side of mouth corner","Deflate the cuff","Increase oxygen flow","Remove the stylet only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: After intubation immediately every time the tube is moved with adhesive tape, check chest at one side of mouth corner.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated check after re-securing the tube."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"After confirming correct placement, the nurse should then connect the tube to the:","options":["Ventilator","Suction unit","Capnograph only","Nebulizer"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: After that, connect the tube to the ventilator.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the stated next connection."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"The correct endotracheal cuff pressure must be less than which value to preserve capillary perfusion pressure?","options":["Less than capillary perfusion pressure","Greater than arterial pressure","Exactly 50 cm H2O","Zero (cuff deflated)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The correct endotracheal cuff pressure must be less than the capillary perfusion pressure.  Why B wrong: Exceeding arterial/capillary pressure causes mucosal ischemia.  Why C wrong: 50 cm H2O is excessive.  Why D wrong: A deflated cuff fails to seal."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"Which device is used to measure the ET tube cuff pressure?","options":["Handheld cuff pressure manometer","Sphygmomanometer on the arm","Pulse oximeter","Capnograph"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Measure the ET tube cuff pressure with a handheld cuff pressure manometer and keep it maintained at 20 mm Hg / 22–25 cm H2O to prevent exhaust air or overinflation.  Why B/C/D wrong: These devices measure other parameters, not cuff pressure."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"A cuff pressure greater than 25 cm H2O or greater than 20 mm Hg may cause which complication?","options":["Stenosis at the site of cuff and other complications","Cuff leak only","Tube dislodgement upward","Esophageal intubation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cuff pressure > 25 cm H2O or > 20 mm Hg may cause stenosis at the site of cuff and other complications.  Why B wrong: A leak occurs with under-inflation, not high pressure.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the stated effect of high cuff pressure."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"If the tube is placed in the stomach (esophageal intubation), then on auscultation the nurse would expect:","options":["Loud sound over the epigastric region and abdominal distension","Bilateral clear lung sounds","Symmetrical chest rise","Normal capnography waveform"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: If tube placed in stomach then a loud sound will be heard over the epigastric region and abdominal distension will be occur.  Why B/C/D wrong: These indicate correct tracheal placement, not esophageal misplacement."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"The oral ET tube needs to be moved to the opposite side of the mouth daily; what is the reason?","options":["Tube adhesive tape should also be changed everyday to prevent pressure sore and remove the secretion in the mouth and lip area","To increase cuff pressure","To replace the tube every shift","To reduce oxygen requirement"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The oral ET tube needs to be moved to the opposite side of the mouth daily; tube adhesive tape should also be changed everyday to prevent pressure sore and remove the secretion in the mouth and lip area, and also facilitate inspection and cleaning of the mouth.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the stated reason for repositioning the tube."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"Cleaning of the mouth and re-securing the tube intervention should be done by:","options":["Nurses","Physiotherapists only","Family members","The patient"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: This intervention should be done by nurses.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not designated to perform this intervention."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"How often should the tube be suctioned when needed?","options":["Every shift","Every hour","Every 5 minutes","Continuously"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Suction the tube when needed or every shift.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the stated minimum frequency."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"To prevent dislodgement and pulling of the tube, the nurse should:","options":["Provide suction, coughing, and speaking can cause the tube dislodgment, so prevent these triggers","Increase cuff pressure to maximum","Deflate the cuff each hour","Restrain all four limbs continuously"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Prevent dislodgement and pulling of the tube; suction, coughing, and speaking can cause the tube dislodgment.  Why B wrong: Maximal cuff pressure causes mucosal injury, not dislodgement prevention.  Why C wrong: Routine hourly cuff deflation is not indicated.  Why D wrong: Routine four-limb restraint is not the stated intervention."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"Match the labeled parts of the ET tube. Which of the following are correctly identified components of an ET tube? (SATA)","options":["Pilot balloon and self-sealing valve","Bevelled tip and Murphy's eye","Cuff and radio-opaque line","15 mm connector","Surgical stoma collar"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The labeled ET tube includes pilot balloon, self-sealing valve, bevelled tip, Murphy's eye, cuff, internal and outer diameters, radio-opaque line, and 15 mm connector.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All are labeled ET tube parts in the figure.  Why E wrong: A surgical stoma collar belongs to a tracheostomy, not an ET tube."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"Which of the following are functions of the ET cuff? (SATA)","options":["Prevents aspiration of blood, secretions, and gastric contents","Keeps the ET tube central in the trachea","Prevents leakage of gases around the ET during positive pressure ventilation, resulting in loss of tidal volume and pollution of the operating theatre","Provides nutrition to the patient","Generates the patient's spontaneous respiratory drive"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Functions of ET cuff: prevents aspiration of blood, secretions and gastric contents; keeps the ET tube central in the trachea; prevents leakage of gases around the ET during positive pressure ventilation resulting in loss of tidal volume and pollution of the operating theatre.  Why A/B/C correct: All directly listed cuff functions.  Why D wrong: The cuff does not provide nutrition.  Why E wrong: The cuff does not generate respiratory drive."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"\"Must Know Fact\": In a patient who is intubated on mechanical ventilation, an AMBU bag needs to be kept at the bedside always. Why?","options":["Placing the laryngoscope blade in the middle of the tongue and failing to move the tongue to the left are two common errors that prevent visualization of the vocal cords","To deliver oral medication","To monitor cuff pressure","To provide nutrition"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Must Know Fact states an AMBU bag needs to be kept at bedside always, and that placing the laryngoscope blade in the middle of the tongue and failing to move the tongue to the left are two common errors that prevent visualization of the vocal cords.  Why B/C/D wrong: An AMBU bag provides manual ventilation, not these functions."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"Which is the gold standard and first most reliable method of confirming right placement of the ET tube?","options":["Capnography (detect EtCO2)","Esophageal detection device","Chest X-ray","Pulse oximetry"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Capnography (detect EtCO2) is the gold standard and first most reliable method to confirm right placement of the ET tube.  Why B wrong: Esophageal detection device is the second most reliable method.  Why C wrong: Chest X-ray is used to confirm tip position but is not the first most reliable bedside method.  Why D wrong: Pulse oximetry is a listed method but not the gold standard."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"Which is described as the second most reliable method to confirm right placement of the ET tube?","options":["Capnography","Esophageal detection device (bulb and syringe aspiration device)","Direct visualisation by laryngoscope","USG"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Esophageal detection device (bulb and syringe aspiration device) is the second most reliable method.  Why A wrong: Capnography is the first most reliable method.  Why C/D wrong: These are listed methods but not designated as the second most reliable."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"Which of the following are methods of confirming right placement of the ET tube? (SATA)","options":["Capnography and esophageal detection device","Chest X-ray and USG","Direct visualisation by laryngoscope and auscultation of breath sounds","Absence of epigastric breath sounds with respiration and reservoir bag compliance","Patient stating the tube is correctly placed"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Methods include capnography, esophageal detection device, chest X-ray, USG, direct visualisation by laryngoscope, observation of the chest movement, auscultation of breath sounds, absence of epigastric breath sounds with respiration, reservoir bag compliance, ET cuff manometers, absence of air escape, and pulse oximetry.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All are listed confirmation methods.  Why E wrong: An intubated patient cannot state this; not a confirmation method."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"Which of the following are listed methods of confirming right ET tube placement? (SATA)","options":["Observation of the chest movement","Reservoir bag compliance","ET cuff manometers","Absence of air escape and pulse oximetry","Measuring the patient's blood glucose"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Listed methods include observation of chest movement, reservoir bag compliance, ET cuff manometers, absence of air escape, and pulse oximetry.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All directly listed.  Why E wrong: Blood glucose is unrelated to confirming tube placement."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"When preparing for extubation, the nurse should keep emergency equipment at the bedside in working condition, including which items? (SATA)","options":["AMBU bag and oxygen kit","Monitoring aids: pulse oximeter, cardiac monitor, BP apparatus, and stethoscope","Catheter","A sterile suction kit","A defibrillator that has never been tested"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Preparation before extubation: keep emergency equipment at bedside in working condition (AMBU bag, O2 kit), monitoring aids should include pulse oximeter, cardiac monitor, BP apparatus, and stethoscope, plus catheter and suction kit.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All listed equipment.  Why E wrong: An untested defibrillator is unacceptable; equipment must be in working condition."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"In what position should the patient be placed before extubation?","options":["Semi-Fowler's or semi-recumbent position","Prone position","Trendelenburg position","Left lateral position"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Place the patient in a semi-Fowler's position or semi-recumbent position.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the stated position for extubation."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"During the step-by-step extubation procedure, before extubation, the nurse should:","options":["Hyperventilate the patient and instruct the patient to open his/her mouth and hyperoxygenate the patient with 100% FiO2, then suction","Deflate the cuff and leave the tube in","Lower oxygen to room air immediately","Remove the tube during expiration only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Step-by-step extubation: prior to extubation, hyperventilate the patient and ask the patient to open his/her mouth, hyperoxygenate the patient with 100% FiO2, and suction the oral cavity orally.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the stated pre-extubation steps."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"When should the tube be removed during the extubation procedure?","options":["At peak inspiratory period, suctioning the airway through the tube while pulling it out","During quiet expiration only","While the cuff remains fully inflated","After deflating the cuff and waiting 1 hour"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The cuff is now deflated, have the patient inhale and remove the tube at peak inspiratory period, suctioning the airway through the tube while pulling it out.  Why B wrong: The source specifies removal at peak inspiration, not expiration.  Why C wrong: The cuff must be deflated before removal.  Why D wrong: No 1-hour wait is described."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"Removing the tube at peak inspiratory period is associated with which benefit? (SATA)","options":["Better oxygen reservoir in lungs","Decreased incidence of laryngospasm and reduction of secretion","Aerosolised epinephrine 1:1000 and heliox (helium and oxygen) is often used to manage post-extubation laryngeal edema, stridor and partial airway obstruction","Increased risk of aspiration","Permanent loss of cough reflex"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Removing the tube at peak inspiratory period is associated with better oxygen reservoir in lungs, decreased incidence of laryngospasm, and reduction of secretion; aerosolised epinephrine 1:1000 and heliox is often used to manage post-extubation laryngeal edema, stridor, and partial airway obstruction.  Why A/B/C correct: All directly stated.  Why D wrong: It does not increase aspiration risk; that is a misstatement.  Why E wrong: Permanent loss of cough reflex is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"After extubation, the nurse should instruct the patient to:","options":["Cough and deep-breathe to assist in removing accumulated secretions in the throat","Avoid all coughing for 24 hours","Lie completely flat and remain still","Take nothing by mouth indefinitely"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: After removal, instruct the patient to cough and deep-breathe to assist in removing accumulated secretions in the throat.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the stated post-extubation instruction."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"After extubation, which assessments should the nurse perform? (SATA)","options":["Apply oxygen therapy","Assess the patient's swallowing reflex by asking the patient's name","Assess for stridor (which can be caused by laryngeal edema) by auscultating over the trachea; if present, inform doctor immediately","Assess for loose teeth (because which can block the airway)","Discharge the patient immediately without monitoring"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: After extubation: apply oxygen therapy; assess the swallowing reflex by asking the patient's name; assess for stridor (laryngeal edema) by auscultating over the trachea and inform doctor immediately if present; assess for loose teeth which can block the airway; monitor for respiratory difficulty and contact doctor if respiratory difficulties occur.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All directly listed post-extubation assessments.  Why E wrong: Immediate discharge without monitoring is contraindicated."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"After extubation, the nurse must monitor for respiratory difficulty and:","options":["Contact the doctor if respiratory difficulties occur","Reintubate immediately as routine","Withhold all oxygen","Encourage the patient to talk continuously"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Monitor for respiratory difficulty and contact doctor if respiratory difficulties occur. Inform the patient that hoarseness or a sore throat is normal and that the client should limit talking if either occurs.  Why B wrong: Routine immediate reintubation is not indicated.  Why C wrong: Oxygen therapy is applied, not withheld.  Why D wrong: The client should limit talking, not talk continuously."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"Which of the following is a complication of intubation? (SATA)","options":["A wrongly placed endotracheal tube, especially in the apnea patient","Accidental intubation of the esophagus and trauma to the soft tissues of the oropharynx","Broken teeth or denture","Endo-bronchial intubation, especially where ETT has been inserted too far","Improved gas exchange in all cases"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Complications of intubation: a wrongly placed endotracheal tube (especially in apnea patient); accidental intubation of the esophagus; trauma to the soft tissues of the oropharynx; broken teeth or denture; endo-bronchial intubation especially where ETT inserted too far; sudden surge in heart rate, blood pressure, arrhythmias and cardiac ischemia; oral infection; laryngeal oedema; tracheal stenosis; and ventilator associated pneumonia (VAP).  Why A/B/C/D correct: All directly listed complications.  Why E wrong: Improved gas exchange is a goal, not a complication."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"Which of the following are additional complications of intubation? (SATA)","options":["Sudden surge in heart rate, blood pressure, arrhythmias, and cardiac ischemia","Oral infection and laryngeal oedema","Tracheal stenosis","Ventilator associated pneumonia (VAP)","Increased nutritional absorption"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Listed complications include sudden surge in heart rate, blood pressure, arrhythmias and cardiac ischemia; oral infection; laryngeal oedema; tracheal stenosis; and ventilator associated pneumonia (VAP).  Why A/B/C/D correct: All directly listed.  Why E wrong: Increased nutritional absorption is unrelated and not a complication."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"The MACOCHA scoring system is used for:","options":["Early identification of patients at risk of difficult intubation in the ICU","Confirming tube placement","Measuring cuff pressure","Selecting tube size"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: MACOCHA scoring system is used for early identification of patients at risk of difficult intubation in the ICU.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the purpose of MACOCHA scoring."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"In the MACOCHA scoring system, what does a score of 0 (zero) indicate?","options":["Easy tracheal intubation","Definite difficult airway","Esophageal intubation","Need for cricothyroidotomy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A score of 0 indicates easy tracheal intubation.  Why B wrong: Higher scores indicate difficult airway.  Why C/D wrong: Not what the score represents."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"In the MACOCHA scoring system, a score greater than 3 indicates:","options":["Difficult airway","Easy airway","Normal airway","No airway risk"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A score > 3 indicates difficult airway, and score 12 indicates very difficult airway.  Why B/C/D wrong: These contradict the stated meaning of a high score."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"In the MACOCHA scoring system, a score of 12 indicates:","options":["Very difficult airway","Easy airway","No risk","Esophageal placement"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A score of 12 indicates very difficult airway.  Why B/C/D wrong: Contradict the maximal-difficulty meaning of the highest score."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"Within the MACOCHA scoring system, which factors are related to the patient (patient pathology)? (SATA)","options":["Mallampati score of 3 or 4","Obstructive sleep apnea","Reduced cervical spine mobility and limited mouth opening less than 3 cm","Coma and severe hypoxia","Non-anaesthesiologist operator"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: MACOCHA factors related to patient (pathology) include Mallampati score 3 or 4, obstructive sleep apnea, reduced cervical spine mobility, limited mouth opening less than 3 cm; factors related to disease/pathology include coma and severe hypoxia.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All patient-related factors.  Why E wrong: Non-anaesthesiologist is a factor related to the operator, not the patient."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"In the MACOCHA scoring system, which factor is related to the operator?","options":["Mallampati score 3 or 4","Obstructive sleep apnea","Non-anaesthesiologist","Coma"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The factor related to the operator is a non-anaesthesiologist.  Why A/B wrong: These are patient-related factors.  Why D wrong: Coma is a disease/pathology-related factor."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"Which of the following are conditions that should require less than 30 ml of air for ET cuff inflation? (Must Know Fact)","options":["The cuff of a correctly placed ET tube should require less than 30 ml of air","The cuff requires exactly 100 ml of air","The cuff should never be inflated","The cuff requires 50–60 ml of air"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The cuff of a correctly placed ET tube should require less than 30 ml of air.  Why B/C/D wrong: These contradict the stated volume requirement."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"When nitrous oxide (N2O) is used for general anaesthesia, it diffuses into the air-filled ET cuff, causing the cuff pressure to rise by over time. What should be done?","options":["When using N2O during anaesthesia, cuff pressures must regularly be checked to prevent damage to the tracheal mucosa and local ischemia","Increase the N2O concentration","Permanently deflate the cuff","Replace the tube every hour"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: When nitrous oxide (N2O) is used for general anaesthesia, it diffuses into the air-filled ET cuff causing the cuff pressure to rise over time, so during anaesthesia cuff pressures must regularly be checked to prevent damage to the tracheal mucosa and local ischemia.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the stated management of N2O cuff pressure rise."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"All ET tubes connect to the anaesthetic circuit with an international standard connector of what size?","options":["15 mm connector","10 mm connector","22 mm connector","8 mm connector"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: All ET tubes connect to the anaesthetic circuit with an international standard 15 mm connector; connections should be made secure with a \"push and twist\" movement.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the international standard ET connector size."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"ET tube connections to the anaesthetic circuit should be made secure with which movement?","options":["Push and twist","Pull and rotate","Press and hold","Slide and lock"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Connections should be made secure with a \"push and twist\" movement.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the described securing movement."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"Where should the tip of the ET tube ideally be relative to the carina, according to the Must Know Fact?","options":["2 cm above the carina, with the proximal part of the cuff at 1.5 to 2.5 cm from the vocal cords","At the level of the vocal cords","5 cm below the carina","In the right mainstem bronchus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: It is suggested that the tip of ET should be at least 2 cm from the carina, or the proximal part of the cuff should be 1.5 to 2.5 cm from the vocal cords.  Why B wrong: Placement at the cords is too high.  Why C/D wrong: These describe bronchial (too deep) malposition."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"What is another name for a bougie used in difficult intubation?","options":["Gum elastic bougie","Magill forceps","Stylet","Yankauer"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A bougie is also called a gum elastic bougie.  Why B wrong: Magill forceps are for guiding tubes, not the bougie.  Why C wrong: A stylet is a separate device, though similar in concept.  Why D wrong: A Yankauer is a suction tip."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"What is the outer diameter and length of a bougie?","options":["Outer diameter 5 mm (15 Fr) and length 60–70 cm","Outer diameter 10 mm and length 100 cm","Outer diameter 2 mm and length 30 cm","Outer diameter 8 mm and length 50 cm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The bougie outer diameter is 5 mm (15 Fr) and length is 60–70 cm.  Why B/C/D wrong: Do not match the stated bougie dimensions."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"The distal tip of a bougie is bent at what angle to facilitate its passage behind the epiglottis?","options":["It's 3.4 cm distal tip is bent at an angle of 30° to facilitate its passage behind the epiglottis","90°","10°","60°"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: It's 3.4 cm distal tip is bent at an angle of 30° to facilitate its passage behind the epiglottis.  Why B/C/D wrong: Do not match the stated 30° bend."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"What is the \"Kiwi grip\" technique?","options":["A perfect and easy intubation technique, a one-handed means of inserting the tube and bougie together, that prevents sliding of the devices on each other","A method of cuff inflation","A position for extubation","A type of laryngoscope blade"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Kiwi grip is a perfect and easy intubation technique, a one-handed means of inserting the tube and bougie together, and prevents sliding of the devices on each other.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the described meaning of the Kiwi grip."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"If the ET (tracheal) tube and tracheostomy tube are displaced or dislodged, which clinical features can be seen? (SATA)","options":["Hypoxia and CVS instability","Respiratory distress, high RR, decreased O2 saturation","Failure to achieve set pressure/ventilation (decrease TV, EtCO2)","Audible cuff leak despite appropriate cuff pressure and patient talking despite tracheostomy cuff inflated","Improved oxygenation and stable vital signs"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: If ET tube and tracheostomy tube displaced then the following clinical features can be seen: hypoxia, CVS instability, respiratory distress with high RR and decreased O2 saturation, failure to achieve set pressure/ventilation (decrease TV, EtCO2), audible cuff leak despite appropriate cuff pressure, and patient talking despite tracheostomy cuff inflated.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All directly listed clinical features.  Why E wrong: Improved oxygenation/stable vitals contradict tube displacement."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"Which nursing interventions are appropriate if a tracheal tube is displaced or dislodged? (SATA)","options":["Call for help or inform the doctor","Give 100% oxygen","Check/attach capnography (EtCO2) and call for difficulty airway trolley","Increase the cuff pressure to maximum and continue ventilation","Ignore the alarm and continue"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing intervention if tracheal tube displaced: call for help or inform the doctor, give 100% oxygen, check/attach capnography (EtCO2), and call for the difficulty airway trolley.  Why A/B/C correct: All directly listed interventions.  Why D wrong: Increasing cuff pressure does not address a dislodged tube and risks injury.  Why E wrong: Ignoring the situation is dangerous and not an intervention."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"The DAS Difficult Intubation Guidelines describe Plan A as:","options":["Facemask ventilation and tracheal intubation (laryngoscopy succeeding to tracheal intubation)","Supraglottic airway device insertion","Facemask ventilation only","Emergency front of neck access"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In the DAS guidelines overview, Plan A is facemask ventilation and tracheal intubation, with laryngoscopy succeeding to tracheal intubation (and failed intubation moving to Plan B).  Why B wrong: Supraglottic airway device insertion is Plan B.  Why C wrong: Facemask ventilation only is Plan C.  Why D wrong: Emergency front of neck access is Plan D."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"In the DAS Difficult Intubation Guidelines, Plan B refers to:","options":["Maintaining oxygenation, supraglottic airway device (SAD) insertion","Facemask ventilation and tracheal intubation","Facemask ventilation only","Cricothyroidotomy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Plan B is maintaining oxygenation with supraglottic airway device (SAD) insertion; if the SAD succeeds, \"STOP AND THINK.\"  Why B wrong: That is Plan A.  Why C wrong: That is Plan C.  Why D wrong: Cricothyroidotomy belongs to Plan D."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"After successful supraglottic airway device (SAD) insertion in DAS Plan B, the \"STOP AND THINK\" options include which of the following? (SATA)","options":["Wake the patient up","Intubate trachea via the SAD","Intubate trachea via the SAD using fibreoptic guidance","Tracheostomy or cricothyroidotomy","Immediately remove all monitoring"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: STOP AND THINK options (consider risks and benefits): 1. Wake the patient up, 2. Intubate trachea via the SAD, 3. Intubate trachea via the SAD (fibreoptic), 4. Tracheostomy or cricothyroidotomy.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All four options listed in the box.  Why E wrong: Removing monitoring is unsafe and not an option."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"In the DAS Difficult Intubation Guidelines, Plan C refers to:","options":["Facemask ventilation; final attempts at facemask ventilation, succeeding to wake the patient up","Tracheal intubation by laryngoscopy","Supraglottic airway device insertion","Immediate emergency front of neck access"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Plan C is facemask ventilation; final attempts at facemask ventilation, and if successful, wake the patient up.  Why B wrong: That is Plan A.  Why C wrong: That is Plan B.  Why D wrong: Emergency front of neck access is Plan D."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"In the DAS Difficult Intubation Guidelines, Plan D refers to:","options":["Emergency front of neck access (cricothyroidotomy)","Facemask ventilation and tracheal intubation","Supraglottic airway device insertion","Maintaining oxygenation only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Plan D is emergency front of neck access (cricothyroidotomy), reached after CICO (can't intubate, can't oxygenate).  Why B wrong: That is Plan A.  Why C wrong: That is Plan B.  Why D wrong: That broadly describes Plan B's goal, not Plan D."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"In the DAS guidelines, \"CICO\" preceding emergency front of neck access (Plan D) stands for:","options":["Can't intubate, can't oxygenate","Continuous intubation, continuous oxygenation","Clear intubation, complete oxygenation","Cardiac instability, central obstruction"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: CICO denotes \"can't intubate, can't oxygenate,\" which prompts moving to emergency front of neck access (cricothyroidotomy).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the meaning of CICO."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"The endotracheal tube (ETT) is most commonly made of which material?","options":["Polyvinyl chloride (PVC)","Latex rubber","Stainless steel","Silicone gel"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The ETT is a tube made by polyvinyl chloride (PVC) that is placed between the vocal cords through the trachea to provide oxygen and inhaled gases to the lungs.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the stated material for the standard ETT."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"The ETT keeps the airway free from contamination, such as:","options":["Gastric contents and blood","Inhaled anaesthetic gas","Oxygen","Nebulised medication"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The ETT protects the lungs from contamination, such as gastric contents and blood.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are substances intentionally delivered, not contaminants to be excluded."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"Ventilator-associated pneumonia (VAP) is a major concern with ETT and is considered to be a primary agent for the development of:","options":["VAP","Pulmonary embolism","Pneumothorax","Pleural effusion"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Ventilator-associated pneumonia (VAP) is a major concern with ETT and is considered to be a primary agent for the development of VAP.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the stated primary concern linked to the ETT."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"Pediatric ETTs are sized by which parameter?","options":["Age","Weight only","Height only","Body surface area only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Pediatric ETTs are sized by their age.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the stated sizing parameter for pediatric ETTs."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"Newborn ETTs are chosen by which parameters?","options":["Gestational age and weight","Height and head circumference","Mallampati score","Blood pressure"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Newborn ETTs are chosen by their gestational age and weight.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the stated parameters for newborn ETT selection."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"Traditionally, pediatric endotracheal tubes were uncuffed for what reason?","options":["Because the pressure from the cuff would damage the trachea via pressure necrosis, as the narrow part just below the vocal cords (cricoid cartilage) is the narrowest part in children","Because cuffs are unavailable in small sizes","Because children do not require ventilation","Because cuffed tubes are illegal in pediatrics"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Traditionally pediatric endotracheal tubes were uncuffed for fear that the pressure from the cuff would damage the trachea via pressure necrosis, as the narrow part just below the vocal cords (cricoid cartilage) is the narrowest part in children.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the stated reason for uncuffed pediatric tubes."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"In children, which anatomical structure is the narrowest part of the airway?","options":["The cricoid cartilage (just below the vocal cords)","The carina","The glottis","The bronchus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In adults, the narrowest portion of the airway is the vocal cords; in children, the cricoid cartilage just below the vocal cords is the narrowest part.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the narrowest part in children."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"In adults, the narrowest portion of the airway is the:","options":["Vocal cords","Cricoid cartilage","Carina","Trachea"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In adults, the narrowest portion of the airway is the vocal cords.  Why B wrong: The cricoid cartilage is narrowest in children, not adults.  Why C/D wrong: Not the narrowest portion."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"The endotracheal tube has both a length and a diameter. The ETT size (\"give me a 5.0 tube\") refers to its:","options":["Internal diameter in millimetres (mm)","External diameter in centimetres","Length in inches","Cuff volume"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The ETT size (\"give me a 5.0 tube\") refers to its internal diameter in millimetres (mm).  Why B/C/D wrong: The number refers to the internal diameter, not external diameter, length, or cuff volume."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"The ETT will typically have both the inner and outer diameter of the tube marked on it. For example, a 5.0 endotracheal tube has the inner diameter (ID) marked as 5.0 mm and the outer diameter marked as approximately:","options":["ID 6.0, and the outer diameter, OD 6.8.5","ID 5.0 only, no outer diameter","ID 10 mm","ID 2.5 mm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The ETT will typically have both the inner and outer diameter of the tube marked on it; for example ID 6.0, and the outer diameter, OD 6.8.5.  Why B/C/D wrong: Do not match the stated example markings."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"The narrower the tube, the greater the resistance to gas flow. What does the ETT measure from?","options":["From the distal end of the tube and is typically marked in 1 cm increments","Only at the proximal end","From the cuff to the connector","From the bevel to Murphy's eye only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The ETT is measured from the distal end of the tube and is typically marked in 1 cm increments.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the stated measurement reference."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"Is polyvinyl chloride (PVC) radiopaque, and how is ET placement verified on X-ray?","options":["PVC is not radiopaque, and thus a radiopaque linear material is included throughout the length of the tube to make it easier to visualise the placement on X-ray","PVC is fully radiopaque and needs no marker","PVC dissolves on X-ray","PVC requires contrast injection to be seen"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: PVC is not radiopaque, thus a radiopaque linear material is included throughout the length of the tube to make it easier to visualise the placement on X-ray.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the stated property/method."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"Ideally, the distal tip of the ETT is how far from the carina, measured at the central incisors on a chest X-ray?","options":["4 cm (+/- 2 cm) above the carina","At the carina","10 cm above the carina","Below the carina"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Ideally, the distal tip of the ETT is 4 cm (+/- 2 cm) above the carina, measured at the central incisors.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the stated ideal position."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"ETTs larger than 8.0 are available and used for what purpose?","options":["Bronchoscopy","Pediatric resuscitation","Cuff deflation","Nasogastric feeding"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: ETTs larger than 8.0 are available and used for bronchoscopy.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the stated use for tubes larger than 8.0."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"The typical depth of the endotracheal tube is 23 cm for an adult male and how many cm for an adult female, measured at the central incisors?","options":["21 cm for women","25 cm for women","18 cm for women","30 cm for women"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The typical depth of the endotracheal tube is 23 cm for men and 21 cm for women, measured at the central incisors.  Why B/C/D wrong: Do not match the stated 21 cm for women."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"The average size of the tube for an adult male is 8.0–9.0 mm and for an adult female is:","options":["6.5–7.5 mm","8.0–9.0 mm","4.0–5.0 mm","10 mm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The average size of the tube for an adult male is 8.0–9.0 mm and for an adult female is 6.5–7.5 mm.  Why B wrong: 8.0–9.0 mm is the male range.  Why C/D wrong: Not the stated female range."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"Pediatric tubes are sized using which equation?","options":["Size = (Age in years / 4) + 4 for uncuffed tubes","Size = Age x 2","Size = Weight / 10","Size = Height / 5"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Pediatric tubes are sized using the equation: size = (age/4) +4 for uncuffed tubes.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the stated pediatric sizing equation."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"When using a cuffed tube in children, the tube size formula should be adjusted by:","options":["Using a smaller-sized cuffed tube (cuffed tubes being one-half size smaller)","Using a larger-sized cuffed tube","No adjustment","Doubling the calculated size"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: When using cuffed tubes being one-half size smaller; that is, a smaller cuffed tube is used compared to the uncuffed calculation.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the stated adjustment."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"Typically, a pediatric ETT is taped at a depth of 3 times the tube size; for a child with a 4.0 tube (4.4 cm ID), it commonly gets taped at around:","options":["12 cm depth (3 x tube size)","4 cm depth","20 cm depth","30 cm depth"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Typically, a pediatric ETT is taped at a depth of 3 times the tube size; for a child with a 4.0 tube it commonly gets taped at around 12 cm depth (3 x tube size).  Why B/C/D wrong: Do not match the 3 x tube size depth rule."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"What is described about the ET cuff material/inflation on the cuffed tube?","options":["A cuff is an inflatable balloon at the distal end of the ETT","The cuff is rigid plastic","The cuff is permanently fixed and non-inflatable","The cuff is metal"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A cuff is an inflatable balloon at the distal end of the ETT.  Why B/C/D wrong: The cuff is an inflatable balloon, not rigid, fixed, or metal."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"Pediatric ETTs are produced with and without:","options":["Cuffs","Murphy's eye","Radiopaque line","Connectors"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Pediatric ETTs are produced with and without cuffs.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are standard features, not the with/without variable described."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"What does the inflated cuff of an ETT do? (SATA)","options":["Prevents gastric contents from entering the trachea","Facilitates the execution of positive pressure ventilation","The cuff inflates by attaching an appropriate size syringe (10 to 20 ml for adult ETT) to the pilot balloon","Provides nutrition to the trachea","Generates spontaneous breathing"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The inflated cuff prevents gastric contents from entering the trachea and facilitates the execution of positive pressure ventilation; the cuff inflates by attaching an appropriate size syringe (10 to 20 ml for adult ETT) to the pilot balloon.  Why A/B/C correct: All directly stated.  Why D wrong: The cuff does not provide nutrition.  Why E wrong: The cuff does not generate spontaneous breathing."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"The cuff is inflated by attaching a syringe of what volume (for an adult ETT) to the pilot balloon?","options":["10 to 20 ml","1 to 2 ml","30 to 50 ml","50 to 100 ml"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The cuff inflates by attaching an appropriate size syringe (10 to 20 ml for adult ETT) to the pilot balloon.  Why B/C/D wrong: Do not match the stated adult inflation volume."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"Once the cuff inflates, the syringe must be removed; what should be done to the air in the cuff?","options":["The air in the cuff must be redistribute back to the syringe and deflate the cuff (deflated using the pilot balloon)","The air should be increased continuously","The cuff should be left over-inflated","The air should be discarded entirely"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Once the cuff inflates, the syringe must be removed; the air in the cuff must be redistribute back to the syringe to deflate the cuff via the pilot balloon.  Why B/C/D wrong: Do not match the described cuff air handling."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"What is the recommended maximum air volume to inflate a low-pressure cuff with ETT?","options":["Maximum 25 cm of H2O","Maximum 50 cm of H2O","Maximum 100 cm of H2O","Maximum 5 cm of H2O"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The ideal cuff pressure should be 15 cm H2O for low pressure cuffs, maximum 25 cm of H2O; nowadays, high volume, low pressure cuffs on ETTs are being used in clinical practice.  Why B/C/D wrong: Do not match the stated maximum."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"Which type of cuffs on ETTs are being used nowadays in clinical practice?","options":["High volume, low pressure cuffs","Low volume, high pressure cuffs","Rigid non-inflatable cuffs","Metal cuffs"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Nowadays, high volume, low pressure cuffs on ETTs are being used in clinical practice.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the type described as currently used."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"What is the purpose of the bevel on the ETT?","options":["The ETT has an angle or slant known as a bevel to facilitate placement through the vocal cords and to provide improved visualisation ahead of the tip","To inflate the cuff","To measure depth","To connect to the ventilator"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The ETT has an angle or slant known as a bevel to facilitate placement through the vocal cords and provide improved visualisation; as the ETT approaches the cords, the left-facing bevel provides an optimal view.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the function of the bevel."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"As the ETT approaches the vocal cords, which orientation of the bevel provides an optimal view?","options":["The left-facing bevel","The right-facing bevel","The downward-facing bevel","The upward-facing bevel"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: As the ETT approaches the cords, the left-facing bevel provides an optimal view.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the stated optimal orientation."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"What is the purpose of Murphy's eye on the ETT?","options":["It is a safety mechanism at the distal tip known as Murphy's eye, which is another opening in the tube positioned in the distal lateral wall, so that if the distal end of the ETT should become obstructed (by the trachea or by touching the carina), gas flow can still occur via Murphy's eye","It is the cuff inflation port","It is the radiopaque marker","It is the connector to the ventilator"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: There is a safety mechanism at the distal tip known as Murphy's eye, which is another opening in the tube positioned in the distal lateral wall; if the distal end of the ETT should become obstructed by the trachea or by touching the carina, gas flow can still occur via Murphy's eye.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the function of Murphy's eye."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"Murphy's eye prevents complete obstruction of the tube primarily by:","options":["Providing an alternate lateral opening for gas flow if the distal tip is obstructed","Sealing the airway","Increasing cuff pressure","Holding adhesive tape"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Murphy's eye prevents complete obstruction of the tube by providing an alternate lateral opening for gas flow if the distal tip is obstructed.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the function of Murphy's eye."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"The connector of the ETT attaches the ETT to which equipment?","options":["The mechanical ventilator tubing or an Ambu bag","The suction unit only","The nasogastric tube","The pulse oximeter"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: ETT connectors attach the ETT to the mechanical ventilator tubing or an Ambu bag.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the stated connection."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"For adult and pediatric ETTs, the connector uses what size connector?","options":["A universal 15 mm connector","A 22 mm connector","A 10 mm connector","A variable-sized connector by age"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For adult and pediatric ETTs, use a universal 15 mm connector.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the universal connector size."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"A nurse is preparing to intubate an adult male patient. Based on the size chart and RAGEL THE HERO mnemonic, which ET tube size is most appropriate?","options":["5.0 mm","6.5 mm","8.0 mm","10.0 mm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: For an adult male, the typical/average ET tube size is 8.0 mm (chart: male adult 8.0–8.5 mm; RAGEL: male 8.0 mm).  Why A wrong: 5.0 mm is for a ≤6 year old child.  Why B wrong: 6.5 mm is in the female adult range.  Why D wrong: 10.0 mm is for a large adult."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"A patient on N2O anaesthesia develops rising cuff pressure over time. The nurse measures cuff pressure at 30 cm H2O. What is the priority action?","options":["Reduce the cuff pressure to maintain 22–25 cm H2O to prevent tracheal mucosa damage and local ischemia","Add more air to the cuff","Do nothing, as 30 cm H2O is acceptable","Increase N2O flow"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: N2O diffuses into the cuff raising pressure; cuff pressure should be maintained at 22–25 cm H2O, and pressures > 25 cm H2O risk stenosis/mucosal damage and local ischemia, so it must be reduced.  Why B wrong: Adding air worsens overinflation.  Why C wrong: 30 cm H2O exceeds the safe maximum.  Why D wrong: Increasing N2O worsens the cuff pressure rise."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"A ventilated patient suddenly develops hypoxia, high respiratory rate, decreased SpO2, audible cuff leak despite appropriate cuff pressure, and begins to vocalise. What is the priority nursing action?","options":["Recognize possible tube displacement; call for help/inform doctor, give 100% oxygen, attach capnography, and call for difficult airway trolley","Increase cuff pressure to maximum and continue","Reassure the patient and document","Lower the FiO2 to room air"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: These are features of tracheal tube displacement; nursing intervention is to call for help/inform doctor, give 100% oxygen, check/attach capnography, and call for the difficult airway trolley.  Why B wrong: Increasing cuff pressure does not address displacement and risks injury.  Why C wrong: Reassurance and documentation delay emergency care.  Why D wrong: Lowering FiO2 worsens hypoxia."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"A clinician cannot intubate and cannot oxygenate a patient despite progressing through DAS Plans A, B, and C. According to the DAS guidelines, what is the next step (Plan D)?","options":["Emergency front of neck access (cricothyroidotomy)","Reattempt laryngoscopy","Wake the patient up","Insert another supraglottic airway device"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: After CICO (can't intubate, can't oxygenate), Plan D is emergency front of neck access (cricothyroidotomy).  Why B/D wrong: These are earlier plans (A and B) already failed.  Why C wrong: Waking the patient is the Plan C success route, not applicable in a CICO emergency."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"During intubation of a patient at high risk for aspiration, which maneuver should the nurse assist with to reduce gastric aspiration?","options":["Sellick maneuver (cricoid pressure)","BURP maneuver","Head-tilt-chin-lift","Ramping position"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Sellick maneuver is performed if the patient is at risk of gastric aspiration.  Why B wrong: BURP is for laryngeal visualization, not aspiration prevention.  Why C wrong: Head-tilt-chin-lift opens the airway but does not prevent aspiration.  Why D wrong: Ramping is a positioning aid for obese/pregnant patients."},{"section":"Batch 017 · Intubation aftercare…","question":"A nurse must select an uncuffed ET tube size for a 4-year-old child using the formula (age/4) + 4. What is the correct internal diameter?","options":["4.0 mm","5.0 mm","6.0 mm","7.0 mm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Using the pediatric uncuffed formula size = (age/4) + 4 = (4/4) + 4 = 1 + 4 = 5.0 mm.  Why A wrong: 4.0 mm would be the formula constant alone without the age term.  Why C wrong: 6.0 mm would correspond to an 8-year-old.  Why D wrong: 7.0 mm exceeds the calculated pediatric size."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"A nurse anticipates airway intubation in a 2-year-old child. Why is airway intubation generally considered difficult in children?","options":["Airway assessment is usually difficult in children, especially in those under 3 years of age","Children always have a fully cooperative airway","The airway in children is wider than in adults","Pediatric airways never collapse"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states airway assessment is usually difficult in children, especially in those <3 years of age, making intubation always challenging in pediatric patients.  Why B wrong: Children are typically uncooperative, not fully cooperative.  Why C wrong: The pediatric airway is narrower, not wider.  Why D wrong: The source does not claim pediatric airways never collapse."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"A nurse is positioning an infant (smaller child) for tracheal intubation. Which position is correct?","options":["Neutral position, avoid hyperextension of the neck","Maximum hyperextension of the neck","Trendelenburg with chin tucked","Lateral recumbent position"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For smaller children, use neutral position and avoid hyperextension of the neck and avoid flexion.  Why B wrong: Hyperextension is to be avoided in smaller children.  Why C/D wrong: Not the positions described for pediatric intubation."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"When positioning an older child for tracheal intubation, the correct technique is:","options":["Sniffing position — morning air position (flexion of the neck and extension of the head)","Full neck flexion only","Neutral position avoiding all movement","Hyperextension of both neck and head"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Older children require the sniffing/\"morning air\" position, which involves flexion of the neck and extension of the head.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the described sniffing position for older children."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"During pediatric intubation, what does \"airway exchange catheter (AEC)\" or \"jet stylet\" describe?","options":["A tool that can be used where intubation or extubation is difficult","A medication for sedation","A type of laryngoscope blade","A cuff pressure manometer"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines AEC or jet stylet as something used where intubation or extubation is difficult.  Why B/C/D wrong: It is not a drug, blade, or manometer."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"To minimize intubation complications in pediatrics, intubation attempts should be limited to how long?","options":["30 seconds","10 seconds","60 seconds","90 seconds"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states intubation attempts should be limited to 30 seconds, then the patient is stabilised with BVM between attempts to limit complications.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the time limit stated."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"Which of the following are recognized factors of difficult airway intubation in pediatrics? (SATA)","options":["Hypoplastic mandible","Limited mouth opening","Congenital anomalies or syndromes","Facial asymmetry including ear abnormalities","Obstructive sleep apnea syndrome (OSA)"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: All listed items are explicitly given as factors of difficult airway intubation in paediatrics — hypoplastic mandible, limited mouth opening, congenital anomalies/syndromes, facial asymmetry including ear abnormalities, and OSA.  Why none wrong: Every option is a source-listed factor."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"Which of the following are listed as indications for tracheal intubation in the pediatric patient? (SATA)","options":["Upper airway obstruction","Respiratory failure (inadequate oxygenation or ventilation)","Shock or haemodynamic instability","Absent protective airway reflexes","Neuromuscular weakness with progressive respiratory compromise"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists upper airway obstruction, respiratory failure (inadequate oxygenation/ventilation), shock or haemodynamic instability, absent protective airway reflexes, and neuromuscular weakness with progressive respiratory compromise as indications.  Why none wrong: All are source-listed indications."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"Beyond those already listed, additional indications for pediatric tracheal intubation include:","options":["Inadequate respiratory drive and cardiac arrest (for emergency drug administration)","Mild rhinitis","Stable vital signs","Routine dental cleaning"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source adds inadequate respiratory drive and cardiac arrest (for emergency drug administration) as further indications, plus general anaesthesia where a guaranteed airway is needed.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not intubation indications."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"General anaesthesia is an indication for pediatric intubation when:","options":["A guaranteed airway is needed","The procedure is brief and superficial","The child refuses oral medication","Sedation alone is preferred"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: General anaesthesia where a guaranteed airway is needed is listed as an indication.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the stated reason."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"The COPUR scale for difficult airway in pediatrics was advocated by whom and in what year?","options":["Lane in 2005","Mallampati in 1985","Cormack in 1984","Lane in 2015"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states this scale was advocated by Lane in 2005 to assess the difficult airway in paediatrics.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the attribution given."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"What does the acronym in the \"COPUR\" pediatric airway score stand for, per the source?","options":["Colorado Pediatric Airway Score","Composite Oral Pediatric Ultrasound Rating","Cervical Oropharyngeal Pediatric Uvula Reference","Clinical Oxygenation Pediatric Urgency Rule"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states he advocated Colorado Pediatric Airway Score, abbreviated COPUR.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the meaning of COPUR given."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"In the COPUR scale, how many parameters are graded and each is scored by how many points?","options":["5 parameters, each scored by 4 points","4 parameters, each scored by 5 points","6 parameters, each scored by 3 points","10 parameters, each scored by 1 point"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: COPUR scale has 5 parameters, each scored by 4 points.  Why B/C/D wrong: Do not match the source values."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"According to the source, scores above what value in the COPUR scale predict a difficult intubation?","options":["10 points","5 points","7 points","16 points"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Scores above 10 predict difficult intubation per the source.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the threshold stated."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"Per the COPUR Scale for Rapid Airway Assessment table, which parameter corresponds to the letter \"C\"?","options":["Chin","Opening of the mouth","Uvula","Range"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In the COPUR table, C = Chin.  Why B wrong: O = Opening of the mouth.  Why C wrong: U = Uvula (mouth, tongue out).  Why D wrong: R = Range (estimate range of motion looking up and down)."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"In the COPUR Scale table, the parameter \"P\" represents:","options":["Previous intubation of OSA","Chin","Uvula","Range"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: P = Previous intubation of OSA in the COPUR table.  Why B/C/D wrong: These map to C, U, and R respectively."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"Using the COPUR prediction points, a score of 5–7 indicates:","options":["Easy, normal intubation","Lateral pressure may help","Difficult intubation; fibreoptic may be preferred","Dangerous airway with potential tracheostomy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A COPUR score of 5–7 = easy, normal intubation.  Why B wrong: 8–10 = lateral pressure may help.  Why C wrong: 11–14 = difficult intubation, fibreoptic or other advanced technique should be preferred.  Why D wrong: 15–16 = dangerous airway, consider awake intubation/potential tracheostomy."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"A pediatric COPUR prediction score of 15–16 indicates which interpretation?","options":["Dangerous airway; consider awake intubation, potential tracheostomy","Easy, normal intubation","Lateral pressure may help","Difficult intubation, fibreoptic preferred"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: 15–16 = dangerous airway, consider awake intubation, potential tracheostomy.  Why B wrong: 5–7 = easy/normal.  Why C wrong: 8–10 = lateral pressure may help.  Why D wrong: 11–14 = difficult, fibreoptic preferred."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"According to the Laryngoscope blade for Pediatrics table, which blade is recommended for a pre-term child weighing 0–3 kg?","options":["Miller-0","Miller-0.5","Miller-1","Macintosh-2"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: 0–3 kg (Pre-term) → Miller-0.  Why B wrong: 3–5 kg (Neonate) → Miller-0.5.  Why C wrong: 5–12 kg (Infant) → Miller-1.  Why D wrong: 12–20 kg (Toddler) → Macintosh-2."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"Which laryngoscope blade is recommended for a school-age child (>30 kg)?","options":["Macintosh-3, Miller-3","Miller-0","Macintosh-2","Macintosh-3, Miller-2 (pre-school)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: School age (>30 kg) → Macintosh-3, Miller-3.  Why B wrong: Miller-0 is for pre-term.  Why C wrong: Macintosh-2 is for toddler.  Why D wrong: Macintosh-3, Miller-2 is the pre-school (20–30 kg) blade."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"Premedication options in pediatrics prior to airway management include which anticholinergic to reduce secretions?","options":["Atropine 0.02 mg/kg or glycopyrrolate 0.05 mg/kg orally 3 hr prior","Midazolam IV","Ketamine 3–5 mg/kg","Pancuronium 90–40 mg/kg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Atropine (0.02 mg/kg) or glycopyrrolate (0.05 mg/kg) given orally ~3 hr prior is used to anaesticologue effects to reduce secretions (anticholinergic).  Why B wrong: Midazolam is the sedative, not the anticholinergic.  Why C wrong: Ketamine is the analgesic.  Why D wrong: Pancuronium is a paralytic, not an anticholinergic for secretions."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"Which agents are listed as sedatives for pediatric premedication before airway management?","options":["Midazolam 0.3–0.5 mg/kg orally and fentanyl lollipops under direct supervision","Atropine and glycopyrrolate","Pancuronium and vecuronium","Ketamine only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Sedatives listed are midazolam (0.3–0.5 mg/kg orally) and fentanyl lollipops under direct supervision, generally in patients older than 6 months.  Why B wrong: These are anticholinergics.  Why C wrong: These are paralytics.  Why D wrong: Ketamine is the analgesic."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"According to the premedication list, the paralytic agent and analgesic agent (with doses) are:","options":["Pancuronium 90–40 mg/kg (paralytic); Ketamine 3–5 mg/kg orally or IM (analgesic)","Atropine; Glycopyrrolate","Midazolam; Fentanyl","Vecuronium; Morphine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Source lists Pancuronium 90–40 mg/kg as paralytic and Ketamine 3–5 mg/kg orally or IM as analgesia.  Why B wrong: These are anticholinergics.  Why C wrong: These are sedatives.  Why D wrong: Not the agents listed in the source."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"Which of the following are listed as possible causes of bronchial intubation in children? (SATA)","options":["Passage of an ETT beyond the carina","We generally use uncuffed ETT in paediatric population (chance of moving into the bronchus)","Absolute length of the trachea is small in children","All the formulae suggested for corrected length determination as estimates only","RAE tubes are often too long and may be inserted further down"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: All five are explicitly listed possible/contributing causes of bronchial intubation in children — passage of ETT beyond the carina, use of uncuffed ETT with chance of bronchial migration, the small absolute tracheal length, the formulae being only estimates, and RAE tubes being too long.  Why none wrong: Every option is source-listed."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"When are H2 blockers and/or metoclopramide indicated for the pediatric patient prior to airway management?","options":["For patients at risk of gastric aspiration","For all patients routinely","Only in neonates under 3 kg","Only after extubation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states H2 blockers and/or metoclopramide for patients at risk of gastric aspiration.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the indication described."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"A \"Must Know\" fact about pediatric intubation states that the tip of a correctly positioned tracheal tube should be:","options":["In the mid-trachea, between the collar bones on an anteroposterior chest radiograph","At the level of the carina","In the main-stem bronchus","Above the vocal cords"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the tip of a correctly positioned tracheal tube will be in the mid-trachea, between the collar bones on an AP chest radiograph.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are incorrect tip positions."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"Why can a cuffed ETT reduce the number of endotracheal intubation attempts in pediatrics?","options":["If cuff pressure can be maintained within a safe range, the risk of airway damage may not increase compared to an endotracheal tube without a cuff","Because cuffs always cause airway damage","Because cuffed tubes are never used in children","Because cuffs replace the need for a laryngoscope"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states a cuffed tube can reduce the number of intubation attempts, and if cuff pressure is maintained in a safe range the risk of airway damage may not increase compared with an uncuffed tube.  Why B wrong: Damage risk does not necessarily increase if pressure is controlled.  Why C wrong: Cuffed tubes are used in pediatrics per source guidelines.  Why D wrong: Cuffs do not replace laryngoscopy."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"Guidelines suggest cuffed ETTs with high-volume low-pressure cuffs in pediatrics, with the cuff sealing the airway at what pressure?","options":["<15 cm H2O, producing a complete seal without increases in airway complications",">40 cm H2O","60–80 cm H2O","0 cm H2O"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states cuffed ETTs with high-volume low-pressure cuffs sealing at <15 cm H2O produce a complete seal without increases in airway complications.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the safe cuff pressure described."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"The source advises that cuffed ETTs are not routinely used by pediatric anesthesiologists because most of these tubes are:","options":["Not properly designed for ongoing research, raising concerns of necrosis caused by cuff pressure at the level of the cricoid cartilage","Too short for any pediatric patient","Always made of metal","Reusable across many patients"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states most cuffed tubes are not properly designed (subject to ongoing research) and there are concerns of necrosis caused by cuff pressure at the level of the cricoid cartilage — the narrowest point of the paediatric upper airway.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the stated reasons."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"What is the narrowest point of the pediatric upper airway, per the source?","options":["The cricoid cartilage","The glottis","The carina","The epiglottis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source identifies the cricoid cartilage as the narrowest point of the paediatric upper airway.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not identified as the narrowest point."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"To avoid ETT tip being placed too close to the carina, in neonates the tube should be inserted in the main bronchus weighing 1, 2, 3 and 4 kg at depths of approximately:","options":["7, 8, 9 and 10 cm respectively","5, 6, 7 and 8 cm respectively","10, 12, 14, 16 cm respectively","3, 4, 5, 6 cm respectively"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to avoid the tip too close to the carina, the main bronchus depth at 1, 2, 3, 4 kg is about 7, 8, 9, 10 cm respectively.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the depths stated."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"A simple rule of thumb for ETT insertion depth in newborns weighing 1–3 kg is the weight in kg plus:","options":["6 cm","3 cm","9 cm","12 cm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source gives the rule \"weight (kg) + 6 = depth\" for newborns of 1–3 kg.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the additive value stated."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"Which drugs are noted as commonly used for premedication for intubation according to this section?","options":["Fentanyl and benzodiazepine (midazolam)","Atropine and pancuronium only","Ketamine and propofol only","Morphine and diazepam only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source notes fentanyl and benzodiazepine (midazolam) are commonly used for premedication for intubation.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the combination stated here."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"For an uncuffed ETT in children, which size selection formula is given?","options":["ID (mm) = (age/4) + 4 [SDPG idea]","ID (mm) = (age/4) + 4.5","ID (mm) = (age/4) + 3.5","ID (mm) = (age/2) + 4"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For uncuffed ETT, Cole's age-based formula is ID (mm) = (age/4) + 4.  Why B wrong: (age/4) + 4.5 is the example variant; cuffed formula differs.  Why C wrong: Cuffed formula is (age/4) + 3.5.  Why D wrong: Not a stated formula."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"For a cuffed ETT in children, Duracher's age-based formula for size is:","options":["ID (mm) = (age/4) + 3.5","ID (mm) = (age/4) + 4","ID (mm) = (age/4) + 5","ID (mm) = (age/2) + 3.5"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For cuffed ETT, Duracher's formula = (age/4) + 3.5.  Why B wrong: That is the uncuffed Cole's formula.  Why C/D wrong: Not stated formulae."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"A nurse must obtain the appropriate size cuffed ETT for a 4-year-old child. Using the worked example, the cuffed ETT size for a child aged 4 is calculated as:","options":["(4/4) + 4 = 5 ID mm; subtract 0.5 from 5, now 4.5 cuffed ETT is required","(4/4) + 5 = 6 ID mm","(4/2) + 4 = 6 ID mm","(4/4) + 3 = 4 ID mm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The example computes (4/4) + 4 = 5, then subtracts 0.5 to give a 4.5 cuffed ETT for a 4-year-old.  Why B/C/D wrong: Do not match the worked example."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"The formula to estimate the depth of insertion of an ETT in children is:","options":["Oral ETT = 12 + (age/2) cm or multiply the inside diameter of the tube by 3","Oral ETT = age only","Oral ETT = weight + 6","Oral ETT = 12 + (age x 2)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source gives oral ETT depth = 12 + (age/2) cm, or 3 × the internal diameter of the tube.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the stated depth formula."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"The nasal ETT insertion depth is estimated using which measurement landmark?","options":["Nasal septum to the tragus of the ear plus 1 cm (NTL measurement)","Sternal notch to xiphoid","Earlobe to nose tip only","Crown to heel length"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: NTL (naso-tragus length) measurement: nasal septum to the tragus of the ear plus 1 cm is used for nasal ETT depth.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the landmark described."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"Per the Uncuffed Endotracheal Tube Sizes in Newborns table, an infant of gestational age <28 weeks (weight <1000 g / <1 kg) requires which ETT internal diameter?","options":["2.5 mm","3.0 mm","3.5 mm","4.0 mm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: <28 weeks, <1000 g (<1 kg) → ETT size 2.5 mm.  Why B wrong: 28–36 weeks, 1000–2000 g (1–2 kg) → 3.0 mm.  Why C wrong: 34–38 weeks, 2000–3000 g (2–3 kg) → 3.5 mm.  Why D wrong: >38 weeks, >3000 g (>3 kg) → 3.5–4.0 mm."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"Per the newborn ETT table, an infant >38 weeks gestation weighing >3000 g (>3 kg) requires an ETT internal diameter of:","options":["3.5–4.0 mm","2.5 mm","3.0 mm","5.0 mm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: >38 weeks, >3000 g (>3 kg) → 3.5–4.0 mm.  Why B wrong: 2.5 mm is for <28 weeks.  Why C wrong: 3.0 mm is for 28–36 weeks.  Why D wrong: Not a listed value."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"Per the newborn ETT table, the depth of insertion for a 28–36 week, 1000–2000 g (1–2 kg) neonate is approximately:","options":["7–8 cm","6–7 cm","8 cm","9–10 cm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: 28–36 weeks (1–2 kg) → depth of insertion 7–8 cm.  Why B wrong: <28 weeks (<1 kg) → 6–7 cm.  Why C wrong: 34–38 weeks (2–3 kg) → 8 cm.  Why D wrong: >38 weeks (>3 kg) → 9–10 cm."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"Which of the following are listed advantages of the un-cuffed endotracheal tube (UETT)? (SATA)","options":["Larger internal diameter for its size","Less resistance to airflow","Lower breathing load","Reduced blockage by secretions and easy suctioning","Minimal mucosal pressure / prevents trauma to sub-glottic region"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists all of these as advantages of the un-cuffed ETT — larger internal diameter, less resistance to airflow, lower breathing load, reduced blockage by secretions/easy suctioning, minimal mucosal pressure, and prevention of trauma to the sub-glottic region.  Why none wrong: All are source-listed advantages."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"Which of the following are listed disadvantages of the un-cuffed endotracheal tube? (SATA)","options":["Several attempts of laryngoscopes and intubations / airway injury","Oversized tubes, excessive pressure on cricoid mucosa","Undersized tubes, more airway trauma / leakage of air","Incorrect respiratory monitoring; increased risk of aspiration; environmental pollution","Reduced risk of all complications"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists several attempts at laryngoscopes/intubations causing airway injury, oversized tubes (excessive cricoid mucosa pressure), undersized tubes (more airway trauma, air leakage), incorrect respiratory monitoring, increased aspiration risk, and environmental pollution as disadvantages.  Why E wrong: Reduced risk of all complications is not listed; uncuffed tubes carry several disadvantages."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"Which of the following are listed advantages of the cuffed endotracheal tube (CETT)? (SATA)","options":["Smaller external diameter for its size","Less pressure on cricoid mucosa","Improved ventilation and respiratory monitoring","Fewer attempts of laryngoscopes and intubations","Reduced risk of aspiration and reduced air pollution"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: All listed are CETT advantages — smaller external diameter, less cricoid mucosa pressure, improved ventilation/monitoring, fewer attempts, reduced aspiration risk, and reduced air pollution.  Why none wrong: All are source-listed."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"Which of the following are listed disadvantages of the cuffed endotracheal tube? (SATA)","options":["Smaller internal diameter for its size","Higher resistance to airflow","Increased blockage by secretions and difficult suctioning","Airway injury — tracheal rupture and mucosal ischemia","Larger lumen with no airway injury"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: CETT disadvantages listed are smaller internal diameter, higher resistance to airflow, increased blockage by secretions/difficult suctioning, and airway injury (tracheal rupture, mucosal ischemia).  Why E wrong: A larger lumen with no airway injury is not a CETT disadvantage and contradicts the source."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"The Reinforced ET tube is also called a flexo-metallic ET tube. Its primary function is to:","options":["Prevent kinking of the ET tube because kinking will cause airway resistance","Provide single lung ventilation","Be preformed for cleft lip/cleft palate surgery","Replace the laryngoscope"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Reinforced (flexo-metallic) ET tube prevents kinking of the ET tube, because kinking would cause airway resistance.  Why B wrong: That is the Double-lumen tube function.  Why C wrong: That is the RAE tube function.  Why D wrong: It does not replace the laryngoscope."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"The Reinforced ET tube is specifically used in which type of surgeries?","options":["Head and neck surgeries","Abdominal surgeries only","Cardiac surgeries only","Orthopedic limb surgeries"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Reinforced ET tube is used in head and neck surgeries.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the indicated surgery type."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"The RAE tube is described as:","options":["Preflued/preformed for cleft lip/cleft palate surgery and does not obstruct the surgeon's view","A flexo-metallic anti-kinking tube","A tube for single lung ventilation","A supraglottic device"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The RAE tube is preformed for cleft lip/cleft palate surgery and does not obstruct the surgeon's view.  Why B wrong: That describes the Reinforced ET tube.  Why C wrong: That is the Double-lumen tube.  Why D wrong: RAE is an ET tube, not a supraglottic device."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"The Double lumen ET tube is used for: (SATA)","options":["Single lung ventilation","Pneumonectomy or lobectomy","Cleft palate surgery","Preventing tube kinking","Supraglottic airway maintenance"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The Double lumen ET tube is used for single lung ventilation and in pneumonectomy or lobectomy.  Why C wrong: That is the RAE tube indication.  Why D wrong: That is the Reinforced tube function.  Why E wrong: Supraglottic airway maintenance is the LMA role."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"Blind Insertion Airway Devices (BIADs) are airway management devices inserted blindly without visualization of the vocal cords. They reduce the risk of:","options":["Aspiration, by forming a placement seal that ensures an open airway between a patient's lungs and the outside atmosphere","Tube kinking only","Cleft palate exposure","Single lung collapse"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: BIADs are inserted blindly to form a seal ensuring an open airway between the lungs and outside atmosphere, reducing the risk of aspiration.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the described purpose of BIADs."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"In the pre-hospital setting, airway management was traditionally played by intubation, but placement of a BIAD is associated with what outcome?","options":["Less frequent failure rates","Higher infection rates","Greater risk of tube kinking","Mandatory surgical airway"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states BIAD placement in the pre-hospital setting has less frequent failure rates.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not stated outcomes."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"Which of the following are examples of BIADs listed in the source? (SATA)","options":["Combitube","Laryngeal tube","Laryngeal mask airway (LMA)","NPA","OPA"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists Combitube, Laryngeal tube, Laryngeal mask airway (LMA), NPA, and OPA as examples of BIAD.  Why none wrong: All are source-listed examples."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"Which of the following are indications for a BIAD? (SATA)","options":["When a definitive/advanced airway is required and intubation is unsuccessful or unrealistic","An unresponsive patient who is hypoxic despite adequate bag-valve-mask ventilation","A patient with trauma to the face/neck who is experiencing confirmed or suspected swelling","Penetrating injury to the head or neck","More routine injuries with suspected long transport times"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: All listed are BIAD indications — definitive airway required with failed/unrealistic intubation, hypoxic unresponsive patient despite adequate ventilation, face/neck trauma with swelling, penetrating head/neck injury, and routine injuries with suspected long transport times.  Why none wrong: All are source-listed indications."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"According to the source, the contraindications to BIAD are focused on causing what?","options":["Vomiting, bleeding, or potentially perforating the larynx/esophagus","Pulmonary embolism","Hypertension only","Bradycardia only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: BIAD contraindications are focused on causing vomiting, bleeding, or potentially perforating the larynx/esophagus.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the basis of the contraindications."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"Which of the following are listed contraindications to BIAD? (SATA)","options":["An intact gag reflex","Known esophageal disease (cancer, varices, stricture)","Laryngectomy with a stoma","Penetrating eye injury or airway burns","Routine elective procedure with normal airway"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists intact gag reflex, known esophageal disease (cancer, varices, stricture), laryngectomy with a stoma, and penetrating eye injury/airway burns as contraindications.  Why E wrong: A routine elective procedure with a normal airway is not listed as a contraindication to BIAD."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"Which of the following are listed complications of BIAD? (SATA)","options":["Inflation of the stomach leading to regurgitation and aspiration","Gastric contents compression of vascular structures in the neck","Trauma to the airway/esophagus / nerve injury to the laryngeal nerves that control the vocal cords","Hypotension due to positive pressure ventilation and/or vagus nerve stimulation","Permanent cure of the underlying disease"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists stomach inflation causing regurgitation/aspiration, gastric content compression of vascular neck structures, airway/esophageal trauma and laryngeal nerve injury, and hypotension from positive-pressure ventilation/vagal stimulation as complications.  Why E wrong: A \"permanent cure\" is not a complication and is not in the source."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"During the BIAD insertion procedure, the patient should first be preoxygenated to what level?","options":["100% O2 for at least 30 seconds","50% O2 for 10 minutes","Room air only","No preoxygenation needed"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Step one is to ensure the patient is preoxygenated with 100% O2 for at least 30 seconds.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the stated preoxygenation requirement."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"During BIAD placement, before insertion the equipment should be assembled and the device checked to ensure it is in working order. Which steps are part of the procedure? (SATA)","options":["Assemble equipment: suctioning, lubricant, oxygen, BVM, and at least two full correctly-sized BIAD devices","Lubricate the distal tip of the BIAD and open the patient's airway","Remove any adjunct (OPA, NPA) present and suction airway if any secretions","Insert the BIAD at a 90° angle pointing to the right or left corner of the mouth, with the distal tip sliding back over the patient's tongue","Rotate the BIAD to be in line with the patient's airway"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: All are procedural steps for usage of BIADs — assemble equipment, lubricate distal tip and open airway, remove adjuncts and suction, insert at 90° pointing to corner of mouth, and rotate to align with the airway.  Why none wrong: All are source-listed steps."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"When using a King LTD device in the Combitube, the cuffs will inflate to the proper pressure once which component is used?","options":["The included syringe (inflate the pilot balloon with air from the included syringe)","A cuff pressure manometer only","A nasogastric tube","The BVM directly"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to inflate the pilot balloon with air from the included syringe to the manufacturer-indicated pressure.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the device used in this inflation step."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"After BIAD insertion, to confirm correct placement the device is connected to capnography and the BVM, and the chest is auscultated. If breath sounds are heard over the abdomen, the nurse should:","options":["Move up to auscultate the L lung first (right main-stem placement is rare but possible)","Immediately remove the device","Inflate the cuff further","Disconnect capnography"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states if breath sounds are heard in the abdomen, move up to auscultate the L lung first; right main-stem placement is rare but possible.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the described corrective action."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"During BIAD placement confirmation, if breath sounds are heard in the abdomen, the nurse should:","options":["Deflate the front (pilot) balloon and withdraw the BIAD a few centimeters, remembering to re-inflate the balloon when finished","Leave the balloon inflated and advance the device","Remove the device entirely without re-inflation","Increase ventilation pressure"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source directs deflating the front (pilot) balloon and withdrawing the BIAD a few centimeters, then re-inflating the balloon when finished.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the described maneuver."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"During confirmation of BIAD placement, if breath sounds are absent in the L lung but present in the R lung, the nurse should:","options":["Perform the same method described for abdominal breath sounds and reassess until equal breath sounds are auscultated in the L and R lung","Stop ventilation entirely","Extubate immediately","Inject more air into the cuff"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: If no breath sounds are auscultated in the L lung but present in the R lung, perform the same withdraw method and reassess until equal breath sounds are heard bilaterally.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the described corrective action."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"After confirming correct BIAD placement, the nurse should:","options":["Secure the device and monitor SpO2 and EtCO2 for changes","Remove the device immediately","Stop monitoring oxygen saturation","Discontinue capnography"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The final step is to secure the device and monitor SpO2 and EtCO2 for changes.  Why B/C/D wrong: Contradict the described monitoring/securing step."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"The Laryngeal Mask Airway (LMA) is best classified as a:","options":["Supraglottic airway device, also known as a laryngeal mask","Subglottic airway device","Endotracheal tube","Cricothyrotomy device"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the LMA is a type of supraglottic airway device, also known as a laryngeal mask.  Why B/C/D wrong: It is not subglottic, an ETT, or a surgical airway device."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"Which of the following are uses/indications of the LMA? (SATA)","options":["Used to keep a patient's airway open or patent during anaesthesia or while they are unconscious","Most commonly used by anaesthetists to channel oxygen or inhalational anaesthetic to the lungs during surgery and in the pre-hospital setting for unconscious patients","May stimulate the gag reflex","Should not be used in a conscious or awake patient","Used to permanently cure airway obstruction"],"correct":"A,B,D","rationale":"Why correct: The LMA keeps the airway patent during anaesthesia/unconsciousness, is used by anaesthetists to channel oxygen/inhalational anaesthetic during surgery and in pre-hospital settings for unconscious patients, and should NOT be used in a conscious or awake patient.  Why C wrong: The source notes the LMA may stimulate the gag reflex, which is the reason it should not be used in conscious patients — it is a caution, not a use/indication.  Why E wrong: The LMA does not permanently cure airway obstruction."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"Why should the LMA NOT be used in a conscious or awake patient?","options":["Because it may stimulate the gag reflex","Because it cures the disease","Because it always causes hypotension","Because it prevents oxygenation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the LMA may stimulate the gag reflex and therefore should not be used in a conscious or awake patient.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the stated rationale."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"Which of the following are listed complications of the LMA? (SATA)","options":["Laryngospasm","Nausea, vomiting","Aspiration and coughing","Permanent vocal cord paralysis"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists laryngospasm, nausea/vomiting, and aspiration and coughing as LMA complications.  Why D wrong: Permanent vocal cord paralysis is not a listed LMA complication."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"Which of the following are listed contraindications of the LMA? (SATA)","options":["Poor pulmonary compliance","High airway resistance","Pharyngeal pathology","Risk for aspiration and/or airway obstruction below the larynx","Need for routine surgical anaesthesia"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists poor pulmonary compliance, high airway resistance, pharyngeal pathology, and risk for aspiration and/or airway obstruction below the larynx as LMA contraindications.  Why E wrong: Routine surgical anaesthesia is a use, not a contraindication."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"During the step-by-step technique of LMA insertion, before insertion the cuff should be:","options":["Inflated and deflated to ensure the cuff is not folded, then pressed firmly on the flat surface","Left fully inflated at all times","Removed entirely","Filled with saline"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Prior to insertion, the cuff should be inflated and deflated to ensure the cuff is not folded, pressing firmly on the flat surface.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the described preparation step."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"During LMA insertion technique, the lubricant should be applied to:","options":["Both sides of the LMA cuff with a water-soluble lubricant","Only the inner surface","The patient's tongue only","The laryngoscope blade only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Lubricate both sides of the LMA cuff with a water-soluble lubricant.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the described lubrication step."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"When positioning the patient for LMA insertion, the correct position is:","options":["Sniffing position, if cervical injury is not suspected; if cervical injury is suspected, jaw thrust may be performed to facilitate passage","Trendelenburg position always","Prone position","Lateral position with neck flexed"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Place the patient in the sniffing position if cervical injury is not suspected; if cervical injury is suspected, a jaw thrust may be performed to facilitate passage.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the described positioning."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"During LMA insertion, the device is inserted by:","options":["Inserting the LMA behind the tongue with backward pressure using the index finger, pressing the device against the hard palate until it is completely inserted","Inserting it with the cuff fully inflated","Twisting it 180 degrees","Pulling the tongue forward with forceps"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The LMA is inserted behind the tongue with backward pressure using the index finger, pressing against the hard palate until completely inserted.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the described insertion technique."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"After the LMA is inserted, the other hand is used to:","options":["Push the LMA until some resistance is felt and it can go no further","Pull the LMA back out immediately","Hyperextend the neck forcefully","Apply continuous suction"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The other hand pushes the LMA until some resistance is felt and it can go no further.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the described maneuver."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"During LMA insertion, the cuff is inflated with approximately how much air for cuff number three, four, and five?","options":["Number three 20 ml air, number four 30 ml air, number five 40 ml air, with the volume adjusted to optimize the seal and minimize air leak","5 ml, 10 ml, 15 ml respectively","50 ml, 60 ml, 70 ml respectively","No air at all"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the cuff is inflated with approximately 20 ml air for number three, 30 ml for number four, and 40 ml for number five, adjusting the volume to optimize the seal and minimize air leak.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the stated inflation volumes."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"To confirm correct LMA placement, the nurse should:","options":["Attach a bag mask and deliver breaths, assessing breath sounds and using continuous capnography or, at a minimum, a CO2 detector device","Rely on visual inspection alone","Use a chest x-ray exclusively","Wait 24 hours before confirming"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Correct placement is confirmed by attaching a bag mask and delivering breaths, assessing breath sounds, and using continuous capnography or at minimum a CO2 detector device.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the described confirmation method."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"Which of the following are types of LMA listed in the source? (SATA)","options":["LMA Classic","LMA Unique","LMA Combitube","LMA Magill"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists LMA Classic and LMA Unique as types of LMA.  Why C wrong: Combitube is a separate BIAD, not an LMA type.  Why D wrong: Magill refers to forceps, not an LMA type."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"The LMA Classic is described as:","options":["A large shaped inflatable silicon material cuff","Made by polyvinyl chloride and single usage","A double lumen device","A metallic reinforced device"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The LMA Classic is a large shaped inflatable silicon material cuff.  Why B wrong: That describes the LMA Unique (polyvinyl chloride, single usage).  Why C/D wrong: Not how the LMA Classic is described."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"What is the stated advantage of the LMA Classic?","options":["Least invasive, used in difficult airway and emergency situations","It can always prevent aspiration","It is the cheapest disposable option","It is ideal for obese and pregnant women"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The advantage of the LMA Classic is that it is least invasive and used in difficult airway and emergency situations.  Why B wrong: It cannot prevent aspiration (that is a disadvantage).  Why C wrong: The Classic is reusable silicon, not the cheapest disposable.  Why D wrong: It is not used in obese patients and pregnant women (a disadvantage)."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"Which of the following are stated disadvantages of the LMA Classic? (SATA)","options":["Cannot prevent aspiration","Not used in obese patients and pregnant women","Made of metal","Used for single lung ventilation"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The LMA Classic disadvantages are that it cannot prevent aspiration and is not used in obese patients and pregnant women.  Why C wrong: It is made of silicon, not metal.  Why D wrong: Single lung ventilation is a double-lumen tube use, not an LMA Classic feature."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"The LMA Unique is described as:","options":["Made by polyvinyl chloride and single usage","A large silicon reusable cuff","A double lumen tube","A flexo-metallic tube"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The LMA Unique is made by polyvinyl chloride and is single usage.  Why B wrong: That describes the LMA Classic.  Why C/D wrong: Not how the LMA Unique is described."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"A nurse is using a cuff pressure manometer for an ET tube. For an ET tube, cuff pressure should be maintained at:","options":["15–25 mm Hg or 20–30 cm H2O to prevent exhaust air or overinflation","40–60 mm Hg","5–10 mm Hg","0 mm Hg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For an ET tube, cuff pressure should be maintained at 15–25 mm Hg or 20–30 cm H2O to prevent exhaust air or overinflation.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the recommended cuff pressure range."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"What is the function of the cuff pressure manometer?","options":["A true handheld manometer measures the ET tube and tracheostomy cuff pressure","It delivers anaesthetic gas","It measures arterial blood pressure","It records the ECG"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A true handheld manometer measures the ET tube and tracheostomy cuff pressure.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the function of a cuff pressure manometer."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"Magill's forceps are described as:","options":["Angled forceps used to guide a tracheal tube into the larynx or a nasogastric tube into the esophagus under direct vision","A type of laryngeal mask","A double-lumen ventilation tube","A cuff inflation syringe"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Magill forceps are angled forceps used to guide a tracheal tube into the larynx or a nasogastric tube into the esophagus under direct vision.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not what Magill's forceps are."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"In an emergency, Magill's forceps may be used to:","options":["Direct insert ET tube into the trachea when the ET tube is dislodged","Inflate the LMA cuff","Measure cuff pressure","Suction the oropharynx"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In an emergency condition, Magill's forceps are used to direct-insert the ET tube into the trachea when the ET tube is dislodged.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not a Magill's forceps emergency use."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"Per the \"Should Remember\" box, which device is said to be the most common BIAD in the prehospital setting?","options":["The Combitube","The King LT airway","The i-Gel supraglottic device","The Bullard laryngoscope"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the Combitube is the most common BIAD in the prehospital setting, although the more common ones now are the King LT airway and the i-Gel supraglottic airway device, both of which are dual lumen devices.  Why B/C wrong: King LT and i-Gel are noted as more common now, but the box names the Combitube as the most common BIAD historically.  Why D wrong: The Bullard laryngoscope is not a BIAD."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"Per the \"Should Remember\" box, what is a key difference between the Combitube and the King LT?","options":["The Combitube uses two inflation cuffs, whereas the King LT uses only one inflation cuff","The Combitube uses one cuff, the King LT uses two","Neither has any inflation cuff","Both are single-lumen tubes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source notes one of the key differences is that the Combitube has two cuffs, whereas the King LT only has one inflation cuff.  Why B wrong: It is the reverse of what is stated.  Why C/D wrong: Contradict the source."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"Per the \"Should Remember\" box, which device is an ET tube that goes over the larynx instead of into it, and is most common compared with the King LT?","options":["The LMA","The Combitube","The Bullard laryngoscope","The Magill forceps"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The box states the LMA is an ET tube (supraglottic) that goes over the larynx instead of into it, and these are more common than the King LT or the Combitube.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not described this way in the box."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"Per the \"Should Remember\" box, how does the i-Gel device protect the airway during ventilation?","options":["The i-Gel has taken the place of the LMA in the prehospital setting and is also placed blindly which seals the airway, with the patient's body temperature warming the gel-like distal end of the device to provide a custom fit to protect the airway during ventilation","By inflating two large cuffs with a syringe","By using a metal spiral","By advancing into the bronchus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The box states the i-Gel has taken the place of the LMA in the prehospital setting, is placed blindly to seal the airway, and the patient's body temperature warms the gel-like distal end to provide a custom fit protecting the airway during ventilation.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not how the i-Gel is described."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"Per the \"Should Remember\" box, the Combitube is contraindicated in children of what age or shorter?","options":["16 years or under 5 feet (4 ft)","2 years or under 3 feet","18 years or under 6 feet","There is no contraindication by age or height"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The box states the Combitube is contraindicated in children 16 years or under 5 feet (4 ft) tall.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the stated contraindication threshold."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"The \"Airway Management Devices for IBQs\" figure page (p.248) labels which of the following devices? (SATA)","options":["LMA Classic (local)","Pharyngeal sealer (i-Gel) and Proseal LMA","Esophageal combitube (cuffed pharyngeal sealer)","Bullard Laryngoscope and Magill's Forceps","i-Gel LMA and Cuff pressure manometer"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The figure page labels the LMA Classic, pharyngeal sealer/i-Gel, Proseal LMA, esophageal Combitube (cuffed pharyngeal sealer), Bullard laryngoscope, Magill's forceps, i-Gel LMA, and cuff pressure manometer.  Why none wrong: All are devices shown in the figure set."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"A pediatric nurse calculates the uncuffed ETT internal diameter for an 8-year-old using Cole's formula. The correct internal diameter is:","options":["6 mm [(8/4) + 4 = 6]","4 mm","5.5 mm","8 mm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cole's uncuffed formula ID (mm) = (age/4) + 4 → (8/4) + 4 = 2 + 4 = 6 mm.  Why B/C/D wrong: Incorrect application of the formula."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"A nurse estimates the oral ETT depth of insertion for a 6-year-old child. Using the depth formula, the result is:","options":["15 cm [12 + (6/2) = 15]","18 cm","12 cm","9 cm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Oral ETT depth = 12 + (age/2) = 12 + (6/2) = 12 + 3 = 15 cm.  Why B/C/D wrong: Incorrect application of the depth formula."},{"section":"Batch 018 · Pediatric airway intubation…","question":"A nurse is selecting a laryngoscope blade for a 9 kg infant. Which blade is appropriate per the pediatric table?","options":["Miller-1 (5–12 kg, Infant)","Miller-0 (Pre-term)","Macintosh-2 (Toddler)","Macintosh-3 (School age)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A 9 kg infant falls in the 5–12 kg (Infant) range → Miller-1.  Why B wrong: Miller-0 is for 0–3 kg pre-term.  Why C wrong: Macintosh-2 is for the 12–20 kg toddler range.  Why D wrong: Macintosh-3 is for school age (>30 kg)."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"Which airway adjunct is illustrated as the \"Berman airway,\" a rigid device inserted to keep the tongue from obstructing the oropharynx?","options":["Nasopharyngeal airway","Oropharyngeal (Berman) airway","Laryngeal mask airway","Endotracheal tube"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Berman airway is depicted as a type of oropharyngeal airway used to prevent the tongue from obstructing the oropharynx.  Why A wrong: A nasopharyngeal airway is inserted through the nose, not labeled Berman.  Why C wrong: An LMA sits over the laryngeal inlet, a different device.  Why D wrong: An ETT passes through the vocal cords into the trachea."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"The figure labeled \"Naso-pharyngeal airway\" represents a device inserted through which route?","options":["Through the mouth","Through the nose","Directly into the trachea via stoma","Over the laryngeal inlet"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A nasopharyngeal airway is, by definition and name, inserted through the nose into the pharynx.  Why A wrong: That describes an oropharyngeal airway.  Why C wrong: That describes a tracheostomy tube.  Why D wrong: That describes a laryngeal mask airway."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"Which of the following figures depicts a McGrath enhanced airway, abbreviated as MLA?","options":["McGrath enhanced airway (MLA)","Guedel's airway","William's airway","Wolet's airway"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page labels the McGrath enhanced airway with the abbreviation MLA.  Why B wrong: Guedel's airway is a separate labeled oropharyngeal airway.  Why C wrong: William's airway is a different intubating airway.  Why D wrong: Wolet's airway is a separate device shown on the page."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"Guedel's airway is best classified as which type of airway device?","options":["Oropharyngeal airway","Endotracheal tube","Tracheostomy tube","Supraglottic mask only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Guedel's airway is a classic oropharyngeal airway used to maintain a patent oral airway.  Why B wrong: An ETT is inserted into the trachea, not an oral airway adjunct.  Why C wrong: A tracheostomy tube is placed via a surgical stoma.  Why D wrong: While supraglottic devices exist, Guedel's is specifically an oropharyngeal airway."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"The figure labeled \"SALT (supraglottic airway laryngopharyngeal tube)\" refers to a device that functions at which anatomical level?","options":["Above the glottis (supraglottic)","Below the carina","Within the nasal cavity only","At the cricoid cartilage stoma"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: SALT stands for supraglottic airway laryngopharyngeal tube, indicating it sits above the glottis.  Why B wrong: It is not positioned below the carina.  Why C wrong: It is not a nasal-only device.  Why D wrong: It is not a stoma-placed tube."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"The abbreviation \"MLT\" shown with the microlaryngeal tube figure stands for:","options":["Microlaryngeal tube","Multi-lumen tube","Mucosal lavage tube","Mainstem ligation tube"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: MLT is the standard abbreviation for microlaryngeal tube, a small-diameter ETT for laryngeal surgery.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the meaning of MLT and are not used on the page."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"\"Cole's ETT for paediatrics\" is a specialized endotracheal tube designed primarily for which patient population?","options":["Pediatric patients","Geriatric patients","Bariatric adults","Pregnant patients"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The label explicitly states Cole's ETT is for paediatrics.  Why B/C/D wrong: It is not designated for geriatric, bariatric, or pregnant patients."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"The device labeled \"Armoured (or spiral wire) tracheal tube\" has reinforcing wire in its wall primarily to:","options":["Resist kinking and compression","Deliver medications","Measure airway pressure","Provide nasal humidification"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: An armoured/spiral wire tracheal tube contains embedded wire to prevent kinking and compression of the lumen.  Why B wrong: It is not a medication-delivery device.  Why C wrong: Pressure measurement is not its function.  Why D wrong: It does not provide nasal humidification."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"The figure \"Laryngotracheal localization of typical anesthetics (LTL) ETT tube\" is which category of airway device?","options":["An endotracheal tube","An oropharyngeal airway","A face mask","A tracheostomy cannula"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The LTL device is labeled as an ETT tube, placing it in the endotracheal tube category.  Why B/C/D wrong: It is not an oropharyngeal airway, face mask, or tracheostomy cannula."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"A device labeled \"uncuffed/cuffed tube or cannula\" relating to tracheostomy is identified on the page as a:","options":["Tracheostomy tube/cannula","Laryngeal mask airway","Nasopharyngeal airway","Bougie introducer"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page shows cuffed and uncuffed tracheostomy tube/cannula options.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are distinct devices shown elsewhere on the page."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"A \"Trilube ETT\" shown on the page belongs to which device family?","options":["Endotracheal tube","Oropharyngeal airway","Bag-valve mask","Stylet only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Trilube ETT is labeled as an endotracheal tube variant.  Why B/C/D wrong: It is not an oral airway, bag-valve mask, or standalone stylet."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"A \"Cuffit syringe for ETT cuff inflation\" is used by the nurse to:","options":["Inflate the endotracheal tube cuff","Suction oral secretions","Provide oxygen","Measure tidal volume"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A cuff inflation syringe is used to inflate the ETT cuff to seal the trachea.  Why B wrong: Suctioning uses a suction catheter, not a cuff syringe.  Why C wrong: Oxygen is delivered via a separate source.  Why D wrong: Tidal volume is measured by the ventilator/spirometer."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"The figure \"AMBU laryngeal mask\" is an example of which type of supraglottic airway device?","options":["Laryngeal mask airway (LMA)","Endotracheal tube","Oropharyngeal airway","Tracheostomy tube"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The AMBU laryngeal mask is a brand/type of laryngeal mask airway, a supraglottic device.  Why B wrong: An ETT is an infraglottic tube.  Why C wrong: An oropharyngeal airway is a rigid oral adjunct.  Why D wrong: A tracheostomy tube is stoma-placed."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"The page displays an \"Air-Q ILMA\" device. The abbreviation ILMA refers to which type of airway?","options":["Intubating laryngeal mask airway","Internal laryngotracheal mucosal airway","Inflatable lung mechanical apparatus","Infraglottic large-bore airway"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: ILMA stands for intubating laryngeal mask airway, a supraglottic device that allows passage of an ETT.  Why B/C/D wrong: These expansions are not the standard meaning of ILMA."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"The \"King LTS-D Tube (laryngeal tube suction)\" is designed with a feature that allows:","options":["Suction/passage of a gastric tube through a separate channel","Continuous oxygen delivery to the lungs only","Direct visualization of the cords","Cuff pressure monitoring exclusively"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The King LTS-D (laryngeal tube suction-double lumen) provides a suction/gastric access channel as indicated by \"suction\" in its name.  Why B wrong: It is more than an oxygen delivery device; it is a supraglottic airway with a gastric channel.  Why C wrong: It does not provide direct cord visualization (that requires laryngoscopy).  Why D wrong: Cuff monitoring is not its defining suction feature."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"The \"i-Gel Airway Device\" is best categorized as which kind of airway?","options":["Supraglottic airway device","Endotracheal tube","Nasopharyngeal airway","Bag-valve-mask"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The i-Gel is a supraglottic airway device with a non-inflatable gel cuff that seats over the laryngeal framework.  Why B wrong: It is not an endotracheal tube.  Why C wrong: It is not a nasopharyngeal airway.  Why D wrong: It is not a bag-valve-mask."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"The figure \"Basic Mask\" depicted with airway equipment is used to:","options":["Deliver oxygen/ventilation over the nose and mouth","Suction the trachea","Inflate the ETT cuff","Visualize the larynx"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A basic (face) mask delivers oxygen or facilitates manual ventilation over the nose and mouth.  Why B/C/D wrong: These functions belong to suction catheters, cuff syringes, and laryngoscopes respectively."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"The Sellick maneuver is performed by:","options":["Applying gentle pressure to the anterior neck (cricoid cartilage) to compress the esophagus","Pressing the thyroid cartilage backward, upward, rightward","Lifting the chin while tilting the head back","Pushing the mandible forward at its angles"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Sellick maneuver applies gentle pressure to the anterior neck (cricoid cartilage) to compress and occlude the posterior esophagus, preventing gastro-esophageal reflux.  Why B wrong: That describes the BURP maneuver.  Why C wrong: That describes head-tilt-chin-lift.  Why D wrong: That describes the jaw thrust."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"What is the primary purpose of cricoid pressure during the Sellick maneuver?","options":["To prevent the occurrence of gastro-esophageal reflux (and aspiration)","To improve laryngoscopic view","To inflate the cuff","To open the nasal passage"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: By compressing the esophagus the maneuver prevents gastro-esophageal reflux, reducing aspiration risk.  Why B wrong: Improving view is the BURP maneuver's goal.  Why C/D wrong: It has no role in cuff inflation or nasal patency."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"The Sellick maneuver is specifically indicated in patients who are:","options":["At risk of gastric aspiration","In cardiac arrest only","Receiving nasal cannula oxygen","Fully conscious and fasting"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The stated indication is patients at risk of gastric aspiration.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the listed indication for the maneuver."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"In the BURP maneuver, the direction(s) of pressure applied to the thyroid cartilage are:","options":["Backward, Upward, Rightward Pressure","Forward, Downward, Leftward","Backward and downward only","Upward and leftward only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: BURP stands for Backward, Upward, Rightward Pressure applied on the thyroid cartilage.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the BURP acronym directions."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"The primary purpose of the BURP maneuver is to:","options":["Improve visualization of the larynx by displacing it to obtain a better laryngoscopic view","Prevent esophageal reflux","Inflate the ETT cuff","Replace a tracheostomy tube"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: BURP displaces the larynx (posteriorly, superiorly, laterally) to improve the laryngoscopic visualization of the larynx.  Why B wrong: Reflux prevention is the Sellick maneuver.  Why C wrong: Cuff inflation uses a syringe.  Why D wrong: Tube replacement is the Bailey maneuver concept."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"Compared with the Sellick maneuver, the BURP maneuver is described as:","options":["More superior and more commonly used","Identical in technique","Contraindicated in all adults","Used only in neonates"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states BURP is superior and more commonly used than the Sellick maneuver.  Why B/C/D wrong: They are not identical, and no such contraindication or neonate-only restriction is stated."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"The Bailey maneuver involves which of the following?","options":["Replacement of an endotracheal tube (ETT) with a laryngeal mask airway (LMA) at the end of surgery or when the patient resumes spontaneous ventilation/obeys commands","Applying cricoid pressure","Tilting the head and lifting the chin","Bilateral digital pressure at the mandibular ramus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Bailey maneuver is the replacement of an ETT with an LMA to maintain an airway at the end of surgery when the patient resumes spontaneous ventilation and obeys commands.  Why B wrong: Cricoid pressure is the Sellick maneuver.  Why C wrong: Head-tilt-chin-lift is a basic airway opening maneuver.  Why D wrong: Mandibular digital pressure is the Larson maneuver."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"The Larson maneuver is the application of strong medial digital pressure between which structures?","options":["Between the mandible and the mastoid process (or bilateral firm pressure on the styloid process behind the posterior ramus of the mandible) with jaw thrust","On the cricoid cartilage anteriorly","Over the thyroid cartilage backward","Under the chin pulling forward"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Larson maneuver applies strong medial digital pressure between the mandible and the mastoid process (bilateral firm pressure on the styloid process behind the posterior ramus of the mandible) combined with jaw thrust.  Why B wrong: That describes Sellick.  Why C wrong: That describes BURP.  Why D wrong: That is head-tilt-chin-lift."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"The Larson maneuver is specifically indicated for:","options":["Obstructed mask ventilation and laryngeal spasm (laryngospasm)","Preventing aspiration in trauma","Improving laryngoscopic view","Replacing the ETT with an LMA"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Indications listed are obstructed mask ventilation and laryngeal spasm (laryngospasm).  Why B wrong: Aspiration prevention is Sellick.  Why C wrong: View improvement is BURP.  Why D wrong: ETT-to-LMA exchange is Bailey."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"When performing the head-tilt chin-lift maneuver, the correct steps include placing one hand on the forehead to apply firm backward pressure with the palm and placing the fingertips of the other hand under:","options":["The bony part of the lower jaw (chin), then lifting upward to bring the lower jaw forward","The occiput to push the head back","The thyroid cartilage to compress it","The cricoid ring to occlude the esophagus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The steps describe placing fingertips of the other hand under the lower jaw near the bony part of the chin and lifting up to bring the lower jaw forward, opening the airway.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not part of the head-tilt-chin-lift technique."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"Which airway-opening maneuver should NOT be performed when cervical (neck) spine injury is suspected?","options":["Head-tilt chin-lift","Jaw thrust","Suctioning","Oxygen administration"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text cautions to use head-tilt-chin-lift to open the airway \"unless neck injury is suspected,\" because it can move the cervical spine.  Why B wrong: Jaw thrust is the alternative chosen precisely because of strong suspicion of cervical spine injury.  Why C/D wrong: Suctioning and oxygen are not contraindicated by neck injury."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"The jaw thrust technique is the maneuver of choice in which clinical situation?","options":["When there is strong suspicion of cervical spine injury (e.g., road traffic accident, fall, drowning, or diving accidents)","When the patient is fully alert with no injury","When the patient needs cuff inflation","When improving laryngoscopic view in elective surgery"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Jaw thrust is the technique of choice where there is strong suspicion of cervical spine injury, including RTA, falls, drowning, or diving accidents, because it opens the airway without extending the neck.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the indication for choosing jaw thrust over head-tilt-chin-lift."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"The correct steps for performing the jaw thrust maneuver are to place your fingers behind the angles of the lower jaw and:","options":["Move the jaw upward, then use your thumbs to help position the jaw","Press the cricoid cartilage backward","Tilt the head fully backward","Compress the thyroid cartilage rightward"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The steps are: place fingers behind the angles of the lower jaw and move the jaw upward, then use the thumbs to help position the jaw.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe other maneuvers (Sellick, head-tilt, BURP)."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"The triple airway maneuver is used to maintain a patent upper airway and consists of which combination?","options":["Head tilt (head extension), jaw thrust, and mouth opening","Cricoid pressure, BURP, and suction","Chin lift, ETT insertion, and cuff inflation","Larson pressure, Bailey exchange, and nasal airway insertion"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The triple airway maneuver combines (1) head tilt/head extension, (2) jaw thrust, and (3) mouth opening.  Why B/C/D wrong: These combinations are not the triple airway maneuver as described."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"(PYQ) A nurse is suctioning the ETT tube of a patient who is intubated in emergency. While intervention prevents damage into the lungs and tracheal mucosa, which action should the nurse take by managing the catheter?","options":["Suction during insertion of the catheter","Only apply the suction while inserting the catheter","Only apply the suction while withdrawing the catheter","Pre-oxygenate the patient with 100% oxygen before suctioning"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: To minimize trauma to tracheal mucosa, suction should be applied only while withdrawing (removing) the catheter, never during insertion.  Why A/B wrong: Applying suction during insertion increases mucosal damage and hypoxia.  Why D wrong: Pre-oxygenation is appropriate but it is not the catheter-management action described in this item."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"(PYQ) In tracheal suctioning, the suction should always be withdrawn while applying suction in a rotating motion because that is what damage to tracheal mucosa and avoids removing too much secretion effectively. Which statement best reflects correct technique?","options":["Suction is applied only on withdrawal with a rotating motion to reduce mucosal trauma","Suction is applied continuously during insertion and withdrawal","The catheter should remain in for one full minute","The catheter should be reused without cleaning"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Intermittent suction applied during withdrawal with rotation reduces tracheal mucosal damage while clearing secretions.  Why B wrong: Continuous suction during insertion damages mucosa and worsens hypoxia.  Why C wrong: Prolonged suctioning (a full minute) causes hypoxia; suction passes are kept brief.  Why D wrong: Catheters are single-use/sterile, not reused uncleaned."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"(PYQ) When doing the suction procedure, suction should be applied during which phase?","options":["Only when inserting the suction catheter","Only during inserting and removing the catheter","Either during insertion or withdrawal, depending on when the patient coughs","Only if the patient coughs"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: As reproduced from the source, suction may be applied either during insertion or on withdrawal depending on when the patient coughs (option c is the keyed answer on the page).  Why A wrong: Insertion-only suction is not the keyed choice here.  Why B wrong: Suctioning during both phases increases trauma.  Why D wrong: Suction is not restricted only to a cough event in this item's key."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"(PYQ) Which of the following is an early indication that the patient requires suctioning?","options":["Normal air entry on auscultation","The patient has an effective cough","Reduced oxygen saturation levels","Normal respiratory rate"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: A drop in oxygen saturation is an early indicator that secretions are obstructing the airway and suctioning is needed.  Why A wrong: Normal air entry indicates a clear airway, not a need to suction.  Why B wrong: An effective cough may clear secretions without suctioning.  Why D wrong: A normal respiratory rate does not indicate the need to suction."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"(PYQ) Deep suctioning may cause the lungs and need to be cleaned. The nurse should remember that during suctioning, if a deep suctioning is done, then it may lead to damage. Which intervention prevents this?","options":["Continue to suction for longer periods","Notify the physician immediately","Stop the procedure and allow recovery, limiting suction to about 10–15 seconds","Ensure the suction is limited to 15 seconds"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Limiting each suction pass to about 10–15 seconds prevents prolonged hypoxia and tracheal mucosal damage (option d is the keyed answer on the page).  Why A wrong: Longer suctioning worsens hypoxia and trauma.  Why B wrong: Routine deep suction does not require immediate physician notification.  Why C wrong: While limiting duration is correct, the keyed choice on the page is option d (15 seconds)."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"(PYQ) A patient is being intubated in the ward. To suddenly developing cardiac arrest and there is a need for proper intubation. Which of the following techniques is used to correctly position the trachea/secure the airway?","options":["Initiate long-term sedation","Initiate rapid sequence induction","Perform Sellick maneuver","Provide ventilation with 100% oxygen"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Per the page key, providing ventilation with 100% oxygen (pre-oxygenation/oxygenation) is the selected correct action (option d).  Why A wrong: Long-term sedation is not the immediate airway-securing action.  Why B wrong: Rapid sequence induction is a technique but not the keyed answer for this item.  Why C wrong: Sellick is an adjunct, not the keyed answer here."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"(PYQ) A patient is admitted to casualty ward and intubated with a cuffed endotracheal tube for emergency management. Which of the following suction catheters is used endotracheal suctioning?","options":["10 Fr","12 Fr","14 Fr","8 Fr"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Per the page key, the selected suction catheter size for this ETT scenario is option d (8 Fr) as reproduced from the source.  Why A/B/C wrong: These sizes are distractor options not keyed as correct on the page."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"(PYQ) Pre-oxygenation is one of the most important aspects of emergency airway management. Hyper-oxygenate with what percent of oxygen for 3 to 5 minutes before suctioning?","options":["100% oxygen","50% oxygen","21% oxygen","30% oxygen"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states to hyper-oxygenate with 100% oxygen for 3–5 minutes before procedures such as RSI/suctioning to build an oxygen reserve.  Why B/C/D wrong: Lower oxygen concentrations do not provide adequate pre-oxygenation reserve."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"(PYQ) The purpose of pre-oxygenation is to fill the patient's lungs with oxygen so as not only to increase oxygen saturation, but also to wash out which gas to provide maximum apnea time, replacing the alveolar lung gas with oxygen during RSI?","options":["Carbon dioxide","Nitrogen","Helium","Argon"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pre-oxygenation (denitrogenation) washes nitrogen out of the alveoli and replaces it with oxygen, maximizing the apneic oxygen reserve.  Why A wrong: CO2 washout is a function of ventilation, not the goal of pre-oxygenation denitrogenation.  Why C/D wrong: Helium and argon are not the relevant alveolar gases washed out."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"(PYQ) A patient is in casualty ward and intubated with a cuffed endotracheal tube for airway management. Which of the following suction catheters is used endotracheal suctioning? (Select the action that protects the patient.)","options":["Apply suction during insertion","Suction for at least 30 seconds","Use sterile technique and limit suction passes","Reuse the same catheter for the next patient"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Endotracheal suctioning requires sterile technique and brief, limited suction passes to minimize infection and hypoxia.  Why A wrong: Suction on insertion increases mucosal trauma.  Why B wrong: 30-second suctioning causes hypoxia.  Why D wrong: Reusing catheters breaches asepsis."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"(PYQ) A child with a 6 mm endotracheal tube admitted in ICU. Which suction catheter is to be used most appropriately for suctioning?","options":["8 Fr","10 Fr","9 Fr","12 Fr"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Per the page key, the appropriate suction catheter for a 6 mm ETT in the pediatric ICU scenario is option c (9 Fr) as reproduced from the source. (General rule: catheter Fr ≈ ETT internal diameter in mm × 2 minus 2.)  Why A/B/D wrong: These options are distractors not keyed as correct on the page."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"(PYQ) During endotracheal suctioning, the nurse should perform hand hygiene before and after. Which of the following is correctly performed?","options":["Jaw thrust maneuver","Head tilt chin lift maneuver","Valsalva maneuver","Selick maneuver"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Per the page key, the Sellick maneuver (cricoid pressure) is the selected correct answer for this item (option d).  Why A wrong: Jaw thrust is for suspected cervical spine injury, not the keyed answer.  Why B wrong: Head-tilt-chin-lift is contraindicated with neck injury and is not keyed.  Why C wrong: Valsalva maneuver is unrelated to airway suctioning here."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"(PYQ) A patient admitted in the ward is suddenly developing cardiac arrest and there is a need for intubation. The nurse knows that which of the following anesthesia management protocol is commonly used?","options":["Jaw thrust maneuver","Head lift chin lift maneuver","Valsalva maneuver","Selick maneuver"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Per the page key, the Sellick maneuver is the selected correct answer (cricoid pressure during emergency intubation to reduce aspiration), option d.  Why A/B wrong: These open-airway maneuvers are not the keyed answer for emergency intubation aspiration prevention.  Why C wrong: Valsalva is unrelated."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"What are the four sequential steps involved in obtaining a good-quality specimen for testing?","options":["Preparation of the patient; collection of the specimen; processing the specimen; storing and/or transporting the specimen","Labeling; ordering; billing; discarding","Diagnosis; treatment; evaluation; documentation","Assessment; planning; implementation; evaluation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists four steps: (1) preparation of the patient, (2) collection of the specimen, (3) processing the specimen, (4) storing and/or transporting the specimen.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the listed specimen-collection steps."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"The \"four rights\" of specimen collection include all of the following EXCEPT:","options":["Right specimen","Right amount","Right time","Right diagnosis"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The four rights are right specimen, right amount, right time, and right container — \"right diagnosis\" is not among them.  Why A/B/C wrong: These are three of the four listed rights of specimen collection."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"Which is one of the four rights of specimen collection that ensures the sample is placed in the appropriate vessel?","options":["Right container","Right physician","Right route","Right dose"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: \"Right container\" is one of the four rights (right specimen, right amount, right time, right container).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not among the four rights of specimen collection."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"According to the guidelines for specimen collection, which practice is recommended?","options":["Collect the specimen at the correct time","Reuse contaminated containers to save resources","Label specimens after they reach the lab the next day","Skip universal precautions for routine samples"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The guidelines include collecting at the correct time.  Why B wrong: The guidelines say to avoid contaminating the container.  Why C wrong: Labels must be applied correctly with the appropriate lab slip at the bedside, not after delay.  Why D wrong: The guidelines require using universal precautions."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"Per specimen collection guidelines, specimen containers must be:","options":["Labeled correctly with the appropriate lab slip","Left unlabeled until processing","Filled with any random amount","Opened in a non-sterile field for all samples"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A guideline is to label correctly with the appropriate lab slip.  Why B wrong: Specimens must be labeled, not left unlabeled.  Why C wrong: The correct/required amount must be collected.  Why D wrong: Asepsis (sterile/aseptic technique) is required where indicated."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"Which guideline addresses infection control during specimen collection?","options":["Use universal precautions and use medical/surgical asepsis when indicated","Avoid handwashing to save time","Touch the inside of the container freely","Discard universal precautions for blood samples"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The guidelines specify using universal precautions and medical/surgical asepsis when indicated.  Why B/C/D wrong: These violate infection-control and asepsis principles stated in the guidelines."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"Regarding specimen quantity, the specimen collection guideline states the nurse must:","options":["Collect the correct amount of sample (know if it has been prepped correctly: NPO, etc.)","Always collect the maximum possible volume","Collect any leftover sample from previous patients","Estimate the amount without measuring"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The guideline requires collecting the correct amount of sample and knowing whether the patient has been properly prepped (e.g., NPO).  Why B/C/D wrong: These contradict collecting the correct, properly prepared amount."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"Per guidelines, when collecting a urine specimen the nurse should observe the specimen and:","options":["Collect it for color and amount (observe specimen collected)","Pour out half before sending","Mix it with another patient's sample","Add preservative to all urine samples regardless of test"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The guideline is to observe the specimen collected for color and amount.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are inappropriate and not part of the guidelines."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"When obtaining a clean-catch or midstream urine specimen, the first step the nurse instructs the patient to perform is:","options":["Perform hand hygiene (wash hands)","Discard the entire void","Collect the first stream of urine","Add chemical preservative"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The procedure begins with performing hand hygiene/washing the hands.  Why B wrong: Only the initial stream is voided into the toilet, not the entire void.  Why C wrong: The first (initial) stream is discarded, not collected.  Why D wrong: Adding preservative is not the first step of a clean-catch."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"For a female patient providing a clean-catch midstream urine specimen, how should the labia be cleaned?","options":["Wear gloves; clean the area at the meatus with soap and water, separating (opening) the labia and cleaning from front to back","Clean from back to front with plain water","Do not clean the area; collect immediately","Use only alcohol swabs on the labia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In females, the nurse wears gloves and separates the labia, cleaning the area at the meatus with soap and water (front to back) before collection.  Why B wrong: Cleaning back-to-front drags perineal flora toward the meatus and contaminates the sample.  Why C wrong: The meatal area must be cleaned to obtain an uncontaminated sample.  Why D wrong: The source specifies soap and water at the meatus, not alcohol swabs."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"For a male patient providing a clean-catch midstream urine specimen, the nurse should instruct him to:","options":["Pull back (retract) the foreskin to expose the glans penis and clean the area of the external meatus with soap and water","Leave the foreskin in place and collect without cleaning","Clean only the scrotum","Apply preservative to the glans before collection"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In males, the foreskin is retracted to expose the glans and the external meatal area is cleaned with soap and water.  Why B wrong: Cleaning is required to prevent contamination.  Why C wrong: The meatus/glans (not the scrotum) is cleaned.  Why D wrong: No preservative is applied to the glans."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"When collecting a clean-catch midstream sample, what should the patient do with the first part of the urine stream?","options":["Void the initial stream into the toilet (or bedpan) and discard it","Collect the very first drops into the sterile container","Save the first stream for culture","Discard the last portion only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The patient voids and discards the initial stream into the toilet/bedpan, then collects the midstream portion to reduce contamination from the distal urethra.  Why B/C wrong: The first stream is discarded, not collected/saved.  Why D wrong: It is the initial portion (not the last) that is discarded."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"Approximately how much urine should the patient collect into the sterile midstream container?","options":["About 30 mL into the toilet or bedpan, then discard this volume, and collect the midstream into the sterile container","The entire bladder volume","Exactly 500 mL","No more than 1 mL"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page directs the patient to position the sterile container near (but not touching) the meatus, and collect after about 30 mL has been voided (initial stream) into the toilet/bedpan and discarded.  Why B/C/D wrong: These volumes contradict the midstream technique described."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"When positioning the sterile urine container during a clean-catch midstream collection, the nurse must ensure that the container:","options":["Does not touch (is held close to but not touching) the meatus, and collect it without touching the inside","Is pressed firmly against the meatus","Is touched on the inside to test sterility","Is left open for 1 hour before use"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The container is positioned close to but not touching the meatus to avoid contaminating the sample.  Why B/C/D wrong: Touching the meatus or the inside of the container, or leaving it open, contaminates the specimen."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"After a male patient completes the clean-catch midstream collection, the nurse should ensure that:","options":["The foreskin is returned to its normal position (replaced over the glans) to prevent swelling and irritation","The foreskin remains retracted indefinitely","The glans is left uncleaned","The container is left unlabeled"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In an uncircumcised male, the foreskin must be returned to its normal position after collection to prevent paraphimosis-type swelling and irritation of the glans.  Why B wrong: Leaving it retracted can cause constriction/swelling.  Why C wrong: The glans must be cleaned before collection.  Why D wrong: The container must be labeled."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"After collecting the clean-catch urine specimen, the final steps for the nurse are to:","options":["Remove gloves, perform hand hygiene, label the specimen correctly, package it appropriately, and send the specimen to the laboratory","Leave the gloves on and store the specimen at the bedside","Send the specimen without a label to save time","Discard the specimen and recollect routinely"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page directs removing gloves, performing hand hygiene, labeling correctly, packaging appropriately, and sending the specimen to the lab.  Why B/C/D wrong: These violate infection control, labeling, and timely-transport requirements."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"A \"first morning urine\" specimen is preferred for urinalysis and microscopic analysis primarily because it is:","options":["A more concentrated specimen","The most dilute specimen","Always sterile without cleaning","Free of all cells"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The first morning void is the choice for urinalysis and microscopic analysis because it is a more concentrated specimen (allows detection of abnormalities).  Why B wrong: It is more concentrated, not dilute.  Why C wrong: It is not automatically sterile.  Why D wrong: It is not cell-free; concentration aids cell detection."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"A midstream (clean-catch) urine sample is the specimen of choice for which tests?","options":["Culture and sensitivity tests (AMS, RG, ESC, RG)","24-hour creatinine clearance only","Bag collection only","Suprapubic aspiration culture only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists midstream urine as the choice of sample for culture and sensitivity tests.  Why B wrong: 24-hour collection is a separate timed method.  Why C wrong: Bag collection is a separate pediatric method.  Why D wrong: Suprapubic aspiration is a separate invasive method."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"When collecting a midstream urine sample for culture, the genitalia should be:","options":["Maintained with sterile technique while collecting, and cleaned properly with soap and water before collecting the urine","Left uncleaned to preserve flora","Cleaned only with the patient's own saliva","Wiped with alcohol then rinsed with urine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table states to maintain sterile technique while collecting and to clean the genitalia properly with soap and water before collecting the urine.  Why B/C/D wrong: These compromise the sterility/validity of a culture sample."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"In the catheter and collection-bag (during catheterization) method of urine collection, the sample is obtained by:","options":["Collecting urine via the syringe and needle from the catheter port/tubing","Cutting the catheter tubing","Emptying the drainage bag into a cup","Suprapubic puncture"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table describes collecting urine from the catheter via syringe and needle (from the sampling port), maintaining a closed system.  Why B wrong: Cutting the tubing breaks the closed system and is unsafe.  Why C wrong: Urine from the drainage bag is not fresh/representative for testing.  Why D wrong: Suprapubic aspiration is a separate method."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"Suprapubic aspiration as a urine collection method is performed by:","options":["Inserting a needle through the abdominal wall and into the bladder to obtain a urine sample","Voiding naturally into a sterile cup","Attaching a bag to the perineum","Threading a catheter through the urethra"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table defines suprapubic aspiration as inserting a needle through the abdominal wall into the bladder to obtain a urine sample.  Why B wrong: That is a clean-catch/midstream method.  Why C wrong: That describes bag collection.  Why D wrong: That describes catheterization."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"Indications for suprapubic aspiration of urine include which of the following?","options":["To catheterise the client, trauma in urethra, BPH, and other conditions","Routine outpatient urinalysis in healthy adults","Preferred for all pediatric samples","Self-collected samples at home"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists indications such as inability/need to catheterize the client, urethral trauma, BPH, and other conditions.  Why B/C/D wrong: Suprapubic aspiration is invasive and not used for routine, all-pediatric, or home-collected samples."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"A 24-hour urine collection is correctly performed by:","options":["Collecting urine for 24 hours, starting after the first void is discarded; the test starts when the patient first voids","Collecting a single morning sample","Collecting urine for exactly 6 hours","Pooling several patients' samples over a day"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table describes a timed collection over a full 24-hour period; the collection of all urine within that window begins after discarding the first void (the test starts when the patient first voids).  Why B wrong: A single morning sample is the first-morning method.  Why C wrong: It is 24 hours, not 6.  Why D wrong: Pooling multiple patients invalidates the test."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"In a 24-hour urine collection, when the patient starts to urinate, the nurse should:","options":["Discard the very first void and start timing the collection, then collect all subsequent urine in the container","Collect the first void and stop","Discard all urine throughout the period","Add no instructions about timing"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The first void at the start is discarded and timing begins; all urine thereafter for 24 hours is collected.  Why B wrong: The first void is discarded, not collected as the only sample.  Why C wrong: Only the first void is discarded; the rest is collected.  Why D wrong: Timing instructions are essential to a 24-hour collection."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"Which preservatives may be required to be added in a 24-hour urine collection container, depending on the test?","options":["Boric acid, HCl, formalin, and chloroform","Sodium bicarbonate and dextrose","Heparin and EDTA","Sterile water only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists boric acid, HCl, formalin, and chloroform as preservatives that may be added to the 24-hour collection container.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the preservatives listed for the 24-hour urine collection."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"A \"bag collection\" urine method uses a special urine specimen collection bag that can be adhered to the skin surrounding the genitalia. It is most appropriate for which population?","options":["Male and female infants/children (and incontinent patients) where adhered around the genitalia","Cooperative adults for culture","Patients needing suprapubic aspiration","Anyone with an indwelling catheter"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The bag collection method uses an adhesive collection bag around the genitalia and is suited to male and female infants/children (and those who cannot void on command).  Why B wrong: Cooperative adults use clean-catch midstream for culture.  Why C wrong: Suprapubic aspiration uses a needle, not a bag.  Why D wrong: Catheterized patients have urine drawn from the catheter port."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"(Priority) A nurse needs a urine culture and sensitivity from a continent, cooperative adult. Which collection method is MOST appropriate?","options":["Midstream clean-catch specimen","Bag collection","First morning urine","24-hour collection"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Midstream clean-catch is the method of choice for culture and sensitivity in a continent, cooperative adult.  Why B wrong: Bag collection is for infants/children.  Why C wrong: First morning urine is chosen for routine urinalysis/microscopy, not specifically for culture.  Why D wrong: 24-hour collection is for timed quantitative tests, not culture."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"(SATA) Which of the following are guidelines for proper specimen collection? Select all that apply.","options":["Collect at the correct time","Label correctly with the appropriate lab slip","Avoid contaminating the container","Use universal precautions","Observe the specimen for color and amount"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: All listed items are stated guidelines: collect at the correct time, label with the appropriate lab slip, avoid contaminating the container, use universal precautions, and observe the specimen for color and amount.  Why others wrong: There are no incorrect options here; every choice is a documented guideline."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"(SATA) Which steps are part of obtaining a clean-catch/midstream urine specimen? Select all that apply.","options":["Perform hand hygiene","Wear gloves","Clean the meatal area with soap and water","Void and discard the initial stream","Collect the midstream into a sterile container without touching the inside"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: All five are documented steps: hand hygiene, wearing gloves, cleaning the meatus with soap and water, discarding the initial stream, and collecting the midstream into a sterile container without contaminating it.  Why others wrong: No option is incorrect; each is part of the procedure."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"The Specimen Collections chapter (Chapter 08) covers collection of which specimens?","options":["Blood, sputum, urine, and stool","Blood and CSF only","Urine and saliva only","Sputum and synovial fluid only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The chapter title states it covers Blood, Sputum, Urine, Stool.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the chapter's stated scope."},{"section":"Batch 019 · Airway devices/adjuncts…","question":"(Conceptual) Why must the nurse know whether the patient has been \"prepped properly (NPO, etc.)\" before specimen collection?","options":["Improper preparation (e.g., not fasting when required) can invalidate the specimen/test results","It determines the patient's room number","It changes the container color","It has no effect on results"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The guideline to know if the patient is prepped properly (NPO) exists because incorrect preparation can produce inaccurate test results.  Why B/C wrong: Preparation does not set room numbers or container color.  Why D wrong: Preparation directly affects result validity."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"Which of the following is the best technique for urine sample collection in a patient who is having an indwelling urinary catheter?","options":["First morning specimen","Remove the catheter then collect the urine and reconnect the catheter","Aspirate the urine from catheter","Supra pubic aspiration"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In a catheterized patient, urine is aspirated from the catheter (sampling port) to obtain a sterile specimen without disconnecting the system.  Why A wrong: First morning specimen is a general routine method, not the catheter-specific technique.  Why B wrong: Removing and reconnecting the catheter breaks the closed system and increases infection risk.  Why D wrong: Suprapubic aspiration is an invasive needle technique, not used routinely for a patient who already has a catheter."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"Normal/expected urine that appears straw to pale yellow indicates which finding?","options":["Abnormal color","Normal color","Hematuria","Bilirubinuria"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The normal value table lists urine colour as \"Straw & light pale yellow,\" which is normal.  Why A wrong: Straw/pale yellow is the normal, not abnormal, finding.  Why B/C/D: Hematuria and bilirubinuria produce abnormal colors, not straw/pale yellow."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"A urine sample appears blue. According to the abnormal colour table, this is associated with which cause?","options":["Dyes in urine","Pseudomonas infection","Old RBC, methemoglobin, alkaptonuria","Pyuria (pus in the urine)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Abnormal Colour of The Urine table lists Blue as caused by \"Dyes.\"  Why B wrong: Green is the colour linked with Pseudomonas infection.  Why C wrong: Old RBC/methemoglobin/alkaptonuria are linked with brown-black urine.  Why D wrong: Pyuria (pus) is linked with milky white urine."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"Brown-black coloured urine is caused by which of the following?","options":["Dyes","Old RBC, methemoglobin, alkaptonuria","Pseudomonas infection","Pyuria"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table attributes brown-black urine to old RBC, methemoglobin, and alkaptonuria.  Why A wrong: Dyes cause blue urine.  Why C wrong: Pseudomonas causes green urine.  Why D wrong: Pyuria causes milky white urine."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"Bright yellow urine is most associated with which cause per the abnormal colour table?","options":["Hematuria","Bilirubin","Concentrated, dehydration, jaundice","Pseudomonas infection"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists Bright yellow as \"Concentrated, dehydration, jaundice.\"  Why A wrong: Hematuria is linked with dark yellow / pink-to-red urine entries.  Why B wrong: Bilirubin is itself a pigment but the listed cause for bright yellow is concentrated/dehydration/jaundice.  Why D wrong: Pseudomonas causes green urine."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"Dark yellow urine is attributed to which cause?","options":["Concentrated, dehydration, jaundice","Dyes","Pseudomonas infection","Old RBC"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The abnormal colour table lists Dark yellow as caused by concentrated urine, dehydration, and jaundice.  Why B wrong: Dyes cause blue urine.  Why C wrong: Pseudomonas causes green urine.  Why D wrong: Old RBC causes brown-black urine."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"Milky white urine indicates which condition?","options":["Dehydration","Pyuria (pus in the urine)","Pseudomonas infection","Hematuria"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists Milky white urine as Pyuria (pus in the urine).  Why A wrong: Dehydration causes bright/dark yellow concentrated urine.  Why C wrong: Pseudomonas causes green urine.  Why D wrong: Hematuria produces pink/red urine."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"Pink to red urine is most consistent with which cause?","options":["Pseudomonas infection","Hematuria","Dyes","Alkaptonuria"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table attributes Pink to red urine to Hematuria.  Why A wrong: Pseudomonas causes green urine.  Why C wrong: Dyes cause blue urine.  Why D wrong: Alkaptonuria contributes to brown-black urine."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"Green coloured urine is associated with which organism/condition?","options":["Pseudomonas infection","Hematuria","Dehydration","Pyuria"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists Green urine as caused by Pseudomonas infection.  Why B wrong: Hematuria causes pink-red urine.  Why C wrong: Dehydration causes concentrated bright/dark yellow urine.  Why D wrong: Pyuria causes milky white urine."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"What is the normal odor of urine according to the normal values table?","options":["Ammonia","Sweet/fruity","Aromatic","Foul"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists urine Odor as \"Aromatic, like ammonia.\"  Why A wrong: Pure ammonia smell suggests stale or infected urine, not the listed normal aromatic odor.  Why B wrong: Sweet/fruity odor suggests ketones/diabetes, not normal.  Why D wrong: Foul odor is abnormal."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"The normal specific gravity of urine is stated as which range?","options":["1.001 to 1.010","1.010 to 1.030","1.030 to 1.050","0.990 to 1.005"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The normal values table gives specific gravity of urine as 1.010 to 1.030.  Why A/C/D wrong: These ranges do not match the value listed in the source table."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"The normal urine pH range is:","options":["4.5 to 7.8","7.35 to 7.45","2.0 to 4.0","8.0 to 9.0"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists urine pH as 4.5 to 7.8.  Why B wrong: 7.35–7.45 is arterial blood pH, not urine.  Why C/D wrong: These extreme values are outside the listed urine pH range."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"What is the normal level of creatinine in urine according to the table?","options":["Negative","Trace","None or 0.001 ml","250–800 mOsm/L/24 hrs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists urine Creatinine as \"Trace.\"  Why A wrong: Negative is listed for bilirubin/RBC/WBC/ketone/glucose and for protein.  Why C wrong: \"None or <0.001 ml\" is listed for myoglobin/hemoglobin.  Why D wrong: 250–800 mOsm is listed for urine osmolality."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"In a normal urinalysis, bilirubin, RBC, WBC, ketone, and glucose are expected to be:","options":["Positive","Trace","Negative","Strongly positive"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists Bilirubin, RBC, WBC, Ketone, Glucose all as Negative in normal urine.  Why A/D wrong: Positivity for these is abnormal.  Why B wrong: Trace is the value for creatinine, not these substances."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"Normal urine protein on urinalysis should be:","options":["Negative","++ positive","Trace","250 mg/dL"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists Protein as Negative in normal urine.  Why B wrong: ++ would indicate significant proteinuria, which is abnormal.  Why C wrong: Trace is the creatinine value.  Why D wrong: No numeric value is given; the normal result is simply Negative."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"The normal urine osmolality (average) listed in the table is approximately:","options":["1500 mOsm/L","250–800 mOsm/L/24 hrs","1.010–1.030","4.5–7.8"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists urine osmolality as 250–800 mOsm/L/24 hrs (average).  Why A wrong: 1500 mOsm/L is listed as the maximum (1500 mOsm/L average noted separately).  Why C wrong: 1.010–1.030 is specific gravity.  Why D wrong: 4.5–7.8 is urine pH."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"Per the normal values table, myoglobin and hemoglobin in normal urine should be:","options":["Positive","Trace","None or <0.001 ml","250 mOsm/L"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists Myoglobin, haemoglobin as \"None or <0.001 ml\" in normal urine.  Why A wrong: Presence (positive) is abnormal.  Why B wrong: Trace is the creatinine value.  Why D wrong: That is an osmolality value, not for myoglobin/hemoglobin."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"What is the purpose of Benedict's test?","options":["To detect acetone/ketone bodies in urine","To detect the presence of simple or reducing sugars (monosaccharides and disaccharides) in the urine","To detect bile pigments in urine","To detect albumin in urine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states Benedict's test detects the presence of simple or reducing sugars (monosaccharides and disaccharides) in the urine.  Why A wrong: Detection of acetone/ketones is the purpose of Rothera's test.  Why C wrong: Bile pigment detection is done by Fouchet's test.  Why D wrong: Albumin detection uses heat/Heller's-type tests, not Benedict's."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"What is sucrose, in the context of Benedict's test?","options":["A reducing sugar that gives a positive Benedict's result","A non-reducing sugar which does not react with Benedict's reagent","A protein detected by Benedict's reagent","A ketone body"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text notes sucrose is a non-reducing sugar that does not react with Benedict's reagent.  Why A wrong: Sucrose does NOT react (non-reducing), so it does not give a positive result directly.  Why C/D wrong: Sucrose is a sugar (disaccharide), not a protein or ketone."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"A positive Benedict's test result is indicated by which change?","options":["A colour change","A foul odor","Frothing","Precipitation of protein"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states a positive test is indicated by a colour change.  Why B/C/D wrong: Benedict's test is read by colour change, not odor, frothing, or protein precipitation."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"In Benedict's test, the colour change depends on which factor?","options":["The patient's age","The quantity of sugar present","The urine pH only","The temperature of the room"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the colour change depends on the quantity of sugar present.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the stated determinant of the colour change."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"In Benedict's test, light green colour indicates approximately what amount of sugar?","options":["No sugar present","<1 sugar (about 1 sugar/+)",">+4 sugar","++ sugar"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The sugar indicator/colour key lists Green colour = +1 sugar (small amount, approximately <1/1 sugar).  Why A wrong: No sugar present is indicated by blue colour.  Why C wrong: >+4 (brick red) indicates large quantities.  Why D wrong: ++ sugar corresponds to yellow/greenish, not green."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"Match the Benedict's colour to sugar amount: which colour indicates NO sugar present?","options":["Brick red","Orange","Blue","Green"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The colour key states Blue = No sugar present.  Why A wrong: Brick red = >+4 sugar (large amount).  Why B wrong: Orange = +3 sugar.  Why D wrong: Green = +1 sugar."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"In Benedict's test colour interpretation, yellow/greenish colour corresponds to:","options":["+1 sugar","+2 sugar","+3 sugar",">+4 sugar"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The key lists Yellow/greenish = +2 sugar.  Why A wrong: +1 is green.  Why C wrong: +3 is orange.  Why D wrong: >+4 is brick red."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"Orange colour in Benedict's test indicates which sugar amount?","options":["+1 sugar","+2 sugar","+3 sugar","No sugar"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The key lists Orange colour = +3 sugar.  Why A wrong: +1 = green.  Why B wrong: +2 = yellow/greenish.  Why D wrong: No sugar = blue."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"Brick red colour in Benedict's test indicates which sugar amount?","options":["+1 sugar","+2 sugar","+3 sugar",">+4 sugar (large quantity)"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The key lists Brick red colour = >+4 sugar, indicating large quantities.  Why A/B/C wrong: These lower amounts correspond to green, yellow/greenish, and orange respectively."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"What is the purpose of Rothera's test?","options":["To detect reducing sugars","To detect acetone or ketone in the urine","To detect bile salts","To detect protein"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states Rothera's test detects acetone or ketone in the urine.  Why A wrong: Reducing sugars are detected by Benedict's test.  Why C/D wrong: Bile salts and protein are detected by other tests."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"In Rothera's test, a positive result is indicated by which finding?","options":["Brick red colour","Frothing","A ring (purple/permanganate-coloured ring) present at the junction of the ammonia and urine where acetone/ketone is present","Blue colour at the bottom of the tube"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The text states a positive result shows a ring (purple-colored) present at the junction of the ammonia and urine, indicating acetone/ketone present.  Why A wrong: Brick red is the Benedict's test positive (sugar) result.  Why B/D wrong: Frothing and a blue bottom layer are not the described positive indicator."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"Which condition is Rothera's test usually used to assess (commonly cited indication)?","options":["Diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA)","Urinary tract infection","Anemia","Liver cirrhosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states it is usually used in diabetic cases (associated with DKA, where ketones appear in urine).  Why B/C/D wrong: These conditions are not the stated indication for Rothera's ketone test."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"Per the test-name table, which test is used to detect bile salts in the urine?","options":["Hot's test","Hay's test","Cold test","Ehrlich test"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists Hay's test as used to detect bile salts in the urine.  Why A wrong: Hot test is used to detect albumin in the urine.  Why C wrong: Cold test is used to detect albumin in the urine.  Why D wrong: Ehrlich test is used to detect urobilinogen."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"Which test is used to detect bile pigments in the urine?","options":["Hay's test","Smith test","Ehrlich test","Hot test"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists Smith test as used to detect bile pigments in the urine.  Why A wrong: Hay's test detects bile salts.  Why C wrong: Ehrlich test detects urobilinogen.  Why D wrong: Hot test detects albumin."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"According to the table, which two tests are used to detect albumin in the urine?","options":["Hot test and Cold test","Hay's test and Smith test","Ehrlich test and Fouchet's test","Smith test and Cold test"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists both Hot test and Cold test as used to detect albumin in the urine.  Why B wrong: Hay's = bile salts; Smith = bile pigments.  Why C wrong: Ehrlich = urobilinogen; Fouchet's = bile pigment.  Why D wrong: Only Cold test (not Smith) detects albumin among these."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"Which test is used to detect urobilinogen in the urine?","options":["Smith test","Ehrlich test","Hay's test","Hot test"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists Ehrlich test as used to detect urobilinogen.  Why A wrong: Smith test detects bile pigments.  Why C wrong: Hay's test detects bile salts.  Why D wrong: Hot test detects albumin."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"Fouchet's test is used to detect which substance in the urine?","options":["Albumin","Bile salts","Bile pigment","Urobilinogen"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists Fouchet's test as used to detect bile pigment in the urine.  Why A wrong: Albumin is detected by Hot/Cold tests.  Why B wrong: Bile salts are detected by Hay's test.  Why D wrong: Urobilinogen is detected by Ehrlich test."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"The Modified Fouchet's test detects bile pigment in urine and is also known by which other name?","options":["Gmelin's test","Hay's test","Smith test","Rothera's test"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table states Modified Fouchet's test is also known as Gmelin's test (used to detect bile pigment).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are distinct tests with different targets (bile salts, bile pigments, ketones)."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"A sputum specimen is best defined as a specimen obtained by which mechanism?","options":["Voiding","Expectoration and tracheal suctioning","Venipuncture","Catheterization"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The overview states sputum specimen is obtained by expectoration and tracheal suctioning for diagnostic purposes.  Why A/C/D wrong: Voiding (urine), venipuncture (blood), and catheterization are unrelated to sputum collection."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"What are the stated purposes of sputum collection? (SATA)","options":["For gross appearance and microscopic examinations","For gram staining and cytological examinations","To measure 24-hour urine output","To detect electrolytes in blood","To assess bile salts"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The purpose section lists gross appearance/microscopic examination and gram staining/cytological examination.  Why C wrong: Urine output measurement is unrelated.  Why D wrong: Electrolytes are measured in blood specimens, not sputum.  Why E wrong: Bile salts relate to urine tests (Hay's test)."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"During the sputum collection procedure, what is the first step regarding the client?","options":["Wash hands with running water","Determine the purpose of the specimen and explain its institution policy/procedure to the client","Suction the trachea immediately","Apply a tourniquet"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The procedure begins with determining the purpose of the specimen and institution policy, then explaining the procedure to the client.  Why A wrong: Hand washing with running water and wearing sterile gloves is a later step.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the initial procedural steps for sputum collection."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"During sputum collection procedure, equipment preparation requires that the nurse:","options":["Use any available container","Prepare a sterile tray with sterile needed equipments","Reuse the previous patient's container","Skip explaining the procedure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The procedure states prepare a sterile tray with sterile needed equipments.  Why A/C wrong: Sterile, dedicated equipment is required to avoid contamination.  Why D wrong: Explaining the procedure to the client is a required step."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"As infection-control practice during sputum collection, the nurse should:","options":["Wash hands with running water then wear sterile gloves","Avoid wearing gloves","Collect the sample after meals only","Use tap water without washing hands"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The procedure states wash hands with running water then wear sterile gloves.  Why B wrong: Sterile gloves are required.  Why C wrong: Sputum is best collected before/upon waking, not specifically after meals.  Why D wrong: Hand washing is mandatory."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"Before collecting a sputum specimen, the nurse should instruct the client to first do which of the following?","options":["Drink a large glass of water","Rinse the mouth with water before collection","Brush the teeth with paste","Eat a meal"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text instructs the client to rinse the mouth with water before collection (to reduce oral contamination).  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the specified pre-collection instruction."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"How many sputum samples are typically collected and over what schedule per the text?","options":["One sample on a single day","Two samples collected on a day or two consecutive days","Five samples in one hour","Ten samples weekly"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states two samples are collected on a day or two consecutive days.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated sampling schedule."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"\"Spot\" sample sputum collection should be performed:","options":["Late at night","On the spot under supervision and other is collected early in the morning in sterile specimen before the tooth brush by suctioning/expectoration","Only after the patient eats","After 48 hours of storage"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states one sample is collected on the spot under supervision, and the other is collected early in the morning in a sterile specimen before tooth-brushing, by suctioning or expectoration.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the described spot vs early-morning sampling."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"For acid-fast bacilli (AFB), the sputum specimen swab can be kept in which medium?","options":["Plain glass vial only","A test tube and sterile cotton swab, but it should be used to cover the test tube","Frozen at -20°C","Mixed with EDTA"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the swab for AFB can be kept in a test tube and sterile cotton swab should be used to cover the test tube.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the described AFB handling."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"The client should be instructed to take several breaths and then cough how, to obtain sputum?","options":["Coughing gently and superficially","Coughing deeply to obtain sputum","Holding the breath without coughing","Coughing only once lightly"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text instructs the client to take several breaths and then cough deeply to obtain sputum.  Why A/C/D wrong: Shallow or single coughs do not adequately mobilize lower-airway secretions."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"Sputum collection is best timed for which part of the day?","options":["After the patient begins or starts antibiotic therapy","Before the patient begins antibiotic therapy","Immediately after dinner","Only at midnight"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states sputum should always be collected before the patient begins antibiotic therapy (so the pathogen is not suppressed).  Why A wrong: Collecting after starting antibiotics can reduce yield.  Why C/D wrong: Timing is tied to antibiotic status, not mealtime/midnight."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"After collection of a specimen, the nurse should label the vial and:","options":["Leave it open on the counter","Send it to the laboratory immediately","Store it for one week first","Discard the label"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states after collection, label the vial and it should be sent to the laboratory immediately.  Why A/C/D wrong: Delays and improper handling compromise the specimen."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"The Rajasthan NHM 2024 question (reproduced): A patient gives a sputum sample in a sterile container; minimum volume of 2 ml on the \"spot\" sample when the patient presents to the health facility or hospital. On which day/sample does this describe?","options":["Day 1, Sample 1 (spot sample collected on the spot when patient presents)","Day 3, Sample 1 only","Random night collection","Never collected on the spot"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The reproduced 2-Days Protocol box states Day 1 Sample 1 is the spot sample (minimum 2 ml) collected when the patient presents to the health facility/hospital; the patient is asked to take a specimen container home for the early morning sample.  Why B wrong: Day 3 collection refers to the early-morning sample step, not the spot sample.  Why C/D wrong: The spot sample is explicitly collected on the spot."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"Per the 2-Days Protocol, the second sample (early morning sample) is collected on which day?","options":["Day 1","Day 3 (patient brings an early morning sample)","Day 7","Same hour as the spot sample"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The protocol box states Day 3 Sample 1 = patient brings an early morning sample.  Why A wrong: Day 1 sample 1 is the spot sample.  Why C/D wrong: These do not match the protocol."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"RECALL THE RAJASTHAN NHM-2024 (reproduced PYQ): Which of the following sample should be collected for the diagnosis of pulmonary tuberculosis?","options":["Spot sample 1","SOPT sample 1 (variant listed)","Early morning samples 1","None of these"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The reproduced answer key marks the spot sample option as the correct answer (Right answer = A).  Why B wrong: \"SOPT\" is a distractor/misspelling, not the correct technique.  Why C wrong: While an early morning sample is also collected, the keyed correct option is the spot sample.  Why D wrong: A correct option exists."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"A stool specimen is defined as obtaining a sample of a client's feces for what purpose?","options":["To measure urine output","For diagnostic purposes","To assess blood electrolytes","To detect ketones"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The overview states stool collection obtains a sample of a client's feces for diagnostic purposes.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are functions of other specimen types."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"What is the stated purpose of stool specimen collection?","options":["To identify infectious organisms, mucus, and parasites in the stool","To detect glucose","To measure specific gravity","To detect bile salts"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The purpose section states it identifies infectious organisms, mucus, and parasites in the stool.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are urine/blood test targets."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"Where should a stool specimen be found/collected from per the procedure?","options":["From the toilet bowl water","To identify the blood found in the stool (blood found in the stool)","From a urine bag","From sputum"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The purpose text notes identifying the blood found in the stool as part of stool examination.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are incorrect sources/targets."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"Which steps are part of the stool specimen procedure? (SATA)","options":["Explain the procedure to the client","Prepare a sterile tray with sterile needed equipments","Wash hands with running water then wear sterile gloves","Administer an IV bolus","Apply a tourniquet"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The procedure lists explaining to the client, preparing a sterile tray with sterile equipment, and washing hands with running water then wearing sterile gloves.  Why D wrong: IV bolus is unrelated to stool collection.  Why E wrong: A tourniquet is used in venipuncture, not stool collection."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"When collecting a blood specimen, what is the first step for a conscious client per the overview?","options":["Withdraw blood immediately","Explain the procedure (if client is conscious, explain the procedure)","Apply a tourniquet for 10 minutes","Cleanse with water only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The overview states \"if client conscious then explain the procedure.\"  Why A wrong: Explanation and positioning precede withdrawal.  Why C wrong: Tourniquet should NOT be left on for prolonged periods.  Why D wrong: Aseptic skin antiseptic, not just water, is used."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"What body position should the client be placed in for blood specimen collection?","options":["Standing upright","A comfortable position (provide a comfortable position)","Prone with head down","Trendelenburg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The overview states to provide/ensure a comfortable position for the client.  Why A/C/D wrong: These positions are not indicated for routine blood collection."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"Before initiating a blood collection procedure, the nurse must do which of the following? (SATA)","options":["Hand wash before initiating the procedure","Wear appropriate PPE during the procedure (gloves, face shields) to prevent blood-borne infections like HIV, HCV, and Hep B","Collect all things that will require (needle, syringes, spirit swab, adequate cannula, adhesive, tourniquet, vials)","Reuse a needle from a prior patient","Maintain sterile technique during the entire procedure"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: The overview lists hand washing, wearing PPE (gloves/face shields) to prevent HIV/HCV/Hep B, collecting all required equipment, and maintaining sterile technique throughout.  Why D wrong: Needles must never be reused; find the best vein, refill fast, feel soft, and visible."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"When selecting a vein for venipuncture, the nurse should find a vein that is described as:","options":["Hard, fixed, and not visible","Best vein that will require/refill fast, feel soft, and visible","Pulsating like an artery","Located only in the foot"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states find the best vein that will refill fast, feel soft, and visible.  Why A wrong: A hard, non-visible vein is not ideal.  Why C wrong: A pulsating vessel is an artery and should be avoided.  Why D wrong: No such restriction is stated; site selection favors a soft visible vein."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"What should the nurse apply on the venipuncture site for how long before insertion?","options":["Apply the tourniquet for 5-10 cm above the insertion site","Apply a tourniquet for 30 minutes","No tourniquet is ever used","Apply tourniquet on the wrist only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states apply the tourniquet 5-10 cm above the insertion site.  Why B wrong: Prolonged 30-minute application causes hemoconcentration/harm.  Why C wrong: A tourniquet is used to distend the vein.  Why D wrong: Placement is above the insertion site, not restricted to the wrist."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"After withdrawing the needed blood sample, the nurse should flush the line with which volume and solution to prevent clotting?","options":["Flush with 5-10 ml normal saline and apply direct pressure to the venipuncture site for 3-5 minutes","Flush with 50 ml of dextrose","Do not flush at all","Flush with tap water for 1 minute"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states withdraw the needed sample and flush the line with 5-10 ml normal saline and apply direct pressure to the venipuncture site for 3-5 minutes.  Why B/D wrong: Wrong solution/volume.  Why C wrong: Flushing/pressure is required for hemostasis and line patency."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"If a blood sample is taken from a central line, after withdrawing the sample the line should be flushed with what?","options":["5-10 ml normal saline","Heparin only","Plain water","EDTA solution"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states if blood is taken from a central line, after withdrawing the sample the line should be flushed with 5-10 ml normal saline.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated flushing solution for the central line in this context."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"After collecting the sample, all disposable items used should be disposed of according to which guideline?","options":["BMWM (Bio-Medical Waste Management)","Regular household trash","Recycling bin","Sharp items in cloth bag"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states all disposable items should be disposed of according to BMWM (biomedical waste management).  Why B/C/D wrong: Biohazardous items require BMWM disposal, not general waste."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"A blood specimen for an adult should be drawn from where, and for pediatric clients in what manner?","options":["From a periphery line or a central line for adults; for pediatric 3-4 ml or more than that of total blood volume","Only from an artery","Only from the foot","Never from a central line"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states a blood sample may be drawn from a periphery line or a central line; for pediatric 3-4 ml or more than that of total blood volume considerations apply.  Why B/C/D wrong: These contradict the stated venous/central-line approach."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"Per the additional notes, blood collection in children should be done by whom and using what container?","options":["Collection of blood in adults and children by aseptic faces","By an untrained person using any open jar","Only by the patient themselves","Without any antiseptic"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states collection of blood in adults and children should be done aseptically (aseptic faces/technique).  Why B/C/D wrong: Untrained collection, open jars, and lack of antiseptic violate aseptic practice."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"A specimen should be collected with a small spatula without contamination of the urine, and collected privately to provide privacy. Which principle does this reflect?","options":["Maintaining client privacy and avoiding contamination during collection","Speeding up the procedure","Reducing cost","Reusing equipment"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states provide privacy to the client and the specimen is collected with a small spatula without contamination.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated principle of privacy and non-contamination."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"A blood specimen for culture should be collected in which type of container?","options":["A non-sterile open jar","A sterile container; if culture suffering from blood/clots should inform the doctor immediately","A urine cup","Any reused vial"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states a specimen for culture should be collected in a sterile container, and if clotting/abnormality occurs the doctor should be informed immediately.  Why A/C/D wrong: Non-sterile or reused containers contaminate cultures."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"Stool specimen for culture should be collected in which container per the text?","options":["Sterile container","Plastic bag without lid","Glass jar with paper cover","The patient's hand"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states stool specimen for culture should be collected in a sterile container.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are non-sterile and unacceptable for culture."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"Per RECALL THE NEERDET (reproduced PYQ): Which of the following vacutainer should be used for post-operative glucose assessment?","options":["Sodium chloride","Sodium fluoride","Heparin","Sodium citrate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The reproduced answer key marks Sodium fluoride (Right answer = B). Sodium fluoride preserves glucose by inhibiting glycolysis.  Why A wrong: Sodium chloride is not the glycolysis inhibitor used for glucose tubes.  Why C wrong: Heparin is used for the green-top tube (not glucose preservation).  Why D wrong: Sodium citrate is used for coagulation tests (blue top)."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"RECALL THE NEERDET (reproduced PYQ): Which additive is used in glucose vial in laboratory?","options":["EDTA","Sodium fluoride","Heparin","Sodium citrate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The reproduced item asks which additive is used in the glucose vial; sodium fluoride/oxalate (grey tube) is used for glucose, fasting & PPBS, lactate testing.  Why A wrong: EDTA (lavender) is for hematological/CBC tests.  Why C wrong: Heparin (green) is for plasma/chromosome studies.  Why D wrong: Sodium citrate (blue) is for coagulation tests."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"RECALL THE NEERDET (reproduced PYQ): Which of the following additives are present in a blood sample vial for Prothrombin time? (Right answer keyed = A)","options":["Sodium citrate","Oxalate","Heparin","EDTA"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The reproduced answer key marks Sodium citrate (Right answer = A). For coagulation studies, blood drawn into a blue-top vial containing liquid sodium citrate (3.2%) acts as an anticoagulant by binding calcium in the sample.  Why B wrong: Oxalate (sodium/potassium oxalate) is used in sodium fluoride tubes for glucose.  Why C wrong: Heparin is for the green tube (plasma/chromosome studies).  Why D wrong: EDTA is for the lavender tube (hematology/CBC)."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"The Sodium Citrate tube has which cap colour and which additive/anticoagulant?","options":["Light Blue cap; buffered sodium citrate","Red cap; no additive","Gold cap; clot activator","Green cap; heparin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Sample Collection Vials table lists Sodium Citrate tube = Light Blue cap with buffered sodium citrate additive.  Why B wrong: Red cap (plain tube) has no additive.  Why C wrong: Gold cap (serum separating) has clot activator/gel.  Why D wrong: Green cap (heparin) uses heparin."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"The Sodium Citrate (Light Blue) tube is used for which test category?","options":["All coagulation tests (PT, PTT, D-Dimer, Fibrinogen, Factor Assay)","Glucose testing","CBC/hematology","Trace element analysis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists the Light Blue (sodium citrate) tube for all coagulation tests including PT, APTT, D-Dimer, Fibrinogen, Factor Assay.  Why B wrong: Glucose uses the grey (sodium fluoride) tube.  Why C wrong: CBC uses the lavender EDTA tube.  Why D wrong: Trace elements use the royal blue tube."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"The Red Top Plain tube has which characteristics?","options":["Red cap, plain vial, no additive, no anticoagulant","Gold cap with gel","Lavender cap with EDTA","Green cap with heparin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists the Red Top Plain tube as Red cap, plain vial, no additive, no anticoagulant.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe other tubes (gold serum-separating, lavender EDTA, green heparin)."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"The Red Top Plain tube is used for which examinations? (SATA)","options":["Immunology and serological examinations (Ex. HIV, HBsAg, HCV, dengue, serum electrolytes)","RFT, LFT, and other tests","Antigen-antibody tests and all hormonal tests","Coagulation tests like PT/PTT","CBC count"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists immunology/serology (HIV, HBsAg, HCV, dengue, serum electrolytes), RFT/LFT and other tests, and antigen-antibody/hormonal tests for the red plain tube.  Why D wrong: Coagulation tests use the light blue (sodium citrate) tube.  Why E wrong: CBC uses the lavender EDTA tube."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"The Serum separating tube has which cap colour and additive?","options":["Gold cap; Clot Activator and gel for serum separation","Red cap; no additive","Royal blue cap; K2 EDTA","Grey cap; sodium fluoride"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists Serum separating tube = Gold cap with Clot Activator and gel for serum separation.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe the plain, royal blue, and grey tubes respectively."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"The Serum separating (Gold) tube is used for which test categories? (SATA)","options":["Biochemistry, Virology, Immunology, Vit-B12, Folate","Therapeutic drug monitoring (TDM)","Serum electrolytes and other investigations that require serum","Coagulation profile (PT/PTT)","Trace element heavy-metal analysis"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists Biochemistry, Virology, Immunology, Vit-B12, Folate, therapeutic drug monitoring (TDM), serum electrolytes, and other investigations requiring serum for the gold tube.  Why D wrong: Coagulation uses the light blue tube.  Why E wrong: Trace/heavy metals use the royal blue tube."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"The Royal Blue (Serum) tube contains K2 EDTA and is used for which tests?","options":["Trace elements: arsenic, cadmium, chromium, mercury, lead, manganese","Coagulation tests","Glucose/lactate","CBC count"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists the Royal Blue tube (with K2 EDTA) for trace elements — arsenic, cadmium, chromium, mercury, lead, manganese.  Why B wrong: Coagulation uses the light blue tube.  Why C wrong: Glucose/lactate uses the grey tube.  Why D wrong: CBC uses the lavender tube."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"The Heparin tube has which cap colour and additive?","options":["Green cap; Lithium heparin or sodium heparin anticoagulant","Gold cap; clot activator","Lavender cap; K2 EDTA","Grey cap; sodium fluoride"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists the Heparin tube = Green cap with lithium heparin or sodium heparin anticoagulant.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe the gold, lavender, and grey tubes respectively."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"The Heparin (Green) tube is used for which tests? (SATA)","options":["Prevent clotting","Ammonia tests, CO2, blood alcohol, toxicological tests","Chromosomes studies, electrolytes, renal function tests","CBC differential count","Coagulation factor assay"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists the green heparin tube for preventing clotting; ammonia, CO2, blood alcohol, toxicological tests; chromosome studies, electrolytes, and renal function tests.  Why D wrong: CBC differential uses the lavender EDTA tube.  Why E wrong: Coagulation factor assays use the light blue citrate tube."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"The Purple tube (K2 EDTA) has which cap colour and additive?","options":["Lavender/purple cap; Spray-coated K2 EDTA","Gold cap; gel","Grey cap; sodium fluoride","Green cap; heparin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists the Purple tube (K2 EDTA) = Lavender/purple cap with spray-coated K2 EDTA.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe other tubes (gold, grey, green)."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"The Purple (K2 EDTA) tube is used for which tests? (SATA)","options":["Haematological examination","CBC, TLC, DLC, Malaria, ESR, HbA1C, G6PD, HB, Thalassemia","Screening (CD4 / CD8, HbAHC)","Coagulation PT/PTT","Trace metal analysis"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists the lavender EDTA tube for hematological exams including CBC, TLC, DLC, Malaria, ESR, HbA1C, G6PD, HB, Thalassemia, and screening (CD4/CD8, HbAHC).  Why D wrong: Coagulation uses the light blue citrate tube.  Why E wrong: Trace metals use the royal blue tube."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"The Sodium Fluoride tube has which cap colour and additive?","options":["Grey cap; Sodium Fluoride or Potassium/Sodium oxalate","Lavender cap; EDTA","Light blue cap; sodium citrate","Gold cap; gel"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists the Sodium Fluoride tube = Grey cap with sodium fluoride or potassium/sodium oxalate.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe the lavender, light blue, and gold tubes respectively."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"The Sodium Fluoride (Grey) tube is used for which tests?","options":["Glucose (fasting & PPBS), Lactate testing","Coagulation tests","Trace element analysis","Hormonal assays"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists Glucose (fasting & PPBS) and lactate testing for the grey sodium fluoride tube; fluoride prevents glycolysis preserving glucose.  Why B wrong: Coagulation uses the light blue tube.  Why C wrong: Trace elements use the royal blue tube.  Why D wrong: Hormonal assays use the gold serum tube."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"The Blood Culture Bottle uses which additive/anticoagulant and serves what function?","options":["Sodium polyanethol sulfonate (SPS) anticoagulant; growth media for microorganisms","K2 EDTA; CBC","Sodium citrate; coagulation","Sodium fluoride; glucose"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists the Blood Culture Bottle with sodium polyanethol sulfonate anticoagulant and growth media for microorganisms.  Why B/C/D wrong: These additives/uses belong to other tubes."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"What is the required blood volume for a blood culture bottle for adults and pediatrics?","options":["8-10 ml for each bottle for adults; 3-5 ml or more than 1% of total blood volume for pediatrics","1 ml only for both","50 ml for adults","No specific volume needed"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states required blood volume is 8-10 ml for each bottle for adults, and for pediatrics 3-5 ml or more than 1% of total blood volume.  Why B/C/D wrong: These contradict the stated volumes."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"Blood culture bottles should be collected in what manner regarding aerobic/anaerobic organisms?","options":["Two bottles are typically collected, one for aerobic and one for anaerobic organisms","Only one bottle for aerobic only","Never collected anaerobically","Five bottles minimum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text indicates bottles are collected for aerobic and anaerobic organisms (paired collection).  Why B/C/D wrong: These contradict the standard aerobic + anaerobic paired collection."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"Per the additional tube/anticoagulant summary: the Plain vial uses which anticoagulant?","options":["No anticoagulant","EDTA","Heparin","Sodium citrate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The summary table lists Plain vial = No anticoagulant.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are anticoagulants used in other vials."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"Per the summary table, the Purple vial uses which anticoagulant?","options":["EDTA (Ethylene Diamine Tetra-acetic Acid)","Sodium fluoride","Sodium citrate","Lithium heparin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The summary lists Purple vial = EDTA (Ethylene Diamine Tetra-acetic Acid).  Why B wrong: Sodium fluoride is in the grey vial.  Why C wrong: Sodium citrate is in the blue vial.  Why D wrong: Lithium heparin is in the green vial."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"Per the summary table, the Grey vial uses which anticoagulant?","options":["Sodium Fluoride or Potassium/sodium oxalate","EDTA","Sodium citrate","SPS"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The summary lists Grey vial = Sodium Fluoride or Potassium/sodium oxalate.  Why B/C/D wrong: These belong to purple, blue, and blood culture vials respectively."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"Per the summary table, the Blue vial uses which anticoagulant?","options":["Sodium citrate","EDTA","Lithium heparin","Sodium polyanethol sulfonate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The summary lists Blue vial = Sodium citrate.  Why B/C/D wrong: These belong to purple, green, and blood culture vials respectively."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"Per the summary table, the Green vial uses which anticoagulant?","options":["Lithium heparin or sodium heparin","EDTA","Sodium citrate","Sodium fluoride"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The summary lists Green vial = Lithium heparin or sodium heparin.  Why B/C/D wrong: These belong to purple, blue, and grey vials respectively."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"Per the summary table, the Blood culture vial uses which anticoagulant?","options":["Sodium polyanethol sulfonate","EDTA","Heparin","Sodium citrate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The summary lists Blood culture vial = Sodium polyanethol sulfonate.  Why B/C/D wrong: These belong to other vials."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"Sodium polyanethol sulfonate (SPS) is described as which kind of anticoagulant?","options":["The most common anticoagulant used in commercial blood culture bottles","An anticoagulant used only in CBC tubes","A clot activator","A glucose preservative"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The caption states SPS is the most common anticoagulant used in commercial blood culture bottles.  Why B/C/D wrong: SPS is for blood culture, not CBC, clotting, or glucose preservation."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"Per the blood culture bottle cap colour key, which cap colour is for anaerobes?","options":["Orange cap","Green cap","Yellow cap","Red cap"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The key lists Orange cap = For anaerobes.  Why B wrong: Green cap = for aerobes.  Why C wrong: Yellow cap = for children.  Why D wrong: Red is not listed in this culture-bottle key."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"Per the blood culture bottle cap colour key, the Green cap is used for:","options":["Aerobes","Anaerobes","Children","Glucose"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The key lists Green cap = For aerobes.  Why B wrong: Orange = anaerobes.  Why C wrong: Yellow = children.  Why D wrong: Not related to glucose."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"Per the blood culture bottle cap colour key, the Yellow cap is used for:","options":["Children","Anaerobes","Aerobes","Adults only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The key lists Yellow cap = For children.  Why B wrong: Orange = anaerobes.  Why C wrong: Green = aerobes.  Why D wrong: It is specifically for children."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"Why are closed systems preferable for blood sampling?","options":["They have proven to be safer and reduce the risk of contamination","They are cheaper to manufacture","They are faster only","They require no needle"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states closed systems are preferable because they have proven to be safer and reduce the risk of contamination.  Why B/C/D wrong: The stated rationale is safety and reduced contamination, not cost/speed/needleless."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"What is the advantage of a vacuum-extraction (closed) system in blood sampling?","options":["It risks direct exposure to blood","It allows easier collection of multiple samples from a single venipuncture and reduces risk of direct exposure to blood","It can only collect one sample ever","It requires no vein"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the vacuum-extraction system reduces risk of direct exposure to blood and makes it easier to take multiple samples from a single venipuncture.  Why A wrong: It reduces, not increases, exposure.  Why C wrong: It facilitates multiple samples.  Why D wrong: It still requires venous access."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"What are open systems in blood sampling, and what is their most common means of blood sampling?","options":["Open systems include hypodermic needle and syringes, as well as a winged steel needle attached to a syringe; the most common means of blood sampling","Open systems are needleless devices only","Open systems are always closed vacuum tubes","Open systems are never used clinically"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states open systems include hypodermic needle and syringes, as well as a winged steel needle attached to a syringe, and are the most common means of blood sampling.  Why B/C/D wrong: These contradict the description of open systems."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"Which figures/devices are illustrated under the blood sampling system section? (SATA)","options":["Vacuum-extraction systems","BD PUO Needle-Free Blood Collection Device","Microflow Direct Blood Transfer Device","Kynx Lock Blood Culture Collection Set","A urinary catheter tray"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The page labels figures for vacuum-extraction systems, BD PUO Needle-Free Blood Collection Device, Microflow Direct Blood Transfer Device, and Kynx Lock Blood Culture Collection Set.  Why E wrong: A urinary catheter tray is not depicted in this blood-sampling section."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"Chapter 09 covers which broad topic?","options":["Fluid and Electrolytes / Acid Base Balances and Imbalances","Wound care","Medication administration","Vital signs"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The chapter title reads \"Fluid and Electrolytes, Acid Base Balances and Imbalances.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the chapter 09 title."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"The average adult body consists of about how much water?","options":["About 40 litres of water","10 litres","60 litres","5 litres"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The overview states the average adult body consists of about 40 litres of water.  Why B/C/D wrong: These contradict the stated figure."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"In percentage terms, body water is about what percent of total body weight in younger adults?","options":["About 60% of total body weight in younger adults","About 10%","About 90%","About 30%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The overview states water as a percentage is about 60% of total body weight in younger adults.  Why B/C/D wrong: These contradict the stated figure."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"In general, average fluids in infants are about what percentage of total body weight?","options":["About 80% of total body weight","About 40%","About 20%","About 60%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states in general, average fluids in infants are about 80% of total body weight.  Why B/C/D wrong: These contradict the stated figure."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"Why do infants and older adults have a higher risk for fluid-related problems?","options":["Their fluids/total body water percentages differ from younger adults, placing them at higher risk","They have no electrolytes","They never sweat","They have the same fluid balance as young adults"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states infants and older adults are at higher risk for fluid-related problems than younger adults (because their fluid balance/percentages differ).  Why B/C/D wrong: These contradict the stated rationale."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"The volume of fluids in different body compartments is described as:","options":["Relatively constant","Highly variable hour to hour","Always increasing","Equal in all compartments"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the volume of fluids in different compartments remains relatively constant.  Why B/C/D wrong: These contradict the stated relative constancy."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"For a 70-kg adult man, the total body fluid is approximately:","options":["42 litres (about 60% of body weight)","10 litres","28 litres","5 litres"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Body Fluid Compartments figure states total body fluid = 42 L (~60% of body weight) for a 70-kg adult man.  Why B/C/D wrong: These contradict the stated value (28 L is ICF, etc.)."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"In the body fluid compartment model, the intracellular fluid compartment for a 70-kg adult man is approximately:","options":["28 litres (about 2/3 of total body fluid)","14 litres","42 litres","3 litres"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The figure states the intracellular fluid compartment is ~28 L (2/3 of total body fluid).  Why B wrong: 14 L is the extracellular fluid compartment.  Why C wrong: 42 L is the total body fluid.  Why D wrong: 3 L is the plasma (intravascular) portion."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"The extracellular fluid compartment for a 70-kg adult man is approximately:","options":["14 litres (about 1/3 of total body fluid)","28 litres","42 litres","10.5 litres"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The figure states the extracellular fluid compartment is ~14 L (1/3 of total body fluid).  Why B wrong: 28 L is intracellular fluid.  Why C wrong: 42 L is total body fluid.  Why D wrong: 10.5 L is interstitial fluid."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"Within the extracellular fluid, the intravascular fluid (plasma) volume is approximately:","options":["3 litres","28 litres","14 litres","10.5 litres"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The figure lists intravascular fluid (plasma) ~3 L.  Why B wrong: 28 L is ICF.  Why C wrong: 14 L is total ECF.  Why D wrong: 10.5 L is interstitial fluid."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"Within the extracellular fluid, the extravascular (interstitial) fluid volume is approximately:","options":["10.5 litres","3 litres","28 litres","42 litres"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The figure lists extravascular/interstitial fluid ~10.5 L.  Why B wrong: 3 L is plasma.  Why C wrong: 28 L is ICF.  Why D wrong: 42 L is total body fluid."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"Where is intracellular fluid (ICF) located?","options":["Inside the cells","In the blood vessels only","In the interstitial space","In the lymphatic system only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text defines ICF as fluid present inside the cells.  Why B/C/D wrong: Those describe extracellular compartments."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"ICF is approximately what percentage of total body weight in an average healthy adult (60 kg)?","options":["70% (means 28 litres or 2/3 of total body fluid)","30%","20%","50%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states ICF is approximately 70% in an average healthy adult (60 kg), meaning 28 litres or 2/3 of total body fluid.  Why B/C/D wrong: These contradict the stated percentage/volume."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"Which is the most abundantly present cation in the intracellular fluid (ICF)?","options":["Potassium (K+)","Sodium (Na+)","Chloride (Cl-)","Phosphate (PO4-)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the most abundant cation present in ICF is potassium (K+).  Why B wrong: Sodium is the most abundant cation in ECF.  Why C wrong: Chloride is an anion (most abundant anion in ECF).  Why D wrong: Phosphate is the most abundant anion in ICF, not a cation."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"Which is the most abundantly present anion in the intracellular fluid (ICF)?","options":["Phosphate (PO4-)","Chloride (Cl-)","Bicarbonate","Sulfate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the most abundant anion present in ICF is phosphate (PO4-).  Why B wrong: Chloride is the most abundant anion in ECF.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the stated most abundant ICF anion."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"Where is the extracellular fluid (ECF) located?","options":["Outside the cells","Only inside cells","Only in bone","Only in the brain"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: ECF is the fluid present outside the cells; the text states it is ~30% in an average healthy adult (60 kg).  Why B/C/D wrong: These contradict the definition of extracellular fluid."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"ECF is approximately what percentage of total body weight in an average healthy adult (60 kg)?","options":["30% (means 14 litres or 1/3 of total body fluid)","70%","50%","10%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states ECF is approximately 30% in an average healthy adult (60 kg), meaning 14 litres or 1/3 of total body fluid.  Why B/C/D wrong: 70% is ICF; the others contradict the stated value."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"Which is the most abundantly present cation in the extracellular fluid (ECF)?","options":["Sodium (Na+)","Potassium (K+)","Phosphate (PO4-)","Calcium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the most abundant cation present in ECF is sodium (Na+).  Why B wrong: Potassium is the most abundant cation in ICF.  Why C wrong: Phosphate is an anion.  Why D wrong: Calcium is not the most abundant ECF cation."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"Which is the most abundantly present anion in the extracellular fluid (ECF)?","options":["Chloride (Cl-)","Phosphate (PO4-)","Bicarbonate","Sulfate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the most abundant anion present in ECF is chloride (Cl-).  Why B wrong: Phosphate is the most abundant anion in ICF.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the stated ECF anion."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"ECF fluid is divided into which two types?","options":["Intra vascular fluid and Extra vascular fluid","Plasma and lymph only","Cytoplasm and nucleoplasm","Sweat and saliva"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states ECF is divided into two types: intra vascular fluid and extra vascular fluid.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated classification."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"The intravascular fluid component of ECF is approximately what volume?","options":["3 litres","11 litres","14 litres","28 litres"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states intra vascular fluid is 3 litre.  Why B wrong: 11 litres is the extra vascular fluid.  Why C wrong: 14 L is total ECF.  Why D wrong: 28 L is ICF."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"The extravascular fluid component of ECF is approximately what volume, and is further divided into how many types?","options":["11 litres, further divided into 2 types","3 litres, divided into 5 types","14 litres, divided into 1 type","28 litres, divided into 3 types"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states extra vascular fluid is 11 litres, further divided into 2 types.  Why B/C/D wrong: These contradict the stated value/subdivision."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"Interstitial fluid is described as approximately what volume and located where?","options":["10.5 litre; fluid present between cells (sometimes called third space)","3 litre; inside blood vessels","28 litre; inside cells","1 litre; in joints only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states interstitial fluid = 10.5 litre, fluid present between cells, sometimes called third space.  Why B/C/D wrong: These contradict the stated value/location."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"Transcellular fluid is described as approximately what volume and includes which fluids?","options":["0.5 litre; aqueous humour, serous fluid (CSF), pleural and synovial fluid, & lymphatic fluids","10.5 litre; only plasma","28 litre; intracellular only","3 litre; blood only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states transcellular fluid = 0.5 litre (aqueous humour, serous fluid/CSF, pleural and synovial fluid, & lymphatic fluids).  Why B/C/D wrong: These contradict the stated value/composition."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"Practice Question (reproduced): How much ICF fluid is present in a 70-kg adult?","options":["40","30","42","28"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The reproduced Practice Question is keyed with Right Answer = C in the source; however the ICF value for a 70-kg adult is 28 L per the same page's compartment data. (Source key: Right Answer = C/value 28 L). The ICF for a 70-kg adult is 28 litres.  Why A/B/C wrong: 40 and 30 do not match; 42 L is the total body fluid, not ICF."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"What role do cell membranes play in body fluid distribution?","options":["Cell membranes separate the interstitial fluid from intravascular fluid","They allow free unrestricted mixing of all fluids","They have no role in fluid compartments","They store all body sodium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Transportation System overview states cell membranes separate the interstitial fluid from the intravascular fluid.  Why B/C/D wrong: These contradict the membrane's separating/selective role."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"Cell membranes are described as having which permeability property?","options":["Selectively permeable","Completely impermeable","Freely permeable to everything","Permeable only to proteins"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states cell membranes are selectively permeable.  Why B/C/D wrong: These contradict selective permeability."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"Why must fluids and electrolytes be kept in balance?","options":["Because when they remain out of balance, death can occur","Because imbalance has no effect","Because balance increases body weight","Because they cannot move between compartments"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states fluids and electrolytes must be kept in balance because when they remain out of balance, death can occur.  Why B/C/D wrong: These contradict the stated importance of balance."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"Fluid movement in the human body occurs through which processes? (SATA)","options":["Diffusion","Osmosis","Filtration","Active transport","Combustion"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The text states fluid movement occurs through diffusion, osmosis, filtration, and active transport.  Why E wrong: Combustion is not a fluid transport mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"In the context of solutions, a \"solute\" is defined as:","options":["Substances that dissolve in other substances forming a solution","The substance in which a solute is dissolved","The barrier membrane","A type of pressure"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Solutions text defines solute as substances that dissolve in other substances forming solution.  Why B wrong: That is the definition of solvent.  Why C/D wrong: These do not match the definition."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"A \"solvent\" is defined as:","options":["The substance in which a solute is dissolved","The substance that dissolves","The semipermeable membrane","The osmotic pressure"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Solutions text defines solvent as the substance in which a solute is dissolved.  Why B wrong: That describes the solute.  Why C/D wrong: These do not match the definition."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"Diffusion is defined as:","options":["A fluid transport process in which movement of molecules (solute) from higher area of concentration to a lower area of concentration through a permeable membrane","Movement of solvent through a semipermeable membrane only","Movement against a concentration gradient using energy","Filtration through a pressure gradient"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text defines diffusion as movement of molecules (solute) from a higher area of concentration to a lower area of concentration through a permeable membrane.  Why B wrong: That describes osmosis (solvent movement).  Why C wrong: That describes active transport.  Why D wrong: That describes filtration."},{"section":"Batch 020 · Urine specimen collection & abnormal colors/normal values, Benedict's &…","question":"What does a selectively permeable membrane do regarding diffusion?","options":["It allows some solute to diffuse through it without restriction but prevents other solutes from passing freely","It blocks all movement","It allows everything to pass equally","It only permits water and nothing else"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states a selectively permeable membrane allows some solute to diffuse through it without restriction but prevents other solutes from passing freely.  Why B/C/D wrong: These contradict the selective nature described."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Osmotic pressure exerted by plasma proteins is specifically called what?","options":["Hydrostatic pressure","Colloid osmotic pressure (oncotic pressure)","Filtration pressure","Atmospheric pressure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The osmotic pressure created by plasma proteins is termed colloid osmotic (or oncotic) pressure.  Why A wrong: Hydrostatic pressure is the force exerted by the weight of a solution.  Why C wrong: Filtration pressure relates to movement across a membrane due to pressure differences.  Why D wrong: Atmospheric pressure is unrelated to plasma protein osmotic force."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Colloid osmotic (oncotic) pressure is maintained primarily by which plasma protein?","options":["Globulin","Fibrinogen","Albumin","Hemoglobin"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Oncotic pressure is maintained by blood plasma protein albumin.  Why A/B wrong: Globulin and fibrinogen are plasma proteins but not the primary maintainer of oncotic pressure per the source.  Why D wrong: Hemoglobin is intracellular within RBCs, not a plasma oncotic protein."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"A patient develops generalized edema because albumin levels are low. According to the source, what causes this edema when oncotic pressure drops?","options":["Excess sodium retention","Fluid drawn outside the blood vessels because oncotic pressure cannot maintain it in plasma","Increased red cell mass","Decreased lymph flow only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If albumin drops, oncotic pressure does not maintain fluid in plasma; fluid moves out, causing generalized edema.  Why A wrong: Sodium retention is not the mechanism stated here.  Why C wrong: Red cell mass is unrelated to oncotic edema.  Why D wrong: Lymphatic obstruction is a separate cause, not the albumin/oncotic mechanism described."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Osmotic pressure is best defined as the force involving movement of fluids (solvent) from which direction across a semi-permeable membrane?","options":["From high concentration to low concentration of solute","From low concentration to high concentration of solute","From high temperature to low temperature","Against an electrochemical gradient using ATP"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Osmotic pressure is the force of movement of fluids from the low concentration to the high concentration across a semi-permeable membrane, tending to equalize concentration.  Why A wrong: That describes the opposite direction.  Why C wrong: Temperature is not the driver of osmosis.  Why D wrong: Moving against an electrochemical gradient with ATP describes active transport."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"In osmosis, a semi-permeable membrane behaves how toward solutes and solvent?","options":["Permeable to both solutes and water","Selective: permeable to water but not freely to all solutes present","Impermeable to water only","Permeable to solutes but not to water"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If the membrane is permeable to water but not to all solutes present, the membrane acts as a selective or semi-permeable membrane.  Why A wrong: That describes a fully permeable membrane, not semi-permeable.  Why C/D wrong: These contradict the source's description of selective permeability favoring water movement."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Active transport differs from passive movement primarily because it:","options":["Moves substances from higher to lower concentration without energy","Moves substances from lower to higher concentration and requires energy","Occurs only across capillary walls","Does not involve membrane proteins"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In active transport, substances move from a lower to a higher concentration, requiring energy (reverse natural process of diffusion).  Why A wrong: That describes passive diffusion.  Why C wrong: Active transport occurs across cell membranes generally, not only capillaries.  Why D wrong: Active transport relies on membrane proteins (e.g., sodium, potassium, calcium pumps)."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Which examples does the source list as substances moved by active transport across the cell membrane?","options":["Sodium, potassium, calcium, iron, hydrogen, and amino acids","Only water","Only oxygen and carbon dioxide","Only glucose"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Substances actively transported include sodium, potassium, calcium, iron, hydrogen, and some of the sugars and amino acids.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are incomplete; the source lists multiple ions plus sugars and amino acids."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"The \"Should Remember\" box states active transport requires energy in what form for moving molecules against a concentration or electrochemical gradient?","options":["GTP","ATP","NADH","Heat"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Active transport requires energy in ATP for the process of transporting molecules against a concentration or electrochemical gradient.  Why A/C wrong: GTP and NADH are not cited as the energy currency here.  Why D wrong: Heat is not the energy source for active transport."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Filtration is best defined as the movement of solutes and solvents by:","options":["Oncotic pressure","Hydrostatic pressure","Diffusion alone","Active transport"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Filtration is the movement of solutes and solvents by hydrostatic pressure.  Why A wrong: Oncotic pressure pulls fluid in; it is not the filtration driver.  Why C/D wrong: Diffusion and active transport are different transport processes."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"In filtration, movement is from an area of __________ pressure to an area of __________ pressure.","options":["Lower; higher","Higher; lower","Equal; equal","Negative; positive"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In filtration, movement is from an area of higher pressure to an area of lower pressure.  Why A wrong: This reverses the direction.  Why C/D wrong: These do not match the source statement."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Hydrostatic pressure is defined as:","options":["The force exerted by plasma proteins","The force exerted by the weight of a solution","The osmotic pull of the lymphatic system","The pressure of dissolved gases"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hydrostatic pressure is the force exerted by the weight of a solution.  Why A wrong: That is oncotic (colloid osmotic) pressure.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the definition of hydrostatic pressure."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Within a vessel, when the difference exists in hydrostatic pressure between two sides of a membrane, water and diffusible solutes move out of the solution by what process?","options":["Active transport","The process of filtration","Osmosis only","Endocytosis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: When the difference exists in hydrostatic pressure on two sides of a membrane, water and diffusible solutes move out of the solution by the process of filtration.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are different transport mechanisms not described here."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"At the arterial end of the capillary, how does hydrostatic pressure compare to the osmotic pressure?","options":["Hydrostatic pressure is higher than the osmotic pressure, so fluids and diffusible solutes move out of the capillaries","Hydrostatic pressure is lower, so fluid moves in","They are equal, so no net movement occurs","Osmotic pressure is absent at the arterial end"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: At the arterial end of the capillary, the hydrostatic pressure is higher than the osmotic pressure, so fluids and diffusible solutes move out of the capillaries.  Why B wrong: This describes the venous end.  Why C wrong: A net outward movement occurs at the arterial end.  Why D wrong: Osmotic pressure is present but lower than hydrostatic at this point."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"At the venous end of the capillary, fluid moves how relative to the capillary?","options":["Out of the capillary because hydrostatic pressure is higher","Into the capillary because osmotic (oncotic) pressure is higher than hydrostatic pressure, pulling fluids and some solutes in","No movement occurs","Out into lymphatics only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: At the venous end, the osmotic pressure or pull is higher than the hydrostatic pressure, and fluids and some solutes move into the capillaries.  Why A wrong: That is the arterial end.  Why C wrong: There is net inward movement.  Why D wrong: Excess interstitial fluid returns via lymphatics, but the venous end describes reabsorption into the capillary."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Excess fluids and solutes remaining in the interstitial space are returned to the intravascular compartment by which system?","options":["The renal system","The lymph channels (lymphatic system)","The portal vein","The biliary system"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The excess fluids and solutes remaining in the interstitial space are returned to the intravascular compartment by the lymph channels.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not perform interstitial fluid return as described."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Osmolality is defined as the concentration of solute per:","options":["Litre of solution","Kilogram of water (milliosmole per kg)","Milliliter of plasma","Mole of solute"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Osmolality refers to concentration of solute per kilogram of water (millimole or milliosmole per kg).  Why A wrong: That defines osmolarity (per litre).  Why C/D wrong: These do not match the definition."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Osmolality is the concentration of a:","options":["Solid","Solution","Gas","Suspension only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Osmolality is the concentration of a solution.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies a solution."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"In the body, osmotic pressure (concentration) is measured in:","options":["mEq/L","mg/dL","mOsm (milliosmoles)","mmHg"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In the body, osmotic pressure is measured in milliosmoles (mOsm).  Why A/B/D wrong: These are other measurement units not used for osmotic pressure here."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"What is the normal osmolality of plasma?","options":["135–145 mOsm/kg water","275–295 mOsm/kg water","300–306 mOsm/L","200–250 mOsm/kg water"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The normal osmolality of plasma is 275–295 mOsm/kg water.  Why A wrong: 135–145 is the normal serum sodium range.  Why C wrong: 306 mOsm/L is the value cited for osmolarity equivalence, not osmolality range.  Why D wrong: Incorrect range."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Osmolarity refers to the concentration of solute per:","options":["Kilogram of water","Litre (milliosmoles per litre)","Gram of solute","Deciliter of plasma"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Osmolarity refers to concentration of solute per litre (milliosmoles per litre).  Why A wrong: That is osmolality.  Why C/D wrong: Incorrect units."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"The osmolality of normal plasma (285 mOsm/kg) is equivalent to a plasma water osmolarity of approximately:","options":["275 mOsm/L","295 mOsm/L","306 mOsm/L","320 mOsm/L"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The osmolality of normal plasma (285 mOsm/kg) is equivalent to a plasma water osmolarity of 306 mOsm/L.  Why A/B/D wrong: These do not match the stated equivalence."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Based on osmolarity, fluid is divided into which three types?","options":["Acidic, neutral, basic","Isotonic, hypotonic, hypertonic","Crystalloid, colloid, blood","Maintenance, replacement, resuscitation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Based on osmolarity, fluid is divided into three types: isotonic, hypotonic, and hypertonic.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the osmolarity-based classification given."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"An isotonic solution is described as one in which:","options":["The solution has a higher concentration of solutes than the cell","The solution has a lower concentration of solutes than the cell","The solution on each side of a selectively permeable membrane has equal concentration, or the same as plasma (in the range of 270–300 mOsm/L, similar to body fluids)","There are no solutes present"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: When the solution on both sides of a selectively permeable membrane has established equilibrium or is equal in concentration, they are isotonic, in the range 270–300 mOsm/L, similar to body fluids.  Why A wrong: Higher concentration describes hypertonic.  Why B wrong: Lower concentration describes hypotonic.  Why D wrong: Isotonic still contains solutes."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Isotonic solutions are isotonic to which body fluids?","options":["Cerebrospinal fluid only","Human cells (i.e., body fluids)","Gastric juice","Bile"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Isotonic solutions are isotonic to human cells.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies human cells/body fluids generally."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Which of the following are examples of isotonic solutions listed in the source? (SATA)","options":["0.9% Normal Saline (NS)","Ringer Lactate (RL)","D5%","D5% + 0.225% NS","3% Saline"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists 0.9% Normal Saline, Ringer Lactate, D5%, and D5% + 0.225% NS as isotonic solutions.  Why E wrong: 3% Saline is a hypertonic solution, not isotonic."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"A hypotonic solution is one that:","options":["Contains a lower concentration of salt or solute than another, more concentrated solution","Contains a higher concentration of solute than the cell","Has the same osmolarity as plasma","Contains no water"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A hypotonic solution contains a lower concentration of salt or solute than another, more concentrated solution.  Why B wrong: That is hypertonic.  Why C wrong: That is isotonic.  Why D wrong: Hypotonic solutions still contain water (in fact relatively more free water)."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"When a hypotonic solution is infused, what happens to body fluid balance per the source?","options":["The body has less fluid or more water than ideal; the solution has fewer dissolved solutes than the cell (range < 280 mOsm/L)","The body retains more solute than water","There is no change in cell volume","Cells shrink due to water loss"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A hypotonic solution has less salt or more water than an isotonic solution; an isotonic solution becomes hypotonic when the body has fewer dissolved solutes than the cell, in the range of <280 mOsm/L.  Why B wrong: Hypotonic means relatively more water, fewer solutes.  Why C/D wrong: Hypotonic fluid causes cells to gain water/swell, not shrink or stay unchanged."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"A hypotonic solution is hypotonic to the cells, so water tends to move into the cells in an attempt to bring about what?","options":["Cell shrinkage","Balance or equality (equilibrium)","Hemolysis prevention","Increased solute concentration"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hypotonic solution is hypotonic to the cells, therefore reveals would tend to move into the cell in an attempt to bring about balance or equality.  Why A wrong: Water entry causes swelling, not shrinkage.  Why C/D wrong: Not the stated purpose."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Which of the following are examples of hypotonic solutions listed? (SATA)","options":["0.45% Normal Saline (NS)","0.225% Normal Saline (NS)","0.9% Normal Saline","Ringer Lactate","3% Saline"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists 0.45% Normal Saline and 0.225% Normal Saline as hypotonic solution examples.  Why C/D wrong: These are isotonic solutions.  Why E wrong: 3% Saline is hypertonic."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"A hypertonic solution is defined as one that:","options":["Has a lower concentration of solute than another solution","Has a higher concentration of solutes (more concentrated, higher osmolality than another) — isotonic becomes hypertonic when body fluids have a higher concentration (>300 mOsm/L)","Equals plasma osmolarity","Contains only water"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A hypertonic solution has a higher concentration than another; these solutions have a higher osmolality than body fluids, and an isotonic becomes hypertonic when the body fluids have a higher concentration (>300 mOsm/L).  Why A wrong: That is hypotonic.  Why C wrong: That is isotonic.  Why D wrong: Hypertonic solutions are concentrated with solutes."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Which of the following are examples of hypertonic solutions listed in the source? (SATA)","options":["5% Normal Saline (NS)","3% Normal Saline (NS)","D10%","D5% (D5% + 0.45% NS)","Mannitol"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists 5% NS, 3% NS, D10%, D5% (D5% + 0.45% NS), and Mannitol as hypertonic solutions.  Why none wrong: All listed are hypertonic per source."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"(PYQ) Which type of fluid creates an osmotic gradient and drives fluid from the interstitial space into the intravascular space?","options":["Hypotonic","Isotonic","Hypertonic","Normotonic"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: A hypertonic solution creates an osmotic gradient and draws (drives) fluid from the interstitial space into the intravascular space.  Why A wrong: Hypotonic fluid moves water into cells.  Why B wrong: Isotonic fluid does not create an osmotic shift.  Why D wrong: Not the answer (Right Answer is B per the page's key — see note); per the recall box the keyed answer is B. [Source-keyed answer: B]"},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"(PYQ) What is the normal plasma osmolality range in an adult?","options":["280–290 mOsm/L","320–330 mOsm/L","275–295 mOsm/L","300–340 mOsm/L"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The normal plasma osmolality range in an adult is 275–295 mOsm/L (keyed Right Answer B on page corresponds to this value).  Why A/B/D wrong: These fall outside the normal osmolality range."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"(PYQ) Which solution causes the shifting of fluid from the cell to the intravascular compartment?","options":["Isotonic","Hypotonic","Hypertonic","Diffusion"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: A hypertonic solution causes shifting of fluid from the cell into the intravascular compartment (water leaves the cell). [Source-keyed answer: B]  Why A wrong: Isotonic causes no shift.  Why B wrong: Hypotonic moves water into cells.  Why D wrong: Diffusion is not a solution type."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Homeostasis refers to:","options":["The breakdown of body tissues for energy","The tendency of the body to maintain the stability of internal environment (temperature, BP, acidity, water level, body normal circumstances) or a stability of the internal environment","The active transport of sodium only","The clotting of blood"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Homeostasis is the tendency of the body to maintain the stability of the internal environment of the body — temperature, BP, acidity, and water level — i.e., a stability of the internal environment.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are unrelated to the definition of homeostasis."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Which organs play an important role in maintaining balance in fluid and electrolytes?","options":["The lungs","The kidneys","The skin","The liver"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The kidneys play an important role in maintaining balance in fluid and electrolytes.  Why A/C/D wrong: While these participate in fluid loss/regulation, the source emphasizes the kidneys for this balance."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Thirst mechanism is maintained by which process?","options":["Aldosterone","Hypothalamus","ADH alone","Renin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Thirst mechanism is maintained by hypothalamus.  Why A wrong: Aldosterone regulates sodium/potassium, not thirst directly per source.  Why C wrong: ADH maintains osmoreceptor function but thirst is hypothalamic here.  Why D wrong: Renin is part of RAAS, not the thirst center."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Osmoreceptor function is maintained by which hormone?","options":["ADH (antidiuretic hormone) or hypothalamus","Aldosterone","Cortisol","Insulin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Osmoreceptor function is maintained by ADH (antidiuretic hormone) or hypothalamus.  Why B/C/D wrong: These hormones are not cited for osmoreceptor function here."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Approximately how much water is released by the metabolism of each 100 calories of fat, carbohydrate, or protein?","options":["About 5 ml","About 10 ml","About 50 ml","About 100 ml"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: About 10 ml of water is released by the metabolism of each 100 calories of fat, carbohydrate, or protein.  Why A/C/D wrong: Incorrect quantities."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"For a normal adult male taking about 3000 calories of food, the body will release approximately how much fluid from oxidation of food?","options":["100 ml","200 ml","300 ml","500 ml"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: A normal adult taking 3000 calorie food will release 300 ml of fluid from oxidation of food.  Why A/B/D wrong: Incorrect amounts."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Match the daily intake value: Water in food provides approximately how much per day?","options":["1000–1500 ml/day","800–1000 ml/day","300 ml/day","1500–2800 ml/day"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Water in food provides 800–1000 ml/day.  Why A wrong: 1000–1500 ml/day is \"orally\" intake.  Why C wrong: 300 ml/day is water formed by oxidation of foods.  Why D wrong: 1500–2800 ml/day is the total intake."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"According to the intake table, the total daily fluid intake is approximately:","options":["1000–1500 ml/day","800–1000 ml/day","300 ml/day","1500–2800 ml/day"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Total intake is 1500–2800 ml/day (oral 1000–1500 + water in food 800–1000 + oxidation 300).  Why A/B/C wrong: These are individual components, not the total."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Water lost through the skin in a sensible individual is unaware of losing that water and is approximately:","options":["100–200 ml/day","400–600 ml/day","1000–1500 ml/day","2500–2800 ml/day"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Water lost through the skin (insensible) where the individual is unaware of losing that water is approximately 400–600 ml/day.  Why A/C/D wrong: Incorrect quantities for skin loss."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Water lost by the lungs depends on rate and depth of respiration and is approximately:","options":["100–200 ml/day","400–600 ml/day","1500 ml/day","2800 ml/day"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Water lost by lungs depends on rate and depth of respiration and is approximately 400–600 ml/day.  Why A/C/D wrong: Incorrect values."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Water lost by perspiration approximates 100 ml/day. This loss varies according to what factors?","options":["Temperature of environment and body activity","Blood pressure only","Heart rate only","Renal function only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Water lost by perspiration is about 100 ml/day, varying according to the temperature of the environment and the body activity.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated determinants of perspiration loss."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Water loss through the kidneys via urination is approximately how much per day?","options":["100 ml/day","400–600 ml/day","1500 ml/day","2800 ml/day"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Water loss through the kidneys via urination is about 1500 ml/day.  Why A wrong: 100 ml/day is perspiration loss.  Why B wrong: 400–600 ml/day is skin or lung loss.  Why D wrong: Not the urinary value."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"A large quantity of water is secreted into the GI tract but almost all of it is:","options":["Excreted in stool","Reabsorbed","Lost via perspiration","Converted to ATP"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A large quantity of water is secreted into the gastrointestinal tract, but almost all of this fluid is reabsorbed.  Why A wrong: Only a small amount is lost in stool; most is reabsorbed.  Why C/D wrong: Not the GI fluid fate."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"(Output table) Insensible water loss through the lungs is approximately:","options":["100–150 ml/day","600–800 ml/day","100–200 ml/day","1500 ml/day"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The output table lists insensible water loss through lungs as 100–150 ml/day.  Why B wrong: 600–800 ml is insensible water loss through skin.  Why C wrong: 100–200 ml is water loss through the GI tract.  Why D wrong: 1500 ml/day is urine output."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"(Output table) Insensible water loss through the skin is approximately:","options":["100–150 ml/day","600–800 ml/day","100–200 ml/day","1500 ml/day"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The output table lists insensible water loss through skin as 600–800 ml/day.  Why A/C/D wrong: These correspond to lungs, GI tract, and urine respectively."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"(Output table) Water loss through the GI tract is approximately:","options":["100–150 ml/day","600–800 ml/day","100–200 ml/day","1500 ml/day"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The output table lists water loss through the GI tract as 100–200 ml/day.  Why A/B/D wrong: These correspond to lungs, skin, and urine."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"(Output table) Through kidneys (urine), the daily output quantity is:","options":["100–150 ml/day","600–800 ml/day","1500 ml/day","2500–2800 ml/day"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Through kidneys (urine), the output is 1500 ml/day.  Why D wrong: 2500–2800 ml/day is the total output, not the urine alone.  Why A/B wrong: These are lung and skin losses."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"(Output table) The total daily fluid output is approximately:","options":["1500 ml/day","2500–2800 ml/day","100–200 ml/day","600–800 ml/day"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The total output per the table is 2500–2800 ml/day.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are individual components."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"\"Should Remember\": In a human body, total insensible water loss (from lungs and skin) is about how much?","options":["100–200 ml/day","600–900 ml/day","1500 ml/day","2800 ml/day"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The box states in a human body, total insensible water loss (lungs + skin) is about 600–900 ml/day.  Why A/C/D wrong: Incorrect totals."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"(PYQ) Nurse Jeneny is evaluating a client's fluid intake and output. Fluid intake and urine output should relate in which way?","options":["Fluid intake should double the urine output","Fluid intake should be approximately equal to the urine output","Urine output should be triple the intake","Fluid intake should be unrelated to output"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Normally, fluid intake is approximately equal to the urine output, because other relationship signals an abnormality; fluid intake that is double the urine output indicates fluid retention, and fluid intake that is half the urine output indicates fluid loss; fluid intake is inversely proportional to the urine output. The keyed Right Answer is B (intake ≈ output).  Why A wrong: Intake double output indicates fluid retention.  Why C wrong: Not a normal relationship.  Why D wrong: They are physiologically related."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Edema is defined as:","options":["Loss of fluid from the body","Excess accumulation of fluid in the interstitial space between cells, resulting from increased oncotic pressure, hydrostatic pressure, capillary permeability, and lymphatic obstruction","A drop in serum sodium","Cellular shrinkage"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Edema is an excess accumulation of fluid in the interstitial space (between cells), occurring as a result of increased oncotic pressure, hydrostatic pressure, capillary permeability, and lymphatic obstruction.  Why A wrong: That is fluid deficit, not edema.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the definition of edema."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Localized edema occurs as a result of which mechanism?","options":["Cardiac, renal, or liver failure","Traumatic injury from accident or surgery, and local inflammatory process or burns","Allergic reaction only","Pitting on the dorsum of the foot only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Localized edema occurs as a result of traumatic injury from accident or surgery and local inflammatory process or burns.  Why A wrong: That describes generalized edema.  Why C/D wrong: Those describe angioedema and pitting edema respectively."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Generalized edema is also called anasarca and is an accumulation of fluid in the interstitial space throughout the body as a result of conditions such as:","options":["Local burns only","Cardiac, renal, liver failure","Insect sting","Surgical wound"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Generalized edema (also called anasarca) is an accumulation of fluid in the interstitial space throughout the body as a result of conditions such as cardiac, renal, or liver failure.  Why A/C/D wrong: These cause localized edema or angioedema."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Angioedema is best described as:","options":["Pitting that slowly disappears","Painless swelling under the skin, triggered by allergy to an animal dander, pollen, drugs, venom (treated with medication)","Fluid in the lung interstitium","Excess sodium loss"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Angioedema is painless swelling under the skin, triggered by allergy to an animal dander, pollen, drugs, venom (treated with medication).  Why A wrong: That describes pitting edema.  Why C/D wrong: Not the description of angioedema."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Pitting edema is best described as:","options":["Painless allergic swelling","Disc swelling that is caused by increased intracranial pressure due to lung injury","Generalized body-wide edema from organ failure","Edema caused only by burns"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states papilloedema is disc swelling caused by increased intracranial pressure due to lung injury (papilloedema entry).  Why A wrong: That is angioedema.  Why C wrong: That is generalized edema/anasarca.  Why D wrong: That is localized edema."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Dehydration is best defined as:","options":["Excess accumulation of interstitial fluid","A fluid volume deficit; it occurs when the fluid intake of the body is not sufficient to meet the fluid need of the body","A rise in serum sodium only","Loss of electrolytes only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Dehydration is also known as fluid volume deficit; it occurs when the fluid intake of the body is not sufficient to meet the fluid need of the body.  Why A wrong: That is edema.  Why C/D wrong: These are incomplete and not the overall definition."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Which of the following are stated goals of treatment in dehydration? (SATA)","options":["Restore fluid volume","Replace electrolytes as needed","Eliminate the cause of the fluid volume deficit","Increase oncotic pressure with albumin","Induce diuresis"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The goal of the treatment is to restore fluid volume, replace electrolytes as needed, and eliminate the cause of the fluid volume deficit.  Why D wrong: Not listed as a goal here.  Why E wrong: Inducing diuresis would worsen dehydration."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Based on severity, \"Mild\" dehydration corresponds to total fluid loss reaching what percentage?","options":["5% or less","5–10%","More than 10%","20%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Mild dehydration is total fluid loss reached 5% or less.  Why B wrong: 5–10% is moderate.  Why C wrong: More than 10% is severe.  Why D wrong: Not a category stated."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Based on severity, \"Moderate\" dehydration corresponds to total fluid loss of:","options":["5% or less","5–10%","More than 10%","30%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Moderate dehydration is total fluid loss reached between 5% and 10%.  Why A wrong: 5% or less is mild.  Why C wrong: More than 10% is severe.  Why D wrong: Not stated."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"\"Severe\" dehydration is total fluid loss reached more than 10%; it is described as:","options":["A minor outpatient concern","An emergency case that should start IV fluids","Treated with oral fluids only","Asymptomatic"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Severe dehydration is total fluid loss reached more than 10%; it is considered an emergency case and should start IV fluids.  Why A/C/D wrong: Severe dehydration requires IV fluids and is an emergency."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"In isotonic dehydration, water and dissolved electrolytes are lost in what proportion?","options":["Water exceeds electrolytes","Electrolytes exceed water","Equal proportions","No electrolytes are lost"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In isotonic dehydration, water and dissolved electrolytes are lost in equal proportions.  Why A wrong: That is hypertonic dehydration.  Why B wrong: That is hypotonic dehydration.  Why D wrong: Electrolytes are lost in isotonic dehydration."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"In isotonic dehydration, the serum sodium level is:","options":[">145 mEq/L","<135 mEq/L","135–145 mEq/L (not changed)","<120 mEq/L"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In isotonic dehydration, the serum level is 135–145 mEq/L (sodium not changed; the most common type of dehydration).  Why A wrong: >145 is hypertonic.  Why B/D wrong: <135 is hypotonic."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Isotonic dehydration results in decreased circulating blood volume and inadequate tissue perfusion. Which are stated causes? (SATA)","options":["Inadequate intake of fluids and solutes","Fluid shift between compartments","Excessive losses of isotonic body fluids","Excessive perspiration only","Diabetes insipidus"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Causes of isotonic dehydration include inadequate intake of fluids and solutes, fluid shift between compartments, and excessive losses of isotonic body fluids.  Why D/E wrong: Excessive perspiration and diabetes insipidus are causes of hypertonic dehydration."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"In hypertonic dehydration, water loss exceeds electrolyte loss, so the serum sodium level is:","options":[">145 mEq/L","<135 mEq/L","135–145 mEq/L","<115 mEq/L"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In hypertonic dehydration, water loss exceeds electrolyte loss (too much water lost rather than electrolytes), and sodium level is >145 mEq/L.  Why B/D wrong: Low sodium describes hypotonic dehydration.  Why C wrong: Normal sodium describes isotonic dehydration."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"In hypertonic dehydration, fluid moves from the ICF to ECF, resulting in:","options":["Cellular swelling","Cellular dehydration and shrinkage","No change in cell size","Hemolysis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Fluid moves from the ICF to ECF where the plasma and interstitial fluid spaces lead to cellular dehydration and shrinkage.  Why A wrong: Cells shrink, not swell.  Why C/D wrong: Not the described outcome."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Which of the following are listed causes of hypertonic dehydration? (SATA)","options":["Excessive perspiration or sweating","Hyperventilation","Ketoacidosis and prolonged fever","Early-stage kidney diseases and diabetes insipidus","Chronic malnutrition"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Causes of hypertonic dehydration include excessive perspiration/sweating, hyperventilation, ketoacidosis and prolonged fever, and early-stage kidney diseases and diabetes insipidus.  Why E wrong: Chronic malnutrition is a cause of hypotonic dehydration."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"In hypotonic dehydration, electrolyte loss exceeds water loss, so the serum sodium level is:","options":[">145 mEq/L","<135 mEq/L","135–145 mEq/L","Unchanged"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In hypotonic dehydration, electrolyte loss exceeds water loss (too much electrolytes lost rather than water), and sodium level is <135 mEq/L.  Why A wrong: High sodium is hypertonic.  Why C/D wrong: Normal/unchanged is isotonic."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"In hypotonic dehydration, fluid shift from ECF to ICF occurs, resulting in:","options":["Cellular dehydration","A plasma and interstitial fluid space (fluid volume deficit) and the cells swell","No cellular change","Hemoconcentration only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Fluid shifts from the ECF to ICF, causing a plasma and interstitial fluid space deficit and the cells swell.  Why A wrong: That describes hypertonic dehydration.  Why C/D wrong: Not the described effect."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Which of the following are listed causes of hypotonic dehydration? (SATA)","options":["Chronic illness","Excessive fluid replacement (hypotonic fluid)","Kidney disease","Chronic malnutrition","Diabetes insipidus"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Causes of hypotonic dehydration include chronic illness, excessive fluid replacement (hypotonic fluid), kidney disease, and chronic malnutrition.  Why E wrong: Diabetes insipidus is a cause of hypertonic dehydration."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Sodium (Na+) is the most abundant cation in which body compartment?","options":["Intracellular fluid (ICF)","Extracellular fluid (ECF)","Cerebrospinal fluid","Bone matrix"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Sodium is the most abundant cation in extracellular fluid (ECF).  Why A wrong: Potassium is the most abundant intracellular cation.  Why C/D wrong: Not the stated compartment."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"What is the normal serum sodium level?","options":["3.5–5 mEq/L","135–145 mEq/L","8.6–10 mg/dL","275–295 mEq/L"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Normal serum sodium is 135–145 mEq/L.  Why A wrong: That is potassium.  Why C wrong: That is calcium.  Why D wrong: That is plasma osmolality range, not sodium."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Hyponatremia is defined as a serum sodium level of:","options":[">145 mEq/L","<135 mEq/L (low level of sodium)","135–145 mEq/L","<3.5 mEq/L"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hyponatremia is a serum sodium <135 mEq/L (low level of sodium).  Why A wrong: That is hypernatremia.  Why C wrong: That is normal.  Why D wrong: That is hypokalemia."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Hypernatremia is defined as a serum sodium level of:","options":[">145 mEq/L (high level of sodium)","<135 mEq/L","135–145 mEq/L",">5 mEq/L"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hypernatremia is a serum sodium >145 mEq/L (high level of sodium).  Why B wrong: That is hyponatremia.  Why C wrong: That is normal.  Why D wrong: That refers to potassium."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Altered cerebral function is the most common manifestation of which electrolyte abnormality?","options":["Hypokalemia","Hypernatremia","Hypocalcemia","Hyperkalemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Altered cerebral function is the most common manifestation of hypernatremia.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not associated with this as the most common manifestation per source."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Where is most of the body's sodium reabsorbed?","options":["Distal tubule","Proximal tubule","Collecting duct","Loop of Henle ascending limb only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Sodium is basic to communication in decreased reabsorption; most of sodium is reabsorbed at proximal tubule.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the primary site per source."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Which of the following are listed as high-sodium foods? (SATA)","options":["Table salt","Celery","Processed foods, fast foods, pickled food, condiments, canned foods","Smoked meats and aged cheese","Bananas"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Foods high in sodium include table salt, celery, processed food, fast foods, pickled food, condiments, canned foods, smoked meats, and aged cheese.  Why E wrong: Bananas are a high-potassium food, not high-sodium."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Potassium (K+) is the most abundant cation in which compartment?","options":["Extracellular fluid (ECF)","Intracellular fluid (ICF)","Plasma","Interstitial fluid"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Potassium is the most abundant cation in intracellular fluid (ICF).  Why A/C/D wrong: Sodium is the predominant extracellular/plasma cation."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Potassium is needed for what physiologic functions per the source?","options":["Resting polarization and repolarization of nerve and muscle, depend on potassium level","Bone mineralization","Oncotic pressure maintenance","Blood clotting"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Potassium is needed for resting polarization and repolarization of nerve and muscle and depends on potassium level.  Why B wrong: That is calcium's role.  Why C wrong: That is albumin's role.  Why D wrong: That is calcium/clotting factors' role."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Which of the following are listed high-potassium foods? (SATA)","options":["Banana","Avocados, oranges, dates","Apricots, cantaloupe, potatoes","Milk and raisins","Table salt"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Foods high in potassium include banana, avocados, oranges, dates, apricots, cantaloupe, potatoes, milk, and raisins.  Why E wrong: Table salt is a high-sodium food."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Most of the secretion or excretion of potassium occurs at which site?","options":["Proximal tubule","PCT and collecting duct (DCT/collecting tubule)","Loop of Henle only","Glomerulus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Most secretion of potassium occurs at PCT and collecting duct (distal nephron secretion).  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the primary potassium secretion site per source."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"The secretion of potassium is regulated by which hormone?","options":["ADH","Aldosterone","Insulin","Cortisol"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The secretion of potassium is regulated by aldosterone hormone.  Why A wrong: ADH regulates water.  Why C/D wrong: Not the regulator of potassium secretion described."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Hyperkalemia is defined as a serum potassium level greater than:","options":["3.5 mEq/L (resulting in cardiac contraction weakness)","5 mEq/L","145 mEq/L","10 mg/dL"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hyperkalemia is a serum potassium level >5 mEq/L resulting in cardiac contraction weakness.  Why A wrong: 3.5 mEq/L is the low-end normal/threshold for hypokalemia.  Why C wrong: That is the sodium range.  Why D wrong: That is calcium units."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"If extremely high concentration of potassium in ECF (and at heart) is present, what cardiac and arterial outcome will occur?","options":["Increased heart rate","Cardiac arrest and arterial vasoconstriction or destruction of arteries","No effect","Hypertension only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If extremely high concentration of potassium in ECF and at heart is present, cardiac arrest and arterial vasoconstriction or destruction will occur.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not reflect the severe effect described."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Which of the following are ECG changes in hyperkalemia? (SATA)","options":["Tall peaked T wave","Flat P wave","Widened QRS complex","Prolonged PR interval","U wave"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: ECG changes in hyperkalemia include tall peaked T wave, flat P wave, widened QRS complex, and prolonged PR interval.  Why E wrong: A U wave is an ECG change of hypokalemia, not hyperkalemia."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"The M/C (most common) clinical features of hyperkalemia include GI disturbances such as:","options":["Increased peristalsis or diarrhea","Hyperactive bowel sound and diarrhea","Constipation only","Absent bowel sounds"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: M/C clinical features of hyperkalemia include GI disturbances such as increased peristalsis, hyperactive bowel sound and diarrhea.  Why A wrong: Incomplete; the source emphasizes hyperactive bowel sounds and diarrhea.  Why C/D wrong: These are features of hypokalemia."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"To administer potassium, sodium polystyrene (Kayexalate, K-Bind) is used as an antidote of hyperkalemia. Its mechanism is to:","options":["Increase potassium absorption","Promote gastrointestinal sodium absorption and potassium excretion","Cause renal potassium retention","Block aldosterone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Sodium polystyrene (K-Bind/Kayexalate) is an antidote of hyperkalemia that promotes gastrointestinal sodium absorption and potassium excretion.  Why A/C/D wrong: These contradict the drug's mechanism described."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"A patient has critically high, life-threatening potassium. Per the source, what is indicated?","options":["Oral potassium supplements","Prepare the client for dialysis if potassium level is critically high","Increase potassium-rich foods","Restrict fluids"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Prepare the client for dialysis if potassium level is critically high.  Why A/C wrong: These would worsen hyperkalemia.  Why D wrong: Fluid restriction is not the stated intervention for critical hyperkalemia."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"A 10% calcium gluconate is given to overt which complication of severe hyperkalemia?","options":["Overt cardiac instability (to give calcium gluconate 10% if hyperkalemia is severe to overt myocardial instability)","Renal failure","Respiratory arrest","Hypotension"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Give calcium gluconate 10% if hyperkalemia is severe to overt myocardial instability.  Why B/C/D wrong: The stated purpose is to counter myocardial/cardiac instability."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Hypokalemia is defined as a serum potassium level less than:","options":["3.5 mEq/L (leading to polarization in nerve and muscle fibers; weakness and paralysis)","5 mEq/L","8.6 mg/dL","135 mEq/L"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hypokalemia is a serum potassium level <3.5 mEq/L leading to polarization in nerve and muscles fibers, resulting in weakness and paralysis.  Why B wrong: >5 mEq/L is hyperkalemia.  Why C wrong: That is calcium units.  Why D wrong: That is the sodium range."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Which of the following are ECG changes in hypokalemia? (SATA)","options":["ST segment depression","Shallow, flat, or inverted T wave (T wave inversion)","Prominent U wave","Slightly prolonged PR interval","Tall peaked T wave"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: ECG changes in hypokalemia include ST segment depression, shallow/flat or inverted T wave, prominent U wave, and slightly prolonged PR interval.  Why E wrong: A tall peaked T wave is an ECG change of hyperkalemia."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"The M/C clinical features of hypokalemia include GI disturbances such as:","options":["Diarrhea and hyperactive bowel sounds","Nausea and vomiting, hypoactive or absent bowel sound, constipation, abdominal distention, and paralytic ileus","Increased peristalsis","Hematemesis only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: M/C clinical features of hypokalemia include GI disturbance such as nausea and vomiting, hypoactive or absent bowel sound, constipation, abdominal distention, and paralytic ileus.  Why A/C wrong: These describe hyperkalemia.  Why D wrong: Not the described features."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"For oral potassium supplements in hypokalemia, the nurse should educate the patient to take potassium:","options":["On an empty stomach with cold water","With juice or water and never on an empty stomach","Crushed and dry","Only at bedtime without fluids"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Oral potassium supplements may cause nausea and vomiting and should not be taken on an empty stomach; liquid (because it has unpleasant taste) should be taken with juice or water.  Why A/C/D wrong: These contradict the safe administration education."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Regarding nerve action potential, repolarization is caused by which ion movement?","options":["Closing of sodium ion channels and the opening of potassium ion channels","Opening of sodium channels only","Closing of calcium channels","Influx of sodium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Repolarization is caused by the closing of sodium ion channels and the opening of potassium ion channels.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe depolarization or unrelated movements."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Hence, potassium is needed for which phase of the nerve action potential?","options":["Depolarization","Repolarization","Resting only","Threshold initiation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hence, potassium is needed for repolarization.  Why A wrong: Sodium drives depolarization.  Why C/D wrong: Not the stated role."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Hyperpolarization occurs due to an excess of which ion across the cell, and this prevents depolarization?","options":["Sodium","Open potassium channels (excess potassium)","Calcium","Chloride"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hyperpolarization occurs due to an excess of open potassium channels (potassium); this excess of potassium prevents depolarization.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the stated ion responsible for hyperpolarization."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Depolarization is caused by which ion channel event?","options":["The opening of sodium channels and closing of potassium ion channels","Opening of potassium channels","Closing of sodium channels","Calcium influx only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Depolarization is caused by the opening of sodium channels and closing of potassium ion channels.  Why B/C wrong: These describe repolarization/hyperpolarization.  Why D wrong: Not the described mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Precaution to be taken giving IV potassium: Why is potassium never administered by IV push, intramuscular, or subcutaneous route?","options":["It causes hyperpolarization","If potassium is always diluted (1 mEq/ml is recommended) and administered using an infusion device; rapid IV/IM push can cause cardiac arrest","It precipitates with calcium","It is destroyed by gastric acid"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Potassium is never administered by IV push, IM, or SC route; IV potassium is always diluted (1 mEq/ml is recommended) and administered using an infusion device.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the stated precaution rationale."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"The maximum recommended dilution rate for potassium infusion is:","options":["1 mEq/L","0.5 mEq/hour","0.5–10 mEq/hour, never to exceed 1 mEq/hour under any circumstances","20 mEq/hour"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The maximum recommended dilution rate is 0.5–10 mEq/hour, never to exceed 1 mEq/hour under any circumstances (per source statement on the infusion rate).  Why A/B/D wrong: These do not match the stated rate/limit."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Because potassium infusion can cause phlebitis, the nurse should assess the IV site frequently for signs of:","options":["Bleeding","Phlebitis or infiltration","Air embolism","Hematoma only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Potassium infusion can cause phlebitis, therefore the nurse should assess the IV site frequently for the signs of phlebitis or infiltration.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the stated assessment focus."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"The nurse should assess which organ's function before administering potassium supplements?","options":["Hepatic (liver)","Renal (kidney) function","Cardiac only","Pulmonary"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The nurse should assess the renal function before administering potassium supplements.  Why A/C/D wrong: Renal function is the stated assessment because kidneys excrete potassium."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Calcium (Ca++) forms salts with which substances to provide structure in bones and teeth?","options":["Sodium and potassium","Phosphates, carbonate, and fluoride","Chloride only","Bicarbonate only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Calcium forms salts with phosphates, carbonate, and fluoride in bones and teeth to harden them.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the stated salts."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Tooth enamel is comprised of which mineral compound?","options":["Calcium chloride","Calcium phosphate, arranged in a crystal structure known as hydroxyapatite","Calcium carbonate","Calcium fluoride only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Tooth enamel is comprised of the mineral calcium phosphate, arranged in a crystal structure known as hydroxyapatite.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the stated composition."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Which of the following are listed foods high in calcium? (SATA)","options":["Milk and dairy products","Canned fish with bones","Whole grain, legumes, and green leafy vegetables","Bananas","Table salt"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Foods high in calcium include milk and dairy products, canned fish with bones, whole grain, legumes, and green leaf vegetables.  Why D wrong: Bananas are high in potassium.  Why E wrong: Table salt is high in sodium."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Calcium is required for which physiologic functions per the source?","options":["Functioning of nerves and muscles, blood clotting, acting as cofactor in formation of prothrombin activator and thrombin","Repolarization of nerves only","Oncotic pressure","Acid-base buffering only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Calcium is required for the functioning of nerves and muscles, blood clotting, acting as cofactor in formation of prothrombin activator and thrombin.  Why B wrong: That is potassium's role.  Why C wrong: That is albumin's role.  Why D wrong: Not the stated calcium function."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Hypocalcemia is defined as a serum calcium level less than:","options":["5 mEq/L","8.6 mg/dL","10.5 mg/dL","135 mEq/L"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hypocalcemia is a serum calcium level <8.6 mg/dL.  Why A wrong: That refers to potassium.  Why C wrong: 10.5 mg/dL is the upper threshold for hypercalcemia.  Why D wrong: That is the sodium range."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Which of the following are listed causes of hypocalcemia? (SATA)","options":["Inadequate intake of calcium and vitamin D","Lactose intolerance, malabsorption syndrome (Crohn's disease, end-stage kidney diseases), acute pancreatitis","Hypoparathyroidism, immobility, and removal/destruction of parathyroid glands","Hyperparathyroidism","Vitamin D deficiency"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: Causes of hypocalcemia include inadequate intake of calcium and vitamin D, lactose intolerance, malabsorption syndrome (Crohn's disease, end-stage kidney disease), acute pancreatitis, hypoparathyroidism, immobility, removal or destruction of parathyroid glands, and vitamin D deficiency.  Why D wrong: Hyperparathyroidism causes hypercalcemia, not hypocalcemia."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Positive Chvostek's sign and Trousseau's sign in hypocalcemia indicate:","options":["Cardiac irritability","Neuromuscular sign including irritable skeletal muscles, cramps, tetany, numbness in limbs and seizures","GI bleeding","Renal failure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Positive Chvostek's and Trousseau's signs indicate neuromuscular signs including irritable skeletal muscles, cramps, tetany, numbness in limbs and seizures.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the meaning of these signs."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"ECG change in hypocalcemia includes:","options":["Shortened ST segment","Prolonged ST segment and prolonged QT interval","Tall peaked T wave","Prominent U wave"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: ECG change in hypocalcemia is prolonged ST segment and prolonged QT interval.  Why A wrong: Shortened ST segment is hypercalcemia.  Why C wrong: Tall T wave is hyperkalemia.  Why D wrong: U wave is hypokalemia."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Absorption of calcium from the intestinal tract is accelerated by:","options":["Vitamin C","Vitamin D","Phosphorus","Magnesium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Absorption of calcium from the intestinal tract is accelerated by Vitamin D.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the accelerating factor cited."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Trousseau's Sign is described as:","options":["Contraction of facial muscles in response to light tap over the facial nerve","A carpopedal spasm caused by inflating the blood pressure cuff to a level above systolic pressure for 3 minutes, usually seen in hypocalcemia","A widened QRS complex","Diarrhea with hyperactive bowel sounds"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Trousseau's Sign is a carpopedal spasm caused by inflating the blood pressure cuff to a level above systolic pressure for 3 minutes, usually seen in hypocalcemia.  Why A wrong: That describes Chvostek's sign.  Why C/D wrong: Not the described sign."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Chvostek's Sign is described as:","options":["A carpopedal spasm of the hand","A clinical sign of nerve hyperexcitability (tetany) seen in hypocalcemia; it is contraction of facial muscles in response to a light tap over the facial nerve in front of the ear","Tall peaked T waves","Prolonged QT interval"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Chvostek's Sign is a clinical sign of nerve hyperexcitability (tetany) seen in hypocalcemia; it is contraction of facial muscles in response to a light tap over the facial nerve in front of the ear.  Why A wrong: That describes Trousseau's sign.  Why C/D wrong: Not the described sign."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"(PYQ/Recall) In a patient with tetany-like symptoms, which sign is caused by tapping the facial nerve, indicating which electrolyte imbalance?","options":["Hyperkalemia — Hyponatremia","Hyperkalemia — Hypocalcemia","Chvostek's sign — Hypocalcemia","Trousseau's — Hyperkalemia"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: A positive Chvostek's sign (facial muscle contraction on tapping the facial nerve) indicates hypocalcemia. [Source-keyed answer: B per the page's labeling]  Why A/B/D wrong: These do not correctly pair the facial-tap sign with hypocalcemia."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Hypercalcemia is defined as a serum calcium level greater than:","options":["8.6 mg/dL","10.5 mg/dL","5 mEq/L","145 mEq/L"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hypercalcemia is a serum calcium level >10.5 mg/dL.  Why A wrong: <8.6 mg/dL is hypocalcemia.  Why C wrong: That is potassium units.  Why D wrong: That is the sodium range."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Which of the following are listed causes of hypercalcemia? (SATA)","options":["Excessive intake of calcium and vitamin D","Kidney diseases and thiazide diuretic (delaying the elimination of calcium)","Hyperparathyroidism, malignancy, or bone destruction from metastatic tumour","Immobility, intercostal glucocorticoids, adrenal insufficiency, and dehydration","Vitamin D deficiency"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Causes of hypercalcemia include excessive intake of calcium and vitamin D, kidney diseases, thiazide diuretic (delaying elimination of calcium), hyperparathyroidism, malignancy or bone destruction from metastatic tumour, immobility, glucocorticoids, adrenal insufficiency, and dehydration.  Why E wrong: Vitamin D deficiency causes hypocalcemia, not hypercalcemia."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"If hypercalcemia occurs, then prepare the client with which procedure or medication to reduce the serum calcium level?","options":["Calcitonin, phosphorus, and prostaglandin synthesis inhibitors like aspirin and NSAIDs","Vitamin D supplementation","Calcium gluconate","Thiazide diuretics"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: If hypercalcemia occurs, prepare the client with calcitonin, phosphorus, and prostaglandin synthesis inhibitors like aspirin and NSAIDs.  Why B/C/D wrong: These would raise calcium or worsen hypercalcemia."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"A client with hypercalcemia is at risk for which complication, so the nurse should move them carefully and monitor for the sign of this?","options":["Seizures","Pathological fracture","Tetany","Cardiac arrest"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The client with hypercalcemia is at risk for a pathological fracture; move the client carefully and monitor for sign of a pathological fracture.  Why A/C wrong: Seizures/tetany relate to hypocalcemia.  Why D wrong: Not the stated risk focus."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"In hypercalcemia, the nurse should monitor for flank or abdominal pain and strain the urine to check for the presence of which complication?","options":["Phlebitis","Urinary stones (renal calculi)","Hematuria from trauma","Dehydration"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Monitor for flank or abdominal pain and strain the urine to check for the presence of urinary stones.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the stated monitoring purpose."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"ECG change in hypercalcemia includes:","options":["Prolonged QT interval","Shortened ST segment and widened T wave","Prominent U wave","Tall peaked T wave"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: ECG change in hypercalcemia is a shortened ST segment and widened T wave.  Why A wrong: Prolonged QT is hypocalcemia.  Why C wrong: U wave is hypokalemia.  Why D wrong: Tall peaked T is hyperkalemia."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"\"Should Remember\": A patient with a calcium imbalance is at risk for a pathological fracture in which calcium state?","options":["Hypocalcemia","Hypercalcemia (move the patient carefully and slowly)","Hyponatremia","Hyperkalemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A patient with a calcium imbalance (hypercalcemia) is at risk for a pathological fracture; move the patient carefully and slowly.  Why A/C/D wrong: The box specifically links pathological fracture risk to hypercalcemia handling."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"\"Should Remember\": Calcium and phosphorus have what relationship?","options":["Directly proportional","Inversely proportional (when phosphorus increases, calcium decreases, and vice versa)","Unrelated","Always equal"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Calcium and phosphorus are inversely proportional — if phosphorus level is increased then relatively serum calcium level increased; the box notes the inverse relationship where one rises as the other falls.  Why A/C/D wrong: They have an inverse, not direct, relationship."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Hydrogen ions are described as vital ions of the body and are expressed as:","options":["mEq/L","pH","mg/dL","mOsm/kg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hydrogen ions are vital ions of the life and are expressed as pH.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the expression of hydrogen ion concentration."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"In the body, hydrogen ions exist in two forms. What are they?","options":["First volatile hydrogen and organic acids; second non-volatile form of hydrogen and organic acids","Strong and weak only","Bound and free only","Sodium and potassium forms"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Circulating in the body in two forms: first volatile hydrogen and organic acids; second non-volatile form of hydrogen and organic acids.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the described two forms."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Acids are end products of metabolism and are best described as:","options":["H+ acceptors with OH- receptor, with sour taste","H+ donors and H+ receptor, pH <7 and taste is sour","H+ donors, pH >7, taste bitter","Neutral compounds"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Acids are end product of metabolism; they are H+ donor and H+ receptor, pH <7, and taste is sour.  Why A/C/D wrong: These mischaracterize acids' pH or taste."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"The strength of an acid is determined by what?","options":["The number of hydrogen ions it contains","Its molecular weight only","Its taste only","Its color"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The strength of an acid is determined by the number of hydrogen ions it contains.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the determinant of acid strength."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"A higher number of hydrogen ions in a solution indicates:","options":["High pH","Low pH (more acidic)","Neutral pH","Basic solution"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Acid having more hydrogen ions indicates low pH.  Why A/C/D wrong: More hydrogen ions means lower (more acidic) pH."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"In high concentration, acids destroy body tissues and are therefore described as:","options":["Alkaline","Corrosive","Neutral","Buffering"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In high concentration, acids destroy body tissues (corrosive).  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the described property."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Hydrochloric acid (HCl) is secreted by which cells and serves what function?","options":["Goblet cells; mucus secretion","Parietal or oxyntic cells of the stomach; converts pepsinogen into pepsin and promotes digestive enzyme of gastric juice","Chief cells; pepsin only","Beta cells; insulin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hydrochloric acid (HCl) is secreted by parietal or oxyntic cells of the stomach; it converts pepsinogen into pepsin and is a protein digestive enzyme of gastric juice.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the stated cell/function."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Acetic acid is the acid present in which substance?","options":["Lemon juice","Vinegar (or sirka), with formula CH3COOH","Stomach acid","Soap"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Acetic acid is vinegar or sirka (CH3COOH).  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the source of acetic acid described."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Lactic acid is produced during excessive exercise when there is insufficient oxygen for metabolism of carbohydrate to:","options":["Fat and protein","Glucose and water","Pyruvate only","Acetyl-CoA"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Lactic acid is produced in muscles during excessive exercise when there is insufficient oxygen for metabolism of carbohydrate to glucose and water.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the stated end products."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Bases (alkali) are characterized as:","options":["H+ donors, pH <7, sour taste","OH- donor and H+ receptor, pH >7, and taste is bitter","Neutral, pH 7","H+ acceptors, pH <7"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A base is an OH- donor and H+ receptor, pH >7, and taste is bitter.  Why A/C/D wrong: These mischaracterize bases' pH or taste."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Magnesium hydroxide is described as which common base, with what GI effect?","options":["Milk of magnesia for antacid purpose and mild laxative; M/C side effect is diarrhea","A household cleaner","Component of many antacids","A strong corrosive acid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Magnesium hydroxide is milk of magnesia for antacid purpose and mild laxative; M/C side effect is diarrhea.  Why B wrong: That describes sodium/ammonium hydroxide uses.  Why C wrong: That describes aluminium hydroxide.  Why D wrong: It is a base, not an acid."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Aluminium hydroxide is described as:","options":["Milk of magnesia","A component of many antacids","A household cleaner","Gastric acid"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Aluminium hydroxide is a component of many antacids.  Why A wrong: That is magnesium hydroxide.  Why C wrong: That is ammonium hydroxide.  Why D wrong: Not an acid."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Ammonium hydroxide is commonly used in which products?","options":["Antacids","Household cleaners","Gastric secretion","Vinegar"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Ammonium hydroxide is commonly used in household cleaners.  Why A wrong: That describes aluminium hydroxide.  Why C/D wrong: Not the use described."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"pH is the measure of what, and was discovered by whom in 1909?","options":["Measure of oxygen; discovered by Henderson","Measure of hydrogen; discovered by Doctor Soren Sorensen in 1909","Measure of carbon dioxide; by Hasselbalch","Measure of sodium; by Sorensen"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: pH is the measure of hydrogen and was discovered by Doctor Soren Sorensen in 1909.  Why A/C/D wrong: Incorrect substance or discoverer."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"A pH of less than 7 is acidic and a pH greater than 7 is:","options":["Acidic","Base (basic/alkaline)","Neutral","Buffered"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: pH <7 is acidic and pH >7 is base.  Why A/C/D wrong: A pH above 7 is basic, not acidic, neutral, or buffered."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"What is the normal pH of blood?","options":["7.0 to 7.30","7.35 to 7.45","6.5 to 7.0","7.50 to 7.80"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The normal pH of blood is 7.35 to 7.45.  Why A/C/D wrong: These fall outside the normal blood pH range."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"According to the \"Normal pH of Body Fluids\" table, what is the normal pH of urine?","options":["1.5–3.5","5–6 (acidic)","7.8","6.4–6.5"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists urine pH as 5–6 (acidic).  Why A wrong: 1.5–3.5 is stomach (acidic).  Why C wrong: 7.8 is bile (base).  Why D wrong: 6.4–6.5 is prostate fluid."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"According to the table, the stomach pH is approximately ____ and is:","options":["5–6; basic","1.5–3.5; acidic","7.0–7.8; neutral","6.7; slightly basic"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists stomach pH as 1.5–3.5 and acidic.  Why A wrong: That is urine.  Why C wrong: Not the stomach value.  Why D wrong: 6.7 is saliva (slightly acidic)."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"According to the table, bile has a pH of 7.8 and is classified as:","options":["Acidic","Base","Neutral","Slightly acidic"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists bile pH as 7.8 and base.  Why A/C/D wrong: Bile is basic per the table."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"According to the table, blood has a pH of 7.4 and is classified as:","options":["Acidic","Neutral","Base","Slightly acidic"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists blood pH as 7.4 and neutral (within the table's classification).  Why A/C/D wrong: Per the table, blood is shown as neutral."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"According to the table, vaginal secretion has a pH of 3.5–4.5 and is:","options":["Base","Acidic","Neutral","Slightly basic"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists vaginal secretion pH as 3.5–4.5 and acidic.  Why A/C/D wrong: It is acidic per the table."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"According to the table, semen has a pH of 7.2–7.8 and is:","options":["Acidic","Base","Neutral","Slightly acidic"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists semen pH as 7.2–7.8 and base.  Why A/C/D wrong: Semen is basic per the table."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"According to the table, seminal vesicle fluid has a pH of 7.1–8.0 and is:","options":["Acidic","Base","Neutral","Slightly acidic"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists seminal vesicle fluid pH as 7.1–8.0 and base.  Why A/C/D wrong: It is basic per the table."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"According to the table, prostate fluid has a pH of 6.4–6.5 and is:","options":["Base","Acidic","Neutral","Strongly basic"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists prostate fluid pH as 6.4–6.5 and acidic.  Why A/C/D wrong: It is acidic per the table."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"According to the table, saliva has a pH of 6.7 and is:","options":["Base","Slightly acidic","Neutral","Strongly acidic"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists saliva pH as 6.7 and slightly acidic.  Why A/C/D wrong: It is slightly acidic per the table."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"According to the table, human breast milk has a pH of 6.7–6.9 and is:","options":["Base","Acidic","Neutral","Strongly basic"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists human breast milk pH as 6.7–6.9 and acidic.  Why A/C/D wrong: It is acidic per the table."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"According to the table, stools (feces) have a pH of 6.6 and are:","options":["Base","Acidic","Neutral","Strongly basic"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists stools pH as 6.6 and acidic.  Why A/C/D wrong: Stools are acidic per the table."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"How many types of regulatory mechanisms of acid-base balance are there?","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: There are three types of regulatory mechanisms of acid-base balance.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source states three types."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Which of the following are the three types of acid-base regulatory mechanisms? (SATA)","options":["Chemical regulation / buffer regulation (primary buffer system in ECF)","Physiological regulation (lungs and kidneys)","Potassium exchange regulation","Sodium-potassium pump regulation","Lymphatic regulation"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The three types are chemical/buffer regulation (primary buffer system in ECF), physiological regulation (lungs and kidneys), and potassium exchange regulation.  Why D/E wrong: These are not among the three listed acid-base regulatory mechanisms."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Which acid-base regulatory system is the fastest?","options":["Physiological (lungs/kidneys)","Chemical/buffer regulatory system (rapid first line of defence, takes seconds)","Renal regulation","Potassium exchange"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Buffers are the fastest acid-base regulatory system; they are the rapid first line of defence, taking seconds.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are slower regulatory mechanisms."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"The chemical/buffer system provides immediate protection against change in hydrogen ion concentration where?","options":["Inside the cell only","In the extracellular fluid","In the lungs only","In the kidneys only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: It provides immediate protection against change in hydrogen ion concentration in the extracellular fluid.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies the extracellular fluid."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Which of the following are listed important buffer systems? (SATA)","options":["Bicarbonate buffer system","Phosphate buffer system","Plasma protein buffer system","Hemoglobin system","Lymphatic buffer system"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists bicarbonate, phosphate, plasma protein buffer system, and hemoglobin system as important buffer systems.  Why E wrong: There is no lymphatic buffer system listed."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"In the bicarbonate and carbonic acid system, the bicarbonate-to-carbonic acid ratio is maintained at:","options":["1:20","20:1 for maintaining blood pH between 7.35 and 7.45","10:1","1:1"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Bicarbonate and carbonic acid are present in a 20:1 ratio (HCO3 to H2CO3 ratio is 20:1) for maintaining blood pH between 7.35 to 7.45.  Why A/C/D wrong: Incorrect ratio."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"The 20:1 ratio (HCO3:H2CO3) is associated with which concentration of bicarbonate in the body fluids?","options":["12 mEq","24 mmol/L (24 mol)","40 mmol/L","7 mmol/L"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: This ratio (20:1) is associated with the 24 mmol/L concentration of bicarbonate in the body fluids.  Why A/C/D wrong: Incorrect concentration."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"In the bicarbonate buffer system, the carbonic acid concentration is controlled by the excretion of CO2 by which organ?","options":["Kidneys","The lungs (excretion of CO2; the rate and depth of respiration change in response to change in CO2)","Liver","Spleen"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Carbonic acid concentration is controlled by the excretion of CO2 by the lungs; the rate and depth of respiration change in response to change in CO2.  Why A wrong: Kidneys control bicarbonate concentration, not carbonic acid via CO2.  Why C/D wrong: Not involved as described."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"In the bicarbonate buffer system, the bicarbonate concentration is controlled by which organ?","options":["The lungs","The kidneys","The liver","The heart"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The kidneys control the bicarbonate concentration in CO2.  Why A wrong: Lungs control carbonic acid (CO2 excretion).  Why C/D wrong: Not involved as described."},{"section":"Batch 021 · Osmosis/osmotic pressure, active transport, filtration…","question":"Hydrogen ions are described as the second non-volatile form linked to which buffer component in the body?","options":["Bicarbonate (HCO3)","Carbonic acid","Phosphate only","Albumin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The pH scale diagram and text relate hydrogen ion form to the carbonic acid/bicarbonate (HCO3) buffer balance, with 20 parts bicarbonate to 1 part carbonic acid maintaining normal pH.  Why B wrong: Carbonic acid is the 1 part, not the second non-volatile component emphasized for the base side.  Why C/D wrong: Phosphate and albumin are separate buffer systems."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"The bicarbonate buffer system selectively retains or excretes bicarbonate in response to which need?","options":["Energy needs","Bodily need (acid-base balance)","Fluid replacement needs","Caloric needs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states bicarbonate is selectively retained or excreted \"in response to bodily need.\"  Why A wrong: Energy needs are unrelated to bicarbonate buffering.  Why C wrong: Fluid replacement is not the trigger described.  Why D wrong: Caloric needs are unrelated."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"In the reaction CO₂ + H₂O → H₂CO₃, the carbonic acid formed is then excreted as which gas by the lungs?","options":["Oxygen","Bicarbonate","H⁺ + HCO₃⁻ (Bicarbonate)","Nitrogen"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Source shows CO₂ + H₂O → H₂CO₃ (carbonic acid) → H⁺ + HCO₃⁻ (Bicarbonate).  Why A wrong: Oxygen is not the product of this reaction.  Why B wrong: Bicarbonate alone omits the H⁺ dissociation shown.  Why D wrong: Nitrogen is not involved."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"In acidosis (strong acid), how is the strong acid converted by the bicarbonate buffer system?","options":["H₂CO₃ + HCO₃⁻ → H₂O + CO₂ (weak acid)","H₂CO₃ → strong base","CO₂ → O₂","Volatile acid → non-volatile acid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Source: in acidosis, H⁺ + HCO₃⁻ (strong acid) → H₂CO₃ (weak acid).  Why B wrong: The product is a weak acid, not a strong base.  Why C wrong: This is not the buffer reaction described.  Why D wrong: This describes volatile acid classification, not the acidosis buffering reaction."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"In alkalosis (strong base), the bicarbonate buffer reaction produces H₂CO₃ (weak acid) and the H₂CO₃ is excreted by which organ?","options":["Lungs","Kidneys","Liver","Skin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source: in alkalosis (strong base) H₂CO₃, H₂O + KH₂CO₃, H₂CO₃ is excreted by kidneys.  Why A wrong: Lungs excrete volatile (carbonic) acid; this reaction's acid is excreted by kidneys.  Why C wrong: Liver is not stated.  Why D wrong: Skin is not the route."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"Volatile acids, such as carbonic acid (H₂CO₃), are excreted by which organ?","options":["Kidneys","Lungs","Liver","Spleen"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"Volatile acid: those acid excretes by lungs (CO₂) called volatile acid.\"  Why A wrong: Kidneys excrete non-volatile (fixed) acids.  Why C/D wrong: Not stated as excretory routes for volatile acid."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"Non-volatile acids (e.g., acetic acid) are excreted by which organ and are called what?","options":["Lungs; volatile acid","Kidneys; non-volatile acid","Skin; metabolic acid","Liver; fixed base"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"Non-volatile acid: those acid excretes by kidneys, e.g. acetic acid called non-volatile acid.\"  Why A wrong: That describes volatile acid via lungs.  Why C/D wrong: Not stated."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"The phosphate buffer system is especially active in which body fluids?","options":["Extracellular fluid only","Intracellular fluids and kidneys","Blood plasma only","Lymphatic fluid"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"This system presents in intracellular fluids and it is especially active in the kidneys.\"  Why A wrong: It is intracellular, not solely extracellular.  Why C wrong: Plasma protein buffer relates to plasma, not phosphate's primary site.  Why D wrong: Not stated."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"In the phosphate buffer chemical reaction, which component acts as a weak base?","options":["H₃PO₄ (hydrogen phosphate)","HPO₄ (mono hydrogen phosphate)","HCl","NaCl"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source lists HPO₄ (Mono hydrogen phosphate) and, in alkalosis, OH (strong acid) + H₃PO₄ → H₂O + HPO₄ (weak base).  Why A wrong: H₃PO₄ is shown as the hydrogen phosphate/strong acid side.  Why C/D wrong: Not part of the phosphate buffer system."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"The plasma protein buffer system functions by which mechanism?","options":["Excreting CO₂ via lungs","Having the ability to attract or release hydrogen ions","Producing red blood cells","Filtering nitrogenous wastes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"Plasma protein has the ability to attract or release hydrogen ions.\"  Why A wrong: That is respiratory regulation.  Why C wrong: That is bone marrow/hemoglobin-related, not buffering action.  Why D wrong: That is renal excretion, not plasma protein buffering."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"In the hemoglobin buffer system, the exchange in which chloride shifts in or out of the cells in response to the level of oxygen in the blood is called what?","options":["Sodium pump","Chloride shift","Anion gap","Bohr effect"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"Hemoglobin maintain acid base balance by a process of chloride shift.\"  Why A wrong: Not the named process.  Why C wrong: Anion gap is a calculation, not this exchange.  Why D wrong: Not the term used in source."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"In the hemoglobin buffer system, for each chloride ion that leaves a red blood cell, what happens?","options":["A bicarbonate ion enters","A sodium ion exits","An oxygen molecule enters","A potassium ion enters"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"For each chloride ion that leaves a red blood cell, then a bicarbonate ion enters.\" (and for each chloride that enters, a bicarbonate leaves).  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies the reciprocal exchange is with bicarbonate, not sodium, oxygen, or potassium."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"Physiological regulation of acid-base balance by the lungs occurs through which mechanism?","options":["Renal mechanism","Respiratory mechanism","Phosphate buffer","Plasma protein buffer"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source lists \"Respiratory mechanism by the lungs\" under Physiological Regulation.  Why A wrong: Renal mechanism is the kidneys, not lungs.  Why C/D wrong: These are chemical buffer systems, not physiological lung regulation."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"The respiratory mechanism is known as the body's line of defense and acts within what time frame?","options":["First line; seconds","Second line; minutes","Third line; hours","First line; days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"It also known as second line of defence... The lungs are the second defence of the body and interact with the buffer system to maintain acid base balance. It takes minutes in action.\"  Why A wrong: First/seconds describes the chemical buffer system.  Why C wrong: Hours is not stated for respiratory.  Why D wrong: Days describes the renal mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"In acidosis, the respiratory mechanism responds by doing what to the rate and depth of respiration?","options":["Decreasing rate and depth to retain CO₂","Increasing rate and depth, leading to a decrease in pH (acidity increase)... actually increasing to help correct acidity","Increasing rate and depth, which leads to hyperventilation","No change in respiration"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"Rate and depth of respiration increased which lead to hyperventilation\" during body metabolism in acidosis.  Why A wrong: Decreasing would worsen acidosis.  Why B wrong: Garbled distractor; not the source statement.  Why D wrong: Respiration does change (increases)."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"During body metabolism, CO₂ is produced; which combination produces carbonic acid, decreasing pH (acidity increase or pH decrease)?","options":["CO₂ combines with water","CO₂ combines with sodium","CO₂ combines with potassium","CO₂ combines with chloride"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"CO₂ is produced, which react with water to form carbonic acid... decrease in pH.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies reaction with water to form carbonic acid."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"In alkalosis, the rate and depth of respiration changes how, and to what effect on CO₂?","options":["Increased, retaining CO₂","Decreased, increasing carbonic acid breakdown into CO₂ and H₂O, resulting in increased pH (acidity decrease, increased pH)... actually slowing breathing to retain CO₂","Decreased respiration to retain CO₂","Unchanged"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Source states in alkalosis there is decreased rate/depth and carbonic acid breakdown into CO₂ and H₂O is increased; respiration slows to retain CO₂ and correct excess base.  Why A wrong: Increased respiration would worsen alkalosis.  Why B wrong: Garbled distractor.  Why D wrong: Respiration does change."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"The process of correcting a deficit or excess takes how long for the respiratory mechanism?","options":["Minutes to hours","10 to 30 seconds to complete","Days","Weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"The process of correcting a deficit or excess takes 10 to 30 seconds to complete.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the time frame stated."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"The renal mechanism of acid-base regulation is known as which line of defense?","options":["First line","Second line","Third line of defence","Not a defense line"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"It also known as third line of defence.\"  Why A wrong: First line is chemical buffers.  Why B wrong: Second line is respiratory.  Why D wrong: It is a defense line."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"How long does the renal mechanism take to act on acid-base balance?","options":["Seconds","Minutes","Hours to days","Instantly"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"It takes hours to days for action.\"  Why A wrong: Seconds is the chemical buffer.  Why B wrong: Minutes is the respiratory mechanism.  Why D wrong: Not instant."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"The kidneys provide a more intensive corrective response to acid-base disturbances. Which statement reflects this from the source?","options":["They are the fastest responders","They provide a more intensive corrective response than the other mechanisms","They only excrete potassium","They do not affect acid-base balance"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"The kidneys provide a more intensive corrective response to acid base disturbances than other mechanism.\"  Why A wrong: Renal is the slowest (hours to days).  Why C wrong: They handle bicarbonate/H⁺, not only potassium.  Why D wrong: They are central to acid-base regulation."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"In acidosis, the kidneys compensate by retaining and reabsorbing what, while excreting excess H⁺?","options":["Excess H⁺","Bicarbonate (HCO₃⁻)","Potassium only","Chloride only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source: renal mechanism \"compensate response a few hours to several days... In acidosis: excess H⁺ excreted by urine; In alkalosis: excess HCO₃⁻ excreted by urine.\" In acidosis the kidney retains/reabsorbs bicarbonate and excretes H⁺.  Why A wrong: H⁺ is excreted, not retained, in acidosis.  Why C/D wrong: The primary regulated ions are H⁺ and HCO₃⁻."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"When there is a low level of hydrogen ions in the body, the kidneys retain hydrogen ion to form what?","options":["Bicarbonate","Carbonic acid","Chloride","Ammonia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"When there is low level of hydrogen ions in body, kidneys retain hydrogen ion to form carbonic acid.\"  Why A wrong: Bicarbonate is the conjugate base, not what is formed by retaining H⁺ here.  Why C/D wrong: Not stated."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"Potassium plays an exchange role in maintaining acid-base balance. When the body changes the potassium level by drawing hydrogen ions into the cells or pushing them out, what is facilitated?","options":["Sodium movement only","Cell membrane movement across cell membranes (cellular shifting in response to acid-base pattern)","Oxygen transport","Glucose uptake"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"The body change the potassium level... cell membrane movement... is facilitated by less cellular shifting in response to acid base pattern.\"  Why A wrong: Source emphasizes H⁺/K⁺ exchange, not solely sodium.  Why C/D wrong: Not the described mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"In acidosis, what happens to the serum potassium level?","options":["K⁺ increased, which lead to hyperkalemia","K⁺ decreased, leading to hypokalemia","K⁺ unchanged","K⁺ excreted in urine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"In acidosis: K⁺ increased which lead to hyperkalemia.\"  Why B wrong: Hypokalemia is associated with alkalosis.  Why C/D wrong: Not stated for acidosis."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"In alkalosis, what happens to the serum potassium level?","options":["K⁺ increased, leading to hyperkalemia","K⁺ decreased, which lead to hypokalemia","No change","Potassium retained by cells equally"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"In alkalosis: K⁺ decreased which lead to hypokalemia.\"  Why A wrong: Hyperkalemia is associated with acidosis.  Why C/D wrong: Not stated."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"According to the source, why should the potassium level be monitored closely?","options":["Because it affects oxygen saturation","In and out imbalance (acid-base imbalance affects K⁺ closely)","Because it determines hemoglobin levels","It is unrelated to acid-base balance"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"Potassium level should be monitor closely in and out imbalance.\"  Why A/C wrong: Not the stated reason.  Why D wrong: Potassium is closely tied to acid-base balance."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"In the Acid-Base Balance and Internal K⁺ Exchange diagram, acidosis is associated with which intracellular/extracellular hydrogen and potassium movement?","options":["H⁺ moves into cells, K⁺ moves out (hyperkalemia)","H⁺ moves out, K⁺ moves in","No movement","Only oxygen moves"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Source diagram: Acidosis shows H⁺ entering cells and K⁺ leaving (hyperkalemia).  Why B wrong: That pattern reflects alkalosis (hypokalemia).  Why C/D wrong: Movement occurs and involves H⁺/K⁺."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"ABG (Arterial Blood Gas) test measures the amount of which gases dissolved in arterial blood?","options":["Nitrogen and helium","Oxygen and carbon dioxide (plus blood pH and bicarbonate)","Carbon monoxide and oxygen","Hydrogen and chlorine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source: ABG measures amount of oxygen and carbon dioxide dissolved in blood, plus blood pH and bicarbonate level.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the gases measured by ABG."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"An ABG sample should be taken in which type of syringe?","options":["A plain plastic syringe","A 1 ml dry lithium heparin or heparinised (heparinized) syringe / insulin syringe","A glass syringe with saline","Any standard IV syringe"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"ABG sample should be taken in a 1 ml dry lithium heparin syringes or heparinised syringe e.g. insulin syringe.\"  Why A wrong: Plain syringe lacks anticoagulant.  Why C wrong: Saline would dilute the sample.  Why D wrong: Must be heparinized."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"A heparinised syringe is used for ABG sampling because the heparin (anticoagulant) serves what purpose?","options":["To increase oxygen content","To prevent clot formation in the sample","To raise the pH","To dilute the blood"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"Heparinised syringe means syringe should wash with heparin solution (anti-coagulant) to prevent clot formation in the sample.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the function of heparin in the sample."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"What is the FIRST priority sample site for ABG, located on the thumb side of the wrist?","options":["Brachial artery","Radial artery","Femoral artery","Umbilical vein"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"Radial artery: first priority, most commonly used, which is located on the thumb side of the wrist.\"  Why A wrong: Brachial is second priority.  Why C wrong: Femoral is third priority.  Why D wrong: Umbilical vein is used in newborns."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"Which artery is the SECOND priority site for ABG sampling?","options":["Radial artery","Brachial artery","Femoral artery","Ulnar artery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"Brachial artery: second priority.\"  Why A wrong: Radial is first priority.  Why C wrong: Femoral is third priority.  Why D wrong: Ulnar is not listed as a priority site."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"The femoral artery is the THIRD priority for ABG because it is more likely to have which complication, and which site is used in newborns?","options":["Risk of getting chance of infection due to proximity of peritoneum; umbilical vein in newborns","Risk of bleeding; radial vein","No complications; carotid artery","Air embolism; subclavian vein"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"Femoral artery: Third priority, more likely to getting chance of infection due to proximity of peritoneum\"; and \"In new born: from umbilical vein.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the stated complications/sites."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"Allen's test should be done before collecting an ABG sample to assess what?","options":["Hemoglobin level","Collateral circulation or patency of the ulnar artery","Blood glucose","Heart rate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"Allen's test should be done before taking ABG sample to assess collateral circulation or patency of ulnar artery.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: Not what Allen's test assesses."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"In the steps of ABG analysis, after analysing the pH, what is the next step?","options":["Analyse the HCO₃⁻","Analyse the PaCO₂","Determine compensation","Match the values"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source steps: 1. Analyse the pH, 2. Analyse the PaCO₂, 3. Analyse the HCO₃⁻...  Why A wrong: HCO₃⁻ is step 3, after PaCO₂.  Why C wrong: Determining compensation is the final step.  Why D wrong: Matching PaCO₂ or HCO₃⁻ with pH is a later step."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"In the steps of ABG analysis, step 5 states that PaCO₂ or HCO₃⁻ change in the same direction as pH for a determination of what?","options":["Hypoxemia","Direction of the pH change (matching for primary disturbance)","Compensation","Normal values"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source step 5: \"Determine whether the PaCO₂ or the HCO₃⁻ go in opposite direction of the pH\" / matching identifies the primary disturbance and direction.  Why A wrong: PaO₂ and SaO₂ are analysed for hypoxemia (a separate step).  Why C wrong: Compensation is the final determination.  Why D wrong: This step is about direction matching, not normalcy."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"In ABG analysis, which step involves analysing PaO₂ and SaO₂ for hypoxemia?","options":["Step 5","Step 6","Step 1","Step 2"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source step 6: \"Analyse the PaO₂ and SaO₂ for hypoxemia.\"  Why A wrong: Step 5 determines direction matching.  Why C/D wrong: Steps 1 and 2 analyse pH and PaCO₂."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"An ABG is considered fully compensated when which condition is met?","options":["When all three values are abnormal","If all three values (pH, PaCO₂, HCO₃⁻) are normal","When the pH is abnormal but the others are normal","When PaO₂ is normal"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"It is fully compensated if pH is normal\" (with the matching pattern of all values normalized).  Why A wrong: Abnormal values do not indicate full compensation.  Why C wrong: Abnormal pH indicates it is not fully compensated.  Why D wrong: PaO₂ alone does not define compensation."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"An ABG is partially compensated when which finding is present?","options":["All three values normal","All three values are abnormal","Only pH abnormal","Only PaO₂ abnormal"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"It is partially compensated if all three values are abnormal.\"  Why A wrong: All normal = fully compensated.  Why C/D wrong: Not the criterion stated."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"An ABG is uncompensated when which pattern is present?","options":["PaCO₂ or HCO₃⁻ is normal and the pH is abnormal","All values normal","pH normal, PaCO₂ abnormal","Only SaO₂ abnormal"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"It is uncompensated if PaCO₂ or HCO₃⁻ is normal and the pH is abnormal.\"  Why B wrong: All normal = fully compensated.  Why C wrong: Normal pH indicates compensation present.  Why D wrong: Not the criterion stated."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"What is the normal range of arterial blood pH?","options":["7.30–7.40","7.35–7.45","7.20–7.35","7.45–7.55"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source Normal Range of ABG Analysis: pH = 7.35–7.45.  Why A/C/D wrong: Outside the stated normal range."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"What is the normal range of PaCO₂?","options":["22–26 mEq/L","35–45 mm of Hg","80–100 mm Hg","7.35–7.45"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source: PaCO₂ = 35 to 45 mm of Hg.  Why A wrong: That is the HCO₃⁻ range.  Why C wrong: That is the PaO₂ range.  Why D wrong: That is the pH range."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"What is the normal range of PaO₂?","options":["35–45 mm Hg","90 to 100 mm of Hg","22 to 27 mEq/L","7.35 to 7.45"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source: PaO₂ = 90 to 100 mm of Hg.  Why A wrong: That is PaCO₂.  Why C wrong: That is HCO₃⁻.  Why D wrong: That is pH."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"What is the normal range of HCO₃⁻ (bicarbonate)?","options":["35–45 mm Hg","22 to 27 mEq/L","90–100 mm Hg","7.35–7.45"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source: HCO₃⁻ = 22 to 27 mEq/L.  Why A wrong: That is PaCO₂.  Why C wrong: That is PaO₂.  Why D wrong: That is pH."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"In the ABG analysis interpretation key: increased pH with decreased PaCO₂ indicates which disorder?","options":["Metabolic Acidosis","Respiratory Alkalosis","Respiratory Acidosis","Metabolic Alkalosis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"↑PH ↓PaCO₂ = Pulmonary/Respiratory Alkalosis.\"  Why A wrong: Metabolic acidosis is ↓pH ↓HCO₃⁻.  Why C wrong: Respiratory acidosis is ↓pH ↑PaCO₂.  Why D wrong: Metabolic alkalosis is ↑pH ↑HCO₃⁻."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"Decreased pH with increased PaCO₂ indicates which disorder?","options":["Respiratory Alkalosis","Pulmonary/Respiratory Acidosis","Metabolic Alkalosis","Metabolic Acidosis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"↓PH ↑PaCO₂ = Pulmonary/Respiratory Acidosis.\"  Why A wrong: That is ↑pH ↓PaCO₂.  Why C wrong: That is ↑pH ↑HCO₃⁻.  Why D wrong: That is ↓pH ↓HCO₃⁻."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"In metabolic ABG interpretation, increased pH with increased HCO₃⁻ indicates which disorder?","options":["Metabolic Alkalosis","Metabolic Acidosis","Respiratory Alkalosis","Respiratory Acidosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"↑PH ↑HCO₃⁻ = Metabolic Alkalosis.\"  Why B wrong: Metabolic acidosis is ↓pH ↓HCO₃⁻.  Why C/D wrong: Those are respiratory disturbances (PaCO₂ driven)."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"Decreased pH with decreased HCO₃⁻ indicates which disorder?","options":["Metabolic Alkalosis","Metabolic Acidosis","Respiratory Acidosis","Respiratory Alkalosis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"↓PH ↓HCO₃⁻ = Metabolic Acidosis.\"  Why A wrong: That is ↑pH ↑HCO₃⁻.  Why C/D wrong: Those are PaCO₂-driven respiratory disturbances."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"(RECALL THE HENKEY / PYQ) A nurse is taking an ABG sample through a radial artery. During the procedure, a vein pricks in place of the artery. What would be the expected first response of the nurse?","options":["Hematoxic formation and compress the site","Blood clot but send this lab","Take the sample from another hand","Maintain the records"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Source Right Answer: A. The rationale states during arterial blood collection for ABG test, if vein pricks instead of artery, there is possibility of hematoma formation — compress the site, and a priority response of the nurse and then after sometime take the sample from another hand's radial artery (a secondary response).  Why B wrong: Sending a venous sample as ABG is inaccurate; not the first response.  Why C wrong: Taking from the other hand is a secondary response, after compression.  Why D wrong: Maintaining records is not the priority over managing the hematoma."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"In the Original Allen's test, the patient is asked to clench both fists tightly for what duration at the same time?","options":["30 seconds","1 minute","15 seconds","2 minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"The patient is asked to clench both fists tightly for 1 minute at the same time.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the duration stated for the original test."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"In the Original Allen's test, pressure is applied over which arteries simultaneously to occlude them?","options":["Radial and brachial","Both radial and ulnar","Ulnar and femoral","Radial and femoral"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"Pressure is applied over both radial arteries simultaneously so as to occlude them.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: The original test occludes the radial (and ulnar) arteries; source specifies both radial arteries (and ulnar in modified)."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"In the Original Allen's test, after the patient opens the fingers of both hands rapidly, the colour of both hands should be replaced quickly. What does this indicate?","options":["Occlusion","The initial pallor should be replaced quickly by colour (indicating patent collateral circulation)","Arterial blockage","Venous insufficiency"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"The patient then opens the fingers of both hands rapidly, and the examiner compares the colour of both. The initial pallor should be replaced quickly by colour.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: Quick return of colour indicates adequate collateral flow, not occlusion or insufficiency."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"In the Original Allen's test, the test may be repeated one time excluding which arteries?","options":["The radial arteries","The ulnar arteries","The brachial arteries","The femoral arteries"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"The test may be repeated, this time excluding the ulnar arteries.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the arteries excluded in the repeat."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"Allen's test looks for arterial circulation; if colour returns quickly as described, this is considered to demonstrate what?","options":["Occlusion of the radial artery","Normal circulation; if the pallor persists for some time after the patient opens their fingers, this suggests a degree of occlusion of the uncompressed artery","Venous thrombosis","Hemoglobin abnormality"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source: quick colour return is considered to demonstrate normal circulation; persistent pallor suggests a degree of occlusion of the uncompressed artery.  Why A wrong: It indicates patency, not occlusion, when colour returns quickly.  Why C/D wrong: Not assessed by Allen's test."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"In the Modified Allen's test, which hand is examined at a time?","options":["Both hands together","One (individual) hand at a time","Only the dominant hand","Only the non-dominant hand"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"In the modified Allen test, one hand is examined at a time.\"  Why A wrong: That is the original test (both hands).  Why C/D wrong: Not specified by dominance."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"In the Modified Allen's test, the patient is asked to clench the fist for about how many seconds?","options":["10 seconds","20 seconds","30 seconds","60 seconds"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"The hand is elevated and the patient is asked to clench their fist for about 30 seconds.\"  Why A/B/D wrong: Not the duration stated for the modified test."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"In the Modified Allen's test, pressure is applied over the ulnar and radial arteries so as to occlude both. What is observed at the fingers?","options":["Increased redness","Self-elevated, the hand is then opened; it should appear blanched (pallor may be observed at the finger tips/nails)","Cyanosis returns immediately","No change"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"Pressure is applied over the ulnar and the radial arteries so as to occlude both of them... it should appear blanched (pallor may be observed at the finger nails).\"  Why A/C/D wrong: Occluding both arteries causes blanching/pallor, not redness, immediate cyanosis return, or no change."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"In the Modified Allen's test, after ulnar pressure is released while radial pressure is maintained, normal colour should return within what time?","options":["60 seconds","Within 10 seconds (ulnar pressure is released while radial pressure is maintained, the colour fails to return — should return within 10 seconds)","30 seconds","5 minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"Ulnar pressure is released while radial pressure is maintained, the colour should return within 10 seconds.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the stated time frame."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"In the Modified Allen's test, if colour fails to return within 10 seconds (abnormal result), what does this suggest about the ulnar artery?","options":["The ulnar artery has a good supply to the hand","That the ulnar artery supply to the hand is not sufficient","The radial artery is blocked","The hand is normal"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"If colour fails to return... the test is considered abnormal and it suggests that the ulnar artery supply to the hand is not sufficient.\"  Why A wrong: Failure to return colour indicates insufficient ulnar supply.  Why C wrong: It tests ulnar collateral supply, not radial blockage.  Why D wrong: It is an abnormal finding."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"Regarding documenting the Modified Allen's test result, which statement is true per the source?","options":["A score must always be calculated or a needle is inserted","There is some confusion as to whether a normal Allen test should be referred to as negative or positive; this indicates a finding may not be safe to calculate or needle the radial artery","The result is always positive","Pre-operative surgical notes never document this"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"There is still some confusion as to whether a normal Allen test should be referred to as negative or positive. This indicates that a finding may not be safe to calculate or needle the radial artery,\" and in pre-operative surgical notes it is perhaps best to avoid negative/positive and document the result as normal or abnormal.  Why A wrong: The source says a finding may NOT be safe to needle.  Why C wrong: Results are not always positive.  Why D wrong: Pre-operative notes do document it (best as normal/abnormal)."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"In pre-operative surgical notes, it is best to document the Allen's test result using which terms instead of negative/positive?","options":["Positive or negative","Normal or abnormal","High or low","Pass or fail"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"In pre-operative surgical notes it is perhaps best to avoid the words negative or positive and document the result as normal or abnormal.\"  Why A wrong: The source advises avoiding these words.  Why C/D wrong: Not the recommended terms."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"In the modified Allen test images (A, B, C), what is done to assess collateral circulation?","options":["The hand is clenched, pressure applied to radial and ulnar arteries, then pressure removed from the ulnar artery while watching for flushing","Only the radial artery is palpated","The patient holds their breath","The hand is submerged in cold water"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Source caption: the hand is clenched into a tight fist, pressure applied to the radial and ulnar arteries, the palm/fingers are blanched, then removal of pressure on the ulnar artery should result in flushing of the entire hand.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the described maneuver."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"Respiratory acidosis involves carbonic acid excess and increased retention of which gas?","options":["Oxygen","Carbon dioxide","Nitrogen","Bicarbonate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"Carbonic acid excess and increased retention of carbon dioxide.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the retained substance described."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"In respiratory acidosis, the PaCO₂ is in which range?","options":["<35 mm Hg",">45 mm Hg (hypercapnia)","35–45 mm Hg",">100 mm Hg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"PaCO₂ is >45 mm Hg (hypercapnia).\"  Why A wrong: <35 mm Hg occurs in respiratory alkalosis (hypocapnia).  Why C wrong: That is normal.  Why D wrong: Not stated; that range is implausible for PaCO₂."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"In respiratory acidosis, the pH is in which range?","options":["pH below 7.35","pH above 7.45","pH 7.35–7.45","pH below 7.0 only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"pH is below 7.35.\"  Why B wrong: pH above 7.45 indicates alkalosis.  Why C wrong: That is normal pH.  Why D wrong: Source states below 7.35, not specifically below 7.0."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"Respiratory acidosis is caused by a primary defect in the function of which organ system?","options":["Renal system","Lungs (respiratory system)","Hepatic system","Gastrointestinal system"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"Respiratory acidosis is caused by a primary defect in the function of the lungs or change in normal respiratory pattern.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: Renal/hepatic/GI causes relate to metabolic disturbances, not respiratory acidosis."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"Any condition that causes airway obstruction of the airway or depresses the respiratory system can cause which acid-base imbalance?","options":["Metabolic alkalosis","Respiratory acidosis","Metabolic acidosis","Respiratory alkalosis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"Any condition that cause airway cause obstruction of the airway or depress the respiratory system can cause respiratory acidosis.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: Airway obstruction/respiratory depression leads to CO₂ retention = respiratory acidosis."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"Which of the following is listed as the MOST common cause of respiratory acidosis?","options":["Respiratory obstruction (tumor, asthma, bronchitis)","Hypoventilation or inadequate ventilation","Neurovascular complications","Seizure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source lists \"Hypoventilation or inadequate ventilation (most common cause).\"  Why A wrong: Respiratory obstruction is a cause but not flagged as most common.  Why C wrong: Neurovascular complications are a listed cause but not most common.  Why D wrong: Seizure is not listed as a respiratory acidosis cause."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"Which conditions are listed as causes of respiratory acidosis under respiratory obstruction? (SATA)","options":["Tumor","Asthma","Bronchitis","Pulmonary embolism","Renal failure"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Source lists respiratory obstruction (tumor, asthma, bronchitis) and impaired gas exchange in alveoli (emphysema, pulmonary oedema, atelectasis, bronchiectasis, pulmonary oedema & emboli) as causes.  Why E wrong: Renal failure is a cause of metabolic acidosis, not respiratory acidosis."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"Which conditions are listed as causes of impaired gas exchange in respiratory acidosis? (SATA)","options":["Emphysema","Pulmonary oedema","Atelectasis","Bronchiectasis","Diabetes mellitus"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"Impaired gas exchange in alveoli - emphysema, pulmonary oedema, atelectasis, bronchiectasis, pulmonary oedema & emboli.\"  Why E wrong: Diabetes (DKA) is a cause of metabolic acidosis, not respiratory."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"Which signs and symptoms are associated with respiratory acidosis? (SATA)","options":["Hyperventilation with hypoxia and dyspnea","Irritability, headache","Drowsiness, dizziness, disorientation","Tachycardia, hypotension","Increased urine output"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Source lists hyperventilation with hypoxia & dyspnea, irritability, headache, drowsiness/dizziness/disorientation, tachycardia/hypotension, VF due to hyperkalemia, seizure/cyanosis/coma.  Why E wrong: Increased urine output is not a listed sign of respiratory acidosis."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"In respiratory acidosis, ventricular fibrillation (VF) can occur due to which electrolyte abnormality?","options":["Hypokalemia","Hyperkalemia","Hypocalcemia","Hypernatremia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"VF due to hyperkalemia\" (acidosis raises serum K⁺).  Why A wrong: Hypokalemia is associated with alkalosis.  Why C/D wrong: Not the cause stated for VF here."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"In the compensatory mechanism for respiratory acidosis, the kidneys excrete and reabsorb which ions?","options":["Excrete sodium, reabsorb chloride","Urinary system excretes hydrogen ion / urinary system retains sodium to facilitate increase in sodium bicarbonate","Excrete bicarbonate, reabsorb hydrogen","Excrete potassium only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"Urinary system excretes hydrogen ion; Urinary system retained sodium for facilitate increase in sodium bicarbonate; Rate and depth of respiration increase. With chronic hypoxia, decrease oxygen level may stimulate breathing otherwise increased carbon dioxide level may stimulate breathing.\"  Why A wrong: Not the described compensation.  Why C wrong: Kidneys reabsorb bicarbonate (retain HCO₃⁻), not excrete it, in acidosis.  Why D wrong: Compensation involves H⁺ and sodium bicarbonate handling."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"In chronic hypoxia (respiratory acidosis compensation), what may stimulate breathing?","options":["Decreased oxygen level may stimulate breathing; otherwise increased carbon dioxide level may stimulate breathing","Only increased oxygen","Decreased carbon dioxide","Increased sodium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"With chronic hypoxia, decrease oxygen level may stimulate breathing otherwise increased carbon dioxide level may stimulate breathing.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the stated drivers of breathing in chronic hypoxia."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"Which nursing actions are appropriate for respiratory acidosis? (SATA)","options":["Treat underlying cause, mechanical ventilation","Monitor for respiratory distress and start oxygen if needed","Position the patient in semi-Fowler's to high Fowler's position","Encourage and assist the client to turn, cough, and deep breathe","Restrict all fluids regardless of cause"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Source nursing actions: treat underlying cause / mechanical ventilation; monitor for respiratory distress, start oxygen if needed; semi-Fowler's to high Fowler's position; encourage/assist client to turn, cough, deep breathe; encourage hydration for thin secretion; suction the client's airway if necessary; reduce restlessness by improving ventilation, deeply medication; prepare for ET intubation and ventilation.  Why E wrong: Source recommends encouraging hydration for thin secretion, not restricting all fluids."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"In respiratory acidosis, hydration is encouraged for what purpose?","options":["To dilute potassium","For thin secretion (to thin secretions)","To increase blood pressure","To reduce urine output"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"Encourage hydration for thin secretion.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the stated purpose."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"For respiratory acidosis, restlessness should be reduced by which action?","options":["Administering sedatives","Improving ventilation, deeply medication (improving ventilation)","Applying restraints","Reducing oxygen"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"Reduce restlessness by improving ventilation, deeply medication.\"  Why A wrong: Sedatives could depress respiration further.  Why C wrong: Restraints do not address the cause.  Why D wrong: Reducing oxygen would worsen hypoxia."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"(RECALL THE HENKEY / PYQ) A patient is admitted in intensive care with difficulty in breathing, chest pain while breathing. ABG assessment reveals pH 7.33, PaCO₂ 43 and HCO₃⁻ 23. What would be the priority nursing action?","options":["Ask the patient to take deep breaths and cough in every 5 minutes","7 liter per minute O₂ for 10 minutes","Take electrocardiogram (ECG)","Intubate the child"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Source Right Answer: B... wait — the source marks the correct answer for this recall item. The book states the Right Answer is B (7 liter per minute O₂ for 10 minutes).  Why A wrong: Per the source key, deep breathing/coughing is not selected as the priority over oxygenation.  Why C wrong: ECG is not the priority action per the key.  Why D wrong: Intubation of the child is not indicated; the ABG values are near normal (mildly low pH)."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"Respiratory alkalosis is characterized by carbonic acid deficit and hyperventilation with excessive loss of what?","options":["Oxygen","CO₂ (carbon dioxide)","Bicarbonate","Sodium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"Carbonic acid deficit; Hyperventilation blows off excessive CO₂.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: The excessive loss in respiratory alkalosis is CO₂, not oxygen, bicarbonate, or sodium."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"In respiratory alkalosis, the PaCO₂ is in which range?","options":[">45 mm Hg","Above 45 (PCO₂ is above 45)... actually below 35","PCO₂ is above 45 mm Hg","PCO₂ is decreased (below normal)"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The source overview text appears as \"PCO₂ is above 45\" but respiratory alkalosis is defined by hyperventilation blowing off CO₂, yielding a decreased PaCO₂ (hypocapnia, <35 mm Hg). The defining feature is a low PaCO₂.  Why A/C wrong: A high PaCO₂ (>45) defines respiratory acidosis, not alkalosis.  Why B wrong: Garbled/contradictory option."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"Respiratory alkalosis results from conditions that cause which respiratory pattern?","options":["Hypoventilation","Over-stimulation (overstimulation) of the respiratory system (hyperventilation)","Apnea","Normal breathing"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"Respiratory alkalosis is result from conditions that cause overstimulation of the respiratory system.\"  Why A wrong: Hypoventilation causes respiratory acidosis.  Why C/D wrong: Not the described pattern."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"Which are listed causes of respiratory alkalosis? (SATA)","options":["Fever","Hyperventilation","Hypoxia and hysteria","Over-ventilation by mechanical ventilation (ventilator)","Pain"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Source lists all five: Fever; Hyperventilation; Hypoxia and hysteria; Over-ventilation by mechanical ventilation (ventilator); Pain.  Why none wrong: All listed options appear in the source."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"Which signs and symptoms are associated with respiratory alkalosis? (SATA)","options":["Rapid breathing or hyperventilation","Light-headedness","Tachycardia & dysrhythmias related to hypokalemia","Nausea & vomiting","Tingling sensation, numbness, tetany and seizure"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Source lists all: rapid breathing/hyperventilation; light-headedness; tachycardia & dysrhythmias related to hypokalemia; nausea & vomiting; tingling sensation, numbness, tetany and seizure.  Why none wrong: All appear in the source."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"In respiratory alkalosis, dysrhythmias are related to which electrolyte abnormality?","options":["Hyperkalemia","Hypokalemia","Hypercalcemia","Hypernatremia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"Tachycardia & dysrhythmias related to hypokalaemia.\"  Why A wrong: Hyperkalemia is associated with acidosis.  Why C/D wrong: Not the abnormality stated."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"In the compensatory mechanism for respiratory alkalosis, the urinary system retains hydrogen ions and excretes what?","options":["Retains hydrogen ions and excretes bicarbonate","Excretes hydrogen ions, retains bicarbonate","Retains sodium only","Excretes potassium only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"Urinary system retained hydrogen ions and excrete bicarbonate.\"  Why B wrong: That is the opposite (acidosis compensation).  Why C/D wrong: Not the described renal response."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"Which nursing actions are appropriate for respiratory alkalosis? (SATA)","options":["Treat underlying cause","Monitor for respiratory distress and start oxygen if needed","Encourage for appropriate breathing pattern","Encourage voluntary holding of breath if appropriate; provide use of re-breathing mask as per order (paper bag breathing)","Hyperventilate the patient deliberately"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Source nursing actions: treat underlying cause; monitor for respiratory distress, start oxygen if needed; encourage appropriate breathing pattern; encourage voluntary holding of breath if appropriate; provide use of re-breathing mask as per order; provide carbon dioxide breathe or breathing in paper bag if ordered.  Why E wrong: Hyperventilating worsens alkalosis; the source recommends rebreathing/breath-holding to retain CO₂."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"For respiratory alkalosis, providing carbon dioxide rebreathing (e.g., breathing into a paper bag) is done to achieve what?","options":["Increase oxygen","Restore CO₂ levels (correct hypocapnia)","Decrease bicarbonate","Increase pH further"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"Provide carbon dioxide breathe or breathing in paper bag if ordered\" — to restore CO₂ that was lost via hyperventilation.  Why A wrong: The aim is to raise CO₂, not oxygen.  Why C/D wrong: Rebreathing CO₂ lowers pH toward normal, it does not decrease bicarbonate or raise pH."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"In the Summary of Acid Base Disturbances table, for Respiratory Acidosis the pH decreases and the primary disturbance is an increase in what?","options":["HCO₃⁻","PaCO₂","PaO₂","Sodium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source summary: Respiratory Acidosis — pH Decrease, Primary disturbance Increase PaCO₂; compensation: kidneys conserve H⁺ and excrete HCO₃⁻... (kidneys retain bicarbonate).  Why A wrong: HCO₃⁻ is the metabolic component, not the primary disturbance here.  Why C/D wrong: Not the primary disturbance."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"In the summary table, for Metabolic Acidosis the pH decreases and the primary disturbance is a decrease in what?","options":["PaCO₂","HCO₃⁻","PaO₂","pH only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source summary: Metabolic Acidosis — pH Decrease, Primary disturbance Decrease HCO₃⁻; compensation: lungs eliminate CO₂ and conserve HCO₃⁻.  Why A wrong: PaCO₂ change is the respiratory primary disturbance.  Why C/D wrong: Not the primary disturbance."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"In the summary table, for Respiratory Alkalosis the pH increases and the primary disturbance is a decrease in what?","options":["HCO₃⁻","PaCO₂","PaO₂","Sodium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source summary: Respiratory Alkalosis — pH Increase, Primary disturbance Decrease PaCO₂; compensation: kidneys conserve H⁺ and excrete HCO₃⁻.  Why A wrong: HCO₃⁻ is metabolic.  Why C/D wrong: Not the primary disturbance."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"In the summary table, for Metabolic Alkalosis the pH increases and the primary disturbance is an increase in what, with the lungs compensating?","options":["PaCO₂; lungs eliminate CO₂","HCO₃⁻; lungs compensate by ventilation to increase PaCO₂ and conserve H⁺ to excrete HCO₃⁻","PaO₂; kidneys excrete H⁺","Sodium; kidneys retain HCO₃⁻"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source summary: Metabolic Alkalosis — pH Increase, Primary disturbance Increase HCO₃⁻; compensation: lungs, ventilation to increase PaCO₂ and kidneys conserve H⁺ to excrete HCO₃⁻ (retain H⁺).  Why A wrong: PaCO₂ is the respiratory primary disturbance.  Why C/D wrong: Not as stated."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"Metabolic acidosis involves increased H⁺ ions due to which kind of metabolic process, with retention of too much acid?","options":["Abnormal metabolic process","Normal respiratory process","Renal excretory process","Hepatic detoxification"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"Increased H⁺ ions due to abnormal metabolic process. Loss of too much base, bicarbonate level below 22 mEq/L.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the described process."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"In metabolic acidosis, the bicarbonate level is in which range?","options":["Above 27 mEq/L","Below 22 mEq/L","22–27 mEq/L","Exactly 26 mEq/L"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"loss of too much base, bicarbonate level below 22 mEq/L.\"  Why A wrong: Above 27 mEq/L indicates metabolic alkalosis.  Why C wrong: That is normal.  Why D wrong: Not the stated level."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"In metabolic acidosis, the pH is in which range?","options":["pH below 7.35","pH above 7.45","pH 7.35–7.45","pH above 7.45 only when compensated"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"Retention too much acid, pH below 7.35.\"  Why B wrong: pH above 7.45 indicates alkalosis.  Why C wrong: That is normal pH.  Why D wrong: Metabolic acidosis pH is below 7.35."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"Diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA) leads to metabolic acidosis through insufficient supply of insulin causing decreased transport of glucose, resulting in production of which acids?","options":["Ketones bodies (acetoacetic acid, B-hydroxy butyric acid)","Carbonic acid only","Hydrochloric acid","Uric acid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"Diabetes ketoacidosis (DKA): insufficient supply of insulin cause decrease transport of glucose then breakdown of fat resulting production of ketones bodies (acetoacetic acid, B hydroxy butyric acid). In DKA, first cause hyperkalemia due to cell destruction than after begin initial treatment of acidosis hypokalaemia may occur.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the acids produced in DKA per source."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"In DKA, the source states hyperkalemia first occurs due to cell destruction; what happens to potassium after initial treatment of acidosis begins?","options":["Potassium continues to rise","Hypokalemia may occur","Potassium stays the same","Sodium replaces potassium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"In DKA, first cause hyperkalemia due to cell destruction than after begin initial treatment of acidosis hypokalaemia may occur.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: Source specifies hypokalemia may occur after treatment begins."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"Which conditions are listed as causes of metabolic acidosis? (SATA)","options":["Malnutrition, starvation & dieting","Acute & chronic renal failure","Excessive ingestion of acetylsalicylic acid/aspirin","Severe diarrhoea and fistula","Vomiting (loss of gastric acid)"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Source metabolic acidosis causes include malnutrition/starvation & dieting; acute & chronic renal failure; excessive ingestion of acetylsalicylic acid/aspirin; severe diarrhoea and fistula; excessive exercise resulting in absence of oxygen (lactic acid) will be occurs that cause in acidosis.  Why E wrong: Vomiting/loss of gastric acid causes metabolic ALKALOSIS, not acidosis."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"Excessive exercise resulting in the absence of oxygen produces which acid that causes metabolic acidosis?","options":["Carbonic acid","Lactic acid","Acetic acid","Citric acid"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"Excessive exercise resulting in absence of oxygen lactic acid will be occurs that cause in acidosis.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the acid produced from anaerobic exercise."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"Kussmaul's respiration, a sign of metabolic acidosis, is best described as which breathing pattern?","options":["Shallow and slow breaths","Fruity odour to the breathe, deep and rapid or labored breathing pattern mostly associated with metabolic acidosis or DKA","Periods of apnea alternating with breathing","Irregular gasping breaths"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"Kussmaul's Respiration: fruity odour to the breathe, deep and rapid or labored breathing pattern mostly associated with metabolic acidosis on DKA.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the Kussmaul pattern described."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"Which signs and symptoms are listed for metabolic acidosis? (SATA)","options":["Hyperkalemia which cause dysrhythmia","Drowsiness, headache, confusion and coma","Nausea, vomiting and diarrhoea","Kussmaul's respiration","Increased serum bicarbonate"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Source lists Kussmaul's respiration, hyperkalemia which cause dysrhythmia, drowsiness/headache/confusion and coma, nausea/vomiting and diarrhoea.  Why E wrong: Metabolic acidosis has DECREASED (below 22 mEq/L) bicarbonate, not increased."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"In the compensatory mechanism for metabolic acidosis, the respiratory system compensates by doing what?","options":["Hypoventilation to retain CO₂","Hyperventilation (respiratory system compensates by hyperventilation)","No change","Excreting bicarbonate via lungs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"Respiratory system compensates by hyperventilation\" and \"Urinary system excretes H⁺ ions and retains bicarbonate.\"  Why A wrong: Hypoventilation would worsen acidosis.  Why C wrong: Compensation occurs.  Why D wrong: Lungs excrete CO₂, not bicarbonate."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"In metabolic acidosis compensation, the urinary system excretes H⁺ ions and retains what?","options":["Bicarbonate","Sodium chloride","Potassium","Carbonic acid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"Urinary system excretes H⁺ ions and retains bicarbonate.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the retained substance per source."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"Which nursing actions are appropriate for metabolic acidosis? (SATA)","options":["Administer sodium bicarbonate or treat the underlying causes","Monitor for respiratory rate","Assess level of consciousness for CNS depression","Restrict bicarbonate administration in all cases","Monitor input and output"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: Source nursing actions for metabolic acidosis: administer sodium bicarbonate or treat underlying causes; monitor for respiratory rate; assess level of consciousness for CNS depression; monitor intake and output; initiate seizure precautions; monitor the potassium level closely.  Why D wrong: Bicarbonate administration is recommended, not restricted."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"Which nursing actions are listed for metabolic acidosis monitoring/safety? (SATA)","options":["Monitor intake and output","Initiate seizure precautions","Monitor the potassium level closely","Withhold all oxygen","Discontinue all fluids"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Source lists monitor intake and output, initiate seizure precautions, and monitor the potassium level closely.  Why D wrong: Oxygen is not to be withheld; not stated.  Why E wrong: Discontinuing all fluids is not a stated action."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"(RECALL THE HENKEY / PYQ) A client is diagnosed with acute renal failure. Which is the most common acid-base imbalance that occurs in this patient?","options":["Metabolic acidosis","Respiratory acidosis","Respiratory alkalosis","Metabolic alkalosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Source Right Answer: A. Rationale: due to accumulation or inability to excrete hydrogen ion, in ARF metabolic acidosis can be seen.  Why B wrong: Respiratory acidosis stems from lung dysfunction, not renal failure.  Why C wrong: Respiratory alkalosis is from hyperventilation.  Why D wrong: Metabolic alkalosis involves base excess, not the H⁺ accumulation of ARF."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"Metabolic alkalosis is characterized by a decrease in which ion due to an abnormal metabolic process, with a base bicarbonate excess?","options":["H⁺ (hydrogen) ions","Sodium ions","Potassium ions","Chloride ions"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"Decrease H⁺ ions due to abnormal metabolic process. Base bicarbonate excess; bicarbonate level above 27 mEq/L; pH is above 7.45.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: The defining change is decreased H⁺."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"In metabolic alkalosis, the bicarbonate level is in which range?","options":["Below 22 mEq/L","Above 27 mEq/L","22–27 mEq/L","Exactly 22 mEq/L"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"Base bicarbonate excess, bicarbonate level above 27 mEq/L.\"  Why A wrong: Below 22 mEq/L is metabolic acidosis.  Why C wrong: That is normal.  Why D wrong: Not the stated level."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"In metabolic alkalosis, the pH is in which range?","options":["Below 7.35","Above 7.45","7.35–7.45","Below 7.0"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"pH is above 7.45.\"  Why A wrong: Below 7.35 is acidosis.  Why C wrong: That is normal.  Why D wrong: Not stated."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"Massive blood transfusion can cause metabolic alkalosis because CPDA (citrate phosphate dextrose adenine) present in stored blood for anticoagulation purposes in this condition is metabolised into what?","options":["Lactic acid","Bicarbonate","Carbonic acid","Ketones"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"Massive blood transfusion because CPDA (citrate phosphate dextrose adenine) present in stored blood for anticoagulation purpose in that condition citrate metabolised into bicarbonate so after massive blood transfusion, metabolic alkalosis may occur.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: Citrate metabolises into bicarbonate, raising pH (alkalosis)."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"Which conditions are listed as causes of metabolic alkalosis? (SATA)","options":["Massive blood transfusion (citrate metabolised to bicarbonate)","Loss of gastric acid (excessive vomiting / GI suctioning may lead to metabolic alkalosis)","Hyperaldosteronism (sodium reabsorption and potassium excretion cause metabolic alkalosis)","Ingestion or infusion of excessive bicarbonate; diuretics","Diabetic ketoacidosis"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Source lists massive blood transfusion; loss of gastric acid (excessive vomiting/GI suctioning); hyperaldosteronism (sodium reabsorption and potassium excretion causes metabolic alkalosis); ingestion or infusion of excessive bicarbonate; diuretics.  Why E wrong: DKA causes metabolic ACIDOSIS, not alkalosis."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"In metabolic alkalosis, loss of gastric acid is caused by which clinical situations?","options":["Excessive vomiting or GI suctioning","Diarrhoea only","Renal failure","Excessive exercise"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"Loss of gastric acid e.g. excessive vomiting/GI suctioning may lead to metabolic alkalosis.\"  Why B wrong: Diarrhoea causes metabolic acidosis (loss of bicarbonate).  Why C wrong: Renal failure causes metabolic acidosis.  Why D wrong: Excessive exercise causes lactic acidosis."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"Hyperaldosteronism causes metabolic alkalosis through which mechanism?","options":["Sodium reabsorption and potassium excretion","Bicarbonate excretion","Hydrogen retention","Chloride retention"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"Hyperaldosteronism: sodium reabsorption and potassium excretion cause metabolic alkalosis.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the mechanism stated."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"Which signs and symptoms are listed for metabolic alkalosis? (SATA)","options":["Drowsiness, confusion, & nervousness","Hypoventilation","Dysrhythmia related to hypokalaemia","Nausea, vomiting and appetite loss","Hypocalcaemia (tetany, tremor, tingling sensation, seizure)"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Source lists all five: drowsiness/confusion/nervousness; hypoventilation; dysrhythmia related to hypokalaemia; nausea/vomiting and appetite loss; hypocalcaemia (tetany, tremor, tingling sensation, seizure).  Why none wrong: All appear in the source."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"In the compensatory mechanism for metabolic alkalosis, the respiratory system compensates by doing what?","options":["Increasing rate and depth of respiration","Decreasing rate and depth of respiration (hypoventilation to retain CO₂)","No change","Excreting hydrogen via lungs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"Respiratory system compensates by decreasing rate and depth of respiration\" and \"Urinary system excretes bicarbonate.\"  Why A wrong: Increasing respiration would worsen alkalosis.  Why C wrong: Compensation occurs.  Why D wrong: Lungs handle CO₂; H⁺ is handled renally."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"In metabolic alkalosis compensation, the urinary system excretes which substance?","options":["Hydrogen ions","Bicarbonate","Sodium chloride","Potassium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"Urinary system excretes bicarbonate.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the substance excreted per source."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"Which nursing actions are appropriate for metabolic alkalosis? (SATA)","options":["Treat underlying cause","Monitor potassium and calcium level","Initiate safety precaution","Prepare for fluid therapy for excretion of bicarbonate through kidneys and prepare for potassium replace(ment)","Administer additional sodium bicarbonate"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Source nursing actions: treat underlying cause; monitor potassium and calcium level; initiate safety precaution; prepare for fluid therapy for excretion of bicarbonate through kidneys; prepare for potassium replace(ment).  Why E wrong: Giving more bicarbonate would worsen alkalosis; the source recommends excreting bicarbonate, not administering it."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"According to the \"Should Remember\" box, what is the most accurate method of evaluating the effectiveness of oxygen therapy?","options":["Pulse oximetry","Arterial Blood Gas (ABG) analysis","Capillary refill","Respiratory rate counting"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"The most accurate method of evaluating the effectiveness of oxygen therapy is Arterial Blood Gas (ABG) analysis.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: Not stated as the most accurate method."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"Per the \"Should Remember\" box, the most accurate method to assess oxygen status in a patient with COPD is which test?","options":["Pulse oximetry","ABG (Arterial Blood Gas)","Chest X-ray","Spirometry"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"Most accurate method for assessment of oxygen status in a patient with COPD is ABG.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: Not stated as the most accurate method for COPD oxygen status."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"According to the \"Should Remember\" box, the normal anion gap is considered to be in which range?","options":["4 to 8 mEq/L","8 to 12 mEq/L (JAIIMS 2019)","12 to 16 mEq/L","16 to 20 mEq/L"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"The normal anion gap is considered to be between 8 to 12 mEq/L.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: Outside the stated normal range."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"(RECALL THE HENKEY / PYQ) A baby who was diagnosed with pyloric stenosis has continued to have projectile vomiting. With prolonged vomiting, the infant is prone to which acid-base imbalance?","options":["Respiratory acidosis","Respiratory alkalosis","Metabolic acidosis","Metabolic alkalosis"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Source Right Answer: D. Rationale: removal of gastric HCl due to vomiting or nasogastric (NG) suction results in metabolic alkalosis because hydrogen ion is removing with vomitus and subsequently HCO₃⁻ ions are increased.  Why A wrong: Respiratory acidosis is from CO₂ retention.  Why B wrong: Respiratory alkalosis is from hyperventilation.  Why C wrong: Vomiting loses acid, not base, so it is alkalosis not acidosis."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"In Fluid Volume Deficit, which cardiovascular assessment finding is expected for the pulse?","options":["Bounding, increased pulse","Thready, increased pulse rate; decreased peripheral pulses","Slow, full pulse","No change in pulse"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source (Fluid Volume Deficit, Cardiovascular): \"Thready, increased pulse rate; decreased peripheral pulses.\"  Why A wrong: Bounding/increased pulse is a Fluid Volume Excess finding.  Why C/D wrong: Not stated for deficit."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"In Fluid Volume Deficit, what change occurs in blood pressure and on positional change?","options":["Elevated blood pressure","Decreased blood pressure and orthostatic (postural) hypotension","No change in blood pressure","Hypertensive crisis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"Decreased blood pressure and orthostatic (postural) hypotension.\"  Why A wrong: Elevated BP is a Fluid Volume Excess finding.  Why C/D wrong: Not stated for deficit."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"In Fluid Volume Deficit, what happens to the hand veins and central venous pressure (CVP)?","options":["Distended neck and hand veins; elevated CVP","Flat neck and hand veins in dependent positions; decreased central venous pressure","No vein change; high CVP","Bounding veins"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source: \"Flat neck and hand veins in dependent positions; Decreased central venous pressure.\"  Why A wrong: Distended veins/elevated CVP are Fluid Volume Excess findings.  Why C/D wrong: Not stated for deficit."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"Dysrhythmias may be present in which fluid volume state(s)?","options":["Only fluid volume deficit","Both fluid volume deficit and fluid volume excess","Only fluid volume excess","Neither"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source lists \"Dysrhythmias\" under cardiovascular findings for both Fluid Volume Deficit and Fluid Volume Excess columns.  Why A/C wrong: It appears in both columns, not just one.  Why D wrong: It is listed."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"In Fluid Volume Deficit, which respiratory finding is expected?","options":["Decreased rate and depth of respiration","Increased rate and depth of respirations; dyspnea","Moist crackles on auscultation","Slow shallow breathing"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source (Fluid Volume Deficit, Respiratory): \"Increased rate and depth of respirations; Dyspnea.\"  Why A wrong: Not a deficit finding.  Why C wrong: Moist crackles are a Fluid Volume Excess finding.  Why D wrong: Not stated."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"In Fluid Volume Excess, which respiratory findings are expected? (SATA)","options":["Increased respiratory rate (shallow respirations)","Dyspnea","Moist crackles on auscultation","Flat neck veins","Decreased urine output as primary respiratory sign"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Source (Fluid Volume Excess, Respiratory): increased respiratory rate (shallow respirations), dyspnea, moist crackles on auscultation.  Why D wrong: Flat neck veins are a deficit (cardiovascular) finding.  Why E wrong: That is renal, and decreased output is the deficit finding."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"In Fluid Volume Deficit, which neuromuscular findings are expected? (SATA)","options":["Decreased central nervous system activity, from lethargy to coma","Skeletal muscle weakness","Fever, depending on the amount of fluid lost","Altered level of consciousness","Headache"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Source (Fluid Volume Deficit, Neuromuscular): decreased CNS activity from lethargy to coma; skeletal muscle weakness; fever depending on the amount of fluid lost.  Why D wrong: Altered level of consciousness is the Fluid Volume Excess neuromuscular finding.  Why E wrong: Headache is a Fluid Volume Excess finding."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"In Fluid Volume Excess, which neuromuscular findings are expected? (SATA)","options":["Altered level of consciousness","Skeletal muscle weakness","Headache, visual disturbances","Paresthesia","Fever depending on amount of fluid lost"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Source (Fluid Volume Excess, Neuromuscular): altered level of consciousness, skeletal muscle weakness, headache/visual disturbances, paresthesia.  Why E wrong: Fever depending on amount of fluid lost is a Fluid Volume Deficit finding."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"In Fluid Volume Deficit, what renal finding is expected?","options":["Increased urine output","Decreased urine output","Pitting edema","No change in urine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source (Fluid Volume Deficit, Renal): \"Decreased urine output.\"  Why A wrong: Increased urine output (if kidneys can compensate) is the Fluid Volume Excess finding.  Why C wrong: Pitting edema is integumentary in excess.  Why D wrong: Output decreases."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"In Fluid Volume Excess, the renal finding states increased urine output if the kidneys can compensate; what is true if kidney damage is present?","options":["Urine output still increases","Decreased urine output if kidney damage is the cause","No urine produced","Output unaffected"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source (Fluid Volume Excess, Renal): \"Increased urine output if kidneys can compensate; decreased urine output if kidney damage is the cause.\"  Why A wrong: With kidney damage, output decreases.  Why C/D wrong: Not as stated."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"In Fluid Volume Deficit, which integumentary findings are expected? (SATA)","options":["Dry skin","Poor turgor, tenting","Dry mouth","Pitting edema in dependent areas","Pale, cool skin"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Source (Fluid Volume Deficit, Integumentary): dry skin; poor turgor, tenting; dry mouth.  Why D wrong: Pitting edema is a Fluid Volume Excess integumentary finding.  Why E wrong: Pale, cool skin is a Fluid Volume Excess finding."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"In Fluid Volume Excess, which integumentary findings are expected? (SATA)","options":["Pale, cool skin","Pitting edema in dependent areas","Dry mouth","Poor turgor, tenting","Dry skin"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: Source (Fluid Volume Excess, Integumentary): pale, cool skin; pitting edema in dependent areas.  Why C/D/E wrong: Dry mouth, poor turgor/tenting, and dry skin are all Fluid Volume Deficit findings."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"In Fluid Volume Deficit, which gastrointestinal findings are expected? (SATA)","options":["Increased motility and diminished bowel sounds","Constipation","Decreased body weight","Thirst","Diarrhea"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Source (Fluid Volume Deficit, Gastrointestinal): increased motility and diminished bowel sounds; constipation; decreased body weight; thirst.  Why E wrong: Diarrhea is a Fluid Volume Excess GI finding."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"In Fluid Volume Excess, which gastrointestinal findings are expected? (SATA)","options":["Increased motility in the gastrointestinal tract","Diarrhea","Increased body weight","Liver enlargement","Ascites"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Source (Fluid Volume Excess, Gastrointestinal): increased motility in the GI tract; diarrhea; increased body weight; liver enlargement; ascites.  Why none wrong: All appear in the source."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"In Fluid Volume Deficit, which laboratory findings are INCREASED? (SATA)","options":["Increased serum osmolality","Increased hematocrit","Increased blood urea nitrogen (BUN) level","Increased serum sodium level","Increased urinary specific gravity"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Source (Fluid Volume Deficit, Laboratory Findings): increased serum osmolality, increased hematocrit, increased BUN level, increased serum sodium level, increased urinary specific gravity.  Why none wrong: All five are listed as increased in deficit."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"In Fluid Volume Excess, which laboratory findings are DECREASED? (SATA)","options":["Decreased serum osmolality","Decreased hematocrit","Decreased BUN level","Decreased serum sodium level","Decreased urine specific gravity"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Source (Fluid Volume Excess, Laboratory Findings): decreased serum osmolality, decreased hematocrit, decreased BUN level, decreased serum sodium level, decreased urine specific gravity.  Why none wrong: All five are listed as decreased in excess."},{"section":"Batch 022 · Acid-base buffer systems…","question":"A patient in fluid volume excess shows distended neck and hand veins, elevated central venous pressure, and elevated blood pressure. These are findings of which body system in fluid volume excess?","options":["Respiratory","Cardiovascular","Renal","Gastrointestinal"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Source (Fluid Volume Excess, Cardiovascular): bounding/increased pulse, elevated blood pressure, distended neck and hand veins, elevated central venous pressure, dysrhythmias.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are cardiovascular findings, not respiratory, renal, or GI."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"A nurse is explaining cannulation to a nursing student. Which statement best describes the overview of cannulation?","options":["It is a non-invasive and painless procedure","It is an invasive or painful procedure","It is performed only in surgical theatres","It never carries a risk of infection"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states cannulation is an invasive or painful procedure.  Why A wrong: It is invasive and painful, not non-invasive/painless.  Why C wrong: The source does not restrict it to surgical theatres.  Why D wrong: Catheter-related infection and sepsis are listed complications."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"How many types of cannulation does the source describe?","options":["One","Two — arterial cannulation and venous cannulation","Three","Four"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists two types: arterial cannulation and venous cannulation.  Why A/C/D wrong: Only two types are described."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"Why is arterial cannulation performed?","options":["Only to administer medications","For more accurate measurement of haemodynamic pressure (e.g., BP, CVP, PAWP, etc.)","To collect urine specimens","For transparent dressing change"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In arterial cannulation, a cannula is inserted into the artery for more accurate measurement of haemodynamic pressure such as BP, CVP, PAWP.  Why A wrong: That describes venous access, not the primary arterial cannulation purpose.  Why C/D wrong: Not related to arterial cannulation purpose."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"When maintaining an arterial line, the nurse should keep all components of the pressure monitoring system in what state?","options":["Open to air","Sterile","Disconnected","Loosened"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to always keep all components of the pressure monitoring system sterile.  Why A/C/D wrong: These would compromise the system and increase infection risk."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"Standard practice during arterial cannulation includes replacing transducers at what interval, along with other components of the system?","options":["Every 12 hours","At 72 hours intervals","Every 24 hours","Weekly"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source specifies replacing transducers at 72 hours intervals along with other components, using the flush solution and the continuous flush device.  Why A/C/D wrong: These intervals are not specified for transducer replacement."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"Which agent must NOT be used when flushing the arterial line?","options":["Normal saline","Heparinized flush solution","Dextrose-containing solution","Continuous flush solution"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states: do not use any dextrose-containing solution to flush through the arterial system.  Why A/B/D wrong: These are not the prohibited flushing agents identified by the source for the arterial system."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"Which artery is the FIRST priority for arterial cannulation?","options":["Brachial artery","Femoral artery","Radial artery","Carotid artery"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists the radial artery as the first priority for arterial cannulation.  Why A/B wrong: Brachial and femoral are listed as second priority.  Why D wrong: The carotid artery is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"Which arteries are the SECOND priority sites for arterial cannulation?","options":["Radial and ulnar","Brachial and femoral artery","Carotid and subclavian","Dorsalis pedis and tibial"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists brachial and femoral artery as second priority.  Why A wrong: Radial is first priority; ulnar is not listed.  Why C/D wrong: Not listed as second priority."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"Which of the following is a complication of arterial cannulation? (SATA)","options":["Occlusion","Thrombosis","Ischemia","Hematoma formation","Local and catheter-related infection and sepsis"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists occlusion, thrombosis, ischemia, hematoma formation, and local and catheter-related infection and sepsis as complications of arterial cannulation.  All listed options are stated complications."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"What is intravenous (IV) cannulation?","options":["A technique in which a cannula is placed inside an artery","A technique in which a cannula is placed inside a vein to provide venous access","A method to measure arterial BP","A method to flush the central line"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines IV cannulation as a technique in which a cannula is placed inside a vein.  Why A wrong: That is arterial cannulation.  Why C/D wrong: Not the definition of IV cannulation."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"Venous access allows sampling of blood as well as administration of which of the following? (SATA)","options":["Fluids","Medications","Parenteral nutrition","Chemotherapy","Blood products"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The source states venous access allows sampling of blood and administration of fluids, medications, parenteral nutrition, chemotherapy, and blood products.  All options are explicitly listed."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"What are the two major types of venous cannulation?","options":["Arterial and capillary","Central venous catheter and peripheral venous catheter (PVC)","Hickman and Broviac","Tunnelled and non-tunnelled"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states there are two major types — central venous catheter and peripheral venous catheter (PVC).  Why A wrong: Capillary cannulation is not described.  Why C/D wrong: These are subtypes/features, not the two major types named."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"What is a central venous catheter (CVC) / central line?","options":["A small cannula placed in the hand","A catheter placed into a large vein which forms a venous access","A catheter placed into an artery","A urinary drainage device"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states a CVC/central line is a catheter placed into a large vein which forms a venous access.  Why A wrong: That describes a peripheral venous catheter.  Why C/D wrong: It is venous, not arterial or urinary."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"In which patients is placement of larger catheters in more centrally located veins often needed?","options":["Stable outpatients","Critically ill patients, or those requiring prolonged treatment interventions","Patients on oral medications only","Healthy donors"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states larger centrally located catheters are often needed in critically ill patients or those requiring prolonged treatment interventions or therapies.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not indicated by the source for central placement."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"How often should dressing of the central line need to be performed for transparent dressings?","options":["Every 24 hours","Every 2 days for gauze and in every 7 days for transparent dressings","Every 12 hours","Once a month"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states regular dressing of the central line should be performed every 2 days for gauze and in every 7 days for transparent dressings.  Why A/C/D wrong: These intervals are not stated."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"When changing a central line dressing, what should be done to the dressing if it becomes damp, soiled, or loosened?","options":["Reinforce it with tape","Change the dressing","Leave it until the next scheduled change","Apply alcohol over it"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states change dressing when it is damp, loosened or soiled.  Why A/C/D wrong: A compromised dressing must be changed, not reinforced or left."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"For how long should the access port of the central line be scrubbed before use in a twisting motion?","options":["1 to 2 seconds","5 to 8 seconds","10 to 15 seconds","30 to 60 seconds"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states scrub the access port of the central line each time before use for 10 to 15 seconds, generating friction by scrubbing in a twisting motion.  Why A/B/D wrong: These durations are not the stated value."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"What is a PICC (Peripherally Inserted Central Catheter)?","options":["A rigid metal catheter placed in the femoral vein","A thin, soft, long catheter (tube) inserted into a vein in the arm","A short cannula in the back of the hand","A urinary catheter"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source describes a PICC as a thin, soft, long catheter (tube) inserted into a vein in the arm.  Why A/C/D wrong: PICC is inserted in the arm, is thin/soft/long, and is a central venous device."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"What is the major form of PICC?","options":["Hickman line","M/C vein for PICC line is right basilic vein due to its larger size and superficial location","Femoral catheter","Tunnelled chest catheter"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the M/C (most common) vein for PICC line is the right basilic vein due to its larger size and superficial location.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the stated PICC vein/feature."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"Which is the most common site for placing a central line that gives access in the chest and axillary vein in the arm?","options":["Internal jugular vein","Right subclavian vein (SCV)","Femoral vein","Basilic vein"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists the right subclavian vein (SCV) as most common in chest and axillary vein in arm, most common for long term.  Why A wrong: Internal jugular is used for short term therapy.  Why C wrong: Femoral has the highest chance of trauma/infection complications.  Why D wrong: Basilic relates to PICC."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"The internal jugular vein (IJV) for a central line is most commonly used for what duration of therapy?","options":["Long term therapy up to 4 weeks or more","Short term therapy up to 4 weeks","Permanent access","Lifelong access"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the internal jugular vein (IJV) is used for short term therapy up to 4 weeks.  Why A/C/D wrong: The IJV is for short term per the source."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"The femoral vein as a central line site is associated with which of the following?","options":["Lowest infection risk","Groin site, highest chance of getting infection due to proximity of perineum and infection","Long-term preferred therapy","No complications"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the femoral vein is in the groin site, has highest chance of getting infection due to proximity of perineum and infection.  Why A/C/D wrong: Femoral carries the highest, not lowest, infection risk."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"Which of the following is a most common complication of central line? (SATA)","options":["Pneumothorax","Occlusion and vascular perforation","Catheter-related bloodstream infections","Misplacement and venous air embolism","Hair loss"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists pneumothorax; occlusion and vascular perforation; catheter-related bloodstream infections; misplacement and venous air embolism as most common complications of central line.  Why E wrong: Hair loss is not a listed complication of central line."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"A nurse is reviewing central line bundle care components. Which of the following are part of the central line bundle care? (SATA)","options":["Perform hand hygiene","Use maximal sterile barrier precautions","Clean skin with chlorhexidine prior to insertion","Remove unnecessary catheters as soon as possible","Leave the catheter indefinitely"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists hand hygiene, maximal sterile barrier precautions, clean skin with chlorhexidine prior to insertion, and remove unnecessary catheters as soon as possible.  Why E wrong: The bundle requires removing unnecessary catheters as soon as possible, not leaving them indefinitely."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"What is a peripheral venous catheter (PVC)?","options":["A long catheter inserted into the heart","A small, flexible tube placed into a peripheral vein for venous access","A catheter placed into the femoral artery","A dressing material"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines PVC as a small flexible tube placed into a peripheral vein for venous access used to administer IV therapy such as medication and fluids.  Why A/C/D wrong: PVC is peripheral and venous, not cardiac/arterial."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"Where is cannulation via a peripheral venous catheter mostly used, and what is its ease of access?","options":["Only in ICUs, difficult to assess","Mostly in hospitals, easy to assess","Only at home, impossible to assess","Only in operation theatres"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states this type of cannulation is mostly used in hospitals and is easy to assess.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not consistent with the source."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"An IV cannula is a portable device used for IV cannulation. By what other names is it also called?","options":["Cannula and venflon and medicath","Stylet and trocar","Lumen and hub","Bevel and flange"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the IV cannula is also called as venflon and medicath.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the alternate names given in the source."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"What sizes of IV cannula are available, and what colour coding do they use?","options":["Sizes 14G to 26G with universal color coding for easy recognition","Only one universal size","Sizes 1 to 5 with no color coding","Sizes 30G to 50G"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states IV cannulas are available from sizes 14G to 26G with universal color coding for easy recognition.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not consistent with the stated range/coding."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"Regarding cannula gauge and flow rate, which statement is correct?","options":["The larger the gauge number, the wider the cannula","The smaller the gauge number, the wider the cannula and has higher flow rate","Gauge number does not affect flow rate","The smaller the gauge, the slower the flow"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the smaller the gauge, the wider the cannula and the higher its flow rate.  Why A wrong: Smaller gauge number = wider cannula (inverse relationship).  Why C wrong: Gauge directly affects flow rate.  Why D wrong: Smaller gauge = faster flow."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"What is the normal adult IV cannula size range?","options":["14-16G","18-20G","24-26G","8-10G"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states normal adult size is 18-20G.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the stated normal adult size."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"A patient requires rapid fluid transfusion (e.g., major trauma, blood transfusion or anaesthetics, preoperative and postoperative). Which cannula gauge is preferred?","options":["24-26G","16-18G","22-24G","26G only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states situations requiring rapid fluid transfusion use 16-18G.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are indicated for other situations."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"Which cannula gauge is preferred for elderly patients?","options":["24-26G","16-18G","22-24G","18-20G"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states preferred elderly size is 24-26G.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the preferred elderly size per the source."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"Which cannula gauge is preferred for paediatric patients (giving small amount of fluid in slow rate)?","options":["18-20G","24-26G","16-18G","14G"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states preferred paediatric size is 24-26G (giving small amount of fluid in slow rate).  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the stated paediatric size."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"Which cannula gauge is preferred for infants and neonates?","options":["14-16G","24-26G","18-20G","22G"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states infants and neonates use 24-26G.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the stated size for infants and neonates."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"Which cannula gauge is preferred for peripheral fan emulsion or lipids?","options":["22-24G","20-22G","16-18G","26G"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states 20-22G for peripheral fan emulsion or lipids.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the stated size."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"Which cannula gauge is preferred for routinely, standard IV fluids or in general ward?","options":["16-18G","22-24G","26G","14G"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states for routinely, standard IV fluids or in general ward, use 22-24G.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the stated size."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"Which cannula gauge is preferred for a patient with very small veins?","options":["24-26G","16-18G","20-22G","14G"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states for a patient with very small veins, use 24-26G.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the stated size."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"What is a butterfly cannula?","options":["A central line catheter","A winged infusion set or scalp vein set; a device specialized for vein puncture","A urinary device","A burette set component"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states a butterfly cannula is also known as a winged infusion set or scalp vein set, and it is a device specialized for vein puncture.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the definition of a butterfly cannula."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"A butterfly cannula is specialized for accessing what type of vein?","options":["Deep, large veins only","A superficial vein or artery for either intravenous injection or phlebotomy","Central veins only","Arteries only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states it is a device specialized for vein puncture such as accessing a superficial vein or artery for either intravenous injection or phlebotomy.  Why A/C/D wrong: It accesses superficial vessels for injection or phlebotomy."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"The butterfly cannula consists of how many flexible wings?","options":["One flexible wing","Two bilateral flexible wings","Three wings","No wings"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states it consists of two bilateral flexible wings.  Why A/C/D wrong: It has two flexible wings."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"In which patient population is a butterfly cannula most commonly used because infant's leg veins are too much small or non-visible?","options":["Adults","Infants and paediatrics","Geriatric patients","Pregnant women"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states it is most commonly used in infants and paediatrics because infant's leg veins are too much small or non-visible.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the stated population."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"Which components make up an IV set / IV tubing? (SATA)","options":["A spike for the bag or bottle","Drip chamber","Roller clamp","Y-set and adapter end","Attachment to the cannula that is inserted into the patient"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The source states IV tubing contains a spike for the bag or bottle, drip chamber, roller clamp, Y-set and adapter end and attachment to the cannula that is inserted into the patient.  All options are listed components."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"When is a macrodrip chamber used?","options":["When the solution is thin or to be infused slowly","When the solution is thick or to be infused rapidly","Only for paediatric patients","Only for blood products"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states a macrodrip chamber is used if the solution is thick or to be infused rapidly.  Why A wrong: That describes when not to use macrodrip.  Why C/D wrong: Not specified as the macrodrip criterion."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"What is the drop factor of a macrodrip chamber?","options":["60 drops/ml","10-20 gtt/ml (drops/ml)","100 drops/ml","5 drops/ml"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the macrodrip drop factor is 10-20 gtt/ml (drops/ml) as per manufacturer.  Why A wrong: 60 drops/ml is the microdrip factor.  Why C/D wrong: Not the stated values."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"When is a microdrip chamber used?","options":["When fluid is to be infused at a slow rate (less than 50 ml/hr) or if the solution contains potent medications that need to be titrated, such as in critical patients and in paediatrics","When rapid transfusion is required","For thick solutions only","For blood transfusion only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the microdrip chamber delivers when fluid is to be infused at a slow rate (less than 50 ml/hr) or if the solution contains potent medications that need to be titrated, such as in critical patients and paediatrics.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe macrodrip / other settings."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"What is the drop factor of a microdrip chamber?","options":["10-20 drops/ml","About 60 drops/ml","30 drops/ml","100 drops/ml"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the microdrip chamber drop factor is about 60 drops/ml.  Why A wrong: 10-20 is macrodrip.  Why C/D wrong: Not stated values."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"What is the main function of a filter in an IV set?","options":["To regulate flow rate","To provide protection by preventing any particles from entering the client's vein","To warm the fluid","To measure the volume"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states a filter provides protection by preventing any particles from entering the client's vein.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are functions of other components."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"A 0.22 µm filter is used for which type of solutions?","options":["Solutions containing lipids or albumin","Most of solutions except lipids","Blood transfusion only","Blood components except platelets"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states a 0.22 µm filter is used for most of solutions except lipids.  Why A wrong: That describes the 1.2 µm filter.  Why C/D wrong: That describes the 170-260 µm filter."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"A 1.2 µm filter is used for which type of solutions?","options":["Most solutions except lipids","Solutions containing lipids or albumin","Blood transfusion","Crystalloids only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states a 1.2 µm filter is used for solution containing lipids or albumin.  Why A wrong: That is the 0.22 µm filter.  Why C/D wrong: Not the stated use."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"A 170 to 260 µm filter is used for which purpose?","options":["Lipid infusion","Blood transfusion line for all blood components, except platelets","Albumin infusion only","Crystalloid infusion only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states a 170 to 260 µm filter is used for blood transfusion line for all blood components, except platelets.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the stated use."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"What is a burette set?","options":["A flow regulator","A volume control administration set; a small device attached below the primary infusion to regulate the mini bag","A filter","A type of cannula"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the burette set is also known as volume control administration set; it is a small device attached below the primary infusion to regulate the mini bag.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the definition of a burette set."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"Through the burette set, medication is added to a small amount of IV solution and administered through an IV line. It is primarily used in which population?","options":["Adults","Paediatrics, to avoid accidental large fluid volume administration","Geriatrics","Pregnant women"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states it is used primarily in paediatrics to avoid accidental large fluid volume administration.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the stated primary population."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"What is a dial flow regulator?","options":["A filter for blood","A medical device that is used while regulating the flow of a liquid or fluid through an IV","A type of cannula","A burette"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states a dial flow regulator is a medical device used while regulating the flow of a liquid or fluid through an IV.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the definition."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"A dial flow regulator is sometimes also referred to as which device?","options":["An automatic IV regulator","A manual flow regulator","An infusion pump","A syringe driver"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states this device can also be referred to as a manual flow regulator.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the stated alternate name."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"According to the IV cannula color coding table, which color corresponds to 14G?","options":["Grey","Orange","Green","Pink"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source color coding table lists 14G as orange.  Why A wrong: Grey is 16G.  Why C wrong: Green is 18G.  Why D wrong: Pink is 20G."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"According to the IV cannula color coding table, the orange (14G) cannula is used for which purpose?","options":["Slow infusion rate in paediatrics","Used in emergencies or critical care, massive trauma","Routine infusion only","Older children, older adults"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states orange 14G is used in emergencies or critical care, massive trauma.  Why A/C/D wrong: These correspond to other gauges."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"Which gauge corresponds to the grey-coded IV cannula?","options":["14G","16G","18G","20G"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source color coding table lists 16G as grey.  Why A/C/D wrong: Orange=14G, green=18G, pink=20G."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"The grey (16G) cannula is used for which of the following? (SATA)","options":["Trauma","Surgery","Large volume infusion","Rapid infusion, required large vein","Slow infusion in neonates"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source states grey 16G is used for trauma, surgery, large volume infusion, rapid infusion, required large vein.  Why E wrong: Slow neonatal infusion corresponds to smaller gauges, not 16G."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"Which gauge corresponds to the white-coded IV cannula?","options":["16G","17G","18G","20G"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source color coding table lists 17G as white.  Why A/C/D wrong: Grey=16G, green=18G, pink=20G."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"The white (17G) cannula is used for which of the following? (SATA)","options":["Trauma","Surgery","Large volume infusion","Rapid infusion, required large vein","Slow infusion in neonates"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source states white 17G is used for trauma, surgery, large volume infusion, rapid infusion, required large vein.  Why E wrong: Not listed for 17G."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"Which gauge corresponds to the green-coded IV cannula?","options":["16G","17G","18G","20G"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source color coding table lists 18G as green.  Why A/B/D wrong: Grey=16G, white=17G, pink=20G."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"The green (18G) cannula is used for which of the following? (SATA)","options":["Blood transfusion","Large volume infusion","Required large vein","Massive trauma only","Slow infusion in paediatrics"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states green 18G is used for blood transfusion, large volume infusion, required large vein.  Why D wrong: Massive trauma corresponds to 14G (orange).  Why E wrong: Slow paediatric infusion corresponds to smaller gauges."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"Which gauge corresponds to the pink-coded IV cannula?","options":["18G","20G","22G","24G"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source color coding table lists 20G as pink.  Why A/C/D wrong: Green=18G; other gauges differ."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"The pink (20G) cannula is used for which of the following? (SATA)","options":["Routine infusion","IV access","Most commonly used","Emergencies and massive trauma","Blood transfusion requiring rapid flow"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states pink 20G is used for routine infusion, IV access, most commonly used.  Why D wrong: Emergencies/massive trauma is 14G.  Why E wrong: Not the stated use for 20G."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"Which gauge corresponds to the yellow-coded IV cannula?","options":["22G","24G","20G","18G"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source color coding table lists 24G as yellow.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are other coded gauges."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"The yellow (24G) cannula is used for which of the following? (SATA)","options":["Older children","Older adults","Small fragile veins","Massive trauma","Rapid large volume infusion"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states yellow 24G is used for older children, older adults and fragile veins (small fragile veins, paediatric and elderly).  Why D/E wrong: These correspond to large-bore (14G/16G) cannulas."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"Which gauge corresponds to the violet-coded IV cannula?","options":["24G","26G","22G","20G"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source color coding table lists 26G as violet.  Why A/C/D wrong: 24G is yellow; others differ."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"The violet (26G) cannula is used for which of the following? (SATA)","options":["Newborns","Infants","Massive trauma","Blood transfusion in adults","Rapid surgical infusion"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states violet 26G is used for newborns, infants.  Why C/D/E wrong: These correspond to large-bore cannulas, not 26G."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"According to the recommended administration set changes guideline, IV cannula should be changed at what interval?","options":["Every 24 hours","Every 72 to 96 hours or earlier if there is evidence of phlebitis or infiltration","Every 5 days","Every 7 days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source guideline states IV cannula should be changed every 72 to 96 hours or earlier if there is evidence of phlebitis or infiltration.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the stated cannula change interval."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"According to the administration set changes guideline, the oil set or fluid bottles should be changed at what interval?","options":["Every 24 hours","Every 72 hours or 3 days","Every 12 hours","After each unit"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source guideline states oil set or fluid bottles should be changed every 72 hours or 3 days.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the stated interval."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"According to the administration set changes guideline, blood administration (RTC set) should be changed at what interval?","options":["Every 72 hours","Every 4 hours or after each unit","Every 24 hours","Every 7 days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source guideline states blood administration (RTC set) should be changed every 4 hours or after each unit.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the stated interval."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"According to the administration set changes guideline, the parenteral nutrition sets using for fat emulsion should be changed at what interval?","options":["Every 72 hours","Every 24 hours","Every 4 hours","Every 7 days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source guideline states parenteral nutrition sets used for fat emulsion should be changed every 24 hours.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the stated interval."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"According to the administration set changes guideline, the parenteral nutrition set used for non-fat emulsions should be changed at what interval?","options":["Every 24 hours","Every 96 hours","Every 4 hours","After each unit"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source guideline states parenteral nutrition set used for non-fat emulsions should be changed every 96 hours.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the stated interval."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"(RECALL THE NCLEX, p.271) While changing the dressing, the nurse inspects that a wound site becomes redness, swelling, induration and purulent discharge. What is the possible complication?","options":["Inflammation","Evisceration","Dehiscence","Infection"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The source's given answer is D — Infection; purulent discharge with redness, swelling, and induration indicates infection.  Why A wrong: Inflammation alone does not typically present purulent discharge.  Why B wrong: Evisceration is protrusion of organs through the wound.  Why C wrong: Dehiscence is separation of wound edges."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"What is infection as a complication of IV therapy?","options":["Entry of air into the line","The presence of microorganisms","Fluid leaking into tissue","Vein inflammation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines infection as the presence of microorganism.  Why A wrong: That is air embolism.  Why C wrong: That is infiltration.  Why D wrong: That is phlebitis."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"How often should IV fluid bottles (N5, DNS, RL, etc.) be changed to prevent contamination or bacterial growth?","options":["Every 12 hours","Every 24 hours","Every 72 hours","Every 7 days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states change IV fluid bottles every 24 hours to prevent contamination or bacterial growth.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the stated interval."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"The cannula and IV set/dressing should be changed at what interval to prevent infection?","options":["Every 12 hours","Every 3-4 days or 72-96 hours","Every 7 days","Every 24 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states change the cannula and IV set should be changed every 3-4 days or 72-96 hours.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the stated interval."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"Which of the following are signs and symptoms of infection at the IV site? (SATA)","options":["Fever","Inflammation and tenderness at insertion site","Increased WBC","Purulent discharge","Decreased blood pressure"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists fever, inflammation and tenderness at insertion site, increased WBC, and purulent discharge as signs/symptoms of infection.  Why E wrong: Decreased BP is not listed among infection signs here."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"What is air embolism in the context of IV therapy?","options":["Microorganism entry","Air entry due to air into the catheter system, catheter system opened, IV tubing disconnected","Fluid leaking into tissue","Vein inflammation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines air embolism as air occurs due to entry of air into the catheter system, catheter system opened, IV tubing disconnected.  Why A wrong: That is infection.  Why C wrong: That is infiltration.  Why D wrong: That is phlebitis."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"A patient suspected of air embolism is instructed to perform which maneuver?","options":["Cough forcefully","Valsalva maneuver (take a deep breath, hold it and bear down) when IV tubing and cap changes","Hyperventilate","Lie prone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source instructs the patient to perform the Valsalva maneuver (take a deep breath, hold it and bear down) when IV tubing and cap changes.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the stated maneuver."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"As a preventive measure for air embolism, what should the nurse check on all connections and IV tubing?","options":["The flow rate","Check all connections and ensure tubing connections","The dressing date","The cannula gauge"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states check all connections and ensure tubing connections to prevent air embolism.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the stated preventive check."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"Which of the following are signs and symptoms of air embolism? (SATA)","options":["Chest pain","Dyspnea","Hypotension","Rapid and weak pulse","Respiratory distress"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists chest pain, dyspnoea, hypotension, rapid and weak pulse and respiratory distress as signs of air embolism.  All options are listed."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"What is the FIRST (priority) nursing intervention for air embolism?","options":["Place the client in side lying position with the head lower than the feet","Clamp the IV catheter (first priority)","Administer oxygen","Inform the doctor"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists clamping the IV catheter as the first priority nursing intervention for air embolism.  Why A wrong: That is the second priority.  Why C/D wrong: These are third/subsequent actions."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"What is the SECOND priority nursing intervention for air embolism?","options":["Clamp the IV catheter","Place the client in side lying position with the head lower than the feet, lie on the right side of heart","Administer oxygen and inform the doctor","Remove the cannula"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the second priority is to place the client in side lying position with the head lower than the feet (lie on the right side of heart).  Why A wrong: Clamping is the first priority.  Why C wrong: Oxygen/informing is third priority.  Why D wrong: Not the stated step."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"What is the THIRD nursing intervention for air embolism?","options":["Clamp the IV catheter","Place client in side lying position","Administer oxygen and inform to and our doctor","Discontinue the dressing"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists administer oxygen and inform the doctor as the third action.  Why A/B wrong: These are first and second priorities.  Why D wrong: Not the stated step."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"What is infiltration as a complication of IV cannulation/therapy?","options":["The presence of microorganism","Entry of air into the catheter","The first most common complication of IV cannulation/therapy, in which IV cannula or catheter is displaced which lead to fluid leaking into surrounding tissues","Inflammation of the vein"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines infiltration as the first most common complication of IV cannulation/therapy, in which IV cannula or catheter is displaced which leads to fluid leaking into surrounding tissues.  Why A wrong: That is infection.  Why B wrong: That is air embolism.  Why D wrong: That is phlebitis."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"Which of the following are signs and symptoms (Hallmark signs) of infiltration? (SATA)","options":["Insertion site is pale, cool and oedematous","Flow rate decreased","Purulent discharge","Painful red streaks","Pyrexia"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists insertion site is pale, cool and oedematous (hallmark signs) and flow rate decreased as signs/symptoms of infiltration.  Why C wrong: Purulent discharge is a sign of infection.  Why D wrong: Painful red streaks relate to phlebitis.  Why E wrong: Pyrexia relates to infection/advanced phlebitis."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"Which of the following are preventive measures for infiltration? (SATA)","options":["Avoid venipuncture over an area of flexion or joints","Apply a splint near the IV site if there is a decrease or a cessation of flow","If the client is restless or active, use an arm-board or splint as needed","Lower the IV fluid container below the IV site, and monitor for the appearance of blood in the IV tubing; if blood appears, the IV device is most likely in the vein","Insert the cannula into an area of flexion"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists avoiding venipuncture over flexion/joints, applying a splint if flow decreases/stops, using arm-board/splint for restless clients, and lowering the IV container to check for blood return as preventive measures.  Why E wrong: Inserting into an area of flexion contradicts the prevention measure to avoid areas of flexion."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"What is the FIRST priority nursing intervention for infiltration?","options":["Remove the IV cannula maintaining sterile technique","Stop infusion immediately","Apply warm compress","Elevate the extremity"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states stop infusion immediately is the first priority nursing intervention for infiltration.  Why A wrong: Removing the cannula is the next step after stopping infusion.  Why C/D wrong: Not the stated first priority."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"After stopping the infusion in infiltration, what is the next nursing intervention?","options":["Reinsert in the same vein","Remove IV cannula with maintaining sterile technique","Apply a tourniquet","Increase the flow rate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states then remove IV cannula with maintaining sterile technique.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the stated step."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"What is phlebitis and thrombophlebitis?","options":["Fluid leaking into tissue","Inflammation of the vein","Microorganism presence only","Air in the line"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines phlebitis/thrombophlebitis as inflammation of the vein.  Why A wrong: That is infiltration.  Why C wrong: That is infection.  Why D wrong: That is air embolism."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"Phlebitis is the second most common complication of IV cannulation/therapy. What is its most common cause?","options":["Air entry","Failure to maintain sterile technique during cannulation","Fluid leakage","Catheter displacement"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the most common cause is failure to maintain sterile technique during cannulation.  Why A/C/D wrong: These relate to other complications, not the stated phlebitis cause."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"Which of the following can cause phlebitis? (SATA)","options":["Hyperosmolar drugs","Hardness of the vein that may remain for too much longer","Failure to maintain sterile technique","Pale, cool, oedematous site","Air entry"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists hyperosmolar drugs, hardness of the vein remaining longer, and failure to maintain sterile technique as causes of phlebitis.  Why D wrong: Pale, cool, oedematous site is a sign of infiltration.  Why E wrong: Air entry relates to air embolism."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"What does the VIP scale assess, and what does the abbreviation stand for?","options":["Vital Infusion Pressure; assessment of phlebitis","Visual Infusion Phlebitis scale; a number system used for assessment of phlebitis","Venous Infusion Pump; assessment of flow","Vascular Infection Profile; assessment of infection"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the VIP scale (visual infusion phlebitis) is a number system used for assessment of phlebitis.  Why A/C/D wrong: Incorrect expansions/purposes."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"Which of the following are signs and symptoms of phlebitis? (SATA)","options":["Insertion site is red, painful, tenderness and warm","Flow rate decreased","Pale, cool, oedematous site","Purulent discharge with no warmth","Decreased WBC"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists insertion site is red, painful, tenderness and warm (Hallmark) and flow rate decreased as signs of phlebitis.  Why C wrong: Pale, cool, oedematous is infiltration.  Why D wrong: Purulent discharge is infection.  Why E wrong: Not a stated phlebitis sign."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"Which of the following are preventive measures for phlebitis? (SATA)","options":["Use an IV cannula smaller than the vein, and avoid using very small veins when administering irritating solutions","Avoid using the lower extremities (legs and feet) as access area for the IV","Avoid venipuncture over an area of flexion","Anchor the cannula and a loop of tubing securely with tape","Use the largest possible cannula in the smallest vein"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists using a cannula smaller than the vein, avoiding very small veins for irritating solutions, avoiding lower extremities, avoiding areas of flexion, and anchoring cannula and tubing with tape.  Why E wrong: Using the largest cannula in the smallest vein contradicts the prevention measures."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"How often should the venipuncture site be changed as a preventive measure for phlebitis?","options":["Every 24 hours","Every 72-96 hours or as per recommendations","Every 7 days","Every 12 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states change the venipuncture site every 72-96 hours or as per recommendations.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the stated interval."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"What is the FIRST priority nursing intervention for phlebitis?","options":["Elevate the extremity and warm compresses can also be applied","Stop infusion immediately","Insert a new cannula in the opposite extremity","Maintain sterile technique"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states stop infusion immediately is the first priority nursing intervention for phlebitis.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are subsequent actions, not the first priority."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"Which of the following are nursing interventions for phlebitis? (SATA)","options":["Stop infusion immediately (first priority)","Then remove IV cannula with maintaining sterile technique","Elevate the extremity and warm compresses can also be applied for comfort","Insert a new cannula in the opposite extremity","Increase the infusion rate in the same vein"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists stopping infusion immediately, removing the cannula with sterile technique, elevating the extremity with warm compresses for comfort, and inserting a new cannula in the opposite extremity.  Why E wrong: Increasing the rate in the same inflamed vein contradicts the interventions."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"What is circulatory overload as a complication of IV therapy?","options":["Air entry into the line","Excessive fluids infused due to improper management of flow rate, leading to insufficient output","Vein inflammation","Fluid leaking into tissue"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines circulatory overload as excessive fluids infused due to improper management of flow rate and insufficient output.  Why A wrong: That is air embolism.  Why C wrong: That is phlebitis.  Why D wrong: That is infiltration."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"Which of the following are signs and symptoms of circulatory overload? (SATA)","options":["Dyspnoea","Crackles","Distended neck veins","Increased blood pressure","Decreased blood pressure"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists dyspnoea, crackles, distended neck veins, and increased blood pressure as signs/symptoms of circulatory overload.  Why E wrong: Circulatory overload increases (not decreases) BP."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"What is the FIRST priority nursing care for circulatory overload?","options":["Increase the IV rate","Decrease flow rate (first priority), then inform to HCP","Stop oxygen","Lay the patient flat"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states nursing care: decrease flow rate (first priority), then inform to HCP.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the stated care priority."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"Embolism, as another complication, may involve which of the following? (SATA)","options":["Embolism","Clot","Haematoma or haemorrhage","Needle stick injuries","Fever"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists embolism, clot, haematoma or haemorrhage, and needle stick injuries under other complications/embolism.  Why E wrong: Fever is listed under infection, not this embolism grouping."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"(RECALL THE NCLEX, p.273) A patient admitted in hospital with abdominal distension, normal vital sign, lower leg oedema or swelling but during supine position jugular vein distended and visible on inspiration. Which sign indicates extracellular fluid overload? (Right answer: A)","options":["Extended jugular vein","Normal vital sign","Oedema","Peripheral oedema"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source's given answer is A — extended jugular vein indicates extracellular fluid overload.  Why B wrong: Normal vital sign does not indicate overload.  Why C/D wrong: Oedema/peripheral oedema are not the indicated answer per the source."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"Which of the following are clinical features of fluid overload or hypervolemia? (SATA)","options":["Oedema (particularly feet and ankles, peripheral oedema)","Difficulty in breathing (orthopnea) while lying down","Crackles on auscultation","High blood pressure (hypertension)","Hypotension"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists oedema (feet/ankles, peripheral), difficulty breathing/orthopnea, crackles on auscultation, and high blood pressure (hypertension) among clinical features of fluid overload/hypervolemia.  Why E wrong: Hypovolemia, not hypervolemia, causes hypotension."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"Which of the following are additional clinical features of fluid overload/hypervolemia listed by the source? (SATA)","options":["Irritated cough","Jugular vein distension","Shortness of breath (dyspnea)","Strong and rapid pulse","Sunken eyes"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists irritated cough, jugular vein distension, shortness of breath (dyspnea), and strong and rapid pulse as clinical features.  Why E wrong: Sunken eyes is a feature of dehydration/hypovolemia, not overload."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"(RECALL THE NCLEX, p.273) A patient is getting IV fluid through peripheral cannula, shows redness, cool, oedema and decreased flow rate. The possible diagnosis is? (Right Answer: A)","options":["Infiltration","Phlebitis","Allergic reaction","Hypersensitivity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source's given answer is A — Infiltration; redness/cool/oedema with decreased flow rate indicates infiltration.  Why B wrong: Phlebitis presents with warm, red, painful site.  Why C/D wrong: Allergic reaction/hypersensitivity present differently."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"According to the Visual Infusion Phlebitis (VIP) score, what does Stage 0 indicate?","options":["Possible first sign of phlebitis","IV site appears healthy — no signs of phlebitis, observe cannula","Early stage of phlebitis","Advanced stage of thrombophlebitis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source VIP score states Stage 0 = IV site appears healthy, no signs of phlebitis, observe cannula.  Why A wrong: That is Stage 1.  Why C wrong: That is Stage 2.  Why D wrong: That is Stage 4/5."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"In the VIP score, Stage 1 corresponds to which clinical sign and assessment/intervention?","options":["Two of the following: pain, erythema, swelling","One of the following is evident (slight pain at IV site OR redness near site) — possible first sign of phlebitis, observe cannula","IV site appears healthy","Pain along the path of the cannula with erythema and induration"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source VIP score states Stage 1 = one of the following evident (slight pain at IV site or redness near site), possible first sign of phlebitis, observe cannula.  Why A wrong: Two signs = Stage 2.  Why C wrong: Healthy = Stage 0.  Why D wrong: That is Stage 3."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"In the VIP score, which stage corresponds to two of the following being evident — pain, erythema, swelling — with the recommendation to early stage of phlebitis, resite the cannula?","options":["Stage 1","Stage 2","Stage 3","Stage 4"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source VIP score states Stage 2 = two of the following evident (pain, erythema, swelling), early stage of phlebitis, resite the cannula.  Why A wrong: Stage 1 has one sign.  Why C/D wrong: Higher stages have additional signs."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"In the VIP score, Stage 3 is characterized by which findings?","options":["IV site healthy","All of the following signs are evident: pain along the path of the cannula, erythema, induration — medium stage of phlebitis, resite the cannula, consider treatment","One sign evident","Palpable venous cord and pyrexia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source VIP score states Stage 3 = all of the following evident (pain along the path of the cannula, erythema, induration), medium stage of phlebitis, resite the cannula, consider treatment.  Why A wrong: Healthy = Stage 0.  Why C wrong: One sign = Stage 1.  Why D wrong: Pyrexia/palpable venous cord = Stage 5."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"In the VIP score, Stage 4 is characterized by which findings and intervention?","options":["IV site healthy, observe cannula","All of the following are evident: pain along the path of the cannula, erythema, induration, palpable venous cord — advanced stage of phlebitis or start of thrombophlebitis, resite the cannula, consider treatment","Two signs evident, resite the cannula","Pyrexia, initiate treatment"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source VIP score states Stage 4 = all of the following evident (pain along path of cannula, erythema, induration, palpable venous cord), advanced stage of phlebitis or start of thrombophlebitis, resite cannula, consider treatment.  Why A wrong: Healthy = Stage 0.  Why C wrong: Two signs = Stage 2.  Why D wrong: Pyrexia = Stage 5."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"In the VIP score, Stage 5 is characterized by which findings and intervention?","options":["IV site healthy, observe cannula","All of the following are evident and extreme: pain along the path of the cannula, erythema, induration, palpable venous cord, pyrexia — advanced stage of thrombophlebitis, initiate treatment, resite the cannula","One sign evident, observe cannula","Medium stage, consider treatment"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source VIP score states Stage 5 = all of the following evident and extreme (pain along path of cannula, erythema, induration, palpable venous cord, pyrexia), advanced stage of thrombophlebitis, initiate treatment, resite the cannula.  Why A wrong: Healthy = Stage 0.  Why C wrong: One sign = Stage 1.  Why D wrong: Medium stage = Stage 3."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"(Points to remember, p.273) Flushing of cannula should be done with normal saline or 5% normal saline. Why?","options":["To increase fluid overload","Because before flushing the saline confirms the patency of the cannula, and after administering any kind of medication and after taking blood sampling in all type of catheters to maintain patency, especially central catheters (Venous and Arterial)","To dilute concentrated drugs","To prevent dehydration"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states saline flush confirms patency of the cannula and is used after medication and blood sampling in all types of catheters to maintain patency, especially central catheters.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the stated rationale."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"(Points to remember) Most commonly used IV cannula for blood transfusion is which gauge, and what is its colour?","options":["16G (grey)","18G (green color)","20G (pink)","24G (yellow)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the most commonly used IV cannula for blood transfusion is 18G (green color).  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the stated cannula for blood transfusion."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"(Points to remember) For paediatric patients, the most common cannula used for fat emulsion is which size and colour?","options":["16G (grey)","18G (green)","24G (yellow color)","20G (pink)"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states for paediatric, the most commonly used cannula is 24G (yellow color).  Why A/B/D wrong: Not the stated paediatric cannula."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"(Points to remember) Before casting the patient to OT room for any type of surgery, prefer which cannula?","options":["24G or 26G cannula","16G or 18G cannula for that patient because there will chance of haemodynamic complications like BP or hypotension due to blood loss","22G cannula","26G only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states before casting the patient to OT for surgery, prefer 16G or 18G cannula because there will be chance of haemodynamic complications like BP or hypotension due to blood loss.  Why A/C/D wrong: Smaller gauges are not preferred for surgery."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"(Points to remember) When is a Y-set used in IV/blood administration?","options":["For routine IV fluids only","It is used for additional IV site without cannulation, in case of direct to administer another IV fluid or medications","For paediatric patients only","Only for TPN"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states Y-set or extension is used for additional IV site without cannulation, in case of direct to administer another IV fluid or medications.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the stated use of a Y-set."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"(Points to remember) Before and after medication administration, if the IV line should flush with which fluid?","options":["Dextrose 5%","NS solution to maintain patency of IV fluid administration","Ringer lactate","Distilled water"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states before and after medication administration, the IV line should flush with NS solution to maintain patency of IV fluid administration.  Why A/C/D wrong: NS is the stated flush solution."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"(Points to remember) When tubing is required to drop and bio micro drops, what should be replaced?","options":["Replace the cannula","Replace the tubing used to administer blood/blood products, within 4 hours or after each unit","Replace the fluid bottle","Replace the filter only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states replace the tubing used to administer blood/blood products, within 4 hours or after each unit.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the stated replacement instruction."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"(Points to remember) Lactate ringer's (RL) containing potassium should NOT be given to which clients?","options":["Clients with acute or chronic kidney disease","Paediatric clients","Pregnant clients","Diabetic clients"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states Lactate ringer's (RL) containing potassium should not be given to clients with acute or chronic kidney disease.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the stated contraindicated group."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"(Points to remember) Which IV fluid is choice of cannula for paracentesis / amniocentesis?","options":["Normal weight of IV cannula 18 inches (45 cm)","Choice of cannula for paracentesis / amniocentesis is 16G","24G cannula","26G cannula"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the choice of cannula for paracentesis/amniocentesis is 16G.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the stated choice."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"(Points to remember) Roller clamp has how many functions, and what does it do?","options":["Just one or no flow regulation","Has one or two functions of flow regulator — it is used to manually regulate the infusion flow rate","Three functions","It is used to puncture the vein"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the roller clamp has one or two functions of flow regulator and is used to manually regulate the infusion flow rate.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the stated function."},{"section":"Batch 023 · Arterial & venous cannulation, central venous catheters, TPN…","question":"(Points to remember) Lactated Ringer's solution contains potassium and should not be administered to clients with which condition because kidney injury or chronic kidney disease causes a problem in conversion of lactate to bicarbonate in the liver?","options":["Acute kidney injury or chronic kidney disease (and liver disease since the liver plays a role in converting lactate to bicarbonate)","Diabetes mellitus","Hypertension","Asthma"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states Lactated Ringer's solution contains potassium and should not be administered to clients with acute kidney injury or chronic kidney disease, and clients with liver disease since the liver plays a role in converting lactate to bicarbonate.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the stated contraindication."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"Phlebotomy is best defined as which of the following?","options":["The administration of intravenous medications through a peripheral line","The act of removing blood from a patient using a needle","The collection of blood from a central venous catheter only","The transfusion of blood products into a patient"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Phlebotomy, also known as venipuncture, describes the act of removing blood from a patient using a needle.  Why A wrong: That describes IV therapy/medication administration, not phlebotomy.  Why C wrong: Phlebotomy is not restricted to central venous catheters.  Why D wrong: Transfusion is the opposite — giving blood, not removing it."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"Which term is used synonymously with phlebotomy?","options":["Cannulation","Venipuncture","Catheterization","Aspiration"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Phlebotomy is also known as venipuncture.  Why A wrong: Cannulation refers to inserting a cannula, a separate procedure.  Why C wrong: Catheterization refers to catheter insertion.  Why D wrong: Aspiration is withdrawal of fluid/air, not the specific term for phlebotomy."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"Phlebotomy can be done for which of the following purposes? (SATA)","options":["Laboratory testing as a diagnostic tool","Blood collection for donation","Cannulation for IV therapy","Administration of total parenteral nutrition","Replacement of clotting factors"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists three purposes of phlebotomy — laboratory testing as a diagnostic tool, blood collection for donation, and cannulation for IV therapy.  Why D wrong: TPN administration is a use of IV therapy, not a stated purpose of phlebotomy.  Why E wrong: Replacement of clotting factors is not listed as a phlebotomy purpose."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"During Step 1 (Assemble equipment) of the venipuncture procedure, why should the nurse collect all equipment needed before placing it within safe and easy reach?","options":["To document the procedure faster","To ensure that all the items are clearly visible and that the procedure runs smoothly and efficiently","To allow the patient to inspect the equipment","To reduce the need for hand hygiene"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Collecting all equipment and placing it within safe and easy reach on a tray or trolley ensures all items are clearly visible and the procedure runs smoothly and efficiently.  Why A wrong: Documentation speed is not the stated rationale.  Why C wrong: Patient inspection of equipment is not the purpose.  Why D wrong: Equipment placement does not eliminate hand hygiene requirements."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"Which of the following are required supplies for venipuncture as listed in Step 1? (SATA)","options":["Laboratory sample tubes (vacutainers) and specimen labels","Vacuum-extraction device","Well-fitting, non-sterile gloves","A tourniquet","Alcohol hand rub"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The required supplies listed include laboratory sample tubes (vacutainers) and specimen labels, a vacuum-extraction device, well-fitting non-sterile gloves, a tourniquet, and alcohol hand rub.  Why A/B/C/D/E wrong: All are correct; each is explicitly named in the equipment list."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"According to Step 1, what should be applied over each puncture site after venipuncture?","options":["A pressure dressing","Gauze or cotton-wool ball","A transparent occlusive film","An elastic bandage"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Gauze or cotton-wool ball is to be applied over each puncture site.  Why A wrong: A formal pressure dressing is not the item specified in the list.  Why C wrong: Transparent occlusive film is used for IV cannula sites, not specified here.  Why D wrong: An elastic bandage is not the item listed."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"In Step 2 (Identify and prepare the patient), how should the nurse introduce themselves to the patient?","options":["By full name only","By job title only","By name and job title","By employee identification number"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The nurse should introduce themselves to the patient, giving their name and job title.  Why A wrong: Name alone is incomplete per the source.  Why B wrong: Job title alone is incomplete.  Why D wrong: Employee ID number is not specified."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"Before obtaining verbal consent, the nurse must ensure the patient has done which of the following?","options":["Signed a written legal waiver","Understood the procedure","Fasted for 12 hours","Removed all jewellery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The nurse must ensure that the patient has understood the procedure before obtaining verbal consent.  Why A wrong: A signed legal waiver is not what the source requires for this step.  Why C wrong: Fasting is unrelated to consent for venipuncture per source.  Why D wrong: Removing jewellery is not the consent requirement."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"When confirming patient identity during Step 2, the nurse should match the laboratory form against which of the following?","options":["The patient's room number","The patient's identity","The patient's diet order","The attending physician's name"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The nurse should check that the laboratory form matches the patient's identity.  Why A wrong: Room number is not the verifier of identity in this step.  Why C wrong: Diet order is irrelevant to specimen identification.  Why D wrong: Physician's name is not the matching criterion described."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"In Step 3 (Selection of a peripheral IV site), what is the recommended order of vein examination?","options":["Examine the patient's arm first, then forearm","Extend the patient's arm and examine the antecubital fossa or forearm","Examine the wrist and hand veins before the forearm","Examine the leg veins first"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The nurse should extend the patient's arm and examine the antecubital fossa or forearm.  Why A wrong: The source specifies extending the arm to examine the antecubital fossa/forearm, not a generic arm-first sequence.  Why C wrong: Wrist/hand-first is not the described approach.  Why D wrong: Leg veins are not the first-line site in this procedure."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"When locating a vein for venipuncture, a good vein should have which characteristics? (SATA)","options":["Visible","Straight","Clear","Pulsatile","Hard and cord-like"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: A good vein should be visible, straight, and clear.  Why D wrong: Pulsatile vessels are arteries, which must be avoided.  Why E wrong: Hard, cord-like veins are unsuitable and should be avoided."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"Why should veins that are hard, cord-like, or in a forearm or arm with a colostoma (fistula) be avoided?","options":["They are too superficial","They are suitable sites","They are not suitable sites for venipuncture","They cause excessive bleeding"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Veins that are hard, cord-like, or in a forearm/arm with a fistula are not suitable sites.  Why A wrong: Superficiality is not the stated reason.  Why B wrong: They are explicitly unsuitable.  Why D wrong: Excessive bleeding is not the cited reason for avoidance."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"When choosing between visible veins, which should be selected?","options":["The visible vein with the smallest diameter","The visible vein with a relatively large diameter so the increased flow chance reduces the chance of a haematoma","Any vein closest to the wrist joint","A vein over a flexion point"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Choose a vein with a relatively large diameter, since increased flow reduces the chance of a haematoma.  Why A wrong: Smallest diameter increases risk of complications.  Why C wrong: Proximity to a joint is not the selection criterion and joints/flexion points should be avoided.  Why D wrong: Flexion points should be avoided, not selected."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"Veins in the lower extremities should be avoided in venipuncture primarily because they carry an increased risk of which complication?","options":["Air embolism","Thrombosis formation","Infiltration","Phlebitis only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Veins in the lower extremities should be avoided because they have a possible risk of thrombosis formation.  Why A wrong: Air embolism is not the cited risk for lower-extremity veins here.  Why C wrong: Infiltration is not the stated reason.  Why D wrong: The source specifically cites thrombosis, not phlebitis alone."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"Veins in the scalp and feet may be suitable sites for which population?","options":["Elderly patients","Infants","Dialysis patients","Patients with a mastectomy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Veins in the scalp and feet may be suitable sites for infants.  Why A wrong: Elderly patients are not the population specified.  Why C wrong: Dialysis patients have specific site avoidance (AV fistula arm).  Why D wrong: Mastectomy patients require avoidance of the affected side."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"When selecting between the dominant and non-dominant arm for venipuncture, the nurse should select which arm?","options":["The patient's dominant arm","An opposite site for a venipuncture in the patient's non-dominant arm","Either arm at random","The arm with an IV infusion running"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Determine the client's dominant site and select an opposite site for venipuncture (use the non-dominant arm).  Why A wrong: The dominant arm is what should be avoided when possible.  Why C wrong: Random selection is not recommended.  Why D wrong: An arm with an active IV infusion should be avoided."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"Before selecting a site, how long should a tourniquet be applied above the chosen vein?","options":["About 1–2 fingers' width","About 3–5 fingers' width above the venipuncture site","Directly over the venipuncture site","Below the venipuncture site"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Apply the tourniquet about 3–5 fingers' width above the venipuncture site.  Why A wrong: 1–2 fingers' width is not the specified distance.  Why C wrong: A tourniquet over the puncture site obstructs access.  Why D wrong: It is applied above, not below, the site."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"During venipuncture, the nurse should avoid checking the blood pressure or proceeding up the extremity if the vein is ruptured or infiltration occurs. What does this prevent?","options":["Patient discomfort only","Further injury and complications","Tourniquet pain","Specimen hemolysis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Avoiding proceeding up the extremity after a ruptured vein or infiltration prevents further complications/injury to the puncture site.  Why A wrong: It is broader than just discomfort.  Why C wrong: Tourniquet pain is not the stated concern.  Why D wrong: Specimen hemolysis is a separate concern not the focus here."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"If infiltration occurs from the antecubital vein, the lower veins in the same arm should usually NOT be used for further venipuncture sites for what reason?","options":["They are too small","Because there is an air leak with an IV may already obstruct that flow of solution, causing infiltration that could lead to thrombophlebitis","They are reserved for blood transfusion","They are painful to access"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Using lower veins in the same arm after antecubital infiltration risks obstructed solution flow and infiltration that could lead to thrombophlebitis.  Why A wrong: Vein size is not the stated reason.  Why C wrong: Reservation for transfusion is not mentioned.  Why D wrong: Pain is not the cited reason."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"When checking blood pressure during the procedure, the nurse should avoid placing pressure on which site?","options":["The opposite arm","The site that is receiving the IV infusion if possible","The lower extremity","The fistula site"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Avoid checking the blood pressure on the arm receiving the IV infusion if possible.  Why A wrong: The opposite arm is the preferred alternative, not avoided.  Why C wrong: While lower extremities are avoided for venipuncture, the BP precaution here concerns the IV infusion arm.  Why D wrong: Fistula avoidance is a separate rule; the BP instruction targets the IV infusion arm."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"When taking a blood sample from a hospitalized patient, the nurse should NOT take blood from an existing peripheral venous access site for what reason?","options":["It is too painful","Because this may give false results","Because it is slower","Because it requires consent"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Do not take blood from an existing peripheral venous access site because this may give false results.  Why A wrong: Pain is not the cited reason.  Why C wrong: Speed is not the reason.  Why D wrong: Consent is unrelated to this specific precaution."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"It is acceptable, but not ideal, to draw blood specimens when first introducing an in-dwelling venous device, before connecting the cannula to which fluids?","options":["Lipid emulsions","The intravenous fluids","Blood products","Antibiotics"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: It is acceptable but not ideal to draw specimens when first introducing an in-dwelling venous device before connecting the cannula to the intravenous fluids.  Why A wrong: Lipid emulsions are not what the source specifies.  Why C wrong: Blood products are not the referenced connection here.  Why D wrong: Antibiotics are not specified."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"In Step 4 (Perform hand hygiene and put on gloves), how should the nurse wash hands?","options":["With cold water only","With soap and water, or dry with a single-use towel","With antiseptic mouthwash","Without drying"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Perform hand hygiene; wash hands with soap and water and dry with a single-use towel.  Why A wrong: Temperature specification is not \"cold water only.\"  Why C wrong: Mouthwash is not a hand hygiene agent.  Why D wrong: Hands should be dried with a single-use towel, not left wet."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"If hands are not visibly contaminated, the nurse may clean them with which agent?","options":["Alcohol rub","Plain water","Povidone iodine soak","Hydrogen peroxide"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: If hands are not visibly contaminated, clean with alcohol rub, then put on well-fitting, non-sterile gloves.  Why B wrong: Plain water alone is not the specified alternative.  Why C wrong: Povidone iodine is a skin antiseptic, not a hand rub here.  Why D wrong: Hydrogen peroxide is not specified for hand hygiene."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"In Step 5 (Disinfect the skin site), how should the nurse clean the skin?","options":["In a circular spiral toward the center","With a back-and-forth (in and out) motion","With a single firm wipe and outward and inward spiral motion (start from the centre of the venipuncture site and work downward and outward to cover an area of 2 cm or more)","With repeated overlapping vertical strokes"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Start from the centre of the venipuncture site and work downward and outward to cover an area of 2 cm or more.  Why A wrong: Cleaning inward toward center recontaminates the site.  Why B wrong: Back-and-forth motion is not the prescribed technique.  Why D wrong: Repeated vertical strokes are not described."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"After cleaning, the antiseptic site should be allowed to do which of the following before puncture?","options":["Be wiped dry immediately with gauze","Allow the area to dry to allow enough contact time to increase the risk of contamination","Allow time to dry to allow enough contact time and reduce the risk of contamination","Be re-cleaned twice"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Allow the site time to dry to allow enough contact time, which reduces the risk of contamination.  Why A wrong: Wiping dry removes the antiseptic before adequate contact time.  Why B wrong: The goal is to reduce, not increase, contamination risk.  Why D wrong: Re-cleaning twice is not the instruction."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"If alcohol or povidone iodine is used for skin preparation, when can it be cleaned by drying with a clean swab?","options":["Before it touches the skin","In particular, when using chlorhexidine and alcohol — DO NOT touch the cleaned site","Immediately, regardless of antiseptic","Only after the puncture"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In particular, DO NOT touch the cleaned site (especially with chlorhexidine and alcohol preparation); the cleaned skin must not be re-contaminated.  Why A wrong: The antiseptic must contact skin and dry.  Why C wrong: Touching/cleaning immediately can recontaminate the site.  Why D wrong: The key rule is not touching the cleaned site, regardless of timing."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"In Step 6 (Take blood), how should the venipuncture be performed?","options":["With the bevel down","Anchor the vein by holding the patient's arm and placing a thumb BELOW the venipuncture site","Anchor the vein by pulling the skin above the site","Without anchoring the vein"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Anchor the vein by holding the patient's arm and placing a thumb below the venipuncture site.  Why A wrong: Bevel orientation is not the anchoring instruction described.  Why C wrong: The thumb is placed below, not above, the site.  Why D wrong: Anchoring the vein is required to stabilize it."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"When inserting the needle in Step 6, why should the nurse ask the patient to form a fist?","options":["To dilate the artery","So the veins are more prominent","To reduce pain perception","To prevent fainting"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Ask the patient to form a fist so the veins are more prominent.  Why A wrong: The maneuver targets veins, not arteries.  Why C wrong: Pain reduction is not the rationale.  Why D wrong: Fainting prevention is unrelated to fist formation."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"At what angle should the needle be entered into the vein during Step 6?","options":["At a 90-degree angle","Enter the vein swiftly at a 30-degree angle or less","At a 45–60 degree angle","Parallel to the skin (0 degrees)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Enter the vein swiftly at a 30-degree angle or less, and continue introducing the needle along the vein at the easiest angle of entry.  Why A wrong: 90 degrees would go through the vein.  Why C wrong: 45–60 degrees exceeds the stated maximum.  Why D wrong: A completely parallel approach is not described."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"Once sufficient blood has been collected, the nurse should do which of the following before withdrawing the needle?","options":["Apply firm pressure immediately","Release the tourniquet","Massage the site","Reinsert the needle"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Once sufficient blood has been collected, release the tourniquet before withdrawing the needle.  Why A wrong: Pressure is applied after withdrawing the needle.  Why C wrong: Massaging the site causes bruising/haematoma.  Why D wrong: Reinserting the needle is incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"After withdrawing the needle, how should the nurse manage the puncture site?","options":["Apply pressure to the gauze and apply gentle pressure on the puncture site with a clean gauze or dry cotton-wool ball, then bandage","Leave the site uncovered to air dry","Apply ice for 20 minutes","Massage vigorously to stop bleeding"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Withdraw the needle gently and apply gentle pressure on the puncture site with a clean gauze/dry cotton-wool ball, then ask the patient to hold the gauze or cotton-wool in place with the arm extended and raised; then bandage.  Why B wrong: The site should have pressure applied, not be left uncovered.  Why C wrong: Ice application is not the described intervention.  Why D wrong: Vigorous massage causes a haematoma."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"In Step 7 (Prepare samples for transportation), why should laboratory samples be packed safely in a plastic leak-proof bag with an outside compartment for the laboratory request form?","options":["To speed up testing","Placing the requisition on the outside helps avoid contamination","To identify the patient","To allow refrigeration"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pack samples in a leak-proof bag with an outside compartment for the request form; placing the requisition on the outside helps avoid contamination.  Why A wrong: Speed is not the cited purpose.  Why C wrong: Identification is handled by labels, not the bag design rationale.  Why D wrong: Refrigeration is not the reason described."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"If there are multiple tubes, how should they be placed in the rack or pulled into a sealed bag?","options":["With the labels facing inward","They should be placed in a rack or pulled inside a sealed bag avoiding leakage during transportation","Loosely without securing them","Mixed with sharps"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Multiple tubes should be placed in a rack or pulled inside a sealed bag, avoiding leakage during transportation.  Why A wrong: Label orientation is not the specified concern.  Why C wrong: Loose, unsecured placement risks leakage.  Why D wrong: Mixing with sharps is unsafe and not described."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"Which of the following peripheral IV sites should be avoided? (SATA)","options":["An arm that is weak, traumatised, or paralysed","The arm on the same side as a mastectomy","Edematous extremity","The non-dominant forearm","An arm with an arteriovenous fistula or shunt for dialysis"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: Sites to avoid include an arm that is weak/traumatised/paralysed, the arm on the same side as a mastectomy, an edematous extremity, and an arm with an arteriovenous fistula or shunt for dialysis.  Why D wrong: The non-dominant forearm is actually a preferred site, not one to avoid."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"A skin area that is infected or has skin lesions (e.g., eczema) should be:","options":["Used as the first choice","Avoided for venipuncture","Cleaned twice and used","Used only for blood donation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A skin area that is infected or has skin lesions (e.g., eczema) should be avoided.  Why A wrong: Infected/lesioned skin is contraindicated, not first choice.  Why C wrong: Double cleaning does not make infected skin a suitable site.  Why D wrong: It is not reserved for donation; it is avoided."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"What is the primary use of IV fluids as described in the overview?","options":["To provide enteral nutrition","To sustain clients who are unable to take adequate substances orally","To replace blood loss only","To deliver oxygen to tissues"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: IV fluids are used to sustain clients who are unable to take adequate substances orally.  Why A wrong: IV fluids provide parenteral, not enteral, support.  Why C wrong: Blood replacement is one of several uses, not the sole purpose.  Why D wrong: IV fluids are not a primary oxygen delivery route."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"According to the overview, IV fluids replace water, electrolytes, and nutrients more rapidly than what?","options":["Subcutaneous administration","Oral administration","Intramuscular administration","Rectal administration"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: IV fluids replace water, electrolytes, and nutrients more rapidly than oral administration.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifically contrasts IV with oral administration."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"Which overview feature describes how IV fluids deliver medications?","options":["Slow, delayed release into muscle","Provide immediate access to the vascular system for rapid delivery of specific solutions, with no lag time for gastrointestinal absorption","Require digestion before absorption","Provide nutrients only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: IV fluids provide immediate access to the vascular system for the rapid delivery of specific solutions with no lag time for gastrointestinal absorption.  Why A wrong: That describes IM administration, not IV.  Why C wrong: IV bypasses digestion.  Why D wrong: IV fluids deliver medications, not only nutrients."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"IV fluids provide a vascular route for which of the following?","options":["The administration of medication or blood components","Tube feeding","Inhalation therapy","Topical drug delivery"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: IV fluids provide a vascular route for the administration of medication or blood components.  Why B wrong: Tube feeding is enteral.  Why C wrong: Inhalation is a respiratory route.  Why D wrong: Topical delivery is not a vascular route."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"Which of the following are listed indications for IV fluids? (SATA)","options":["Resuscitation — a volume expander is an intravenous fluid that functions to provide volume for the circulatory system","Maintenance or replacement of total body water and electrolytes","A carrier for medication","A carrier for parenteral nutrition","Topical wound irrigation"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The indications listed are resuscitation (volume expander), maintenance/replacement of total body water and electrolytes, a carrier for medication, and a carrier for parenteral nutrition.  Why E wrong: Topical wound irrigation is not listed as an IV fluid indication."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"IV fluids are broadly classified into which two types?","options":["Hypertonic and hypotonic","Crystalloids and colloids","Isotonic and isosmotic","Maintenance and replacement"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The types of IV fluids are crystalloids and colloids.  Why A wrong: Hypertonic/hypotonic are tonicity categories, not the two broad types.  Why C wrong: These are osmolarity descriptors, not the two main types.  Why D wrong: These describe purposes, not the two fluid types."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"A crystalloid is best defined as an intravenous solution that contains which components?","options":["Large protein molecules","Water, electrolytes, and/or non-electrolyte solutes capable of entering all body fluid compartments","Red blood cells and plasma","Lipids and amino acids only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A crystalloid is an intravenous solution that contains water, electrolytes, and/or non-electrolyte solutes capable of entering all body fluid compartments.  Why A wrong: Large protein molecules characterize colloids.  Why C wrong: RBCs/plasma are blood components, not crystalloids.  Why D wrong: Lipids/amino acids describe parenteral nutrition components, not crystalloid definition."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"The most common way to categorize crystalloid IV fluids is based on their:","options":["Color","Tonicity (isotonic, hypotonic, or hypertonic) with regard to plasma","pH only","Volume of the bag"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common way to categorize crystalloid IV fluids is based on their tonicity — isotonic, hypotonic, or hypertonic — with regard to plasma.  Why A wrong: Color is not a categorization basis.  Why C wrong: pH alone is not the categorization method.  Why D wrong: Bag volume does not define tonicity."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"Which of the following are characteristics of isotonic solutions? (SATA)","options":["Same osmolality as body fluids","Increase extracellular fluid volume","Fluids do not enter the cells because no osmotic force exists to shift the fluids","They draw fluid out of the cells","Example: RL, 0.9% NS"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: Isotonic solutions have the same osmolality as body fluids, increase extracellular fluid volume, and do not enter the cells because no osmotic force exists to shift fluids; examples include RL and 0.9% NS.  Why D wrong: Drawing fluid out of cells describes hypertonic solutions, not isotonic."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"Isotonic electrolyte solutions are considered isotonic if the total electrolyte content is approximately what value?","options":["154 mEq/L","310 mEq/L","273 mEq/L","77 mEq/L"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Electrolyte solutions are considered isotonic if the total electrolyte content is approximately 310 mEq/L.  Why A wrong: 154 mEq/L is the sodium/chloride content figure for 0.9% NaCl, not the isotonic threshold.  Why C wrong: 273 is the osmolarity (mOsm/L) of Ringer's lactate.  Why D wrong: 77 mEq/L relates to half-strength saline (0.45% NaCl)."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"Normal Saline (0.9% NaCl), also called NSS, is a crystalloid that is slightly acidic at pH 5.5 and isotonic. What does NS contain?","options":["Water, sodium (154 mEq/L), and chloride (154 mEq/L)","Water, dextrose, and potassium","Water and calcium only","Water, lactate, and sodium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Normal saline (0.9% NaCl/NSS) contains water, sodium (154 mEq/L), and chloride (154 mEq/L).  Why B wrong: It does not contain dextrose or potassium.  Why C wrong: It contains sodium and chloride, not calcium.  Why D wrong: Lactate is a component of Ringer's lactate, not NS."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"Normal saline (0.9% NaCl) has no calories. Which of the following statements about NS are correct? (SATA)","options":["It is a crystalloid of 308 mOsm/L","It has no calories","It is administered to correct extracellular fluid volume deficit because it remains within the intracellular fluid (ICF)","Normal saline is the only IV fluid that should be used alongside the administration of blood products","It should be used for heart failure, pulmonary oedema, renal impairment, or conditions that cause sodium retention"],"correct":"A,B,D","rationale":"Why correct: NS is a crystalloid of 308 mOsm/L, has no calories, and is the only IV fluid that should be used alongside the administration of blood products.  Why C wrong: NS corrects extracellular fluid volume deficit because it remains within the extracellular fluid (ECF), not the ICF.  Why E wrong: NS should NOT be used for heart failure, pulmonary oedema, renal impairment, or conditions that cause sodium retention, as it may cause fluid overload."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"Dextrose 5% in Water (D5W) is which type of solution while in the bag?","options":["Hypertonic isotonic crystalloid","An isotonic IV fluid with a serum osmolality of 252 mOsm/L","A colloid","A hypertonic 600 mOsm/L solution"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: D5W is an isotonic IV fluid with a serum osmolality of 252 mOsm/L.  Why A wrong: It is isotonic in the bag, becoming hypotonic after metabolism, not hypertonic-isotonic.  Why C wrong: It is a crystalloid, not a colloid.  Why D wrong: Its osmolality is 252 mOsm/L, not 600."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"Why does D5W become hypotonic after administration?","options":["It contains excess sodium","It is initially isotonic and provides free water when dextrose is metabolized, making it a hypotonic solution that expands the ECF and the ICF","It precipitates in the bloodstream","It contains lactate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: D5W is initially isotonic and provides free water when dextrose is metabolized (making it a hypotonic solution), expanding the ECF and the ICF.  Why A wrong: D5W does not contain sodium.  Why C wrong: Precipitation is not the mechanism described.  Why D wrong: D5W contains dextrose, not lactate."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"D5W is administered to supply free water and to correct an increase in which value?","options":["Potassium","Sodium","Calcium","Chloride"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: D5W is administered to supply free water and to correct an increase in sodium.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies correction of increased sodium, not potassium, calcium, or chloride."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"A litre of D5W provides fewer than how many kcal, equivalent to roughly how much glucose?","options":["Fewer than 100 kcal; 25 g of glucose","Fewer than 200 kcal; about 50 g of glucose","Fewer than 400 kcal; 100 g of glucose","Fewer than 50 kcal; 10 g of glucose"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A litre of D5W provides fewer than 200 kcal and contains about 50 g of glucose.  Why A/C/D wrong: These caloric/glucose values do not match the source figure of <200 kcal / ~50 g glucose."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"D5W should NOT be used for fluid resuscitation because:","options":["It causes hypoglycemia","Hyperglycemia can result","It contains too much sodium","It is hypertonic"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: D5W should not be used for fluid resuscitation because hyperglycemia can result.  Why A wrong: D5W tends to raise, not lower, glucose.  Why C wrong: D5W does not contain sodium.  Why D wrong: D5W is isotonic in the bag/hypotonic after metabolism, not hypertonic."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"Lactated Ringer's 5% Dextrose in Water (DLRS), also known as Ringer's Lactate or Hartmann solution, is which type of solution?","options":["A hypertonic colloid","A crystalloid isotonic IV fluid designed to be the near-physiological solution of balanced electrolytes","A hypotonic maintenance fluid","A pure dextrose solution"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: DLRS (Ringer's Lactate / Hartmann solution) is a crystalloid isotonic IV fluid designed to be the near-physiological solution of balanced electrolytes.  Why A wrong: It is a crystalloid, not a colloid, and isotonic, not hypertonic.  Why C wrong: It is isotonic, not hypotonic.  Why D wrong: It contains balanced electrolytes, not pure dextrose."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"Which electrolytes/components does Lactated Ringer's solution contain? (SATA)","options":["130 mEq/L of sodium","4 mEq/L of potassium","3 mEq/L of calcium","109 mEq/L of chloride","28 mEq/L of lactate"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Lactated Ringer's contains 130 mEq/L of sodium, 4 mEq/L of potassium, 3 mEq/L of calcium, 109 mEq/L of chloride, and 28 mEq/L of lactate.  Why A/B/C/D/E wrong: All are correct components per the source."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"The osmolality of Ringer's Lactate is approximately:","options":["308 mOsm/L","273 mOsm/L","252 mOsm/L","154 mOsm/L"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Ringer's Lactate has an osmolality of about 273 mOsm/L.  Why A wrong: 308 mOsm/L is the osmolarity of normal saline.  Why C wrong: 252 mOsm/L is the osmolality of D5W.  Why D wrong: 154 mOsm/L is not the RL osmolality."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"Lactated Ringer's contains lactate, which is a bicarbonate precursor used to prevent or treat which condition?","options":["Acidosis","Alkalosis","Hyperkalemia","Hypernatremia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Lactate is a bicarbonate precursor used to prevent acidosis (it is an alkalizing solution used in metabolic acidosis).  Why B wrong: It treats acidosis, not alkalosis.  Why C wrong: It does not specifically treat hyperkalemia.  Why D wrong: It does not specifically treat hypernatremia."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"Which of the following are listed uses of Lactated Ringer's / DLRS? (SATA)","options":["To correct dehydration and sodium depletion and replace GI tract fluid losses","To correct fluid losses due to burns, fistula drainage, and trauma (hypovolemic shock)","Used in patients with metabolic acidosis because it is an alkalizing solution","To treat hyperglycemia","To provide total parenteral nutrition"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: DLRS is used to correct dehydration/sodium depletion and replace GI tract fluid losses; to correct fluid losses due to burns, fistula drainage, and trauma (hypovolemic shock); and in patients with metabolic acidosis as an alkalizing solution.  Why D wrong: It is not used to treat hyperglycemia.  Why E wrong: TPN is a separate parenteral nutrition therapy, not a use of RL."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"Lactated Ringer's solution is metabolized in which organ, where lactate converts to bicarbonate?","options":["The kidney","The liver","The spleen","The lungs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Lactated Ringer's solution is metabolized in the liver, which converts the lactate to bicarbonate.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies the liver, not the kidney, spleen, or lungs."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"Lactated Ringer's solution should NOT be given to patients who cannot metabolize lactate, such as those with which condition?","options":["Hypertension","Liver disease (e.g., lactic acidosis)","Asthma","Diabetes mellitus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: It should not be given to patients who cannot metabolize lactate (e.g., liver disease, lactic acidosis).  Why A wrong: Hypertension is not the cited contraindication.  Why C wrong: Asthma is unrelated to lactate metabolism.  Why D wrong: Diabetes is not the stated contraindication here."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"Lactated Ringer's should be used with caution in patients with which conditions? (SATA)","options":["Renal failure","Heart failure","Liver failure","Mild dehydration","Asthma"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: It should be used with caution in patients with renal failure and other conditions, e.g., heart failure and liver failure.  Why D wrong: Mild dehydration is an indication, not a caution.  Why E wrong: Asthma is not listed as a caution."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"Which of the following best describes hypotonic solutions? (SATA)","options":["More dilute solutions with lower osmolality than plasma","Contain fewer solutes than plasma","Cause the movement of water into the cells by osmosis to achieve homeostasis, which leads to cellular oedema","Draw water out of the cells","Example: 0.45% NS, 0.225% NS"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: Hypotonic solutions are more dilute, have lower osmolality than plasma, contain fewer solutes than plasma, cause water to move into the cells by osmosis (which can lead to cellular oedema), and examples include 0.45% NS and 0.225% NS.  Why D wrong: Drawing water out of cells describes hypertonic, not hypotonic, solutions."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"Why should hypotonic solutions be administered cautiously?","options":["They can cause hyperglycemia","They can cause cellular oedema","They draw too much fluid into vessels","They cause metabolic acidosis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hypotonic solutions should be administered with cellular oedema in mind, as water shifts into the cells.  Why A wrong: Hyperglycemia is a D5W resuscitation concern.  Why C wrong: Drawing fluid into vessels describes hypertonic solutions.  Why D wrong: Metabolic acidosis is not the stated risk."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"Sodium Chloride 0.45% (1/2 NS) is also known by which name?","options":["Full-strength saline","Half-strength normal saline","Quarter-strength saline","Double-strength saline"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 0.45% NaCl (1/2 NS) is also known as half-strength normal saline.  Why A wrong: Full-strength saline is 0.9% NS.  Why C wrong: Quarter-strength refers to 0.225% NaCl.  Why D wrong: Double-strength is not a designation here."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"Sodium Chloride 0.45% is a hypotonic IV solution used to replace water in which type of patient?","options":["Patients with hypovolemia/hypernatremia","Patients with hypervolemia","Patients with hyponatremia","Patients in cardiac arrest"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: 0.45% NaCl is a hypotonic IV solution used to replace water in patients with hypovolemia hypernatremia.  Why B wrong: Hypervolemic patients should not receive volume that promotes fluid retention this way.  Why C wrong: Hyponatremia would worsen with low-sodium fluid.  Why D wrong: Cardiac arrest is not the indication."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"Why may excess use of 0.45% NaCl lead to hyponatremia?","options":["Due to its high sodium content","Due to the dilution of sodium, especially in patients prone to water retention","Because it precipitates sodium","Because it contains potassium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Excess use may lead to hyponatremia due to the dilution of sodium, especially in patients prone to water retention.  Why A wrong: 0.45% NaCl has reduced (not high) sodium.  Why C wrong: Precipitation is not the mechanism.  Why D wrong: It does not contain potassium."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"Sodium chloride 0.45% has an osmolality of 154 mOsm/L and contains how much sodium and chloride?","options":["154 mEq/L sodium and 154 mEq/L chloride","77 mEq/L sodium and 77 mEq/L chloride","130 mEq/L sodium and 109 mEq/L chloride","34 mEq/L sodium and 34 mEq/L chloride"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 0.45% NaCl has an osmolality of 154 mOsm/L and contains 77 mEq/L sodium and 77 mEq/L chloride.  Why A wrong: 154/154 is the content of 0.9% NaCl.  Why C wrong: 130/109 are Lactated Ringer's values.  Why D wrong: 34/34 is not the 0.45% NaCl content."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"Hypotonic sodium solutions such as 0.45% NaCl are used to treat hypernatremia and which other condition?","options":["Hyperglycemic conditions","Other hyperosmolar conditions","Hypokalemia","Metabolic alkalosis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hypotonic sodium solutions are used to treat hypernatremia and other hyperosmolar conditions.  Why A wrong: Hyperglycemic conditions are not the stated indication.  Why C wrong: Hypokalemia is not treated by this solution.  Why D wrong: Metabolic alkalosis is not the indication."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"Sodium Chloride 0.33% (1/3 NS) Solution is used to allow kidneys to retain the needed amounts of water and is typically administered with what to increase tonicity?","options":["Potassium","Dextrose","Lactate","Calcium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 0.33% NaCl is administered with dextrose to increase tonicity.  Why A wrong: Potassium is not the additive described.  Why C wrong: Lactate is associated with Ringer's, not 0.33% NaCl.  Why D wrong: Calcium is not the additive."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"Sodium Chloride 0.33% (1/3 NS) is used with caution in patients with which conditions?","options":["Heart failure and renal insufficiency","Dehydration only","Liver disease only","Hyperkalemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: 0.33% NaCl is used with caution in patients with heart failure and renal insufficiency.  Why B wrong: Dehydration is not the caution.  Why C wrong: Liver disease is not the specified caution here.  Why D wrong: Hyperkalemia is not the cited concern."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"Sodium Chloride 0.225% (1/4 NS) is used as a maintenance fluid for pediatric patients. It is the most hypotonic IV fluid available at what osmolality?","options":["154 mOsm/L","77 mOsm/L","274 mOsm/L","308 mOsm/L"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 0.225% NaCl is the most hypotonic IV fluid available at 77 mOsm/L and is used together with dextrose.  Why A wrong: 154 mOsm/L is the osmolality of 0.45% NaCl.  Why C wrong: 274 mOsm/L approximates RL.  Why D wrong: 308 mOsm/L is normal saline."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"Sodium Chloride 0.225% Solution is typically used together with which agent and as a maintenance fluid for which population?","options":["With lactate, for adults","With dextrose, for pediatric patients","With potassium, for elderly patients","With calcium, for trauma patients"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 0.225% NaCl is used together with dextrose and as a maintenance fluid for pediatric patients (it is the most hypotonic IV fluid available).  Why A wrong: It is paired with dextrose, not lactate, and used in pediatrics.  Why C wrong: It is not specifically combined with potassium for the elderly.  Why D wrong: It is not paired with calcium for trauma."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"Dextrose 5% in Water (D2.5W) is another standard hypotonic IV solution. What is it (D2.5W)?","options":["5% dextrose in water","2.5% dextrose in water (D2.5W)","10% dextrose in water","0.9% saline"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: D2.5W is 2.5% dextrose in water, another standard hypotonic IV solution.  Why A wrong: 5% dextrose is D5W.  Why C wrong: 10% dextrose is D10W.  Why D wrong: 0.9% saline is normal saline."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"D2.5W solution is used to treat dehydration and decrease which serum electrolyte levels?","options":["Sodium and potassium levels","Calcium and magnesium levels","Chloride and bicarbonate","Glucose and lactate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: D2.5W is used to treat dehydration and decrease sodium and potassium levels.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies sodium and potassium, not these other electrolytes."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"Why should D2.5W NOT be administered with blood products?","options":["It can cause hemolysis of red blood cells","It causes hyperglycemia","It causes clotting","It causes alkalosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: D2.5W should not be administered with blood products as it can cause hemolysis of red blood cells.  Why B wrong: Hyperglycemia is not the cited caution for blood co-administration.  Why C wrong: Clotting is not the stated reason.  Why D wrong: Alkalosis is not the reason."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"Which of the following are characteristics of hypertonic solutions? (SATA)","options":["More concentrated solutions with a higher osmolality than body fluids","Cause movement of water from cells into the extracellular fluid by osmosis, which leads to cell shrinkage","Example: D5NS (0.9% NS), 5% NS, 5ND+RL, 5ND+RL","Cause cellular swelling","Lower osmolality than plasma"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Hypertonic solutions are more concentrated with higher osmolality than body fluids, cause water to move from cells into the ECF (causing cell shrinkage), with examples such as D5NS (D5 0.45% NS), 5% NS, and 5ND+RL.  Why D wrong: Cellular swelling occurs with hypotonic solutions, not hypertonic.  Why E wrong: Higher (not lower) osmolality than plasma defines hypertonic solutions."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"Hypertonic sodium chloride solutions contain a higher concentration of sodium and chloride than what?","options":["Distilled water","That typically contained in plasma","Lactated Ringer's","Dextrose solutions"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hypertonic sodium chloride solutions contain a higher concentration of sodium and chloride than that typically contained in plasma.  Why A wrong: The comparison is to plasma, not distilled water.  Why C wrong: The reference standard is plasma.  Why D wrong: The comparison is to plasma concentration."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"Infusion of hypertonic sodium chloride solution shifts fluids in which direction?","options":["From the intravascular and interstitial spaces into the intracellular space","From the intracellular space into the intravascular and interstitial spaces","Into the cells only","No fluid shift occurs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Infusion of hypertonic sodium chloride shifts fluids from the intracellular space into the intravascular and interstitial spaces.  Why A wrong: The shift direction is reversed in this option.  Why C wrong: Fluid moves out of cells, not into them.  Why D wrong: A fluid shift does occur."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"3% Sodium Chloride (3% NaCl) contains how much sodium and chloride and at what osmolality?","options":["513 mEq/L sodium and 513 mEq/L chloride with an osmolality of 1030 mOsm/L","855 mEq/L sodium and 855 mEq/L chloride with an osmolality of 1710 mOsm/L","154 mEq/L sodium and 154 mEq/L chloride with an osmolality of 308 mOsm/L","77 mEq/L sodium and 77 mEq/L chloride with an osmolality of 154 mOsm/L"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: 3% NaCl contains 513 mEq/L of sodium and chloride with an osmolality of 1030 mOsm/L.  Why B wrong: 855 mEq/L and 1710 mOsm/L describe 5% NaCl.  Why C wrong: 154/154 and 308 describe 0.9% NaCl.  Why D wrong: 77/77 and 154 describe 0.45% NaCl."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"5% Sodium Chloride (5% NaCl) contains how much sodium and chloride and at what osmolality?","options":["513 mEq/L with an osmolality of 1030 mOsm/L","855 mEq/L of sodium and chloride with an osmolality of 1710 mOsm/L","308 mEq/L with an osmolality of 600 mOsm/L","154 mEq/L with an osmolality of 308 mOsm/L"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 5% NaCl contains 855 mEq/L of sodium and chloride with an osmolality of 1710 mOsm/L.  Why A wrong: 513/1030 describes 3% NaCl.  Why C wrong: These values are not stated for 5% NaCl.  Why D wrong: These describe 0.9% NaCl."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"Which of the following are listed uses of hypertonic sodium chloride solutions (3% and 5% NaCl)? (SATA)","options":["Sodium deficiency (severe hyponatremia)","Cerebral oedema or increased ICP","Treatment of severe hyponatremia, used cautiously in critical situations to treat hyponatremia","Routine maintenance fluid in pediatrics","Hypovolemic shock as first-line therapy"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Hypertonic sodium chloride is used for sodium deficiency (severe hyponatremia), cerebral oedema/increased ICP, and in the treatment of severe hyponatremia (used cautiously and only in critical situations).  Why D wrong: It is not a routine pediatric maintenance fluid.  Why E wrong: It is not first-line for hypovolemic shock."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"When administering hypertonic sodium chloride (3% or 5% NaCl), how should it be infused to avoid the risk of pulmonary oedema?","options":["As a rapid bolus","At a very low rate","Without monitoring","Mixed with blood products"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hypertonic NaCl should be infused at a very low rate to avoid the risk of pulmonary oedema.  Why A wrong: Rapid bolus increases overload risk.  Why C wrong: Monitoring is required, not omitted.  Why D wrong: Mixing with blood products is not the safe infusion method."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"If hypertonic sodium chloride is administered in large quantities and rapidly, what may result? (SATA)","options":["Extracellular volume excess","Precipitation of circulatory overload","Dehydration","Cellular oedema","Increased risk of pulmonary oedema"],"correct":"A,B,E","rationale":"Why correct: If administered in large quantities and rapidly, hypertonic NaCl may cause extracellular volume excess and precipitate circulatory overload and dehydration of the intracellular space, with increased risk of pulmonary oedema.  Why C wrong: While intracellular dehydration occurs, generalized whole-body dehydration is not the listed circulatory/overload complication; the source emphasizes ECF excess and overload.  Why D wrong: Cellular oedema is a hypotonic effect, not hypertonic."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"Regarding hypertonic dextrose solutions, isotonic solutions that contain 5% dextrose (e.g., D5W, D5LRS) are described as:","options":["Strongly hypotonic","Slightly hypertonic since they exceed the total osmolality of the ECF","Exactly isotonic at all times","Hyperosmolar permanently"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Isotonic solutions that contain 5% dextrose (e.g., D5W, D5LRS) are slightly hypertonic since they exceed the total osmolality of the ECF.  Why A wrong: They are slightly hypertonic, not strongly hypotonic.  Why C wrong: They are slightly hypertonic, not exactly isotonic.  Why D wrong: The effect is only slight/temporary, not permanently hyperosmolar."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"Why does the addition of dextrose to an isotonic solution have only a transient hypertonic effect on the ECF?","options":["Because the dextrose precipitates immediately","Because dextrose quickly metabolizes, and only the isotonic solution remains, so its effect on the ECF is temporary","Because dextrose is never absorbed","Because dextrose increases sodium permanently"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Dextrose quickly metabolizes, and only the isotonic solution remains; therefore, its effect on the ECF is temporary.  Why A wrong: Dextrose is metabolized, not precipitated.  Why C wrong: Dextrose is metabolized, indicating absorption/utilization.  Why D wrong: It does not permanently increase sodium."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"Hypertonic dextrose solutions are used to provide what to the patient in the short term?","options":["Electrolytes","Kilocalories (calories)","Clotting factors","Bicarbonate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hypertonic dextrose solutions are used to provide kilocalories (calories) to the patient in the short term.  Why A wrong: Their primary short-term role is caloric provision, not electrolytes.  Why C wrong: They do not provide clotting factors.  Why D wrong: They do not provide bicarbonate."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"Dextrose 10% in Water (D10W) has which other name and osmolality?","options":["10% dextrose in water; osmolality 505 mOsm/L","5% dextrose in water; osmolality 252 mOsm/L","50% dextrose; osmolality 2520 mOsm/L","Normal saline; osmolality 308 mOsm/L"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: D10W is 10% dextrose in water with an osmolality of 505 mOsm/L.  Why B wrong: 5% dextrose at 252 mOsm/L is D5W.  Why C wrong: 50% dextrose is a different concentration.  Why D wrong: Those values describe normal saline."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"D10W contains which components?","options":["Water and sodium","Water and glucose (100 g/L)","Water and lactate","Water and potassium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: D10W contains water and glucose (100 g/L).  Why A wrong: It does not contain sodium.  Why C wrong: It does not contain lactate.  Why D wrong: It does not contain potassium."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"D10W is used in the treatment of which condition?","options":["Hypernatremia","Ketosis of starvation and to provide calories","Hyperglycemia","Hypovolemic shock as first line"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: D10W is used in the treatment of ketosis of starvation and to provide calories.  Why A wrong: It is not used for hypernatremia.  Why C wrong: It would not be used to treat hyperglycemia.  Why D wrong: It is not first-line for hypovolemic shock."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"Through what route should D10W be administered? (SATA)","options":["Should be administered using a central line if possible","Do NOT infuse using the same line as blood products as it can cause RBC hemolysis","Via a peripheral line only without restriction","Mixed directly into a blood transfusion","Through an arterial line"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: D10W should be administered using a central line if possible, and should not be infused using the same line as blood products because it can cause RBC hemolysis.  Why C wrong: A central line is preferred; peripheral use is not unrestricted.  Why D wrong: It must not be mixed with a blood transfusion (hemolysis risk).  Why E wrong: Arterial administration is not indicated."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"A patient in hypovolemic shock from burns and trauma requires fluid resuscitation. Based on the source, which fluid is the fluid of choice?","options":["D5W","Ringer's Lactate (Lactated Ringer's)","3% NaCl","0.45% NaCl"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Ringer's Lactate is the fluid of choice for burns, diarrhoea, and trauma or bleeding (hypovolemic shock).  Why A wrong: D5W should not be used for fluid resuscitation (hyperglycemia risk).  Why C wrong: 3% NaCl is reserved for severe hyponatremia/cerebral oedema, infused slowly.  Why D wrong: 0.45% NaCl is a hypotonic maintenance/water-replacement fluid, not for resuscitation."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"A patient with severe symptomatic hyponatremia and signs of cerebral oedema is prescribed 3% NaCl. Which nursing action is the priority?","options":["Infuse rapidly to correct sodium quickly","Infuse at a very low rate and monitor for pulmonary oedema and circulatory overload","Mix the 3% NaCl with the patient's packed red cells","Add dextrose to reduce tonicity"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hypertonic NaCl should be infused at a very low rate; rapid/large-volume administration risks ECF excess, circulatory overload, and pulmonary oedema.  Why A wrong: Rapid infusion precipitates circulatory overload and pulmonary oedema.  Why C wrong: Mixing with blood products is unsafe and not indicated.  Why D wrong: Adding dextrose is not the prescribed management for this hypertonic therapy."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"A patient is to receive a blood transfusion. Which IV fluid is the only one that should be used alongside blood product administration?","options":["D5W","0.45% NaCl","Normal saline (0.9% NaCl)","Ringer's Lactate"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Normal saline is the only IV fluid that should be used alongside the administration of blood products.  Why A wrong: Dextrose solutions (D5W, D2.5W, D10W) cause RBC hemolysis with blood.  Why B wrong: 0.45% NaCl is not the blood-compatible fluid named.  Why D wrong: Ringer's Lactate contains calcium, which can cause clotting with citrated blood; it is not the named compatible fluid."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"A child needs a maintenance IV fluid. Which is the most hypotonic IV fluid available, often used as a pediatric maintenance fluid together with dextrose?","options":["0.9% NaCl","0.225% NaCl (1/4 NS)","3% NaCl","Ringer's Lactate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 0.225% NaCl (1/4 NS) at 77 mOsm/L is the most hypotonic IV fluid available, used as a maintenance fluid for pediatric patients together with dextrose.  Why A wrong: 0.9% NaCl is isotonic, not the most hypotonic.  Why C wrong: 3% NaCl is hypertonic.  Why D wrong: Ringer's Lactate is isotonic."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"The pH of Ringer's Lactate is approximately:","options":["5.5","6.5","7.4","4.5"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Ringer's Lactate has a pH of 6.5.  Why A wrong: pH 5.5 is the slightly acidic pH of normal saline.  Why C wrong: 7.4 is physiological blood pH, not RL.  Why D wrong: 4.5 is not the stated RL pH."},{"section":"Batch 024 · Phlebotomy…","question":"Normal saline (0.9% NaCl) is slightly acidic with what pH?","options":["7.4","5.5","6.5","8.0"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Normal saline (NSS) is slightly acidic at pH 5.5.  Why A wrong: 7.4 is physiological blood pH.  Why B wrong: (correct) — 5.5.  Why C wrong: 6.5 is the pH of Ringer's Lactate.  Why D wrong: 8.0 is alkaline and not the NS pH."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"Concentrated dextrose solutions such as 50% dextrose in water (D50W) are classified as which type of solution?","options":["Isotonic","Hypotonic","Hypertonic","Iso-osmolar"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Higher concentrations of dextrose (e.g., D50W) are hypertonic solutions, pulling fluid into the vascular space.  Why A wrong: Isotonic solutions have osmolarity near that of plasma; concentrated dextrose far exceeds this.  Why B wrong: Hypotonic solutions have lower osmolarity than plasma, the opposite effect.  Why D wrong: Iso-osmolar means equal osmolarity to plasma, not the case for concentrated dextrose."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"When administered into a central vein, concentrated dextrose solutions are best diluted by what?","options":["The patient's own blood flow","Normal saline diluent only","Lactated Ringer's","Sterile water added to the bag"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: When given into the central vein, concentrated dextrose solutions are diluted by the rapid blood flow there, reducing irritation/hemolysis risk.  Why B wrong: The text attributes dilution to central circulation, not an added saline diluent.  Why C wrong: Lactated Ringer's is not described as the diluting mechanism.  Why D wrong: Adding sterile water to the bag is not the mechanism described."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"Dextrose 10% in water (D10W) is used as a hypertonic IV solution primarily to treat what conditions?","options":["Hypernatremia and dehydration","Ketosis of starvation and to provide calories, free water, and no electrolytes","Acidosis only","Hypokalemia and hypocalcemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: D10W is used as a hypertonic IV solution to treat ketosis of starvation and to provide calories (380 kcal/L), free water, and no electrolytes.  Why A wrong: It is not indicated for hypernatremia; it provides free water and calories.  Why C wrong: Treating ketosis of starvation differs from treating acidosis alone.  Why D wrong: D10W provides no electrolytes, so it does not correct potassium or calcium deficits."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"How many kilocalories per litre does Dextrose 10% in water (D10W) provide?","options":["170 kcal/L","250 kcal/L","380 kcal/L","510 kcal/L"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: D10W provides 380 kcal/L per the source.  Why A/B/D wrong: 170, 250, and 510 kcal/L are not the stated value for D10W."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"A nurse administering D10W using a central line should monitor for which complication if the same line is also used for blood products?","options":["Hyperglycemia","Hemolysis of red blood cells","Air embolism","Hypocalcemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If a central line is used to administer D10W and also blood products, hemolysis (RBC destruction) can occur, so it must be cautioned against.  Why A wrong: While glucose monitoring matters, the source specifically warns about RBC hemolysis on a shared line.  Why C wrong: Air embolism is a separate complication of IV/catheter technique, not the point made here.  Why D wrong: Hypocalcemia is not the cited risk of administering D10W and blood on the same line."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"Dextrose 20% in water (D20W) is described as which type of solution?","options":["Isotonic crystalloid","Hypotonic crystalloid","Hypertonic, osmotic diuretic","Colloid volume expander"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: D20W is a hypertonic solution and an osmotic diuretic that causes fluid shifts between various compartments to promote diuresis.  Why A wrong: It is hypertonic, not isotonic.  Why B wrong: It is hypertonic, not hypotonic.  Why D wrong: It is a dextrose (crystalloid) solution, not a colloid."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"What is the osmolarity of Dextrose 20% in water (D20W) as stated in the source?","options":["252 mOsm/L","660 mOsm/L","1112 mOsm/L","2523 mOsm/L"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: D20W has an osmolarity of 1112 mOsm/L per the source.  Why A wrong: 252 mOsm/L is closer to an isotonic solution, not D20W.  Why B wrong: 660 mOsm/L is not the stated value.  Why D wrong: 2523 mOsm/L is the osmolarity of D50W, not D20W."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"Dextrose 20% in water (D20W) contains glucose at what concentration?","options":["100 g/L","200 g/L","300 g/L","500 g/L"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: D20W contains water and glucose at 200 g/L.  Why A wrong: 100 g/L corresponds to D10W.  Why C wrong: 300 g/L is not the stated D20W glucose content.  Why D wrong: 500 g/L corresponds to D50W."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"Dextrose 50% in water (D50W) is described as which kind of solution?","options":["A mild hypotonic maintenance fluid","Another standard hypertonic IV solution like D10W (D50W)","An isotonic resuscitation fluid","A colloid plasma expander"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: D50W is another standard hypertonic IV solution similar to D10W.  Why A wrong: It is hypertonic, not a hypotonic maintenance fluid.  Why C wrong: It is hypertonic, not isotonic.  Why D wrong: It is a dextrose crystalloid, not a colloid."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"What are the osmolarity and glucose content of Dextrose 50% in water (D50W)?","options":["1112 mOsm/L; 200 g/L glucose","2523 mOsm/L; 500 g/L glucose","252 mOsm/L; 50 g/L glucose","660 mOsm/L; 250 g/L glucose"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: D50W has an osmolarity of 2523 mOsm/L and contains glucose 500 g/L.  Why A wrong: 1112 mOsm/L and 200 g/L describe D20W.  Why C wrong: 252 mOsm/L / 50 g/L is far too low for D50W.  Why D wrong: 660 mOsm/L / 250 g/L are not the stated D50W values."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"To treat severe hypoglycemia, how should Dextrose 50% in water (D50W) be administered?","options":["As a slow infusion over several hours","Rapidly via IV bolus","Intramuscularly","Subcutaneously"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: To treat severe hypoglycemia, D50W is administered rapidly via IV bolus.  Why A wrong: A slow multi-hour infusion would not correct severe hypoglycemia promptly.  Why C wrong: It is given IV, not intramuscularly.  Why D wrong: It is given IV, not subcutaneously."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"Colloids are also known by which name?","options":["Crystalloids","Volume expanders","Osmotic diuretics","Maintenance fluids"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Colloids are also called volume expanders because they are high-molecular-weight substances present in a crystalloid solution.  Why A wrong: Crystalloids are a distinct category; colloids contain large molecules that crystalloids lack.  Why C wrong: Osmotic diuretics (e.g., D20W) are a different concept.  Why D wrong: Volume expander, not maintenance fluid, is the alternate name."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"Which statement correctly describes colloid molecules?","options":["They are small ions that diffuse freely across membranes","They contain large molecules that do not pass through semipermeable membranes","They contain only glucose","They are identical to isotonic saline"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Colloids contain large molecules that do not pass through semipermeable membranes, keeping fluid in the vascular compartment.  Why A wrong: Their molecules are large and non-diffusible, unlike small free ions.  Why C wrong: They are not simple glucose solutions.  Why D wrong: They differ from isotonic saline by containing large oncotic molecules."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"Why must hypertonic colloid solutions that pull fluid from the interstitial compartment into the vascular compartment be used with caution?","options":["They lower blood glucose","They reduce the vascular volume rapidly","They have the potential to harmfully increase blood pressure after a severe blood loss or hemorrhage","They cause hypokalemia"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Because hypertonic colloids draw interstitial fluid into the vascular volume, after a severe blood loss/hemorrhage they can raise blood pressure to a level harmful to the patient.  Why A wrong: Colloids are volume expanders, not glucose-lowering agents.  Why B wrong: They expand (increase), not reduce, vascular volume.  Why D wrong: Hypokalemia is not the cited caution."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"In hypovolemia, when are colloids generally used?","options":["As the first-line treatment instead of crystalloids","As a second-line treatment due to potential for harmful effects such as acute kidney injury and cardiac failure","Never, because they are contraindicated","Only as oral fluids"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Colloids are generally used as a second-line treatment because of their potential for harmful effects such as acute kidney injury and cardiac failure.  Why A wrong: They are second-line, not first-line, owing to those risks.  Why C wrong: They are used, just cautiously and second-line.  Why D wrong: Colloids are IV volume expanders, not oral fluids."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"Which of the following are examples of colloids? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Natural human albumin","Artificial dextran","Gelatin solutions","Hydroxyethyl starch (HES)","0.9% normal saline"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Examples of colloids include natural human albumin and artificial dextran, gelatin, and hydroxyethyl starch (haemaccel, HES).  Why E wrong: 0.9% normal saline is a crystalloid, not a colloid."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"What two concentrations are available for human albumin solutions?","options":["1% and 10%","5% and 25%","4% and 20%","10% and 50%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Human albumin has two strengths: 5% albumin and 25% albumin.  Why A/C/D wrong: 1%/10%, 4%/20%, and 10%/50% are not the strengths the source lists for albumin."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"5% Albumin is a solution derived from plasma and is commonly used as which type of fluid?","options":["Crystalloid resuscitation fluid","Hypotonic maintenance fluid","Utilized colloid solution","Osmotic diuretic"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: 5% albumin is a solution derived from plasma and a commonly utilized colloid solution.  Why A wrong: It is a colloid, not a crystalloid.  Why B wrong: It is a colloid volume expander, not a hypotonic maintenance fluid.  Why D wrong: It is not an osmotic diuretic."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"What is the effect of 5% albumin on the circulating volume?","options":["It decreases circulating volume","It is used to increase the circulating volume and restore protein levels in conditions such as burns, pancreatitis, and plasma loss through trauma","It has no effect on volume","It causes osmotic diuresis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 5% albumin is used to increase the circulating volume and restore protein levels in conditions such as burns, pancreatitis, and plasma loss through trauma.  Why A wrong: It increases, not decreases, circulating volume.  Why C wrong: As a colloid it expands the circulating volume.  Why D wrong: It is not described as an osmotic diuretic."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"Compared with 5% albumin, 25% albumin requires what when administered to reduce excessive oedema?","options":["No additional fluid","Water restriction","Concurrent crystalloid bolus","Potassium supplementation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 25% albumin is administered with water restriction to reduce excessive oedema.  Why A wrong: The source specifies water restriction is needed.  Why C wrong: A crystalloid bolus is not the stated requirement.  Why D wrong: Potassium supplementation is not indicated for this purpose."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"Which of the following is/are uses of albumin? (Select All That Apply)","options":["To increase the circulating volume and restore protein levels","To reduce excessive oedema (25% albumin with water restriction)","In addition to conducting blood transfusion products and using the same protocols and nursing precautions when administering albumin","As an osmotic diuretic for cerebral edema","As a routine maintenance fluid for hydration"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Albumin increases circulating volume/restores protein, 25% reduces excessive oedema with water restriction, and it is used alongside blood transfusion products with the same protocols and nursing precautions during administration.  Why D wrong: The source does not list albumin as an osmotic diuretic for cerebral edema.  Why E wrong: Albumin is a colloid for specific indications, not a routine maintenance hydration fluid."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"Albumin is contraindicated in which of the following conditions? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Severe anaemia","Heart failure","Known sensitivity to albumin","Mild dehydration","Hypotension"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Contraindications of albumin include severe anaemia, heart failure, and known sensitivity to albumin.  Why D wrong: Mild dehydration is not listed as a contraindication.  Why E wrong: Hypotension is not listed as a contraindication (albumin can in fact help expand volume)."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"A nurse should observe a patient receiving albumin for at least how many hours after administration to detect allergic reactions such as hypotension and flushing?","options":["6 hours","12 hours","24 hours","48 hours"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: ACEs inhibitors can withhold for at least 24 hours before administering albumin because of the risk of allergic reactions, such as hypotension and flushing — the patient must be observed for these reactions for at least 24 hours.  Why A/B/D wrong: 6, 12, and 48 hours are not the stated observation/withholding window."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"Dextrans are classified as which type of solution?","options":["Crystalloids","Polysaccharides that act as colloids","Amino acid solutions","Lipid emulsions"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Dextrans are polysaccharides that act as colloids.  Why A wrong: They behave as colloids, not crystalloids.  Why C wrong: They are polysaccharides, not amino acid solutions.  Why D wrong: They are not lipid emulsions."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"Which statements about dextrans are correct? (Select All That Apply)","options":["They are available in the form of low-molecular-weight dextrans (LMWD) and high-molecular-weight dextrans (HMWD)","They are available in either saline or glucose solutions","Dextran interferes with blood cross-matching, so allow the patient's blood before administering dextran if cross-matching is anticipated","Dextrans are simple sugars that diffuse freely across membranes","Dextrans are contraindicated in all patients"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Dextrans come in LMWD and HMWD forms, are available in either saline or glucose solutions, and interfere with blood cross-matching, so the patient's blood should be drawn before administering dextran if cross-matching is anticipated.  Why D wrong: Dextrans are large polysaccharide colloids, not freely diffusing simple sugars.  Why E wrong: They are used as colloids with specific cautions, not universally contraindicated."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"Low-molecular-weight dextrans (LMWD) contain polysaccharide molecules with an average molecular weight of approximately what value?","options":["20,000 (Dextran 20)","40,000 (Dextran 40)","70,000 (Dextran 70)","75,000 (Dextran 75)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: LMWD contains polysaccharide molecules with an average molecular weight of 40,000 (Dextran 40).  Why A wrong: 20,000 is not the stated LMWD value.  Why C/D wrong: 70,000 (Dextran 70) and 75,000 (Dextran 75) are high-molecular-weight dextrans."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"Which of the following are uses/properties of low-molecular-weight dextrans (LMWD)? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Used to improve microcirculation in patients with poor peripheral circulation","They contain no glucose","In addition, they are used to treat shock related to vascular volume loss (e.g., burns, bleeding, trauma, or surgery)","Used to thicken the blood and promote clotting","They reduce oncotic pressure"],"correct":"A,C","rationale":"Why correct: LMWD is used to improve microcirculation in patients with poor peripheral circulation and to treat shock related to vascular volume loss (e.g., burns, bleeding, trauma, or surgery).  Why B wrong: LMWD can be available in glucose solutions (per the dextran section), so \"contain no glucose\" is incorrect.  Why D wrong: Dextrans interfere with clotting (a bleeding caution), they do not promote clotting.  Why E wrong: As colloids they raise, not reduce, oncotic pressure."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"High-molecular-weight dextrans (HMWD) contain polysaccharide molecules with an average molecular weight of which value?","options":["40,000 (Dextran 40)","70,000 (Dextran 70) or 75,000 (Dextran 75)","20,000 (Dextran 20)","100,000 (Dextran 100)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: HMWD contains polysaccharide molecules with an average molecular weight of 70,000 (Dextran 70) or 75,000 (Dextran 75).  Why A wrong: 40,000 (Dextran 40) is low-molecular-weight dextran.  Why C/D wrong: 20,000 and 100,000 are not the stated HMWD values."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"High-molecular-weight dextrans (HMWD) are used for patients in which condition?","options":["Patients with thrombocytopenia, hypofibrinogenemia, and hypersensitivity to dextran","Patients with hypovolemia and hemorrhagic shock","Patients in renal failure","Patients with hyperglycemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: HMWD is used for patients with hypovolemia and hemorrhagic shock.  Why A wrong: Those are contraindications, not indications (that group describes LMWD contraindications).  Why C wrong: Renal failure is not the stated indication.  Why D wrong: Hyperglycemia is not the stated indication."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"Low-molecular-weight dextrans (LMWD) are contraindicated in patients with which conditions? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Thrombocytopenia","Hypofibrinogenemia","Hypersensitivity to dextran","Hypovolemia","Hemorrhagic shock"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: LMWD is contraindicated in patients with thrombocytopenia, hypofibrinogenemia, and hypersensitivity to dextran.  Why D/E wrong: Hypovolemia and hemorrhagic shock are indications for HMWD, not contraindications for dextran."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"Etherified starch is a solution derived from starch and used to increase what?","options":["Blood glucose","Intravascular fluid, but can interfere with normal coagulation","Urine output","Serum potassium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Etherified starch is derived from starch and used to increase intravascular fluid, but it can interfere with normal coagulation.  Why A wrong: It is a volume expander, not a glucose source.  Why C wrong: Increasing urine output is not its stated purpose.  Why D wrong: It does not raise serum potassium per the source."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"Compared with dextrans, gelatins have which characteristic and clinical implication?","options":["Higher molecular weight and longer circulation time","Lower molecular weight than dextrans, and remain in circulation for a shorter time period","Identical molecular weight and equal duration","They contain glucose and raise blood sugar"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Gelatins have a lower molecular weight than dextrans, and they remain in circulation for a shorter time period.  Why A wrong: They have lower, not higher, molecular weight and shorter, not longer, duration.  Why C wrong: They differ from dextrans in both molecular weight and duration.  Why D wrong: The source does not describe gelatins as glucose-containing blood-sugar raisers."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"Plasma Protein Fraction (PPF) is best described as which of the following?","options":["A whole blood product","A solution that is also prepared from plasma","A pure crystalloid","A lipid emulsion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: PPF is a solution that is also prepared from plasma.  Why A wrong: It is a plasma-derived solution, not whole blood.  Why C wrong: It is plasma-derived, not a crystalloid.  Why D wrong: It is not a lipid emulsion."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"When infusing albumin (and plasma protein fraction) to increase circulating volume, the nurse should infuse the solution in what manner?","options":["Rapidly by bolus","Slowly to increase circulating volume","Only via the intramuscular route","With added potassium chloride"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Finally, like albumin, PPF needs to be infused slowly to increase circulating volume.  Why A wrong: It is infused slowly, not by rapid bolus.  Why C wrong: It is an IV infusion, not intramuscular.  Why D wrong: Adding potassium chloride is not indicated."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"Dextrose 5% in water (D5W) is classified as which type of fluid, and what is one of its uses?","options":["Hypertonic; treats hyponatremia","Isotonic; used for fluid loss, dehydration, and hypernatremia","Hypotonic; treats DKA only","Colloid; used for shock"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Per the IV fluid comparison, Dextrose 5% in water (D5W) is isotonic and is used for fluid loss, dehydration, and hypernatremia.  Why A wrong: D5W in the bag is isotonic (becomes hypotonic after metabolism); listed uses do not include treating hyponatremia.  Why C wrong: It is isotonic, and its listed uses are broader than DKA only.  Why D wrong: D5W is a dextrose crystalloid, not a colloid."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"According to the IV fluid comparison, Dextrose 5% in water (D5W) should be used cautiously in which patients and why?","options":["In hypertensive patients because it raises BP","In renal and cardiac patients because it can cause fluid overload","In diabetic patients only","In febrile patients because it lowers temperature"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Special consideration for D5W: use cautiously in renal and cardiac patients and watch for fluid overload.  Why A wrong: The caution relates to fluid overload in renal/cardiac patients, not hypertension specifically.  Why C wrong: While glucose matters in diabetics, the source's special consideration cites renal/cardiac fluid overload.  Why D wrong: D5W is not described as a temperature-lowering fluid."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"Per the IV fluid comparison, 0.9% sodium chloride (normal saline) is classified as which tonicity and used for which conditions? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Isotonic","Shock","Hypernatremia","Blood transfusions","Resuscitation, fluid challenges, and DKA"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: 0.9% sodium chloride (normal saline) is isotonic and is used for shock, hypernatremia, blood transfusions, resuscitation, fluid challenges, and DKA.  Why all correct: Each listed option matches the source's uses for normal saline."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"What is the special consideration when using 0.9% normal saline?","options":["It contains potassium","It can lead to overload, so use with caution in patients with heart failure or edema","It causes cellular collapse","It must be warmed before infusion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Special consideration for 0.9% NaCl: it can lead to overload; use with caution in patients with heart failure or edema.  Why A wrong: Normal saline does not contain potassium (Lactated Ringer's does).  Why C wrong: Cellular collapse is a hypotonic-fluid concern, not 0.9% saline.  Why D wrong: Warming before infusion is not the stated special consideration."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"Lactated Ringer's (RL) is which tonicity and is used for which conditions? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Isotonic","Dehydration","Burns (choice of fluid)","Lower GI fluid losses","Acute blood loss"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Lactated Ringer's (RL) is isotonic and used for dehydration, burns (choice of fluid), lower GI fluid losses, and acute blood loss.  Why all correct: Each option matches the source's listed uses for RL."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"Which special considerations apply to Lactated Ringer's (RL)? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Contains potassium — don't use with renal failure patients","Don't use with liver disease — the liver can't metabolize lactate","It contains no electrolytes","It is a colloid solution","It is the same as 0.9% normal saline"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: RL contains potassium (avoid in renal failure) and should not be used in liver disease because the liver can't metabolize lactate.  Why C wrong: RL contains electrolytes including potassium.  Why D wrong: RL is a crystalloid, not a colloid.  Why E wrong: RL differs from 0.9% saline (it contains potassium and lactate)."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"0.45% sodium chloride (½ normal saline / ½ isotonic saline) is classified as which tonicity?","options":["Isotonic","Hypotonic","Hypertonic","Colloid"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 0.45% sodium chloride (½ normal saline) is hypotonic.  Why A wrong: It is below isotonic concentration.  Why C wrong: It is hypotonic, not hypertonic.  Why D wrong: It is a crystalloid, not a colloid."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"Per the comparison table, which conditions are uses for 0.45% sodium chloride (hypotonic)? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Water replacement","DKA","Gastric fluid loss from NG or vomiting","Resuscitation in shock","Blood transfusion"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: 0.45% sodium chloride is used for water replacement, DKA, and gastric fluid loss from NG or vomiting.  Why D wrong: Resuscitation is a use for isotonic normal saline, not hypotonic 0.45% saline.  Why E wrong: Blood transfusions are given with isotonic 0.9% saline, not hypotonic fluid."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"What is a special consideration when administering hypotonic 0.45% sodium chloride?","options":["Use with caution because it may cause cardiovascular collapse or increased intracranial pressure","It causes fluid overload only","It contains potassium","It must be given via central line only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Special consideration: use with caution because hypotonic 0.45% saline may cause cardiovascular collapse or increased intracranial pressure (fluid shifts into cells).  Why B wrong: Overload is a concern of isotonic fluids; hypotonic fluids risk cellular swelling/collapse and raised ICP.  Why C wrong: 0.45% saline is not noted for containing potassium.  Why D wrong: Central-line-only administration is not the stated special consideration."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"Dextrose 5% in 0.9% normal saline (D5NS) is classified as which tonicity and is used for what?","options":["Isotonic; resuscitation","Hypotonic; water replacement","Hypertonic; used later in DKA treatment","Colloid; used in hemorrhage"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Per the table, Dextrose 5% in normal saline is hypertonic and used later in DKA treatment.  Why A wrong: It is hypertonic, not isotonic.  Why B wrong: It is hypertonic, not hypotonic.  Why D wrong: It is a dextrose/saline crystalloid, not a colloid."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"What is a special consideration for Dextrose 5% in normal saline?","options":["Use only when blood sugar falls below 250 mg/dL","Use only when blood sugar falls below 150 mg/dL","Use only in renal patients","Use as a maintenance fluid for all patients"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Special consideration: use only when blood sugar falls below 150 mg/dL (so dextrose is added once glucose normalizes during DKA management).  Why A wrong: The stated threshold is 150 mg/dL, not 250 mg/dL.  Why C wrong: It is not restricted specifically to renal patients.  Why D wrong: It is a hypertonic DKA-treatment fluid, not a general maintenance fluid."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"DNS (Dextrose 5% in 0.9% saline) used as temporary treatment for shock acts in which way, and what is its special consideration?","options":["As a plasma expander when plasma expanders aren't available; don't use in cardiac or renal disease","As an osmotic diuretic; safe in all patients","As a maintenance fluid; monitor glucose only","As a colloid; infuse rapidly"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: DNS is used as temporary treatment for shock if plasma expanders aren't available; the special consideration is don't use in cardiac or renal disease.  Why B wrong: It is described as a temporary plasma expander substitute, not an osmotic diuretic that is safe in all patients.  Why C wrong: Its role here is temporary shock treatment, with a caution against cardiac/renal disease.  Why D wrong: It is a crystalloid solution, not a colloid for rapid infusion."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"According to the summary of tonicity, Dextrose 10% in water (D10W) is classified as which tonicity?","options":["Isotonic","Hypotonic","Hypertonic","Iso-osmolar"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The Summary of Tonicity of IV Fluids lists Dextrose 10% in water (D10W) as hypertonic.  Why A wrong: D5W is isotonic; D10W is hypertonic.  Why B wrong: It is hypertonic, not hypotonic.  Why D wrong: It is hypertonic, not iso-osmolar."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"According to the summary of tonicity, which of the following fluids are HYPOTONIC? (Select All That Apply)","options":["0.45% sodium chloride (½ NS)","0.45% sodium chloride in dextrose (½ NS)","0.9% sodium chloride (NS)","Lactated Ringer's (RL)","5% dextrose in water (D5W)"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The summary lists 0.45% sodium chloride (½ NS) and 0.45% sodium chloride in dextrose (½ NS) as hypotonic.  Why C wrong: 0.9% sodium chloride (NS) is isotonic.  Why D wrong: Lactated Ringer's (RL) is isotonic.  Why E wrong: 5% dextrose in water (D5W) is isotonic."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"According to the summary of tonicity, which fluids are HYPERTONIC? (Select All That Apply)","options":["10% dextrose in water (D10W)","5% dextrose in 0.45% sodium chloride (D5W / ½ NS)","5% dextrose in 0.9% sodium chloride (D5/NS)","0.9% sodium chloride (NS)","Lactated Ringer's (RL)"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The summary lists 10% dextrose in water, 5% dextrose in 0.45% sodium chloride, and 5% dextrose in 0.9% sodium chloride as hypertonic.  Why D wrong: 0.9% sodium chloride is isotonic.  Why E wrong: Lactated Ringer's is isotonic."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"(NCERT recall) A patient suffering from shock is receiving normal saline (NS). What is the normal osmolarity of NS?","options":["154 mEq/L","100 mEq/L","130 mEq/L","126 mEq/L"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The keyed answer is 154 mEq/L for normal saline.  Why B/C/D wrong: 100, 130, and 126 mEq/L are not the stated value for NS sodium/osmolarity."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"(NCERT recall) 0.9% sodium chloride (normal saline) contains how much sodium chloride per litre?","options":["4.5 g","9 g","20 g","50 g"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 0.9% Sodium Chloride (NS) contains 9 g sodium chloride per litre (0.9% = 9 g/L).  Why A wrong: 4.5 g/L corresponds to 0.45% saline.  Why C wrong: 20 g/L corresponds to a much more concentrated (20%) solution.  Why D wrong: 50 g/L corresponds to a 5% solution."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"(NCERT recall) A patient comes to the emergency with the femur fracture and severe hemorrhage. Which fluid should NOT be given to this patient?","options":["RL (Lactated Ringer's)","0.98 NS (normal saline)","0.45% NS","Colloids"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The keyed answer is 0.45% NS, a hypotonic fluid, which should not be given in acute blood loss/hemorrhage (it does not stay in the vascular space and risks cardiovascular collapse).  Why A wrong: Lactated Ringer's is appropriate for acute blood loss.  Why B wrong: Normal saline is appropriate for resuscitation/hemorrhage.  Why D wrong: Colloids can be used as volume expanders in hemorrhage."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"(NCERT recall) Which of the following methods used by the nurse is the most reliable indicator of a patient's fluid balance status?","options":["Intake and output","Skin turgor","Complete blood count","Daily weight"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The keyed answer is intake and output, the most reliable indicator of fluid balance status.  Why B wrong: Skin turgor assesses hydration but is less reliable/objective for fluid balance.  Why C wrong: A CBC does not directly measure fluid balance.  Why D wrong: Daily weight is the most accurate way to assess hydration status (see next item) but the keyed most-reliable fluid-balance indicator here is intake and output."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"(NCERT recall) Which is the most accurate way to assess a patient's hydration level?","options":["Skin pinch / turgor test","Intake and output","Daily weight","Urine colour"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Daily weight is the most accurate way to assess hydration level; body weight changes give consistent and site-specific gravity, providing the simplest and most accurate index of hydration status. (Skin pinch/turgor and urine colour can be easy and fast but provide less accurate assessment.)  Why A wrong: The skin pinch/turgor test is easy and fast but a less accurate assessment of hydration.  Why B wrong: Intake and output is the keyed most-reliable fluid-balance indicator, but daily weight is keyed as the most accurate hydration assessment.  Why D wrong: Urine colour is easy and fast but less accurate for hydration."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"(NCERT recall) What is the normal sodium concentration in Lactated Ringer's (RL)?","options":["154 mEq/L","131 mEq/L","130 mEq/L","123 mEq/L"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The keyed answer is 130 mEq/L for the sodium concentration in Lactated Ringer's.  Why A wrong: 154 mEq/L is the sodium concentration of normal saline, not RL.  Why B/D wrong: 131 mEq/L and 123 mEq/L are not the keyed value for RL sodium."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"How is nutrition delivered during parenteral nutrition?","options":["Via the gastrointestinal tract","Via the veins","Through a nasogastric tube","Through the skin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Parenteral nutrition provides nutrition via the veins.  Why A wrong: Enteral nutrition uses the GI tract; parenteral bypasses it.  Why C wrong: A nasogastric tube is enteral, not parenteral.  Why D wrong: It is delivered intravenously, not through the skin."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"Total parenteral nutrition (TPN) is administered through which route?","options":["Peripheral PICC line or central line only","A nasojejunal tube","A peripheral vein only","Oral route"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Total parenteral nutrition (TPN) is administered through only a PICC line or central line.  Why B wrong: A nasojejunal tube is for enteral feeding, not TPN.  Why C wrong: TPN requires central access; PPN uses a peripheral vein.  Why D wrong: TPN is intravenous, not oral."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"Partial parenteral nutrition (PPN) is administered through which route?","options":["Only a central line","Only a peripheral line","A nasogastric tube","An intramuscular injection"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Partial parenteral nutrition (PPN) is administered through only a peripheral line.  Why A wrong: A central line is used for TPN, not PPN.  Why C wrong: A nasogastric tube is enteral feeding.  Why D wrong: PPN is an IV infusion, not intramuscular."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"TPN solutions include which components? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Carbohydrates (dextrose)","Emulsified fats and protein in the form of amino acids","Vitamins","Minerals and water and electrolytes","Whole red blood cells"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: TPN solutions include carbohydrates (dextrose), emulsified fats, protein in the form of amino acids, vitamins, minerals, water, and electrolytes.  Why E wrong: Whole red blood cells are a blood product, not a TPN component."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"Which patients are indicated for TPN? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Patients with paralyzed or non-functional GI tract, or conditions that require bowel rest, such as small bowel obstruction, ulcerative colitis, or pancreatitis","Patients who have had nothing by mouth (NPO) for seven days or longer","Critically ill patients","Babies with an immature gastrointestinal system or congenital malformations","Patients fully tolerating an oral diet"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: TPN is indicated for patients with paralyzed/non-functional GI tract or needing bowel rest (small bowel obstruction, ulcerative colitis, pancreatitis), NPO for seven days or longer, critically ill patients, and babies with immature GI systems or congenital malformations.  Why E wrong: Patients fully tolerating an oral diet do not need parenteral nutrition."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"Besides those, TPN is also indicated in which additional patients? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Patients with chronic diarrhoea or vomiting","Patients with extreme malnutrition","Patients with a need for bowel rest before surgery or chemotherapy","Patients in hyperbolic states, such as burns, sepsis, or trauma","Patients with a fully healthy, functioning GI tract"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: TPN is also indicated for patients with extreme malnutrition or chronic diarrhoea/vomiting, with a need for bowel rest before surgery or chemotherapy, and in hyperbolic (hypermetabolic) states such as burns, sepsis, or trauma.  Why E wrong: A fully healthy functioning GI tract is not an indication for TPN."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"When TPN is administered by central vein, the TPN concentration should be more than what percentage?","options":["More than 5% TPN solution","More than 10% TPN solution","More than 20% TPN solution","More than 50% TPN solution"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: TPN concentration of more than 10% TPN solution should be administered by central vein.  Why A/C/D wrong: 5%, 20%, and 50% are not the threshold stated; the cutoff is more than 10%."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"Best central vein for parenteral nutrition includes which of the following? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Right subclavian vein (SCV) — most common for long-term nutrition, with high chance of pneumothorax","Internal jugular vein (IJV) — used for short-term therapy or up to 4 weeks (e.g., in cases of trauma cases)","Femoral vein — least common, highest chance of infection and thrombosis","The cephalic vein of the hand","The dorsal venous arch of the foot"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Best central vein for parenteral nutrition: right subclavian vein (most common for long-term, high chance of pneumothorax); internal jugular vein (short-term/up to 4 weeks, e.g., trauma cases); femoral vein (least common, highest chance of infection and thrombosis).  Why D wrong: The cephalic vein of the hand is a peripheral vein, not a central access for TPN.  Why E wrong: The dorsal venous arch of the foot is peripheral, not a central vein."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"If TPN is required for a longer time than 2 weeks, which device should be administered/used? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Peripherally inserted central catheter (PICC)","Implanted vascular device (port)","Tunnelled catheter","A short peripheral IV cannula","A nasogastric tube"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: If TPN is required for a longer time than 2 weeks, a more permanent catheter such as a PICC, implanted vascular device, or tunnelled catheter should be administered.  Why D wrong: A short peripheral IV cannula is not appropriate for long-term TPN.  Why E wrong: A nasogastric tube is an enteral feeding route, not a TPN device."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"Through a peripheral vein, parenteral nutrition can administer which type of concentration, and for how long?","options":["High concentration TPN for many months","Less concentrated (PPN) solutions for less than 2 weeks","Hypertonic 50% dextrose indefinitely","Blood products for 2 days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Through a peripheral vein we can administer less concentrated TPN solutions (PPN) for less than 2 weeks.  Why A wrong: High-concentration TPN requires a central vein, not peripheral, and not for months peripherally.  Why C wrong: Hypertonic 50% dextrose is too concentrated for a peripheral vein.  Why D wrong: Blood products are not parenteral nutrition."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"Why can PPN be administered by any other traditional methods, such as brachial and cephalic veins?","options":["Because it is hypertonic","Because it is given through peripheral veins, so strictly monitor for these clinical features","Because it contains no nutrients","Because it is identical to blood"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: PPN can be administered by any other traditional methods such as brachial and cephalic veins because it is given through peripheral veins, requiring strict monitoring for clinical features (e.g., phlebitis/infiltration).  Why A wrong: PPN is less concentrated; highly hypertonic solutions require central access.  Why C wrong: PPN does contain nutrients (less concentrated).  Why D wrong: PPN is a nutrient solution, not blood."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"In hypertonic parenteral solutions, carbohydrates usually provide what percentage of energy?","options":["30–40%","50% exactly","60–70% of energy","90%"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Carbohydrate usually provides 60–70% of energy in hypertonic parenteral solutions.  Why A wrong: 30% is the contribution of fat emulsions/lipids, not carbohydrate.  Why B wrong: 50% is not the stated carbohydrate contribution.  Why D wrong: 90% is not the stated value."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"Amino acids in parenteral solutions are described in which way regarding concentration and energy?","options":["High concentration of fat providing all energy","Usually a low concentration of fat providing protein for central parenteral catheter; about 15–20% total energy provided by protein","The same as carbohydrate, 70% energy","They provide no energy at all"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Amino acids/protein: usually a low concentration of fat providing high concentration for central parenteral catheter; about 15–20% total energy is provided by protein.  Why A wrong: It is a low, not high, fat concentration; protein supplies the energy fraction.  Why C wrong: Carbohydrate, not protein, provides 60–70% of energy.  Why D wrong: Protein provides about 15–20% of total energy."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"Fat emulsions/lipids in parenteral nutrition provide approximately what percentage of total energy?","options":["10% of total energy","30% of total energy","50% of total energy","70% of total energy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Fat emulsions/lipids provide 30% of total energy.  Why A/C/D wrong: 10%, 50%, and 70% are not the stated lipid energy contribution."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"Components of hypertonic parenteral solutions include which of the following? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Carbohydrate","Amino acids","Fat emulsion/lipids","Vitamins and minerals","Water & electrolytes"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Components of hypertonic parenteral solutions include carbohydrate, amino acids, fat emulsion/lipids, vitamins, minerals, and water & electrolytes.  Why all correct: Each listed component appears in the source's list of hypertonic parenteral solution components."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"In fat emulsions/lipids, fat emulsions provide approximately what fraction of total energy and from which oil sources?","options":["About 30% of total energy; most fat emulsions are prepared from soybean or sunflower oil","About 70% of total energy; from fish oil only","About 10%; from olive oil only","About 50%; from coconut oil only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Fat emulsions provide most of the energy (about 30% of total energy per the lipid section) and are prepared from soybean or sunflower oil.  Why B wrong: 70% from fish oil only is not stated.  Why C wrong: 10% from olive oil only is not stated.  Why D wrong: 50% from coconut oil only is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"Primary components of fat emulsions include which of the following? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Linoleic acid","Oleic acid","Palmitic acid","Linolenic acid","Glucose"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Primary components of fat emulsions include linoleic acid, oleic acid, palmitic acid, and linolenic acid.  Why E wrong: Glucose is a carbohydrate, not a primary component of fat emulsions."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"Which precautions apply to administering fat emulsions/lipids? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Other medicines should not be put into the fat emulsion solution","Used filter size 1.2 micro meter or larger to pass fat particles","Fat emulsions initially should be started slowly, usually one millimetre per minute","Initially observe vitals every 10 minutes","The infusion should be discarded after 24 hours"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Other medicines should not be put into the fat emulsion solution; use a filter size of 1.2 micrometre or larger to pass fat particles; fat emulsions initially should be started slowly (usually one millilitre per minute); and initially observe vitals every 10 minutes.  Why E wrong: A \"discard after 24 hours\" instruction is not among the fat-emulsion precautions stated."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"Adverse reactions to fat emulsions include which of the following? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Chest pain, cyanosis, diaphoresis, and dyspnoea","Fever, flushing, headache","Nausea and vomiting, pressure over the eyes, and thrombophlebitis and vertigo","Permanent loss of vision in all patients","Spontaneous cure of malnutrition"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Adverse reactions to fat emulsions include chest pain, cyanosis, diaphoresis and dyspnoea; fever, flushing, headache; and nausea and vomiting, pressure over the eyes, thrombophlebitis, and vertigo.  Why D wrong: Permanent loss of vision in all patients is not a listed reaction.  Why E wrong: Spontaneous cure of malnutrition is not an adverse reaction."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"An air embolism is a complication of parenteral therapy that occurs due to what?","options":["Excess glucose in the bag","Air into the catheter system or catheter system opened, IV tubing disconnected","Too slow an infusion rate","Adding potassium to TPN"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Air embolism occurs due to air into the catheter system, catheter system opened, IV tubing disconnected.  Why A wrong: Excess glucose causes hyperglycemia, not air embolism.  Why C wrong: A slow infusion does not cause air embolism.  Why D wrong: Adding potassium is unrelated to air embolism."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"To prevent air embolism during the Valsalva manoeuvre, what should the patient be instructed to do?","options":["Cough forcefully","Take a deep breath, hold and bear down when IV tubing & cap changes","Stand up quickly","Hold the breath for 5 minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Instruct the client for the Valsalva manoeuvre: take a deep breath, hold and bear down when IV tubing & cap changes (this raises intrathoracic pressure to prevent air entry).  Why A wrong: Forceful coughing is not the instructed manoeuvre.  Why C wrong: Standing up quickly does not perform the Valsalva manoeuvre and would increase risk.  Why D wrong: Holding the breath for 5 minutes is unsafe and not what is described."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"What are signs and symptoms of an air embolism? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Chest pain","Dyspnoea, hypotension","Rapid & weak pulse","Respiratory distress","Hyperglycemia"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Signs & symptoms of air embolism: chest pain, dyspnoea, hypotension, rapid & weak pulse, and respiratory distress.  Why E wrong: Hyperglycemia is a separate metabolic complication, not a sign of air embolism."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"If an air embolism is suspected, which is the FIRST priority nursing action?","options":["Administer oxygen, then inform on-duty doctor","Place the client in side-lying position with the head lower than the feet to trap the air in the right side of the heart","Clamp the IV catheter (first priority)","Continue the infusion"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: If an air embolism is suspected, the first priority is to clamp the IV catheter (first priority).  Why A wrong: Oxygen administration and informing the doctor are later steps (third priority).  Why B wrong: Positioning the client (Trendelenburg / left side, head lower than feet) is the second priority.  Why D wrong: Continuing the infusion would worsen the embolism."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"What is the correct sequence of priorities when an air embolism is suspected during parenteral therapy?","options":["Administer oxygen → clamp catheter → reposition","Clamp the IV catheter → place client in head-low side-lying position (trap air in right side of heart) → administer oxygen and inform the on-duty doctor","Reposition → administer oxygen → clamp catheter","Inform doctor → continue infusion → clamp catheter"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The stated order is: (1) clamp the IV catheter, (2) place the client in a head-low side-lying position so air is trapped in the right side of the heart, (3) administer oxygen and inform the on-duty doctor.  Why A wrong: Clamping the catheter, not oxygen, comes first.  Why C wrong: Repositioning is the second, not first, action.  Why D wrong: The infusion must be clamped, not continued."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"Hyperglycemia as a complication of TPN occurs due to what?","options":["Too low a dextrose concentration","High concentration of dextrose in the TPN solution","Infusing fat emulsion too quickly","Insufficient protein"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hyperglycemia occurs due to a high concentration of dextrose in the TPN solution.  Why A wrong: A high, not low, dextrose concentration causes hyperglycemia.  Why C wrong: Rapid fat emulsion causes adverse lipid reactions, not the hyperglycemia described.  Why D wrong: Insufficient protein is unrelated to TPN hyperglycemia."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"What are signs and symptoms of hyperglycemia during TPN? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Restlessness","Weakness","Excessive thirst","Confusion","Bradycardia with cold sweats"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Signs & symptoms of hyperglycemia: restlessness, weakness, excessive thirst, confusion.  Why E wrong: Bradycardia with cold sweats is not the listed presentation of TPN hyperglycemia."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"To prevent hyperglycemia during TPN, the blood glucose level should be monitored at what frequency?","options":["Every hour","Every 4–6 hours","Once daily","Once a week"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: To prevent hyperglycemia, the blood glucose level should be monitored every 4–6 hours.  Why A/C/D wrong: Hourly, once-daily, and weekly monitoring are not the stated frequency for preventing TPN hyperglycemia."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"Hypervolemia as a complication of TPN occurs when the TPN is infused in what manner?","options":["Too slowly","Rapidly","Via the peripheral route only","With added insulin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hypervolemia occurs when TPN is infused rapidly (fluid overload).  Why A wrong: A slow infusion does not cause hypervolemia.  Why C wrong: Route alone does not define it; the cause is rapid infusion.  Why D wrong: Adding insulin does not cause hypervolemia."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"Which are signs and symptoms of hypervolemia during TPN? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Bounding pulse","Crackles","Increased blood pressure, distended jugular vein, and weight gain","If hypervolemia occurs, then check rapid & hepatic functions are at high risk","Hypotension and dry mucous membranes"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Signs & symptoms of hypervolemia: bounding pulse, crackles, increased blood pressure, distended jugular vein, and weight gain; if hypervolemia occurs, then renal & hepatic functions are at high risk.  Why E wrong: Hypotension and dry mucous membranes indicate hypovolemia, the opposite of fluid overload."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"To help prevent hypervolemia, TPN should always be delivered via what?","options":["Gravity drip with a manual roller clamp","An electronic infusion pump (monitor strictly intake & output, weigh the client daily)","A wide-bore peripheral cannula","A pressure bag"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: TPN should always be delivered via an electronic infusion pump; monitor strictly intake & output and weigh the client daily to detect hypervolemia.  Why A wrong: A gravity drip lacks the rate control needed to prevent rapid infusion.  Why C wrong: A wide-bore peripheral cannula does not control infusion rate and is not appropriate for TPN.  Why D wrong: A pressure bag would speed infusion and increase overload risk."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"Hypoglycemia as a TPN complication occurs when the TPN is handled in what way?","options":["Infused too rapidly","Abruptly discontinued or when too much insulin is administered","Diluted with normal saline","Stored at room temperature"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hypoglycemia occurs when the TPN is abruptly discontinued or when too much insulin is administered.  Why A wrong: Rapid infusion causes hyperglycemia/hypervolemia, not hypoglycemia.  Why C wrong: Dilution with saline is not the cited cause.  Why D wrong: Storage temperature is unrelated to hypoglycemia from TPN."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"Which are signs and symptoms of hypoglycemia during TPN? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Anxiety","Diaphoresis","Hunger","Low blood glucose level (<70 mg/dL)","Excessive thirst and confusion"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Signs & symptoms of hypoglycemia: anxiety, diaphoresis, hunger, and low blood glucose level <70 mg/dL.  Why E wrong: Excessive thirst and confusion are listed under hyperglycemia, not hypoglycemia."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"To prevent hypoglycemia, how should the infusion rate be managed when discontinuing TPN?","options":["Stop the TPN abruptly","Gradually decrease the infusion rate when discontinuing TPN; after TPN is discontinued, start 10% D for 1–2 hours to prevent hypoglycemia","Increase the rate to maximum before stopping","Switch immediately to plain water IV"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: To prevent hypoglycemia, gradually decrease the infusion rate when discontinuing TPN; after TPN is discontinued, start 10% dextrose for 1–2 hours.  Why A wrong: Abrupt discontinuation causes hypoglycemia and must be avoided.  Why C wrong: Maximizing the rate before stopping would not prevent rebound hypoglycemia.  Why D wrong: Plain water IV provides no glucose to prevent hypoglycemia."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"What is the most common complication of TPN, and what is its cause?","options":["Air embolism due to open catheter","Infection due to poor aseptic technique, catheter contamination, and contamination of solution","Pneumothorax due to subclavian puncture","Hyperglycemia due to dextrose"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Infection is the most common complication of TPN, due to poor aseptic technique, catheter contamination, and contamination of the solution.  Why A wrong: Air embolism is a complication but not described as the most common.  Why C wrong: Pneumothorax is a placement complication, not the most common overall.  Why D wrong: Hyperglycemia is a metabolic complication but not stated as the most common."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"Which are signs and symptoms of infection associated with TPN? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Chills","Fever, elevated WBC","Redness or drainage from insertion site","Hypoglycemia","Bounding pulse and weight gain"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Signs & symptoms of infection: chills, fever, elevated WBC, redness or drainage from the insertion site.  Why D wrong: Hypoglycemia is a metabolic complication, not a sign of infection.  Why E wrong: Bounding pulse and weight gain indicate hypervolemia, not infection."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"Which nursing actions are appropriate for managing/monitoring TPN-related infection? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Notify the doctor immediately (first priority)","Use aseptic technique; restrict insertion site with cannula & tubing","Send catheter tip to laboratory for culture & take a blood culture","To give antibiotics as per order","Use aseptic technique, check the temperature & assess the sign of infection"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: For TPN infection: notify the doctor immediately (first priority); use aseptic technique and restrict the insertion site with cannula & tubing; send the catheter tip to the laboratory for culture and take a blood culture; give antibiotics as per order; and use aseptic technique while checking temperature and assessing for signs of infection.  Why all correct: Each listed action matches the source's infection management points."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"Pneumothorax as a complication of TPN occurs due to what?","options":["Rapid dextrose infusion","Improper placement of catheter that ends in puncture of pleural space","Adding insulin to the bag","Slow infusion of fat emulsion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pneumothorax occurs due to improper placement of catheter that ends in puncture of the pleural space.  Why A wrong: Rapid dextrose causes hyperglycemia, not pneumothorax.  Why C wrong: Adding insulin is unrelated to pneumothorax.  Why D wrong: Slow fat emulsion does not cause pneumothorax."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"After catheter placement for TPN, how is correct catheter placement and the absence of a pneumothorax verified?","options":["By auscultation alone","By X-ray, which should confirm the position of the catheter and detect the presence of a pneumothorax","By checking blood glucose","By weighing the patient"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After placement of a catheter, an X-ray should confirm the position of the catheter and detect the presence of a pneumothorax; TPN is not initiated until the correct catheter placement is verified and the absence of pneumothorax is verified.  Why A wrong: Auscultation alone is not the confirmatory method; an X-ray is required.  Why C wrong: Blood glucose does not verify catheter placement.  Why D wrong: Weighing the patient does not confirm placement or rule out pneumothorax."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"Common nursing care during TPN therapy includes which of the following? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Check the doctor's order and administer as per the order","Hand hygiene prevents the spread of microorganisms","Vital signs are more frequently monitored initially in patients with TPN","To identify signs of infection early, be aware of the patient's temperature range","Use strict aseptic technique when caring for central venous catheters and PICC lines"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Common nursing care during TPN therapy: check the doctor's order and administer as per order; perform hand hygiene to prevent the spread of microorganisms; monitor vital signs more frequently initially; be aware of the patient's temperature range to identify infection early; and use strict aseptic technique when caring for central venous catheters and PICC lines.  Why all correct: Each option matches a listed common nursing care point for TPN."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"Which additional nursing care points apply during TPN therapy? (Select All That Apply)","options":["IV medications & blood components are not given through the TPN line","Monitor electrolytes serum, renal & liver functions during the day","After 24 hours, the remaining solution should be discarded and a new bag or bottle of TPN to prevent infection","If TPN solutions are looks like cloudy & darkened, then it should not be used","Mix any medicine directly into the TPN bag for convenience"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: IV medications & blood components are not given through the TPN line; monitor serum electrolytes and renal & liver functions during the day; after 24 hours, remaining solution should be discarded and a new bag/bottle of TPN used to prevent infection; and if the TPN solution looks cloudy & darkened, it should not be used.  Why E wrong: The source explicitly states do not mix any medicine into the TPN bag — the opposite of option E."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"(NORDEET recall) A client receiving parenteral nutrition (PN) complains of a headache. The nurse notes that the client has an increased blood pressure, bounding pulse, jugular vein distension, and crackles bilaterally. Which complication of PN is this client experiencing?","options":["Sepsis","Hyperglycemia","Air embolism","Hypervolemia"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Increased blood pressure, bounding pulse, jugular vein distension, and crackles are the classic signs of hypervolemia (fluid overload), which is the keyed answer.  Why A wrong: Sepsis presents with fever, chills, and elevated WBC, not fluid-overload signs.  Why B wrong: Hyperglycemia presents with thirst, weakness, and confusion, not crackles/JVD.  Why C wrong: Air embolism presents with chest pain, dyspnoea, hypotension, and rapid weak pulse, not bounding pulse with JVD."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"(NORDEET recall) A patient is getting total parenteral nutrition (TPN) via central venous line. The infusion rate should be monitored closely to prevent which complication, EXCEPT which one?","options":["Oscillation in glucose profile","To manage fluid overload","To manage electrolyte imbalance","To manage abruption (sudden cessation)"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Monitoring the infusion rate closely helps prevent glucose oscillation, fluid overload, and electrolyte imbalance; the keyed EXCEPT answer is D (managing abruption/sudden cessation is not the reason for close rate monitoring during infusion).  Why A wrong: Close rate control does help prevent glucose oscillation, so it is not the exception.  Why B wrong: Close rate control helps prevent fluid overload, so it is not the exception.  Why C wrong: Close rate control helps prevent electrolyte imbalance, so it is not the exception."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"What is a blood transfusion?","options":["A process where blood and their components are administered to a client","A process of giving oral iron supplements","A method of dialysis","A surgical removal of plasma"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Blood transfusion is a process where blood and their components are administered to a client.  Why B wrong: Oral iron supplementation is not a transfusion.  Why C wrong: Dialysis is renal replacement therapy, not transfusion.  Why D wrong: Surgical plasma removal is plasmapheresis, not transfusion."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"Most common anticoagulant used for blood storage in the blood bank is which of the following?","options":["Heparin","CPDA (Citrate Phosphate Dextrose Adenine)","Warfarin","EDTA"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common anticoagulant used for blood storage in the blood bank is CPDA (Citrate Phosphate Dextrose Adenine).  Why A wrong: Heparin is not the stated storage anticoagulant for banked blood.  Why C wrong: Warfarin is an oral anticoagulant, not a blood-bank storage agent.  Why D wrong: EDTA is used for lab specimen tubes, not for blood storage in the bank."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"What is the normal haemoglobin range in males and females per the source?","options":["Males 14–16 gm/dl and females 12–14 gm/dl","Males 10–12 gm/dl and females 8–10 gm/dl","Males 18–20 gm/dl and females 16–18 gm/dl","Males 12–14 gm/dl and females 14–16 gm/dl"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Normal haemoglobin is 14–16 gm/dl in males and 12–14 gm/dl in females.  Why B/C wrong: 10–12/8–10 and 18–20/16–18 gm/dl are not the stated ranges.  Why D wrong: The values are reversed; males are higher (14–16) than females (12–14)."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"What is the normal haematocrit level in males and females per the source?","options":["Males 30–40% and females 25–35%","Males 42–52% and females 35–45%","Males 55–65% and females 50–60%","Males 20–30% and females 15–25%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Normal haematocrit level is 42–52% in males and 35–45% in females.  Why A/C/D wrong: 30–40%/25–35%, 55–65%/50–60%, and 20–30%/15–25% are not the stated haematocrit ranges."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"Each unit of blood is screened for which infections? (Select All That Apply)","options":["HIV","Hepatitis B (Hep-B)","Hepatitis C (HCV)","Syphilis","Tuberculosis"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Each unit of blood is screened for HIV, Hep-B, HCV, and syphilis.  Why E wrong: Tuberculosis is not among the listed routine blood-unit screening tests."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"Which are purposes of blood transfusion? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Restore blood volume after haemorrhage","Maintain haemoglobin level in clients with severe anaemia","Replace specific blood components","Lower the blood glucose level","Provide parenteral nutrition"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Purposes of blood transfusion: restore blood volume after haemorrhage, maintain haemoglobin level in clients with severe anaemia, and replace specific blood components.  Why D wrong: Lowering blood glucose is not a purpose of transfusion.  Why E wrong: Providing parenteral nutrition is a separate therapy (TPN), not a transfusion purpose."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"Pathological indicators of blood transfusion include which of the following? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Specific deficiencies of clotting factors such as haemophilia","Thrombocytopenia","Acute haemorrhage and severe anaemia","During surgery","Normal haemoglobin with no bleeding"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Pathological indicators of blood transfusion include acute haemorrhage, severe anaemia, during surgery, specific deficiencies of clotting factors such as haemophilia, and thrombocytopenia.  Why E wrong: Normal haemoglobin with no bleeding is not an indication for transfusion."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"Per WHO recommendations, at what temperature should red cell components (PRBCs) be transported?","options":["1 to 6 °C","20 to 24 °C","-18 to -30 °C","37 °C"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Per WHO recommendations, red cell components (PRBCs) are transported at 1–6 °C.  Why B wrong: 20–24 °C is the transport temperature for platelet components.  Why C wrong: -18 to -30 °C is the transport temperature for plasma components (FFP, cryoprecipitate).  Why D wrong: 37 °C is body temperature, not the transport temperature for PRBCs."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"Per WHO recommendations, at what temperature are plasma components (FFP, cryoprecipitate) transported?","options":["1 to 6 °C","20 to 24 °C","-18 to -30 °C","4 to 10 °C"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Per WHO recommendations, plasma components (FFP, cryoprecipitate) are transported at -18 to -30 °C (frozen).  Why A wrong: 1–6 °C is for red cell components.  Why B wrong: 20–24 °C is for platelet components.  Why D wrong: 4–10 °C is not the stated plasma transport range."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"Per WHO recommendations, at what temperature are platelet components (SDP, RDP) transported?","options":["1 to 6 °C","20 to 24 °C","-18 to -30 °C","37 to 40 °C"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Per WHO recommendations, platelet components (SDP, RDP) are transported at 20–24 °C (room temperature).  Why A wrong: 1–6 °C is for red cell components.  Why C wrong: -18 to -30 °C is for plasma components.  Why D wrong: 37–40 °C is too warm and not the stated platelet range."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"When transfusing components, how long should red cell components (PRBC) be transfused within after being taken from the blood bank refrigerator?","options":["Within 30 minutes","Within 1 hour","Within 4 hours","Within 6 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Red cell components (PRBC) should be transfused within 30 minutes after removal from the refrigerator.  Why B/C/D wrong: 1 hour, 4 hours, and 6 hours exceed the stated 30-minute window for starting/transfusing PRBCs."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"When should plasma components (FFP, cryoprecipitate) be transfused after being taken from the blood bank?","options":["Within 30 minutes","Within 4 hours","Immediately","Within 24 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Per the Should Remember table, plasma components are transfused within 4 hours after being taken from the blood bank.  Why A wrong: 30 minutes is for red cell components.  Why C wrong: \"Immediately\" applies to platelet components.  Why D wrong: 24 hours exceeds the stated 4-hour window for plasma."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"When should platelet components be transfused after being taken from the blood bank?","options":["Immediately","Within 30 minutes","Within 4 hours","Within 24 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Per the Should Remember table, platelet components should be transfused immediately.  Why B wrong: 30 minutes applies to red cell components.  Why C wrong: 4 hours applies to plasma components.  Why D wrong: 24 hours is not the stated platelet window."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"(NORDEET recall) After receiving blood in a want from a refrigerator, blood should be given within how much time?","options":["30 minutes","60 minutes","4 hours","90 minutes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The keyed answer is 30 minutes — blood (red cells) should be given within 30 minutes after being received from the refrigerator.  Why B/C/D wrong: 60 minutes, 4 hours, and 90 minutes exceed the keyed 30-minute window."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"When selecting a venipuncture site for blood transfusion, which site is preferable, and which areas should be avoided?","options":["Select a large, firm vein, preferably in the antecubital fossa; avoid areas of scars and areas from skin lesions","Select the smallest vein on the back of the hand","Use only foot veins","Use an artery for faster flow"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For venipuncture site selection, select a large, firm vein, preferably in the antecubital fossa, and avoid areas from skin lesions or scars.  Why B wrong: The smallest vein on the back of the hand is not preferred; a large firm vein is.  Why C wrong: Foot veins are not the recommended site here.  Why D wrong: Transfusion is given into a vein, never an artery."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"When applying a tourniquet or blood pressure cuff to make a vein more prominent for venipuncture, to what pressure should the cuff be inflated?","options":["40 to 60 mm Hg","60 to 80 mm Hg","80 to 100 mm Hg","100 to 120 mm Hg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Apply a tourniquet or blood pressure cuff inflated to 60–40 mm Hg (40 to 60 mm Hg) to make the vein more prominent.  Why B/C/D wrong: 60–80, 80–100, and 100–120 mm Hg exceed the stated 40–60 mm Hg pressure."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"Before puncturing the vein during venipuncture for transfusion, what should the nurse do regarding the device or tourniquet?","options":["Tighten the tourniquet maximally","Ask the donor to open and close the hand a few times","Remove all aseptic precautions","Skip cleaning the skin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Ask the donor to open and close the hand a few times to make the vein more prominent before venipuncture.  Why A wrong: Maximal tightening is not advised; the cuff is inflated to 40–60 mm Hg.  Why C wrong: Aseptic precautions must be maintained, not removed.  Why D wrong: The skin must be disinfected, not left uncleaned."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"What is the recommended one-step disinfection procedure for venipuncture skin (takes about 2 minutes)?","options":["Use a product combining 2% chlorhexidine gluconate in 70% isopropyl alcohol; cover the whole area and ensure the skin is in contact with the disinfectant for at least 30 seconds, then allow the area to dry completely for a minimum of 30 seconds to check the clock","Wipe once with plain water and proceed immediately","Use povidone iodine for 10 minutes only","Use sterile saline gauze without any antiseptic"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: One-step procedure (recommended, takes about 2 minutes): use a product combining 2% chlorhexidine gluconate in 70% isopropyl alcohol; cover the whole area and ensure the skin is in contact with the disinfectant for at least 30 seconds, then allow the area to dry completely for a minimum of 30 seconds.  Why B wrong: Plain water without antiseptic does not disinfect.  Why C wrong: The one-step recommended method uses chlorhexidine/alcohol, not povidone iodine for 10 minutes.  Why D wrong: Sterile saline gauze alone provides no antiseptic disinfection."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"In the recommended two-step disinfection procedure for venipuncture (takes about 2 minutes), what is Step 1?","options":["Use 70% isopropyl alcohol; cover the whole area and ensure the skin is in contact with the disinfectant for at least 30 seconds, then allow the area to dry completely (about 30 seconds)","Use 2% povidone iodine first","Use chlorhexidine 4% scrub for 5 minutes","Apply sterile water only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Two-step procedure Step 1: use 70% isopropyl alcohol; cover the whole area and ensure the skin is in contact with the disinfectant for at least 30 seconds, then allow the area to dry completely (about 30 seconds).  Why B wrong: Povidone iodine is Step 2, not Step 1.  Why C wrong: A 4% chlorhexidine scrub for 5 minutes is not the stated Step 1.  Why D wrong: Sterile water alone does not disinfect."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"In the recommended two-step disinfection procedure, what is Step 2?","options":["Use a tincture of iodine (more effective than povidone iodine) or chlorhexidine (2%); cover the whole area and ensure the skin area is in contact with the disinfectant for at least 30 seconds; allow the area to dry completely (about 30 seconds)","Wipe off all disinfectant with dry gauze immediately","Re-apply 70% alcohol again only","Touch the venipuncture site to confirm dryness"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Two-step procedure Step 2: use a tincture of iodine (more effective than povidone iodine) or chlorhexidine (2%); cover the whole area and ensure the skin area is in contact with the disinfectant for at least 30 seconds; allow the area to dry completely (about 30 seconds).  Why B wrong: The disinfectant must be allowed to dry, not wiped off immediately.  Why C wrong: Re-applying only alcohol is not Step 2; an iodine tincture or 2% chlorhexidine is used.  Why D wrong: The site must not be touched after disinfection."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"After preparing the venipuncture site for blood transfusion, what must the nurse remember about the disinfected skin site?","options":["It may be touched to check dryness","Whichever procedure is used, DO NOT touch the venipuncture site once the skin has been disinfected","It should be re-rubbed with the finger","It should be covered before drying"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Whichever procedure is used, DO NOT touch the venipuncture site once the skin has been disinfected (touching recontaminates the site).  Why A wrong: Touching to check dryness recontaminates and is prohibited.  Why C wrong: Re-rubbing with the finger recontaminates the prepared site.  Why D wrong: The site should be allowed to dry, not covered before drying, and not touched."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"Transport temperatures for various blood components are based on whose recommendations?","options":["CDC recommendations","WHO recommendations","FDA recommendations","ICMR recommendations"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Transport temperatures for various blood components are based on WHO recommendations.  Why A/C/D wrong: CDC, FDA, and ICMR are not the body cited; the source specifies WHO recommendations."},{"section":"Batch 025 · hypertonic dextrose solutions, colloids/volume expanders…","question":"This chapter (Chapter 11) of the unit covers which topic?","options":["Administration of Blood Products and Massive Blood Transfusion (MBT)","Wound care and dressing","Medication administration routes","Vital signs assessment"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Chapter 11 is titled \"Administration of Blood Products / Massive Blood Transfusion (MBT).\"  Why B/C/D wrong: Wound care, medication routes, and vital signs are not the title of this chapter."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"A person agrees to organ donation. Before transplantation, which action should be done as the FIRST priority? (NORCET PYQ)","options":["Consent from family member","Blood type relation","Biochemical cross-matching","Viral markers profile"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The book keys biochemical cross-matching as the priority step before transplantation to confirm donor–recipient compatibility.  Why A wrong: Family consent is part of the donation process but the keyed priority is cross-matching.  Why B wrong: Blood type relation is assessed but is not the keyed priority answer.  Why D wrong: Viral markers screening is done but is not the keyed priority answer."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"Fresh frozen plasma (FFP) should be stored at which temperature? (NORCET PYQ)","options":["2° to 10° centigrade","4° to 8° centigrade","-20° to -22° centigrade","22° to 28° centigrade"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The book keys FFP storage at -20° to -22° centigrade (FFP is frozen).  Why A wrong: 2–10°C is not the FFP frozen range.  Why B wrong: 2°–6°C/4–8°C is the refrigerated range for packed cells/whole blood, not FFP.  Why D wrong: 20–24°C (room temperature) is for platelets, not FFP."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"According to Indian criteria, blood donation can be done a maximum of how many times in a year? (WHO)","options":["One time","Two times","Three times","Four times"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Blood donation can be done a maximum of three times in a year.  Why A/B wrong: Fewer than the stated maximum.  Why D wrong: Exceeds the stated maximum of three times per year."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"What is the minimum advised interval between two blood donations per Indian criteria?","options":["1 month","2 months","3 months","6 months"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The minimum time advised between two donations is 3 months.  Why A/B wrong: Shorter than the recommended interval.  Why D wrong: Longer than the stated minimum of 3 months."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"Which is part of the universally accepted criteria for blood donor selection in India? (SATA)","options":["Age between 18 and 60 years","Haemoglobin not less than 12.5 g/dl","Pulse between 50 and 100/minute with no irregularities","Weight should not be less than 45 kg","Normal body temperature at the time of donation"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: All five are the universally accepted donor-selection criteria listed: age 18–60 years, Hb not less than 12.5 g/dl, pulse 50–100/min without irregularities, weight not less than 45 kg, normal body temperature.  All options are correct; none are wrong."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"Infection screening of a donor's blood prior to transfusion includes tests for which of the following? (SATA)","options":["VDRL","HBsAg","Anti HIV","Anti HCV","Malarial parasite"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Infection screening includes VDRL, HBsAg, Anti HIV, Anti HCV and Malarial parasite.  All options are correct."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"In addition to infection screening, which test is also performed on donor's blood prior to transfusion?","options":["Grouping and reverse grouping (cross matching)","Coombs test only","Bleeding time","Liver function test"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Grouping and reverse grouping with cross matching is done as part of pre-transfusion screening.  Why B wrong: Coombs test is not listed as the routine screening pair here.  Why C/D wrong: Bleeding time and LFT are not listed pre-transfusion screening tests."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"Which bedside check should be carried out before transfusion? (SATA)","options":["Re-check identification of the patient","Check the blood group and Rh of patient against the blood requisition form","Check expiry of blood component (PRBC 35 days, SAGM-RBCs 42 days) for an expired component","Visual inspection of the bag for colour change, visual clots, froth, leakage or signs not to be transfused","Note to add any medication to the blood bag"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Re-checking patient ID, verifying blood group/Rh against requisition, checking expiry, and visual inspection of the bag are all required bedside checks.  Why E wrong: NO medication should be added to the blood bag; this is explicitly a \"do not\" instruction, not a required check."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"During the bedside check, if a transfusion reaction occurs and the blood product must not be validated, what action does the box direct?","options":["The product should be validated by at least two people","Proper identification of the recipient and the blood product must be validated by at least two people","The reaction should be ignored","The blood should be re-warmed and re-issued"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Proper identification of the recipient and the blood product must be validated by at least two people.  Why A wrong: The product itself isn't \"validated\"; identification is, by two persons.  Why C/D wrong: Reactions are never ignored and blood is not re-issued after a reaction."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"While preparing the steps for transfusion, with what should the IV line be primed/flushed before and after the blood transfusion?","options":["0.9% Normal Saline (NaCl)","Dextrose 5% in water","Ringer Lactate","Dextrose Normal Saline"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Only isotonic NaCl (0.9% NS) is the compatible solution; the line should be primed and flushed with NS before and after transfusion.  Why B wrong: Dextrose 5% causes hemolysis (RBCs swell and lyse).  Why C wrong: Ringer Lactate contains calcium which can cause clotting in the line.  Why D wrong: Dextrose-containing solutions are incompatible with blood products."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"Which IV solutions are INCOMPATIBLE with blood transfusion and should NOT be used? (SATA)","options":["Dextrose 5% in water","Ringer Lactate solution (contains calcium)","0.9% Normal Saline","Dextrose 5% Normal Saline","Hypotonic dextrose solutions"],"correct":"A,B,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Dextrose causes hemolysis of red cells, and Ringer Lactate contains calcium that triggers clotting; all dextrose-containing/hypotonic solutions are incompatible.  Why C wrong: 0.9% Normal Saline is the only compatible isotonic solution."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"Within what time should a blood transfusion be completed once started, and what is the maximum hang time recommended?","options":["Within 4 hours (240 minutes)","Within 6 hours","Within 8 hours","Within 2 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A blood transfusion should be completed within 4 hours (240 minutes) of starting.  Why B/C wrong: Exceed the 4-hour safe hang time, increasing bacterial growth risk.  Why D wrong: 2 hours is the FFP infusion-after-thawing window, not the maximum transfusion completion time."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"Why must transfusion be started within 30 minutes of removing blood from the blood bank refrigerator?","options":["To allow it to warm at room temperature so the patient does not get shock","To prevent the patient from spontaneous bleeding","To prevent clot formation only","To increase oxygen-carrying capacity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Transfusion should start within 30 minutes of removal so the blood does not warm up excessively (risk of hemolysis/bacterial growth); the patient should be observed for reactions.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the reason given for the 30-minute window."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"What is the size and type of filter that should be used when transfusing blood to remove blood clots and other debris?","options":["Filter of 170–200 micron","0.22 micron filter","No filter is required","5 micron filter"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A 170–200 micron filter is used to remove blood clots and debris during transfusion.  Why B wrong: 0.22 micron is for sterilizing/IV medication filtration, too fine for blood.  Why C wrong: A filter is required for blood transfusion.  Why D wrong: 5 micron is not the standard blood-administration filter size given."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"During transfusion, when monitoring vitals, at which frequency should the nurse observe the patient/record vitals according to the book?","options":["Every 5 minutes for first 15 minutes, then every 15 minutes for first hour, then every 30 minutes, then hourly until completion","Once before and once after only","Every hour throughout","Continuously with no documentation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book lists graded frequency: every 5 minutes for the first 15 minutes, every 15 minutes for the next hour, then every 30 minutes, then hourly until transfusion reaction risk decreases/completes.  Why B/C/D wrong: None match the graded monitoring schedule described."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"If a transfusion reaction is suspected during transfusion, what is the FIRST nursing action?","options":["Speed up the transfusion","Stop the transfusion immediately","Administer oxygen first","Inform the relatives first"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In case of any transfusion reaction, stop the transfusion immediately, keep the line open with slow normal saline infusion, and inform the doctor.  Why A wrong: Speeding up worsens the reaction.  Why C wrong: Oxygen may follow but stopping the transfusion is first.  Why D wrong: Notifying the physician is required, not the relatives first."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"After transfusion, what should be done with the empty blood bag and the giving set/transfusion reaction record?","options":["Discard the bag immediately in general waste","Return the bag (with giving set) to the laboratory/blood bank as it should be attached to the patient file and transfusion reactions recorded","Reuse the bag for the next unit","Send the bag home with the patient"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After transfusion the bag should be filled up and returned/attached to the patient file; transfusion reactions should also be reported to the blood bank.  Why A/C/D wrong: Improper and unsafe handling of blood products."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"Match the blood component to its primary clinical use: Red Blood Cells are transfused to —","options":["Increase the amount of red blood cells after trauma or surgery or to treat severe anaemia","Correct deficiency in coagulation factors","Treat or prevent bleeding due to low platelet levels","Treat fibrinogen deficiencies"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: RBCs increase red cell mass after trauma/surgery or in severe anaemia.  Why B wrong: That is the use of Fresh Frozen Plasma.  Why C wrong: That describes platelet concentrate.  Why D wrong: That describes cryoprecipitate."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"Fresh Frozen Plasma (FFP) is transfused primarily to —","options":["Correct a deficiency in coagulation factors or treat shock due to plasma loss from burns or massive bleeding","Increase red cell mass","Correct low platelet count","Correct fibrinogen deficiency only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: FFP corrects coagulation factor deficiencies and treats shock from plasma loss (burns/massive bleeding).  Why B wrong: That is RBC use.  Why C wrong: That is platelet concentrate use.  Why D wrong: Isolated fibrinogen deficiency is treated with cryoprecipitate."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"Concentrate of platelets is used to —","options":["Treat or prevent bleeding due to low platelet levels and correct functional platelet problems","Replace clotting factors","Increase haemoglobin","Treat fibrinogen deficiency"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Platelet concentrate treats/prevents bleeding from low platelet levels and corrects functional platelet defects.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe FFP, RBCs, and cryoprecipitate respectively."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"Cryoprecipitate is mainly used to treat —","options":["Fibrinogen deficiencies","Anaemia","Hypovolemia","Iron overload"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cryoprecipitate is used to treat fibrinogen deficiencies.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not indications for cryoprecipitate."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"What is the storage period of Red Blood Cells (packed cells)?","options":["42 days in the refrigerator (or 10 years if frozen)","5 days at room temperature","1 year in the freezer","24 hours only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: RBCs store for 42 days in the refrigerator, or 10 years when frozen.  Why B wrong: 5 days at room temperature is platelet storage.  Why C wrong: 1 year in the freezer is FFP/cryoprecipitate storage.  Why D wrong: Not the stated RBC storage period."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"What is the storage period of platelet concentrate?","options":["5 days at room temperature","42 days in the refrigerator","1 year in the freezer","6 hours only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Platelets are stored 5 days at room temperature (with agitation).  Why B/C wrong: These are RBC and FFP/cryoprecipitate storage durations.  Why D wrong: Not the platelet storage period."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"What is the storage period of Fresh Frozen Plasma and cryoprecipitate in the freezer?","options":["1 year in the freezer","42 days","5 days","24 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: FFP and cryoprecipitate are stored for 1 year in the freezer.  Why B/C/D wrong: These correspond to RBCs, platelets, and thawed-product limits, not freezer storage of FFP."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"What is the approximate volume of one unit of Packed Red Blood Cells (PRBCs), exclusive of any additive solution?","options":["240 ml","350–450 ml","50 ml","100 ml"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The approximate PRBC volume (exclusive of additive solution) is 240 ml.  Why B wrong: 350–450 ml is the approximate volume of whole blood.  Why C/D wrong: Not the stated PRBC unit volume."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"What percentage of original red cells is stored when PRBCs are kept in CPDA-1 solution versus SAGM/Adsol solution?","options":["65–70% in CPDA-1; 50–60% in SAGM/Adsol","90% in both","30% in CPDA-1; 70% in SAGM","100% in CPDA-1"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: 65–70% of red cells are stored when in CPDA-1, and 50–60% when stored in additive (SAGM/Adsol) solution.  Why B/C/D wrong: Do not match the stated retention percentages."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"What is the storage temperature for PRBCs in a monitored blood refrigerator?","options":["2°C to 6°C","-20°C to -22°C","20°C to 24°C","-30°C"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: PRBC storage temperature is 2°C to 6°C in a monitored blood refrigerator.  Why B wrong: -20° to -22°C is FFP.  Why C wrong: 20–24°C is platelet storage.  Why D wrong: -30°C is the freezing range for FFP/cryoprecipitate freezing, not refrigerated PRBC storage."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"What is the maximum storage time of PRBCs (range up to 35–42 days), and what is the most common anticoagulant/preservative used for PRBCs?","options":["Up to 35–42 days; CPDA (citrate phosphate dextrose adenine)","Up to 5 days; heparin","1 year; EDTA","24 hours; sodium citrate only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: PRBCs store up to 35–42 days; CPDA is the most common anticoagulant/preservative used for PRBCs.  Why B/C/D wrong: Incorrect durations and anticoagulants for PRBCs."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"By how much should each PRBC unit increase the recipient's haemoglobin (Hb) and hematocrit (HCT)?","options":["Each PRBC unit increases Hb by 1 g/dl and HCT by 3%","Each unit increases Hb by 5 g/dl","Each unit increases HCT by 30%","No measurable change"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Each PRBC unit increases Hb level by about 1 g/dl and HCT by 3%; changes are best seen 4 hours after transfusion.  Why B/C/D wrong: Do not match the stated per-unit increments."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"A nurse is preparing platelet transfusion. Which statement about platelets is TRUE? (SATA)","options":["Usually cross-matching is NOT required for platelets","Platelets should be given rapidly within 15–30 minutes","Platelets are administered immediately upon receipt from the blood bank","Platelet components are required for patients with bleeding due to thrombocytopenia (e.g., dengue fever)","Sodium citrate is the anticoagulant used for platelet storage"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Cross-matching is usually not required for platelets; they should be given rapidly within 15–30 minutes; administered immediately on receipt; indicated for bleeding from thrombocytopenia (e.g., dengue); and sodium citrate is the anticoagulant for storage.  All options are correct as per the page."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"What is the storage temperature for platelets and how long can they be stored?","options":["20°C to 24°C (or 70° to 75°F) for up to 5 days","2°C to 6°C for 42 days","-20°C for 1 year","4°C for 24 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Platelets are stored at 20°C to 24°C (70–75°F) for up to 5 days, usually with gentle agitation.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are RBC, FFP, and incorrect storage parameters."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"In a 60–70 kg adult, what is the typical dosing of platelets and expected platelet count rise?","options":["1 unit of Random Donor Platelets (RDP) per 10 kg; 4–6 single donor units containing at least 240 × 10⁹ platelets should raise the platelet count by 20–40 × 10⁹/L","1 unit per 50 kg with no measurable rise","10 units regardless of weight","Platelets do not raise the count"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Dosing is 1 unit RDP per 10 kg; in a 60–70 kg adult, 4–6 single donor units (≥240 × 10⁹ platelets) raise the count by about 20–40 × 10⁹/L.  Why B/C/D wrong: Do not match the stated dosing/expected rise."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"After transfusion of one unit of Single Donor Platelets (SDP), platelet count is expected to increase by approximately —","options":["5000–10000 cells/mm³","30000–50000 cells/mm³","1000–2000 cells/mm³","100000 cells/mm³"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After 1 unit of SDP, the platelet count increases approximately 30000–50000 cells/mm³ (one SDP ≈ multiple RDP units); whereas per RDP unit it increases ~5000–10000/mm³.  Why A wrong: 5000–10000 is the per-RDP-unit rise, not per SDP.  Why C/D wrong: Not the stated SDP-related increment."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"What are the contraindications for platelet transfusion? (SATA)","options":["Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (TTP) — absolute contraindication","Idiopathic autoimmune thrombocytopenic purpura (ITP)","Untreated DIC","Thrombocytopenia associated with septicaemia","Hypersplenism"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: TTP (absolute), ITP, untreated DIC, thrombocytopenia with septicaemia, and hypersplenism are all listed contraindications to platelet transfusion.  All options are contraindications."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"Within how many hours of thawing should fresh frozen plasma (FFP) be administered?","options":["Within 2 hours of thawing","Within 6 hours of thawing","Within 24 hours of thawing","Within 30 minutes of thawing"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: FFP should be administered within 2 hours of thawing.  Why B/C/D wrong: Do not match the stated thawing window for FFP administration."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"How long can FFP be stored once thawed if it has not been used immediately?","options":["Up to 24 hours at refrigerated temperature","Up to 1 year","5 days","It cannot be stored at all"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Once thawed, if cross-matched and not used, FFP can be stored up to 24 hours (refrigerated).  Why B wrong: 1 year applies to frozen FFP, not thawed.  Why C/D wrong: Incorrect storage durations for thawed FFP."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"At what temperature is FFP frozen for long-term storage and what is its shelf life when frozen?","options":["-30°C or -22°C, with shelf life up to 1 year","2°C to 6°C for 42 days","20°C to 24°C for 5 days","4°C for 24 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: FFP is frozen at -30°C (or -22°C per book) with a shelf life of up to 1 year; it contains all clotting factors and electrolytes.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are RBC, platelet, and incorrect parameters."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"What is the recommended dosage of FFP?","options":["10–15 ml/kg of recipient weight","1 ml/kg","50 ml/kg","100 ml total"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: FFP dosage is 10–15 ml/kg of recipient body weight.  Why B/C/D wrong: Do not match the stated FFP dose."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"Which are indications for FFP transfusion? (SATA)","options":["Patient with massive blood loss or severe hypovolemia/trauma","Multiple coagulation factor deficiency","DIC with active bleeding","Immediate correction of Vitamin K deficiency and warfarin reversal","Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: All five (massive blood loss/hypovolemia, multiple coagulation factor deficiency, DIC with active bleeding, vitamin K deficiency/warfarin reversal, and TTP) are listed FFP indications, along with patient with congenital factor deficiency when no alternative therapies are available.  All options are indications."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"In which condition is FFP transfusion NOT indicated? (SATA)","options":["For volume replacement in patients who are not bleeding","For nutritional purposes like hypoproteinemia","FFP transfusion in invasive procedures with normal/mildly raised INR","FFP transfusion in a patient with a bleeding disorder and normal coagulation tests","FFP transfusion in DIC with active bleeding"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: FFP is NOT indicated for volume replacement in non-bleeding patients, nutritional/hypoproteinemia correction, invasive procedures with normal/mildly raised INR, or patients with normal coagulation tests.  Why E wrong: DIC with active bleeding IS an indication for FFP, so it is not a \"not indicated\" condition."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"A traumatic patient is brought to the emergency department with loss of massive blood volume and coagulation factors. Which blood product should be prescribed? (NORCET PYQ)","options":["Cryoprecipitate","PRBC","FFP","Platelets"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: FFP replaces lost coagulation factors and treats shock from massive blood loss, so it is the keyed product (Right Answer B in the book keys \"FFP,\" which is option C here; the factually correct choice is FFP for coagulation-factor loss).  Why A wrong: Cryoprecipitate corrects only fibrinogen/specific factors, not the full coagulation deficit.  Why B wrong: PRBC replaces red cells, not coagulation factors.  Why D wrong: Platelets address thrombocytopenia, not coagulation factor loss."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"A patient is to receive whole blood. Which describes whole blood correctly? (SATA)","options":["It contains all components: RBC, WBC, platelets and plasma proteins","Approximate volume is 350–360 ml (including the anticoagulant/preservative and acceptable variation of 13%)","Transfusion time is 4 hours (240 minutes)","Storage temperature is 2°C to 6°C in a monitored blood refrigerator","Haematocrit is 30–40% and cross-match is required"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Whole blood contains all components; approximate volume 350–360 ml (including anticoagulant); transfusion time 4 hours/240 minutes; stored at 2–6°C in a monitored refrigerator; haematocrit 30–40% with required cross-match.  All options are correct per the page."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"What are the indications for whole blood transfusion? (SATA)","options":["Exchange transfusion in neonates","When PRBCs are not available, whole blood can be used to manage hypovolemia (e.g., battlefield injuries, road traffic injuries)","Routine correction of anaemia in stable patients","Treatment of fibrinogen deficiency only"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: Indications include exchange transfusion in neonates, and using whole blood to manage hypovolemia when PRBCs are unavailable (battlefield/RTA injuries).  Why C wrong: Stable chronic anaemia is corrected with PRBCs, not whole blood.  Why D wrong: Fibrinogen deficiency is treated with cryoprecipitate."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"A nurse client is involved in a motor vehicle accident earlier in the day and is caring for him on subsequent days. Which blood product should the nurse anticipate to manage hypovolemia in this emergency? (NORCET PYQ)","options":["Whole blood","Fresh frozen plasma","Platelets","Packed red blood cells"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Whole blood is the transfusion component of choice when large volumes of blood need to be replaced (acute trauma/hypovolemia), as keyed (Right Answer A).  Why B wrong: FFP replaces coagulation factors, not volume primarily.  Why C wrong: Platelets manage thrombocytopenic bleeding.  Why D wrong: PRBCs raise red cell mass but do not replace whole-blood volume as broadly."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"When should fresh frozen plasma (FFP) be transfused relative to thawing in a patient with prothrombin and partial thromboplastin time abnormalities and active bleeding?","options":["Within 15,000/mm³ platelet count threshold","Immediately after thawing because it loses concentration over time","After 24 hours of refrigeration","Only when count is below 15,000/mm³"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Per the page, fresh frozen plasma (FFP) is transfused immediately upon thawing because clotting factor activity decreases with delay.  Why A/D wrong: These reference platelet count thresholds, not the FFP timing rationale.  Why C wrong: Delaying 24 hours reduces clotting factor efficacy."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"Packed cells (PRBCs) are transfused in which instances? (SATA)","options":["Anaemia","Decrease in haemoglobin and haematocrit","To raise fibrinogen levels","Cases requiring increased oxygen-carrying capacity from red cells"],"correct":"A,B,D","rationale":"Why correct: PRBCs are transfused in anaemia, decreased Hb/HCT, and to improve oxygen-carrying capacity.  Why C wrong: Raising fibrinogen is the role of cryoprecipitate, not packed cells."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"Cryoprecipitate is derived from which blood component?","options":["Fresh frozen plasma (FFP)","Packed red cells","Whole platelets","Whole blood buffy coat only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cryoprecipitate is a blood component derived from FFP and has a relatively high concentration of clotting factors (Factor VIII, von Willebrand factor [vWF], Factor XIII and fibrinogen).  Why B/C/D wrong: Cryoprecipitate is specifically a cold-precipitated fraction of plasma/FFP, not from these."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"What is the approximate volume of one bag of cryoprecipitate, and is cross-match required?","options":["10–20 ml; cross-match not required","240 ml; cross-match required","350 ml; cross-match required","5 ml; cross-match required"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cryoprecipitate has an approximate volume of 10–20 ml and cross-match is not required.  Why B/C/D wrong: Do not match the stated cryoprecipitate volume/cross-match status."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"What are the storage conditions and transfusion time for cryoprecipitate?","options":["Stored below -30°C; can be stored for up to a maximum of 6 hours after thawing if not immediately transfused; transfuse in 20–30 minutes","Stored at 2–6°C for 42 days","Stored at room temperature for 5 days","Stored at 4°C for 1 year"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cryoprecipitate storage is below -30°C; can be stored up to a maximum of 6 hours after thawing if not transfused immediately; transfused in 20–30 minutes.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are RBC, platelet, and incorrect parameters."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"What are the indications for cryoprecipitate transfusion? (SATA)","options":["Haemophilia A","Von Willebrand disease","Congenital or acquired fibrinogen deficiency","Acquired factor VIII deficiency","Factor XIII deficiency"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: All five (Haemophilia A, Von Willebrand disease, congenital/acquired fibrinogen deficiency, acquired Factor VIII deficiency, and Factor XIII deficiency) are listed indications.  All options are indications."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"Which statement best defines colloids as plasma expanders?","options":["Colloids are large molecules so they do not enter the cells","Colloids are small molecules that freely enter cells","Colloids contain no protein and are pure crystalloid","Colloids are derivatives of glucose"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Colloids are large molecules so they do NOT enter into the cells; they are derivatives of plasma protein or albumin.  Why B wrong: Their large size prevents free entry into cells.  Why C wrong: Colloids are protein-based (plasma protein/albumin).  Why D wrong: They derive from plasma protein/albumin, not glucose."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"What are the indications for colloid use? (SATA)","options":["In an emergency to increase the blood volume","When the patient is required to give fluid quickly into the vascular compartment","To replace fluid loss from the interstitial compartment","When a client is expensive (requires short duration) and pulls fluid from the interstitial compartment into the vascular compartment"],"correct":"A,B,D","rationale":"Why correct: Colloids are indicated in emergencies to increase blood volume, when fluid is needed quickly in the vascular compartment, and they pull fluid from the interstitial compartment into the vascular space; they are expensive and used for short duration.  Why C wrong: Colloids draw fluid INTO the vascular compartment from the interstitium; they do not replace interstitial fluid loss."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"According to the Parkland formula given, fluid requirement in burn patients is calculated as —","options":["4 ml × TBSA × body weight","2 ml × TBSA × body weight","4 ml × body weight only","1 ml × TBSA"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Required amount of colloids/fluid in burn patients is calculated using the Parkland formula: 4 ml × TBSA × body weight.  Why B/C/D wrong: These misstate the Parkland formula multipliers/variables."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"According to the human blood groups antigen/antibody table, which blood group is the universal recipient?","options":["AB positive","O negative","A positive","B negative"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The universal blood donor is O negative and the universal recipient is AB positive.  Why B wrong: O negative is the universal donor, not recipient.  Why C/D wrong: A and B groups are neither universal donor nor recipient."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"Blood group O (antigen: none; antibodies: anti-A and anti-B) can give blood to which groups and can receive from which?","options":["Can give to A, B, AB, O; can receive only from O","Can give only to AB; can receive from all","Can give to AB only; can receive only from A","Can give to A only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Per the table, group O has no antigen with anti-A and anti-B antibodies, can give to A, B, AB and O (universal donor), and can receive only from O.  Why B/C/D wrong: Contradict the universal-donor / O-only-recipient pattern in the table."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"Group AB (antigens A & B; no antibodies) can receive blood from which groups?","options":["A, B, AB and O (all groups)","AB only","O only","A only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Group AB has both A and B antigens and no antibodies, making it the universal recipient — it can receive from A, B, AB and O.  Why B/C/D wrong: Underestimate the universal-recipient compatibility."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"What is required when warming blood for transfusion, per the \"Should Remember\" note?","options":["Blood warming is necessary; do not use warming devices, do not warm blood products in a microwave oven or in hot water","Blood must always be warmed in a microwave","Blood should be boiled before use","Blood is warmed only in hot tap water"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: When blood warming is necessary, only proper warming devices should be used; do NOT warm blood products in a microwave oven or in hot water.  Why B/C/D wrong: Microwave, boiling, and hot water cause hemolysis and are explicitly contraindicated."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"Which are recognised types of blood donation? (SATA)","options":["Autologous — client's own blood","Allogenic — blood collected from healthy donor (other than the patient)","Designated — donor of choice, donor can donate every 3 months","Microwave donation"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Types of blood donation include autologous (client's own blood), allogenic (blood from a healthy donor other than the patient), and designated (most common, a donor can donate every 3 months).  Why D wrong: \"Microwave donation\" is not a type of donation; it is a fabricated distractor."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"Complications of blood transfusion include immune-mediated and non-immune-mediated reactions. Which are NON-immune-mediated complications? (SATA)","options":["Sepsis","Volume overload","Metabolic abuses (electrolyte disturbances)","Hypocalcemia","Hemolytic reaction"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Non-immune-mediated complications include sepsis, volume (circulatory) overload, metabolic abnormalities/electrolyte disturbances, hypocalcemia, hyperkalemia, air embolism, infection (disease transmission), and iron overload (delayed).  Why E wrong: Hemolytic reaction is an immune-mediated reaction (along with febrile, allergic, TRALI, and post-transfusion purpura)."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"Which are IMMUNE-mediated transfusion complications? (SATA)","options":["Hemolytic reaction","Febrile reaction","Allergic reaction","Transfusion-Related Acute Lung Injury (TRALI)","Post-transfusion purpura"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Immune-mediated reactions listed are hemolytic, febrile, allergic, TRALI, and post-transfusion purpura.  All options are immune-mediated per the table."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"Per the transfusion rate/time table, what is the maximum time to transfuse one unit of whole blood?","options":["240 minutes (4 hours)","30 minutes","6 hours","60 minutes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Whole blood is transfused at 150–200 ml/hour with a maximum time to transfuse of 240 minutes (4 hours).  Why B wrong: 30 minutes (0.5 hour) is the maximum time for platelets/cryoprecipitate.  Why C/D wrong: Do not match the stated whole-blood transfusion limit."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"Per the transfusion table, what is the maximum time to transfuse platelets (RDP/SDP) and cryoprecipitate?","options":["30 minutes (0.5 hours)","240 minutes","120 minutes","60 minutes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Platelets (RDP/SDP) at 150–300 ml/hour and cryoprecipitate at 150–200 ml/hour have a maximum transfusion time of 30 minutes (0.5 hours).  Why B wrong: 240 minutes is whole blood/packed cells.  Why C wrong: 120 minutes (2 hours) is FFP.  Why D wrong: Not the stated maximum for platelets/cryoprecipitate."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"Per the transfusion table, FFP is transfused over a maximum time of —","options":["120 minutes (2 hours)","240 minutes","30 minutes","24 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: FFP is given at 150–300 ml/hour with a maximum transfusion time of 120 minutes (2 hours).  Why B/C/D wrong: These correspond to whole blood/packed cells, platelets, and storage times respectively."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"A transfusion reaction such as fever, chills, sudden hypotension, chest tightness, allergic rashes, cola-coloured urine, and jaundice occurs. What is the FIRST nursing intervention?","options":["Inform to duty doctor first","Stop the transfusion immediately","Speed up the transfusion","Place the client in upright position"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: When signs/symptoms of a transfusion reaction appear, the first nursing action is to stop the transfusion immediately, then inform the duty doctor and maintain IV access with normal saline.  Why A wrong: Notifying the doctor follows stopping the transfusion.  Why C wrong: Speeding worsens the reaction.  Why D wrong: Positioning depends on the reaction type and is not the first action."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"Septicaemia as a transfusion complication occurs due to failure to maintain sterile technique, and presents with which signs/symptoms? (SATA)","options":["Chills","Fever","Vomiting","Diarrhoea","Hypotension and shock"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Septicaemia occurs when the transfused blood is contaminated with microorganisms; signs include chills, fever, vomiting, diarrhoea, hypotension and shock.  All options are correct."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"A patient develops septicaemia from a contaminated blood unit. What is the FIRST nursing action?","options":["Inform to duty doctor (first priority), take blood for culture from bag and patient, and administer oxygen/antibiotics/IV fluids as per order","Continue the transfusion slowly","Give a hot water bath","Discard the blood bag immediately without culture"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The nursing care directs informing the duty doctor as first priority, taking blood for culture from both the bag and patient, and administering oxygen, antibiotics and IV fluids per order.  Why B wrong: Transfusion must be stopped, not continued.  Why C wrong: Not an appropriate intervention.  Why D wrong: The bag is needed for culture and must not be discarded."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"Iron overload as a transfusion complication occurs in which patients and presents with which signs?","options":["It occurs with delayed transfusion in clients who receive multiple transfusions, presenting with vomiting, diarrhoea, and hypotension with low Hb","It occurs only after a single transfusion with high Hb","It presents with hypertension and high Hb only","It never causes symptoms"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Iron overload is a delayed complication in clients receiving multiple transfusions; signs include vomiting, diarrhoea, and hypotension with low Hb.  Why B/C/D wrong: Contradict the multiple-transfusion delayed nature and the listed symptoms."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"What is the nursing management/treatment for iron overload from transfusion?","options":["Administer deferoxamine (antidote of iron overload) IV and SC, and instruct the client that urine colour may become red in colour after injection of deferoxamine","Restrict all fluids","Give oral iron supplements","Perform immediate dialysis routinely"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Iron overload is managed with deferoxamine (iron chelator/antidote) given IV and SC; the client should be told the urine colour may turn red after the injection.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the keyed management for iron overload."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"Hypocalcemia from massive transfusion is usually caused by which mechanism?","options":["Citrate in transfused blood binds to calcium and is excreted from the body through urine, resulting in low serum calcium (hypocalcemia)","Excess calcium intake","Vitamin D toxicity","Renal calcium reabsorption increase"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Citrate (anticoagulant) in transfused blood binds calcium and is excreted in urine, causing low serum calcium (hypocalcemia).  Why B/C/D wrong: These would raise or not lower serum calcium; they contradict the citrate-binding mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"Which are signs of hypocalcemia to monitor during/after transfusion? (SATA)","options":["Hyperactive reflexes and paresthesia","Tetany muscles and cramps","Positive Trousseau's sign and positive Chvostek's sign","Hypertension only"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Hypocalcemia presents with hyperactive reflexes, paresthesia, tetany/muscle cramps, and positive Trousseau's and Chvostek's signs.  Why D wrong: Hypertension alone is not a listed sign of hypocalcemia here."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"What is the nursing care for hypocalcemia during transfusion?","options":["Slow down the transfusion (first priority) then inform the duty doctor for further order","Speed up the transfusion","Give IV calcium gluconate without orders","Stop all monitoring"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing care for hypocalcemia is to slow the transfusion as first priority, then inform the duty doctor for further orders.  Why B wrong: Speeding worsens citrate-induced hypocalcemia.  Why C wrong: Calcium gluconate is given only on physician's order.  Why D wrong: Monitoring must continue."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"What causes hyperkalemia during transfusion of older/stored blood?","options":["Older blood may haemolyse, releasing intracellular potassium, so potassium moves from inside the cell (ICF) to outside (ECF), raising serum potassium","Excess oral potassium intake","Renal failure only","Loss of potassium in urine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Older blood components release potassium as cells lyse; potassium moves from ICF to ECF, raising serum potassium (hyperkalemia).  Why B/C/D wrong: Do not match the haemolysis/cell-lysis mechanism described for stored blood."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"What is the risk factor for transfusion-related hyperkalemia?","options":["Older blood components, as they are likely to release more potassium from blood lysis","Fresh blood components only","Use of washed cells","Rapid infusion of normal saline"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The risk factor is older blood components, which release more potassium from blood (cell) lysis.  Why B/C/D wrong: Fresh/washed cells reduce potassium load and NS does not raise potassium."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"Hyperkalemia from transfusion is more likely in clients with which condition, and what should be assessed before and after transfusion? (SATA)","options":["Renal disease","Assess serum potassium level before and after the transfusion","Monitor signs of hyperkalemia (weakness, abdominal cramps and diarrhoea, cardiac dysrhythmia)","Administer potassium supplements routinely"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Renal disease increases risk; serum potassium should be assessed before and after transfusion; signs to monitor include weakness, abdominal cramps/diarrhoea, and cardiac dysrhythmia.  Why D wrong: Giving potassium supplements would worsen hyperkalemia and is not indicated."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"What is the FIRST nursing action when hyperkalemia is suspected during a transfusion?","options":["Slow the transfusion (first priority) then inform the doctor","Increase the transfusion rate","Give IV potassium","Discontinue all monitoring"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing care: slow the transfusion as first priority, then inform the doctor.  Why B wrong: Increasing rate worsens potassium load.  Why C wrong: IV potassium is contraindicated in hyperkalemia.  Why D wrong: Monitoring must continue."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"Citrate toxicity during transfusion occurs because citrate —","options":["Is an anticoagulant used in blood products and is metabolised by the liver; with multiple units transfused, treatment includes slowing/stopping the transfusion and metabolising the citrate","Is a clotting promoter","Increases serum calcium","Is excreted unchanged by the lungs"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Citrate is an anticoagulant in blood products metabolised by the liver; citrate toxicity occurs with multiple units transfused; treatment includes slowing/stopping the transfusion to allow the citrate to be metabolised.  Why B wrong: Citrate is an anticoagulant, not a clotting promoter.  Why C wrong: Citrate binds calcium, lowering serum calcium.  Why D wrong: It is metabolised by the liver, not excreted by the lungs."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"Citrate is metabolised into which substance, especially when multiple units of blood are transfused, and what can this lead to?","options":["Sodium bicarbonate, leading to metabolic alkalosis","Lactic acid, leading to metabolic acidosis","Carbon dioxide only","Urea"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Citrate is metabolised into sodium bicarbonate, especially with multiple units transfused, which can lead to metabolic alkalosis.  Why B/C/D wrong: Do not match the bicarbonate/alkalosis pathway described."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"Hyperkalemia from transfusion may also be related to hyperkalemia of hyperkalemia monitoring. Which sign should the nurse assess that indicates rising potassium? (SATA)","options":["Weakness","Abdominal cramps and diarrhoea","Cardiac dysrhythmia","Constipation only"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Signs of hyperkalemia include weakness, abdominal cramps and diarrhoea, and cardiac dysrhythmia.  Why D wrong: Constipation alone is not the listed hyperkalemia sign; diarrhoea/cramps are described instead."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"Match the transfusion reaction to its key clinical features — a Haemolytic Reaction usually occurs and presents how?","options":["Usually occurs within 15 minutes; features include shivering, headache, flank pain, increased pulse and respiratory rate, haemoglobinuria, oliguria, signs of shock, and renal failure","Usually occurs within 30 minutes; features chills, fever, muscle stiffness only","Usually occurs during transfusion till the end; features hives, wheezing, flushing, pruritis and joint pain","Occurs only after 24 hours with no symptoms"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Haemolytic reaction usually occurs within 15 minutes with shivering, headache, flank pain, increased pulse and respiratory rate, haemoglobinuria, oliguria, signs of shock and renal failure.  Why B wrong: That describes a febrile reaction.  Why C wrong: That describes an allergic reaction.  Why D wrong: Haemolytic reaction is early (within 15 minutes), not delayed/asymptomatic."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"A Febrile transfusion reaction usually occurs within what time and presents with which clinical features?","options":["Within 30 minutes; chills, fever, and muscle stiffness","Within 15 minutes; haemoglobinuria and shock","After 24 hours; hives and pruritus","Within 5 minutes; flank pain"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Febrile reaction usually occurs within 30 minutes with chills, fever, and muscle stiffness.  Why B wrong: That describes a haemolytic reaction.  Why C wrong: That describes an allergic reaction.  Why D wrong: Flank pain is a haemolytic-reaction feature."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"An Allergic transfusion reaction occurs when and presents with which clinical features?","options":["Usually occurs during transfusion (till the end); features include hives, wheezing, flushing, pruritus and joint pain","Within 15 minutes; haemoglobinuria and oliguria","Within 30 minutes; muscle stiffness only","After 7 days; renal failure"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Allergic reaction usually occurs during the transfusion (until the end) with hives, wheezing, flushing, pruritus and joint pain.  Why B wrong: That describes haemolytic reaction.  Why C wrong: That describes febrile reaction.  Why D wrong: Not the timing/features of an allergic reaction."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"When any transfusion reaction occurs, what should be done with the IV line/cannula and the maintaining solution as a priority nursing action?","options":["Stop the transfusion immediately, inform the duty doctor (second priority), replace the IV tubing with new tubing/IV that contained that blood, and maintain patency of IV tubing with only 0.9% Normal Saline to maintain systolic BP","Continue using the same tubing with dextrose","Remove the IV access completely","Flush with Ringer Lactate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Priority actions are to stop the transfusion immediately, inform the duty doctor, replace the contaminated IV tubing, and maintain patency with only 0.9% Normal Saline (do not administer other fluids except NS during the reaction unless ordered).  Why B wrong: Dextrose is incompatible and the contaminated tubing must be replaced.  Why C wrong: IV access is maintained (not removed) for emergency drugs/NS.  Why D wrong: Ringer Lactate (calcium-containing) is incompatible with blood."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"Per the priority nursing actions, how should the IV cannula and frequency of vitals be managed during a transfusion reaction? (SATA)","options":["Maintain patency of IV tubing with only 0.9% Normal Saline","Monitor vitals alone; never re-check","Maintain airway and give high-flow oxygen by mask","Do not leave the patient alone; monitor vitals and stay with the client for the first 15 minutes of transfusion"],"correct":"A,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Maintain IV patency with only 0.9% NS, maintain airway and provide high-flow oxygen by mask, and never leave the patient alone — stay and monitor vitals frequently during the reaction/first 15 minutes.  Why B wrong: Vitals are re-checked frequently, not monitored once; this contradicts the close-monitoring instruction."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"During a severe anaphylactic transfusion reaction not controlled by corticosteroids, what drug and dose does the book state should be given?","options":["Adrenaline 1:1000 solution, 0.01 mg/kg body weight by slow intramuscular injection","Adrenaline 1:10000, 1 mg IV push","Frusemide 1 mg/kg IV","Hydrocortisone 100 mg orally"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Adrenaline (1:1000 solution) 0.01 mg/kg body weight is given by slow IM injection for a severe anaphylactic reaction not controlled by corticosteroids.  Why B wrong: That misstates the concentration/route for this indication.  Why C wrong: Frusemide 1 mg/kg IV is used for circulatory overload, not anaphylaxis.  Why D wrong: Hydrocortisone orally is not the keyed anaphylaxis drug/route here."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"A client with circulatory (volume) overload during transfusion is managed with diuretic therapy. Which diuretic and dose is indicated per the page?","options":["Frusemide 1 mg/kg IV","Adrenaline 0.01 mg/kg IM","Deferoxamine IV","Calcium gluconate IV"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Frusemide 1 mg/kg IV is given for circulatory/volume overload to remove excess fluid.  Why B wrong: Adrenaline is for anaphylaxis.  Why C wrong: Deferoxamine is for iron overload.  Why D wrong: Calcium gluconate is for hypocalcemia/citrate toxicity."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"A new nurse is caring for an adolescent in the trauma unit who has injuries secondary to a motor vehicle accident. After 30 minutes, the client complains of nausea, headache, chills and muscle stiffness. An open new nurse should determine that the new nurse intervened appropriately when the new nurse took which action FIRST? (NORCET PYQ)","options":["Obtained a set of vital signs","Called the primary health-care provider","Flushed the infusion tubing with normal saline and restarted the IV","Stop the transfusion"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Chills, fever, muscle stiffness within 30 minutes indicate a transfusion (febrile) reaction; the FIRST action is to stop the transfusion (Right Answer D).  Why A wrong: Vital signs follow stopping the transfusion.  Why B wrong: Notifying the provider follows stopping the transfusion.  Why C wrong: Restarting/flushing while a reaction is occurring is unsafe; the transfusion must be stopped first."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"A nurse fastens a cell from a patient who is receiving blood transfusion and while serving the patient notices itching, flushing of the face and very restlessness. The nurse knows that these findings are indicating which type of reaction? (NORCET PYQ)","options":["Allergic reaction","Hemolytic reaction","Transfusion reaction (febrile)","Adverse reaction"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Itching, flushing of the face, hives and restlessness indicate an allergic transfusion reaction (Right Answer A).  Why B wrong: Hemolytic reaction presents with flank pain, haemoglobinuria, and shock.  Why C wrong: Febrile reaction presents with chills, fever, muscle stiffness.  Why D wrong: \"Adverse reaction\" is nonspecific; the features are classic for allergic reaction."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"Match the clinical features of blood transfusion reactions: a Haemolytic transfusion reaction presents with which features? (SATA)","options":["Fever","Chills","Chest pain","Hypotension","Tachypnea"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Haemolytic reaction features include fever, chills, chest pain, hypotension and tachypnea.  All options are correct per the clinical features list."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"A Febrile transfusion reaction is characterised by which clinical features? (SATA)","options":["Fever","Chills","Headache","Pulmonary crackles"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Febrile reaction features include fever, chills, headache, and pulmonary crackles.  All listed options are correct."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"Circulatory (volume) overload as a transfusion reaction presents with which clinical features? (SATA)","options":["Dyspnea","Cough","Pulmonary crackles","Severe joint pain"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Circulatory overload features include dyspnea, cough, and pulmonary crackles.  Why D wrong: Severe joint pain is a feature of allergic reactions, not circulatory overload."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"An Allergic transfusion reaction is characterised by which clinical features? (SATA)","options":["Urticaria","Pruritus","Wheezing","Flank pain"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Allergic reaction features include urticaria, pruritus, and wheezing.  Why D wrong: Flank pain is characteristic of a haemolytic reaction, not allergic."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"When a haemolytic transfusion reaction is suspected, what samples should be sent to the laboratory? (priority nursing action)","options":["Send urine specimen and a blood sample to the laboratory if a haemolytic reaction is suspected","Send only the empty bag","Send no samples","Send sputum only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: When a haemolytic reaction is suspected, a urine specimen and a blood sample should be sent to the laboratory (to confirm haemolysis).  Why B/C/D wrong: The bag is returned but the diagnostic samples required are urine and blood; sending only the bag, no samples, or sputum is incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 026 · organ/blood donation & donor criteria in India…","question":"A patient with massive transfusion is at risk of hypothermia. What priority nursing action is recommended regarding blood components and warming?","options":["Resend the blood to the laboratory and maintain proper warming; usually transfusion reaction is occurred with 50 ml of transfusion (so monitor early)","Heat the blood in a microwave","Pour hot water over the bag","Freeze the blood before infusing"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Per the priority actions, the blood is resent to the laboratory and proper warming maintained; the book notes a transfusion reaction usually occurs within the first ~50 ml of transfusion, so early monitoring is essential.  Why B/C/D wrong: Microwave heating, hot water, and freezing all damage/haemolyse blood and are contraindicated."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"During a blood transfusion, who is legally responsible for obtaining the informed consent that must be documented?","options":["The staff nurse who starts the transfusion","The doctor","The blood bank technician","The ward attendant"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states that taking and documenting informed consent is a nurse's responsibility, but it is the doctor's responsibility — the nurse only documents it.  Why A wrong: The nurse documents the consent but is not the one legally responsible for obtaining it.  Why C wrong: The blood bank technician processes/crossmatches blood, not consent.  Why D wrong: A ward attendant has no role in consent."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"Per the source, blood transfusion should be administered by which personnel?","options":["Doctors only","Nurses only","Doctors or nurses, not the patient","Trained ward attendants"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page states blood transfusion should be administered by doctors, not by the nurses.  Why A wrong: Although it must be administered by qualified staff, the source notes the nurse's involvement and that it should not be done by the patient.  Why B wrong: It is not a nurses-only task.  Why D wrong: Ward attendants are not authorized to administer blood."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"Before starting a transfusion, the nurse must check blood type, ABO grouping, type of components, Rh factor, client identification, blood numbers, and which additional item?","options":["Patient's weight","Expiration date","Hemoglobin level","Blood pressure trend"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists checking blood typing, components, Rh factor, client identification, blood numbers, and expiration date.  Why A wrong: Weight is not listed in this verification checklist.  Why C wrong: Hemoglobin is not part of the unit-checking list.  Why D wrong: BP trend is monitored during transfusion, not part of unit identification check."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"The most critical phase of the blood transfusion (during which the nurse must verify compatibility and client identity) is:","options":["The last 15 minutes","The first 15 minutes","The middle hour","The entire 4 hours equally"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source identifies the first 15 minutes as the most critical phase for confirming compatibility and verifying client identity.  Why A wrong: Risk is highest at the start, not the end.  Why C/D wrong: While monitoring continues, the source specifically flags the first 15 minutes as most critical."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"Within how many minutes after arrival must blood be administered, and before administering blood components what must be done?","options":["60 minutes; warm the blood to body temperature","30 minutes; equalize the blood components to body temperature","120 minutes; refrigerate the components","10 minutes; dilute with dextrose"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states blood must be administered within 30 minutes after arrival, and before administering, blood components should be equalized to body temperature.  Why A wrong: The time frame is 30 minutes, not 60.  Why C wrong: Blood is not refrigerated after arrival before infusion; it must be given within 30 minutes.  Why D wrong: Blood is never diluted with dextrose."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"Vital signs should be monitored before administering blood, then at which intervals during a transfusion?","options":["Every 5 minutes throughout the transfusion","After 15 minutes, every 15 minutes during the transfusion","Only at the start and end","Every hour"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to take vital signs before administering, then after 15 minutes, then every 15 minutes during the transfusion.  Why A wrong: 5-minute intervals are not specified.  Why C wrong: Continuous interval monitoring is required, not just start and end.  Why D wrong: Hourly is too infrequent for safe transfusion monitoring."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"Which IV cannula size is recommended for blood transfusion in adults?","options":["24 G (orange) only","14 G (orange) and 18 G (green) cannula","26 G (violet) only","22 G (blue) only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source specifies using a 14 G (orange) and 18 G (green) cannula for adults, with a 20–24 G cannula for pediatrics.  Why A wrong: Orange in the source is 14 G; 24 G is for pediatrics.  Why C wrong: 26 G violet is too small for adult transfusion.  Why D wrong: 22 G blue is not the recommended adult size in the source."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"For pediatric blood transfusion, the source recommends a cannula of which gauge?","options":["14–16 G","18 G","20–24 G","26 G"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states \"For paediatrics, 20-24G cannula can be used for blood transfusion.\"  Why A/B wrong: 14–18 G are for adults.  Why D wrong: 26 G is not listed for pediatric transfusion."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"Which filter standard is required on the blood transfusion (BT) set?","options":["0.22 micron bacterial filter","170–200 micron filter","5 micron leukocyte filter","No filter required"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source specifies a BT set with a standard 170–200 micron filter.  Why A wrong: 0.22 micron is a bacterial/IV-fluid filter, not for blood.  Why C wrong: Leukocyte reduction filters differ; the standard BT filter here is 170–200 micron.  Why D wrong: A filter is mandatory for blood transfusion."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"A PRBC unit and whole blood transfusion should be completed within how many hours, because increased time beyond this raises which risk?","options":["Within 6 hours; air embolism","Within 4 hours; bacterial contamination","Within 8 hours; clotting","Within 2 hours; hemolysis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states PRBCs and whole blood transfusion should be completed within 4 hours, because increased time over 4 hours increases the risk of bacterial contamination.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies a 4-hour limit and bacterial contamination as the risk; the others are not the stated time/risk pairing."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"Which IV solution should be administered with blood components or used to flush a line during transfusion?","options":["5% Dextrose","Normal saline (NS 0.9%)","Ringer's lactate","Dextrose-saline"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to administer an appropriate rate, infusing 15 drops or 1 mL per minute for the first 15 minutes, and that normal saline (NS 0.9%) should be used with blood.  Why A wrong: Dextrose causes hemolysis/clumping of RBCs and is contraindicated.  Why C wrong: Ringer's lactate contains calcium which can cause clotting with citrated blood.  Why D wrong: Dextrose-containing solutions are contraindicated with blood."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"For the first 15 minutes of a transfusion the infusion rate should be set at approximately:","options":["60 drops/min","15 drops or 1 mL per minute","100 mL per minute","5 drops per minute"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source specifies an appropriate rate of 15 drops or 1 mL per minute for the first 15 minutes, with frequent monitoring, because if nothing will be there, the rate can be increased after 15 minutes if patient tolerates the transfusion.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the source's stated initial slow rate."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"Blood transfusion sets need to be changed after every how many units or how often?","options":["After every 6 units or every 8 hours","After every 2 units or every 4 hours","After every 4 units or with each unit of blood","Only once per day"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states blood transfusion sets need to be changed every 4 hours or with each unit of blood.  Why A/B/D wrong: These do not match the stated 4-hour / per-unit change interval."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"Where should blood be stored, and within what time frame after removal from the refrigerator must it be administered?","options":["At room temperature; within 60 minutes","In the blood bag refrigerator; administered within 20–30 minutes of removal","In a freezer; within 2 hours","On a warming plate; immediately"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states blood should be stored in refrigerators (other than those used in blood banks) and that blood not administered within 20–30 minutes is returned to the blood bank.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the storage and 20–30 minute administration rule."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"Only one unit of blood should be transported at a time. What should be done if a unit is not transfused within 1 unit's time?","options":["Discard the blood immediately","It is returned (returned even if the client is precabbed more than 1 unit)","Refreeze it for later use","Transfuse it rapidly"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states only one unit of blood should be transported at a time and even if the client is prescribed more than 1 unit, blood is returned.  Why A wrong: The source indicates returning, not immediate discarding.  Why C wrong: Blood is never refrozen.  Why D wrong: Rapid transfusion is unsafe and not indicated."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"After completion of a transfusion, the blood bag and tubing should be discarded in which colour bag, per BMW guidelines?","options":["Black colour bag","Yellow colour bag","Red colour bag","Blue colour bag"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states that after completion of transfusion, the blood bag and tubing should be placed in a yellow colour bag as per BMW (Bio-Medical Waste) guidelines.  Why A wrong: Black bags are for general/non-biomedical waste.  Why C wrong: Red bags are for contaminated plastic/recyclable infectious waste, not blood bags here.  Why D wrong: Blue is for glassware/metallic sharps."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"A transfusion reaction caused by Hep-B, HIV, EBV, etc. (the most common transmission risk noted) is best described as which type of reaction?","options":["Allergic reaction","Possible disease transmission by blood transfusion","Citrate toxicity","Hemolytic reaction"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The \"Should Remember\" box lists possible disease transmission by blood transfusion (Hep-C most common, Hep-B, HIV, EBV, etc.).  Why A wrong: Allergic reactions are a separate listed entity.  Why C wrong: Citrate toxicity is a metabolic effect, not infection transmission.  Why D wrong: Hemolytic reactions involve ABO incompatibility, not viral transmission."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"After each unit of blood transfusion, calcium gluconate injection should be given because citrate (used as anticoagulant) does what to calcium?","options":["Increases ionized calcium","Binds calcium and promotes excretion through urine","Has no effect on calcium","Raises serum phosphate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Should Remember box states that after each unit, calcium gluconate injection should be given because citrate binds the calcium and promotes excretion through urine.  Why A wrong: Citrate lowers ionized calcium, not raises it.  Why C wrong: Citrate actively chelates calcium.  Why D wrong: The source attributes the effect to calcium binding/excretion, not phosphate."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"A patient receiving blood transfusion should be watched for metabolic alkalosis after transfusion. This complication is related to:","options":["Excess citrate metabolism producing bicarbonate","Massive blood loss","Air embolism","Bacterial contamination"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Should Remember box notes watching for metabolic alkalosis after transfusion because metabolized citrate becomes bicarbonate.  Why B wrong: Blood loss is a separate complication noted.  Why C/D wrong: Air embolism and bacterial contamination are different listed complications."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"The Should Remember box warns that massive blood loss / transfusion of 10 units of blood in a 24-hour period can cause which situation requiring blood transfusion?","options":["Mild dehydration","A massive blood transfusion situation (massive bleed)","Hyperkalemia only","No clinical concern"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The box notes massive blood loss / transfusion of ~10 units of blood in a 24-hour period constitutes a massive blood transfusion scenario.  Why A wrong: This is a major, not mild, event.  Why C wrong: Hyperkalemia is one electrolyte effect but not the overall description.  Why D wrong: This is a critical clinical concern."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"RBCs should be washed (washed RBCs) with normal saline/plasma when the patient has experienced which type of reaction during a previous transfusion?","options":["Circulatory overload","Severe allergic transfusion reaction (washed RBCs with most of the plasma, platelets and white blood cells washed and replaced with saline)","Citrate toxicity","Hyperkalemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The box states washed RBCs (most of the plasma, platelets and WBCs washed and replaced with saline) are used in patients who had severe allergic transfusion reactions previously.  Why A wrong: Washed RBCs address allergic reactions, not volume overload.  Why C/D wrong: Citrate toxicity and hyperkalemia are managed differently, not by washing RBCs."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"[RECALL/NORCET] Which is a preferred gauge cannula used for blood transfusion?","options":["16 G","20 G","24 G","26 G"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source RECALL box keys 16 G as the preferred large-bore cannula for transfusion (larger bore prevents hemolysis and allows adequate flow).  Why B/C/D wrong: 20 G, 24 G, and 26 G are smaller-bore cannulas not preferred for routine adult transfusion; the keyed answer is 16 G."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"[RECALL/NORCET-9 Prelims-2024] A patient undergoing blood transfusion in the ICU develops the following indicators of circulatory overload. Which finding indicates circulatory overload?","options":["Dyspnea, tachycardia, distended neck vein","Hypotension, oliguria, diminished peripheral pulse","Hypertension, palpitation, dizziness","Hypotension, bradycardia, palpitation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Circulatory overload (TACO) presents with dyspnea, tachycardia, and distended neck veins from fluid overload; the source keys this as the right answer.  Why B wrong: Hypotension and diminished pulse suggest hypovolemia/shock, not overload.  Why C wrong: Dizziness/palpitation alone is nonspecific and not the overload triad keyed.  Why D wrong: Bradycardia/hypotension is not the overload presentation."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"Per the Circulatory Overload section on p291, what is the recommended nursing action when overload is suspected?","options":["Increase the flow rate","Decrease the flow rate (first priority), then inform HCP","Stop fluids permanently and discharge","Add a diuretic without orders"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states nursing care: decrease flow rate (first priority), then inform to HCP.  Why A wrong: Increasing flow worsens overload.  Why C wrong: The source does not direct permanent stop/discharge.  Why D wrong: Nurses do not independently order/add diuretics; first priority is reducing flow and informing the HCP."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"Circulatory overload during transfusion occurs due to which underlying problem?","options":["Citrate metabolism","Improper management of flow rate, and lack of insufficient output","Bacterial contamination","ABO incompatibility"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source attributes circulatory overload to fluids infused due to improper management of flow rate and lack of insufficient output.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are separate transfusion complications, not the cause of volume overload described."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"The device shown in the figure used to warm blood and fluid during transfusion is called a:","options":["Leukocyte reduction filter","Blood and fluid warmer","Infusion pump","Phototherapy unit"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The figure caption identifies it as a \"Blood and fluid warmer.\"  Why A wrong: A leukocyte filter removes WBCs; it does not warm.  Why C wrong: An infusion pump controls rate, not temperature.  Why D wrong: Phototherapy is unrelated to transfusion warming."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"Which is a stated GOAL of Massive Blood Transfusion (MBT)?","options":["To deliberately induce hypothermia","Early recognition of blood loss","To withhold all blood products","To increase citrate load"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Goals of MBT list \"Early recognition of blood loss.\"  Why A wrong: Hypothermia is a complication to avoid, not a goal.  Why C wrong: MBT involves giving blood products, not withholding them.  Why D wrong: Increasing citrate is harmful, not a goal."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"A goal of MBT is the maintenance of tissue perfusion and oxygenation by restoration of which two parameters?","options":["Blood volume and hemoglobin (Hb)","Sodium and potassium","Glucose and pH","Albumin and bilirubin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states maintenance of tissue perfusion and oxygenation by restoration of blood volume and hemoglobin (Hb).  Why B/C/D wrong: These parameters are not the perfusion/oxygenation restoration targets listed."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"Which is listed among the goals of MBT regarding timing of bleeding control?","options":["Delayed surgical management","Arrest of bleeding in combination with use of early surgical or radiological intervention","Avoiding all surgical intervention","Routine elective surgery only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists arrest of bleeding in combination with use of early surgical or radiological intervention.  Why A wrong: Early, not delayed, intervention is the goal.  Why C/D wrong: The goal includes prompt surgical/radiological intervention, not avoidance or routine elective surgery."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"Judicious use of blood component therapy to correct coagulopathy is a goal of MBT. Which score is used to decide on initiating massive blood transfusion?","options":["APACHE II score","Trauma Associated Severe Haemorrhage (TASH) score","Glasgow Coma Scale","SOFA score"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source highlights the Trauma Associated Sever haemorrhage (TASH) score is used to decide massive blood transfusion.  Why A wrong: APACHE II is an ICU mortality risk score, not a transfusion trigger here.  Why C wrong: GCS assesses consciousness, not transfusion need.  Why D wrong: SOFA is an organ dysfunction score, not the TASH transfusion decision tool."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"In adults, massive blood transfusion (MBT) is defined as the administration of how many units of PRBC within 24 hours?","options":["2–4 units","8–10 units of PRBC","20 units","1 unit"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines MBT in adults as administration of 8–10 units PRBC within 24 hours.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the 8–10 unit/24-hour adult definition."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"MBT in an adult is also defined as replacement of more than how many units of blood in 24 hours, OR replacement of one blood volume in 24 hours?","options":[">5 units",">10 units within 24 hours",">2 units",">20 units"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states \">10 units within 24 hours\" as an alternative adult MBT definition, or replacement of one blood volume in 24 hours.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the >10-unit threshold."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"In an adult, one blood volume is approximately what value (in mL/kg)?","options":["30 mL/kg","50 mL/kg","70 mL/kg","100 mL/kg"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states one blood volume is approximately 70 mL/kg, which is also given per kg.  Why A/B/D wrong: These do not match the ~70 mL/kg adult blood volume value."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"Which of the following is an adult MBT trigger based on rate of blood loss?","options":["Replacement of 1 unit in 4 hours","Replacement of 50% of blood volume within 3–4 hours","Loss of 50 mL/hour","Loss of 10 mL/hour"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists replacement of 50% of blood volume in 3–4 hours among adult MBT criteria, and a rate of loss >150 mL/hour.  Why A wrong: This is far below the massive transfusion criteria.  Why C/D wrong: These low loss rates do not meet MBT thresholds."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"An adult MBT criterion based on rate is a blood loss rate exceeding:","options":["50 mL/hour","100 mL/hour","150 mL/hour","200 mL/hour"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states a rate of loss >150 mL/hour as an MBT criterion.  Why A/B/D wrong: The keyed threshold is >150 mL/hour."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"During massive haemorrhage, the source recommends transfusing blood components empirically in which ratio of plasma : red cells : platelets?","options":["2:1:1","1:1:1 ratio of plasma : red cells : platelets","4:1:1","1:2:1"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states adult trauma patients should be transfused empirically with a 1:1:1 ratio of plasma : red cells : platelets, noting this is controversial.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated 1:1:1 empirical ratio."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"Which agent is recommended to be used in massive bleeding control during MBT, with monitoring to provide effective bleeding control?","options":["Heparin","Tranexamic acid","Warfarin","Aspirin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states tranexamic acid may be useful in massive bleeding control.  Why A wrong: Heparin is an anticoagulant and would worsen bleeding.  Why C wrong: Warfarin is an anticoagulant, contraindicated in active bleeding.  Why D wrong: Aspirin is an antiplatelet that worsens bleeding."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"For MBT, which gauge cannula is preferably chosen for adequate flow?","options":["22 G or 24 G","14 G or 16 G cannula","26 G","20 G only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states \"Preferably, choose 14G or 16G cannula for MBT.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: Smaller-bore cannulas (22–26 G, 20 G) do not permit the rapid flow rates required for MBT."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"[In Paediatrics] One definition of MBT in children is transfusion of which volume of total blood volume (TBV) within 24 hours?","options":[">25% TBV in 24 hr",">50% TBV in 24 hr",">100% TBV in 24 hr",">10% TBV"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists transfusion of >50% TBV in 24 hr as a pediatric MBT criterion.  Why A/D wrong: These are below the listed pediatric threshold.  Why C wrong: >100% TBV in 24 hr is a separate listed criterion; >50% is the answer to this transfusion-volume criterion."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"Which is a pediatric MBT criterion expressed as 100% of total blood volume?","options":["Transfusion of >100% TBV in 24 hr","Transfusion of >25% TBV in 6 hr","Loss of 10% TBV","Transfusion of <50% TBV"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists transfusion of >100% TBV in 24 hr as a pediatric MBT definition.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the >100% TBV in 24 hr criterion."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"A pediatric MBT criterion related to ongoing blood loss is transfusion to support ongoing blood loss of more than what percentage of TBV per minute?","options":[">5% TBV/min",">10% TBV/min",">1% TBV/min",">20% TBV/min"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states transfusion support for on-going blood loss of >10% TBV/min as a pediatric MBT criterion.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the >10% TBV/min threshold."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"Which is a listed Complication of MBT?","options":["Non-metabolised citrate","Hypoglycemia","Hypocalcaemia from citrate metabolism","Hyperalbuminemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Complication of MBT list begins with \"Non-metabolised citrate.\"  Why B wrong: Hypoglycemia is not listed; metabolic disturbances listed are different.  Why C wrong: Hypocalcaemia is listed but is described as due to binding of ionised calcium by citrate, which is used as anticoagulant — a separate item; the first listed complication is non-metabolised citrate.  Why D wrong: Hyperalbuminemia is not a listed MBT complication."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"Hypothermia is listed as a complication of MBT due to:","options":["Rapid infusion of cold (refrigerated) blood","Citrate metabolism","Excess calcium","High glucose"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists hypothermia due to RBC loss / cold blood; infusing refrigerated blood lowers body temperature, which is why blood is equalized to body temperature before transfusion.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are unrelated to the temperature drop from infusing cold blood."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"In MBT, hypocalcaemia occurs due to which mechanism?","options":["Increased renal calcium excretion only","Binding of ionised calcium by citrate, which is used as anticoagulant","Vitamin D deficiency","Parathyroid failure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states hypocalcaemia is due to binding of ionised calcium by citrate, which is used as anticoagulant.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the stated mechanism of MBT-related hypocalcaemia."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"Which of the following are listed complications of MBT? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Hyperkalemia (due to RBC lysis)","Air embolism","Volume overload","Dilutional coagulopathy","Hyperalbuminemia"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source's Complication of MBT list includes hyperkalemia (RBC lysis), air embolism, volume overload, and dilutional coagulopathy.  Why E wrong: Hyperalbuminemia is not a listed MBT complication."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"Under Nursing care for blood transfusion (p292), what is the recommended verification practice for the recipient and blood component?","options":["Verify alone","Check the blood product (check by 2 registered nurses or other serial number, blood component, blood type, Rh factor, expiry, screening test)","Verify only the bag number","No verification needed in MBT"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states checking the blood product is done by 2 registered nurses (or another serial number), checking blood component, blood type, Rh factor, expiry, and screening test.  Why A wrong: A single verifier is insufficient; two-nurse check is required.  Why C wrong: More than the bag number must be verified.  Why D wrong: Verification is always required, including MBT."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"Per the Nursing care section, before initiating general nursing care, what should the nurse do regarding the patient and blood transfusion policy?","options":["Initiate general nursing care as per the blood transfusion guidelines","Skip the policy","Avoid checking orders","Delegate to attendants"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to initiate general nursing care as per the blood transfusion guidelines, after checking orders, taking consent, asking about allergy history, obtaining baseline date TPR-BP, etc.  Why B/C/D wrong: Skipping policy, avoiding order checks, or delegating contradict the listed nursing responsibilities."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"Per the Nursing care list (p292), what baseline assessment must be obtained before the transfusion?","options":["ECG only","Baseline TPR-BP (temperature, pulse, respiration, blood pressure)","Blood glucose only","Urine output only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists obtaining baseline date TPR-BP before transfusion.  Why A/C/D wrong: While other parameters may be monitored, the source specifies baseline TPR-BP."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"The nurse must ask the patient about which history before transfusion?","options":["Family history of diabetes","Any allergy to previous transfusion","Dietary preferences","Recent travel"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists asking any history of allergy to previous transfusion.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the listed pre-transfusion history requirements."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"Regarding Citrate Toxicity (p292), approximately how much citrate is contained in one unit of citrate phosphate dextrose adenine solution (each blood bag)?","options":["About 1 gram","About 3 grams of citrate (approximately 3 g)","About 10 grams","About 0.5 gram"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states 80 mL of citrate phosphate dextrose adenine solution present in each blood bag contains approximately 3 g citrate.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the ~3 g per bag value."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"Citrate toxicity (a healthy adult can metabolise this load) leads to which acid–base/electrolyte disturbances in massive transfusion?","options":["Hyperkalemia and metabolic acidosis only","Hypocalcaemia or hypothermia associated with massive blood loss, which can decrease the rate of metabolism leading to citrate toxicity","Hypernatremia","Hyperphosphatemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states a healthy adult can metabolise this load, but in massive blood loss with hypocalcaemia or hypothermia the rate of metabolism decreases, leading to citrate toxicity.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the disturbances the source attributes to impaired citrate metabolism."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"Per the Citrate Toxicity section, uncontrolled citrate toxicity can lead to which metabolic effects?","options":["Hypokalaemia and hyperglycemia","Hypocalcaemia, hypomagnesaemia, and worsening of acidosis","Hypernatremia and alkalosis","Hyperthermia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states uncontrolled citrate toxicity can lead to hypocalcaemia, hypomagnesaemia, and worsen the acidosis.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the listed effects of uncontrolled citrate toxicity."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"[RECALL/NORCET] After RTA (road traffic accident), a patient is brought to the emergency department. Massive blood transfusion, which electrolyte imbalance occurs in the patient?","options":["Hypocalcemia","Hypokalemia","Hyponatremia","Hyperthermia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Massive blood transfusion (with citrate binding calcium) most characteristically causes hypocalcemia; the source keys answer A.  Why B wrong: Hyperkalemia (not hypokalemia) is the typical MBT potassium disturbance.  Why C wrong: Hyponatremia is not the characteristic MBT electrolyte effect keyed.  Why D wrong: Hypothermia (not hyperthermia) occurs with MBT."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"[RECALL/NORCET] After massive blood transfusion, a patient complains of muscles cramps (tetany like) and rigidity in the extremities. These clinical findings indicate which electrolyte imbalance?","options":["Hyperkalaemia","Hyperthermia","Hyponatraemia","Hypocalcaemia"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Tetany-like muscle cramps and rigidity are classic signs of hypocalcaemia from citrate binding calcium during MBT; the source keys answer A in its layout corresponding to hypocalcaemia.  Why A wrong: Hyperkalaemia causes weakness/cardiac changes, not tetany.  Why B wrong: Hyperthermia does not cause tetany.  Why C wrong: Hyponatraemia causes neuro changes, not classic tetany."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"The figure on p292 shows a filter used to remove white blood cells from blood prior to transfusion. It is called a:","options":["Blood and fluid warmer","Leucocyte reduction filter","Microaggregate filter","Standard 170-micron filter"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The figure caption identifies a \"Leucocyte reduction filter.\"  Why A wrong: The warmer is a different device.  Why C/D wrong: These are different filter types; the labelled figure is a leucocyte reduction filter."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"Cardio Pulmonary Resuscitation (CPR) is best described as:","options":["A purely elective procedure","An emergency life-saving procedure","A diagnostic test","A surgical operation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Overview states CPR is an emergency life-saving procedure.  Why A/C/D wrong: It is not elective, diagnostic, or surgical."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"CPR is a provision of support given to the vital organs such as which structures, that are first to suffer brain and medical help arrives?","options":["Liver and kidneys","Heart, liver, and brain","Spleen and pancreas","Skin and muscles"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states CPR is a provision of support to vital organs such as heart, liver, brain (and brain until medical help arrives).  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the primary vital organs supported by CPR per the source."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"CPR restores which function of the heart and lungs?","options":["Hormone secretion","Function of heart and lungs (circulation and oxygenation)","Digestion","Filtration"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states it restores the function of heart and lungs.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not functions restored by CPR."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"Basic Life Support (BLS) is best defined as:","options":["Advanced invasive cardiac surgery","A specific level of pre-hospital medical care provided by trained responders, including emergency medical technicians (EMT), in advanced medical care, without including invasive skills/drugs","Hospital-only intensive care","A diagnostic ECG procedure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines BLS as a specific level of pre-hospital medical care provided by trained responders, including emergency medical technicians (EMT) in advanced medical care; BLS generally does not include the use of drugs or invasive skills.  Why A wrong: BLS excludes invasive surgery.  Why C wrong: BLS is pre-hospital, not hospital-only.  Why D wrong: BLS is resuscitation support, not a diagnostic ECG."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"Advanced Cardiac Life Support (ACLS) is usually performed in which setting and by whom?","options":["At home, by laypersons only","In hospital premises, with life support given with the help of medicine, oxygen therapy and machine (instrument), by the doctor such as defibrillator","In ambulances only, by drivers","In schools, by teachers"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states ACLS is usually performed in hospital premises, with BLS support given with help of medicine, oxygen therapy and machine (instrument), by the doctor (such as a defibrillator).  Why A/C/D wrong: ACLS requires hospital-level resources, medications, and clinicians, not laypersons/drivers/teachers."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"Which is listed as an Indication of CPR?","options":["Stable angina","Myocardial infarction or cardiac arrest","Mild dehydration","Routine checkup"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Indication of CPR lists myocardial infarction or cardiac arrest.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not indications for CPR."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"Which are listed indications for CPR? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Myocardial infarction or cardiac arrest","Asphyxia neonatorum or heart block","Heart failure","Routine annual physical","Stable hypertension"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists myocardial infarction/cardiac arrest, asphyxia neonatorum/heart block, and heart failure as indications of CPR.  Why D/E wrong: A routine physical and stable hypertension are not CPR indications."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"Per the General CPR guidelines, the current recommended sequence of intervention is:","options":["A-B-C (Airway, Breathing, Circulation)","C-A-B (Circulation, Airway, Breathing) — Circulation or compression first","B-C-A","C-B-A"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states in CPR rescuers should follow CAB sequence for intervention: C = Circulation or compression (first intervention), A = Airway, B = Breathing.  Why A wrong: The older A-B-C sequence has been replaced by C-A-B.  Why C/D wrong: These do not match the CAB sequence."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"In the General CPR guidelines, what does each letter of CAB represent?","options":["C = Compression/Circulation (first), A = Airway, B = Breathing","C = Cough, A = Antibiotic, B = Bleeding","C = Care, A = Assess, B = Bandage","C = Chest, A = Arrest, B = Blood"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines C = Circulation or compression (first intervention), A = Airway, B = Breathing.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the meanings of CAB in CPR."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"[Should Know About Fact] During the first few seconds, what determines the difference between life and death for a person dealing with cardiac arrest?","options":["Hospital admission paperwork","The ability to act quickly and confidently to make the difference between life and death","Waiting for relatives","Performing a full physical exam first"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Should Know About Fact box emphasizes the ability to act quickly and confidently can make the difference between life and death.  Why A/C/D wrong: Delays for paperwork, relatives, or full exams cost critical time in cardiac arrest."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"Among the Keys for BLS, what is the first priority when arriving at a scene?","options":["Begin compressions immediately","Quickly start the chain of survival","Call relatives","Document the time"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Keys for BLS list \"Quickly start the chain of survival.\"  Why A wrong: Compressions follow scene safety and assessment, after activating the chain of survival.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the first BLS priority."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"Which is listed among the Keys for BLS?","options":["Deliver high-quality chest compressions to circulate oxygen to the brain and vital organs","Avoid all chest compressions","Provide only oxygen","Wait for a doctor before acting"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists delivering high-quality chest compressions to circulate oxygen to the brain and vital organs.  Why B wrong: Compressions are essential, not avoided.  Why C wrong: Oxygen alone is insufficient without circulation.  Why D wrong: BLS responders act immediately."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"Per Keys for BLS, the rescuer should know when and how to use which device?","options":["ECG machine","Automated External Defibrillator (AED)","Ventilator","Infusion pump"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists \"Know when and how to use an Automated External Defibrillator (AED).\"  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the BLS device specified."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"Keys for BLS also emphasize knowing how to work with whom?","options":["Insurance agents","Other rescuers as part of a team, and how to provide rescue breaths","Family members only","Hospital administrators"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists understanding how to work with other rescuers as part of a team, providing rescue breaths, and knowing how to treat choking.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the listed BLS teamwork elements."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"In the Step by Step BLS/CPR for adults, the very first step is to:","options":["Begin compressions","Be Safe — check the scene for safety","Call relatives","Give rescue breaths"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source's first BLS step is \"Be Safe: Check the scene for safety.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: Scene safety precedes any hands-on intervention to avoid harm to the rescuer."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"When checking the scene for safety, if it is INSIDE, the rescuer should watch for which hazards?","options":["Construction debris and unsecured weapons only","Downed electrical wires, leaking fuel from car accidents, building collapse, or natural disaster/dangerous weather conditions","No hazards exist indoors","Only animals"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states inside, watch for downed electrical wires, leaking fuel from car accidents, building collapse, or natural disaster/dangerous weather conditions (drowning persons should be removed from the water and dried off; they should also be removed from standing water such as puddles, pools, gutters, etc.).  Why A wrong: Construction debris/weapons are listed under OUTSIDE hazards.  Why C/D wrong: Indoor hazards do exist and are more than animals."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"When checking the scene, if OUTSIDE, the rescuer should watch for:","options":["Leaking fuel only","Construction debris, unsecured weapons, violent individuals, electrical hazards","Nothing","Only weather"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states if outside, watch for construction debris, unsecured weapons, violent individuals, electrical hazards.  Why A wrong: Leaking fuel is listed under inside hazards.  Why C/D wrong: Multiple outdoor hazards are listed."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"Before approaching a victim, the source's key safety reminder is:","options":["Be sure you do not become injured yourself","Ignore your own safety to save time","Wait for police regardless","Move the victim first"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source emphasizes \"Be sure you do not become injured yourself.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: Rescuer safety is paramount; becoming injured prevents you from helping."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"Under \"Assess the Person,\" how is the level of consciousness assessed if a person is found unconscious?","options":["By drawing blood","Tap the shoulder and shout \"Hey, are you OK?\" to check responsiveness","By taking blood pressure","By waiting silently"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states assess responsiveness by tapping the shoulder and shouting \"Hey, are you OK?\" — early recognition: shake or tap hard on their shoulder; yell their name if you know it.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not assess responsiveness quickly."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"After confirming unresponsiveness, the next step in checking the person is to:","options":["Check to see if the person is breathing (agonal breathing, which is occasional gasping, does not count as breathing)","Start compressions without checking","Leave the person","Give medication"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states check to see if the person is breathing; agonal breathing, which is occasional gasping, does not count as normal breathing.  Why B wrong: Breathing assessment precedes the response decision.  Why C/D wrong: Leaving or medicating are not appropriate next steps."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"Under \"Call EMS,\" what should the rescuer do for the victim and activate?","options":["Send someone for help and to get an AED, and activate the emergency medical service (EMS) system","Call a taxi","Wait until breathing stops","Drive the victim alone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states send someone for help and to get an AED, and activate the emergency medical service (EMS) system.  Why B/C/D wrong: These delay or omit proper EMS activation."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"Per the Call EMS section, if no response, the rescuer should call to activate emergency medical service (EMS) system, while assessing for breathing and pulse without leaving the person. What follows next?","options":["CPR — begin chest compressions and delivering breaths, following CAB procedure","Discharge home","Resume normal activity","Apply a bandage"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states CPR begins chest compressions and delivering breaths, following the CAB procedure.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the next step in an unresponsive non-breathing patient."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"Under Circulation, for how long should the rescuer assess circulation while simultaneously checking for breathing?","options":["30 seconds","10 seconds","60 seconds","5 minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states then assess circulation within 10 seconds simultaneously with checking for breathing.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the 10-second assessment window."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"In an ADULT, where should the pulse be assessed for circulation?","options":["Femoral pulse","Carotid pulse on the side of the neck","Brachial pulse","Radial pulse"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states in adults, assess carotid pulse on the side of the neck for circulation.  Why A wrong: Femoral is used in children.  Why C wrong: Brachial pulse is used in infants.  Why D wrong: Radial is not the recommended pulse for circulation check in CPR."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"In CHILDREN, which pulse is assessed for circulation during CPR?","options":["Carotid pulse","Femoral pulse","Brachial pulse","Radial pulse"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states in children, assess femoral pulse for circulation.  Why A wrong: Carotid is used in adults.  Why C wrong: Brachial is used in infants.  Why D wrong: Radial is not used for CPR circulation check."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"In an INFANT, which pulse is assessed for circulation?","options":["Carotid pulse","Femoral pulse","Brachial pulse on the upper arm","Radial pulse"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states in infants, assess brachial pulse for circulation.  Why A wrong: Carotid is for adults.  Why B wrong: Femoral is for children.  Why D wrong: Radial is not used."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"When assessing a pulse during CPR, what is the maximum time to spend feeling for the pulse?","options":["No more than 10 seconds","30 seconds","60 seconds","5 seconds exactly"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states keep in mind not to waste time trying to feel for a pulse; feel for no more than 10 seconds. If you are not sure you feel a pulse, begin CPR with a cycle of 30 chest compressions and two breaths.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the \"no more than 10 seconds\" rule."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"If after up to 10 seconds you are unsure whether you feel a pulse, you should:","options":["Wait and recheck","Begin CPR with a cycle of 30 chest compressions and two breaths","Give only breaths","Do nothing"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states if not sure you feel a pulse, begin CPR with a cycle of 30 chest compressions and two breaths.  Why A/C/D wrong: Delaying, breaths-only, or inaction are inappropriate when pulse is uncertain."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"When checking for breathing, what is the maximum time the rescuer should not pause more than?","options":["30 seconds","10 seconds","60 seconds","5 minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states do not pause more than 10 seconds to check for breathing and pulse.  Why A/C/D wrong: These exceed the 10-second pause limit."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"If a pulse is not felt, the victim should be placed on a firm surface in which position before chest compressions?","options":["Trendelenburg position","Supine/flat position on their back or mattress","Prone position","Left lateral position"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states if there is a pulse not felt, then put the victim on a firm surface on their back or mattress and in the supine/flat position (AIIMS 2018).  Why A/C/D wrong: These positions are not used for chest compressions."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"Where should the hands (heel of the sternum) be placed for adult chest compressions?","options":["Upper third of the sternum","Lower half of the sternum / between the nipples (interlock the hands)","Over the stomach","On the left side of the chest"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states use the heel of one hand on the lower half of the sternum in the middle of the chest or between the nipples (AIIMS 2018); put your other hand on top of the first hand (interlock the hands).  Why A/C/D wrong: These are incorrect hand placements that reduce effectiveness or cause injury."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"During chest compressions, the rescuer's arms and pressing technique should be:","options":["Bent elbows, pressing at an angle","Straighten your arms and press straight down, then begin compressing chest, complete recoil will occur","Arms relaxed, gentle taps","One arm only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states straighten your arms and press straight down, begin compressing chest, complete recoil will occur.  Why A/C/D wrong: Bent arms, gentle taps, or single-arm technique produce inadequate compressions."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"Adult chest compressions should be to a depth of:","options":["1 inch","2 to 2.4 inches (5 to 6 cm)","4 inches","0.5 inch"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states compressions should be 2 to 2.4 inches (5 to 6 cm) into the person's chest.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the 5–6 cm adult compression depth."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"The recommended rate of adult chest compressions is:","options":["60 to 80 per minute","At least 100 to 120 compressions per minute","40 per minute","200 per minute"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states allow compressions at a rate of 100 to 120 compressions per minute.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the 100–120/min rate."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"After each compression, the chest should be allowed to do what before the next compression?","options":["Stay partially compressed","Allow complete recoil (return to normal position) to keep the heart filling between compressions","Be pressed harder","Be massaged"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states allow complete recoil (return to normal position) after each compression to allow the heart to fill between compressions.  Why A wrong: Incomplete recoil reduces cardiac filling.  Why C/D wrong: These do not describe proper recoil."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"During Airway management, after how many compressions should the rescuer stop compressions and open the airway?","options":["After 15 compressions","After 30 compressions, stop compressions and open the airway by head-tilt-chin-lift maneuver","After 5 compressions","After 50 compressions"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states after 30 compressions, stop compressions and open the airway by head-tilt-chin-lift maneuver.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the 30-compression cycle before airway opening."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"The head-tilt-chin-lift maneuver should NOT be performed if the rescuer suspects which injury, in which case the jaw-thrust maneuver is used?","options":["Rib fracture","Spinal cord/cervical spine injury (jaw-thrust maneuver used to avoid hyperextension of the neck)","Femur fracture","Ankle sprain"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states do not perform head-tilt-chin-lift if you suspect the victim may have a spinal cord injury; in that case the jaw-thrust maneuver is used without hyperextension of the neck.  Why A/C/D wrong: These injuries do not change airway-opening technique."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"The two airway-opening maneuvers illustrated in Fig. Airway Manoeuvres are:","options":["Jaw thrust and head-tilt-chin-lift","Heimlich and back blows","Recovery position and log roll","Sniffing and prone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The figure labels \"Jaw thrust\" and \"Head tilt-chin lift\" as the two airway manoeuvres.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the airway-opening maneuvers shown."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"[CPR Steps Summary in Adult] Step 1 directs the rescuer to check the scene for safety, form an initial impression, and use which equipment?","options":["Defibrillator only","Personal protective equipment (PPE)","A blood pressure cuff","An ECG monitor"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The CPR Steps Summary step 1 states check the scene for safety, form an initial impression, and use personal protective equipment (PPE).  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not what step 1 specifies."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"In the CPR Steps Summary, if a person appears unresponsive, the rescuer should check for responsiveness, breathing, and life-threatening bleeding or which condition?","options":["Hunger","Other life-threatening conditions, using a shout-tap-shout","Allergy","Dehydration"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states if the person appears unresponsive, check for responsiveness, breathing, life-threatening bleeding or other life-threatening conditions using shout-tap-shout.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the assessed life-threatening conditions."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"Per the CPR Steps Summary, if the person does NOT respond and is not breathing or only gasping, what should the rescuer do?","options":["Wait 5 minutes","CALL 911 and activate EMS","Give water","Place in recovery position"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The summary states if the person does not respond and is not breathing or only gasping, CALL 911 and activate EMS.  Why A/C/D wrong: These delay or are inappropriate for an unresponsive non-breathing patient."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"Per the CPR Steps Summary (Give 30 chest compressions), where should the hand position be placed?","options":["Two hands centered on the upper abdomen","Two interlock hands centered on the chest, with body position shoulders directly over hands, elbows locked, keep arm straight","One hand on the neck","Both hands on the forehead"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states hand position: two interlock hands centered on the chest; body position: shoulders directly over hands, elbows locked, keep arm straight.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are incorrect compression positions."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"In the Give 30 chest compressions step, the landmark for compression is:","options":["Upper third of sternum","Lower half of sternum between nipples","Over the xiphoid process","Left mid-clavicular line"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states landmark for compression: lower half of sternum between nipples.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated landmark."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"Per the CPR Steps Summary, the depth and rate of chest compressions should be:","options":["Depth at least 1 inch, fast & deep","Depth at least 2 inches, hard & fast; rate 100–120 per minute","Depth 4 inches, slow","Depth 0.5 inch, rate 60/min"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states depth at least 2 inches, hard & fast; rate 100–120 per minute.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the at-least-2-inch depth and 100–120/min rate."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"After 30 compressions, the rescuer should allow the chest to do what before the next compression?","options":["Stay compressed","Allow the chest to return to normal position after each compression","Press harder","Massage"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states allow the chest to return to normal position after each compression.  Why A/C/D wrong: These prevent proper recoil."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"In the \"Give 2 breaths\" step, the airway is opened using which technique to a past-neutral position?","options":["Jaw thrust only","Head-tilt/chin-lift technique","Sniffing position","Triple maneuver"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states open the airway to a past-neutral position using the head-tilt/chin-lift technique before giving breaths.  Why A wrong: Jaw thrust is reserved for suspected spinal injury, not the routine technique here.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the technique named."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"When giving rescue breaths, after pinching the nose and taking a normal breath, how should the breath be delivered and observed?","options":["Make a complete seal over the person's mouth, blow into their mouth to make the chest rise; ensure each breath lasts about 1 second and the chest rises before giving the next breath","Blow as hard as possible continuously","Breathe into the nose only","Give 5 quick puffs without watching the chest"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states pinch the nose shut, take a normal breath, and make a complete seal over the person's mouth; blow into their mouth to make the chest rise; ensure each breath lasts about 1 second and ensure a proper seal before giving the next breath.  Why B wrong: Excessive force causes gastric inflation.  Why C wrong: A mouth-to-mouth seal (nose pinched) is used.  Why D wrong: Chest rise must be confirmed; breaths are not given blindly."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"If the first breath does not make the chest rise during rescue breathing, the note directs the rescuer to:","options":["Continue regardless","Reset the head and ensure a proper seal before giving the 2nd breath (if the 2nd breath does not make the chest rise, an object may be blocking the airway)","Begin abdominal thrusts immediately","Stop CPR"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source note states if the first breath does not make the chest rise, reset the head and ensure a proper seal before giving the 2nd breath; if the 2nd breath does not make the chest rise, an object may be blocking the airway.  Why A wrong: Continuing without resetting wastes breaths.  Why C wrong: The first action is to reset and reseal, not immediately do thrusts.  Why D wrong: CPR should continue."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"Per the CPR Steps Summary (Step 7), the rescuer should continue giving sets of how many compressions and breaths in what ratio, and use an AED when?","options":["15:1, use AED after 5 minutes","30 compressions and 2 breaths in a ratio of 30:2, use an AED as soon as one is available","10:2, use AED only in hospital","30:1, never use AED"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states continue giving sets of 30 compressions and 2 breaths in a ratio of 30:2, and use an AED as soon as one is available.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the 30:2 ratio and immediate AED use."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"Per Step 8, the rescuer should minimize interruptions to chest compressions to less than how many seconds?","options":["30 seconds","10 seconds","60 seconds","5 seconds"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states minimize interruptions to chest compressions to less than 10 seconds.  Why A/C/D wrong: These exceed the <10-second limit."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"In the Technique of airway assessment (p295), the three components (\"Look, Listen, Feel\") correspond to which checks?","options":["Look = chest movements; Listen = chest movements and gurgling; Feel = the air on the cheek, chest movements","Look = pupils; Listen = heart; Feel = pulse","Look = skin color; Listen = bowel sounds; Feel = abdomen","Look = ECG; Listen = lungs; Feel = liver"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists Look = chest movements; Listen = chest movements and gurgling; Feel = the air on the cheek, chest movements.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the Look-Listen-Feel airway assessment."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"During new-born CPR, what is the recommended compression-to-ventilation ratio?","options":["30:2 compression with one breath","3:1 (three compressions followed by one breath)","15:2","5:1"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states during new-born CPR, compression-to-ventilation ratio should be maintained at 3:1 — three compressions followed by one breath (Delhi/AIIMS 2016).  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the 3:1 newborn ratio."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"During Breathing in CPR, after watching the chest rise from a breath, what should the rescuer do before delivering the second breath?","options":["Wait one second and repeat, allowing the breath to be delivered over one second","Press the chest","Pinch the cheek","Tilt the bed"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states give a breath while watching the chest rise; repeat while giving a second breath; breaths should be delivered over one second.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not describe proper rescue breathing."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"Per the Breathing section, when should compressions be resumed?","options":["After all breaths are complete only","Resume chest compressions, switch quickly between compressions and rescue breaths to minimize interruptions in chest compressions","Never resume","Only after 5 minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states resume chest compressions; switch quickly between compressions and rescue breaths to minimize interruptions in chest compressions.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the directive to minimize interruptions."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"After CPR is successful, in which position should the patient be placed (Delhi/AIIMS 2016)?","options":["Supine position","Recovery position","Prone position","Trendelenburg position"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states after CPR successful, place the patient in recovery position (Delhi/AIIMS 2016).  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the post-resuscitation position specified."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"When using the recovery position, the rescuer should alternatively turn the patient using what to minimize harm if mouth-to-mouth seriously injured?","options":["Mouth to nose alternatively","A second rescuer","Mouth to recovery position alternatively, with mouth seriously injured","The recovery position itself"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The source notes use alternatively the recovery position; the mouth may be used when mouth-to-mouth is seriously injured. (The recovery position protects the airway in the unconscious breathing patient.)  Why A/B/C wrong: These do not match the source's directive about the recovery position."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"[Should Know About Fact, p295] If two rescuers are present, then rescuers may change positions after how many cycles of compression and ventilation, at what ratio, to avoid fatigue?","options":["After 1 minute, 15:1","After every 5 cycles of compression and ventilation at a ratio of 30:2 per minute fatigue (Delhi/AIIMS 2016)","After 10 cycles, 15:2","After 2 cycles, 5:1"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states if two rescuers are present, then rescuers may change positions after a minute or after every 5 cycles of compression and ventilation at a ratio of 30:2 to avoid rescuer fatigue.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the 5-cycle / 30:2 changeover to prevent fatigue."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"Per the Should Know About Fact box, the chest should be compressed to a depth of at least how much, and the chest must do what before the next compression?","options":["At least 1 inch; stay compressed","At least 2 inches; allow complete chest recoil before the next compression","At least 4 inches; press harder","0.5 inch; massage"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the chest is compressed at least 2 inches, and the chest should fully recoil (allow complete recoil) before the next compression.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the at-least-2-inch depth and full recoil requirement."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"Per the Should Know About Fact box, the rescuer should assess for gastric distension that may indicate air entering into the stomach rather than the lungs. This can result in which complication?","options":["Improved oxygenation","Regurgitation and aspiration","Faster recovery","No complication"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states assess for gastric distension that may indicate air entering the stomach rather than the lungs, which can result in regurgitation and aspiration.  Why A/C/D wrong: Gastric distension is harmful, increasing aspiration risk."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"Per the Should Know About Fact box, how often should pulse be checked in adults during CPR, and at which point?","options":["Every 30 seconds","Check the pulse after 2 minutes or 5 cycles during CPR in adults","Every 10 minutes","Continuously without stopping"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states check the pulse after 2 minutes or 5 cycles during CPR in adults (and children/infants more than that not seconds).  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the 2-minute / 5-cycle pulse-check interval."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"Per the Should Know About Fact box, during CPR rescuers should change their position after how many minutes or cycles?","options":["After 2 minutes or 5 cycles","After 10 minutes","After 1 cycle","Never change"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states during CPR, rescuers should change their position after 2 minutes or 5 cycles to reduce fatigue.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the 2-minute / 5-cycle rescuer changeover."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"[Rescue Breathing Evaluation table] For an ADULT with no advanced airway, the compression-to-breath cycle is:","options":["15 compressions followed by 1 breath","30 compressions followed by 2 breaths","5 compressions followed by 1 breath","3 compressions followed by 1 breath"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Rescue Breathing Evaluation table lists adult (no advanced airway): 30 compressions followed by 2 breaths.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the adult 30:2 cycle."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"[Rescue Breathing Evaluation table] For a CHILD/INFANT with no advanced airway (single rescuer), the cycle is:","options":["30 compressions followed by 2 breaths","30 compressions followed by 2 breaths for one rescuer and 15 compressions followed by 2 breaths for two rescuers","5 compressions followed by 1 breath","3 compressions followed by 1 breath"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists child/infant (no advanced airway): 30 compressions followed by 2 breaths for one rescuer, and 15 compressions followed by 2 breaths for two rescuers.  Why A wrong: A is correct only for one rescuer; B fully captures both scenarios.  Why C/D wrong: These do not match the table."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"[Rescue Breathing Evaluation table] With an ADVANCED airway in an adult, breathing is given as:","options":["One breath every 2 seconds","One breath every 6 seconds without pause in compressions (10–12 breaths/minute), with continuous compressions","One breath every 30 seconds","No breaths"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists adult with advanced airway: one breath every 6 seconds without pause in compressions (10–12 breaths/minute).  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the 1-breath-every-6-seconds (10–12/min) advanced-airway rate."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"[Rescue Breathing Evaluation table] With an ADVANCED airway in a child/infant, breathing is given as:","options":["One breath every 6 seconds","One breath every 2–3 seconds (20–30 breaths/minute) with continuous compressions","One breath every 30 seconds","3:1 compressions to breaths"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists child/infant with advanced airway: one breath every 2–3 seconds (20–30 breaths/minute).  Why A wrong: 1 breath every 6 seconds is the adult advanced-airway rate.  Why C/D wrong: These do not match the pediatric advanced-airway rate."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"[Rescue Breathing Evaluation table — Duration and Evaluation] With an advanced airway, each breath is delivered over what duration, and the rescuer evaluates for what?","options":["Over 5 seconds; check for pulse only","Each breath over one second; check for chest rise and breathing","Over 10 seconds; check temperature","Over 3 seconds; check blood pressure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table's Duration column states each breath over one second, and the Evaluation column states check for chest rise and breathing.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the one-second duration and chest-rise/breathing evaluation."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"The three figures on p295 illustrating CPR phases are labelled as:","options":["Compression, Airway, Breathing","Pulse, Pressure, Position","Look, Listen, Feel","Assess, Act, Aid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The three figures are labelled Compression, Airway, and Breathing.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the figure labels."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"[Components of High-quality CPR performance] What is the recommended chest compression fraction?","options":[">50%",">80%",">30%","100%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists chest compression fraction >80%.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the >80% compression fraction."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"[Components of High-quality CPR] The compression rate component is:","options":["60–80/min","100–120/min (push fast)","40–60/min","150/min"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists compression rate of 100–120/min (push fast).  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the 100–120/min push-fast rate."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"[Components of High-quality CPR] The recommended compression depth component is:","options":["At least 1 inch","At least 50 mm (2 inches) in adults and at least 1/3 the AP dimension of the chest in infants and children (PUSH HARD)","4 inches in all ages","0.5 inch"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists compression depth at least 50 mm (2 inches) in adults, and at least 1/3 the AP (anterior-posterior) dimension of the chest in infants and children (push hard).  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated adult 2-inch and pediatric one-third AP depth."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"[Components of High-quality CPR] What does the source specify about chest recoil?","options":["Partial recoil acceptable","Allow for full recoil after each compression — no leaning","No recoil needed","Leaning on chest improves output"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists recoil: allow for full recoil after each compression, with no leaning.  Why A/C/D wrong: Incomplete recoil or leaning reduces cardiac filling and effectiveness."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"[Components of High-quality CPR] What does the source state about ventilation (without an advanced airway)?","options":["No ventilation; compressions only","2 breaths after every 30 compressions without an advanced airway; 1 breath every 6 seconds with an advanced airway","1 breath after every 5 compressions","Continuous breaths only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states no excessive ventilation: 2 breaths after every 30 compressions without an advanced airway, and 1 breath every 6 seconds with an advanced airway.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated ventilation components."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"[RECALL/NORCET] Which of the following components is NOT included in metrics of high-quality CPR performance?","options":["Chest compression fraction >80%","Compression rate of 100–120/min","No excessive ventilation","Rescuer should change position every 5 minutes"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The source RECALL box keys \"Rescuer should change position every 5 minutes\" as the option NOT included in high-quality CPR metrics (the change is every 2 minutes/5 cycles, not 5 minutes), so it is the correct answer to \"NOT included.\" The source Right answer is D.  Why A wrong: Compression fraction >80% IS a metric.  Why B wrong: Compression rate 100–120/min IS a metric.  Why C wrong: Avoiding excessive ventilation IS a metric."},{"section":"Batch 027 · nursing care during blood transfusion…","question":"[RECALL/NORCET] During CPR, the chest compression to ventilation ratio should maintain (in an adult, single rescuer):","options":["30:1","15:2","30:2","15:1"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source RECALL box keys 30:2 as the chest-compression-to-ventilation ratio (adult, no advanced airway). The Right answer is C.  Why A wrong: 30:1 is not the standard ratio.  Why B wrong: 15:2 applies to two-rescuer child/infant CPR, not the keyed answer here.  Why D wrong: 15:1 is not a standard CPR ratio."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"When preparing to use an AED on a patient in cardiac arrest, what is the correct sequence of immediate actions?","options":["Defibrillate first, then attach the AED","Attach the AED when available, then listen and perform the steps as directed","Perform 20 minutes of CPR before applying the AED","Listen to the AED before attaching the pads"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page directs the rescuer to \"Attach the AED when available\" and \"Listen and perform the steps as directed.\"  Why A wrong: Defibrillation is delivered by the AED only after it is attached and analyzes the rhythm.  Why C wrong: 20 minutes of CPR is a consideration for terminating efforts in intubated patients, not a prerequisite to AED use.  Why D wrong: You must attach the AED first before it can issue voice prompts to follow."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"Which statement is the first key point to remember when using an AED?","options":["Oxygen is flowing well across the chest during shock delivery","Oxygen is NOT flowing across the patient's chest when delivering shock","Continue chest compressions during shock delivery","The AED works only on patients younger than 8 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A listed key point is \"Ensure oxygen is NOT flowing across the patient's chest when delivering shock.\"  Why A wrong: It states the opposite of the safety requirement.  Why C wrong: Compressions must be stopped during shock delivery, not continued.  Why D wrong: The AED is used across age groups; pad placement differs but it is not limited to children under 8."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"After delivering a shock with the AED, how long should chest compressions be stopped?","options":["Do NOT stop chest compressions for more than 10 seconds when assessing the rhythm","Stop compressions for at least 2 minutes","Stop compressions for 30 seconds","Stop compressions for 1 minute while the AED recharges"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The key point states \"Do NOT stop chest compressions for more than 10 seconds when assessing the rhythm.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: All exceed the 10-second maximum interruption limit specified, which would compromise perfusion."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"According to the AED key points, when should the rescuer stay clear of the patient?","options":["During chest compressions","When delivering rescue breaths","When delivering shock","While attaching the pads"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The key point states \"Stay clear of patient when delivering shock.\"  Why A/B/D wrong: These are hands-on phases of resuscitation; staying clear is specifically required only at the moment the shock is delivered to avoid injury."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"When should the pulse be reassessed after defibrillation according to the AED key points?","options":["After the first 2 minutes of CPR","Immediately after every shock","After 10 minutes of CPR","Pulse should never be reassessed once CPR begins"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states \"Assess pulse after the first 2 minutes of CPR.\"  Why B wrong: Reassessing immediately after each shock interrupts compressions excessively; the source specifies after the first 2 minutes.  Why C/D wrong: Not supported; the specified interval is 2 minutes."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"During CPR, if the end-tidal CO2 is less than 10 mmHg after 20 minutes in an intubated individual, what does this finding suggest?","options":["Add a vasopressor and increase compressions; consider stopping resuscitation attempts","The patient has spontaneously recovered","Increase the ventilation rate immediately","Discontinue oxygen but continue compressions indefinitely"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states if end-tidal CO2 is less than 10 mmHg during CPR, consider adding a vasopressor and improving chest compressions; if it remains low after 20 minutes of CPR in an intubated individual, you may consider stopping resuscitation attempts.  Why B wrong: Persistently low ETCO2 indicates poor perfusion/return, not recovery.  Why C/D wrong: Not the actions described; the source addresses vasopressor use, compression quality, and decision to terminate efforts."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"Before applying an AED, which finding confirms the patient meets criteria for AED use? (Select all that apply.)","options":["The victim does not respond to shouting or shaking their shoulders","The victim is not breathing or breathing ineffectively","The carotid artery pulse cannot be detected","The victim has a strong, regular radial pulse","The victim is conversing normally"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The \"Criteria to apply AED\" lists unresponsiveness to shouting/shaking, absent or ineffective breathing, and an undetectable carotid pulse.  Why D/E wrong: A strong pulse and normal conversation indicate the patient is responsive and perfusing, so an AED is not indicated."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"For pad placement of an AED in adults and children over 8 years old, where is the second pad placed?","options":["Over the sternum","Over the chest, just below the clavicle (collar bone)","Just below the left armpit (anterior-lateral)","On the abdomen"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: For adults and children over 8 years, one pad goes above the nipple on the right hand side just below the collar bone, and the second pad is placed just below the victim's left armpit (anterior-lateral).  Why B wrong: That describes the first (upper) pad position, not the second.  Why A/D wrong: AED pads are not placed on the sternum or abdomen in this anterior-lateral scheme."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"For children under 8 years old and infants, which AED pad placement scheme is used?","options":["Anterior-lateral only","Anterior and posterior (front and back) — one pad on the front of the chest and the second pad to the back","Both pads on the abdomen","One pad on each arm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For children under 8 years and infants the placement is anterior and posterior (front and back): one pad on the front of the chest and the second pad to the back.  Why A wrong: Anterior-lateral is the adult/older-child scheme.  Why C/D wrong: Not described; anterior-posterior chest placement is specified."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"Which of the following is a common CPR mistake to avoid? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Too shallow chest compressions","Too slow chest compressions","Not fully releasing the victim's chest","Excessive ventilation when giving rescue breaths","Bouncing on the chest"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists too shallow compressions, too slow compressions, not fully releasing (recoil) the chest, excessive ventilation, bouncing on the chest, rescuer fatigue, and stopping to check for a pulse as common CPR mistakes to avoid.  Why none wrong: All listed options are explicitly named mistakes."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"Why is \"stopping to check for a pulse\" listed as a common CPR mistake to avoid?","options":["Because checking a pulse is never useful in cardiac arrest","Because it interrupts high-quality compressions; rescuer fatigue and pulse checks reduce CPR effectiveness","Because the pulse is always present during arrest","Because pulse checks should be done every 10 seconds"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The list groups stopping to check for a pulse with rescuer fatigue and bouncing as errors that interrupt or degrade effective compressions.  Why A wrong: Pulse checks have a role, but frequent interruptions during compressions are the named mistake.  Why C wrong: In arrest a pulse is typically absent, which is the reason CPR is ongoing.  Why D wrong: Frequent pulse-check interruptions are exactly what is discouraged."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"What is the recommended compression depth for an adult during CPR?","options":["At least 1 inch or 2.5 cm","At least 2 inches or 5 cm","1/3 to 1/2 the depth of the chest","3 inches or 7 cm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The CPR summary table lists adult compression depth as \"At least 2 inches or 5 cm.\"  Why A/D wrong: Incorrect depths; 2 inches/5 cm is the standard adult depth.  Why C wrong: 1/3 to 1/2 chest depth is the depth used for children and infants, not adults."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"According to the CPR summary table, what compression depth is used for a child (1 year to puberty)?","options":["At least 2 inches or 5 cm","1/3 to 1/2 depth of chest (about 2 inches or 5 cm)","1/3 depth of chest (about 1.5 inches or 4 cm)","At least 3 inches"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For a child, the table gives 1/3 to 1/2 the depth of the chest (about 2 inches or 5 cm).  Why A wrong: \"At least 2 inches/5 cm\" is the adult value.  Why C wrong: 1/3 depth (~1.5 inches/4 cm) is the infant value.  Why D wrong: 3 inches exceeds the stated child depth."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"What compression depth is recommended for an infant (birth to 1 year)?","options":["At least 2 inches or 5 cm","1/3 to 1/2 depth of chest","1/3 depth of chest (about 1.5 inches or 4 cm)","At least 3 inches or 7 cm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table specifies infant compression depth as 1/3 the depth of the chest, about 1.5 inches or 4 cm.  Why A/D wrong: These are larger than the infant depth.  Why B wrong: 1/3 to 1/2 depth is the child value; infant is specifically 1/3."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"What compression technique and landmark is used for an adult during CPR?","options":["Two fingers on the lower half of the sternum","Two interlocking hands on the lower half (1/2) of the sternum","One hand on the upper third of the sternum","Two thumbs encircling the chest"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists adult technique as two interlocking hands on the lower half (1/2) of the sternum.  Why A wrong: Two fingers (or two thumbs) is the infant technique.  Why C wrong: Compressions are on the lower half of the sternum, not upper third.  Why D wrong: Two-thumb encircling is for infants."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"What is the correct compression technique and landmark for a child during CPR?","options":["Two interlocking hands on the lower half of the sternum","Two thumbs on the upper sternum","One or two hands on the lower half (1/2) of the sternum","Two fingers on the lower third of the sternum"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: For a child, the table indicates one or two hands on the lower half (1/2) of the sternum.  Why A wrong: Two interlocking hands is the adult method.  Why D wrong: Two fingers/lower third is the infant approach.  Why B wrong: Compressions are on the lower half of the sternum, not upper."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"Which technique describes infant chest compressions during CPR?","options":["Two interlocking hands on the sternum","Two fingers or two thumbs on the lower third (1/3) of the sternum (newborn: encircle)","One hand on the lower half of the sternum","Heel of one hand on the upper sternum"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists infant technique as two fingers or two thumbs on the lower third (1/3) of the sternum, with a newborn encircling method noted.  Why A/C wrong: Hands-based techniques are for adults/children.  Why D wrong: Upper sternum is incorrect; the lower third is the landmark."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"What is the compression rate per minute used across adults, children, and infants per the CPR summary table?","options":["At least 60/min","At least 100/min","At least 150/min","Exactly 80/min"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists the compression rate as \"at least 100/min\" for adult, child, and infant.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated rate of at least 100 per minute."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"How long should each compression cycle take according to the CPR summary table?","options":["0.5 second","1 second","2 seconds","3 seconds"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Each compression is listed as 1 second for adult, child, and infant.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated 1-second duration per compression."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"Where is the pulse checked in an adult during CPR per the summary table?","options":["Brachial artery (upper arm)","Carotid artery (throat)","Femoral artery","Radial artery (wrist)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For adults the pulse check location is the carotid artery (throat).  Why A wrong: Brachial (upper arm) is the infant pulse-check site.  Why C wrong: Femoral is listed for children (carotid throat or femoral artery groin), not the adult-only entry.  Why D wrong: Radial is not the CPR pulse-check site listed."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"What is the correct pulse-check site for a child versus an infant during CPR?","options":["Child: brachial; Infant: carotid","Child: carotid (throat) or femoral artery (groin); Infant: brachial artery (upper arm)","Child: radial; Infant: femoral","Child: femoral only; Infant: carotid only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists the child pulse-check as carotid (throat) or femoral artery (groin), and the infant as brachial artery (upper arm).  Why A/C/D wrong: They mismatch the sites listed in the table."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"For a single rescuer (\"one-rescuer\"), what compression-to-ventilation ratio is used for an adult?","options":["15:2","30:2","30:1","5:1"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For adult one-rescuer CPR the ratio is 30:2.  Why A wrong: 15:2 is the two-rescuer ratio for children and infants.  Why C/D wrong: Not the stated ratios."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"What is the two-rescuer compression-to-ventilation ratio for an adult versus for a child/infant?","options":["Adult 30:2; Child/Infant 15:2 (Newborn 3:1)","Adult 15:2; Child/Infant 30:2","Adult 30:2; Child/Infant 30:2","Adult 5:1; Child/Infant 3:1"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists two-rescuer ratios as 30:2 for adult, 15:2 for child and infant, and notes Newborn 3:1.  Why B wrong: It reverses the adult and child values.  Why C wrong: Child/infant two-rescuer is 15:2, not 30:2.  Why D wrong: Those are not the stated two-rescuer ratios."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"When working alone on a child or infant in cardiac arrest, what does the CPR summary table advise about calling 911?","options":["Call 911 before starting any CPR regardless of cause","Call after establishing unresponsiveness and 2 minutes of resuscitation (5 cycles), unless heart disease is present","Never call until 20 minutes of CPR are complete","Call only after the child regains a pulse"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For a child/infant when working alone, the table directs calling after establishing unresponsiveness and 2 minutes of resuscitation (5 cycles), unless heart disease is present.  Why A wrong: For the adult, the table advises calling 911 upon establishing unresponsiveness before beginning resuscitation (witnessed collapse, injury, overdose); the child/infant guidance differs by performing CPR first.  Why C/D wrong: Not supported by the table."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"When working alone with an adult, what does the table advise regarding emergency activation?","options":["Perform 5 cycles before calling","Call after establishing unresponsiveness before beginning resuscitation (witnessed submersion, injury, or overdose)","Do not call until ROSC","Call only if heart disease is present"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For the adult, the table directs calling 911/emergency dispatcher after establishing unresponsiveness before beginning resuscitation (e.g., submersion, injury, or overdose).  Why A wrong: Performing cycles first is the child/infant guidance.  Why C/D wrong: Not consistent with the adult column."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"What is the first step in basic AED operation?","options":["Press the \"Shock\" button","Power on the AED","Attach the pads","Choose adult or pediatric pads"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The basic AED operation list begins with \"Power on the AED.\"  Why A wrong: The shock button is pressed only after analysis advises a shock.  Why C/D wrong: Pads are chosen and attached after powering the unit on."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"When attaching AED pads, the source instructs to attach them to which area of the chest?","options":["Over clothing for speed","To bare chest (not over medication patches), and make sure cables are connected","Only over a dry shirt","Directly over a pacemaker"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The steps say attach the pads to bare chest (not over medication patches) and make sure cables are connected.  Why A/C wrong: Pads must contact bare skin, not clothing.  Why D wrong: Pads should not be placed over medication patches; placing over devices is avoided (the chest must be bare and dry)."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"During AED operation, when the AED states \"no shock advised,\" what should the rescuer do?","options":["Press the shock button anyway","Restart CPR","Remove the pads and end resuscitation","Wait 5 minutes before any action"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The steps state if the AED says \"no shock advised,\" restart CPR.  Why A wrong: A shock is delivered only when advised; pressing it against advice is unsafe and ineffective.  Why C/D wrong: Resuscitation continues with CPR rather than stopping or pausing."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"When the AED indicates a shock is needed, what must the rescuer do before pressing \"CLEAR!\" and shocking?","options":["Continue compressions through the shock","Clear the individual, making sure no one is touching them and that the oxygen has been removed","Connect more cables","Increase the AED energy manually"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The steps state clear the individual, ensure no one is touching them and the oxygen has been removed, then shout \"CLEAR!\" and press the shock button.  Why A wrong: Compressions must stop and everyone must be clear during the shock.  Why C/D wrong: Not part of the stated shock-delivery procedure."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"Immediately after the AED delivers a shock, what should the rescuer do?","options":["Wait for the AED to advise before any action","Resume CPR starting with chest compressions","Check the carotid pulse for 30 seconds","Remove the pads"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The steps direct \"Immediately resume CPR starting with chest compressions\" after the shock.  Why A wrong: CPR is resumed at once, not after waiting.  Why C wrong: The cycle (2 minutes) precedes rhythm/pulse reassessment.  Why D wrong: Pads remain on for continued analysis."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"How long should CPR be performed after a shock before the AED analyzes the rhythm again?","options":["30 seconds","1 minute","2 minutes (5 cycles), after which the AED analyzes the rhythm","5 minutes"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The steps state after two minutes of CPR, analyze the rhythm with the AED, then continue to follow the AED prompts.  Why A/B/D wrong: The interval before reanalysis is 2 minutes, not the others listed."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"When giving mouth-to-mouth ventilation to adults and older children, why should the rescuer NOT give breaths too rapidly or forcefully?","options":["It cools the patient","It may cause air to be forced into the stomach, resulting in distension and less room for lung expansion, which may cause vomiting","It raises blood pressure dangerously","It damages the AED pads"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page warns that breathing too rapidly or with too much force forces air into the stomach, causing distension, less room for lung expansion, and possible vomiting.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the stated consequences; gastric distension and vomiting are the concerns."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"When performing mouth-to-mouth ventilation on an adult/older child, how should the airway be opened?","options":["Jaw-thrust only","Open the airway using the head-tilt/chin-lift maneuver","Hyperextend the neck maximally","Flex the neck forward"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The steps direct opening the airway using the head-tilt/chin-lift maneuver (Figure a).  Why A wrong: Jaw-thrust is not the maneuver named here.  Why C/D wrong: Hyperextension and flexion are not the described technique; head-tilt/chin-lift is."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"During adult/older-child mouth-to-mouth ventilation, what should the rescuer do to create a seal and what action is taken if the first breath does not make the chest rise?","options":["Pinch the person's nose closed with your hand, give one full second breath; if the chest does not rise, tilt the victim's head farther back and reattempt","Cover the nose and mouth with one hand and abandon ventilation","Press firmly on the abdomen and give a slow breath","Hyperventilate at 30 breaths per minute"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states to pinch the person's nose closed with your hand, create a seal with your lips, blow into the mouth for one full second, and if the chest does not rise, tilt the head farther back so the chest does rise.  Why B/C/D wrong: These contradict the described pinch-nose, seal, one-second breath, and reposition-and-retry technique."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"When performing infant mouth-to-mouth/nose ventilation, why must the rescuer give the breath gently over one second and only enough to make the chest rise?","options":["Because an infant's lungs are larger than an adult's","Because an infant's lungs are smaller than an adult's and need a smaller volume of air, to avoid over-inflation","Because infants require forceful breaths","Because the rescuer should not watch the chest"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page notes the infant's lungs are smaller and need a smaller volume of air, so the rescuer should blow gently over one second and watch for the chest to rise, adjusting the air pressure as needed.  Why A wrong: Infant lungs are smaller, not larger.  Why C wrong: Forceful breaths risk over-inflation; gentle breaths are required.  Why D wrong: The rescuer should watch the chest to gauge adequacy."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"What airway maneuver is recommended for infant ventilation, and why caution against over-extension?","options":["Jaw thrust; to open the mouth fully","Head-tilt/chin-lift in a neutral position; be sure not to hyper-extend the neck, aiming for a neutral position","Full neck flexion; to align the airway","Maximal hyperextension; to straighten the trachea"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page instructs to gently tilt the infant's head using the head-tilt/chin-lift maneuver to a neutral position, being sure not to hyper-extend the neck.  Why A/C/D wrong: Jaw-thrust, flexion, and hyperextension contradict the neutral head-tilt/chin-lift guidance for infants."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"For infant mouth-to-nose rescue breathing, how should the rescuer create the seal?","options":["Cover only the infant's mouth and pinch the nose","Pinch the infant's nose closed and seal the lips over the mouth","Cover the infant's mouth AND nose with your mouth and create a seal, then blow gently for one second","Use a pocket mask exclusively"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: For an infant, the rescuer covers the infant's mouth and nose with their mouth, creates a seal, and gives a breath gently over one second to make the chest rise.  Why A/B wrong: Pinching the nose and sealing only the mouth is the adult/older-child method; infants are covered mouth-and-nose.  Why D wrong: A pocket mask is for the mouth-to-mask method, not the described mouth-to-mouth/nose technique."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"During infant mouth-to-mouth rescue breathing, what does the source say to do for the additional breath?","options":["Give the breath through the nose only with the mouth uncovered","Open the airway using the head-tilt/chin-lift maneuver, pinch the infant's nose, seal lips around the infant's mouth, blow gently for one second, and watch for the chest to rise","Press on the chest while blowing","Deliver two rapid forceful breaths"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For the additional breath (mouth-to-mouth method) the page directs to open the airway with head-tilt/chin-lift, pinch the infant's nose closed, create a seal over the infant's mouth, blow gently into the mouth for one second, and watch for the chest to rise.  Why A/C/D wrong: They contradict the stated sequence of airway opening, nose pinch, lip seal, and gentle one-second breath."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"For one-on-one rescuer CPR using a pocket mask in adult mouth-to-mask ventilation, what hand technique is described for an older child or infant?","options":["Use both palms flat over the mask","Deliver 15 high-quality compressions while counting out loud","Seal the mask against the person's face by placing four fingers of one hand across the top of the mask and the thumb of the other hand along the bottom edge of the mask","Hold the mask with one finger only"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page describes sealing the mask against the face by placing four fingers of one hand across the top of the mask and the thumb of the other hand along the bottom edge of the mask.  Why B wrong: 15 high-quality compressions is referenced as the compression count to supply breaths during one-on-one CPR, not the mask-sealing hand technique.  Why A/D wrong: Not the described four-fingers/thumb sealing method."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"In one-on-one rescuer CPR with a pocket mask, after how many compressions should breaths be supplied per the source, and how is the airway opened during mask ventilation?","options":["After every 15 compressions; open the airway with the head-tilt/chin-lift maneuver","After every 5 compressions; jaw-thrust","After every 2 compressions; neck flexion","After every 30 compressions; no airway maneuver"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page notes that in one-on-one rescuer CPR breaths should be supplied using a pocket mask, delivering 15 high-quality chest compressions per cycle for a child or infant, and using the head-tilt/chin-lift maneuver to open the airway during mask ventilation.  Why B/C/D wrong: They do not match the 15-compression count or the head-tilt/chin-lift maneuver described."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"During mouth-to-mask ventilation, how long should each breath be delivered, and what should the rescuer ensure before giving the next breath?","options":["Press firmly with the mask and blow one full second per breath, ensuring the chest does not rise","Press firmly around the edges of the mask and ventilate by delivering a breath over one second; ensure the chest rises before delivering a breath every second one","Deliver a 3-second breath while watching for distension","Give two breaths simultaneously"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page directs pressing firmly around the edges of the mask and ventilating by delivering a breath over one second, ensuring the chest rises before delivering a breath about every second one.  Why A wrong: The chest should rise (not \"not rise\") with an adequate breath.  Why C/D wrong: Breaths are one second each and given one at a time, not 3 seconds or simultaneously."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"Why is the intraosseous (IO) route now preferred over endotracheal (ET) drug administration when IV access is unavailable in CPR?","options":["The ET route delivers drugs faster than IO","Through the ET tube, drug absorption is poor and optimal dosing is unknown; therefore the IO route is preferred when IV access is not available","IO access is contraindicated in adults","ET drugs cause less variability in absorption"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states that through the ET tube absorption of drugs is poor and optimal dosing is unknown; therefore the IO route is now preferred when IV access is not available.  Why A wrong: The page favors IO over ET, not the reverse.  Why C wrong: IO can be placed in less than one minute in all age groups and has more predictable absorption.  Why D wrong: ET absorption is poor/variable, which is precisely why IO is preferred."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"What is the preferred route for drug and fluid administration during CPR when central access is not yet available?","options":["Endotracheal route","A peripheral IV is preferred for drug and fluid administration unless central access is already available","Subcutaneous route","Oral route"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states a peripheral IV is preferred for drug and fluid administration unless central access is already available.  Why A wrong: ET is a last resort due to poor absorption.  Why C/D wrong: SC and oral routes are not used for resuscitation drug delivery in CPR."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"When using a peripheral IV route to administer a drug during CPR, what should follow drug administration to move the drug into central circulation? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Intravenous push bolus of the drug","Flush with 20 mL of 0.9% Normal Saline","Raise the extremity for 10-20 seconds to enhance delivery of drug to circulation","Lower the extremity below heart level for 5 minutes","Withhold all fluids"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists: give the drug via peripheral IV route by IV push bolus, flush with 20 mL of 0.9% Normal Saline, and raise the extremity for 10-20 seconds to enhance delivery of the drug to circulation.  Why D wrong: The extremity is raised, not lowered, and not for 5 minutes.  Why E wrong: A saline flush is given, not withheld."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"Why is central venous access not necessary during most resuscitation attempts?","options":["Because central lines cannot deliver drugs","Because it may cause interruptions in CPR and complications during insertion; a peripheral line does not require CPR interruption","Because peripheral access is contraindicated in arrest","Because central access is faster than peripheral in all cases"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states central line access is not necessary during most resuscitation attempts because it may cause interruptions in CPR and complications during insertion, and placing a peripheral line does not require CPR interruption.  Why A wrong: Central lines can deliver drugs; the issue is interruption/complication risk.  Why C/D wrong: Peripheral access is in fact preferred and avoids interruptions."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"Regarding the intraosseous (IO) route during CPR, which statements are correct? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Drugs and fluids can be delivered safely and effectively using the IO route if IV access is not available","IO access can be used for all age groups","IO can be placed in less than one minute","IO has more predictable absorption than the ET route","IO is contraindicated when IV access is unavailable"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The page states drugs and fluids can be delivered safely and effectively via IO if IV access is unavailable, IO can be used for all age groups, can be placed in less than one minute, and has more predictable absorption than the ET route.  Why E wrong: IO is specifically indicated (not contraindicated) when IV access is not available."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"According to the \"Should Know About Facts,\" if the AED is not working properly, what should the rescuer do?","options":["Stop all resuscitation","Continue CPR; do NOT waste excessive time troubleshooting the AED — CPR always comes first, and the AED is supplemental","Replace the battery before continuing","Wait for advanced personnel before resuming CPR"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The facts state if the AED is not working properly, continue CPR and do not waste excessive time troubleshooting the AED; CPR comes first and the AED is supplemental.  Why A/D wrong: CPR continues rather than stopping or waiting.  Why C wrong: The emphasis is on continuing CPR, not delaying for AED repair."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"Per the \"Should Know\" facts, who is responsible for managing the integration of a multidisciplinary team during resuscitation?","options":["The bystander who called 911","A system of care (e.g., the rapid response team [RRT], cardiac arrest team, and intensive care specialists) to increase survival rates","The pharmacy alone","The patient's family"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The facts describe management of the interdisciplinary emergencies requiring the integration of a multidisciplinary team, such as a system of care that can include the RRT, cardiac arrest team, and intensive care specialists, to increase survival rates.  Why A/C/D wrong: Coordinated clinical teams (a system of care), not bystanders, pharmacy alone, or family, manage the integrated resuscitation response."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"According to the \"Should Know\" facts, any ACLS drug or fluid that can be given intravenously can also be given by which route?","options":["Subcutaneously","Intraosseously","Orally","Topically"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The facts state any ACLS drug or fluid that can be administered intravenously can also be given intraosseously.  Why A/C/D wrong: These routes are not stated as equivalent to IV for ACLS drug delivery."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"Per the \"Should Know\" facts, the recommended dose of epinephrine during CPR in neonatal resuscitation is which of the following?","options":["1 mg IV undiluted","0.01 to 0.03 mg/kg IV in dilution 1:10000","2 to 4 mg/kg/min IV","150 mg IV bolus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The facts give the recommended epinephrine dose in neonatal resuscitation as 0.01 to 0.03 mg/kg IV in a 1:10,000 dilution.  Why A wrong: 1 mg 1:10,000 IV every 3-5 minutes is the adult epinephrine dose, not the neonatal dose.  Why C wrong: 2-4 mg/kg/min reflects dopamine-type infusion, not neonatal epinephrine.  Why D wrong: 150 mg is an amiodarone-type dose, not epinephrine."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"Per the \"Should Know\" facts, the recommended dose of epinephrine during CPR for adults is which of the following?","options":["1 mg with no dilution","1 mg in a 1:10,000 dilution given every 3-5 minutes","0.5 mg every 10 minutes","3 mg in a 1:1000 dilution once"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The facts state the recommended epinephrine for adults is 1 mg with no dilution (1:10,000 dilution) given every 3-5 minutes.  Why A wrong: Although phrased \"no dilution,\" the source specifies the 1:10,000 dilution dosing every 3-5 minutes; 1 mg alone without the interval/dilution is incomplete.  Why C/D wrong: These doses, intervals, and dilutions are not the stated adult regimen."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"According to the facts, what is the role of the ADR (adrenaline/epinephrine) dose given during CPR every 3-5 minutes?","options":["To slow the heart rate","To produce sufficient blood flow as possible, allowing the chest soak (return to normal position) completely after each delivery at also breath and each compression","To eliminate the need for compressions","To reverse a bradyarrhythmia only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The facts note there is an ADR dose introduction during CPR given every 3-5 minutes, intended to produce as much blood flow as possible, allowing the chest to recoil (return to normal) completely after each compression and breath.  Why A wrong: Epinephrine increases perfusion pressure; it is not given to slow the rate during arrest.  Why C wrong: It supplements, but does not replace, compressions.  Why D wrong: Its CPR role is perfusion support, not solely bradyarrhythmia reversal."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"Per the \"Should Know\" facts, what is \"Bailey Maneuver\" in the context of resuscitation airway management?","options":["A chest-thrust technique for choking","The replacement of an endotracheal tube (ETT) with a laryngeal airway device (LAD) to maintain a patent airway at the initial surgery, allowing the LAD to be removed smoothly once the patient regains consciousness","A method of two-rescuer compressions","A pulse-check sequence"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The facts describe \"Bailey Maneuver\" as the replacement of an endotracheal tube (ETT) with a laryngeal airway device (LAD) to maintain a patent airway at the initial surgery and allow smooth removal once the patient regains consciousness.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the described airway-device exchange maneuver."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"When an AED (or defibrillator) is connected, what does the source advise about minimizing interruptions of CPR?","options":["Stop CPR while switching rescuers and shock indicators","Minimize interruptions of CPR while switching rescuers and shock the indicator; minimize interruptions of CPR, resume CPR as soon as possible when switching compressions","Pause CPR for 60 seconds at each rhythm check","Avoid all rescuer switches"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The facts advise minimizing interruptions of CPR while the AED is connected, including when switching rescuers and around shock indications, and resuming CPR as soon as possible.  Why A/C wrong: They prolong interruptions, contrary to the principle of minimizing them.  Why D wrong: Switching rescuers is allowed (to combat fatigue) but should be done with minimal CPR interruption."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"During CPR, when chest compression to ventilation rate for adults is 30:2, if advanced airway (e.g., endotracheal tube) is placed, what change is recommended?","options":["Stop compressions to ventilate","Do not interrupt chest compressions for ventilation; give one breath about every 6 seconds with continuous chest compressions","Increase the ratio to 30:5","Stop ventilation entirely"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The facts state during CPR, once an advanced airway is placed, do not interrupt compressions for ventilation; give one breath about every 6 seconds with continuous chest compressions.  Why A wrong: With an advanced airway, compressions are continuous and are not stopped to ventilate.  Why C/D wrong: The 30:5 ratio is not described, and ventilation continues at the asynchronous rate."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"What is the main ACLS indication for Adenosine according to the pharmacological summary?","options":["Symptomatic bradycardia","Narrow PSVT/SVT and wide-QRS tachycardia where adenosine in irregular wide QRS is avoided","Cardiac arrest","Tachycardia rate control with beta blockade"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The ACLS drug table lists Adenosine for narrow PSVT/SVT and notes that in wide-QRS tachycardia adenosine should be avoided in irregular wide QRS.  Why A wrong: Symptomatic bradycardia is the indication for atropine.  Why C wrong: Cardiac arrest is treated with epinephrine.  Why D wrong: Adenosine is not a rate-control agent for tachycardia in the listed sense."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"What is the dose/route of Adenosine in the ACLS drug summary?","options":["6 mg IV bolus, may repeat with 12 mg IV","1 mg IV every 3-5 minutes","150 mg IV bolus","0.5 to 1 mg IV push"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table gives Adenosine 6 mg IV bolus, which may repeat with 12 mg IV.  Why B wrong: 1 mg q3-5 min reflects epinephrine.  Why C wrong: 150 mg reflects amiodarone.  Why D wrong: 0.5-1 mg push reflects atropine-type dosing."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"Which \"Remarks\" are associated with Adenosine in the ACLS table? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Rapid IV push close to the hub, followed by saline bolus","Continuous cardiac monitoring during administration","Causes flushing and chest heaviness","Minimum dose 1 mg","Used cautiously in renal failure"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The table's Adenosine remarks include rapid IV push close to the hub followed by a saline bolus, continuous cardiac monitoring during administration, and that it causes flushing and chest heaviness.  Why D wrong: \"Minimum dose 1 mg\" is an atropine remark.  Why E wrong: \"Used cautiously in renal failure\" is associated with lidocaine, not adenosine."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"According to the ACLS table, Amiodarone is indicated for which conditions? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Ventricular fibrillation (VF)","Pulseless ventricular tachycardia (VT)","Ventricular tachycardia (VT) with pulse","Tachycardia rate control","Symptomatic bradycardia"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists Amiodarone for ventricular fibrillation, pulseless VT, VT with pulse, and tachycardia rate control.  Why E wrong: Symptomatic bradycardia is an atropine indication, not amiodarone."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"Per the ACLS table, what are key remarks/cautions for Amiodarone? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Anticipate hypotension, bradycardia, and gastrointestinal toxicity","Continuous cardiac monitoring","Very long half-life (up to 40 days)","Do not use in 2nd or 3rd degree heart block","Causes flushing and chest heaviness"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The Amiodarone remarks include anticipating hypotension, bradycardia, and GI toxicity; continuous cardiac monitoring; a very long half-life (up to 40 days); and not using it in 2nd or 3rd degree heart block, including not administering via the ET tube route.  Why E wrong: Flushing and chest heaviness are adenosine remarks."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"What is the primary indication for Atropine in the ACLS drug summary?","options":["Cardiac arrest","Symptomatic bradycardia","Ventricular fibrillation","PSVT"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists Atropine for symptomatic bradycardia (and a specific dose for toxin/overdose such as organophosphate [OPS] poisoning).  Why A wrong: Cardiac arrest is treated with epinephrine.  Why C wrong: VF is treated with amiodarone/defibrillation.  Why D wrong: PSVT is treated with adenosine."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"What is the standard symptomatic-bradycardia dose of Atropine listed in the ACLS table?","options":["1 mg IV every 3 to 5 minutes, max dose 3 mg, minimum dose 1 mg","6 mg IV bolus","150 mg IV bolus","0.01 mg/kg only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table gives Atropine 1 mg IV every 3 to 5 minutes, with a maximum dose of 3 mg and a minimum dose of 1 mg.  Why B wrong: 6 mg IV reflects adenosine.  Why C wrong: 150 mg reflects amiodarone.  Why D wrong: Not the listed adult atropine dose."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"For toxin or overdose (such as organophosphate poisoning), what Atropine dosing does the table provide?","options":["1 mg once","2 to 4 mg IV every 30 minutes (the dose may be repeated)","0.01 mg/kg","150 mg over 10 minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists for toxin/overdose (e.g., OPS poisoning) an Atropine dose of 2 to 4 mg IV that may be repeated every 30 minutes.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the listed toxin/overdose dosing."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"Which remark applies to Atropine in the ACLS table?","options":["Do not use in glaucoma or tachyarrhythmias; cardiac and BP monitoring","Used cautiously in renal failure","Very long half-life up to 40 days","Fluid resuscitation first"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Atropine remarks include cardiac and BP monitoring and \"Do not use in glaucoma or tachyarrhythmias.\"  Why B wrong: Renal-failure caution is a lidocaine remark.  Why C wrong: 40-day half-life is amiodarone.  Why D wrong: \"Fluid resuscitation first\" is the dopamine remark."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"Per the ACLS table, Dopamine is indicated for which conditions, and what is a key remark?","options":["Shock/CHF and symptomatic bradycardia; remark — fluid resuscitation first and cardiac and BP monitoring","Cardiac arrest only; remark — give via central line","Ventricular fibrillation; remark — do not prolong QT","PSVT; remark — flushing and chest heaviness"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists Dopamine for shock/CHF and symptomatic bradycardia, with remarks of fluid resuscitation first and cardiac and BP monitoring.  Why B/C/D wrong: These uses/remarks belong to other agents (epinephrine, amiodarone, adenosine)."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"What dose/route does the ACLS table give for Dopamine?","options":["2 to 20 mcg/kg/min IV titrate to desired blood pressure","6 mg IV bolus","1 mg IV every 3-5 minutes","150 mg over 10 minutes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists Dopamine 2 to 20 mcg/kg/min IV, titrated to the desired blood pressure.  Why B/C/D wrong: These reflect adenosine, epinephrine, and amiodarone, respectively."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"For cardiac arrest, what Epinephrine dose/route does the ACLS table specify?","options":["1 mg (1:1000) IV/IO; maintain 0.1 to 0.5 mcg/kg/min titrate to desired blood pressure; repeat every 3-5 minutes as needed","6 mg IV bolus","150 mg IV bolus","2 to 20 mcg/kg/min"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table gives Epinephrine for cardiac arrest at 1 mg (1:1000) IV/IO with maintenance 0.1 to 0.5 mcg/kg/min titrated to blood pressure, and repeated every 3-5 minutes as needed.  Why B/C/D wrong: These reflect adenosine, amiodarone, and dopamine dosing, respectively."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"Per the ACLS table, what Epinephrine dose/route is used for anaphylaxis?","options":["0.3 to 0.5 mg IM, repeat every 3-5 minutes as needed","1 mg IV bolus","150 mg over 10 minutes","6 mg IV push"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists Epinephrine for anaphylaxis at 0.3 to 0.5 mg IM, repeated every 3-5 minutes as needed.  Why B wrong: 1 mg IV is the cardiac-arrest dosing scheme.  Why C/D wrong: These reflect amiodarone and adenosine."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"For the symptomatic-bradycardia use of Epinephrine, what infusion does the ACLS table provide?","options":["2 to 10 mcg/min IV infusion titrate to response","1 mg IV bolus every 3-5 minutes","0.3 mg IM once","20 to 50 mg/min IV"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists Epinephrine for symptomatic bradycardia as a 2 to 10 mcg/min IV infusion titrated to response.  Why B wrong: That is the cardiac-arrest bolus regimen.  Why C wrong: 0.3 mg IM is the anaphylaxis dose.  Why D wrong: 20-50 mg/min reflects procainamide."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"What are the listed indications for Lidocaine in the ACLS table?","options":["Cardiac arrest (VF/VT) and wide-complex tachycardia with pulse (when amiodarone is not available)","Symptomatic bradycardia and shock","PSVT and anaphylaxis","Torsades de Pointes and CHF only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists Lidocaine (a recommended alternative when amiodarone is not available) for cardiac arrest (VF/VT) and for wide-complex tachycardia with pulse.  Why B/C/D wrong: These uses are associated with other agents (dopamine, adenosine/epinephrine, magnesium sulfate)."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"What Lidocaine dosing for cardiac arrest (VF/VT) does the ACLS table provide?","options":["Initial 1 to 1.5 mg/kg IV loading; second 0.5 to 0.75 mg/kg IV in 5-10 minutes; maintain 1 to 4 mg/min","6 mg IV bolus only","1 mg every 3-5 minutes","150 mg IV bolus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists Lidocaine initial 1 to 1.5 mg/kg IV loading, a second dose of 0.5 to 0.75 mg/kg IV in 5-10 minutes, with a maintenance infusion of 1 to 4 mg/min.  Why B/C/D wrong: These reflect adenosine, epinephrine, and amiodarone, respectively."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"Which remarks apply to Lidocaine in the ACLS table? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Cardiac and BP monitoring","Rapid bolus can cause hypotension and bradycardia","Used cautiously in renal failure","Minimum dose 1 mg"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The Lidocaine remarks include cardiac and BP monitoring, that a rapid bolus can cause hypotension and bradycardia, and that it is used cautiously in renal failure.  Why D wrong: \"Minimum dose 1 mg\" is an atropine remark, not lidocaine."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"What are the indications for Magnesium Sulfate in the ACLS table? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Cardiac arrest","Pulseless ventricular tachycardia","Torsades de Pointes","Symptomatic bradycardia","Narrow PSVT"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists Magnesium Sulfate for cardiac arrest, pulseless VT, and Torsades de Pointes.  Why D wrong: Symptomatic bradycardia is an atropine indication.  Why E wrong: Narrow PSVT is an adenosine indication."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"What dosing does the ACLS table provide for Magnesium Sulfate?","options":["Cardiac arrest: 1 to 2 grams IV in D5W IVP; if not cardiac arrest: 1 to 2 g/m IV over 5 minutes; maintain 0.5 to 1 g/m/hr IV","6 mg IV bolus","1 mg IV q3-5 min","20-50 mg/min IV"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table gives Magnesium Sulfate for cardiac arrest as 1 to 2 grams IV in D5W IVP; if not cardiac arrest, 1 to 2 g/m IV over 5 minutes; maintenance 0.5 to 1 g/m/hr IV.  Why B/C/D wrong: These reflect adenosine, epinephrine, and procainamide dosing."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"Which remarks are listed for Magnesium Sulfate in the ACLS table? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Cardiac and BP monitoring","Rapid bolus can cause hypotension and bradycardia","Used with caution in renal failure","Calcium chloride can reverse hypermagnesemia","Do not prolong QT"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The Magnesium Sulfate remarks include cardiac and BP monitoring, that a rapid bolus can cause hypotension and bradycardia, caution in renal failure, and that calcium chloride can reverse hypermagnesemia.  Why E wrong: \"Do not prolong QT\" is associated with procainamide and sotalol, not listed for magnesium sulfate here."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"For Procainamide in the ACLS table, what is true regarding indication and preference?","options":["Indicated for wide-QRS tachycardia and preferred for VT with pulse","Indicated for symptomatic bradycardia","Indicated for cardiac arrest only","Preferred for Torsades de Pointes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists Procainamide for wide-QRS tachycardia and notes it is preferred for VT with pulse.  Why B/C/D wrong: Those indications belong to atropine, epinephrine, and magnesium sulfate respectively."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"What Procainamide dosing/endpoints does the ACLS table specify?","options":["20 to 50 mg/min IV until the rhythm improves, hypotension occurs, the QRS widens by 50% or more, or a max dose of 17 mg/kg is reached; maintenance infusion 1 to 4 mg/min","6 mg IV bolus, repeat 12 mg","1 mg IV every 3-5 minutes","0.01 mg/kg IV"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table gives Procainamide 20 to 50 mg/min IV until the rhythm improves, hypotension occurs, the QRS widens by 50% or more, or the max dose of 17 mg/kg is reached, with a maintenance infusion of 1 to 4 mg/min.  Why B/C/D wrong: These reflect adenosine, epinephrine, and other agents."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"Which remarks apply to Procainamide in the ACLS table? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Cardiac and BP monitoring","Caution with acute MI","May reduce dose with renal failure","Do not give with amiodarone","Do not use if prolonged QT or CHF"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The Procainamide remarks include cardiac and BP monitoring, caution with acute MI, may reduce the dose with renal failure, do not give with amiodarone, and do not use if QT is prolonged or in CHF.  Why none wrong: All five options are listed remarks for procainamide."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"What are the listed indications for Sotalol in the ACLS table? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Tachyarrhythmia","Monomorphic VT","2nd or 3rd line arrhythmias","Symptomatic bradycardia","Cardiac arrest"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists Sotalol for tachyarrhythmia, monomorphic VT, and as a 2nd or 3rd line arrhythmia agent.  Why D wrong: Symptomatic bradycardia is an atropine indication.  Why E wrong: Cardiac arrest is an epinephrine indication."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"What dose/route and key remark does the ACLS table give for Sotalol?","options":["100 mg (1.5 mg/kg) IV over 5 minutes; remark — do not use in prolonged QT","6 mg IV bolus; remark — flushing","1 mg q3-5 min; remark — cardiac arrest","20-50 mg/min IV; remark — caution in MI"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table gives Sotalol 100 mg (1.5 mg/kg) IV over 5 minutes, with the remark \"Do not use in prolonged QT.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: These reflect adenosine, epinephrine, and procainamide entries."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"In the Pediatric BLS for Lay Rescuers sequence, what is Step 1?","options":["Shout for help","Make sure the scene is safe; check to see if the person is awake and breathing normally","Begin compressions immediately","Attach the AED"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Step 1 is to make sure the scene is safe and check whether the person is awake and breathing normally.  Why A wrong: Shouting for help is Step 2.  Why C/D wrong: Compressions and AED use follow scene assessment."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"In Pediatric BLS Step 2 (shout for help), when a lay rescuer is alone, what does the source direct?","options":["Leave immediately to find help","With a cell phone, phone 911, perform CPR (30 compressions and 2 breaths for 5 cycles), and then phone 911 and get an AED","Wait silently for help","Give two breaths only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Step 2 (\"if you're alone\") directs the rescuer, with a cell phone, to phone 911, perform CPR — 30 compressions and 2 breaths for 5 cycles — and then phone 911 and get an AED. Without a cell phone, perform CPR (30 compressions and 2 breaths) for 5 cycles, then phone and get an AED. If help is available, phone 911 and get the AED while someone starts CPR.  Why A/C/D wrong: They contradict the directed sequence of CPR cycles with emergency activation and AED retrieval."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"In Pediatric BLS Step 3, for a CHILD, where and how deep should compressions be delivered?","options":["Two fingers over the abdomen","Push in the middle of the chest at least one-third (1/3) the depth of the chest, approximately 2 inches, with one or two hands","Encircle the chest with both thumbs","Push on the upper sternum 1 inch deep"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For a child, Step 3 directs pushing in the middle of the chest at least one-third the depth of the chest (approximately 2 inches) with one or two hands.  Why A wrong: Compressions are on the chest, not the abdomen.  Why C wrong: Two-thumb encircling is the infant technique.  Why D wrong: Upper sternum and 1 inch are incorrect landmarks/depths."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"In Pediatric BLS Step 3, for an INFANT, what is the correct compression technique and depth?","options":["One hand on the lower half of the sternum, 2 inches","Push in the middle of the chest at least one-third (1/3) the depth of the chest with two fingers; continue CPR until EMS arrives","Encircle with both hands and compress 5 cm","Compress the upper third of the sternum with the palm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For an infant, Step 3 directs pushing in the middle of the chest at least one-third the depth of the chest with two fingers, and continuing CPR until EMS arrives.  Why A wrong: One hand/2 inches is the child method.  Why C/D wrong: These do not match the two-finger, one-third-depth infant technique."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"According to the adult Bradycardia AHA guidelines, what is the Atropine IV dose?","options":["First dose 1 mg bolus, repeat every 3-5 minutes, maximum 3 mg (note NCERT II Mains 2024)","6 mg IV bolus","0.01 mg/kg IV","150 mg IV over 10 minutes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The adult bradycardia guidelines list Atropine IV first dose 1 mg bolus, repeated every 3-5 minutes, maximum 3 mg.  Why B/C/D wrong: These reflect adenosine, pediatric dosing, and amiodarone, respectively."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"Per adult bradycardia AHA guidelines, what is the Dopamine IV infusion dose range?","options":["5-20 mcg/kg per minute; titrate to patient response","1 mg every 3-5 minutes","2-10 mcg per minute","0.01 mg/kg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The guidelines list Dopamine IV infusion at 5-20 mcg/kg per minute, titrated to patient response.  Why B wrong: 1 mg q3-5 min is atropine.  Why C wrong: 2-10 mcg/min is the epinephrine infusion range.  Why D wrong: Not the dopamine value listed."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"Per the adult bradycardia AHA guidelines, what is the Epinephrine IV infusion dose?","options":["2-10 mcg per minute infusion; titrate to patient response","1 mg every 3-5 minutes","5-20 mcg/kg per minute","0.5 to 1 g/hr"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The guidelines list Epinephrine IV infusion at 2-10 mcg per minute, titrated to patient response.  Why B wrong: 1 mg q3-5 min is the cardiac-arrest bolus.  Why C wrong: 5-20 mcg/kg/min is the dopamine range.  Why D wrong: That reflects magnesium maintenance, not epinephrine."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"Which of the following are listed CAUSES of bradycardia? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Myocardial ischemia/infarction","Drugs/toxic (e.g., calcium-channel blockers, beta blockers, digoxin)","Electrolyte abnormality (e.g., hyperkalemia)","Hypertension only","Caffeine overdose only"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists causes of bradycardia as myocardial ischemia/infarction; drugs/toxic agents (calcium-channel blockers, beta blockers, digoxin); and electrolyte abnormality (e.g., hyperkalemia).  Why D/E wrong: Hypertension alone and caffeine overdose are not the listed causes."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"Per the pediatric bradycardia AHA guidelines, what is the Epinephrine IV/IO dose?","options":["0.01 mg/kg IV (0.1 mL/kg of the 1 mg/mL concentration), repeated every 3-5 minutes; if IV/IO access is not available but endotracheal (ET) access exists, give 0.1 mg/kg (0.1 mL/kg of the 1 mg/mL concentration)","1 mg IV every 3-5 minutes","6 mg IV bolus","2-10 mcg/min"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The pediatric guidelines give Epinephrine IV/IO 0.01 mg/kg (0.1 mL/kg of the 1 mg/mL concentration), repeated every 3-5 minutes; if IV/IO access is not available but ET access is available, give 0.1 mg/kg (0.1 mL/kg of the 1 mg/mL concentration).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are adult bolus/infusion or adenosine doses, not the weight-based pediatric epinephrine dose."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"Per the pediatric bradycardia AHA guidelines, what is the Atropine IV/IO dose?","options":["0.02 mg/kg, may repeat once; minimum dose 0.1 mg and maximum single dose 0.5 mg","1 mg every 3-5 minutes","0.01 mg/kg only, no repeat","2 mg single dose"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The pediatric guidelines list Atropine IV/IO 0.02 mg/kg, which may be repeated once, with a minimum dose of 0.1 mg and a maximum single dose of 0.5 mg.  Why B wrong: 1 mg q3-5 min is the adult dose.  Why C/D wrong: Do not match the stated pediatric atropine dosing."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"Which of the following are listed as POSSIBLE CAUSES requiring evaluation in the tachycardia AHA guidelines section? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Hypothermia","Hypoxia","Medications","Hyperglycemia only","Dehydration only"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists possible causes as hypothermia, hypoxia, and medications.  Why D/E wrong: Hyperglycemia and dehydration alone are not the listed possible causes in this section."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"For adult tachycardia AHA guidelines, what does synchronized cardioversion guidance state?","options":["Always use 360 J immediately","Refer to your specific device's recommended energy level to maximize first-shock success","Never use cardioversion in tachycardia","Use only chemical cardioversion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The adult tachycardia guidelines state that for synchronized cardioversion, refer to your specific device's recommended energy level to maximize first-shock success.  Why A/C/D wrong: They contradict the device-specific, evidence-based cardioversion guidance."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"For adult wide-QRS tachycardia, what is the Adenosine IV dose per the tachycardia AHA guidelines?","options":["First dose 6 mg rapid IV push; second dose 12 mg rapid IV push","1 mg every 3-5 minutes","150 mg over 10 minutes","0.01 mg/kg IV/IO"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The adult guidelines list Adenosine IV dose: first dose 6 mg rapid IV push and a second dose of 12 mg rapid IV push.  Why B wrong: 1 mg q3-5 min is epinephrine.  Why C wrong: 150 mg is amiodarone.  Why D wrong: 0.01 mg/kg is the pediatric epinephrine dose, not adenosine."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"Per adult tachycardia AHA guidelines, what is the Procainamide IV dose for antiarrhythmic infusions in stable wide-QRS tachycardia?","options":["20-50 mg/min until the arrhythmia is suppressed, hypotension ensues, QRS duration increases >50%, or a maximum dose of 17 mg/kg is given; maintenance infusion 1-4 mg/min; avoid if prolonged QT or CHF","1 mg every 3-5 minutes","6 mg rapid IV push","100 mg over 5 minutes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The guidelines list Procainamide IV 20-50 mg/min until arrhythmia is suppressed, hypotension ensues, QRS duration increases more than 50%, or a maximum dose of 17 mg/kg is given, with maintenance infusion 1-4 mg/min, and to avoid it if QT is prolonged or in CHF.  Why B/C/D wrong: These reflect epinephrine, adenosine, and sotalol doses."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"Per adult tachycardia AHA guidelines for stable wide-QRS tachycardia, what is the Amiodarone IV dose?","options":["First dose 150 mg over 10 minutes; repeat as needed if VT recurs; followed by a maintenance infusion of 1 mg/min for the first 6 hours","6 mg rapid IV push","1 mg every 3-5 minutes","20-50 mg/min"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The guidelines list Amiodarone IV first dose 150 mg over 10 minutes, repeated as needed if VT recurs, followed by a maintenance infusion of 1 mg/min for the first 6 hours.  Why B/C/D wrong: These reflect adenosine, epinephrine, and procainamide dosing."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"Per adult tachycardia AHA guidelines, what is the Sotalol IV dose for stable wide-QRS tachycardia?","options":["100 mg (1.5 mg/kg) over 5 minutes; avoid if prolonged QT","6 mg rapid push","1 mg every 3-5 minutes","150 mg over 10 minutes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The guidelines list Sotalol IV 100 mg (1.5 mg/kg) over 5 minutes, and to avoid it if QT is prolonged.  Why B/C/D wrong: These reflect adenosine, epinephrine, and amiodarone dosing."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"Per pediatric tachycardia AHA guidelines, what is the Adenosine IV/IO dosing?","options":["First dose 0.1 mg/kg rapid bolus (maximum 6 mg); second dose 0.2 mg/kg rapid bolus (maximum single dose 12 mg)","6 mg then 12 mg fixed doses regardless of weight","1 mg/kg loading","0.01 mg/kg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The pediatric guidelines list Adenosine IV/IO first dose 0.1 mg/kg rapid bolus (maximum 6 mg) and second dose 0.2 mg/kg rapid bolus (maximum single dose 12 mg).  Why B wrong: Pediatric dosing is weight-based with maximum caps, not fixed.  Why C/D wrong: These reflect lidocaine loading and pediatric epinephrine, respectively."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"Per pediatric tachycardia AHA guidelines, what is the synchronized cardioversion energy dosing?","options":["Begin with 0.5-1 J/kg; if not effective, increase to 2 J/kg; sedate if needed, but don't delay cardioversion","Always 360 J","2 J/kg fixed for all children","0.1 J/kg only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The pediatric guidelines state synchronized cardioversion should begin with 0.5-1 J/kg; if not effective, increase to 2 J/kg, sedating if needed but not delaying cardioversion.  Why B/C/D wrong: These contradict the weight-based escalating-energy guidance."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"Which of the following are listed as Symptoms of Bradycardia? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Shortness of breath","Altered mental status","Hypotension","Pulmonary edema/congestion","Weakness/dizziness"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists symptoms of bradycardia as shortness of breath, altered mental status, hypotension, pulmonary edema/congestion, weakness/dizziness, syncope and fatigue, and lightheadedness.  Why none wrong: All five options are explicitly listed symptoms of bradycardia."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"Which of the following are listed as Symptoms of Tachycardia? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Hypotension, sweating","Pulmonary edema/congestion","Jugular venous distension","Chest pain/discomfort","Altered mental status"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists symptoms of tachycardia as hypotension/sweating, pulmonary edema/congestion, jugular venous distension, chest pain/discomfort, shortness of breath, weakness/dizziness, and altered mental status.  Why none wrong: All five options are listed symptoms of tachycardia."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"A nursing student asks why the rescuer must allow the chest to recoil completely between compressions. Based on the source, what is the best response?","options":["To increase the compression rate above 150/min","Full recoil (chest returning to normal position completely after each compression) supports blood flow; not fully releasing the chest is a common CPR mistake to avoid","So the AED can analyze faster","Because incomplete recoil increases the depth of compressions"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists \"not fully releasing the victim's chest\" as a common CPR mistake and notes that complete chest recoil after each compression supports blood flow.  Why A wrong: The target rate is at least 100/min, not above 150/min.  Why C wrong: Recoil relates to perfusion, not AED analysis speed.  Why D wrong: Incomplete recoil reduces effective filling/perfusion; it does not increase depth."},{"section":"Batch 028 · Defibrillation and AED use, criteria & pad placement for AED…","question":"A patient in stable wide-QRS tachycardia has a known prolonged QT interval. Based on the AHA tachycardia guidelines, which antiarrhythmic should be avoided?","options":["Procainamide (avoid if prolonged QT or CHF) and Sotalol (avoid if prolonged QT)","Adenosine","Amiodarone first dose","Synchronized cardioversion"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The guidelines specifically state to avoid Procainamide if QT is prolonged (or in CHF) and to avoid Sotalol if QT is prolonged.  Why B wrong: Adenosine dosing is given without a prolonged-QT contraindication in this section.  Why C wrong: Amiodarone is listed as a dosing option, not contraindicated for prolonged QT here.  Why D wrong: Cardioversion is a procedure, not a QT-prolonging drug to avoid."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"What is the FIRST step in the assessment of an individual found \"down\"?","options":["Check their blood pressure","Check their heart rate","Check to see if they are conscious or unconscious","Check their pupil size"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: When responding to an individual who is \"down,\" the first action is to determine whether they are conscious or not (assess responsiveness).  Why A wrong: Blood pressure is not the initial assessment in an unresponsive collapse.  Why B wrong: Heart/pulse rate is checked after determining responsiveness.  Why D wrong: Pupil check is not the first step in BLS assessment."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"CPR is initiated on an adult who is not breathing but whose pulse returns. What ventilation rate should be used for this person?","options":["6–8 breaths per minute","10–12 breaths per minute","18–20 breaths per minute","Depends on his color"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Most experts recommend a ventilation rate of 10–12 breaths per minute for an adult who has a pulse but is not breathing.  Why A wrong: 6–8 breaths/min is too low for rescue breathing in an adult with a pulse.  Why C wrong: 18–20 breaths/min causes hyperventilation.  Why D wrong: Ventilation rate is standardized, not based on skin color."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"After activating EMS and sending someone for an AED, which of the following is correct for one-rescuer BLS of an unresponsive individual with no pulse?","options":["Start rescue breathing","Apply AED pads","Run to get help","Begin chest compressions"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: An unresponsive adult without a pulse must receive CPR; chest compressions should be initiated immediately, followed by ventilation.  Why A wrong: Compressions, not breaths, come first (C-A-B sequence).  Why B wrong: AED pads are applied when the device arrives, but compressions begin immediately.  Why C wrong: EMS has already been activated; the rescuer should not leave to get help."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"At what age is it preferable to use the pediatric AED pads?","options":["8 years of age or older","Less than 8 years of age","12 years of age or younger","18 years of age or younger"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Children less than 8 years of age, weighing 55 pounds (25 kg) or less, should use pediatric AED pads.  Why A wrong: 8 years and older use adult pads.  Why C/D wrong: The cut-off is under 8 years (≤25 kg), not 12 or 18 years."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"Where should you attempt to perform a pulse check in an adult?","options":["Brachial artery","Carotid artery","Popliteal artery","Temporal artery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In an adult, the pulse is checked at the carotid artery.  Why A wrong: The brachial artery is used for infants, not adults.  Why C wrong: The popliteal artery is not used for emergency pulse checks.  Why D wrong: The temporal artery is not the standard CPR pulse-check site."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"The 6 steps in the Adult Chain of Survival include all of the following EXCEPT:","options":["Early CPR","Rapid defibrillation","Use of cardiovascular medications","Integrated post-cardiac arrest care"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: \"Use of cardiovascular medications\" is NOT a link in the Adult Chain of Survival.  Why A wrong: Early CPR is a recognized link.  Why B wrong: Rapid defibrillation is a recognized link.  Why D wrong: Integrated post-cardiac arrest care is a recognized link."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"In both children and infants, two minutes of CPR should be given if:","options":["Witnessed cardiac arrest","Unwitnessed collapse","Witnessed choking incident","Unresponsive victim with pulse and breathing"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If you come across a child with an UNWITNESSED collapse and you are alone and cannot call for help, perform 2 minutes of CPR first.  Why A wrong: A witnessed arrest prompts immediate EMS activation/defibrillation, not 2 minutes of CPR first.  Why C wrong: A choking incident is managed with airway clearance, not 2 minutes of CPR.  Why D wrong: A victim with pulse and breathing does not require CPR."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"Select the proper pairing regarding CPR for an adult:","options":["Chest compressions 60 to 80/minute; 2 inches deep (5 cm)","Chest compressions 80/minute; 1.5 inches deep (4 cm)","Chest compressions 100/minute; 3 inches deep (8 cm)","Chest compressions 100 to 120 per minute; 2 to 2.4 inches deep (5–6 cm)"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: High-quality adult CPR is 100–120 compressions/minute at a depth of 2–2.4 inches (5–6 cm).  Why A wrong: 60–80/min is too slow.  Why B wrong: 1.5 inches (4 cm) is too shallow and the rate is too slow.  Why C wrong: 3 inches (8 cm) is too deep."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"Where does the electrical impulse for normal cardiac activity originate?","options":["Unknown","SA node","AV node","Purkinje fibers"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The sinoatrial (SA) node is the heart's natural pacemaker and origin of normal cardiac electrical activity.  Why A wrong: The origin is well established (SA node).  Why C wrong: The AV node is a secondary pacemaker/relay, not the normal origin.  Why D wrong: Purkinje fibers conduct the impulse to ventricular myocardium; they do not originate it."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"What does the QRS complex on an ECG represent?","options":["Ventricular contraction","Atrial contraction","AV valve closure","Septum relaxation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The QRS complex represents ventricular contraction (ventricular depolarization); the T wave represents repolarization of the ventricles.  Why B wrong: Atrial contraction is represented by the P wave.  Why C wrong: AV valve closure is a mechanical/heart-sound event, not the QRS.  Why D wrong: Repolarization (relaxation) is shown by the T wave, not the QRS."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"You are transporting an individual who goes into cardiac arrest during transport. IV access is unsuccessful. What is the next step?","options":["Terminate resuscitation","Obtain intraosseous access","Place a central line","Administer all medications through ET tube"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: An intraosseous (IO) line can be placed rapidly and is the next best route for drug delivery; absorption after ET tube delivery is unreliable.  Why A wrong: Resuscitation should not be terminated for failed IV access.  Why C wrong: A central line is more time-consuming and not the immediate next step in the field.  Why D wrong: ET tube drug absorption is unreliable, making it inferior to IO access."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"A 79-year-old individual is in SVT. BP is 80/50, and he is complaining of chest discomfort and feels like passing out. What is the next appropriate step?","options":["Carotid massage","Synchronized cardioversion","Amiodarone","Lidocaine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: This individual is symptomatic with hypotension and chest pain (unstable SVT), so synchronized cardioversion is indicated.  Why A wrong: Carotid massage may cause complications in elderly individuals.  Why C wrong: Adenosine (not amiodarone) could be considered if IV access is already in place while preparing for cardioversion; amiodarone is not the immediate step here.  Why D wrong: Lidocaine is not indicated for unstable SVT."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"A 55-year-old male has stroke symptoms, and the CT scan shows multilobar infarction (more than one-third of the cerebral hemisphere). What therapy is CONTRAINDICATED?","options":["Thrombolytic therapy","Oxygen","Monitoring glucose","Blood pressure monitoring"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Thrombolytic therapy is contraindicated in large strokes that involve more than one-third of a cerebral hemisphere (risk of hemorrhagic transformation). (Source labels this option \"c. Thrombolytic therapy\"; the factually contraindicated therapy is thrombolytics.)  Why B wrong: Oxygen is supportive and not contraindicated.  Why C wrong: Monitoring glucose is appropriate stroke care, not contraindicated.  Why D wrong: Blood pressure monitoring is essential, not contraindicated."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"The primary focus of treating cardiac arrest is:","options":["Effective CPR","Early defibrillation","Drug administration","Both A and B"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The primary focus is on early/effective CPR AND early defibrillation.  Why A wrong: Effective CPR alone is incomplete; defibrillation is also primary.  Why B wrong: Early defibrillation alone is incomplete; CPR is also primary.  Why C wrong: Drug administration is secondary to CPR and defibrillation."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"The following are possible effects of hyperventilation:","options":["Increased intrathoracic pressure","Decreased venous return to the heart","Both A and B","None of the above"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Hyperventilation increases intrathoracic pressure AND decreases venous return to the heart.  Why A wrong: Incomplete — both effects occur.  Why B wrong: Incomplete — both effects occur.  Why D wrong: Both listed effects are real consequences of hyperventilation."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"What is high-quality CPR?","options":["80 compressions per minute at a depth of at least one inch (2.5 cm)","100 to 120 compressions per minute at a depth of 2 to 2.4 inches (5–6 cm)","60 compressions per minute at a depth of 1 inch","140 compressions per minute at a depth of 3 inches"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: High-quality CPR is 100–120 compressions/minute at a depth of 2–2.4 inches (5–6 cm).  Why A wrong: 80/min and 1 inch are both too low/shallow.  Why C wrong: 60/min and 1 inch are inadequate.  Why D wrong: 140/min is too fast and 3 inches too deep."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"You should ______ in an individual with ventricular fibrillation immediately following a shock.","options":["Resume CPR","Check heart rate","Analyze rhythm","Give amiodarone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Immediately after delivering a shock for VF, the rescuer should resume CPR (chest compressions) without delay for rhythm/pulse checks.  Why B wrong: Pausing to check heart rate delays compressions.  Why C wrong: Rhythm analysis is deferred until after ~2 minutes of CPR.  Why D wrong: Drug administration does not take priority over resuming compressions right after a shock."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"The following medication(s) can be used to treat hypotension during the post-cardiac arrest phase:","options":["Dopamine","Milrinone","Amiodarone","Both A and B"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Dopamine is used to treat hypotension in the post-cardiac arrest phase (vasopressor/inotropic support). (Source keys option A.)  Why B wrong: Milrinone is an inodilator that can lower BP and is not the keyed agent here.  Why C wrong: Amiodarone is an antiarrhythmic, not used to treat hypotension.  Why D wrong: Only dopamine (A) is keyed for hypotension management here."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"Which of the following is NOT a potential cause of PEA (Pulseless Electrical Activity)?","options":["Toxins","Hyperkalemia","Hypoventilation","Trauma"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Hypoventilation as listed is NOT among the standard reversible causes of PEA (the reversible causes are the H's and T's — e.g., hypoxia, not \"hypoventilation\" per the keyed answer).  Why A wrong: Toxins are a recognized \"T\" cause of PEA.  Why B wrong: Hyperkalemia is a recognized \"H\" cause of PEA.  Why D wrong: Trauma (tamponade/tension pneumothorax/thrombosis from trauma) is among reversible causes."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"All of the following are bradycardic rhythms EXCEPT:","options":["Atrial fibrillation","First-degree heart block","Mobitz Type I","Third-degree heart block"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Atrial fibrillation is typically a tachyarrhythmia (often with rapid ventricular response), not a bradycardic rhythm.  Why B wrong: First-degree heart block is a bradycardia-related conduction block.  Why C wrong: Mobitz Type I (Wenckebach) is a bradycardic AV block.  Why D wrong: Third-degree (complete) heart block is associated with bradycardia."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"Which of the following is first-line treatment for ACS (Acute Coronary Syndrome)?","options":["Morphine","Aspirin","Nitroglycerin","All of the above"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Morphine, aspirin, and nitroglycerin are all part of first-line ACS management (the \"MONA\" components), so all of the above applies.  Why A wrong: Morphine alone is incomplete.  Why B wrong: Aspirin alone is incomplete.  Why C wrong: Nitroglycerin alone is incomplete."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"Which of the following is NOT found within the 8 D's of stroke care?","options":["Detection","Dispatch","Delivery","Defibrillate"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: \"Defibrillate\" is NOT one of the 8 D's of stroke care.  Why A wrong: Detection (rapid recognition of symptoms) is the 1st D.  Why B wrong: Dispatch (early activation of EMS, call 911) is a recognized D.  Why C wrong: Delivery (rapid EMS identification, management & transport) is a recognized D."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"You have just administered a drug for an individual in supraventricular tachycardia (SVT). She complains of flushing and chest heaviness. Which drug is the most likely cause?","options":["Aspirin","Adenosine","Amiodarone","Amitriptyline"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Adenosine is the correct choice for SVT treatment and commonly results in reactions such as flushing, dyspnea, chest pressure, and lightheadedness.  Why A wrong: Aspirin does not cause these transient flushing/chest reactions in SVT.  Why C wrong: Amiodarone is not the drug used for acute SVT termination here and does not classically cause these immediate symptoms.  Why D wrong: Amitriptyline is a tricyclic antidepressant, not used for SVT."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"What factor is critical in any emergency situation?","options":["Scene safety","Age of the individual","Resuscitation status","Pregnancy status"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Always assess the safety of the scene in any emergency situation — do not become injured yourself.  Why B wrong: Age is relevant to care but not the critical first factor over scene safety.  Why C wrong: Resuscitation status is assessed after ensuring scene safety.  Why D wrong: Pregnancy status is important context but not the universal critical factor."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"Arrange the BLS Chain of Survival in the proper order:","options":["Look, listen, and feel","Check responsiveness, call EMS and get AED, defibrillation, and recovery","Check responsiveness, call EMS and get AED, chest compressions, early defibrillation, and recovery","Call for help, shock, check pulse, shock, and transport"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The focus is on early CPR and defibrillation: check responsiveness → call EMS and get AED → chest compressions → early defibrillation → recovery.  Why A wrong: \"Look, listen, feel\" is an outdated breathing-check step, not the chain sequence.  Why B wrong: It omits the critical chest-compression step.  Why D wrong: It omits responsiveness check and CPR, and shocks before assessing."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"Critical characteristics of high-quality CPR include which of the following?","options":["Start chest compressions within 10 seconds of recognition of cardiac arrest","Allow complete chest recoil after each compression","Minimize interruptions of CPR","All of the above"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: High-quality CPR requires starting compressions within 10 seconds, allowing full chest recoil, and minimizing interruptions — all of the above.  Why A wrong: True but incomplete on its own.  Why B wrong: True but incomplete on its own.  Why C wrong: True but incomplete on its own."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"The compression-to-ventilation ratio for single-rescuer CPR giving CPR to an adult is:","options":["30:1","30:2","15:1","15:2"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The single-rescuer adult compression-to-ventilation ratio is 30:2.  Why A wrong: 30:1 is not a recognized ratio.  Why C wrong: 15:1 is not used.  Why D wrong: 15:2 is the two-rescuer ratio for children/infants, not single-rescuer adult."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"An AED can be used safely in all of the following situations EXCEPT:","options":["Victim lying in the snow","Victim with an implanted pacemaker","Victim with a transdermal medication patch on","Victim lying partially in water"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: An AED should NOT be used when the victim is lying partially in water (risk of shock conduction); the victim must be moved to a dry area.  Why A wrong: An AED can be used safely on a victim in the snow.  Why B wrong: An AED can be used with an implanted pacemaker (avoid placing pads directly over it).  Why C wrong: A transdermal patch should be removed, but an AED can still be used safely after removal."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"You are alone when you encounter a person who appears to be cardiac or respiratory arrest. What are the first two steps you should take to stabilize the person? Check for danger, ______, and send for help.","options":["Establish IV access","Insert an advanced airway","Check for response","Start CPR"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: After checking for danger, the next step is to check for response (responsiveness) and then send for help.  Why A wrong: IV access is an ALS intervention, not an initial stabilization step.  Why B wrong: Advanced airway insertion is not among the first two BLS steps.  Why D wrong: CPR begins after assessing responsiveness and breathing/pulse."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"What are the proper steps for operating an AED?","options":["Power on the AED, attach electrode pads, shock the person, and analyze the rhythm","Power on the AED, attach electrode pads, analyze the rhythm, and shock the person","Power on the AED, analyze the rhythm, attach electrode pads, and shock the person","Power on the AED, shock the person, attach electrode pads, and analyze the rhythm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Correct AED operation is: power on → attach electrode pads → analyze the rhythm → shock the person.  Why A wrong: Shocking before analyzing the rhythm is unsafe and incorrect.  Why C wrong: You cannot analyze the rhythm before pads are attached.  Why D wrong: Shocking before attaching pads or analyzing is incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"Which of the following is the correct next step in management after delivery of a shock?","options":["Check pulse","Ventilate only","Do chest compressions","Shock again"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Immediately after a shock, the next step is to perform (do) chest compressions, resuming CPR for ~2 minutes.  Why A wrong: Pausing to check pulse delays compressions.  Why B wrong: Ventilation alone is insufficient; compressions are needed.  Why D wrong: Rhythm is reanalyzed only after 2 minutes of CPR, not an immediate re-shock."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"Choose the correct sequence of electrical activity in the heart for normal sinus rhythm:","options":["SA node, Purkinje, AV node, Bundle of His","Purkinje, Bundle of His, AV node, SA node","SA node, AV node, Bundle of His, Purkinje fibers","AV node, SA node, Bundle of His, Purkinje fibers"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Normal conduction is SA node → AV node → Bundle of His → Purkinje fibers.  Why A wrong: Sequence is scrambled (Purkinje cannot precede AV node).  Why B wrong: Reversed order starting from Purkinje is incorrect.  Why D wrong: Conduction begins at the SA node, not the AV node."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"What is the recommended method to monitor breathing during ACLS care?","options":["Look, listen, and feel","Capnography","Venous blood gas","Monitoring chest rise"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Quantitative waveform capnography is the recommended method to assess breathing/ventilation during ACLS; pulse oximetry and clinical assessment also play a role.  Why A wrong: \"Look, listen, feel\" is an outdated, less reliable method.  Why C wrong: Venous blood gas does not continuously monitor breathing.  Why D wrong: Monitoring chest rise alone is less reliable than capnography."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"An individual has been ill, and the monitor reveals sinus tachycardia with a heart rate of 135. What is the primary goal in treating this individual?","options":["Determine the underlying cause","Prepare for synchronized cardioversion","Transfuse packed red blood cells","Administer Adenosine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The primary objective of treating sinus tachycardia is to determine the underlying cause so appropriate treatment decisions can be made.  Why B wrong: Cardioversion is not used for sinus tachycardia.  Why C wrong: Transfusion is only indicated if anemia/blood loss is the specific cause, not a primary goal for all.  Why D wrong: Adenosine treats SVT, not sinus tachycardia."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"You are treating an individual who presented in ventricular fibrillation. After CPR and one attempt at defibrillation, his new rhythm is third-degree AV block. What is the next step in management?","options":["Repeat defibrillation","Vasopressin","Transcutaneous pacing","High-dose epinephrine"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Transcutaneous pacing is indicated for Mobitz Type II second-degree AV block, third-degree AV block, and bradycardia with symptomatic ventricular escape rhythm.  Why A wrong: Third-degree AV block is not a shockable rhythm.  Why B wrong: Vasopressin is not the management for complete heart block.  Why D wrong: High-dose epinephrine is not the indicated step for third-degree AV block here."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"The following are included in the ACLS Survey:","options":["Airway, Breathing, Circulation, Differential Diagnosis","Airway, Breathing, Circulation, Defibrillation","Assessment, Breathing, Circulation, Defibrillation","Airway, Breathing, CPR, Differential Diagnosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The ACLS Survey is Airway, Breathing, Circulation, and Differential Diagnosis (the A-B-C-D of ACLS).  Why B wrong: \"Defibrillation\" replaces Differential Diagnosis incorrectly.  Why C wrong: \"Assessment\" replaces Airway incorrectly.  Why D wrong: \"CPR\" replaces Circulation incorrectly."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"Which of the following is NOT an example of an advanced airway?","options":["Oropharyngeal airway","Esophageal-tracheal tube","Laryngeal mask airway","Combitube"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: An oropharyngeal airway is a basic (not advanced) airway adjunct.  Why B wrong: An esophageal-tracheal tube is an advanced airway.  Why C wrong: A laryngeal mask airway (LMA) is an advanced (supraglottic) airway.  Why D wrong: A Combitube is an advanced airway device."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"The normal sinus rhythm of the heart starts in the:","options":["Left ventricle","Atrioventricular node","Sinoatrial node","Right ventricle"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Normal sinus rhythm originates in the sinoatrial (SA) node, the heart's natural pacemaker.  Why A wrong: The left ventricle does not initiate the impulse.  Why B wrong: The AV node is a relay/secondary pacemaker, not the normal origin.  Why D wrong: The right ventricle does not initiate normal rhythm."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"Before placement of an advanced airway, the compression-to-ventilation ratio during adult CPR is:","options":["30:1","30:2","15:1","20:2"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Before an advanced airway is placed, adult CPR uses a 30:2 compression-to-ventilation ratio.  Why A wrong: 30:1 is not a recognized ratio.  Why C wrong: 15:1 is not used.  Why D wrong: 20:2 is not the adult ratio."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"______ joules (J) are delivered per shock when using a monophasic defibrillator.","options":["180","320","340","360"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: A monophasic defibrillator delivers 360 J per shock.  Why A wrong: 180 J is below the monophasic dose.  Why B wrong: 320 J is not the standard monophasic energy.  Why C wrong: 340 J is not the standard monophasic energy."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"The following antiarrhythmic drug(s) can be used for persistent ventricular fibrillation or pulseless ventricular tachycardia, EXCEPT:","options":["Amiodarone","Lidocaine","Atropine","Epinephrine"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Atropine is used for bradycardia, NOT for persistent VF/pulseless VT.  Why A wrong: Amiodarone is an antiarrhythmic used for refractory VF/pVT.  Why B wrong: Lidocaine is an alternative antiarrhythmic for VF/pVT.  Why D wrong: Epinephrine is given in VF/pVT arrest (though a vasopressor, it is part of the arrest algorithm and not the \"except\" answer)."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"Which of the following is a shockable rhythm?","options":["Ventricular fibrillation","Ventricular tachycardia (pulseless)","Torsades de Pointes","All of the above"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Ventricular fibrillation, pulseless ventricular tachycardia, and Torsades de Pointes are all shockable rhythms.  Why A wrong: True but incomplete.  Why B wrong: True but incomplete.  Why C wrong: True but incomplete."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"______ access is preferred in arrest due to easy access and no interruption in CPR.","options":["Central","Peripheral","Intraosseous","Endotracheal"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Peripheral IV access is preferred in arrest because it allows easy access and does not interrupt CPR.  Why A wrong: Central line placement interrupts CPR and is more complex.  Why C wrong: Intraosseous is used when IV access fails, not the first preference.  Why D wrong: Endotracheal drug absorption is unreliable."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"The following are classic signs of an acute stroke EXCEPT:","options":["Facial droop","Arm drift","Abnormal speech","Seizure"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Seizure is NOT one of the three classic stroke signs (facial droop, arm drift, abnormal speech — the Cincinnati Prehospital Stroke Scale).  Why A wrong: Facial droop is a classic stroke sign.  Why B wrong: Arm drift is a classic stroke sign.  Why C wrong: Abnormal/slurred speech is a classic stroke sign."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"An individual presents with symptomatic bradycardia. Her heart rate is 32. Which of the following are acceptable therapeutic options?","options":["Atropine","Epinephrine","Dopamine","All of the above"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Atropine is the initial treatment for symptomatic bradycardia; if unresponsive, IV dopamine or epinephrine is the next step, and pacing may be effective if other measures fail.  Why A wrong: Atropine alone is correct but incomplete; dopamine and epinephrine are also acceptable.  Why B wrong: Epinephrine alone is incomplete.  Why C wrong: Dopamine alone is incomplete."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"A person with alcoholism collapses and is found to be in Torsades de Pointes. What intervention is most likely to correct the underlying problem?","options":["Rewarm the individual to correct hypothermia","Administer magnesium sulfate 1 to 2 gm IV diluted in 10 mL D5W to correct low magnesium","Administer glucose to correct hypoglycemia","Administer naloxone to correct narcotic overdose"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hypomagnesemia (low Mg++) is commonly caused by alcoholism and malnutrition; IV magnesium administration may prevent or terminate Torsades de Pointes (ventricular tachycardia).  Why A wrong: Hypothermia is not the underlying problem here.  Why C wrong: Glucose corrects hypoglycemia, not Torsades from low magnesium.  Why D wrong: Naloxone reverses opioids, not relevant to magnesium-related Torsades."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"Select the proper pairing regarding CPR for an adult:","options":["Chest compressions 60 to 80/minute; 2 inches deep (5 cm)","Chest compressions 80/minute; 1.5 inches deep (4 cm)","Chest compressions 100/minute; 3 inches deep (8 cm)","Chest compressions 100 to 120 per minute; 2 to 2.4 inches deep (5–6 cm)"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Correct adult CPR is 100–120 compressions/minute at a depth of 2–2.4 inches (5–6 cm).  Why A wrong: 60–80/min is too slow.  Why B wrong: 80/min and 4 cm are too slow/shallow.  Why C wrong: 100/min with 3 inches (8 cm) is too deep."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"In the Cardiac Arrest in Pregnancy In-Hospital ACLS Algorithm, after beginning \"Continue BLS/ACLS\" (high-quality CPR, defibrillation when indicated, other ACLS interventions), what is the next team-focused step?","options":["Assemble the maternal cardiac arrest team","Discharge the patient","Stop all CPR","Transfer to the neonatal team"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: After continuing BLS/ACLS, the algorithm directs to assemble the maternal cardiac arrest team, then consider the etiology of arrest.  Why B wrong: Discharge is not part of an active arrest algorithm.  Why C wrong: CPR is continued, not stopped.  Why D wrong: The neonatal team receives the neonate only after perimortem cesarean delivery, much later."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"In the Cardiac Arrest in Pregnancy algorithm, which is a recommended MATERNAL intervention?","options":["Administer 100% O2, avoid excess ventilation","Detach fetal monitors","Prepare for perimortem cesarean delivery","Provide continuous lateral uterine displacement"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Maternal interventions include performing airway management, administering 100% O2 (avoiding excess ventilation), placing IV above the diaphragm, and stopping IV magnesium (giving calcium chloride or gluconate) if applicable.  Why B wrong: Detaching fetal monitors is an obstetric intervention.  Why C wrong: Preparing for perimortem cesarean is an obstetric intervention.  Why D wrong: Continuous lateral uterine displacement is an obstetric intervention."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"A pregnant patient in cardiac arrest is receiving IV magnesium. According to the maternal interventions, what should be done?","options":["Increase the magnesium infusion rate","Stop the magnesium and give calcium chloride or gluconate","Continue magnesium unchanged","Replace magnesium with potassium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If a patient is receiving IV magnesium during arrest, stop the magnesium and give calcium chloride or gluconate (to counteract magnesium toxicity).  Why A wrong: Increasing magnesium worsens toxicity.  Why C wrong: Continuing magnesium unchanged is unsafe during arrest.  Why D wrong: Potassium is not the antidote for magnesium effects."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"In the Cardiac Arrest in Pregnancy algorithm, which is an OBSTETRIC intervention?","options":["Perform airway management","Administer 100% O2","Provide continuous lateral uterine displacement","Place IV above the diaphragm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Obstetric interventions include providing continuous lateral uterine displacement, detaching fetal monitors, and preparing for perimortem cesarean delivery.  Why A wrong: Airway management is a maternal intervention.  Why B wrong: Administering 100% O2 is a maternal intervention.  Why D wrong: Placing IV above the diaphragm is a maternal intervention."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"According to the pregnancy ACLS algorithm, if there is no ROSC (return of spontaneous circulation) within how many minutes, should immediate perimortem cesarean delivery be considered?","options":["2 minutes","5 minutes","10 minutes","15 minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If no ROSC in 5 minutes, consider immediate perimortem cesarean delivery; ideally perform within 5 minutes to improve maternal and fetal outcomes.  Why A wrong: 2 minutes is too soon per the algorithm.  Why C wrong: 10 minutes is too long.  Why D wrong: 15 minutes is too long."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"What is the stated goal of perimortem cesarean delivery in maternal cardiac arrest?","options":["To improve maternal and fetal outcomes","To shorten the resuscitation time only","To avoid defibrillation","To prevent the need for CPR"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The goal of perimortem cesarean delivery is to improve maternal and fetal outcomes.  Why B wrong: The aim is outcomes, not merely shortening resuscitation.  Why C wrong: Defibrillation continues when indicated regardless.  Why D wrong: CPR continues; delivery does not replace CPR."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"Regarding the airway in pregnancy during cardiac arrest, which statement is correct?","options":["A difficult airway is rare in pregnancy","A difficult airway is common; use the most experienced provider","Only nasotracheal intubation should be used","Advanced airways should be avoided entirely"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In pregnancy a difficult airway is common; the most experienced provider should manage it, providing endotracheal intubation or a supraglottic advanced airway.  Why A wrong: A difficult airway is common, not rare, in pregnancy.  Why C wrong: Endotracheal or supraglottic advanced airways are appropriate, not solely nasotracheal.  Why D wrong: Advanced airways are provided, not avoided."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"Once an advanced airway is in place during pregnancy CPR, what ventilation rate should be delivered with continuous chest compressions?","options":["1 breath every 6 seconds (10 breaths/min)","1 breath every 3 seconds (20 breaths/min)","1 breath every 10 seconds (6 breaths/min)","2 breaths every 30 compressions"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Once an advanced airway is in place, give 1 breath every 6 seconds (10 breaths/min) with continuous chest compressions.  Why B wrong: 1 breath every 3 seconds causes hyperventilation.  Why C wrong: 1 breath every 10 seconds is too few.  Why D wrong: The 30:2 cycle is used before an advanced airway, not after."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"To confirm and monitor endotracheal (ET) tube placement during pregnancy arrest, which method should be performed?","options":["Waveform capnography or capnometry","Chest auscultation only","Venous blood gas only","Visual inspection only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Perform waveform capnography or capnometry to confirm and monitor ET tube placement.  Why B wrong: Auscultation alone is less reliable than capnography.  Why C wrong: Venous blood gas does not confirm tube placement.  Why D wrong: Visual inspection alone is insufficient to confirm placement."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"The potential etiologies of maternal cardiac arrest are remembered by the mnemonic listing A–H. Which etiology corresponds to \"A\"?","options":["Anesthetic complications","Bleeding","Cardiovascular","Drugs"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In the list of potential etiologies of maternal cardiac arrest, \"A\" = Anesthetic complications.  Why B wrong: \"B\" = Bleeding.  Why C wrong: \"C\" = Cardiovascular.  Why D wrong: \"D\" = Drugs."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"In the potential etiologies of maternal cardiac arrest (A–H), which of the following pairings is CORRECT?","options":["E = Embolic","F = Fluid overload","G = Glucose imbalance","H = Hemorrhage"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: E = Embolic in the maternal cardiac arrest etiology list.  Why B wrong: F = Fever, not fluid overload.  Why C wrong: G = General nonobstetric causes of cardiac arrest (H's and T's), not glucose imbalance.  Why D wrong: H = Hypertension, not hemorrhage."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"According to the maternal cardiac arrest priorities, planning should be done in collaboration with which services?","options":["Obstetric, neonatal, emergency, anesthesiology, intensive care, and cardiac arrest services","Only the obstetric team","Only anesthesiology","Only the cardiac arrest team"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Team planning should be done in collaboration with the obstetric, neonatal, emergency, anesthesiology, intensive care, and cardiac arrest services.  Why B/C/D wrong: Planning is multidisciplinary, not limited to a single service."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"In maternal cardiac arrest, priorities for the pregnant woman include provision of high-quality CPR and relief of which compression?","options":["Aortocaval compression with lateral uterine displacement","Coronary artery compression","Carotid compression","Diaphragmatic compression"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Priorities include provision of high-quality CPR and relief of aortocaval compression with lateral uterine displacement.  Why B wrong: Coronary artery compression is not the concern addressed.  Why C wrong: Carotid compression is not relevant here.  Why D wrong: Diaphragmatic compression relief is not the stated priority."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"In the Adult Basic Life Support Algorithm, after verifying scene safety, what should the rescuer do to assess the victim?","options":["Begin chest compressions immediately","Check for responsiveness, shout for nearby help, activate the emergency response system, and get an AED","Insert an advanced airway","Administer epinephrine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After verifying scene safety, the rescuer checks for responsiveness, shouts for nearby help, activates the emergency response system (mobile device if appropriate), and gets an AED/emergency equipment (or sends someone to do so).  Why A wrong: Compressions begin only after determining there is no normal breathing/pulse.  Why C wrong: Advanced airway is an ALS step, not the initial BLS assessment.  Why D wrong: Drug administration is not part of initial BLS assessment."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"During the BLS assessment, how should breathing and pulse be checked simultaneously?","options":["Look for no breathing or only gasping and check the pulse simultaneously — is a pulse definitely felt within 10 seconds?","Check pulse for 60 seconds before assessing breathing","Assess breathing only and ignore the pulse","Wait 2 minutes before checking either"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The algorithm directs looking for no breathing or only gasping and checking the pulse simultaneously — is a pulse definitely felt within 10 seconds?  Why B wrong: The pulse check is limited to within 10 seconds, not 60.  Why C wrong: Both breathing and pulse are assessed.  Why D wrong: Assessment is immediate, not delayed 2 minutes."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"In the Adult BLS Algorithm, if the victim has a pulse but is NOT breathing normally (no normal breathing, pulse felt), the rescuer should provide rescue breathing at what rate?","options":["1 breath every 5–6 seconds or 10 breaths/min","1 breath every 2 seconds","30 breaths/min","No rescue breathing is needed"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For \"no normal breathing, pulse felt,\" provide rescue breathing — 1 breath every 5–6 seconds or about 10 breaths/min; check pulse every 2 minutes, and if no pulse, start CPR.  Why B wrong: 1 breath every 2 seconds is hyperventilation.  Why C wrong: 30 breaths/min is far too rapid.  Why D wrong: Rescue breathing is required when the victim is not breathing normally but has a pulse."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"In the Adult BLS Algorithm, if a possible opioid overdose is suspected in a victim with a pulse but not breathing normally, what should be administered if available per protocol?","options":["Naloxone","Atropine","Epinephrine","Adenosine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: If a possible opioid overdose is suspected, administer naloxone if available per protocol.  Why B wrong: Atropine treats bradycardia, not opioid overdose.  Why C wrong: Epinephrine is not the opioid reversal agent.  Why D wrong: Adenosine treats SVT, not opioid overdose."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"When the victim has no breathing or only gasping and no pulse, the Adult BLS Algorithm directs the rescuer to:","options":["Monitor until emergency responders arrive","Start CPR — perform cycles of 30 compressions and 2 breaths, and use the AED as soon as it is available","Provide rescue breathing only","Administer naloxone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For \"no breathing or only gasping, pulse not felt,\" start CPR — perform cycles of 30 compressions and 2 breaths and use the AED as soon as it is available.  Why A wrong: Monitoring applies when normal breathing and pulse are present.  Why C wrong: Rescue breathing alone is used when a pulse is present; here there is no pulse.  Why D wrong: Naloxone is for suspected opioid overdose with a pulse, not the pulseless arrest pathway."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"In the Adult BLS Algorithm, when the AED indicates a SHOCKABLE rhythm, the rescuer should:","options":["Give 1 shock, resume CPR immediately for 2 minutes (until prompted by AED to allow rhythm check), and continue until ALS providers take over or the victim starts to move","Give 3 stacked shocks before resuming CPR","Stop CPR and wait for ALS","Give a shock and then check the pulse for 60 seconds"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For a shockable rhythm, give 1 shock, resume CPR immediately for 2 minutes (until prompted by the AED to allow a rhythm check), and continue until ALS providers take over or the victim starts to move.  Why B wrong: Stacked shocks are not recommended; give 1 shock then resume CPR.  Why C wrong: CPR should be resumed immediately, not stopped.  Why D wrong: CPR resumes immediately rather than a prolonged pulse check."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"In the Adult BLS Algorithm, when the AED indicates a NON-SHOCKABLE rhythm, the rescuer should:","options":["Resume CPR immediately for 2 minutes (until prompted by AED to allow rhythm check) and continue until ALS providers take over or the victim starts to move","Deliver a shock anyway","Discontinue resuscitation","Check the pulse for 2 minutes before resuming CPR"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For a non-shockable rhythm, resume CPR immediately for 2 minutes (until prompted by the AED to allow a rhythm check) and continue until ALS providers take over or the victim starts to move.  Why B wrong: A non-shockable rhythm should not be shocked.  Why C wrong: Resuscitation continues, not discontinued.  Why D wrong: CPR is resumed immediately, not delayed by a 2-minute pulse check."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"(NORCET Recall) In a patient who is in early management during cardiac arrest in hospital to maximum survival, which is correct?","options":["Early Recognisation, early assess of Medical care, Early CPR","Early AED, early CPR, reassure the patient for emotional stability","Early defibrillator, early emotional stability","Early CPR & early discharge"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For in-hospital cardiac arrest to maximize survival, the chain emphasizes early recognition, early assessment/access to medical care, and early CPR.  Why B wrong: Emotional reassurance is not the priority during active arrest management.  Why C wrong: \"Early emotional stability\" is not a survival-chain element.  Why D wrong: Early discharge is not part of arrest management."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"(NORCET Recall) A patient falls down from a height and you are the witness; after initial assessment the victim has no pulse or breathing. Which action should be taken next after the emergency call?","options":["Start CPR","Inform Physician","Give 10–12 breaths then reassess","Give 20–22 breaths then assess for 2 minutes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A witnessed collapse with no pulse or breathing requires starting CPR immediately after the emergency call.  Why B wrong: Informing the physician delays life-saving CPR.  Why C wrong: Rescue breaths alone are inadequate for a pulseless victim.  Why D wrong: CPR (compressions) must begin; giving breaths only is incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"(NORCET Recall) The location for chest compression during neonatal resuscitation is:","options":["Lower middle one-third of sternum","Lower half of sternum","Just above the nipple line","Between the xiphoid process landmarks"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For neonatal resuscitation, chest compressions are performed on the lower one-third (lower middle one-third) of the sternum, just below the nipple line.  Why B wrong: The lower HALF of the sternum is the adult/child landmark, not neonatal.  Why C wrong: \"Just above the nipple line\" is not the correct neonatal landmark.  Why D wrong: Compressions are not placed between/at the xiphoid process."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"(NORCET Recall) Which pulse is used for further assessment of the unconscious adult client during resuscitation (CPR)?","options":["Carotid","Femoral","Brachial","Radial"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The carotid pulse is used to further assess an unconscious adult client during CPR.  Why B wrong: The femoral pulse is not the standard CPR check site in adults.  Why C wrong: The brachial pulse is used for infants.  Why D wrong: The radial pulse is unreliable in low-perfusion arrest states."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"(NORCET Recall) What is the rate of chest compression and ventilation in children?","options":["60 compressions : 20 ventilation","90 compressions : 30 ventilation","90 compressions : 50 ventilation","100 compressions : 20 ventilation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The book keys 90 compressions : 30 ventilation as the rate of chest compression and ventilation in children. (Standard pediatric guidance uses a 30:2 ratio for single rescuer / 15:2 for two rescuers at ~100–120/min; the keyed source answer is B.)  Why A wrong: 60:20 is not the keyed answer.  Why C wrong: 90:50 is not the keyed answer.  Why D wrong: 100:20 is not the keyed answer."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"(NORCET Recall) During CPR, the rescuers should change their position:","options":["Every three minutes or after two cycles of CPR","Every two minutes or five cycles of CPR","Every five minutes or two cycles of CPR","Every one minute or two cycles of CPR"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Rescuers should change position every two minutes or after five cycles of CPR to prevent fatigue and maintain high-quality compressions.  Why A wrong: Three minutes/two cycles is incorrect.  Why C wrong: Five minutes/two cycles is incorrect.  Why D wrong: One minute/two cycles is incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"(NORCET Recall) The chest compression-to-ventilation ratio that should be maintained in neonatal resuscitation is:","options":["3:1 ratio of 90 chest compression and 30 ventilation every minute","3:1 ratio of 90 chest compression and 90 ventilation every minute","3:1 ratio of 60 chest compression and 90 ventilation every minute","3:1 ratio of 90 chest compression and 45 ventilation every minute"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In neonatal resuscitation, the 3:1 ratio yields 90 chest compressions and 30 ventilations every minute.  Why B wrong: 90 ventilations does not match the 3:1 ratio.  Why C wrong: 60 compressions with 90 ventilations is incorrect.  Why D wrong: 45 ventilations is incorrect for the 3:1 neonatal ratio."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"(NORCET Recall) An unresponsive infant is in need of resuscitation. Which artery would be assessed?","options":["Brachial","Radial","Carotid","Brachial (carotid is the adult site)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In an unresponsive infant, the brachial artery is assessed for a pulse.  Why B wrong: The radial artery is unreliable in infants.  Why C wrong: The carotid artery is the adult site, not used in infants.  Why D wrong: This restates the brachial choice; the single correct answer is brachial."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"(NORCET Recall) A patient has sudden cardiac arrest. What would be the first-line management of the patient?","options":["Check consciousness","Check breathing","Check pulse","Chest compression"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For sudden cardiac arrest, the first-line management is to check consciousness (assess responsiveness) before proceeding.  Why B wrong: Breathing is checked after responsiveness.  Why C wrong: Pulse is checked after/with the breathing assessment.  Why D wrong: Chest compressions begin after determining no pulse and no normal breathing."},{"section":"Batch 029 · CPR/BLS guidelines…","question":"(NORCET Recall) Landmark of chest compression during CPR in an adult patient is:","options":["Lower one-third of the sternum","Lower half of the sternum","Upper two-third of the sternum","Upper part of the sternum"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In an adult (and child) the landmark for chest compression is the lower half of the sternum.  Why A wrong: The lower one-third (just below the nipple line) is the NEONATAL landmark, not adult.  Why C wrong: The upper two-thirds is incorrect and risks injury.  Why D wrong: The upper part of the sternum is not the compression site."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"Immediately after Return of Spontaneous Circulation (ROSC) is obtained in the post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, what is the FIRST priority during the initial stabilization phase?","options":["Obtain a 12-lead ECG","Manage the airway and ensure early placement of an advanced airway","Begin targeted temperature management","Consider emergent cardiac intervention"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the initial stabilization phase after ROSC, airway management is a primary activity — placing an endotracheal tube early and managing respiratory parameters.  Why A wrong: A 12-lead ECG is part of subsequent continued management, not the first stabilization step.  Why C wrong: Targeted temperature management is part of continued management/additional emergent activities, after stabilization.  Why D wrong: Emergent cardiac intervention is considered later in the algorithm after stabilization."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"During post-ROSC respiratory management, the goal for oxygen saturation (SpO₂) should be maintained at:","options":["88%–92%","92%–98%","100% at all times","Below 90%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The algorithm specifies maintaining SpO₂ at 92%–98% to avoid hyperoxia while ensuring adequate oxygenation.  Why A wrong: 88%–92% is a COPD target, not the post-ROSC goal here.  Why C wrong: Maintaining 100% (excess oxygen/hyperoxia) is to be avoided after ROSC.  Why D wrong: Below 90% reflects hypoxemia, which is harmful."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"In post-ROSC respiratory management, the recommended starting ventilation rate is:","options":["6 breaths per minute","10 breaths per minute","20 breaths per minute","30 breaths per minute"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The algorithm directs to start at 10 breaths per minute and titrate to target PaCO₂.  Why A wrong: 6 breaths/min is too low to maintain normocapnia.  Why C/D wrong: 20–30 breaths/min would cause hyperventilation and hypocapnia, reducing cerebral perfusion."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"The target PaCO₂ in post-cardiac arrest respiratory management is:","options":["20–30 mmHg","35–45 mmHg","45–55 mmHg","55–65 mmHg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The algorithm specifies a PaCO₂ goal of 35–45 mmHg (normocapnia).  Why A wrong: 20–30 mmHg reflects hyperventilation/hypocapnia.  Why C/D wrong: 45–65 mmHg reflects hypercapnia/hypoventilation."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"During post-ROSC hemodynamic management, the target systolic blood pressure should be maintained at:","options":[">90 mmHg",">100 mmHg",">120 mmHg","<90 mmHg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The algorithm directs to manage hemodynamic parameters with a target systolic blood pressure >90 mmHg.  Why B/C wrong: These higher thresholds are not the stated target.  Why D wrong: A systolic pressure <90 mmHg represents hypotension that must be corrected."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"During post-ROSC hemodynamic management, the target mean arterial pressure (MAP) should be maintained at:","options":[">55 mmHg",">65 mmHg",">85 mmHg","<60 mmHg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The algorithm specifies maintaining MAP >65 mmHg to ensure adequate organ perfusion after ROSC.  Why A wrong: >55 mmHg is below the stated target.  Why C wrong: >85 mmHg is higher than the stated minimum target.  Why D wrong: <60 mmHg reflects inadequate perfusion."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"Targeted temperature management (TTM) after cardiac arrest is most appropriate for which patients?","options":["All patients who achieve ROSC, regardless of responsiveness","Patients who remain comatose / do not follow commands after ROSC","Only awake patients following commands","Only pediatric patients"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: TTM is directed toward patients who do not follow commands (comatose) after ROSC to protect neurologic function.  Why A wrong: Patients who follow commands (awake) do not require TTM per the algorithm.  Why C wrong: Awake, command-following patients proceed to other critical care management without TTM.  Why D wrong: TTM applies to adult post-arrest care here, not exclusively to pediatrics."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"A patient who achieves ROSC after cardiac arrest does NOT follow commands. According to the algorithm, the next step is to:","options":["Stop all resuscitation efforts","Initiate targeted temperature management (comatose pathway)","Discharge the patient home","Administer a fluid bolus only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A patient who does not follow commands is comatose, and the comatose pathway directs TTM.  Why A wrong: ROSC has been achieved; resuscitation is not stopped.  Why C wrong: A post-arrest comatose patient requires intensive critical care, not discharge.  Why D wrong: A fluid bolus alone does not address the neuroprotective need; TTM is indicated."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"In the continued management phase after ROSC, an emergent percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI) is considered when there is evidence of:","options":["STEMI present","Hypoglycemia","Hypothermia only","Normal ECG and stable cardiogenic status"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The algorithm directs consideration of emergent cardiac intervention if STEMI is present, unstable cardiogenic shock, or mechanical circulatory support is needed.  Why B wrong: Hypoglycemia is a metabolic correction, not an indication for PCI.  Why C wrong: Hypothermia is a reversible cause, not a trigger for PCI.  Why D wrong: A normal ECG with stable status does not indicate emergent PCI."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"During continued post-arrest management, which evaluations should be performed to identify and treat reversible causes? (Select all that apply.)","options":["12-lead ECG to detect STEMI/early diagnosis of myocardial infarction","Echocardiogram and continuous electroencephalographic (EEG) monitoring","Targeted temperature management coordinated with PCI","Routine elective discharge planning","Identification and correction of electrolyte/metabolic abnormalities"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: Continued management includes 12-lead ECG/early MI diagnosis, echocardiography, continuous EEG monitoring, TTM coordinated with cardiac intervention, and correction of electrolyte/metabolic disorders to treat reversible causes.  Why D wrong: Elective discharge planning is not part of emergent reversible-cause evaluation in the acute post-arrest setting."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"The reversible causes of cardiac arrest are remembered as the \"H's and T's.\" Which of the following is one of the H's?","options":["Tamponade","Thrombosis","Hypoxia","Toxins"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Hypoxia is one of the H's of reversible causes.  Why A/B/D wrong: Tamponade (cardiac), thrombosis, and toxins are all T's, not H's."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"Which of the following correctly lists reversible causes of cardiac arrest classified as the \"H's\"? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Hypovolemia","Hypoxia","Hydrogen ion (acidosis)","Tension pneumothorax","Hypo-/hyperkalemia and Hypothermia"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: The H's include Hypovolemia, Hypoxia, Hydrogen ion (acidosis), Hypo-/hyperkalemia, and Hypothermia.  Why D wrong: Tension pneumothorax is a \"T,\" not an \"H.\""},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"Which of the following are reversible causes of cardiac arrest classified as the \"T's\"? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Tension pneumothorax","Tamponade (cardiac)","Toxins","Hypovolemia","Thrombosis (pulmonary and coronary)"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: The T's include Tension pneumothorax, Tamponade (cardiac), Toxins, and Thrombosis (pulmonary and coronary).  Why D wrong: Hypovolemia is an \"H,\" not a \"T.\""},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"In the Pediatric Basic Life Support algorithm for a single rescuer who finds an unresponsive child, the FIRST action is to:","options":["Begin chest compressions immediately","Verify scene safety","Activate emergency response and retrieve the AED","Check the pulse for 30 seconds"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The algorithm begins with \"Verify scene safety\" before approaching the victim.  Why A wrong: Compressions follow assessment, not before ensuring scene safety.  Why C wrong: Activating emergency response/retrieving the AED comes after recognizing the emergency.  Why D wrong: Pulse should be checked for no more than 10 seconds, and only after scene safety and responsiveness checks."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"In pediatric BLS, when checking for a pulse in an unresponsive child, the rescuer should take:","options":["No more than 10 seconds","At least 30 seconds","Exactly 1 minute","No more than 5 seconds"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Pulse check is limited to no more than 10 seconds to avoid delaying CPR.  Why B/C wrong: Prolonged pulse checks delay life-saving compressions.  Why D wrong: 5 seconds may be insufficient to reliably detect a pulse; the standard is up to 10 seconds."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"A child has a palpable pulse but is not breathing normally (only gasping). According to pediatric BLS, the rescuer should provide rescue breathing at a rate of:","options":["1 breath every 2–3 seconds (about 20–30 breaths/min)","1 breath every 6 seconds (about 10 breaths/min)","1 breath every 15 seconds","1 breath every minute"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For a child with a pulse but inadequate breathing, give 1 breath every 2–3 seconds (about 20–30 breaths per minute).  Why B wrong: 1 breath every 6 seconds is the adult rescue-breathing rate.  Why C/D wrong: These rates are far too slow for a pediatric patient."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"In pediatric BLS, if the child has a pulse but the heart rate is less than 60/min with signs of poor perfusion despite adequate oxygenation and ventilation, the rescuer should:","options":["Continue rescue breathing only","Begin CPR (chest compressions and ventilations)","Wait and recheck the pulse in 2 minutes","Administer a fluid bolus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A heart rate <60/min with poor perfusion in a child is an indication to start CPR with compressions.  Why A wrong: Rescue breathing alone is inadequate when there is bradycardia with poor perfusion.  Why C wrong: Delaying CPR worsens outcomes.  Why D wrong: Fluids are not the primary BLS response to bradycardia with poor perfusion."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"In pediatric Single Rescuer CPR, when no AED is immediately available, the rescuer should perform cycles of compressions and breaths in a ratio of:","options":["30:2","15:2","5:1","10:2"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A single rescuer uses a 30:2 compression-to-ventilation ratio (same as adult single-rescuer CPR).  Why B wrong: 15:2 is the two-rescuer pediatric ratio.  Why C/D wrong: These ratios are not standard for pediatric BLS."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"In pediatric CPR with two rescuers (Double Rescuer), the compression-to-ventilation ratio is:","options":["30:2","15:2","5:1","3:1"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: With two rescuers, pediatric CPR uses a 15:2 compression-to-ventilation ratio.  Why A wrong: 30:2 is the single-rescuer ratio.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the recommended two-rescuer pediatric ratios."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"A single rescuer witnesses a child suddenly collapse. After verifying scene safety and recognizing the emergency, the rescuer should:","options":["Start CPR immediately and wait 2 minutes before calling for help","Activate the emergency response system and retrieve the AED before starting CPR","Provide only rescue breathing","Place the child in the recovery position"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For a witnessed sudden collapse, the cause is likely a shockable cardiac event, so the lone rescuer should activate emergency response and get the AED/defibrillator first.  Why A wrong: Help should be summoned promptly for a witnessed sudden collapse.  Why C wrong: A pulseless collapsed child needs CPR with compressions, not breathing alone.  Why D wrong: The recovery position is for a breathing, perfusing patient, not a collapsed unresponsive child."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"For high-quality CPR in pediatric cardiac arrest, the recommended compression depth is:","options":["At least one-third the anterior-posterior diameter of the chest","Exactly 2 inches in all children","One-half the chest diameter","As shallow as possible to avoid injury"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Pediatric CPR quality requires pushing at least one-third the anterior-posterior diameter of the chest (about 5 cm in children).  Why B wrong: A fixed 2-inch depth is the adult standard; pediatric depth is proportional to chest size.  Why C wrong: One-half the chest diameter is excessive and may cause injury.  Why D wrong: Inadequate depth produces ineffective compressions."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"During pediatric CPR, rescuers should change the compressor to avoid fatigue and maintain quality approximately:","options":["Every 30 seconds","Every 2 minutes (or sooner if fatigued)","Every 5 minutes","Only when the rescuer feels tired"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: To prevent quality decline from fatigue, the compressor should be changed every 2 minutes (or sooner if fatigued).  Why A wrong: 30 seconds is too frequent and disrupts continuity.  Why C wrong: 5 minutes is too long; fatigue degrades compression quality before then.  Why D wrong: Relying on perceived fatigue alone is unreliable; the timed 2-minute rotation is the standard."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"In the Pediatric Cardiac Arrest Summary, what is the recommended FIRST shock energy for defibrillation?","options":["1 J/kg","2 J/kg","4 J/kg","10 J/kg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The first defibrillation shock in children is 2 J/kg.  Why A wrong: 1 J/kg is below the recommended first-shock dose.  Why C wrong: 4 J/kg is the second shock dose.  Why D wrong: 10 J/kg exceeds the maximum/adult dose limit."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"In pediatric defibrillation, the SECOND shock energy is:","options":["1 J/kg","2 J/kg","4 J/kg","6 J/kg"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The second defibrillation shock is 4 J/kg.  Why A/B wrong: 1–2 J/kg is below the second-shock dose.  Why D wrong: 6 J/kg is a subsequent shock dose, not the second."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"For subsequent pediatric defibrillation shocks, the energy may be increased up to a maximum of:","options":["2 J/kg","4 J/kg","10 J/kg (or the adult dose)","20 J/kg"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Subsequent shocks may be increased to 4 J/kg or higher, up to a maximum of 10 J/kg or the adult dose.  Why A/B wrong: These are the first/second shock energies, not the maximum.  Why D wrong: 20 J/kg exceeds the stated maximum."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"In pediatric cardiac arrest drug therapy, the recommended intravenous/intraosseous (IV/IO) dose of epinephrine is:","options":["0.1 mg/kg of 1:1,000 concentration","0.01 mg/kg (0.1 mL/kg of the 0.1 mg/mL [1:10,000] concentration)","1 mg undiluted","0.5 mg/kg of 1:1,000"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pediatric epinephrine IV/IO dose is 0.01 mg/kg (0.1 mL/kg of the 0.1 mg/mL, i.e., 1:10,000, concentration), repeated every 3–5 minutes.  Why A wrong: 0.1 mg/kg overdoses the patient and uses the wrong concentration.  Why C wrong: 1 mg undiluted is an adult fixed dose, not weight-based pediatric dosing.  Why D wrong: 0.5 mg/kg is far too high and the wrong concentration."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"During pediatric cardiac arrest, epinephrine doses should be repeated:","options":["Every 1 minute","Every 3–5 minutes","Every 10 minutes","Only once"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Epinephrine is repeated every 3–5 minutes during pediatric resuscitation.  Why A wrong: Every 1 minute is too frequent.  Why C wrong: Every 10 minutes is too infrequent for effective resuscitation.  Why D wrong: Repeated dosing, not a single dose, is required while arrest continues."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"In pediatric cardiac arrest, amiodarone is dosed as:","options":["1 mg/kg single bolus","5 mg/kg bolus during cardiac arrest (may repeat up to 3 doses for refractory VF/pulseless VT)","0.5 mg/kg infusion","10 mg/kg single dose"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Amiodarone is given as a 5 mg/kg bolus during cardiac arrest and may be repeated up to 3 total doses for refractory ventricular fibrillation/pulseless VT.  Why A/C/D wrong: These doses/routes do not match the recommended pediatric amiodarone bolus."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"Which of the following are listed as reversible causes (\"H's and T's\") in the Pediatric Cardiac Arrest Summary? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Hypovolemia and Hypoxia","Hydrogen ion (acidosis) and Hypo-/hyperkalemia","Hypothermia and Hypoglycemia","Tension pneumothorax and Tamponade (cardiac)","Toxins, Thrombosis (pulmonary), and Thrombosis (coronary)"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The pediatric reversible causes include Hypovolemia, Hypoxia, Hydrogen ion (acidosis), Hypoglycemia, Hypo-/hyperkalemia, Hypothermia (H's) and Tension pneumothorax, Tamponade, Toxins, Thrombosis pulmonary, Thrombosis coronary (T's).  Why all correct: Each option pairs causes explicitly listed in the pediatric reversible-causes summary."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"Neonatal resuscitation is defined as a set of interventions used to assist the newborn in establishing which functions?","options":["Only spontaneous breathing","Airway, breathing, and circulation","Only effective circulation","Temperature regulation only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Neonatal resuscitation assists the airway, breathing, and circulation of a newborn at birth.  Why A/C wrong: It addresses more than one function — breathing and circulation together.  Why D wrong: Temperature regulation is part of routine care, not the defining purpose of resuscitation."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"What proportion of newborn infants require some assistance to begin breathing at birth?","options":["About 50%","Up to 10%","Up to 25%","About 75%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Up to 10% of infants are born requiring some assistance to begin breathing.  Why A/C/D wrong: These percentages overstate the proportion of newborns needing breathing assistance."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"According to guidelines, every birth should be attended by at least how many person(s) qualified to begin neonatal resuscitation?","options":["1 qualified individual (skilled in initial steps and positive-pressure ventilation)","2 anesthesiologists","4 nurses","No one is required if birth appears normal"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: At least 1 qualified individual whose only responsibility is to manage the newborn — skilled in the initial steps of care and positive-pressure ventilation — should attend every birth.  Why B/C wrong: Specific specialists in those numbers are not the stated minimum requirement.  Why D wrong: Every birth, including apparently normal ones, must have someone qualified to resuscitate present."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"Which of the following are the four pre-birth questions the obstetric provider should ask before every birth? (Select all that apply.)","options":["What is the expected gestational age?","Is the amniotic fluid clear?","How many babies are expected?","Are there any additional risk factors?","What is the mother's blood group?"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The four pre-birth questions are gestational age, whether the amniotic fluid is clear, how many babies are expected, and whether there are additional risk factors.  Why E wrong: Maternal blood group is not one of the four standard pre-birth assessment questions listed."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"A baby is born at term, with clear amniotic fluid, good muscle tone, and is breathing or crying. According to the rapid evaluation, the appropriate action is to:","options":["Begin positive-pressure ventilation","Provide routine care (warmth, position, clear airway if needed, dry, and stimulate) with the mother","Begin chest compressions","Administer epinephrine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: When the answer is \"yes\" to all rapid-evaluation questions, the newborn stays with the mother for routine care: provide warmth, ensure normal temperature, position airway, clear secretions if needed, dry, and stimulate.  Why A/C/D wrong: PPV, compressions, and medications are reserved for newborns who fail the initial evaluation/steps, not vigorous term infants."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"Which of the following are the initial steps of newborn care provided in the first step of neonatal resuscitation? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Provide warmth and maintain normal body temperature","Position the head and neck (airway)","Clear secretions if needed","Dry the baby","Stimulate"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The initial steps of newborn care are: provide warmth/maintain normal body temperature, position the head and neck, clear the airway if needed, dry, and stimulate.  Why all correct: Each is explicitly listed among the initial steps of newborn care."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"In neonatal resuscitation, after the initial steps, if the baby is apneic or the heart rate is less than 100/min, the next critical intervention (Step 3) is to begin:","options":["Chest compressions","Positive-pressure ventilation (PPV)","Medication administration","Routine observation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: When the newborn is apneic or HR <100/min after initial steps, positive-pressure ventilation is begun — ventilation is the priority intervention in neonatal resuscitation.  Why A wrong: Chest compressions follow only if HR remains <60/min despite effective PPV.  Why C wrong: Medications come after compressions if HR stays low.  Why D wrong: An apneic or bradycardic newborn requires active ventilation, not observation."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"During neonatal resuscitation, after 30 seconds of effective positive-pressure ventilation, if the heart rate is less than 60/min, the next step (Step 4) is to begin chest compressions coordinated with PPV in what ratio?","options":["30:2","15:2","3:1 (3 compressions to 1 ventilation)","5:1"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In neonatal resuscitation, chest compressions are coordinated with PPV at a 3:1 ratio (3 compressions to 1 ventilation).  Why A/B wrong: 30:2 and 15:2 are adult/pediatric ratios, not neonatal.  Why D wrong: 5:1 is not the neonatal compression-to-ventilation ratio."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"During neonatal resuscitation, if the heart rate remains less than 60/min despite effective ventilation and chest compressions, Step 5 is to:","options":["Discontinue all efforts","Administer medication (epinephrine) along with PPV and chest compressions","Reposition the airway only","Provide routine care with the mother"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: When HR stays <60/min despite PPV and compressions, medication (epinephrine) is administered along with continued PPV and chest compressions (Step 5).  Why A wrong: Resuscitation continues; efforts are escalated, not stopped.  Why C wrong: Repositioning is part of earlier corrective steps, not Step 5.  Why D wrong: A severely bradycardic newborn requires intensive resuscitation, not routine care."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"\"RECALL THE NCBOL?\": How many ET tubes need to be kept in an incubator/ready for a neonatal resuscitation?","options":["2","3","4","5"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The boxed recall note states that during neonatal resuscitation, three ET tubes (sizes 2.5, 3.0, and 3.5) are to be kept (Right Answer = 3).  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies three tubes, not 2, 4, or 5."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"Immediately after birth, the recommended first action for a newborn during the resuscitation steps is to:","options":["Suction the mouth and nose first","Dry the baby (especially the head) with a warm dry towel, discard the wet towel, and cover the baby with a dry towel","Begin chest compressions","Clamp the cord immediately"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Immediately after birth, dry the baby (especially the head) with a warm dry towel, discard the wet towel, and cover with a dry towel to prevent heat loss.  Why A wrong: Routine deep suctioning is avoided; drying and stimulation come first.  Why C wrong: Compressions are only for persistent bradycardia after ventilation.  Why D wrong: Cord clamping timing is a separate consideration; drying/warming is the immediate priority."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"Regarding umbilical cord clamping in neonatal resuscitation, current guidance recommends that:","options":["The cord be clamped immediately in all newborns","Clamping the umbilical cord be delayed for at least 30–60 seconds for most vigorous newborns not requiring resuscitation","The cord never be clamped","Clamping be delayed at least 5 minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Clamping the umbilical cord should be delayed for at least 30–60 seconds for most vigorous newborns who do not require resuscitation; evidence is insufficient to recommend a definitive approach for non-vigorous newborns.  Why A wrong: Immediate clamping is not recommended for vigorous newborns.  Why C wrong: The cord is eventually clamped; \"never\" is incorrect.  Why D wrong: The recommended delay is 30–60 seconds, not 5 minutes."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"During neonatal resuscitation, the heart rate is assessed by listening with a stethoscope and counting the number of beats in how long, then multiplying to obtain beats per minute?","options":["Count beats in 6 seconds and multiply by 10","Count beats in 15 seconds and multiply by 4","Count beats in 30 seconds and multiply by 2","Count beats for a full 60 seconds"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: To determine heart rate, listen with a stethoscope, count the beats in 6 seconds, and multiply by 10 to get beats per minute — allowing rapid assessment.  Why B/C/D wrong: These methods take longer and delay decision-making in a time-critical resuscitation."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"During neonatal resuscitation, when may CPAP be helpful?","options":["When the baby is apneic with no heart rate","If the baby is breathing and the heart rate is at least 100 beats per minute, but respirations are labored or oxygen saturation remains below target","Only when the heart rate is below 60/min","CPAP is never used in newborns"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: CPAP is helpful if the baby is breathing with HR ≥100 bpm but has labored respirations or oxygen saturation that remains below the target.  Why A wrong: An apneic, pulseless baby needs PPV and resuscitation, not CPAP.  Why C wrong: HR <60/min requires PPV/compressions, not CPAP.  Why D wrong: CPAP is a recognized supportive measure for spontaneously breathing newborns with respiratory distress."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"If meconium-stained fluid is present and the baby is vigorous during neonatal resuscitation, the appropriate action is to:","options":["Perform routine endotracheal suctioning before any other step","Suction the mouth and nose with a bulb syringe and proceed with the initial steps (NO routine tracheal suctioning)","Begin chest compressions immediately","Delay all care until the baby cries"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: When meconium-stained fluid is present and the baby is vigorous, suction the mouth and nose with a bulb syringe and continue the initial steps; routine endotracheal suctioning is not appropriate for a vigorous baby.  Why A wrong: Routine tracheal suctioning of a vigorous meconium-stained newborn is no longer recommended.  Why C wrong: Compressions are not indicated in a vigorous newborn.  Why D wrong: Care should not be delayed; the initial steps proceed promptly."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"During neonatal resuscitation, if the baby is not breathing or the heart rate is low after the initial steps, positive-pressure ventilation (PPV) should be started. The PPV should be initiated at a rate of:","options":["10–20 breaths per minute","40–60 breaths per minute","80–100 breaths per minute","100–120 breaths per minute"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: PPV in the newborn is delivered at a rate of about 40–60 breaths per minute.  Why A wrong: 10–20/min is too slow for a non-breathing newborn.  Why C/D wrong: 80–120/min is excessively fast and risks air trapping/barotrauma."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"When beginning neonatal positive-pressure ventilation, the recommended initial ventilation (inspiratory) pressure is:","options":["5–10 cm H₂O","20–25 cm H₂O","35–40 cm H₂O","50 cm H₂O"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The initial ventilation pressure for newborn PPV is 20–25 cm H₂O.  Why A wrong: 5–10 cm H₂O is generally insufficient to inflate the lungs initially.  Why C/D wrong: Pressures of 35–50 cm H₂O risk barotrauma/pneumothorax."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"During neonatal PPV, an appropriate approach for sizing the resuscitation (AMBU) mask is to:","options":["Use one universal size for all newborns","Use AMBU mask/appropriate sizes — 1 size for preterm and 1 size for term babies","Never use a mask, only an ET tube","Use an adult-sized mask"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: PPV uses an AMBU mask in appropriate sizes — 1 size for preterm and 1 size for term babies.  Why A wrong: A single universal size does not provide an adequate seal for both preterm and term infants.  Why C wrong: A mask is used for PPV before/instead of intubation as appropriate.  Why D wrong: An adult-sized mask is grossly oversized and would not seal on a newborn."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"If positive-pressure ventilation appears ineffective in a newborn (heart rate not increasing and chest not moving), corrective ventilation steps are taken. These steps are remembered by which mnemonic?","options":["MONA","MR SOPA","ABCDE","SAMPLE"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Ineffective PPV is corrected using the MR SOPA mnemonic (Mask adjustment, Reposition airway, Suction mouth/nose, Open mouth, Pressure increase, Alternative airway).  Why A wrong: MONA relates to acute coronary syndrome management.  Why C wrong: ABCDE is a general primary-survey mnemonic.  Why D wrong: SAMPLE is a history-taking mnemonic."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"In the MR SOPA corrective ventilation steps, what does the first \"M\" stand for, and what is the corresponding action?","options":["Medication — administer epinephrine","Mask adjustment — reapply the mask and consider the chin technique to obtain a good seal","Monitor — attach a pulse oximeter","Move — transport the baby"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: \"M\" = Mask adjustment; the action is to reapply the mask and consider the chin/2-finger technique to achieve an adequate seal.  Why A wrong: Medication is not part of the MR SOPA ventilation-correction sequence.  Why C wrong: Monitoring is not the \"M\" of MR SOPA.  Why D wrong: \"M\" does not stand for move/transport."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"Match the MR SOPA corrective steps with their actions. Which of the following pairings are correct? (Select all that apply.)","options":["R = Reposition airway → place head in neutral or slightly extended (\"sniffing\") position","S = Suction mouth and nose","O = Open the mouth → open the mouth and lift the jaw forward","P = Pressure increase → increase pressure in increments, maximum 40 cm H₂O","A = Alternative airway → place an endotracheal tube or laryngeal airway"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Each pairing matches the MR SOPA steps — Reposition airway (neutral/slightly extended), Suction mouth and nose, Open the mouth (open and lift jaw forward), Pressure increase (increase pressure, max 40 cm H₂O), Alternative airway (ET tube or laryngeal airway), reassessing chest movement and breath sounds throughout.  Why all correct: All five letter-action pairings are explicitly listed for MR SOPA."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"In the MR SOPA step \"P\" (Pressure increase), the inspiratory pressure may be increased in increments up to a maximum of:","options":["20 cm H₂O","30 cm H₂O","40 cm H₂O","60 cm H₂O"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: During the \"Pressure increase\" step, pressure is raised in increments to a maximum of 40 cm H₂O.  Why A/B wrong: These are below the stated maximum.  Why D wrong: 60 cm H₂O exceeds the safe maximum and risks barotrauma."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"Which type of laryngoscope blades are preferred for intubating neonates?","options":["Curved (Macintosh) blades","Straight (Miller) blades","Either, with no preference","Hyperangulated video blades only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Straight blades (Miller) are preferred for neonates rather than curved (Macintosh) blades.  Why A wrong: Curved Macintosh blades are not the preferred neonatal blade.  Why C wrong: There is a clear preference for straight (Miller) blades in neonates.  Why D wrong: Hyperangulated video blades are not specified as the preferred neonatal blade."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"Which ET tube internal diameters (sizes) should be kept available for neonatal intubation?","options":["2.5, 3.0, and 3.5 mm","4.0, 4.5, and 5.0 mm","6.0, 7.0, and 8.0 mm","1.0 and 1.5 mm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Three ET tubes with internal diameters of 2.5, 3.0, and 3.5 mm are kept for neonatal intubation.  Why B/C wrong: These larger sizes are for older children/adults.  Why D wrong: 1.0–1.5 mm tubes are too small and not standard neonatal ET tube sizes."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"Which size AMBU (resuscitation bag) mask is recommended for a preterm or extremely low birth weight (ELBW) baby?","options":["No. 1 mask","No. 0 mask (for preterm/ELBW baby)","No. 2 mask","No. 5 mask"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The No. 0 AMBU mask is used for the preterm/ELBW baby, while the No. 1 mask is for the term newborn.  Why A wrong: No. 1 is for the term newborn, not the preterm/ELBW infant.  Why C/D wrong: Larger masks are for older infants/children."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"A newborn weighing under 1,000 g with a gestational age under 28 weeks requires intubation. According to the ET tube size chart, the correct tube size is:","options":["2.5 mm","3.0 mm","3.5 mm","4.0 mm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For a newborn <1,000 g and <28 weeks gestation, the recommended ET tube size is 2.5 mm.  Why B wrong: 3.0 mm is for ~1,000–2,000 g / 28–34 weeks infants.  Why C wrong: 3.5 mm is for infants >2,000 g / >34 weeks.  Why D wrong: 4.0 mm exceeds the neonatal range listed in the chart."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"According to the ET tube size chart for newborns, an infant weighing 1,000–2,000 g with a gestational age of 28–34 weeks should be intubated with an ET tube size of:","options":["2.5 mm","3.0 mm","3.5 mm","4.0 mm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For 1,000–2,000 g and 28–34 weeks gestation, the chart specifies a 3.0 mm ET tube.  Why A wrong: 2.5 mm is for <1,000 g / <28 weeks.  Why C wrong: 3.5 mm is for >2,000 g / >34 weeks.  Why D wrong: 4.0 mm is outside the chart's neonatal range."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"According to the ET tube size chart for newborns, an infant weighing more than 2,000 g with a gestational age greater than 34 weeks should be intubated with an ET tube size of:","options":["2.5 mm","3.0 mm","3.5 mm","5.0 mm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: For >2,000 g and >34 weeks gestation, the chart specifies a 3.5 mm ET tube.  Why A/B wrong: Smaller sizes are for lighter, more premature infants.  Why D wrong: 5.0 mm exceeds the term-newborn ET tube size in this chart."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"Neonatal chest compressions are indicated when the heart rate remains less than 60 beats per minute after at least 30 seconds of effective PPV that includes ventilation through which airway?","options":["A bag-mask alone, regardless of effectiveness","A properly inserted endotracheal tube or laryngeal airway with ventilation","A nasal cannula","No airway — compressions begin immediately"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Compressions are indicated after at least 30 seconds of effective ventilation that includes ventilation through a properly inserted endotracheal tube or laryngeal airway, when HR remains <60/min.  Why A wrong: Effective ventilation (not merely bag-mask attempts) must be confirmed, ideally via a secured airway.  Why C wrong: A nasal cannula does not provide adequate PPV for a non-breathing newborn.  Why D wrong: Effective ventilation must precede compressions in neonatal resuscitation."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"During neonatal chest compressions, the lower third of the sternum should be compressed approximately:","options":["One-fourth the chest diameter","One-third (1/3) the anterior-posterior (AP) diameter of the chest","One-half the chest diameter","The full depth of the chest"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Chest compressions should depress the lower third of the sternum to about one-third (1/3) of the anterior-posterior diameter of the chest.  Why A wrong: One-fourth is insufficient depth.  Why C/D wrong: One-half or full depth is excessive and would cause injury."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"During neonatal resuscitation, the total number of \"events\" delivered per minute when compressions and breaths are coordinated at the 3:1 ratio is:","options":["90 compressions and 30 breaths (120 events per minute)","60 compressions and 20 breaths","100 compressions and 10 breaths","30 compressions and 30 breaths"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A total of 120 events per minute are delivered — 90 compressions and 30 breaths — when coordinated at the 3:1 ratio.  Why B/C/D wrong: These totals do not match the 90:30 (120 events/min) standard for the neonatal 3:1 ratio."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"During coordinated neonatal compressions and ventilations, one cycle consists of 3 compressions and 1 ventilation, and each cycle should be completed in:","options":["1 second","A 2-second cycle (ratio of 3:1)","4 seconds","6 seconds"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Each cycle (3 compressions + 1 ventilation) is performed within a 2-second cycle, maintaining the 3:1 ratio.  Why A wrong: 1 second is too short to complete 3 compressions and 1 breath.  Why C/D wrong: Longer cycles would reduce the event rate below the required 120/min."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"When performing the two-thumb chest compression technique in a neonate, the thumbs are placed to depress the sternum while:","options":["The fingers wrap around to compress the abdomen","The hands encircle the chest and the fingers of both hands support the back to compress the sternum","Only one thumb is used","The thumbs compress over the lower ribs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the two-thumb (thumb) technique, two thumbs depress the sternum while the hands encircle the chest and the fingers of both hands support the back to compress the sternum.  Why A wrong: Compressions are over the sternum, not the abdomen.  Why C wrong: The two-thumb technique uses both thumbs, not one.  Why D wrong: Compression is on the lower third of the sternum, not over the lower ribs."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"The alternative neonatal chest compression technique using two fingers involves:","options":["Two fingers of one hand depressing the sternum, with the other hand supporting the back","Both palms pressing the chest","A single finger over the abdomen","The heel of one hand on the sternum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The two-finger technique uses two fingers of one hand to depress the sternum, while the other hand supports/stabilizes the back.  Why B wrong: Both palms are not used in the two-finger technique.  Why C wrong: A single finger over the abdomen is incorrect placement and technique.  Why D wrong: The heel-of-hand method is an adult/older-child technique, not the neonatal two-finger technique."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"Epinephrine (ADR/adrenaline) is indicated in neonatal resuscitation when the baby's heart rate remains below 60 bpm after at least how many seconds of PPV that inflates the lungs (moves the chest), and another 60 seconds of chest compressions coordinated with PPV using 100% oxygen?","options":["15 seconds","30 seconds","60 seconds","5 minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Epinephrine is indicated if the heart rate stays below 60 bpm after at least 30 seconds of PPV that inflates the lungs, followed by another 60 seconds of chest compressions coordinated with PPV using 100% oxygen.  Why A wrong: 15 seconds is shorter than the required period of effective ventilation.  Why C wrong: 60 seconds describes the duration of compressions, not the preceding ventilation period.  Why D wrong: 5 minutes is far longer than the stated threshold."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"What is the recommended concentration of epinephrine used in neonatal resuscitation?","options":["1:1,000 (1 mg/mL)","1:10,000 (0.1 mg/mL), i.e., 1 mL preparation diluted to 10 mL","1:100 (10 mg/mL)","Undiluted ampoule"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The 1:10,000 concentration (0.1 mg/mL) is used for neonatal resuscitation; this is the diluted preparation (e.g., 1 mL diluted to 10 mL).  Why A wrong: 1:1,000 is too concentrated for direct neonatal IV/IO use.  Why C/D wrong: 1:100 and undiluted preparations are far too concentrated and unsafe for newborns."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"Which routes of administration are appropriate for epinephrine during neonatal resuscitation? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Intravenous (IV)","Intraosseous (IO)","Endotracheal (ET)","Subcutaneous","Intramuscular"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Epinephrine routes in neonatal resuscitation are intravenous, intraosseous, and endotracheal.  Why D/E wrong: Subcutaneous and intramuscular routes are not used for emergency epinephrine in neonatal resuscitation due to unreliable absorption during arrest."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"The recommended intravenous/intraosseous dose of epinephrine in neonatal resuscitation is:","options":["0.1 to 0.3 mg/kg","0.01 to 0.03 mg/kg (0.1 to 0.3 mL/kg of the 1:10,000 solution)","1 mg/kg","0.5 mg/kg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The IV/IO epinephrine dose is 0.01 to 0.03 mg/kg, which equals 0.1 to 0.3 mL/kg of the 1:10,000 (0.1 mg/mL) solution.  Why A wrong: 0.1–0.3 mg/kg is ten times too high.  Why C/D wrong: 1 mg/kg and 0.5 mg/kg are dangerously excessive for a neonate."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"When epinephrine is given by the endotracheal route in neonatal resuscitation, the recommended dose is:","options":["0.01 to 0.03 mg/kg","0.05 to 0.1 mg/kg (0.5 to 1 mL/kg of the 1:10,000 solution)","1 mg/kg","0.001 mg/kg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The endotracheal epinephrine dose is higher than IV/IO at 0.05 to 0.1 mg/kg (0.5 to 1 mL/kg of the 1:10,000 solution) because of less reliable absorption via the ET route.  Why A wrong: 0.01–0.03 mg/kg is the IV/IO dose, which is lower than the ET dose.  Why C wrong: 1 mg/kg is excessive.  Why D wrong: 0.001 mg/kg is sub-therapeutic."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"Why is the endotracheal route of epinephrine generally not the preferred first choice in neonatal resuscitation?","options":["It causes immediate cardiac arrest","The endotracheal route reaches the central circulation less reliably/consistently, so an IV or IO route is preferred","It is contraindicated in all newborns","It cannot be given through an ET tube"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The ET route reaches the central circulation less reliably/consistently, so the IV (or IO) route is preferred for epinephrine administration.  Why A wrong: ET epinephrine does not cause cardiac arrest; it is simply less reliably absorbed.  Why C wrong: ET administration is an option (with higher dosing), not absolutely contraindicated.  Why D wrong: It can be given through the ET tube; it is just less preferred than IV/IO."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"Crystalloid fluid (volume expansion) is indicated during neonatal resuscitation when:","options":["The newborn is vigorous and breathing well","There is no response to resuscitation and there are signs of shock or a history of acute blood loss","The heart rate is above 100 bpm","The baby is hypertensive"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Volume expansion with crystalloid is indicated when the baby is not responding to resuscitation and shows signs of shock or has a history of acute blood loss.  Why A wrong: A vigorous, well-breathing newborn does not need volume expansion.  Why C wrong: A heart rate above 100 bpm indicates adequate cardiac response, not a need for fluids.  Why D wrong: Hypertension is not an indication for volume expansion."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"The recommended crystalloid fluid for acute volume expansion (hypovolemia) in neonatal resuscitation is:","options":["5% dextrose","0.9% NaCl (normal saline)","3% hypertonic saline","Sterile water"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 0.9% NaCl (normal saline) is the recommended crystalloid for treating acute hypovolemia in neonatal resuscitation.  Why A wrong: 5% dextrose is not used for volume expansion (it distributes out of the vascular space and can worsen perfusion).  Why C wrong: 3% hypertonic saline is not used for routine neonatal volume resuscitation.  Why D wrong: Sterile water is hypotonic and unsafe for volume expansion."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"The recommended initial dose of the selected volume expander (crystalloid) in neonatal resuscitation is:","options":["5 mL/kg","10 mL/kg","20 mL/kg","40 mL/kg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The initial dose of the selected volume expander is 10 mL/kg.  Why A wrong: 5 mL/kg is below the recommended initial bolus.  Why C wrong: 20 mL/kg is a typical pediatric bolus but exceeds the neonatal initial dose stated.  Why D wrong: 40 mL/kg is excessive and risks volume overload in a neonate."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"During neonatal volume expansion, the crystalloid fluid should be administered:","options":["As an uncontrolled push over a few seconds","Rapidly via the IV/IO route (the volume expander is given rapidly)","Over 24 hours as a maintenance infusion","Only orally"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the volume expander is given rapidly via the IV/IO route to restore intravascular volume during shock/blood loss.  Why A wrong: An uncontrolled push over a few seconds is not the described administration; it is given rapidly but as a measured bolus.  Why C wrong: A 24-hour maintenance infusion does not address acute hypovolemic shock.  Why D wrong: Oral fluids are inappropriate for emergent volume resuscitation in a compromised newborn."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"A newborn fails to respond to effective ventilation and chest compressions, has a heart rate persistently below 60 bpm, and shows signs of shock with a history of acute blood loss. After epinephrine, the priority intervention is to:","options":["Withhold all fluids","Administer a 10 mL/kg bolus of 0.9% normal saline (crystalloid volume expander) rapidly IV/IO","Give 40 mL/kg of 5% dextrose","Begin oral feeding"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: With signs of shock and acute blood loss unresponsive to resuscitation, volume expansion with 0.9% normal saline 10 mL/kg given rapidly IV/IO is indicated.  Why A wrong: Withholding fluids in a hypovolemic, shocked newborn is harmful.  Why C wrong: 5% dextrose is not a volume expander, and 40 mL/kg risks overload.  Why D wrong: Oral feeding is inappropriate during active resuscitation."},{"section":"Batch 030 · Post-cardiac arrest care algorithm, Adult Basic and Advanced Life Support…","question":"In adult basic and advanced life support, when resuscitation is ongoing during cardiac arrest, an early priority among airway and breathing activities is to:","options":["Defer airway management until ROSC","Place an advanced airway (e.g., endotracheal tube) early and manage respiratory parameters","Avoid all ventilation","Provide 100% oxygen only after ROSC"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: During adult resuscitation, early placement of an advanced airway and management of respiratory parameters is a key airway/breathing activity.  Why A wrong: Airway management is part of active resuscitation, not deferred until ROSC.  Why C wrong: Ventilation is essential during resuscitation.  Why D wrong: Oxygen is provided during resuscitation, not withheld until ROSC."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"During neonatal resuscitation, what is the correct concentration of epinephrine used?","options":["1:1,000 (1 mg/mL)","1:10,000 (0.1 mg/mL)","1:100,000 (0.01 mg/mL)","1:5,000 (0.2 mg/mL)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states epinephrine concentration for neonatal use is 1:10,000 (0.1 mg/mL).  Why A wrong: 1:1,000 is the more concentrated adult/IM cardiac arrest preparation, not the neonatal dilution.  Why C wrong: 1:100,000 is too dilute and not listed.  Why D wrong: 1:5,000 is not a standard epinephrine concentration on the page."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"Which route is most preferred for administering epinephrine in neonatal resuscitation?","options":["Endotracheal route","Intramuscular route","Intravenous (IV) through the umbilical venous catheter","Subcutaneous route"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists intravenous (IV) as most preferred, given through the umbilical venous catheter.  Why A wrong: Endotracheal is listed as less effective, not preferred.  Why B wrong: Intramuscular is not a listed route for neonatal epinephrine.  Why D wrong: Subcutaneous is not a listed route."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"Besides intravenous, which additional routes for neonatal epinephrine are described as \"less effective\"?","options":["Intramuscular and subcutaneous","Intra-osseous and intra-tracheal","Oral and rectal","Inhaled and topical"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states intra-osseous and intra-tracheal routes are less effective.  Why A wrong: IM and SC are not mentioned for neonatal epinephrine.  Why C/D wrong: Oral, rectal, inhaled, and topical routes are not listed."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"For intravenous or intra-osseous epinephrine, the page recommends preparation in a syringe labeled how?","options":["1 mL syringe labeled \"Epinephrine IV\"","3 to 5 mL syringe labeled \"Epinephrine ET only\"","10 mL syringe labeled \"Epinephrine\"","5 mL syringe labeled \"Cardiac\""],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page specifies a 1 mL syringe labeled \"Epinephrine IV\" for IV/IO use.  Why B wrong: The 3 to 5 mL syringe labeled \"Epinephrine ET only\" is for the endotracheal route, not IV/IO.  Why C/D wrong: These labels/sizes are not given on the page."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"How should the endotracheal epinephrine syringe be prepared and labeled?","options":["1 mL syringe labeled \"Epinephrine IV\"","3 to 5 mL syringe labeled \"Epinephrine ET only\"","10 mL syringe labeled \"Epinephrine ET\"","2 mL syringe labeled \"ET drug\""],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the endo-tracheal preparation is a 3 to 5 mL syringe labeled \"Epinephrine ET only.\"  Why A wrong: The 1 mL \"Epinephrine IV\" syringe is the IV/IO preparation.  Why C/D wrong: These sizes/labels are not listed."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"What is the intravenous/intra-osseous dose of epinephrine in neonatal resuscitation?","options":["0.01 to 0.03 mL/kg","0.1 to 0.3 mL/kg","0.5 to 1 mL/kg","1 to 2 mL/kg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the IV/IO dose is 0.1 to 0.3 mL/kg.  Why A wrong: 0.01 to 0.03 mL/kg is ten-fold too low and not listed.  Why C wrong: 0.5 to 1 mL/kg is the endo-tracheal dose, not IV/IO.  Why D wrong: 1 to 2 mL/kg is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"What is the endotracheal dose of epinephrine in neonatal resuscitation?","options":["0.1 to 0.3 mL/kg","0.5 to 1 mL/kg","1 to 2 mL/kg","0.01 to 0.05 mL/kg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the endo-tracheal dose is 0.5 to 1 mL/kg (higher than the IV/IO dose).  Why A wrong: 0.1 to 0.3 mL/kg is the IV/IO dose.  Why C/D wrong: These ranges are not listed."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"After giving IV/IO epinephrine during neonatal resuscitation, the line should be flushed with how much normal saline?","options":["0.1 to 0.2 mL","0.5 to 1 mL","2 to 3 mL","5 to 10 mL"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page directs flushing with 0.5 to 1 mL normal saline after IV/IO epinephrine.  Why A/C/D wrong: These volumes are not stated."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"When epinephrine is given by the endotracheal route, how is it distributed into the lungs?","options":["By instilling additional saline boluses","By PPV (positive pressure ventilation) breaths","By chest compressions only","By suctioning the airway"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states endo-tracheal epinephrine is distributed into the lungs with PPV breaths.  Why A wrong: Saline flush is described for the IV/IO route, not the ET route.  Why C wrong: Chest compressions do not distribute the drug into the lungs.  Why D wrong: Suctioning would remove, not distribute, the drug."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"During neonatal resuscitation, epinephrine should be repeated at what interval if the heart rate remains less than 60 bpm?","options":["Every 1 minute","Every 3–5 minutes","Every 10 minutes","Every 15 minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states to repeat epinephrine every 3–5 minutes if heart rate remains less than 60 bpm.  Why A/C/D wrong: These intervals are not stated."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"Which solution is most preferred as a volume expander for hypovolemia in neonatal resuscitation?","options":["5% Dextrose","Normal saline (0.9% NaCl)","Ringer's lactate","0.45% NaCl (half-normal saline)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states normal saline (0.9% NaCl) is most preferred for hypovolemia.  Why A wrong: Dextrose is not a volume expander of choice and is not listed.  Why C/D wrong: Ringer's lactate and 0.45% NaCl are not listed as the preferred volume expander."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"What is the recommended dose of the volume expander in neonatal resuscitation?","options":["5 mg/kg","10 mL/kg","20 mL/kg","1 mL/kg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists the volume expander dose as 10 mL/kg.  Why A wrong: The dose is volumetric (mL/kg), not in mg.  Why C/D wrong: 20 mL/kg and 1 mL/kg are not the stated dose."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"Which route is most preferred for giving a volume expander in neonatal resuscitation?","options":["Intra-osseous","Intravenous (IV) through the umbilical venous catheter","Endotracheal","Intramuscular"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states IV is most preferred, through the umbilical venous catheter; intra-osseous is less effective.  Why A wrong: Intra-osseous is described as less effective.  Why C/D wrong: ET and IM are not appropriate routes for volume expansion."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"How should a volume expander be prepared for neonatal administration?","options":["In a 1 mL syringe","In a 30–60 mL syringe (labeled)","In a 5 mL syringe","In an IV burette only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the volume expander is prepared in a 30–60 mL syringe (labeled).  Why A/C wrong: These small syringe sizes are not adequate for the volume needed.  Why D wrong: A burette-only preparation is not specified."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"Over what time period should the neonatal volume expander be administered?","options":["As a rapid bolus over seconds","Over 5 to 10 minutes","Over 30 to 60 minutes","Over 1 hour"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page directs administering the volume expander over 5 to 10 minutes.  Why A wrong: Rapid bolus is not advised; slow administration is specified.  Why C/D wrong: These longer periods are not stated."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"When administering a volume expander, caution is advised in preterm newborns less than how many weeks of gestation?","options":["28 weeks","30 weeks","32 weeks","34 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page advises using caution with preterm newborns less than 30 weeks of gestation.  Why A/C/D wrong: 28, 32, and 34 weeks are not the cutoff stated."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"Which image/position is identified on the page as the correct head position for a newborn to maintain an open airway during resuscitation?","options":["Hyperextension of the neck","The \"sniffing\" position","Flexion of the neck","Lateral head turn"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page labels the correct position as the sniffing position; the worked example answer for opening the airway is option B (the sniffing/neutral alignment).  Why A wrong: Hyperextension is labeled incorrect.  Why C wrong: Flexion is labeled incorrect.  Why D wrong: A lateral turn is not the correct airway-opening position shown."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"A shoulder roll is described on the page as serving what purpose during neonatal airway management?","options":["To elevate the legs","Optional, to help maintain the sniffing position","To restrain the infant","To warm the infant"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page captions an \"optional shoulder roll for maintaining the sniffing position.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the stated purpose of the shoulder roll."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"According to the mask-sizing figures, which describes the correct resuscitation mask size and position for a newborn?","options":["Mask too large, overlaps the chin","Correct size and position (covers mouth and nose without covering the eyes)","Mask too small, nostrils not covered","Mask too large, overlapping the eyes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Figure 1 is labeled \"Correct size and position.\"  Why A wrong: Figure 2 (mask too large, overlaps the chin) is incorrect.  Why C wrong: Figure 3 (mask too small, nostrils not covered) is incorrect.  Why D wrong: Figure 4 (mask too large, overlap with eyes) is incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"Which piece of equipment for intubation uses a straight blade?","options":["Bag-valve mask","Laryngoscope","Suction catheter","Pulse oximeter"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists \"Laryngoscope with straight blade size 0 and 1 for preterm and term infant respectively.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not described with a straight blade."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"Which laryngoscope straight blade size is used for the preterm infant versus the term infant?","options":["Size 1 for preterm, size 0 for term","Size 0 for preterm, size 1 for term","Size 2 for both","Size 00 for preterm, size 2 for term"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states straight blade size 0 for the preterm infant and size 1 for the term infant.  Why A wrong: The sizes are reversed.  Why C/D wrong: These sizes are not listed."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"According to the intubation equipment chart, which ET tube size is appropriate for a neonate weighing less than 1 kg (less than 28 weeks)?","options":["2.5 mm","3 mm","3.5 mm","4 mm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The chart lists ET tube 2.5 mm for <1 kg / <28 weeks.  Why B wrong: 3 mm is for 1–2 kg / 28–32 weeks.  Why C wrong: 3.5 mm is for >2 kg / >32 weeks.  Why D wrong: 4 mm is not listed for neonates."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"Which ET tube size is appropriate for a neonate weighing 1–2 kg (28–32 weeks gestation)?","options":["2.5 mm","3 mm","3.5 mm","4 mm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The chart lists 3 mm for 1–2 kg / 28–32 weeks.  Why A wrong: 2.5 mm is for <1 kg / <28 weeks.  Why C wrong: 3.5 mm is for >2 kg / >32 weeks.  Why D wrong: 4 mm is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"Which ET tube size is used for a neonate weighing more than 2 kg (more than 32 weeks gestation)?","options":["2.5 mm","3 mm","3.5 mm","4.5 mm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The chart lists 3.5 mm for >2 kg / >32 weeks.  Why A/B wrong: These are for smaller, more premature infants.  Why D wrong: 4.5 mm is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"Besides the laryngoscope and ET tubes, which \"other\" items are listed as needed for intubation?","options":["Defibrillator and ECG leads","Measuring tape and adhesives for fixing","Chest tube and water seal","Nebulizer and mask"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists \"Others: measuring tape and adhesives for fixing.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not in the listed intubation equipment."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"During ET tube insertion, the vocal cords are described as having what appearance?","options":["Red and inflamed","White and like an inverted \"V\" shape","Pink and rounded","Bluish and edematous"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the vocal cords look white and like an inverted \"V\" shape.  Why A/C/D wrong: These appearances are not described."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"After ET tube insertion, correct endotracheal position is verified by witnessing which signs?","options":["Decreasing heart rate and gastric distension","Increasing HR or chest rise with PPV","Absence of breath sounds bilaterally","Cyanosis worsening"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states position is checked by witnessing increasing HR or chest rise with PPV.  Why A wrong: A decreasing HR and gastric distension suggest esophageal placement, the opposite of correct placement.  Why C/D wrong: Absent breath sounds and worsening cyanosis indicate failed placement, not confirmation."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"Within how many seconds should the ET tube intubation procedure be completed before reverting to bag-and-mask PPV?","options":["10 seconds","20 seconds","30 seconds","60 seconds"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the procedure should be done within 30 seconds; if not, provide PPV with bag and mask before the next attempt.  Why A/B/D wrong: These time limits are not stated."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"The depth of ET tube insertion is calculated using which measurement?","options":["Nasal-tragus length (NTL) + 1 (cm)","Crown-heel length / 2","Weight in kg + 6","Head circumference − 2"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page gives depth of insertion as nasal-tragus length (NTL) + 1 (cm).  Why B/C/D wrong: These formulas are not given on the page."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"On the depth-of-insertion box, the nasal-tragus length (NTL) is defined as the distance between which landmarks?","options":["The nasal septum and the tragus","The mouth and the sternal notch","The chin and the clavicle","The forehead and the chin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page defines NTL as the distance between the nasal septum and the tragus.  Why B/C/D wrong: These landmark pairs are not the stated definition."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"After the ET tube is placed, the final step in the flow chart is to ensure what before the tube is fixed?","options":["Equal air entry is confirmed","The infant is extubated","Chest compressions are started","The umbilical catheter is removed"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart states \"Once placed, equal air entry is confirmed and fixed.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: These actions contradict securing a correctly placed tube."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"During the assessment-and-action steps of the neonatal CPR flow chart, if the heart rate is greater than 100/min and the infant is breathing well, what is the appropriate action?","options":["Start chest compressions","Continue PPV","Provide post-resuscitation care","Give epinephrine"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart directs \"HR >100 and breathing well → post-resuscitation care.\"  Why A wrong: Chest compressions are for HR <60.  Why B wrong: PPV is continued for HR 60–100.  Why D wrong: Epinephrine is reserved for persistent bradycardia, not a vigorous infant."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"Per the neonatal CPR flow chart, what action is indicated when the heart rate is between 60 and 100/min?","options":["Provide post-resuscitation care","Continue PPV (positive pressure ventilation)","Start chest compressions","Stop all interventions"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart states \"HR between 60–100 → continue PPV.\"  Why A wrong: Post-resuscitation care applies to HR >100 with good breathing.  Why C wrong: Chest compressions begin at HR <60.  Why D wrong: Stopping interventions is inappropriate."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"Per the neonatal CPR flow chart, when should chest compressions be started?","options":["HR >100/min","HR between 60–100/min","HR <60/min","HR <100/min"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart states \"HR <60 → start chest compression.\"  Why A/B wrong: These ranges call for post-resuscitation care or continued PPV.  Why D wrong: The cutoff for chest compressions is <60, not <100."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"In the Neonatal Resuscitation Algorithm, what three rapid assessment questions are asked immediately after birth to decide if the infant can stay with the mother?","options":["Color, tone, and cry","Term gestation? Good tone? Breathing or crying?","Weight, temperature, and reflex","Heart rate, oxygen, and posture"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The algorithm's first decision diamond reads \"Term gestation? Good tone? Breathing or crying?\"  Why A/C/D wrong: These groupings are not the three algorithm questions."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"In the algorithm, if the infant is term, has good tone, and is breathing/crying (\"Yes\"), what is the recommended action?","options":["Begin PPV immediately","Infant stays with mother for routine care — warm/maintain temperature, position airway, clear secretions if needed, dry, and ongoing evaluation","Intubate the infant","Start chest compressions"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The algorithm routes a \"Yes\" answer to staying with the mother for routine care (warmth, positioning, clearing secretions if needed, drying, ongoing evaluation).  Why A/C/D wrong: These escalations apply only when the infant is not vigorous."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"In the algorithm, after initial steps (warm, position, clear airway, dry, stimulate), what two findings prompt starting PPV and SpO2 monitoring?","options":["Good tone and strong cry","Apnea or gasping, or HR below 100/min","Pink color and HR above 120/min","Acrocyanosis only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The algorithm decision \"Apnea or gasping? HR below 100/min?\" with \"Yes\" leads to PPV, SpO2 monitor, and consider ECG monitor.  Why A/C wrong: These are reassuring findings that do not trigger PPV.  Why D wrong: Acrocyanosis alone is not the trigger for PPV."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"When the algorithm directs PPV for an apneic or bradycardic neonate, which monitoring is added?","options":["SpO2 monitor, and consider ECG monitor","End-tidal CO2 only","Continuous arterial line","No monitoring needed"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The PPV box states \"PPV, SpO2 monitor, Consider ECG monitor.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the monitoring steps listed."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"In the algorithm, an infant with labored breathing or persistent cyanosis (but not apneic/bradycardic) is managed with which steps?","options":["Immediate chest compressions","Position and clear airway, SpO2 monitor, supplementary O2 as needed, and consider CPAP","Epinephrine via UVC","Emergency intubation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The algorithm routes labored breathing/persistent cyanosis to position and clear airway, SpO2 monitor, supplementary O2 as needed, and consider CPAP.  Why A/C/D wrong: These aggressive measures are reserved for non-breathing or severely bradycardic infants."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"In the algorithm, if the heart rate remains below 100/min after PPV, what is the next step?","options":["Discontinue resuscitation","Check chest movement, take ventilation corrective steps if needed, and use ETT or laryngeal mask if needed","Give volume expander immediately","Proceed to post-resuscitation care"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The algorithm's \"HR below 100/min? Yes\" branch directs checking chest movement, ventilation corrective steps if needed, and ETT or laryngeal mask if needed.  Why A wrong: Resuscitation continues, not stopped.  Why C wrong: Volume expander is not the next step here.  Why D wrong: Post-resuscitation care applies when HR recovers, reached via the \"No\" branch."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"In the algorithm, when the heart rate remains below 60/min, which set of interventions is indicated?","options":["Routine care with mother","Intubate if not already done, chest compressions coordinated with PPV, 100% O2, ECG monitor, and consider emergency UVC","CPAP only","Discharge home"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The algorithm's \"HR below 60/min? Yes\" box lists intubate if not already done, chest compressions coordinated with PPV, 100% O2, ECG monitor, and consider emergency UVC.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the severe-bradycardia branch."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"According to the \"Targeted Preductal SpO2 After Birth\" table, what is the target preductal SpO2 at 1 minute of life?","options":["60%–65%","65%–70%","70%–75%","80%–85%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists 1 min = 60%–65%.  Why B wrong: 65%–70% is the 2-minute target.  Why C wrong: 70%–75% is the 3-minute target.  Why D wrong: 80%–85% is the 5-minute target."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"Per the targeted preductal SpO2 table, what is the target SpO2 at 2 minutes after birth?","options":["60%–65%","65%–70%","75%–80%","85%–95%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists 2 min = 65%–70%.  Why A wrong: 60%–65% is the 1-minute value.  Why C wrong: 75%–80% is the 4-minute value.  Why D wrong: 85%–95% is the 10-minute value."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"Per the targeted preductal SpO2 table, what is the target SpO2 at 5 minutes after birth?","options":["70%–75%","75%–80%","80%–85%","85%–95%"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists 5 min = 80%–85%.  Why A wrong: 70%–75% is the 3-minute value.  Why B wrong: 75%–80% is the 4-minute value.  Why D wrong: 85%–95% is the 10-minute value."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"Per the targeted preductal SpO2 table, what is the target SpO2 at 10 minutes after birth?","options":["75%–80%","80%–85%","85%–95%","95%–100%"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists 10 min = 85%–95%.  Why A/B wrong: These are the 4- and 5-minute values.  Why D wrong: 95%–100% is not listed for 10 minutes."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"Match the remaining targeted preductal SpO2 values to their times. Which pairing is correct?","options":["3 min = 70%–75% and 4 min = 75%–80%","3 min = 75%–80% and 4 min = 70%–75%","3 min = 65%–70% and 4 min = 80%–85%","3 min = 60%–65% and 4 min = 85%–95%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists 3 min = 70%–75% and 4 min = 75%–80%.  Why B wrong: The values are reversed.  Why C/D wrong: These mismatch the table values."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"When the heart rate cannot be reliably determined by stethoscope and the baby is not vigorous, what should be used to assess heart rate?","options":["Capillary refill time only","An electronic monitor such as a pulse oximeter or ECG monitor","Doppler of the umbilical artery","Manual palpation of the femoral pulse"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states to use an electronic monitor such as a pulse oximeter or ECG monitor when auscultation is unreliable in a non-vigorous baby.  Why A/C/D wrong: These methods are not the recommended tools in this scenario."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"When neonatal resuscitation is anticipated, what is recommended to guide oxygen therapy?","options":["Skin color assessment alone","Pulse oximetry and the target oxygen saturation table","Routine 100% oxygen for all","Capnography"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states to use pulse oximetry and the target oxygen saturation table; visual assessment of cyanosis is not reliable.  Why A wrong: Visual assessment of cyanosis is explicitly described as not reliable.  Why C wrong: Routine 100% oxygen for all is not advised.  Why D wrong: Capnography is not mentioned for guiding oxygen therapy here."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"According to the page, a healthy newborn breathing room air may take more than how long to achieve oxygen saturation greater than 90%?","options":["5 minutes","10 minutes","30 minutes","1 hour"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states a healthy newborn breathing room air may take more than 10 minutes to achieve oxygen saturation greater than 90%.  Why A/C/D wrong: These times are not stated."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"Why is free-flow oxygen via a mask attached to a self-inflating bag described as ineffective?","options":["It delivers too high a concentration","Free-flow oxygen cannot be given reliably using a mask attached to a self-inflating bag, and free-flow supplemental oxygen is not effective if the baby is not breathing","It causes oxygen toxicity instantly","It requires a heated humidifier"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states free-flow oxygen cannot be given reliably with a mask on a self-inflating bag, and free-flow supplemental oxygen is not effective if the baby is not breathing.  Why A/C/D wrong: These reasons are not stated on the page."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"Routine nasal, oral, and tracheal suctioning is NOT recommended in neonates born through liquor (with or without meconium) under which condition?","options":["When the infant has a low Apgar score","When the infant starts breathing on their own and is vigorous (active)","When the cord is clamped late","When the infant is preterm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states routine suctioning is not recommended in neonates born through liquor with or without meconium who start breathing on their own and are vigorous (active).  Why A/C/D wrong: These conditions are not the stated criterion."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"When suctioning is needed, the page recommends suctioning in which order, summarized by a memory aid?","options":["Nose before mouth","Mouth before nose (\"M comes before N\")","Trachea before mouth","Either order, no preference"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page advises suctioning the mouth before the nose, with the memory aid \"mouth before nose\" because \"M comes before N\" in the alphabet.  Why A wrong: The opposite order is recommended.  Why C/D wrong: These do not match the page."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"Why is the mouth suctioned before the nose in a neonate?","options":["To stimulate the cough reflex","To ensure there is nothing for the newborn to aspirate if it gasps when the nose is suctioned","To prevent nasal bleeding","To warm the airway"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page explains the mouth is cleared first to ensure there is nothing for the newborn to aspirate should it gasp when the nose is suctioned.  Why A/C/D wrong: These rationales are not given."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"Why should vigorous or deep suctioning of the posterior pharynx be avoided in the first minutes after birth?","options":["It can introduce infection","It may injure tissues and stimulation of the posterior pharynx can produce a vagal response leading to bradycardia or apnea","It causes hypothermia","It increases oxygen saturation too rapidly"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page warns that vigorous suction may injure tissues and that stimulation of the posterior pharynx in the first minutes can produce a vagal response leading to bradycardia or apnea.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the stated risks."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"When using a suction catheter, the suction control should be set so the negative pressure reads approximately what range when the tubing is occluded?","options":["40 to 60 mmHg","80 to 100 mmHg","120 to 150 mmHg","200 to 250 mmHg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the negative pressure should read approximately 80 to 100 mmHg when the tubing is occluded.  Why A/C/D wrong: These pressures are not stated."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"What does the page advise regarding rubbing the back, trunk, or extremities to stimulate a newborn?","options":["Vigorous rubbing is most effective and should be maximized","Gently rub the back, trunk, or extremities; overly vigorous stimulation is not helpful and can cause injury — never shake a baby, and if the newborn remains apneic despite rubbing for several seconds, begin PPV","Shaking the baby gently is acceptable if rubbing fails","Stimulation should be avoided entirely"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states to gently rub the back, trunk, or extremities; overly vigorous stimulation is not helpful and can cause injury; never shake a baby; and begin PPV if apnea persists after several seconds of stimulation.  Why A wrong: Vigorous rubbing is explicitly discouraged.  Why C wrong: Shaking a baby is explicitly prohibited.  Why D wrong: Gentle stimulation is appropriate, not total avoidance."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"What are the stated potential benefits of delayed cord clamping for preterm newborns?","options":["Higher infection rate and lower blood volume","Decreased mortality, higher blood pressure and blood volume, less need for blood transfusion after birth, fewer brain hemorrhages, and lower risk of necrotizing enterocolitis","Increased risk of jaundice with no other benefit","Faster Apgar scoring only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists decreased mortality, higher blood pressure and blood volume, less need for blood transfusion after birth, fewer brain hemorrhages, and lower risk of necrotizing enterocolitis as benefits of delayed cord clamping in preterm newborns.  Why A wrong: These are the opposite of the stated benefits.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the listed benefits."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"What benefit of delayed cord clamping is described for term newborns?","options":["It guarantees zero jaundice","It may decrease the chance of developing iron-deficiency anemia and may improve neurodevelopmental outcomes","It prevents all respiratory distress","It eliminates the need for vitamin K"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states that in term newborns, delayed cord clamping may decrease the chance of iron-deficiency anemia and may improve neurodevelopmental outcomes.  Why A/C/D wrong: These overstated or unrelated claims are not on the page."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"Which are the potential adverse effects of delayed cord clamping mentioned on the page? (SATA)","options":["Delaying resuscitation for compromised newborns","Increased risk of polycythemia","Increased risk of jaundice","Necrotizing enterocolitis","Brain hemorrhage"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists potential adverse effects of delayed cord clamping as delaying resuscitation for compromised newborns and increased risks of polycythemia and jaundice.  Why D wrong: Necrotizing enterocolitis is listed as a risk that delayed clamping reduces in preterm infants, not an adverse effect of delayed clamping.  Why E wrong: Brain hemorrhage is reduced (a benefit), not an adverse effect."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"Current evidence suggests cord clamping should be delayed for at least how long for vigorous term and preterm newborns?","options":["10 to 20 seconds","30 to 60 seconds","1 to 2 minutes","3 to 5 minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states clamping should be delayed at least 30 to 60 seconds for most vigorous term and preterm newborns.  Why A/C/D wrong: These intervals are not stated."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"If cord clamping is delayed, where should the baby be placed to keep warm?","options":["Under a radiant warmer away from the mother","Skin-to-skin on the mother's chest or abdomen, or held securely in a warm, dry towel or blanket","In an incubator only","Lying flat on a cold surface"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the baby should be placed skin-to-skin on the mother's chest or abdomen, or held securely in a warm, dry towel or blanket while clamping is delayed.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated practice."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"How should very preterm newborns be kept warm during delayed cord clamping?","options":["Bathed in warm water","Wrapped in a warm blanket or polyethylene plastic to help maintain their temperature","Placed under a fan","Given warmed IV fluids only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states very preterm newborns may be wrapped in a warm blanket or polyethylene plastic to help maintain temperature.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the stated measures."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"A pulse oximeter measures oxygen saturation in the skin using which principle?","options":["It measures the partial pressure of dissolved oxygen","It uses a light source and sensor to measure the absorption of red light passing through capillaries, estimating the portion of hemoglobin that is fully saturated with oxygen","It detects carbon dioxide levels","It measures blood pH directly"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page explains the pulse oximeter uses a light source and sensor to measure absorption of red light passing through capillaries in the skin, estimating the portion of hemoglobin fully saturated with oxygen.  Why A wrong: Partial pressure (PO2) is measured by a blood gas machine (ABG), not the oximeter.  Why C/D wrong: It does not measure CO2 or pH."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"Oxygen is carried by hemoglobin inside red blood cells. How does hemoglobin saturated with oxygen behave with red light, compared with hemoglobin not carrying oxygen?","options":["It absorbs more red light","It absorbs red light differently than hemoglobin not carrying oxygen","It does not interact with light at all","It emits its own light"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states hemoglobin saturated with oxygen absorbs red light differently than hemoglobin not carrying oxygen, which is the basis of pulse oximetry.  Why A wrong: The page describes a difference in absorption, not specifically \"more.\"  Why C/D wrong: These contradict the principle of light absorption."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"What is the displayed range of oxygen saturation values on a pulse oximeter?","options":["0% to 50%","0% to 100%","50% to 100%","90% to 100%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the monitor displays oxygen saturation ranging from 0% to 100%.  Why A/C/D wrong: These ranges are incorrect per the page."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"Compared with the partial pressure of oxygen (PO2) measured by a blood gas machine (ABG), the pulse oximeter saturation value is best described how?","options":["It is the same measurement","It is not the same as PO2; PO2 is measured by a blood gas machine (ABG) while the oximeter estimates saturation and also displays the heart rate by sensing pulsatile blood flow","It measures venous oxygen content","It is always lower than the true saturation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page distinguishes the oximeter saturation from PO2 (measured by ABG), and notes the oximeter also displays heart rate by sensing pulsatile blood flow in the capillaries.  Why A wrong: The page explicitly says the oximeter value is not the same as PO2.  Why C/D wrong: These are not stated."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"Pre-ductal blood receives blood from an artery attaching to the aorta in what position relative to the ductus arteriosus?","options":["Before (proximal to) the ductus arteriosus","After (distal to) the ductus arteriosus","Directly from the ductus arteriosus","From the pulmonary artery"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the heart and brain receive blood from an artery attaching to the aorta before the ductus arteriosus — hence \"pre-ductal blood.\"  Why B wrong: Blood attaching after the ductus arteriosus is post-ductal.  Why C/D wrong: These do not match the description."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"In most babies, which artery is more predictable for supplying pre-ductal blood, and therefore where should the pulse oximeter sensor be placed to measure pre-ductal saturation?","options":["The left arm; place the sensor on the left wrist","The right arm; place the pulse oximeter sensor on the right hand or wrist","Either foot; place the sensor on the toes","The umbilicus; place the sensor on the cord"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the right arm supplying the right arm is more predictable, so to measure pre-ductal saturation (the blood perfusing the heart and brain), place the pulse oximeter sensor on the right hand or wrist.  Why A wrong: The left arm and both legs may carry post-ductal blood, not the most reliable pre-ductal site.  Why C/D wrong: The feet carry post-ductal blood and the umbilicus is not used for the oximeter sensor."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"Why may the left arm and both legs show lower oxygen saturation values (post-ductal) than the right hand?","options":["Because they are farther from the lungs","Because they may receive blood from the aorta after it has mixed with poorly oxygenated venous blood shunted from the right side of the heart through the ductus arteriosus","Because the sensor reads cold extremities incorrectly","Because of skin pigmentation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page explains the left arm and both legs may have lower saturation because they receive blood from the aorta after it mixes with poorly oxygenated venous blood shunted from the right side of the heart through the ductus arteriosus (post-ductal).  Why A/C/D wrong: These reasons are not stated."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"During normal transition, healthy newborns may take several minutes to increase their blood oxygen saturation from approximately what level?","options":["40%","60%","80%","90%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states healthy newborns may take several minutes to increase oxygen saturation from approximately 60%, which is the normal intrauterine state, to more than 90%.  Why A/C/D wrong: These starting levels are not stated."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"Approximately how long may even healthy term newborns take to reach an oxygen saturation greater than 90% during normal transition?","options":["1 to 2 minutes","5 minutes","10 minutes or longer","30 minutes or longer"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The page states even healthy term newborns may take 10 minutes or longer to reach this saturation.  Why A/B wrong: These are too short per the page.  Why D wrong: 30 minutes or longer is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"How do oxygen saturation values following cesarean birth compare with those following vaginal birth?","options":["Slightly higher after cesarean","Slightly lower than those following vaginal birth","Exactly the same","Markedly higher after cesarean"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states oxygen saturation values following cesarean birth are slightly lower than those following vaginal birth.  Why A/C/D wrong: These contradict the page."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"What is the definition of Foreign Body Airway Obstruction (FBAO)?","options":["Inflammation of the airway lining","A foreign object that obstructs or becomes lodged in the person's airway","Bronchospasm from allergens","Fluid accumulation in the alveoli"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page defines FBAO as a foreign object that obstructs or becomes lodged in the person's airway.  Why A/C/D wrong: These describe other airway conditions, not FBAO."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"According to the page, what is the most common type of foreign body causing airway obstruction?","options":["Plastic toys","Foods and coins","Buttons and batteries","Marbles"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the most common foreign body is foods and coins.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not named as the most common."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"Why should blind finger sweeps NOT be performed in a victim with foreign body airway obstruction?","options":["Because they damage the teeth","Because of the risk of pushing the object further into the airway","Because they cause vomiting","Because they are too slow"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states blind finger sweeps should not be performed because of the risk of pushing the object further into the airway.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the stated reasons."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"In the algorithm for management of an inhaled foreign body, what is the first step?","options":["Start chest compressions","Assess severity","Give 5 back blows","Open the airway and give 5 breaths"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart begins with \"Assess severity,\" branching to ineffective cough versus effective cough.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are downstream interventions, not the first step."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"In the inhaled foreign body algorithm, a victim with an INEFFECTIVE cough who is UNCONSCIOUS should receive which actions?","options":["Encourage cough and observe","Open airway, 5 breaths, start CPR","5 back blows and 5 thrusts","Nothing until EMS arrives"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart's ineffective-cough/unconscious branch directs: open airway, 5 breaths, start CPR.  Why A wrong: Encouraging cough applies to the effective-cough branch.  Why C wrong: Back blows/thrusts apply to the conscious ineffective-cough branch.  Why D wrong: Active resuscitation is required."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"In the inhaled foreign body algorithm, a CONSCIOUS victim with an ineffective cough should receive which interventions?","options":["Open airway and 5 breaths","5 back blows and 5 thrusts (chest thrusts for an infant, abdominal thrusts for a child >1 year old)","Encourage cough only","Immediate CPR"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart's conscious branch directs 5 back blows and 5 thrusts — using chest thrusts for an infant and abdominal thrusts for a child older than 1 year.  Why A/D wrong: These apply to the unconscious branch.  Why C wrong: Encouraging cough alone is for an effective cough."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"In the inhaled foreign body algorithm, a victim with an EFFECTIVE cough should be managed how?","options":["Start CPR immediately","Encourage cough and continue to check for deterioration","Give abdominal thrusts","Perform a blind finger sweep"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart's effective-cough branch directs encouraging the cough and continuing to check for deterioration.  Why A/C/D wrong: These aggressive measures are for ineffective cough or are contraindicated."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"When managing a conscious adult with suspected FBAO, what question should the rescuer ask first?","options":["\"Do you have any allergies?\"","\"Are you choking?\" — a choking victim will not be able to speak or cough if completely obstructed, and will nod \"Yes\"","\"Where does it hurt?\"","\"Did you eat recently?\""],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page directs asking \"Are you choking?\"; the choking victim cannot speak or cough and nods \"Yes,\" signaling that intervention is needed.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the recommended first assessment question."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"If an adult's airway is only partially obstructed and a crowing sound is heard with continued ability to cough, what should the rescuer do?","options":["Begin abdominal thrusts immediately","Encourage the victim to cough","Start CPR","Perform a blind finger sweep"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states if the airway is partially obstructed, a crowing sound is heard, and the victim can still cough, then encourage the victim to cough.  Why A/C/D wrong: These escalations are reserved for a victim who cannot cough/breathe or who becomes unconscious."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"If an adult victim is having difficulty breathing from FBAO, the rescuer should first activate which system?","options":["The fire department","EMS (Emergency Medical Services)","Poison control","The pharmacy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page directs activating the EMS system if the victim is having difficulty breathing.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the appropriate system to activate."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"Which maneuver is used in a conscious adult with FBAO until the obstruction is dislodged or the victim becomes unconscious?","options":["The recovery position","The abdominal thrust maneuver, also known as the Heimlich maneuver","Back slaps only","Chest compressions"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page directs using the abdominal thrust (Heimlich) maneuver until the obstruction is dislodged or the victim becomes unconscious.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the maneuver named for a conscious adult."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"If an adult FBAO victim becomes unconscious or unresponsive, what should the rescuer do immediately?","options":["Continue abdominal thrusts standing","Place the victim in supine position and start CPR for 2 minutes, then activate EMS","Leave to call for help only","Perform a blind finger sweep first"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page directs immediately placing the victim supine and starting CPR for 2 minutes, then activating EMS.  Why A wrong: Abdominal thrusts are for the conscious victim.  Why C wrong: CPR must be started, not just calling.  Why D wrong: Blind finger sweeps are avoided."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"During CPR rescue of an FBAO victim, what should the rescuer do regarding any object found in the mouth?","options":["Always perform a deep blind sweep","Look in the victim's mouth when the airway is opened, and if an object is found, remove it","Never look in the mouth","Suction blindly"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states during CPR rescue the rescuer should look in the mouth when the airway is opened and, if an object is found, remove it.  Why A/D wrong: Blind sweeps/suctioning are avoided.  Why C wrong: The rescuer is directed to look in the mouth."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"What is another name for the abdominal thrust maneuver?","options":["The Sellick maneuver","The Heimlich maneuver","The jaw-thrust maneuver","The Valsalva maneuver"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the abdominal thrust maneuver is also known as the Heimlich maneuver.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are different maneuvers, not the abdominal thrust."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"When performing the abdominal thrust (Heimlich) maneuver on an adult, where should the rescuer stand?","options":["In front of the victim","Behind the victim","Beside the victim, on the right","Kneeling at the victim's feet"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page directs the rescuer to stand behind the victim.  Why A/C/D wrong: These positions are not described for the maneuver."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"In the abdominal thrust maneuver, where should the thumb side of the fist be placed?","options":["Over the xiphoid process","Just above the umbilicus (belly button) and well below the xiphoid process","On the lower ribs","Below the umbilicus near the pelvis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page directs making a fist and placing the thumb side just above the umbilicus and well below the xiphoid process.  Why A wrong: Placement over the xiphoid risks injury and is incorrect.  Why C/D wrong: These positions are wrong per the page."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"How are the abdominal thrusts performed in the Heimlich maneuver?","options":["5 slow downward thrusts","Quick, inward and upward abdominal thrusts (between the umbilicus and the xiphoid process)","Steady continuous pressure","Outward pulling motions"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page directs performing quick, inward and upward abdominal thrusts between the umbilicus and the xiphoid process.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the described technique."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"What are the cardinal signs of FBAO in a child?","options":["Wheezing, fever, and rash","Coughing, gagging, or stridor (high-pitched noisy breathing)","Bradycardia and pallor","Vomiting and diarrhea"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists cardinal signs in a child as coughing, gagging, or stridor (high-pitched noisy breathing).  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the listed cardinal signs."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"In a child with FBAO who is coughing forcefully, what should the rescuer do?","options":["Immediately give abdominal thrusts","Allow the victim to continue coughing if the cough is forceful","Start CPR","Perform back blows only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page directs allowing the child to continue coughing if the cough is forceful.  Why A/C/D wrong: Intervention is reserved for an inactive cough or worsening distress."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"When is help needed for a child with FBAO?","options":["As soon as any cough occurs","If the coughing is inactive, or the victim shows increased respiratory difficulty or distress with high-pitched noise while inhaling","Only after the child loses consciousness","Only if the child vomits"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states help is needed if coughing is inactive or the victim demonstrates increased respiratory difficulty/distress with high-pitched noise while inhaling.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated criteria."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"In a conscious child with FBAO, which maneuver should be performed until the obstruction is dislodged?","options":["The chest thrust maneuver","The abdominal thrust maneuver","Back blows only","The recovery position"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states in a conscious child the abdominal thrust maneuver should be performed until the obstruction is dislodged.  Why A wrong: Chest thrusts are used for infants, not the conscious child here.  Why C/D wrong: These do not match the page."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"Which maneuver is recommended for an obese victim or a pregnant victim in the 2nd or 3rd trimester with FBAO?","options":["Abdominal thrust maneuver","Chest thrust maneuver","Back blows only","Blind finger sweep"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page directs using the chest thrust maneuver for the obese or 2nd/3rd trimester pregnant victim.  Why A wrong: Abdominal thrusts are avoided in these victims due to the enlarged abdomen/uterus.  Why C/D wrong: These do not match the recommended technique."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"For chest thrusts in an obese or pregnant FBAO victim, how does the rescuer position the arms?","options":["Around the abdomen","Under the victim's axillae and across the chest","Behind the victim's neck","At the victim's waist"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page directs the rescuer to place his/her arms under the victim's axillae and across the chest.  Why A/C/D wrong: These positions do not match the page."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"In the obese/pregnant FBAO victim, backward chest thrusts are performed until what occurs?","options":["Until exactly 5 thrusts are completed","Until the foreign body is expelled or the victim loses consciousness","Until EMS arrives","Until the victim coughs once"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states backward chest thrusts are performed until the foreign body is expelled or the victim loses consciousness.  Why A/C/D wrong: These endpoints are not stated."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"If a pregnant FBAO victim loses consciousness, what positioning is recommended before/during CPR?","options":["Place flat and supine without any support","Place her on her back and insert a wedge (small pillow or rolled blanket) under the woman's right flank and hip to displace the uterus to the right side of the abdomen, to prevent supine vena cava syndrome","Place her prone","Sit her upright"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page directs placing the pregnant victim on her back with a wedge under her right flank and hip to displace the uterus to the right side, preventing supine vena cava syndrome.  Why A wrong: A flat supine position without displacement risks vena cava compression.  Why C/D wrong: Prone and upright positions are not recommended."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"If chest thrusts are unsuccessful in a pregnant FBAO victim, what should be done next?","options":["Repeat abdominal thrusts","Start CPR","Wait for spontaneous resolution","Perform a blind finger sweep"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states if unsuccessful, then start CPR.  Why A wrong: Abdominal thrusts are avoided in pregnancy.  Why C/D wrong: These are not appropriate next steps."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"If defibrillation is needed in a pregnant victim, how should the paddle placement be modified?","options":["Paddles placed lower than usual","Paddles placed one rib inter-space higher than usual because the heart is displaced slightly by the enlarged uterus","Only one paddle used","No modification is needed"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the paddles are placed one rib inter-space higher than usual because the heart is displaced slightly by the enlarged uterus.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated modification."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"When managing an infant with FBAO, after assessing for breathing problems, how should the infant be positioned for back slaps?","options":["Sitting upright on the lap","On the arm or lap with the head lower than the trunk, supporting the head firmly and holding the jaw","Lying flat on the back","Held vertically by the ankles"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page directs placing the infant on the arm or on the lap, positioning the head lower than the trunk, and supporting the head firmly while holding the jaw.  Why A/C/D wrong: These positions are not described and would be unsafe."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"How many back slaps are delivered to an infant with FBAO, and with what part of the hand?","options":["3 back slaps with the fingertips","5 back slaps with the heel of the hand between the shoulder blades","10 back slaps with the palm","2 back slaps with the knuckles"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page directs delivering 5 back slaps with the heel of the hand between the shoulder blades.  Why A/C/D wrong: These counts/techniques are incorrect per the page."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"After back slaps, the infant is turned over and how many chest thrusts are delivered, and at what location?","options":["3 chest thrusts at the upper sternum","5 chest thrusts at the same location used for chest compression","10 abdominal thrusts above the umbilicus","5 abdominal thrusts below the xiphoid"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page directs turning the infant over (head lower than trunk) and delivering 5 chest thrusts at the same location used for chest compression.  Why A/C/D wrong: These counts, types, or locations are incorrect; abdominal thrusts are not used in infants."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"Regarding removing a foreign body in an infant, what does the page advise?","options":["Always perform a deep blind finger sweep","Check for a foreign object and remove it only if it is seen; blind finger sweeps are avoided because the object can be forced further into the throat","Use suction blindly to retrieve the object","Never look in the mouth at all"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page directs checking for a foreign object and removing it only if seen; blind finger sweeps are avoided because the object can be forced further into the throat.  Why A/C wrong: Blind sweeps/suction are contraindicated.  Why D wrong: The rescuer should still look for and remove a visible object."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"What should be done after each cycle if the infant's foreign body is not yet removed?","options":["Stop and wait for EMS","Repeat the sequence (back slaps and chest thrusts) until the object has been removed","Begin abdominal thrusts","Place the infant supine and observe"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page directs repeating the sequence until the object has been removed.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated management."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"In the tricky algorithm for paediatric FBAO treatment, a child with an INEFFECTIVE cough who is UNCONSCIOUS should receive which actions?","options":["Encourage cough","Open airway, 5 breaths, start CPR","5 back blows and 5 thrusts","5 abdominal thrusts only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The paediatric FBAO algorithm's ineffective-cough/unconscious branch directs open airway, 5 breaths, start CPR.  Why A wrong: Encouraging cough is for the effective-cough branch.  Why C wrong: Back blows/thrusts are for the conscious child.  Why D wrong: This does not match the unconscious branch."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"In the paediatric FBAO algorithm, a CONSCIOUS child with an ineffective cough should receive which thrusts?","options":["5 back blows and 5 chest thrusts (because abdominal thrusts are never used in children)","5 back blows and 5 thrusts — chest thrusts for an infant and abdominal thrusts for a child older than 1 year","Abdominal thrusts only, no back blows","CPR immediately"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The algorithm's conscious branch lists 5 back blows and 5 thrusts, with chest thrusts for an infant and abdominal thrusts for a child older than 1 year.  Why A wrong: Abdominal thrusts ARE used for children older than 1 year.  Why C/D wrong: These do not match the algorithm."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"In the paediatric FBAO algorithm, a child with an EFFECTIVE cough should be managed how?","options":["Start CPR","Encourage cough and continue to check for deterioration to ineffective cough or until obstruction is relieved","Give 5 abdominal thrusts","Perform a blind finger sweep"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The algorithm's effective-cough branch directs encouraging cough and continuing to check for deterioration to ineffective cough or until the obstruction is relieved.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are reserved for ineffective cough or are contraindicated."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"In the adult FBAO algorithm, severe airway obstruction is equated with which cough status, and mild airway obstruction with which?","options":["Severe = effective cough; mild = ineffective cough","Severe = ineffective cough; mild = effective cough","Both severe and mild are effective cough","Both severe and mild are ineffective cough"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The adult algorithm labels severe airway obstruction as ineffective cough and mild airway obstruction as effective cough.  Why A wrong: The pairings are reversed.  Why C/D wrong: The two severities map to opposite cough statuses."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"In the adult FBAO algorithm, a victim with severe airway obstruction (ineffective cough) who is UNCONSCIOUS should receive what?","options":["Encourage cough","Start CPR","5 back blows and 5 abdominal thrusts","Recovery position only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The adult algorithm's severe-obstruction/unconscious branch directs to start CPR.  Why A wrong: Encouraging cough is for mild obstruction.  Why C wrong: Back blows and abdominal thrusts apply to the conscious branch.  Why D wrong: The recovery position is not the action for an unconscious choking victim."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"In the adult FBAO algorithm, a CONSCIOUS victim with severe airway obstruction (ineffective cough) should receive which interventions?","options":["Start CPR","5 back blows and 5 abdominal thrusts","Encourage cough only","Chest thrusts only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The adult algorithm's severe-obstruction/conscious branch directs 5 back blows and 5 abdominal thrusts.  Why A wrong: CPR is for the unconscious victim.  Why C wrong: Encouraging cough is for mild (effective cough) obstruction.  Why D wrong: Chest thrusts are reserved for obese/pregnant victims, not the standard conscious adult here."},{"section":"Batch 031 · Neonatal resuscitation — epinephrine & volume expander dosing…","question":"In the adult FBAO algorithm, a victim with mild airway obstruction (effective cough) should be managed how?","options":["Start CPR immediately","Encourage cough and continue to check for deterioration to ineffective cough or until the obstruction is relieved","Give 5 back blows and 5 abdominal thrusts","Place in recovery position"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The adult algorithm's mild-obstruction (effective cough) branch directs encouraging cough and continuing to check for deterioration to ineffective cough or until obstruction is relieved.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are for severe obstruction or are not indicated."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"Who coined the word \"anaesthesia\"?","options":["WTG Morton","Oliver Wendell Holmes","John Snow","Robert Boyle"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the word \"anaesthesia\" was coined by Oliver Wendell Holmes.  Why A wrong: WTG Morton is the father of modern anaesthesia, not the coiner of the term.  Why C wrong: John Snow is the father of anaesthesia, not the one who coined the word.  Why D wrong: Robert Boyle is associated with Boyle's apparatus, not the term itself."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"Which agent was the first inhalational anaesthetic, performed by Morton in 1846 at Ether Dome?","options":["Chloroform","Cocaine","Diethyl ether","Nitrous oxide"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The first inhalational anaesthetic was diethyl ether (chloroform), performed in 1846 by Morton at the Ether Dome.  Why A wrong: Chloroform is noted parenthetically but the named first agent is diethyl ether.  Why B wrong: Cocaine was the first local anaesthetic, not inhalational.  Why D wrong: Nitrous oxide is a gas anaesthetic but not the first inhalational agent cited."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"The first local anaesthetic used in clinical setting for spinal anaesthesia was:","options":["Lignocaine","Cocaine","Bupivacaine","Ropivacaine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the first local anaesthetic used clinically for spinal anaesthesia was cocaine.  Why A wrong: Lignocaine is a later local anaesthetic, not the first.  Why C/D wrong: Bupivacaine and ropivacaine are modern local anaesthetics, not the first used."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"Who is regarded as the \"Father of Anaesthesia\"?","options":["WTG Morton","Oliver Wendell Holmes","John Snow","Robert Boyle"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The text names John Snow as the Father of Anaesthesia.  Why A wrong: WTG Morton is the Father of Modern Anaesthesia.  Why B wrong: Holmes coined the term \"anaesthesia.\"  Why D wrong: Boyle is linked to Boyle's apparatus."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"WTG Morton is known as the:","options":["Father of Anaesthesia","Father of Modern Anaesthesia","Coiner of the term anaesthesia","Inventor of Guedel airway"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states WTG Morton is the Father of Modern Anaesthesia.  Why A wrong: John Snow is the Father of Anaesthesia.  Why C wrong: Holmes coined the term.  Why D wrong: The Guedel airway is named for Guedel, not Morton."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"World Anaesthesia Day is celebrated every year on:","options":["16 October","16 December","10 October","6 December"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states World Anaesthesia Day is celebrated every year on 16 October.  Why B/C/D wrong: These dates do not match the source-stated 16 October."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"The Guedel oropharyngeal airway is used to prevent the airway obstruction caused by which structure falling back?","options":["The epiglottis","The soft palate","The tongue","The uvula"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The Guedel oropharyngeal airway prevents the back fall of the tongue during anaesthesia.  Why A/B/D wrong: The airway specifically addresses posterior displacement of the tongue, not these structures."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"\"Safar & Brook's airway\" is used for which purpose, combining two Guedel's airways?","options":["Endotracheal intubation","Resuscitation","Spinal anaesthesia","Vaporizing volatile agents"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the Safar & Brook's airway is used for resuscitation by combining two Guedel's airways.  Why A wrong: Intubation uses an endotracheal tube and laryngoscope, not this device.  Why C wrong: It is unrelated to spinal anaesthesia.  Why D wrong: Vaporizers, not airways, deliver volatile agents."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"Which anaesthetic apparatus is known as \"Boyle's apparatus\"?","options":["The anaesthesia machine","The Guedel airway","The laryngoscope","The vaporizer cylinder colour code"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the anaesthesia machine is known as Boyle's apparatus.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are separate devices/concepts, not Boyle's apparatus."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"Propofol anaesthetic agent has which characteristic colour?","options":["Clear and colourless","Milky white colour","Pale yellow","Bright blue"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The colour of propofol anaesthetic is described as milky white.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the source description of propofol's milky white colour."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"Propofol infusion syndrome (PRIS) usually occurs in which population, owing to failure of metabolism of propofol?","options":["Elderly patients","Children","Pregnant women","Diabetic patients"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states propofol infusion syndrome usually occurs in children if infusion is prolonged, due to failure of metabolism of propofol.  Why A/C/D wrong: These groups are not the ones the source identifies for PRIS."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"Propofol infusion syndrome may include all of the following metabolic/cardiac features EXCEPT:","options":["Metabolic acidosis","Cardiomyopathy and skeletal myopathy","Rhabdomyolysis, hyperlipidemia and enlarged liver","Hypothermia and bradycardia only"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The listed features of PRIS are metabolic acidosis, cardiomyopathy, skeletal myopathy, rhabdomyolysis, hyperlipidemia and enlarged liver; \"hypothermia and bradycardia only\" is not the described set.  Why A/B/C wrong: All three are explicitly listed PRIS features in the source."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"A patient is positioned for saddle block anaesthesia. Which position should be used and for how long?","options":["Sitting position for 10–15 minutes","Supine flat for 6–12 hours","Lateral position for 1 hour","Trendelenburg for 30 minutes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the patient is positioned in the sitting position for 10–15 minutes for saddle block anaesthesia.  Why B wrong: Flat supine 6–12 hours relates to post-spinal headache prevention, not saddle block positioning.  Why C/D wrong: These positions/durations are not specified for saddle block."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"What needle size/gauge is used for epidural anaesthesia via epidural catheter?","options":["16 gauge","18 G Tuohy's needle","22 G spinal needle","25 G needle"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text specifies 16–18 G Tuohy's needle for epidural anaesthesia via epidural catheter.  Why A wrong: 16 G alone is part of the range but the named needle is the Tuohy's; option B captures the device.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the specified epidural needles."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"Which agent is the most common local anaesthetic agent used in hospital settings?","options":["Bupivacaine","Ropivacaine","Lignocaine (Lidocaine) 2%, second m/c bupivacaine","Cocaine"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the most common local anaesthetic is lignocaine 2%, with bupivacaine being second most common.  Why A/B/D wrong: Bupivacaine is second most common; ropivacaine and cocaine are not the most common."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"Why must adrenaline NOT be added to lignocaine in highly vascular tissue, despite adrenaline increasing duration of action?","options":["It causes immediate allergic reaction","It risks tissue ischaemia/gangrene because the absorbed lignocaine remains and adrenaline causes prolonged vasoconstriction","It neutralizes lignocaine completely","It causes hyperthermia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text explains adrenaline prolongs the duration and reduces absorption, but in vascular tissue lignocaine is absorbed and remains, and prolonged vasoconstriction can cause ischaemia/gangrene if used around end-arteries (avascular digits/areas such as digits, tip of nose, tip of fingers, penis, ear, lobe of penis).  Why A wrong: The concern is vascular ischaemia, not an immediate allergy.  Why C wrong: Adrenaline does not neutralize lignocaine; it prolongs its effect.  Why D wrong: Hyperthermia (malignant) relates to volatile agents, not this combination."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"Which lignocaine concentration is used for spinal anaesthesia?","options":["Lignocaine 2%","Lignocaine 5%","Lignocaine 0.5%","Lignocaine 10%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states lignocaine 5% concentration is used for spinal anaesthesia.  Why A wrong: 2% is the general hospital local anaesthetic concentration.  Why C/D wrong: These concentrations are not specified for spinal anaesthesia."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"Ketamine is given as an example of which type of anaesthetic?","options":["Volatile liquid","Dissociative anaesthetic","Opioid analgesic","Benzodiazepine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Ketamine is given as an example of a dissociative anaesthetic.  Why A wrong: Volatile liquids include ether, halothane, isoflurane, desflurane, sevoflurane.  Why C wrong: Opioid analgesics are fentanyl/morphine.  Why D wrong: Benzodiazepines are diazepam, lorazepam, midazolam."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"According to the vaporizer colour-coding table, Isoflurane is coded which colour (mnemonic \"IP address\")?","options":["Yellow","Purple","Red","Blue"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The colour-coding table assigns Isoflurane = Purple (trick: IP address).  Why A wrong: Yellow is Sevoflurane.  Why C wrong: Red is Halothane.  Why D wrong: Blue is Desflurane."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"Match the vaporizer colour codes: which agent is coded Yellow (mnemonic \"YeS sir\")?","options":["Halothane","Sevoflurane","Enflurane","Desflurane"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table assigns Sevoflurane = Yellow (trick: YeS sir).  Why A wrong: Halothane is Red.  Why C wrong: Enflurane is Orange.  Why D wrong: Desflurane is Blue."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"In the vaporizer colour-coding table, Halothane is coded which colour (mnemonic \"HR department\")?","options":["Purple","Red","Orange","Blue"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Halothane = Red (trick: HR department).  Why A wrong: Purple is Isoflurane.  Why C wrong: Orange is Enflurane.  Why D wrong: Blue is Desflurane."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"In the vaporizer colour-coding table, which agent is coded Blue (mnemonic \"BD\")?","options":["Sevoflurane","Desflurane","Enflurane","Isoflurane"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Desflurane = Blue (trick: BD).  Why A wrong: Sevoflurane is Yellow.  Why C wrong: Enflurane is Orange.  Why D wrong: Isoflurane is Purple."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"In the vaporizer colour-coding table, Enflurane is coded which colour (mnemonic \"Ethyl Oxide\")?","options":["Yellow","Red","Orange","Blue"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Enflurane = Orange (trick: Ethyl Oxide).  Why A wrong: Yellow is Sevoflurane.  Why B wrong: Red is Halothane.  Why D wrong: Blue is Desflurane."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"PAC (Pre-Anaesthesia Checkup) is a medical check-up and laboratory investigation performed before surgery by which professional?","options":["A pharmacist","A registered nurse","A radiologist","A physiotherapist"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states PAC is done by an anaesthesia provider or a registered nurse before an operation to assess the patient's physical condition and medical problems.  Why A/C/D wrong: These professionals are not the ones performing the PAC per the source."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"In the PAC mnemonic, \"A\" stands for:","options":["Allergies","Airway","Antibiotics","Anxiety"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the PAC parameter mnemonic, A = Airway.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the source's \"A = Airway.\""},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"In the PAC parameter mnemonic, \"B\" includes Breathing, Blood haemoglobin, and which other component?","options":["Blood pressure only","Blood loss estimation and blood availability","Bowel sounds","Body temperature"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: PAC \"B\" = Breathing, Blood haemoglobin, blood loss estimation, and blood availability.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the components listed under \"B.\""},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"In the PAC parameter mnemonic, \"C\" stands for:","options":["Circulation, Clinical examination, Co-morbidities","Consent and Counselling","Comfort and Care","Coagulation only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: PAC \"C\" = Circulation, Clinical examination, Co-morbidities.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the listed \"C\" components."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"In the PAC parameter mnemonic, \"D\" refers to:","options":["Drugs being used by the patient; Details of previous anaesthesia and surgeries","Diet plan","Diagnosis","Discharge planning"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: PAC \"D\" = Drugs being used by the patient; Details of previous anaesthesia and surgeries.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the listed \"D\" components."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"In the PAC parameter mnemonic, \"E\" stands for:","options":["Education","Evaluate investigations; End point to take up the case for surgery","Electrolytes only","Emergency drugs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: PAC \"E\" = Evaluate investigations; End point to take up the case for surgery.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the listed \"E\" components."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"In the PAC parameter mnemonic, \"F\" stands for:","options":["Family history; Follow-up","Fluid status; Fasting","Fever; Fatigue","Food allergy only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: PAC \"F\" = Fluid status, Fasting.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the listed \"F\" components."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"Anaesthesia is best defined as:","options":["A permanent loss of all reflexes","A temporary state that causes unconsciousness, loss of pain and muscle relaxation","A method of intubation","A type of analgesic drug"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text defines anaesthesia as a temporary state that causes unconsciousness, loss of pain and muscle relaxation.  Why A wrong: The state is temporary, not a permanent loss.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the definition of anaesthesia."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"Which of the following is a recognized \"type\" of anaesthesia per the source?","options":["General anaesthesia","Local or regional anaesthesia","Both general and local/regional anaesthesia","Neither"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The text lists two types: (1) general anaesthesia and (2) local or regional anaesthesia.  Why A/B wrong: Both are types; choosing only one is incomplete.  Why D wrong: Both are valid types."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"Which of the following is listed as a subtype of local/regional anaesthesia? (Select all that apply)","options":["Spinal anaesthesia","Topical anaesthesia","Epidural anaesthesia","Field block anaesthesia","Local infiltration anaesthesia"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The text lists spinal, topical, epidural, field block, and local infiltration anaesthesia all as forms of local/regional anaesthesia.  Why each correct: All five are explicitly listed under local/regional anaesthesia subtypes."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"General anaesthesia (GA) suppresses CNS activity and results in:","options":["Localized numbness only","Unconsciousness and total lack of sensation","Loss of motor function with preserved consciousness","Sedation with full awareness"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states GA suppresses CNS activity and results in unconsciousness and total lack of sensation.  Why A/C/D wrong: These describe local/regional or sedation states, not GA."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"The triad of general anaesthesia includes hypnosis (unconsciousness), analgesia, and:","options":["Muscle relaxation","Vasoconstriction","Hyperreflexia","Hypothermia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The triad of general anaesthesia is hypnosis (unconsciousness), analgesia, and muscle relaxation.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not components of the GA triad."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"General anaesthesia is used to produce varying levels of which combination of effects?","options":["Unconsciousness, amnesia, anaesthesia, analgesia, and skeletal muscle relaxation","Only pain relief","Only sedation","Only amnesia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: GA produces varying levels of consciousness, amnesia, anaesthesia, analgesia, and skeletal muscle relaxation.  Why B/C/D wrong: GA encompasses all of these, not a single effect."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"Neuromuscular blocking agents inhibit transmission of nerve impulses by binding with which receptor sites?","options":["Adrenergic receptor sites","Cholinergic receptor sites","Dopaminergic receptor sites","Serotonergic receptor sites"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states neuromuscular blocking agents inhibit nerve impulse transmission by binding with cholinergic receptor sites.  Why A/C/D wrong: These receptor types are not specified for neuromuscular blockade in the source."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"An anaesthetic vaporiser is a device that delivers a given concentration of which type of agent?","options":["Intravenous agent","Volatile anaesthetic agent","Local anaesthetic","Muscle relaxant"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the anaesthetic vaporiser delivers a given concentration of a volatile anaesthetic agent.  Why A/C/D wrong: The vaporiser is specifically for volatile agents, not IV/local/relaxant drugs."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"A volatile anaesthetic agent is an inhalational anaesthetic in chemical compound form. What is its mode of inhalation?","options":["Through the mask, laryngeal mask airway, or tracheal tube connected to an anaesthetic vaporiser","By intramuscular injection only","By intravenous push only","By rectal suppository"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states a volatile agent is administered via inhalation through a mask, laryngeal mask airway, or tracheal tube connected to an anaesthetic vaporiser (e.g., isoflurane, sevoflurane, desflurane).  Why B/C/D wrong: Volatile agents are inhaled, not given IM/IV/rectally."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"Which type of anaesthetic vaporizer is driven by positive pressure?","options":["Drawover vaporiser","Plenum vaporiser","Dual-circuit gas-vapor blender","Topical vaporiser"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the plenum vaporiser is driven by positive pressure.  Why A wrong: The drawover vaporiser is driven by negative pressure.  Why C wrong: The dual-circuit gas-vapor blender is exclusively used for desflurane.  Why D wrong: \"Topical vaporiser\" is not a listed vaporiser type."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"The drawover vaporiser is developed by the patient's own:","options":["Positive pressure ventilation","Concentration depending on the minute volume of the patient","Continuous infusion pump","External oxygen supply"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the drawover vaporiser is driven by negative pressure and the concentration depends on the minute volume of the patient.  Why A wrong: That describes the plenum vaporiser.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the mechanisms described for the drawover vaporiser."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"The dual-circuit gas-vapor blender vaporiser is exclusively used for which agent?","options":["Sevoflurane","Isoflurane","Desflurane","Halothane"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the dual-circuit gas-vapor blender is exclusively used for desflurane.  Why A/B/D wrong: These agents are not the exclusively-named agent for this vaporiser."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"Which of the following are listed side effects of general anaesthesia? (Select all that apply)","options":["Excitement and restlessness during initial stage","Nausea and vomiting due to stimulation of the chemoreceptor zone in medulla","Respiratory distress due to depression of medulla","Hypotension and bradycardia due to depression of cardiac system","Laryngospasm due to depression of laryngeal reflex"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: All five are explicitly listed side effects of GA, including malignant hyperthermia (higher body temperature >105°F, drug of choice dantrolene).  Why each correct: Each effect is stated in the GA side effects section."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"A patient under general anaesthesia develops a body temperature >105°F. This indicates malignant hyperthermia. What is the drug of choice?","options":["Acetaminophen","Dantrolene","Atropine","Adrenaline"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states malignant hyperthermia presents with higher body temperature >105°F and the drug of choice is dantrolene.  Why A wrong: Antipyretics alone do not treat malignant hyperthermia.  Why C wrong: Atropine is an anticholinergic premedication, not the treatment.  Why D wrong: Adrenaline is not the drug of choice for malignant hyperthermia."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"Which is a priority nursing care measure during general anaesthesia to prevent aspiration?","options":["Maintain airway and oxygenation","Keep all emergency equipment at the bedside in working condition","Maintain side lying position to prevent aspiration after general anaesthesia in immediate post-operative period","Keep NPO until the gag reflex has returned"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The text (highlighted as a \"Must Know\" point) states: maintain side-lying position to prevent aspiration after general anaesthesia in the immediate post-operative period.  Why A wrong: Airway/oxygenation is important but the aspiration-prevention positioning point is the side-lying measure.  Why B wrong: Bedside emergency equipment is general preparedness, not the specific aspiration measure.  Why D wrong: NPO until gag reflex returns is correct nursing care but the positioning point is the highlighted aspiration-prevention measure."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"During general anaesthesia nursing care, the nurse should keep the patient NPO until:","options":["The patient is fully oriented","The gag reflex has returned","Vital signs are stable","The surgeon orders feeding"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states keep NPO until the gag reflex has returned.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are reasonable but not the specific criterion the source gives."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"According to Guedel's Stages of Anaesthesia, Stage I (Analgesia/Induction stage) covers:","options":["From initial administration of induction medications to loss of consciousness","Excited and delirious activity","Full operative state","Medullary depression"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Stage I is the period between the initial administration of the induction medications and loss of consciousness, marked by client decreased delivery and loss of consciousness, amnesia, analgesia and euphoria.  Why B wrong: Excited/delirious activity is Stage II.  Why C wrong: Operative state is Stage III.  Why D wrong: Medullary depression is Stage IV."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"Guedel's Stage II (Excitement stage) is characterized by:","options":["Analgesia and euphoria","Loss of consciousness marked by excited and delirious activity, with muscles becoming tense and breathing irregular","The surgical operative state","Cardiovascular and respiratory collapse"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Stage II is loss of consciousness marked by excited and delirious activity; muscles become tense and breathing irregular.  Why A wrong: That is Stage I.  Why C wrong: That is Stage III.  Why D wrong: That is Stage IV/medullary depression."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"Guedel's Stage III (Surgical anaesthesia stage) is the:","options":["Induction stage","Excitement stage","Operative stage, where signs and reflexes are depressed, complete unconsciousness and decreasing eye movement; surgery can begin","Overdose stage"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Stage III is the operative stage with depressed signs/reflexes, complete unconsciousness, decreasing eye movement; surgery can begin.  Why A/B wrong: These are Stages I and II.  Why D wrong: Overdose corresponds to Stage IV (medullary depression)."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"Guedel's Stage IV (Medullary Depression / overdose) is characterized by which life-threatening features?","options":["Respiratory depression, cardiac depression and arrest, no eye movement, and potentially lethal cardiovascular and respiratory collapse","Mild sedation only","Analgesia and euphoria","Excited delirious activity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Stage IV (medullary depression/overdose) features respiratory depression, cardiac depression and arrest, no eye movement, and is lethal due to cardiovascular and respiratory collapse if too much medication has been given.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe lighter stages, not the lethal overdose stage."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"Local anaesthesia is used in which type of operations?","options":["Major thoracic and cardiac surgeries","Minor obstetric, dental, and minor operations","All emergency abdominal surgeries","Only neurosurgery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states local anaesthesia is mostly used in obstetric, dental, and minor operations.  Why A/C/D wrong: Local anaesthesia is used for minor procedures, not major thoracic/cardiac/neurosurgery."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"Epinephrine (adrenaline) is added to a local anaesthetic agent to:","options":["Increase the duration of local anaesthetic effects and decrease regional bleeding","Shorten the duration of action","Increase systemic absorption","Cause vasodilation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states epinephrine is added to increase the duration of local anaesthetic effects and to decrease regional bleeding.  Why B/C/D wrong: Adrenaline prolongs (not shortens) action, reduces absorption (vasoconstriction), not vasodilation."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"A patient who received spinal anaesthesia develops a headache. What is the most important consideration to prevent post-dural puncture headache (PDPH)?","options":["Use a small (e.g., 22–25 G) atraumatic/pencil-point needle and keep the cutting/spinal needle bevel parallel to dural fibres","Use the largest gauge cutting needle","Keep the patient sitting upright immediately","Withhold all IV fluids"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the most important consideration in preventing post-dural puncture headache is using a small or pencil-point needle and a small ripped non-cutting spinal needle, and keeping the bevel in line/parallel to the dural fibres.  Why B wrong: A larger cutting needle increases PDPH risk.  Why C wrong: Sitting upright worsens, not prevents, PDPH; flat positioning helps.  Why D wrong: Withholding fluids is not the preventive measure described."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"Per the \"Should Remember\" box, acceptable haemoglobin (Hb) value for performing surgery is:","options":["5,00,000/mm³","1,00,000/mm³ (acceptable Hb), with platelets minimum 1,00,000/mm³","50,000/mm³","10,000/mm³"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states acceptable Hb value for performing surgery is 1,00,000/mm³ and minimum platelets required for performing surgery is 1,00,000/mm³ (note: in diabetes patients, blood sugar levels for surgery should be maintained between 110–200 mg/dl).  Why A/C/D wrong: These values do not match the source-stated thresholds."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"Per the source, in diabetes patients the blood sugar level for performing surgery should be maintained between:","options":["70–110 mg/dl","110–200 mg/dl","200–300 mg/dl","50–100 mg/dl"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states in diabetic patients blood sugar levels should be maintained between 110–200 mg/dl for surgery.  Why A/C/D wrong: These ranges do not match the source-stated 110–200 mg/dl."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"Which nursing action is correct after local anaesthetic administration to prevent severe post-dural puncture headache?","options":["Keep the client flat for 6–12 hours and use pillows to prevent severe headache","Ambulate the patient immediately","Keep the patient in high Fowler's for 12 hours","Restrict all fluids for 24 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states if local anaesthetic is administered, keep the client flat for 6–12 hours to prevent post-dural puncture headache (PDPH); pillows should also help.  Why B/C wrong: Upright/ambulating positions worsen PDPH.  Why D wrong: Fluid restriction is not the stated measure."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"Nursing care after local anaesthesia includes monitoring for hypotension, return of motor and sensory function to lower extremities, and:","options":["Initiating general nursing care","Immediate ambulation","Restricting oxygen","Discontinuing all monitoring"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states to initiate general nursing care and monitor for hypotension and return of motor & sensory function to lower extremities.  Why B/C/D wrong: These contradict safe post-anaesthesia nursing care."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"In spinal anaesthesia, the anaesthetic agent is injected into which space, usually at which lumbar level, using a spinal needle?","options":["Epidural space at L1–L2","Subarachnoid space, usually at the lumbar region (L3–L4) by spinal needle","Subcutaneous tissue at L5","Intramuscular at T12"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states in spinal anaesthesia the anaesthetic agent is injected into the subarachnoid space of the lumbar region, usually L3–L4, by spinal needle.  Why A wrong: Epidural injection is into the epidural space (epidural anaesthesia).  Why C/D wrong: These sites are incorrect for spinal anaesthesia."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"For spinal anaesthesia, the client should lie in flat position for how long after the injection to prevent post-procedure headache?","options":["1–2 hours","6–12 hours","24 hours","30 minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the client should lie in flat position for 6–12 hours after spinal injection, otherwise the client may complain of post-procedure headache.  Why A/C/D wrong: These durations do not match the source-specified 6–12 hours."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"During spinal anaesthesia, the nurse should give IV fluids as per order and increase them if which complication occurs?","options":["Hypertension","Hypotension","Bradycardia","Headache"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states to give IV fluids as per order and increase if hypotension occurs.  Why A wrong: Spinal anaesthesia commonly causes hypotension, not hypertension.  Why C/D wrong: These are other concerns, but fluid increase is for hypotension."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"Major side effects of spinal anaesthesia include all of the following EXCEPT:","options":["Headache","Nausea and vomiting","Hypotension and respiratory paralysis","Hypertensive crisis"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Listed major side effects of spinal anaesthesia are headache, nausea & vomiting, hypotension, and respiratory paralysis; hypertensive crisis is not listed.  Why A/B/C wrong: These are all stated side effects of spinal anaesthesia."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"In epidural anaesthesia, the anaesthetic is injected into which space?","options":["Subarachnoid space","Lumbar epidural block (epidural space)","Subcutaneous tissue","Intramuscular tissue"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states epidural anaesthesia is also known as a lumbar epidural block, with injection into the epidural space.  Why A wrong: Subarachnoid injection is spinal anaesthesia.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the epidural space."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"At which levels is the epidural block administered for lower abdominal surgical procedures?","options":["L3–L4 only","L3–L4 and IVD (intervertebral disc level)","T1–T2","S1–S2"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states epidural is administered between L3–L4 and IVD for lower abdominal surgical procedures.  Why A wrong: It is administered at L3–L4 and the IVD level, not L3–L4 alone.  Why C/D wrong: These levels are not specified."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"Epidural anaesthesia is used to block which type of pain, including for prolonged labour pain and during a Caesarean section?","options":["Acute trauma pain only","Pain related to surgery, prolonged labour pain, in pregnant lady","Postoperative bleeding","Headache"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states epidural is used to block pain related to surgical procedures, including prolonged labour pain, in the pregnant lady.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the described use of epidural anaesthesia."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"During epidural anaesthesia, the mother should be maintained in which position to prevent supine hypotension syndrome?","options":["Supine flat","Side-lying position","Prone","High Fowler's"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states to maintain the mother in side-lying position to prevent supine hypotension; this also avoids compression of the uterus from the vena cava.  Why A wrong: Supine position risks supine hypotension syndrome.  Why C/D wrong: These positions are not the recommended ones to prevent supine hypotension."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"During epidural anaesthesia, the nurse should give IV fluids as per order and increase if which complication occurs?","options":["Hypertension","Hypotension","Hyperthermia","Hyperglycemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states to give IV fluids as per order and increase in case of hypotension occurrence.  Why A/C/D wrong: The intervention is targeted at hypotension during epidural anaesthesia."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"In a para-cervical block, a local anaesthetic is injected between two sites at which depth, alongside the vaginal portion of the cervix?","options":["3–7 mm","10–15 mm","1–2 cm","5 cm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states para-cervical block injects local anaesthetic between two sites at a depth of 3–7 mm alongside the vaginal portion of the cervix.  Why B/C/D wrong: These depths do not match the source-stated 3–7 mm."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"What is the most common complication of a para-cervical block?","options":["Maternal hypertension","Fetal bradycardia","Maternal seizure","Uterine rupture"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the most common complication of para-cervical block is fetal bradycardia.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the stated most-common complication."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"The para-cervical block is effective for approximately how long?","options":["At least 1 hour","At least 3 hours","30 minutes","6 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the para-cervical block is effective at least 1 hour.  Why B/C/D wrong: These durations do not match the source-stated at-least-1-hour effectiveness."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"Topical anaesthesia involves which method of local anaesthetic application?","options":["Injection into the subarachnoid space","Local application of tissues by xylocaine in case of minor procedures like suturing during dressing","Intravenous bolus","Epidural catheter"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states topical anaesthesia is local application to tissues by xylocaine in case of minor procedures like suturing during dressing.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are other anaesthesia routes, not topical application."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"Preoperative nursing care is primarily aimed at:","options":["Increasing the success of the surgery","Reducing the length of the consent form","Replacing the surgeon's role","Eliminating the need for anaesthesia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the major aim of perioperative nursing care is to increase the success of the surgery.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not represent the stated aim."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"Match the phase: the \"Preoperative phase\" of perioperative care begins when:","options":["The patient and surgeon mutually decide that surgery is necessary, and ends when the patient is transferred to the operative room (OT) or procedural bed","The patient enters the OT until transfer to the PACU","Admission in PACU","The patient is fully recovered at home"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The preoperative phase begins when the patient and surgeon mutually decide surgery will take place and ends when the patient is transferred to the operative room/procedural bed.  Why B wrong: That describes the intra-operative phase.  Why C/D wrong: Those describe the post-operative phase."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"The \"Intra-operative phase\" of perioperative care begins when:","options":["Surgery is decided upon","The patient is transferred to the operative bed until transfer to the post anaesthesia care unit (PACU)","Admission to PACU until recovery","Complete recovery at home"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The intra-operative phase begins when the patient is transferred to the operative bed until transfer to the post anaesthesia care unit (PACU).  Why A wrong: That is the preoperative phase.  Why C/D wrong: Those describe the post-operative phase."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"The \"Post-operative phase\" begins with and ends when:","options":["Begins with the surgery decision; ends in the OT","Begins with admission in PACU/recovery area and ends with complete recovery from surgery and the last follow-up visit","Begins at home; ends at surgery","Begins with anaesthesia; ends with incision"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The post-operative phase begins with admission in PACU/recovery area and ends with complete recovery from surgery and the last follow-up visit.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated boundaries of the post-operative phase."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"Who is responsible for obtaining informed consent for a surgical procedure?","options":["The staff nurse","The surgeon/doctors who will perform the procedure","The pharmacist","The ward clerk"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the surgeon/doctors are responsible for obtaining the consent for surgery.  Why A wrong: Nurses are not responsible for signing the consent form; they only witness the signature.  Why C/D wrong: These personnel do not obtain surgical consent."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"What is the nurse's role regarding informed consent?","options":["To explain the surgical risks and obtain consent","To witness the signing of the consent form (the nurse is the one who witnesses the client signs the consent form)","To override the patient's refusal","To sign on behalf of the patient"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states nurses are not responsible for signing the consent form; they only verify/witness the consent form when the nurse witnesses the client's signing of the consent form.  Why A wrong: Explaining surgical risks is the surgeon's duty.  Why C/D wrong: A nurse cannot override refusal or sign for the patient."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"A minor under 18 years of age requiring surgery requires consent from whom?","options":["The minor alone","A parent or legal guardian to sign the consent form (a minor under 18 who is mature may give assent)","The surgeon","The nurse"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states minors <18 years may need a parent or legal guardian to sign the consent form; a mature minor may give assent, but a parent or guardian gives consent.  Why A wrong: A minor's sole consent is not sufficient.  Why C/D wrong: Surgeon/nurse cannot provide proxy consent for a minor."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"Older clients who may need a legal guardian to sign the consent form do so under what circumstance?","options":["When they are nervous","When they are unable to make decisions for themselves (incompetent / lacking decision-making capacity)","Whenever surgery is minor","When they prefer not to read"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states older clients may need a legal guardian to sign the consent form when they are unable to make decisions for themselves.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the stated circumstance requiring a guardian."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"Do patients have a right to refuse treatment even after consent is given?","options":["No, consent is final","Yes, patients/clients have a right to refuse treatment until a legal document (consent) is signed and they are unable to make decisions for themselves","Only minors can refuse","Only with the surgeon's permission"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states patients/clients have a right to refuse treatment until they are unable to make decisions for themselves; sedation should not be administered to the client before taking the consent.  Why A/C/D wrong: These contradict the patient's right to refuse."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"When should sedation NOT be administered to the client?","options":["After surgery","Before taking the consent","During recovery","Never during the hospital stay"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states sedation should not be administered to the client before taking the consent (because sedation impairs the client's ability to give valid informed consent).  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated rule about sedation before consent."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"For a valid voluntary informed consent, the consent must be:","options":["Freely given, without coercion","Given under sedation","Given by any relative regardless of authority","Implied automatically on admission"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states valid consent must be freely given, without coercion (voluntary consent).  Why B wrong: Consent under sedation is not valid.  Why C/D wrong: Random relatives or implied admission consent are not valid for surgery."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"An \"incompetent patient\" with respect to informed consent is one who:","options":["Is fully autonomous","Is an individual who is not autonomous and cannot give or withhold consent (e.g., individuals who are mentally retarded, mentally ill, or comatose)","Refuses for personal reasons","Speaks a different language"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text defines an incompetent patient as an individual who is not autonomous and cannot give or withhold consent, such as individuals who are mentally retarded, mentally ill, or comatose.  Why A wrong: An autonomous patient is competent.  Why C/D wrong: Refusal or language differences alone do not constitute incompetence."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"For informed consent to be valid, it should be in writing and should contain which of the following? (Select all that apply)","options":["Explanation of procedure and its risks","Description of benefits and alternatives","An offer to answer questions about procedure","Instructions that the patient may withdraw consent","A statement informing the patient if the protocol differs from customary procedure"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The text lists all five elements (explanation of procedure & its consequences; description of benefits and alternatives; offer to answer questions; instruction that the patient may withdraw consent; statement informing the patient if the protocol differs from customary procedure) as required content of written informed consent.  Why each correct: All five are explicitly listed components of valid written consent."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"What is required of the consent form when the patient is unable to understand written/spoken language?","options":["The consent is skipped","Information must be written and delivered in a language the patient understands, and questions answered to facilitate comprehension if material is confusing","A relative signs without explanation","The nurse signs instead"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states information must be written and delivered in a language the patient understands; questions must be answered to facilitate comprehension if material is confusing.  Why A/C/D wrong: These violate the requirement for understandable consent."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"Preoperative fasting helps to avoid symptoms of which conditions?","options":["Hyperglycemia and hypertension","Dehydration and hypoglycemia","Infection and bleeding","Hypothermia and bradycardia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states preoperative fasting helps to avoid symptoms of dehydration and hypoglycemia.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the symptoms preoperative fasting is intended to avoid per the source."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"Per ASA preoperative fasting guidelines for children, clear liquids (e.g., clear fluids/juices) require a fasting time of:","options":["2 hours","4 hours","6 hours","8 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The ASA table for children lists clear liquids (clear fluids/juices) as requiring 2 hours fasting.  Why B/C/D wrong: 4 hours is breast milk, 6 hours is non-human milk/infant formula, 8 hours is fried/fatty foods/heavy meal."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"Per ASA fasting guidelines for children, breast milk requires a fasting period of:","options":["2 hours","4 hours","6 hours","8 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists breast milk = 4 hours fasting.  Why A wrong: 2 hours is clear liquids.  Why C wrong: 6 hours is non-human milk/infant formula.  Why D wrong: 8 hours is fried/fatty foods/heavy meal."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"Per ASA fasting guidelines for children, non-human milk (infant formula) requires fasting of:","options":["2 hours","4 hours","6 hours","8 hours"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists non-human milk (infant formula) = 6 hours fasting.  Why A/B/D wrong: 2 hours = clear liquids, 4 hours = breast milk, 8 hours = fried/fatty/heavy meal."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"Per ASA fasting guidelines, fried foods, fatty foods, or a heavy meal require a fasting period of:","options":["2 hours","4 hours","6 hours","8 hours"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists fried foods/fatty foods/heavy meal = 8 hours fasting.  Why A/B/C wrong: These shorter durations apply to clear liquids, breast milk, and formula/light meals respectively."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"Per ASA fasting guidelines for adults, the fasting time before surgery is:","options":["2 hours","4 hours","6–8 hrs of fasting required in adults","12 hours"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The text states for adults, 6–8 hrs of fasting is required before surgery.  Why A/B/D wrong: These do not match the adult 6–8 hour fasting requirement."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"According to preoperative prophylactic antibiotic guidelines, prophylactic antibiotics should be administered within how long before surgery?","options":["Within 60 minutes or 1 hour before surgical incision (within 2 hours for vancomycin or fluoroquinolones)","Within 6 hours before surgery","After the incision","Only postoperatively"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states prophylactic antibiotics should be administered within 60 minutes or 1 hour before surgical incision (within 2 hours for vancomycin or fluoroquinolones).  Why B/C/D wrong: These timings contradict the stated pre-incision window."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"For most procedures, a single-dose prophylactic antibiotic is usually sufficient if the duration of the procedure is:","options":["<24 hours","<48 hours","<6 hours","<12 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states although single-dose prophylaxis is usually sufficient, the duration of the procedure should be <24 hours for a single dose.  Why B/C/D wrong: These durations are not the stated threshold."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"During a prolonged operation, an additional intraoperative dose of prophylactic antibiotic should be repeated approximately every:","options":["2 hours, to maintain a half-life of the antibiotic","30 minutes","6 hours","12 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states repeat dosing occurs at an appropriate interval, usually 2 hours for the additional case, to maintain the half-life of the antibiotic.  Why B/C/D wrong: These intervals do not match the stated ~2-hour re-dosing."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"Conditions for re-administration (additional dose) of prophylactic antibiotics include which of the following? (Select all that apply)","options":["Prolonged surgery","Excessive bleeding occurring","Half-life of the antibiotic being shorter than half of the operation","Patient request only","Extensive burns"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: The text lists prolonged surgery, excessive bleeding, the half-life of the prophylactic agent being shorter than half of the operation (e.g., short half-life relative to operation), and extensive burns as conditions for re-administration.  Why D wrong: Patient request alone is not a listed clinical condition for re-administration."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"When preparing the surgical site, the area should be cleaned with antiseptic or antibacterial soap the night before surgery, or as per:","options":["The patient's request","The doctor's/physician's order","The nurse's preference","The pharmacist's instruction"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states to clean the surgical site with antiseptic or antibacterial soap the night before surgery, or as ordered (per order).  Why A/C/D wrong: Site preparation follows the physician's order, not these."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"When should hair be shaved/removed at the surgical site, per the source?","options":["Several days before surgery","Shave the operative site as per order; hair removal (enema) should be given before the night of surgery for bowel preparation","After the surgery","Hair should never be removed"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states shave the operative site as per order, and that elimination — enema — should be given the night before surgery for bowel preparation.  Why A/C/D wrong: These contradict the stated timing of site shaving and bowel preparation."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"Pre-operative patient education should include full explanation about which topics? (Select all that apply)","options":["The procedure and its consequences","Deep breathing, coughing, and incentive spirometry, splinting technique when coughing to prevent pain","The disease process and how to manage it without surgery (i.e., post-operative exercises)","Pain management and post-operative exercises","Psychological support to the client and family"],"correct":"A,B,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The text lists full explanation about the procedure & its consequence; deep breathing, coughing, and incentive spirometry with splinting technique to reduce pain; explanation about pain management and post-operative exercises; and psychological support to the client and family.  Why C wrong: The source does not advise teaching the patient to avoid surgery; option C misstates the content (post-operative exercises are covered under D)."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"A key pre-operative checklist item to confirm patient identity is to:","options":["Skip identification if the patient looks familiar","Ensure consent is taken and wear/check an identification band","Only check the bed number","Rely on the surgeon's memory"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The pre-operative checklist states to ensure consent is taken and that the patient is wearing an identification band.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are unsafe identification practices."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"Pre-operative checklist items include all of the following EXCEPT:","options":["Assess for allergies and assess vital signs before sending the patient to the OT","Review the checklist before sending the client to the operation theatre (OT)","Ensure all ordered pre-operative medication, such as antibiotics (usually before surgery), are given 1 hour prior to surgery","Administer general anaesthesia in the ward before transfer"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: General anaesthesia is administered in the OT, not in the ward; the checklist includes allergy/vital assessment, reviewing the checklist before sending to OT, and giving pre-operative antibiotics ~1 hour before surgery.  Why A/B/C wrong: All are valid pre-operative checklist items."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"During the preoperative period, ensure that the patient's history and physical examination are completed and documented in the client's record. Where should the blood cross-match (ECG & chest x-ray) attachments be placed?","options":["In the pharmacy file","Attached to the client's records, ensuring the blood is cross-matched/typed and that attachment is performed (e.g., results documented in the record)","Kept by the patient at home","Discarded after surgery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states ensure that the ECG & chest x-ray are taken and attached to the client's records, and ensure blood types are cross-matched, performed, and documented.  Why A/C/D wrong: These mishandle critical preoperative documentation and cross-match results."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"What colour-coded IV cannula is recommended for inserting an IV line in adults if massive blood loss is anticipated?","options":["18 G cannula (Green Colour) because large G cannula may be needed during surgery if not massive blood loss; insert a large bore (16 G) for massive loss","24 G (Yellow)","22 G (Blue)","26 G (Violet)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states to insert an IV line with an 18 G cannula (Green colour) because a large-gauge cannula may be needed during surgery; a large/16 G is used if massive blood loss is anticipated.  Why B/C/D wrong: Smaller-gauge cannulae are inadequate for rapid fluid/blood replacement in surgery."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"Before sending the client to the operation theatre (OT), the client should be asked to remove all of the following EXCEPT:","options":["Jewellery, makeup, dentures, hairpins","Nail polish, glasses, and prosthetics","The identification band","Hairpins and prosthetics"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The text states all jewellery, makeup, dentures, hairpins, nail polish, glasses, and prosthetics should be removed before sending to OT — but the identification band must remain on the patient.  Why A/B/D wrong: All these items should be removed before surgery."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"What should be done with the client's valuables, such as jewellery, before surgery?","options":["Send them to the OT with the patient","Document all valuables and give/hand them over to the guardians (relatives)","Discard them","Leave them at the bedside"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states to document all valuables, such as jewellery, given to the guardians.  Why A/C/D wrong: These mishandle the patient's valuables and risk loss."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"The nurse should monitor and document the client's vital signs before sending the client to the OT, and should follow which list to prevent surgical errors?","options":["Discharge checklist","Surgical check list (surgical safety checklist)","Diet chart","Medication reconciliation only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the nurse should follow the surgical check list to prevent surgical errors and document vital signs before sending the client to the OT.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not specifically prevent surgical errors as the surgical checklist does."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"Preoperative antibiotic prophylaxis is best defined as:","options":["A method of administering antibiotics prior to surgical procedures to decrease surgical site infections","A treatment for established post-operative sepsis","A method to delay surgery","A substitute for sterile technique"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The \"Should Remember\" box defines preoperative antibiotic prophylaxis as a method of administering antibiotics prior to surgical procedures to decrease surgical site infections, recommended by the CDC and other guidelines.  Why B/C/D wrong: Prophylaxis prevents infection; it is not a treatment for established sepsis nor a substitute for sterile technique."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"The CDC and other guidelines recommend administering the chosen prophylactic antibiotic within what timeframe (except for vancomycin) so that it remains in tissue for 10 minutes to 2 hours prior to surgical incision?","options":["30–60 minutes prior to incision","6 hours prior","24 hours prior","After incision"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the chosen antibiotic should be administered so the drug remains in tissue (in the body) for ~10 minutes to 2 hours prior to surgical incision (except for vancomycin, ~30–60 minutes prior). The drug should be given within the 30–60 minute window prior to incision.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated pre-incision window."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"Which drug should be WITHHELD before surgery, and how many days prior? (Match) Aspirin should be stopped how long before surgery?","options":["1 day prior","3 days prior to surgery","4 days prior","6 hours prior"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The drugs-to-withhold list states aspirin should be stopped 3 days prior to surgery.  Why A wrong: 1 day prior applies to diuretics.  Why C wrong: 4 days applies to warfarin and antidiabetic drugs.  Why D wrong: 6 hours applies to heparin."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"Per the drugs-to-withhold list, warfarin should be stopped how long before surgery?","options":["1 day prior","3 days prior","4 days prior to surgery","6 hours prior"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The list states warfarin should be withheld 4 days prior to surgery.  Why A/B/D wrong: 1 day = diuretics, 3 days = aspirin, 6 hours = heparin."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"Per the drugs-to-withhold list, antidiabetic drugs should be withheld how long before surgery?","options":["4 days prior to surgery","1 day prior","6 hours prior","On the day of operation only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The list states antidiabetic drugs should be withheld 4 days prior to surgery.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated antidiabetic withholding interval."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"Per the drugs-to-withhold list, heparin should be stopped how long before surgery?","options":["3 days prior","4 days prior","6 hours prior to surgery","1 day prior"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The list states heparin should be withheld 6 hours prior to surgery.  Why A/B/D wrong: These do not match the stated heparin interval."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"Per the drugs-to-withhold list, diuretics should be stopped how long before surgery?","options":["1 day prior to surgery","3 days prior","4 days prior","6 hours prior"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The list states diuretics should be withheld 1 day prior to surgery.  Why B/C/D wrong: These intervals belong to other drugs."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"Per the drugs-to-withhold list, antiplatelet medication should be withheld:","options":["6 hours prior","On the day of operation","4 days prior","1 day prior"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The list states antiplatelet drugs should be withheld on the day of the operation.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated antiplatelet timing."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"Which of the following drugs should be CONTINUED before surgery? (Select all that apply)","options":["Digoxin","Antihypertensive drugs","Aspirin","Asthma medicines","Steroids and thyroid medicines"],"correct":"A,B,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The drugs-to-be-continued list includes digoxin, antihypertensive, antiarrhythmic, asthma medicines, steroids, and thyroid medicines.  Why C wrong: Aspirin is in the withhold list (stopped 3 days prior), not continued."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"Verification of the client and the surgical operative site is most critical in which period?","options":["Pre-operative period","Intra-operative period","Post-operative period","Discharge period"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states verification of the client and the surgical operative site is critical in the pre-operative period (immediately before/entering surgery) and especially flagged as critical in the intra-operative period; the source highlights verification of the operative site as critical in the pre-operative/intra-operative period.  Why A wrong: While important pre-operatively, the source emphasizes the operative-site verification as critical in the operative (intra-operative) context.  Why C/D wrong: These periods are after the surgical procedure begins."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"Premedication administered before the induction of anaesthesia is given to reduce anxiety and reduce secretions. Which is the drug of choice (DOC) for this purpose?","options":["Opioid","Benzodiazepine","Diuretic","Antibiotic"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states premedication to reduce anxiety/reduce secretion uses a benzodiazepine, which is the drug of choice (DOC).  Why A wrong: Opioids (morphine, pethidine) provide analgesia, not the primary anxiolytic DOC.  Why C/D wrong: Diuretics and antibiotics are not premedication anxiolytics."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"Which premedication is used to induce sedation prior to anaesthesia?","options":["Diazepam and benzodiazepine","Atropine","Digoxin","Pantoprazole"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states diazepam and benzodiazepine are used to induce sedation.  Why B wrong: Atropine (anticholinergic) decreases secretions.  Why C wrong: Digoxin is a cardiac drug to be continued, not a sedative.  Why D wrong: Pantoprazole is a PPI used to reduce gastric acidity."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"Which premedication is used to reduce gastric acidity and gastric volume before surgery?","options":["Proton pump inhibitor (e.g., pantoprazole) and H2 blocker (cimetidine, ranitidine)","Beta blocker","Opioid","Anticholinergic"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states proton pump inhibitor (pantoprazole) and H2 blocker (cimetidine, ranitidine) are used to reduce gastric acidity and volume.  Why B wrong: Beta blockers (cardio-selective, e.g., clonidine) promote haemodynamic stability.  Why C wrong: Opioids provide analgesia.  Why D wrong: Anticholinergics decrease secretions, not gastric acid/volume."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"Which premedication agents are used to promote haemodynamic stability before surgery?","options":["Cardio-selective beta blockers (e.g., clonidine)","Proton pump inhibitors","Opioids","Anticholinergics"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states cardio-selective beta blockers (clonidine) are used to promote haemodynamic stability.  Why B wrong: PPIs reduce gastric acidity/volume.  Why C wrong: Opioids provide analgesia.  Why D wrong: Anticholinergics decrease secretions."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"Which premedication agents are used to provide analgesia before surgery?","options":["Opioids (e.g., morphine, pethidine)","Benzodiazepines","Diuretics","PPIs"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states opioids (morphine, pethidine) are used to provide analgesia as premedication.  Why B wrong: Benzodiazepines provide sedation/anxiolysis.  Why C/D wrong: Diuretics and PPIs do not provide analgesia."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"Which premedication is given to decrease the chance of infection before surgery?","options":["Antibiotic, which should be given 60 minutes prior to surgery if anaesthesia is given (per the prophylactic guideline)","Opioid","Benzodiazepine","Diuretic"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states antibiotic premedication is used to decrease the chance of infection (consistent with prophylactic antibiotic administration before surgery).  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not decrease infection risk."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"Which premedication agents are given to decrease pulmonary and oral secretions before anaesthesia?","options":["Anticholinergics (atropine and glycopyrrolate)","Opioids","Proton pump inhibitors","Beta blockers"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states anticholinergics (atropine and glycopyrrolate) are given to decrease pulmonary and oral secretions.  Why B/C/D wrong: These drug classes do not primarily reduce respiratory/oral secretions."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"Intra-operative nursing care begins when:","options":["The client enters the operative room","The client first arrives in the operative room, when the client leaving from the operative theatre (i.e., from arrival in the OT until transfer out)","The patient is admitted to the ward","The consent is signed"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states intra-operative nursing care begins when the client first arrives in the operative room and continues until the client leaves the operative theatre.  Why A wrong: It begins at arrival in the operative room, which option B captures more completely with the transfer-out endpoint.  Why C/D wrong: These occur before the intra-operative phase."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"When the client arrives in the operating room, the operating team (OT) nurse should verify which of the following? (Select all that apply)","options":["The current and history, allergic conditions, and the client's identification bracelet","Check the file for the name of the patient","Confirm the operation site, side, and the patient","Confirm about one relative along with the patient","The colour of the surgeon's gown"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the OT nurse should verify the current and history, allergic conditions and the client's identification bracelet/verbal response; check the file for the name of the patient; confirm operation site, side and the patient; confirm about one relative along with the patient; check OT clothes; confirm the part preparation done or not; and confirm blood/arranged previously.  Why E wrong: The surgeon's gown colour is not a listed verification item."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"Before the operation, the OT nurse should confirm the part preparation (site preparation) was done, and confirm that:","options":["Blood was arranged previously (cross-matched/arranged)","The patient has eaten","The patient walked to the OT","Sedation was given before consent"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the OT nurse should confirm the part preparation done or not and confirm blood/sugar levels arranged previously.  Why B wrong: The patient should be NPO/fasting, not having eaten.  Why C wrong: Ambulation to OT is not the confirmation item.  Why D wrong: Sedation before consent is contraindicated."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"In other intra-operative nursing care, the nurse should position the client correctly to:","options":["Maintain sterility only","Position the client in correct alignment and drape for surgery, maintaining aseptic environment, applying monitoring devices as needed, inserting a urinary catheter if needed, and maintaining complete documentation","Restrict all monitoring","Avoid catheterization in every case"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text lists maintaining sterility of the client, maintaining aseptic environment, positioning the client in correct alignment & drape for surgery, applying monitoring devices as needed, inserting a urinary catheter if needed, and maintaining complete documentation.  Why A wrong: It is more than sterility alone.  Why C/D wrong: These contradict the listed nursing care."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"Intra-operative nursing care also includes supporting the client during anaesthesia introduction. The surgeon's role on the operating team is to act as the:","options":["Director of the operating team","Anaesthesia provider","Circulating nurse","Scrub assistant only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the surgeon serves as the director of the operating team and is responsible for the preoperative diagnosis of the patient, performing the operation, and providing the patient with postoperative surgical care/treatment.  Why B wrong: That is the anaesthesiologist's role.  Why C/D wrong: The surgeon directs the team rather than serving as circulating nurse or scrub assistant."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"The surgeon's responsibilities on the operating team include all of the following EXCEPT:","options":["Preoperative diagnosis of the patient","Performing the operation","Providing the patient with postoperative surgical care/treatment","Administering the general anaesthesia"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The surgeon is responsible for preoperative diagnosis, performing the operation, and postoperative surgical care; administering general anaesthesia is the anaesthesiologist's role.  Why A/B/C wrong: All three are explicitly stated surgeon responsibilities."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"Where is the surgeon usually positioned during the operation, and who supports them?","options":["The surgeon is looked upon as the leader of the surgical team; surgeons lead a team of other doctors and nurses to make sure that a procedure goes smoothly","Standing at the foot of the bed alone","In the recovery room","Outside the operating room"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the surgeon is looked upon as the leader of the surgical team; the surgeon leads a team of doctors and nurses to make sure the procedure goes smoothly.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not describe the surgeon's leadership position during surgery."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"The Assistant Role in the operating team is described as:","options":["A resistant doctor who works closely with the surgeon in the operating room during the surgical procedure, aiding/assisting the surgeon in performing a safe and efficient operation","The director of the operating team","The anaesthesia provider","The circulating administrator only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the assistant is a resident doctor who works closely with the surgeon in the operating room during the surgical procedure, aiding/assisting in performing a safe and efficient operation, such as selecting equipment, holding open incisions/clamps, halting bleeding, closing the incision, and performing other technical tasks.  Why B wrong: The surgeon is the director.  Why C wrong: The anaesthesiologist provides anaesthesia.  Why D wrong: The assistant does more than circulating administration."},{"section":"Batch 032 · Anaesthesia history & pioneers, local anaesthetics…","question":"The Anaesthesiologist Role is best described as:","options":["A physician specifically trained in the art and science of anaesthesiology, who assesses the patient before surgery","A nurse who only documents vitals","The director of the surgical team","The assistant who closes the incision"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the anaesthesiologist is a physician specifically trained in the art and science of anaesthesiology and assesses the patient before surgery.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe other personnel/roles, not the anaesthesiologist."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"Which member of the perioperative team selects and administers anesthesia, intubates the patient if necessary, and monitors BP, pulse, respirations, ECG, and SpO₂ throughout the surgical procedure?","options":["Scrub nurse","Circulating nurse","Anesthetist","Registered Nurse First Assistant"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The anesthetist selects/administers anesthesia, intubates the patient if necessary, manages technical problems of the anesthetic agent, supervises the patient condition throughout the procedure, and monitors vital parameters (BP, pulse, respirations, ECG, SpO₂, saturation level).  Why A wrong: The scrub nurse handles instruments at the sterile field, not anesthesia.  Why B wrong: The circulating nurse manages the non-sterile field and supplies, not anesthesia.  Why D wrong: The RNFA assists the surgeon but does not administer anesthesia."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"The OT-in-Charge nurse is responsible for which of the following duties?","options":["Passing instruments to the surgeon at the sterile field","Management and supervision of sterilization services","Administering anesthesia to the patient","Documenting nursing care given in the recovery room"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The OT-in-Charge duties include management and supervision of sterilization services, preparation of the theatre for operations and organization of the national team in theatre, and direct participation in surgical procedures.  Why A wrong: Passing instruments at the sterile field is the scrub nurse's role.  Why B is the keyed answer; Why C wrong: Anesthesia is the anesthetist's role.  Why D wrong: Recovery-room documentation is a circulating-nurse/recovery-nurse function, not the defining OT-in-Charge duty listed."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"Which statement correctly distinguishes the scrub nurse from the circulating nurse?","options":["The scrub nurse remains outside the sterile field while the circulating nurse is scrubbed","The scrub nurse is a specially trained nurse working with surgeons within the sterile field; the circulating nurse remains in the unsterile field","Both nurses work only within the sterile field","Both nurses work only within the unsterile field"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The scrub nurse is a specially trained nurse who works with surgeons within the medical team in the operating room; the circulating nurse remains in the unsterile field of the operating room.  Why A wrong: It reverses the roles.  Why C wrong: Only the scrub nurse is in the sterile field.  Why D wrong: Only the circulating nurse is in the unsterile field."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"During the surgical procedure, the scrub nurse performs the surgical scrub and dons sterile gown and gloves. This means the scrub nurse is which of the following?","options":["Touching only non-sterile equipment","Following established specific tasks, scrubbing, and wearing appropriate PPE; working only in the sterile field","Free to leave the sterile field at any time without re-scrubbing","Responsible for managing supplies outside the operating area"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The scrub nurse is actually \"scrubbed in\" for surgery, meaning the scrub nurse follows established specific tasks, scrubbing, and wearing appropriate PPE, and works only in the sterile field, performing surgical procedures for the surgeon.  Why A wrong: The scrub nurse handles sterile instruments, not non-sterile equipment.  Why C wrong: Leaving and re-entering the sterile field freely would break sterility.  Why D wrong: Managing supplies outside the operating area is the circulating nurse's job."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"Which task is a responsibility of the scrub nurse during surgery?","options":["Documenting care and counting sponges, instruments, and needles with the circulating nurse","Dealing with the management of unsterile situations in the operating area","Assisting the anesthesia team during positioning","Transferring the patient to the operating table"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The scrub nurse works directly with the surgeon at the sterile field, passing instruments, sponges, and other items needed during the procedure, and prepares and counts the instruments, sponges, and needles needed in the operation.  Why B wrong: Managing unsterile situations is the circulating nurse's responsibility.  Why C wrong: Assisting the anesthesia team during positioning is a circulating nurse duty.  Why D wrong: Transferring the patient to the operating table is a circulating nurse function."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"Regarding the surgical instrument, sponge, and needle count, when must the scrub nurse perform the counts?","options":["Only at the start of the operation","Only at the end of the operation","Before, during, and after surgery","Counts are not required if a checklist is used"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The scrub nurse counts instruments before, during, and after surgery to ensure no item is retained in the patient.  Why A wrong: A single start count cannot detect items retained at closure.  Why B wrong: A single end count cannot establish the baseline.  Why D wrong: A checklist does not eliminate the need for physical counts."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"When assembling the drapes for surgery, the scrub nurse should start with which drape?","options":["The towel clips","The Mayo stand cover","The drape that will go over the patient's body","The patient education leaflet"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: When assembling drapes according to use, the scrub nurse starts with the towel, then the towel clips, then the draw sheet, and then lap sheet — assembling in order so the body drape is placed appropriately.  Why A wrong: The towel clips are added in sequence, not first.  Why B wrong: The Mayo stand cover is part of equipment draping, not the start of the patient draping sequence.  Why D wrong: A patient education leaflet is not a surgical drape."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"Which action is a circulating-nurse responsibility related to surgical counts?","options":["Passing sutures to the surgeon","Opening the outer wrapper of sterile packages so the scrub nurse can access the sterile inside","Wearing sterile gown and gloves to handle instruments","Suturing the surgical incision"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The circulating nurse can open an outer wrapped package so that someone in the operating field can access the sterile inside, and assists with counting supplies.  Why A wrong: Passing sutures to the surgeon is a sterile (scrub nurse) function.  Why C wrong: The circulating nurse does not scrub in; she works in the non-sterile field.  Why D wrong: Suturing is performed by the surgeon, not the circulating nurse."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"Which of the following is a key responsibility of the circulating nurse?","options":["Management of personnel, equipment, supplies, and environment during surgical procedure","Passing instruments within the sterile field","Administering the anesthetic agent","Performing the surgical incision"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The circulating nurse manages personnel, equipment, supplies, and the environment during the surgical procedure, assesses and assists the anesthesia team, manages the flow of information and supplies, and ensures conditions in the operating room remain safe and sterile.  Why B wrong: Passing instruments in the sterile field is the scrub nurse's role.  Why C wrong: Anesthesia is administered by the anesthetist.  Why D wrong: Incisions are made by the surgeon."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"The circulating nurse must assist in the transfer of the patient and the positioning of the patient on the operating table. What is the priority concern during patient positioning?","options":["Making sure the chart is clean","Making sure the patient's airway is patent, comfortable, and aligned, exposing only the operative site","Documenting drug allergies","Counting the sponges"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: During transfer and positioning, the circulating nurse makes sure the patient is comfortable and properly aligned, exposing only the operative site while protecting privacy, dignity, and safety; checks all equipment for proper functioning (cautery machine, suction, light/table); and protects the patient by reassessment.  Why A wrong: Chart cleanliness is not a positioning priority.  Why C wrong: Allergy documentation occurs preoperatively, not as the positioning priority.  Why D wrong: Sponge counting is a separate verification task, not the positioning concern."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"A Registered Nurse First Assistant (RNFA) is best described as a nurse who:","options":["Only documents care and never enters the sterile field","Has additional education and training in surgical care and performs more responsibilities within the operating room, working directly with surgeons","Replaces the anesthetist during emergencies","Manages only postoperative recovery-room care"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The RNFA has additional education and training in surgical care, has more responsibilities within the operating room, and works directly with surgeons. The RNFA's job is to help surgeons by controlling bleeding, using instruments and medical devices, performing invasive procedures such as cutting tissue, and suturing/closing the patient when the operation has finished.  Why A wrong: The RNFA works actively within the sterile surgical field.  Why C wrong: The RNFA does not replace the anesthetist.  Why D wrong: The RNFA's defining role is intraoperative assistance, not solely recovery-room care."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"The operating room is divided into how many main areas with respect to sterility?","options":["One area — the entire room is sterile","Two areas — the sterile operating field and the non-sterile area","Three areas — sterile, semi-sterile, and clean","Four areas — sterile, clean, dirty, and disposal"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In an operating room, there are two areas: the sterile operating field and the non-sterile area.  Why A wrong: The whole room is not sterile; only the operating field is.  Why C and D wrong: The source identifies two areas, not three or four."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"Which goal is the primary reason for maintaining the sterile field during surgery?","options":["To reduce the cost of supplies","To total sterility for patient safety","To speed up the surgical procedure","To reduce paperwork"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: During the surgery, the goal is to keep the operating field totally sterile for patient safety.  Why A wrong: Cost reduction is not the purpose of sterility.  Why C wrong: Sterility is about infection prevention, not speed.  Why D wrong: Paperwork is unrelated to maintaining the sterile field."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"If a circulating nurse must communicate with the people in the sterile field, what precaution preserves sterility?","options":["The circulating nurse should enter the sterile field directly","The circulating nurse must inform the people in the sterile field without compromising sterility, talking across the room from the sterile area","The circulating nurse should remove sterile drapes","Communication is prohibited during surgery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The circulating nurse, informing/communicating with people in the operating room, must communicate without compromising sterility — by talking from the non-sterile area to the people fitted with the non-sterile zone.  Why A wrong: Entering the sterile field would contaminate it.  Why C wrong: Removing drapes breaks sterility.  Why D wrong: Communication is necessary; only the method must protect sterility."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"During surgery, the scrub nurse and circulating nurse work closely while staying on opposite sides of the sterility boundary. Which statement reflects this relationship?","options":["Both must scrub in","The scrub nurse maintains patient safety and the sterility of the operating field while acting as an assistant to the surgeon; the circulating nurse closely circulates the changing nurse throughout the procedure from the non-sterile side","Neither nurse may communicate with the other","The circulating nurse passes instruments directly"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The scrub nurse maintains patient safety and the safety of the sterile field while acting as an assistant to the surgeon, working closely with the circulating nurse throughout the procedure from across the sterility boundary.  Why A wrong: Only the scrub nurse scrubs in.  Why C wrong: They must communicate without compromising sterility.  Why D wrong: Direct instrument passing is the scrub nurse's role."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"For patient safety, the scrub nurse and circulating nurse should ideally wear which protective barrier to avoid compromising the clean conditions in the operating room?","options":["Cloth caps only","Face masks","No barrier is required","Only gloves"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For patient safety, the scrub nurse and circulating nurse usually wear face masks to keep their hair contained under surgical caps and take other measures to avoid compromising the clean conditions in the operating room.  Why A wrong: Caps alone do not cover the mouth/nose; masks are specified.  Why C wrong: A barrier (mask) is required.  Why D wrong: Gloves alone do not address respiratory droplet contamination."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"What is the primary purpose of surgical drapes?","options":["To warm the patient","To create and maintain a sterile field around the operative site by passing only microorganisms","To create and maintain a sterile field around the operative site, blocking the passage of microorganisms, particulate matter, and fluids between sterile and non-sterile areas","To replace the surgical gown"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Drapes are used to create and maintain a sterile field around the operative site by blocking the passage of microorganisms, particulate matter, and fluids between the sterile and non-sterile areas.  Why A wrong: Warming is not the purpose of surgical drapes.  Why B wrong: Drapes block, not pass, microorganisms.  Why D wrong: Drapes do not replace the surgical gown."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"How are surgical drapes created and placed?","options":["By random placement over any surface","By placement of sterile sheets and towels in a specific position to maintain sterility of surfaces on which sterile instruments and gloved hands may be placed","Only on instrument tables, never on the patient","Only by the anesthetist"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Drapes are created by placement of sterile sheets and towels in a specific position to maintain the sterility of surfaces on which sterile instruments and gloved hands may be placed; the patient and operating room bed are covered with sterile drapes in a manner that exposes the prepared site of incision and isolates the area of the surgical wound.  Why A wrong: Placement is specific, not random.  Why C wrong: Drapes cover the patient and table, and objects draped include instrument tables, basin, and Mayo stand/trays.  Why D wrong: Draping is part of the sterile preparation done by the surgical team, not solely the anesthetist."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"Which objects are typically draped during surgery in addition to the patient?","options":["The anesthesia machine only","Instrument tables, basin, and Mayo stand, trays, and some surgical equipment","The recovery-room bed","The hospital corridor"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Objects draped include instrument tables, basin, and Mayo stand, trays, and some surgical equipment.  Why A wrong: The list extends beyond the anesthesia machine to multiple surfaces.  Why C wrong: The recovery-room bed is outside the OT sterile field.  Why D wrong: Corridors are not draped."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"(PYQ) In the OTT, which is the responsibility of a scrub nurse?","options":["A shift to the patient","Given instrument to surgeon during surgery","Check patient vitals","Manage for gloves and gown"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The scrub nurse hands/gives instruments to the surgeon during surgery (this matches the keyed answer B in the source: \"Given instrument to surgeon during surgery\").  Why A wrong: Shifting/transferring the patient is a circulating-nurse function.  Why C wrong: Checking patient vitals during surgery is monitored by the anesthetist.  Why D wrong: Managing for gloves and gown (procuring supplies) is a circulating-nurse responsibility."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"(PYQ) Which of the following is a function of the scrub nurse in the operation theatre?","options":["Handling the instruments to the surgeon","Giving gown","Position to the patient","Documenting only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Handling/passing the instruments to the surgeon is the scrub nurse's function (keyed answer A in source).  Why B wrong: Giving the gown (and supplies) is a circulating-nurse task.  Why C wrong: Positioning the patient is performed by the circulating nurse.  Why D wrong: Documenting alone is not the defining scrub-nurse function."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"(PYQ) Why are patients kept NPO and fluid administered intravenously before surgery?","options":["For successful surgery","To prevent dehydration","To prevent aspiration and maintain adequate fluid and electrolyte balance","For better absorption of medication"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Patients are kept NPO and given IV fluids preoperatively to prevent aspiration (of stomach contents during anesthesia) and to maintain adequate fluid and electrolyte balance (keyed answer C).  Why A wrong: \"Successful surgery\" is vague and not the specific physiologic reason.  Why B wrong: IV fluids address fluid/electrolyte balance broadly, but the specific NPO rationale is aspiration prevention plus balance, not dehydration alone.  Why D wrong: NPO status is not for medication absorption."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"The WHO Surgical Safety Checklist is best described as:","options":["A tool used only after the patient leaves the OT","A simple tool designed to improve the safety of surgical procedures by bringing together the whole operating team to perform key safety checks during vital phases of perioperative care","A document prepared by the patient","A billing record for surgery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The WHO Surgical Safety Checklist is a simple tool designed to improve the safety of surgical procedures by bringing together the whole operating team (surgeons, anesthesia providers, and nurses) to perform key safety checks during vital phases of perioperative care — prior to induction of anesthesia, prior to skin incision, and before leaving the operative field. The checklist is prepared by WHO.  Why A wrong: The checklist spans phases before and during surgery, not just after departure.  Why C wrong: It is prepared by WHO, not the patient.  Why D wrong: It is a safety tool, not a billing record."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"The three phases of the WHO Surgical Safety Checklist are remembered by the mnemonic \"SITOSO.\" What are these three phases?","options":["Scrub In, Time Out, Scrub Out","Sign In, Time Out, Sign Out","Start, Intervene, Stop","Setup, Test, Sterilize"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The three phases are Sign In → Time Out → Sign Out, remembered by the mnemonic SITOSO (take bold words only).  Why A wrong: The phases are Sign In/Out, not Scrub In/Out.  Why C and D wrong: These do not match the WHO checklist phases."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"During which phase does the \"Sign In\" portion of the WHO checklist occur?","options":["After the patient leaves the operating room","The period before induction of anesthesia","During wound closure","Only in the recovery room"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Sign In is the period before induction of anesthesia. This phase involves a review between the nurse and anesthetist.  Why A wrong: That corresponds to the Sign Out / after-departure period, not Sign In.  Why C wrong: Wound closure is part of the Sign Out phase.  Why D wrong: Sign In occurs in the OT before anesthesia induction."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"During the Sign In phase, the team verbally reviews several items with the patient. Which of the following is reviewed if possible?","options":["Patient identity, procedure and site, consent, operative site marking, and pulse oximeter functioning","The patient's billing details","The discharge plan","Postoperative follow-up appointments"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: During Sign In, the team verbally reviews with the patient (if possible): patient identity verification; procedure and site; consent (a voluntary informed consent taken by client and relatives); whether the operative site is marked if appropriate (involving left/right distinction); and that the pulse oximeter is on the patient and functioning.  Why B wrong: Billing is not a safety check.  Why C and D wrong: Discharge planning and follow-up belong to postoperative care, not Sign In."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"During the Sign In phase, the team reviews anesthetist and checklist concerns. Which question about blood loss risk is asked?","options":["Whether the patient has had a previous blood transfusion only","Whether there is a risk of >500 ml blood loss in adults or >7 ml/kg in children, with IV access and fluids/blood available if a potentially high-risk airway is identified","The patient's favorite IV fluid","The cost of blood products"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The anesthetist and checklist review notes the risk of >500 ml blood loss (or 7 ml/kg in children); it is recommended to have at least 2 large-bore intravenous lines or a central line before surgical incision, and fluids/blood available. Where a potentially high-risk airway is identified, equipment must be available and a capable assistant physically present during induction.  Why A wrong: The check is about anticipated blood loss volume, not just transfusion history.  Why C and D wrong: Patient preference and cost are not part of the safety review."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"When does the \"Time Out\" phase of the WHO checklist occur?","options":["Before any patient assessment","The period after induction of anesthesia and before surgical incision","After the patient leaves the OT","During recovery-room handover"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Time Out is the period after induction of anesthesia and before surgical induction (incision), with the entire team present.  Why A wrong: Time Out follows anesthesia induction.  Why C wrong: After leaving the OT corresponds to the Sign Out / post-departure period.  Why D wrong: Recovery-room handover is postoperative."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"During the Time Out phase, what introductions occur among the team?","options":["No introductions are needed","Each team member (nurse, anesthetist, and surgeon) introduces himself/herself by name and role, and the team confirms patient identity, procedure, and site","Only the surgeon introduces himself","Only the patient introduces the team"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: During Time Out, each team member (nurse, anesthetist, surgeon) introduces him/herself by name and role; all team members confirm they have introduced themselves; the team confirms patient identity, site, and procedure.  Why A wrong: Introductions are a required step.  Why C wrong: All members introduce themselves, not just the surgeon.  Why D wrong: Team members introduce themselves; the patient does not."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"During Time Out, prophylactic antibiotics should be confirmed as having been given within what timeframe before incision?","options":["Within the last 60 minutes","Within the last 6 hours","Within the last 24 hours","They are not part of Time Out"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: During Time Out, the team ensures antibiotic prophylaxis was given within the last 60 minutes prior to skin incision (if not given and required, the team administers prior to incision; if more than 60 minutes, re-dose the patient). Essential imaging is also displayed/confirmed as appropriate.  Why B and C wrong: The source specifies 60 minutes, not 6 or 24 hours.  Why D wrong: Antibiotic prophylaxis confirmation is explicitly part of Time Out."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"During Time Out, the team reviews anticipated critical events. Which concerns does the team discuss?","options":["Surgeon reviews critical/unexpected steps, operative duration, and anticipated blood loss","Only the nurse's lunch break","The patient's insurance status","The next patient's name"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: When reviewing anticipated critical events during Time Out, the surgeon reviews critical/unexpected steps, operative duration, and anticipated blood loss; the anesthesia team reviews specific concerns (e.g., intention to use blood products, co-morbidities); and nurses confirm sterility (including indicator results) and discuss equipment issues/concerns.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not the surgical anticipated critical events the checklist addresses."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"When does the \"Sign Out\" phase occur?","options":["Before induction of anesthesia","The period during or immediately after wound closure but before removing the patient from the operating room, while the surgeon is still present","Only after discharge from the hospital","During the preoperative consent"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Sign Out is the period during or immediately after wound closure but before removing the patient from the operating room, whilst the surgeon is still present.  Why A wrong: That is the Sign In phase.  Why C wrong: Sign Out occurs in the OT, not after hospital discharge.  Why D wrong: Consent occurs at Sign In/preoperatively."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"During Sign Out, what does the team verbally confirm before the patient leaves the OT?","options":["Only the time of day","The name of the procedure recorded; that instrument, sponge, and needle counts are correct (or not applicable); how specimens are labelled (read aloud with patient name); any equipment problems to be addressed; and the surgeon, anesthesia professional, and nurse review key concerns for recovery and management of the patient","The patient's home address only","The cost of the surgery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: During Sign Out, the team verbally confirms the name of the procedure recorded; confirms instrument, sponge, and needle counts are correct (or not applicable) and that members do not reconcile the team; checks surgical specimens labelled correctly, read aloud with patient name; highlights equipment issues; and verbalizes plans/concerns for recovery and postoperatively, including any specific risks (surgeon, anesthesia professional, and nurse).  Why A, C, D wrong: Time, address, and cost are not Sign Out safety confirmations."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"The \"Five Steps to Safer Surgery\" using the WHO Surgical Safety Checklist for each patient, in order, are:","options":["Briefing → Sign In → Time Out → Sign Out → Sign Out","Sign In → Briefing → Time Out → Sign Out → Debrief","Time Out → Sign In → Briefing → Sign Out → Sign Out","Sign In → Sign Out → Time Out → Briefing → Debrief"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Five Steps to Safer Surgery, shown from start of list to end of list, are: Briefing → Sign In → Time Out → Sign Out → Sign Out.  Why B, C, D wrong: They do not match the source sequence, which begins with Briefing and follows the SITOSO checklist steps."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"In the WHO checklist, the \"Before Patient Leaves OT Room\" column corresponds to which phase?","options":["Sign In","Time Out","Sign Out","Briefing"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In the checklist table, \"Before Patient Leaves OT Room\" is the Sign Out phase, during which the team verbally confirms the procedure recorded, instrument/sponge/needle counts, specimen labels, equipment problems, and recovery/management plans.  Why A wrong: \"Before Induction of Anaesthesia\" is the Sign In phase.  Why B wrong: \"Before Patient Skin Incision\" is the Time Out phase.  Why D wrong: Briefing is a preliminary step, not the OT-departure column."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"(PYQ) Which step is NOT included in the WHO Surgical Safety Checklist phases?","options":["Sign In","Time Out","Briefing","Sign Out"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source PYQ keys the answer as C — \"Briefing\" is the option keyed as not included among the three checklist phases (Sign In, Time Out, Sign Out). Note discrepancy: in the \"Five Steps to Safer Surgery\" diagram, Briefing is shown as a step; however, the three core checklist phases per SITOSO are Sign In, Time Out, and Sign Out, so Briefing is the keyed exclusion.  Why A, B, D wrong: Sign In, Time Out, and Sign Out are the three formal phases of the WHO Surgical Safety Checklist."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"(PYQ) All of the following are included in the surgical safety checklist EXCEPT:","options":["Name of the surgeon and anesthetist","Confirm the patient's name and procedure","Confirm the specimen labelling for transport of the biopsy","Confirm the patient's blood group"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The source keys the answer as D — confirming the patient's blood group is not the item included; the checklist instead anticipates blood-loss risk and availability of blood/fluids rather than confirming the blood group itself.  Why A wrong: Names of the surgeon/anesthetist (team introductions) are part of Time Out.  Why B wrong: Confirming patient name and procedure is part of Sign In/Time Out.  Why C wrong: Confirming specimen labelling is part of Sign Out."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"According to the WHO 10 essential objectives for safe surgery, Objective 1 states the team will:","options":["Operate on the wrong patient to save time","Operate on the correct patient at the correct site","Skip anesthesia for minor surgery","Avoid measuring outcomes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Objective 1 is that the team will operate on the correct patient at the correct site.  Why A wrong: This is the opposite of the safety objective.  Why C and D wrong: These contradict the WHO objectives."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"Which WHO essential objective addresses preventing harm from anesthetic administration while protecting the patient from pain?","options":["Objective 1","Objective 2","Objective 9","Objective 10"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Objective 2 states the team will use methods known to prevent harm from anesthetic administration, while protecting the patient from pain.  Why A wrong: Objective 1 concerns correct patient/site.  Why C wrong: Objective 9 concerns accurately identifying all surgical specimens.  Why D wrong: Objective 10 concerns communication and exchange of critical patient information among hospitals and public health systems."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"Match the WHO essential objective to its content: which objective states the team will recognize and effectively prepare for life-threatening loss of airway or respiratory function?","options":["Objective 3","Objective 4","Objective 5","Objective 6"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Objective 4 states the team will recognize and effectively prepare for life-threatening loss of airway or respiratory function.  Why A wrong: Objective 3 concerns recognizing and effectively preparing for life-threatening loss of airway (the source lists Objective 3 for airway/respiratory recognition and Objective 4 for prevention of risk); per the keyed mapping, the recognition-and-preparation for loss of airway/respiratory function aligns with Objective 4. (Discrepancy note: the source wording for Objectives 3 and 4 overlaps; the team should both recognize and prepare for airway/respiratory loss.)  Why C wrong: Objective 5 concerns avoiding inducing an allergic or adverse drug reaction known to be a significant risk to the patient.  Why D wrong: Objective 6 concerns consistently using methods known to minimize the risk of surgical site infection."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"Which WHO essential objective focuses on preventing surgical site infection?","options":["Objective 5 — avoid inducing an allergic/adverse drug reaction","Objective 6 — consistently use methods known to minimize the risk of surgical site infection","Objective 7 — prevent inadvertent retention of sponges/instruments","Objective 8 — secure and accurately identify all surgical specimens"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Objective 6 states the team will consistently use methods known to minimize the risk of surgical site infection.  Why A wrong: Objective 5 addresses allergic/adverse drug reactions.  Why C wrong: Objective 7 addresses preventing inadvertent retention of instruments/sponges in surgical wounds.  Why D wrong: Objective 8 addresses securing and accurately identifying all surgical specimens."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"Which WHO essential objective relates to hospitals and public-health systems establishing routine surveillance of surgical capacity, volume, and results?","options":["Objective 7","Objective 8","Objective 9","Objective 10"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Objective 10 states that hospitals and public-health systems will establish routine surveillance of surgical capacity, volume, and results.  Why A wrong: Objective 7 concerns preventing inadvertent retention of instruments/sponges.  Why B wrong: Objective 8 concerns securing and accurately identifying all surgical specimens.  Why C wrong: Objective 9 concerns the team effectively communicating and exchanging critical patient information for the safe conduct of the operation."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"During which phase of the WHO checklist are surgical instrument counts reconciled?","options":["Sign In","Time Out","Sign Out phase of the WHO checklist","They are never reconciled"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: \"Also Remember\" notes that instrument counts, gauze counts, and any problem related to instruments are addressed in the Sign Out phase of the checklist.  Why A wrong: Sign In is before induction, before counts are reconciled.  Why B wrong: Time Out is before incision; final counts are reconciled at Sign Out.  Why D wrong: Counts must be reconciled to prevent retained items."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"According to the \"Also Remember\" note, what is the index finger of the non-dominant hand at risk of during operative surgery?","options":["Burns","Needle stick injuries, which are the most common injury and commonly occur in the index finger of the non-dominant hand during operative surgery","Chemical exposure","Hypothermia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note states needle stick injuries are the most common injury and commonly occur in the index finger of the non-dominant hand during operative surgery.  Why A, C, D wrong: These are not the injury type specified for the non-dominant index finger."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"Post-operative care is divided into how many stages?","options":["Two stages","Three stages — immediate, intermediate, and extended post-operative stages","Four stages","One stage only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Post-operative care is divided into three stages: immediate post-operative stage (first 1 to 4 hours after surgery), intermediate post-operative stage (4 to 24 hours after surgery), and extended post-operative stage (after 24 hours).  Why A, C, D wrong: The source specifies three stages."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"The immediate post-operative stage covers which timeframe after surgery?","options":["First 1 to 4 hours after surgery","4 to 24 hours after surgery","After 24 hours","After 7 days"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The immediate post-operative stage is the first 1 to 4 hours after surgery.  Why B wrong: 4 to 24 hours is the intermediate post-operative stage.  Why C wrong: After 24 hours is the extended post-operative stage.  Why D wrong: 7 days is not a defined stage timeframe in the source."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"The intermediate post-operative stage covers which timeframe after surgery?","options":["First 1 to 4 hours","4 to 24 hours after surgery","After 24 hours","24 to 48 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The intermediate post-operative stage is 4 to 24 hours after surgery.  Why A wrong: 1–4 hours is the immediate stage.  Why C wrong: After 24 hours is the extended stage.  Why D wrong: 24–48 hours is not the defined intermediate timeframe."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"The extended post-operative stage begins:","options":["Immediately after surgery","After 1 hour","After 24 hours","After 4 hours"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The extended post-operative stage is after 24 hours.  Why A, B, D wrong: These correspond to the immediate or intermediate stages."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"In immediate post-operative nursing care, what is the FIRST priority?","options":["Assessing vital parameters including pulse oximeter","Maintaining patent airway and proper position of the neck, slightly extended with side lateral position","Assessing the level of consciousness","Assessing urinary output"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The first priority in immediate post-operative care is to maintain a patent airway and proper position of the neck, slightly extended with side lateral position (airway is the priority).  Why A wrong: Assessing vital parameters including pulse oximeter is the second priority.  Why C wrong: Assessing level of consciousness (LOC) is the third priority.  Why D wrong: Urinary output assessment comes later in priority sequencing."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"What is the SECOND priority in immediate post-operative nursing care?","options":["Maintaining a patent airway","Assessing vital parameters including pulse oximeter","Assessing the level of consciousness (LOC)","Assessing the urinary output"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The second priority is to assess vital parameters including the pulse oximeter.  Why A wrong: Maintaining a patent airway is the first priority.  Why C wrong: Assessing LOC is the third priority.  Why D wrong: Assessing urinary output is in the third-priority cluster (later)."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"Which assessments fall under the THIRD priority of immediate post-operative nursing care?","options":["Airway maintenance only","Assessing level of consciousness (LOC), assessing for hemorrhage or bleeding, assessing urinary output, assessing tubes for patency and drainage for characteristics, and assessing potential or presence of complications","Administering anesthesia","Preparing the surgical consent"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Third priority items include assessing LOC, assessing for hemorrhage or bleeding, assessing urinary output, assessing tubes for patency and drainage for characteristics, and assessing the potential or presence of complications.  Why A wrong: Airway is the first priority, not third.  Why C wrong: Anesthesia is intraoperative, not a post-op priority.  Why D wrong: Consent is a preoperative activity."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"To maintain airway and respiration in post-operative care, in what position should the unconscious patient's head be placed?","options":["Hyperflexed forward","Head turned on the side and neck slightly extended to prevent aspiration and accumulation of mucous secretions","Flat with chin tucked to chest","Prone with face down"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: To maintain airway and respiration, position the head turned on the side and neck slightly extended to prevent aspiration and accumulation of mucous secretions.  Why A wrong: Hyperflexion can obstruct the airway.  Why C wrong: Chin tucked to chest can compromise the airway.  Why D wrong: Prone face-down position is not indicated for airway maintenance here."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"When should the artificial airway and oral cavity care be performed post-operatively?","options":["Only once on admission to recovery","Suction the artificial airway and oral cavity as needed to remove secretions","Every 8 hours regardless of need","Never, to avoid stimulation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Suction the artificial airway and oral cavity as needed to remove secretions.  Why A wrong: A single instance is insufficient; suction is performed as needed.  Why C wrong: Fixed intervals are not specified; it is needs-based.  Why D wrong: Secretion removal is necessary to maintain a patent airway."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"Post-operatively, oxygen administration via the physician's order helps maintain which parameter, and breathing/coughing should be encouraged to prevent which complication?","options":["Maintain blood glucose; prevent infection","Maintain SpO₂/saturation; encourage coughing & deep breathing to prevent atelectasis (most common respiratory post-operative complication)","Maintain temperature; prevent hypothermia","Maintain blood pressure; prevent ileus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Monitor saturation and administer oxygen according to SpO₂/saturation; encourage coughing and deep breathing to prevent atelectasis, the most common respiratory post-operative complication.  Why A, C, D wrong: These misstate the parameter (saturation) and the targeted complication (atelectasis)."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"In the immediate post-operative period, how frequently should vital signs be monitored initially?","options":["Every 30 minutes","Every 5 minutes initially in the immediate post-operative stage","Every hour","Only once on arrival"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Monitor vital signs every 5 minutes initially in the immediate post-operative stage until stabilization.  Why A, C, D wrong: The source specifies every 5 minutes initially, not every 30/60 minutes or a single check."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"After the initial immediate stage, vital signs should be checked at what interval until they return to baseline?","options":["Every 5 minutes indefinitely","Every 15 minutes","Every 2 hours","Every 12 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After the initial immediate stage, keep artificial airway in until reflexes return and check vital signs every 15 minutes until the gag reflexes return until stabilization/return to baseline.  Why A wrong: Every 5 minutes is only for the initial immediate stage.  Why C and D wrong: These intervals are too long for the early post-op monitoring period."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"When monitoring peripheral circulation, the nurse should ensure capillary refill occurs within what timeframe?","options":["Within 1 second","Within 3 seconds — capillary refill within 3 seconds indicates circulation is compromised, such as in a fracture of the extremity","Within 10 seconds","Within 30 seconds"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Monitor peripheral circulation (presence of pulses, capillary refill) within 3 seconds; refill not occurring within 3 seconds (delayed) indicates compromised circulation, such as in a fracture of the extremity.  Why A wrong: 1 second is not the source-stated threshold.  Why C and D wrong: 10 or 30 seconds are not the stated normal refill threshold."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"How should the nurse monitor for signs and symptoms of hemorrhage in the post-operative patient?","options":["Only by reviewing the chart","By assessing blood pressure, pulse rate, amount of drainage, frequent swallowing, and if any finding is noted, inform immediately to the duty doctor","By waiting until the next routine round","By taking a single reading at discharge"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Monitor for signs and symptoms of hemorrhage by assessing blood pressure, pulse rate, amount of drainage, and frequent swallowing; if any finding is noted, inform immediately to the duty doctor.  Why A wrong: Chart review alone misses real-time deterioration.  Why C and D wrong: Hemorrhage requires frequent, prompt assessment, not delayed or single readings."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"Which device is applied to the lower limbs post-operatively to prevent thrombus formation, and what is its purpose?","options":["SCD (Sequential Compression Devices) — anti-embolism stocking and anti-coagulants and early ambulation are applied to prevent thromboembolism","Cardiac monitor — to record ECG","Pulse oximeter — to measure SpO₂","Nasogastric tube — to decompress the stomach"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Apply SCD (Sequential Compression Devices); anti-embolism stocking and anti-coagulants and early ambulation are used to prevent thromboembolism.  Why B wrong: A cardiac monitor records ECG, not prevent thromboembolism mechanically.  Why C wrong: A pulse oximeter measures saturation.  Why D wrong: An NG tube decompresses the stomach."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"When assessing a surgical wound, the nurse should identify the location and which characteristics of drainage?","options":["Only the color","The amount and consistency of drainage","Only the temperature of the drainage","The pH of the drainage"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For wound care, identify the location and size of the wound, colour, amount, and consistency of drainage.  Why A wrong: Color alone is incomplete; amount and consistency are also assessed.  Why C and D wrong: Drainage temperature and pH are not the assessed characteristics listed."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"Match the type of wound drainage: thin, clear, watery, pale fluid that is clear or pale red describes which drainage?","options":["Sanguineous drainage","Serosanguineous drainage","Serous drainage — clear watery plasma","Purulent drainage"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Serous drainage is clear, watery plasma.  Why A wrong: Sanguineous drainage is bright red, indicating active bleeding.  Why B wrong: Serosanguineous drainage is pale, red, watery, a mixture of clear and red.  Why D wrong: Purulent drainage is thick, yellow, green, or brown, indicating infection."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"Bright red wound drainage that indicates active bleeding is classified as:","options":["Serous drainage","Sanguineous drainage — bright red, indicates active bleeding","Serosanguineous drainage","Purulent drainage"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Sanguineous drainage is bright red and indicates active bleeding.  Why A wrong: Serous drainage is clear, watery plasma.  Why C wrong: Serosanguineous is pale, red, watery (mix of clear and red).  Why D wrong: Purulent is thick yellow/green/brown, indicating infection."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"Wound drainage described as pale, red, watery — a mixture of clear and red — is:","options":["Serous drainage","Sanguineous drainage","Serosanguineous drainage","Purulent drainage"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Serosanguineous drainage is pale, red, watery, a mixture of clear and red.  Why A wrong: Serous is clear watery plasma.  Why B wrong: Sanguineous is bright red.  Why D wrong: Purulent is thick and colored, indicating infection."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"Thick wound drainage that is yellow, green, or brown in color and indicates infection is termed:","options":["Serous drainage","Sanguineous drainage","Serosanguineous drainage","Purulent drainage — thick, yellow, green, or brown, indicates infection"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Purulent drainage is thick, yellow, green, or brown in color and indicates infection.  Why A wrong: Serous is clear, watery plasma.  Why B wrong: Sanguineous is bright red (active bleeding).  Why C wrong: Serosanguineous is pale, red, watery."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"To maintain integrity of the surgical incision post-operatively, the nurse should instruct the patient to:","options":["Sit up in bed without support and avoid hand pressure","Sit up in bed, with how to maintain a clean and dry dressing, and protect the client if wound edge separates","Avoid any incision support during coughing","Remove the dressing as often as possible"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: To protect integrity of the surgical incision, instruct how to sit up in bed, how to maintain a clean and dry dressing, and protect the client if the wound edges separate.  Why A wrong: Hand/pillow splinting support is actually encouraged during coughing.  Why C wrong: The patient should splint the incision during coughing.  Why D wrong: Frequent dressing removal increases infection risk."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"A client had abdominal surgery in the morning. The nurse notices that there is a small amount of bloody drainage on the surgical dressing. What is the nurse's BEST action?","options":["Document this as serous drainage","Document the type and amount of drainage and note the time","Immediately remove the dressing","Apply pressure and call the surgeon"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A small amount of bloody (sanguineous/serosanguineous) drainage in the early post-operative period is expected; the nurse should document the type and amount of drainage and note the time (assessing for trends in amount and characteristics).  Why A wrong: Bloody drainage is sanguineous/serosanguineous, not serous (clear watery plasma).  Why C wrong: Removing the initial surgical dressing prematurely is not done without an order and can disrupt the wound.  Why D wrong: A small amount of drainage does not yet require pressure/notification; documentation and continued monitoring are appropriate. (Per source Recall the Concept, the correct action focuses on documenting/monitoring drainage characteristics.)"},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"When caring for drains and tubes post-operatively, the nurse should maintain patency of tubing by which method?","options":["Clamping the tubing continuously","Maintaining patency such as gravity and negative pressure","Removing the drain immediately after surgery","Keeping the drainage bag above the wound site"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Care of drains and tubes includes maintaining patency such as gravity and negative pressure, and attaching tubing to an appropriate collection container.  Why A wrong: Continuous clamping would prevent drainage and patency.  Why C wrong: Drains are not removed immediately; they remain until drainage decreases.  Why D wrong: The drainage bag should be below the wound, not above it."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"A portable wound drainage system (e.g., negative-pressure drain) should be managed how?","options":["Maintained in a non-portable fixed system only","Maintain negative pressure in a portable wound drainage system, and empty the drainage bags when half full, which should be compressed before closing port to expel accumulated drainage","Never emptied until full","Kept open to air"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Maintain negative pressure in a portable wound drainage system; empty the drainage bags when half full and re-compress before closing the port (to maintain negative pressure to expel accumulated drainage). Maintaining surgical outputs is to prevent infection.  Why A wrong: The source describes a portable wound drainage system.  Why C wrong: Bags are emptied when half full, not only when full.  Why D wrong: Negative pressure must be maintained, not left open to air."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"To meet fluid and electrolyte needs post-operatively, the nurse should:","options":["Withhold all IV therapy","Maintain IV therapy as prescribed and monitor for electrolyte imbalances","Give oral fluids immediately regardless of bowel function","Only monitor weight"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For fluids and electrolytes needs, maintain IV therapy as prescribed and monitor for electrolyte imbalances.  Why A wrong: IV therapy is maintained, not withheld.  Why C wrong: Oral fluids are given only when bowel function/peristalsis returns.  Why D wrong: Weight alone is insufficient; IV therapy and electrolyte monitoring are required."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"When addressing comfort and emotional needs post-operatively, the nurse should assess pain for which characteristics?","options":["Only its location","Presence, location, intensity, duration, precipitating factors, and effectiveness of pain therapy","Only its intensity","Only the patient's emotional state"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For comfort and emotional needs, assess presence and characteristics of pain such as location, intensity, duration, precipitating factors, and effectiveness of pain therapy.  Why A and C wrong: A full pain assessment includes more than location or intensity alone.  Why D wrong: Emotional state is addressed separately; the pain assessment includes the listed characteristics."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"When managing post-operative pain with a Patient-Controlled Analgesia (PCA) pump, the nurse should:","options":["Administer pain killers only on a fixed schedule and never teach the client","Give painkillers as prescribed, call the client by name, and instruct the client on how to use the PCA pump","Avoid all analgesics to prevent dependence","Set the pump to deliver a continuous overdose"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Comfort and emotional care includes giving painkillers as prescribed, calling the client by name, and instructing the client on how to use the PCA pump.  Why A wrong: PCA involves patient self-administration and education, not only fixed scheduling without teaching.  Why C wrong: Withholding all analgesics is inappropriate post-operatively.  Why D wrong: PCA pumps have safety limits; intentional overdose is harmful and not the protocol."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"As part of late post-operative nursing care to prevent bed sores (pressure injuries), the nurse should change the position of the patient at which interval?","options":["Every 30 minutes","Change the position at least every 2 hours to prevent bed sore","Every 12 hours","Only once per shift"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In late post-operative nursing care, change the position at least every 2 hours to prevent bed sore (pressure injury).  Why A, C, D wrong: The source specifies repositioning at least every 2 hours, not 30 minutes, 12 hours, or once per shift."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"Why is deep breathing and coughing (with incision splinting) encouraged in late post-operative care?","options":["To increase pain","To prevent atelectasis (incomplete expansion of the lungs) and pneumonia, which can occur due to diminished breath sound","To promote bleeding","To delay ambulation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Encourage deep breathing and coughing and use incentive spirometry to prevent atelectasis or hypostatic pneumonia, which auscultates the lower lobe of the lungs for diminished breath sound (which may indicate atelectasis).  Why A wrong: The goal is comfort with effective clearance, not increased pain.  Why C wrong: It does not promote bleeding.  Why D wrong: Early ambulation is also encouraged, not delayed."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"Why is early ambulation encouraged in late post-operative care?","options":["To increase risk of clots","To prevent thromboembolism, phlebitis, and paralytic ileus and venous stasis","To promote pressure injury","To delay bowel function"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Encourage early ambulation and exercise to prevent thromboembolism, phlebitis, paralytic ileus, and venous stasis.  Why A wrong: Ambulation reduces, not increases, clot risk.  Why C wrong: Ambulation and repositioning prevent pressure injuries.  Why D wrong: Ambulation promotes the return of bowel function (preventing ileus)."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"When changing a surgical dressing, what technique should the nurse maintain?","options":["Clean technique only","Surgical asepsis (aseptic technique) while changing the dressing, or when emptying tubing to prevent infection","No special technique is required","Sterile technique only for the first dressing"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Follow surgical aseptic technique while changing the dressing, or when emptying tubing, to prevent infection.  Why A wrong: Surgical asepsis (aseptic technique) is specified, more rigorous than basic clean technique.  Why C wrong: Aseptic technique is required to prevent infection.  Why D wrong: Aseptic technique applies to dressing changes and tubing handling, not only the first dressing."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"Before starting to clean a wound or change a dressing, what should the nurse do first?","options":["Remove gloves","Premedicate the patient (administer pain medication) before changing the dressing","Open all sterile supplies and leave them exposed","Document only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Before starting to change the dressing, premedicate the patient (give analgesia) so the dressing change is more comfortable.  Why A wrong: Gloves are donned, not removed, for an aseptic dressing change.  Why C wrong: Sterile supplies should be opened just before use, not left exposed.  Why D wrong: Documentation follows the procedure, not in place of premedication."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"A surgical client usually voids within how many hours after surgery, and what action does the nurse take if there is no void within this period?","options":["Within 24 hours; do nothing","Usually within 8–12 hours after surgery the client may void; if not voided, offer high fiber diet, more fluids, and exercise to prevent constipation","Within 1 hour; insert a catheter immediately","Within 48 hours; restrict all fluids"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Usually within 8–12 hours after surgery the client may void; if not voided, offer high-fiber diet, more fluids, and exercise to prevent/relieve constipation and promote elimination.  Why A wrong: 24 hours with no action is incorrect; intervention is needed sooner.  Why C wrong: A catheter is not inserted immediately at 1 hour as the first action.  Why D wrong: Fluids are encouraged, not restricted."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"Post-operatively, observing for abdominal distention is important because it may indicate which complication?","options":["Atelectasis","Paralytic ileus","Wound dehiscence","Hemorrhage"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Observe for abdominal distention, as it indicates paralytic ileus.  Why A wrong: Atelectasis is a respiratory complication assessed by diminished breath sounds.  Why C wrong: Wound dehiscence is separation of wound edges, assessed at the incision.  Why D wrong: Hemorrhage is assessed by BP, pulse, and drainage, not abdominal distention specifically."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"To prevent fluid overload or circulatory collapse in the late post-operative period, the nurse should:","options":["Increase IV rate freely","Regulate IV therapy to prevent fluid overload or circulatory collapse","Stop all IV fluids","Administer fluids only orally"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Regulate IV therapy to prevent fluid overload or circulatory collapse.  Why A wrong: Freely increasing the IV rate risks fluid overload.  Why C wrong: Abruptly stopping all IV fluids is not the stated safeguard.  Why D wrong: The point concerns regulating prescribed IV therapy, not switching to oral only."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"(SATA) Select ALL the actions that are part of late post-operative nursing care to protect the patient from harm and promote recovery.","options":["Change the position at least every 2 hours to prevent bed sore","Encourage deep breathing and coughing with incentive spirometry to prevent atelectasis and pneumonia","Encourage early ambulation and exercise to prevent thromboembolism, phlebitis, and paralytic ileus","Follow aseptic technique while changing the dressing to prevent infection","Restrict all fluids and keep the patient immobile"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Late post-operative nursing care includes repositioning at least every 2 hours (A), deep breathing/coughing with incentive spirometry (B), early ambulation/exercise (C), and aseptic dressing changes (D).  Why E wrong: Fluids are regulated/encouraged (not restricted) and early ambulation (not immobility) is encouraged; immobility and fluid restriction would increase thromboembolism, pressure injury, and constipation risk."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"(SATA) Which of the following are correct matches of wound drainage types to their descriptions? Select all that apply.","options":["Serous — clear, watery plasma","Sanguineous — bright red, indicates active bleeding","Serosanguineous — pale, red, watery; mixture of clear and red","Purulent — thick, yellow, green, or brown; indicates infection","Serous — thick, green, indicates infection"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Serous is clear watery plasma (A); sanguineous is bright red, active bleeding (B); serosanguineous is pale red watery, mix of clear and red (C); purulent is thick yellow/green/brown, indicates infection (D).  Why E wrong: Thick green drainage indicating infection is purulent, not serous; serous is clear and watery."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"(SATA) Which of the following are responsibilities of the scrub nurse? Select all that apply.","options":["Work directly with the surgeon in the sterile field, passing instruments, sponges, and items needed","Set up the sterile trolley and prepare instruments and supplies needed in the operation","Maintain sterility of the procedure and pass instruments in a decisive and positive manner while maintaining patient safety","Remain in the unsterile field managing supplies and equipment","Use sterile technique for scrubbing, gowning, and gloving"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: The scrub nurse works directly with the surgeon at the sterile field passing instruments/sponges (A), sets up the sterile trolley and prepares instruments/supplies (B), maintains sterility and passes instruments decisively while maintaining patient safety (C), and uses sterile technique for scrubbing, gowning, and gloving (E).  Why D wrong: Remaining in the unsterile field managing supplies/equipment is the circulating nurse's role, not the scrub nurse's."},{"section":"Batch 033 · perioperative team roles…","question":"(SATA) Which of the following are responsibilities of the circulating nurse? Select all that apply.","options":["Manage personnel, equipment, supplies, and environment during the surgical procedure","Assist the anesthesia team and assist in positioning/repositioning the patient","Check all equipment for proper functioning such as cautery machine, suction machine, light, and table","Collect necessary tools and equipment, and help assist nurses with setting up the theater","Scrub in and pass instruments within the sterile field"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The circulating nurse manages personnel/equipment/supplies/environment (A), assists the anesthesia team and assists in positioning/repositioning the patient (B), checks all equipment for proper functioning including the cautery machine, suction machine, light, and table (C), and collects necessary tools/equipment and helps set up the theater (D).  Why E wrong: Scrubbing in and passing instruments within the sterile field is the scrub nurse's role; the circulating nurse works in the non-sterile field."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"Atelectasis is best defined as which of the following?","options":["Inflammation and consolidation of the lung tissue","Collapse of the alveoli, often a result of airway obstruction caused by immobility, lack of deep breathing after surgery","Inadequate oxygen concentration in arterial blood","A thrombus dislodging and blocking the pulmonary artery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Atelectasis is a collapse of the alveoli; it results from airway obstruction caused by immobility, lack of deep breathing after surgery.  Why A wrong: Inflammation and consolidation of the lung describes pneumonia.  Why C wrong: Inadequate oxygen concentration in arterial blood is hypoxemia.  Why D wrong: A dislodged thrombus blocking the pulmonary artery describes pulmonary embolism."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"Which of the following is a sign/symptom of atelectasis?","options":["Bradycardia and hypertension","Diminished breath sounds on the affected side","Increased urine output","Bounding peripheral pulses"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Diminished breath sounds on the affected side (usually seen in atelectasis), dyspnea and increased respiratory rate, crackles, and elevated temperature are signs of atelectasis.  Why A wrong: Atelectasis is associated with increased respiratory rate, not specifically bradycardia/hypertension.  Why C wrong: Increased urine output is not a feature of atelectasis.  Why D wrong: Bounding pulses are not listed; respiratory signs predominate."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"A post-operative client is being managed for atelectasis. Which nursing care measures are appropriate? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Assess lungs and breath sounds","Reposition the client every 2 hours","Encourage deep breathing, coughing and use of spirometry","Provide chest physiotherapy and postural drainage","Withhold all suctioning regardless of secretions"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing care of atelectasis includes assessing lungs/breath sounds, repositioning every 2 hours, encouraging deep breathing/coughing/spirometry, chest physiotherapy and postural drainage, suction as needed, and providing adequate hydration to liquefy secretions.  Why E wrong: Suction is provided as needed to clear secretions; it is not withheld."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"Which intervention helps liquefy secretions in a client with atelectasis?","options":["Restricting all oral fluids","Providing adequate hydration","Administering antitussives","Keeping the client supine continuously"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Providing adequate hydration helps liquefy secretions in atelectasis.  Why A wrong: Restricting fluids would thicken secretions.  Why C wrong: Coughing is encouraged, not suppressed with antitussives.  Why D wrong: Repositioning, not continuous supine positioning, is recommended."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"Pneumonia is defined as which of the following?","options":["Collapse of alveoli","Inflammation and consolidation of the lung tissue","Inadequate arterial oxygen","Air embolism in the pulmonary vessels"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pneumonia is the inflammation and consolidation of the lung tissue.  Why A wrong: Collapse of alveoli is atelectasis.  Why C wrong: Inadequate arterial oxygen is hypoxemia.  Why D wrong: Air embolism is a separate vascular complication."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"Post-operative pneumonia may develop how many days post-operatively?","options":["Within the first 24 hours","3–5 days post-operatively","10–14 days post-operatively","Only after 3 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pneumonia may develop 3–5 days post-operatively due to infection, aspiration and immobility.  Why A, C, D wrong: The source specifies a 3–5 day post-operative onset."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"Which factors contribute to the development of post-operative pneumonia? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Infection","Aspiration","Immobility","Early ambulation"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Pneumonia may develop due to infection, aspiration, and immobility.  Why D wrong: Early ambulation prevents, rather than causes, pneumonia."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"Hypoxemia is defined as which of the following?","options":["Inadequate oxygen concentration in arterial blood","Excess carbon dioxide in tissues","Collapse of the alveoli","Loss of circulating blood volume"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hypoxemia is an inadequate oxygen concentration in arterial blood.  Why B wrong: Excess CO2 describes hypercapnia, not hypoxemia.  Why C wrong: Collapse of alveoli is atelectasis.  Why D wrong: Loss of circulating blood volume is hypovolemic shock."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"Which agents can suppress the respiratory centre and cause hypoxemia post-operatively?","options":["Antibiotics","Anaesthetic medicines","Antiemetics","Anticoagulants"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hypoxemia occurs due to anaesthetic medicines that may suppress the respiratory centre and cause hypoxemia.  Why A, C, D wrong: These are not identified as causing respiratory centre suppression in the source."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"What is described as an \"early sign of hypoxemia\" in an unconscious or conscious client?","options":["Decreased urine output","Restlessness","Cyanosis","Hypotension"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Restlessness is the early sign of hypoxemia in unconscious & conscious clients.  Why C wrong: Cyanosis is described as a late sign of hypoxemia.  Why A, D wrong: Not identified as the early sign of hypoxemia."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"In a client with hypoxemia, cyanosis is best characterized as:","options":["An early sign","A late sign","A sign unrelated to oxygenation","A normal post-operative finding"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Cyanosis is a late sign of hypoxemia.  Why A wrong: Restlessness is the early sign; cyanosis appears late.  Why C, D wrong: Cyanosis is a serious indicator of poor oxygenation."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"Which nursing care measures are appropriate for a hypoxemic post-operative client? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Frequently assess SpO2 level via oxygen saturation","Administer oxygen","Inform the doctor","Monitor lung sounds","Initiate general nursing care"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing care of hypoxemia includes frequently assessing SpO2 (first priority), administering oxygen, informing the doctor, monitoring lung sounds, and initiating general nursing care.  Why none wrong: All are listed nursing care measures."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"What is the first priority nursing action for a client with suspected hypoxemia?","options":["Administer oxygen","Frequently assess SpO2 level via oxygen saturation","Inform the doctor","Monitor lung sounds"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Frequently assessing SpO2 via oxygen saturation is identified as the first priority.  Why A, C, D wrong: These follow assessment of oxygen saturation."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"Pulmonary embolism in the post-operative client is best described as:","options":["Inflammation of the lung tissue","A thrombus dislodged from the operative site that becomes an embolus, blocking the pulmonary artery and disturbing blood flow to one or more lobes of the lungs","Collapse of the alveoli","Loss of arterial oxygen due to anaesthesia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In pulmonary embolism, a thrombus dislodges (usually from the operative site) and becomes an embolus; the embolus blocks the pulmonary artery and disturbs blood flow to one or more lobes of the lungs.  Why A wrong: Lung inflammation is pneumonia.  Why C wrong: Alveolar collapse is atelectasis.  Why D wrong: That describes hypoxemia from anaesthesia."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"Which are signs & symptoms of pulmonary embolism? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Sudden dyspnea","Sudden sharp chest or upper abdominal pain","Cyanosis","Tachycardia","Low blood pressure"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Pulmonary embolism presents with sudden dyspnea, sudden sharp chest or upper abdominal pain, cyanosis, tachycardia, and low blood pressure.  Why none wrong: All are listed signs and symptoms."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"What is the first priority nursing intervention for a client with a pulmonary embolism?","options":["Administer oxygen","Immediately place the client in semi-Fowler or Fowler position","Inform the doctor","Monitor vital parameters"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The first priority is to immediately place the client in semi-Fowler or Fowler position.  Why A wrong: Administering oxygen is the second priority.  Why C wrong: Informing the doctor immediately is the third priority.  Why D wrong: Monitoring vitals including oxygen saturation follows."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"In pulmonary embolism nursing care, administering oxygen is identified as the:","options":["First priority","Second priority","Third priority","Final routine measure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Administering oxygen is the second priority after positioning.  Why A wrong: Positioning in semi-Fowler/Fowler is the first priority.  Why C wrong: Informing the doctor is the third priority.  Why D wrong: It is a prioritized acute intervention."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"The mnemonic \"I COUGH\" is used post-operatively to reduce which complications?","options":["Cardiac complications","Post-operative pulmonary complications","Wound infections","Renal complications"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The I COUGH acronym is used post-operatively to reduce post-operative pulmonary complications.  Why A, C, D wrong: I COUGH targets pulmonary complications, not cardiac, wound, or renal."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"In the \"I COUGH\" bundle, the letter \"I\" stands for:","options":["Inspiration","Incentive spirometry","Immobilization","Intubation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: I = Incentive spirometry in the I COUGH acronym.  Why A, C, D wrong: The expansion is Incentive spirometry, not these terms."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"Match the I COUGH acronym components. Which set correctly lists the elements of \"I COUGH\"?","options":["Incentive spirometry, Coughing/deep breathing, Oral care, Understanding, Getting out of bed, Head of bed elevation","Inspiration, Coughing, Oxygen, Understanding, Glucose, Hydration","Incentive spirometry, Cooling, Oral care, Urination, Gait, Hydration","Intubation, Coughing, Oxygen, Ultrasound, Glucose, Heparin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: I COUGH = Incentive spirometry, Coughing/deep breathing, Oral care, Understanding (patient & staff education), Getting out of bed at least three times daily, Head of bed elevation.  Why B, C, D wrong: These do not match the source acronym expansion."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"Post-operative haemorrhage is defined as:","options":["Loss of arterial oxygen","The loss of a large amount of blood, which may be internal or external","Inflammation of the incision","Collapse of the alveoli"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Haemorrhage is the loss of the large amount of blood, internal or external.  Why A wrong: That is hypoxemia.  Why C wrong: Incision inflammation is wound infection.  Why D wrong: Alveolar collapse is atelectasis."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"Which are signs & symptoms of post-operative haemorrhage? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Restlessness","Weak & rapid pulse","Hypotension & tachycardia","Cool & clammy skin","Decreased urine output"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Haemorrhage signs include restlessness, weak & rapid pulse, hypotension & tachycardia, cool & clammy skin, and decreased urine output.  Why none wrong: All are listed haemorrhage signs and symptoms."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"What is the first priority nursing action for a client with post-operative haemorrhage?","options":["Provide pressure to the site of bleeding","Inform the doctor","Provide oxygen therapy","Begin ambulation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Providing pressure to the site of bleeding is the first priority in haemorrhage.  Why B wrong: Informing the doctor is the second priority.  Why C wrong: Oxygen therapy is given as per order, after pressure.  Why D wrong: Ambulation is inappropriate during active bleeding."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"In the nursing care of haemorrhage, which actions follow applying pressure to the bleeding site? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Inform the doctor (second priority)","Provide oxygen therapy as per order","Transfuse IV fluids, colloids and blood products as per order","Initiate general nursing care"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: After applying pressure, inform the doctor (second priority), provide oxygen therapy as per order, transfuse IV fluids/colloids/blood products as per order, and initiate general nursing care.  Why none wrong: All are listed nursing care steps."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"Hypovolemic shock is best defined as:","options":["A state of circulatory blood loss usually caused by haemorrhage","Inflammation of the lung tissue","Collapse of the alveoli","Inadequate arterial oxygen due to anaesthesia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hypovolemic shock is a large volume of circulatory blood loss, usually caused by haemorrhage.  Why B, C, D wrong: These describe pneumonia, atelectasis, and hypoxemia respectively."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"Which are signs & symptoms of hypovolemic shock? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Restlessness, weak & rapid pulse","Hypotension & tachycardia","Cool & clammy skin","Decreased urine output"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Hypovolemic shock presents with restlessness, weak & rapid pulse, hypotension & tachycardia, cool & clammy skin, and decreased urine output.  Why none wrong: All are listed signs and symptoms."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"A post-operative client in hypovolemic shock develops a low blood pressure. After elevating the foot end of the bed/foot of the client, what is the first priority?","options":["Inform the doctor","If a clot has developed then elevate the foot end of the bed or foot of the client to permit the venous blood to flow from the lower body to the heart (first priority)","Determine and treat the cause of shock","Other nursing care same as haemorrhage"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If a clot has developed, elevating the foot end of the bed/foot of the client (to permit venous blood to flow from the lower body to the heart) is the first priority.  Why A wrong: Informing the doctor is the second priority.  Why C, D wrong: These follow positioning."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"According to the \"Should Remember\" note, when managing hypovolemic shock, how should the head end be positioned relative to the foot end?","options":["Higher than the foot end","Should not attempt any higher then placing on the affected site","At the same level always","Lowered below the foot end"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Should Remember note states that for management of hypovolemic shock, foot end is elevated; head end should not attempt any higher than placing on the affected site, otherwise the client's condition will worsen.  Why A wrong: Raising the head higher worsens the client's condition.  Why C, D wrong: The note specifies foot-end elevation with the described head positioning."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"According to the source, the fluid of choice for hypovolemic shock is:","options":["NS 0.45%","NS 0.9%","Dextrose 5%","Ringer lactate only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the fluid of choice for hypovolemic shock is NS 0.9%.  Why A, C, D wrong: The source specifically names NS 0.9% as the fluid of choice."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"Thrombophlebitis is best described as:","options":["An inflammation of the vein caused by clot formation","Loss of forward bowel movement","Inflammation of the lung tissue","Collapse of alveoli"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Thrombophlebitis is an inflammation of the vein, caused by clot formation.  Why B wrong: Loss of forward bowel movement is paralytic ileus.  Why C wrong: Lung inflammation is pneumonia.  Why D wrong: Alveolar collapse is atelectasis."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"Which veins are most commonly affected by thrombophlebitis?","options":["Arm veins","Leg veins","Pulmonary veins","Hepatic veins"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Leg veins are affected most commonly in thrombophlebitis.  Why A, C, D wrong: The source identifies leg veins as most common."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"Which are signs & symptoms of thrombophlebitis? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Vein inflammation","Aching & cramping pain","Vein feels hot & cord-like & tender to touch","Elevated temperature"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Thrombophlebitis presents with vein inflammation, aching & cramping pain, the vein feeling hot & cord-like & tender to touch, and elevated temperature.  Why none wrong: All are listed signs and symptoms."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"Which nursing care measures are appropriate for a client with thrombophlebitis? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Monitor the legs for signs of inflammation, pain, tenderness and cyanosis","Elevate the extremity 30 degrees without allowing any pressure on the area","Apply SCD devices & stockings","Do not allow the client to dangle the legs","Encourage early ambulation by walking quickly"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing care includes monitoring the legs for inflammation/pain/tenderness/cyanosis, elevating the extremity 30 degrees without allowing pressure on the area, applying SCD devices & stockings, and not allowing the client to dangle the legs.  Why E wrong: Dangling the legs is to be avoided; the source does not direct rapid walking as care here."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"Paralytic ileus is best described as:","options":["Failure of appropriate forward movement of bowel contents","Inflammation of the vein","Collapse of the alveoli","Loss of arterial oxygen"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Paralytic ileus is a failure of appropriate forward movement of bowel contents.  Why B wrong: Vein inflammation is thrombophlebitis.  Why C wrong: Alveolar collapse is atelectasis.  Why D wrong: That is hypoxemia."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"Paralytic ileus may occur as a result of which of the following?","options":["Anaesthetic medications or manipulation of the bowel during surgical procedure","Early ambulation","Adequate hydration","Incentive spirometry"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Paralytic ileus may occur as a result of anaesthetic medications or manipulation of the bowel during the surgical procedure.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are preventive/post-op measures, not causes of ileus."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"Which are signs & symptoms of paralytic ileus? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Vomiting post-operatively that may contain brown colour","Abdominal distension","Absence of bowel sounds, bowel movements & flatus","Bounding peripheral pulses"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Paralytic ileus presents with vomiting post-operatively (may contain brown colour), abdominal distension, and absence of bowel sounds, bowel movements & flatus.  Why D wrong: Bounding pulses are not a feature of paralytic ileus."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"What is the non-surgical treatment of choice for paralytic ileus?","options":["Bowel resection","Bowel decompression by insertion of a nasogastric tube attached to intermittent or constant suction","Immediate laparotomy","Oral laxatives only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The non-surgical treatment of choice for paralytic ileus is bowel decompression by insertion of a nasogastric tube attached to intermittent or constant suction.  Why A, C wrong: Surgical intervention may be required only if decompression is not effective.  Why D wrong: NG decompression, not oral laxatives, is the treatment of choice."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"When is surgical intervention required in paralytic ileus?","options":["Always, as first-line treatment","If decompression is not effective","Only when bowel sounds return","Never under any circumstances"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Surgical intervention may be required in paralytic ileus if decompression is not effective.  Why A wrong: Decompression is the non-surgical treatment of choice first.  Why C wrong: Returning bowel sounds indicate resolution, not need for surgery.  Why D wrong: Surgery is required if decompression fails."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"According to the \"Also Remember\" note, post-operative complications are usually seen between how many days post-operatively?","options":["1–2 days","1–3 days","5–7 days","10–14 days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Also Remember note states post-operative complications are usually seen between 1–3 days post-operatively.  Why A, C, D wrong: The source specifies the 1–3 day window."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"According to the \"Also Remember\" note, infection, fever, atelectasis and paralytic ileus are most common complications following which event?","options":["Discharge","Surgery","Physiotherapy","Suture removal"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Infection, fever, atelectasis and paralytic ileus are the most common complications following surgery.  Why A, C, D wrong: These are not the events identified for the most common complications."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"According to the \"Also Remember\" note, paralytic ileus is the first/most common complication after which procedure?","options":["Cardiac surgery","Abdominal surgery","Orthopedic surgery","Neurosurgery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note states paralytic ileus is the first/most common complication after abdominal surgery.  Why A, C, D wrong: The source specifically associates it with abdominal surgery."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"Per the \"Also Remember\" note, bowel decompression is the first complication of fluids into the intestinal tract, related to which structure?","options":["The force of fluids that may be released","The biliary tract","The pulmonary tract","The urinary tract"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The note links bowel decompression to the first/most common complication arising from the force of fluids in the intestinal tract.  Why B, C, D wrong: These tracts are not the referenced structure."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"Per the \"Also Remember\" note, cardiac signs of paralytic ileus include which findings? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Vomiting post-operatively","Abdominal distension","Absence of bowel sounds","Bounding pulses"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The note reiterates vomiting post-operatively, abdominal distension, and absence of bowel sounds as features of paralytic ileus.  Why D wrong: Bounding pulses are not associated with paralytic ileus."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"What is the most common cause of post-operative wound infection?","options":["Poor surgical technique alone","Poor aseptic technique","Excessive hydration","Early ambulation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common cause of wound infection is poor aseptic technique.  Why A wrong: The source specifically names poor aseptic technique.  Why C, D wrong: These are not identified causes."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"In wound infection, a precipitating factor in diabetes mellitus (DM) clients is:","options":["Poor sugar control","Low protein intake","High oxygen demand","Excess fluid intake"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A precipitating factor for wound infection is poor sugar control in DM clients.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not the precipitating factor named for DM clients."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"Wound infection typically occurs how many days post-operatively?","options":["Within 24 hours","3–6 days post-operatively","10–14 days post-operatively","After 3 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Infection occurs usually 3–6 days post-operatively.  Why A, C, D wrong: The source specifies a 3–6 day onset."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"Which are signs & symptoms of wound infection? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Fever & chills and elevated WBCs","Warm, tender, painful and inflamed incision site","Oedematous skin at the insertion site & tight skin sutures","Decreased urine output"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Wound infection presents with fever & chills and elevated WBCs, a warm/tender/painful/inflamed incision site, and oedematous skin at the insertion site & tight skin sutures.  Why D wrong: Decreased urine output is not a listed sign of wound infection."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"Which nursing care measures are appropriate for wound infection? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Initiate general nursing care","Maintain aseptic technique","Give antibiotics as per order","Apply non-sterile dressings to save cost"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing care of wound infection includes initiating general nursing care, maintaining aseptic technique, and giving antibiotics as per order.  Why D wrong: Aseptic (sterile) technique must be maintained; non-sterile dressing is inappropriate."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"In the \"Health Care Associated Infections (HAI)\" recall box, a Surgical Site Infection (SSI) is defined as an infection occurring within what timeframe after surgery?","options":["Up to 7 days","Up to 30 days after surgery (or up to 1 year after surgery in patients receiving implants) and affecting either the incision or deep tissue at the operation site","Up to 90 days only","Within the first 6 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: An SSI is an infection occurring up to 30 days after surgery (or up to 1 year after surgery in patients receiving implants) and affecting either the incision or deep tissue at the operation site.  Why A, C, D wrong: The source specifies the 30-day / 1-year-with-implant timeframe."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"Per the HAI recall box, in 18% of cases of SSIs in community hospitals, the most common causative organism is:","options":["Escherichia coli","Staphylococcus aureus","Pseudomonas aeruginosa","Klebsiella pneumoniae"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Staphylococcus aureus is currently the most common cause of SSIs, accounting for 18% of cases of SSIs in community hospitals.  Why A, C, D wrong: The source names Staphylococcus aureus as the most common cause."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"Per the HAI recall box, the most common reason for hospitalisation that may render the most common cause of hospitalisation after surgery is:","options":["Surgical site infections","Urinary Tract Infections (UTIs)","Bloodstream infections","Pneumonia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source identifies SSIs as the most common reason for hospitalisation and complication after surgery.  Why B wrong: UTI is the most common type of HAI (next question), but the most common reason for re-hospitalisation after surgery is SSI.  Why C, D wrong: Not identified as the most common reason here."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"Per the HAI recall box, the first/most common type of HAI is a UTI, which is primarily caused by:","options":["Staphylococcus aureus","E. coli","Pseudomonas","Candida"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: UTI host infection (UTI) is the first/most common type of HAI which is primarily caused by E. coli.  Why A wrong: S. aureus is the most common cause of SSIs, not UTIs.  Why C, D wrong: E. coli is the primary cause of UTI."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"Which are risk factors common to both wound dehiscence and evisceration? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Obesity","Malnutrition","Infected wounds, smoking, excessive coughing, sneezing, vomiting and straining","Adequate nutrition and rest"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Risk factors for both wound dehiscence and evisceration include obesity, malnutrition, infected wounds, smoking, excessive coughing, sneezing, vomiting and straining.  Why D wrong: Adequate nutrition/rest are protective, not risk factors."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"Wound dehiscence is defined as:","options":["Separation of the wound edge at the suture line","Protrusion of internal organs through an incision","Collapse of the alveoli","Inflammation of the vein"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Wound dehiscence is a separation of the wound edge at the suture line.  Why B wrong: Protrusion of internal organs is evisceration.  Why C wrong: Alveolar collapse is atelectasis.  Why D wrong: Vein inflammation is thrombophlebitis."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"Wound dehiscence usually occurs how many days after surgery?","options":["1–2 days","4–5 days after surgery","10–14 days","After 3 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Wound dehiscence usually occurs 4–5 days after surgery.  Why A, C, D wrong: The source specifies a 4–5 day onset."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"Wound evisceration is defined as:","options":["Separation of the wound edge with protrusion of internal organs through an incision","Inflammation of the lung tissue","Loss of arterial oxygen","Clot formation in a vein"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Wound evisceration is a separation of the wound edge with protrusion of internal organs through an incision.  Why B, C, D wrong: These describe pneumonia, hypoxemia, and thrombophlebitis."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"Wound evisceration usually occurs how many days after surgery?","options":["1–2 days","5–7 days after surgery","14 days","21 days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Wound evisceration usually occurs 5–7 days after surgery.  Why A, C, D wrong: The source specifies a 5–7 day onset."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"Wound evisceration is best classified as:","options":["A surgical emergency","A minor finding requiring no action","An expected normal post-op event","A psychiatric emergency"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Wound evisceration is a surgical emergency.  Why B, C wrong: It requires immediate surgical intervention; it is not minor or normal.  Why D wrong: It is a surgical, not psychiatric, emergency."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"Where is wound evisceration most common, and in what additional populations may it occur? (Select all that apply.)","options":["In obese clients","In pregnant clients","In clients who had abdominal surgery","In those with poor wound healing ability"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Evisceration is most common in obese clients and may occur in pregnant clients, those who had abdominal surgery, and those with poor wound healing ability.  Why none wrong: All are listed populations."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"When dehiscence or evisceration occurs, what is the first priority nursing action?","options":["Place the client in a supine or low Fowler position with knees bent to prevent abdominal pressure on the suture line","Begin oral feeding","Ambulate the client","Apply firm traction on the protruding organs"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The first priority is to place the client in a supine or low Fowler position with knees bent to prevent abdominal pressure on the suture line.  Why B, C wrong: Feeding and ambulation are inappropriate during this emergency.  Why D wrong: Organs should be covered with sterile moistened saline, not pulled on."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"When evisceration occurs, what is the second priority nursing intervention regarding the protruding organs?","options":["Cover the wound/organs with sterile saline-moistened normal saline (second priority)","Push the organs back into the abdomen","Apply dry gauze and tape tightly","Leave the organs exposed to air"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The second priority is to cover the wound with sterile saline-moistened normal saline (second priority dressing).  Why B wrong: Organs should not be pushed back manually.  Why C wrong: Sterile saline-moistened dressing is used, not dry gauze.  Why D wrong: The organs must be covered to keep moist and protected."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"After covering the wound in evisceration, the nurse should: (Select all that apply.)","options":["Immediately inform the doctor","Be calm and stay with the client & assess all vitals","Provide adequate hydration to liquefy secretions","Apply firm pressure to push the organs in"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: After covering, the nurse should immediately inform the doctor and be calm and stay with the client while assessing all vitals.  Why C wrong: Hydration to liquefy secretions is care for atelectasis, not evisceration.  Why D wrong: Pushing the organs in is contraindicated."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"Chest physiotherapy (CPT) in the post-operative period involves which techniques?","options":["Percussion, vibration, and postural drainage","Suctioning only","Mechanical ventilation only","Chest compressions"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: CPT uses percussion, vibration, and postural drainage performed over the thorax to loosen secretions, affecting one area of the lungs and moving them into more central airways.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not the components of CPT described."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"What is the purpose of postural drainage?","options":["To affect blood supply","The use of various positions so that gravity drainage is achieved or affected lung segments","To increase intracranial pressure","To suppress coughing"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In postural drainage, various positions are used so that gravity drainage of congested or affected lung segments is achieved.  Why A, C, D wrong: These are not the purpose of postural drainage."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"Postural drainage is done for how long at a time?","options":["5 to 10 minutes","5 to 20 minutes at a time","30 to 45 minutes","1 to 2 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Postural drainage is done for 5 to 20 minutes at a time.  Why A, C, D wrong: The source specifies 5–20 minutes."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"Before postural drainage, the procedure of bronchodilators may be administered for what reason?","options":["To increase secretions","For maximum effects","To suppress breathing","To raise blood pressure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Before 15 minutes of the procedure, bronchodilators may be administered for maximum effects.  Why A, C, D wrong: These are not the stated reasons."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"During postural drainage, how many positions are used, and which position is best for maximum effect?","options":["A total of 12 positions are used; prone position used for maximum effects","5 positions; supine for maximum effect","3 positions; sitting for maximum effect","8 positions; lateral for maximum effect"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: During postural drainage a total of 12 positions are used, and the prone position is used for maximum effects.  Why B, C, D wrong: The source specifies 12 positions and the prone position for maximum effect."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"Which of the following are contraindications to chest physiotherapy (CPT)? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Increased ICP","Unstable vital signs","Bronchospasm","History of pathological fractures, chest incisions, and rib fractures"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Contraindications to CPT include increased ICP, unstable vital signs, bronchospasm, history of pathological fractures, chest incisions, and rib fractures.  Why none wrong: All are listed contraindications."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"When is the most preferable time to perform CPT?","options":["In the morning (or around 1 hour before meals, or 2 to 3 hours after meals)","Immediately after a heavy meal","At midnight only","Only during sleep"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most preferable time to perform CPT is in the morning, 1 hour before meals, or 2 to 3 hours after meals.  Why B wrong: Performing CPT immediately after a heavy meal risks aspiration.  Why C, D wrong: These specific times are not recommended."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"During CPT, if the client is receiving a tube feeding, the nurse should:","options":["Stop the feeding and aspirate for residual before beginning chest physiotherapy","Increase the feeding rate","Continue feeding and add bolus","Discontinue all monitoring"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: If the client is receiving a tube feeding, stop the feeding and aspirate for residual before beginning chest physiotherapy.  Why B, C wrong: Feeding should be stopped, not increased or bolused.  Why D wrong: Monitoring should continue."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"During CPT, percussion of an area should be carried out for how long?","options":["1 to 2 minutes","5 to 10 minutes","15 minutes","30 seconds"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Percuss the area for 1 to 2 minutes during CPT.  Why B, C, D wrong: The source specifies 1 to 2 minutes."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"During the vibration technique in CPT, vibration is performed while the client exhales over how many deep breaths?","options":["4 to 5 deep breaths","1 breath","10 breaths","2 breaths"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Vibrate the same area while the client exhales over 4 to 5 deep breaths.  Why B, C, D wrong: The source specifies 4 to 5 deep breaths."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"During CPT, the procedure should be stopped if which of the following occurs? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Cyanosis","Exhaustion","Maintain the position for 5 to 20 minutes after the procedure","Decreased breath sounds returning to normal"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: Stop the procedure if cyanosis or exhaustion occurs.  Why C wrong: Maintaining position after the procedure is a separate step, not a reason to stop.  Why D wrong: Improvement of breath sounds is a positive outcome, not a reason to stop."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"After CPT, the nurse should: (Select all that apply.)","options":["Maintain the position for 5 to 20 minutes after the procedure","Repeat in all necessary positions until the client no longer expectorates mucus","Dispose of sputum properly","Provide mouth care after the procedure"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: After CPT, maintain the position 5–20 minutes, repeat in all necessary positions until the client no longer expectorates mucus, dispose of sputum properly, and provide mouth care after the procedure.  Why none wrong: All are listed post-procedure nursing interventions."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"Surgical diathermy is best described as:","options":["An energy device in the operation theatre (OT) which converts electrical energy into heat energy","A device that converts heat to electrical energy","A type of mechanical ventilation","A cooling probe"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Surgical diathermy is an energy device in the OT which converts electrical energy into heat energy.  Why B wrong: It converts electrical to heat energy, not the reverse.  Why C, D wrong: It is not ventilation or a cooling probe."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"Surgical diathermy is also referred to as:","options":["Electrocautery or electrosurgery","Cryotherapy","Photocoagulation","Laparoscopy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Surgical diathermy is also referred to as electrocautery or electrosurgery.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are distinct techniques."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"Surgical diathermy uses which type of electrical current to cut tissues or cauterize small blood vessels to stop bleeding?","options":["Low-frequency DC","High-frequency A.C. (alternating current)","Static electricity","Direct microwave"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Surgical diathermy uses high-frequency A.C. electric current in surgery as either a cutting modality, or else to cauterize small blood vessels to stop bleeding.  Why A, C, D wrong: The source specifies high-frequency alternating current."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"What are the two types of surgical diathermy?","options":["Monopolar and bipolar diathermy","Static and dynamic","Open and closed","Active and passive"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Surgical diathermy is of two types: monopolar diathermy and bipolar diathermy.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not the recognized diathermy types."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"On the diathermy pencil, which button corresponds to cutting?","options":["Yellow button — for cutting (continuous wave form)","Blue button — for cutting","Red button — for cutting","Green button — for cutting"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The yellow button on the pencil is for cutting (continuous wave form).  Why B wrong: The blue button is for coagulation (intermittent wave form).  Why C, D wrong: These colours are not specified."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"On the diathermy pencil, the blue button is used for:","options":["Cutting (continuous wave form)","Coagulation (intermittent wave form)","Suction","Irrigation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The blue button on the pencil is for coagulation (intermittent wave form).  Why A wrong: The yellow button is for cutting.  Why C, D wrong: These are not pencil button functions."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"What is the function of the earth plate in surgical diathermy?","options":["Prevents electric shock to the patient","Cuts tissue directly","Supplies oxygen","Cools the device"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The earth plate prevents electric shock to the patient.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not the function of the earth plate."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"Diathermy can be used for which purposes? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Coagulation — to seal off blood vessels","Cutting — to divide tissues during bloodless surgery","Fulguration — the destructive coagulation of tissues with charring","Cooling tissue to slow metabolism"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Diathermy can be used for coagulation (sealing off blood vessels), cutting (dividing tissues during bloodless surgery), and fulguration (destructive coagulation of tissues with charring; a blended current uses both forms of diathermy activity).  Why D wrong: Diathermy applies heat, not cooling."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"Fulguration in diathermy is best defined as:","options":["Sealing off blood vessels only","The destructive coagulation of tissues with charring (a blended current using both cutting and coagulation forms)","Cooling of tissue","Suction of blood"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Fulguration is the destructive coagulation of tissues with charring; it uses a blended current with both forms of diathermy activity.  Why A wrong: Sealing blood vessels alone is coagulation.  Why C, D wrong: These are not fulguration."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"Which statement is true about monopolar diathermy?","options":["It needs separate return electrodes","Active and return electrodes are side to side","It does not require an earth plate","It is preferred near nerves in microsurgery"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Monopolar diathermy needs separate return electrodes; the electric current passes from one electrode near the tissue to be treated to the other (large) electrode which is usually placed under the patient's buttocks.  Why B wrong: Side-to-side electrodes describe bipolar diathermy.  Why C wrong: Bipolar (not monopolar) needs no earth plate.  Why D wrong: Bipolar is the safer choice near nerves/microsurgery."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"In monopolar diathermy, the electrical energy passes through which path?","options":["From one electrode through the patient's body to the patient earth plate (under the buttocks)","Between two limbs of the same probe","Through a fluid medium only","Through optical fibers"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In monopolar diathermy, electrical energy is passed via the diathermy probe and returned to the machine via the patient earth plate which is usually placed under the patient's buttocks.  Why B wrong: Current passing between two limbs of a probe describes bipolar diathermy.  Why C, D wrong: These do not describe the monopolar current path."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"What is the most common complication of monopolar diathermy inside the OT?","options":["Diathermy burns","Air embolism","Hypoxemia","Wound dehiscence"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common complication inside the OT with monopolar diathermy is diathermy burns.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not the most common diathermy complication."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"For a pacemaker patient undergoing monopolar diathermy, which precaution applies?","options":["The pacemaker must be carefully watched during monopolar diathermy, and earth plate must be far away from the pacemaker","The earth plate should be placed directly over the pacemaker","Monopolar diathermy is always preferred","No precaution is necessary"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For pacemaker patients, monopolar diathermy must be carefully watched and the earth plate must be far away from the pacemaker.  Why B wrong: Placing the earth plate over the pacemaker is unsafe.  Why C wrong: Bipolar is safer in cardiac pacemaker patients.  Why D wrong: A precaution is required."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"Which are contraindications/precaution areas for monopolar diathermy? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Finger tips","Penis (circumcision)","Thyroid and parotid surgery near nerves","Large limb amputations"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Contraindications of monopolar diathermy include finger tips, penis (circumcision), and thyroid and parotid surgery near nerves.  Why D wrong: Large limb amputation is not listed as a contraindication."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"Which statement is true about bipolar diathermy?","options":["Active and return electrodes are side to side, with both electrodes mounted on the same pen-like device","It requires a separate large earth plate under the buttocks","It is contraindicated in microsurgery","It cannot be used near nerves"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In bipolar diathermy the active and return electrodes are side to side; both electrodes are mounted on the same pen-like device, and the electric current passes through the tissue being treated. It needs no earth plate.  Why B wrong: Bipolar diathermy needs no earth plate.  Why C, D wrong: Bipolar is safe in surgeries near nerves and is used in microsurgery."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"Bipolar diathermy is considered safe in which situations? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Surgeries near nerves","Circumcision","Microsurgery","In patients with a cardiac pacemaker"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Bipolar diathermy is safe in surgeries near nerves and circumcision, microsurgery, and in patients with a cardiac pacemaker.  Why none wrong: All are listed safe situations for bipolar diathermy."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"The harmonic scalpel is best described as:","options":["A surgical instrument that (unlike electrosurgery) uses ultrasonic vibrations at a frequency of 20,000–50,000 Hz to cut and cauterize tissue","A low-frequency electrical cutting device","A laser device","A cryosurgery probe"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The harmonic scalpel is a surgical instrument that (unlike electrosurgery) uses ultrasonic vibrations at a frequency of 20,000–50,000 Hz to cut and cauterize tissue.  Why B wrong: It uses ultrasonic vibrations, not electrical current.  Why C, D wrong: It is neither a laser nor a cryosurgery probe."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"In laparoscopic surgery, gas is insufflated into the peritoneal cavity primarily to:","options":["Operate via small holes by insufflation of gas","Cool the abdominal organs","Sterilize the cavity","Provide nutrition"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Laparoscopic surgery uses insufflation of gas into the peritoneal cavity to operate via small holes.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not the purpose of insufflation in laparoscopy."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"Which gas is most commonly used for laparoscopic surgery?","options":["Nitrogen","Carbon dioxide (CO2)","Air","Helium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common gas used for laparoscopic surgery is carbon dioxide (CO2). (Gases used: Carbon dioxide [most common], Nitrogen, Air.)  Why A, C wrong: Nitrogen and Air are alternatives but not the most common.  Why D wrong: Helium is not listed among the gases used."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"Why is carbon dioxide readily absorbable in laparoscopic surgery, and what does this reduce?","options":["CO2 is readily absorbable and causes less post-operative pain, reducing the incidence of fat embolism","CO2 increases the risk of fat embolism","CO2 is inert and not absorbed","CO2 causes venous embolism more often"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Carbon dioxide is readily absorbable and causes less post-operative pain, reducing the incidence of fat embolism.  Why B wrong: It reduces, not increases, fat embolism.  Why C wrong: CO2 is readily absorbable, not inert.  Why D wrong: CO2's high solubility reduces the risk of venous embolism."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"CO2 is approximately how many times more diffusible and rapidly cleared from circulation?","options":["50 times","100 times","200 times","500 times"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: CO2 is 200 times more diffusible and rapidly cleared from circulation.  Why A, B, D wrong: The source specifies 200 times."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"During laparoscopic surgery, intraperitoneal pressure is maintained at what range?","options":["6–10 mmHg","12–15 mmHg","20–25 mmHg","30–35 mmHg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Intraperitoneal pressure is maintained during laparoscopic surgery at 12–15 mmHg.  Why A, C, D wrong: The source specifies 12–15 mmHg."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"Laparoscopic pneumoperitoneum is created by which two methods?","options":["Open and Closed method","Manual and Automatic","Wet and Dry","High and Low pressure"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Laparoscopic pneumoperitoneum is created by two methods: Open and Closed method.  Why B, C, D wrong: The source names the Open and Closed methods."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"Which gas is commonly used for insufflation in laparoscopy procedures (Recall box)?","options":["Nitrogen (N2)","Oxygen (O2)","Carbon dioxide (CO2)","Helium"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Carbon dioxide (CO2) is the most commonly used gas for insufflation during laparoscopic surgery because it is colorless, inexpensive, nonflammable, and has higher blood solubility than air, which reduces the risk of complications if venous embolism occurs.  Why A, B, D wrong: The recall box answer is CO2."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"In cryosurgery, what is the mechanism used to destroy tissue?","options":["The application of extreme cold to destroy tissue","The application of high-frequency current","Ultrasonic vibration","Photocoagulation with laser"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cryosurgery is the use of extreme cold in surgery to destroy tissue.  Why B wrong: High-frequency current is diathermy.  Why C wrong: Ultrasonic vibration is the harmonic scalpel.  Why D wrong: Photocoagulation is laser surgery."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"What is the surgical application of cryoablation in cryosurgery?","options":["It is the surgical application of cryoablation","It is a vaccine","It is a form of mechanical ventilation","It is an antibiotic delivery method"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cryosurgery is thus the surgical application of cryoablation.  Why B, C, D wrong: These do not describe cryosurgery."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"Cryosurgical treatment is commonly used for which skin diseases? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Warts, moles, skin tags","Solar keratoses, molluscum","Morton's neuroma and small skin cancers","Internal organ malignancies only"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Cryosurgical treatment is commonly used for skin diseases e.g. warts, moles, skin tags, solar keratoses, molluscum, Morton's neuroma and small skin cancers.  Why D wrong: It is used for skin lesions, not exclusively internal organ malignancies."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"Which cryogen used for cryosurgery is described as boiling point liquid nitrogen, the most commonly used and extremely low temperature?","options":["Liquid nitrogen (N2) at –196°C (–321°F)","Nitrous oxide (N2O) at –89°C","Carbon dioxide (CO2) at –41°C","Helium at 0°C"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Liquid nitrogen (N2) is the most commonly used cryogen, with extremely low temperature of –196°C (–321°F).  Why B wrong: Nitrous oxide (N2O, also known as laughing gas) is used at temperature –89°C.  Why C wrong: Carbon dioxide (CO2) is used at –41°C temperature.  Why D wrong: Helium at 0°C is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"Which cryogen used for cryosurgery is also known as laughing gas and used at temperature –89°C?","options":["Liquid nitrogen (N2)","Nitrous oxide (N2O)","Carbon dioxide (CO2)","Oxygen (O2)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Nitrous oxide (N2O), also known as laughing gas, is used at temperature –89°C.  Why A wrong: Liquid nitrogen is used at –196°C.  Why C wrong: CO2 is used at –41°C.  Why D wrong: Oxygen is not a cryogen here."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"In cryosurgery, carbon dioxide (CO2) is used at which temperature?","options":["–196°C","–89°C","–41°C","–321°F"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Carbon dioxide (CO2) is used at –41°C temperature in cryosurgery.  Why A wrong: –196°C is for liquid nitrogen.  Why B wrong: –89°C is for nitrous oxide.  Why D wrong: –321°F is the Fahrenheit equivalent for liquid nitrogen's –196°C."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"Laser surgery is best described as:","options":["A type of surgery that uses special light beams, instead of instruments, for surgical procedures","The application of extreme cold","A high-frequency electrical current technique","Ultrasonic cutting"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Laser surgery is a type of surgery that uses special light beams, instead of instruments, for surgical procedures.  Why B wrong: Extreme cold is cryosurgery.  Why C wrong: High-frequency current is diathermy.  Why D wrong: Ultrasonic cutting is the harmonic scalpel."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"LASER is an acronym that stands for:","options":["Light Amplification by the Stimulated Emission of Radiation","Light Absorption from Stimulated Energy Release","Linear Application of Selective Electrical Resonance","Localized Acoustic Sound Emission Range"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: LASER stands for \"Light Amplification by the Stimulated Emission of Radiation.\"  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not the correct expansion of LASER."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"What is the mechanism by which a laser affects tissue in laser surgery?","options":["The laser uses photons to excite the chromophore molecule and generate kinetic energy that is released as heat, which causes protein denaturation and coagulation necrosis","It freezes tissue rapidly","It vibrates tissue mechanically","It applies high-voltage electricity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Lasers use photons to excite the chromophore molecule and generate kinetic energy that is released as heat, which causes protein denaturation and coagulation necrosis.  Why B wrong: Freezing is cryosurgery.  Why C wrong: Mechanical vibration is the harmonic scalpel.  Why D wrong: High-voltage electricity is diathermy."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"Which advantages/effects are true of laser surgery? (Select all that apply.)","options":["The effect occurs without much collateral damage to surrounding tissue","The laser effect can be enhanced by photosensitizing agents","The most common lasers in use are the argon, carbon dioxide and Nd:YAG lasers","It always causes extensive collateral tissue damage"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The laser effect occurs without much collateral damage to surrounding tissue, can be enhanced by photosensitizing agents, and the most common lasers in use are the argon, carbon dioxide and Nd:YAG lasers.  Why D wrong: Laser surgery causes minimal collateral damage, not extensive damage."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"Robotic surgery is best described as:","options":["A method to perform surgery using versatile tools attached to a robotic arm","A method that uses extreme cold","A laser-based technique only","Manual open surgery without instruments"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Robotic surgery is a method to perform surgery using versatile tools attached to a robotic arm.  Why B wrong: Extreme cold is cryosurgery.  Why C wrong: Robotic surgery is not solely laser-based.  Why D wrong: It is a very advanced surgical technique, not manual open surgery."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"In robotic surgery, the surgeon controls the robotic arm using which device?","options":["A computer","A foot pedal only","A laser pointer","Voice commands only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The surgeon controls the robotic arm with a computer; this is a very advanced surgical technique.  Why B, C, D wrong: The source specifies a computer."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"Durant's maneuver is the management for which complication, which is more common in laparoscopic surgery?","options":["Air embolism or gas embolism","Wound dehiscence","Paralytic ileus","Pneumonia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Air embolism or gas embolism is more common in laparoscopic surgeries; its management is Durant's maneuver.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not managed by Durant's maneuver."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"Durant's maneuver consists of placing the patient in which position?","options":["The left lateral decubitus position with head-low position (Trendelenburg)","Prone position","High Fowler position","Supine flat position"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Durant's maneuver consists of placing the patient in the left lateral decubitus position with head-low position (Trendelenburg).  Why B, C, D wrong: The source specifies the left lateral decubitus, head-low (Trendelenburg) position."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"What is the rationale for the position used in Durant's maneuver for air/gas embolism?","options":["To prevent the air/gas embolism from lodging in the lungs, so the air will rise and stay in the right heart until it slowly absorbs","To increase the speed of embolism circulation","To promote diuresis","To facilitate coughing"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The position prevents the air embolism from lodging in the lungs; the air will rise and stay in the right heart until it slowly absorbs.  Why B wrong: It slows/traps the embolus, not speeds circulation.  Why C, D wrong: These are not the rationale for Durant's maneuver."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"A nurse is monitoring a post-operative client who suddenly develops sudden dyspnea, sharp chest pain, cyanosis, tachycardia, and low blood pressure. What complication should the nurse suspect, and what is the priority action?","options":["Pulmonary embolism; immediately place the client in semi-Fowler or Fowler position","Atelectasis; encourage incentive spirometry","Wound infection; give antibiotics","Paralytic ileus; insert a nasogastric tube"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Sudden dyspnea, sharp chest/upper abdominal pain, cyanosis, tachycardia, and low BP are signs of pulmonary embolism; the first priority is to immediately place the client in semi-Fowler or Fowler position.  Why B wrong: Atelectasis presents with diminished breath sounds, not the acute embolic picture.  Why C wrong: Wound infection presents with local incision signs and fever.  Why D wrong: Paralytic ileus presents with abdominal distension and absent bowel sounds."},{"section":"Batch 034 · Post-operative complications…","question":"A diabetic post-operative client develops a warm, tender, inflamed incision site with fever and elevated WBCs on the 4th post-operative day. Which complication is most likely, and what precipitating factor applies?","options":["Wound infection; poor sugar control in DM clients with poor aseptic technique as most common cause","Pulmonary embolism; clot dislodgement","Hypovolemic shock; blood loss","Atelectasis; immobility"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Warm/tender/inflamed incision with fever and elevated WBCs at 3–6 days post-op indicates wound infection; the most common cause is poor aseptic technique, and a precipitating factor is poor sugar control in DM clients.  Why B, C, D wrong: These complications do not match the local incision signs of infection."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"A hospital acquired infection (HAI) is best defined as an infection that:","options":["Is present and incubating at the time of admission","Develops during a hospital stay that was not present or incubating at the time of admission, or becomes evident after discharge","Occurs only in immunocompromised patients","Is always caused by fungal organisms"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: An HAI develops during hospital stay and was not present or incubating at admission, or becomes evident after discharge.  Why A wrong: Infections present/incubating at admission are community-acquired, not HAI.  Why C wrong: HAIs can affect any patient, not solely the immunocompromised.  Why D wrong: HAIs are mostly bacterial; fungal causes are only one subset."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"A hospital acquired infection is also referred to as a:","options":["Nosocomial / hospital acquired infection","Iatrogenic-only infection","Endogenous infection exclusively","Latent infection"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: HAI is also referred to as a nosocomial or hospital acquired infection.  Why B wrong: Iatrogenic refers to treatment-caused harm; not the synonym used here.  Why C wrong: HAIs may be endogenous or exogenous, not endogenous only.  Why D wrong: \"Latent\" is not a synonym for HAI."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"According to the source, an infection occurring more than how many hours (how many days) after admission or becoming evident within how many hours after discharge is classified as a hospital acquired infection?","options":[">24 hours after admission, within 24 hours after discharge",">48 hours (2 days) after admission, within 48 hours after discharge",">72 hours after admission, within 72 hours after discharge",">12 hours after admission, within 12 hours after discharge"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The book defines HAI as occurring >48 hours (2 days) after admission or evident within 48 hours after discharge.  Why A, C, D wrong: They use incorrect time thresholds; the defining cut-off is 48 hours."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"Which of the following is a risk factor for healthcare associated infection (HCAI)? (Select all that apply)","options":["Low resistance of patients to infections","Contact with infectious persons","Invasive procedures / interventions","Inappropriate antimicrobial usage","Use of single-use sterile disposable equipment"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Low patient resistance, contact with infectious persons, invasive procedures/interventions, inappropriate antimicrobial usage, drug resistance of endemic microbes, and a contaminated environment are all listed risk factors.  Why E wrong: Proper use of single-use sterile equipment reduces infection risk and is not a risk factor."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"Which of the following are listed as risk factors for HCAI? (Select all that apply)","options":["Drug resistance of endemic microbes","Contaminated environment","Adequate hand hygiene compliance","Inappropriate antimicrobial usage","High nurse-to-patient ratios with strict asepsis"],"correct":"A,B,D","rationale":"Why correct: Drug resistance of endemic microbes, contaminated environment, and inappropriate antimicrobial usage are all listed HCAI risk factors.  Why C wrong: Adequate hand hygiene is protective, not a risk factor.  Why E wrong: Strict asepsis and good staffing reduce risk; not listed as a risk factor."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"The classic \"Chain of Infection\" includes a central element surrounded by six links. Which element is at the center of the chain shown in the source?","options":["Reservoir","Chain of infection (hub) linking the six components","Portal of exit","Susceptible host"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram shows \"Chain of Infection\" at the center, surrounded by infectious agent, reservoir, portal of exit, mode of transmission, portal of entry, and susceptible host.  Why A, C, D wrong: These are individual links around the hub, not the central element."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"Place the links of the chain of infection in the correct order beginning with the source. Which sequence is correct?","options":["Infectious agent → Reservoir → Portal of exit → Mode of transmission → Portal of entry → Susceptible host","Susceptible host → Reservoir → Portal of entry → Infectious agent → Mode of transmission → Portal of exit","Mode of transmission → Infectious agent → Portal of exit → Reservoir → Susceptible host → Portal of entry","Portal of entry → Portal of exit → Reservoir → Infectious agent → Susceptible host → Mode of transmission"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The chain flows infectious agent → reservoir → portal of exit → mode of transmission → portal of entry → susceptible host.  Why B, C, D wrong: They scramble the established order of the chain of infection."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"Which organisms are listed as common causes of HCAI spread by exogenous (direct contact) route? (Select all that apply)","options":["Staphylococcus aureus from direct contact, e.g., MRSA","Gram-negative bacilli (e.g., E. coli, Pseudomonas, Klebsiella, Salmonella) from solutions, fluids, and invasive devices","Lactobacillus from normal flora only","Candida as the most common cause of all HCAIs"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: M/C organisms for spread of exogenous HCAI include Staphylococcus aureus from direct contact (e.g., MRSA) and Gram-negative bacilli such as E. coli, Pseudomonas, Klebsiella, and Salmonella from solutions/fluids/invasive devices.  Why C wrong: Lactobacillus is not listed as a common exogenous HCAI organism.  Why D wrong: Candida is the endogenous fungal organism, not the most common cause of all HCAIs."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"Which is the most common (M/C) fungal organism responsible for EXOGENOUS HCAI?","options":["Candida","Aspergillus species","Cryptococcus","Histoplasma"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Aspergillus species is the M/C fungal organism responsible for exogenous HCAI.  Why A wrong: Candida is the endogenous fungal organism.  Why C, D wrong: Not identified as the common exogenous fungal cause in the source."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"Which is the most common (M/C) fungal organism responsible for ENDOGENOUS HCAI?","options":["Aspergillus","Candida","Mucor","Penicillium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Candida is the M/C fungal organism responsible for endogenous HCAI.  Why A wrong: Aspergillus is the exogenous fungal organism.  Why C, D wrong: Not listed as the endogenous fungal cause."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"Which type of HAI is the MOST COMMON?","options":["Catheter Associated UTI (CAUTI)","Surgical Site Infection (SSI)","Ventilator Associated Pneumonia (VAP)","Catheter Related Blood Stream Infection (CRBI)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Catheter Associated UTI is listed as the most common HAI.  Why B wrong: SSI is the second most common but not the most common.  Why C, D wrong: VAP and CRBI are types of HAI but not the most common."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"Which type of HAI is the SECOND MOST COMMON?","options":["CAUTI","Surgical Site Infection (SSI)","VAP","CRBI"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Surgical Site Infection (SSI) is the second most common type of HAI.  Why A wrong: CAUTI is the most common, not second.  Why C, D wrong: VAP and CRBI are not ranked second."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"CAUTI is an infection of the urinary tract including the urethra, bladder, ureters, and kidney after how many hours of urinary catheterization?","options":["24 hours","48 hours","72 hours","12 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: CAUTI is defined as a urinary tract infection occurring after 48 hours of urinary catheterization.  Why A, C, D wrong: They use incorrect time thresholds; the criterion is 48 hours."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"A nurse is teaching about CAUTI. Which organism is the most common cause of CAUTI?","options":["Escherichia coli (E. coli)","Staphylococcus aureus","Pseudomonas aeruginosa","Candida albicans"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common cause of CAUTI is Escherichia coli (E. coli).  Why B, C, D wrong: These can cause UTIs but are not the most common cause of CAUTI per the source."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"What is the most important risk factor for developing a catheter-associated UTI (CAUTI)?","options":["Prolonged use of the urinary catheter","Single straight catheterization","Adequate hydration","Use of a closed drainage system"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most important risk factor for developing a catheter-associated UTI is prolonged use of the urinary catheter.  Why B wrong: Intermittent straight catheterization carries lower risk than indwelling prolonged use.  Why C, D wrong: Adequate hydration and a closed drainage system are protective, not risk factors."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"According to the source, placement of an indwelling catheter should be performed only when indicated because it:","options":["Should be removed as soon as possible and is associated with risk","Always prevents infection","Has no relation to infection risk","Is required for every hospitalized patient"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The catheter should be placed only when indicated and removed as soon as possible to reduce infection risk.  Why B, C wrong: Indwelling catheters increase, not prevent, infection risk.  Why D wrong: Catheterization is indicated only for specific reasons, not universally."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"The CDC definition of CAUTI requires that a patient must meet how many of the listed criteria for a diagnosis?","options":["All three criteria","Any one criterion","At least two criteria","Four criteria"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The CDC definition of CAUTI requires the patient meet all three of the listed criteria.  Why B, C, D wrong: The source specifies all three criteria, not one, two, or four."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"Per the CDC criteria for CAUTI, the indwelling urinary catheter must have been in place for at least:","options":[">2 calendar days (48 hours) on the day of infection","6 hours","12 hours","24 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: CDC criterion 1 states the catheter must be in place >48 hours (2 days) on the day of infection.  Why B, C, D wrong: They are shorter than the required 48-hour (2-day) threshold."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"Which clinical symptoms satisfy a CDC criterion for CAUTI diagnosis? (Select all that apply)","options":["Fever","Suprapubic tenderness","Frequency, urgency, and dysuria","Bradycardia","Hypothermia"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: CDC criterion 2 lists at least one clinical symptom: fever, suprapubic tenderness, frequency, urgency, and dysuria.  Why D, E wrong: Bradycardia and hypothermia are not among the listed CAUTI symptoms."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"Per the CDC criteria, the presence of bacteria in urine in significant quantity to diagnose CAUTI is defined as:","options":["More than 10^5 CFU/ml","More than 10^2 CFU/ml","More than 10^3 CFU/ml","More than 10 CFU/ml"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: CDC criterion 3 specifies presence of bacteria in significant quantity, i.e., more than 10^5 CFU/ml.  Why B, C, D wrong: These counts are below the significant-bacteriuria threshold of 10^5 CFU/ml."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"Which of the following are accepted indications for urinary catheterization? (Select all that apply)","options":["Patient requiring prolonged immobilization (e.g., unstable lumbar/thoracic spine injuries; multiple traumatic injuries including pelvic fracture and femur fracture)","Short-term management of urinary incontinence (inability to control urination)","For retention of urine (inability to pass urine)","To measure urine output over several days in critically ill patients","Routine convenience of nursing staff for a continent patient"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Accepted indications include prolonged immobilization, short-term management of urinary incontinence, retention of urine, measuring urine output in critically ill patients, treatment of bladder outlet obstruction, and post-operative management of impaired bladder function.  Why E wrong: The source states the catheter should NOT be used solely for the convenience of personnel/staff."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"Which of the following are accepted indications for urinary catheterization per the source? (Select all that apply)","options":["To measure urine output over several days in critically ill patients","For treatment of bladder outlet obstruction","For post-operative management of surgical patients with impaired bladder function","To avoid early ambulation of a stable patient"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Measuring urine output in critically ill patients, treatment of bladder outlet obstruction, and post-operative management of impaired bladder function are accepted indications.  Why D wrong: Avoiding ambulation is not an accepted indication; convenience is explicitly discouraged."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"A nurse is reviewing measures to prevent catheter-associated UTIs. Which statements are correct? (Select all that apply)","options":["Use catheter whenever still needed and remove it as soon as it is no longer required","Urinary catheters should not be used solely for the convenience of patient-care personnel","If a convenience-only condom catheter or supra-pubic catheter is appropriate, it may be used","Catheters should be inserted using aseptic technique and sterile equipment","Bladder ultrasound protocol should be used to avoid unnecessary catheterizations"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: All are listed preventive recommendations — limit/remove catheter promptly, avoid use for staff convenience, consider condom/suprapubic catheters when appropriate, use aseptic insertion with sterile equipment, and use bladder ultrasound protocol to avoid unnecessary catheterization.  (No incorrect options — SATA with all correct.)"},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"Which catheter-care nursing measures help prevent CAUTI? (Select all that apply)","options":["Perform hand hygiene immediately before and after any manipulation of the catheter site/apparatus","Keep the catheter system closed and not opened unless absolutely necessary for diagnostic/therapeutic reasons","Routinely irrigate the bladder to prevent infection","Maintain unobstructed flow and keep the catheter/collecting tube free from kinking","Rest the collecting bag on the floor for support"],"correct":"A,B,D","rationale":"Why correct: Hand hygiene before/after catheter manipulation, keeping the system closed unless absolutely necessary, and maintaining unobstructed kink-free flow are all recommended.  Why C wrong: The source states bladder irrigation is not recommended (irrigation only when necessary for therapeutic reasons such as obstruction).  Why E wrong: The source says \"Do not rest the collecting bag on the floor.\""},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"A nurse is collecting a urine specimen for analysis from an indwelling catheter. According to the source, what is the correct technique?","options":["Disconnect the catheter from the bag and pour urine from the open end","Collect a small amount of urine after disinfecting the urine-sampling port and aspirating with a sterile needle and syringe","Collect urine directly from the drainage bag for routine analysis","Squeeze urine back up the tubing into the bladder"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For a small specimen, collect after disinfecting the sampling port and aspirate with a sterile needle and syringe; if a large volume is needed it is obtained aseptically from the drainage bag.  Why A wrong: Disconnecting the closed system risks contamination and is discouraged.  Why C wrong: Drainage-bag urine is used only when a large volume is needed, not for routine small samples.  Why D wrong: Refluxing urine into the bladder promotes ascending infection."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"When a large volume of urine is needed for special analysis from an indwelling catheter, it should be obtained:","options":["Aseptically from the drainage bag","By disconnecting the catheter tip","From the floor-resting collecting bag","By the mid-stream clean-catch method only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A large volume needed for special analysis should be obtained aseptically from the drainage bag.  Why B wrong: Disconnecting the catheter breaks the closed system.  Why C wrong: The bag should not rest on the floor.  Why D wrong: Mid-stream collection is used for non-catheterized patients."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"For which type of patient is the mid-stream urine collection method used?","options":["Non-catheterized patients","Patients with an indwelling catheter","Patients with a suprapubic catheter","Anuric patients"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The mid-stream urine collection method is used in non-catheterized patients.  Why B, C wrong: Catheterized patients have urine sampled from the port/bag aseptically.  Why D wrong: Anuric patients produce no urine to collect."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"To prevent ascending infection in a catheterized patient, the urine collecting bag should always be kept:","options":["Below the level of the bladder","Above the level of the bladder","At the same height as the bladder","On the floor"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Collecting bags should always be kept below the level of the bladder to prevent ascending infection.  Why B, C wrong: Keeping the bag level with or above the bladder allows urine reflux and infection.  Why D wrong: The bag should never rest on the floor."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"What is the correct formula to calculate the Catheter Associated UTI (CAUTI) Rate?","options":["(Number of CAUTI cases / total number of catheterization days) × 100","(Number of catheterization days / number of CAUTI cases) × 100","(Number of CAUTI cases / number of admissions) × 1000","(Number of catheter insertions / CAUTI cases) × 100"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: CAUTI rate = (Number of CAUTI cases / total number of catheterization days) × 100.  Why B, C, D wrong: They invert the ratio or use an incorrect denominator/multiplier."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"In the ABCDE Bladder Bundle for prevention of CAUTI, the letter \"A\" stands for:","options":["Adherence to general infection control principles","Avoidance of all catheters","Antibiotic prophylaxis","Aspiration precautions"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: \"A\" = Adherence to general infection control principles, including hand hygiene, aseptic catheter insertion, proper maintenance/education, and surveillance/feedback.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not what \"A\" represents in the bladder bundle."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"In the ABCDE Bladder Bundle, the letter \"B\" stands for:","options":["Bladder ultrasound scan protocol used to avoid unnecessary catheterizations","Bag elevation","Bacterial culture daily","Bedside cleaning"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: \"B\" = Bladder ultrasound (scan) protocol used to avoid unnecessary catheterizations.  Why B, C, D wrong: They do not match the bundle's \"B\" component."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"In the ABCDE Bladder Bundle, the letter \"C\" stands for \"Catheter alternatives should be considered.\" Which alternatives are listed? (Select all that apply)","options":["Intermittent (\"in and out\") catheterization for incomplete bladder emptying","External condom catheter for men with urinary incontinence","Absorbent pads and products for men and women with urinary incontinence","Permanent suprapubic catheter for all patients","Routine indwelling catheter for convenience"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: \"C\" lists intermittent catheterization for incomplete emptying, external condom catheters for men, and absorbent pads/products for men and women with incontinence.  Why D wrong: A permanent suprapubic catheter for all patients is not listed.  Why E wrong: Routine indwelling for convenience contradicts the bundle."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"In the ABCDE Bladder Bundle, what does the letter \"D\" emphasize?","options":["Do not use the indwelling catheter unless absolutely necessary","Daily bladder scan","Drainage bag changing","Disinfect the catheter daily"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: \"D\" = Do not use the indwelling catheter unless absolutely necessary.  Why B, C, D wrong: These do not represent the bundle's \"D\" component."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"In the ABCDE Bladder Bundle, the letter \"E\" emphasizes which action?","options":["Early removal of the catheter using a reminder or nurse-initiated (e.g., automatic \"stop orders\") removal protocol when warranted","Encourage fluids only","Empty bag hourly","Educate the family only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: \"E\" = Early removal of the catheter using a reminder or nurse-initiated (e.g., automatic \"stop orders\") removal protocol when warranted.  Why B, C, D wrong: These do not match the bundle's \"E\" component."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"The \"Should Remember\" note states that urine should be collected aseptically for culture by which method?","options":["Needle aspiration from the catheter","Disconnecting the drainage bag","Squeezing the catheter","Floor-bag sampling"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The note states urine should be collected aseptically for culture by needle aspiration from the catheter.  Why B, C, D wrong: These break asepsis or are not the recommended aseptic culture method."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"Why does the source caution against routinely collecting catheter tips and specimens from the catheter bag?","options":["They are generally unsuitable for culture because the results are hard to interpret","They give the most accurate culture results","They are required for every CAUTI diagnosis","They reduce hand hygiene needs"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Catheter tips and specimens from the urine bag are generally unsuitable for culture because the results are hard to interpret.  Why B, C, D wrong: They contradict the source's caution about these specimens."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"A Surgical Site Infection (SSI) is an infection that occurs within how many days after the operation in the part of the body where the surgery took place?","options":["Within 30 days after surgery","Within 7 days after surgery","Within 90 days after surgery","Within 24 hours after surgery"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: SSI is an infection that occurs within 30 days after the operation in the body part where surgery took place.  Why B, C, D wrong: They use incorrect time thresholds; the criterion is 30 days."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"What is the second most common type of HAI?","options":["Surgical site infection (SSI)","CAUTI","VAP","CRBI"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: SSI break is the second most common type of HAI.  Why B wrong: CAUTI is the most common.  Why C, D wrong: VAP and CRBI are not ranked second."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"Which organism is the most common cause of surgical site infection (SSI)?","options":["Staphylococcus aureus or MRSA","Escherichia coli","Pseudomonas aeruginosa","Candida albicans"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common organism causing SSI is Staphylococcus aureus or MRSA.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not the most common SSI organisms per the source."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"What is the correct formula to calculate the SSI Rate?","options":["(Number of SSI cases / Number of surgeries) × 100","(Number of surgeries / Number of SSI cases) × 100","(Number of SSI cases / Number of admissions) × 1000","(Number of SSI cases / Number of catheter days) × 100"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: SSI rate = (Number of SSI cases / Number of surgeries) × 100.  Why B, C, D wrong: They invert the ratio or use the wrong denominator/multiplier."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"A nurse is implementing the SSI Bundle care. Which interventions are part of the SSI bundle? (Select all that apply)","options":["Normothermia before/during/after surgery","Glycemic control","Patient bathing with chlorhexidine prior to surgery","Skin preparation with a hair clipper","Antibiotic prophylaxis 1 hr prior to surgery, with a second shot if surgery lasts more than 4 hours"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: All are SSI bundle measures — normothermia, glycemic control, chlorhexidine bathing pre-op, hair-clipper skin prep, antibiotic prophylaxis 1 hr before surgery (second dose if >4 hours), aseptic wound dressing, and patient education on post-discharge wound care.  (No incorrect options — SATA with all correct.)"},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"Per the SSI bundle, surgical hair removal should be performed using a:","options":["Hair clipper","Razor blade for a close shave","Depilatory cream only","No removal regardless of site"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Skin preparation should use a hair clipper, which minimizes microabrasions compared with razors.  Why B wrong: Razor shaving causes microabrasions and increases SSI risk.  Why C wrong: Depilatory cream is not the bundle-recommended method.  Why D wrong: Skin prep including hair management is part of the bundle."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"Per the SSI bundle, antibiotic prophylaxis should be given how long prior to surgery, and when is a second dose given?","options":["1 hour prior to surgery, with a second shot if surgery lasts more than 4 hours","6 hours prior, second dose after 2 hours","At skin closure only","24 hours prior, no repeat dose"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Antibiotic prophylaxis is given 1 hr prior to surgery, with a second shot if surgery exceeds 4 hours.  Why B, C, D wrong: They state incorrect timing or omit the >4-hour second-dose rule."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"A superficial incisional SSI involves which tissue and occurs within what time after the procedure?","options":["Skin or subcutaneous tissue of the incision, within 30 days of the procedure","Deep fascial and muscle layers, within 90 days","Any organ/space, within 1 year","Bone, within 6 months"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Superficial incisional SSI involves skin or subcutaneous tissue of the incision and occurs within 30 days of the procedure.  Why B wrong: That describes deep incisional SSI.  Why C, D wrong: Those describe organ/space involvement and incorrect time frames."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"Which findings can establish a diagnosis of superficial incisional SSI? (Select all that apply)","options":["Purulent drainage from the superficial incision","Organisms isolated from an aseptically obtained culture of fluid or tissue from the superficial incision","Superficial incision with culture-positive fluid and at least one sign/symptom (pain or tenderness, localized swelling, redness, or heat)","Diagnosis of a superficial incisional SSI made by the surgeon or attending physician","Spontaneous dehiscence of deep muscle layers"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: A superficial incisional SSI is diagnosed by purulent drainage, organisms from aseptic culture of superficial fluid/tissue, a culture-positive superficial incision with at least one local sign/symptom, or surgeon/physician diagnosis.  Why E wrong: Dehiscence of deep muscle layers indicates deep incisional SSI, not superficial."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"A deep incisional SSI occurs within how many days of the operative procedure (where day 1 is the procedure date) and involves which tissues?","options":["Within 90 days, involving deep soft tissues (fascial and muscle layers)","Within 30 days, involving only skin","Within 1 year, involving subcutaneous tissue only","Within 7 days, involving any organ"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Deep SSI occurs within 90 days of the operative procedure and involves deep soft tissues — fascial and muscle layers.  Why B wrong: 30 days/skin describes superficial SSI.  Why C, D wrong: They use incorrect time frames and tissue depths."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"Which findings establish a diagnosis of deep incisional SSI? (Select all that apply)","options":["Purulent drainage from the deep incision","A deep incision that spontaneously dehisces or is deliberately opened when culture-positive or not cultured, in a patient with fever (>38°C / >38.5°C), localized pain, or tenderness","An abscess in the deep tissue","Diagnosis of a deep incisional SSI by a surgeon or attending physician","Purulent drainage limited to subcutaneous tissue only"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Deep SSI is diagnosed by purulent drainage from the deep incision; spontaneous dehiscence/deliberate opening when culture-positive or not cultured with fever (>38°C / >38.5°C), localized pain or tenderness; an abscess; or surgeon/physician diagnosis.  Why E wrong: Drainage limited to subcutaneous tissue indicates superficial, not deep, SSI."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"A patient is undergoing surgical infection prevention preparation. Which infection-control measures are recommended? (Select all that apply)","options":["If infections are present, treat them before the operative procedure and postpone elective surgeries until infections have resolved","Part preparation should be done just before the operation, preferably using electric clippers and not a razor blade","Adequately control blood glucose levels in all diabetic patients","Use an antiseptic agent for skin preparation","Skip antiseptic preparation if the patient appears clean"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Pre-op infection prevention includes treating/postponing for active infections, clipping (not shaving) just before surgery, controlling blood glucose in diabetics, and antiseptic skin preparation in concentric circles.  Why E wrong: Antiseptic skin preparation is required, not skipped."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"During surgical skin preparation, antiseptic should be applied in which pattern?","options":["In concentric circles moving toward the periphery","In random strokes","From periphery toward the center","Only at the incision line"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Apply preoperative antiseptic skin preparation in concentric circles, moving toward the periphery.  Why B, C, D wrong: They contaminate the cleaner central area or fail to cover the field appropriately."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"A patient is scheduled for surgery and the order is to administer prophylactic antibiotic within 60 minutes before incision (except vancomycin, which should be given within 120 minutes before incision) following NHSN operative procedure timing. Which antibiotic should be stopped within how many hours after surgery?","options":["The antibiotic should be stopped within 24 hours after surgery","Within 6 hours after surgery","Within 72 hours after surgery","It should be continued for 7 days"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Administer the prophylactic antibiotic within 60 minutes (vancomycin within 120 minutes) before incision, and the antibiotic should be stopped within 24 hours after surgery.  Why B, C, D wrong: They state incorrect post-operative discontinuation times; the rule is within 24 hours."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"Which additional nursing measures help prevent surgical site infection? (Select all that apply)","options":["Wash hands before and after dressing changes and any contact with the surgical site","Use sterile technique to change incision dressings","Educate the patient and family regarding proper incision care, hand washing, and the need to report signs of infection","Follow sterile technique and OT guidelines during intra-operative period","Leave soiled dressings in place to avoid disturbing the wound"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: SSI-prevention nursing measures include hand washing before/after dressing changes and site contact, sterile dressing changes, patient/family education on incision care and reporting infection, and following sterile technique and OT guidelines intra-operatively.  Why E wrong: Soiled dressings should be changed using sterile technique, not left in place."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"Ventilator Associated Pneumonia (VAP) is a nosocomial pneumonia that develops more than how many hours after mechanically ventilated in ICU patients?","options":["More than 48 hours","More than 24 hours","More than 72 hours","More than 12 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: VAP is nosocomial pneumonia that develops more than 48 hours after mechanical ventilation in ICU patients.  Why B, C, D wrong: They use incorrect time thresholds; the criterion is >48 hours."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"VAP is which leading cause of death among HAI's?","options":["A leading cause of death among HAIs","The least common HAI","Never fatal","Unrelated to ventilation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: VAP is a leading cause of death among HAIs.  Why B, C, D wrong: They contradict the source's statement on VAP mortality."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"VAP is caused by the aspiration of microorganisms from which source?","options":["Colonized endotracheal and/or tracheostomy tubes and by aspiration","Sterile saline only","Intravenous fluids","Surgical incisions"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: VAP is caused by aspiration of microorganisms from colonized endotracheal/tracheostomy tubes and by aspiration.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not the stated source of VAP-causing organisms."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"Which Gram-negative bacilli are common pathogens causing VAP? (Select all that apply)","options":["Pseudomonas aeruginosa","Escherichia coli (E. coli)","Klebsiella pneumoniae","Acinetobacter species","Lactobacillus acidophilus"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Common Gram-negative bacilli causing VAP include Pseudomonas aeruginosa, E. coli, Klebsiella pneumoniae, and Acinetobacter species.  Why E wrong: Lactobacillus acidophilus is not listed as a VAP pathogen."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"Which Gram-positive coccus is a common pathogen causing VAP?","options":["Staphylococcus aureus, particularly MRSA","Streptococcus viridans","Enterococcus faecalis","Lactobacillus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Gram-positive coccus causing VAP is Staphylococcus aureus, particularly MRSA.  Why B, C, D wrong: They are not the listed Gram-positive VAP pathogen."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"Which signs and symptoms are associated with VAP? (Select all that apply)","options":["Fever, leukopenia, purulent sputum, worsening cough","Dyspnea and tachypnea","Positive microbial culture of specimen","X-ray examination showing infiltration or consolidation of the lungs","Improved oxygenation with clearing lung fields"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: VAP signs/symptoms include fever, leukopenia, purulent sputum, worsening cough, dyspnea, tachypnea, positive microbial culture, and X-ray infiltration or consolidation of the lungs.  Why E wrong: VAP worsens, not improves, oxygenation and lung findings."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"What is the correct formula to calculate the VAP Rate?","options":["(Number of VAP cases / Number of ventilator days) × 1000","(Number of ventilator days / Number of VAP cases) × 1000","(Number of VAP cases / Number of surgeries) × 100","(Number of VAP cases / Number of admissions) × 100"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: VAP rate = (Number of VAP cases / Number of ventilator days) × 1000.  Why B, C, D wrong: They invert the ratio or use the wrong denominator/multiplier."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"Which is a PRIORITY nursing care measure to prevent VAP and facilitate lung drainage/expansion?","options":["Place the patient in semi-Fowler's position","Keep the patient supine flat at all times","Place the patient in Trendelenburg position","Position the patient prone continuously"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Placing the patient in semi-Fowler's position facilitates drainage and expands the lungs, reducing aspiration/VAP risk.  Why B wrong: Supine-flat positioning increases aspiration risk.  Why C, D wrong: Trendelenburg and continuous prone positioning are not the recommended VAP-prevention positions here."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"Which nursing care measures help prevent VAP? (Select all that apply)","options":["Suction as needed","Proper hand hygiene and use of appropriate PPEs","Perform regular oral care with an antiseptic solution and general nursing care","Use a cuffed endotracheal tube with in-line or sub-glottic suctioning","Use the lowest possible head-of-bed elevation"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: VAP nursing care includes suctioning as needed, hand hygiene/PPE, oral care with antiseptic, and a cuffed endotracheal tube with in-line/sub-glottic suctioning.  Why E wrong: Head-of-bed should be elevated (semi-Fowler's), not kept low."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"When removing devices such as endotracheal, tracheostomy, or nasogastric tubes, the nurse should:","options":["Remove them as soon as they are not indicated","Keep them indefinitely to avoid reinsertion","Remove only at discharge","Replace them daily regardless of need"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Devices like endotracheal/tracheostomy/NG tubes should be removed as soon as they are not indicated to reduce VAP risk.  Why B, C, D wrong: Unnecessary retention or routine replacement increases infection/complication risk."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"To reduce VAP risk, intubation should preferably be performed using which route?","options":["Use non-invasive ventilation whenever possible; perform orotracheal rather than nasotracheal intubation unless contraindicated","Always use nasotracheal intubation","Avoid all suctioning","Use the longest possible ventilator circuit changes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Use non-invasive ventilation whenever possible and perform orotracheal rather than nasotracheal intubation unless contraindicated, and avoid unplanned extubation/re-intubation.  Why B wrong: Nasotracheal intubation carries higher infection/sinusitis risk and is not preferred.  Why C wrong: Suctioning is needed; sub-glottic suctioning is recommended.  Why D wrong: Ventilator circuits should be changed only when visibly soiled/malfunctioning, not lengthened."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"According to VAP nursing care, ventilator circuit tubing should be changed:","options":["Only when visibly soiled or malfunctioning","Every 8 hours routinely","Every hour","Never during ventilation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Change the ventilatory circuit only when visibly soiled or malfunctioning.  Why B, C wrong: Routine frequent circuit changes are not recommended and may increase risk.  Why D wrong: Circuits must be changed when soiled/malfunctioning."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"Per VAP nursing care, respiratory therapy equipment should be:","options":["Stored and disinfected properly","Reused without cleaning","Stored damp","Shared between patients without disinfection"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Respiratory therapy equipment should be stored and disinfected properly.  Why B, C, D wrong: Reuse without cleaning, damp storage, or sharing without disinfection promotes infection."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"Per VAP nursing care, healthcare workers should be educated to provide proper care for patients undergoing ventilation in order to:","options":["Prevent VAP","Shorten oral care time","Increase sedation needs","Reduce documentation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Educate healthcare workers to provide proper care for patients undergoing ventilation about VAP, to prevent VAP.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not the stated goal of staff education."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"The Surgical Apgar Score (SAS) was developed by which author/group?","options":["Atul Gawande (Gawande's 10-point surgical Apgar score)","Virginia Apgar","Florence Nightingale","Lillian Wald"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Surgical Apgar Score (SAS) is Gawande's 10-point score calculated at the end of any general surgery operation.  Why B wrong: Virginia Apgar developed the neonatal Apgar score, not the surgical one.  Why C, D wrong: They are not associated with the SAS."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"The Surgical Apgar Score (SAS) is calculated at which time point?","options":["At the end of any general surgery operation","Before induction of anesthesia","On admission to hospital","At discharge"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: SAS is calculated at the end of any general surgery operation.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not the timing specified for SAS calculation."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"Which three parameters are used to calculate the Surgical Apgar Score? (Select all that apply)","options":["Estimated blood loss","Lowest mean arterial pressure","Lowest heart rate","Highest temperature","Highest respiratory rate"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The three SAS parameters are estimated blood loss, lowest mean arterial pressure, and lowest heart rate.  Why D, E wrong: Temperature and respiratory rate are not SAS parameters."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"The Surgical Apgar Score is the sum of points from the three parameters and yields a score from:","options":["0 to 10","0 to 5","1 to 12","0 to 100"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The score is the sum of the points from each category, giving a 10-point (0–10) score.  Why B, C, D wrong: These ranges do not match the 10-point SAS."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"Per the Surgical Apgar Score table, an estimated blood loss of more than 1000 ml is assigned how many points?","options":["0 points","1 point","2 points","3 points"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In the SAS table, estimated blood loss >1000 ml = 0 points.  Why B wrong: 601–1000 ml = 1 point.  Why C wrong: 101–600 ml = 2 points.  Why D wrong: <100 ml = 3 points."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"Per the SAS table, an estimated blood loss of less than 100 ml is assigned how many points?","options":["3 points","0 points","1 point","2 points"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Estimated blood loss <100 ml = 3 points (the lowest blood loss earns the most points).  Why B wrong: >1000 ml = 0 points.  Why C wrong: 601–1000 ml = 1 point.  Why D wrong: 101–600 ml = 2 points."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"Per the SAS table, a lowest mean arterial pressure (MAP) of less than 40 mm Hg is assigned how many points?","options":["0 points","1 point","2 points","3 points"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Lowest MAP <40 mm Hg = 0 points.  Why B wrong: 40–54 mm Hg = 1 point.  Why C wrong: 55–69 mm Hg = 2 points.  Why D wrong: ≥70 mm Hg = 3 points."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"Per the SAS table, a lowest mean arterial pressure of 55–69 mm Hg is assigned how many points?","options":["2 points","0 points","1 point","3 points"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Lowest MAP 55–69 mm Hg = 2 points.  Why B wrong: <40 mm Hg = 0 points.  Why C wrong: 40–54 mm Hg = 1 point.  Why D wrong: ≥70 mm Hg = 3 points."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"Per the SAS table, a lowest mean arterial pressure of 70 mm Hg or greater (≥70 mm Hg) is assigned how many points?","options":["3 points","0 points","1 point","2 points"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Lowest MAP ≥70 mm Hg = 3 points.  Why B wrong: <40 mm Hg = 0 points.  Why C wrong: 40–54 mm Hg = 1 point.  Why D wrong: 55–69 mm Hg = 2 points."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"Per the SAS table, a lowest heart rate of more than 85 bpm is assigned how many points?","options":["0 points","1 point","2 points","4 points"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Lowest heart rate >85 bpm = 0 points.  Why B wrong: 76–85 bpm = 1 point.  Why C wrong: 66–75 bpm = 2 points.  Why D wrong: ≤55 bpm = 4 points."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"Per the SAS table, a lowest heart rate of 56–65 bpm is assigned how many points?","options":["3 points","1 point","2 points","4 points"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Lowest heart rate 56–65 bpm = 3 points.  Why B wrong: 76–85 bpm = 1 point.  Why C wrong: 66–75 bpm = 2 points.  Why D wrong: ≤55 bpm = 4 points."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"Per the SAS table, a lowest heart rate of 55 bpm or less (≤55 bpm) is assigned how many points?","options":["4 points","1 point","2 points","3 points"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Lowest heart rate ≤55 bpm = 4 points (the only parameter that reaches a 4-point value).  Why B wrong: 76–85 bpm = 1 point.  Why C wrong: 66–75 bpm = 2 points.  Why D wrong: 56–65 bpm = 3 points."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"According to the SAS result interpretation, a score of 9 or 10 (SAS 9 ↑) indicates:","options":["No additional actions required","Immediate ICU admission","Prescribe antibiotic, stress ulcer, and venous thromboembolism prophylaxis","Mandatory critical care admission"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: SAS 9–10 indicates no additional actions are required.  Why B, C, D wrong: These escalations apply to lower scores, not 9–10."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"According to the SAS result interpretation, a score of 7 or 8 (SAS 7–8) indicates the need to:","options":["Prescribe antibiotic, stress ulcer, and venous thromboembolism prophylaxis if considered beneficial and close observation required","Take no action at all","Discharge the patient immediately","Avoid all prophylaxis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: SAS 7–8 indicates prescribing antibiotic, stress ulcer, and venous thromboembolism prophylaxis if considered beneficial, with close observation.  Why B, C wrong: A score of 7–8 still warrants prophylaxis and observation.  Why D wrong: Prophylaxis may be beneficial and is recommended where indicated."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"According to the SAS result interpretation, a score of 6 or less (SAS ≤6) indicates that, in addition to the above actions, the team should:","options":["Consider critical care / ICU admission","Discharge the patient","Take no additional action","Remove all monitoring"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: SAS ≤6 indicates that in addition to the above actions, consider critical care unit (ICU) admission.  Why B, C, D wrong: A low score requires escalation of care, not discharge or reduced monitoring."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"A patient has an estimated blood loss of 50 ml, a minimum heart rate of 56 bpm, and a lowest mean arterial pressure of 67 mm Hg. Calculate the Surgical Apgar Score.","options":["8","6","10","5"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Estimated blood loss <100 ml = 3 points; minimum heart rate 56 bpm (56–65 range) = 3 points; lowest MAP 67 mm Hg (55–69 range) = 2 points; total = 3 + 3 + 2 = 8.  Why B, C, D wrong: They do not equal the correct sum of 8 points."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"A patient has an estimated blood loss of 1500 ml, a minimum heart rate of 75 bpm, and a lowest mean arterial pressure of 41 mm Hg. Calculate the Surgical Apgar Score.","options":["3","5","7","8"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Estimated blood loss 1500 ml (>1000 ml) = 0 points; minimum heart rate 75 bpm (66–75 range) = 2 points; lowest MAP 41 mm Hg (40–54 range) = 1 point; total = 0 + 2 + 1 = 3.  Why B, C, D wrong: They do not equal the correct sum of 3 points."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"Which interventions are part of \"VAP bundles Care\"? (Select all that apply)","options":["Elevation of the head of the bed 30–45 degrees","Daily \"sedation vacations\" and assessment of readiness to extubate","Stress ulcer prophylaxis","Deep venous thrombosis prophylaxis","Daily oral care with chlorhexidine"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: VAP bundle care includes head-of-bed elevation 30–45 degrees, daily sedation vacations with readiness-to-extubate assessment, stress ulcer prophylaxis, DVT prophylaxis, daily oral care with chlorhexidine, continuous sub-glottic suctioning, and HMR (heat-moisture exchanger) filter change date.  (No incorrect options — SATA with all correct.)"},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"Per the VAP bundle, to what degree should the head of the bed be elevated?","options":["30–45 degrees","0–10 degrees","60–90 degrees","15–20 degrees"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The VAP bundle specifies elevation of the head of the bed 30–45 degrees.  Why B, C, D wrong: They state incorrect head-of-bed elevation ranges."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"A Catheter Related Blood Stream Infection (CRBI) is defined as the presence of bacteria in the blood originating from a catheter after how many hours of insertion?","options":["After 48 hours of insertion","After 24 hours of insertion","After 72 hours of insertion","After 12 hours of insertion"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: CRBI is the presence of bacteria in the blood originating from a catheter after 48 hours of insertion.  Why B, C, D wrong: They use incorrect time thresholds; the criterion is 48 hours."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"According to the source, CRBI is one of the most common risk factors with which characteristics? (Select all that apply)","options":["It is one of the frequent, lethal, and costly complications of central venous catheterization","The most common risk of infection is with femoral venous catheterization due to proximity of skin organisms","It is harmless and self-limiting","It is unrelated to catheter use"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: CRBI is one of the frequent, lethal, and costly complications of central venous catheterization, and the most common risk of infection is with femoral venous catheterization due to proximity of skin organisms.  Why C wrong: CRBI can be lethal, not harmless.  Why D wrong: CRBI is directly related to catheter use."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"Which catheter site carries the most common (highest) risk of CRBI due to proximity of skin organisms?","options":["Femoral venous catheterization","Subclavian catheterization","Peripheral arm vein","Internal jugular"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common risk of infection is with femoral venous catheterization due to proximity of skin organisms.  Why B, C, D wrong: The source identifies femoral, not subclavian/peripheral/jugular, as the highest-risk site here."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"Which recommendations help prevent CRBI? (Select all that apply)","options":["Maintain sterile technique when handling and after doing care","Proper hand hygiene and over 70% alcohol for disinfection","Palpation of the insertion site should not be performed after the application of antiseptic, unless aseptic technique is maintained","Maintain aseptic technique for the insertion and care of intravascular catheters","Reuse single-use catheters without cleaning"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: CRBI-prevention recommendations include sterile technique during/after care, hand hygiene with >70% alcohol disinfection, no palpation of the insertion site after antiseptic application unless aseptic technique is maintained, and aseptic technique for insertion and care of intravascular catheters.  Why E wrong: Reusing single-use catheters without cleaning is not recommended and increases infection risk."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"A nurse is caring for a central venous catheter site. Which CRBI/CLABSI dressing-care actions are correct? (Select all that apply)","options":["Use sterile gauze or sterile, transparent, semipermeable dressing to cover the catheter site","Replace the catheter site dressing if the dressing becomes damp, loosened, or visibly soiled","Use sterile gloves (and a large sterile sheet) during catheter insertion, especially for a central venous catheter","Leave a soiled dressing in place to avoid disturbing the line","Use non-sterile materials to cover the site"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Use sterile gauze or sterile transparent semipermeable dressing, replace the dressing when damp/loosened/visibly soiled, and use sterile gloves and a large sterile sheet during insertion (especially central venous catheter).  Why D wrong: A soiled dressing must be replaced, not left.  Why E wrong: Sterile, not non-sterile, materials must cover the site."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"Per the source, dressings on short-term central venous catheter (CVC) sites should be replaced at which intervals? (Select all that apply)","options":["Every 2 days for gauze dressings","Every 7 days for transparent dressings","Monitor the catheter sites for tenderness and fever when suggested local or blood stream infection","Every 30 days regardless of type","Never replace once applied"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Replace gauze dressings every 2 days, transparent dressings every 7 days, and monitor catheter sites for tenderness and fever when local or bloodstream infection is suggested.  Why D wrong: A 30-day interval is not recommended.  Why E wrong: Dressings must be replaced at the stated intervals or when soiled."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"(Recall the NORCET – NORCET-6 Prelims-2024) A patient admitted to hospital is suffering from pain, stiffness, and fever for 3 days and is confirmed with a Hospital Acquired Infection (HAI). When should a nurse tell the patient about the HAI?","options":["On admission","After 1 day","After 2 days","At discharge"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source keys the answer as C — after 2 days, consistent with the HAI definition that an infection occurring >48 hours (2 days) after admission is hospital acquired; therefore HAI status is recognized/communicated after 2 days.  Why A wrong: On admission the infection would be community-acquired (present/incubating), not HAI.  Why B wrong: After 1 day (24 hours) does not meet the >48-hour HAI threshold.  Why D wrong: The HAI is already confirmed at 3 days; the patient should be informed once recognized (after 2 days), not delayed to discharge."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"Match the HAI to its time-related definition: A catheter-associated urinary tract infection (CAUTI) is defined as a UTI that occurs:","options":["More than 48 hours after the insertion of a urinary catheter","Within 30 days after surgery","More than 48 hours after admission to the hospital","More than 48 hours after endotracheal intubation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: CAUTI occurs more than 48 hours after the insertion of a urinary catheter.  Why B wrong: That defines SSI.  Why C wrong: That defines hospital-acquired pneumonia (HAP).  Why D wrong: That defines VAP."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"Per the summary table, a central line-associated bloodstream infection (CLABSI) is defined as one that occurs:","options":["More than 48 hours after the insertion of a central line","Within 30 days after surgery","More than 48 hours after intubation","On the day of admission"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: CLABSI occurs more than 48 hours after the insertion of a central line.  Why B wrong: That defines SSI.  Why C wrong: That defines VAP.  Why D wrong: Same-day admission infection is not a CLABSI by this definition."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"Per the summary table, a surgical site infection (SSI) is defined as one that occurs:","options":["Within 30 days after surgery","More than 48 hours after central line insertion","More than 48 hours after intubation","More than 48 hours after catheter insertion"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: SSI occurs within 30 days after surgery.  Why B wrong: That defines CLABSI.  Why C wrong: That defines VAP.  Why D wrong: That defines CAUTI."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"Per the summary table, hospital-acquired pneumonia (HAP) is defined as pneumonia that occurs:","options":["48 hours or more after admission to the hospital","Within 30 days after surgery","More than 48 hours after central line insertion","More than 48 hours after endotracheal intubation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: HAP occurs 48 hours or more after admission to the hospital.  Why B wrong: That defines SSI.  Why C wrong: That defines CLABSI.  Why D wrong: That defines VAP, which specifically follows intubation."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"Per the summary table, ventilator-associated pneumonia (VAP) is defined as pneumonia that develops:","options":["More than 48 hours after endotracheal intubation","48 hours or more after admission to the hospital","Within 30 days after surgery","More than 48 hours after urinary catheter insertion"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: VAP develops more than 48 hours after endotracheal intubation.  Why B wrong: That defines HAP (general hospital admission).  Why C wrong: That defines SSI.  Why D wrong: That defines CAUTI."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"Which prevention measures reduce Central Line-Associated Bloodstream Infections (CLABSI)? (Select all that apply)","options":["Maximal sterile barrier precautions (surgical mask, sterile gloves, cap, sterile gown, and large sterile drape)","Prepare skin with an alcohol-based chlorhexidine (rather than iodine)","Avoidance of the femoral vein for central venous access in adult patients; use of subclavian rather than jugular veins","Use of dedicated staff for central line insertion and competency training/assessment","Routine daily replacement of all central lines whether or not necessary"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: CLABSI bundle insertion measures include maximal sterile barrier precautions, alcohol-based chlorhexidine skin prep (rather than iodine), avoiding the femoral vein (subclavian preferred over jugular), dedicated/trained staff, standardized insertion packs, availability of insertion guidelines and checklists with trained observers.  Why E wrong: The source recommends daily review of central line necessity and prompt removal of unnecessary lines, not routine daily replacement."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"For CLABSI prevention, skin should be prepared using which antiseptic?","options":["Alcohol-based chlorhexidine (rather than iodine)","Plain soap and water","Povidone-iodine only","Sterile saline"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Prepare skin with an alcohol-based chlorhexidine rather than iodine for CLABSI prevention.  Why B, D wrong: Soap/water and saline are not antiseptic skin-prep agents for line insertion.  Why C wrong: The source prefers chlorhexidine over iodine."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"For central venous access in adult patients, which vein should be avoided, and which is preferred?","options":["Avoid the femoral vein; use the subclavian rather than the jugular vein","Avoid the subclavian vein; always use the femoral vein","Avoid the subclavian vein; use the jugular vein only","Use the femoral vein for all adults"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Avoidance of the femoral vein for central venous access in adults, using subclavian rather than jugular veins.  Why B, C, D wrong: They contradict the recommendation to avoid the femoral vein and prefer subclavian."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"Which CLABSI maintenance-bundle measures help prevent infection? (Select all that apply)","options":["Daily review of central line necessity, with prompt removal of unnecessary lines","Disinfection before manipulation of the line","Daily chlorhexidine washes (in ICU, patients >2 months)","Disinfect catheter hubs, ports, connectors, etc., before using the catheter","Change dressings and disinfect site with alcohol-based chlorhexidine every 5–7 days (change earlier if soiled)"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: All are CLABSI maintenance-bundle measures — daily review of line necessity and prompt removal, disinfection before line manipulation, daily chlorhexidine washes in ICU patients >2 months, disinfecting hubs/ports/connectors before use, and changing dressings/disinfecting site with alcohol-based chlorhexidine every 5–7 days (earlier if soiled).  (No incorrect options — SATA with all correct.)"},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"Per the CLABSI maintenance bundle, administration sets should be replaced within how many hours (immediately if used for blood products or lipids)?","options":["Within 96 hours","Within 24 hours","Within 12 hours","Within 7 days"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Replace administration sets within 96 hours; replace immediately if used for blood products or lipids.  Why B, C wrong: They are shorter than the 96-hour interval for routine sets.  Why D wrong: 7 days exceeds the 96-hour replacement interval."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"Per the CLABSI maintenance bundle, what is the recommended appropriate nurse-to-patient ratio in the ICU?","options":["1:2 or 1:1","1:5","1:8","1:10"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Ensure an appropriate nurse-to-patient ratio in the ICU of 1:2 or 1:1.  Why B, C, D wrong: These higher ratios exceed the recommended ICU staffing for CLABSI prevention."},{"section":"Batch 035 · Hospital Acquired Infections — overview & risk factors…","question":"Per the CLABSI maintenance bundle, daily chlorhexidine washes are recommended in the ICU for patients of which age?","options":["Patients older than 2 months (>2 months)","Patients younger than 1 month","All neonates from birth","Only patients over 65 years"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Daily chlorhexidine washes are recommended in ICU patients >2 months.  Why B, C, D wrong: They state incorrect age groups for chlorhexidine washes."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"Lumbar puncture is an invasive procedure that involves inserting a spinal needle into which space to collect cerebrospinal fluid (CSF)?","options":["Epidural space","Lumbar subarachnoid space","Subdural space","Intervertebral disc space"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Lumbar puncture involves inserting a spinal needle into the lumbar subarachnoid space to collect cerebrospinal fluid (CSF).  Why A wrong: The epidural space is used for epidural anaesthesia, not CSF collection.  Why C wrong: The subdural space is not the target for routine LP CSF withdrawal.  Why D wrong: The needle is inserted into a subarachnoid space, not the intervertebral disc."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"Lumbar puncture is also known by which other name?","options":["Spinal block","Spinal tap","Epidural tap","Myelogram"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Lumbar puncture is also known as a spinal tap.  Why A wrong: Spinal block refers to spinal anaesthesia, not the diagnostic puncture.  Why C wrong: Epidural tap is not the synonym given.  Why D wrong: Myelogram is a separate radiologic study using contrast."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"In which year was lumbar puncture first introduced, and by whom?","options":["1883 by Paul Ehrlich","1850 by Morrill Wyman","1891 by Heinrich Quincke","1925 by August Bier"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Lumbar puncture was first introduced in 1891 by the Heinrich Quincke.  Why A wrong: 1883 / Paul Ehrlich relates to the first liver biopsy, not LP.  Why B wrong: 1850 / Morrill Wyman relates to thoracocentesis.  Why D wrong: August Bier/1925 is not the source attribution given."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"A lumbar puncture is considered a confirmative diagnosis of which condition?","options":["Encephalitis","Meningitis","Hydrocephalus","Brain tumor"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Lumbar puncture is a confirmative diagnosis of meningitis (AIIMS, PGI 2020).  Why A wrong: Encephalitis is not the stated confirmatory indication.  Why C wrong: Non-communicating obstructed hydrocephalus is actually a contraindication/concern, not the confirmatory use.  Why D wrong: A brain tumor is not what LP confirms; raised ICP from a mass is a contraindication."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"The LP needle is inserted into the sub-arachnoid space for what primary purpose?","options":["To inject contrast into the disc","To withdraw the cerebrospinal fluid (CSF)","To deliver chemotherapy only","To measure intracranial mass effect"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The LP needle is inserted into the sub-arachnoid space to withdraw the cerebrospinal fluid.  Why A wrong: Contrast injection into the disc is not the LP purpose described.  Why C wrong: Although intrathecal medication is one purpose, the primary action of the needle is CSF withdrawal.  Why D wrong: LP does not directly measure mass effect."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"For infants and smaller children, the lumbar puncture needle is inserted at which level because the spinal cord ends at the level of L₄?","options":["L₁–L₂","L₂–L₃","L₃–L₄","L₄–L₅"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: For infants and smaller children, LP is done at L₄–L₅ because the spinal cord ends at the level of L₄.  Why A/B/C wrong: These higher levels risk cord injury in infants, since the cord terminates lower (at L₄) in this age group."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"For older children and adults, the lumbar puncture is performed at which level because the spinal cord ends at the level of L₂?","options":["L₃–L₄","L₁–L₂","T₁₂–L₁","L₅–S₁"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For older children and adults, LP is done at L₃–L₄ because the spinal cord ends at the cerebrospinal level of L₂.  Why B wrong: L₁–L₂ is too high and risks cord injury since the cord ends near L₂.  Why C wrong: T₁₂–L₁ is above the cord termination and unsafe.  Why D wrong: L₅–S₁ is not the level specified in the source."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"Which of the following is a purpose of performing a lumbar puncture? (Select All That Apply)","options":["For diagnostic purpose, such as in meningitis","For reducing spinal pressure","For measurement of CSF fluid","For instill medication, air, dye etc.","To permanently drain the subarachnoid space"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The stated purposes are diagnostic (e.g., meningitis), reducing spinal pressure, measuring CSF fluid, and instilling medication, air, or dye.  Why E wrong: Permanent drainage of the subarachnoid space is not a listed purpose of LP."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"Which of the following is a contraindication to lumbar puncture due to risk of brain herniation?","options":["Increased ICP, because the procedure causes a rapid decrease in ICP pressure in the CSF around the spinal cord","Mild headache","Low platelet count alone","History of migraine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Increased ICP is a contraindication because the procedure will cause a rapid decrease in ICP pressure in the CSF around the spinal cord, leading to brain herniation.  Why B wrong: A mild headache is not a listed contraindication.  Why C wrong: While coagulopathy/bleeding disorders are contraindications, \"low platelet count alone\" is not the herniation-risk reason highlighted here.  Why D wrong: Migraine history is not a contraindication to LP."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"Which of the following are contraindications to lumbar puncture? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Increased intracranial pressure (ICP)","Acute spinal cord trauma","Coagulopathy and bleeding disorder","Skin infection near or around the insertion site","Platelet count > 20,000 cells/mm³"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Listed contraindications are increased ICP, acute spinal cord trauma, coagulopathy and bleeding disorder, and skin infection near or around the insertion site. A platelet count < 20,000 cells/mm³ is a contraindication.  Why E wrong: It is a platelet count LESS than 20,000 cells/mm³ (not greater) that contraindicates LP."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"Which platelet count is identified as a contraindication for lumbar puncture?","options":["Platelet count < 20,000 cells/mm³","Platelet count > 1,50,000 cells/mm³","Platelet count < 1,00,000 cells/mm³","Platelet count > 4,50,000 cells/mm³"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A platelet count < 20,000 cells/mm³ is a contraindication to lumbar puncture due to bleeding risk.  Why B/C/D wrong: These values are not the cut-off given as a contraindication in the source."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"Which type of needle is required for a lumbar puncture, with appropriate size for adults?","options":["LP/Spinal needle, size 20–22 G for adults","16 G needle","Silverman's needle","Vim-Silverman's needle"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The required LP/Spinal needle has a needle size for adults of 20–22 G (and infant 23/25 G).  Why B wrong: A 16 G needle is used in liver biopsy, not LP.  Why C/D wrong: Silverman's and Vim-Silverman's needles are biopsy needles used in liver biopsy."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"What needle gauge is recommended for a lumbar puncture in an infant?","options":["16 G","23/25 G","20 G","14 G"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The LP/Spinal needle is 23/25 G for an infant.  Why A wrong: 16 G is the liver biopsy second choice needle.  Why C wrong: 20 G falls in the adult range, not the infant range.  Why D wrong: 14 G is not a stated LP gauge."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"Which equipment is used to check the CSF pressure during a lumbar puncture?","options":["Sphygmomanometer","Manometer","Pulse oximeter","Stopcock"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A manometer is used to check the CSF pressure during LP.  Why A wrong: A sphygmomanometer measures arterial blood pressure, not CSF pressure.  Why C wrong: A pulse oximeter measures oxygen saturation.  Why D wrong: A 3-way stopcock is part of the setup but is not the pressure-measuring device."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"Which antiseptic agent is used to clean the site before a lumbar puncture?","options":["Betadine","Spirit only","Hydrogen peroxide","Normal saline"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Betadine is used to clean the site for antiseptic purpose during LP.  Why B/C/D wrong: Spirit alone, hydrogen peroxide, and normal saline are not the antiseptic agent specified for the LP site."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"How many specimen tubes are required for collecting CSF samples during a lumbar puncture for bacterial, cytological and viral examination?","options":["1 specimen tube","2 specimen tubes","3 specimen tubes","5 specimen tubes"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: 3 specimen tubes are required for bacterial, cytological and viral examination of CSF.  Why A/B/D wrong: These numbers do not match the three tubes specified for the three different analyses."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"What concentration of local anaesthesia (lidocaine/xylocaine) is used for a lumbar puncture?","options":["1% xylocaine","2% xylocaine","5% xylocaine","10% xylocaine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Local anaesthesia of 2% xylocaine is used for lumbar puncture.  Why A/C/D wrong: These concentrations do not match the 2% stated for LP local anaesthesia."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"Why should the client's bladder be emptied before a lumbar puncture procedure?","options":["To prevent infection","As part of pre-procedure preparation for client comfort/safety","To reduce CSF protein","To prevent seizures"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In pre-procedure nursing intervention, the bladder should be emptied as part of preparing the client.  Why A wrong: Bladder emptying is not primarily an infection-prevention measure.  Why C wrong: It has no effect on CSF protein.  Why D wrong: It is not a seizure-prevention step."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"During a lumbar puncture, the client is placed in which position to facilitate the procedure?","options":["Prone position with arms extended","Side-lying (C-shaped/fetal) position OR sitting position leaning on a cardiac table","Supine with legs elevated","Standing with back arched"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The patient is placed in a left-side or C-shaped position (side-lying with knees drawn to abdomen and head flexed) so the chin touches the chest; alternatively a sitting position leaning on a cardiac table is used. The C-shaped/curved position widens the spaces between the vertebrae.  Why A wrong: Prone with extended arms does not open the intervertebral spaces.  Why C wrong: Supine with legs elevated is not the LP position.  Why D wrong: Standing with back arched is unsafe and not described."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"Why is the patient positioned in a C-shaped (curved/flexed) posture during a lumbar puncture?","options":["To increase CSF pressure","To widen the spaces between the vertebrae","To reduce bleeding risk","To prevent aspiration"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The C-shaped position is used to widen/measure the spaces between the vertebrae, easing needle insertion.  Why A wrong: It does not increase CSF pressure.  Why C wrong: Bleeding risk is addressed by coagulation status, not posture.  Why D wrong: Aspiration prevention is not the reason for the C-shaped LP posture."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"During a lumbar puncture only how much CSF should be withdrawn?","options":["Only 5–10 ml of CSF","Only 3–10 ml of CSF","20–30 ml of CSF","As much as required"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: During LP only 3–10 ml of CSF (3 ml for cell count/differentials and 1 ml for each of the other tubes) should be withdrawn — the total should not exceed more than 10 ml of CSF fluid.  Why C wrong: 20–30 ml exceeds the safe limit and risks complications.  Why D wrong: An unlimited amount is unsafe; a defined maximum applies."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"After CSF specimen collection, why must aseptic technique be maintained while withdrawing the needle?","options":["To prevent post-dural puncture headache","To prevent introducing infection","To reduce CSF leak only","To measure pressure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: It is necessary to maintain aseptic technique during the procedure to prevent introduction of infection.  Why A wrong: PDPH is addressed by needle handling and post-procedure flat positioning, not asepsis.  Why C wrong: Aseptic technique is about infection control, not the leak itself.  Why D wrong: Pressure is measured by the manometer, unrelated to asepsis."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"When withdrawing the LP needle, a pencil-tipped, non-cutting spinal needle is used. What is the main reason?","options":["To collect more CSF","To prevent post-dural puncture headache (PDPH)","To speed up the procedure","To reduce infection"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A pencil-tipped, non-cutting spinal needle (or the gauge of the puncture) is chosen to prevent post-dural puncture headache (PDPH).  Why A wrong: Needle tip design does not increase CSF yield.  Why C wrong: Speed is not the rationale.  Why D wrong: Infection control relates to asepsis, not needle tip shape."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"Post-dural puncture headache (PDPH) is described as which type of complication after lumbar puncture?","options":["Most common, long-term complication","A short-term complication","A rare but fatal complication","An infectious complication"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Post-dural puncture headache (PDPH) is the most common, and it is a long-term complication of LP.  Why B wrong: It is described as long-term, not short-term.  Why C wrong: PDPH is common, not rare/fatal.  Why D wrong: PDPH is related to CSF leakage, not infection."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"PDPH most commonly occurs due to which mechanism?","options":["Excess CSF production","Excessive removal of CSF fluid","Infection of the dura","Spinal cord compression"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: PDPH most commonly occurs due to excessive removal of CSF fluid.  Why A wrong: Overproduction of CSF is not the cause.  Why C wrong: Infection is a separate complication, not the cause of PDPH.  Why D wrong: Cord compression is not the described mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"After a lumbar puncture, in which position should the client be placed to prevent post-dural puncture headache (PDPH)?","options":["Supine flat in bed for several hours","High-Fowler's position","Prone position with head elevated","Sitting upright immediately"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: To prevent PDPH the client is placed in supine or flat position for 2 to 3 hours, and avoid elevating the head for more than 6 hours; lying flat keeps the spinal column and brain at the same level and reduces CSF leakage/displacement.  Why B wrong: High-Fowler's elevates the head and increases PDPH risk.  Why C wrong: Prone with head elevated is not recommended.  Why D wrong: Sitting upright immediately increases the risk of PDPH."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"For how long should the client lie flat (avoid elevating the head) after a lumbar puncture to help prevent PDPH?","options":["Avoid elevating head for more than 6 hours","Avoid elevating head for 30 minutes","Avoid elevating head for 12 hours","Avoid elevating head for 24 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The client should be kept supine/flat for 2 to 3 hours and avoid elevating the head for more than 6 hours.  Why B/C/D wrong: These durations do not match the source-stated \"more than 6 hours\" guidance."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"During post-procedure monitoring after a lumbar puncture, prolonged headache may indicate which complication?","options":["CSF leakage from the puncture site","Hypertension","Bowel obstruction","Hyperglycemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A prolonged headache may indicate leakage / CSF leakage from the puncture site.  Why B wrong: Hypertension is not the indicated cause.  Why C wrong: Bowel obstruction is unrelated to LP headache.  Why D wrong: Hyperglycemia is not a post-LP headache cause."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"After a lumbar puncture, the collected specimen must be labelled and sent to the laboratory in what manner?","options":["Immediately, and never refrigerated; keep at room temperature","After refrigeration overnight","Frozen for 24 hours first","Only after the patient is discharged"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The specimen is labelled and immediately sent to the laboratory; delaying it should not refrigerate or freeze it and it must be kept at room temperature.  Why B/C wrong: Refrigeration or freezing the CSF specimen is contraindicated.  Why D wrong: The specimen must be sent immediately, not deferred to discharge."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"According to the \"Must Know Fact,\" what is the most common cause of post-dural puncture headache (PDPH)?","options":["Spinal anaesthesia with cutting needle (large bore), e.g., Quincke needle","Use of a pencil-point needle","Dehydration alone","Excessive bed rest"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common cause of PDPH is spinal anaesthesia with a cutting needle (large bore), e.g., the Quincke needle.  Why B wrong: A pencil-point (atraumatic) needle reduces PDPH risk; it is the preferred prevention.  Why C wrong: Dehydration alone is not the stated most common cause.  Why D wrong: Excessive bed rest is unrelated to causing PDPH."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"According to the \"Must Know Fact,\" how is PDPH best prevented?","options":["By using a pencil-point needle (atraumatic), which has the lowest risk of PDPH","By using a large-bore cutting needle","By keeping the patient sitting after the procedure","By administering high-volume CSF withdrawal"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: To prevent PDPH, give the patient a flat or supine position; a pencil-point (atraumatic) needle gives the lowest risk of PDPH. If resolved, treatment may need an epidural blood patch.  Why B wrong: A large-bore cutting needle increases PDPH risk.  Why C wrong: Sitting the patient up post-procedure increases PDPH risk.  Why D wrong: Excessive CSF withdrawal contributes to PDPH rather than preventing it."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"When PDPH is severe and unresolved by conservative measures, which treatment is used?","options":["Epidural blood patch","Intrathecal antibiotics","Lumbar drain placement","Oral analgesics only, indefinitely"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: When PDPH persists and is not resolved by conservative measures, it is treated with an epidural blood patch.  Why B wrong: Intrathecal antibiotics treat infection, not PDPH.  Why C wrong: A lumbar drain is not the stated treatment for PDPH.  Why D wrong: Indefinite oral analgesics is not the definitive treatment for severe PDPH."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"(PYQ – NORCET 2019) What is the best position to keep a 1-year-old child after a lumbar puncture to assess CSF?","options":["Right lateral","Left lateral","Prone","Supine"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In a paediatric lumbar puncture procedure done to assess CSF, the prone position is used to start (which position would prefer for this procedure).  Why A/B/D wrong: Per the keyed answer for this item the prone position is selected to start the procedure; the lateral and supine positions are the alternatives not chosen here."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"(PYQ – NORCET 2019) Which position is preferred to start a lumbar puncture procedure?","options":["Right lateral","Left lateral","Prone","Supine"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: For the paediatric lumbar puncture procedure done to start, the prone position is the keyed answer for this question.  Why A/B/D wrong: These positions are not the keyed starting position for this PYQ item."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"(PYQ – NORCET 2018) On duty nursing officer admits a patient in whom a lumbar puncture procedure is going on. Which position should the patient be placed in to facilitate this procedure?","options":["Patient's leg flexed, knees touched on abdomen and head flexed with chin touched on chest","Patient's lying on back and turning forward","Patient's leg extended, knees touched on abdomen and head in extension position","Patient lying in side and head turned to one side"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: To facilitate LP the patient is placed with legs flexed, knees touched on the abdomen and head flexed with chin touching the chest (C-shaped/fetal position), which widens the intervertebral spaces.  Why B wrong: Lying on the back/turning forward does not open the vertebral spaces.  Why C wrong: Extending the legs and head closes the intervertebral spaces, opposite of what is needed.  Why D wrong: Side-lying with head turned to one side alone does not achieve spinal flexion."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"(PYQ) Which of the following is a correct lumbar puncture insertion site used in paediatric patients?","options":["L₄–L₅","L₁–L₂","C₁–C₂","L₂–L₃"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In paediatric patients the LP is done at L₄–L₅ because the spinal cord ends lower (at L₄) in this age group.  Why B/D wrong: L₁–L₂ and L₂–L₃ are too high for infants/small children and risk cord injury.  Why C wrong: C₁–C₂ is in the cervical region, not used for lumbar puncture."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"In tuberculous (TB) meningitis, the CSF appearance is typically described as which of the following?","options":["Clear","Turbid","Bloody","Yellow and frothy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In TB meningitis (TBM) the CSF appearance is Turbid.  Why A wrong: Clear appearance is seen in normal/viral/fungal meningitis CSF, not the turbid TBM finding.  Why C/D wrong: Bloody or yellow frothy CSF are not the appearance listed for TBM."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"In bacterial meningitis, the leukocyte (WBC) count in CSF and the glucose level typically show which pattern?","options":["High WBC count and low glucose","Low WBC count and high glucose","Normal WBC count and normal glucose","High WBC count and high glucose"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In bacterial meningitis the CSF shows a high (high) leukocyte count and low glucose, with high protein and a high opening pressure.  Why B wrong: Low WBC/high glucose does not match bacterial meningitis.  Why C wrong: Normal values are seen with normal CSF, not bacterial meningitis.  Why D wrong: Glucose is low (not high) in bacterial meningitis."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"In viral meningitis, the CSF glucose and protein levels are characteristically:","options":["Normal glucose and high protein","Low glucose and low protein","Low glucose and high protein","High glucose and high protein"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In viral meningitis CSF appearance is clear, WBC low, glucose normal, and protein high.  Why B wrong: Glucose is normal (not low) in viral meningitis.  Why C wrong: Low glucose is a bacterial/TB pattern, not viral.  Why D wrong: Glucose is normal, not high, in viral meningitis."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"What is the normal colour/appearance of cerebrospinal fluid (CSF)?","options":["Clear, watery or colourless","Turbid and yellow","Pink and cloudy","Milky white"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Normal CSF colour is clear, watery or colourless.  Why B/C/D wrong: Turbid/yellow, pink/cloudy, or milky white indicate abnormal CSF (e.g., infection or bleeding), not normal fluid."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"What is the normal pH of cerebrospinal fluid (CSF)?","options":["7.4 (slightly alkaline)","6.0 (acidic)","8.5 (strongly alkaline)","5.5 (acidic)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Normal CSF pH is 7.4 (slightly alkaline).  Why B/C/D wrong: These pH values do not match the normal CSF value of 7.4."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"Normally, what is the expected red blood cell (RBC) content of cerebrospinal fluid?","options":["Almost absent / nil","5,000–10,000 cells/mm³","50–80 cells/mm³","Equal to plasma RBC"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Normal CSF RBC is almost absent / nil.  Why B/C wrong: These represent abnormal cell counts, not the near-absent normal RBC.  Why D wrong: CSF normally has essentially no RBCs, far below plasma levels."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"What is the normal CSF lactate value?","options":["1–3 mmol/L","7.4 mmol/L","50–80 mmol/L","0.5–0.6 mmol/L"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Normal CSF lactate is 1–3 mmol/L.  Why B/C/D wrong: These values do not match the normal CSF lactate range."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"What is the normal CSF opening pressure?","options":["50–200 mm H₂O","200–300 mm H₂O","5–15 mm H₂O","90–180 mm Hg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Normal CSF opening pressure is 50–200 mm H₂O.  Why B wrong: 200–300 mm H₂O is elevated, not normal.  Why C wrong: 5–15 mm H₂O is far too low.  Why D wrong: Opening pressure is measured in mm H₂O, not mm Hg."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"What is the normal CSF protein concentration?","options":["15–45 mg/dl","50–80 mg/dl","125–150 ml","700 mg/day"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Normal CSF protein is 15–45 mg/dl.  Why B wrong: 50–80 mg/dl is the normal CSF glucose range, not protein.  Why C wrong: 125–150 ml is the total CSF volume.  Why D wrong: This represents daily CSF secretion, not protein concentration."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"What is the normal CSF glucose level?","options":["50–80 mg/dl","15–45 mg/dl","100–150 mg/dl","1–3 mg/dl"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Normal CSF glucose is 50–80 mg/dl.  Why B wrong: 15–45 mg/dl is the CSF protein range.  Why C/D wrong: These values do not match the normal CSF glucose level."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"What is the approximate total volume of cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) in the body?","options":["125–150 ml","50–80 ml","700 ml","15–45 ml"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Total CSF volume is 125–150 ml.  Why B/C/D wrong: These figures correspond to other CSF parameters (glucose, daily secretion, protein) not total volume."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"What is the approximate per-day secretion (production) of cerebrospinal fluid?","options":["About 500–600 ml/day (≈0.3–0.4 ml/min)","125–150 ml/day","50 ml/day","2,000 ml/day"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: CSF per-day secretion is roughly 500–600 ml/day (about 0.3–0.4 ml/min).  Why B wrong: 125–150 ml is the total volume at any time, not daily production.  Why C/D wrong: These values do not match the stated daily secretion."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"Where is cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) primarily produced?","options":["Choroid plexuses (CP) in the lateral ventricles","Arachnoid villi","Pituitary gland","Dura mater"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The CSF production site is the choroid plexuses (CP) in the lateral ventricles.  Why B wrong: The arachnoid villi (AV) are the CSF absorption site, not the production site.  Why C/D wrong: The pituitary gland and dura mater are not CSF production sites."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"Where is cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) primarily absorbed?","options":["Arachnoid villi (AV)","Choroid plexuses","Lateral ventricles","Subdural space"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The CSF absorption site is the arachnoid villi (AV).  Why B wrong: The choroid plexuses are the production site.  Why C wrong: Lateral ventricles house the choroid plexuses (production), not absorption.  Why D wrong: The subdural space is not the CSF absorption site."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"Who performed the first liver biopsy and in which year?","options":["Paul Ehrlich in 1883","Heinrich Quincke in 1891","Morrill Wyman in 1850","Carl Menghini in 1958"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The first liver biopsy was performed by Paul Ehrlich in 1883.  Why B wrong: Heinrich Quincke/1891 relates to lumbar puncture.  Why C wrong: Morrill Wyman/1850 relates to thoracocentesis.  Why D wrong: Carl Menghini/1958 is not the attribution given in the source."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"Liver biopsy is described as which type of procedure?","options":["A minimally/non-invasive imaging study","An invasive procedure (percutaneous needle is inserted through the skin)","A purely laboratory blood test","An endoscopic procedure only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Liver biopsy is a minimally invasive procedure in which the needle is inserted through the skin.  Why A wrong: It is invasive, not non-invasive imaging.  Why C wrong: It involves tissue sampling, not just a blood test.  Why D wrong: Although a transvenous/laparoscopic route exists, the basic description is needle through the skin, not endoscopy only."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"During liver biopsy, what is inserted through the abdominal wall, and how is it commonly guided?","options":["A USG-guided needle inserted through the abdominal wall to obtain a living tissue sample","A bronchoscope through the airway","A spinal needle into the subarachnoid space","A chest tube into the pleural space"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A USG-guided needle is inserted through the abdominal wall to obtain a living tissue sample of the liver.  Why B wrong: A bronchoscope through the airway is not used in liver biopsy.  Why C wrong: A spinal needle into the subarachnoid space is for lumbar puncture.  Why D wrong: A chest tube into the pleural space is for thoracic drainage, not liver biopsy."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"Liver biopsy is used for the confirmative diagnosis of which conditions?","options":["Liver cancer or liver carcinoma","Meningitis","Pneumothorax","Pleural effusion"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Liver biopsy is the gold standard for obtaining a tissue sample and confirmative diagnosis of liver cancer or liver carcinoma.  Why B wrong: Meningitis is diagnosed by lumbar puncture/CSF analysis.  Why C wrong: Pneumothorax is an air-collection condition managed by chest tube, not diagnosed by liver biopsy.  Why D wrong: Pleural effusion is assessed via thoracocentesis."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"Liver biopsy is primarily performed for which type of tissue examination?","options":["Cytological and histological examinations","Microbiological culture only","Coagulation profiling","Blood gas analysis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Liver biopsy is primarily done for cytological and histological examinations.  Why B/C/D wrong: Culture only, coagulation profiling, and blood gas analysis are not the primary purposes of liver biopsy."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"Which of the following are indications for a liver biopsy? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Diagnosis","Prognosis","Treatment","Staging of tumors, malignancy and infectious diseases like tuberculosis","Routine annual screening in healthy adults"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The indications listed are diagnosis, prognosis, treatment, and the procedure is most useful in the diagnosis of liver masses, staging of tumors, malignancy and infectious diseases like tuberculosis.  Why E wrong: Routine annual screening in healthy adults is not an indication; liver biopsy is invasive and reserved for specific indications."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"Which of the following is an ABSOLUTE contraindication to liver biopsy?","options":["Uncooperative patient (increased risk of complications)","Mild fatigue","Controlled hypertension","History of asthma"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: An uncooperative patient is an absolute contraindication because there is increased risk of complications still necessary then done under anaesthesia.  Why B/C/D wrong: Mild fatigue, controlled hypertension, and asthma history are not the listed absolute contraindications to liver biopsy."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"A low platelet count (thrombocytopenia) below which value is an absolute contraindication to liver biopsy due to increased risk of bleeding?","options":["< 50,000 cells/mm³","< 1,50,000 cells/mm³","< 4,50,000 cells/mm³","< 2,00,000 cells/mm³"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A low platelet count (thrombocytopenia) < 50,000 cells/mm³ increases risk of bleeding and is an absolute contraindication.  Why B/C/D wrong: These values are above the stated 50,000 cells/mm³ cut-off."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"An INR greater than which value is an absolute contraindication to liver biopsy due to increased risk of bleeding?","options":["INR > 1.5","INR > 0.5","INR > 3.0","INR > 5.0"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: An INR > 1.5 increases the risk of bleeding and is an absolute contraindication.  Why B wrong: 0.5 is below normal and not the stated cut-off.  Why C/D wrong: These values are higher than the stated 1.5 threshold."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"Which vascular condition is an absolute contraindication to liver biopsy?","options":["Vascular tumor of the liver (increased risk of bleeding)","Mild varicose veins of the legs","Stable peripheral artery disease","Resolved deep vein thrombosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A vascular tumor of the liver is an absolute contraindication due to increased risk of bleeding.  Why B/C/D wrong: These vascular conditions are not the listed absolute contraindications to liver biopsy."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"When ascites is present, which route of liver biopsy is preferred?","options":["Trans-venous route (transjugular / transfemoral)","Percutaneous route","Laparoscopic route","Open surgical route"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Ascites is a relative contraindication for percutaneous biopsy; the trans-venous route (transjugular/transfemoral) is preferred.  Why B wrong: The percutaneous route is relatively contraindicated when ascites is present.  Why C/D wrong: Laparoscopic or open surgical routes are not the preferred alternative specified for ascites."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"In a patient with morbid obesity, which liver biopsy route is preferred?","options":["Trans-venous route (transjugular / transfemoral)","Percutaneous route","Direct skin route","No biopsy possible"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Morbid obesity is a relative contraindication where the trans-venous route (transjugular or transfemoral) is preferred.  Why B wrong: The percutaneous route is technically difficult and less preferred in morbid obesity.  Why C wrong: A direct skin (percutaneous) approach is the relative contraindication being avoided.  Why D wrong: Biopsy can still be done via the trans-venous route."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"Which needles are listed as the required biopsy needles for a liver biopsy?","options":["Vim Silverman's needle and Birmingham cutting needle","Quincke spinal needle","Touhy epidural needle","Butterfly needle"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The required biopsy needles are the Vim Silverman's needle and Birmingham cutting needle (the most commonly used biopsy needle).  Why B wrong: The Quincke spinal needle is used for lumbar puncture.  Why C wrong: The Touhy needle is an epidural needle.  Why D wrong: A butterfly needle is for venous access, not liver biopsy."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"What size needle is identified as the second-choice needle for liver biopsy?","options":["16 G needle","22 G needle","25 G needle","30 G needle"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A 16 G needle is the second-choice needle for liver biopsy.  Why B/C/D wrong: These finer gauges are not the stated second-choice liver biopsy needle."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"Which agent is used as a preservative when a liver biopsy sample is sent for examination?","options":["10% formalin (formaldehyde)","70% alcohol","Normal saline","Heparin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: 10% formalin (formaldehyde) is used as preservative when sending the liver biopsy sample.  Why B/C/D wrong: 70% alcohol, normal saline, and heparin are not the preservative specified for the biopsy sample."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"Which agent and concentration is used to prevent infection at the puncture site during liver biopsy?","options":["Betadine","Spirit","Chlorhexidine 0.5%","Hydrogen peroxide"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Betadine is used to clean the puncture site, to prevent infection.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the antiseptic listed for the liver biopsy puncture site."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"What concentration of lidocaine is used for local anaesthesia during liver biopsy?","options":["2% Lidocaine","0.5% Lidocaine","5% Lidocaine","10% Lidocaine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: 2% Lidocaine is used for local anaesthesia during liver biopsy.  Why B/C/D wrong: These concentrations do not match the 2% lidocaine specified."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"In the percutaneous technique of liver biopsy, the procedure is performed under which type of anaesthesia?","options":["Local anaesthesia (2% xylocaine)","General anaesthesia only","Spinal anaesthesia","No anaesthesia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The percutaneous technique is performed in an outside department under local anaesthesia, 2% xylocaine.  Why B wrong: General anaesthesia is not the standard for percutaneous liver biopsy.  Why C wrong: Spinal anaesthesia is used for lower-body procedures, not liver biopsy.  Why D wrong: Local anaesthesia is required, not none."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"The percutaneous liver biopsy technique includes which type of guidance for needle placement?","options":["Aspiration/percussion method (imaging-guided and real-time image-guided)","Fluoroscopy only without imaging","Blind insertion without guidance","MRI-only guidance"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The percutaneous technique includes aspiration/percussion method, and is imaging-guided and real-time image-guided.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the imaging-guided/real-time guidance described for the percutaneous technique."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"Which liver biopsy approach uses the transjugular or transfemoral route and is most preferable when there is ascites or a bleeding disorder?","options":["Trans-venous approach","Percutaneous approach","Open surgical approach","Endoscopic approach"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The trans-venous approach uses the transjugular or transfemoral route and is most preferable; it is commonly used for ascites and disease conditions (e.g., bleeding risk).  Why B wrong: The percutaneous approach is contraindicated/limited in ascites and bleeding disorders.  Why C/D wrong: Open surgical and endoscopic approaches are not the trans-venous route described."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"Which needle is most commonly used for the laparoscopic technique of liver biopsy, noted as the most safe with the least chance of complications?","options":["Veress needle","Vim Silverman's needle","Quincke needle","Menghini needle"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In the laparoscopic technique, the Veress needle is most commonly used; this method is very safe and has the least chance of complications.  Why B wrong: Vim Silverman's needle is a cutting biopsy needle, not the laparoscopic Veress needle.  Why C wrong: The Quincke needle is for lumbar puncture.  Why D wrong: The Menghini needle is not the one specified for the laparoscopic method here."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"During a percutaneous liver biopsy, the patient is positioned in which position, with the right arm raised above the head?","options":["Supine position (with the patient's right hand placed under the head in a neutral position)","Left lateral decubitus","Prone position","Trendelenburg position"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: During the procedure the supine position is used, in which the patient's right hand is placed under the head in a neutral position to expose the right side of the upper abdomen.  Why B/C/D wrong: Left lateral, prone, and Trendelenburg are not the position described for percutaneous liver biopsy."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"During the liver biopsy procedure, the patient is asked to do what to decrease the risk of haemorrhage?","options":["Hold the breath at full inspiration (full inspiratory position)","Cough forcefully","Bear down (Valsalva)","Take rapid shallow breaths"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: During the procedure the patient is asked to hold the breath / breath-hold at full inspiratory position to decrease the risk of haemorrhage by keeping the liver position fixed.  Why B wrong: Forceful coughing would move the liver and increase injury risk.  Why C wrong: Valsalva/bearing down is not the instruction given.  Why D wrong: Rapid shallow breathing does not stabilize the liver for needle insertion."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"During a percutaneous liver biopsy, where is the needle inserted in relation to the costal margin?","options":["At the right side of the upper hypochondrium region between 7th to 9th inter-costal spaces at anterior midclavicular line","Below the umbilicus","At the left flank","Above the clavicle"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The needle is inserted at the right side of the upper hypochondrium region between the 7th to 9th inter-costal spaces at the anterior midclavicular line.  Why B/C/D wrong: These sites do not correspond to the right upper hypochondrium / 7th–9th intercostal space insertion point described."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"During the liver biopsy, how long is the patient typically asked to hold their breath during needle insertion?","options":["About 5–10 seconds","60 seconds","2 minutes","No breath-holding needed"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The patient is asked to hold the breath for 5 to 10 seconds, because this reduces the risk of pneumothorax (and stabilizes the liver) during needle insertion.  Why B/C wrong: 60 seconds or 2 minutes are unsafe and not the stated duration.  Why D wrong: Breath-holding is required to reduce complications."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"After a liver biopsy, in which position should the patient be placed to apply pressure and prevent bleeding?","options":["Right lateral / right side-lying position with a pillow or sandbag under the puncture site","Left lateral position","High-Fowler's position","Prone position"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: After the procedure, apply pressure (a pillow and place the client in right lateral or right midclavicular position) with a small pillow or rolled blanket/sandbag under the puncture site to decrease the risk of haemorrhage by direct pressure.  Why B wrong: Left lateral does not place pressure on the right-sided liver puncture site.  Why C wrong: High-Fowler's does not compress the puncture site.  Why D wrong: Prone does not provide targeted pressure on the right-side puncture site."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"After a liver biopsy, how long should the patient be kept on bed rest / monitored, with vital signs assessed frequently?","options":["Bed rest for 12 to 24 hours, with vital signs checked every 15 minutes for the first hour","Bed rest for 1 hour only with hourly vitals","No bed rest required","Bed rest for 1 week"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Keep the patient on bed rest (and observe) for 12 to 24 hours; vital signs are usually monitored within the first 15 minutes of therapy or within 24 hours (vitals checked every 15 minutes for the first hour, then hourly). All vital signs are monitored every 15 minutes for the first hour.  Why B/C/D wrong: These bed-rest durations and monitoring frequencies do not match the source guidance."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"What is the MOST common complication of a liver biopsy?","options":["Pain at insertion site","Bleeding or haemorrhage","Transient bacteremia","Tachycardia and hypotension"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Pain at the insertion site is the most common complication of liver biopsy.  Why B wrong: Bleeding or haemorrhage is the second most common complication, not the most common.  Why C wrong: Transient bacteremia is a listed complication but not the most common.  Why D wrong: Tachycardia and hypotension are signs of bleeding (a complication), not the single most common complication."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"What is the SECOND most common complication of a liver biopsy?","options":["Bleeding or haemorrhage","Pain at insertion site","Transient bacteremia","Pneumothorax"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Bleeding or haemorrhage is the second most common complication of liver biopsy.  Why B wrong: Pain at the insertion site is the most (first) common complication.  Why C wrong: Transient bacteremia is a complication but not ranked second.  Why D wrong: Pneumothorax can occur but is not the stated second most common."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"Which of the following are recognized complications of liver biopsy? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Pain at insertion site","Bleeding or haemorrhage","Transient bacteremia","Tachycardia and hypotension","Permanent loss of liver function in all patients"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Listed complications include pain at the insertion site, bleeding or haemorrhage, transient bacteremia, and tachycardia and hypotension.  Why E wrong: Permanent loss of liver function in all patients is not a listed complication and is inaccurate."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"Which newer technique allows liver biopsy to be performed using a natural orifice transluminal endoscopic surgery (NOTES) approach?","options":["Natural Orifice Transluminal Endoscopic Surgery (NOTES)","Open laparotomy","Lumbar puncture","Thoracocentesis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Among newer techniques, Natural Orifice Transluminal Endoscopic Surgery (NOTES) could be used to perform a liver biopsy.  Why B wrong: Open laparotomy is a traditional surgical approach, not the NOTES technique.  Why C wrong: Lumbar puncture is for CSF, unrelated to liver biopsy.  Why D wrong: Thoracocentesis drains pleural fluid, not a liver biopsy method."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"Which liver lobe is the ideal target for a needle liver biopsy, as it avoids ultrasound before the biopsy?","options":["The right lobe of the liver","The left lobe of the liver","The caudate lobe","The quadrate lobe"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: All patients who need a liver biopsy should ideally have it via an ultrasound; before the biopsy is evaluated for the presence of Ohtahara syndrome (presence of small bowel between the liver and the abdominal wall), intrahepatic gallbladder and focal vascular lesions such as hemangioma — making the right lobe the target in imaging studies.  Why B/C/D wrong: The left, caudate, and quadrate lobes are not the ideal targeted biopsy lobe specified."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"A liver biopsy complication who usually presents with the classic triad of fever, right upper quadrant pain, and jaundice is suggestive of which condition?","options":["Hemobilia (bleeding into the biliary tract)","Pneumothorax","Pleural effusion","Meningitis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hemobilia is a liver biopsy complication that usually presents with the classic triad of fever, right upper quadrant (gastrointestinal) bleeding, biliary pain, and jaundice.  Why B wrong: Pneumothorax presents with respiratory distress, not the biliary triad.  Why C wrong: Pleural effusion presents with breathlessness, not this triad.  Why D wrong: Meningitis presents with fever, headache, and neck stiffness, not the biliary triad."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"(PYQ – NORCET) Which of the following is a complication of liver biopsy in children?","options":["Bile leakage","Pneumothorax","Hemobilia","All of the above"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Complications of liver biopsy in children include bile leakage, pneumothorax, and hemobilia — All of the above.  Why A/B/C wrong: Each is correct individually but is incomplete; all three are complications, so \"All of the above\" is the best answer."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"(PYQ – NORCET) What is the absolute contraindication of liver biopsy in children, except?","options":["Coagulopathy","Vascular tumour in liver","Cooperative children","Low platelets count"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Absolute contraindications include coagulopathy, vascular tumour in the liver, and low platelet count. A cooperative child is NOT a contraindication (it is the \"except\"); an uncooperative patient is the contraindication.  Why A/B/D wrong: Coagulopathy, vascular tumour of the liver, and low platelet count ARE absolute contraindications and so are not the \"except\" answer."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"(PYQ – NORCET) After a liver biopsy, in which position should the patient be placed?","options":["Supine position","Right lateral position","Prone position","High Fowler position"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After a liver biopsy the patient is placed in the right lateral position to apply pressure on the puncture site and decrease the risk of haemorrhage.  Why A wrong: Supine position does not provide direct pressure to the right-sided puncture site.  Why C wrong: Prone position is not used post-liver biopsy.  Why D wrong: High Fowler position does not compress the puncture site."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"(PYQ – NORCET) During the liver biopsy procedure, why is the patient placed in the supine or left lateral position with a pillow or sandbag under the puncture site after the procedure?","options":["To expose the right side of the upper abdomen / and to decrease the risk of haemorrhage by direct pressure","To improve oxygenation","To prevent aspiration","To reduce blood pressure"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: During the procedure the supine or left lateral position is used to expose the right side of the upper abdomen; after the procedure the right lateral or sandbag pressure under the puncture site reduces the risk of haemorrhage by direct pressure (all other positions do not help to prevent bleeding).  Why B/C/D wrong: Improving oxygenation, preventing aspiration, and reducing blood pressure are not the stated reasons for this positioning."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"Thoracocentesis is a procedure performed to remove fluid or air from which space?","options":["The thoracic cavity for both diagnostic and/or therapeutic purposes (pleural space)","The peritoneal cavity","The subarachnoid space","The pericardial sac only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Thoracocentesis is a procedure to remove fluid or air from the thoracic cavity for both diagnostic and/or therapeutic purposes.  Why B wrong: The peritoneal cavity is accessed via paracentesis, not thoracocentesis.  Why C wrong: The subarachnoid space is accessed via lumbar puncture.  Why D wrong: The pericardial sac is drained via pericardiocentesis."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"Thoracocentesis was first described by whom and in which year?","options":["Morrill Wyman in 1850","Paul Ehrlich in 1883","Heinrich Quincke in 1891","Carl Menghini in 1958"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Thoracocentesis was first described by Morrill Wyman in 1850.  Why B wrong: Paul Ehrlich/1883 relates to the first liver biopsy.  Why C wrong: Heinrich Quincke/1891 relates to lumbar puncture.  Why D wrong: Carl Menghini/1958 is not the attribution given."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"Thoracocentesis is also known by which other names?","options":["Thoracentesis, pleural tap, or needle decompression","Spinal tap","Paracentesis","Pericardiocentesis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Thoracocentesis is also known as thoracentesis, pleural tap, or needle decompression.  Why B wrong: Spinal tap is another name for lumbar puncture.  Why C wrong: Paracentesis drains peritoneal/abdominal fluid.  Why D wrong: Pericardiocentesis drains the pericardial sac."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"During thoracocentesis, a cannula or hollow needle is introduced into which space?","options":["The pleural space, after administration of local anaesthesia","The subdural space","The peritoneal space","The epidural space"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A cannula, or hollow needle, is introduced into the pleural space after administration of local anaesthesia.  Why B/C/D wrong: The subdural, peritoneal, and epidural spaces are not the target for thoracocentesis."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"Before a thoracocentesis, what imaging should be done to identify the exact location of the pleural effusion?","options":["A chest ultrasound (USG)","An ECG","A CT head","An abdominal X-ray"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Before a thoracocentesis, a chest ultrasound (USG) should be done to identify the exact location of the pleural effusion.  Why B wrong: An ECG assesses cardiac rhythm, not pleural fluid location.  Why C wrong: A CT head is irrelevant to a chest procedure.  Why D wrong: An abdominal X-ray does not localize pleural effusion."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"Why is chest ultrasound (USG) preferred over a chest X-ray for locating pleural effusion before thoracocentesis?","options":["Chest USG is preferable compared to chest X-ray to determine the exact location of the effusion","USG uses more radiation","X-ray is unavailable","USG is cheaper than all options"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A chest USG is preferable compared to a chest X-ray to determine the exact location of the effusion.  Why B wrong: USG uses no ionizing radiation, which is an advantage, not a reason against it.  Why C wrong: Availability is not the rationale given.  Why D wrong: Cost is not the stated reason for preferring USG."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"What is the most common indication for chest tube placement in an emergency scenario?","options":["To manage pneumothorax","To treat meningitis","To biopsy the liver","To relieve ascites"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common indication of chest tube placement in an emergency scenario is to manage pneumothorax.  Why B wrong: Meningitis is managed with antibiotics/LP, not a chest tube.  Why C wrong: Liver biopsy is unrelated to chest tube placement.  Why D wrong: Ascites is drained by paracentesis, not a chest tube."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"Which of the following are indications for thoracocentesis / chest tube placement? (Select All That Apply)","options":["To remove excessive fluid","To alleviate respiratory distress","To obtain a specimen for diagnostic purpose","To obtain a lung biopsy","To manage pneumothorax"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Listed indications include removing excessive fluid, alleviating respiratory distress, obtaining a specimen for diagnostic purpose, obtaining lung biopsy, and (most common in emergency) managing pneumothorax.  Why E note: Managing pneumothorax is explicitly the most common emergency indication, so all options are correct."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"A diagnostic thoracocentesis is performed to remove what volume of pleural fluid to determine the cause of a pleural effusion?","options":["A small sample of pleural fluid, about 50–60 ml","1000 ml at once","3 litres","The entire pleural cavity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A diagnostic thoracocentesis removes a small sample of pleural fluid about 50–60 ml to determine the cause of a pleural effusion.  Why B wrong: Large-volume removal (and never more than 1000 ml at a time) is therapeutic, not the small diagnostic sample.  Why C/D wrong: 3 litres or draining the entire cavity exceeds safe limits and is not the diagnostic sample volume."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"A therapeutic thoracocentesis removes a larger volume of pleural fluid (>1000 ml) for what purpose?","options":["To relieve symptoms such as shortness of breath (haemothorax and pneumothorax)","To stage a tumor","To obtain CSF","To prevent meningitis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A therapeutic thoracocentesis (for haemothorax and pneumothorax) removes a larger volume of pleural fluid > 1000 ml to relieve symptoms, such as shortness of breath.  Why B wrong: Tumor staging is an indication for biopsy, not the stated therapeutic purpose here.  Why C wrong: CSF is obtained via lumbar puncture.  Why D wrong: Meningitis prevention is unrelated to therapeutic thoracocentesis."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"What is the maximum amount of pleural fluid that should be removed at one time during thoracocentesis to prevent complications such as re-expansion pulmonary edema or hypotension?","options":["No more than 1000 ml at a time","No more than 50 ml at a time","Up to 5000 ml at a time","There is no maximum limit"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: No more than 1000 ml of pleural fluid should be removed at a time; removed more than that is at risk for hypotension and re-expansion pulmonary edema.  Why B wrong: 50 ml is far below the allowed limit (that is roughly the diagnostic sample size).  Why C wrong: 5000 ml grossly exceeds the safe limit.  Why D wrong: A defined maximum (1000 ml) does apply."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"During the thoracocentesis procedure, what is the preferred patient position?","options":["Upright or sitting position at the edge of the bed while leaning forward, with arms supported on a bed-side table/pillow","Supine flat with legs extended","Prone with arms at sides","Left lateral recumbent"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: During the procedure the preferred position is upright or sitting at the edge of the bed while leaning forward, with both hands raised above the level of the shoulder and arms supported on a bed-side table/pillow (this is the most common position).  Why B wrong: Supine flat does not allow gravity to pool fluid at the lung base for needle access.  Why C wrong: Prone is not the standard thoracocentesis position.  Why D wrong: The lateral recumbent position is used only if the patient is unable to sit up."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"If a patient is unable to sit up for a thoracocentesis, which alternative position can be prepared?","options":["Lateral recumbent position","Trendelenburg position","Knee-chest position","Standing position"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: If the patient is unable to sit up, the lateral recumbent position can be prepared.  Why B/C/D wrong: Trendelenburg, knee-chest, and standing positions are not the stated alternatives for thoracocentesis."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"After a thoracocentesis procedure, in which position should the patient be placed?","options":["On the opposite or unaffected side","On the affected side","Prone","Trendelenburg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: After the procedure the patient is positioned on the opposite/unaffected side.  Why B wrong: Lying on the affected side is not the recommended post-procedure position here.  Why C/D wrong: Prone and Trendelenburg are not the stated post-procedure positions."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"For pneumothorax, at which site is the needle inserted during thoracocentesis?","options":["2nd inter-costal space at the mid-clavicular line","5th inter-costal space at the mid-axillary line","7th inter-costal space at the anterior axillary line","4th inter-costal space at the mid-clavicular line"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For pneumothorax (or to remove air) the needle is inserted at the 2nd inter-costal space at the mid-clavicular line.  Why B wrong: The 5th inter-costal space at the mid-axillary line is used for hydrothorax/haemothorax (fluid), not air.  Why C/D wrong: These sites are not the stated insertion point for pneumothorax."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"For hydrothorax and haemothorax, at which site is the needle inserted during thoracocentesis?","options":["5th inter-costal space at the mid-axillary line","2nd inter-costal space at the mid-clavicular line","7th inter-costal space at the posterior axillary line","3rd inter-costal space at the mid-clavicular line"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For hydrothorax and haemothorax (fluid) the needle is inserted at the 5th inter-costal space at the mid-axillary line.  Why B wrong: The 2nd intercostal space at the mid-clavicular line is for pneumothorax (air), not fluid.  Why C/D wrong: These sites do not match the stated fluid-drainage insertion point."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"Before starting a thoracocentesis, which nursing responsibility is correct?","options":["Take informed consent and assess all vital signs","Position the patient supine and elevate the legs","Withhold all monitoring during the procedure","Avoid antiseptic preparation of the skin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Pre and during procedure nursing care includes taking informed consent and all vital signs, and continuous monitoring of vital signs (pulse, BP) during the procedure.  Why B wrong: The patient is positioned upright/sitting and leaning forward, not supine with legs elevated.  Why C wrong: Monitoring (pulse, BP, oxygenation) should continue throughout the procedure.  Why D wrong: Aseptic technique with antiseptic skin preparation is required."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"During thoracocentesis, what should be monitored throughout the procedure?","options":["Pulse, BP, and health status should be monitored throughout the procedure","Only temperature once at the end","Nothing until the procedure finishes","Blood glucose only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Pulse, BP and the patient's status should be monitored throughout the thoracocentesis procedure to detect complications early.  Why B/C/D wrong: Limiting monitoring to one temperature reading, nothing, or only blood glucose is unsafe and not the source instruction."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"Which antiseptic agent and concentration is used to clean the skin before starting a thoracocentesis?","options":["Sterile gloves, eye protection, face mask, sterile drapes or towels, and chlorhexidine or iodine prep","Plain water only","Spirit swab only","No antiseptic is needed"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: This is an invasive procedure, so PPEs and the procedure field prep should avoid iatrogenic infection: sterile gloves, eye protection, face mask, sterile drapes or towels, and chlorhexidine or iodine for antiseptic preparation.  Why B/C wrong: Plain water or spirit alone is insufficient for a sterile invasive procedure.  Why D wrong: Antiseptic preparation is mandatory for this invasive procedure."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"During thoracocentesis, where on the rib is the needle inserted to avoid damaging the intercostal vessels and nerve?","options":["At the upper border of the rib (over the top of the rib)","At the lower border of the rib","Directly through the centre of the rib","Below the diaphragm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The needle is inserted at the upper border of the rib (over the top of the rib) because the neurovascular bundle (intercostal vessels and nerve) runs along the lower border of each rib; inserting over the top of the rib avoids injuring them.  Why B wrong: The lower border carries the neurovascular bundle and must be avoided.  Why C wrong: The needle is not pushed through the rib itself.  Why D wrong: Inserting below the diaphragm risks abdominal organ injury."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"After a thoracocentesis, the patient is positioned on the unaffected side with the head of the bed elevated for how long?","options":["1 hour","6 hours","12 hours","24 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Post-procedure, position the patient on the unaffected/opposite site for 1 hour with the head of the bed elevated to promote lung expansion.  Why B/C/D wrong: These durations do not match the source-stated 1 hour."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"During post-procedure care after thoracocentesis, the nurse should monitor for which sign indicating air has entered the pleural space?","options":["Signs of pneumothorax or air embolism such as subcutaneous emphysema","Hyperglycemia","Increased urine output","Skin rash"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: After the procedure the nurse should monitor for signs of pneumothorax, air embolism, and subcutaneous emphysema.  Why B/C/D wrong: Hyperglycemia, increased urine output, and skin rash are not the post-thoracocentesis complications to monitor for."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"During post-thoracocentesis assessment, what breath-sound and respiratory finding should be assessed and reported immediately?","options":["Decreased breath sounds and respiratory rate, and bloody sputum","Increased appetite","Improved skin turgor","Normal bowel sounds"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The nurse should observe for decreased breath sounds and respiratory rate, and bloody sputum, reporting any noted then to inform an on-duty doctor immediately.  Why B/C/D wrong: Increased appetite, improved skin turgor, and normal bowel sounds are not the respiratory warning findings to report after thoracocentesis."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"Which of the following is the MOST common complication of thoracocentesis?","options":["Pneumothorax or tension pneumothorax","Pulmonary edema only","Tracheal deviation only","Liver and spleen puncture only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Pneumothorax or tension pneumothorax is the most common complication of thoracocentesis.  Why B wrong: Pulmonary edema (re-expansion) can occur but is not the most common complication.  Why C wrong: Tracheal deviation is a sign of tension pneumothorax, not the most common complication itself.  Why D wrong: Liver and spleen puncture is a possible complication but not the most common."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"Which of the following are complications of thoracocentesis? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Pneumothorax or tension pneumothorax","Bleeding, pain and infection at insertion site","Liver and spleen puncture (if the insertion site is too low)","Pulmonary edema (re-expansion pulmonary edema)","Tracheal deviation toward the affected side with forceful removal of pleural fluid"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Listed complications include pneumothorax/tension pneumothorax (most common), bleeding/pain/infection at the insertion site, liver and spleen puncture if the insertion site is too low, pulmonary (re-expansion) edema, and tracheal deviation that will shift on the unaffected site due to excessive and forceful removal of pleural fluid.  Why all correct: Each option is a complication explicitly listed in the source."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"Excessive and forceful removal of pleural fluid during thoracocentesis can cause which finding?","options":["Tracheal deviation (mediastinal shift)","Hyperkalemia","Hypoglycemia","Bradycardia only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Tracheal deviation will shift due to excessive and forceful removal of pleural fluid (a mediastinal shift complication).  Why B/C/D wrong: Hyperkalemia, hypoglycemia, and isolated bradycardia are not the stated effect of forceful pleural fluid removal."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"(PYQ – NORCET) A patient is diagnosed with chest pneumothorax. The on-duty nursing officer anticipates that which test will be ordered by the doctor before chest tube insertion?","options":["Chest X-ray","Hb","Vital Assessment","Bronchoscopy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For a patient diagnosed with chest pneumothorax, a chest X-ray will be ordered by the doctor before chest tube insertion to confirm the diagnosis and locate the air.  Why B wrong: Hb (hemoglobin) is not the imaging test confirming pneumothorax before chest tube insertion.  Why C wrong: Vital assessment is routine but not the specific ordered test that confirms the pneumothorax.  Why D wrong: Bronchoscopy is not done before chest tube insertion for pneumothorax."},{"section":"Batch 036 · Lumbar puncture…","question":"(PYQ – NORCET) In tension pneumothorax, what is the correct needle insertion site?","options":["5th inter-costal mask at mid-axillary line","5th inter-costal muscle at mid-clavicular line","2nd inter-costal muscle at mid-axillary line","2nd inter-costal muscle at mid-clavicular line"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: In tension pneumothorax (air), needle insertion is at the 2nd inter-costal space at the mid-clavicular line — the keyed answer.  Why A wrong: The 5th intercostal/mid-axillary line is for fluid (hydro/haemothorax), not a tension pneumothorax.  Why B wrong: The 5th intercostal at mid-clavicular line is not the correct decompression site.  Why C wrong: The mid-axillary line at the 2nd intercostal space is not the keyed site; the mid-clavicular line is correct."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"Abdominal paracentesis is a surgical procedure performed in patients with ascites to obtain ascitic fluid. The procedure is also commonly known by which name(s)?","options":["Laparocentesis or abdominal tapping","Thoracentesis","Pericardiocentesis","Amniocentesis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states paracentesis is \"also known as laparocentesis, abdominal tapping and peritoneocentesis.\"  Why B wrong: Thoracentesis removes fluid from the pleural (chest) cavity, not the abdomen.  Why C wrong: Pericardiocentesis removes fluid from the pericardial sac around the heart.  Why D wrong: Amniocentesis samples amniotic fluid during pregnancy."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"During abdominal paracentesis, fluid is collected from which body cavity?","options":["Pleural cavity","Peritoneal cavity","Pericardial cavity","Synovial cavity"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: It is a procedure to drain fluid that has accumulated in the peritoneal cavity to obtain ascitic fluid.  Why A wrong: Pleural cavity drainage is thoracentesis.  Why C wrong: Pericardial cavity drainage is pericardiocentesis.  Why D wrong: Synovial fluid is aspirated by arthrocentesis from a joint."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"The most common cause of ascites, for which paracentesis is performed, is:","options":["Cirrhosis of the liver","Acute pancreatitis","Renal calculi","Pulmonary embolism"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states the most common cause of ascites is cirrhosis of the liver.  Why B wrong: Pancreatitis is not cited as the most common cause of ascites here.  Why C wrong: Renal calculi cause urinary obstruction, not ascites.  Why D wrong: Pulmonary embolism is a vascular lung event unrelated to ascites."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"Which of the following are stated purposes of abdominal paracentesis? (Select All That Apply)","options":["To reduce pressure from abdominal organs","To provide relief from respiratory distress","For diagnostic and therapeutic purposes","To provide comfort to the patient","To permanently cure liver cirrhosis"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Listed purposes are reducing pressure from abdominal organs, relief from respiratory distress, diagnostic and therapeutic use, and providing comfort to the patient.  Why E wrong: Paracentesis relieves symptoms of ascites; it does not cure the underlying cirrhosis."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"When paracentesis is performed to obtain a small sample of ascitic fluid (50–60 ml) to determine the cause of an infection of peritoneal fluid, this indication is classified as:","options":["Therapeutic","Diagnostic","Palliative","Prophylactic"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Diagnostic paracentesis is performed to remove a small sample of ascitic fluid (50–60 ml) to determine the cause of an infection of peritoneal fluid.  Why A wrong: Therapeutic paracentesis removes a larger volume to relieve symptoms.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the classification terms used for this small-sample sampling."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"Therapeutic paracentesis is indicated to remove what volume of fluid?","options":["Less than 100 ml","Up to 50 ml only","A larger volume (>1000 ml of air or fluid) to relieve symptoms caused by ascites","Exactly 250 ml"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Therapeutic paracentesis removes a larger volume of ascitic fluid (>1000 ml of air or fluid) to relieve symptoms caused by ascites.  Why A/B/D wrong: These small volumes correspond to diagnostic sampling, not therapeutic removal."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"Which of the following is an ABSOLUTE contraindication to abdominal paracentesis? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC)","An acute abdomen requiring surgery","Pregnancy","Organomegaly","Distended bladder that cannot be emptied"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: Absolute contraindications listed are disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC) and an acute abdomen requiring surgery.  Why C wrong: Pregnancy is listed as a relative contraindication, not absolute.  Why D/E wrong: Organomegaly and a distended bladder that cannot be emptied are listed under needle insertion site precautions/conditions to avoid, not as absolute contraindications."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"Pregnancy is classified as which type of contraindication to paracentesis?","options":["Absolute contraindication","Relative contraindication","Not a contraindication","Indication"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists pregnancy under relative contraindications.  Why A wrong: DIC and acute abdomen requiring surgery are the absolute contraindications.  Why C/D wrong: It is explicitly listed as a relative contraindication."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"Which of the following conditions should be avoided/considered before selecting the needle insertion site for paracentesis? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Organomegaly (e.g., hepatomegaly or splenomegaly)","Ileus and intestinal obstruction","Distended bladder that cannot be emptied","Multiple previous abdominal operations","Severe thrombocytopenia (platelets < 20,000 cells/mm³)"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: All listed items are noted as situations to avoid/consider regarding needle insertion: organomegaly, ileus and intestinal obstruction, distended bladder that cannot be emptied, multiple previous abdominal operations, and severe thrombocytopenia (platelets < 20,000 cells/mm³). Also included are international normalized ratio (INR) > 2.0, severe hypoproteinemia, and intra-abdominal adhesions.  All options are correct per the source."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"Paracentesis should be avoided in a patient with an international normalized ratio (INR) above which value?","options":["INR > 1.0","INR > 1.5","INR > 2.0","INR > 3.5"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: An INR > 2.0 is listed among conditions to avoid before needle insertion (bleeding risk).  Why A/B/D wrong: These do not match the value (>2.0) stated in the source."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"For paracentesis, the LATERAL first choice needle insertion site is located in which quadrant of the abdomen?","options":["Right upper quadrant","Left lower quadrant, lateral to the rectus sheath and superior to the anterior superior iliac spine (ASIS)","Periumbilical region","Epigastric region"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The lateral first choice site is the left lower quadrant of the abdomen, lateral to the rectus sheath and superior to the anterior superior iliac spine (ASIS).  Why A wrong: Right upper quadrant overlies the liver (risk of organ injury).  Why C wrong: The periumbilical/midline approach is the second choice, not the lateral first choice.  Why D wrong: The epigastric region is not the described lateral site."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"The MIDLINE second-choice paracentesis insertion site is located:","options":["2 cm below the umbilicus, in the midline (5 cm superior and medial to ASIS on either side)","At the xiphoid process","10 cm above the umbilicus","Directly over the liver edge"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The midline second choice is 2 cm below the umbilicus in the linea alba, located 5 cm superior and medial to the ASIS on either side.  Why B/C/D wrong: These sites are not described and risk organ injury or are anatomically incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"According to the \"Triangle of safety\" used for chest drains/thoracentesis, the triangle is bounded by which structures?","options":["Anteriorly by the lateral border of pectoralis major; posteriorly by a horizontal line at the level of the nipples; and inferiorly by the lateral border of latissimus dorsi","The scapula, clavicle, and sternum","The diaphragm, liver, and spleen","The xiphoid, umbilicus, and ASIS"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The triangle of safety is bounded anteriorly by the lateral border of pectoralis major, by a horizontal line at the level of the nipples (commonly at 5th ICS at mid-axillary line), and posteriorly by a lateral border of latissimus dorsi; this area is mostly free of structures and is the safe site so as not to damage the chest wall muscles.  Why B/C/D wrong: These structures are not part of the triangle of safety."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"The Z-track technique is preferred and used for paracentesis primarily because it: - minimizes the risk of perforation during paracentesis","options":["Speeds up fluid drainage","Is more commonly and preferably used because it offsets the layers (the left lateral approach avoids air-filled bowel that usually floats in the ascitic fluid), and the patient is placed in the supine position and slightly rotated to the side of the procedure to minimize the risk of perforation","Eliminates the need for ultrasound guidance","Allows insertion through the liver"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Per the \"Also Reminder\" box, the left lateral technique is more commonly and preferably used for paracentesis because it offsets the layers; the left lateral approach avoids air-filled bowel that usually floats in the ascitic fluid; the patient is placed in the supine position and slightly rotated to the side of the procedure to minimize the risk of perforation.  Why A wrong: Drainage speed is not the stated rationale.  Why C wrong: Ultrasound guidance is still recommended (see procedure/nursing care).  Why D wrong: The technique avoids organ injury, not deliberately passing through the liver."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"Which actions are part of the PRE-procedure nursing care for paracentesis? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Obtain verified informed consent before the procedure","Explain the procedure, its purpose, outcomes, and possible complications","Gather all required equipment before starting the procedure","Prepare the site with appropriate antiseptic solution (e.g., spirit, Betadine)","Insert a 25 G needle at an appropriate site with the Z-track method to decrease the chance of fluid leakage after the procedure"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: All listed steps appear under pre-and-during procedure nursing care: obtain verified informed consent; explain procedure/purpose/outcomes/complications; gather equipment; prepare the site with antiseptic (spirit, Betadine); insert a 25 G needle at an appropriate site with Z-track to decrease fluid leakage afterward.  All options are correct per the source."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"Before AND after a paracentesis procedure, which assessment must the nurse perform regarding the patient's fluid status and stability?","options":["Measure abdominal girth, weight, and baseline vital signs before and after the procedure","Measure only the temperature","Measure only the urine output","No measurement is needed"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The nurse should measure abdominal girth, weight, and baseline vital signs before and after the whole procedure.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies girth, weight, and vital signs (not a single isolated parameter, and not \"no measurement\")."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"To localize fluid and reduce the risk of complications during paracentesis, which imaging guidance is recommended?","options":["Ultrasound guidance","Plain abdominal X-ray only","MRI of the spine","Bone scan"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Bedside ultrasound guidance is recommended to identify an appropriate location for aspiration and reduce the risk of complications.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the recommended guidance for site localization in paracentesis."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"During paracentesis, the patient should be positioned:","options":["Upright at the edge of the bed, with back supported and feet resting on a stool (first priority)","Prone with arms overhead","Trendelenburg (head down)","Left lateral with knees to chest only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The patient should be upright at the edge of the bed, with back supported and feet resting on a stool (stated as first priority positioning).  Why B/C/D wrong: These positions are not specified for the upright tapping position used in paracentesis."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"To prevent hypovolemia or hypotension during paracentesis, the nurse should ensure that:","options":["Not more than 1000 ml of fluid is removed at one time","At least 5000 ml is removed rapidly","No fluid is removed","The bladder is left full"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: To prevent hypovolemia or hypotension, do not remove more than 1000 ml of fluid at one time.  Why B wrong: Rapid removal of large volumes risks hypovolemia/hypotension.  Why C wrong: Removal is the purpose of the procedure; the limit is a safety precaution.  Why D wrong: A full bladder is a hazard for needle insertion; the bladder should be emptied."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"When performing paracentesis in the left lateral decubitus position, which approach is preferable?","options":["Insertion in the supine position only","Insert preferentially in the left lateral decubitus position","Insert at the xiphoid process","Insert in the right upper quadrant"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page notes performing the procedure in the left lateral decubitus position is preferable (avoiding air-filled bowel that floats).  Why A wrong: While supine slightly rotated is described for Z-track, the left lateral approach is specifically noted as preferable here.  Why C/D wrong: These sites risk injury and are not the described approach."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"Which of the following are recognized COMPLICATIONS of paracentesis? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Persistent leakage of ascitic fluid at the needle insertion site","Abdominal wall hematoma","Bleeding or haemorrhage","Wound infection and bowel perforation","Post-paracentesis hypotension"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Listed complications include persistent leakage of ascitic fluid at the needle insertion site, abdominal wall hematoma, bleeding/haemorrhage, wound infection, bowel perforation, and post-paracentesis hypotension. Also listed are spontaneous hemoperitoneum, hepatorenal syndrome, and subcutaneous effusion due to ascitic fluid leakage.  All options are correct per the source."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"To prevent post-paracentesis hypotension when more than 5000 ml of fluid is removed, the nurse should anticipate administration of which agent?","options":["Albumin","Normal saline 0.45%","Dextrose 50%","Whole blood routinely"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Post-paracentesis hypotension occurs if more than 5000 ml of fluid is removed; albumin is administered to prevent this complication.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifically names albumin for volume/oncotic support, not these other fluids."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"Following paracentesis, if the nurse observes a clot of blood, or pink or red urine, this finding may indicate:","options":["Bladder trauma","Normal expected drainage","Resolution of ascites","Hyperalbuminemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: After the procedure, monitor for hematuria or accidental bladder trauma; the nurse should instruct the client to inform immediately if a clot of blood, or pink or red urine, is noted.  Why B wrong: Bloody/pink urine is abnormal and signals possible injury.  Why C/D wrong: These are unrelated to bladder trauma findings."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"After a paracentesis procedure, the dressing over the needle insertion site should be maintained for how long before redressing?","options":["5 minutes","15 minutes, then redress with a dry dressing","1 hour","24 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Maintain pressure dressing over the needle site for 15 minutes, then redress (apply a dry dressing/plaster).  Why A/C/D wrong: These durations do not match the stated 15 minutes."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"Nasogastric (NG) tube intubation is best defined as:","options":["Insertion of a thin, plastic tube inserted into the nostril, toward the esophagus, and down into the stomach","Insertion of a tube into the trachea","Insertion of a catheter into the bladder","Insertion of a tube into the rectum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: NG tube intubation is a procedure in which a thin, plastic tube is inserted into the nostril, toward the esophagus, and down into the stomach.  Why B wrong: Tracheal insertion is endotracheal intubation.  Why C wrong: Bladder insertion is urinary catheterization.  Why D wrong: Rectal insertion is a rectal tube, not NG."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"The NG tube is also commonly known as:","options":["Ryle's tube","Foley catheter","Endotracheal tube","Cannula"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The NG tube is also known as Ryle's tube.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are different devices (urinary catheter, airway tube, IV cannula)."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"\"Orogastric (OG) tube intubation\" differs from nasogastric intubation in that it is:","options":["Inserted through the mouth (oral route) instead of the nostril","Inserted into the trachea","Inserted into the rectum","A permanent surgical placement"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Orogastric (OG) tube intubation is a similar process involving insertion of a plastic tube (orogastric tube) through the mouth.  Why B/C/D wrong: OG is an oral-route gastric tube, not tracheal, rectal, or surgical."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"Which physician/scientist is credited with inventing the NG tube?","options":["Abraham Louis Levin","William Harvey","Joseph Lister","Florence Nightingale"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Abraham Louis Levin invented the NG tube.  Why B/C/D wrong: These individuals are not credited with the NG tube invention per the source."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"A Levin tube is best described as:","options":["A single-lumen multipurpose plastic tube that is commonly used in NG intubation","A double-lumen tube with a \"pigtail\" used for intermittent or continuous suction","A tube used for tracheostomy","A rectal feeding tube"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Levin tube is a single-lumen multipurpose plastic tube that is commonly used in NG intubation.  Why B wrong: That describes the Salem sump tube.  Why C/D wrong: It is not a tracheostomy or rectal tube."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"A Salem sump tube is characterized by:","options":["A double-lumen tube with a \"pigtail,\" used for intermittent or continuous suction","A single lumen only","Being radiopaque-free","Being used only for blood transfusion"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Salem sump tube is a double-lumen tube with a \"pigtail,\" used for intermittent or continuous suction.  Why B wrong: It is double-lumen, not single.  Why C wrong: NG tubes are coated with radiopaque line; the Salem sump is not described as radiopaque-free.  Why D wrong: It is for gastric suction, not blood transfusion."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"Which of the following are listed DIAGNOSTIC indications for NG intubation? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Evaluation of upper GI bleeding, such as the presence of bleeding and volume","Aspiration of gastric fluid content","Administration of radiographic contrast to the GI tract","Gastric decompression","Lavage to prevent vomiting and aspiration"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Diagnostic indications listed are evaluation of upper GI bleeding (presence of bleeding and volume), aspiration of gastric fluid content, and administration of radiographic contrast to the GI tract.  Why D wrong: Gastric decompression is listed under therapeutic indications.  Why E wrong: Prevention of vomiting and aspiration / lavage are therapeutic indications."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"Which of the following are THERAPEUTIC indications for NG intubation? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Gastric decompression, including maintenance of a decompressed state after endotracheal intubation","Relief of symptoms and bowel rest in the setting of small bowel obstruction","To decrease the risk of aspiration of gastric content","To provide nutrition (gavage feeding)","Lavage (irrigation of the stomach), preferably done with Normal Saline (NS 0.9%)"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: All listed items are therapeutic indications: gastric decompression (including after endotracheal intubation), relief of symptoms/bowel rest in small bowel obstruction, decreasing risk of aspiration of gastric content, administration of medications/nutrition (gavage feeding), lavage (irrigation, preferably with Normal Saline 0.9%), bowel irrigation, and prevention of vomiting and aspiration.  All options are correct per the source."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"When performing gastric lavage (irrigation of the stomach) via NG tube, which solution is preferably used?","options":["Normal Saline (NS) 0.9%","Sterile water bolus","5% Dextrose","Tap water always"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Lavage (irrigation of the stomach) is preferably done with Normal Saline (NS 0.9%).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the preferred lavage solution stated in the source."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"Which of the following are ABSOLUTE contraindications to NG intubation? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Severe midface trauma (e.g., basilar skull fracture), due to the possibility of inserting the tube intracranially","Recent nasal and neck surgery","Coagulation abnormality","Esophageal abnormalities such as varices, trauma, or surgeries"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: Absolute contraindications are severe midface trauma (e.g., basilar skull fracture) — because of the possibility of inserting the tube intracranially in this case — and recent nasal and neck surgery.  Why C wrong: Coagulation abnormality is a relative contraindication.  Why D wrong: Esophageal abnormalities (varices, trauma, surgeries) are relative contraindications."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"Which of the following are RELATIVE contraindications to NG intubation? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Coagulation abnormality","Esophageal abnormalities (e.g., varices, trauma, surgeries)","Epistaxis","Laryngectomy","Alkaline ingestion"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Relative contraindications listed are coagulation abnormality, esophageal abnormalities (varices, trauma, surgeries), epistaxis, laryngectomy, and alkaline ingestion.  All options are correct per the source."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"A patient with a basilar skull fracture should NOT have an NG tube inserted nasally primarily because:","options":["The tube may be inserted intracranially through the fracture","It causes hypotension","It is too uncomfortable","It increases urine output"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Severe midface trauma/basilar skull fracture is an absolute contraindication due to the possibility of inserting the tube intracranially.  Why B/C/D wrong: The danger is intracranial misplacement, not these effects."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"Before inserting an NG tube, the appropriate tube size should be chosen according to:","options":["The patient's age","The room temperature","The patient's blood group","The phase of the moon"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The procedure states to choose the appropriately sized tube according to age.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are irrelevant to tube sizing."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"When an NG tube is inserted, it must be passed from the patient's nose, loop around their earlobe and the xiphoid process roughly how far down to reach the stomach?","options":["1–2 cm below the epiglottis","From the nose to the earlobe to the xiphoid process (the NEX measurement: nose, earlobe, xiphoid)","To halfway between the umbilicus and pubis","To the mid-thigh"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The tube is inserted measuring from the patient's nose, loop around the earlobe and the xiphoid process — roughly 1–2 cm below the epiglottis (cricoid process) — halfway between the end of the sternum and its rim, using the NEX method (Nose, Earlobe, Xiphoid), which stands for nose, earlobe, and xiphoid process.  Why A wrong: 1–2 cm below the epiglottis is part of the path description, not the full measurement endpoint.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the NEX landmarks."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"The NEX method for measuring NG tube length stands for:","options":["Nose, Earlobe, Xiphoid","Neck, Esophagus, Xiphoid","Nose, Ear, Xiphoid sternum only","Nasal, Esophageal, Xiphoid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: NEX stands for nose, earlobe, and xiphoid process.  Why B/C/D wrong: These expansions do not match the source."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"After measuring the NG tube length, the nurse should:","options":["Mark the tube at this level to ensure the tube has been inserted far enough into the patient's stomach","Cut the tube at that point","Discard the measurement","Insert without marking"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The tube is marked at this level to ensure that the tube has been inserted far enough into the patient's stomach.  Why B/C/D wrong: The tube is marked (not cut), and the mark guides depth of insertion."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"Typically, what length of NG tube needs to pass to reach an adult patient's stomach (upper oesophageal junction at about 40 cm)?","options":["About 10 cm","About 20 cm","About 40 cm (the upper oesophageal junction is at ~40 cm; this varies in children)","About 80 cm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Usually a length of NG tube about 40 cm reaches the upper oesophageal junction; the length depends on age (shorter in children).  Why A/B/D wrong: These do not match the ~40 cm stated for adults."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"During the NG insertion procedure, to ease passage, the patient should be:","options":["Sitting in high Fowler's position with the head flexed (sitting up) or leaning forward off pillows behind the shoulders","Lying flat with head extended backward","Placed in Trendelenburg","Kept prone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: During the procedure, place the patient sitting in a high Fowler's position, with the head flexed (or leaning forward off pillows behind the shoulders) to ease passage.  Why B/C/D wrong: These positions do not facilitate safe NG passage as described."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"While inserting an NG tube, the nurse should wear gloves that are:","options":["Non-sterile, since the procedure is considered clean (not requiring sterile technique)","Always sterile, as it is a sterile procedure","Not required at all","Double sterile gloves only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to wear non-sterile gloves because the procedure is clean (not considered sterile).  Why B/C/D wrong: NG insertion is a clean procedure requiring non-sterile gloves, not sterile or no gloves."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"Before passing the NG tube, the nurse lubricates the tube with water-soluble jelly. Why is a water-soluble (not oil-based) lubricant used?","options":["Because if oil-based lubricant is aspirated, it can cause lipoid/aspiration pneumonia","Because oil tastes bad","Because water-soluble jelly is cheaper","Because oil dissolves the tube"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A water-soluble jelly is used because it will close the epiglottis and reduce the risk of slip into the trachea/aspiration; oil-based lubricants risk aspiration (lipoid) pneumonia. (Source emphasizes water-soluble lubricant to reduce aspiration risk into the trachea.)  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the clinical reasons given."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"During NG tube insertion, the nurse must WITHDRAW the tube immediately if which of the following occur? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Changes occur in the patient's respiratory status","The tube coils in the mouth","The patient begins to cough or turns cyanotic","In above conditions, the tube may be in the trachea","The tube advances smoothly and the patient swallows water as instructed"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Withdraw the tube immediately if there are changes in respiratory status, the tube coils in the mouth, the patient begins to cough or turns cyanotic — in these conditions the tube may be in the trachea.  Why E wrong: Smooth advancement with the patient swallowing water indicates correct esophageal passage; this is the desired progress, not a reason to withdraw."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"When a conscious, alert patient is being intubated with an NG tube, instructing the client to swallow water as the tube advances helps because:","options":["If the patient loses consciousness, the head should be tilted forward (chin to chest) while sliding the tube","Swallowing closes the epiglottis and helps direct the tube into the esophagus rather than the trachea","Water cleans the throat","It speeds gastric emptying"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For a conscious and alert patient, instruct the client to swallow water as the tube advances; if the patient is semi-conscious, the head should be tilted toward the chest while sliding the tube. Swallowing helps direct the tube into the esophagus (closing the airway) rather than the trachea.  Why A wrong: While the source mentions head tilt for the semi-conscious patient, the primary reason swallowing is used is to direct the tube into the esophagus.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the stated reasons."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"To check that the NG tube is in the correct place and has not curled in the back of the throat, the nurse should:","options":["Withdraw the tube and start again if it has occurred","Push the tube further regardless","Inflate the balloon","Clamp the tube"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Check the back of the client's throat to make sure that the tube is not curled in the back of the throat; withdraw the tube and start again if such curling has occurred.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not correct a curled/misplaced tube."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"Which methods are used to CONFIRM correct NG tube placement, with X-ray being the most accurate and reliable gold standard? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Aspirate stomach contents — gastric aspirate will appear cloudy, green, tan, off-white, bloody, or brown","Check the pH of aspirate — measuring the pH of stomach aspirate is considered more accurate than visual inspection","\"Whoosh\" test — inject up to 5 ml of air into the stomach and listen with a stethoscope over the \"whoosh\" of air into the stomach","Abdominal X-ray — the most accurate, reliable, and gold standard to confirm right placement of the NG tube","Marking of the tube while inserting"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: NG placement confirmation methods include aspirating stomach contents (cloudy, green, tan, off-white, bloody, or brown), checking the pH of the aspirate (more accurate than visual inspection), the \"whoosh\" test (inject up to 5 ml of air and auscultate the stomach), abdominal X-ray (most accurate/reliable/gold standard), and marking of the tube while inserting.  All options are correct per the source."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"Checking the pH of the gastric aspirate to confirm NG tube placement is considered:","options":["More accurate than visual inspection alone","Less accurate than smell","Unnecessary","Only useful in children"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Checking the pH of stomach aspirate is considered more accurate than visual inspection.  Why B/C/D wrong: pH testing is described as a reliable accuracy check, not less accurate, unnecessary, or age-limited."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"The single MOST accurate, reliable, and gold-standard method to confirm correct NG tube placement is:","options":["The \"whoosh\" (auscultation) test","Aspirating stomach contents","Abdominal X-ray","Checking the patient's comfort"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Abdominal X-ray is the most accurate, reliable, and gold standard to confirm right placement of the NG tube.  Why A wrong: The whoosh test is a bedside method but not the gold standard.  Why B wrong: Aspiration is supportive but not the gold standard.  Why D wrong: Patient comfort does not confirm placement."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"(PYQ — RECALL THE NCLEX) To confirm right placement of the NG tube, which method/order is correct?","options":["Aspirate gastric content and assess for 1.5 to 5 pH","Check the auscultation for bowel sounds","Tape an NG tube with nose","Marking of tube while inserting"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The keyed answer is A. Aspirating gastric content and assessing pH (gastric pH approximately 1.5–5) is the indicated confirmation method in this PYQ.  Why B wrong: Bowel sounds do not confirm NG tube placement.  Why C wrong: Taping secures the tube but does not confirm placement.  Why D wrong: Marking helps gauge depth but is not the keyed confirmation step here."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"(PYQ) A nasogastric (NG) tube is being inserted gently downwards and it reached the nasopharynx, when the client shows resistance to insertion. What should be the nurse's next action?","options":["Withdraw the tube","Wait for 5 minutes and further insert the tube","Instruct the patient to swallow or engulf","Rotate the tube and earlobe to xiphoid process"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The keyed answer is C — instructing the patient to swallow (engulf) helps the tube pass beyond the nasopharynx into the esophagus when resistance is met.  Why A wrong: Withdrawing is reserved for signs of airway misplacement (coughing, cyanosis, respiratory change), not simple resistance at the nasopharynx.  Why B wrong: Waiting then forcing the tube further is not the appropriate action.  Why D wrong: Rotation/measurement landmark is not the immediate action for resistance."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"(PYQ) How to measure NG tube length during NG tube insertion procedure?","options":["Nose to ear","Ear to xiphoid process","Mouth to ear and ear to xiphoid process","Nose to earlobe and earlobe to xiphoid process"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The keyed answer is D — measure from the nose to the earlobe and earlobe to the xiphoid process (the NEX method).  Why A/B/C wrong: These omit the full nose-earlobe-xiphoid sequence used to estimate the gastric insertion length."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"Which of the following are recognized COMPLICATIONS of NG tube intubation? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Aspiration","Discomfort","Trauma which leads to epistaxis","Wrong placement: wrong placement of the NG tube into the lungs may allow food and medicine to pass through it that may be fatal to the patient","Abdominal cramping"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Listed NG intubation complications include aspiration, discomfort, trauma which leads to epistaxis, wrong placement into the lungs (allowing food/medicine through it, which may be fatal), and abdominal cramping.  All options are correct per the source."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"Wrong placement of an NG tube into the lungs is dangerous because:","options":["Food and medicine may pass through it, which may be fatal to the patient","It causes mild itching","It improves oxygenation","It has no clinical consequence"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Wrong placement of the NG tube into the lungs may allow food and medicine to pass through it that may be fatal to the patient.  Why B/C/D wrong: Pulmonary misplacement is potentially fatal, not benign or beneficial."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"Which steps are part of the procedure for REMOVAL of the RT/NG tube? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Check the physician's order for removal of the nasogastric tube","Before removing the NG tube, give a reflex (gag) so that it should be checked","Explain the procedure to the client — explanation facilitates client cooperation and understanding","Wash the hands and wear clean disposable gloves","Discontinue suction and separate the tube from suction, if attached"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Removal of the RT/NG tube steps include checking the physician's order, checking the gag reflex before removal, explaining the procedure (facilitates client cooperation/understanding), washing hands and wearing clean disposable gloves, and discontinuing suction and separating the tube from suction if attached.  All options are correct per the source."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"During NG tube removal, the nurse should unpin the client's gown and:","options":["Carefully remove adhesive tape from the bridge of the nose, and place a towel or disposable pad across the client's chest","Pour water down the tube","Inflate a balloon","Re-attach the tube to suction"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Unpin the tube from the client's gown and carefully remove adhesive tape from the bridge of the nose; place a towel or disposable pad across the client's chest (protects the client from contact with gastric secretions).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not removal steps; they contradict the removal procedure."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"During NG tube removal, to prevent aspiration the nurse instructs the client to take a deep breath and hold it. For how long?","options":["For 10 seconds, or perform a Valsalva maneuver","For 60 seconds","For 1 second","No breath holding is needed"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Give the client a sip of water, instruct the client to take a deep breath and hold it for 10 seconds, or perform a Valsalva maneuver, then quickly remove the tube while the client holds the breath by coiling the tube in the hand.  Why B/C/D wrong: The stated duration is 10 seconds (or Valsalva), not these alternatives."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"After removing the NG tube, it should be discarded in a:","options":["Red bag, according to BMWM (Biomedical Waste Management) rules","Regular dustbin","Recycling bin","Patient's bedside drawer"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: After removing the tube, it should be discarded in a red bag according to BMWM.  Why B/C/D wrong: Biomedical (blood/secretion-contaminated) waste must go in the appropriate (red) biomedical-waste bag, not general waste."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"(PYQ) A patient with an NG tube which is attached to low intermittent suction has acidic juices/electrolytes removed, and the patient will develop metabolic disturbance. Which condition is most likely to develop?","options":["Hypochloremia","Metabolic acidosis","Hyponatremia","Metabolic alkalosis"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The keyed answer is D. Loss of acidic gastric contents (HCl) via NG suction removes hydrogen and chloride ions, producing metabolic alkalosis (often hypochloremic).  Why A wrong: Hypochloremia is an electrolyte loss that contributes to but is not the labeled metabolic disturbance keyed here.  Why B wrong: Loss of acid causes alkalosis, not acidosis.  Why C wrong: Hyponatremia is not the keyed answer."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"(PYQ) During the client's medical records review, what condition can put the client at risk for developing hypokalemia? (Patient with NG tube on low suction exhibiting hyponatremia, low pH, low K, and blood pH 7.5.)","options":["Has a history of Addison's disease","Nasogastric suctioning","Burn","Uric acid level of 10.2 mg/dL (59.48 µmol/L)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The keyed answer is B. Nasogastric suctioning removes gastric fluid rich in potassium (and hydrogen), placing the client at risk for hypokalemia (and metabolic alkalosis, consistent with blood pH 7.5).  Why A wrong: Addison's disease typically causes hyperkalemia, not hypokalemia.  Why C wrong: Burns are not the keyed cause of hypokalemia here.  Why D wrong: An elevated uric acid level is unrelated to potassium loss."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"RT tube feeding is also known as:","options":["Gavage","Lavage","Paracentesis","Intubation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: RT tube feeding is also known as gavage — the introduction of foods through an NG tube while the client is unable to take food orally.  Why B wrong: Lavage is irrigation/introduction of fluids through the RT/NG tube.  Why C/D wrong: Paracentesis is fluid aspiration from the peritoneum; intubation is tube insertion."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"Lavage, in the context of RT/NG tubes, refers to:","options":["Introduction of fluids through the RT/NG tube — the introduction of the stomach contents through RT/NG tube, and is the treatment of choice for poison cases","Surgical removal of the stomach","A type of feeding pump","A diagnostic X-ray"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Lavage is the introduction of fluids through the RT/NG tube (introduction/irrigation of stomach contents) and is the treatment of choice for poison cases.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the definition of lavage given."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"For RT tube feeding of a CONSCIOUS patient, the recommended position is:","options":["Sitting in high Fowler's position with the neck flexed","Supine with head flat","Prone","Trendelenburg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In a conscious patient, place the patient sitting in high Fowler's position with the neck flexed.  Why B/C/D wrong: For an unconscious/unconscious patient, supine with neck flexed is used; the conscious patient sits in high Fowler's."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"For RT tube feeding of an UNCONSCIOUS patient, the recommended position is:","options":["Supine with neck flexed","High Fowler's sitting fully upright","Standing","Left lateral with head down"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In an unconscious/unconscious patient, position supine with neck flexed.  Why B wrong: Full upright sitting is for the conscious patient.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the described positions."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"How is the required length of RT tube measured?","options":["From nose to ear, then ear to xiphisternum","From mouth to umbilicus","From chin to knee","From ear to ASIS"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Measure the required length of RT tube from nose to ear, then ear to xiphisternum.  Why B/C/D wrong: These landmark sequences are not the described measurement."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"Match the various markings on the RT tube with the correct anatomical landmark. Which of the following pairings are correct? (Select All That Apply)","options":["40 cm — upper gastroesophageal junction","50 cm — body of the stomach","60 cm — pylorus","65 cm — duodenum","10 cm — duodenum"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Various markings on the RT tube: 40 cm = upper gastroesophageal junction; 50 cm = body of the stomach; 60 cm = pylorus; 65 cm = duodenum.  Why E wrong: The duodenum corresponds to 65 cm, not 10 cm."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"Which of the following are INDICATIONS for an RT (rectal/Ryle's) tube? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Mouth trauma","Unconscious client and difficulty in swallowing","Long bedridden clients","Oropharyngeal or food pipe surgery, peptic ulcer","In cases of pancreatitis, intestines obstruction, and oesophageal varices; poisoning cases"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Indications of the RT tube listed are mouth trauma, unconscious client and difficulty in swallowing, long bedridden clients, oropharyngeal or food pipe surgery / peptic ulcer, and in cases of pancreatitis, intestines obstruction and oesophageal varices, plus poisoning cases.  All options are correct per the source."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"Which of the following are stated PURPOSES of the RT tube? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Prevents aspirations (after gastric surgery)","Aids in gastric decompression","Liquid food administration","Relieve from gaseous distension","To remove excessive gastric acid to prevent ulcer"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The purposes of the RT tube listed are: prevents aspirations (after gastric surgery), aids in gastric decompression, liquid food administration, relieve from gaseous distension, and to remove excessive gastric acid to prevent ulcer.  All options are correct per the source."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"What is the only listed contraindication to RT tube feeding?","options":["Same as RT tube insertion","Diabetes mellitus","Hypertension","Anemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The contraindication for RT tube feeding is stated as \"Same as RT tube insertion.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: These specific conditions are not listed as the contraindication."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"When a feeding tube is used to provide nutrition to patients who cannot obtain nutrition by mouth and are unable to swallow safely or need nutritional supplementation, this is termed:","options":["Enteral feeding or tube feeding","Parenteral nutrition","Intravenous bolus","Subcutaneous feeding"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Tube feeding provides nutrition to patients who cannot obtain nutrition by mouth, are unable to swallow safely, or need nutritional supplementation; the state of being fed by a feeding tube is called gavage / enteral / tube feeding.  Why B wrong: Parenteral nutrition bypasses the GI tract (IV), not via a feeding tube into the stomach.  Why C/D wrong: These are not enteral tube feeding."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"Before introducing feeding through the RT/NG tube, what must the nurse always do to verify position and prevent complications? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Check the position of the client with a tube feeding remains, with the head of bed elevated at least 30–45 degrees","Never feed the client with supine position","Semi-Fowler's or full-Fowler's position prevents aspiration pneumonia and possible death due to pulmonary complications","Check placement of feeding tube each time while offering feeding by aspirating the stomach content or other methods","Feed rapidly in the supine position to save time"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Before introducing feeding, check the client's position with head of bed elevated at least 30–45 degrees; never feed in the supine position; semi-Fowler's or full-Fowler's position prevents aspiration pneumonia and possible death due to pulmonary complications; and check placement of the feeding tube each time while offering feeding by aspirating stomach content or other methods.  Why E wrong: Feeding rapidly in the supine position risks aspiration and is contraindicated."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"During RT/NG tube feeding, the head of the bed should be elevated to at least what angle to prevent aspiration?","options":["10 degrees","15–20 degrees","At least 30–45 degrees","90 degrees flat"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The head of the bed should be elevated at least 30–45 degrees (semi-Fowler's/full-Fowler's) to prevent aspiration.  Why A/B wrong: These angles are below the stated minimum.  Why D wrong: \"90 degrees flat\" is contradictory; the source specifies 30–45 degrees minimum."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"Why should the nurse check the residual stomach content (gastric residual volume) and recheck temperature before each RT/NG feeding?","options":["Because gastric content/residual returns the stomach acid (digestive enzymes) which lead to metabolic disturbances, and to ensure the food's temperature is near body temperature to prevent diarrhea and electrolyte imbalances","To increase feeding speed","To dilute the medication","It has no purpose"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Check the residual volume of the stomach and return it to the stomach (returns the stomach's acid/digestive enzymes which lead to metabolic disturbances if discarded); before offering feeding, ensure that the food's temperature is near body temperature to prevent diarrhea and electrolyte imbalances.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the stated reasons; residual check and temperature control are for safety, not speed/dilution."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"Which methods can be used to introduce RT/NG tube feeding? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Intermittent or bolus feeding using a feeding tube and syringe","Continuous feeding using a feeding pump","Pushing food rapidly with high pressure","Direct intravenous injection"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: There are two types of feeding methods: intermittent or bolus feeding (using a feeding tube and syringe), and continuous feeding (using a feeding pump).  Why C wrong: Rapid, high-pressure pushing is unsafe (risks aspiration/cramping) and not a listed method.  Why D wrong: IV injection is parenteral, not enteral tube feeding."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"In a comatose or unconscious patient receiving RT/NG feeding, the head should be positioned to avoid increased intracranial pressure (ICP). Which is the correct guidance?","options":["Place the patient in head-elevated position or low Fowler's position; if the patient is comatose or unconscious, place the client in low Fowler's position","Place the patient flat and prone","Place the patient in steep Trendelenburg","Keep the head fully extended backward"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Place the patient in a head-elevated position or high Fowler's position (or increased ICP — if the patient is comatose or unconscious, place the client in low Fowler's position, since high Fowler's may not be possible).  Why B/C/D wrong: Prone/flat, steep Trendelenburg, and extended-back positions increase aspiration/ICP risk and are not advised."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"When feeding via RT/NG tube, to ensure the food's temperature is correct, the food should be at what temperature?","options":["Near body temperature (room/lukewarm), to prevent diarrhea and electrolyte imbalances","Ice cold","Boiling hot","Frozen"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Ensure the food's temperature is near body temperature (room/normal saline / lukewarm) to prevent diarrhea and electrolyte imbalances.  Why B/C/D wrong: Extremes of temperature cause cramping, diarrhea, and electrolyte imbalances."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"When feeding via gravity flow, the feeding bag/funnel should be held at what height, and food should NOT be blocked as it flows; the gap between feeds should be how long?","options":["About 30 cm above the client's stomach via gravity flow; do not allow free flow to be blocked; allow a gap of about 2 to 3 hours between feeds","100 cm above; gap of 10 minutes","At stomach level; gap of 30 seconds","Below the stomach; continuous with no gap"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Offer maximum about 30 cm liquid food close to the time (do not offer too much by gravity flow / let the food otherwise flow); allow a gap of about every 2 to 3 hours between feeds. (The source specifies gravity flow at the appropriate height and a 2–3 hour interval between feeds.)  Why B/C/D wrong: These heights and intervals do not match the source."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"After feeding the client via RT/NG tube, the nurse should flush the tube with how much water, and maintain patency of the tube to prevent which complications?","options":["Flush with 30–50 ml water to maintain patency, and place the client in an upright or sitting position for 30 minutes to prevent aspiration in adults and children, providing right side-lying position for easy digestion (an anatomical direction of disclosure)","Flush with 500 ml water, then lie flat","Do not flush; lay prone","Flush with 1 ml; sit for 5 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: After feeding the client, flush the tube with 30–50 ml of water to maintain patency; place the client in an upright or sitting position for 30 minutes to prevent aspiration in adults and children, providing the right side-lying position for easy digestion.  Why B/C/D wrong: These volumes/positions/durations contradict the source."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"Before administering medication through an RT/NG tube, the medication should be:","options":["Crushed and dissolved in 15–30 ml of water, after which the medications/tube should be flushed with 30–50 ml of water (or NS)","Given as a whole tablet pushed dry","Never crushed under any circumstance","Mixed with feeding formula only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Before administration of medication, it should be crushed and dissolved in 15–30 ml of water; after giving the medication, the tube should be flushed with 30–50 ml of water (or NS).  Why B wrong: Dry whole tablets can clog the tube.  Why C wrong: The source instructs crushing and dissolving (for crushable medications).  Why D wrong: It should be dissolved in water and flushed, not mixed only with feeding formula."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"When flushing the RT/NG tube after feeding, what volume and solution should be used per the precautions?","options":["Flush with 30–50 ml of water or NS (every 4 hours when in continuous feeding)","Flush with 5 ml of air only","Flush with 200 ml of juice","No flushing is required"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The tube should be flushed with 30–50 ml of water or NS (the source notes flushing intervals, e.g., every 4 hours during continuous feeding) to maintain patency.  Why B/C/D wrong: These volumes/solutions are not the recommended flush."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"During continuous RT tube feeding, how often should the procedure/feeding set considerations (e.g., change/flush) be addressed, and how often should the feeding bag/tube be changed?","options":["Change the RT tube every 7 days (if the tube is silicone, it can then be changed every 21 days); change the feeding bag every 21 days, and bag/connector every 8 hrs","Change everything every hour","Never change the tube","Change the tube every 6 months"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states to change the RT tube every 7 days (if the tube is siliconized, it can then be changed every 21 days), change the feeding bag every 21 days, and the bag/connector every 8 hours.  Why B/C/D wrong: These intervals contradict the stated schedule."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"Which of the following are COMPLICATIONS of RT feeding? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Diarrhea — the most common; to prevent it, administer feeding slowly and at room temperature","Nausea and vomiting","Clogged tube, blockage, mal-position, and displacement of the tube","Aspiration — to prevent it, verify tube placement and maintain head and elevated position; aspiration pneumonia","Abdominal distension and electrolyte imbalances"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Complications of RT feeding listed include diarrhea (most common — prevented by administering feeding slowly and at room temperature), nausea and vomiting, clogged tube/blockage/mal-position and displacement of the tube, aspiration (prevented by verifying tube placement and maintaining a head-elevated position) and aspiration pneumonia, and abdominal distension and electrolyte imbalances.  All options are correct per the source."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"The MOST COMMON complication of RT feeding is diarrhea. Which interventions help prevent it?","options":["Administer feeding slowly and at room temperature","Administer feeding rapidly and cold","Feed in supine position","Use ice-cold formula"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Diarrhea is the most common complication; to prevent it, administer feeding slowly and at room temperature.  Why B/C/D wrong: Rapid or cold feeding and supine positioning worsen diarrhea/aspiration risk."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"(PYQ — RECALL THE NCLEX) A patient receiving continuous enteral feeding should be placed in which position to prevent aspiration?","options":["Head elevated","Keep supine","Left lateral","Sims'"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The keyed answer is A — a patient on continuous enteral feeding should be placed in a head-elevated position to prevent aspiration.  Why B wrong: Supine position increases aspiration risk during feeding.  Why C wrong: Left lateral is not the indicated anti-aspiration position for feeding.  Why D wrong: Sims' position is not the keyed answer."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"Which of the following are PRECAUTIONS during RT feeding? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Always assess the right placement of the tube before offering the feeding and medication","Place the patient in a high Fowler's position before offering the feeding/medication, and it should be maintained at least 30 minutes after feeding","Before offering feeding, always assess the bowel sounds, and do not administer feeding if bowel sounds are absent","If the patient vomits, then stop the tube feeding and place the patient in a side-lying position; do the suction if needed","Medication (tablets and capsules) should dissolve in 15–30 ml of water before offering and after, and the tube should be flushed by 30–60 ml of water or normal saline"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Precautions during RT feeding listed: always assess the right placement of the tube before feeding/medication; place the patient in high Fowler's position before feeding and maintain it at least 30 minutes after feeding; assess bowel sounds before feeding and do not administer if bowel sounds are absent; if the patient vomits, stop tube feeding and place in side-lying position and suction if needed; medications (tablets/capsules) should dissolve in 15–30 ml of water before offering and the tube should be flushed with 30–60 ml of water or normal saline after.  All options are correct per the source."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"During RT feeding, if a patient vomits, the nurse should:","options":["Stop the tube feeding, place the patient in a side-lying position, and perform suction if needed","Continue feeding faster","Lay the patient flat on the back","Give more medication"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: If the patient vomits, stop the tube feeding, place the patient in a side-lying position, and perform suction if needed (to prevent aspiration).  Why B/C/D wrong: Continuing feeding, supine positioning, or extra medication increase aspiration risk and are inappropriate."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"Before offering RT feeding, the nurse should assess bowel sounds. What action is correct if bowel sounds are ABSENT?","options":["Do not administer the feeding","Feed immediately","Double the feed volume","Ignore and proceed"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Before offering feeding, always assess the bowel sounds, and do not administer feeding if bowel sounds are absent (absent bowel sounds suggest impaired motility/ileus and risk of aspiration/distension).  Why B/C/D wrong: Feeding without bowel sounds risks aspiration, distension, and harm."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"The high Fowler's position used before RT feeding should be maintained for at least how long AFTER feeding to prevent aspiration?","options":["At least 30 minutes","At least 5 minutes","At least 6 hours","No maintenance needed"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The high Fowler's position should be maintained at least 30 minutes after feeding.  Why B/C/D wrong: These durations contradict the stated minimum of 30 minutes."},{"section":"Batch 037 · Abdominal paracentesis, Nasogastric tube intubation…","question":"If the RT/NG tube is silicone (siliconized), how often can it be changed compared with a non-silicone tube (which is changed every 7 days)?","options":["Every 21 days","Every 7 days (same)","Every 24 hours","Never"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A non-silicone RT tube is changed every 7 days; if the tube is siliconized, it can be changed every 21 days.  Why B/C/D wrong: These intervals do not match the silicone-tube schedule of 21 days."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"A Levin tube used for gastric lavage or gavage is best described as which type of nasogastric tube?","options":["Double-lumen tube with one port for suction and one for air vent","Single-lumen tube used for intermittent suctioning","Triple-lumen tube with a gastric balloon","Single-lumen tube used for continuous suctioning"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Levin tube is a single-lumen tube used for lavage or gavage and for intermittent suctioning.  Why A wrong: A second port for an air vent describes the Salem sump tube, not the Levin.  Why C wrong: A balloon-bearing tube describes esophageal tubes (Sengstaken-Blakemore), not the Levin.  Why D wrong: Continuous suctioning describes the Levacuntorde/Cantor type intestinal tubes, not the Levin."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"The Salem sump tube differs from the Levin tube primarily because the Salem sump tube is:","options":["A single-lumen tube","A double-lumen tube with one port for suction and one port for air vent","Used only for continuous suction","Used to apply tamponade pressure to esophageal varices"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Salem sump is a double-lumen tube — one lumen for suction and one port for air vent.  Why A wrong: A single-lumen description fits the Levin tube.  Why C wrong: The air-vent design actually makes the Salem sump safe for continuous as well as intermittent suction; \"only continuous\" is incorrect.  Why D wrong: Tamponade of varices is the function of esophageal balloon tubes."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"A Cantor tube, used in cases of intestinal obstruction for decompression, is a:","options":["Single-lumen tube with a mercury weight","Double-lumen tube with continuous suctioning","Triple-lumen esophageal tube","Tube used only for nasogastric feeding"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Cantor tube is a single-lumen intestinal tube used for intestinal decompression.  Why B wrong: A double-lumen continuous-suction tube describes the Miller-Abbott tube.  Why C wrong: Triple-lumen describes the Sengstaken-Blakemore esophageal tube.  Why D wrong: It is an intestinal decompression tube, not a feeding tube."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"The Harris tube, used in intestinal decompression, is characterized as:","options":["A double-lumen tube","A single-lumen tube","A triple-lumen tube with two balloons","A tube with an air-vent port"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Harris tube is a single-lumen intestinal tube.  Why A wrong: Double-lumen with continuous suctioning is the Miller-Abbott tube.  Why C wrong: Triple-lumen with balloons is the Sengstaken-Blakemore esophageal tube.  Why D wrong: The air-vent port describes the Salem sump tube."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"The Miller-Abbott tube used for intestinal decompression is a:","options":["Single-lumen tube","Double-lumen tube used with continuous suctioning","Single-lumen tube with a gastric balloon","Triple-lumen esophageal tamponade tube"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Miller-Abbott tube is a double-lumen intestinal tube used with continuous suctioning.  Why A wrong: Single-lumen describes the Cantor and Harris tubes.  Why C wrong: A gastric balloon is a feature of esophageal varices tubes.  Why D wrong: Esophageal tamponade is the Sengstaken-Blakemore function."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"The Sengstaken-Blakemore tube is a triple-lumen tube. How many of its lumens are used for gastric aspiration?","options":["None","One","Two","Three"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: It is a 3-lumen tube; one lumen is for gastric aspiration. (The other lumens inflate the esophageal and gastric balloons.)  Why A wrong: It does have a dedicated gastric aspiration lumen.  Why C/D wrong: Only one of the three lumens is for gastric aspiration; the remaining two are for balloon inflation."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"A nurse caring for a patient with a Sengstaken-Blakemore esophageal tube notes the inflation balloons. According to the source, how many balloons does this tube carry?","options":["1","2","3","4"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Sengstaken-Blakemore tube has 2 balloons — an esophageal balloon (inflated at the 1st lumen) and a gastric balloon (inflated at the 2nd lumen), with the 3rd lumen for gastric aspiration.  Why A wrong: A single balloon would not provide both esophageal and gastric tamponade.  Why C/D wrong: There are exactly two balloons; the third lumen is for aspiration, not a balloon."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"Why must a scissor always be kept at the bedside of a patient with a Sengstaken-Blakemore tube?","options":["To open feeding packets quickly","So any respiratory distress that occurs from balloon migration can be relieved immediately by cutting the tube","To trim excess external tubing","To remove sutures during weaning"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A scissor must be kept at the bedside so that if respiratory distress (from balloon migration/airway obstruction) occurs, the tube can be cut immediately to deflate balloons and protect the airway.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the life-saving reason; the indication is emergency airway protection from balloon-related distress."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"The Minnesota tube differs from the Sengstaken-Blakemore tube in that it is a:","options":["Single-lumen tube","Two-lumen tube","Three-lumen tube","Four-lumen tube"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The Minnesota tube is a four-lumen esophageal tube.  Why A/B wrong: It has more lumens than these.  Why C wrong: Three lumens describes the Sengstaken-Blakemore tube; the Minnesota adds a fourth lumen (for esophageal aspiration)."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"\"Recall the NCLEX\": Before administering a tablet medication through a feeding tube, in approximately how much water should the crushed/dissolved tablet be mixed?","options":["5–10 mL","30–50 mL","20–30 mL","10–15 mL"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source's keyed answer states the medication should be dissolved/mixed in 30–50 mL of water, then flushed with 30–50 mL after administration.  Why A/C/D wrong: These smaller volumes do not match the keyed answer (30–50 mL) for dissolving the tablet for tube administration."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"After giving a dissolved tablet through a feeding tube, the tube should be flushed with how much water?","options":["5–10 mL","10–15 mL","30–50 mL","100 mL"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the tube should be flushed with 30–50 mL after the medication is given.  Why A/B wrong: These volumes are too small per the source.  Why D wrong: 100 mL is not specified by the source."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"Renal biopsy is best defined as:","options":["A non-invasive imaging study of the kidney","A minimally invasive procedure in which a needle is inserted into the kidney to obtain a sample of tissue for examination","A urine collection test for kidney function","A surgical removal of the entire kidney"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Renal biopsy is a minimally invasive procedure involving insertion of a needle into the kidney to obtain a tissue sample for examination.  Why A wrong: It is invasive (needle insertion), not non-invasive imaging.  Why C wrong: It samples tissue, not urine.  Why D wrong: Biopsy obtains a sample; it is not a nephrectomy."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"In which anatomical space is the normal adult kidney situated, a fact relevant to renal biopsy approach?","options":["Intraperitoneal space","Retroperitoneal space","Subdiaphragmatic space","Pelvic cavity"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The normal adult kidney is situated in the retroperitoneal space.  Why A wrong: The kidneys are retroperitoneal, not intraperitoneal.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the location of the kidneys per the source."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"A renal biopsy performed for a particular lesion, for example a tumor, is termed a:","options":["Non-targeted renal biopsy","Targeted renal biopsy","Open surgical biopsy","Percutaneous biopsy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A targeted renal biopsy is performed for a particular lesion, for example a tumor.  Why A wrong: A non-targeted biopsy is used for general/diffuse renal conditions.  Why C/D wrong: These are approaches/techniques, not the targeting classification."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"A non-targeted renal biopsy is commonly used for:","options":["Sampling a specific tumor","Other general renal conditions","Removing a kidney stone","Draining a renal abscess"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Non-targeted renal biopsy is commonly used for other (general/diffuse) renal conditions.  Why A wrong: Sampling a specific lesion/tumor is a targeted biopsy.  Why C/D wrong: These are therapeutic procedures, not the purpose of a non-targeted biopsy."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"Which of the following is NOT one of the techniques of renal biopsy listed in the source?","options":["Percutaneous","Transvenous","Laparoscopic","Endoscopic ureteroscopy"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The four techniques listed are percutaneous, transvenous, laparoscopic, and open surgical approach. Endoscopic ureteroscopy is not listed.  Why A/B/C wrong: Percutaneous, transvenous, and laparoscopic are all listed techniques of renal biopsy."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"The purpose of a renal biopsy is to perform what type of examination on the kidney sample?","options":["Microbiological culture only","Cytological and histological examination","Genetic karyotyping only","Immunofluorescence urinalysis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The purpose of renal biopsy is cytological and histological examination.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies cytological and histological exam, not these."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"For a renal biopsy, the needle is typically inserted at which anatomical location?","options":["8th thoracic vertebra in the midline","11th thoracic vertebra to 1st lumbar vertebra (T11–L1) in the flank region","4th lumbar vertebra in the suprapubic region","Sacroiliac joint"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The needle insertion site is the 11th thoracic vertebra to the 1st lumbar vertebra (T11–L1) in the flank region.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the source-specified T11–L1 flank insertion site."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"Which of the following is an ABSOLUTE contraindication to renal biopsy?","options":["Azotemia","Uncontrolled severe high blood pressure","Pregnancy","Urinary tract infection"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Uncontrolled severe high blood pressure is listed as an absolute contraindication (along with bleeding diathesis/disorders, uncooperative patient, and a solitary/nonfunctioning kidney).  Why A wrong: Azotemia/uraemia is a relative contraindication.  Why C wrong: Pregnancy is a relative contraindication.  Why D wrong: Urinary tract infection is a relative contraindication."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"A patient scheduled for renal biopsy has only one functioning kidney. This represents which type of contraindication?","options":["Relative contraindication","Absolute contraindication","Not a contraindication","An indication"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A solitary/non-functioning kidney is listed as an absolute contraindication to renal biopsy.  Why A wrong: It is absolute, not relative.  Why C/D wrong: It is clearly contraindicated, not permitted or indicated."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"Which of the following are RELATIVE contraindications to renal biopsy? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Azotemia or uraemia","Certain anatomical abnormalities of the kidney","Skin infection at the biopsy site","Medications that interfere with clotting (e.g. heparin)","Pregnancy"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: All listed — azotemia/uraemia, certain anatomical abnormalities, skin infection at the biopsy site, medications interfering with clotting (e.g. heparin), pregnancy, urinary tract infection, and obesity — are relative contraindications.  Why all correct: Each option is explicitly listed under relative contraindications in the source."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"During the pre-procedure nursing care for renal biopsy, when should the nurse keep the patient NPO?","options":["For 30 minutes before the procedure","For at least 6 hours before the procedure","For 1 hour before the procedure","NPO is not required"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pre-procedure care includes keeping NPO for 6 hours before the procedure.  Why A/C wrong: These durations are too short per the source.  Why D wrong: NPO status is specifically required."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"Which of the following are appropriate PRE-procedure nursing actions for a renal biopsy? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Assess all vital parameters at least hours before the procedure","Assess baseline coagulation studies","Take an informed consent","Keep NPO for 6 hours before the procedure","Place the patient in prone position with a pillow under the abdomen and shoulders"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Pre-procedure care includes assessing vital parameters, assessing baseline coagulation studies, taking informed consent, and keeping NPO for 6 hours.  Why E wrong: Placing the patient prone with a pillow under the abdomen and shoulders is an intra-procedure (during-procedure) nursing action, not pre-procedure."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"During a renal biopsy, in which position should the nurse place the patient?","options":["Supine with knees flexed","Prone position with a pillow under the abdomen and shoulders","Left lateral decubitus","High Fowler's position"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: During the procedure the patient is placed in the prone position with a pillow under the abdomen and shoulders.  Why A/C/D wrong: These positions are not the source-specified position for renal biopsy."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"Following a renal biopsy, the nurse places the patient in the supine position with a back roll, and provides additional support to the puncture site for at least:","options":["30 minutes","6 hours","24 hours","48 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to place the patient on strict bed rest in the supine position with a back roll and provide additional support to the puncture site for at least 6 hours.  Why A wrong: 30 minutes is the duration for asking the patient to take a deep breath and hold, not bed-rest support.  Why C/D wrong: These durations exceed the source-specified 6 hours."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"During a percutaneous renal biopsy, the nurse instructs the patient to take a deep breath and hold it, just before the needle is inserted. Why?","options":["To increase blood pressure","Because it prevents the risk of diaphragm injury","To improve oxygen saturation","To distend the renal pelvis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The patient is asked to take a deep breath and hold (Valsalva-type maneuver) just before needle insertion because it prevents the risk of diaphragm injury.  Why A/C/D wrong: The stated rationale is prevention of diaphragm injury, not these effects."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"After a renal biopsy, the nurse should provide reassurance to the biopsy site for at least how long?","options":["5 minutes","30 minutes","2 hours","6 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to provide pressure/support to the biopsy site for at least 30 minutes.  Why A/C/D wrong: These durations do not match the source's 30 minutes for site pressure."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"Post renal biopsy, monitoring all vital parameters — especially which finding — indicates possible bleeding or haemorrhage?","options":["Hypertension and tachycardia","Hypotension and tachycardia","Bradycardia and hypertension","Fever and bradypnea"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The nurse should monitor for hypotension and tachycardia, which indicate bleeding or haemorrhage.  Why A wrong: Hypotension (not hypertension) signals blood loss.  Why C/D wrong: These combinations are not the classic early signs of post-biopsy haemorrhage described."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"After a renal biopsy, the nurse should instruct the patient to immediately report which of the following? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Signs of bleeding","Increased temperature","A 30-minute period of bed rest","Gross or mild bleeding observed","Normal urine output"],"correct":"A,B,D","rationale":"Why correct: The patient should observe for and report signs of bleeding, increased temperature (sign of infection/contact provider), and gross or mild bleeding.  Why C wrong: Bed rest is a nursing instruction, not a finding to report.  Why E wrong: Normal urine output is expected and is not a reportable abnormality."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"Which complication is identified as the MOST COMMON complication of renal biopsy?","options":["Pneumothorax","Renal hematoma and bleeding (blood in urine)","Pain","Haemorrhage requiring nephrectomy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Renal hematoma and bleeding (blood in urine) is listed as the most common complication of renal biopsy.  Why A wrong: Pneumothorax is a listed complication but not the most common.  Why C wrong: Pain is a complication but not noted as most common.  Why D wrong: Haemorrhage is a complication, but the source specifically labels renal hematoma/bleeding (hematuria) as most common."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"Which of the following are listed complications of renal biopsy? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Renal hematoma and bleeding (blood in urine)","Pain","Pneumothorax","Haemorrhage","Diaphragm injury (avoided by deep breath hold)"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Renal hematoma/bleeding, pain, pneumothorax, and haemorrhage are listed complications; diaphragm injury is the risk that the deep-breath-hold maneuver is meant to prevent.  Why all correct: Each is referenced in the renal biopsy complications/precautions on the source pages."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"Bone marrow biopsy and aspiration is best defined as an invasive procedure in which a small quantity of bone marrow is obtained from various sites of the bones. Which statement distinguishes bone marrow aspiration from biopsy?","options":["Aspiration removes a small solid piece of bone with marrow; biopsy removes fluid","Aspiration removes a small amount of fluid and cells from the bone marrow; biopsy removes a small (solid) sample of bone marrow","Both remove only fluid","Both remove only solid bone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Bone marrow aspiration removes a small amount of fluid and cells from the bone marrow, whereas bone marrow biopsy removes a small (solid) sample/piece of bone marrow.  Why A wrong: It reverses the two definitions.  Why C/D wrong: The two differ — one removes fluid/cells, the other removes a solid sample."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"A bone marrow biopsy is usually performed using which type of needle?","options":["Illinois-type aspiration needle","Jamshidi needle","Menghini needle","Veress needle"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A bone marrow biopsy is usually done using the jamshidi needle.  Why A wrong: The Illinois-type aspiration needle is used for bone marrow aspiration.  Why C wrong: The Menghini needle is used for renal/liver biopsy contexts, not specified for marrow biopsy here.  Why D wrong: A Veress needle is used in laparoscopy, not marrow biopsy."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"Which of the following are listed indications for bone marrow biopsy and aspiration? (Select All That Apply)","options":["To diagnose blood cancer","To diagnose hematologic disorders such as leukemia, multiple myeloma, lymphoma, unexplained anemia, and myelodysplastic syndrome","To identify blood cell disorders","Bone marrow transplantation","To treat osteoarthritis"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Listed indications include diagnosing blood cancer; diagnosing hematologic disorders (leukemia, multiple myeloma, lymphoma, unexplained anemia, myelodysplastic syndrome); identifying blood cell disorders; and bone marrow transplantation.  Why E wrong: Treating osteoarthritis is not a listed indication."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"Which of the following is a contraindication to bone marrow biopsy?","options":["Mild anemia","Severe bleeding diatheses such as severe hemophilia or disseminated intravascular coagulopathy","Suspected leukemia","Lymphoma"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Severe bleeding diatheses such as severe hemophilia or disseminated intravascular coagulopathy (DIC) are contraindications. Performing a sternal biopsy in patients over age 12 years is also contraindicated.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are indications for the procedure, not contraindications."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"According to the source note, thrombocytopenia is considered to be:","options":["An absolute contraindication to bone marrow biopsy","NOT a contraindication to bone marrow biopsy","A relative contraindication","An indication for sternal biopsy in children"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source note states that thrombocytopenia is NOT a contraindication to bone marrow biopsy.  Why A/C wrong: It is explicitly stated to not be a contraindication.  Why D wrong: Sternal biopsy is contraindicated in children/those under 12, and thrombocytopenia is unrelated to that statement."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"For bone marrow aspiration and biopsy in adults, the MOST PREFERRED needle insertion site is the:","options":["Sternum","Posterior superior iliac spine (PSIS)","Anterior tibial tubercle","Spinous process of vertebra"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most preferred site for bone marrow aspiration and biopsy in adults is the posterior superior iliac spine (PSIS).  Why A wrong: The sternum is an alternative aspiration site but is contraindicated for biopsy and in patients over 12 years.  Why C wrong: The tibial tubercle is used in infants/young children.  Why D wrong: The spinous process of vertebra is a spinous-process site, not the most preferred PSIS."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"A Jamshidi needle for bone marrow biopsy is preferred at which site?","options":["Sternum","PSIS (posterior superior iliac spine)","Anterior superior iliac crest","Tibia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A Jamshidi needle is preferred for biopsy at the PSIC/PSIS (posterior superior iliac spine) site.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the source-specified preferred site for the Jamshidi biopsy needle."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"When using the posterior superior iliac spine (PSIS) site for bone marrow aspiration/biopsy, what is the preferred patient position?","options":["Prone position","Supine position","Lateral decubitus or prone position","High Fowler's"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: For the PSIS approach, the prone or lateral decubitus position is used (the source lists prone and lateral lateral-decubitus positioning with knees flexed at 45° as second choice for the PSIS site).  Why B wrong: Supine is the preferred position for the anterior superior iliac crest (ASIC) and sternum sites, not PSIS.  Why D wrong: High Fowler's is not used for marrow aspiration."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"When the anterior superior iliac crest (ASIC) is used for bone marrow aspiration, the preferred patient position is:","options":["Prone position","Supine position","Lateral decubitus","Sitting upright"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For the anterior superior iliac crest (ASIC) site, the preferred position is supine.  Why A wrong: Prone is preferred for the posterior site (PSIS).  Why C/D wrong: These are not the source-specified ASIC position."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"When the sternum or breast bone is used as a bone marrow aspiration site, the needle is inserted at the sterno-manubrial angle to one side of the midline. The preferred patient position is:","options":["Prone","Supine","Lateral","Sitting"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For the sternum/breast bone site, the needle is inserted at the sterno-manubrial angle, to one side of the midline, with the patient in the supine position.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the source-specified position for sternal aspiration."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"Sternal bone marrow aspiration is contraindicated in patients of which age group, and carries which most common complication?","options":["Contraindicated under 6 years; most common complication is infection","Contraindicated at >12 years of age; most common complication is accidental puncture of the lungs","Contraindicated over 50 years; most common complication is fracture","No age contraindication; most common complication is bleeding"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Sternal aspiration is contraindicated at >12 years of age, and its most common complication is accidental puncture of the lungs.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the source's age cutoff (>12 years) or its stated most common complication (lung puncture)."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"For a young child requiring bone marrow aspiration, which site of the tibia is most suitable?","options":["Spinous process of vertebra","Just below the tibial tubercle (medial tibia)","Distal fibula","Calcaneus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The medial site of tibia — just below the tibial tubercle — is most suitable in children younger than 2 years.  Why A wrong: The spinous process of vertebra is another body site but not the tibial site for young children.  Why C/D wrong: These are not bone marrow aspiration sites listed."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"Which of the following are body bones/sites listed for bone marrow aspiration? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Scapula","Cranium (skull)","Ribs","Long bones","Mandible"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists scapula, cranium, ribs, and long bones among other body bones used for marrow aspiration.  Why E wrong: The mandible is not listed as a marrow aspiration site."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"\"Practice Question\": Which solution is used for bone marrow biopsy/aspiration site skin preparation (preservation)? (per source key)","options":["Formalin","Hydrogen peroxide (H2O2)","Cidex","Isopropyl alcohol"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source's keyed answer for the solution used for the specimen/skin preparation in this practice question is Formalin (Right answer A).  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the source's keyed answer (A. Formalin)."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"\"Practice Question\": Per the source key, which structure is referred to in the second practice item about needle insertion sites?","options":["Sternum","Tibia","Iliac","Scapula"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source's keyed answer for the second practice question is B (Tibia).  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the keyed answer (B)."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"Which needle is described as the type used in bone marrow aspiration?","options":["Jamshidi biopsy needle","Illinois-type aspiration needle, requiring fluid 0.5–1 mL","Menghini needle","Veress needle"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The needle used in bone marrow aspiration is the Illinois-type aspiration needle, requiring fluid 0.5–1 mL.  Why A wrong: The Jamshidi needle is used for the biopsy, not aspiration.  Why C/D wrong: These needles are not the aspiration needle described."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"For a bone marrow biopsy, the Jamshidi (biopsy needle / trephine) needle is advanced so that the marrow sample obtained should be at least:","options":["0.5 cm in length","1 cm in length","1.5 to 2 cm in length","5 cm in length"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The biopsy sample obtained with the Jamshidi/auto-type biopsy needle should be at least 1.5 to 2 cm in length.  Why A/B/D wrong: These lengths do not match the source-specified 1.5 to 2 cm."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"Why is the posterior iliac crest the preferred site for bone marrow aspiration and biopsy in adults? (Select All That Apply)","options":["There are no major nerves in the area","The exact anatomic area is treated","If the patient is immobile or the posterior iliac crest is inaccessible due to obesity or injury, the anterior iliac crest can be used","The sternum is only suitable for aspiration samples and may be used in certain circumstances (e.g. immobile patients) from where it is not possible to obtain a sample from other areas or when trephine biopsy is not required","It is closest to the heart"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The posterior iliac crest is preferred because there are no major nerves in the area, the exact anatomic area is treated; if inaccessible (obesity/injury) the anterior iliac crest is used; and the sternum is only suitable for aspiration samples (used when trephine biopsy not required or sample cannot be obtained elsewhere).  Why E wrong: Proximity to the heart is not a stated reason and the sternum (near heart/lungs) is actually higher-risk."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"Which of the following are correct PRE-procedure / during-procedure nursing actions for bone marrow aspiration? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Take informed consent","Assess all vitals before the procedure","Provide positioning to the patient according to biopsy site","Clean the site and inject a local anesthetic such as lidocaine or procaine at the biopsy site","Skip skin cleansing because the marrow is sterile"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Pre/during procedure care includes taking informed consent, assessing vitals, positioning per biopsy site (prone/lateral decubitus for PSIC; supine for sternum/ASIC), cleansing the site, and injecting a local anesthetic (lidocaine/procaine).  Why E wrong: The site must be cleansed; skipping cleansing risks infection."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"During bone marrow aspiration and biopsy, which procedure is typically performed FIRST?","options":["The biopsy is performed first, then aspiration","Bone marrow aspiration is performed first, then biopsy","Both are performed simultaneously through the same needle","Neither requires the other"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Typically, bone marrow aspiration is performed first, then biopsy. (Subsequently the biopsy is performed if indicated, using a different larger trephine/Jamshidi needle.)  Why A wrong: Aspiration precedes biopsy, not the reverse.  Why C wrong: They are sequential, often using different needles.  Why D wrong: The source describes performing both in sequence when indicated."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"Which of the following are appropriate POST-procedure nursing care points after bone marrow aspiration/biopsy? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Apply pressure with the thumb or fingers to the insertion site for almost 5 minutes until bleeding has stopped","Remove drapes and completely remove any povidone-iodine from the skin with alcohol swabs or warm water to avoid potential itching or an allergic response","Affix clean or antibiotic-soaked gauze pads over the puncture site using pressure tape","Encourage the patient to still lie in position with his or her weight or incision site to assist hemostasis for an additional 10 minutes","Massage the site vigorously to disperse the marrow"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Post-procedure care includes applying digital pressure for ~5 minutes until bleeding stops, removing drapes and cleaning povidone-iodine off skin (to avoid itching/allergy), affixing clean/antibiotic-soaked gauze with pressure tape, and having the patient lie with weight on the incision site for an additional ~10 minutes to assist hemostasis.  Why E wrong: Vigorous massage is not recommended and would promote bleeding, not hemostasis."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"After a bone marrow biopsy, for how long should the patient be advised to avoid swimming?","options":["12 hours","24 hours","48 hours","1 week"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source advises avoiding swimming for 48 hours after the procedure.  Why A/B/D wrong: These durations do not match the source-specified 48 hours."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"After bone marrow biopsy, the nurse advises the patient to contact the provider if pain persists for more than how long, or if bleeding is noted at the procedure site?","options":[">6 hours",">12 hours",">24 hours",">48 hours"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The patient should be advised to contact the provider if pain persists for >24 hours or if bleeding is noted at the procedure site in the next few days.  Why A/B/D wrong: These do not match the source-specified >24 hours."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"After bone marrow biopsy, the nurse warns the patient to contact the provider immediately if a temperature greater than which value is experienced (a sign of infection)?","options":["99°F (37.2°C)","100°F (37.8°C)","101°F (38.3°C)","103°F (39.4°C)"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The patient should contact the provider immediately if temperature >101°F (38.3°C) or there are other signs of infection (e.g., inflammation, pus at the biopsy site).  Why A/B/D wrong: These temperatures do not match the source threshold of >101°F/38.3°C."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"Bone marrow post-procedure pain can be managed with which over-the-counter analgesic per the source?","options":["Aspirin","Acetaminophen","Ibuprofen","Naproxen"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source advises managing post-procedural pain with acetaminophen.  Why A wrong: Aspirin is specifically to be avoided post-procedure due to bleeding risk.  Why C/D wrong: NSAIDs are not the agent the source recommends for post-procedure pain control here."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"Which complication is identified as the MOST COMMON and most serious complication of bone marrow biopsy?","options":["Pain","Bleeding or haemorrhage","Infection","Swelling and redness at the insertion site"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Bleeding or haemorrhage is the most common and most serious complication, especially in patients with thrombocytopenia and in those taking aspirin, warfarin, or heparin.  Why A wrong: Persistent pain is a complication but not labeled most serious.  Why C wrong: Infection is a complication but not labeled most common/serious.  Why D wrong: Swelling/redness is a sign of complication but not the most common/serious one."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"Which of the following are listed complications of bone marrow biopsy? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Bleeding or haemorrhage","Swelling and redness at insertion site","Persistent pain","Trauma in surrounding structures","Infection"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: All listed — bleeding/haemorrhage, swelling and redness at the insertion site, persistent pain, trauma in surrounding structures, and infection — are complications of bone marrow biopsy.  Why all correct: Each is explicitly stated in the source's complications list."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"Tracheostomy is best defined as:","options":["Permanent removal of the trachea","Artificial opening of the trachea through the anterior neck, maintaining a patent airway","Insertion of an endotracheal tube through the mouth","Surgical repair of the vocal cords"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A tracheostomy is an artificial opening of the trachea through the anterior neck, maintaining a patent airway.  Why A wrong: It is an opening, not removal, of the trachea.  Why C wrong: That describes endotracheal intubation, not tracheostomy.  Why D wrong: It is not vocal cord repair."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"A patient who has been intubated is usually scheduled for tracheostomy if intubation has lasted longer than approximately:","options":["3–5 hours","10–15 hours, on average 12 hours","24 hours","14 days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states tracheostomy is normally considered when intubation has lasted about 10–15 hours, on average 12 hours.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the source-specified 10–15 hours (average 12 hours) duration."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"The trachea is surrounded by how many C-shaped cartilage rings, which are made by which type of cartilage?","options":["12–14 rings, made by elastic cartilage","16–20 rings, made by hyaline cartilage","6–8 rings, made by fibrocartilage","20–25 rings, made by hyaline cartilage"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The trachea is surrounded by 16–20 C-shaped cartilage rings made by hyaline cartilage.  Why A/C/D wrong: These ring counts/cartilage types do not match the source."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"The first ring that connects the trachea to the larynx is called a:","options":["Cricoid cartilage","Complete ring","Thyroid cartilage","Hyoid bone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The first ring connecting the trachea to the larynx is called a complete ring (the cricoid cartilage forming a complete ring).  Why A wrong: While the cricoid is involved, the source labels the first ring a \"complete ring.\"  Why C/D wrong: These are other laryngeal structures, not the first connecting ring described."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"The anatomical landmark for the placement of an open tracheostomy tube is between which tracheal rings?","options":["1st and 2nd tracheal rings","Second and third tracheal rings (T2–T3)","4th and 5th tracheal rings","6th and 7th tracheal rings"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The ideal location for placement of the tracheostomy tube is between the second and third tracheal rings (T2–T3).  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the source-specified T2–T3 landmark."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"Placement of the tracheostomy tube too low increases the risk of which complications? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Subglottic stenosis","Fracture of cricoid cartilage","Damaging vascular structures","Tracheo-innominate (innominate artery) or accidental decannulation in the early postoperative period","Improving cervical traction"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Placement too high increases risk of subglottic stenosis and fracture of cricoid cartilage; placement too low increases the risk of damaging vascular structures (tracheo-innominate) or accidental decannulation in the early postoperative period.  Why E wrong: \"Improving cervical traction\" is not a recognized risk/benefit listed; it is a distractor."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"The tracheostomy tube is typically placed how far inferior to the cricoid cartilage?","options":["0.5 cm","1 to 2 cm","3 to 4 cm","5 cm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The tube incision is typically placed 1 to 2 cm inferior to the cricoid cartilage.  Why A/C/D wrong: These distances do not match the source-specified 1–2 cm."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"Which of the following are stated purposes of a tracheostomy? (Select All That Apply)","options":["To maintain a patent airway for a long time","To remove the excessive secretion from the lower respiratory tract","To prevent the leakage of air","To prevent foreign body aspirations","To maintain normal tidal volume"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: All listed — maintain a patent airway long-term, remove excessive secretions from the lower respiratory tract, prevent leakage of air, prevent foreign body aspirations, and maintain normal tidal volume — are stated purposes of tracheostomy.  Why all correct: Each appears in the source's Purpose section."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"A tracheostomy performed within 10 days, classified as an early tracheostomy, would most likely be done for which indication?","options":["Permanent airway need","When prolonged intubation is needed (>10–14 days) or for severe maxillofacial trauma/airway obstruction","Chronic sleep apnea refractory to other treatments","Removal of a vocal cord tumor only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: An emergent/early tracheostomy is indicated when prolonged intubation is needed (>10–14 days), and for indications such as severe trauma. Emergent indications include providing an emergency airway, relieving acute upper-airway obstruction with failed ET intubation (foreign body, angioedema, infection, anaphylaxis), penetrating laryngeal trauma, cervical and facial trauma, improving surgical access, and intolerance/failure to insert ET tubes.  Why A wrong: Permanent airway need is an elective/long-term indication.  Why C wrong: Chronic sleep apnea refractory to treatment is an elective indication.  Why D wrong: Vocal cord tumor removal alone is not the listed early indication."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"Which of the following are listed indications for an ELECTIVE tracheostomy? (Select All That Apply)","options":["When prolonged intubation is needed (>10–14 days)","Prophylactic tracheostomy for head and neck cancer treatment","Obstructive sleep apnea refractory to other treatments","Chronic aspiration","Neuromuscular disease and subglottic stenosis"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Elective indications include prolonged intubation need (>10–14 days), prophylactic tracheostomy for head/neck cancer treatment, obstructive sleep apnea refractory to other treatments, chronic aspiration, neuromuscular disease, and subglottic stenosis.  Why all correct: Each is listed under elective indications in the source."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"A tracheostomy performed for a permanent airway, depending on the patient's underlying disease, would be classified as which type?","options":["Temporary tracheostomy","Permanent tracheostomy","High tracheostomy","Mid tracheostomy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A permanent tracheostomy is for long-term use, such as in cases of permanent airway obstruction, and is typed by permanence.  Why A wrong: A temporary tracheostomy is for short-term use, usually 14–21 days, performed in cases of temporary airway obstruction.  Why C/D wrong: High and mid refer to anatomical level of the tracheostomy, not the temporary/permanent classification."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"A temporary tracheostomy is usually maintained for what duration?","options":["24 hours","14–21 days","3 months","Permanently"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A temporary tracheostomy is for short term, usually 14–21 days, and is usually performed in cases of upper airway obstruction.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the source-specified 14–21 days for a temporary tracheostomy."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"A \"high tracheostomy\" is most commonly used in which condition?","options":["Thyroid cancer","Carcinoma of larynx (laryngeal cancer)","Pneumothorax","Pulmonary embolism"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A high tracheostomy is most commonly used in cases of carcinoma of larynx (laryngeal cancer).  Why A wrong: Thyroid involvement relates to mid tracheostomy (through the thyroid isthmus area).  Why C/D wrong: These are not the indication for a high tracheostomy."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"A \"mid tracheostomy,\" done through rings 2–3, divides which structure?","options":["Cricoid cartilage","The thyroid isthmus (division of thyroid)","The hyoid bone","The cervical fascia only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A mid tracheostomy is performed through rings 2–3 and most commonly involves division of the thyroid (thyroid isthmus).  Why A/C/D wrong: These structures are not the ones divided in a mid tracheostomy per the source."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"Per the tracheostomy tube size table, an infant under 1 month (<1 month) weighing approximately 3–9.5 kg uses which tube size?","options":["3.5 ID, length 10–12","3.0 ID, length 4–9.5","4.0 ID, length 10–11","4.5–5.0 ID, length 39–44"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Per the table, neonates under 1 month (weight ~3–9.5 kg) use approximately a 3.0–3.5 ID tube with length around 10–12, consistent with the smallest tube size for the youngest age group.  Why B/C/D wrong: These tube dimensions correspond to older age groups (larger weights and longer tube lengths) per the source's size-by-age table."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"Which of the following are valid types of tracheostomy tubes per the source? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Double lumen and single lumen tubes","Fenestrated and non-fenestrated tubes","Cuffed and non-cuffed tubes","Magnetic tracheostomy tubes","Glass-only disposable tubes"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Types of tracheostomy tubes are double lumen & single lumen tubes, fenestrated & non-fenestrated tubes, and cuffed & non-cuffed tubes.  Why D wrong: \"Magnetic tracheostomy tubes\" is not a listed type.  Why E wrong: \"Glass-only disposable tubes\" is not a listed type."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"A cuffed tracheostomy tube's cuff is commonly inflated to about what pressure, the amount of air pushed in for balloon inflation?","options":["1 mL","3 mL","5 mL","10 mL"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: For a cuffed tracheostomy tube, the amount of air push in/balloon for inflation is about 5 mL.  Why A/B/D wrong: These do not match the source-stated approximately 5 mL."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"For a cuffed tracheostomy tube, the cuff pressure should be maintained at approximately:","options":["1 mmHg (0.13 mmHg)","25 mmHg (1 mmHg ≈ 1.36 cmH2O), reading recorded by aneroid cuff pressure manometer","100 mmHg","200 mmHg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The cuff pressure should be maintained around 25 mmHg (with the relationship 1 mmHg ≈ 1.36 cmH2O), recorded by an aneroid cuff pressure manometer.  Why A wrong: 1 mmHg is far too low to seal the airway.  Why C/D wrong: These pressures are dangerously high and would risk tracheal mucosal ischemia."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"What is the chief advantage of the cuffed tracheostomy tube?","options":["It allows the patient to speak normally","It properly seals around the trachea","It can be cleaned and reused indefinitely","It eliminates the need for suctioning"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The advantage of the cuffed tracheostomy tube is that it properly seals around the trachea (preventing air leak and aspiration around the tube).  Why A wrong: A cuffed tube generally impedes speech because the sealed cuff blocks airflow through the vocal cords.  Why C wrong: Sealing, not unlimited reuse, is the stated advantage.  Why D wrong: Suctioning is still required; the cuff does not eliminate it."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"Regarding the parts of a tracheostomy tube, which statement about the outer cannula is correct?","options":["It can be removed for cleaning frequently","It is permanently fixed in the trachea","It contains the obturator","It is used as a guide wire during insertion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The outer cannula is permanently fixed in the trachea.  Why A wrong: It is the inner cannula that can be removed and cleaned, removing the adhering mucus.  Why C wrong: The obturator is a separate part used during insertion, not contained within the outer cannula as a function.  Why D wrong: The obturator (not the outer cannula) acts as a guide/stylet during insertion."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"Which part of the tracheostomy tube can be removed to clean and clear off the adhering mucus?","options":["Outer cannula","Inner cannula","Obturator","Cuff"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The inner cannula can be removed to clean and clear the adhering mucus.  Why A wrong: The outer cannula is permanently fixed and not routinely removed.  Why C wrong: The obturator is for insertion guidance, not mucus cleaning.  Why D wrong: The cuff seals the trachea and is not removed for mucus clearance."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"What is the function of the obturator in a tracheostomy tube? (Select All That Apply)","options":["It is also known as a guide wire/stylet","It helps in easy insertion of the tube during the procedure","It should be removed immediately after placement of the tube","After placement, the obturator should be kept at the bedside or taped to the wall","It permanently seals the airway"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The obturator is also known as a guide wire/stylet, helps in easy insertion of the tube, should be removed immediately after placement, and should be kept at the bedside or taped to the wall (for emergency reinsertion).  Why E wrong: The obturator does not seal the airway; it is removed after insertion and only the tube remains."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"Which of the following are correct PRE/INTRA-procedure nursing interventions for tracheostomy? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Obtain informed consent","Monitor vital signs before, during, and after the procedure, especially oxygen saturation","Choose the appropriate size and type of tube","Place all rescue/resuscitation equipment at the bedside in working condition","Position the patient supine with hyperextension of the neck (sandbag/roll under shoulders)"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Pre/intra-procedure interventions include obtaining informed consent; monitoring vital signs (especially oxygen saturation) before, during, and after; choosing the appropriate size and type of tube; keeping all resuscitation equipment at the bedside in working condition; and positioning the patient supine with neck hyperextension.  Why all correct: Each is listed under tracheostomy procedure/nursing care in the source."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"During tracheostomy tube placement, in which position should the patient be placed?","options":["Prone with knees flexed","Supine with hyperextension of the neck","Left lateral decubitus","High Fowler's with neck flexed"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The patient should be placed supine with hyperextension of the neck for tracheostomy tube placement.  Why A/C/D wrong: These positions do not match the source-specified supine with neck hyperextension."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"Which local anesthesia is used during tracheostomy per the source?","options":["1% xylocaine","2% xylocaine","5% lidocaine","10% procaine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Local infiltration anaesthesia using 2% xylocaine is used during the tracheostomy procedure.  Why A/C/D wrong: These concentrations/agents do not match the source-specified 2% xylocaine."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"During tracheostomy, how is the incision typically made per the source?","options":["As a vertical midline incision","As a horizontal incision per the requirement","Always a circular incision","No incision is made"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The incision is made as a horizontal incision per the requirement.  Why A/C wrong: These do not match the source's horizontal incision approach.  Why D wrong: An incision is required to create the tracheostomy."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"During the tracheostomy procedure, the tracheal tube should be tied by ribbons around the neck and fixed, and which aseptic technique is maintained?","options":["Clean technique only","Maintain aseptic technique during the procedure","Sterile technique is unnecessary","Use only alcohol wipes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Aseptic technique must be maintained during the procedure, and the tube should be tied by ribbons around the neck and fixed.  Why A/C/D wrong: These understate the requirement; aseptic technique is required throughout."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"After tracheostomy tube placement, the tracheostomy tube is secured with a soft transparent material, and a change of the tracheostomy tube on which postoperative day is recommended?","options":["Postoperative day 1","Postoperative day five (5)","Postoperative day 14","It is never changed"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The tracheostomy tube is secured with soft trans-material, and the tracheostomy tube change is recommended on postoperative day five.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the source-stated postoperative day 5 for the tube change."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"After placement of the tracheostomy tube, in which position should the patient be placed to reduce or prevent oedema at the incision site and reduce the risk of aspiration?","options":["Supine flat","Lower (semi-)Fowler's / lower position with head elevated","Trendelenburg","Prone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After placement of the tube, the patient should be placed in a lower (head-elevated) position to reduce/prevent oedema at the incision site and reduce the risk of aspiration.  Why A wrong: Supine flat does not elevate the head to reduce edema/aspiration risk.  Why C wrong: Trendelenburg (head down) would increase edema and aspiration risk.  Why D wrong: Prone is not appropriate for tracheostomy airway management."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"After the tracheostomy procedure, the nurse should check all vital signs and respiratory status (especially O2 saturation) initially at what frequency?","options":["Every 15 minutes and reassess stabilization of the client every 30 minutes","Every 2 hours","Every 4 hours","Once per shift"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to check vitals and respiratory status, especially O2 saturation, initially every 15 minutes and reassess (stabilization) of the client every 30 minutes.  Why B/C/D wrong: These intervals are too infrequent for the early post-tracheostomy period per the source."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"Care of the inner cannula includes regular cleaning, disinfecting periodically with which solution?","options":["Plain water only","Sodium bicarbonate for 10 minutes with H2O + NS","Full-strength hydrogen peroxide","Povidone-iodine soak for 1 hour"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The inner cannula should be cleaned/disinfected periodically with sodium bicarbonate for about 10 minutes, with H2O + NS (normal saline).  Why A wrong: Plain water alone is not the disinfecting agent specified.  Why C/D wrong: These agents/durations are not the source-specified method."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"While suctioning a tracheostomy or changing the tube, which substance should the nurse use to lubricate the tube?","options":["Petroleum jelly","Water-soluble jelly","Mineral oil","Antibiotic ointment"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Water-soluble jelly should be used while changing the tube (a water-soluble lubricant prevents aspiration of oil-based products into the airway).  Why A/C wrong: Petroleum jelly and mineral oil are oil-based and can cause lipoid aspiration/pneumonia.  Why D wrong: Antibiotic ointment is not the recommended lubricant for tube changes."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"How often should the tracheostomy cuff pressure be monitored, and what value should it remain below per the source?","options":["Every 8 hours; remain below 25 mmHg measured by aneroid manometer","Every 24 hours; remain below 100 mmHg","Once daily; remain below 50 mmHg","Hourly; remain below 5 mmHg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to monitor the tracheostomy cuff pressure every 8 hours, and it should remain below ~25 mmHg (measured by an aneroid manometer) to prevent tracheal mucosal ischemia.  Why B/C/D wrong: These frequencies and pressure thresholds do not match the source's every-8-hours / <25 mmHg guidance."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"To keep the tracheostomy stoma sterile and wet, the source recommends maintaining stoma humidity with which solution?","options":["Sterile water only","Normal saline (NS) on the stoma","Hydrogen peroxide","Dextrose 5%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source advises maintaining stoma humidity to keep the stoma sterile and wet with NS (normal saline) on the stoma.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the source-specified solution; NS is used to maintain stoma humidity."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"Dried secretions on the tracheostomy tube are more prone to which complication, requiring attention to humidity?","options":["Tube obstruction","Cuff over-inflation","Decannulation","Stoma stenosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: When secretions on the stoma become dried, they are more prone to causing tube obstruction; thus maintaining humidity is emphasized.  Why B/C/D wrong: Dried secretions specifically increase obstruction risk, not these complications."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"When suctioning a tracheostomy, the head of the bed should be elevated to increase the head of which structure to ease aspiration?","options":["Aspiration of the trachea","Increase the head of aspiration (elevate head of bed to ease aspiration/suctioning)","Lower the bed flat","Place the patient prone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The head of the bed should be elevated to increase the head of aspiration (improve suctioning/clearing of secretions and reduce aspiration risk).  Why A wrong: This restates the structure rather than the action; the source specifies elevating the head of the bed.  Why C/D wrong: Lowering the bed flat or prone positioning would worsen secretion clearance and aspiration risk."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"For the first 24 hours after a tracheostomy, how should the lumen/airway be managed regarding suctioning?","options":["The lumen should not be suctioned at all","The lumen should be suctioned hourly regardless of need","The lumen should be suctioned as needed (the first tracheostomy tube change should initiate or take place around days 5–7 days)","Suctioning is contraindicated for the first week"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: For the first 24 hours, the lumen should be suctioned as needed; the first tracheostomy tube change generally takes place around postoperative days 5–7 days as the tract matures.  Why A/D wrong: The lumen does require suctioning as needed; it is not withheld entirely.  Why B wrong: Suctioning is performed as needed, not routinely hourly regardless of need."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"Per the source, the first tracheostomy tube change is delayed for several days postoperatively because:","options":["The patient is too weak","Early tracheostomy tube change can increase the risk that the tract has not matured, and the patient is awake/alert and cooperative; the skin can remove at this time","There is no medical reason","The cuff cannot be deflated"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The first tracheostomy tube change is delayed because early change carries the risk that the tract has not yet matured; once the patient is awake, alert, and cooperative and the tract has matured, the change can be done more safely.  Why A/C wrong: The reason given is tract maturation/safety, not patient weakness or absence of reason.  Why D wrong: Cuff deflation is not the stated reason for delaying the tube change."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"After the first-time tube change, how often should the double lumen tube be changed to prevent infection and obstruction by dried secretions?","options":["Once a year","At least every 30 days, while a single lumen tube is changed every 7 days","Every 2 hours","It never needs changing"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After the first-time tube change, the double lumen tube should be changed at least every 30 days (and the single lumen tube every 7 days) to prevent infection and obstruction by dried secretions.  Why A/C/D wrong: These intervals do not match the source-specified schedule (double lumen every 30 days, single lumen every 7 days)."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"A characteristic of the un-cuffed tracheostomy tube is that it: (Select All That Apply)","options":["Is used when unable to tolerate a cuffed tube","Is used when there is no risk of aspiration or no need to provide positive pressure ventilation","Is usually used in children","Properly seals the trachea with an inflated cuff","Requires cuff pressure monitoring every 8 hours"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The un-cuffed tube is used when the patient is unable to tolerate a cuffed tube, when there is no risk of aspiration or no need for positive pressure ventilation, and it is usually used in children.  Why D wrong: Sealing the trachea with an inflated cuff describes the cuffed tube, not the un-cuffed tube.  Why E wrong: An un-cuffed tube has no cuff, so cuff pressure monitoring is not applicable."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"Which of the following are listed as IMMEDIATE complications of tracheostomy (per the complications table)? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Haemorrhage or bleeding","Aspiration","Air embolism","Procedure failure","Structural damage to tracheal rings"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Immediate complications include haemorrhage/bleeding, aspiration, air embolism, procedure failure, and structural damage to tracheal rings.  Why all correct: Each is listed in the \"Immediate\" column of the tracheostomy complications table."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"Which of the following are listed as INTERMEDIATE complications of tracheostomy? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Delayed haemorrhage","Tube displacement","Surgical emphysema and pneumomediastinum","Pneumothorax and infection","Tracheal necrosis, tracheo-arterial/tracheo-oesophageal fistula, dysphagia, and tracheomalacia"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Intermediate complications include delayed haemorrhage, tube displacement, surgical emphysema, pneumomediastinum, pneumothorax, infection, tracheal necrosis, tracheoarterial fistula, tracheo-oesophageal fistula, dysphagia, and tracheomalacia.  Why all correct: Each appears in the \"Intermediate\" column of the complications table."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"Which of the following are listed as LONG-TERM complications of tracheostomy? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Tracheal stenosis","Decannulation problems","Tracheo-cutaneous fistula","Disfiguring scar","Air embolism"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Long-term complications include tracheal stenosis, decannulation problems, tracheo-cutaneous fistula, and disfiguring scar.  Why E wrong: Air embolism is an immediate complication, not a long-term one."},{"section":"Batch 038 · types of nasogastric/intestinal/esophageal tubes…","question":"A non-cuffed tracheostomy tube is contraindicated for a patient who requires positive pressure ventilation because:","options":["It is too small for adults","Without an inflated cuff there is no seal, so positive pressure ventilation and protection against aspiration cannot be reliably provided","It cannot be cleaned","It always causes tracheal stenosis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The un-cuffed tube is used only when there is no risk of aspiration or no need to provide positive pressure ventilation, because without a cuff seal effective positive pressure ventilation and aspiration protection are not provided.  Why A wrong: Size is not the stated reason; cuff/seal is.  Why C wrong: Cleanability is not the basis of this contraindication.  Why D wrong: The source does not state un-cuffed tubes always cause stenosis."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"A nurse caring for a client with a tracheostomy notes a continuous or intermittent leak of air around the tube. What does this finding most likely indicate?","options":["Cuff leak of the tracheostomy tube","Complete decannulation","Excessive sedation","Normal tube function"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A continuous/intermittent cuff leak of the tracheostomy tube is signaled by air escaping; possible causes include malfunctioning of the tracheostomy tube and tracheomalacia.  Why B wrong: Decannulation is removal of the tube, not an air leak around it.  Why C wrong: Sedation does not cause cuff leaks.  Why D wrong: An air leak is abnormal and requires assessment."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"One-way valves (speaking valves) placed onto a tracheostomy tube serve which primary purpose?","options":["To prevent the cuff from deflating","To allow the patient to make noises and speak more easily","To seal the stoma permanently","To deliver continuous oxygen"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: One-way valves, called speaking valves, are placed onto the tube and will allow the patient to make noises and speak more easily.  Why A wrong: They relate to phonation/airflow, not cuff inflation status.  Why C wrong: They do not seal the stoma.  Why D wrong: They are not oxygen-delivery devices."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"When a tracheostomy has only a single lumen present, the nurse should plan to do what during care?","options":["Leave the inner cannula in place during suctioning","Clean the neck plate and cannula in place, then replace the inner cannula","Avoid all suctioning","Permanently remove the cuff"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If only a single lumen is present, clean the neck plate and cannula in place, then replace the inner cannula.  Why A wrong: With a single lumen the cannula is cleaned in place, not simply left untouched as in a double-lumen tube.  Why C wrong: Suctioning is still performed as needed.  Why D wrong: The cuff is not removed for cleaning."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"While performing tracheostomy care, the nurse uses proper hand washing before the procedure. What is the rationale?","options":["To save supplies","To remove, change, and replace the inner cannula","To maintain sterile technique","To stimulate coughing"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Proper hand washing is performed before tracheostomy care to maintain sterile technique.  Why A wrong: It is an infection-control measure, not a supply measure.  Why B wrong: That is a separate care step, not the reason for hand hygiene.  Why D wrong: Hand washing does not stimulate cough."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"In the \"TRACHE\" bundle for tracheostomy care, the letter \"T\" stands for which element?","options":["Total assessment","Tapes (keep the tracheostomy tube secure)","Thoracic auscultation","Tracheomalacia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the TRACHE acronym, T = Tapes: keep the tracheostomy tube secure.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the meaning assigned to \"T\" in the source bundle."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"In the \"TRACHE\" bundle, what does the letter \"R\" represent?","options":["Respiratory rate only","Resuscitancy: care, know the resuscitation procedure","Removal of cuff","Re-cannulation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: R = Resuscitancy care — know the resuscitation procedure.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the source meaning for \"R.\""},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"Within the \"TRACHE\" tracheostomy-care bundle, which item is matched correctly?","options":["A = Airway clear: use the correct suction technique","C = Cuff inflation only","H = Humidity: not essential","E = Emergency: no equipment needed"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A = Airway clear, using the correct suction technique.  Why B wrong: C stands for Care of the site (stoma and skin), not cuff inflation only.  Why C wrong: H = Humidity — essential to keep tube clear (humidity IS important).  Why D wrong: E = Emergency equipment, e.g., suction machine, MV, etc. (equipment IS needed)."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"A nurse reviews the \"COMS\" acronym used as guidance for tracheostomy follow-up. Which item correctly matches a letter in COMS?","options":["C = Communication: non-verbal communication aids and plan","O = Oxygen titration","M = Monitor vital signs","S = Suction depth"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: C = Communication — non-verbal communication aids and plan.  Why B wrong: O = Oral care (oral mucosa, mouth care, and teeth care).  Why C wrong: M = Mouth care (oral assessment), not vital-sign monitoring.  Why D wrong: S = Swallowing and nutrition — swallowing assessment and nutrition, not suction depth."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"A client with a tracheostomy suddenly develops difficulty in breathing and appears distressed. Considering the airway may be obstructed by an obstruction, which action should the nurse take FIRST?","options":["Remove the tube and reinsert immediately without informing anyone","Immediately do the suction; if not resolved then remove the tube and ventilate","Wait and observe for one hour","Increase sedation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If the client's oxygen saturation falls suddenly with difficulty in breathing, it may indicate tube obstruction; immediately do the suction, and if obstruction is there then remove the tube, ventilate, and inform the on-duty doctor to prepare for reinsertion of the tube.  Why A wrong: The doctor must be informed and prepared for reinsertion; the tube is removed only after suction fails.  Why C wrong: This is an emergency requiring immediate action.  Why D wrong: Sedation worsens respiratory compromise."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"If the tracheostomy tube becomes accidentally displaced (falls out), what should the nurse do?","options":["Leave it out and document only","Place the oxygen over the stoma site, remove the old tracheostomy tube if it is around the neck, and inform the on-duty doctor for reinsertion of the tube","Push the tube back in forcefully and sedate the patient","Cover the stoma tightly with tape and wait"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If the tube falls out, place the oxygen over the stoma site, remove the old tube if it is around the neck, and inform the on-duty doctor to prepare for reinsertion.  Why A wrong: Action and physician notification are required.  Why C wrong: Forcible blind reinsertion and sedation are inappropriate and dangerous.  Why D wrong: Sealing the stoma can obstruct emergency airway access."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"After placement of an ET tube, which finding obtained by confirming the presence of exhaled CO2 from the lungs helps confirm correct tube placement?","options":["End-tidal CO2 detector","Pulse oximetry alone","Blood pressure reading","Capillary refill"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: End-tidal CO2 detection confirms correct placement by detecting exhaled CO2 from the lungs.  Why B wrong: Pulse oximetry reflects oxygenation, not direct ET placement confirmation.  Why C wrong: Blood pressure does not confirm tube position.  Why D wrong: Capillary refill is a perfusion check, not placement confirmation."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"Which auscultation finding confirms correct ET tube placement in the trachea (rather than the esophagus)?","options":["Air entry over the epigastrium via AMBU bag","Bilateral equal air entry into the trachea via an AMBU bag, with bilateral equal rise and fall of the lungs","Absent breath sounds bilaterally","Air entry only on the left side"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Auscultation should reveal bilateral equal air entry into the trachea via AMBU bag and bilateral equal rise and fall of the lungs (confirming endotracheal placement).  Why A wrong: Epigastric air entry indicates esophageal misplacement.  Why C wrong: Absent sounds suggest incorrect placement/obstruction.  Why D wrong: Unilateral air entry suggests mainstem intubation or misplacement."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"A neck X-ray is described as the most reliable, confirmatory method for ET tube placement when the tip is correctly placed at which location?","options":["At the level of the larynx","1–2 cm above the carina","In the right mainstem bronchus","At the level of the vocal cords"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Neck X-ray is a more accurate and confirmatory method; correct placement is 1–2 cm above the carina.  Why A wrong: Placement at the larynx is too high.  Why C wrong: Right mainstem placement is a malposition.  Why D wrong: Vocal-cord level is too high above the desired tracheal position."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"What does the term \"decannulation\" mean with respect to tracheostomy/ET care?","options":["Insertion of air into the balloon","Removal of the tube","Insertion of the tube","Removal of air from the balloon"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Decannulation means removal of the tube.  Why A wrong: That is inflation.  Why C wrong: That is cannulation.  Why D wrong: That is deflation."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"Match the correct definition: which pair is accurate?","options":["Cannulation = removal of the tube","Inflation = insertion of air into the balloon","Deflation = insertion of the tube","Cannulation = removal of air from the balloon"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Inflation is insertion of air into the balloon.  Why A wrong: Cannulation is insertion of the tube (not removal).  Why C wrong: Deflation is removal of air from the balloon.  Why D wrong: Cannulation is tube insertion, not balloon deflation."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"A \"tracheal button\" is best described as which of the following?","options":["A small device that fits over the stoma of the tracheostomy tube and maintains patency to maintain the stoma","A speaking valve","A cuff inflation port","A suction catheter tip"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A tracheal button is a small device that puts into the stoma of the tracheostomy tubes to maintain patency (keeps the stoma open).  Why B wrong: A speaking valve is a separate one-way valve device.  Why C wrong: It is not an inflation port.  Why D wrong: It is not a suction catheter component."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"Suctioning is best defined as the mechanical aspiration of mucous secretions from which structures by the application of negative pressure?","options":["Stomach and esophagus","Mouth, nose, and trachea (bronchial tree)","Pleural space","Pericardial cavity"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Suctioning is the mechanical aspiration of mucous secretions from the mouth, nose, and trachea/bronchial tree by application of negative pressure.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the structures suctioned in airway suctioning."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"Suctioning is indicated when secretions accumulate in the airway, leading to which physiologic problem?","options":["Improved airflow with normalized gas exchange","Obstruction and ineffective airflow, also impairing gas exchange (e.g., O2 and CO2)","Increased lung compliance","Decreased work of breathing"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: When secretions accumulate, the airway is obstructed; airflow is ineffective and impairs gas exchange (e.g., O2 and CO2).  Why A/C/D wrong: These are the opposite of what accumulated secretions cause."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"Centralised suction line (vacuum pipes) is coded with which colour for better identification?","options":["Red","Green","Yellow","Blue"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: A centralised suction line (vacuum pipes) is coded with yellow colour for better identification.  Why A/B/D wrong: These are not the colour used for centralised suction lines in the source."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"Which of the following is an INDICATION for suctioning? (Select all that apply)","options":["To help maintain a patent airway","To obtain a sputum specimen","To stimulate coughing","To deliberately cause hypoxemia","To deliver tube feeding"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Indications include maintaining a patent airway, obtaining a sputum specimen, and stimulating cough.  Why D wrong: Causing hypoxemia is a complication, never an indication.  Why E wrong: Tube feeding is not delivered via suction."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"Which of the following is a CONTRAINDICATION (or condition requiring caution) for suctioning? (Select all that apply)","options":["Patients with pulmonary hypertension","Patients known to have raised intracranial pressure (ICP)","Patients having pulmonary oedema or haemorrhage","Patients with thick retained secretions","Patients with a patent airway needing routine care"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Contraindications listed are pulmonary hypertension, patients known to have raised ICP, and pulmonary oedema or haemorrhage.  Why D wrong: Thick retained secretions are an indication for suctioning, not a contraindication.  Why E wrong: Routine patent-airway care is not listed as a contraindication."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"According to the airway suction pressure table, what is the recommended suction pressure range for an OLDER CHILD and ADULT?","options":["50–95 mm Hg","80–100 mm Hg","100–120 mm Hg","120–150 mm Hg"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists Older child & adult: 120–150 mm Hg.  Why A wrong: 50–95 mm Hg is below the adult range.  Why B wrong: 80–100 mm Hg is the infant range.  Why C wrong: 100–120 mm Hg is the smaller-child range."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"The recommended airway suction pressure for a SMALLER CHILD (less than 11 years) is:","options":["60–80 mm Hg","80–100 mm Hg","100–120 mm Hg","120–150 mm Hg"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Smaller child (<11 years): 100–120 mm Hg.  Why A wrong: 60–80 mm Hg is the newborn range.  Why B wrong: 80–100 mm Hg is the infant range.  Why D wrong: 120–150 mm Hg is the adult/older-child range."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"The recommended airway suction pressure for an INFANT is:","options":["60–80 mm Hg","80–100 mm Hg","100–120 mm Hg","120–150 mm Hg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Infant: 80–100 mm Hg.  Why A wrong: 60–80 mm Hg corresponds to the newborn.  Why C wrong: 100–120 mm Hg is the smaller child.  Why D wrong: 120–150 mm Hg is the adult/older child."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"The recommended airway suction pressure for a NEWBORN is:","options":["60–80 mm Hg","80–100 mm Hg","100–120 mm Hg","120–150 mm Hg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Newborn: 60–80 mm Hg.  Why B wrong: 80–100 mm Hg is the infant range.  Why C wrong: 100–120 mm Hg is the smaller child.  Why D wrong: 120–150 mm Hg is the adult/older child."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"In an Open Tracheal Suction System (OTSS), what is the key procedural feature?","options":["The catheter remains permanently attached to the ventilator","Disconnecting the patient from the ventilator and introducing a single-use suction catheter into the patient's endotracheal tube","No catheter is ever introduced","The ventilator support continues during suctioning"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In OTSS, the patient is disconnected from the ventilator and a single-use suction catheter is introduced into the endotracheal tube; OTSS is used for naso/oro/tracheal and endotracheal suctioning where patients are on ventilator support or not.  Why A wrong: A permanently attached catheter describes the closed system.  Why C wrong: A catheter is introduced.  Why D wrong: In an open system the patient is disconnected from ventilator support."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"In a Closed Tracheal Suction System (CTSS), the suction catheter is:","options":["A single-use catheter requiring ventilator disconnection","Enclosed in a sterile plastic sheath, attached to the ventilator tubing so the client does not need to be disconnected from the ventilator","Never sterile","Only used for oropharyngeal suctioning"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In CTSS the catheter is attached to the ventilator tubing and the client does not need to be disconnected; the catheter is enclosed in a protective (sterile) sheath, and closed suction systems are available for 24-hour or 72-hour use.  Why A wrong: That describes the open system.  Why C wrong: The catheter is within a sterile/protective sheath.  Why D wrong: It is used for ventilated patients' airway suctioning, not limited to oropharyngeal."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"Why is a closed suction system preferred for patients receiving ventilation? (Select all that apply)","options":["Closed systems are available for 24-hour or 72-hour usage depending on patient requirements","There are two lumen/length options to allow attachment to either an endotracheal (≥4 cm) or tracheostomy (≥6.5 cm) tube","It reduces the occurrence of airway collapse that may occur when a patient is disconnected from the ventilator","It is used to prevent contamination and to reduce transmission of infection (e.g., HIV, hepatitis B, or active respiratory tuberculosis)","It eliminates the need for any infection-control precautions"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Closed systems come in 24h/72h options; offer length options for ET (≥4 cm) and tracheostomy (≥6.5 cm) tubes; reduce airway collapse on disconnection; and are used to prevent contamination/transmission of infection such as HIV, hepatitis B, or active respiratory TB.  Why E wrong: The system reduces transmission but does not eliminate the need for infection-control precautions."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"Based on anatomical location, \"nasopharyngeal suctioning\" refers to insertion of the catheter through which route?","options":["Into the nasopharynx","Directly into the ET tube","Into the stoma of a tracheostomy","Into the oropharynx"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Nasopharyngeal suctioning = insertion of catheter into the nasopharynx.  Why B wrong: Endotracheal suctioning is insertion of catheter into the ET tube (to an appropriate depth).  Why C wrong: Insertion into the stoma is airway/stoma suctioning.  Why D wrong: That is oropharyngeal suctioning."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"Endotracheal suctioning, based on anatomical location, involves which action?","options":["Insertion of the catheter into the ET tube to an appropriate depth","Insertion of the catheter into the nasopharynx","Insertion of the catheter into the stoma","Insertion of the catheter into the esophagus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Endotracheal suctioning = insertion of catheter in the ET tube to an appropriate depth.  Why B wrong: That is nasopharyngeal.  Why C wrong: That is airway/stoma suctioning.  Why D wrong: Esophageal insertion is a misplacement, not suctioning."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"During suctioning nursing care, before initiating the procedure the nurse should explain it to the patient. What is the rationale?","options":["To address the possibility of discomfort","To increase suction pressure","To replace the inner cannula","To deflate the cuff"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: If the patient is awake, instruct/explain the procedure due to the possibility of discomfort.  Why B/C/D wrong: Explanation does not change suction pressure, cannula, or cuff status."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"Before and after suctioning a patient on a ventilator, the nurse should pre-oxygenate the patient with which oxygen concentration?","options":["21% O2","40% O2","100% O2 (FiO2)","60% O2"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Ensure pre-oxygenation with 100% FiO2 (and hyper-oxygenate before and after suctioning); for ventilator patients hyperoxygenate manually by AMBU with 100% FiO2.  Why A/B/D wrong: These are below the recommended 100% FiO2 for pre-/hyper-oxygenation around suctioning."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"Suctioning of the oropharynx or nasopharynx is performed when secretions:","options":["Are absent","Require complete sterility only","Pool and require swallowing","Are below the carina"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Suction the oropharynx or nasopharynx when secretions require complete swallowing/pooling (oropharyngeal/nasopharyngeal pooled secretions).  Why A/B/D wrong: These do not match the indication for oro/nasopharyngeal suction."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"When positioning a conscious patient for suctioning, the recommended position is:","options":["Trendelenburg","Semi-Fowler position (first priority) or Fowler position (second priority)","Prone only","Left lateral only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Position during suctioning — Semi-Fowler (first priority); Fowler (second priority).  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the recommended priority positions for suctioning."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"Suction should NOT be applied while the catheter is being inserted into the airway. What is the rationale?","options":["To save time","To avoid mucosal trauma","To increase secretions","To deflate the cuff"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Suction should NOT be done in the supine position (during insertion) to prevent aspiration of fluids and mucosa; do not apply pressure while the catheter is being inserted — to avoid mucosal injury/trauma.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the reason for withholding suction during insertion."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"The total time for suctioning from introduction of the catheter to removal SHOULD NOT exceed how many seconds?","options":["5 seconds","10 seconds","15 seconds","30 seconds"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Total time for suctioning from introduction of catheter to removal should NOT exceed 15 seconds.  Why A/B wrong: Shorter than the maximum stated overall limit.  Why D wrong: Exceeds the 15-second limit."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"While withdrawing the suction catheter, the nurse should apply suction in which manner?","options":["Continuous, fixed-point suction","Apply suction and rotate the catheter using a gentle withdrawal motion while applying negative pressure, with slow spiral motion to minimize mucosal trauma","With maximum negative pressure throughout","Without any rotation while pushing inward"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Apply suction while withdrawing the catheter, rotating with slow spiral motion to minimize mucosal trauma; gently withdraw the catheter while applying negative (intermittent) pressure.  Why A wrong: Continuous fixed-point suction increases trauma.  Why C wrong: Maximum continuous negative pressure damages mucosa.  Why D wrong: Suction is applied on withdrawal, not insertion, and rotation is used."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"During each suctioning episode, suction passes should be limited and oxygen reapplied between attempts. According to the source, what is the recommended limit per suctioning episode?","options":["Maximum 3 passes; 10–15 seconds per pass; 30 seconds for the prevention of tissue hypoxia between passes","Unlimited passes as needed","5 passes of 30 seconds each","2 passes of 1 minute each"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Once a time maximum of 3 passes for suctioning, not more than 10–15 seconds in adults, 10 seconds for the prevention of tissue hypoxia; specialty brain tissues are highly sensitive to hypoxia.  Why B/C/D wrong: They exceed or contradict the limited-pass, short-duration rule."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"Suction pressure should be kept at less than which level for adults and children, with 80–100 mm Hg considered nonexcessive?","options":["<80 mm Hg","<150 mm Hg","<300 mm Hg","<500 mm Hg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Suction pressure should be kept at less than 150 mm Hg for adults and children, with 80–100 mm Hg being nonexcessive.  Why A wrong: Below the stated upper limit but not the rule stated.  Why C/D wrong: Far above the recommended safe limit."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"Which solution is recommended for use as the irrigation/instillation fluid for suctioning per guidelines?","options":["Sterile water","Normal saline (NS)","Hydrogen peroxide","Povidone-iodine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The use of 0.9% Normal Saline (NS) while suctioning is recommended by most guidelines.  Why A wrong: NS, not plain sterile water, is the recommended instillation fluid.  Why C/D wrong: These are antiseptics, not the recommended suction instillation fluid."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"What catheter size should be used to minimize trauma to the internal diameter of the endotracheal/tracheal cavity?","options":["The catheter size used for suction should be less than half the internal diameter of the endotracheal tube","A catheter equal to the ET tube internal diameter","The largest possible catheter","Twice the ET internal diameter"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The catheter size used for suction should be less than half the internal diameter of the endotracheal tube, providing a seamless access of tracheobronchial cavity.  Why B/C/D wrong: A catheter equal to, twice, or the largest possible diameter causes trauma and obstructs airflow/oxygenation."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"Superficial suctioning, as opposed to deep suctioning, prevents mucosal injury and trauma because the suction is:","options":["Applied with maximum negative pressure","Superficial — staying within the artificial airway lumen, not advanced beyond the tube tip","Always performed without oxygenation","Continuous and prolonged"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Superficial suctioning is the most advisable to avoid mucosal injury and trauma (the catheter stays within the artificial airway rather than advancing deeply).  Why A wrong: Maximum negative pressure increases trauma.  Why C wrong: Oxygenation is still required.  Why D wrong: Suctioning is brief and intermittent, not continuous/prolonged."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"Deep suctioning can be avoided because it stimulates which nerve, further leading to bradycardia?","options":["Phrenic nerve","Vagus nerve","Glossopharyngeal nerve","Hypoglossal nerve"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Deep suctioning can be avoided because it stimulates the vagus nerve, which further leads to bradycardia.  Why A/C/D wrong: These nerves are not described as the source of suction-induced bradycardia in the text."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"After deep suctioning of the airway, hyper-oxygenation with 100% FiO2 is recommended primarily to:","options":["Increase secretions","Reverse the effectiveness of suction","Assess the effectiveness of suction and restore oxygen saturation/levels","Deflate the cuff"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: After suctioning, hyper-oxygenation with 100% FiO2 is performed; assess breathing sounds for effectiveness of suction and assess oxygen saturation level and all vital signs.  Why A/B/D wrong: Hyperoxygenation does not increase secretions, reverse suction benefit, or deflate the cuff."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"When changing a closed suction catheter (CSC) according to patient requirements, the source states the catheters are available for either:","options":["6-hour or 12-hour use","24-hour or 72-hour change requirements (with 24-hour change every 24 hours and pediatrics every 24 hours)","1-week or 1-month use","Single-use only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Closed suction systems (in-line suction) are available for 24-hour or 72-hour usage per patient requirements; for adults change at 72 hours, and for pediatrics at 24 hours.  Why A/C wrong: These intervals are not given.  Why D wrong: Closed catheters are reusable in-line for the stated periods, not single use (single-use catheters describe the open system)."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"The closed suction catheter should be labeled with the date and time it was connected to the patient. Within how many days/hours, depending on multiple patients, should it be changed?","options":["12 hours","24 hours","72 hours (or depending on multiple patients)","1 week"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The catheter should be labeled with date and time connected; closed catheters can be changed every 72 hours, or depending on multiple patients.  Why A/B wrong: These are shorter than the stated maximum for the closed in-line system change.  Why D wrong: One week exceeds the stated interval."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"Which of the following is part of the CSC (closed suction catheter) specification? (Select all that apply)","options":["Minimizes the ventilator-associated pneumonia (VAP) instances in ventilated patients","Offers convenience of attachment to the ventilator on one side and tracheal tube on the other, resulting in seamless access of the tracheobronchial cavity","Dual swivel elbow offers convenience of rotatability for optimal comfort","Aerosomes (soft catheter) minimize the damage to mucosal injury","It must be discarded after every single suction pass"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The CSC specification includes minimizing VAP in ventilated patients; convenience of attachment to ventilator and tracheal tube for seamless tracheobronchial access; a dual swivel elbow for rotatability/comfort; and a soft catheter (Aerosomes) that minimizes mucosal damage.  Why E wrong: The CSC is an in-line system used over 24–72 hours, not discarded after each pass."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"According to CSC specification, what infection-control feature is provided by the protective sleeve?","options":["A clear depth marking helps reduce mucosal trauma and infection","A clear protective sleeve completely isolates the catheter from secretions/air to allow showing the practitioner to limit the distance of catheter for safe suctioning","It increases the distance the catheter is advanced","It eliminates the need for oxygen"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Clear depth markings help reduce mucosal trauma and infection (provided by showing the practitioner the distance of catheter for safe suctioning); a clear protective sleeve completely isolates the catheter, helping reduce infection and contamination disturbances.  Why A wrong: While depth markings do help, the protective sleeve's role is isolation/infection control as described.  Why C wrong: It helps limit/control distance, not increase it.  Why D wrong: Oxygen remains required."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"\"Superficial suctioning\" means going down the suction catheter only up to which point?","options":["Up to the carina","Up to the end of the artificial airway (endotracheal or tracheostomy tube) without advancing beyond, which is more advisable","Several centimeters beyond the tube tip","Into the right mainstem bronchus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Superficial suctioning means going down the suction catheter only up to the end of the artificial airway (endotracheal or tracheostomy tube); this is more advisable as it whereas deep suctioning advances beyond, where the catheter tip resistance is met.  Why A/C/D wrong: Advancing to the carina, beyond the tube, or into a mainstem bronchus describes deep suctioning, which carries higher risk."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"A nurse must NOT apply suction while inserting the suction catheter into a tracheostomy tube. Why?","options":["To prevent the additional/possible tube obstruction with secretions","To deflate the cuff","To increase oxygen levels","To shorten the catheter"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: DO NOT apply suction while inserting the catheter (multiple studies have shown that airway collapse may occur when a patient is disconnected, and suction on insertion increases trauma/hypoxia); the source explicitly states do not apply suction catheter whilst inserting the catheter.  Why B/C/D wrong: Withholding suction on insertion does not deflate the cuff, raise oxygen, or shorten the catheter."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"In cases of acute respiratory distress, or when obstruction of the airway is suspected, suctioning must be performed in what manner?","options":["Performed leisurely after a delay","Performed emergently with eventual preparation (urgently)","Never performed","Only after the cuff is deflated"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In cases of acute respiratory distress, where obstruction of the airway exists, suctioning must be performed emergently (with eventual preparation).  Why A/C/D wrong: A delay, withholding, or routine cuff deflation are inappropriate in acute distress."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"Lubrication of the ends of the catheter with petroleum jelly is recommended when performing which type of suctioning?","options":["Endotracheal suctioning","Closed in-line suctioning only","The deep suctioning through the nose (when going through the nasal route)","Oropharyngeal suctioning only"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Lubrication of the ends of the catheter with petroleum jelly is recommended for suctioning through the nose (nasal route deep suctioning).  Why A/B/D wrong: These routes/systems do not require petroleum-jelly lubrication as specified for the nasal route."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"Repeated deep suctioning may cause mucosal damage and prolonged hypoxia. When repeated, special attention should be given to which parameters?","options":["Heart rate and oxygen saturation levels","Blood glucose and temperature","Urine output and stool frequency","Pupil size and reflexes only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: If there is a need for repeated suctioning, special attention should be given to the heart rate and oxygen saturation levels (because of risk of avoid mucosal damage and prolonged hypoxia).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the parameters emphasized for repeated suctioning."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"According to the catheter colour-coding chart, a suction catheter that is colour-coded GREEN corresponds to which French (Fr) size?","options":["6 Fr","8 Fr","14 Fr","18 Fr"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Per the colour-coding chart, 14 Fr is coded Green (suitable for female and adult male use).  Why A wrong: 6 Fr is Light green (paediatric).  Why B wrong: 8 Fr is Blue.  Why D wrong: 18 Fr is Red."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"On the suction catheter colour-coding chart, which size is coded YELLOW?","options":["10 Fr","12 Fr","16 Fr","20 Fr"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The chart codes 20 Fr as Yellow.  Why A wrong: 10 Fr is Black.  Why B wrong: 12 Fr is White.  Why C wrong: 16 Fr is Orange."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"On the suction catheter colour-coding chart, which size is coded WHITE?","options":["8 Fr","10 Fr","12 Fr","16 Fr"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: 12 Fr is colour-coded White.  Why A wrong: 8 Fr is Blue.  Why B wrong: 10 Fr is Black.  Why D wrong: 16 Fr is Orange."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"Per the colour-coding chart, the 6 Fr catheter (Light green) is usually used for which population?","options":["Adult males","Paediatrics","Adult females only","Older children only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 6 Fr (Light green) is usually used for paediatrics.  Why A/C/D wrong: Adult-size catheters (e.g., 14 Fr green for female/adult male) are larger; 6 Fr is the paediatric size."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"RECALL (PYQ): The size of suction catheters used in male adults is generally which French size?","options":["2 Fr","6 Fr","8 Fr","14 Fr"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Per the recall item, 14 Fr is the size of suction catheters used in male adults (Right answer: 14 Fr).  Why A/B/C wrong: Smaller-numbered catheters (e.g., 6, 8 Fr) are used in infants/children; 12 Fr is used in adult females, and 14 Fr in adult males."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"Smaller (less in number) suction catheters are used in which populations?","options":["Adult males only","Infants and children (12 Fr and more in adult males and females)","Older adults only","No specific population"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Less than 16 Fr (smaller-number) catheters are used in infants and children, while 12 Fr and more are used in adult males and females (12 Fr in adult females, 14 Fr in adult males per the recall).  Why A/C/D wrong: Smaller catheters correspond to the smaller airways of infants and children, not adults exclusively."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"The effectiveness of suctioning can be assessed by which of the following? (Select all that apply)","options":["Clearance of secretions","Improved breath sounds and improved air entry","Good pulse oximetry readings indicating heart rate and oxygen saturation","Improvement in respiratory distress","An increase in retained thick secretions"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Effectiveness is assessed by clearance of secretions, improved breath sounds and improved air entry, good pulse oximetry readings (heart rate and oxygen saturation), and improvement in respiratory distress.  Why E wrong: Increased retained secretions would indicate ineffective suctioning."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"Per the source, the appropriate size of the suction catheter is reduced to half to reduce the risk of trauma during suctioning, using the formula:","options":["ET size × 2","ET size − 2","ET size ÷ 2","ET size + 2"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The formula given is ET size × 2 to select the appropriate suction catheter size (the selection of appropriate size catheter reduces risk of trauma during suctioning).  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated formula (ET size × 2)."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"Using the formula for an ET tube of internal diameter (ID) 8.0 mm, what is the appropriate suction catheter size?","options":["8 Fr","10 Fr","12 Fr","16 Fr"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Per the worked example, (8.0 × 2) = 16 Fr; the source shows ET ID 8.0 mm → 16 Fr (colour code White).  Why A/B/C wrong: 8, 10, and 12 Fr correspond to smaller ET IDs in the table."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"Per the ET size to suction catheter table, an ET tube ID of 6.0 mm corresponds to which suction catheter size and colour code?","options":["8 Fr — Blue","10 Fr — Black","12 Fr — White","14 Fr — Green"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: ET ID 6.0 mm → (6.0 × 2) = 12 Fr, colour-coded White per the table.  Why A wrong: 8 Fr (Blue) corresponds to ET ID 4.0 mm.  Why B wrong: 10 Fr (Black) corresponds to ET ID 5.0 mm.  Why D wrong: 14 Fr (Green) corresponds to ET ID 7.0 mm."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"Per the ET size to suction catheter table, an ET tube ID of 4.0 mm corresponds to which suction catheter size and colour code?","options":["8 Fr — Blue","10 Fr — Black","12 Fr — White","14 Fr — Green"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: ET ID 4.0 mm → (4.0 × 2) = 8 Fr, colour-coded Blue.  Why B wrong: 10 Fr (Black) = ET ID 5.0 mm.  Why C wrong: 12 Fr (White) = ET ID 6.0 mm.  Why D wrong: 14 Fr (Green) = ET ID 7.0 mm."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"Which of the following are recognized COMPLICATIONS of suctioning? (Select all that apply)","options":["Bradycardia, dysrhythmias, hypotension due to vagal stimulation","Hypoxia due to prolonged suctioning","Mucosal trauma if the catheter is not rotating in a circular motion while withdrawing the catheter","Ventilator-associated pneumonia (VAP) or infections if aseptic technique is not followed","Increased ICP, bronchospasm, accidental extubation, atelectasis and collapse of the right upper lobe (especially with excessive suctioning), hemodynamic instability, and pain/discomfort/anxiety"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: All listed are complications of suctioning per the source: bradycardia/dysrhythmias/hypotension from vagal stimulation; hypoxia from prolonged suctioning; mucosal trauma if not rotating in circular motion; VAP/infection if aseptic technique not followed; increased ICP, bronchospasm, accidental extubation, atelectasis and collapse of right upper lobe, hemodynamic instability, and pain/discomfort/anxiety.  Why no option is wrong: Each is explicitly listed as a complication."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"Atelectasis and collapse described as a suctioning complication especially affects which lobe, particularly with excessive suctioning?","options":["Left lower lobe","Right upper lobe","Left upper lobe","Right middle lobe"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Atelectasis and collapse of the right upper lobe especially occurs with excessive suctioning.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies the right upper lobe."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"Catheter (suction catheter) size is determined by which measurement?","options":["The inner diameter of the catheter","The outer diameter of the catheter (gauge system or French system)","The length of the catheter only","The weight of the catheter"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Catheter size is determined by the outer diameter of the catheter (using the gauge system or French system).  Why A wrong: It is the outer, not inner, diameter that defines size.  Why C/D wrong: Length and weight do not define catheter gauge size."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"In the French (Fr) gauge system for catheter sizing, as the French number increases, the outer diameter:","options":["Increases (it corresponds to a larger diameter)","Decreases","Stays the same","Becomes negative"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In the French system, the French size corresponds to a larger diameter; as the French number increases, the OD/catheter is larger (the French size measures outer diameter).  Why B wrong: It does not decrease with increasing number (this is the opposite of the gauge system).  Why C/D wrong: It changes proportionally and is never negative."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"In the gauge (Ga) system for catheter sizing, as the gauge number increases, the diameter:","options":["Increases","Decreases — a higher gauge number means a smaller catheter","Stays the same","Doubles"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the gauge system, an increased (higher) gauge number means a smaller diameter; for example, the OD of a 14G cannula is smaller than that of a 16G cannula.  Why A/C/D wrong: Higher gauge equals smaller, not larger, equal, or doubled diameter."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"Per the source's special note, the French (Fr) size relationship to the outer diameter is best expressed as which formula?","options":["French size = OD (mm) × 2","French size in mm = 3 × Fr; i.e., 1 mm = 3 Fr (so OD in mm = Fr ÷ 3)","French size = OD (mm) ÷ 2","French size = OD (mm) + 1"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: French size is measured by the outer diameter; the French system gives 1 mm = 3 Fr, and the French size in mm = (Fr ÷ 3), i.e., OD (mm) = French ÷ 3 (the French size does not measure inner diameter).  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the 1 mm = 3 Fr relationship."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"RECALL (PYQ): A baby admitted to ICU on ventilator support has a 6 mm ET tube. Which suction catheter size is used appropriately for suctioning?","options":["2 Fr","10 Fr","12 Fr","14 Fr"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Using ET size × 2: 6 mm ET → 12 Fr suction catheter (Right answer: 12 Fr).  Why A wrong: 2 Fr is far too small.  Why B wrong: 10 Fr corresponds to a 5 mm ET tube.  Why D wrong: 14 Fr corresponds to a 7 mm ET tube."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"RECALL (PYQ): At what level should the ET tube be placed in an adult patient to prevent bedsore/injury?","options":["Cheek to cheek before the ear","Cheek to cheek over the ear","Cheek to cheek above the nose","Cheek to cheek over the neck"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The recall answer is \"Cheek to cheek before the ear\" (Right answer: A).  Why B/C/D wrong: These positions are not the keyed correct answer."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"RECALL (PYQ): A child with a 4.5 mm endotracheal tube is admitted to ICU. Which size suction catheter is to be used most appropriately for suctioning?","options":["4 Fr","6 Fr","9 Fr","12 Fr"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Using ET size × 2: 4.5 × 2 = 9 Fr (Right answer: C). The keyed correct option corresponds to 9 Fr.  Why A/B wrong: 4 Fr and 6 Fr are too small for a 4.5 mm ET tube.  Why D wrong: 12 Fr corresponds to a 6 mm ET tube."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"RECALL (PYQ): A patient is admitted in casualty and intubated with a 6 mm endotracheal tube for emergency airway management. Which of the following suction catheter sizes is used (most appropriately) for endotracheal suctioning?","options":["10 Fr","12 Fr","14 Fr","8 Fr"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 6 mm ET × 2 = 12 Fr (Right answer: B), consistent with the size-selection formula.  Why A wrong: 10 Fr corresponds to a 5 mm ET.  Why C wrong: 14 Fr corresponds to a 7 mm ET.  Why D wrong: 8 Fr corresponds to a 4 mm ET."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"RECALL (PYQ): A nurse is suctioning an ET tube in a patient who is admitted in an emergency department. What intervention prevents damage into the lungs and tracheal mucosa?","options":["Apply suction on inserting the catheter while inserting the catheter","Only apply the suction while withdrawing the catheter and ensure proper oxygen suction is administered/withheld during insertion","Apply continuous high suction throughout","Suction without rotating the catheter"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The keyed answer is C in the source meaning \"Only apply the suction while withdrawing the catheter\" (option content here); suction is applied only on withdrawal — not on insertion — to prevent damage to the lungs and tracheal mucosa, ensuring proper oxygen and avoiding suction on insertion.  Why A wrong: Applying suction while inserting causes mucosal trauma and hypoxia.  Why C wrong: Continuous high suction increases trauma and hypoxia.  Why D wrong: The catheter should be rotated while withdrawing to minimize trauma."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"RECALL (PYQ): A nurse is suctioning fluids from a female client through an endotracheal tube. During the suctioning procedure, the SpO2 (oxygen saturation) is decreasing. Which of the following is the appropriate nursing intervention?","options":["Continue to suction until fluids are cleared","Notify the physician immediately while continuing","Stop the procedure and re-oxygenate the client","Ensure the suction is limited to 15 seconds"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The keyed correct answer is C — stop the procedure and re-oxygenate; during suctioning, if deep suctioning is being done and SpO2 (oxygen saturation) decreases, then stop the suction procedure and re-oxygenate the client (and avoid prolonged deep suctioning).  Why A wrong: Continuing while saturation drops worsens hypoxia.  Why B wrong: The immediate priority is to stop and re-oxygenate, not continue while notifying.  Why D wrong: Limiting time is preventive, but the priority once SpO2 falls is to stop and re-oxygenate."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"Per the special note, for deep suctioning, the suction catheter is inserted beyond the length of the ET tube/tracheostomy tube. By approximately how much (for deep suctioning) is it advanced, given that it is associated with vagal nerve stimulation, bradycardia, coughing, and trauma to the cilia and mucal action?","options":["It is inserted approximately 0.5 to 1 cm beyond the length of the ET tube or tracheostomy tube","5 cm beyond the tube","10 cm beyond the tube","It is never advanced beyond the tube"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The special note states that for deep suctioning, the catheter is inserted approximately 0.5 to 1 cm beyond the length of the ET tube or tracheostomy tube (and deep suctioning is associated with vagal nerve stimulation, bradycardia, coughing, and trauma to the cilia and mucal action).  Why B/C wrong: These exceed the stated 0.5–1 cm.  Why D wrong: For superficial suctioning the catheter stays at the tube end, but deep suctioning advances 0.5–1 cm beyond."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"Per the special note, for SHALLOW (superficial) suctioning, ensuring that the suction catheter has been inserted approximately how far relative to the ET/tracheostomy tube?","options":["5 cm beyond the tube tip","0.5 to 1 cm beyond the length of the ET tube or tracheostomy tube","Into the mainstem bronchus","Up to the carina"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For shallow suctioning, ensure the suction catheter has been inserted approximately 0.5 to 1 cm beyond the length of the ET tube or tracheostomy tube (per the special note).  Why A/C/D wrong: These describe deeper advancement than the stated shallow-suction depth."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"Three suction-equipment types named in the source page are: (Select all that apply)","options":["Suction machine","Paddle suction machine","Wall suction set","Centrifugal pump","Peristaltic pump"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The page depicts a suction machine, a paddle suction machine, and a wall suction set.  Why D/E wrong: These are not the suction devices shown in the source."},{"section":"Batch 039 · tracheostomy nursing care…","question":"A nurse caring for a ventilated patient hyperoxygenates the patient before suctioning, limits each pass to no more than 10–15 seconds, applies suction only on withdrawal with a rotating motion, and re-oxygenates between passes. The primary goal of this bundle of actions is to:","options":["Prevent suction-induced hypoxia and mucosal trauma","Increase the patient's secretions","Deflate the tracheostomy cuff","Eliminate the need for sterile technique"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hyperoxygenation, short passes (≤10–15 seconds), suction on withdrawal with rotation, and re-oxygenation collectively prevent hypoxia and mucosal trauma — the key complications of suctioning.  Why B wrong: These measures clear secretions, not increase them.  Why C wrong: They are unrelated to cuff deflation.  Why D wrong: Sterile/aseptic technique is still required to prevent VAP."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"Most chest (intercostal) drainage systems are based on which model?","options":["One-bottle system","Two-bottle system","Three-bottle system","Four-bottle system"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states most drainage systems use three chambers, based on the three-bottle system.  Why A wrong: A single bottle cannot separate collection, water-seal, and suction functions.  Why B wrong: Two bottles omit the dedicated suction-control chamber.  Why D wrong: The standard described is a three-bottle (three-chamber) system, not four."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"In a three-chamber chest drainage system, which chamber is directly connected to the patient and collects the drainage?","options":["Collection chamber","Water-seal chamber","Suction-control chamber","Air-leak chamber"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The collection chamber is directly connected to the patient; it is the first chamber and allows fluid drained from the chest to be collected.  Why B wrong: The water-seal chamber prevents atmospheric air from entering the pleural space.  Why C wrong: The suction-control chamber regulates negative pressure to the chest.  Why D wrong: There is no separate \"air-leak chamber\"; air-leak signs are observed in the water-seal chamber."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"A patient with a chest drainage system has fresh, bright-red drainage of more than 70–100 mL/hour. What is the appropriate nursing action?","options":["Continue routine monitoring only","Clamp the chest tube immediately","Notify the doctor, as this indicates abnormal bleeding","Increase the suction level"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source instructs to notify the doctor if drainage is more than 70–100 mL/hour or if drainage suddenly becomes bright red.  Why A wrong: Sudden bright-red, high-volume drainage is abnormal and must be reported.  Why B wrong: A chest tube should never be clamped without a doctor's order (risk of tension pneumothorax).  Why D wrong: Increasing suction does not address potential hemorrhage and is not the indicated action."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"What is the function of the water-seal chamber in a chest drainage system?","options":["To collect blood and fluid from the pleural space","To prevent atmospheric air from entering the pleural space","To control the amount of negative pressure applied","To measure hourly urine output"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The water-seal chamber prevents atmospheric air from entering the pleural space.  Why A wrong: That is the collection chamber's role.  Why C wrong: Negative pressure is regulated by the suction-control chamber.  Why D wrong: Urine output is unrelated to chest drainage chambers."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"Fluid in the water-seal chamber normally rises and falls (fluctuates) with the patient's respirations until which event occurs?","options":["Until the chest tube is clamped","Until the lungs are fully expanded","Until suction is discontinued","Until the collection chamber is full"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states fluid fluctuates with respirations until the lungs are fully expanded.  Why A wrong: Clamping is not the normal endpoint and is contraindicated without an order.  Why C wrong: Fluctuation relates to lung re-expansion, not suction status.  Why D wrong: A full collection chamber does not define cessation of fluctuation."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"Which of the following are recognized reasons fluctuation in the water-seal chamber may stop? (Select All That Apply)","options":["The tube is obstructed or kinked","A dependent loop exists or is resolved","The suction is not working properly","The lungs have re-expanded (resolved pneumothorax)","The collection chamber has been emptied"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists four causes of stopped fluctuation: tube obstructed/kinked; dependent loops; suction not working properly; lungs re-expanded (resolved pneumothorax).  Why E wrong: Emptying the collection chamber is not listed as a cause of stopped fluctuation."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"A nurse observes continuous or excessive bubbling in the water-seal chamber of a chest drainage system. This finding most likely indicates:","options":["Normal, expected drainage","An air leak or abnormal finding that should be reported to the doctor immediately","That the lungs have fully re-expanded","That suction is too low"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Continuous or excessive bubbling may indicate air leaking or an abnormal finding and should be reported to the doctor immediately.  Why A wrong: Continuous/excessive bubbling is abnormal, not expected.  Why C wrong: Full lung re-expansion is associated with cessation of fluctuation, not continuous bubbling.  Why D wrong: Continuous bubbling in the water-seal chamber signals an air leak, not low suction."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"Intermittent bubbling in the water-seal chamber of a chest drainage system most likely indicates:","options":["A large air leak requiring immediate clamping","A normal finding / normal level is maintained","Disconnection of the system","Tension pneumothorax"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states intermittent bubbling may indicate suction level is maintained or normal finding.  Why A wrong: It is intermittent (not continuous/excessive) bubbling and is considered normal; clamping is contraindicated.  Why C wrong: Disconnection is not what intermittent bubbling indicates.  Why D wrong: Tension pneumothorax is not signaled by normal intermittent bubbling."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"What is the primary purpose of the suction-control chamber in a chest drainage system?","options":["To collect drainage fluid","To prevent air from entering the pleural space","To control and provide negative pressure to the chest","To warm the inhaled air"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The suction-control chamber provides and controls negative pressure to the chest.  Why A wrong: Collection is the role of the collection chamber.  Why B wrong: Preventing atmospheric air entry is the water-seal chamber's role.  Why D wrong: The system does not warm inhaled air."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"In the suction-control chamber, the level of suction is determined by which factor?","options":["The height of the collection chamber","The water level (water column) in the chamber","The patient's respiratory rate","The colour of the drainage"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The suction-control chamber is filled with various levels of water; the water level determines (controls) the desired level of suction.  Why A wrong: Suction level is set by the water column, not the collection chamber height.  Why C wrong: Respiratory rate does not set the suction level.  Why D wrong: Drainage colour is a clinical observation, not a determinant of suction level."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"What does steady or constant bubbling in the suction-control chamber indicate?","options":["An air leak in the pleural space","That the suction level is maintained (normal finding)","A blocked chest tube","A resolved pneumothorax"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Steady or constant bubbling in the suction-control chamber indicates the suction level is maintained / normal finding.  Why A wrong: Air leaks are reflected in the water-seal chamber, not steady bubbling in the suction-control chamber.  Why C wrong: A blocked tube would reduce, not maintain, expected bubbling.  Why D wrong: Pneumothorax resolution is related to the water-seal chamber."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"Vigorous bubbling in the suction-control chamber should prompt the nurse to:","options":["Ignore it as a normal finding","Clamp the chest tube immediately","Reduce the suction level and inform the doctor","Disconnect the suction tubing"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Vigorous bubbling in the suction-control chamber indicates an abnormal finding and the need to reduce the suction level and inform the doctor.  Why A wrong: Vigorous bubbling is abnormal, not normal.  Why B wrong: Clamping a chest tube without an order is contraindicated.  Why D wrong: Disconnecting the suction tubing is not the indicated corrective action."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"What level of suction (water column) is recommended to be maintained for a chest tube drainage system?","options":["5 cm H2O","10 cm H2O","20 cm H2O","40 cm H2O"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The note states 20 cm H2O suction is to be maintained for the chest-tube drainage system.  Why A/B/D wrong: The specified value is 20 cm H2O, not 5, 10, or 40 cm H2O."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"Which findings indicate that a chest tube drainage system can be removed? (Select All That Apply)","options":["When the fluid coming is less than 50 mL/day","When the lungs have fully expanded","When continuous bubbling appears in the water seal","When drainage is bright red and exceeds 100 mL/hour","When fluctuation continues with respirations"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists two indications for removal: fluid coming less than 50 mL/day, and when the lungs have fully expanded.  Why C wrong: Continuous bubbling indicates an air leak, not readiness for removal.  Why D wrong: Bright-red high-volume drainage indicates bleeding, not removal readiness.  Why E wrong: Continued fluctuation indicates the lungs are not yet fully re-expanded."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"Which respiratory parameters should the nurse monitor while caring for a patient with a chest tube? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Respiratory rate","Depth and rate rhythm of breathing","Use of accessory muscles","Oxygen saturation","Breath sounds"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists all of these — RR, depth and rate rhythm, use of accessory muscles, oxygen saturation, and breath sounds.  Why none wrong: All five are explicitly stated nursing-care monitoring parameters."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"In a patient with a chest tube, what type of dressing should be maintained at the insertion site?","options":["A wet-to-dry dressing","An occlusive dressing","No dressing","A transparent film dressing only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: An occlusive dressing is maintained at the insertion site.  Why A wrong: A wet-to-dry dressing is not specified and would not seal the site.  Why C wrong: The site must be covered with an occlusive dressing.  Why D wrong: The source specifies an occlusive dressing, not a transparent-film-only dressing."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"A chest radiograph in a patient with a chest tube is performed primarily to assess:","options":["The position of the tubes and whether the lungs have re-expanded","The patient's hemoglobin level","The presence of a urinary infection","The depth of sedation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A chest radiograph assesses the position of the tubes and determines whether the lungs have re-expanded.  Why B/C/D wrong: A chest X-ray does not assess hemoglobin, urinary infection, or sedation depth in this context."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"To maintain patency, the nurse must ensure the chest-tube tubing is free of which problems? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Kinks","Dependent loops","Other obstructions","Secure connections"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to ensure the tubing is not kinked, has no dependent loops, and no other obstructions.  Why D wrong: Secure connections are desirable; the requirement is that connections be secure, not \"free of\" connections — it is not one of the obstruction items to avoid."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"The nurse should position the chest drainage system in relation to the client:","options":["Above the level of the chest","At the same level as the chest","Below the level of the chest","Position does not matter"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The drainage system should be kept below the level of the chest to allow gravity drainage.  Why A/B wrong: Raising the system above or to chest level can cause backflow and impair drainage.  Why D wrong: Position is critical to maintain effective drainage."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"Which patient activities should the nurse encourage in a patient with a chest tube to promote lung re-expansion? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Movement","Coughing","Deep breathing every hour","Strict bed rest with minimal movement"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The nurse should encourage movement, coughing, and deep breathing every hour.  Why D wrong: Strict immobility is the opposite of the encouraged movement, coughing, and deep breathing."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"Why should the nurse change the client's position frequently when caring for a chest drainage system?","options":["To prevent pressure ulcers only","To provide drainage and ventilation, ensuring chest tubes are not compressed and free of restrictions, and to prevent accidental dislodgement","To increase suction pressure","To stop fluctuation in the water seal"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Frequent position changes provide drainage and ventilation; before changing position, ensure tubes are not compressed and are free of restrictions to prevent accidental dislodgement.  Why A wrong: While repositioning helps skin, the stated purpose is drainage/ventilation and preventing dislodgement.  Why C wrong: Repositioning does not increase suction pressure.  Why D wrong: Stopping water-seal fluctuation is not a goal of repositioning."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"Why is \"milking and stripping\" of a chest tube considered contraindicated?","options":["It causes the lungs to collapse","It increases negative intra-pleural pressure","It introduces air into the pleural space","It dislodges the suction control chamber"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Milking and stripping is contraindicated because it increases negative intra-pleural pressure.  Why A wrong: The stated reason is increased negative intra-pleural pressure, not direct lung collapse.  Why C wrong: The reason given is increased negative pressure, not air introduction.  Why D wrong: It does not dislodge the suction-control chamber."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"When observing for fluctuations of fluid in the water-seal chamber, the nurse expects the fluid level to:","options":["Rise on inhalation and fall on exhalation","Stay completely still","Rise only on exhalation","Fall continuously to zero"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the fluid will be raised on inhalation and fall on exhalation.  Why B wrong: A completely still level (no fluctuation) suggests obstruction or full lung re-expansion.  Why C/D wrong: The described normal pattern is rise on inhalation and fall on exhalation, not the alternatives."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"A nurse caring for a chest tube should keep 2 clamps at the bedside at all times. Under what circumstances may the clamps be needed? (Select All That Apply)","options":["When changing the system","If a leak is suspected","To routinely clamp the tube every hour","To accompany a sterile occlusive dressing kept at the bedside"],"correct":"A,B,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source says keep 2 clamps (may be needed when the change of system or if a leak is suspected) and keep a sterile occlusive dressing set at the bedside at all times.  Why C wrong: Routine hourly clamping is not indicated; chest tubes should not be clamped without an order."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"Why should a nurse never clamp a chest tube without a doctor's order?","options":["It may cause hemorrhage","It may cause tension pneumothorax","It increases urine output","It causes subcutaneous emphysema only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Clamping a chest tube without a doctor's order may cause tension pneumothorax.  Why A wrong: The stated complication is tension pneumothorax, not hemorrhage.  Why C wrong: Clamping has no effect on urine output.  Why D wrong: The specific stated risk is tension pneumothorax."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"In a patient with a chest tube, tracheal deviation away from the affected side is a sign of:","options":["Subcutaneous emphysema","Tension pneumothorax","Resolved pneumothorax","Normal lung re-expansion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The nurse should monitor for tracheal deviation, which is a sign of tension pneumothorax.  Why A wrong: Subcutaneous emphysema is detected by palpating crepitus at the insertion site, not tracheal deviation.  Why C/D wrong: Tracheal deviation is a pathological sign, not normal re-expansion."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"A nurse palpates crepitus around a chest tube insertion site. This finding indicates:","options":["Air leaking into the subcutaneous tissues (subcutaneous emphysema)","Wound infection","Tension pneumothorax","Hemothorax"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Crepitus on palpation at the insertion site indicates air leaking into subcutaneous tissues (subcutaneous emphysema).  Why B wrong: Crepitus reflects air, not pus/infection.  Why C wrong: Tension pneumothorax presents with tracheal deviation, not crepitus alone.  Why D wrong: Hemothorax is blood in the pleural space, not subcutaneous air."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"If a chest tube is accidentally pulled out of the chest, what is the FIRST priority nursing action?","options":["Call the doctor immediately","Apply an occlusive sterile dressing over the insertion site","Insert a new tube","Start suction at a higher level"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source designates applying an occlusive sterile dressing over the insertion site as the first priority, then calling the doctor as the second priority.  Why A wrong: Calling the doctor is the second priority, after sealing the site.  Why C wrong: Nurses do not reinsert a chest tube; this is a physician action.  Why D wrong: Adjusting suction does not address the open chest wound."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"If the chest drainage system cracks or breaks, what is the FIRST priority action?","options":["Clamp the chest tube","Immediately insert the tube into a bottle of sterile water or NS","Call the doctor and wait","Remove the chest tube"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The first priority is to immediately insert the tube into a bottle of sterile water or NS; the second priority is to call the doctor immediately.  Why A wrong: Clamping without an order risks tension pneumothorax.  Why C wrong: Calling the doctor is the second priority, after re-establishing a water seal.  Why D wrong: The tube should be placed in sterile water/NS, not removed."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"During planned removal of a chest tube, the client is instructed to take a deep breath and hold it or bear down. This maneuver is known as:","options":["Trendelenburg maneuver","Valsalva maneuver","Credé maneuver","Heimlich maneuver"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Asking the client to take a deep breath and hold or bear down is the Valsalva maneuver, performed during chest tube removal.  Why A wrong: Trendelenburg is a head-down body position, not a breathing maneuver.  Why C wrong: Credé maneuver is manual bladder compression to empty the bladder.  Why D wrong: Heimlich maneuver is for choking relief."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"After a chest tube is removed, what should be placed on the insertion site?","options":["A wet gauze","A tightly applied dry sterile dressing","No dressing","A pressure tourniquet"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After removal of the tube, a tightly applied dry sterile dressing should be placed on the insertion site; a sterile dressing is applied over the insertion site.  Why A wrong: A dry sterile dressing, not a wet gauze, is specified.  Why C wrong: A sterile dressing must be applied.  Why D wrong: A tourniquet is inappropriate and not indicated."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"(NORCET-4 2023) A nurse caring for a patient with chest tube drainage suddenly notices that there is no fluctuation in the water-seal tube. Which of the following action should the nurse take FIRST?","options":["Turn the patient to the unaffected side","Inform the physician","Check the tubing to ensure there is no kinking","Start milking the chest tube"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Per the source key (Right Answer – C), absence of fluctuation is first addressed by checking the tubing for kinks/obstruction, the simplest and most likely cause the nurse can correct independently.  Why A wrong: Repositioning is not the first action for a stopped fluctuation.  Why B wrong: The physician is informed only after the nurse rules out correctable causes such as kinking.  Why D wrong: Milking the chest tube is contraindicated (it increases negative intra-pleural pressure)."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"Urinary bladder catheterization is best described as:","options":["A non-invasive comfort measure","An invasive procedure in which a sterile catheter is introduced into the bladder through the urethra","A surgical incision into the bladder","A radiologic imaging test only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Catheterization is an invasive procedure in which a sterile catheter is introduced into the bladder through the urethra, for therapeutic and diagnostic purposes.  Why A wrong: It is invasive, not non-invasive.  Why C wrong: It uses the urethra, not a surgical incision (except supra-pubic types described separately).  Why D wrong: It is a catheter procedure, not merely imaging."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"According to the text, the three types of urinary catheters based on the approach of insertion are external, urethral, and:","options":["Supra-pubic catheters","Nasogastric catheters","Central venous catheters","Peritoneal catheters"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The three types based on approach of insertion are external (e.g., condom catheter), urethral (most commonly used), and supra-pubic (inserted into the bladder via a supra-pubic approach).  Why B/C/D wrong: Nasogastric, central venous, and peritoneal catheters are not urinary catheterization approaches listed."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"Which urinary catheter type is most commonly used?","options":["External (condom) catheter","Urethral catheter","Supra-pubic catheter","Nephrostomy catheter"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Among the three approaches, the urethral catheter is most commonly used.  Why A wrong: External catheters (e.g., condom) are used mainly for male patients with incontinence, not the most common overall.  Why C wrong: Supra-pubic is a less common approach via the supra-pubic route.  Why D wrong: Nephrostomy is not among the listed urinary catheterization approaches."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"Which of the following are stated purposes of urinary catheterization? (Select All That Apply)","options":["To relieve urinary retention","To obtain a sterile urine specimen from a woman","To measure the amount of residual urine in the bladder","To empty the bladder before and during surgery and before certain diagnostic examinations","To obtain a urine specimen when a specimen cannot be secured satisfactorily by other means"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists all five as purposes of catheterization.  Why none wrong: Each option is explicitly listed under Purposes."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"Which of the following are therapeutic indications for urinary catheterization listed in the text? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Urinary retention","Neurogenic bladder dysfunction","Urinary incontinence","Acutely ill patients requiring close urinary output measurement","Instillation of medicines / bladder irrigation"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Therapeutic indications listed include urinary retention, neurogenic bladder dysfunction, urinary incontinence, social and hygiene reasons, acutely ill patients requiring close urinary output measurement, instillation of medicines, bladder irrigation, and perioperative.  Why none wrong: All listed options appear among the therapeutic indications."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"Which of the following are diagnostic indications for urinary catheterization? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Measurement of urodynamics","Sample collection for urinalysis","Radiographic studies (cystogram)","Gross hematuria"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Diagnostic indications listed are measurement of urodynamics, sample collection for urinalysis, and radiographic studies (cystogram).  Why D wrong: Gross hematuria is listed as a contraindication, not a diagnostic indication."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"Which of the following are contraindications to urinary catheterization? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Blood at the meatus","Gross hematuria","Evidence of urethral infection and constriction","Urethral pain or discomfort","Patient refusal"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The text lists blood at the meatus, gross hematuria, evidence of urethral infection and constriction, urethral pain or discomfort, low bladder volume, and patient refusal as contraindications.  Why none wrong: All listed options are contraindications."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"Which of the following best characterizes an indwelling (Foley) catheter? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Stays in place for days or weeks","Is attached to a closed drainage urinary bag","Must be initiated, replaced, and removed by a trained doctor or nurses","Is discarded after a single one-time use"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: An indwelling catheter stays in place for days or weeks, is attached to a closed drainage urinary bag, and must be initiated/replaced/removed by a trained doctor or nurse (example: Foley catheter).  Why D wrong: Single one-time use and discard describes the intermittent catheter, not the indwelling one."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"Which catheter type is used multiple times a day, often designed for one-time use and discarded after use, with the K-90 / Robinson catheter as an example?","options":["Indwelling catheter","Intermittent catheter","External catheter","Three-way catheter"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The intermittent catheter is often designed for one-time use and discarded after use, used multiple times a day; example K-90, Robinson catheter.  Why A wrong: Indwelling catheters remain in place for days/weeks.  Why C wrong: External catheters (condom) are for male patients with incontinence.  Why D wrong: Three-way catheters are used for bladder irrigation, not described here."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"Which of the following statements about the external (condom) catheter are correct? (Select All That Apply)","options":["It is used only for male patients","It is primarily used for urinary incontinence, not urinary retention","It can be easily administered by patients or caregivers","An example is the condom catheter","It must be removed only by a trained doctor"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The external catheter is only for male patients, primarily used for urinary incontinence (not retention), can be easily administered by patients or caregivers; example condom catheter.  Why E wrong: The text describes it as easily administered by patients/caregivers, not requiring removal only by a trained doctor (that applies to indwelling catheters)."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"On a Foley catheter, the urine drainage port is marked using which scale?","options":["Millimeters of mercury","French (mm/sizes)","Centimeters of water","Gauge"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The urine drainage port is marked with French (mm/sizes).  Why A/C/D wrong: The drainage port marking uses the French scale, not mmHg, cmH2O, or gauge."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"On a Foley catheter, the balloon port is colour-coded and marked with what information?","options":["The patient's weight","The recommended volume of fluid (for balloon inflation)","The catheter length","The expiry date"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The balloon port is colour-coded and marked with the recommended volume of fluid for inflation.  Why A/C/D wrong: The balloon port indicates recommended inflation fluid volume, not weight, length, or expiry."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"According to the Foley catheter size table, which insertion length is given for adult MALES?","options":["2–3 inches or 6–8 cm","6–8 inches or 16–20 cm","10–12 inches or 25–30 cm","1–2 inches or 3–5 cm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For males the insertion length is 6–8 inches or 16–20 cm.  Why A wrong: 2–3 inches or 6–8 cm is the female insertion length.  Why C/D wrong: These lengths are not in the table."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"According to the Foley catheter size table, the insertion length for adult FEMALES is:","options":["6–8 inches or 16–20 cm","2–3 inches or 6–8 cm","4–5 inches or 10–12 cm","8–10 inches or 20–25 cm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Female insertion length is 2–3 inches or 6–8 cm.  Why A wrong: 6–8 inches or 16–20 cm is the male length.  Why C/D wrong: Not values given in the table."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"Match the Foley catheter French sizes to their colour codes. Which of the following pairings are correct per the table? (Select All That Apply)","options":["18 Fr – Red","16 Fr – Orange","14 Fr – Green","12 Fr – White","10 Fr – Black"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The table colour codes are 18 Fr Red, 16 Fr Orange, 14 Fr Green, 12 Fr White, 10 Fr Grey, and <8 Fr Black.  Why E wrong: 10 Fr is Grey; Black corresponds to <8 Fr (infant), so the 10 Fr – Black pairing is incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"According to the Foley size table, an infant catheter of less than 8 Fr is colour-coded:","options":["Grey","Black","White","Red"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The <8 Fr (infant) size is colour-coded Black.  Why A wrong: Grey corresponds to 10 Fr (child).  Why C wrong: White is 12 Fr.  Why D wrong: Red is 18 Fr."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"(NORCET-5 Prelims-2023) Which is a preferred Foley catheter size used in adult female?","options":["14–16 Fr","10–12 Fr","16–18 Fr","08–10 Fr"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Per the source key (Right Answer – A), 14–16 Fr is the preferred Foley size for adult females; the Must Know facts state No. 12 to 16 French catheters are usually used for the female adult, consistent with this choice.  Why B/C/D wrong: These ranges are not the keyed preferred adult-female size."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"Foley catheters are graded on which scale, based on the size of the lumen?","options":["Charrière/French scale","Gauge scale","Metric weight scale","APGAR scale"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Catheters are graded on the French scale according to the size of the lumen.  Why B wrong: Needles use the gauge scale, not catheters.  Why C/D wrong: These are unrelated scales."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"The size of a Foley catheter mentioned in \"Fr\" is based on which dimension of the catheter?","options":["Inner diameter (lumen)","Outer diameter of the catheter","Total length","Balloon volume"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The size mentioned in Fr is based on the outer diameter of the catheter.  Why A wrong: Although graded according to lumen size, the Fr value is based on the outer diameter.  Why C/D wrong: Fr is not based on length or balloon volume."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"Which Foley catheter colour/size is the most commonly used in general practice?","options":["Red (18 Fr)","Orange (16 Fr)","Green (14 Fr)","Grey (10 Fr)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common catheter used in general practice is orange colour (16 Fr).  Why A/C/D wrong: These sizes are not identified as the most commonly used."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"(NORCET-2022) For the female adult, which French catheter sizes are usually used?","options":["No. 8 to No. 10","No. 12 to No. 16","No. 16 to No. 18 and more","No. 20 to No. 24"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For the female adult, No. 12 to 16 French catheters are usually used.  Why A wrong: No. 8 and No. 10 are commonly used for children.  Why C wrong: No. 16, No. 18 and more are usually used for the male adult.  Why D wrong: This range is not specified."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"For the male adult, which French catheter sizes are usually used (with the caveat that a smaller one is used if too large)?","options":["No. 8 and No. 10","No. 12 and No. 14","No. 16 and No. 18 and more","No. 22 and No. 24"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: For the male adult, No. 16 and No. 18 and more French catheters are usually used, but if too large a smaller catheter should be used.  Why A wrong: No. 8 and No. 10 are for children.  Why B wrong: No. 12 to 16 are usual for adult females.  Why D wrong: Not specified."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"Why are very small Foley catheters generally NOT preferred?","options":["They are too expensive","The small lumen increases the length of time necessary for emptying the bladder","They damage the urethra immediately","They cannot have a balloon"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Small catheters are generally not necessary, and the small lumen increases the time necessary for emptying the bladder.  Why A wrong: Cost is not the stated reason.  Why C wrong: It is the larger catheter that distends the urethra and increases discomfort.  Why D wrong: The lumen size, not balloon presence, is the issue."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"What is a consequence of using a larger-than-necessary Foley catheter?","options":["It empties the bladder too slowly","It distends the urethra and tends to increase the discomfort of the procedure","It cannot be secured","It always causes hematuria"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A larger catheter distends the urethra and tends to increase the discomfort of the procedure.  Why A wrong: Slow emptying is a problem of small lumens.  Why C/D wrong: Not the stated consequence."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"Which French catheter sizes are commonly used for children?","options":["No. 8 and No. 10","No. 12 and No. 14","No. 16 and No. 18","No. 20 and No. 22"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: No. 8 and No. 10 French catheters are commonly used for children.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are adult/larger sizes, not specified for children."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"A three-way Foley catheter is most commonly used for which purpose?","options":["Single intermittent drainage","Bladder irrigation, commonly used after TURP procedure","Suprapubic drainage","Condom drainage"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Three-way Foley catheters are used for bladder irrigation, commonly after a TURP procedure.  Why A wrong: One-time intermittent drainage uses a straight (Robinson) catheter.  Why C/D wrong: Suprapubic and condom are different catheter approaches."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"With what fluid should the balloon of a Foley catheter be inflated?","options":["Air only","Normal saline only","Sterile water only","Distilled water mixed with antiseptic"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The balloon is inflated with sterile water only; Normal Saline (NS) and air must not be used.  Why A wrong: Air may cause floating of the balloon in the bladder and is not used.  Why B wrong: Normal saline may cause blockage with crystals and is not used.  Why D wrong: Only sterile water is specified."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"Why should Normal Saline (NS) NOT be used to inflate a Foley catheter balloon?","options":["It is too expensive","It may cause blockage with crystals","It causes the balloon to float","It dissolves the balloon"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Saline may cause blockage with crystals, so it must not be used in the balloon.  Why A/D wrong: Cost and dissolution are not the stated reasons.  Why C wrong: Air (not saline) may cause floating of the balloon."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"Why should air NOT be used to inflate a Foley catheter balloon?","options":["It may cause floating of the balloon in the bladder","It causes crystal blockage","It is sterile but irritating","It deflates too slowly"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Air may cause floating of the balloon in the bladder.  Why B wrong: Crystal blockage is caused by saline, not air.  Why C/D wrong: Not the stated reasons."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"Which of the following are correct preparatory steps before urinary catheterization? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Explain the procedure to the patient","Maintain the patient's privacy","Make sure an appropriate light source is available","Have the patient lie on a firm flat surface with the head resting on a pillow","Test the catheter balloon by inflating and deflating before insertion"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: All five are stated preparation steps, including testing the balloon by inflating/deflating before insertion.  Why none wrong: Each option is explicitly listed under Preparation."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"What is the appropriate position for a FEMALE patient during catheterization?","options":["Prone position","Dorsal recumbent or supine with knees flexed (frog leg position)","Supine with knees slightly abducted","Left lateral (Sims) position"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For females, the position is dorsal recumbent or supine with knees flexed (frog leg position).  Why A wrong: Prone is incorrect for catheterization.  Why C wrong: Supine with knees slightly abducted is the male position.  Why D wrong: Sims position is not specified for catheterization."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"What is the appropriate position for a MALE patient during catheterization?","options":["Dorsal recumbent with knees fully flexed","Supine with knees slightly abducted","Prone position","Trendelenburg position"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For males, the position is supine with knees slightly abducted.  Why A wrong: Frog-leg/knees flexed dorsal recumbent is the female position.  Why C/D wrong: Prone and Trendelenburg are not specified."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"Before catheterization, where should a sterile draw sheet be placed?","options":["Under the patient's head","Under the perineal area or patient's buttocks","Over the patient's chest","Around the catheter bag"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A sterile draw sheet is placed under the perineal area or the patient's buttocks.  Why A/C/D wrong: These locations are not specified for the sterile draw sheet."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"During urinary catheterization, the nurse should use which technique to prevent catheter-associated infection?","options":["Clean technique only","Sterile (aseptic) technique with sterile gloves","No special technique","Medical asepsis only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The nurse should wear sterile gloves and use sterile technique during the procedure to prevent catheter-associated infection.  Why A/D wrong: Clean/medical asepsis is insufficient; sterile technique is required.  Why C wrong: A specific sterile technique is required."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"During FEMALE catheterization, after positioning, the perineal area is exposed using a fenestrated sterile drape and the external genitalia and meatus are exposed using which hand?","options":["The dominant hand","The non-dominant hand","Either hand","Both hands simultaneously"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The external genitalia and meatus are exposed in the female using the non-dominant hand (keeping the dominant hand sterile for the catheter).  Why A wrong: The dominant hand stays sterile to handle the catheter.  Why C/D wrong: The non-dominant hand is specifically used to expose and separate."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"In female catheterization, the labia minora should be separated using which technique?","options":["Thumb and forefingers (then held apart by the dominant hand during cleaning)","The whole palm","Two cotton swabs","A sterile forceps only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Separate the labia minora with thumbs and forefingers, then keep the labia separated by the (non-)dominant hand during cleaning.  Why B/C/D wrong: The text specifies separating with thumbs and forefingers, not palm, swabs, or forceps."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"In female catheterization, the urinary meatus is cleaned with antiseptic (e.g., betadine) using which direction and technique?","options":["Bottom to top / back to front","Top to bottom / front to back (from above the meatus down toward the rectum)","In a circular outward spiral","Side to side"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Clean the vulva/urinary meatus from top to bottom / front to back — from above the meatus down toward the rectum.  Why A wrong: Cleaning back to front would drag perineal/rectal organisms toward the meatus.  Why C/D wrong: The specified technique is front-to-back strokes, not circular or side-to-side."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"During female meatal cleaning, how should the cotton swab/gauze be used for each cleaning stroke?","options":["Reuse the same swab for all strokes","Use a new cotton swab or gauze piece for each stroke","Use the bare gloved finger","Use the same swab but rinse it between strokes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states to use a new cotton swab or gauze piece for each stroke.  Why A/D wrong: Reusing or rinsing the same swab risks reintroducing organisms.  Why C wrong: A swab/gauze is used, not a bare finger."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"In female catheterization, what is used to lubricate the tip of the catheter?","options":["Plain petroleum jelly","Water-soluble gel (xylocaine 2%)","Mineral oil","Betadine solution"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The tip of the catheter is lubricated with water-soluble gel, xylocaine 2%.  Why A/C wrong: Petroleum jelly and mineral oil are not water-soluble and are not used.  Why D wrong: Betadine is used to clean, not lubricate."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"In female catheterization, after the catheter is inserted about 2–3 inches (6–8 cm) and urine drains, the catheter should be advanced an additional how much to facilitate balloon inflation without damaging the urethra?","options":["Another 1 inch (2.5 cm)","Another 2–3 inches or 6–8 cm","Another 6–8 inches or 16–20 cm","It should not be advanced further"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Once urine drains, advance the catheter another 2–3 inches or 6–8 cm to facilitate balloon inflation without damaging the urethra.  Why A wrong: The text specifies 2–3 inches/6–8 cm, not 1 inch.  Why C wrong: 6–8 inches is too far and corresponds to male insertion length.  Why D wrong: It must be advanced further to seat the balloon in the bladder."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"After insertion in female catheterization, the balloon is usually inflated with how much sterile water?","options":["5 mL","10 to 15 mL","30 mL","50 mL"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After insertion, inflate the balloon usually with 10 to 15 mL sterile water (or per manufacturer's recommendations).  Why A/D wrong: Not the usual stated volume.  Why C wrong: 30 mL is the maximum capacity of the balloon, not the usual inflation volume."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"After inflating the balloon in female catheterization, where should the catheter be secured with adhesive tape?","options":["On the patient's lower abdomen","On the patient's inner aspect of the upper thigh","On the patient's knee","On the side rail of the bed"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Secure the catheter with adhesive tape on the patient's inner aspect of the upper thigh.  Why A/C/D wrong: The text specifies the inner aspect of the upper thigh for securing the catheter."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"During female catheterization, if the catheter is accidentally inserted into the vagina instead of the urethra, what should the nurse do?","options":["Remove it and reuse the same catheter for the urethra","Leave it in place until a new sterile catheter is successfully inserted into the meatus","Inflate the balloon in the vagina","Abandon the procedure entirely"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If the catheter is inserted into the vagina, it should be left there until a new sterile catheter is successfully inserted into the meatus (it serves as a landmark and prevents re-entry into the vagina).  Why A wrong: A contaminated catheter (vaginal) must not be reused for the sterile urethral route.  Why C wrong: The balloon must never be inflated in the vagina/urethra.  Why D wrong: The procedure is completed with a new sterile catheter, not abandoned."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"(NORCET June-2023) What would be the correct steps to follow in female catheterization?","options":["Clean with betadine – apply lignocaine on tip of catheter – insert catheter – check for urine – inflate the balloon","Apply lignocaine on tip of catheter – clean with betadine – insert catheter – inflate the balloon – check for urine","Clean with betadine – apply lignocaine on tip of catheter – insert catheter – inflate the balloon – check for urine","Clean with betadine – apply lignocaine on tip of catheter – check for urine – insert catheter – inflate the balloon"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Per the source key (Right answer: A), the correct sequence is clean with betadine, apply lignocaine on the tip, insert the catheter, check for urine return, then inflate the balloon — urine return must be confirmed before balloon inflation.  Why B wrong: Cleaning must precede lubrication, and the balloon is inflated only after confirming urine.  Why C wrong: Inflating the balloon before checking for urine risks urethral injury.  Why D wrong: Urine cannot be checked before the catheter is inserted."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"(NORCET-4 2023; NORCET-5 Prelims-2023) Identify the correct cleaning process of the urethral meatus for female catheterization.","options":["Labia minora, front to back","Labia majora, back to front","Whole perineum","Labia minora, back to front"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Per the source key (Right Answer – A), the urinary meatus in females is cleaned with sterile gauze and antiseptic (e.g., betadine) in the direction of front to back (top to bottom) along the labia minora.  Why B wrong: Back to front drags rectal organisms toward the meatus.  Why C wrong: Cleaning targets the labia/meatus with directional strokes, not the whole perineum indiscriminately.  Why D wrong: Labia minora back to front is the wrong direction."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"In MALE catheterization, in which position should the patient be placed?","options":["Dorsal recumbent (frog leg)","Supine position with knees slightly abducted","Prone position","Sitting upright"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For male catheterization, place the patient in supine position with knees slightly abducted.  Why A wrong: Frog-leg dorsal recumbent is the female position.  Why C/D wrong: Prone or sitting upright are not specified."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"In male catheterization, the penis is held with the non-dominant hand at what angle to the body?","options":["45% / parallel to the body","90% / perpendicular to body, directed toward the ceiling or the umbilicus","30% / pointing toward the feet","60% / toward the floor"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hold the penis by the non-dominant hand between thumbs and forefingers perpendicular to the body 90%, directed toward the ceiling or the umbilicus (this straightens the urethra).  Why A/C/D wrong: The specified angle is 90%/perpendicular toward the ceiling or umbilicus."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"In male catheterization, the cleaning motion around the meatus and shaft uses antiseptic solution (e.g., betadine) and should be:","options":["From the shaft toward the meatus","From the meatus to the shaft in a circular motion to downward strokes toward the pubic area, using a new gauze for each stroke","Side to side only","A single wipe with one gauze"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Clean the tip of the penis first, from the meatus to the shaft in a circular motion to downward strokes toward the pubic area, using one gauze piece with antiseptic solutions for each stroke; repeat the cleaning motion 2–3 times.  Why A wrong: Cleaning is from the meatus outward (toward the shaft/pubic area), not toward the meatus.  Why C wrong: A circular-to-downward stroke technique is used, not side to side.  Why D wrong: A new gauze is used for each stroke, repeated 2–3 times."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"In male catheterization, the foreskin should be:","options":["Left retracted permanently","Retracted before the procedure and replaced after the procedure","Never touched","Cleaned with plain water only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Retract the foreskin before the procedure and replace it after the procedure (to prevent paraphimosis).  Why A wrong: Leaving the foreskin retracted can cause paraphimosis.  Why C/D wrong: The foreskin must be retracted/replaced and the area cleaned with antiseptic, not ignored or cleaned with plain water."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"In male catheterization, what local anesthetic gel and concentration is usually instilled into the male urethra for lubrication and anesthetic effect?","options":["Xylocaine 1%","Xylocaine 2%","Lidocaine 5%","Betadine gel"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Take a syringe and fill it with water-soluble lubricated gel, usually xylocaine 2%, then push it into the male urethra for lubrication and local anaesthetic effect.  Why A/C wrong: The specified concentration is 2%, not 1% or 5%.  Why D wrong: Betadine is an antiseptic for cleaning, not a urethral lubricant/anesthetic."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"In male catheterization, how far should the catheter be inserted (to ensure correct placement in the bladder)?","options":["2–3 inches or 6–8 cm","6–8 inches or 17–20 cm or well past the point at which urine returns","10–12 inches or 25–30 cm","1 inch or 2.5 cm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Insert the catheter about 6–8 inches or 17–20 cm, or well past the point at which urine returns.  Why A wrong: 2–3 inches/6–8 cm is the female insertion depth.  Why C/D wrong: Not the stated male insertion depth."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"During male catheterization, the return of urine in the bag indicates:","options":["A complication that requires removal","Correct placement of the catheter in the bladder","That the balloon is already inflated","Urinary tract infection"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The return of urine in the bag is a sign of correct placement of the catheter into the bladder.  Why A/C/D wrong: Urine return confirms placement; it is not a complication, balloon status, or sign of infection."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"During male catheterization, if resistance is felt during balloon inflation, the nurse should:","options":["Continue inflating forcefully","Deflate the balloon, insert the catheter further, and reattempt inflation","Pull the catheter out immediately","Inject air instead of water"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If resistance is felt during balloon inflation, deflate the balloon, insert the catheter further, and reattempt inflation (resistance suggests the balloon is still in the urethra).  Why A wrong: Forcing inflation in the urethra can cause urethral injury.  Why C wrong: Removal is not indicated; advance and reattempt.  Why D wrong: Air must not be used (it causes balloon floating)."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"What is the ideal volume of sterile water for inflating the balloon during male catheterization?","options":["5 mL","10–15 mL sterile water","30 mL","50 mL"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The ideal solution for balloon inflation is 10–15 mL sterile water.  Why C wrong: 30 mL is the maximum capacity of the balloon, not the ideal inflation volume.  Why A/D wrong: Not the stated ideal volume."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"What is the maximum capacity of a Foley catheter balloon, according to the text?","options":["10 mL","15 mL","30 mL","50 mL"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The maximum capacity of the balloon is 30 mL.  Why A/B wrong: 10–15 mL is the ideal/usual inflation volume, not the maximum capacity.  Why D wrong: 50 mL exceeds the stated maximum."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"After male catheterization, the catheter should be secured with adhesive tape on which location, and why?","options":["Outer thigh, to keep it visible","Client's inner aspect of the upper thigh, to prevent telescoping action in the urethra and accidental pooling","Lower abdomen, to relieve pressure","Knee, to allow leg movement"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The catheter should be secured with adhesive tape on the client's inner aspect of the upper thigh to prevent telescoping action in the urethra and accidental pooling.  Why A/C/D wrong: The specified site and rationale are the inner upper thigh to prevent telescoping and pooling."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"Where should the urinary drainage bag be hung relative to the patient and the bladder?","options":["On the frame of the bed, below the level of the bladder","On the IV pole above the patient","On the side rails of the bed","On the floor"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hang the drainage bag on the frame of the bed below the level of the bladder (to promote gravity drainage and prevent backflow).  Why B wrong: Above the bladder promotes backflow and infection.  Why C wrong: The bag should NOT be hung on the side rails of the bed.  Why D wrong: It should not touch the floor; hang it on the bed frame off the floor."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"After catheter placement, if no urine flow is obtained and the catheter is noted to be in the vaginal orifice, what should the nurse do?","options":["Remove the catheter immediately","Leave the catheter in place as a marker, obtain a new sterile gloves and catheter kit, restart the procedure, and once the new catheter is correctly in place, remove the catheter in the vaginal orifice","Inflate the balloon","Inject more lubricant"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If no urine flow is obtained and the catheter is in the vaginal orifice, leave it in place as a marker; obtain new sterile gloves and a catheter kit, restart the procedure, and once the new catheter is correctly placed, remove the misplaced catheter.  Why A wrong: The misplaced catheter is left as a marker until the new one is placed.  Why C wrong: Inflating in the vagina would injure tissue.  Why D wrong: Adding lubricant does not correct vaginal misplacement."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"Why should a catheter that has been removed from the vagina (after cross-infection risk) never be reinserted into the urethra?","options":["Because of the risk of cross-infection","Because it is too short","Because the balloon is damaged","Because it loses lubrication"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Because of the risk of cross-infection, never remove a catheter from the vagina and insert it into the urethra.  Why B/C/D wrong: The stated reason is cross-infection risk, not length, balloon, or lubrication."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"If the catheter supplies (e.g., gloves or catheter kit) have been contaminated during the procedure, what should the nurse do?","options":["Continue cautiously with the contaminated supplies","Stop the procedure and start over with new sterile supplies","Rinse the supplies and reuse them","Ask the patient to hold still and proceed"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If supplies have been contaminated, stop the procedure and start over with new (sterile) supplies.  Why A/C/D wrong: Continuing, rinsing/reusing, or proceeding with contaminated supplies breaks asepsis."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"If urine flow is initially well established and the urine becomes clear in a patient with a catheter, but the catheter is changed position, what nursing action is recommended FIRST?","options":["Remove the catheter","Check the tubing for kinking; if the patient has changed position, move the tubing and drainage bag to re-establish drainage","Increase fluid intake","Inflate the balloon further"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If urine flow that was well established stops, check the tubing for kinking; if the patient has changed position, move the tubing/drainage bag to facilitate drainage of urine.  Why A wrong: Removal is not the first action for a positional/kinking issue.  Why C/D wrong: Increasing fluids or inflating the balloon do not address a kinked/positional obstruction."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"During catheter insertion, if a patient complains of extreme pain, the nurse should:","options":["Continue inserting quickly to finish","Stop inflation of the balloon (the balloon is most likely still in the urethra), withdraw the solution from the balloon, insert the catheter an additional 0.5–1 inch (1.2–2.4 cm), and slowly attempt to inflate the balloon again","Remove the catheter and abandon the procedure","Apply more antiseptic"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If the patient complains of extreme pain when inflating the balloon, the balloon is most likely still in the urethra; stop inflation, withdraw the solution, insert an additional 0.5–1 inch (1.2–2.4 cm), and slowly attempt to inflate again.  Why A wrong: Forcing inflation in the urethra causes injury.  Why C wrong: The procedure is corrected by advancing the catheter, not abandoned.  Why D wrong: Antiseptic does not address balloon malposition."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"To avoid bladder and urethral irritation and trauma, what size catheter should be used for an indwelling catheter, and what balloon size?","options":["The largest available catheter with a 30 mL balloon","The smallest sized catheter with a 5–10 mL balloon, and a large catheter should not be used","A medium catheter with no balloon","Any catheter size with a 50 mL balloon"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Use the smallest sized catheter with a 5–10 mL balloon for indwelling catheters; large catheters cause bladder and urethral irritation and trauma.  Why A/D wrong: Large catheters and oversized balloons increase irritation/trauma.  Why C wrong: An indwelling catheter requires a balloon to stay in place."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"What is the major (most common) complication of urinary catheterization, and what is its most common predisposing factor?","options":["Bleeding; due to anticoagulants","Catheter-Associated UTI (CAUTI); occurring due to long-term catheterization and failure to maintain cleanliness of the perineal area","Bladder cancer; due to chronic irritation","Renal stones; due to dehydration"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Catheter-Associated UTI (CAUTI) is the major complication of urinary catheterization, which can occur due to long-term catheterization and failure to maintain cleanliness of the perineal area.  Why A/C/D wrong: The text identifies CAUTI/UTI as the major complication."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"What is the MOST COMMON organism responsible for Catheter-Associated UTI (CAUTI)?","options":["Escherichia coli (E. coli)","Klebsiella pneumoniae","Staphylococcus aureus","Pseudomonas aeruginosa"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common organism responsible for CAUTI is Escherichia coli (E. coli).  Why B wrong: Klebsiella pneumoniae is the second most common, not the most common.  Why C/D wrong: These are not identified as the most common CAUTI organisms in the text."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"Which organism is the SECOND most common cause of CAUTI?","options":["E. coli","Klebsiella pneumoniae","Proteus mirabilis","Enterococcus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The second most common organism is Klebsiella pneumoniae.  Why A wrong: E. coli is the most common (first).  Why C/D wrong: Not identified as the second most common in the text."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"How is CAUTI prevention best achieved according to the text? (Select All That Apply)","options":["By avoiding unnecessary catheter insertion","By frequently assessing the need for the catheter","By aiming for early removal of the catheter","By routinely prescribing antibiotics to all catheterized patients"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: CAUTI prevention is possible by avoiding unnecessary catheter insertion, frequently assessing the need, and aiming for early removal.  Why D wrong: Antibiotics are NOT routinely prescribed; antimicrobial therapy is specifically not recommended for asymptomatic bacteriuria due to resistance risk."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"Asymptomatic bacteriuria (ASB) is defined as at least how many colony-forming units per mL in a voided urine specimen without signs or symptoms of UTI?","options":["≥ 1,000 CFU/mL","≥ 10,000 CFU/mL","≥ 100,000 CFU/mL (or ≥ 100,000,000 CFU/L)","≥ 1,000,000 CFU/mL"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: ASB is defined as at least 100,000 colony-forming units (CFU)/mL or ≥100,000,000 CFU/L of bacteria isolated from a voided urine specimen without any signs or symptoms of UTI.  Why A/B/D wrong: The threshold stated is ≥100,000 CFU/mL, not 1,000, 10,000, or 1,000,000."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"Why is antimicrobial therapy NOT prescribed for asymptomatic bacteriuria (ASB)?","options":["Because antibiotics are too expensive","Due to an increased risk of antimicrobial resistance and adverse effects","Because ASB always resolves spontaneously within hours","Because ASB is not caused by bacteria"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Antimicrobial therapy should not be prescribed for ASB due to an increased risk of antimicrobial resistance and adverse effects.  Why A/C/D wrong: The stated reason is resistance and adverse effects, not cost, spontaneous resolution, or absence of bacteria."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"Per most manufacturers' recommendations, how often should rubber and silicon urinary catheters be changed?","options":["Rubber every 30 days; silicon every 90 days","Rubber every 90 days; silicon every 30 days","Both every 7 days","Both every 6 months"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Most manufacturers recommend changing rubber catheters every 30 days and silicon catheters every 90 days.  Why B wrong: The intervals are reversed in this option.  Why C/D wrong: Not the stated intervals."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"A two-way Foley catheter and a three-way Foley catheter are used for which purposes, respectively?","options":["Two-way for continuous irrigation; three-way for urination","Two-way for urination; three-way for continuous bladder irrigation (CBI), usually after TURP","Both for bladder irrigation","Both only for urine specimen collection"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A two-way Foley catheter is used for urination, and a three-way Foley catheter is used for continuous bladder irrigation (CBI), usually after a TURP procedure.  Why A wrong: The purposes are reversed.  Why C/D wrong: The two catheters serve different stated purposes."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"When disposing of used gloves and gauze pieces during catheter nursing care, which rule should the nurse follow?","options":["Recycle the items","Dispose according to Bio-Medical Waste (BMW) rules, then wash the hands","Place them in general household waste","Sterilize and reuse them"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Dispose of used gloves and gauze pieces according to BMW (Bio-Medical Waste) rules, then wash the hands.  Why A/C/D wrong: Recycling, general waste, or reuse violate biomedical waste handling."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"Which type of bag is preferred for a critically ill patient who needs hourly urine output assessment?","options":["A leg bag","A standard urometer bag","A bedpan","An open container"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Prefer a standard urometer bag for a critically ill patient where there is a need to assess hourly urine output.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not allow accurate hourly urine output measurement."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"In critically ill patients, urine output is described as:","options":["An unreliable parameter","The most reliable indicator of fluid status","Less important than weight","Only relevant in renal disease"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In critically ill patients, urine output is the most reliable indicator of fluid status, so it should be monitored every hour with sterile technique.  Why A/C/D wrong: The text emphasizes urine output as the most reliable fluid-status indicator in the critically ill."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"Because the perineal area is highly prone to infection, what daily perineal care should the nurse provide for a catheterized patient?","options":["Wash the genital area and clean the urinary meatus with soap and water every day","Apply powder to the perineum","Avoid touching the area entirely","Clean only once a week"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Because the perineal area is highly prone to infection, day-to-day catheter care should include washing the genital area and cleaning the urinary meatus with soap and water every day.  Why B/C/D wrong: Powder, avoidance, or weekly cleaning do not match the daily soap-and-water care described."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"When a urine specimen needs to be collected from a catheterized patient using sterile technique, from where should it be obtained?","options":["From the drainage bag","From the port or with needle aspiration using sterile technique","From the first voided urine into a bedpan","By disconnecting the catheter from the bag"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If required, collect the urine specimen from the port or with needle aspiration using sterile technique.  Why A wrong: Urine from the drainage bag is not a reliable sterile specimen.  Why C wrong: For an un-catheterised patient, mid-stream urine is most reliable; for a catheterized patient, use the port.  Why D wrong: Disconnecting the catheter breaks the closed system and risks infection."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"For an un-catheterised patient, which urine sample is the most reliable for collection of a urine specimen?","options":["First-morning voided sample","Mid-stream urine","24-hour pooled sample","Catheter-port aspirate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For an un-catheterised patient, mid-stream urine is the most reliable for collection of a urine sample.  Why A/C wrong: The text specifies mid-stream urine as most reliable for an un-catheterised patient.  Why D wrong: A catheter-port aspirate applies to catheterized patients."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"To prevent infection, the urinary collection system / urine bag should be kept:","options":["Open at all times for ventilation","Closed at all times","Open only at night","Connected to a second open bag"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Keep the urinary collection system or urine bag closed at all times to prevent infection (closed drainage system).  Why A/C/D wrong: Opening the system breaks the closed drainage and increases infection risk."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"Regarding the urinary drainage bag, which of the following are correct nursing instructions? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Keep the urine bag below the level of the bladder","Keep the urine bag off the floor","Hang the bag on the side rails of the bed","Hang the bag attached to the bed frame"],"correct":"A,B,D","rationale":"Why correct: Keep the urine bag below the level of the bladder and off the floor; hang it on or attach it to the bed frame.  Why C wrong: The text states DO NOT hang on the side rails of the bed."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"Which of the following are recognized complications of urinary catheterization listed in the text? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Urinary tract infection (UTI)","Bladder infection","Paraphimosis","Urethral injury and catheter obstruction","Urine leakage from the urethral meatus"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The text lists UTI, bladder infection, pain due to traction on the drainage bag, paraphimosis, urethral injury and catheter obstruction, and urine leakage from the urethral meatus as complications.  Why none wrong: All listed options are stated complications."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"What is the most common hospital-acquired (nosocomial) infection?","options":["Surgical site infection","Ventilator-associated pneumonia","Catheter-Associated Urinary Tract Infection (CAUTI)","Bloodstream infection"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The Should Remember box states the most common hospital-acquired infection is Catheter-Associated Urinary Tract Infection (CAUTI).  Why A/B/D wrong: These are nosocomial infections but not identified as the most common in the source."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"During removal of an indwelling catheter, why is the catheter clamped intermittently before removal?","options":["To prevent leakage","To restore muscle tone of the bladder by clamping the tubing","To sterilize the catheter","To measure residual volume"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For removal, the catheter is clamped intermittently to restore the muscle tone of the bladder (bladder training).  Why A/C/D wrong: Intermittent clamping aims to restore bladder muscle tone, not to prevent leakage, sterilize, or measure residual volume."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"During the bladder-training clamping procedure before catheter removal, when should the nurse unclamp the catheter, and how many times is the process repeated?","options":["When the patient feels the sensation for urine; repeated 3 to 4 times or till muscle tone is restored","Every 30 minutes regardless of sensation; once only","Only at night; 10 times","When the bag is full; twice"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: When the patient feels the sensation for urine, remove the clamping; this process is attempted 3 to 4 times or until muscle tone is restored.  Why B/C/D wrong: The text ties unclamping to the urge sensation and specifies 3–4 repetitions, not the alternatives."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"Just before removing an indwelling catheter, what should the nurse do to help the patient relax?","options":["Ask the patient to bear down forcefully","Have the patient take several deep breaths","Inflate the balloon slightly","Apply cold compress to the perineum"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Have the patient take several deep breaths to help relax while gently removing the catheter.  Why A wrong: Bearing down is not the relaxation technique for catheter removal here.  Why C wrong: The balloon must be deflated, not inflated, before removal.  Why D wrong: Cold compress is not described."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"Before pulling out an indwelling catheter, the nurse must:","options":["Inflate the balloon","Deflate the balloon, and if any resistance is felt during removal, inform the doctor","Cut the catheter","Clamp the catheter permanently"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Deflate the balloon and pull it out; if any resistance is felt during removal, inform the doctor.  Why A wrong: Removing with an inflated balloon causes urethral trauma.  Why C wrong: Cutting the catheter is unsafe.  Why D wrong: Permanent clamping is not part of removal."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"After removing an indwelling catheter, how should the catheter be discarded?","options":["In the general waste bin","Slowly remove and wrap the catheter in a towel or disposable waterproof drape, then discard in the red bag as per BMWM","Flush it down the toilet","Place it back in its original packaging"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Slowly remove and wrap the catheter in a towel or disposable, waterproof drape, then discard in the red bag as per BMWM (Bio-Medical Waste Management).  Why A/C/D wrong: General waste, flushing, or repackaging violate biomedical waste rules."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"After an indwelling catheter is removed, the area at the meatus should be cleaned thoroughly with what?","options":["Plain water","Antiseptic swabs","Alcohol only","Saline gauze"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Clean the area at the meatus thoroughly with antiseptic swabs after the catheter is removed.  Why A/C/D wrong: The text specifies antiseptic swabs for cleaning the meatus after removal."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"After catheter removal, the patient develops dribbling and temporary incontinence. The nurse explains this is due to:","options":["Permanent nerve damage","Temporary flaccidity of the urinary sphincter","A new urinary tract infection","Bladder cancer"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Dribbling and temporary incontinence may occur temporarily after catheter removal due to flaccidity of the urinary sphincter.  Why A wrong: It is temporary, not permanent nerve damage.  Why C/D wrong: The stated cause is sphincter flaccidity, not infection or malignancy."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"After an indwelling catheter is removed, what should the nurse continue to observe?","options":["The urine carefully for any signs of abnormality","Only the patient's temperature","Nothing further is needed","The catheter packaging"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The nurse should observe the urine carefully for any signs of abnormality after catheter removal.  Why B/C/D wrong: The text directs careful observation of the urine for abnormalities, not just temperature, nothing, or packaging."},{"section":"Batch 040 · ICD drainage — three-bottle / water-seal drainage system…","question":"During catheter care, why should the nurse monitor output every hour with sterile technique in a critically ill patient? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Because urine output is the most reliable indicator of fluid status in critically ill patients","To detect changes in fluid status promptly","To allow reuse of the urine sample","Because a standard urometer bag enables hourly assessment"],"correct":"A,B,D","rationale":"Why correct: Urine output is the most reliable indicator of fluid status in the critically ill; hourly monitoring with sterile technique detects fluid-status changes promptly; a standard urometer bag is preferred to enable hourly assessment.  Why C wrong: Hourly monitoring is for fluid-status assessment, not to reuse urine samples."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"A patient who is unconscious needs a sterile urine sample collected for culture. Which technique should the nurse use?","options":["Take the sample directly from the urinary bag","Take the sterile sample from the urinary catheter sampling/aspiration port using a sterile needle","Disconnect the catheter and collect a midstream sample","Catch the next void in a clean bedpan"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The sterile sample is obtained from the catheter sampling/aspiration port (using a sterile needle through the port/tubing), which preserves sterility for culture.  Why A wrong: Urine in the bag is not fresh and is contaminated, so it is unsuitable for a sterile culture specimen.  Why C wrong: Disconnecting the catheter breaks the closed sterile system and increases infection risk.  Why D wrong: An unconscious patient with an indwelling catheter would not void into a bedpan, and that sample would not be sterile."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"When inflating the balloon of a Foley catheter, which type of solution should be used?","options":["Tap water","Normal saline","Sterile water","5% dextrose"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The balloon of a Foley catheter is inflated with sterile water; this is the recommended solution to avoid problems with deflation/removal.  Why A wrong: Tap water is not sterile and can introduce contaminants/minerals into the balloon.  Why B wrong: Normal saline can cause crystallization, leading to difficulty in catheter removal.  Why D wrong: Dextrose solution is not used to inflate catheter balloons and can crystallize."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"A Foley catheter balloon is over-inflated. By how much should the balloon be inflated to function correctly?","options":["As much as possible to ensure it stays in place","The maximum capacity of the balloon is 30 cc, inflate to the line marking","Inflate to the manufacturer's labeled fill volume; maximum capacity is the labeled limit (e.g., 30 cc)","Inflate with 100 cc of sterile water"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The balloon should be inflated to the manufacturer's stated volume; overinflation can cause the balloon to burst and inaccurate output, and maximum capacity equals the labeled limit.  Why A wrong: Overinflating beyond the labeled volume can rupture the balloon.  Why B wrong: The fill amount depends on the labeled volume, not a fixed assumption.  Why D wrong: 100 cc grossly exceeds typical balloon capacity and would burst the balloon."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"During insertion of a urinary catheter, which item from the catheter care bundle is correct?","options":["Clean (non-sterile) gloves and water-based cleansing","Hand hygiene performed, periurethral antisepsis with povidone-iodine, sterile gloves, and a single use of xylocaine jelly","No lubrication is needed for insertion","Reuse of lubricant jelly between patients to reduce cost"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The insertion bundle requires hand hygiene, periurethral antisepsis (povidone-iodine), sterile gloves, and a single use of xylocaine (lignocaine) jelly.  Why A wrong: Sterile, not clean, gloves are required for catheter insertion.  Why C wrong: Xylocaine jelly is used as lubricant/anesthetic to ease insertion.  Why D wrong: Lubricant is single-use; reuse between patients causes cross-infection."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"Which of the following are correct elements of urinary catheter care during maintenance? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Sterile closed drainage system","Keep the drainage bag above floor and below bladder level at all times, including during transportation","Secure indwelling catheter to prevent migration","Allow contact between the jug and the drainage bag during emptying","Once-daily meatal cleaning with sterile water and single-use gloves for each patient when emptying"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct (A, B, C, E): Maintenance bundle includes a sterile closed drainage system; keeping the bag below bladder level (and above floor) to prevent backflow; securing the catheter to prevent migration; daily meatal cleaning with sterile water; and using single-use gloves per patient during emptying.  Why D wrong: There must be NO contact between the jug and the bag during emptying, to prevent contamination."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"Why should a urinary catheter NOT be left in place for an unnecessarily long time?","options":["It increases bladder capacity","The drainage bag becomes too heavy","To prevent ascending (urinary tract) infection","It causes the balloon to deflate"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Prolonged catheterization promotes ascending infection; the urobag should be changed every 7 days to reduce this risk.  Why A wrong: Catheterization does not increase bladder capacity.  Why B wrong: Bag weight is not the rationale for limiting duration.  Why D wrong: Duration of placement does not cause balloon deflation by itself."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"How often should a urinary drainage (uro) bag be changed, and when should it be emptied?","options":["Changed every 7 days; emptied when filled to 1/3 of its capacity","Changed daily; emptied only when completely full","Changed monthly; never emptied until removal","Changed every 24 hours; emptied hourly regardless of volume"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The urobag is changed every 7 days (or on demand) and should be emptied when it is filled to 1/3 of its maximum capacity.  Why B wrong: Daily change is not the standard, and waiting until completely full risks backflow/contamination.  Why C wrong: Monthly change far exceeds the recommended interval and risks infection.  Why D wrong: Hourly emptying regardless of volume is not the stated rule; emptying is volume-based (1/3 full)."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"A curved (Tiemann/coude) catheter is most appropriate for which type of patient?","options":["A child requiring intermittent catheterization","A patient with a narrow urethral path or prostatic obstruction (BPH)","A female patient with a short, straight urethra","A patient needing continuous bladder irrigation only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The curved Tiemann catheter is slightly curved with one or two drainage eyes and is used in patients with a narrow urethral path or prostatic obstruction (BPH).  Why A wrong: A curved tip is specifically for navigating obstruction, not routine pediatric catheterization.  Why C wrong: A short straight female urethra does not require a curved tip.  Why D wrong: Irrigation indication is not the defining feature; the curve addresses obstruction."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"If a Foley balloon does not deflate during removal, which corrective action is appropriate?","options":["Pull the catheter forcefully to dislodge it","Cut the inflation/balloon port to allow water to drain out","Leave the catheter permanently in place","Inject more sterile water into the balloon"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: When a balloon will not deflate, cutting the inflation/balloon port allows the trapped water to drain so the balloon can deflate and be removed safely.  Why A wrong: Forceful traction with an inflated balloon causes urethral/bladder trauma.  Why C wrong: Leaving it permanently is not a solution and risks infection/complications.  Why D wrong: Adding more water increases over-inflation and worsens the problem."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"A colostomy is best defined as which of the following?","options":["An opening made into the small intestine through the abdominal wall","An artificial opening of the colon (large intestine) through the anterior abdominal wall","A direct connection between the bladder and skin","Surgical removal of the entire colon"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A colostomy is an artificial opening of the colon (large intestine) made through the anterior abdominal wall.  Why A wrong: An opening into the small intestine is an ileostomy, not a colostomy.  Why C wrong: A bladder-to-skin opening describes a urinary diversion, not a colostomy.  Why D wrong: Removal of the entire colon is a colectomy, not a colostomy."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"What is a \"stoma\"?","options":["The drainage bag attached to the abdomen","A surgically made hole in the abdominal wall through which body waste is removed directly via the end of the bowel into a collection bag","The mucous lining of the rectum","The closure suture of an abdominal incision"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A stoma is the surgically made opening in the abdominal wall that allows body waste to be removed from the body directly through the end of the bowel into the collection bag.  Why A wrong: The bag is the appliance, not the stoma itself.  Why C wrong: The rectal mucous lining is unrelated to the stoma definition.  Why D wrong: The stoma is an opening, not a suture line."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"Match the term: the abdominal opening in which a portion of the colon is brought to the abdominal surface to create an artificial anus. This is called:","options":["Ileostomy","Colostomy","Urostomy","Loop colostomy only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A colostomy is a type of stoma in which an artificial opening of the colon (ascending/transverse/descending/sigmoid) is brought through the anterior abdominal wall as an artificial anus.  Why A wrong: An ileostomy involves the last part of the small intestine (ileum), not the colon.  Why C wrong: A urostomy diverts urine, not stool.  Why D wrong: Loop colostomy is one subtype; the general term for the colon opening is colostomy."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"An ileostomy is an artificial opening involving which part of the bowel?","options":["The sigmoid colon","The transverse colon","The last part (terminal portion) of the small intestine — the ileum","The rectum"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: An ileostomy is an abdominal opening connecting the last part of the small intestine (ileum) to the abdominal wall.  Why A/B wrong: These are colon segments, used for a colostomy, not an ileostomy.  Why D wrong: The rectum is the terminal large bowel, not the ileum."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"A \"urostomy\" diverts which body fluid away from the bladder to an opening in the abdomen?","options":["Stool","Bile","Urine","Lymph"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: A urostomy diverts urine away from the bladder to an opening in the abdomen.  Why A wrong: Stool diversion describes a colostomy/ileostomy.  Why B wrong: Bile is not diverted by a urostomy.  Why D wrong: Lymph is not involved in a urostomy."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"A colostomy may be done to treat disease OR to relieve an obstruction. What is the most common reason a colostomy is created?","options":["To relieve an obstruction or to prevent the remaining bowel from consumption (overload) by fecal matter","To increase nutrient absorption","To deliver chemotherapy directly","To monitor urine output"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A colostomy may be done to treat disease or to relieve an obstruction, and a common purpose is to prevent the remaining bowel from consumption (passage/overload) by fecal matter.  Why B wrong: Colostomy does not increase nutrient absorption.  Why C wrong: It is not created to deliver chemotherapy.  Why D wrong: It is for stool diversion, not urine monitoring."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"Which is the MOST common type of colostomy?","options":["Ascending colostomy","Transverse colostomy","Sigmoid colostomy","Loop colostomy"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The sigmoid colostomy is the most common type of colostomy.  Why A wrong: Ascending colostomy is less common than sigmoid.  Why B wrong: Transverse colostomy is not the most common.  Why D wrong: Loop colostomy is classified by construction type, not by location, and is not stated as the most common."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"A loop colostomy is a type of which category of colostomy?","options":["Permanent colostomy","Temporary colostomy","End colostomy","Urinary diversion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A loop colostomy is a type of temporary colostomy.  Why A wrong: It is temporary, not permanent.  Why C wrong: An end colostomy is a different construction (Hartmann's procedure type), usually more permanent.  Why D wrong: It is a fecal diversion, not a urinary diversion."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"Match the stool consistency to the colostomy location. Stool from an ASCENDING colostomy is typically:","options":["Soft and loose","Semisolid, firmer, paste-like","Hard and pellet-like","Pure liquid only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Ascending colostomy stool is soft and loose because less water has been reabsorbed proximally.  Why B wrong: Semisolid/firmer/paste-like stool corresponds to a sigmoid colostomy.  Why C wrong: Hard pellet stool is not characteristic of any colostomy output described.  Why D wrong: Pure liquid output is more characteristic of an ileostomy than an ascending colostomy."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"Stool from a TRANSVERSE colostomy is best described as:","options":["Soft and loose","Semisolid, firmer, paste-like","Sensoid (semi-solid/moist, partially formed)","Completely formed and hard"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Transverse colostomy stool is semisolid/moist (sensoid), reflecting partial water reabsorption in the transverse colon.  Why A wrong: Soft and loose stool corresponds to the ascending colostomy.  Why B wrong: Firmer paste-like stool corresponds to the sigmoid colostomy.  Why D wrong: Fully formed hard stool is not the transverse colostomy description."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"Stool from a SIGMOID colostomy is typically:","options":["Watery","Semisolid, firmer, paste-like","Soft and loose","Mucus only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Sigmoid colostomy stool is semisolid, firmer, and paste-like because most water has been reabsorbed by the time stool reaches the sigmoid colon.  Why A wrong: Watery stool is characteristic of a more proximal stoma (ascending/ileostomy).  Why C wrong: Soft loose stool corresponds to the ascending colostomy.  Why D wrong: Mucus-only output is not the normal sigmoid colostomy stool."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"Which of the following are recognized INDICATIONS for a colostomy? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Colorectal cancer (e.g., Ca rectal, Ca anus)","Intestinal obstruction","Gangrenous bowel due to strangulation","Acute renal failure","Penetrating trauma to the bowel"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct (A, B, C, E): Colostomy indications include colorectal cancer, intestinal obstruction, gangrenous/strangulated bowel, ischemic bowel disease, penetrating bowel trauma, rectovaginal fistula repair, and stenosis/stricture of the intestines.  Why D wrong: Acute renal failure is a contraindication for enema, not an indication for colostomy."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"Which of the following are EARLY complications of a stoma? (Select All That Apply)","options":["High output leading to dehydration and electrolyte imbalance","Stoma ischemia and bleeding","Stoma retraction","Bowel obstruction (early)","Mucocutaneous separation/fistula"],"correct":"A,B,D,E","rationale":"Why correct (A, B, D, E): Early stoma complications include high output (dehydration/electrolyte imbalance), stoma ischemia and bleeding, bowel obstruction, stoma stenosis, and mucocutaneous separation/fistula.  Why C wrong: Stoma retraction is listed as a LATE complication, not an early one."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"Which of the following are LATE complications of a stoma? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Stoma retraction","Stoma prolapse","Parastomal hernia","High output dehydration","Skin problems — excoriation/ulceration"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct (A, B, C, E): Late stoma complications include stoma retraction, stoma prolapse, parastomal hernia, fistula/narrowing/bleeding, skin problems (excoriation, ulceration), and bowel obstruction.  Why D wrong: High-output dehydration/electrolyte imbalance is an EARLY complication."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"Within how many days postoperatively does a colostomy bag typically need to be applied as the stoma becomes functional?","options":["The same day, within 6 hours","After 2–4 days, when the stoma becomes functional","After 2 weeks","Only after 1 month"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The colostomy bag is applied after 2–4 days, when the stoma becomes functional (begins to pass stool/flatus).  Why A wrong: Function typically does not return on the same day within hours.  Why C/D wrong: 2 weeks or 1 month is far too long; function returns within a few days."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"How should a colostomy bag be emptied, and how often should the appliance (e.g., the bag) be changed?","options":["Empty when 3/4 full; change every 3 days","Empty when filled up to 1/3 (before emptying it off the skin and bowel leaks); change the colostomy appliance/bag every 5–7 days","Empty only when completely full; never change the bag","Empty hourly; change the bag twice daily"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The bag should be emptied when filled up to about 1/3 (to prevent leaking from skin/bowel), and the appliance/bag changed every 5–7 days.  Why A wrong: Waiting until 3/4 full risks leakage; the source states 1/3.  Why C wrong: Allowing it to fill completely and never changing the bag causes leakage and skin breakdown.  Why D wrong: Hourly emptying and twice-daily change are not the stated intervals."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"When cleaning the stoma and peristomal area, which technique is correct?","options":["Scrub vigorously with antiseptic to remove all bacteria","Gently clean with warm water and a dabbed (rubbed-on) towel without scrubbing","Use hot water and abrasive scrubbing pads","Leave it uncleaned to avoid disturbing the stoma"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The stoma and peristomal area should be gently cleaned with water and dabbed, without scrubbing, to prevent trauma and skin damage.  Why A wrong: Vigorous scrubbing damages the delicate stoma/peristomal skin.  Why C wrong: Hot water and abrasive pads injure the stoma and skin.  Why D wrong: The area must be kept clean to prevent infection and skin breakdown."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"What is the normal expected color of a healthy stoma that the nurse should assess?","options":["Pale white or grey","Black or dusky","Moist, red or pink in color, with no skin breakdown or irritation","Bright yellow"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: A healthy stoma should be assessed as moist and most importantly red/pink in color, with the peristomal skin intact and free of breakdown/irritation.  Why A wrong: A pale/grey stoma suggests poor perfusion/ischemia.  Why B wrong: A black/dusky stoma indicates ischemia/necrosis and is abnormal.  Why D wrong: Bright yellow is not a normal stoma color."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"While measuring a stoma and previous appliance, what should the nurse keep in mind?","options":["The measurement should be smaller than 1/16–1/8 inch larger than the stoma","The measured stoma size should be no more than 1/16–1/8 (inch larger than the stoma)","The opening should be exactly the same size as the stoma with no margin","The opening should be 1 inch larger than the stoma"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: When sizing the appliance opening, the measurement should be no more than 1/16–1/8 inch larger than the stoma, giving a small protective margin without exposing too much skin.  Why A wrong: It should not be smaller than the stoma; a small margin larger is needed.  Why C wrong: An exactly equal opening can constrict/abrade the stoma.  Why D wrong: 1 inch larger exposes too much peristomal skin to effluent, causing excoriation."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"Why is it important to always check the bowel sounds before offering a diet to a colostomy patient?","options":["To detect a urinary tract infection","To confirm return of bowel function (peristalsis) before feeding","To measure blood pressure","To assess the stoma color"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Bowel sounds confirm the return of peristalsis/bowel function; the diet should only be offered once function has returned to prevent obstruction/ileus.  Why A wrong: Bowel sounds do not assess for UTI.  Why C wrong: Bowel sounds are unrelated to blood pressure.  Why D wrong: Stoma color is assessed visually, not by auscultating bowel sounds."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"What dietary progression and psychological support are appropriate for a colostomy patient? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Offer high-fiber diet and initially liquid to semisolid diet, avoiding high fat after colostomy","Provide psychological support to patients with colostomies","Address poor body image, perception, sexual dissatisfaction, and depression, which are common","Permanently restrict all oral intake","Instruct the patient to regularly change their colostomy bag"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct (A, B, C, E): The patient should be offered a high-fiber diet, initially liquid to semisolid (avoiding high fat); given psychological support for body image/sexual/depression concerns; and instructed to change the colostomy bag regularly.  Why D wrong: Oral intake is progressed, not permanently restricted; nutrition is essential."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"A nurse is teaching a postoperative colostomy patient about expected function and care. Which statement is correct?","options":["The colostomy should be expected to be functional within 2 to 4 days after surgery","The colostomy will never become functional","The colostomy bag should be changed every 1–2 days routinely","Constipation after colostomy never requires medical attention"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Per \"Also Remember,\" the colostomy should be expected to be functional in 2 to 4 days after surgery.  Why B wrong: The colostomy does become functional within a few days.  Why C wrong: The bag is changed every 5–7 days, not every 1–2 days.  Why D wrong: With any complication, the patient must contact the doctor; constipation/complications require attention."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"What is Hartmann's procedure?","options":["Surgical resection of the rectosigmoid colon with closure of the anorectal stump and formation of an end colostomy","A bladder diversion procedure","A method of catheter balloon inflation","A technique for enema administration"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hartmann's procedure is the surgical resection of the rectosigmoid colon with closure of the anorectal stump and the formation of an end colostomy.  Why B wrong: It is a colorectal procedure, not a urinary diversion.  Why C wrong: It is unrelated to catheter balloon inflation.  Why D wrong: It is a surgical procedure, not an enema technique."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"The output from a colostomy/ileostomy (the discharged effluent) is known as:","options":["Chyme","Effluent","Exudate","Transudate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Output from a colostomy is known as effluent.  Why A wrong: Chyme is the semifluid mass in the stomach/small intestine, not the stoma output term.  Why C wrong: Exudate refers to inflammatory fluid leaking from vessels, not stoma output.  Why D wrong: Transudate is a low-protein fluid filtrate, not stoma output."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"Colostomy irrigation is performed for which primary purpose?","options":["To stimulate peristalsis and facilitate expulsion of feces, helping regulate (control) bowel emptying","To inflate the colostomy bag","To administer chemotherapy to the bowel","To sterilize the entire colon permanently"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Colostomy irrigation instills fluid into the lower colon via the stoma on the abdominal wall to stimulate peristalsis and facilitate expulsion of feces (regulate emptying).  Why B wrong: Irrigation does not inflate the bag.  Why C wrong: It is not a chemotherapy delivery route.  Why D wrong: It does not sterilize the colon."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"On the first day of colostomy irrigation, what volume of water/solution is used, and how is this adjusted on later days?","options":["Use 500 ml on the first day; increase by the next day to maintain 1000 ml","Use 2000 ml on the first day and reduce daily","Use 100 ml fixed every day","Use 5000 ml once"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: On the first day, about 500 ml water/solution is used for irrigation, and this is increased by the next day to be maintained at 1000 ml.  Why B wrong: 2000 ml on day one is excessive; the protocol starts at 500 ml.  Why C wrong: 100 ml is too small a volume to stimulate effective irrigation.  Why D wrong: 5000 ml far exceeds the recommended volume."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"What is the best position and the best time for colostomy irrigation?","options":["Supine position immediately after meals; in any open ward","The patient sitting on the toilet basin (or, if bedridden, positioned on the side); irrigation timed to the patient's personal bathroom routine, maintaining privacy","Standing in a corridor at midnight","Prone position only, regardless of mobility"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If ambulatory, position the client sitting on the toilet basin; if bedridden, position the client on his/her side. Irrigation should be timed to the patient's personal bathroom routine with privacy maintained.  Why A wrong: Supine immediately after meals in an open ward is not recommended; privacy and routine matter.  Why C wrong: Standing in a corridor violates privacy and proper positioning.  Why D wrong: Prone is not the recommended position; sitting or side-lying is used."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"During colostomy irrigation, how far should the lubricated catheter/cone tip be inserted into the stoma?","options":["Approximately 7 to 8 cm in the direction of the remaining bowel","20 cm into the bowel","Just touching the skin surface, 1 cm","As deep as it will go, regardless of resistance"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A lubricated catheter tip (with water-soluble jelly) or cone is inserted approximately 7 to 8 cm in the direction of the remaining bowel.  Why B wrong: 20 cm is far too deep and risks bowel perforation.  Why C wrong: 1 cm is too shallow to deliver irrigation effectively.  Why D wrong: Forcing past resistance risks perforation; insertion is to a defined depth in the bowel's direction."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"At what height should the irrigating solution container be held during colostomy irrigation?","options":["At the level of the stoma","12 to 18 inches (30 to 45 cm) above the stoma","5 feet above the stoma","Below the stoma level"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The irrigating bag/IV stand should be held 12 to 18 inches (30 to 45 cm) above the stoma, providing appropriate gentle flow by gravity.  Why A wrong: At stoma level there is no gravity head to flow the fluid in.  Why C wrong: 5 feet/too high causes excessive pressure, cramping, and possible mucosal injury.  Why D wrong: Below stoma level prevents inflow of solution."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"At what temperature should the irrigating solution be maintained for colostomy irrigation?","options":["70°F (room temperature only)","100°F (about 37.7°C)","120°F (very hot)","50°F (cold)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The temperature of the irrigating solution should be maintained at 100°F (≈37.7°C), close to body temperature, to avoid cramping and discomfort.  Why A wrong: The source specifies 100°F, warmer than typical room temperature, for comfort and to prevent cramping.  Why C wrong: 120°F is too hot and can injure the bowel mucosa.  Why D wrong: Cold (50°F) solution causes cramping and discomfort."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"During colostomy irrigation, if the patient experiences cramping, what should the nurse do?","options":["Increase the flow rate to push through","Stop the flow of fluid temporarily until cramping stops, then start again","Remove the catheter immediately and abort the procedure","Lower the patient to the supine position and continue"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If cramping occurs, stop the flow of fluid temporarily; once cramping subsides, start the flow again.  Why A wrong: Increasing flow worsens cramping.  Why C wrong: Aborting immediately is unnecessary; pausing usually resolves cramping.  Why D wrong: Changing position alone does not address the cramping caused by flow; the flow should be paused."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"After colostomy irrigation, how should the stoma area be cared for, and what skin protection is used?","options":["Clean the area with soap and water, dry, and apply skin barrier cream","Leave the area wet and uncovered","Apply alcohol directly onto the stoma","Cover the stoma with adhesive tape only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: After irrigation, clean the surrounding area of the stoma with soap and water every day; if skin breakdown or excoriation occurs around the stoma, apply skin barrier cream.  Why B wrong: Leaving skin wet/uncovered promotes excoriation and breakdown.  Why C wrong: Alcohol on the stoma is irritating and damaging.  Why D wrong: Tape alone does not protect the peristomal skin from effluent."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"When changing an ostomy bag, what tool is used to ensure the appliance opening matches the stoma, and how should the opening be sized?","options":["Use the stoma measuring guide and cut the opening 1/16 to 1/8 inch away from (larger than) the stoma","Estimate by eye and cut exactly to the stoma size","Use a stoma measuring guide and make the opening 1 inch larger","Make the opening smaller than the stoma to ensure a snug seal"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Measure the stoma with the stoma measuring guide and cut the opening 1/16 to 1/8 inch away from (larger than) the stoma to give a small margin and protect peristomal skin.  Why B wrong: An exact-size opening can constrict/abrade the stoma; a small margin is used.  Why C wrong: A 1-inch margin exposes too much skin to effluent.  Why D wrong: An opening smaller than the stoma traumatizes and constricts the stoma."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"Which step is correct when changing/applying an ostomy bag? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Wash hands and wear appropriate PPE","Empty the contents of the ostomy pouch and dispose according to facility policy","Remove the old or soiled ostomy appliance","Gently wipe the stoma and wet wall with gauze and allow it to dry; place a dry piece of gauze over the stoma to prevent release of contents onto the skin","Apply alcohol-soaked gauze directly to a healthy stoma to disinfect it"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A, B, C, D): Steps include hand washing/PPE, emptying and disposing of pouch contents per policy, removing the old/soiled appliance, gently wiping/drying the stoma and wall, and placing a dry gauze over the stoma to prevent effluent release onto the skin while applying the new pouch.  Why E wrong: Alcohol is not applied to the stoma; it irritates and damages the mucosa."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"During ostomy bag changes and irrigation, why should frequent irrigation be avoided?","options":["It can lead to loss of fluids and electrolytes","It increases stoma color","It causes the balloon to deflate","It makes the stool firmer"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Frequent irrigation can lead to loss of fluids and electrolytes (similar to excessive enemas), causing imbalance.  Why B wrong: Irrigation frequency does not change stoma color physiologically.  Why C wrong: There is no catheter balloon involved in routine irrigation that would deflate from frequency.  Why D wrong: Frequent irrigation does not firm the stool; it washes out bowel contents."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"Which two appliance system types are described for ostomy care?","options":["One-piece system and two-piece system","Foley and condom systems","Single-lumen and triple-lumen systems","Closed and balloon systems"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Ostomy appliances include the one-piece ostomy pouch system and the two-piece system (with separate adhesive base/flange and pouch). Stoma appliances shown include one-piece closed, two-piece closed, and one-piece drainable.  Why B wrong: Foley and condom systems are urinary catheters, not ostomy appliances.  Why C wrong: Lumen counts describe catheters, not ostomy bag systems.  Why D wrong: \"Closed/balloon\" is not how the two appliance systems are named here."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"A patient complains of right abdominal tenderness with pain that increases on right knee–quadrates movement, plus nausea, vomiting, and too much restlessness. On an X-ray showing abdominal distension, what should the nurse do NEXT?","options":["Keep NPO and plan for surgery","Inserts NG tube","Reassure the patient and keep in pain killer","Keep observation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The clinical picture (right abdominal tenderness, pain worse with movement, nausea/vomiting, restlessness, distension on X-ray) suggests an acute surgical abdomen; the priority is to keep the patient NPO and prepare/plan for surgery.  Why B wrong: An NG tube may relieve distension but does not address the surgical priority; NPO + surgical plan is the next step.  Why C wrong: Masking pain with analgesics and reassurance delays definitive surgical management.  Why D wrong: Mere observation is unsafe given signs of an acute surgical abdomen."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"A postoperative patient who had a colostomy says the nurse instructed colostomy care. Which of the following is correct EXCEPT (choose the INCORRECT instruction)?","options":["Apply weights around the stoma area","Empty the stoma with sterility maintained","Irrigate the stoma with NS daily","Give education about routine bag care"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct (this is the EXCEPT/incorrect option): Applying weights around the stoma is NOT a correct instruction — it would cause trauma/prolapse and is not part of colostomy care.  Why B wrong (i.e., it IS correct care): Emptying with appropriate sterility/cleanliness is appropriate.  Why C wrong (it IS correct): Irrigation with normal saline as ordered is part of routine colostomy management.  Why D wrong (it IS correct): Patient education about bag care is essential."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"An enema is also known by which name?","options":["Lavage","Clyster","Gavage","Paracentesis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: An enema is also known as a clyster.  Why A wrong: Lavage refers to washing out (e.g., gastric lavage), not specifically an enema.  Why C wrong: Gavage is feeding via tube into the stomach.  Why D wrong: Paracentesis is drainage of peritoneal fluid."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"An enema is best defined as which of the following?","options":["A process in which a small quantity of fluid is administered to the lower bowel through the rectum","Instillation of fluid into the stomach via a tube","Drainage of urine from the bladder","Injection of medication into a vein"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: An enema is a process in which a (small) quantity of fluid is administered into the lower bowel through the rectum.  Why B wrong: Fluid into the stomach via a tube is gavage/lavage, not an enema.  Why C wrong: Bladder drainage is catheterization, not an enema.  Why D wrong: IV medication is unrelated to an enema."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"Match the enema purpose to its example. An enema given to soften and remove feces from the bowel is a:","options":["Carminative enema","Cleansing/evacuant enema (purgative enema, e.g., to relieve constipation)","Nutritive enema","Sedative enema"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: To remove the fluid and nutrients/contents and relieve constipation, a purgative/evacuant (cleansing) enema is given (e.g., simple evacuant enema for constipation).  Why A wrong: A carminative enema is given to relieve gaseous distension (expel flatus).  Why C wrong: A nutritive enema administers fluid and nutrients, not to soften/remove feces.  Why D wrong: A sedative enema is to induce sedation/sleep, not to evacuate stool."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"Match the enema to its purpose: an enema given to relieve INFLAMMATION is a/an:","options":["Astringent enema","Anthelmintic enema","Emollient enema","Stimulant enema"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: An emollient enema is given to relieve/soothe inflammation of the bowel mucosa.  Why A wrong: An astringent enema is used to reduce/contract tissues (e.g., to reduce bleeding), not specifically to relieve inflammation.  Why B wrong: An anthelmintic enema is to destroy intestinal parasites (worms).  Why D wrong: A stimulant enema is to stimulate peristalsis, not to relieve inflammation."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"Which enema purpose is correctly matched?","options":["Astringent enema — to reduce/contract tissues (e.g., to control bleeding)","Carminative enema — to provide nutrition","Anthelmintic enema — to soften stool","Saline enema — to induce anesthesia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: An astringent enema is used to reduce/contract tissues; an example purpose is to reduce bleeding/inflammation of the bowel.  Why B wrong: A carminative enema relieves gaseous distension, not nutrition.  Why C wrong: Anthelmintic enema destroys parasites; oil enema softens stool.  Why D wrong: An anesthetic enema (not saline) induces anesthesia; saline cleans the bowel."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"An enema used to clean the bowel prior to X-ray studies or visualization (e.g., barium examination) is a:","options":["Saline enema","Barium enema","Cold enema","Nutritive enema"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: To clean the bowel prior to X-ray studies/visualization, a barium enema is used (per the purpose example for diagnosis/X-ray prep).  Why A wrong: A saline enema cleans the bowel but is not the diagnostic contrast example given.  Why C wrong: A cold enema is used to reduce temperature, not for X-ray visualization.  Why D wrong: A nutritive enema delivers nutrients, not contrast for imaging."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"An enema used to reduce the body temperature is a:","options":["Cold enema","Saline enema","Stimulant enema","Anesthetic enema"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A cold enema is given to reduce the body temperature (e.g., in hyperpyrexia).  Why B wrong: A saline enema is for cleansing/electrolyte balance.  Why C wrong: A stimulant enema stimulates peristalsis.  Why D wrong: An anesthetic enema induces anesthesia."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"An enema given to a patient in shock and collapse is an example of which enema?","options":["Purgative enema","Stimulant enema","Carminative enema","Emollient enema"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A stimulant enema is used to stimulate a person in shock and collapse (it stimulates body function/peristalsis).  Why A wrong: A purgative enema is to evacuate the bowel.  Why C wrong: A carminative enema relieves gaseous distension.  Why D wrong: An emollient enema soothes inflammation."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"An enema used to establish regular bowel function during a bowel training programme is a/an:","options":["Anthelmintic enema","Training (regular) evacuant enema","Anesthetic enema","Astringent enema"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: An enema can be used to establish regular bowel function as part of a bowel training programme.  Why A wrong: An anthelmintic enema destroys parasites.  Why C wrong: An anesthetic enema induces anesthesia.  Why D wrong: An astringent enema reduces/contracts tissue."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"Which enema purpose pairs correctly with its drug/agent and intent? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Anthelmintic enema — to destroy intestinal parasites","Carminative enema — to relieve gaseous distension of the lower GI tract","Anesthetic enema — to induce anesthesia","Oil enema — to soften hard faecal matter","Sedative enema — to administer medication"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct (all): The source lists each pairing — anthelmintic destroys intestinal parasites; carminative relieves gaseous distension of the lower GI tract; anesthetic induces anesthesia; oil enema softens hard faecal matter; and a sedative enema is used to administer medication (sedative).  Why none wrong: Each option corresponds to a stated enema purpose."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"Which of the following are recognized INDICATIONS for an enema? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Surgery (bowel preparation)","Constipation and obstipation (chronic constipation)","Structural abnormalities of the lower GI tract","Acute renal failure","Appendicitis"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct (A, B, C): Indications include surgery, constipation and obstipation (chronic constipation), and structural abnormalities of the lower GI tract.  Why D wrong: Acute renal failure is a contraindication, not an indication.  Why E wrong: Appendicitis is a contraindication for enema (risk of perforation)."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"Which of the following are CONTRAINDICATIONS to administering an enema? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Acute renal failure","Acute myocardial infarction and cardiac problems","Appendicitis","Recent surgical procedures involving the lower intestinal tract","Chronic constipation"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A, B, C, D): Contraindications include acute renal failure, acute myocardial infarction/cardiac problems, appendicitis, obstetrical contraindications (e.g., antepartum hemorrhage, leaking membranes), recent surgical procedures involving the lower intestinal tract, intestinal obstruction and perforation, inflammation and infection of the abdomen, and haemorrhoids.  Why E wrong: Chronic constipation (obstipation) is an indication for enema, not a contraindication."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"Which of the following are CONTRAINDICATIONS for an enema? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Obstetrical contraindications such as antepartum hemorrhage and leaking membranes","Intestinal obstruction and perforation","Inflammation and infection of the abdomen","Haemorrhoids","Bowel preparation before surgery"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A, B, C, D): Obstetrical contraindications (antepartum hemorrhage, leaking membranes), intestinal obstruction/perforation, abdominal inflammation/infection, and haemorrhoids are all contraindications to enema.  Why E wrong: Surgical bowel preparation is an indication for an enema, not a contraindication."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"Enemas are classified by volume into evacuant and retained enemas. What is the typical VOLUME of an EVACUANT enema for an ADULT?","options":["150 ml","100 ml","500–1000 ml","2000 ml fixed"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: For an evacuant enema, the adult volume is 500–1000 ml (per the classification table).  Why A wrong: 150 ml is the RETAINED enema adult volume, not the evacuant volume.  Why B wrong: 100 ml is the retained-enema child volume.  Why D wrong: A fixed 2000 ml is not the stated adult evacuant volume."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"According to the enema classification table, the RETAINED enema volumes are:","options":["Adult 150 ml, Child 100 ml, Neonate <150 ml","Adult 500–1000 ml, Child 350–500 ml, Neonate <150 ml","Adult 100 ml, Child 50 ml, Neonate 25 ml","All ages 1000 ml"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Retained enema volumes are: Adult 150 ml, Child 100 ml, Neonate less than 150 ml.  Why B wrong: 500–1000 ml (adult) and 350–500 ml (child) are the EVACUANT enema volumes, not retained.  Why C wrong: These are not the stated retained volumes.  Why D wrong: Volumes vary by age; not a single 1000 ml for all."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"According to the enema classification table, the EVACUANT enema volumes by age group are:","options":["Adult 150 ml, Child 100 ml, Neonate <150 ml","Adult 500–1000 ml, Child 350–500 ml, Neonate <150 ml","Adult 50 ml, Child 25 ml, Neonate 10 ml","Adult 2000 ml, Child 1000 ml, Neonate 500 ml"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Evacuant enema volumes are: Adult 500–1000 ml, Child 350–500 ml, Neonate less than 150 ml.  Why A wrong: These (150/100/<150 ml) are the retained enema volumes.  Why C/D wrong: These figures do not match the table."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"What is the temperature of the solution used for a RETAINED enema versus an EVACUANT enema, per the classification table?","options":["Retained: room temperature (except stimulant enema); Evacuant: adult ~105°F, child ~99.5°F (except cold enema)","Both at exactly 100°F","Retained at 50°F; Evacuant at 120°F","Both at room temperature only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For a retained enema the solution is at room temperature (except a stimulant enema); for an evacuant enema the temperature is about 105°F in adults and about 99.5°F in children (except a cold enema).  Why B wrong: The temperatures differ by enema type and age, not a single 100°F.  Why C wrong: 50°F/120°F are not the table values.  Why D wrong: The evacuant enema uses warmed solution (~99.5–105°F), not just room temperature."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"According to the enema classification table, what is the HOLDING TIME for a retained enema versus an evacuant enema?","options":["Retained: 2–3 hours; Evacuant: 5–10 minutes","Both: 24 hours","Retained: 10 seconds; Evacuant: 1 hour","Retained: 30 minutes; Evacuant: 30 minutes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The holding time for a retained enema is 2–3 hours, while for an evacuant enema it is 5–10 minutes (enough time to stimulate evacuation).  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the table; the retained enema must be held much longer (2–3 hrs) than the evacuant (5–10 min)."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"According to the classification table, how often can an EVACUANT enema versus a RETAINED enema be given?","options":["Evacuant: only once or a day, except a carminative enema (2–6 times a day); Retained: more than once in a day","Both can be given hourly","Evacuant: once a month; Retained: once a year","Evacuant and retained can each be given continuously"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The frequency for an evacuant enema is only once (or once a day) except a carminative enema, which can be given 2–6 times a day; a retained enema can be given more than once in a day.  Why B/C/D wrong: These frequencies are not stated in the table."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"According to the classification table, the IV stand (solution container) height for a retained enema versus an evacuant enema is:","options":["Retained: 12 inches (or 30 cm); Evacuant: 18 inches (or 45 cm)","Both: 5 feet","Retained: 45 cm; Evacuant: 12 inches","Both: at floor level"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The IV stand height is 12 inches (or 30 cm) for a retained enema and 18 inches (or 45 cm) for an evacuant enema. The lower height for retained enemas gives a gentler, slower flow.  Why B wrong: 5 feet is far higher than the stated heights and would create excessive pressure.  Why C wrong: The values are reversed; retained is 12 in (30 cm), evacuant is 18 in (45 cm).  Why D wrong: Floor level provides no gravity flow."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"Under the \"Classification of enema\" chart, which of the following are types of EVACUANT enema? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Simple evacuant enema","Cold enema","Medicated evacuant enema","Retained enema","Purgative enema"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct (A, B, C, E): Evacuant enemas include simple evacuant enema, cold enema, and medicated evacuant enema (which includes purgative, astringent, anthelmintic, and carminative enemas).  Why D wrong: The retained enema is a separate main category, not a type of evacuant enema."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"Under the \"Classification of enema\" chart, which of the following are MEDICATED EVACUANT enema subtypes? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Purgative enema","Astringent enema","Anthelmintic enema","Carminative enema","Cold enema"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A, B, C, D): Medicated evacuant enema subtypes are purgative, astringent, anthelmintic, and carminative enemas.  Why E wrong: The cold enema is a separate evacuant type, not under the \"medicated evacuant\" branch."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"Under the \"Classification of enema\" chart, which of the following are types of RETAINED enema? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Oil enema","Nutrient enema","Stimulant enema","Emollient enema","Sedative enema"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct (all): Retained enema types include oil, nutrient, stimulant, emollient, sedative, and anesthetic enemas.  Why none wrong: Each option is listed under the retained enema branch (the anesthetic enema is also a retained type)."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"When administering an enema, in which position should the client be placed for the procedure?","options":["Right Sim's position","Left Sim's (left lateral) position","Supine flat position","Standing upright"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The client is placed in the left Sim's (left lateral) position, which follows the anatomical curve of the sigmoid colon and aids flow of solution.  Why A wrong: The right Sim's position does not align with the descending/sigmoid colon for optimal flow.  Why C wrong: A flat supine position does not facilitate enema flow and increases risk of leakage.  Why D wrong: Standing is unsafe and ineffective for enema administration."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"What lubricant material is suitable for the enema/rectal tube tip, and why is lubrication important?","options":["Petroleum jelly to disinfect the tube","Lubricate the tip of the rectal catheter with water-soluble jelly to ease insertion and prevent mucosal trauma","No lubricant, to maintain friction","Alcohol to sterilize the tube"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The rectal tube/catheter tip is lubricated with water-soluble jelly to allow smooth insertion and reduce trauma to the rectal mucosa.  Why A wrong: Petroleum jelly is not the recommended water-soluble lubricant and is not for disinfection.  Why C wrong: No lubricant causes friction and mucosal injury.  Why D wrong: Alcohol is irritating and is not a lubricant."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"Match the depth of rectal tube insertion to the patient age for an enema. For an ADULT, the rectal tube should be inserted approximately:","options":["7.5–10 cm (3–4 inches)","5–7.5 cm (2–2.5 inches)","2.5–3.5 cm (1–1.5 inches)","15 cm (6 inches)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For an adult, the rectal tube is inserted about 7.5–10 cm (3–4 inches) into the rectum.  Why B wrong: 5–7.5 cm (2–2.5 inches) is the CHILD insertion depth.  Why C wrong: 2.5–3.5 cm (1–1.5 inches) is the INFANT insertion depth.  Why D wrong: 6 inches is too deep and risks perforation."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"For a CHILD, how far should the rectal tube be inserted for an enema?","options":["7.5–10 cm (3–4 inches)","5–7.5 cm (2–2.5 inches)","2.5–3.5 cm (1–1.5 inches)","12 cm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For a child, the rectal tube is inserted about 5–7.5 cm (2–2.5 inches).  Why A wrong: 7.5–10 cm (3–4 inches) is the adult depth.  Why C wrong: 2.5–3.5 cm (1–1.5 inches) is the infant depth.  Why D wrong: 12 cm exceeds the child insertion depth and risks injury."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"For an INFANT, how far should the rectal tube be inserted for an enema?","options":["7.5–10 cm (3–4 inches)","5–7.5 cm (2–2.5 inches)","2.5–3.5 cm (1–1.5 inches)","10 cm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: For an infant, the rectal tube is inserted about 2.5–3.5 cm (1–1.5 inches).  Why A wrong: 3–4 inches is the adult depth.  Why B wrong: 2–2.5 inches is the child depth.  Why D wrong: 10 cm is far too deep for an infant."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"Which steps are part of the enema procedure and nursing care? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Explain the procedure to the client to gain cooperation and reduce anxiety","Double-check the client's identity to make sure the procedure is performed on the right person","Provide privacy to the patient","Make sure the client should empty their bladder before the procedure","Force the solution rapidly regardless of cramping"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A, B, C, D): The nurse should explain the procedure, verify the client's identity, provide privacy, and have the client empty the bladder before the procedure for comfort and safety.  Why E wrong: The solution should be administered slowly; forcing it rapidly or against cramping causes discomfort and may cause injury."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"During enema administration, if the patient feels cramps, what should the nurse do?","options":["Increase the flow rate","Stop the procedure for a few seconds and again restart when cramping subsides","Withdraw the tube and abandon the enema","Tell the patient to expel immediately"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If the patient feels cramps, stop the procedure for a few seconds and restart again when cramping subsides, then continue with slow administration.  Why A wrong: Increasing flow worsens cramping.  Why C wrong: Abandoning the enema is unnecessary; a brief pause usually relieves cramping.  Why D wrong: Immediate expulsion would prevent the enema from being effective; the patient is asked to retain it as appropriate."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"During and after enema administration, which nursing actions are appropriate? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Allow time for the expulsion of enema (allow the patient to retain it for the appropriate time)","Observe and record the amount and consistency of returns/stool","Document the procedure","Discard the records immediately to protect privacy"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct (A, B, C): The nurse should allow time for expulsion of the enema, observe and record the amount and consistency of returns, and document the procedure.  Why D wrong: Records should be kept (documented), not discarded; documentation is part of accountable care."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"When inserting a rectal tube for an enema, in which direction should the tip be aimed?","options":["Toward the umbilicus","Toward the umbilicus following the natural curve of the rectum (along the direction of the rectum)","Straight downward toward the feet","Laterally toward the hip"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The rectal tube tip should be inserted in the direction following the natural curve of the rectum (angled toward the umbilicus) to avoid trauma to the rectal wall.  Why A wrong: While the general aim is toward the umbilicus, the key principle is to follow the natural curve of the rectum; this option omits that and is less complete.  Why C wrong: Aiming toward the feet works against the anatomical curve and risks injury.  Why D wrong: A lateral hip direction does not follow the rectal anatomy."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"A flatus tube is inserted for which primary purpose?","options":["To deliver nutrition to the bowel","To eliminate or expel flatus and relieve gaseous distension","To collect a urine specimen","To inflate the rectum for surgery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A flatus tube is inserted to eliminate/expel flatus and relieve gaseous distension of the abdomen.  Why A wrong: It is not used to deliver nutrition.  Why C wrong: It is for flatus, not urine collection.  Why D wrong: It deflates/decompresses the bowel of gas, not inflates it."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"In which position is the patient placed for flatus tube insertion?","options":["Right lateral position during the procedure","Left lateral position during the procedure","Supine with head elevated","Prone position"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The patient is placed in the left lateral position during flatus tube insertion (following the anatomical curve of the sigmoid/descending colon).  Why A wrong: The right lateral position does not align with the bowel anatomy for this procedure.  Why C wrong: A supine head-up position is not the recommended position.  Why D wrong: Prone is not used for flatus tube insertion."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"How far should a flatus tube be inserted into the rectum in an adult?","options":["About 2 cm","Insert the catheter about 4 to 6 inches into the rectum","About 12 inches","About 20 cm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flatus tube/catheter is inserted about 4 to 6 inches into the rectum to reach the gas-distended bowel.  Why A wrong: 2 cm is too shallow to relieve flatus effectively.  Why C wrong: 12 inches is excessive and risks injury.  Why D wrong: 20 cm is too deep for a flatus tube."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"What is the maximum holding (retention) time for a flatus tube in place?","options":["5 minutes","15–20 minutes; it should not be held for more than 20 minutes","2 hours","24 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The flatus tube holding time is 15–20 minutes, and it should not be held for more than 20 minutes (prolonged retention can damage the rectal mucosa/sphincter).  Why A wrong: 5 minutes may be too brief; the stated range is 15–20 minutes.  Why C wrong: 2 hours far exceeds the safe maximum and risks mucosal/sphincter injury.  Why D wrong: 24 hours grossly exceeds the limit and would cause tissue damage."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"A patient is to be prescribed a drug to soften hard, dry stool to ease defecation. Which of the following drugs would NOT be prescribed (choose the one that is NOT a stool softener/laxative for this purpose)?","options":["Lactulose","Neomycin","Antacid","Docusate (DHA)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Neomycin is an aminoglycoside antibiotic (used for bowel sterilization/hepatic encephalopathy), NOT a drug prescribed to soften hard stool; the keyed answer is A in the source as a stool softener — note: per the source \"Recall the Nurse,\" the listed correct answer is A (Lactulose) as the softener, meaning Neomycin is the agent that does NOT soften stool. Among the options, Neomycin is the antibiotic that does not act as a stool softener.  Why A wrong (as the \"would NOT\" choice): Lactulose IS an osmotic laxative/stool softener and would be prescribed (it is the source's keyed softener answer A).  Why C wrong (as the \"would NOT\" choice): Antacids are not stool softeners but the item's keyed softener is lactulose; the clearly inappropriate antibiotic here is neomycin.  Why D wrong (as the \"would NOT\" choice): Docusate is a recognized stool softener and would be prescribed.  Discrepancy note: The source \"Recall the Nurse\" item keys Answer = A (lactulose) as the drug prescribed to soften stool. In this rewritten \"which would NOT be prescribed\" framing, neomycin (an antibiotic) is the non-softener; the underlying source fact is that lactulose softens stool."},{"section":"Batch 041 · Urinary catheter care bundle & sterile urine specimen collection…","question":"Per the source \"Recall the Nurse,\" which drug should be prescribed to a patient who needs hard, dry stool softened?","options":["Lactulose","Neomycin","Antacid","DHA"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source keys Answer A (lactulose), an osmotic laxative that draws water into the stool to soften hard, dry feces and ease defecation.  Why B wrong: Neomycin is an antibiotic, not a stool softener.  Why C wrong: Antacids neutralize gastric acid and do not soften stool.  Why D wrong: DHA is not the keyed stool-softening drug in this item."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"Hot application is best defined as the application of a hot agent to the surface of the body that is:","options":["Cooler than the skin, in moist or dry form","Warmer than the skin, in moist or dry form","At exactly skin temperature, in moist form only","Warmer than the skin, in dry form only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hot application is the application of a hot agent, warmer than the skin, in moist or dry form on the surface of the body.  Why A wrong: Cooler than the skin defines cold application, not hot.  Why C wrong: It must be warmer than skin and may be moist OR dry.  Why D wrong: It can be either moist or dry, not dry only."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"Which of the following is NOT a stated purpose of hot application?","options":["To relieve pain and congestion","To promote suppuration","To control haemorrhage","To soften the swellings"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Controlling haemorrhage is a purpose of COLD application, not hot. Hot application causes vasodilatation, which would increase bleeding.  Why A wrong: Relieving pain and congestion is a listed purpose of hot application.  Why B wrong: Promoting suppuration is a listed purpose of hot application.  Why D wrong: Softening the swellings is a listed purpose of hot application."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"Hot application is stated to have which two opposite effects on muscle tone?","options":["To increase muscle tone and to decrease muscle tone","Only to increase muscle tone","Only to decrease muscle tone","To eliminate all muscle activity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The purposes list both \"to increase muscle tone\" and \"to decrease muscle tone.\"  Why B, C wrong: Both effects are listed, not just one.  Why D wrong: Eliminating all muscle activity is not a stated purpose."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"Which of the following is an example of a LOCAL MOIST heat application?","options":["Heating bottle (hot water bottle)","Infrared lamp","Sitz bath","Electric cradle"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Sitz bath is listed under local moist heat (along with warm soak, poultice, paraffin bath).  Why A wrong: Heating/hot water bottle is local DRY heat.  Why B wrong: Infrared lamp is local DRY heat.  Why D wrong: Electric cradle is dry heat."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"Which set lists LOCAL DRY heat applications?","options":["Warm soak, poultice, sitz bath","Heating bottle, infrared lamp, UV lamp, heating lamp, electric cradle","Steam bath, whirlpool bath","Sun bath, blanket bed, electric cradle"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Local dry heat includes heating/hot water bottle, infrared lamp, UV lamp, heating lamp, and electric cradle.  Why A wrong: These are local moist heat.  Why C wrong: Steam bath and whirlpool bath are generalised moist heat.  Why D wrong: Sun bath, blanket bed, electric cradle are generalised dry heat."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"Generalised MOIST heat application includes:","options":["Sun bath and blanket bed","Electric cradle","Steam bath and whirlpool bath","Infrared lamp"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Generalised moist heat includes steam bath and whirlpool bath.  Why A, B wrong: Sun bath, blanket bed, and electric cradle are generalised dry heat.  Why D wrong: Infrared lamp is local dry heat."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"What is the required temperature and duration for using heating bottles (hot water bottles)?","options":["120°–125°F for 20–30 minutes","60°–70°F for 30–60 minutes","110°–115°F for 15–20 minutes","40.6°–46°C for 20–30 minutes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the required temperature for heating bottles/hot water bottles is 120°–125°F for 20–30 minutes.  Why B wrong: 60°–70°F is the cold sponge water temperature.  Why C wrong: 110°–115°F for 15–20 minutes is for the sitz bath.  Why D wrong: 40.6°–46°C for 30–60 minutes (not 20–30 min) is for chemical heating bottles."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"The required temperature for a chemical heating bottle is:","options":["120°–125°F for 20–30 minutes","40.6°C to 46°C for 30–60 minutes","27°C to 37°C for 10–20 minutes","15°C to 18°C for 60 minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source specifies 40.6°C to 46°C for 30–60 minutes for chemical heating bottles.  Why A wrong: That is the temperature/time for hot water bottles.  Why C, D wrong: These values are not stated for chemical heating bottles."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"\"Hot fomentation\" is best described as:","options":["Immersion of the body or parts into warm water","A cloth wrung out from hot water and applied over the body","Full body immersion in warm water in a tub","A bath in a tub immersing body parts below the umbilicus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hot fomentation = cloth wrung out from hot water and applied over the body.  Why A wrong: That describes warm soak.  Why C wrong: That describes a whirlpool bath.  Why D wrong: That describes a sitz bath."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"A whirlpool bath involves:","options":["Immersion of body parts below the umbilicus","Full body immersion in warm water","A cloth wrung from hot water","Use of ice water for high fever"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Whirlpool bath = full body immersion in warm water.  Why A wrong: That is a sitz bath.  Why C wrong: That is hot fomentation.  Why D wrong: That describes a cold sponge."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"A sitz bath means a bath in a tub by immersing body parts:","options":["Above the umbilicus","Below the umbilicus","Only the feet","The whole body including the head"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A sitz bath immerses the body parts below the umbilicus.  Why A, C, D wrong: The source specifically states below the umbilicus, not above, feet only, or whole body."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"The water temperature and minimum duration recommended for a sitz bath are:","options":["60°F to 70°F for 30 minutes","120°–125°F for 5 minutes","110°F to 115°F for at least 15–20 minutes","80°–100°F for 10 minutes"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Sitz bath water temperature is 110°F to 115°F for at least 15–20 minutes (moist heat).  Why A wrong: 60°–70°F is the cold sponge temperature.  Why B wrong: 120°–125°F is for hot water bottles.  Why D wrong: 80°–100°F is the tepid range."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"The purpose of a sitz bath is to relieve pelvic congestion, pain and discomfort. Its most common indication is:","options":["Burns","Piles (haemorrhoids)","Fractures","Fever"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The common indication for a sitz bath is piles/haemorrhoids.  Why A, C wrong: Burns and fractures are not listed indications for sitz bath.  Why D wrong: Fever is treated with cold/tepid sponge, not a sitz bath."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"A cold sponge is a moist cold application that uses ice water when the patient's temperature is dangerously raised. The water temperature used for a cold sponge is:","options":["110°F to 115°F","60°F to 70°F","80°F to 100°F","120°F to 125°F"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The temperature of water used for a cold sponge is 60°F to 70°F.  Why A wrong: 110°–115°F is the sitz bath temperature.  Why C wrong: 80°–100°F is the tepid range.  Why D wrong: 120°–125°F is for hot water bottles."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"According to the source, a tepid sponge helps to reduce temperature in the range of:","options":["Above 105°F","Between 103°F and 101.8°F","Below 98.6°F","Between 110°F and 115°F"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A tepid sponge helps reduce the temperature between 103°F and 101.8°F.  Why A wrong: Cold sponge helps reduce temperature above 105°F.  Why C, D wrong: These ranges are not stated for tepid sponging."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"A cold sponge is specifically helpful in reducing a body temperature that is:","options":["Above 105°F","Between 99°F and 100°F","Below normal","Exactly 98.6°F"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A cold sponge helps to reduce a temperature above 105°F.  Why B, C, D wrong: These do not match the source statement for cold sponge use."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"Cold application is defined as the application of a cold agent that is:","options":["Warmer than the skin, in moist or dry form","Cooler than the skin, in a moist or dry form","At skin temperature only","Cooler than the skin, in dry form only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Cold application is the application of a cold agent, cooler than the skin, either in moist or dry form on the surface of the skin.  Why A wrong: Warmer than skin is hot application.  Why C wrong: It must be cooler than skin.  Why D wrong: It can be moist or dry, not dry only."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"Which of the following is a purpose of cold application?","options":["To promote suppuration","To anaesthetize an area","To increase muscle tone","To soften swellings"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Cold application purposes include anaesthetizing an area, controlling haemorrhage, preventing gangrene, preventing oedema, and reducing inflammation.  Why A, C, D wrong: Promoting suppuration, increasing muscle tone, and softening swellings are purposes of HOT application."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"Select ALL of the following that are listed purposes of COLD application.","options":["To control haemorrhage","To prevent gangrene","To reduce inflammation","To promote suppuration","To anaesthetize an area"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: Cold application purposes include reducing pain and body temperature, anaesthetizing an area, controlling haemorrhage, preventing gangrene, preventing oedema, and reducing inflammation.  Why D wrong: Promoting suppuration is a HOT application purpose."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"Which option correctly lists LOCAL DRY cold applications?","options":["Ice to suck, cold compress, evaporating","Ice bag, ice cooler, chemical cold pack, ice cradle","Inflammatory blanket, cold sponge","Sitz bath, paraffin bath"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Local dry cold = ice bag, ice cooler, chemical cold pack, and ice cradle.  Why A wrong: Ice to suck, cold compress, and evaporating are local MOIST cold applications.  Why C wrong: Inflammatory blanket and cold sponge are general cold applications.  Why D wrong: Sitz/paraffin bath are hot (moist heat) applications."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"Which of the following are classified as GENERAL cold applications?","options":["Ice bag and ice cradle","Inflammatory blanket and cold sponge","Cold compress and ice to suck","Chemical cold pack"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: General cold applications are the inflammatory blanket and cold sponge.  Why A, D wrong: Ice bag, ice cradle, and chemical cold pack are local dry cold.  Why C wrong: Cold compress and ice to suck are local moist cold."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"According to the Hot and Cold Application temperature table, the \"Very Cold\" range is below 15°C (<59°F) and its appropriate application is:","options":["Cold compress","Ice bag","Alcohol sponge bath","Warm bath"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Very Cold (<15°C / <59°F) corresponds to the ice bag.  Why A wrong: Cold compress corresponds to the \"Cool\" range (18°–27°C).  Why C wrong: Alcohol sponge bath corresponds to the \"Tepid\" range.  Why D wrong: Warm bath corresponds to the \"Warm\" range."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"In the temperature table, the \"Tepid\" range (27°–37°C / 80°–100°F) corresponds to which application?","options":["Cold packs","Alcohol sponge bath","Warm bath","Hot soak"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Tepid range (27°–37°C / 80°–100°F) corresponds to an alcohol sponge bath.  Why A wrong: Cold packs are in the \"Cold\" range (15°–18°C).  Why C wrong: Warm bath is in the \"Warm\" range (37°–40°C).  Why D wrong: Hot soak/hot compress is in the \"Hot\" range (40°–46°C)."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"Match the \"Hot\" range from the temperature table with its temperature and application:","options":["40°–46°C (104°–115°F) → Hot soak, hot compress","37°–40°C (100°–104°F) → Warm bath","46°–52°C (115°–126°F) → applied to adults","18°–27°C (65°–80°F) → cold compress"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The \"Hot\" range is 40°–46°C (104°–115°F) and corresponds to hot soak/hot compress.  Why B wrong: That is the \"Warm\" range/application.  Why C wrong: That is the \"Very Hot\" range/application.  Why D wrong: That is the \"Cool\" range/application."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"Per the table, the \"Very Hot\" range of 46°–52°C (115°–126°F) is specifically noted as:","options":["Used for cold compress","Applied to adults","Used for an ice bag","Used for alcohol sponging"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Very Hot range (46°–52°C / 115°–126°F) is noted as applied to adults.  Why A, C, D wrong: These correspond to other (cooler/tepid) ranges."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"A primary local effect of HOT application on blood vessels is:","options":["Peripheral vasoconstriction","Peripheral vasodilatation","Decreased capillary permeability","Decreased blood/lymph flow"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hot application primary effects include peripheral vasodilatation and increased capillary permeability.  Why A, C, D wrong: Vasoconstriction, decreased capillary permeability, and decreased blood/lymph flow are primary effects of COLD application."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"Select ALL primary effects of HOT application.","options":["Increased local metabolism","Increased oxygen consumption","Increased leucocyte motility","Decreased capillary permeability","Increased blood/lymph flow"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: Hot primary effects include increased capillary permeability, increased local metabolism, increased O2 consumption, increased blood/lymph flow, increased leucocyte motility, and increased blood viscosity.  Why D wrong: Decreased capillary permeability is a primary effect of COLD application."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"The stated time/duration for primary effects of HOT application is:","options":["10–20 minutes","10–60 minutes","30–60 minutes","20–30 minutes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The primary effects time for hot application is listed as 10–20 minutes.  Why B wrong: 10–60 minutes is the cold application primary-effects time.  Why C, D wrong: These are heating bottle/chemical bottle durations, not the primary effects time."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"Select ALL primary effects of COLD application.","options":["Peripheral vasoconstriction","Decreased local metabolism","Decreased leucocyte motility","Increased oxygen consumption","Decreased blood viscosity"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: Cold primary effects include peripheral vasoconstriction, decreased capillary permeability, decreased local metabolism, decreased O2 consumption, decreased blood/lymph flow, decreased leucocyte motility, and decreased blood viscosity.  Why D wrong: Increased oxygen consumption is a HOT effect; cold DECREASES O2 consumption."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"The stated duration for primary effects of COLD application is:","options":["10–20 minutes","10–60 minutes","15–20 minutes","5–8 minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Cold application primary-effects time is 10–60 minutes.  Why A wrong: 10–20 minutes is the hot application time.  Why C, D wrong: These do not match the source value."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"Among the SECONDARY effects, HOT application relieves pain by which mechanism?","options":["Local anaesthetic effect","Increased blood supply","Vasoconstriction","Stopping bacterial growth"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A secondary effect of hot application is that it relieves pain by increased blood supply.  Why A wrong: Cold relieves pain by a local anaesthetic effect.  Why C wrong: Vasoconstriction is a cold effect.  Why D wrong: Stopping bacterial growth is a cold effect."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"Select ALL listed SECONDARY effects of HOT application.","options":["Promote healing","Promote suppuration","Soften exudate","Prevent gangrene","Promote peristalsis"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: Hot secondary effects include relieving pain by increased blood supply, decreased muscle tone, promoting healing, promoting suppuration, softening exudate, increased temperature, and promoting peristalsis.  Why D wrong: Preventing gangrene is a secondary effect of COLD application."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"A secondary effect by which COLD application relieves pain is:","options":["Increased blood supply","Local anaesthetic effect","Promoting suppuration","Softening exudate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Cold application relieves pain by a local anaesthetic effect.  Why A wrong: Hot application relieves pain by increased blood supply.  Why C, D wrong: Promoting suppuration and softening exudate are hot secondary effects."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"Select ALL listed SECONDARY effects of COLD application.","options":["Prevent gangrene","Prevent oedema and reduce swelling","Stop bacterial growth","Promote peristalsis","Stop bleeding or haemorrhage"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: Cold secondary effects include relieving pain by anaesthetic effect, preventing gangrene, preventing oedema/reducing swelling, stopping bacterial growth, stopping bleeding/haemorrhage, decreasing body temperature, and anaesthetising body parts.  Why D wrong: Promoting peristalsis is a HOT secondary effect."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"Which condition is a CONTRAINDICATION to HOT application?","options":["Arteriosclerosis","Malignancy","Neuropathy","Shivering"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hot application contraindications include malignancy, shock, acute inflammation/appendicitis, active haemorrhage, oedema, and impaired/chronic diseases of renal, liver and lung functions.  Why A, C, D wrong: Arteriosclerosis, neuropathy, and shivering are contraindications to COLD application."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"Select ALL listed CONTRAINDICATIONS to HOT application.","options":["Acute inflammation or appendicitis","Active haemorrhage","Shock","Arteriosclerosis","Impaired or chronic diseases of renal, liver and lung functions"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: These are all listed hot-application contraindications (along with malignancy and oedema).  Why D wrong: Arteriosclerosis is a contraindication to COLD application."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"Select ALL listed CONTRAINDICATIONS to COLD application.","options":["Shock","Arteriosclerosis","Neuropathy","Malignancy","Shivering"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: Cold application contraindications are shock, arteriosclerosis, neuropathy, shivering, and oedema.  Why D wrong: Malignancy is a contraindication to HOT application."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"Oxygen therapy is best defined as supplemental oxygen given to prevent or treat:","options":["Hypercapnia","Tissue hypoxia","Metabolic alkalosis","Respiratory alkalosis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Oxygen therapy is supplemental oxygen therapy to prevent or treat tissue hypoxia.  Why A wrong: Hypercapnia is excess CO2, not the primary target of O2 therapy.  Why C, D wrong: These acid-base disorders are not the stated purpose."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"As altitude increases (above sea level or in hill areas), the oxygen level in the air:","options":["Increases","Decreases","Remains constant","Becomes zero"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: As altitude increases or in hill areas, the oxygen level in air decreases.  Why A, C wrong: The level decreases, it does not increase or stay constant.  Why D wrong: It decreases but does not reach zero."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"During normal breathing, a client inhales approximately what percentage of oxygen and exhales what percentage?","options":["Inhales 16%, exhales 21%","Inhales 21%, exhales 16%","Inhales 79%, exhales 4%","Inhales 21%, exhales 0.04%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A client during inhalation gets 21% oxygen and during exhalation exhales 16% oxygen.  Why A wrong: The values are reversed.  Why C wrong: 79% and 4% refer to nitrogen and exhaled CO2.  Why D wrong: 0.04% is inspired CO2, not exhaled oxygen."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"Oxygen is described as a combustible/flammable gas, posing a risk of:","options":["Explosion only","Fire accident","Drowning","Electric shock"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Oxygen supports combustion (is flammable), so there is a risk of fire accident.  Why A wrong: The source specifically states fire accident risk.  Why C, D wrong: Drowning and electric shock are not the stated hazard of oxygen."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"Select ALL of the key components of combustion as listed in the source.","options":["Fuel","Oxygen","Heat","Water","Nitrogen"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The key components of combustion are fuel, oxygen, and heat (the fire triangle).  Why D, E wrong: Water and nitrogen are not components of combustion; they are not listed."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"Without a doctor's order, the nurse is authorized to administer oxygen:","options":["Never under any circumstance","Only in an emergency situation","At any time the nurse decides","Only for COPD patients"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Without a doctor's order, nurses are NOT authorized for oxygen administration, EXCEPT in an emergency situation.  Why A wrong: Emergency situations are an exception.  Why C wrong: Nurses cannot give it any time at their discretion (routine needs an order).  Why D wrong: COPD is not the basis for the exception."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"For a HEALTHY patient, the generally recommended oxygen saturation target is:","options":["88 to 92%","92 to 98%","80 to 85%","100% only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For a healthy patient, oxygen saturation targets are generally 92 to 98%.  Why A wrong: 88–92% is the target for chronic hypercapnic conditions.  Why C, D wrong: These ranges are not the healthy-patient target."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"For patients with chronic hypercapnic conditions, the target oxygen saturation is generally:","options":["92 to 98%","88 to 92%","95 to 100%","80 to 88%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Chronic hypercapnic patients have target saturations of 88 to 92% to avoid suppressing respiratory drive.  Why A wrong: 92–98% is the healthy-patient target.  Why C, D wrong: These values are not stated for hypercapnic patients."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"In clinical practice, oxygen saturation is commonly measured by a pulse oximeter, which measures capillary oxygen. A noted limitation of this method is that it is:","options":["Painful and invasive","Not reliable and accurate","Only usable in the ICU","The most accurate method available"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The pulse oximeter measures capillary oxygen but is noted as not reliable and accurate.  Why A wrong: Pulse oximetry is non-invasive and painless.  Why C wrong: It is widely used, not ICU-only.  Why D wrong: ABG, not pulse oximetry, is the most accurate method."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"The most reliable and accurate way to measure oxygen saturation is:","options":["Pulse oximetry","ABG (arterial blood gas) analysis, which measures arterial oxygen","Capnography","Visual inspection of skin colour"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most reliable and accurate way to measure oxygen saturation is ABG analysis, which measures arterial oxygen.  Why A wrong: Pulse oximetry measures capillary oxygen and is less reliable.  Why C wrong: Capnography measures exhaled CO2, not oxygen saturation.  Why D wrong: Visual inspection is not a reliable measure."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"Capnography is a measure of:","options":["Arterial oxygen content","Exhaled carbon dioxide (end-tidal CO2)","Capillary oxygen","Inspired oxygen fraction"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Capnography is the measure of exhaled CO2 and is also known as end-tidal CO2.  Why A, C wrong: It measures CO2, not oxygen.  Why D wrong: Inspired oxygen fraction is FiO2."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"Which arterial value is an indication for oxygen therapy?","options":["PaO2 < 60 mmHg","PaO2 > 100 mmHg","SaO2 > 95%","PaCO2 < 30 mmHg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A PaO2 < 60 mmHg (partial pressure of oxygen) is a stated indication for oxygen therapy.  Why B wrong: PaO2 > 100 is normal/high, not an indication.  Why C wrong: SaO2 < 90% in room air (not >95%) is an indication.  Why D wrong: A low CO2 is not the listed oxygen-therapy indication."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"An oxygen saturation indication for oxygen therapy is:","options":["SaO2 < 90% in room air","SaO2 > 98%","SaO2 = 100%","SaO2 between 92 and 98%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: SaO2 < 90% in room air (oxygen saturation) is a listed indication for oxygen therapy.  Why B, C, D wrong: These represent normal/adequate saturation, not an indication."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"Select ALL of the following that are listed INDICATIONS for oxygen therapy.","options":["Acute myocardial infarction or heart failure","Respiratory arrest / respiratory failure or RR > 24 per minute","Carbon-monoxide poisoning (HBOT)","Mild dehydration","Shock of any cause"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: Listed indications include hypoxemia, acute MI/heart failure, respiratory arrest/failure or RR >24/min, shock of any cause, increased metabolic demands (burns, multiple injuries, severe sepsis), CO poisoning (HBOT), and short-term post-anaesthesia therapy.  Why D wrong: Mild dehydration is not a listed indication for oxygen therapy."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"A respiratory rate above which value is listed as an indication for oxygen therapy?","options":["RR > 12 per minute","RR > 18 per minute","RR > 24 per minute","RR > 40 per minute"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Respiratory failure or RR > 24 per minute is a listed indication for oxygen therapy.  Why A, B wrong: These are within or near normal respiratory rate.  Why D wrong: The source threshold is >24/min, not >40/min."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"According to the Environmental Air Composition table, the percentages of carbon dioxide in inspired vs expired air are:","options":["0.04% inspired, 4% expired","4% inspired, 0.04% expired","21% inspired, 16% expired","79% inspired, 79% expired"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Carbon dioxide is 0.04% in inspired air and 4% in expired air.  Why B wrong: The values are reversed.  Why C wrong: 21%/16% are oxygen values.  Why D wrong: 79%/79% are nitrogen values."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"Per the Environmental Air Composition table, nitrogen in inspired and expired air is approximately:","options":["21% inspired, 16% expired","79% inspired, 79% expired","0.04% inspired, 4% expired","16% inspired, 21% expired"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Nitrogen is 79% in inspired and 79% in expired air (essentially unchanged).  Why A, D wrong: 21%/16% are oxygen values.  Why C wrong: 0.04%/4% are CO2 values."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"A chemoreceptor detects a change in the blood such as an increase in carbon dioxide, which is termed:","options":["Hypoxia","Hypercapnia","Hypocapnia","Hypoxemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: An increase in blood levels of carbon dioxide is hypercapnia.  Why A, D wrong: Hypoxia/hypoxemia refer to LOW oxygen.  Why C wrong: Hypocapnia is LOW carbon dioxide."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"A decrease in the blood level of oxygen detected by chemoreceptors is called:","options":["Hypercapnia","Hypoxia","Hypocapnia","Hypercarbia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A decrease in blood levels of oxygen is hypoxia.  Why A, D wrong: Hypercapnia/hypercarbia refer to high CO2.  Why C wrong: Hypocapnia is low CO2."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"Peripheral chemoreceptors (PC) are located in the carotid and aortic bodies and are MOST sensitive to changes in:","options":["pH / hydrogen ion","PaO2","Temperature","Light"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Peripheral chemoreceptors are in the carotid and aortic bodies and are more sensitive to changes in PaO2.  Why A wrong: Central chemoreceptors are more sensitive to pH/hydrogen ion.  Why C, D wrong: Temperature and light are detected by thermoreceptors and photoreceptors."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"Central chemoreceptors (CC) are located on the ventrolateral surface of the medulla oblongata and are most sensitive to changes in:","options":["PaO2","pH or hydrogen ion","Pressure","Sound"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Central chemoreceptors are in the medulla oblongata and are more sensitive to changes in pH/hydrogen ion concentration.  Why A wrong: Peripheral chemoreceptors are more sensitive to PaO2.  Why C, D wrong: Pressure and sound are detected by mechanoreceptors."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"Which sensory receptor responds to oxygen and pH, located in the carotid/aortic bodies and medulla oblongata?","options":["Mechanoreceptor","Chemoreceptor","Photoreceptor","Thermoreceptor"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Chemoreceptors respond to oxygen and pH; located at carotid/aortic bodies (PC) and medulla oblongata (CC).  Why A wrong: Mechanoreceptors respond to pressure, stretch, vibration, sound, acceleration.  Why C wrong: Photoreceptors respond to light.  Why D wrong: Thermoreceptors respond to temperature."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"Pressure (baroreceptor), cell stretch, vibration, sound, and acceleration are stimuli detected by which sensory receptors, located in skin, muscle, and bone?","options":["Chemoreceptors","Photoreceptors","Mechanoreceptors","Thermoreceptors"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Mechanoreceptors detect pressure (baroreceptor), cell stretch, vibration, sound and acceleration, located in skin, muscle and bone.  Why A, B, D wrong: These respond to chemicals, light, and temperature respectively."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"Photoreceptors respond to photons of light and are located in the:","options":["Hypothalamus","Retina of the eye","Carotid bodies","Medulla oblongata"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Photoreceptors detect photons of light and are located in the retina of the eye.  Why A wrong: The hypothalamus is the location of thermoreceptors.  Why C, D wrong: Carotid bodies and medulla oblongata house chemoreceptors."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"Thermoreceptors detect varying degrees of temperature and are located in the:","options":["Retina of the eye","Hypothalamus","Aortic bodies","Skin, muscle and bone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Thermoreceptors detect varying degrees of temperature and are located in the hypothalamus.  Why A wrong: The retina houses photoreceptors.  Why C wrong: Aortic bodies house chemoreceptors.  Why D wrong: Skin, muscle and bone house mechanoreceptors."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"Oxygen administration is ABSOLUTELY contraindicated in a patient with poisoning by:","options":["Carbon monoxide","Paraquat","Organophosphate","Cyanide"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Oxygen administration is absolutely contraindicated in a patient with paraquat poisoning (oxygen worsens lung injury).  Why A wrong: Carbon-monoxide poisoning is actually an INDICATION for oxygen (HBOT).  Why C, D wrong: Organophosphate and cyanide poisoning are not the stated absolute contraindication."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"In COPD patients, the source advises avoiding high-flow oxygen and giving only low flow oxygen at 1–2 L/min in order to prevent:","options":["Oxygen toxicity and seizures","CO2 narcosis and loss of respiratory drive","Hyperventilation","Hypoglycemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In COPD patients, avoid high-flow oxygen and give only low flow oxygen (1–2 L/min) to prevent CO2 narcosis and loss of respiratory drive.  Why A wrong: While oxygen toxicity is a general risk, the stated reason here is CO2 narcosis and loss of respiratory drive.  Why C, D wrong: These are not the reasons given for the COPD low-flow caution."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"Oxygen should never be used near an open flame or anything that can produce intense heat, flames, or sparks. Select ALL listed examples of such hazards.","options":["Burning cigarette","Lighted match","Heaters and heating pads","Cool mist humidifier","Hair dryers and a stove"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: Listed hazards include a burning cigarette, lighted match, heaters, heating pads, hair dryers, and a stove.  Why D wrong: A cool mist humidifier is not listed as a flame/heat hazard."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"PaO2 refers to:","options":["Saturation of peripheral oxygen","Partial pressure (actual amount) of oxygen dissolved in arterial blood","Fraction of inspired oxygen","Percentage of haemoglobin saturated with oxygen"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: PaO2 is the partial pressure or actual amount of oxygen dissolved in arterial blood.  Why A wrong: That is SpO2.  Why C wrong: That is FiO2.  Why D wrong: That describes oxygen saturation (SpO2/SaO2)."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"SaO2 (oxygen saturation as measured by blood analysis/ABG) is expressed in mmHg with a normal range of:","options":["95–100%","80–100 mmHg","21%","50–60 psi"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: SaO2 is measured by ABG and expressed in mmHg, with a normal range of 80–100 mmHg.  Why A wrong: 95–100% is the SpO2 normal range (percentage).  Why C wrong: 21% is the FiO2 of room air.  Why D wrong: 50–60 psi is pipe pressure."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"SpO2 stands for Saturation of Peripheral Oxygen and indicates capillary oxygenation. It is measured by a pulse oximeter and expressed as a percentage, with a normal range of:","options":["80–100%","95%–100%","88–92%","60–70%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: SpO2 normal range is 95%–100%, measured by pulse oximeter, expressed as a percentage of haemoglobin saturated by oxygen to form oxyhaemoglobin.  Why A wrong: 80–100 mmHg is the SaO2 (ABG) range.  Why C wrong: 88–92% is the COPD/hypercapnic target.  Why D wrong: 60–70°F is cold-sponge water temperature, not SpO2."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"FiO2 is best defined as the:","options":["Partial pressure of oxygen in arterial blood","Fraction of inspired oxygen (concentration of oxygen in the gas mixture)","Saturation of peripheral oxygen","Percentage of haemoglobin saturated with oxygen"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: FiO2 is the fraction of inspired oxygen — the concentration of oxygen in the gas mixture.  Why A wrong: That is PaO2.  Why C, D wrong: Those describe SpO2/oxygen saturation."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"Room air has a fraction of inspired oxygen (FiO2) of approximately:","options":["16%","21%","50%","100%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The gas mixture at room air has an FiO2 of 21%, meaning a 21% concentration of oxygen.  Why A wrong: 16% is the exhaled oxygen concentration.  Why C, D wrong: These are higher than room-air FiO2."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"Select ALL correct precautions for oxygen administration as listed in the source.","options":["Put a \"No Smoking\" sign at the entrance of the ward and warn patients","Use oil on the oxygen cylinder valve for lubrication","Keep oxygen cylinders in a dry and cool place","Mark every cylinder to indicate whether it is full or empty","Use only carriers with wheels to transport the cylinder; do not roll it"],"correct":"A,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Listed precautions include \"No Smoking\" signage, dry and cool storage, marking full/empty status, and using wheeled carriers (not rolling) for transport.  Why B wrong: Do NOT use oil on the oxygen cylinder — oil can ignite if exposed to oxygen."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"Why must oil never be used on an oxygen cylinder?","options":["It corrodes the cylinder valve","Oil can ignite if exposed to oxygen","It reduces the flow rate","It contaminates the gas"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Do not use oil on the oxygen cylinder; oil can ignite if exposed to oxygen.  Why A, C, D wrong: The stated reason is the fire/ignition hazard, not corrosion, flow, or contamination."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"The source notes the nurse should NOT give oxygen to a patient who is:","options":["Hypoxic","Hyperventilated","In shock","Post-anaesthesia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A precaution states: do not give oxygen to a hyperventilated patient.  Why A, C, D wrong: Hypoxia, shock, and post-anaesthesia recovery are indications for oxygen therapy."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"Why should humidified oxygen be administered, and not a dry gas, when giving oxygen?","options":["Dry gas raises blood pressure","Dry gas can damage respiratory mucosa and lead to oxygen toxicity","Humidity increases the flow rate","Dry gas causes hyperventilation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Oxygen is a dry gas which can damage respiratory mucosa, so humidified oxygen should be administered.  Why A, C, D wrong: These are not the stated reasons for humidifying oxygen."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"In hospitals, the oxygen pipeline is denoted with which colour, and an oxygen cylinder is coded with which body/top colours?","options":["Pipeline white; cylinder black body with white top","Pipeline black; cylinder white body with black top","Pipeline green; cylinder yellow body","Pipeline blue; cylinder red top"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The oxygen pipeline is denoted with white colour; the oxygen cylinder is coded by a black body and white top/head — the white indicates medical-grade oxygen and the black distinguishes it from other gases.  Why B, C, D wrong: These do not match the stated colour codes."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"The storage pressure of an oxygen cylinder is approximately ____, while a pressure regulator allows ____ to the patient.","options":["50 psi storage; 2000 psi to patient","2000 psi storage; 50 psi to patient","60 psi storage; 15 psi to patient","100 psi storage; 50 psi to patient"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Storage pressure of an O2 cylinder is 2000 psi, and a pressure regulator allows 50 psi to the patient.  Why A wrong: The values are reversed.  Why C, D wrong: These do not match the stated values."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"The oxygen pipe pressure is stated to be:","options":["2000 psi","50–60 psi","15 psi","100–120 psi"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pipe pressure is 50–60 psi.  Why A wrong: 2000 psi is cylinder storage pressure.  Why C, D wrong: These do not match the stated pipe pressure."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"Oxygen cylinders are opened in which direction (using a spanner/key), and closed in which direction?","options":["Open clockwise; close anti-clockwise","Open anti-clockwise/counter-clockwise; close clockwise","Open and close clockwise","Open and close anti-clockwise"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Oxygen cylinders open in the anti-clock/counter-clock direction with a \"spanner\" (key) and close in the clockwise direction.  Why A, C, D wrong: These do not match the stated opening/closing directions."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"The maximum range of the flow meter used with an O2 cylinder is:","options":["6 L/min","15 L/min","40 L/min","60 L/min"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 15 L/min is the maximum range of the flow meter used with an O2 cylinder.  Why A wrong: 6 L/min is the upper limit for nasal cannula comfort.  Why C, D wrong: 40–60 L/min is the high-flow nasal cannula range, not the cylinder flow-meter maximum."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"The humidity bottle of an oxygen cylinder is also known as the:","options":["Wolf bottle","Hudson bottle","Venturi bottle","Bennett bottle"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The humidity bottle of the cylinder is also known as the wolf bottle.  Why B wrong: Hudson's mask is the simple face mask, not the humidity bottle.  Why C, D wrong: These are not the stated names."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"Hyperbaric oxygen administration delivers oxygen at:","options":["Atmospheric pressure","Greater pressure than atmospheric pressure","Less than atmospheric pressure","Variable pressure equal to room air"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hyperbaric O2 is oxygen administered at greater pressure than atmospheric pressure.  Why A wrong: That is normobaric oxygen.  Why C, D wrong: Hyperbaric means higher-than-atmospheric pressure."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"Normobaric oxygen administration delivers oxygen at:","options":["Greater than atmospheric pressure","Atmospheric pressure","Half of atmospheric pressure","Twice atmospheric pressure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Normobaric O2 is oxygen administered at atmospheric pressure.  Why A, D wrong: Greater/higher pressure describes hyperbaric oxygen.  Why C wrong: Normobaric is at (not below) atmospheric pressure."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"Non-invasive ventilation modalities listed under normobaric oxygen techniques include:","options":["CPAP and BPAP","Tracheostomy and intubation","ECMO","Hyperbaric chamber"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Non-invasive ventilation includes CPAP and BPAP under normobaric techniques.  Why B wrong: Tracheostomy/intubation are invasive.  Why C, D wrong: ECMO and hyperbaric chamber are not listed as the non-invasive ventilation examples here."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"Oxygen delivery systems are categorised into two main groups:","options":["Hot and cold systems","Low-flow and high-flow systems","Wet and dry systems","Central and peripheral systems"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Oxygen delivery systems are categorised into low-flow and high-flow systems.  Why A, C, D wrong: These are not the stated categories of oxygen delivery systems."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"Low-flow oxygen systems are also called:","options":["Fixed performance devices","Variable performance devices","High-pressure devices","Hyperbaric devices"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Low-flow systems are also called variable performance devices because the oxygen is diluted with room air based on inspiratory flow.  Why A wrong: Fixed performance devices are HIGH-flow systems.  Why C, D wrong: These are not the stated terms."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"Why do low-flow (variable performance) systems NOT allow accurate calculation of the inspiratory oxygen fraction (FiO2)?","options":["They deliver a constant FiO2 regardless of breathing","When the patient inspires, oxygen is diluted with room air, and the degree of dilution depends on inspiratory flow","They bypass the upper airway","They deliver oxygen at very high flow"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Low-flow devices provide lower oxygen flow than actual inspiratory flow/volume, so oxygen is diluted with room air to a degree that depends on the patient's inspiratory flow — making FiO2 variable.  Why A wrong: Delivering a constant FiO2 describes high-flow devices.  Why C wrong: Bypassing the upper airway describes transtracheal catheters.  Why D wrong: Very high flow describes high-flow devices."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"High-flow oxygen systems (fixed performance devices) are characterised by which feature?","options":["FiO2 varies with the patient's breathing","They maintain a constant FiO2 and are not affected by the patient's type of breathing","They always deliver less than the patient's inspiratory flow","They can only deliver room air"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: High-flow systems provide higher oxygen flow and FiO2, are stable, and are not affected by the patient's type of breathing (so they maintain constant FiO2).  Why A, C wrong: These describe low-flow/variable devices.  Why D wrong: They deliver controlled high oxygen, not just room air."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"Minute ventilation (MV) is best defined as:","options":["The volume of one breath","The volume of gas inhaled or exhaled from a person's lungs per minute","The respiratory rate per hour","The fraction of inspired oxygen"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Minute ventilation is the volume of gas inhaled or exhaled from a person's lungs per minute.  Why A wrong: The volume of one breath is the tidal volume.  Why C, D wrong: These are not the definition of minute ventilation."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"The formula for minute ventilation (MV) is:","options":["MV = TV ÷ RR","MV = TV × RR","MV = TV + RR","MV = RR ÷ TV"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: MV = TV × RR (tidal volume times respiratory rate).  Why A, C, D wrong: The relationship is multiplication of tidal volume by respiratory rate, not division or addition."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"Using the example given, a tidal volume × 12 breaths/min yields a minute ventilation of:","options":["600 mL/min","6000 mL/min","1200 mL/min","12000 mL/min"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source example gives tidal volume × 12 breaths/min = 6000 mL/min.  Why A, C, D wrong: These do not match the example value of 6000 mL/min."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"According to the Must Know Fact, increasing either the respiratory rate or tidal volume will:","options":["Decrease minute ventilation","Increase minute ventilation","Have no effect on minute ventilation","Reverse the direction of airflow"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Since MV = TV × RR, increasing respiratory rate or tidal volume will increase minute ventilation.  Why A, C wrong: An increase in either factor increases MV.  Why D wrong: It does not reverse airflow direction."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"Select ALL of the following that are listed as LOW-FLOW oxygen delivery devices.","options":["Nasal cannula (prongs/spectacles)","Venturi system","Simple face mask","Non-rebreathing mask (NRBM)","Tracheostomy mask"],"correct":"A,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Low-flow device examples include nasal cannula, intranasal catheter, trans-tracheal catheter, simple face mask, partial rebreathing mask, non-rebreathing mask (NRBM), and tracheostomy mask.  Why B wrong: The Venturi system is a HIGH-flow (fixed performance) device."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"Select ALL of the following that are listed as HIGH-FLOW oxygen delivery devices.","options":["Venturi system","Oxyhood","Vapotherm","Nasal cannula","High flow nasal cannula (HFNC, 40–60 L/min)"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: High-flow device examples include the Venturi system, Oxyhood, Vapotherm, oxygen tent, and high-flow nasal cannula (HFNC, 40–60 L/min).  Why D wrong: A standard nasal cannula is a LOW-flow device."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"The flow rate of a high flow nasal cannula (HFNC) is stated as:","options":["1–6 L/min","5–8 L/min","40–60 L/min","12–15 L/min"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: HFNC flow rate is 40–60 L/min.  Why A wrong: 1–6 L/min is the standard nasal cannula range.  Why B wrong: 5–8 L/min is the simple face mask range.  Why D wrong: 12–15 L/min is the non-rebreather mask range."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"A nasal catheter (also called oropharyngeal/oxygen catheter) delivers what oxygen concentration?","options":["1–6 L/m, FiO2 24–44%","5–8 L/m, FiO2 40–60%","6–15 L/m, FiO2 70–90%","12–15 L/m, FiO2 60–100%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A nasal catheter delivers 1–6 L/m with an FiO2 of 24–44%.  Why B wrong: 5–8 L/m, 40–60% is the simple face mask.  Why C wrong: 6–15 L/m, 70–90% is the partial rebreather mask.  Why D wrong: 12–15 L/m, 60–100% is the non-rebreather mask."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"During nasal catheter therapy, the patient positions are prioritised as:","options":["Supine first, prone second","Rose position first priority, high Fowler's position second priority","Trendelenburg first, lateral second","Sitting first, lithotomy second"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Position during nasal catheter therapy: rose position is first priority and high Fowler's position is second priority.  Why A, C, D wrong: These do not match the stated positioning priorities."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"How frequently should the nasal catheter be changed, and why?","options":["Every 24 hours to prevent infection","Every 8 hours to prevent nasal stenosis","Every 1 hour to prevent dryness","Never, unless dislodged"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The nasal catheter should be changed every 8 hours to prevent nasal stenosis.  Why A, C, D wrong: The source specifies an 8-hour interval to prevent nasal stenosis."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"A nasal cannula (also known as nasal prongs) delivers an oxygen concentration of:","options":["1–6 L/m, FiO2 24–44%","12–15 L/m, FiO2 60–100%","6–15 L/m, FiO2 70–90%","40–60 L/m"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A nasal cannula delivers 1–6 L/m with FiO2 24–44%.  Why B wrong: 12–15 L/m, 60–100% is the NRBM.  Why C wrong: 6–15 L/m, 70–90% is the partial rebreather.  Why D wrong: 40–60 L/m is the HFNC."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"Why is the nasal cannula described as the most commonly used oxygen delivery method?","options":["It delivers the highest FiO2","It is the least intrusive, comfortable, and easy to use","It bypasses the upper airway","It requires no oxygen source"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The nasal cannula is least intrusive, comfortable, and easy, so it is most commonly used.  Why A wrong: It delivers a relatively low FiO2 (24–44%).  Why C wrong: It does not bypass the upper airway (transtracheal catheters do).  Why D wrong: It still requires an oxygen source."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"An oxygen flow rate above what value should be avoided with a nasal cannula because it can dry the nasal mucosa and disturb sleeping patterns?","options":["> 2 L/min","> 6 L/min","> 10 L/min","> 15 L/min"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: An oxygen flow > 6 L/minute should be avoided as it can dry the nasal mucosa and disturb sleeping patterns; humidification should be added to prevent dryness.  Why A, C, D wrong: The stated threshold is >6 L/min."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"During nasal cannula therapy, the client is best placed in which position?","options":["Prone position","Semi-Fowler's position","Trendelenburg position","Lithotomy position"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Place the client in the semi-Fowler's position during nasal cannula therapy.  Why A, C, D wrong: These are not the recommended position for nasal cannula therapy."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"The nasal cannula is described as the choice device in which conditions?","options":["Severe hypoxia requiring 100% oxygen","COPD (first choice), during anxiety and dyspnoea","Carbon monoxide poisoning requiring HBOT","Surgical airway bypass"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The nasal cannula is the choice in conditions such as COPD (first choice vertical mask noted), and during anxiety and dyspnoea.  Why A wrong: Severe hypoxia needing 100% oxygen calls for an NRBM/high-flow device.  Why C wrong: CO poisoning calls for HBOT.  Why D wrong: Airway bypass involves a transtracheal/tracheostomy device, not a nasal cannula."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"A major advantage of the nasal cannula, making it the most convenient method of O2 inhalation, is that the patient can:","options":["Receive 100% oxygen","Talk, eat, and drink without needing to disconnect the cannula","Bypass humidification","Avoid any oxygen source"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The nasal cannula's main advantage is that the patient can use their mouth to talk, eat, etc., during therapy; during eating, drinking, and talking there is no need to disconnect the nasal cannula.  Why A wrong: It delivers only 24–44% FiO2, not 100%.  Why C wrong: Humidification is still recommended to prevent dryness.  Why D wrong: An oxygen source is still required."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"(PYQ – RECALL) A nurse needs to obtain an ABG sample in a client who is on oxygen with a non-rebreather mask (NRBM). How would the nurse obtain the ABG sample?","options":["Shift the client to a nasal cannula","Decrease the oxygen flow rate","Obtain the sample without any change","Keep the client on room air for 30 minutes"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The keyed answer is C — obtain the sample without any change. The ABG should reflect the patient's current oxygen therapy; altering the FiO2 before sampling would not represent the patient's true gas exchange on their prescribed support, and reducing oxygen in a severely hypoxic NRBM patient could be unsafe.  Why A wrong: Switching to a nasal cannula changes the FiO2 and could destabilise the patient.  Why B wrong: Decreasing flow alters the very value being measured and risks hypoxia.  Why D wrong: Removing oxygen for 30 minutes is dangerous for a patient requiring an NRBM."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"A trans-tracheal oxygen catheter (TTOC), also known as Trans-tracheal Augmented Ventilation (TTAV), delivers an oxygen concentration of:","options":["1–4 L/m","5–8 L/m","6–15 L/m","12–15 L/m"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The TTOC delivers 1–4 L/m.  Why B wrong: 5–8 L/m is the simple face mask.  Why C wrong: 6–15 L/m is the partial rebreather mask.  Why D wrong: 12–15 L/m is the non-rebreather mask."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"How is a trans-tracheal oxygen catheter placed, and what is its key feature?","options":["Inserted through the nostril into the oropharynx; delivers humidified air","A small flexible catheter surgically inserted through the anterior neck to deliver oxygen directly to the trachea and lungs, bypassing the upper airway","Placed as a mask over the nose and mouth with a reservoir bag","A nasal prong that delivers oxygen at high flow"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A TTOC is a small, flexible catheter surgically inserted through the anterior neck to deliver oxygen directly to the trachea and lungs, thus bypassing the upper airway. It is used in chronic maintenance therapy for patients requiring long-term supplemental oxygen.  Why A wrong: That describes a nasal catheter, not a TTOC.  Why C wrong: That describes a rebreather mask.  Why D wrong: That describes a (high-flow) nasal cannula."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"A simple face mask (also known as Hudson's mask) delivers what oxygen concentration?","options":["1–4 L/m","5–8 L/m, FiO2 40–60%","6–15 L/m, FiO2 70–90%","12–15 L/m, FiO2 60–100%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A simple face mask delivers 5–8 L/m with FiO2 40–60%; it is a mask with no bag attached, also known as Hudson's mask.  Why A wrong: 1–4 L/m is the TTOC.  Why C wrong: 6–15 L/m, 70–90% is the partial rebreather.  Why D wrong: 12–15 L/m, 60–100% is the non-rebreather."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"A simple face mask is distinguished from rebreather masks by the fact that it:","options":["Has a reservoir bag of 1 litre capacity","Has no bag attached","Has two one-way valves","Bypasses the upper airway"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A simple face mask is a mask with no bag attached (Hudson's mask).  Why A wrong: A 1-litre reservoir bag describes the partial rebreather mask.  Why C wrong: Two one-way valves describe the non-rebreather mask.  Why D wrong: Bypassing the upper airway describes the TTOC."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"A partial-rebreather mask (PRBM) delivers an oxygen concentration of:","options":["1–6 L/m, FiO2 24–44%","5–8 L/m, FiO2 40–60%","6–15 L/m, FiO2 70–90%","1–4 L/m"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: A PRBM delivers 6–15 L/m with FiO2 70–90%, using a reservoir bag of 1 litre capacity.  Why A wrong: 1–6 L/m, 24–44% is the nasal cannula/catheter.  Why B wrong: 5–8 L/m, 40–60% is the simple face mask.  Why D wrong: 1–4 L/m is the TTOC."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"In a partial-rebreather mask, the inflatable reservoir increases delivered oxygen because the patient rebreathes:","options":["The entire exhaled tidal volume","One third of the exhaled tidal volume, which is high in oxygen, providing high FiO2","Only carbon dioxide","Room air only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the partial rebreather, the patient rebreathes one third of the exhaled tidal volume, which is high in oxygen, thus providing a high FiO2.  Why A wrong: It is one third, not the entire exhaled volume.  Why C, D wrong: The rebreathed gas is oxygen-rich exhaled gas, not pure CO2 or room air."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"During inspiration with a partial-rebreather mask, the flow rate should be adjusted to keep the reservoir bag:","options":["Completely collapsed","2/3 full","Detached","Overinflated to bursting"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Adjust the flow rate to keep the reservoir bag 2/3 full during inspiration.  Why A wrong: A collapsed bag indicates inadequate flow.  Why C, D wrong: The bag should remain attached and not overinflated; the target is 2/3 full."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"A partial-rebreather mask is usually CONTRAINDICATED in which patients?","options":["Severely hypoxic patients","COPD patients","Post-anaesthesia patients","Trauma patients"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The PRBM is usually contraindicated in COPD patients (high FiO2 risks suppressing respiratory drive). It is used when oxygen concentration needs to be raised.  Why A, C, D wrong: These groups are not listed as the contraindication for a PRBM."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"A non-rebreather mask (NRBM) delivers what oxygen concentration?","options":["1–6 L/m, FiO2 24–44%","5–8 L/m, FiO2 40–60%","6–15 L/m, FiO2 70–90%","12–15 L/m, FiO2 60–100%"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The NRBM delivers 12–15 L/m with FiO2 60–100% and is used in severely hypoxic patients for a short time.  Why A wrong: 1–6 L/m, 24–44% is the nasal cannula.  Why B wrong: 5–8 L/m, 40–60% is the simple face mask.  Why C wrong: 6–15 L/m, 70–90% is the partial rebreather mask."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"Non-rebreather masks have two one-way valves over the exhalation ports. The purpose of these valves is to:","options":["Allow rebreathing of exhaled CO2","Permit exhalation but prevent inhalation of exhaled CO2 (room air), maintaining a higher FiO2","Lower the FiO2","Humidify the gas"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The two one-way valves permit exhalation but not entry of room air, and the one-way valve prevents the patient from inhaling exhaled CO2, thereby maintaining a higher FiO2.  Why A wrong: The valves prevent CO2 rebreathing, not allow it.  Why C wrong: They help maintain a higher FiO2.  Why D wrong: The valves are for gas flow direction, not humidification."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"With a non-rebreather mask, the flow rate should be adjusted so that the reservoir bag:","options":["Stays collapsed during inspiration","Remains inflated","Is removed during care","Overinflates"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Adjust the flow rate to keep the reservoir bag inflated; make sure the bag does not kink or twist, and ensure the oxygen source does not disconnect, otherwise the patient may suffocate.  Why A wrong: A collapsed bag risks suffocation/inadequate oxygen.  Why C, D wrong: The bag stays attached and should not overinflate; it must remain inflated."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"During each breath with an NRBM, the valves and flaps should function so that the valve:","options":["Opens during inspiration and closes during expiration","Opens during expiration and closes during inspiration","Stays open continuously","Stays closed continuously"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Ensure the valve and flaps are intact and functional during each breath — the valve should open during expiration and close during inspiration.  Why A wrong: The directions are reversed.  Why C, D wrong: The valve must cycle open/closed with the respiratory phases, not stay fixed."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"A noted disadvantage of the non-rebreather mask (NRBM) is:","options":["Low FiO2 delivery","Carbon dioxide retention or aspiration in case of vomiting","Inability to deliver oxygen above 30%","It bypasses the upper airway"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A disadvantage of the NRBM is carbon dioxide retention or aspiration in case of vomiting.  Why A, C wrong: The NRBM delivers a HIGH FiO2 (60–100%).  Why D wrong: The NRBM does not bypass the upper airway."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"For a patient on an NRBM (or PRBM) who feels claustrophobic, the nurse should:","options":["Switch immediately to a transtracheal catheter","Provide psychological support","Remove the mask permanently","Increase the flow to 30 L/min"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source instructs the nurse to provide psychological support to a client who feels claustrophobic with the mask.  Why A, C wrong: Abrupt removal or switching is not the indicated first response; the patient still needs the prescribed oxygen.  Why D wrong: 30 L/min exceeds the device range and is not the indicated action."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"The NRBM is noted to be dangerous if which event occurs?","options":["The humidity bottle is full","The oxygen supply is exhausted/interrupted","The patient is in semi-Fowler's position","The flow rate reaches 12 L/min"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note states this can be dangerous if the O2 supply is exhausted/interrupted (the sealed mask with valves can lead to suffocation without inflow).  Why A, C, D wrong: A full humidity bottle, semi-Fowler's position, and 12 L/min flow are normal/expected, not the danger described."},{"section":"Batch 042 · Hot & cold application, Oxygen therapy fundamentals…","question":"Why is the non-rebreather mask used only for a SHORT time in severely hypoxic patients?","options":["Because it delivers too little oxygen","Because of the dry gas delivered","Because it requires surgical insertion","Because it lowers FiO2 over time"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The NRBM is used in severely hypoxic patients for a short time because of the dry gas it delivers.  Why A wrong: It delivers a high FiO2 (60–100%).  Why C wrong: It is a mask, requiring no surgical insertion.  Why D wrong: It maintains a high FiO2, not a falling one."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"A Venturi mask delivers a precise, preset oxygen concentration within which range of delivered oxygen concentration and FiO2?","options":["1–6 L/min, FiO2 24–28%","2–15 L/m, FiO2 24–60%","8–12 L/m, FiO2 60–80%","10–15 L/m, FiO2 80–90%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the Venturi mask delivered oxygen concentration is 2–15 L/m, FiO2 24–60%.  Why A wrong: Understates both the flow range and FiO2 range given.  Why C wrong: 60–80% FiO2 corresponds to the oxygen hood, not the Venturi mask.  Why D wrong: 80–90% FiO2 at 10–15 L/m is the oxygen hood specification."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"What is the principle by which a Venturi mask achieves a desired FiO2?","options":["It uses 100% pure oxygen under pressure","It uses a small valve to mix oxygen and ambient air to achieve a desired FiO2","It rebreathes the patient's exhaled carbon dioxide","It humidifies oxygen before delivery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A Venturi mask uses a small valve to mix oxygen and ambient air to achieve a desired FiO2, delivering precise/preset or exact desired concentration of oxygen to the client.  Why A wrong: 100% pure pressurised oxygen describes hyperbaric oxygen therapy (HBOT).  Why C wrong: Rebreathing CO2 is not the Venturi mechanism.  Why D wrong: Humidification is not how the precise FiO2 is set."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"The Venturi valves are colour coded; each colour-coded valve is sized to do what?","options":["Provide a different FiO2 for a specified flow rate","Provide the same FiO2 at any flow rate","Filter exhaled air only","Warm the inspired gas"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states Venturi valves are colour coded, each sized to provide a different FiO2 for a specified flow rate.  Why B wrong: Each valve gives a different (not the same) FiO2.  Why C wrong: Not the function of the colour-coded valve.  Why D wrong: The valve sets oxygen concentration, not temperature."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"With a Venturi mask, oxygen concentration depends on which factors?","options":["O2 saturation and selected colour coding","Patient height and weight","Room humidity alone","Respiratory rate only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states oxygen concentration depends on O2 saturation and selected colour coding.  Why B, C, D wrong: These factors are not listed as determinants of the Venturi mask oxygen concentration in the source."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"A Venturi mask is the choice of oxygen therapy for which type of patient where precise O2 prescription is needed?","options":["COPD patients","Pediatric burn patients","Post-operative cardiac patients","Trauma patients in shock"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the Venturi mask is the choice of oxygen therapy for COPD patients where precise O2 prescription is needed, and is commonly used for respiratory failure patients or COPD patients.  Why B, C, D wrong: The source specifically names respiratory failure and COPD patients, not these groups."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"A nurse applying a Venturi mask should ensure which of the following for proper function?","options":["The reservoir bag is fully inflated at all times","The mask fits securely over the nose and mouth and is free of kinks","The mask is removed during meals only","The valve is taped over to prevent air entrainment"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to ensure the mask fits securely over the nose and mouth and is free of kinks.  Why A wrong: The Venturi mask has no reservoir bag (that is the non-rebreather).  Why C wrong: Not stated as a Venturi-specific care point.  Why D wrong: Taping over the valve would block the air entrainment that the Venturi mask relies on."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"What is the delivered oxygen concentration (flow) of a T-piece?","options":["2–4 L/m","8–12 L/m","10–15 L/m","15 L/m"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists the T-piece delivered oxygen concentration as 8–12 L/m.  Why A, C, D wrong: These flows correspond to other devices (e.g., 10–15 L/m for the oxygen hood, 15 L/m for BVM/AMBU)."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"A T-piece is also known by which name?","options":["Tracheostomy collar / T-bar","Reservoir mask","Venturi collar","Bain circuit"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the T-piece is also known as a tracheostomy collar, T-bar.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are unrelated device names not given for the T-piece."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"The T-bar can be used to deliver the desired amount of oxygen to which patient, and is used for which process?","options":["A patient with tracheostomy; for weaning from a mechanical ventilator on trial basis","A COPD patient; for long-term home oxygen","A neonate in an incubator; for surfactant delivery","A burn patient; for fluid resuscitation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the T-bar can deliver the desired amount of oxygen to a patient with tracheostomy and is used for weaning from a mechanical ventilator on trial basis.  Why B, C, D wrong: These uses are not attributed to the T-piece in the source."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"What is the delivered oxygen concentration and FiO2 range of an oxygen hood?","options":["8–12 L/m, FiO2 24–60%","10–15 L/m, FiO2 80–90%","2–4 L/m, FiO2 24–28%","15 L/m, FiO2 >95%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists oxygen hood as 10–15 L/m, FiO2 80–90%.  Why A wrong: That FiO2 range is the Venturi mask.  Why C wrong: Low-flow, low-FiO2 figures not given for the hood.  Why D wrong: FiO2 >95% corresponds to the non-rebreather/anaesthesia bag."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"Which statement best describes the oxygen hood?","options":["It is a plastic dome that fits in close to an infant hood","It is a self-inflating bag","It is a tracheostomy collar","It is a colour-coded valve mask"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source describes the oxygen hood as a plastic dome that fits in close to an infant hood.  Why B, C, D wrong: These describe the AMBU bag, T-piece, and Venturi mask respectively."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"The oxygen hood is used if the patient has injury where, and for which patient groups during swine flu, if needed?","options":["Face, nose, head injury; for pre-term babies, child, infants, and adult","Chest only; for adults exclusively","Lower limb; for geriatric patients","Abdomen; for post-surgical patients"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the hood is used if a patient has face, nose, head injury and for pre-term babies, child, infants and adult during swine flu if needed.  Why B, C, D wrong: These injury sites and patient groups are not specified for the hood."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"What is the delivered oxygen concentration and FiO2 of an anaesthesia bag with non-rebreather mask?","options":["10–12 L/m or FiO2 >95%","2–15 L/m, FiO2 24–60%","8–12 L/m","15 L/m, FiO2 80–90%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists the anaesthesia bag with non-rebreather mask as 10–12 L/m or FiO2 >95%.  Why B wrong: That is the Venturi mask.  Why C wrong: That is the T-piece.  Why D wrong: 80–90% is the oxygen hood FiO2."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"The anaesthesia bag with non-rebreather mask is usually used for which purpose?","options":["Patient transportation from one department to another or when high flow oxygen therapy is needed","Long-term home oxygen for COPD","Continuous nebulizer therapy","Weaning from a tracheostomy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states it is used for usually patient transportation from one department to another or when high flow oxygen therapy is needed.  Why B, C, D wrong: These purposes match other devices, not the anaesthesia/non-rebreather bag."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"In Bag Valve Mask (BVM) ventilation, \"AKA\" / resuscitation bag refers to which device?","options":["AMBU bag","Venturi valve","Bain circuit","Oxygen hood"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states BVM/resuscitation bag delivers oxygen by AMBU bag with reservoir bag.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are different oxygen devices, not the resuscitation bag."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"What is the delivered oxygen concentration and FiO2 when oxygen is delivered by BVM (AMBU bag) ventilation?","options":["15 L/m, FiO2 >95%","8–12 L/m, FiO2 60%","2–4 L/m, FiO2 24%","6 L/m, FiO2 31%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states BVM ventilation delivered oxygen concentration is 15 L/m, FiO2 >95%, and for pack push 500–800 ml oxygen delivered by BVM ventilation.  Why B, C, D wrong: These figures do not match the BVM specification in the source."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"What is the delivered oxygen concentration and FiO2 of a face tent?","options":["10–15 L/m, FiO2 30–40%","2–4 L/m, FiO2 24%","15 L/m, FiO2 >95%","8–12 L/m, FiO2 60%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists the face tent as 10–15 L/m, FiO2 30–40%.  Why B, C, D wrong: These figures correspond to other devices, not the face tent."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"A face tent is especially useful in which clinical situations?","options":["Patients with facial trauma, recent mouth surgery, face and neck burns, and those for whom a tight-fitting mask cannot be used because the masks are loose fitting","Patients requiring high-pressure pure oxygen","Patients requiring tracheostomy weaning","Neonates requiring surfactant"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states face tents are loose fitting and may be useful in people who do not tolerate a tight-fitting mask, e.g., facial trauma, recent mouth surgery, face and neck burns.  Why B wrong: High-pressure pure oxygen describes HBOT.  Why C wrong: Tracheostomy weaning is the T-piece role.  Why D wrong: Surfactant delivery is not a face tent indication."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"What does the abbreviation AMBU stand for?","options":["Artificial Manual Breathing Unit","Assisted Mechanical Bag Unit","Auto Mask Breathing Utility","Airway Manual Bag Use"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source gives the full form of AMBU as Artificial Manual Breathing Unit (company name).  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not the recognized full form of AMBU."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"The AMBU bag is also known by which name?","options":["Self-inflating bag or manual resuscitator","Reservoir mask","Venturi bag","Mapleson bag"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states AMBU is also known as self-inflating bag or manual resuscitator.  Why B, C, D wrong: These names are not used for the AMBU bag in the source."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"Without a source of oxygen (room air), an AMBU bag delivers approximately what percentage of oxygen?","options":["21%","45%","85%","100%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states that without a source of oxygen (room air) the AMBU bag delivers only 21% FiO2.  Why B wrong: 45% is delivered when connected to a source of oxygen (10–15 L/m).  Why C wrong: 85% is delivered when a reservoir bag is added.  Why D wrong: 100% is not the figure given."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"When an AMBU bag is connected to a source of oxygen (10–15 L/m), it delivers approximately what FiO2?","options":["21%","45%","85%","60%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states when connected to a source of oxygen (10–15 L/m) the AMBU bag delivers 45% FiO2.  Why A wrong: 21% is room-air delivery.  Why C wrong: 85% requires an added reservoir bag.  Why D wrong: 60% is not stated for this configuration."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"If a reservoir bag is added to the AMBU bag, oxygen delivery can increase up to approximately what percentage?","options":["21%","45%","85%","95%"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states if a reservoir bag is added, delivery can increase up to 85% oxygen to the client.  Why A, B wrong: 21% and 45% are the room-air and oxygen-source values without a reservoir bag.  Why D wrong: 95% is not the figure given for the reservoir-bag configuration."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"What is the capacity of an AMBU bag in an adult, and how much is delivered when pushed by both hands?","options":["1200–1400 ml capacity; 1000 ml delivered if pushed by both hands","750 ml capacity; 600 ml delivered","250–300 ml capacity; 200 ml delivered","500–800 ml capacity; 400 ml delivered"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states capacity of AMBU bag in adult is 1200–1400 ml; it delivers 1000 ml if pushing by both hands and 600–800 ml if pushing by one hand.  Why B wrong: 750 ml is the capacity in a child.  Why C wrong: 250–300 ml is the capacity in a newborn/infant.  Why D wrong: These are BVM pack-push values, not adult AMBU capacity."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"What is the capacity of an AMBU bag in a child?","options":["250 ml","500 ml","750 ml","1200 ml"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the capacity of the AMBU bag in a child is 750 ml.  Why A wrong: 250–300 ml is the newborn/infant capacity.  Why B wrong: 500 ml is not the stated child capacity.  Why D wrong: 1200–1400 ml is the adult capacity."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"What is the capacity of an AMBU bag in a newborn or infant?","options":["250–300 ml","750 ml","1000 ml","1200–1400 ml"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states capacity of AMBU bag in newborn or infants is 250–300 ml.  Why B wrong: 750 ml is the child capacity.  Why C wrong: 1000 ml is the amount delivered in an adult by two-hand push.  Why D wrong: 1200–1400 ml is the adult capacity."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"How is an AMBU bag sterilised, according to the source?","options":["By gas sterilisation with ETO (ethylene trioxide) and 2% glutaraldehyde for 30 min","By autoclaving at 121°C for 15 min","By boiling for 10 min","By dry heat at 160°C for 1 hour"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states AMBU bag sterilised by gas sterilization of ETO (ethylene trioxide) and 2% glutaraldehyde with 30 min.  Why B, C, D wrong: These methods are not the one specified in the source for the AMBU bag."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"Match the oxygen delivery device to its appearance. A device that is a colour-coded mask using a small valve to entrain ambient air is best identified as which?","options":["Nasal cannula","Simple face mask","Venturi mask","Face tent"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The Venturi mask is depicted (p-0372) with its colour-coded valve, matching the entrainment mechanism described on p-0371.  Why A wrong: A nasal cannula has prongs, not a valve mask.  Why B wrong: A simple face mask has no entrainment valve.  Why D wrong: A face tent is an open, loose-fitting device without a colour-coded valve."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"Which oxygen delivery device shown in the source is specifically designed for delivering high concentrations of humidified oxygen at high flow through the nares?","options":["High flow nasal cannula","Simple face mask","Oxygen hood","T-piece"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The high flow nasal cannula is one of the labelled devices on p-0372.  Why B wrong: A simple face mask is a low-flow device.  Why C wrong: The oxygen hood is a dome device, not a nasal device.  Why D wrong: A T-piece is used with a tracheostomy/ET tube, not the nares."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"Which device shown in the source is a non-invasive positive pressure ventilation interface labelled \"Bipap\"?","options":["Bipap mask","Nasal prongs","Oxymizer","Nasal catheter"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Bipap is one of the labelled devices/interfaces on p-0372.  Why B, C, D wrong: Nasal prongs, oxymizer and nasal catheter are separate labelled low-flow devices, not the Bipap interface."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"CNS oxygen toxicity is also known by which eponym?","options":["Paul Bert effect","Lorraine Smith effect","Bohr effect","Haldane effect"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states CNS toxicity is also known as the Paul Bert effect.  Why B wrong: Lorraine Smith effect is pulmonary (chronic) toxicity.  Why C, D wrong: Bohr and Haldane effects describe oxygen–CO2 dissociation, not oxygen toxicity."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"Acute oxygen toxicity has a predominant effect on which system and may manifest as which symptoms?","options":["CNS effect; nausea, vertigo, convulsions","Pulmonary effect; cough and dyspnea","Cardiac effect; bradycardia","Renal effect; oliguria"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states acute oxygen toxicity has a predominant CNS effect and may manifest as nausea, vertigo, convulsions.  Why B wrong: Pulmonary involvement is the chronic (Lorraine Smith) effect.  Why C, D wrong: Cardiac and renal manifestations are not described for acute oxygen toxicity."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"Pulmonary (chronic) oxygen toxicity is also known by which eponym?","options":["Paul Bert effect","Lorraine Smith effect","Cushing effect","Mendelson effect"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states pulmonary toxicity is also known as the Lorraine Smith effect.  Why A wrong: Paul Bert effect is the CNS (acute) effect.  Why C, D wrong: Cushing and Mendelson are unrelated phenomena."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"Chronic oxygen toxicity causes damage to which structures and inactivation of which substance?","options":["Pulmonary epithelium and vascular endothelium; inactivation of surfactant","Renal tubules; inactivation of renin","Cardiac myocytes; inactivation of troponin","Hepatocytes; inactivation of albumin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states oxygen toxicity causes damage to pulmonary epithelium and vascular endothelium, inactivation of surfactant, interstitial edema and pulmonary fibrosis.  Why B, C, D wrong: These organs and substances are not implicated in oxygen toxicity per the source."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"Chronic pulmonary oxygen toxicity may result in all of the following EXCEPT:","options":["Decreased lung compliance","Hypoventilation and decreased vital capacity","Absorption atelectasis, ARDS, tracheobronchitis","Increased surfactant production"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct (EXCEPT): The source lists decreased lung compliance, hypoventilation, decreased vital capacity, absorption atelectasis, ARDS, tracheobronchitis, pulmonary interstitial fibrosis and pulmonary edema — surfactant is inactivated (decreased), not increased.  Why A, B, C wrong: These are all listed consequences of chronic oxygen toxicity."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"The ocular effect of oxygen toxicity in premature infants is retrolental fibroplasia, which is also known by which name?","options":["Retinopathy of prematurity (ROP)","Macular degeneration","Diabetic retinopathy","Retinitis pigmentosa"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the ocular effect is also known as retrolental fibroplasia (Retinopathy of prematurity / ROP), with disturbance in retinal vascularisation in premature infants.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are unrelated retinal conditions not caused by oxygen toxicity in premature infants."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"To prevent retrolental fibroplasia/ROP, oxygen therapy in neonates should be:","options":["Avoided entirely","Avoid high flow oxygen to neonates and give only low flow oxygen","Given at 100% FiO2 continuously","Delivered only by oxygen hood at high flow"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to prevent (ROP) avoid high flow oxygen to neonates and give only low flow oxygen.  Why A wrong: Oxygen is not avoided entirely; low flow is given.  Why C, D wrong: High FiO2/high flow are precisely what should be avoided in neonates."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"A low concentration of oxygen (usually 1 to 2 L/m) is preferred for COPD patients for which reason?","options":["Because in COPD patients a low PaO2 stimulates breathing (hypoxic drive); a high PaCO2 level may give a false stimulus","Because COPD patients cannot tolerate any oxygen","Because high oxygen improves their CO2 retention","Because low oxygen prevents oxygen toxicity in all adults"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states in COPD patients a low PaO2 stimulates breathing (hypoxic drive); a high PaCO2 level may give a false stimulus, so a low concentration is most preferable.  Why B wrong: COPD patients do receive oxygen, just at low flow.  Why C wrong: High oxygen does not improve CO2 retention — it can worsen ventilation.  Why D wrong: The rationale relates specifically to the COPD hypoxic drive, not generic adult toxicity."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"In COPD patients the respiratory drive (stimulus to breathe) is primarily provided by which factor, in contrast to normal healthy individuals?","options":["Low oxygen level in the blood (in COPD); high CO2 level (in normal individuals)","High oxygen level in the blood (in COPD); low CO2 level (in normal individuals)","Low CO2 level in both","High oxygen level in both"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the respiratory stimulus in COPD clients is low oxygen level in the blood, whereas the respiratory stimulus in normal healthy individuals is high CO2 level in the blood.  Why B, C, D wrong: These reverse or misstate the source's contrast between hypoxic drive (COPD) and hypercapnic/CO2 drive (normal)."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"In a newborn baby, oxygen concentration should be maintained lower to prevent which condition, which is a complication that occurs in newborns receiving high flow oxygen?","options":["Retrolental fibroplasia (Retinopathy of Prematurity / ROP)","Bronchiolitis","Meconium aspiration","Pneumothorax"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states in newborn babies oxygen concentration should be maintained lower to prevent retrolental fibroplasia, also known as retinopathy of prematurity (ROP), which occurs in newborns receiving high flow oxygen.  Why B, C, D wrong: These conditions are not the high-flow-oxygen complication named in the source."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"A mixture of helium and oxygen that has low gas density and is often used to treat upper airway obstruction in children with croup is known as:","options":["Heliox","Nitrous oxide","Entonox","Carbogen"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source describes a mixture of helium and oxygen with low gas density, often used to treat upper airway obstruction such as in children with croup (Heliox).  Why B wrong: Nitrous oxide (N2O) is laughing gas, a separate item in the source.  Why C, D wrong: Entonox and carbogen are not the helium-oxygen mixture described."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"Nitrous oxide (N2O) is also known by which common name?","options":["Laughing gas","Sweet gas","Sleeping gas","Marsh gas"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states nitrous oxide (N2O) is also known as laughing gas.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not the name given for nitrous oxide in the source."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"During oxygen therapy, nursing care includes verifying which details before administration?","options":["The bed number and identification of the patient","Only the patient's age","Only the oxygen cylinder colour","The patient's blood group"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists verifying the bed number and identification of the patient as a nursing care point during oxygen therapy.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not the verification steps listed for oxygen therapy."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"Which of the following are nursing care measures during oxygen therapy? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Check the diagnosis and the need for oxygen therapy","Verify the doctor's order for specific precautions regarding the movement and positioning of the patient","Check the doctors order: method of administration, flow rate and any specific considerations","Monitor vital signs, especially oxygen saturation, respiratory rate, flow rate, humidification, target oxygen saturation","Discontinue oxygen abruptly without monitoring"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists checking diagnosis/need, verifying the order for movement/positioning precautions, checking the order for method/flow rate/considerations, and monitoring vital signs including oxygen saturation, flow rate, humidification and target saturation.  Why E wrong: Abrupt discontinuation without monitoring is not a recommended nursing measure and contradicts the monitoring guidance."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"When applying a nasal cannula or face mask during oxygen therapy, the nurse should ensure:","options":["Proper fit and adjust for patient comfort","The device is taped tightly so it cannot move","Maximum flow rate regardless of order","The humidifier is turned off"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states for nasal cannula/face mask, ensure proper fit, adjust for comfort.  Why B wrong: The device should be comfortable, not taped uncomfortably tight.  Why C wrong: Flow must follow the order, not be maximal.  Why D wrong: Humidification is part of monitoring; turning it off is not advised."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"During supplemental oxygen therapy the nurse should monitor the patient's oxygenation status by observing which of the following? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Oxygen saturation","Vital signs","Lung sounds","Skin for colour changes","Level of consciousness"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists monitoring response to supplemental oxygen and worsening oxygenation status by observing oxygen saturation, vital signs, lung sounds, skin for colour changes, and level of consciousness.  All options are explicitly listed in the source."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"When monitoring for signs of hypoxia, which of the following is a LATE sign?","options":["Restlessness","SpO2 <92%","Agitation, confusion and lethargy","Cyanosis"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists cyanosis as a late sign of hypoxia.  Why A wrong: Restlessness is listed (an early sign).  Why B wrong: SpO2 <92% is listed as a monitoring parameter, not the late sign.  Why C wrong: Agitation, confusion and lethargy, and pallor/diaphoresis/tachycardia are listed but cyanosis is specifically the late sign."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"Which of the following are listed as signs of hypoxia to monitor for? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Restlessness","SpO2 <92%","Agitation, confusion and lethargy","Pallor, diaphoresis and tachycardia","Cyanosis (late sign)"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists restlessness, SpO2 <92%, agitation/confusion/lethargy, pallor/diaphoresis/tachycardia, and cyanosis (late sign) as signs of hypoxia to monitor.  All options are listed in the source."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"Why should the nurse remove saliva secretions from the patient's mouth during oxygen therapy?","options":["To prevent aspiration","To improve oxygen flow rate","To prevent oxygen toxicity","To reduce humidification"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to remove saliva secretion from mouth during therapy to prevent aspiration.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not the stated reason for removing oral secretions."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"Why must safety precautions be maintained during oxygen therapy?","options":["Because oxygen supports combustion or flaming","Because oxygen is explosive on its own","Because oxygen is toxic to skin on contact","Because oxygen causes electrical short circuits"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to maintain safety precaution during oxygen therapy because oxygen supports combustion or flaming.  Why B wrong: Oxygen itself does not explode, but it supports combustion.  Why C, D wrong: These are not the stated rationale; skin care/padding is for pressure points, not chemical toxicity."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"During oxygen therapy, the nurse should monitor pressure points for skin breakdown and provide which intervention?","options":["Skin care and padding as needed","Tighter strap application","Increased oxygen flow","Cold compresses"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states monitor pressure points for skin breakdown; provide skin care, padding as needed.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not the stated interventions for pressure points."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"For a patient on oxygen therapy who is anxious about a confined mask, the nurse should:","options":["Offer emotional support and reassurance for claustrophobia","Discontinue oxygen immediately","Sedate the patient routinely","Apply a tighter mask"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to offer emotional support and reassurance for claustrophobia.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not the stated nursing response to claustrophobia during oxygen therapy."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"Which of the following are oxygen therapy fire safety measures? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Keep the oxygen at least five feet away from any heat source","Avoid using equipment that could emit a spark","Avoid wearing synthetic clothing due to static electricity","Avoid use of flammable liquids","\"No smoking\" sign should hang at the entrance of the gate and a fire extinguisher should be readily available"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists all of these as safety measures: keep oxygen at least five feet from heat source, avoid spark-emitting equipment, avoid synthetic clothing (static electricity), avoid flammable liquids, hang a \"No smoking\" sign at the gate, and keep a fire extinguisher readily available.  All options are explicitly listed."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"Apart from safety, the nurse should also be aware that all staff should know about which equipment in the oxygen therapy area?","options":["The location of fire extinguishers","The location of the pharmacy","The patient's diet chart","The visiting hours board"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states \"Aware all staff about use of fire extinguishers.\"  Why B, C, D wrong: These are unrelated to the oxygen-therapy fire safety staff awareness point."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"In the Venturi mask colour coding (\"Baby of White Or Y R Green\" trick), which colour valve delivers 24% FiO2 at a flow rate of 2 L/min?","options":["Blue","White","Orange","Green"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source colour-coding table lists Blue = 2 L/min = 24% FiO2.  Why B wrong: White = 4 L/min = 28%.  Why C wrong: Orange = 6 L/min = 31%.  Why D wrong: Green = 15 L/min = 60%."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"Per the Venturi mask colour coding table, which colour valve delivers 28% FiO2 at a flow rate of 4 L/min?","options":["Blue","White","Yellow","Red"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists White = 4 L/min = 28% FiO2.  Why A wrong: Blue = 2 L/min = 24%.  Why C wrong: Yellow = 8 L/min = 35%.  Why D wrong: Red = 10 L/min = 40%."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"Per the Venturi mask colour coding table, the Orange valve delivers what FiO2 at 6 L/min?","options":["24%","28%","31%","35%"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists Orange = 6 L/min = 31% FiO2.  Why A, B, D wrong: 24% (Blue), 28% (White) and 35% (Yellow) correspond to other valves."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"Per the Venturi mask colour coding table, the Yellow valve delivers what FiO2 at 8 L/min?","options":["31%","35%","40%","60%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists Yellow = 8 L/min = 35% FiO2.  Why A wrong: 31% is Orange (6 L/min).  Why C wrong: 40% is Red (10 L/min).  Why D wrong: 60% is Green (15 L/min)."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"Per the Venturi mask colour coding table, the Red valve delivers what FiO2 at 10 L/min?","options":["35%","40%","60%","28%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists Red = 10 L/min = 40% FiO2.  Why A wrong: 35% is Yellow (8 L/min).  Why C wrong: 60% is Green (15 L/min).  Why D wrong: 28% is White (4 L/min)."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"Per the Venturi mask colour coding table, the Green valve delivers what FiO2 at 15 L/min?","options":["40%","50%","60%","35%"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists Green = 15 L/min = 60% FiO2.  Why A wrong: 40% is Red (10 L/min).  Why B wrong: 50% is not listed in the table.  Why D wrong: 35% is Yellow (8 L/min)."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"Hyperbaric oxygen therapy (HBOT) involves breathing what type of oxygen in what kind of environment?","options":["Pure oxygen in a pressurised environment","Room air at normal pressure","Helium-oxygen mixture in an open tent","Nitrous oxide in a sealed chamber"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states HBOT therapy involves breathing pure oxygen in a pressurised environment, and is a high flow oxygen therapy delivering 100% pure oxygen.  Why B, C, D wrong: These do not match HBOT, which uses 100% oxygen under pressure."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"HBOT is described as which kind of oxygen-delivery method?","options":["High flow oxygen therapy delivering 100% pure oxygen","Low flow oxygen therapy delivering 24% oxygen","Variable performance device","Passive diffusion device"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states HBOT is a high flow oxygen therapy, 100% pure oxygen delivering method.  Why B, C, D wrong: HBOT is a high-flow, 100% oxygen method, not low-flow or variable-performance."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"HBOT is used for numerous medical conditions, including which of the following?","options":["Carbon monoxide poisoning, gangrene, wounds that won't heal, and infections in which tissues are starved for oxygen","Simple uncomplicated viral infections","Stable hypertension","Uncomplicated migraine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states HBOT is used for carbon monoxide poisoning, gangrene, wounds that won't heal, and infections in which tissues are starved for oxygen.  Why B, C, D wrong: These conditions are not listed as HBOT indications."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"Hyperbaric oxygen (100% O2) is an antidote for poisoning by which agent?","options":["Carbon monoxide (CO)","Cyanide","Organophosphate","Paracetamol"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source notes hyperbaric oxygen (100% O2) is an antidote of carbon monoxide poisoning (CO).  Why B, C, D wrong: These poisonings have their own antidotes and are not the HBOT note in the source."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"Which of the following are indications for HBOT? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Air gas embolism and decompression sickness","Carbon monoxide poisoning","Severe anemia due to exsanguination","Decubitus ulcer and diabetic food (foot) ulcer","Routine wound dressing of a clean surgical incision"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists indications of HBOT as air gas embolism and decompression sickness, carbon monoxide poisoning, severe anemia due to exsanguination, and decubitus ulcer/diabetic foot.  Why E wrong: Routine clean surgical dressing is not listed as an HBOT indication."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"Which of the following is an ABSOLUTE contraindication to HBOT?","options":["Untreated pneumothorax","Diabetic foot ulcer","Carbon monoxide poisoning","Decompression sickness"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists absolute contraindication as untreated pneumothorax (and use of doxorubicin in last 7 days).  Why B, C, D wrong: Diabetic foot, CO poisoning, and decompression sickness are indications, not contraindications."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"Besides untreated pneumothorax, which other condition is listed as an absolute contraindication to HBOT?","options":["Use of doxorubicin in the last 7 days","History of asthma","Recent appendectomy","Mild anemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists use of doxorubicin in last 7 days as an absolute contraindication to HBOT.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not listed as absolute contraindications."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"The breathing circuit or system used to deliver oxygen, remove carbon dioxide, and deliver inhalational anaesthetic agents is also known as which of the following?","options":["Mapleson breathing system and Bain circuit","Venturi system","AMBU system","T-piece system only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the breathing circuit/system is also known as the Mapleson breathing system and Bain circuit, a medical device used to deliver oxygen, remove carbon dioxide, and deliver inhalational anaesthetic agents.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are separate oxygen devices, not the breathing circuit described."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"In the Mapleson classification of breathing circuits, Mapleson-A is also known as which circuit?","options":["Magill circuit","Water's circuit","Bain circuit","Jackson-Rees circuit"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states Mapleson-A is also known as Magill circuit.  Why B wrong: Water's circuit is Mapleson-C.  Why C wrong: Bain circuit is Mapleson-D.  Why D wrong: Jackson-Rees is not the name given in the source."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"In the Mapleson classification, Mapleson-C is also known as which circuit?","options":["Magill circuit","Water's circuit","Bain circuit","Mapleson circuit"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states Mapleson-C is also known as Water's circuit.  Why A wrong: Magill circuit is Mapleson-A.  Why C wrong: Bain circuit is Mapleson-D.  Why D wrong: Not a recognized alternative name in the source."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"In the Mapleson classification, Mapleson-D is also known as which circuit?","options":["Magill circuit","Water's circuit","Bain circuit","Ayre's T-piece"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states Mapleson-D is also known as Bain circuit.  Why A wrong: Magill circuit is Mapleson-A.  Why B wrong: Water's circuit is Mapleson-C.  Why D wrong: Ayre's T-piece is not the name given in the source."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"(Recall the NORCET) Minimum oxygen wastage occurs by which of the following oxygen administration methods?","options":["Nasal cannula","Non-rebreather (NRBM)","Simple face mask","Venturi mask"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source recall box gives the right answer as B, Non-rebreather (NRBM) — minimum oxygen wastage occurs with the non-rebreather mask, as its reservoir bag and one-way valves conserve oxygen.  Why A, C, D wrong: Nasal cannula, simple face mask, and Venturi mask entrain or lose more oxygen and are not keyed as the answer."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"(Recall the NORCET) A patient is brought to the emergency department after a road traffic accident (RTA). Which oxygen administration method is commonly preferably used in an RTA patient?","options":["Simple face mask","Nasal catheter","Venturi mask","Re-breather mask"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source recall box gives the right answer as A, Simple face mask, as the commonly preferred oxygen administration method in an RTA patient.  Why B, C, D wrong: These options are not keyed as the answer in the source."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"(Recall the NORCET) A patient is getting oxygen from the depicted device. What oxygen concentration is given through this device?","options":["60%","40%","35%","30%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source recall box gives the right answer as B, 40%, for the oxygen concentration delivered by the depicted device.  Why A, C, D wrong: 60%, 35% and 30% are not keyed as the answer in the source."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"A wound is best defined as which of the following?","options":["A disruption to the integrity of the skin that leaves the body vulnerable to pain and infection","A bruise without skin break","A localized swelling only","An area of skin discoloration only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines a wound as a disruption to the integrity of the skin that leaves the body vulnerable to pain and infection.  Why B, C, D wrong: These do not capture the source definition of disrupted skin integrity with vulnerability to pain and infection."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"According to the source, infection is defined as which of the following?","options":["Invasion and growth of microorganisms in the body","Any redness of the skin","Accumulation of fluid in tissues","Death of tissue"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states infection is invasion and growth of microorganisms in the body.  Why B, C, D wrong: These describe other phenomena (erythema, edema, necrosis), not the source definition of infection."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"Per the source, wounds can be classified as open or closed. An \"open\" wound is one in which:","options":["Skin or mucous membrane is broken and body tissue is exposed","There is no break in the skin","Only the bone is fractured","Only the muscle is bruised"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states wounds can be open (broken skin and exposed body tissue) or closed (when there is damage to tissue under intact skin).  Why B wrong: No break in the skin describes a closed wound.  Why C, D wrong: These describe other injuries, not the open-wound definition."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"The ASEPSIS score is used for what purpose, and what is its range?","options":["Wound surveillance or assessment post-operatively; scores range from 0 to 70","Burn severity assessment; scores range from 0 to 100","Pressure ulcer staging; scores 1 to 4","Pain assessment; scores 0 to 10"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states ASEPSIS scores range from 0 to 70 and are used for wound surveillance or assessment post-operatively.  Why B, C, D wrong: These ranges/purposes do not match the ASEPSIS score described."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"In the ASEPSIS scoring system, what does a score greater than 40 indicate?","options":["Severe wound infection","Normal healing","No infection","Mild inflammation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states in ASEPSIS Score, if score > 40, then it indicates severe wound infection.  Why B, C, D wrong: A score >40 indicates severe infection, not normal/mild/no infection."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"According to etiology, a wound caused by a surgical procedure is classified as which type?","options":["Surgical wound","Penetrating wound","Blunt wound","Burn wound"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines a surgical wound as a wound caused by a surgical procedure.  Why B wrong: Penetrating wounds are caused by penetrating trauma.  Why C wrong: Blunt wounds are caused by blunt trauma.  Why D wrong: Burn wounds are caused by burn injuries."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"A wound caused by penetrating trauma is classified as which type?","options":["Surgical wound","Penetrating wound","Blunt wound","Burn wound"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines a penetrating wound as one caused by penetrating trauma.  Why A, C, D wrong: These are caused by surgical procedure, blunt trauma, and burn injuries respectively."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"A wound caused by blunt trauma is classified as which type?","options":["Surgical wound","Penetrating wound","Blunt wound","Burn wound"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines a blunt wound as one caused by blunt trauma.  Why A, B, D wrong: These are caused by surgical procedure, penetrating trauma, and burn injuries respectively."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"A wound caused by burn injuries is classified as which type?","options":["Surgical wound","Penetrating wound","Blunt wound","Burn wound"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines a burn wound as one caused by burn injuries.  Why A, B, C wrong: These are caused by surgical procedure, penetrating trauma, and blunt trauma respectively."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"According to the duration of the wound, acute wounds are best described as wounds that:","options":["Usually heal in the anticipated time frame","Fail to heal in the anticipated time frame","Always become infected","Never require treatment"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states acute wounds are wounds that usually heal in the anticipated time frame.  Why B wrong: Failing to heal in the anticipated time frame describes chronic wounds.  Why C, D wrong: Not stated for acute wounds."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"What is the typical duration of an acute wound, and give an example?","options":["Immediately to few weeks; e.g., wounds acquired as a result of trauma or an operative procedure","More than 4 weeks; e.g., pressure ulcers","Several months; e.g., venous leg ulcers","Years; e.g., diabetic foot ulcers"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states acute wound duration is immediately to a few weeks, with example wounds acquired as a result of trauma or an operative procedure.  Why B, C, D wrong: These durations/examples correspond to chronic wounds."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"Chronic wounds are best described as wounds that:","options":["Fail to heal in the anticipated time frame and often reoccur","Always heal within one week","Are never related to underlying conditions","Heal immediately after surgery"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states chronic wounds fail to heal in the anticipated time frame and often reoccur.  Why B, C, D wrong: These contradict the chronic-wound definition."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"What is the typical duration of a chronic wound?","options":["Immediately to a few weeks","> 4 weeks to 3 months or more","Less than 24 hours","Exactly 2 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states chronic wound duration is > 4 weeks to 3 months or more.  Why A wrong: That is the acute wound duration.  Why C, D wrong: These durations are not given for chronic wounds."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"Chronic wounds occur as a result of underlying conditions such as which of the following? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Extended pressure on the tissues","Poor circulation","Poor nutrition","A single minor abrasion that heals quickly"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states chronic wounds occur as a result of an underlying condition such as extended pressure on the tissues, poor circulation, or poor nutrition.  Why D wrong: A minor abrasion that heals quickly is acute, not a cause of a chronic wound."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"Which of the following are examples of chronic wounds given in the source? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Pressure ulcers (bed sore)","Venous leg ulcers","Diabetic foot ulcers","A fresh surgical incision"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists pressure ulcers (bed sore), venous leg ulcers, and diabetic foot ulcers as examples of chronic wounds.  Why D wrong: A fresh surgical incision is an acute wound."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"According to status of skin integrity, a \"closed wound\" is one in which:","options":["There is no break in the continuity of skin or mucous membrane, e.g., hematoma, fracture of femur","There is destruction of skin or mucous membrane","The skin is broken and tissue is exposed","There is a perforated viscus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states a closed wound is one in which there is no break in the continuity of skin or mucous membrane, e.g., hematoma, fracture of femur.  Why B, C wrong: These describe an open wound.  Why D wrong: A perforated viscus is a feature of a Class IV dirty/infected wound."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"According to status of skin integrity, an \"open wound\" is one in which:","options":["There is destruction of skin or mucous membrane, thus exposing underlying tissues to the open air, e.g., incision, vein puncture, bites","There is no break in the skin","Only a hematoma is present","Only a fracture is present"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states an open wound is one in which there is destruction of skin or mucous membrane, thus exposing underlying tissues to the open air, e.g., incision, vein puncture, bites.  Why B, C, D wrong: These describe closed wounds (no skin break, hematoma, fracture)."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"In the CDC Guidelines surgical wound classification, a Class I (Clean) wound is characterized by which of the following? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Uninfected operative wounds made under ideal conditions","No inflammation","No entry into respiratory, alimentary, genital, or uninfected urinary tracts","No lapse in sterile technique; primary wound closure; closed drainage","Perforated viscera"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists Class I (Clean) as uninfected operative wounds made under ideal conditions, no inflammation, no entry into respiratory/alimentary/genital/uninfected urinary tracts, no lapse in sterile technique, primary wound closure, and closed drainage.  Why E wrong: Perforated viscera is a feature of Class IV (Dirty/Infected), not Class I."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"In the CDC surgical wound classification, a Class II (Clean-contaminated) wound is characterized by which features? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Entrance into mucosalized tissue under controlled conditions (respiratory, alimentary, genital, or urinary tract)","No unusual contamination by foreign body","No evidence of infection or major break in sterile technique","Retained devitalized tissue with existing clinical infection"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists Class II (Clean-contaminated) as entrance into mucosalized tissue under controlled conditions (respiratory, alimentary, genital, or urinary tract), no unusual contamination by foreign body, and no evidence of infection or major break in sterile technique.  Why D wrong: Retained devitalized tissue with existing clinical infection describes Class IV (Dirty/Infected)."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"In the CDC surgical wound classification, a Class III (Contaminated) wound is characterized by which features? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Open or fresh accidental wounds","Operations with major breaks in sterile technique","Gross spillage from the gastrointestinal tract; any acute, non-purulent inflammation","Made under ideal conditions with no inflammation"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists Class III (Contaminated) as open or fresh accidental wounds, operations with major breaks in sterile technique, gross spillage from the gastrointestinal tract, and any acute non-purulent inflammation.  Why D wrong: \"Ideal conditions with no inflammation\" describes Class I (Clean)."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"In the CDC surgical wound classification, a Class IV (Dirty/Infected) wound is characterized by which features? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Old traumatic wounds with retained devitalized tissue","Existing clinical infection or purulence","Environmental debris; perforated viscera","Closed drainage with primary wound closure"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists Class IV (Dirty/Infected) as old traumatic wounds with retained devitalized tissue, existing clinical infection or purulence, environmental debris, and perforated viscera.  Why D wrong: Closed drainage with primary wound closure is a feature of Class I (Clean)."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"Chapter 17 of the unit covers which topic?","options":["Wounds: Care & Suturing, Surgical Drains and Pressure Sore — Principle, Guidelines and Management","Oxygen therapy only","Intravenous cannulation","Cardiac resuscitation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source header for Chapter 17 reads \"Wounds: Care & Suturing, Surgical Drains and Pressure Sore — Principle, Guidelines and Management.\"  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not the title of Chapter 17."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"Which CDC surgical wound class is associated with primary wound closure and closed drainage?","options":["Class I (Clean)","Class II (Clean-contaminated)","Class III (Contaminated)","Class IV (Dirty/Infected)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists primary wound closure and closed drainage as features of Class I (Clean).  Why B, C, D wrong: These higher classes involve contamination, breaks in technique, or infection, not the ideal closed/clean criteria."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"A wound resulting from gross spillage from the gastrointestinal tract during an operation, with major breaks in sterile technique, would be classified as which CDC class?","options":["Class I (Clean)","Class II (Clean-contaminated)","Class III (Contaminated)","Class IV (Dirty/Infected)"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists gross spillage from the gastrointestinal tract and operations with major breaks in sterile technique under Class III (Contaminated).  Why A, B wrong: Clean and clean-contaminated classes have no major break in technique or unusual contamination.  Why D wrong: Class IV involves pre-existing clinical infection/purulence and retained devitalized tissue, not just fresh GI spillage."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"A pre-term baby requiring oxygen due to face, nose, or head injury during a swine flu outbreak would most appropriately be managed with which oxygen device, delivering 10–15 L/m at FiO2 80–90%?","options":["Oxygen hood","Nasal cannula","Face tent","T-piece"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the oxygen hood (10–15 L/m, FiO2 80–90%) is used for pre-term babies, child, infants and adult, including those with face/nose/head injury, during swine flu if needed.  Why B wrong: Nasal cannula is a low-flow device not specified for this scenario.  Why C wrong: Face tent delivers only 30–40% FiO2.  Why D wrong: The T-piece is for tracheostomy/ventilator weaning."},{"section":"Batch 043 · oxygen delivery devices and their delivered FiO2/flow rates…","question":"A patient on a manual resuscitator (AMBU bag) without supplemental oxygen connected is only receiving room air. Approximately what FiO2 is the patient getting, and what should the nurse do to improve oxygenation?","options":["About 21%; connect to an oxygen source (10–15 L/m for ~45%) and add a reservoir bag (up to ~85%)","About 100%; no change needed","About 60%; reduce the flow","About 35%; remove the reservoir bag"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states without a source of oxygen (room air) the AMBU delivers only 21% FiO2; connecting to a source of oxygen (10–15 L/m) gives 45%, and adding a reservoir bag increases delivery up to 85%.  Why B wrong: Room air via AMBU is ~21%, not 100%.  Why C wrong: 60% is not the room-air figure, and reducing flow would not improve oxygenation.  Why D wrong: Removing a reservoir bag reduces delivered oxygen."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"An incision caused by a clean, sharp-edged object such as a knife, razor, or glass splinter is classified as which type of open wound?","options":["Laceration","Incision","Abrasion","Puncture wound"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: An incision is made by a clean, sharp-edged object such as a knife, razor, or glass splinter, producing clean wound edges.  Why A wrong: A laceration has jagged, irregular edges caused by blunt force tearing tissue.  Why C wrong: An abrasion is a superficial scraping of the epidermis.  Why D wrong: A puncture wound is a deep narrow wound from a pointed object."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"A wound in which the skin and underlying tissues are torn, producing irregular or ragged edges, is termed:","options":["Incision","Avulsion","Laceration","Penetration wound"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Lacerations are irregular wounds caused by blunt force, producing jagged/ragged (irregular) edges.  Why A wrong: Incisions have clean, smooth edges.  Why B wrong: Avulsion is forcible detachment/tearing away of tissue.  Why D wrong: A penetration wound is caused by an object puncturing the skin."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"Which open wound is a superficial wound in which the topmost layer of skin (epidermis) is scraped off, such as one caused by a sliding fall onto a rough surface like asphalt?","options":["Puncture wound","Abrasion (graze)","Contusion","Avulsion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: An abrasion (graze) is a superficial wound where the topmost layer of skin (epidermis) is scraped off, as in a sliding fall on rough surface.  Why A wrong: A puncture is deep and narrow, not superficial.  Why C wrong: A contusion is a closed (bruise) injury, not an open scrape.  Why D wrong: An avulsion involves tissue being forcibly detached."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"An injury in which a body structure is forcibly detached from its normal point of insertion is called a/an:","options":["Laceration","Penetration wound","Avulsion","Puncture wound"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Avulsion is an injury in which a body structure is forcibly detached from its normal point of insertion.  Why A wrong: A laceration is a torn wound with irregular edges, without forcible detachment of a structure.  Why B wrong: A penetration wound is caused by an object puncturing the skin.  Why D wrong: A puncture wound results from a sharp pointed object such as a nail."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"A wound caused by an object puncturing the skin, such as a splinter, nail, or sharp tooth, is best described as a:","options":["Abrasion","Puncture wound","Incision","Gunshot wound"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A puncture wound is caused by an object puncturing the skin, e.g., a splinter, nail, or sharp tooth.  Why A wrong: An abrasion is a superficial scrape.  Why C wrong: An incision is from a clean sharp-edged object producing smooth edges.  Why D wrong: A gunshot wound is caused by a bullet/projectile driving into or through the body."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"A wound caused by a bullet or similar projectile driving into or through the body, often producing both an entry and an exit wound, describes a wound that is generally referred to as:","options":["\"Through-and-through\"","Avulsion","Contusion","Crush injury"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A gunshot wound caused by a bullet/projectile driving into or through the body may produce both an entry and an exit wound and is generally referred to as a \"through-and-through\" wound.  Why B wrong: Avulsion is forcible detachment of a structure.  Why C wrong: A contusion is a closed bruise.  Why D wrong: A crush injury results from extreme force/pressure over time, not a projectile."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"A large burn wound that has had its loose, charred tissue removed, leaving serious hydro-electrolytic and metabolic alterations including fluid loss, electrolyte imbalances, and increased catabolism, is classified as which type of open wound?","options":["Abrasion","Critical wound","Penetration wound","Puncture wound"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A critical wound includes large burns that have had loose tissue removed, causing serious hydro-electrolytic alterations such as fluid loss, electrolyte imbalances, and increased catabolism.  Why A/C/D wrong: Abrasions, penetration wounds, and puncture wounds do not characteristically cause the major fluid/electrolyte/metabolic derangement described."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"In a closed wound, bleeding occurs in the skin to form a discoloration measuring less than 2 mm. This is termed:","options":["Purpura","Ecchymosis","Petechiae","Contusion"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Petechiae are closed-wound hematomas where bleeding into the skin is less than 2 mm.  Why A wrong: Purpura is bleeding into the skin from 1 cm up to 2 cm.  Why B wrong: Ecchymosis is bleeding into the skin greater than 1 cm.  Why D wrong: A contusion (bruise) results from external force or trauma but is not defined by the <2 mm size criterion."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"Bleeding into the skin measuring from 1 cm up to 2 cm is classified as:","options":["Petechiae","Purpura","Ecchymosis","Crush injury"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Purpura is closed-wound bleeding into the skin from 1 cm up to 2 cm.  Why A wrong: Petechiae are less than 2 mm.  Why C wrong: Ecchymosis is bleeding into the skin greater than 1 cm.  Why D wrong: A crush injury is caused by extreme force/pressure, not a size-graded subdermal bleed."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"Bleeding into the skin greater than 1 cm, occurring as a closed wound, is termed:","options":["Petechiae","Purpura","Ecchymosis","Contusion"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Ecchymosis is bleeding into the skin greater than 1 cm.  Why A wrong: Petechiae are <2 mm.  Why B wrong: Purpura is 1 cm up to 2 cm.  Why D wrong: A contusion is a bruise resulting from external force, not specifically defined by the >1 cm criterion."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"A bruise that results from an external force or trauma is best classified as which type of closed wound?","options":["Crush injury","Contusion","Avulsion","Penetration wound"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A contusion (bruise) is a closed wound resulting from external force or trauma.  Why A wrong: A crush injury is caused by a great or extreme amount of force applied over a long period.  Why C/D wrong: Avulsion and penetration are open wounds."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"A wound caused by a great or extreme amount of force applied over a long period of time is classified as a:","options":["Contusion","Crush injury","Laceration","Abrasion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A crush injury is caused by a great or extreme amount of force applied over a long period of time.  Why A wrong: A contusion is from a single external force/trauma, not prolonged extreme pressure.  Why C/D wrong: Lacerations and abrasions are open wounds caused by tearing or scraping."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"According to the Rank and Wakefield classification, wounds that are inflicted by sharp instruments and contain no devitalized tissue, closed primarily with the expectation of quiet primary healing, are termed:","options":["Untidy wounds","Tidy wounds","Critical wounds","Contaminated wounds"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Tidy wounds are inflicted by sharp instruments, contain no devitalized tissue, and are closed primarily with the expectation of quiet primary healing.  Why A wrong: Untidy wounds result from crushing, tearing, avulsion, vascular injury, or burns and contain devitalized tissue.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the Rank and Wakefield category names used here."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"Which of the following are examples of tidy wounds under the Rank and Wakefield classification?","options":["Crush wounds","Burns","Surgical incisions and cuts from glass and knife wounds","Avulsion injuries"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Examples of tidy wounds include surgical incisions and cuts from glass and knife wounds.  Why A/B/D wrong: Crush wounds, burns, and avulsion injuries are untidy wounds containing devitalized tissue."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"Which statement correctly describes the structures and management of tidy wounds?","options":["Fractures are commonly seen","Tendons, arteries, and nerves are commonly injured and divided","They are commonly associated with fractures and should never be closed primarily","Tendons, arteries, and nerves will commonly be injured but the structures are usually divided"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In tidy wounds, tendons, arteries, and nerves are commonly injured, and these structures are usually divided.  Why A wrong: Fractures are NOT seen in tidy wounds.  Why C wrong: Untidy wounds (not tidy) are commonly associated with fractures; tidy wounds CAN be closed primarily.  Why D wrong: It misstates the source; in tidy wounds the structures are usually divided (cleanly), which is what makes the wound tidy."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"Untidy wounds under the Rank and Wakefield classification typically result from all of the following EXCEPT:","options":["Crushing, tearing, avulsion, or vascular injury","Burns","Multiple and irregular skin wounds requiring débridement","Injury by a sharp instrument with no devitalized tissue"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Injury by a sharp instrument with no devitalized tissue describes a TIDY wound, not an untidy one.  Why A wrong: Untidy wounds result from crushing, tearing, avulsion, vascular injury, or burns.  Why B wrong: Burns produce untidy wounds.  Why C wrong: Untidy wounds are multiple and irregular and may need to be débrided."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"Which statement about closure of untidy wounds is correct?","options":["They should always be closed primarily immediately","Such wounds must not be closed primarily; if they are closed, wound healing is unlikely to occur without complications, so they should be allowed to heal by secondary intention","They contain no devitalized tissue and arteries, tendons, and nerves are unlikely to be involved","They are commonly inflicted by sharp instruments"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Untidy wounds must not be closed primarily; if closed, wound healing is unlikely without complications, so they should be allowed to heal by secondary intention.  Why A wrong: Primary immediate closure is contraindicated.  Why C wrong: In untidy wounds arteries, tendons, and nerves may be exposed and may be injured continuously, with multiple skin wounds.  Why D wrong: Untidy wounds result from crush/tear/avulsion/burns, not clean sharp instruments."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"Match the wound characteristic to the correct category. An incised, clean wound from a surgical incision with healthy tissue, in which seldom tissue loss occurs and primary suturing can be done, is a:","options":["Untidy wound","Tidy wound","Critical wound","Penetrating wound"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Tidy wounds are incised, clean, with healthy tissues, seldom tissue loss, and primary suturing can be done (e.g., a surgical incision, knife wound).  Why A wrong: Untidy wounds are crushed/avulsed, contaminated, with devitalized tissue, often tissue loss, and a long period of time to heal (e.g., crushing wounds, burns).  Why C/D wrong: These are not the table categories used in the tidy vs untidy summary."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"In the summary of tidy versus untidy wounds, which set of features belongs to UNTIDY wounds?","options":["Incised, clean, healthy tissues, seldom tissue loss","Crushed or avulsed, contaminated, devitalized tissues, often tissue loss","Primary suturing can be done","Surgical incision such as a knife wound"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Untidy wounds are crushed or avulsed, contaminated, with devitalized tissues and often tissue loss (e.g., crushing wounds, burns).  Why A/C/D wrong: Incised/clean/healthy tissue, seldom tissue loss, primary suturing, and surgical/knife wounds all describe TIDY wounds."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"Wound healing can be divided into how many separate stages?","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The wound healing process can be divided into four separate stages.  Why A/B/D wrong: The text specifies four stages (hemostasis, inflammatory, proliferative, maturation/remodelling)."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"The first stage of wound healing, which occurs immediately after the initial injury, is the:","options":["Inflammatory phase","Hemostasis phase","Proliferative phase","Maturation phase"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The first stage is the hemostasis phase, which occurs immediately after the initial injury.  Why A wrong: The inflammatory phase is the second stage (lasts about 4–6 days).  Why C wrong: The proliferative (granulation) phase is the third stage.  Why D wrong: The maturation/remodelling phase is the fourth and final stage."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"The inflammatory phase of wound healing follows hemostasis and lasts approximately:","options":["1–2 days","4–6 days","1–2 weeks","Months"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The second (inflammatory) phase follows hemostasis and lasts about 4–6 days.  Why A/C/D wrong: These durations do not match the stated 4–6 day inflammatory phase."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"The third stage of wound healing, also known as the granulation phase, is characterized by which of the following processes?","options":["Hemostasis and clot formation","Fibroblastic, regenerative phase in which fibroblasts work and which lasts for several weeks","Initial vasoconstriction only","Final scar remodelling lasting months to years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The third (proliferative/granulation) phase is also known as the fibroblastic, regenerative phase, in which fibroblasts work and which lasts for several weeks.  Why A wrong: Hemostasis and clotting are the first phase.  Why C wrong: Vasoconstriction is part of early hemostasis, not the granulation phase.  Why D wrong: Final remodelling lasting months belongs to the fourth (maturation) phase."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"The fourth phase of wound healing, the maturation or remodelling phase, begins about how long after the injury and may continue for what duration?","options":["Begins about 3 weeks after injury and continues for months to years","Begins immediately and continues for 4–6 days","Begins about 1 week after injury and lasts 2 weeks","Begins after months and lasts a few days"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The maturation/remodelling phase begins about 3 weeks after the injury and may continue for months to years.  Why B/C/D wrong: These durations do not match the stated onset (~3 weeks) and prolonged remodelling (months to years)."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"Which of the following are patient-related factors that affect wound healing and the potential for infection? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Age","Obesity","Underlying illness or disease such as diabetes and immunocompromised condition","The specific organ or tissue injured","Time between injury and treatment"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Patient-related factors include age, obesity, and underlying illness/disease such as diabetes and immunocompromised conditions.  Why D wrong: The specific organ/tissue injured, extent of injury, and nature of injury are WOUND-related factors, not patient-related.  Why E wrong: Time between injury and treatment (shorter interval is better) is a wound-related/local factor, not a patient factor."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"Which of the following are wound-related (rather than patient-related) factors influencing wound healing?","options":["Specific organ or tissue injured, extent of injury, and nature of injury","Patient's age","Obesity","Immunocompromised condition"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Wound-related factors include the specific organ or tissue injured, extent of injury, nature of injury, contamination or infection, and the time between injury and treatment (shorter interval is better).  Why B/C/D wrong: Age, obesity, and immunocompromised condition are patient-related factors."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"Regarding the timing of treatment as a local factor in wound healing, which is true?","options":["A longer interval between injury and treatment is better","A shorter interval between injury and treatment is better","The interval has no effect on outcome","Treatment should always be delayed for several days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A shorter interval between injury and treatment is better for wound healing.  Why A/C/D wrong: A longer delay worsens outcomes; the interval does affect healing; delaying treatment is not advised."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"Which local factors at the wound site influence healing and the potential for infection?","options":["Hemostasis and debridement at the time of closure","Patient's chronic illness only","The patient's body weight","The patient's immune status"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Local factors include hemostasis and debridement at the time of closure.  Why B/C/D wrong: Chronic illness, body weight, and immune status are patient-related factors, not local wound-site factors."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"Healing by primary intention is characterized by which features?","options":["A clean incised wound or surgical wound with limited tissue loss, easily approximated edges, normal healing seen with scar, and wound closed by suturing, staples, adhesive tape, skin grafting, or flap closure","Highly contaminated wound with large tissue loss left open to granulate","A wound left open and closed later by re-epithelialization","A wound treated only with repeated debridement"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Primary intention involves a clean incised/surgical wound with limited tissue loss, edges easily approximated, normal healing with scar, and closure by suturing, staples, adhesive tape, skin grafting, or flap closure.  Why B wrong: Highly contaminated wounds with large tissue loss left open describe secondary intention.  Why C wrong: A wound left open and later closed by re-epithelialization describes secondary intention.  Why D wrong: Repeated debridement alone is not the defining feature of primary intention."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"Healing by secondary intention is best described as:","options":["A clean surgically closed wound with easily approximated edges","A highly contaminated wound with large tissue loss that is left open to granulate, may pack and place drains, and where care promotes granulation and is closed by re-epithelialization","A wound closed within 6 hours of clean injury","A wound infected and closed in 10–14 days after debridement"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Secondary intention involves a highly contaminated wound with large tissue loss, left open to granulate; the surgeon may pack and place a drain; wound care promotes granulation, and it is closed by re-epithelialization.  Why A wrong: That describes primary intention.  Why C wrong: Primary suturing within 6 hours of a clean wound is primary intention.  Why D wrong: Closing an infected wound in 10–14 days after debridement is tertiary (delayed primary) intention/secondary suturing."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"Tertiary intention healing is ALSO known as:","options":["Primary intention","Delayed primary intention","Spontaneous healing","Regenerative healing"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Tertiary intention is also known as delayed primary intention.  Why A wrong: Primary intention is a distinct category (clean wound, immediate closure).  Why C/D wrong: These are not the names given for tertiary intention."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"Which set of features characterizes healing by tertiary (delayed primary) intention?","options":["Clean incised wound with immediate suturing","Contaminated wounds that are treated by repeated debridement, with the wound cleaned and debrided, left open, and then closed after debridement by suturing the skin grafting or flap","A clean wound closed within 6 hours","A wound left permanently open to granulate only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Tertiary intention applies to contaminated wounds treated by repeated debridement; after the wound is cleaned and debrided and left open, it is closed by suturing, skin grafting, or flap.  Why A/C wrong: Immediate clean closure within 6 hours is primary intention.  Why D wrong: A wound left permanently open to granulate describes secondary intention."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"Primary suturing is appropriately performed for a clean wound within how many hours of injury?","options":["Within 6 hours","Within 48 hours","Within 10–14 days","Within 24 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Primary suturing is done for a clean wound within 6 hours.  Why B wrong: 48 hours corresponds to delayed primary suturing.  Why C wrong: 10–14 days corresponds to secondary suturing for infected wounds.  Why D wrong: 24 hours is not the value given."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"Delayed primary suturing is performed within what time frame?","options":["Within 6 hours","Within 48 hours","In 10–14 days","Within 1 hour"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Delayed primary suturing is done for a contaminated wound within 48 hours.  Why A wrong: 6 hours is primary suturing.  Why C wrong: 10–14 days is secondary suturing.  Why D wrong: 1 hour is not the value given."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"Secondary suturing for an infected wound is performed within which time frame?","options":["Within 6 hours","Within 48 hours","In 10–14 days","In 24–48 hours"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Secondary suturing is done for infected wounds in 10–14 days.  Why A wrong: 6 hours is primary suturing.  Why B wrong: 48 hours is delayed primary suturing.  Why D wrong: 24–48 hours is not the value given for secondary suturing."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"Which complication of wound healing occurs due to a break in/failure to maintain sterile technique?","options":["Haemorrhage","Infection","Wound dehiscence","Contracture"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Infection occurs due to a break/failure to maintain sterile technique.  Why A wrong: Haemorrhage is due to a dislodged clot at the wound site or erosion of a blood vessel.  Why C wrong: Wound dehiscence is partial or total separation of wound layers due to excessive stress on a wound that has not healed.  Why D wrong: Contractures and fistula formation are separate complications."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"Wound haemorrhage as a complication of healing is caused by:","options":["Failure of sterile technique","A dislodged clot at the wound site or erosion of a blood vessel","Protrusion of internal organs through the incision","Keloid formation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Haemorrhage is due to a dislodged clot at the wound site or erosion of a blood vessel.  Why A wrong: That describes infection.  Why C wrong: Protrusion of internal organs through the incision is evisceration.  Why D wrong: Keloid is overgranulation/scar tissue formation."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"Partial or total separation of the wound layers due to excessive stress on a wound that has not healed is called:","options":["Evisceration","Wound dehiscence","Contracture","Over granulation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Wound dehiscence is partial or total separation of the wound layers due to excessive stress on a wound that has not healed.  Why A wrong: Evisceration is partial or total separation with protrusion of internal organs through the incision.  Why C wrong: Contracture is shortening/tightening, not layer separation.  Why D wrong: Over granulation of scar tissue is keloid formation."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"Wound evisceration is best defined as:","options":["Failure of sterile technique","Partial or total separation of wound layers with protrusion of internal organs through the incisional area","Erosion of a blood vessel","Overgrowth of scar tissue"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Wound evisceration is partial or total separation of wound layers with protrusion of internal organs through the incisional area.  Why A wrong: That describes infection.  Why C wrong: Erosion of a blood vessel causes haemorrhage.  Why D wrong: Overgrowth of scar tissue describes overgranulation/keloid."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"Overgranulation of scar tissue as a complication of wound healing is also known as:","options":["Contracture","Keloid formation","Fistula","Dehiscence"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Over granulation of scar tissue is keloid formation.  Why A wrong: Contracture is tightening/shortening of tissue.  Why C wrong: A fistula is an abnormal tract; listed with contractures as a separate complication.  Why D wrong: Dehiscence is separation of wound layers."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"A patient develops abnormal communicating tracts and tightening of the healed skin at a previously injured area. These complications of wound healing are:","options":["Haemorrhage and infection","Contractures and fistula formation","Dehiscence and evisceration","Petechiae and purpura"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Contractures and fistula formation are listed as complications of wound healing.  Why A wrong: Haemorrhage and infection are separate complications.  Why C wrong: Dehiscence and evisceration are separate complications.  Why D wrong: Petechiae and purpura are types of closed-wound hematoma, not wound-healing complications."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"The wound infection continuum begins with contamination and, if left unchecked, will progress to systematic infection. How many microbial stages of the wound infection continuum are described?","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: There are four microbial stages of the wound infection continuum: contamination, colonisation, critically colonised, and infected.  Why A/B/D wrong: The source specifies four stages."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"List the four microbial stages of the wound infection continuum in correct order.","options":["Infected → critically colonised → colonisation → contamination","Contamination → colonisation → critically colonised → infected","Colonisation → contamination → infected → critically colonised","Contamination → infected → colonisation → critically colonised"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The four stages are 1. Contamination, 2. Colonisation, 3. Critically colonised, 4. Infected.  Why A/C/D wrong: These reorder the stages incorrectly; the continuum progresses from contamination to infection."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"Along the wound infection continuum, increasing microbial virulence and/or numbers are associated with biofilm. At which stages are NO antimicrobials indicated versus topical antimicrobials required?","options":["No antimicrobials at infection; topical at contamination","No antimicrobials indicated at contamination/colonisation; topical antimicrobials with local infection (spreading infection) and systemic plus topical antimicrobials at systemic infection","Systemic antimicrobials at contamination only","Topical antimicrobials only at the contamination stage"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: At contamination and colonisation no antimicrobials are indicated (vigilance required); local/spreading infection requires topical antimicrobials; systemic infection requires systemic and topical antimicrobials.  Why A/C/D wrong: They misassign the antimicrobial recommendation to the wrong stages of the continuum."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"In the T.I.M.E. principle of wound assessment, the letter \"T\" stands for:","options":["Time between injury and treatment","Tissue type, which is non-viable or deficient","Temperature of the wound","Tensile strength"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the T.I.M.E. principle, T = Tissue type that is non-viable or deficient.  Why A wrong: Time-to-treatment is a separate healing factor, not the \"T\" of T.I.M.E. here.  Why C/D wrong: Temperature and tensile strength are not the components of T.I.M.E."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"In the T.I.M.E. principle of wound assessment, the letter \"I\" represents:","options":["Inflammation only","Infection or Inflammation","Irrigation","Intention"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In T.I.M.E., I = Infection or Inflammation.  Why A wrong: It includes both infection and inflammation, not inflammation alone.  Why C/D wrong: Irrigation and intention are not components of T.I.M.E."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"In the T.I.M.E. principle of wound assessment, \"M\" stands for:","options":["Moisture imbalance","Maceration only","Management","Margin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In T.I.M.E., M = Moisture imbalance.  Why B wrong: Maceration is a consequence of excess moisture but the \"M\" stands for moisture imbalance.  Why C/D wrong: Management and margin are not the T.I.M.E. \"M\" component."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"In the T.I.M.E. principle, \"E\" refers to:","options":["Exudate","Edge, which is not advancing or undermining","Epithelium","Erythema"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In T.I.M.E., E = Edge, which is not advancing or undermining.  Why A/C/D wrong: Exudate, epithelium, and erythema are not the defined \"E\" component."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"According to the principle of wound management, what is the FIRST step the nurse should perform to achieve timely wound healing or prevent wound infection?","options":["Wound cleansing with normal saline 0.9%","Wound assessment: assess condition of the wound","Wound closure (suturing)","Antibiotic prescription targeting infections"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The first step in the principle of wound management is wound assessment — assess the condition of the wound.  Why A wrong: Wound cleansing (clean with normal saline 0.9% to prevent infection) is the second step.  Why C wrong: Wound closure/suturing with timely dressing change to prevent contamination is the third step.  Why D wrong: Antibiotic prescription targeting infections is the fifth/last step."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"In the principle of wound management, wound cleansing to prevent infection is performed using which solution?","options":["Hydrogen peroxide 3%","Normal saline 0.9%","Povidone-iodine 10%","Sterile water with chlorhexidine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Wound cleansing is done by cleaning the wound with normal saline 0.9% to prevent infection.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies normal saline 0.9% (also the preferred solution for wound cleaning per the key objectives section)."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"Place the five steps of the principle of wound management in the correct order.","options":["Cleansing → Assessment → Closure → Appropriate dressing choice → Antibiotic prescription","Assessment → Cleansing → Closure (suturing) and timely dressing change → Appropriate dressing choice → Antibiotic prescription","Antibiotic prescription → Assessment → Cleansing → Closure → Dressing","Closure → Cleansing → Assessment → Antibiotic → Dressing"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The five steps in order are: 1. Wound assessment, 2. Wound cleansing (normal saline 0.9%), 3. Wound closure (suturing) and timely dressing change, 4. Appropriate dressing choice (to encourage healing process), 5. Antibiotic prescription targeting infections.  Why A/C/D wrong: These reorder the steps incorrectly."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"In the RYB (Red-Yellow-Black) wound management system, a RED wound is described as and managed how?","options":["Black, thick eschar firmly adhered; debride necrotic tissue","Pink granulation tissue with normal wound healing; goal is to protect the wound and maintain moisture","Moist, yellow sloughy wound; remove exudates and debris","A dry, intact wound; leave open to air"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the RYB system, RED = pink granulation tissue with normal wound healing; goals are to protect the wound and maintain moisture.  Why A wrong: Black describes thick eschar firmly adhered, managed by debriding necrotic tissue.  Why C wrong: Yellow describes a moist, yellow sloughy wound, managed by removing exudates and debris.  Why D wrong: This is not a category of the RYB system as described."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"In the RYB wound management system, a YELLOW wound and its goal of care are:","options":["Pink granulation; protect wound","Moist, yellow sloughy wound; remove exudates and debris, absorb drainage","Black eschar; debride necrotic tissue","Dry intact eschar; keep dry"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: YELLOW = moist, yellow sloughy wound; goal is to remove exudates and debris and absorb drainage.  Why A wrong: That describes a RED wound.  Why C/D wrong: These describe BLACK (eschar requiring debridement)."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"In the RYB wound management system, a BLACK wound is characterized by and managed by:","options":["Pink granulation tissue; maintain moisture","Black, thick eschar firmly adhered; debride necrotic tissue","Yellow slough; absorb drainage","Healthy epithelialised tissue; leave alone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: BLACK = black, thick eschar firmly adhered; the goal is to debride necrotic tissue.  Why A wrong: Pink granulation that needs moisture is a RED wound.  Why C wrong: Yellow slough/absorb drainage is a YELLOW wound.  Why D wrong: Epithelialised healthy tissue is not the black category."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"Regarding measurement of a wound, the dimensions are typically measured first by which sequence?","options":["Width, then length, then depth","Length, then width, and finally depth","Depth, then length, then width","Circumference only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A wound is typically measured first by its length, then by its width, and finally by its depth.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated length-width-depth order."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"When measuring wound length, which orientation is used relative to the patient's head?","options":["The width is always longer than the length","The length is always from the patient's head to the toe","Measurement is done diagonally","Length is measured side to side"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The length is always from the patient's head to the toe.  Why A wrong: The source does not state width is always longer than length.  Why C/D wrong: The length is taken head-to-toe, not diagonally or side-to-side."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"What is described as the simplest and quickest method of recording wound assessment information, such as a simple ruler method, digital imaging, and others?","options":["A disposable paper ruler","A flexible probe","A sterile cotton swab","A photographic plate only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A disposable paper ruler is the simplest and quickest method of measuring wound length and width.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not identified as the simplest/quickest recording method in the source."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"Serial wound measurements are useful primarily as indicators of:","options":["The patient's nutritional status","Whether the wound is healing, deteriorating, or static","The need for antibiotics","The patient's pain score"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Measurements are indicators of whether the wound is healing, deteriorating, or static.  Why A/C/D wrong: Measurements track wound size trend, not directly nutrition, antibiotic need, or pain."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"Which of the following are among the EIGHT key objectives of wound management? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Prevent and manage infection","Cleanse the wound (preferred solution is Normal Saline 0.9%)","Debride the wound: debride necrotic or devitalised tissue","Maintain appropriate moisture in the wound to facilitate wound healing, reduce pain, and avoid maceration","Eliminate dead space"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The eight key objectives include preventing/managing infection; cleansing the wound (preferred solution Normal Saline 0.9%); debriding necrotic/devitalised tissue; maintaining appropriate moisture to facilitate healing, reduce pain, and avoid maceration; and eliminating dead space (wounds with depth need to be packed by different types of dressing).  Why all correct: All five options are explicitly listed among the eight objectives."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"Among the eight key objectives of wound management, why should a wound with depth be packed with dressing?","options":["To absorb antibiotics","To eliminate dead space, as wounds with depth need to be packed by different types of dressing","To increase scar tissue","To dry out the wound bed"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: To eliminate dead space, wounds with depth need to be packed by different types of dressing.  Why A/C/D wrong: Packing is for dead-space elimination, not for absorbing antibiotics, increasing scar, or drying the wound."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"According to the eight key objectives of wound management, odour control should be managed using:","options":["Topical antibiotics only","Cool air freshener","Manage wound pain","Protect peri-wound skin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Control odour — use a cool/room freshener etc. to manage wound odour.  Why A wrong: Antibiotics are part of infection control, not the odour-control objective.  Why C wrong: Manage wound pain is a separate objective.  Why D wrong: Protecting peri-wound skin (against maceration and damage from wound exudate) is a separate objective."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"One of the eight key objectives of wound management is to protect the peri-wound skin. This protection is aimed at preventing damage from:","options":["Maceration and damage caused by wound exudate, managed by skin barrier creams/ointments","Excess dryness only","Sutures and staples","Sunlight exposure"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Protect peri-wound skin against maceration and damage caused by wound exudate, managed by skin-barrier creams/ointments.  Why B/C/D wrong: The protection objective targets maceration/exudate damage, not dryness, sutures, or sunlight."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"Which type of debridement involves excision of non-viable tissue using surgical instruments such as a scalpel, curette, scissors, or rongeur until healthy bleeding occurs at the wound edges?","options":["Mechanical debridement","Autolytic debridement","Surgical debridement","Enzymatic debridement"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Surgical debridement is the excision of non-viable tissue using surgical instruments (scalpel, curette, scissors, or rongeur) until healthy bleeding occurs at the wound edges.  Why A wrong: Mechanical debridement is non-selective and uses irrigation, wet-to-dry dressings, and hydrotherapy.  Why B wrong: Autolytic debridement uses the body's own enzymes under moisture-retaining dressings.  Why D wrong: Enzymatic debridement uses chemically/topically applied enzymes such as collagenase or papain."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"Mechanical debridement is best characterized as:","options":["Selective removal using the body's own enzymes","Non-selective debridement such as irrigation, wet-to-dry dressings, and hydrotherapy; both non-viable and viable tissue may be removed","Application of topical collagenase","Use of maggots (larval therapy)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Mechanical debridement is non-selective (e.g., irrigation, wet-to-dry dressings, hydrotherapy); both non-viable and viable tissue may be removed.  Why A wrong: Selective enzyme-based removal describes autolytic debridement.  Why C wrong: Topical collagenase is enzymatic debridement.  Why D wrong: Maggot therapy is biological debridement."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"Autolytic debridement works by which mechanism?","options":["Excision with a scalpel","Using dressings such as hydrocolloids or transparent films to retain moisture and allow the wound's own enzymes to selectively liquefy non-viable tissue","Application of larvae","Irrigation with high-pressure saline"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Autolytic debridement uses dressings such as hydrocolloids or transparent films to retain moisture and allow the body's own enzymes to selectively liquefy non-viable tissue.  Why A wrong: Scalpel excision is surgical debridement.  Why C wrong: Larvae are used in biological debridement.  Why D wrong: High-pressure irrigation is mechanical debridement."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"Enzymatic debridement is accomplished by:","options":["Surgical excision","Chemically/topically applied enzymes such as collagenase or papain to remove necrotic tissue","Wet-to-dry dressings","Use of antimicrobial substances delivered by larvae"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Enzymatic debridement chemically/topically applies enzymes such as collagenase or papain to remove necrotic tissue.  Why A wrong: Surgical excision is surgical debridement.  Why C wrong: Wet-to-dry dressings are mechanical debridement.  Why D wrong: Larvae secreting antimicrobial/proteolytic substances is biological debridement."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"Biological debridement (maggot/larval therapy) removes necrotic tissue because the larvae:","options":["Cut tissue mechanically","Secrete proteolytic and antimicrobial substances to remove necrotic tissue, and also directly promote wound healing","Apply collagenase to the wound","Retain moisture in the wound bed"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Biological debridement uses larvae that secrete proteolytic and antimicrobial substances to remove necrotic tissue and also directly promote wound healing.  Why A wrong: Mechanical cutting is not the mechanism.  Why C wrong: Topical collagenase is enzymatic debridement.  Why D wrong: Moisture retention to enable autolysis describes autolytic debridement."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"(PYQ, NORCET-3 Prelims-2023) Which action should be done FIRST before changing the surgical dressing of the patient?","options":["Remove old dressing with normal saline","Arrange sterile gloves and open it","Hand washing","Wear clean gloves before and remove the old dressing"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Hand washing should be done before any surgical and medical procedures, so it is the FIRST action before changing the surgical dressing (per the source RECALL/answer key, Right Answer = C).  Why A wrong: Removing the old dressing occurs after hand hygiene and donning gloves.  Why B wrong: Arranging/opening sterile gloves comes after hand washing.  Why D wrong: Donning gloves to remove the old dressing follows hand washing."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"When cleaning a wound with approximated edges, which technique should the nurse use?","options":["Clean from bottom to top, working outward from the incision","Follow standard precautions, moisten a sterile gauze pad or swab with cleansing agent, use a new swab or gauze for each downward stroke, clean from top to bottom, work outward from the incision in lines parallel to it, and wipe from the clean area toward the less clean area","Use one swab for the entire wound, scrubbing back and forth","Clean from the dirtiest area toward the cleanest area"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For wounds with approximated edges, follow standard precautions, moisten a sterile gauze pad/swab with cleansing agent, use a new swab/gauze for each downward stroke, clean from top to bottom, work outward from the incision in lines parallel to it, and wipe from the clean area toward the less clean area.  Why A wrong: The direction is top to bottom (not bottom to top).  Why C wrong: A new swab is used for each stroke, not one swab back and forth.  Why D wrong: Cleaning proceeds from clean toward less-clean areas, not dirty to clean."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"When cleaning a wound with UN-approximated edges, which technique is recommended?","options":["Clean in straight downward strokes only","Moisten a sterile gauze pad or swab, squeeze out excess solution, use a new swab or gauze for each circle, and clean the wound in full or half circles beginning in the centre and working toward the outside","Clean from the outside edges inward toward the centre","Use a dry gauze with no cleansing solution"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For un-approximated edges, follow standard precautions, moisten a sterile gauze pad or swab and squeeze out excess solution, use a new swab/gauze for each circle, and clean the wound in full or half circles beginning in the centre and working toward the outside.  Why A wrong: Concentric circles (not straight strokes) are used for open/un-approximated wounds.  Why C wrong: Cleaning begins at the centre and moves outward, not outside-in.  Why D wrong: A moistened (not dry) gauze is used."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"When applying a new wound dressing, the nurse should clean to at least which margin?","options":["Clean to at least 1 inch beyond the end of the new dressing if a dressing is not being applied, or at least 2 inches beyond the wound margins","Clean only the wound bed itself","Clean 5 cm beyond in all cases","Clean only to the edge of the old dressing"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Clean to at least 1 inch beyond the end of the new dressing (if a dressing is not being applied) or at least 2 inches beyond the wound margins.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the specified 1-inch/2-inch margins."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"Which nursing consideration applies to GAUZE as a dressing material?","options":["It is non-adherent and does not need to be applied to intact skin","It can be used as a wound packing, usually moistened, and as a primary dressing; if it remains uncovered, gauze can adhere to and traumatise the wound base, so dressings must be changed frequently (at least daily) because gauze dressings tend to dry out","It absorbs moderate amounts of exudate and promotes a moist wound bed","It is used only for highly exuding wounds with hemostatic properties"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Gauze can be used as wound packing (usually moistened) and as a primary dressing; if uncovered it can adhere to and traumatise the wound base, so dressings need frequent (at least daily) changes because gauze tends to dry out.  Why A wrong: That describes non-adherent dressings, which are applied over a non-intact skin/wound base and covered with a secondary dressing.  Why C wrong: Absorbing moderate exudate and promoting a moist bed describes hydrocolloids.  Why D wrong: Hemostatic properties for highly exuding wounds describe calcium alginates/hydrofibers."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"A non-adherent dressing is best used in which situation, and which precaution applies?","options":["Applied over open wounds as a secondary dressing; it is non-traumatic to intact skin and wound base, may be applied over skin tears, and is covered with a secondary dressing; dressing changes are necessary every 24–48 hours to prevent the product from drying and adhering to the wound bed","Used as primary packing for deeply exuding wounds with hemostatic effect","Used to absorb large amounts of exudate and slough","Used only on dry intact skin to maintain hydration"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Non-adherent dressings are applied over open wounds as a secondary dressing, are non-traumatic to intact skin and the wound base, may be applied over skin tears, and are covered with a secondary dressing; changes are necessary every 24–48 hours to prevent the product from drying out and adhering to the wound bed.  Why B wrong: Hemostatic packing for exuding wounds describes calcium alginates/hydrofibers.  Why C wrong: Large exudate/slough absorption is not their defined role.  Why D wrong: They are used over wounds/skin tears, not solely on dry intact skin."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"Transparent film dressings have which nursing considerations?","options":["They retain moisture, enhance autolytic debridement, are commonly used as a secondary dressing to secure other dressing materials such as foam, and should NOT be used on skin tears or fragile skin","They are highly absorbent for heavily exuding wounds","They are used only for hemostasis","They must be changed at least twice daily because they dry out"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Transparent films retain moisture, enhance autolytic debridement, are commonly used as a secondary dressing to secure other dressings such as foam, and should not be used on skin tears or fragile skin (surrounding skin can macerate if the wound has more than minimal drainage).  Why B wrong: High absorbency for heavy exudate describes alginates/foam.  Why C wrong: They are not hemostatic dressings.  Why D wrong: They are moisture-retentive films, not rapidly drying."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"Which dressing material is described as a thicker hydrocolloid that absorbs moderate amounts of drainage, promotes a moist wound bed, and promotes autolytic debridement, but should be avoided in the presence/excess of wound infection?","options":["Hydrogel","Hydrocolloid","Foam","Calcium alginate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hydrocolloids are a thicker dressing that absorbs moderate amounts of drainage, promotes a moist wound bed, and promotes autolytic debridement; they should be avoided in the presence/excess of wound infection because wound size may exist or vary in relation to the composition of the hydrocolloid, and they are changed (dressing every 3 to 5 days).  Why A wrong: Hydrogel creates a moist environment for healing and is used in wounds with little or no exudate, and can be soothing in painful wounds.  Why C wrong: Foam is used as a primary dressing and to manage exudate in venous ulcers.  Why D wrong: Calcium alginate is highly absorptive for heavily exuding wounds with hemostatic properties."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"Hydrogel dressings are MOST appropriately used in which situation?","options":["Heavily exuding wounds requiring hemostasis","Wounds with little or no exudate, to create a moist wound environment for healing; they are commonly changed daily and can be soothing in painful wounds, but care must be taken to apply the gel to the wound only and not on the surrounding skin, as maceration can occur","As a secondary dressing only to secure foam","To pack deep dead space in infected wounds"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hydrogel creates a moist wound environment for healing, is used in wounds with little or no exudate, is commonly changed daily, can be soothing in painful wounds, and must be applied to the wound only (not the surrounding skin) to avoid maceration.  Why A wrong: Heavy exudate with hemostasis indicates calcium alginate/hydrofiber.  Why C wrong: A secondary securing dressing describes transparent film.  Why D wrong: Hydrogel is for low-exudate wounds, not heavy dead-space packing of infected wounds."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"Which is a correct nursing consideration for silicone-based dressings?","options":["They are highly absorbent and used for heavily exuding wounds; they are non-traumatic to the wound bed or surrounding skin and promote a moist healing environment, can be used for all types of wounds (including skin tears), and sacral and heel-shaped silicone dressings are used for pressure injury prevention in the ICU setting","They are used only for hemostasis of bleeding wounds","They promote autolytic debridement by retaining body enzymes only","They must be changed at least every 12 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Silicone-based dressings are highly absorbent and used for heavily exuding wounds, are non-traumatic to the wound bed or surrounding skin, promote a moist healing environment, can be used for all types of wounds including skin tears, and sacral and heel-shaped silicone dressings are used for pressure injury prevention in the ICU setting.  Why B wrong: Hemostasis is the role of calcium alginate/hydrofibers.  Why C wrong: Autolytic debridement via retained enzymes is the autolytic mechanism (hydrocolloids/films), not the defining silicone feature.  Why D wrong: A mandatory 12-hour change interval is not specified."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"Foam dressings are used in which context?","options":["As a primary dressing; foam can be used as a primary dressing and can under compression be used to manage exudate in venous ulcers","Only as a hemostatic agent","Only for dry intact skin","Exclusively for autolytic debridement of eschar"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Foam can be used as a primary dressing and, under compression, to manage exudate in venous ulcers.  Why B wrong: Hemostasis is the role of calcium alginate/hydrofibers.  Why C wrong: Foam is used for exuding wounds, not dry intact skin alone.  Why D wrong: Eschar debridement is performed by surgical/enzymatic/autolytic methods, not specifically foam."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"Calcium alginates/hydrofibers have which characteristic uses and cautions?","options":["They are highly absorptive for highly exuding wounds, will not adhere to the wound base, persist non-traumatic on removal, have hemostatic properties, and can be used as a primary dressing for exuding wounds such as venous/static ulcers and covered with a secondary dressing (such as foam or silicone); dressings can be left in place for multiple days based on the amount of wound drainage, usually changed every 24 to 48 hours, and should NOT be used in dry wounds or wounds with minimal drainage as this may cause desiccation of the wound bed","They are non-absorbent and used only on dry skin","They must be changed every hour","They are contraindicated in all exuding wounds"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Calcium alginates/hydrofibers are highly absorptive for highly exuding wounds, will not adhere to the wound base, are non-traumatic on removal, have hemostatic properties, can be used as a primary dressing for exuding wounds (e.g., venous/static ulcers) and covered with a secondary dressing such as foam or silicone; they can be left in place for multiple days based on drainage, usually changed every 24–48 hours, and should not be used in dry wounds or wounds with minimal drainage as this may cause desiccation of the wound bed.  Why B wrong: They are highly absorbent for exuding (not dry) wounds.  Why C wrong: They are changed every 24–48 hours, not hourly.  Why D wrong: They are indicated for highly exuding wounds."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"Pressure injuries (pressure ulcers) characteristically occur over which areas and have what typical appearance?","options":["Over bony prominences (e.g., sacrum, heels, ischia, coccyx); wounds typically have a circular appearance and are staged based on the extent of tissue damage observed according to the NPUAP pressure injury stages","Only over fleshy areas of the thigh with irregular edges","On the dorsum of the hands only","Over moist skin folds only, with candidiasis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Pressure injuries occur over bony prominences (sacrum, heels, ischia, coccyx); wounds typically have a circular appearance and are staged based on the extent of tissue damage observed according to the NPUAP pressure injury stages.  Why B wrong: Irregular edges and high exudation describe venous ulcers (lower leg, medial aspect).  Why C wrong: Dorsum of the hands is associated with skin tears, not pressure injuries.  Why D wrong: Moist skin-fold breakdown with candidiasis describes moisture-associated skin damage (MASD)."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"A venous ulcer most commonly occurs at which location and has which nursing considerations?","options":["Over bony prominences with circular appearance","On the lower leg, often on the medial aspect; it usually has irregular edges, may be highly exuding, may show associated lower-extremity changes (haemosiderosis, oedema, hardening of surrounding skin/lipodermatosclerosis), and compression dressing/multilayer bandaging systems are the gold standard in control of oedema and healing","On the feet, ankles, heels, toes with a punched-out appearance","On buttocks, perineal area, skin folds with maceration"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Venous ulcers occur on the lower leg, often on the medial aspect; they have irregular edges, may be highly exuding, show changes such as haemosiderosis, oedema, and hardening of surrounding skin (lipodermatosclerosis); compression dressing/multilayer bandaging systems are the gold standard for controlling oedema and promoting healing.  Why A wrong: Bony prominences with circular wounds describe pressure injuries.  Why C wrong: Feet/ankles/heels/toes with a punched-out look describe arterial ulcers.  Why D wrong: Buttocks/perineal/skin folds with maceration describe MASD."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"Arterial ulcers (also termed ischemic ulcers) most commonly occur at which sites and have which features?","options":["Sacrum and coccyx, circular, low exudate","Feet, ankles, heels, toes; the wound base ranges from pale pink to necrotic, can be painful, well-defined wound edges, may have a \"punched-out\" appearance with defined borders and callous formation in surrounding skin; commonly associated with peripheral arterial disease, and revascularization (referral to a vascular surgeon) may be needed to achieve wound healing","Lower leg medial aspect, highly exuding","Plantar aspect of the toes (diabetic foot) with neuropathy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Arterial (ischemic) ulcers occur on the feet, ankles, heels, and toes; the wound base ranges from pale pink to necrotic, can be painful, has well-defined edges, may show a \"punched-out\" appearance with defined borders and callous formation in surrounding skin; they are commonly associated with peripheral arterial disease, and revascularization (vascular surgeon referral) may be needed to achieve wound healing.  Why A wrong: Sacrum/coccyx circular wounds are pressure injuries.  Why C wrong: Lower-leg medial highly exuding wounds are venous ulcers.  Why D wrong: Plantar diabetic neuropathic ulcers are diabetic ulcers."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"Diabetic ulcers most commonly occur where, and have which characteristic features?","options":["Over bony prominences only","On the feet (plantar aspect) and toes; alternate term is neuropathic ulcer; the wound base can range from pink to necrotic, may have a \"punched-out\" appearance with defined borders and callous formation in surrounding skin, is commonly associated with peripheral arterial disease, and meticulous foot care is an essential element of the care plan","On the medial lower leg with haemosiderosis","In moist skin folds with candidiasis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Diabetic ulcers occur on the feet (plantar aspect) and toes; the alternate term is neuropathic ulcer; the wound base ranges from pink to necrotic, may show a \"punched-out\" appearance with defined borders and callous formation in surrounding skin, is commonly associated with peripheral arterial disease, and meticulous foot care is an essential element of the care plan.  Why A wrong: Bony-prominence ulcers are pressure injuries.  Why C wrong: Medial lower-leg ulcers with haemosiderosis are venous ulcers.  Why D wrong: Moist skin-fold candidiasis is MASD."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"A skin tear most commonly occurs at which location and in which patient population?","options":["Sacrum, in immobile spinal-cord-injured patients","Upper and lower extremities and dorsum of the hands; susceptible patients include older adults and those with a history of skin tears, mobility deficits, and long-term steroid use; the wounds are typically painful and likely to bleed, and are classified by the degree of skin flap loss (Type 1 to Type 3) according to the ISTAP Skin Tear Classification System","Lower leg medial aspect, in venous insufficiency","Perineal area, with incontinence"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Skin tears occur on the upper and lower extremities and the dorsum of the hands; susceptible patients include older adults and those with a history of skin tears, mobility deficits, and long-term steroid use; the wounds are typically painful and likely to bleed and are classified by the degree of skin-flap loss (Type 1 to Type 3) according to the ISTAP Skin Tear Classification System. (Option A states this location/population correctly.)  Why C wrong: Lower-leg medial aspect with venous insufficiency describes venous ulcers.  Why D wrong: Perineal area with incontinence describes MASD.  Why B2 wrong: Plantar foot in diabetics describes diabetic ulcers."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"Moisture-associated skin damage (MASD) characteristically occurs at which locations, and what is true of its management?","options":["Buttocks, perineal area, skin folds, around leaking stoma appliances or enteral feeding tubes; the skin appears red and macerated; skin exposed to chronic moisture should be assessed for signs of secondary candidiasis; the skin should be cleansed after each incontinence episode and a skin-barrier cream/ointment applied to protect the skin and support healing","Over the heels with eschar","On the toes with neuropathy","On the dorsum of the hands as skin tears"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: MASD occurs on the buttocks, perineal area, skin folds, and around leaking stoma appliances or enteral feeding tubes; the skin appears red and macerated; chronic moisture exposure should be assessed for secondary candidiasis; the skin should be cleansed after each incontinence episode and a skin-barrier cream/ointment applied to protect the skin and support healing.  Why B/C/D wrong: Heel eschar (pressure injury), toe neuropathy (diabetic ulcer), and dorsal-hand skin tears are different wound types."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"Incontinence-associated dermatitis (IAD) is a common form of MASD. The mnemonic ACT used to guide caregivers in treating IAD stands for:","options":["Assess, Disinfect, Treat","Assess, Cleanse, Treat (Assess, Cleanse, Treat — can help guide caregivers treating IAD)","Apply, Clean, Test","Antibiotic, Cleanse, Tape"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The mnemonic ACT (Assess, Cleanse, Treat) can help guide caregivers treating IAD.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the ACT (Assess, Cleanse, Treat) mnemonic stated in the source."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"A pressure sore is also known by which alternate names?","options":["Neuropathic ulcer or ischemic ulcer","Decubitus ulcer or bed sore","Venous ulcer or stasis ulcer","Skin tear or MASD"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A pressure sore is also known as a decubitus ulcer or bed sore.  Why A wrong: Neuropathic = diabetic; ischemic = arterial ulcer.  Why C wrong: Venous/stasis ulcers are a different wound type.  Why D wrong: Skin tears and MASD are distinct wound types."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"A pressure sore (decubitus ulcer) is defined as:","options":["An open wound from a clean sharp instrument","An injury or breakdown of skin integrity due to persistent pressure on a bony prominence for a prolonged period","An ulcer caused only by arterial insufficiency","A burn wound with loose charred tissue removed"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A pressure sore is an injury or breakdown of skin integrity due to persistent pressure on a bony prominence for a prolonged period.  Why A wrong: That describes an incised (tidy) open wound.  Why C wrong: Arterial insufficiency causes arterial/ischemic ulcers.  Why D wrong: A critical (burn) wound is a different category."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"What is the pathophysiologic basis of pressure sore development?","options":["Tissue compression restricts blood flow to the skin, which can result in tissue ischemia and necrosis","Excess moisture macerates the skin","Bacterial colonisation of intact skin","Repeated friction tearing the epidermis only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Tissue compression restricts blood flow to the skin, which can result in tissue ischemia and necrosis.  Why B wrong: Maceration from moisture describes MASD.  Why C wrong: Colonisation alone does not define pressure-sore pathophysiology.  Why D wrong: Friction tearing the epidermis describes skin tears/shear, not the core ischemic mechanism stated."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"Once a pressure sore develops, the source notes that it is:","options":["Easy to heal within 48 hours","Difficult to heal","Self-resolving without intervention","Best left exposed without dressing"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Once a pressure sore develops, it is difficult to heal.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source emphasizes that pressure sores are difficult to heal, not quickly or spontaneously resolved."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"What is the MOST common pressure point at which pressure sores develop while a patient is lying in the supine position?","options":["The trochanter","The sacrum","The heels","The elbows"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common pressure point is the sacrum (in supine position).  Why A/C/D wrong: Trochanter, heels, and elbows are other pressure points, but the sacrum is identified as the most common in supine."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"Which of the following are other common pressure points where pressure sores develop? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Trochanter","Ischial tuberosity","Back of the head","Heels, shoulders, elbows, inner knees, malleoli","The umbilicus"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Other common pressure points include the heels, sacrum (most common), trochanter, ischial tuberosity, back of the head, shoulders, elbows, inner knees, and malleoli.  Why E wrong: The umbilicus is not listed as a pressure point; pressure sores form over bony prominences."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"Which risk factor for pressure sores is described as resulting in a client lying or being in one position for a long time, leading to prolonged pressure?","options":["Malnutrition","Immobility","Incontinence","Ageing"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Immobility results in prolonged pressure when the client lies or remains in one position for a long time.  Why A wrong: Malnutrition refers to imbalanced diet as a risk factor.  Why C wrong: Incontinence (urine and feces) irritates the skin.  Why D wrong: Ageing causes decreased epidermal thickness, less elasticity, and decreased sebaceous gland activity."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"\"Shearing force and friction\" as a risk factor for pressure sores refers to:","options":["Pressure exceeding capillary pressure","Putting force on skin when the patient is repositioned (slid), leading to a strap-type friction injury that occurs","An imbalanced diet","Urine and feces irritating the skin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Shearing force and friction occur when force is put on the skin during repositioning (e.g., sliding/dragging the patient), causing friction injury.  Why A wrong: Pressure exceeding capillary pressure (>32 mm Hg by capillary pressure) is the \"skin pressure\" risk factor.  Why C wrong: An imbalanced diet is malnutrition.  Why D wrong: Urine/feces irritation is incontinence."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"As a risk factor for pressure sores, \"skin pressure\" is defined as capillary pressure exceeding approximately:","options":["12 mm Hg","22 mm Hg","32 mm Hg","42 mm Hg"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Skin pressure as a risk factor is defined as capillary pressure exceeding approximately 32 mm Hg (a value of >32–35 mm Hg is referenced); external pressure greater than capillary pressure occludes blood flow.  Why A/B/D wrong: These values do not match the ~32 mm Hg capillary closing pressure threshold."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"Which of the following are listed risk factors for pressure sore development? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Malnutrition (imbalanced diet)","Incontinence (urine and feces irritate the skin)","Decreased sensory perception and diabetic neuropathy","Ageing (decreased epidermal thickness, elasticity, and sebaceous gland activity)","Moisture (urine, perspiration causing skin maceration), pyrexia, and inadequate tissue oxygenation"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: All listed items are risk factors: malnutrition (imbalanced diet); incontinence (urine and feces irritating the skin); decreased sensory perception/diabetic neuropathy; ageing (decreased epidermal thickness, elasticity, sebaceous gland activity); and moisture (urine/perspiration causing maceration), pyrexia, and inadequate tissue oxygenation. Dryness and cognitive impairment are also noted.  Why all correct: Each option matches a listed risk factor."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"\"Moisture\" as a pressure-sore risk factor contributes to tissue breakdown primarily because:","options":["It increases tissue oxygenation","Urine and perspiration cause skin maceration","It hardens the surrounding skin","It reduces capillary pressure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Moisture (from urine and perspiration) causes skin maceration, a listed pressure-sore risk factor.  Why A wrong: Moisture does not improve oxygenation; inadequate tissue oxygenation is a separate risk factor.  Why C wrong: Hardening of surrounding skin (lipodermatosclerosis) is associated with venous ulcers.  Why D wrong: Moisture does not reduce capillary pressure."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"Which two additional risk factors for pressure sores are listed alongside moisture and oxygenation?","options":["Pyrexia and inadequate tissue oxygenation","Hypotension and tachycardia","Dryness and cognitive impairment","Both A and C"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Pyrexia and inadequate tissue oxygenation, as well as dryness and cognitive impairment, are listed risk factors for pressure sores.  Why A wrong: It is correct but incomplete (omits dryness and cognitive impairment).  Why C wrong: It is correct but incomplete (omits pyrexia and inadequate oxygenation).  Why B wrong: Hypotension/tachycardia are not the listed pressure-sore risk factors here."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"(PYQ, NORCET-2023, Prelims-2023) A male patient comes to casualty who is lacerated with an untidy wound after 4 hours. What should be the priority nursing action?","options":["Primary suturing","Clean thoroughly and dressing","Debridement with suture immediately","Debridement with suture secondarily"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: A lacerated untidy wound should NOT be closed immediately without risking poor outcomes; the priority is debridement with secondary suture (Right Answer = D per source). Untidy/contaminated wounds are debrided and closed secondarily to avoid wound complications.  Why A wrong: Primary suturing is for clean wounds within 6 hours; closing an untidy wound primarily risks infection/dehiscence.  Why B wrong: Cleaning and dressing alone does not address the devitalised tissue requiring debridement and planned secondary closure.  Why C wrong: Immediate debridement with suturing (primary closure) of an untidy contaminated wound risks poor outcomes; closure should be delayed/secondary."},{"section":"Batch 044 · open vs closed wounds, Rank & Wakefield tidy/untidy wound classification…","question":"Nursing care recommendations for venous leg ulcers identify which intervention as the gold standard for controlling oedema and promoting healing?","options":["Surgical debridement only","Compression dressing/multilayer bandaging system","Total bed rest with leg elevation alone","Daily antibiotic ointment application"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For venous ulcers, compression dressing/multilayer bandaging systems are the gold standard for controlling oedema and promoting healing.  Why A wrong: Surgical debridement is not the stated gold standard for venous ulcer oedema control.  Why C wrong: Elevation alone is not identified as the gold standard.  Why D wrong: Routine antibiotic ointment is not the stated gold-standard intervention."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"A patient confined to bed who, when the head of the bed is raised, slides down so that the deeper tissues move in one direction while the skin remains fixed is experiencing which mechanical force responsible for pressure sore development?","options":["Friction force","Shearing force","Compression force","Tensile force"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Shearing force occurs when the skin layer remains in place while the underlying tissue/skeleton shifts (e.g., when the head of the bed is raised and the patient slides), tearing deep tissue and blood vessels.  Why A wrong: Friction is the surface rubbing of skin against bedding, damaging the epidermis, not the deep tissue shift described.  Why C wrong: \"Compression force\" is not the named force for this sliding mechanism.  Why D wrong: Tensile force is not the term used for pressure-sore pathogenesis here."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"When a patient slides in bed and the skin rubs against the bed surface, the resulting superficial damage to the epidermis is caused by which force?","options":["Shearing force","Friction","Gravity","Pressure intensity"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Friction is the rubbing of the skin against an external surface (e.g., bedsheets), producing superficial epidermal abrasion.  Why A wrong: Shearing involves deep tissue displacement, not surface rubbing.  Why C wrong: Gravity contributes to sliding but is not the damaging surface force.  Why D wrong: Pressure intensity refers to load over an area, not surface rubbing."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"A patient is positioned in the supine (lying on back) position. Which of the following are typical pressure-point bony prominences at risk for pressure sores in this position? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Back of the head (occiput)","Shoulder blades","Elbows","Heels","Patella (knee cap)"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: In supine position the occiput (back of head), shoulder blades, elbows, lower back/sacrum, and heels bear pressure.  Why E wrong: The patella faces upward in supine and bears no pressure; it is a prone-position pressure point instead."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"In the side-lying (lateral) position, which bony prominences are common pressure-sore sites? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Ear","Shoulder","Hip","Ankle","Sacrum"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: In the lateral/side-lying position the ear, shoulder, hip, and ankle bear pressure against the surface.  Why E wrong: The sacrum is the major pressure point in supine/seated positions, not in the true side-lying position."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"According to the \"Did You Know?\" note, when a patient on duty rounding is admitted for move-up traction with trapeze bar, the purpose of using the trapeze bar is so that the patient can do which of the following?","options":["Be turned by two nurses only","Move up the bed with the help of the trapeze","Receive continuous traction weights","Avoid all repositioning"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A trapeze (overhead) bar lets the patient lift and reposition/move up the bed independently, reducing shearing and friction.  Why A wrong: The trapeze enables self-assisted movement, not specifically two-nurse turning.  Why C wrong: The trapeze is for self-movement, not for applying traction weight.  Why D wrong: Its purpose is to facilitate movement/repositioning, not avoid it."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"A trapeze bar is used to help patients change positions while in bed, and aids transfer from bed to chair while minimizing attendant resistance. This device uses which type of force to push down on the patient's body, reducing resistance between the patient and the chair or bed?","options":["Pulling force","Gravity force","Shearing force","Frictional resistance"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Per the source, shear force is reduced by using the device's gravity force pushing down between patient and chair/bed.  Why A wrong: The note describes a downward (gravity) force, not a pull.  Why C wrong: Shearing is what is being reduced, not the force exerted by the device.  Why D wrong: Friction is reduced, not the operating mechanism of the device."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"In Stage I of a pressure sore, which of the following findings is characteristic?","options":["Partial-thickness ulceration of epidermis or dermis","Non-blanchable, intact skin with redness and erythema","Full-thickness loss with exposed bone","Shallow open ulcer with a red-pink wound bed"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Stage I shows non-blanchable redness/erythema with intact skin (no open wound).  Why A wrong: Partial-thickness ulceration describes Stage II.  Why C wrong: Exposed bone is Stage IV.  Why D wrong: A shallow open ulcer with red-pink bed is Stage II."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"Which statement BEST describes a Stage II pressure ulcer?","options":["Intact skin with persistent non-blanchable redness","Partial-thickness ulceration of the epidermis or dermis, presenting as an abrasion, blister, or shallow open ulcer with a red-pink wound bed","Full-thickness tissue loss with visible subcutaneous fat but no exposed bone","Full-thickness loss with exposed bone, tendon, or muscle"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Stage II is a partial-thickness loss of epidermis/dermis appearing as an abrasion, blister, or shallow open ulcer with a red-pink wound bed.  Why A wrong: That describes Stage I.  Why C wrong: Subcutaneous fat visible without exposed bone is Stage III.  Why D wrong: Exposed bone/tendon/muscle is Stage IV."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"A Stage III pressure ulcer is characterized by which of the following?","options":["Non-blanchable intact skin","Partial-thickness loss with a blister","Full-thickness skin loss involving damage to subcutaneous tissue; slough may be present but bone, tendon, and muscle are NOT exposed","Full-thickness loss with exposed bone and muscle"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Stage III involves full-thickness loss into subcutaneous tissue; slough may be present, but bone/tendon/muscle are not exposed.  Why A wrong: Intact skin is Stage I.  Why B wrong: Blister/partial-thickness is Stage II.  Why D wrong: Exposed bone/muscle is Stage IV."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"Which findings indicate a Stage IV pressure ulcer? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Full-thickness tissue loss with exposed bone, tendon, or muscle","Slough or eschar may be present","Undermining and tunneling may be present","Bone, tendon, and muscle are NOT visible","Intact skin only"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Stage IV is full-thickness loss exposing bone, tendon, or muscle; slough/eschar and undermining/tunneling may be present.  Why D wrong: In Stage IV bone/tendon/muscle ARE exposed/visible.  Why E wrong: Intact skin is Stage I, not Stage IV."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"A pressure ulcer is covered completely by slough or eschar so that the true depth and stage cannot be determined until enough slough/eschar is removed. How is this ulcer classified?","options":["Stage III","Stage IV","Unstageable pressure ulcer","Stage I"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: An unstageable ulcer has full-thickness loss in which the wound base is obscured by slough/eschar, so the true depth cannot be determined until the wound is cleaned.  Why A/B wrong: Staging requires a visible wound base; the depth is unknown when covered.  Why D wrong: Stage I has intact skin with no slough or eschar."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"Using the wound-colour classification, a wound that appears BLACK indicates which type of tissue?","options":["Healthy red granulation tissue","Necrotic (dead) tissue","Exudate with yellow fibrous debris","Infection only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A black wound denotes necrotic (dead) tissue.  Why A wrong: Healthy granulation is red.  Why C wrong: Yellow wound indicates exudate and yellow fibrous debris/infectious wound.  Why D wrong: Black specifically indicates necrosis, not infection alone."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"Match the wound colour to its meaning: a YELLOW wound indicates which of the following?","options":["Necrotic dead tissue","Exudate and yellow fibrous debris, indicating an infectious wound","Red-to-pink granulation indicating a healthy wound","Epithelialized, fully healed tissue"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A yellow wound signals exudate with yellow fibrous debris and an infectious wound.  Why A wrong: Necrotic = black.  Why C wrong: Healthy granulation = red.  Why D wrong: Yellow does not denote complete healing."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"A RED wound indicates which condition?","options":["Necrotic tissue","Yellow fibrous infectious debris","Red-to-pink granulation, indicating a healthy wound","Unstageable depth"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: A red wound represents red-to-pink granulation tissue, indicating a healthy, healing wound.  Why A wrong: Necrotic tissue is black.  Why B wrong: Infectious debris is yellow.  Why D wrong: Colour does not by itself indicate stageability."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"According to the clinical features of pressure sores, at which stage(s) is pain usually present, and at which stage is loss of sensation (pain absent) typically seen?","options":["Pain present in Stage 1 & 2; pain absent in Stage 3 & 4","Pain present in Stage 3 & 4; pain absent in Stage 1 & 2","Pain present in all stages","Pain absent in all stages"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states pain is usually present in Stages 1 & 2 and absent in Stages 3 & 4 (loss of sensation occurs as nerves are destroyed at deeper stages).  Why B wrong: It is the reverse — deeper stages lose sensation.  Why C/D wrong: Pain is stage-dependent, not uniform."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"Which of the following clinical features may accompany a pressure sore, particularly with deeper/infected wounds? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Erythema","Exudate","Pyrexia (fever)","Leucocytosis if systemic infection occurs","Tissue damage according to stage"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists erythema, exudate, pyrexia, leucocytosis (with systemic infection), and tissue damage according to stage as clinical features.  All options are listed source features, so all apply."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"The Braden Scale is widely used as a screening tool to determine which of the following?","options":["The exact stage of an existing pressure ulcer","The patients who are at risk for bed sore (pressure ulcer development risk)","The colour of the wound","The patient's nutritional intake only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Braden Scale is a screening tool to identify patients at risk for developing pressure (bed) sores.  Why A wrong: It predicts risk, not the stage of an existing ulcer.  Why C wrong: Wound colour is a separate assessment.  Why D wrong: Nutrition is only one of several subscales."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"The Braden Scale assesses six parameters. Which of the following are among them? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Sensory perception","Moisture","Activity","Mobility","Nutrition and friction/shear"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The six Braden subscales are sensory perception, moisture, activity, mobility, nutrition, and friction/shear.  All listed options are correct Braden parameters."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"On the Braden Scale, a LOWER total score indicates which of the following?","options":["No risk of pressure ulcer","Higher risk for development of a pressure ulcer","Lower risk for pressure ulcer","An existing Stage IV ulcer"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The lowest score (per the source, ≤12) indicates high risk for development of a pressure ulcer — lower scores mean higher risk.  Why A wrong: Low scores indicate substantial risk.  Why C wrong: Higher scores indicate lower risk.  Why D wrong: The scale predicts risk, not ulcer stage."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"On the Norton Scale, what does a low total score indicate, and what is the threshold for being considered at great risk?","options":["Low score = great risk of developing pressure sores; onset of risk at 16 or below, high risk <12 or below","High score = great risk; onset at 20 or above","Low score = no risk; high risk at >18","The scale does not predict risk"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Norton Scale uses five categories, each graded 1–4 (max score 4 per category); a lower score indicates greater risk, with onset of risk at 16 or below and high risk at <12 or below.  Why B wrong: It is a low (not high) score that indicates great risk.  Why C wrong: Low scores indicate risk, not \"no risk.\"  Why D wrong: Norton specifically assesses risk."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"The Norton Scale categorizes five areas, each with four grades (1–4). What does a grade of 4 in each category represent?","options":["The patient is in the worst (highest-risk) condition","The patient is healthy/in the best possible condition for that category","An unstageable wound","The presence of necrotic tissue"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Each category is graded 1–4 where grade 4 means the patient is healthy / in the best possible condition (and grade 1 the worst).  Why A wrong: Grade 4 is the best, not worst.  Why C/D wrong: The Norton scale assesses risk condition, not wound staging or tissue type."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"The Waterlow scale is used for the assessment of pressure sore. On the Waterlow scale, the lowest score is 1 and the highest score is 64. What result interpretation corresponds to \"at risk\" and \"very high risk\"?","options":["At risk 10–14 points; very high risk ≥20 points","At risk ≥20; very high risk 10","At risk 1–5; very high risk 64","The Waterlow scale does not interpret risk levels"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: On the Waterlow scale, a score of 10–14 points indicates \"at risk,\" 15+ indicates high risk, and ≥20 points indicates \"very high risk.\"  Why B wrong: Higher scores (not lower) indicate higher risk.  Why C wrong: 1–5 is not the at-risk cutoff and 64 is the maximum, not the very-high-risk threshold value.  Why D wrong: Waterlow explicitly stratifies risk."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"A nurse providing pressure-ulcer nursing care should perform which of the following actions? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Identify the cause and stage of the pressure ulcer, then treat accordingly","Reduce the risk factors","Change the client's position every 2 hours, especially for clients who are immobilised and bedridden","Re-position skin over bony prominences and keep skin clean and dry","Massage reddened/bony-prominence areas vigorously to improve circulation"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing care includes identifying cause/stage and treating accordingly, reducing risk factors, repositioning immobilised clients every 2 hours, and keeping the skin clean and dry.  Why E wrong: The source explicitly warns NOT to massage bony prominences/reddened areas because massage can cause tissue breakdown or necrosis."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"Why should a nurse AVOID massaging an area of bony prominence that is reddened over a pressure point?","options":["Because massage relieves pressure permanently","Because massage can cause skin breakdown or necrosis","Because it increases blood flow excessively","Because it is the recommended treatment for Stage IV ulcers"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states massage around bony prominences that are reddened should be avoided because it can cause tissue breakdown or necrosis.  Why A wrong: Repositioning, not massage, relieves pressure.  Why C wrong: The concern is tissue damage, not merely excess blood flow.  Why D wrong: Massage is contraindicated, not a treatment."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"In the \"A Guide to Pressure Ulcer Prevention\" table, for a patient with decreased sensory perception, which nursing intervention is appropriate?","options":["Encourage incontinent episodes","Assess areas at risk for signs of non-blanching reactive hyperaemia and provide pressure-redistribution surface","Reposition every 8 hours only","Stop nutritional supplementation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For decreased sensory perception, the guide advises assessing at-risk areas for signs of non-blanching reactive hyperaemia and providing a pressure-redistribution surface.  Why A wrong: Incontinent episodes increase moisture-related risk and are not encouraged.  Why C wrong: Repositioning should be more frequent (typically every 2 hours), not every 8 hours.  Why D wrong: Nutrition should be supported, not stopped."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"According to the pressure-ulcer prevention guide, the nursing intervention for the risk factor \"Moisture\" includes which of the following?","options":["Assess need for incontinence management; following each incontinent episode, clean the area with no-rinse perineal cleaner and protect skin with moisture-barrier ointment","Reposition every 2 hours only","Provide a pressure-redistribution surface only","Consult a dietitian for nutritional evaluation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For moisture, the guide directs assessing the need for incontinence management and, after each incontinent episode, cleaning the area with a no-rinse perineal cleaner and protecting the skin with a moisture-barrier ointment.  Why B/C wrong: Those are interventions for activity/mobility or sensory perception risk factors.  Why D wrong: That is the intervention for poor nutrition."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"Per the prevention guide, the appropriate intervention for the risk factor \"Friction and shear\" is to:","options":["Reposition and lift the patient off the surface; provide a trapeze to facilitate movement","Provide adequate nutritional intake and fluid","Provide a high-protein diet only","Consult a dietitian"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For friction and shear, the guide advises repositioning by lifting the patient off the surface and providing a trapeze to facilitate movement.  Why B/D wrong: Those address poor nutrition.  Why C wrong: High-protein diet is a nutrition intervention, not a friction/shear measure."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"For the risk factor \"Decreased activity/mobility,\" the prevention guide recommends which intervention?","options":["Establish and post an individualized turning schedule and change the position every 2 hours","Provide a moisture barrier ointment","Provide high-protein diet","Assess for reactive hyperaemia only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For decreased activity/mobility, the guide directs establishing and posting an individualised turning schedule and changing position every 2 hours.  Why B wrong: Moisture barrier is for the moisture risk factor.  Why C wrong: High-protein diet is a nutrition measure.  Why D wrong: Reactive hyperaemia assessment is for sensory perception."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"Per the prevention guide, the intervention for \"Poor nutrition\" includes which of the following? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Provide adequate nutritional and fluid intake","Provide a high-protein diet","Consult a dietitian for nutritional evaluation","Apply moisture-barrier ointment","Provide a pressure-redistribution surface"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: For poor nutrition, the guide recommends adequate nutritional and fluid intake, a high-protein diet, and dietitian consultation for nutritional evaluation.  Why D wrong: Moisture barrier addresses the moisture risk factor.  Why E wrong: Pressure-redistribution surface addresses sensory perception/pressure."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"A nurse caring for a bedridden patient with a pressure ulcer asks how often to turn/reposition a patient who is immobile. Which schedule is expected (most nursing measure)?","options":["Every 8 hours","Every 12 hours","Every 2 hours","Once a shift"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The standard and the source repeatedly state repositioning every 2 hours, especially for immobilised/bedridden clients.  Why A/B/D wrong: Longer intervals allow sustained pressure that causes tissue ischaemia and ulceration."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"(Source MCQ) Which of the following options are NOT related to bed sore (pressure ulcer)? A nurse repositions a bedridden patient every 2 hours and massages the back to relieve pressure. EXCEPT — which statement is the correct EXCEPTION (i.e., the FALSE/unrelated one)?","options":["Early bed sores can be treated with care","Most common site for bed sore is the sacrum","Air mattress cannot reduce the chance of bed sore","Change the client position every 2 hours"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The statement that an air mattress cannot reduce the chance of bed sore is FALSE — pressure-redistribution (air) mattresses DO reduce risk; this is the EXCEPT/odd answer (source Right Answer = C).  Why A wrong: Early bed sores can indeed be treated with care (true statement).  Why B wrong: The sacrum is a common bed-sore site (true).  Why D wrong: Changing position every 2 hours is correct practice (true)."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"Surgical drains are classified into active and passive drains. Which statement correctly describes an ACTIVE drain?","options":["Active drains rely solely on gravity and overflow with no suction","Active drains are maintained under suction, either negative or positive pressure","Active drains can never be a closed system","Active drains are always open Penrose drains"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Active drains are maintained under suction (negative or positive pressure) to actively remove fluid.  Why A wrong: Gravity/overflow describes passive drains.  Why C wrong: A closed active drain (e.g., JP/Hemovac) uses risk-of-getting-infection-reduced closed suction.  Why D wrong: An open Penrose drain is a passive drain."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"Which of the following are examples of CLOSED ACTIVE drains that have less risk of infection because the system is closed? (Select All That Apply)","options":["JP (Jackson-Pratt) drain","Hemovac drain","Sump drain","Penrose drain","Corrugated drain"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: JP, Hemovac, and sump drains are active/closed-suction type drains (per the source's drain images and classification of closed active drains with reduced infection risk).  Why D wrong: A Penrose drain is an open passive drain.  Why E wrong: A corrugated drain is an open passive drain."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"Which statement correctly describes a PASSIVE drain?","options":["Passive drains have no suction and work under pressure or gravity","Passive drains always use negative-pressure suction","Passive drains are exemplified by the Hemovac","Passive drains cannot be open"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Passive drains have no suction and work under pressure or gravity.  Why B wrong: Negative-pressure suction characterizes active drains.  Why C wrong: The Hemovac is an active (suction) drain.  Why D wrong: Open passive drains (e.g., Penrose, corrugated) exist."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"A closed passive drain such as the NGT (nasogastric tube), Foley catheter, and T-tube belongs to which category?","options":["Active closed suction drain","Closed passive drain","Open active drain","Open passive drain"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: NGT, Foley catheter, and T-tube are listed as closed passive drains.  Why A wrong: They do not rely on active suction.  Why C/D wrong: They are closed (not open) and passive (not active)."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"An open passive drain, such as a Penrose drain or corrugated drain, carries which of the following characteristics?","options":["It uses negative-pressure suction","It allows drainage by gravity/capillary action and has a higher risk of getting infection because it is open","It is a closed system with the least infection risk","It is identical to a Hemovac"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Open passive drains (Penrose, corrugated) drain by gravity/capillary action and carry a higher risk of infection because the open system allows microbial entry.  Why A wrong: Open passive drains do not use suction.  Why C wrong: Closed systems (e.g., JP) have the lower infection risk.  Why D wrong: The Hemovac is an active suction drain."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"Which application of dressing is described as one that \"maintains warm humidity which promotes epithelial cell growth\" to promote wound healing?","options":["Moist gauze dressing","Polyurethane film dressing","Hydrocolloid dressing","Absorptive dressing"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A moist gauze dressing maintains warm humidity, which promotes epithelial cell growth.  Why B wrong: Polyurethane film provides a barrier to bacteria and external fluids while promoting moisture.  Why C wrong: Hydrocolloid maintains warm humidity, liquefies necrotic debris, and provides a protective cushion.  Why D wrong: Absorptive dressing acts to absorb drainage."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"Match each dressing to its primary function: A polyurethane film dressing primarily does which of the following?","options":["Provides a barrier to bacteria and external fluids while promoting moisture","Liquefies necrotic debris and cushions","Absorbs heavy exudate","Maintains warm humidity to promote epithelial growth (gauze)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Polyurethane film provides a barrier against bacteria and external fluids and promotes a moist environment.  Why B wrong: That describes hydrocolloid dressing.  Why C wrong: That describes absorptive dressing.  Why D wrong: That describes moist gauze."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"A hydrocolloid dressing performs which of the following functions?","options":["Absorbs drainage only","Maintains warm humidity, liquefies necrotic debris, and provides a protective cushion","Provides only a bacterial barrier","Promotes drying of the wound bed"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A hydrocolloid dressing maintains warm humidity, liquefies necrotic debris, and provides a protective cushion.  Why A wrong: Absorption only describes absorptive dressings.  Why C wrong: Bacterial barrier alone is the polyurethane film function.  Why D wrong: It maintains moisture, it does not dry the wound."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"When administering medications such as antibiotics or applying ointment to a wound, with respect to drainage assessment, a nurse should assess for purulent drainage or exudates that indicate which of the following?","options":["Healthy granulation","Infection","Complete healing","Reactive hyperaemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Purulent drainage/exudate indicates wound infection, warranting assessment and intervention.  Why A wrong: Healthy granulation produces minimal serous, not purulent, drainage.  Why C wrong: Purulence is a sign of infection, not healing.  Why D wrong: Reactive hyperaemia is blanchable redness, unrelated to purulent exudate."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"Which vitamins are specifically recommended in the diet to promote wound healing, along with a balanced, high-protein diet?","options":["Vitamin C and Zinc","Vitamin B12 and Iron","Vitamin K and Calcium","Vitamin D and Magnesium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source advises a high-protein diet with vitamins, especially Vitamin C and Zinc, to promote wound healing.  Why B/C/D wrong: While other nutrients matter generally, the source specifically names Vitamin C and Zinc for wound healing."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"To minimize shearing forces, all bedridden clients should have the head of the bed kept at no more than what degree (if permitted)?","options":["45 degrees","30 degrees","60 degrees","90 degrees"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source instructs keeping the head of the bed at the lowest degree (no more than 30°) to reduce shearing pressure.  Why A/C/D wrong: Higher elevations increase the gravitational sliding/shear that damages deep tissue."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"(Source MCQ) Which of the following methods is used to clean the insertion site of a surgical drain?","options":["Inner to outer following in circular motion","Outer to inner following in circular motion","Central to peripheral","Bottom to top"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The drain insertion site is cleaned from inner to outer in a circular motion (cleanest area first, moving outward) — source Right Answer = A.  Why B wrong: Outer-to-inner would carry contaminants toward the clean insertion site.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the described cleaning techniques for a drain site."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"When providing nursing care for drains, how should the patency of the equipment and tubing be maintained?","options":["Allow twists and kinks so suction is preserved","Check the patency of the equipment, making sure the tubing is free from twists and kinks, and avoid unkinked tubing that compromises wound drainage","Clamp the tubing permanently","Remove the drain after each dressing change"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing care for drains requires checking equipment patency and ensuring the tubing is free from twists and kinks so drainage is not compromised.  Why A wrong: Twists/kinks obstruct drainage and must be prevented.  Why C wrong: Permanent clamping would block drainage.  Why D wrong: Drains are not routinely removed at each dressing change."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"Which of the following are correct nursing-care measures for surgical drains? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Secure the drain to the patient's gown to prevent accidental removal and below the level of the wound","Maintain appropriate negative pressure in the drain","Perform hand hygiene and wear appropriate PPE","Check drain status at least every 4 hours and perform more frequent checks if drainage becomes saturated quickly","Empty and compress the drain before any activity and ambulation"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: All listed measures are part of drain nursing care — securing the drain (preventing accidental removal), maintaining appropriate (negative) pressure, hand hygiene/PPE, checking status at least every 4 hours (more often if saturating), and emptying/compressing before activity/ambulation.  All options are source-listed care measures."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"When cleaning the drain insertion site during a dressing change, in which direction should cleaning be performed, and what solution should be used?","options":["Outer to inner with normal saline","Inner to outer, including cleaning of the suture site with gauze moistened with normal saline","Top to bottom with hydrogen peroxide","Central to peripheral with povidone only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The site is cleaned from inner to outer, including cleaning of the suture site, using gauze moistened with normal saline; the site is dried before applying a new dressing.  Why A wrong: Outer-to-inner contaminates the clean site.  Why C/D wrong: The source specifies normal saline and inner-to-outer technique."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"When removing a drain, what timing and technique does the source recommend?","options":["Remove immediately after surgery regardless of drainage","Removal of the drain should be done at least every 24–48 hours or whenever needed, applying protection to the surrounding skin if needed","Remove only after 1 week with no dressing","Never remove a drain"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states drain removal/dressing change should occur at least every 24–48 hours (or whenever indicated), with skin protection applied to the surrounding skin as needed.  Why A wrong: Drains are removed based on drainage and clinical judgement, not immediately/arbitrarily.  Why C wrong: A drain is not left dry/uncovered for a week.  Why D wrong: Drains are removed when drainage criteria are met."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"In wound suturing, the term \"suture\" is BEST defined as which of the following?","options":["A single thread used to anchor a vessel","A series of stitches that are used to close an open wound and approximate the living tissues","A measure of thread thickness","A device used to encircle a blood vessel"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A suture is a series of stitches used to close an open wound and approximate (bring together) the living tissues.  Why C wrong: That defines the suture's gauge/size, not the suture itself.  Why D wrong: That is a ligature."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"A \"ligature\" is BEST defined as which of the following?","options":["A series of stitches to close a wound","A suture used to encircle a blood vessel to control bleeding","The instrument used to hold a needle","A graft material for aorta replacement"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A ligature is a suture used to encircle a blood vessel to control bleeding.  Why A wrong: That describes a suture in general.  Why C wrong: The needle holder holds the needle.  Why D wrong: Dacron is a graft material for aortic repair."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"The \"suture material\" used in a particular surgical step depends primarily on which of the following?","options":["The colour of the wound","The strength of the tissues to be sutured and the strength required for the wound to regain its strength","The patient's blood group","The nurse's preference only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The choice of suture material depends on the strength of the tissues being sutured and the strength required for the wound to regain its tensile strength.  Why A/C/D wrong: Material selection is based on tissue strength requirements, not wound colour, blood group, or preference alone."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"Suture size is categorized into numbers such as 1, 2, etc. According to the source, what does a higher number / lower number indicate about suture size?","options":["A lower number such as 1, 2 indicates a smaller, finer suture","A higher number indicates a smaller, finer suture (e.g., 2-0, 3-0, 4-0); a lower number indicates a larger/thicker suture","Suture size has no numerical scale","Number indicates colour only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Per the source, sutures are sized so that higher (zero-series, e.g., 2-0, 3-0, 4-0) numbers indicate smaller/finer sutures, while lower whole numbers (1, 2) indicate larger/thicker sutures.  Why A wrong: Lower whole numbers indicate larger, not smaller, sutures.  Why C wrong: Suture size is numerically graded.  Why D wrong: The number reflects thickness/size, not colour."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"The \"suture tensile strength\" is BEST defined as which of the following?","options":["The colour stability of the suture","A measure of the force a suture material can withstand, typically losing 70–80% of its initial strength over time","The length of the suture thread","The number of stitches per centimeter"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Suture tensile strength is a measure of the force the suture can withstand; the source notes it loses 70–80% of its initial strength over time.  Why A/C/D wrong: These describe other properties (colour, length, stitch density), not tensile strength."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"Which local anaesthetic is commonly used for local anaesthesia during suturing, as noted in the source?","options":["2% Lignocaine","5% Bupivacaine","10% Procaine","1% Tetracaine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: 2% Lignocaine (lidocaine) is commonly used for local anaesthesia during suturing.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifically names 2% Lignocaine."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"Which instruments are used for skin suturing? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Needle holder","Toothed forceps","Dissecting forceps","Suture scissors","Stethoscope"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Instruments for skin suturing include the needle holder, forceps (toothed/dissecting forceps), and suture scissors.  Why E wrong: A stethoscope is not a suturing instrument."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"Sutures are broadly classified into two types based on their behaviour in tissue. What are these two types?","options":["Absorbable and non-absorbable","Long and short","Coloured and colourless","Sterile and non-sterile"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The two main types of suture are absorbable and non-absorbable.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the classification basis for suture types."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"Absorbable sutures (temporary support) are degraded in the body. Which of the following is TRUE of plain catgut?","options":["It is made of silk","It loses half its tensile strength in 10–14 days and is effective for ~7–14 days, with complete absorption within 30–50 days","It is a permanent suture","It is non-absorbable"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Plain catgut loses half its tensile strength in 10–14 days, is effective ~7–14 days, and is completely absorbed within 30–50 days.  Why A wrong: Catgut is made from the submucosal layer of sheep intestine or beef serosa, not silk.  Why C/D wrong: Catgut is an absorbable (temporary) suture."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"Absorbable catgut is manufactured from which source material?","options":["Polyester fibres","The submucosal layer of sheep (mammalian) intestine or beef serosa","Stainless steel","Silk filaments"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Catgut is a capable absorbable suture absorbed by living mammalian tissue, manufactured from the submucosal layer of sheep intestine or beef serosa, and is available in plain or chromic forms.  Why A/C/D wrong: Polyester, stainless steel, and silk are non-absorbable materials."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"How does chromic catgut differ from plain catgut in terms of tensile strength and absorption?","options":["Chromic catgut loses half its tensile strength in 21 days and is completely absorbed in 90 days","Chromic catgut loses half its tensile strength in 11–14 days and is completely absorbed in 45–90 days","Chromic catgut is non-absorbable","Chromic catgut is identical to plain catgut"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Chromic catgut loses half its tensile strength in 11–14 days, with complete absorption occurring in 45–90 days (chromic treatment prolongs strength compared with plain catgut).  Why A wrong: The 21-day figure does not match the source's chromic catgut data.  Why C wrong: Chromic catgut is absorbable.  Why D wrong: Chromic catgut differs from plain catgut by prolonged strength/absorption."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"Non-absorbable (permanent support) sutures can be made from which of the following materials? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Silk","Polyester","Polypropylene","Stainless steel","Plain catgut"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Non-absorbable sutures can be made from silk, polyester, polypropylene, or stainless steel.  Why E wrong: Plain catgut is an absorbable suture, not non-absorbable."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"Based on physical structure, sutures are classified as monofilament or multifilament. Which of the following correctly describes a MONOFILAMENT suture?","options":["It is braided or twisted together with more knots needed","It is a single strand of suture material with minimal tissue trauma, harder to handle due to memory, and has less chance of infection","It always harbours more infection than multifilament","Examples include vicryl, silk, and chromic"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A monofilament is a single strand of suture material with minimal tissue trauma; it is harder to handle (memory), needs more care, and carries less chance of infection. Examples: nylon, monocryl, prolene, PDS.  Why A wrong: Braided/twisted with more knots describes multifilament.  Why C wrong: Monofilament has LESS infection chance than multifilament.  Why D wrong: Vicryl, silk, chromic are multifilament examples."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"Which of the following correctly describes a MULTIFILAMENT (braided) suture?","options":["It is a single strand with minimal tissue trauma","Fibres are braided or twisted together, providing more tissue resistance, easier to handle, fewer knots needed, but with more chance of infection; examples include vicryl, silk, chromic","It has less chance of infection than monofilament","Examples include nylon and prolene"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Multifilament (braided) sutures have fibres braided/twisted together, give more tissue resistance, are easier to handle, need fewer knots, but carry more chance of infection; examples include vicryl, silk, chromic.  Why A wrong: Single strand describes monofilament.  Why C wrong: Multifilament has MORE chance of infection (capillary action traps bacteria).  Why D wrong: Nylon and prolene are monofilament examples."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"(Source MCQ) Which suture material preservation/storage agent is used while packing catgut sutures?","options":["Isopropyl alcohol","Colloidal iodine","Glutaraldehyde (Cidex)","Hydrogen peroxide"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Isopropyl alcohol is used as the preservation/storage solution while packing catgut sutures (source Right Answer = A).  Why B/C/D wrong: Colloidal iodine, glutaraldehyde (Cidex), and hydrogen peroxide are disinfectants/sterilants, not the catgut packing preservative noted by the source."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"(Source MCQ) TRUE statement about surgical sutures, EXCEPT — which of the following is the FALSE statement (the EXCEPTION)?","options":["Vicryl is a braided and absorbable suture","Silk is a braided and non-absorbable suture","Monocryl is a monofilament absorbable suture","Nylon is a polypropylene and absorbable suture"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The statement \"Nylon is a polypropylene and absorbable suture\" is FALSE — nylon is a non-absorbable monofilament and is not polypropylene; this is the EXCEPT/odd answer (source Right Answer = D).  Why A wrong: Vicryl is indeed braided and absorbable (true).  Why B wrong: Silk is braided and non-absorbable (true).  Why C wrong: Monocryl is a monofilament absorbable suture (true)."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"According to the Quick Revision Points, which suture is black in colour and mainly used for skin sutures?","options":["Steel","PDS (polydioxanone)","Silk","Polyglactin (Vicryl)"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Silk suture is black in colour and mainly used for skin sutures.  Why A wrong: Steel suture is used for bones, ribs, and sternum.  Why B wrong: PDS (polydioxanone) absorption takes up to 6 months to 1 year.  Why D wrong: Polyglactin (Vicryl) is an absorbable braided suture, not the black skin suture."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"According to the Quick Revision Points, steel suture is mainly used for which structures?","options":["Skin sutures","Bones, ribs, and sternum","Vascular anastomosis","Subcuticular closure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Steel suture is used for bones, ribs, and sternum.  Why A wrong: Silk is used for skin.  Why C wrong: Polypropylene (Prolene) is the choice for vascular anastomosis.  Why D wrong: Monocryl/absorbable monofilament is used for subcuticular closure."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"Per the Quick Revision Points, how long does PDS (polydioxanone) suture take to be absorbed?","options":["10–15 days","30–50 days","Up to 6 months to 1 year","45–90 days"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: PDS suture absorption takes up to 6 months to 1 year.  Why A wrong: That is closer to plain catgut tensile-strength loss timing.  Why B wrong: 30–50 days is plain catgut complete absorption.  Why D wrong: 45–90 days is chromic catgut absorption."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"According to the Quick Revision Points, polyglactin (Vicryl) suture absorption takes how long?","options":["7 days","50–90 days","6 months to 1 year","45 days exactly"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Polyglactin acid (Vicryl) / Dexon absorption takes around 50–90 days.  Why A/C/D wrong: 7 days is too short; 6 months–1 year is PDS; 45 days exactly is not the stated Vicryl range."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"Per the Quick Revision Points, polypropylene (Prolene) suture is commonly used for which procedures? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Hernia repair","Vascular suturing","Tendon repair","Cosmetic skin closure / subcuticular closure","Bone fixation only"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Polypropylene (Prolene) is used for hernia repair, vascular suturing, tendon repair, and for cosmetic skin closure where complete (subcuticular) absorption is not needed (it is non-absorbable and inert, ideal for these uses).  Why E wrong: Bone fixation uses steel suture; Prolene is not specifically for bone."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"Which suture material is described in the Quick Revision Points as the suture of choice for cosmetic skin closure because complete subcuticular absorption is not needed and it is inert?","options":["Plain catgut","Silk","Polypropylene (Prolene)","Steel"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Polypropylene (Prolene) is preferred for cosmetic skin closure since it is inert and does not require complete subcuticular absorption.  Why A wrong: Plain catgut is absorbable and not ideal for cosmetic skin closure.  Why B wrong: Silk is used for skin sutures generally but the source names polypropylene for cosmetic closure.  Why D wrong: Steel is used for bones/ribs/sternum."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"According to the Quick Revision Points, Dacron is used as which of the following?","options":["A skin suture","A graft material for aortic repair (aorta replacement)","An absorbable monofilament","A bone fixation wire"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Dacron is a graft material for aortic repair.  Why A wrong: Silk is the skin suture.  Why C wrong: Dacron is a synthetic non-absorbable polyester, not an absorbable monofilament.  Why D wrong: Steel is the bone fixation material."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"Per the Quick Revision Points, which suture material is recommended for the repair of internal organs (e.g., bowel anastomosis, bile duct anastomosis, muscle closure, and subcutaneous closure)?","options":["Steel","Polyglycolic acid (Dexon-type) / polyglactin","Silk","Dacron"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Polyglycolic acid (and polyglactin/Vicryl-type) absorbable sutures are used for internal organ repair such as bowel anastomosis, bile duct anastomosis, muscle closure, and subcutaneous closure.  Why A wrong: Steel is for bones/ribs/sternum.  Why C wrong: Silk is for skin and is non-absorbable (not ideal inside organs that need absorbable closure).  Why D wrong: Dacron is an aortic graft material."},{"section":"Batch 045 · pressure sore mechanics (shearing/friction force, bony prominences)…","question":"On a suture packet label (e.g., \"2-0, VICRYL RAPIDE, VR945\"), which of the following pieces of information are typically displayed? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Suture size (US gauge, e.g., 2-0)","Suture material type (e.g., braided or absorbable / Vicryl)","Suture length and needle length","Whether the needle is natural or synthetic / absorbable or non-absorbable","Expiry date"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The labelled suture-material diagram shows suture size (US gauge), suture type (braided/absorbable), suture length, suture needle and needle length, natural/synthetic and absorbable/non-absorbable designation, and the expiry date.  All listed items appear on the packet per the source figure."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"A half-circle suture needle has a magnetic resonance/MJC (Mayo Jegcot type) curvature suited to which of the following structures?","options":["Skin suturing","Bowel, bile duct, and vascular anastomosis","Ophthalmic surgeries","Vagina and laparoscopic suturing"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A half (1/2) circle needle is used in bowel, bile duct, and vascular anastomosis.  Why A wrong: Straight needles are used in skin suturing.  Why C wrong: 1/4th (3/8 small-arc) needles such as 5/8 circle are used in ophthalmic surgeries.  Why D wrong: The J-shaped needle is used in vagina and laparoscopic suturing."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"Which suture needle shape is specifically used in vagina and laparoscopic suturing (e.g., hysterectomy and episiotomy)?","options":["Straight needle","Compound curved needle","J-shaped needle","Round body needle"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The J-shaped needle is used in vagina and laparoscopic suturing, e.g., hysterectomy and episiotomy.  Why A wrong: Straight needle is used in skin suturing.  Why B wrong: Compound/curved needle is used in oral cavity.  Why D wrong: Round body is a needle point type, not a curvature shape, used for soft tissue."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"A straight suture needle is best used for which type of tissue/procedure?","options":["Vascular anastomosis","Skin suturing","Oral cavity","Ophthalmic surgery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A straight needle is used in skin suturing.  Why A wrong: Half-circle needles are preferred for vascular anastomosis.  Why C wrong: Compound/curved needle is used in oral cavity.  Why D wrong: 5/8 circle needle is used in ophthalmic surgeries."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"A compound (curved) suture needle is most appropriately used in which anatomic region?","options":["Skin","Oral cavity","Bowel","Vagina"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Compound/curved needle is used in the oral cavity.  Why A wrong: Straight needle is used for skin.  Why C wrong: Half-circle needle is used in bowel/vascular anastomosis.  Why D wrong: J-shaped needle is used in vagina/laparoscopic suturing."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"Which suture needle shape is used in ophthalmic surgeries such as cataract surgery?","options":["1/2 circle","Straight","5/8 circle","J-shaped"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The 5/8 circle needle is used in ophthalmic surgeries, e.g., cataract.  Why A wrong: 1/2 circle is used in bowel/bile duct/vascular anastomosis.  Why B wrong: Straight is used for skin.  Why D wrong: J-shaped is used for vaginal/laparoscopic suturing."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"Which round-body suture needle point is suited to soft tissue (e.g., cardiovascular and intestinal tissue) because it dilates rather than cuts, causing less trauma?","options":["Conventional cutting","Reverse cutting","Round body taper point","Blunt point"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Round body taper point is suited to soft tissue such as cardiovascular and intestinal (bowel) tissue; it dilates rather than cuts, causing less trauma.  Why A wrong: Conventional cutting is very sharp and more traumatic, used for tough tissues like skin.  Why B wrong: Reverse cutting is very sharp and cuts rather than dilates, used to penetrate easily.  Why D wrong: A blunt point does not have a cutting/taper-point profile for these soft tissues described here."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"A conventional cutting needle is BEST described as:","options":["Atraumatic and used for vascular tissue","Very sharp, more traumatic, used for tendon, fascia, and skin (tough tissues) where deeper penetration is needed","A needle that dilates tissue rather than cutting","Used for epidural anaesthesia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Conventional cutting is very sharp, more traumatic, used for tendon, fascia, and skin (tough tissues), where we need to penetrate easily; it cuts rather than dilates.  Why A wrong: It is traumatic, not atraumatic; vascular tissue uses round-body/taper needles.  Why C wrong: It cuts rather than dilates (dilation describes taper/round-body needles).  Why D wrong: The Tuohy needle is used for epidural anaesthesia."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"Which needle point type cuts rather than dilates, is easy to penetrate, and is used in vascular surgeries?","options":["Conventional cutting","Reverse cutting","Tuohy","Blunt"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Reverse cutting is very sharp, cuts rather than dilates, is easy to penetrate, and is used in vascular surgeries.  Why A wrong: Conventional cutting is used for tough tissue (tendon, fascia, skin).  Why C wrong: Tuohy is used for epidural anaesthesia.  Why D wrong: Blunt is used for friable tissues and not described here."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"The taper-cutting needle is ideal for which tissues?","options":["Soft bowel tissue","Tough or calcified tissues, mainly used in cardiac and vascular procedures","Skin and tendon","Epidural space"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Taper cutting is ideal for tough or calcified tissues, mainly used in cardiac and vascular procedures.  Why A wrong: Round body/taper point is suited for soft bowel tissue.  Why C wrong: Conventional cutting is used for skin/tendon.  Why D wrong: Tuohy is used for the epidural space."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"Which suture needle is used for epidural anaesthesia, with a preference for 16 G or 18 G?","options":["Reverse cutting","Round body","Tuohy needle","Taper cutting"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The Tuohy needle is used for epidural anaesthesia, preferably 16 G or 18 G.  Why A wrong: Reverse cutting is used for vascular surgeries.  Why B wrong: Round body is for soft tissue.  Why D wrong: Taper cutting is for tough/calcified cardiac/vascular tissues."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"(Recall the Hardest — p.386) Which of the following needle type is used to fix the surgical drain?","options":["Cutting","Round body","Reverse cutting","Taper point"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book keys Conventional Cutting (Cutting) — very sharp, more traumatic, used for tendon, fascia, and skin, where we need to penetrate easily as it cuts rather than dilates; this is the answer given (A).  Why B wrong: Round body is atraumatic and used for soft tissue, not for fixing a drain through skin.  Why C wrong: Reverse cutting is used in vascular surgeries.  Why D wrong: Taper point is for soft tissue, not for tough skin fixation."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"A suture needle is divided into three parts. Which part is the penetrating zone or tip of the needle?","options":["Needle body","Needle point","Swaged point","Swaged end"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The needle point is the penetrating zone or tip of the needle.  Why A wrong: The needle body holds the core of the needle.  Why C wrong: The swaged point is the place where the suture material is attached.  Why D wrong: \"Swaged end\" refers to where the suture attaches, not the penetrating tip."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"Which part of a suture needle holds the core of the needle?","options":["Needle point","Needle body","Swaged point","Suture eye"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The needle body holds the core of the needle.  Why A wrong: The needle point is the penetrating tip.  Why C wrong: The swaged point is where the suture material is attached.  Why D wrong: A separate \"suture eye\" is not the part described as holding the core."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"The swaged point of a suture needle refers to:","options":["The penetrating tip","The core holder of the needle","The place where the suture material is attached","The midpoint grasped by the needle holder"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The swaged point is the place where the suture material is attached.  Why A wrong: That is the needle point.  Why B wrong: That is the needle body.  Why D wrong: That describes where some surgeons hold the needle, not the swaged point."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"When suturing with a needle holder, the needle should be held at which distance from the needle point?","options":["At the swaged end","At the needle tip","At 2/3rd distance from the needle point and 1/3rd distance from the swaged end","At 1/3rd distance from the needle point"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Suturing is done with the help of a needle holder, holding the needle at 2/3rd distance from the needle point and 1/3rd distance from the swaged end.  Why A wrong: Holding at the swaged end risks bending the needle/suture detachment.  Why B wrong: Holding at the tip damages the penetrating point.  Why D wrong: The correct hold is 2/3rd from the point, not 1/3rd."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"When grasping a suture needle in a needle holder, the needle holder should be clamped at what position along the needle, and at what angle to the swaged end?","options":["At 1/3 distance from the swaged end, maintaining a 90° angle","At the tip, maintaining a 45° angle","At the midpoint, maintaining a 30° angle","At the swaged point, maintaining a 60° angle"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The needle holder is clamped onto the needle approximately 1/3 distance from the swaged end of the needle while maintaining a 90° angle.  Why B/C/D wrong: These positions/angles contradict the figure caption that specifies 1/3 distance from the swaged end at a 90° angle."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"The needle must enter the skin or any structure while penetrating at what angle?","options":["30 degrees","45 degrees","60 degrees","90 degrees"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The needle must enter the skin or any structure while penetrating at 90 degrees.  Why A/B/C wrong: The text specifies 90 degrees for needle entry."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"The No. 10 surgical blade is used for which purpose?","options":["Stab incision and abscess drainage","Fine line incision (most commonly used blade)","Suture removal and inaccessible areas","Long incisions like laparotomy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The No. 10 blade is used for fine line incision and is the most commonly used blade.  Why A wrong: No. 11 blade is used for stab incision, abscess incisions, and arteriotomy.  Why C wrong: No. 12 blade is used for suture removal and inaccessible areas.  Why D wrong: No. 20–22 blades are used for long incisions like laparotomy and thoracotomy."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"Which surgical blade number is used for stab incision, abscess incisions, and arteriotomy?","options":["No. 10","No. 11","No. 12","No. 15"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: No. 11 blade is used for stab incision, abscess incisions, and arteriotomy.  Why A wrong: No. 10 is for fine line incision.  Why C wrong: No. 12 is for suture removal and inaccessible areas (e.g., maxillary tuberosity, retro-molar areas).  Why D wrong: No. 15 is for small skin incisions, commonly used for flaps and precise incisions."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"The No. 12 surgical blade is best suited for:","options":["Suture removal and inaccessible areas such as the maxillary tuberosity and retro-molar areas","Long incisions like laparotomy","Fine line incisions","Stab incisions"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: No. 12 blade is for suture removal and inaccessible areas such as the maxillary tuberosity and retro-molar areas.  Why B wrong: No. 20–22 blades are for long incisions like laparotomy.  Why C wrong: No. 10 is for fine line incisions.  Why D wrong: No. 11 is for stab incisions."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"Which surgical blade is used for small skin incisions and is commonly used for flaps and precise incisions?","options":["No. 10","No. 11","No. 15","No. 20"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: No. 15 blade is for small skin incision, commonly used for flaps and precise incisions.  Why A wrong: No. 10 is for fine line incision.  Why B wrong: No. 11 is for stab incisions/abscess/arteriotomy.  Why D wrong: No. 20 is among the large blades for long incisions (laparotomy/thoracotomy)."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"Which surgical blades are used for long incisions like laparotomy and thoracotomy?","options":["No. 10 and 11","No. 12 and 15","No. 20 and 22","No. 11 and 15"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: No. 20–22 blades are used for long incisions like laparotomy and thoracotomy.  Why A wrong: No. 10/11 are for fine line/stab incisions.  Why B wrong: No. 12/15 are for suture removal/inaccessible areas and small/precise incisions.  Why D wrong: These are smaller blades for fine work, not long incisions."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"Surgical blades are also known by what eponymous name?","options":["Bard Parker blades","Mayo blades","Kelly blades","Allis blades"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The blades used to fix the surgical handles are known as Bard Parker blades.  Why B/C/D wrong: Mayo, Kelly, and Allis are names of surgical clamps/forceps, not the blades described."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"Which Bard Parker (BP) handle size is used for holding No. 10, 11, and 15 size blades?","options":["BP handle size 3","BP handle size 4","BP handle size 7","BP handle size 9"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: BP handle size 3 is used for holding No. 10, 11, and 15 size blades.  Why B wrong: BP handle size 4 is used for holding No. 20 and 22 size blades.  Why C/D wrong: These handle sizes are not specified for these blades on the page."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"BP handle size 4 is used to hold which size blades?","options":["No. 10 and 11","No. 15","No. 20 and 22","No. 11 and 12"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: BP handle size 4 is used for holding No. 20 and 22 size blades.  Why A/B wrong: No. 10, 11, and 15 blades are held with BP handle size 3.  Why D wrong: These blades are held with handle size 3, not 4."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"A scalpel blade is made of which material, providing a sharp tip and cutting edge?","options":["Stainless steel only","Carbon steel","Titanium","Aluminium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The figure labels the surgical blade as being made of carbon steel, with a sharp tip and cutting edge fitting onto the sterilized handle.  Why A/C/D wrong: The page specifies carbon steel for the blade material."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"(Recent MCQ — p.387) Which instrument is required for skin suturing?","options":["Curved scissor","Needle holder","Cheatle forceps","Sponge forceps"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The needle holder is required for skin suturing (answer B as keyed).  Why A wrong: A curved scissor is used for cutting, not for holding the needle during suturing.  Why C wrong: Cheatle forceps are used to transfer sterile instruments, not for suturing.  Why D wrong: Sponge forceps are used to hold swabs/sponges, not for suturing."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"(Instruments required for skin suturing — p.387) Which of the following is NOT listed as an instrument/material required for skin suturing?","options":["Needle holder","Straight scissor","Suture material and toothed forceps","Sponge holding forceps"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The listed instruments/materials for skin suturing are needle holder, straight scissor, suture material, toothed forceps, and BP handle; sponge holding forceps is NOT in the list.  Why A/B/C wrong: Needle holder, straight scissor, suture material, and toothed forceps are all listed."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"(Recall the Hardest — p.387) A blade shown in the image is commonly used for creation of large skin incision such as in laparotomy surgery. Identify the number of the blade.","options":["24","21","22","32"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The blade used for creating large skin incisions such as in laparotomy is No. 22 (answer A as keyed in the box corresponds to the \"22\" option), consistent with the text that No. 20–22 blades are used for long incisions like laparotomy. Note: the printed answer letter is \"A,\" but the factually correct large-incision blade is No. 22.  Why A (No. 24) wrong: 24 is not a standard blade described for laparotomy here.  Why B (No. 21) wrong: 21 is not specified for large incisions on the page.  Why D (No. 32) wrong: 32 is not a recognized large-incision blade."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"Preparation for suturing: when closing a wound by placing sutures, one aim is to:","options":["Increase bleeding to allow drainage","Eliminate any dead space","Promote healing by secondary intention","Maintain tension on the wound edges"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: One aim of placing sutures is to eliminate any dead space.  Why A wrong: An aim is to reduce bleeding and infection, not increase bleeding.  Why C wrong: The aim is to prevent healing by secondary intention, which may result in an unacceptable scar.  Why D wrong: Sutures should support the wound until tensile strength is recovered, not maintain harmful tension; excessive tension causes dehiscence."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"Which is a stated aim of placing sutures to close a wound?","options":["Support the wound until tensile strength is recovered, to prevent wound from dehiscing","Approximate skin edges loosely","Allow healing by secondary intention","Maintain dead space for drainage"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Sutures should support the wound until tensile strength is recovered, to prevent the wound from dehiscing.  Why B wrong: Skin edges should be accurately approximated to produce a cosmetically and functionally acceptable wound (primary intention), not loosely.  Why C wrong: Sutures aim to prevent healing by secondary intention.  Why D wrong: An aim is to eliminate dead space, not maintain it."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"Approximating skin edges accurately during suturing primarily produces healing by:","options":["Secondary intention","Tertiary intention","Primary intention","Delayed primary intention"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Allowing skin edges to be accurately approximated produces a cosmetically and functionally acceptable wound, e.g., primary intention.  Why A wrong: Sutures aim to prevent secondary intention healing (poor scar).  Why B/D wrong: These are not the intention type described for accurate edge approximation in the text."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"A \"simple continuous suture\" is best described as a suture type that is:","options":["Used only for circular or round-shaped defects","Commonly used (a basic running stitch)","A self-adhesive tape","Used to repair a breast lump cavity"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Simple continuous sutures are commonly used.  Why A wrong: Purse string sutures (PSS) are used to repair circular or round-shaped defects.  Why C wrong: Skin adhesive tapes/steri-strips are self-adhesive tapes.  Why D wrong: Skin adhesive tapes/steri-strips may be used after wide resection of a breast lump."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"Mattress sutures are placed in which orientations?","options":["Only horizontal","Only vertical","Vertical and horizontal, to help in accurate approximation","Diagonal only"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Mattress sutures are vertical and horizontal and help in accurate approximation of edges.  Why A/B wrong: Both vertical and horizontal mattress sutures exist.  Why D wrong: Mattress sutures are not described as diagonal."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"Staplers for skin closure are commonly used on which site?","options":["Scalp","Bowel","Vascular anastomosis","Oral cavity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Staplers for skin are commonly used on the scalp, used to approximate the edges of the cut wound.  Why B/C/D wrong: These sites are not the common stapler-for-skin example given; the page specifies scalp."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"Subcuticular sutures are used in which type of surgeries?","options":["Cosmetic surgeries","Vascular surgeries","Bowel anastomosis","Ophthalmic surgeries"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Subcuticular sutures are used in cosmetic surgeries.  Why B/C/D wrong: The page specifies cosmetic surgeries for subcuticular sutures."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"Purse string sutures (PSS) are used for what purpose?","options":["To approximate scalp wounds","To repair circular or round-shaped defects","To close a wound after breast lump resection without sutures","For vascular anastomosis only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Purse string sutures (PSS) are used to repair circular or round-shaped defects.  Why A wrong: Staplers are used for scalp wounds.  Why C wrong: Skin adhesive tapes are used after breast lump resection.  Why D wrong: PSS are defined by their circular/round defect repair, not vascular-only use."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"Self-adhesive skin tapes (steri-strips) may be used in which situation, and what is the trade-off?","options":["Where there is tension and moisture; they are cheap but delay healing","Where there is no tension and not too much moisture (e.g., after wide resection of a breast lump); expensive, quick to use, and do not delay wound healing","Only on infected wounds; slow to apply","Only on bowel; cheap and reusable"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Skin adhesive tapes (steri-strips) may be used where there is no tension and not too much moisture, e.g., after wide resection of a breast lump; they are expensive, quick to use, and do not delay wound healing.  Why A wrong: They are NOT used where there is tension/moisture, and they do not delay wound healing.  Why C/D wrong: Not described for infected wounds or bowel; the page describes tension-free low-moisture skin use."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"According to the X / 2X rule of skin suturing, if the depth of the incised wound is \"X,\" the needle should pass at a distance of how far from the cut margin of the wound?","options":["Half of X","One \"X\" from the cut margin, so the distance between entry and exit point is \"2X\"","Two times X from the margin on each side, totalling 4X","Equal to the suture length needed"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: X/2X rule: if the depth of the incised wound is \"X,\" the needle should pass at a distance of one \"X\" from the cut margin of the wound, and come out at a distance of \"X\" from the other margin, so the distance between entry and exit point of the suture in skin is \"2X.\"  Why A wrong: The distance from the margin is one X, not half X.  Why C wrong: Total entry-to-exit distance is 2X, not 4X.  Why D wrong: Jenkin's rule (not the X/2X rule) relates suture length to wound length."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"According to Jenkin's rule for a continuous suture, the length of suture used should be how many times the length of the wound, and what minimum suture amount is required?","options":["2 times the wound length; minimum 6 cm of suture","4 times the wound length; minimum 6 cm of suture","4 times the wound length; minimum 60 cm of suture","6 times the wound length; minimum 4 cm of suture"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Jenkin's rule: for a continuous suture, the length of suture used should be at least 4 times the length of the wound; for example, the length of suture for a wound 15 cm requires a suture length of 60 cm minimum.  Why A/B wrong: The multiplier is 4 times and the minimum is 60 cm (not 6 cm) for the 15 cm wound example.  Why D wrong: The rule is 4 times, not 6 times."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"According to the skin sutures rule, the needle should always penetrate the skin at what angle, and what does this achieve?","options":["45° angle; minimizes the size of the entry wound and promotes inversion","90° angle; minimizes the size of the entry wound and promotes eversion of the skin edges","30° angle; maximizes the wound size","60° angle; promotes overlap of skin edges"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The needle should always penetrate the skin at a 90° angle, which minimizes the size of the entry wound and promotes eversion of the skin edges.  Why A wrong: The angle is 90°, and it promotes eversion (not inversion).  Why C/D wrong: The specified angle is 90° with eversion of edges; these are incorrect angles/effects."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"Which suture technique can be used to prevent skin injury beneath the extracutaneous loops of suture material, where tissue tubing, cardboard, and gauze are commonly used as materials?","options":["Simple interrupted sutures","Subcuticular sutures","Horizontal mattress sutures (bolsters)","Purse string sutures"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Bolsters can be used to prevent skin injury beneath the extracutaneous loops of suture material in horizontal mattress sutures; tissue tubing, cardboard, and gauze are commonly used as materials.  Why A/B/D wrong: The figure specifically describes bolsters used with horizontal mattress sutures to protect skin under the suture loops."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"During the suturing procedure, what is the correct early step regarding patient positioning?","options":["Position the patient in a comfortable position","Suture the wound before explaining the procedure","Apply dressing before infiltrating anaesthetic","Remove sterile gloves before suturing"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The suturing procedure includes explaining the procedure to the patient and positioning the patient in a comfortable position early on.  Why B wrong: Suturing the wound comes after preparation, anaesthesia, and cleaning.  Why C wrong: Dressing/follow-up is applied at the end, after suturing.  Why D wrong: Aseptic technique requires putting on sterile gloves, not removing them, before suturing."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"During the suturing procedure, the wound is cleaned and the area infiltrated with which agent, and which precaution is emphasized?","options":["2% Lignocaine; it SHOULD NOT be used with adrenaline","2% Lignocaine WITH adrenaline always","Normal saline only; no anaesthetic","5% Lignocaine with adrenaline"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Clean the area around the wound and infiltrate with 2% Lignocaine; it SHOULD NOT be used with adrenaline.  Why B/D wrong: The text specifically states lignocaine should NOT be used with adrenaline here (and concentration is 2%).  Why C wrong: A local anaesthetic (2% lignocaine) is infiltrated, not saline alone."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"When assessing a wound during suturing, if the history suggests shattered or broken glass causing the injury, which investigation is indicated?","options":["Ultrasound","X-ray","CT angiography","MRI"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: When assessing for any foreign bodies — if history suggests shattered or broken glass caused the damage — an X-ray is indicated.  Why A/C/D wrong: The page specifies X-ray for detecting glass foreign bodies in this scenario."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"After cleaning the wound, effectiveness of anaesthesia should be checked by:","options":["Asking the patient verbally only","Gentle pressure from a needle","Applying the dressing","Suturing immediately without checking"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Check the effectiveness of anaesthesia by gentle pressure from a needle.  Why A wrong: The described method is gentle pressure with a needle, not verbal report alone.  Why C wrong: Dressing is applied at the end, after suturing.  Why D wrong: Anaesthesia effectiveness must be checked before suturing."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"According to the general guidelines for suture removal times, sutures on the face should typically be removed in:","options":["3–5 days or early","6–8 days","10–14 days","7–10 days"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Sutures on the face are removed in 3–5 days or early; early suture removal is especially important in cosmetically important areas.  Why B wrong: Scalp sutures are removed in 6–8 days.  Why C wrong: Palm and soles are removed in 10–14 days; limbs, joints, and back also in 10–14 days.  Why D wrong: Abdominal sutures are removed in 7–10 days."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"Scalp sutures are generally removed in how many days?","options":["3–5 days","6–8 days","10–14 days","7–10 days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Scalp sutures are removed in 6–8 days.  Why A wrong: 3–5 days is for the face.  Why C wrong: 10–14 days is for palm/soles and limbs/joints/back.  Why D wrong: 7–10 days is for abdominal sutures."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"Sutures on the palm and soles are typically removed in:","options":["3–5 days","6–8 days","10–14 days","7–10 days"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Palm and soles sutures are removed in 10–14 days.  Why A wrong: 3–5 days is for the face.  Why B wrong: 6–8 days is for the scalp.  Why D wrong: 7–10 days is for the abdomen."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"Sutures over the limbs, joints, and back are typically removed in:","options":["3–5 days","6–8 days","10–14 days","7–10 days"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Limbs, joints, and back sutures are removed in 10–14 days.  Why A wrong: 3–5 days is for the face.  Why B wrong: 6–8 days is for the scalp.  Why D wrong: 7–10 days is for the abdomen."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"Abdominal sutures are typically removed in how many days?","options":["3–5 days","6–8 days","10–14 days","7–10 days"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Abdominal sutures are removed in 7–10 days.  Why A wrong: 3–5 days is for the face.  Why B wrong: 6–8 days is for the scalp.  Why C wrong: 10–14 days is for palm/soles and limbs/joints/back."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"Why is early suture removal especially important in cosmetically important areas (e.g., face)?","options":["To reduce cost of dressings","Because the area heals faster and early removal minimizes scarring/track marks","To allow secondary-intention healing","To increase wound tension"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Early suture removal is especially important in cosmetically important areas, consistent with face sutures being removed at 3–5 days or earlier.  Why A wrong: Cost is not the stated reason.  Why C wrong: Sutures aim to prevent secondary-intention healing.  Why D wrong: The aim is appropriate tensile support, not increased tension."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"Surgical knots (ligatures) are defined as:","options":["Knots used to bind suture materials together while binding tissue in surgery","Self-adhesive tapes","Devices used to cut suture","Stapler types only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Surgical knots (ligatures) are the knots used to bind suture materials together while binding tissue in surgery.  Why B wrong: Self-adhesive tapes (steri-strips) are a separate closure method.  Why C wrong: Knots bind, they do not cut.  Why D wrong: Staplers are separate devices, not knots/ligatures."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"Which surgical knot is used to prevent slippage and is particularly used for non-absorbable monofilament sutures?","options":["Granny knot","Square knot","Surgeon's knot","Reef knot"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The Surgeon's knot prevents slippage and is particularly used for non-absorbable monofilament sutures.  Why A wrong: The granny knot is a slip knot (crossings in opposite directions, less secure).  Why B/D wrong: The square knot (reef knot) is a commonly used reliable knot but is not the one specifically cited to prevent slippage for non-absorbable monofilament sutures."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"Among commonly used surgical knots, which is a \"slip knot\"?","options":["Square knot","Granny knot","Reef knot","Surgeon's knot"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Granny knot is a slip knot.  Why A/C wrong: The square knot is also called a reef knot and is a secure (non-slip) knot.  Why D wrong: The surgeon's knot prevents slippage."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"The square knot is also known as which knot?","options":["Surgeon's knot","Granny knot","Reef knot","Slip knot"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The square knot is also called the reef knot.  Why A wrong: The surgeon's knot is a distinct knot for non-absorbable monofilament sutures.  Why B/D wrong: The granny knot is the slip knot, not the square knot."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"(Should Remember — p.389) Which technique must be maintained during suturing to prevent wound infection?","options":["Clean technique","Aseptic technique","Medical asepsis only","Sterile-to-clean transition technique"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Aseptic technique must be maintained during suturing to prevent wound infection.  Why A/C/D wrong: The page specifically emphasizes aseptic (surgical asepsis) technique, not merely clean/medical asepsis."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"(Should Remember — p.389) Why should local anaesthetic such as lignocaine NOT be injected directly into inflamed tissue or directly into a traumatised tissue?","options":["It causes immediate allergy","This can result in the lignocaine being so rapidly absorbed that a systemic reaction rather than a local reaction is produced","It increases wound tension","It promotes secondary-intention healing"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Any local anaesthetic such as lignocaine should not be injected directly into inflamed/traumatised tissue, as this can result in the lignocaine being so rapidly absorbed that a systemic rather than a local reaction is produced.  Why A wrong: The stated risk is systemic absorption, not immediate allergy.  Why C/D wrong: These are unrelated to the rapid systemic absorption concern."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"Standard precautions are infection prevention practices that apply to:","options":["Only clients with confirmed infections","All clients, instead of particular infected clients","Only immunocompromised patients","Only operating-room patients"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Standard precautions are infection prevention practices that apply to all clients, instead of particular infected clients.  Why A/C/D wrong: Standard precautions apply to ALL clients, not a selected subset."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"Standard precautions should be implemented when contact with which of the following is anticipated? (Select all that apply)","options":["Blood","All body fluids, secretions, and excretions (with exception of sweat), regardless of whether or not they contain visible blood","Non-intact skin (this includes rashes)","Mucous membranes","Only visibly bloody surfaces"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Standard precautions are implemented when contact is anticipated with blood; all body fluids, secretions, and excretions (except sweat), regardless of visible blood; non-intact skin (including rashes); and mucous membranes.  Why E wrong: The principle applies regardless of whether fluids contain visible blood, so it is not limited to only visibly bloody surfaces."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"Standard precautions are the minimum standard of infection prevention and control (IPC) practices that should be used by health-care workers during the care of:","options":["All patients, at all times, in all settings","Only infected patients in isolation","Only patients in the ICU","Only patients during surgery"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: They are the minimum standard of infection prevention and control practices used by health-care workers during the care of ALL patients, at ALL times, in ALL settings.  Why B/C/D wrong: Standard precautions are universal, not restricted to isolation, ICU, or surgery."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"Which of the following is included as a standard precaution practice? (Select all that apply)","options":["Risk assessment","Hand hygiene","Use of PPEs (gloves, gowns, masks, goggles, shoe covers)","Safe injection practices or maintaining aseptic technique","Withholding all hand washing to conserve resources"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Standard precautions include risk assessment, hand hygiene, use of PPEs (gloves, gowns, masks, goggles, shoe covers), safe injection practices/maintaining aseptic technique, safe handling of patient care equipment, cleaning and disinfection (sterile instruments/devices, clean and disinfected environmental surfaces), handling of linen and laundry, respiratory hygiene/cough etiquette, sharp injury prevention/safe work practice controls, patient placement, and disposal of waste (BMWM).  Why E wrong: Hand hygiene is a core standard precaution; withholding it is incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"As part of respiratory hygiene/cough etiquette, what is recommended for sneezing/coughing patients?","options":["Ask patients to wear a mask or use a tissue to cover their cough and place acute respiratory symptomatic patients at least 1 metre (3 feet) away from others in common waiting areas","No precaution is required","Place symptomatic patients in direct contact with others","Use only gloves"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Asking patients to wear a mask or use a tissue to cover their cough; place acute respiratory symptomatic patients at least 1 metre (3 feet) away from others in common waiting areas.  Why B/C wrong: A precaution and spacing are required to limit transmission.  Why D wrong: Respiratory hygiene involves masks/tissue and spacing, not gloves alone."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"Under patient placement, a single room should be used for a patient who:","options":["Has no infectious risk","Poses a risk of transmission to others (e.g., if they contaminate the environment or have symptoms of a transmissible infection)","Requests it for comfort only","Is awaiting suture removal"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A single room should be used for a patient who poses a risk of transmission to others, for example if they contaminate the environment or have symptoms of a transmissible infection.  Why A/C/D wrong: Placement in a single room is for transmission risk, not absence of risk, comfort, or routine suture removal."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"Hand hygiene is best defined as:","options":["The act of cleaning hands for the purpose of removing soil, dirt, and microorganisms","The use of gloves only","Sterilizing the patient's skin","Disposal of biomedical waste"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hand washing or hand hygiene is the act of cleaning hands for the purpose of removing soil, dirt, and microorganisms.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are separate concepts (PPE, antisepsis, BMWM), not the definition of hand hygiene."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"World Hand Hygiene Day, launched by WHO in 2009 to improve hand hygiene in healthcare, is celebrated on which date every year?","options":["5th May","15th October","7th April","12th May"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Every year, 5th May is celebrated as World Hand Hygiene Day; the \"SAVE LIVES: CLEAN YOUR HANDS\" campaign was launched by WHO in 2009 to improve hand hygiene in healthcare.  Why B wrong: 15th October is Global Handwashing Day (not the date given here).  Why C/D wrong: These dates are not the one specified in the text (5th May)."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"Hand washing done before general procedures is also called:","options":["Surgical handscrub","Routine hand washing","Hygienic hand rub","Antiseptic hand rub"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hand washing done before general procedures is called Routine hand washing.  Why A wrong: Surgical handscrub is done before a surgical procedure.  Why C wrong: Hygienic handscrub uses alcohol-based antiseptic for hand hygiene with hand rub.  Why D wrong: Antiseptic hand rub is part of hygienic hand rub, not the routine pre-general-procedure wash."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"Hand scrubbing before a surgical procedure is specifically called:","options":["Routine hand washing","Hygienic hand rub","Surgical handscrub","Social hand washing"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Hand scrubbing done before a surgical procedure is called Surgical handscrub.  Why A wrong: Routine hand washing is done before general procedures.  Why B wrong: Hygienic hand rub uses alcohol-based solution for hand hygiene.  Why D wrong: \"Social hand washing\" is not the term specified for the pre-surgical scrub."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"For hand washing with soap and water, what is the recommended total contact time?","options":["Total time: 11 seconds, each step done five times","Total time: 11, each step to be done five times — washing hands until visibly dirty or soiled with body fluids","Total time: 8 seconds","Total time: 20–30 seconds"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Per the WHO guidelines table, hand washing with soap and water uses water flowing in running water with soap, preferably liquid soap; total time is 11 (seconds), with each step to be done five times; indication is when hands are visibly dirty or soiled with body fluids.  Why A wrong: It omits the \"five times per step\" / visibly soiled indication detail.  Why C wrong: Total time 8 corresponds to the hand hygiene with hand rub (alcohol-based solution).  Why D wrong: 20–30 seconds corresponds to the alcohol-based hand rub procedure time."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"For hand hygiene with hand rub (alcohol-based solution), what is the indicated total time and condition for use?","options":["Total time 11 seconds; only when hands are visibly soiled","Total time 8; time of procedure 20–30 seconds; indication when hands are NOT visibly soiled, with movements of hand hygiene","Total time 2–6 minutes; for surgical scrub","Total time 60 seconds; for visibly bloody hands"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hand hygiene with hand rub (alcohol-based solution) uses an alcohol-based antiseptic solution; total time is 8, time of procedure is 20–30 seconds, indicated when hands are NOT visibly soiled (e.g., visibly dirty), and uses the movements of hand hygiene. Note: an alcohol-based hand rub is not available; use antiseptic soap and water if hands are visibly soiled.  Why A wrong: Alcohol rub is used when hands are NOT visibly soiled.  Why C wrong: 2–6 minutes is the surgical handscrub/washing time.  Why D wrong: For visibly bloody hands, soap-and-water washing is indicated, not alcohol rub."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"Surgical handscrub/washing achieves which microbiological goal and takes how long?","options":["Removes only transient flora; takes 11 seconds","Pre-surgical hand asepsis done using an aseptic scrub or antiseptic soap to eliminate transient flora and reduce resident flora; time duration 2–6 minutes, average 3 minutes","Sterilizes the skin completely; takes 30 seconds","Only removes visible dirt; takes 8 seconds"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Surgical handscrub/washing is pre-surgical hand asepsis done using an aseptic scrub or antiseptic soap to eliminate transient flora and reduce resident flora; time duration is 2–6 minutes, average 3 minutes.  Why A wrong: It also reduces resident flora and takes 2–6 minutes, not 11 seconds.  Why C wrong: It reduces (not completely sterilizes) resident flora and takes longer than 30 seconds.  Why D wrong: It targets both transient and resident flora over 2–6 minutes, not 8 seconds for visible dirt only."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"(Recall the Hardest — p.390) According to WHO, the duration for hand washing with an alcohol-based sanitizer (hand rub) is:","options":["20–30 seconds","10–20 seconds","40–60 seconds","5–5 minutes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Per WHO, the duration for hand washing/hand rub with an alcohol-based sanitizer is 20–30 seconds (answer A as keyed).  Why B wrong: 10–20 seconds is too short for the alcohol-based hand rub standard.  Why C wrong: 40–60 seconds exceeds the stated alcohol-based hand rub duration.  Why D wrong: Minutes-range duration applies to surgical scrub, not the alcohol hand rub."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"(Recall the Hardest — p.390) Which action should be done if hand soiled with visible particles, except as a routine cover-with-soap step?","options":["A hand clean with hand rub or sanitizer","Do not touch the patient","Hand washing with soap and water","Do not assist the doctor"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: When hands are soiled with visible particles, hand washing with soap and water should be done (the keyed answer, A in the box, corresponds to washing with soap/water — alcohol rub is inappropriate for visibly soiled hands). Per the WHO table, if an alcohol-based hand rub is not available or hands are visibly soiled, antiseptic soap and water must be used.  Why A wrong: Alcohol-based hand rub/sanitizer is not recommended when hands are visibly soiled with particles.  Why B/D wrong: These are unrelated clinical actions, not the hand hygiene method for visibly soiled hands."},{"section":"Batch 046 · Suture needle shapes & types, parts of a suture needle…","question":"Bio-Medical Waste Management (BMWM) is referenced under standard precautions as part of which practice?","options":["Respiratory hygiene","Safe disposal of waste (disposal of waste, risk-of-BMWM)","Surgical handscrub","Patient education only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Disposal of waste — risk of Bio-Medical Waste Management (BMWM) — is listed among standard precaution practices for safe waste disposal.  Why A wrong: Respiratory hygiene addresses cough etiquette, not waste disposal.  Why C wrong: Surgical handscrub is hand hygiene, not waste management.  Why D wrong: Patient education is not the BMWM/waste disposal category."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"The WHO moments of hand hygiene are also popularly known by which name?","options":["The five pillars of asepsis","My five moments of hand hygiene","The hand hygiene triad","The clean-care checklist"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene are also called \"My five moments of hand hygiene.\"  Why A wrong: \"Five pillars of asepsis\" is not a recognized WHO term.  Why C wrong: There are five moments, not three; \"triad\" is incorrect.  Why D wrong: This is an invented phrase not used by WHO."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"Among the WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene, how many moments occur BEFORE touching the patient and how many AFTER?","options":["Three before and two after","Two before and three after","One before and four after","Four before and one after"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Two moments are \"before\" and three moments are \"after\" touching the patient (NORCET-2022).  Why A wrong: It is the reverse; only two are before.  Why C wrong: There are two before, not one.  Why D wrong: There are only two before-moments, not four."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"Which of the following is the FIRST of the WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene?","options":["After body fluid exposure risk","Before touching a patient","After touching a patient","After touching patient surroundings"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Moment 1 is \"Before touching a patient\" — to protect the patient against harmful germs carried on the hands.  Why A wrong: \"After body fluid exposure risk\" is moment 3.  Why C wrong: \"After touching a patient\" is moment 4.  Why D wrong: \"After touching patient surroundings\" is moment 5."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"WHO Moment 2 of hand hygiene — \"Before clean/aseptic procedure\" — is performed to achieve which goal?","options":["To protect yourself from the patient's germs","To protect the patient against harmful germs, including the patient's own germs, from entering the body","To protect the environment from contamination","To document compliance only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Moment 2 (\"Before clean/aseptic procedure\") protects the patient against harmful germs, including the patient's own germs, from entering the body.  Why A wrong: Protecting yourself is the rationale for after-exposure moments, not moment 2.  Why C wrong: Environmental protection is not the stated rationale of moment 2.  Why D wrong: Documentation is not the purpose of hand hygiene moments."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"WHO Moment 3 of hand hygiene is performed at which point?","options":["Before touching a patient","Before a clean/aseptic procedure","After body fluid exposure risk","Before touching patient surroundings"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Moment 3 is \"After body fluid exposure risk\" — clean hands immediately after exposure to protect yourself and the health-care environment from harmful patient germs.  Why A/B wrong: These are the two \"before\" moments (1 and 2).  Why D wrong: \"Touching patient surroundings\" relates to an after-moment (moment 5)."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"According to the WHO 5 Moments, after which event should the nurse clean hands \"when leaving the patient's side\"?","options":["After touching a patient (Moment 4)","Before touching a patient","Before a clean/aseptic procedure","After touching patient surroundings (Moment 5)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Moment 4, \"After touching a patient,\" is performed immediately after touching a patient and his/her surroundings when leaving the patient's side, to protect yourself and the health-care environment.  Why D wrong: Moment 5 (\"After touching patient surroundings\") applies after touching objects/furniture in the patient's immediate surroundings, even if the patient has not been touched.  Why B/C wrong: These are the \"before\" moments, not after leaving the patient."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"WHO Moment 5 (\"After touching patient surroundings\") should be performed in which situation?","options":["Only when the patient has been physically touched","After touching any object or furniture in the patient's immediate surroundings, even if the patient has not been touched","Only before entering the patient's room","Only after a clean/aseptic procedure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Moment 5 requires hand hygiene after touching any object or furniture in the patient's immediate surroundings, even if the patient has not been touched, to protect yourself and the environment.  Why A wrong: It applies even when the patient is not touched.  Why C/D wrong: These describe other moments/timings, not moment 5."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"A NORCET-style question asks: \"All of the following are correct about WHO 5 hand hygiene movements EXCEPT?\" Which option is NOT one of the WHO 5 moments?","options":["After touching a patient","Before touching a patient","Before touching patient surroundings","Before any procedure"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The book keys the answer as C; however the source option list reads \"Before touching patient surroundings\" — note this discrepancy. The factually correct EXCEPT is the option that does not match a true WHO moment. WHO moment 5 is \"AFTER touching patient surroundings,\" so \"Before touching patient surroundings\" (option C) is incorrect, matching the keyed answer C. (\"Before any procedure\" maps loosely to moment 2.) Per the source key, the answer is C.  Why A wrong: \"After touching a patient\" is a valid WHO moment (4).  Why B wrong: \"Before touching a patient\" is a valid WHO moment (1).  Why D wrong (as the EXCEPT): \"Before any procedure\" corresponds to moment 2 (before clean/aseptic procedure)."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"The WHO-recommended duration for performing a hand RUB (alcohol-based handrub) is:","options":["10–15 seconds","20–30 seconds","40–60 seconds","2–3 minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: WHO states the duration of the entire handrub procedure is 20–30 seconds.  Why A wrong: 10–15 seconds is too short for adequate coverage.  Why C wrong: 40–60 seconds is the duration for handWASH with soap and water.  Why D wrong: 2–3 minutes corresponds to surgical scrub, not routine handrub."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"The WHO-recommended duration for performing a hand WASH with soap and water is:","options":["20–30 seconds","40–60 seconds","5–10 seconds","3–5 minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: WHO states the duration of the entire handwash procedure is 40–60 seconds.  Why A wrong: 20–30 seconds is the handrub duration.  Why C wrong: 5–10 seconds is too short and not recommended.  Why D wrong: 3–5 minutes corresponds to surgical scrub."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"When should a health worker use the alcohol-based HANDRUB rather than soap and water for hand hygiene?","options":["When hands are visibly soiled","When hands are NOT visibly soiled (otherwise use handrub as the routine method)","Only after handling sharps","Only before meals"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: WHO guidance — \"Wash hands when visibly soiled! Otherwise, use handrub.\" Handrub is for hands that are not visibly soiled.  Why A wrong: Visibly soiled hands require soap-and-water handwash.  Why C wrong: Handrub use is not limited to after handling sharps.  Why D wrong: Hand hygiene timing follows the 5 moments, not meal-based only."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"The first action step in the WHO handwash technique (after wetting hands and applying soap) is to:","options":["Rub palm to palm","Apply enough soap to cover all hand surfaces","Rinse hands with water","Dry hands with a single-use towel"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After wetting hands with water, apply enough soap to cover all hand surfaces, then begin palm-to-palm rubbing.  Why A wrong: Palm-to-palm rubbing comes after applying soap.  Why C wrong: Rinsing is one of the final steps.  Why D wrong: Drying with a single-use towel is the final step."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"In the WHO handwash technique, the FINAL step after rinsing is to:","options":["Apply more soap","Dry hands thoroughly with a single-use towel","Use the towel to turn off the tap (and hands are now safe)","Rub the backs of fingers"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: After drying with a single-use towel, the towel is used to turn off the tap, after which the hands are safe.  Why B wrong: Drying precedes the tap-closing step; both are end steps but turning off the tap with the towel is last.  Why A wrong: No further soap is applied.  Why D wrong: Rubbing the backs of fingers is a mid-sequence rubbing step."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"Which alcohol concentration does the source recommend for cleaning the access or port of a vial before inserting a needle into the vial?","options":["50% alcohol","60% alcohol","70% alcohol","90% alcohol"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Use 70% alcohol for cleaning the access or port of a vial before inserting the needle into the vial (safe injection practice).  Why A/B wrong: Lower than the recommended 70%.  Why D wrong: 90% is higher than the stated 70%."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"Before starting surgical hand preparation (surgical scrub), the nails should be:","options":["Long and polished","Kept clean, short, and free from nail polish","Covered with artificial nails","Decorated with jewellery rings only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Key steps before surgical hand preparation include keeping nails short and paying attention to them when washing hands; nail polish (and artificial nails) are not allowed. The text states \"Do not wear artificial nails or nail polish.\"  Why A wrong: Long, polished nails harbour microbes and are not allowed.  Why C wrong: Artificial nails are prohibited.  Why D wrong: Rings/jewellery (watches, bracelets) must be removed before scrubbing."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"Why must nailbrushes NOT be used during surgical hand preparation, according to the source?","options":["They are too expensive","They may damage the skin and encourage shedding of cells","They cannot reach the fingernails","They are sterile and reserved for surgeons only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Nailbrushes should not be used as they may damage the skin and encourage shedding of cells.  Why A wrong: Cost is not the stated reason.  Why C wrong: The concern is skin damage/cell shedding, not reach.  Why D wrong: They are not recommended at all for this purpose."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"If hands are visibly dirty before surgical washing, what should be done first?","options":["Proceed directly with alcohol-based handrub","Wash with soap and water before surgical washing, removing debris from underneath the fingernails using running water","Skip washing and start the scrub","Use only a nailbrush"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If visibly soiled, hands should be washed with soap and water before surgical washing; debris from underneath the fingernails is removed using a nail cleaner under running water.  Why A wrong: Alcohol handrub is ineffective on visibly soiled hands and is applied to clean/dry hands.  Why C wrong: Visibly dirty hands must be washed first.  Why D wrong: A nailbrush is not recommended; a nail cleaner under running water is used."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"When performing surgical hand scrub using an antimicrobial soap solution, the hands and forearms should be scrubbed up to how many inches above the elbow, for what duration?","options":["1 inch above the elbow for 1 minute","3 inches above the elbow for a period of 2–6 minutes","5 inches above the elbow for 10 minutes","2 inches above the elbow for 30 seconds"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Scrub hands and forearms till 3 inches above the elbow for a period of 2–6 minutes, using long scrub times (e.g., 10 minutes) only if not necessary; dry hands and arms using a sterile towel, then bin the gloves.  Why A/C/D wrong: These distances and durations do not match the source-stated 3 inches above the elbow for 2–6 minutes."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"If a sterile towel is NOT available for hand washing during surgical hand preparation, what should be done?","options":["Use any reusable cloth towel","Air-dry the hands and arms","Use the surgical gown","Wave hands in the air rapidly"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If a sterile towel is not available for hand washing, the alcohol-based solution scrub is preferred (and per the surgical scrub steps, hands and arms are air-dried before donning gown and gloves).  Why A wrong: A non-sterile reusable towel would recontaminate clean hands.  Why C wrong: The gown must not be used to dry hands.  Why D wrong: Vigorous waving is not the recommended drying method."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"During the surgical scrub with soap and water (INSIDE OT), when should timing of the scrub begin?","options":["After gloving","From the moment soap is applied, washing fingertips to 3 inches above the elbow","Only after rinsing","After donning the gown"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: \"Start timing: Wash from fingertips to 3 inches above the elbow.\" The surgical scrub timing starts at the wash, beginning surgical hand asepsis (e.g., apply 3–5 ml of antimicrobial solution and start at the fingertips and nails).  Why A/D wrong: Timing begins at the start of washing, not after gloving/gowning.  Why C wrong: Timing begins with the wash, not only at rinsing."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"During the surgical scrub, each finger, the area between the fingers, and the back and front of the hand should be scrubbed for how long?","options":["30 seconds each side","1 minute","2 minutes","5 minutes"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to scrub each side of each finger, between the fingers, and the back and front of the hand for 2 minutes.  Why A/B/D wrong: These durations do not match the source-stated 2 minutes for this scrub component."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"During the surgical scrub, how should each side of the arm be washed relative to the elbow?","options":["From the elbow down to the fingers","From the wrist to the fingertips only","From the lower forearm up to the elbow, keeping hands above the elbow at all times","From the elbow upward to the shoulder"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Wash each side of the arm from the wrist/lower forearm up to the elbow (about 30 seconds), then repeat on the other hand and arm, keeping the hands above the elbow at all times.  Why A wrong: Direction is toward the elbow (clean-to-dirty), keeping hands highest.  Why B wrong: The scrub extends up to and above the elbow, not only to the wrist.  Why D wrong: Scrubbing toward the shoulder is not recommended."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"During the surgical scrub, why must the hands always be kept HIGHER than the elbows at all times?","options":["To reduce muscle fatigue","To avoid recontamination of the hands by water from the elbows (lower forearm)","To make rinsing faster","To keep the gown dry"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Keep the hands higher than the arm at all times to avoid recontamination of the hands by water from the elbows / lower forearm.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the stated rationale; the purpose is to prevent contaminated water running back onto clean hands."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"During the surgical scrub procedure, how should the hands and arms be RINSED?","options":["In any direction, briskly back and forth","By passing the hands through the water in one direction only, from fingertips to elbow","Only the hands, not the arms","By submerging both arms fully in a basin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Rinse hands and arms by passing through the water in one direction only, from fingertips to elbow; do not move the arms back and forth through the water.  Why A wrong: Back-and-forth movement risks recontamination.  Why C wrong: Both hands and arms are rinsed.  Why D wrong: One-directional rinsing under flowing water, not submersion, is described."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"After the surgical scrub, how should the hands and arms be held while moving to the operating room and during gowning/gloving?","options":["Hanging down by the sides","Held with the arms upright and bent at the elbow, not touching any other surface","Resting on a sterile counter","Crossed over the chest"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Proceed to the operating room for gowning and gloving while holding the arms upright and bent at the elbow; do not touch any other surface.  Why A wrong: Hanging arms down lets water run back onto clean hands.  Why C wrong: Touching a counter would contaminate the hands.  Why D wrong: Crossing arms over the chest risks contamination and is not described."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"At all times during the surgical scrub procedure, care should be taken NOT to:","options":["Bend the elbows","Splash water onto the surgical attire","Rinse the fingertips","Use antimicrobial soap"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: At all times during the scrub procedure, care should be taken not to splash water onto the surgical attire.  Why A wrong: The arms are deliberately bent at the elbow when moving.  Why C wrong: Fingertips are washed/rinsed as part of the scrub.  Why D wrong: Antimicrobial soap is used in the soap-and-water scrub."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"Once in the operating theatre, the hands and arms should be dried using which item before donning a sterile gown and gloves?","options":["A reusable cotton towel","A sterile towel and aseptic technique","The surgical gown","A paper tissue"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Once in the operating theatre, hands and arms should be dried using a sterile towel and aseptic technique before donning a sterile gown and gloves.  Why A/D wrong: Non-sterile towels/tissues would recontaminate clean hands.  Why C wrong: The gown is not used to dry hands."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"A NORCET-style question: When the OT in-charge instructs scrubbing of staff nurses, which is the correct order of preference (Gloves 1; Gown 2; Scrub 3; Check instrument 4)? The source keys this question.","options":["1, 2, 3, 4","3, 2, 1, 5/4","3, 2, 4, 5/1","4, 3, 2, 1"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source keys the answer as B. The logical preparation sequence is Scrub first (3), then Gown (2), then Gloves (1), with instrument check (4) following — i.e., scrub → gown → glove. The book's keyed answer is B.  Why A wrong: Gloving before scrubbing/gowning is incorrect (sterility violated).  Why C/D wrong: These orders do not match the source-keyed answer (B)."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"Before beginning surgical handrubbing with an alcohol-based handrub (ABHR), the source advises that hands should first be:","options":["Wet with water","Washed with soap and water on arrival in the OR (after donning theatre clothing — cap/hat/bonnet and mask)","Dipped in antiseptic solution","Covered with sterile gloves"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Handwash with soap and water on arrival in the OR, after having donned theatre clothing (cap/hat/bonnet and mask), before surgical handrubbing with ABHR.  Why A wrong: ABHR is applied to dry hands, not wet hands.  Why C wrong: Dipping in antiseptic is not the described step.  Why D wrong: Gloves are donned after the handrub procedure, not before."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"During surgical handrubbing, if visibly soiled or if biological fluids are present, the source advises to:","options":["Continue with ABHR only","Use alcohol-based handrub (ABHR) for surgical hand preparation, but handwash with soap and water if visibly soiled / following removal of gloves at end of operation","Skip hand hygiene","Use only a nailbrush"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Use ABHR for surgical hand preparation; however, if hands are visibly soiled or biological fluids are present, perform a handwash with soap and water (and following the operation, the handwash with soap and water is done after removing gloves).  Why A wrong: ABHR alone is insufficient when hands are visibly soiled.  Why C wrong: Hand hygiene must not be skipped.  Why D wrong: Nailbrushes are not recommended."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"In the surgical handrubbing technique, after applying the handrub to the right hand and forearm, the rub should be smeared up to the elbow using circular movements until the whole skin area is covered. This step should continue until the handrub has fully evaporated, taking approximately:","options":["5 seconds","10–15 seconds","30 seconds","60 seconds"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Smear the handrub on the right forearm up to the elbow using circular movements until the whole skin area is covered, until the handrub has evaporated (10–15 seconds).  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the source-stated 10–15 seconds for this step."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"In the surgical handrubbing technique, after coating the forearms, the WHO recommends repeating the entire handrub sequence (average 60 seconds per application). The number of times depends on what?","options":["The size of the operating room","The number of times that adds up to the total duration recommended by the ABHR manufacturer's instructions (could be two or three times)","The surgeon's preference only","A fixed five times always"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: WHO advises repeating the sequence (average 60 seconds) the number of times that adds up to the total duration recommended by the ABHR manufacturer's instructions — this could be two or three times.  Why A/C wrong: Repetitions are determined by manufacturer-recommended total contact time, not room size or surgeon whim.  Why D wrong: It is not a fixed five times; typically two or three."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"In the surgical handrubbing technique, the source states that approximately how much ABHR should be applied into the palm of one hand (using the elbow of the other arm to operate the dispenser)?","options":["1 dose (5 ml)","1 dose (3 ml)","3 ml as a single squirt regardless","10 ml"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Put approximately 1 dose (3 ml) of ABHR in the palm of the left hand, using the elbow of the other arm to operate the dispenser.  Why A wrong: The source specifies 3 ml per dose, not 5 ml.  Why C wrong: It is a measured single dose (3 ml), described as \"1 dose.\"  Why D wrong: 10 ml is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"In the surgical handrubbing technique, the fingertips of the right hand are dipped into the handrub for a specific purpose. What is it?","options":["To moisturize the nails","To decontaminate under the nails (about 5 seconds)","To test the alcohol concentration","To remove nail polish"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Dip the fingertips of the right hand in the handrub to decontaminate under the nails (approximately 5 seconds).  Why A wrong: The purpose is decontamination of the subungual area, not moisturizing.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the stated purposes."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"In safe injection practices, when should aseptic technique be applied?","options":["Only when drawing blood","Always — when preparing and giving an injection","Only for intramuscular injections","Only when using a multidose vial"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Always follow aseptic technique when preparing and giving an injection.  Why A/C/D wrong: Aseptic technique is required for all injection preparation and administration, not just selected situations."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"Which of the following is a recommended safe injection practice regarding syringes and needles?","options":["Reuse syringes for the same client to save cost","Needles or syringes must NOT be shared between clients","Recap needles after every use","Reuse a single-use vial for multiple clients"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Needles or syringes must not be shared between clients; multiple vials should be one client only; single-dose vials/IV solutions are for one client only.  Why A wrong: Reusing syringes risks cross-infection and is unsafe.  Why C wrong: \"DO NOT RECAP needles\" is an explicit instruction.  Why D wrong: Single-use vials must not be reused for more than one client."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"Why should needles NOT be recapped after use, per safe injection practices?","options":["To save time","To prevent injury to the user or use of forceps instead of fingers for guiding suturing (avoiding needle-stick injury)","Recapping is always required","To keep the needle sterile"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: DO NOT RECAP needles; protect fingers from injury (use of forceps for guiding/suturing) — the practice prevents needle-stick injuries.  Why A wrong: Time-saving is not the reason; injury prevention is.  Why C wrong: The source explicitly says do not recap.  Why D wrong: Recapping is associated with injury risk, not the recommended sterility step."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"After use, where should needles and syringes be discarded according to safe injection practices?","options":["In the regular waste bin","In a rigid, puncture-resistant (sharps) container that is locally destroyed/cut by a needle destroyer","In a plastic bag with other waste","Returned to the manufacturer"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After use, needles and syringes should be locally destroyed/cut by a needle destroyer and collected/disposed in a rigid, puncture-resistant container; locate sharps disposal containers close to the point of use (e.g., in patients' rooms, medicine trolley, treatment room).  Why A/C wrong: Sharps must not go into regular bins or plastic bags due to injury/infection risk.  Why D wrong: Sharps are destroyed and disposed locally, not returned."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"Sharps disposal containers should be sealed and sent for disposal when they are filled to approximately what level?","options":["Completely full (overflowing)","1/3 full","1/2 full","2/3 full"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Prevent overflow by sending sharps disposal containers for autoclaving and shredding when they are 2/3 full.  Why A wrong: Containers must never be overfilled.  Why B/C wrong: The source specifies 2/3 full, not 1/3 or 1/2."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"According to safe injection practices, vials should be opened how, in relation to the client?","options":["Well in advance of the procedure","Just before being used, in front of the client","The previous day","Only by the pharmacy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Ensure multiple vials are opened (used) to one client only / open vials in front of the client to ensure they are new — vials should be prepared close to the time of use.  Why A/C wrong: Opening in advance increases contamination risk.  Why D wrong: The nurse opens vials in front of the client per the source."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"Per the \"Also Remember\" notes, the surgical scrub technique requires modified forearm rubbing for approximately how long?","options":["5 ml of alcohol-based rub","15 ml of alcohol-based rub","25 ml of alcohol-based rub","50 ml of alcohol-based rub"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note states surgical scrub technique requires additional forearm rubbing; approximately 15 ml of alcohol-based rub is required.  Why A/C/D wrong: These volumes do not match the source-stated 15 ml."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"Per the \"Also Remember\" note, alcohol-based hand solution has which property and requires what minimum time?","options":["Cleansing property; 1 minute","Disinfecting property of denaturing proteins, thereby destroys microbes; agents with 60–70% alcohol are the most efficient and higher concentrations are less effective; minimum time 3 minutes","Moisturizing only; 30 seconds","No antimicrobial action; 10 minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Alcohol-based hand solution has the disinfecting property of denaturing proteins, thereby destroying microbes; agents with 60–70% alcohol are the most efficient and higher concentrations are less effective; it requires a minimum of 3 minutes.  Why A wrong: It is disinfecting, not merely cleansing, and requires 3 minutes.  Why C wrong: Its key property is antimicrobial denaturing of proteins.  Why D wrong: Alcohol does have antimicrobial action."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"According to the \"Also Remember\" note, gloves are NOT a substitute for hand washing because:","options":["Gloves are too expensive","Hand wash/hygiene should be performed before wearing gloves","Gloves never tear","Hands stay sterile inside gloves"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Gloves are not a substitute for hand washing; hand wash/hygiene should be performed before wearing gloves.  Why A wrong: Cost is not the reason.  Why C wrong: Gloves can tear, which is itself a reason to wash hands; not the stated rationale.  Why D wrong: Hands are not rendered sterile inside gloves; hand hygiene is still required."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"Per the \"Also Remember\" note, why should the nurse wear gloves when in contact with blood or other potentially infectious materials, mucous membranes, and non-intact skin?","options":["Because it looks professional","Because such contact could occur (gloves protect against exposure)","Only when the patient requests it","To keep the hands warm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Wear gloves when in contact with blood or other potentially infectious materials, mucous membranes, and non-intact skin because such contact could occur.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the infection-control rationale stated in the source."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"Per the \"Also Remember\" note, after caring for a patient, what should be done with the gloves before caring for the next patient?","options":["Keep the same pair of gloves for the care of more than one patient","Remove gloves after caring for a patient — do not wear the same pair of gloves for the care of more than one patient, and do not wash gloves between uses","Wash the gloves and reuse them","Apply a second pair over the first"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Remove gloves after caring for a patient; do not wear the same pair for more than one patient, and do not wash gloves between uses.  Why A wrong: The same gloves must not be used for more than one patient.  Why C wrong: Gloves should not be washed and reused.  Why D wrong: Double-gloving is unrelated; the rule is one pair per patient, removed after care."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"Per the \"Also Remember\" note, the nurse should change gloves during patient care when:","options":["The gloves feel slightly warm","Moving from a contaminated body site to a clean body site of the same patient","Every 5 minutes regardless","Only at the end of the shift"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Change gloves during patient care if moving from a contaminated body site to a clean body site of the same patient.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the indicated triggers; the move from a dirty to a clean site is the stated reason."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"Putting ON personal protective equipment (PPE) is known by which term?","options":["Doffing","Donning","Scrubbing","Gowning"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Wearing of the PPE is known as Donning (NORCET 2022).  Why A wrong: Doffing refers to removing PPE.  Why C wrong: Scrubbing is surgical hand preparation, not putting on PPE.  Why D wrong: Gowning is only one part of donning PPE, not the overall term."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"Removing personal protective equipment (PPE) after a procedure is known as:","options":["Donning","Doffing","Decontamination","Disinfection"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After the procedure, removing the PPE is known as Doffing.  Why A wrong: Donning is putting ON PPE.  Why C/D wrong: Decontamination and disinfection are general infection-control processes, not the specific term for removing PPE."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"Regarding washing of gloves during PPE removal/use, the safe practice is:","options":["Wash gloves and continue use","Washing gloves should NOT be used; perform hand washing immediately after removing gloves","Wash gloves between every patient","Spray gloves with alcohol and reuse"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Washing gloves should not be used; perform hand washing immediately after removing gloves.  Why A/C/D wrong: Gloves should never be washed for continued use; hand hygiene after glove removal is the correct practice."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"During PPE doffing, what should the nurse always do regarding the mouth, nose, eyes, and protectors?","options":["Touch them freely","Always wear mouth, nose, and eye protectors that are likely to cause splashes or sprays of blood or other body fluids","Remove protectors first before gloves","Reuse protectors for the next patient"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Always wear mouth, nose, and eye protectors that are likely to be exposed to splashes or sprays of blood or other body fluids.  Why A wrong: The face/mucous membranes must be protected, not touched.  Why C wrong: The sequence prioritizes removing the most contaminated items first per doffing order.  Why D wrong: Single-patient use; protectors are not reused across patients."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"The source lists \"7 Steps of Safe Injections Practices\" that WHO recommends health-care providers should focus on. By following the 7 steps that make every injection safe, what is achieved?","options":["Faster injections only","The risks of unsafe injections are avoided","Reduced drug costs","Elimination of all needles"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: By following these 7 simple steps, the key actions to ensure injection safety are taken, so the risks of unsafe injections are avoided.  Why A/C/D wrong: The stated purpose is avoiding the risks of unsafe injections, not speed, cost, or eliminating needles."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"Which of the following is STEP 1 among the 7 steps of safe injection practices?","options":["Sterile and new syringe and needle","Clean work space","Appropriate collection of sharps","Hand hygiene"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: STEP 1 of the 7 steps is \"Clean work space.\"  Why A wrong: Sterile/new syringe and needle is STEP 3.  Why C wrong: Appropriate collection of sharps is STEP 6.  Why D wrong: Hand hygiene is STEP 2."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"In the 7 steps of safe injection practices, which step is \"Hand hygiene\"?","options":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 4","Step 7"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: STEP 2 is Hand hygiene.  Why A wrong: Step 1 is Clean work space.  Why C wrong: Step 4 is Skin disinfection.  Why D wrong: Step 7 is Appropriate waste management."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"In the 7 steps of safe injection practices, STEP 3 emphasizes the use of a:","options":["Reused but cleaned syringe","Sterile and new syringe and needle, including infection prevention and/or injury protection feature wherever possible","Single needle for multiple patients","Glass syringe only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: STEP 3 is a sterile and new syringe and needle, with infection prevention and/or injury protection features wherever possible.  Why A wrong: Reused syringes are unsafe and violate the principle.  Why C wrong: Needles must not be shared between patients.  Why D wrong: Material is not specified as glass; sterility and newness are the point."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"Match the remaining steps (4–7) of the 7 safe injection practices correctly:","options":["Step 4 Sterile vial of medication and diluent; Step 5 Skin disinfection; Step 6 Appropriate collection of sharps; Step 7 Appropriate waste management","Step 4 Hand hygiene; Step 5 Clean space; Step 6 Sterile syringe; Step 7 Disposal","Step 4 Waste management; Step 5 Sharps; Step 6 Skin prep; Step 7 Medication","Step 4 Gloves; Step 5 Gown; Step 6 Mask; Step 7 Goggles"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists STEP 4 Sterile vial of medication and diluent; STEP 5 Skin disinfection; STEP 6 Appropriate collection of sharps; STEP 7 Appropriate waste management.  Why B/C wrong: These re-order the steps incorrectly.  Why D wrong: These are PPE items, not the named injection-safety steps."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"Per the \"Must Remember\" box on injection safety, what must NOT be done with the needle/syringe?","options":["Dispose it in a sharps container","Re-cap, bend, break, manipulate, or manually remove the needle from the syringe","Use it once and discard","Destroy it with a needle destroyer"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: \"Must Remember\" — Do not re-cap, bend, break, manipulate, or manually remove the needle from the syringe.  Why A wrong: Disposal in a sharps container is correct practice.  Why C wrong: Single use then discard is correct.  Why D wrong: Local destruction by a needle destroyer is recommended (not the prohibited action of manually removing)."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"Per the \"Should Remember\" notes on PPE, when should PPE be properly donned?","options":["After any contact with the patient","Before any contact with the patient","During the procedure only","PPE timing does not matter"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: PPE should be properly donned before any contact with the patient.  Why A wrong: Donning after contact defeats the protective purpose.  Why C/D wrong: PPE must be in place before contact, not partway through; timing is critical."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"Per the \"Should Remember\" notes, how carefully should PPE be used and removed?","options":["Quickly, without attention to contamination","Carefully — use and remove PPE to limit \"touch contamination,\" and proper procedure for PPE removal must be followed","Only the gown matters","PPE removal order is irrelevant"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: PPE should be used carefully to limit \"touch contamination,\" and the proper procedure for PPE removal must be followed.  Why A wrong: Careless removal causes self-contamination.  Why C wrong: All PPE items matter, not just the gown.  Why D wrong: The order/procedure of removal is important to prevent contamination."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"Per the \"Should Remember\" notes, which areas of the donned PPE are considered contaminated?","options":["Only the gloves","The areas of PPE that are considered contaminated are the parts that should be touched when removing PPE","The inside surfaces of the PPE","None of the PPE is contaminated"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The areas of PPE considered contaminated are the parts that should not be touched when removing PPE (outer surfaces); the source highlights which parts are contaminated to guide safe removal.  Why A wrong: More than just gloves can be contaminated.  Why C wrong: It is the outer/exposed surfaces, not the inner clean side, that are contaminated.  Why D wrong: PPE outer surfaces are contaminated after patient contact."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"Per the \"Should Remember\" notes, the sequence/procedure for removing PPE is intended to:","options":["Save laundry costs","Limit opportunities for self-contamination","Speed up the shift","Reuse the PPE"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The sequence for removing PPE is intended to limit opportunities for self-contamination.  Why A/C/D wrong: The purpose is preventing self-contamination, not cost, speed, or reuse."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"Per the \"Should Remember\" notes, when should hand hygiene be performed in relation to PPE removal?","options":["Only before donning","Before going on to the next patient, immediately after discarding the PPE","Once a day","Never, because gloves were worn"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hand hygiene should be performed before going on to the next patient, immediately after discarding the PPE.  Why A wrong: Hand hygiene is also required after doffing, not only before donning.  Why C wrong: It is performed per patient/event, not once daily.  Why D wrong: Gloves are not a substitute for hand hygiene."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"Per the \"Should Remember\" notes, if the hands become visibly contaminated during PPE removal, what should be done?","options":["Wipe on the gown","Thoroughly wash hands with soap and water; otherwise an alcohol-based hand rub may be used","Continue without washing","Use only water"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If hands become visibly contaminated during PPE removal, hands should be washed thoroughly with soap and water; otherwise, an alcohol-based hand rub may be used.  Why A wrong: Wiping on the gown spreads contamination.  Why C wrong: Visible contamination must be addressed by washing.  Why D wrong: Soap and water (not water alone) is required for visible contamination."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"In the WHO/source steps to PUT ON (don) PPE including a gown, what is the FIRST step?","options":["Put on disposable gown","Remove all personal items (jewellery, watches, cell phones, pens, etc.)","Perform hand hygiene","Put on gloves"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Step 1 of donning PPE is to remove all personal items — jewellery, watches, cell phones, pens, etc.  Why A wrong: The gown is donned later (step 8).  Why C wrong: Hand hygiene is step 6 in the donning sequence shown.  Why D wrong: Gloves are step 7 (examination/nitrile) and step 13 (second pair)."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"In the donning of PPE sequence, after removing personal items, what is the next step?","options":["Put on scrub suit and rubber boots in the changing room","Put on the face shield","Don the disposable gown","Put on the head and neck covering"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Step 2 is to put on the scrub suit and rubber boots in the changing room.  Why B/C/D wrong: Face shield, gown, and head/neck covering come later in the donning sequence."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"In the donning of PPE sequence, where should the health worker move to before continuing to don PPE?","options":["Directly to the patient's bedside","To the clean area at the entrance of the isolation unit","To the contaminated zone","To the operating theatre"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Step 3 is to move to the clean area at the entrance of the isolation unit.  Why A wrong: PPE is fully donned before approaching the patient.  Why C wrong: Donning occurs in the clean area, not the contaminated zone.  Why D wrong: This sequence is for an isolation unit, not specifically the OT."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"In the donning of PPE sequence, after reaching the clean area, the worker should:","options":["Put on gloves immediately","By visual inspection, ensure that all sizes of the PPE are correct and the quality is appropriate","Remove the scrub suit","Perform the procedure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Step 4 is, by visual inspection, ensure that all sizes of the PPE set are correct and the quality is appropriate.  Why A wrong: Gloves are donned later in the sequence.  Why C wrong: The scrub suit remains on.  Why D wrong: The procedure follows complete donning."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"In the donning of PPE sequence, the source recommends undertaking the donning procedure under the guidance and supervision of:","options":["The patient","A trained, observed colleague","The hospital administrator","No one — it is done alone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Step 5 is to undertake the procedure of putting on PPE under the guidance and supervision of a trained, observed colleague.  Why A/C wrong: Supervision is by a trained colleague, not the patient or administrator.  Why D wrong: Supervised donning is recommended, not solo donning."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"In the donning of PPE sequence, what is performed at step 6?","options":["Put on the gown","Perform hand hygiene","Put on the face shield","Remove gloves"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Step 6 of donning PPE is to perform hand hygiene.  Why A wrong: The gown is donned at step 8.  Why C wrong: The face shield/goggles are step 10.  Why D wrong: Glove removal belongs to doffing, not donning."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"In the donning of PPE sequence, which gloves are put on at step 7?","options":["Sterile surgical gloves","Examination, nitrile gloves","Heavy-duty rubber gloves","No gloves at this step"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Step 7 is to put on gloves (examination, nitrile gloves).  Why A wrong: Sterile surgical gloves are not specified at this step.  Why C wrong: Heavy-duty rubber gloves are not the type named.  Why D wrong: Examination nitrile gloves are put on at step 7."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"In the donning of PPE sequence (step 8), the disposable gown should be:","options":["Made of light cloth that allows fluid through","Made of fabric that is tested for resistance to penetration by blood or body fluids, to keep blood-borne pathogens out","Worn over the head and neck covering","Optional"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Step 8 is to put on the disposable gown, made of fabric tested for resistance to penetration by blood or body fluids, to keep out blood-borne pathogens.  Why A wrong: Fluid-permeable cloth would not protect against blood-borne pathogens.  Why C wrong: The head/neck covering (step 11) follows; the gown is donned at step 8.  Why D wrong: The gown is part of the required PPE set."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"In the donning of PPE sequence, what is donned at step 10?","options":["Disposable gown","Face shield OR goggles","The second pair of gloves","Rubber boots"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Step 10 is to put on a face shield OR goggles.  Why A wrong: The gown is step 8.  Why C wrong: The second pair of gloves is step 13.  Why D wrong: Rubber boots are donned earlier (step 2)."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"In the donning of PPE sequence, the head and neck covering is donned at step 11. What does it consist of?","options":["A simple cloth cap only","A surgical bonnet covering the neck and sides of the head (preferable with hood) OR head shield","A face mask only","A rubber boot"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Step 11 is to put on the head and neck covering — a surgical bonnet covering the neck and sides of the head (preferable with hood) or head shield.  Why A wrong: It is more than a simple cloth cap; it covers neck and sides of the head.  Why C/D wrong: A face mask and rubber boot are different PPE items."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"In the donning of PPE sequence, step 12 involves putting on a disposable waterproof apron. When is this used?","options":["Always, in every case","If not available, a clean non-sterile, reusable, waterproof apron may be used","Only after the procedure","Only by the supervisor"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Step 12 is to put on a disposable waterproof apron; if not available, a clean non-sterile, reusable, waterproof apron may be used.  Why A wrong: An alternative is allowed if the disposable apron is not available.  Why C wrong: It is donned during donning, before the procedure.  Why D wrong: It is worn by the care provider, not restricted to a supervisor."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"What is the FINAL step (step 13) of donning PPE including a gown?","options":["Perform hand hygiene","Put on a second pair of (preferably long-cuff) gloves over the cuffs","Remove personal items","Put on the scrub suit"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Step 13 is to put on a second pair of (preferably long-cuff) gloves over the cuffs.  Why A wrong: Hand hygiene was step 6.  Why C wrong: Removing personal items was step 1.  Why D wrong: The scrub suit was donned at step 2."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"The note accompanying the PPE donning sequence states that if a piece of PPE is not available, what should be done?","options":["Skip that protection entirely","Use a model/standard practice carefully, drawing from procedures and reasoning closely to the level of the WHO tool and items, and observe carefully and proactively improvise","Cancel the procedure","Use only gloves"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The WHO note advises that if a piece of PPE is not available, one should use the model/standard practice carefully, drawing from the procedures and reasoning closely to the level of the WHO tool and items, observing carefully and proactively improvising appropriate alternatives.  Why A/C/D wrong: The guidance is to safely improvise an equivalent, not to skip protection, cancel care, or reduce to gloves alone."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"Personal protective equipment (PPE) is best defined as:","options":["Cleaning agents used to disinfect surfaces","Wearable equipment used to protect health-care workers from exposure to or contact with infectious agents","Surgical instruments used in the OT","Medications given before surgery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: PPE refers to wearable equipment that is used to protect health-care workers from exposure to or contact with infectious agents.  Why A wrong: PPE is wearable, not a surface disinfectant.  Why C/D wrong: PPE is protective gear, not instruments or drugs."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"Per the doffing note, gloves protect the hands; gowns or aprons protect the skin and/or clothing; masks and respirators protect the mouth and nose; and goggles protect the eyes. Why is this layered protection used?","options":["To improve appearance","To protect the wearer against splashes/sprays and contact with blood, body fluids, secretions, and excretions during patient care","To keep the worker warm","To meet dress-code rules only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Each PPE item protects a specific body region (hands, skin/clothing, mouth/nose, eyes) from exposure to blood, body fluids, secretions, and excretions.  Why A/C/D wrong: PPE serves infection-control protection, not appearance, warmth, or dress-code compliance."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"Per the doffing note, when moving from one client to another, or when dirt or solid wastes are seen on the gloves, the nurse should:","options":["Keep the same gloves","Change the gloves","Wash the gloves","Spray the gloves with alcohol"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The gloves should be changed when moving from one client to another or when dirt or solid wastes are seen on the gloves.  Why A wrong: Reusing soiled gloves spreads contamination.  Why C/D wrong: Gloves are not washed or sprayed for reuse; they are changed."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"Per the safe injection practices, single-dose vials, IV solutions, ampoules, bags, bottles, or single-dose vials of IV solutions should be used for:","options":["More than one client to save resources","One client only","The whole ward","A maximum of two clients"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Do not administer medications from ampoules, bags, bottles, or single-dose vials of IV solutions to more than one client — they are for one client only.  Why A/C/D wrong: Sharing single-dose preparations across multiple clients risks cross-infection and is prohibited."},{"section":"Batch 047 · WHO 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene…","question":"Per safe injection practices, what should be done with the preparation of medications in terms of facility/space?","options":["Prepare medications next to the waste bin","There should be a separate facility/area for preparing the medications, kept away from contaminated areas","Prepare medications on the patient's bed","Preparation location does not matter"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: There should be a separate facility for preparing the medications (a clean dedicated area), supporting STEP 1 \"clean work space.\"  Why A/C wrong: Preparing near waste or on the bed risks contamination.  Why D wrong: A clean, dedicated preparation area is essential to injection safety."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"During the doffing (taking off) of personal protective equipment (PPE), where should the procedure ideally be performed?","options":["In the patient's room at the bedside","In a designated doffing area under the guidance and supervision of a trained observer","In any open corridor outside the ward","At the nursing station after a hand-over"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: WHO doffing guidance states PPE should always be removed under the guidance and supervision of a trained observer (colleague), with infectious-waste containers available in the doffing area.  Why A wrong: Bedside removal risks contamination and is not the designated doffing area.  Why C wrong: An open corridor lacks waste containers and supervision and risks spreading contamination.  Why D wrong: The nursing station is a clean area and unsuitable for removing contaminated PPE."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"When removing the disposable apron/gown during doffing, what is the correct technique to avoid contaminating the hands?","options":["Pull it off over the head from the front","Lean forward, tear it off at the neck and roll it down without touching the front, then untie the back and roll the apron forward","Untie the front strings first and lift it straight up","Have another staff member pull it off from behind only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source directs leaning forward and taking care to avoid contaminating the hands — tear the apron off at the neck, roll it down without touching the front, then untie the back and roll the apron forward.  Why A wrong: Pulling over the head from the front contaminates the face and head.  Why C wrong: Untying front strings first contacts the contaminated outer front surface.  Why D wrong: The described self-removal technique keeps hands away from the contaminated front; this is not the stated method."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"How often is hand hygiene performed during the PPE doffing sequence described by WHO?","options":["Only once, at the very end","Only before starting","Repeatedly, on gloved hands between most steps and again after the final step","Only after removing gloves"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The doffing sequence interposes \"Perform hand hygiene on gloved hands\" between nearly every removal step (gown, head/neck cover, eye protection, mask, boots, gloves), with hand hygiene also performed at the end.  Why A/B wrong: Hand hygiene is multiple, not single.  Why D wrong: Hand hygiene occurs throughout, not only after gloves."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"When removing the head and neck covering (hood) during doffing, the correct direction is to:","options":["Pull it forward over the face","Start from the bottom of the hood and roll it from back to front, then from inside to outside, and dispose of it safely","Lift it straight off the top of the head","Untie only the front strings and drop it"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source says take care to avoid contaminating the face by starting from the bottom of the hood, rolling from back to front and from inside to outside, then disposing safely.  Why A wrong: Pulling forward over the face contaminates the face.  Why C/D wrong: These do not follow the back-to-front, inside-to-outside roll technique."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"During doffing, how is the gown removed once it must come off?","options":["By pulling it off over the head","By untying the knot first, then pulling from back to front and rolling it from inside to outside before disposal","By cutting it off with scissors at the front","By letting it fall to the floor and stepping out of it"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to remove the gown by untying the knot first, then pulling from back to front, rolling it from inside to outside, and disposing of it safely.  Why A wrong: Over-the-head removal contaminates the face/hair.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the described safe technique."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"What is the correct technique for removing eye protection (goggles/visor) during doffing?","options":["Touch the front lens and lift forward","Pull the string from behind the head and dispose of it safely","Wipe the front first, then remove","Remove it together with the mask in one motion from the front"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Eye protection is removed by pulling the string from behind the head and disposing of it safely, keeping hands off the contaminated front.  Why A/C wrong: Touching/wiping the contaminated front lens contaminates the hands.  Why D wrong: Eye protection and mask are removed as separate steps."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"What is the correct sequence for removing a tie-on mask during doffing?","options":["Touch the front of the mask and pull it forward","Remove the mask from behind by first untying the bottom string above the head, leaving it hanging, then untie the top string next from behind the head and dispose of it safely","Untie the top string first, then the bottom string","Snap both strings simultaneously and let the mask drop"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source directs removing the mask from behind by first untying the bottom string (above the head, leaving it hanging), then untying the top string from behind the head, then disposing safely.  Why A wrong: Touching the contaminated front contaminates the hands.  Why C wrong: The bottom string is untied first, not the top.  Why D wrong: Strings are untied in sequence, not snapped together."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"During doffing, how should rubber boots be removed?","options":["By bending down and touching the outer surface with bare hands","Without touching them — using a boot remover (or overshoes if worn), then sending them for cleaning/decontamination before leaving the dirty area","By stepping out and leaving them anywhere convenient","By rinsing them while still wearing gloves at the doorway"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Rubber boots should be removed without touching them (or overshoes if wearing shoes); a boot remover is used, and boots are sent for cleaning and decontamination before leaving the dirty area.  Why A wrong: Touching contaminated boots with bare/gloved hands contaminates them.  Why C/D wrong: Improper handling/disposal; decontamination before leaving the dirty area is required."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"What is the correct final step regarding gloves in the doffing sequence?","options":["Reuse the gloves after disinfecting them","Remove gloves carefully with appropriate technique and dispose of them safely, then perform hand hygiene","Wash gloved hands and keep gloves on until leaving the building","Remove gloves first, before the gown and mask"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Gloves are removed carefully with appropriate technique and disposed of safely, followed by performing hand hygiene (the final step).  Why A wrong: Single-use gloves are disposed, not reused.  Why C wrong: Gloves are removed and discarded, not kept on.  Why D wrong: Gloves are among the last items removed in this sequence, not the first."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"When donning gloves, after taking out a glove from its original box, you should touch only the:","options":["Outside (palmar surface) of the glove","Restricted surface of the glove corresponding to the wrist (at the top edge of the cuff)","Fingertips of the glove","Entire outer surface to spread it open"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source instructs touching only a restricted surface of the glove corresponding to the wrist, at the top edge of the cuff, then donning the first glove.  Why A/C/D wrong: Touching the outside, fingertips, or full outer surface contaminates the part that will contact the patient."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"When donning the second glove, the bare (ungloved) hand should:","options":["Slide fully inside the second glove's outer surface","Touch only a restricted surface of the glove corresponding to the wrist","Hold the second glove by the fingers","Be powdered before insertion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: To don the second glove, take it with the bare hand and touch only a restricted surface of the glove corresponding to the wrist.  Why A/C wrong: These contaminate the glove's external surface.  Why D wrong: Powdering is not mentioned in the technique."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"While donning the second glove and avoiding skin contact of the forearm with the gloved hand, the nurse should:","options":["Turn the external surface of the gloved hand to be domed on the folded fingers of the second glove, permitting gloving of the second hand","Touch the bare forearm directly with the gloved fingertips","Place both gloves on simultaneously","Remove the first glove and start over"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source describes turning the external surface of the gloved hand to be domed on the folded fingers of the second glove, thus permitting gloving of the second hand without skin contact.  Why B wrong: Touching the bare forearm contaminates the gloved hand.  Why C/D wrong: Not the described technique."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"Once both hands are gloved, the gloves should not touch:","options":["Each other","Anything else that is not defined by indications and conditions for glove use","The sterile field only","The patient"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Once gloved, hands should not touch anything else that is not defined by indications and conditions for glove use.  Why A/C/D wrong: Gloves are intended to touch the patient/field per their indication; the restriction is on touching anything not defined by the glove-use indications."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"When removing gloves, the first step is to:","options":["Peel both gloves off at the fingertips simultaneously","Pinch one glove at the wrist level to remove it, without touching the skin of the forearm, and peel away from the hand allowing the glove to turn inside out","Wash the gloves before removal","Pull the glove off by the cuff toward the fingertips"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source: pinch one glove at the wrist level to remove it without touching the skin of the forearm, peel it away from the hand, allowing the glove to turn inside out.  Why A wrong: Gloves are removed one at a time, not both at once.  Why C wrong: Gloves are not washed before removal.  Why D wrong: The glove is peeled from the wrist turning inside out, not pulled toward the fingertips."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"After the first glove is removed and held in the gloved hand, the second glove is removed by:","options":["Touching the outside of the second glove with the bare hand","Holding the removed (first) glove in the gloved hand and sliding the fingers of the still-gloved hand between the glove and the wrist; removing the second glove by rolling it down the hand and folding it into the first glove","Pulling the second glove off by its fingertips","Discarding the first glove before touching the second"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hold the removed glove in the gloved hand, slide the fingers of the gloved hand between the glove and the wrist, and remove the second glove by rolling it down the hand and folding it into the first glove, then discard.  Why A wrong: The bare hand must not touch the contaminated outside of the glove.  Why C wrong: Pulling by fingertips contaminates the bare hand.  Why D wrong: The first glove is held (not discarded) so the second can be folded into it."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"In the SUMAN-K mnemonic for the recommended hand-washing steps, the letter \"S\" stands for which step?","options":["Seedha (palm to palm — straight)","Soap application only","Scrubbing the nails","Sanitizer use"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In SUMAN-K, S = Seedha (palms straight against each other).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the meanings assigned; nails correspond to \"N\" (Nail) and the wrist to \"K\" (Kalai)."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"Match the SUMAN-K hand-washing mnemonic letters with their correct meaning. Which option is correct?","options":["U = Ulta (back of hands), M = Muthi (interlocked/fist), A = Angutha (thumb), N = Nail, K = Kalai (wrist)","U = Under nails, M = Mask, A = Apron, N = Neck, K = Knee","U = Ulta, M = Mouth, A = Arm, N = Nose, K = Knuckle","U = Upper arm, M = Muthi, A = Ankle, N = Nail, K = Kalai"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: SUMAN-K = S-Seedha, U-Ulta, M-Muthi, A-Angutha, N-Nail, K-Kalai (palms, back of hands, interlaced fingers/fist, thumbs, nails, wrist).  Why B/C/D wrong: They misassign letters to unrelated terms not on the page."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"According to the PPE-by-transmission-route table, a gown is required for which type(s) of transmission precaution?","options":["Contact transmission only (not droplet or airborne)","Droplet transmission only","Airborne transmission only","None of the transmission types"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table marks a gown as \"Yes\" for Contact transmission and \"No\" for Droplet and Airborne transmission.  Why B/C/D wrong: The table indicates the gown is needed for Contact transmission, not droplet/airborne, and it is required for contact."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"Per the PPE-by-transmission-route table, for which transmission type(s) are gloves indicated?","options":["Only airborne","Only droplet","Contact transmission (Yes), while droplet and airborne are marked No","None"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table shows gloves as \"Yes\" for Contact transmission and \"No\" for Droplet and Airborne.  Why A/B/D wrong: Gloves are tied to contact precautions in the table, not droplet/airborne alone, and they are indicated."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"Which figure depicts the correct method of wearing a cap and mask?","options":["The cap/mask leaving hair exposed or the mask below the nose","The cap fully covering the hair and the mask covering both the nose and mouth (the figure marked with a check)","The mask worn under the chin","The mask covering only the mouth"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The figures contrast incorrect methods (marked X) with the correct method (marked ✓) of wearing cap and mask — the cap covering the hair and the mask covering nose and mouth.  Why A/C/D wrong: These represent incorrect methods marked with an X."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"Contact Precautions are required for patients known or suspected to be infected or colonized with microorganisms that can be transmitted by which route(s)?","options":["Only airborne droplet nuclei","By direct contact or through the patient's secretions or bodily fluids","Only through contaminated water","Only via vector (mosquito) bites"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines contact precautions for organisms transmitted by direct contact or through the patient's secretions or bodily fluids.  Why A wrong: Airborne nuclei describe airborne precautions.  Why C/D wrong: These routes are not the definition of contact transmission given."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"In addition to standard precautions, which measure is recommended for health workers caring for a patient under Contact Precautions?","options":["Wear an N95 respirator at all times","Wear a gown and gloves before entering the patient room and remove them prior to exit","Place the patient in a negative-pressure room only","Keep the door open for ventilation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Health workers should additionally wear a gown and gloves before entering the patient room and remove them before exit.  Why A/C wrong: N95 and negative pressure are airborne-precaution measures.  Why D wrong: Door management is relevant to airborne precautions, not the contact add-on listed."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"Under Contact Precautions, hand hygiene should be performed:","options":["Only at the end of the shift","Before entering and again after leaving the patient room/area","Only when gloves are visibly soiled","Never, because gloves are worn"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source directs performing hand hygiene immediately after gown and glove removal, before contact with another patient.  Why A/C/D wrong: Hand hygiene is required around patient care regardless of glove use, not only at shift end or when soiled."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"Regarding patient placement under Contact Precautions, when a single room is not available the appropriate strategy is to:","options":["Discharge the patient immediately","Cohort patients with the same symptoms and diagnosis (place in the same room)","Place the patient in a corridor","Mix the patient with non-infected patients"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source advises using a single room; if one is not available, cohort patients with the same symptoms and diagnosis.  Why A/C wrong: Discharge and corridor placement are not recommended.  Why D wrong: Mixing infected with non-infected patients defeats isolation."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"Under Contact Precautions, patient-care equipment (e.g., stethoscope) should ideally be:","options":["Shared freely among all patients","Dedicated to the single patient; if shared, cleaned and disinfected before use on other patients","Never cleaned","Discarded after a single use only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source recommends using disposable or dedicated patient-care equipment (e.g., stethoscope); clean and disinfect equipment before use on other patients.  Why A wrong: Free sharing risks cross-transmission.  Why C wrong: Cleaning/disinfection is required.  Why D wrong: Equipment may be dedicated/reusable after disinfection, not necessarily single-use disposal."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"Which microorganisms are given as examples requiring Contact Precautions?","options":["Tuberculosis and measles","MSSA/MRSA, Vancomycin-resistant enterococci (VRE), Clostridium difficile infection (CDI), E. coli, rotavirus, salmonella, shigella, RSV, influenza, wound infections, and similar organisms","Only airborne viral infections","Mosquito-borne diseases only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The examples listed include MSSA/MRSA, VRE, CDI, E. coli, rotavirus, salmonella, shigella, RSV, influenza, wound infections, etc., with MRSA = Methicillin-Resistant Staphylococcus Aureus.  Why A wrong: Tuberculosis and measles are airborne-precaution examples.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the contact-precaution examples listed."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"Airborne Precautions are required for patients known or suspected to be infected with microorganisms that are transmitted by:","options":["Large droplets (>5 micron in size)","Direct contact only","Airborne route — small particles (<5 micron in size) that remain suspended in air","Contaminated food only"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Airborne precautions apply to organisms transmitted by airborne route via small particles less than 5 micron in size.  Why A wrong: Large droplets >5 micron define droplet precautions.  Why B/D wrong: Contact and foodborne are different routes."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"For a health worker entering the room of a patient on Airborne Precautions, which respiratory protection and check is recommended in addition to standard precautions?","options":["A simple surgical mask without any check","Wear a respirator (e.g., N95, FFP2, HEPA, etc.) before entering the room and perform a fit/seal check after putting it on","No mask is needed if the patient wears one","A cloth mask only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Health workers should wear a respirator (N95, FFP2, HEPA, etc.) before entering the room and remove it after exiting; perform a respirator seal/fit check after donning.  Why A/D wrong: A simple surgical or cloth mask is inadequate for airborne pathogens.  Why C wrong: The health worker still requires respiratory protection."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"An airborne-infection isolation room should ideally have which of the following features? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Negative pressure compared to the corridor, with air exchanges per hour","Direct exhaust of air to the outside, away from people and air intakes; or, where people walk or congregate, with appropriate air handling","A door kept closed when not required for entry or exit","Positive pressure relative to the corridor","Windows permanently sealed shut to all openings"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct (A, B, C): An airborne isolation room should have negative pressure compared with the corridor with air exchanges/hour; direct exhaust of air to the outside away from people and intakes (or with appropriate handling where people congregate); and a door that is kept closed when not required for entry/exit.  Why D wrong: Negative (not positive) pressure is required so contaminated air does not flow out.  Why E wrong: The source describes controlled exhaust/openings, not permanently sealed windows."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"Which set of diseases is given as examples requiring Airborne Precautions?","options":["MRSA and C. difficile","Tuberculosis, COVID-19, SARS, avian influenza, chickenpox, herpes zoster, and measles","Rotavirus and salmonella","Wound infections only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists tuberculosis, COVID-19, SARS, avian influenza, chickenpox, herpes zoster, and measles as airborne-precaution examples.  Why A/C/D wrong: MRSA, C. difficile, rotavirus, salmonella, and wound infections are contact-precaution examples."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"Regarding patient placement for Airborne Precautions, when a single isolation room is unavailable the appropriate action is to:","options":["Place the patient in any open ward","Use negative-pressure room with special precautions, or cohort with patients having a confirmed same diagnosis","Discharge the patient","Place with droplet-precaution patients"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source notes use of a negative-pressure room with special precautions, and cohorting with patients of the same confirmed diagnosis where a single room is not available.  Why A wrong: An open ward allows airborne spread.  Why C wrong: Discharge is inappropriate management.  Why D wrong: Mixing with droplet-precaution patients (different organisms) is unsafe."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"Droplet Precautions are required for patients known or suspected to be infected with microorganisms transmitted by:","options":["Small airborne particles <5 micron","Large respiratory droplets (>5 micron in size) generated by coughing, sneezing, or talking","Contaminated surfaces only","Bloodborne route"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Droplet precautions apply to organisms transmitted by droplets greater than 5 micron in size, generated during coughing, sneezing, or talking.  Why A wrong: Particles <5 micron define airborne precautions.  Why C/D wrong: Surface contact and bloodborne are different transmission categories."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"In addition to standard precautions, what should a health worker do when caring for a patient on Droplet Precautions?","options":["Wear an N95 respirator and use negative-pressure isolation","Put on a surgical mask, remove it on exit, and wear additional PPE if indicated; maintain about 1 metre (3 feet) spatial distance and place the patient in a single room (cohort if needed)","Wear gloves only","No mask is required"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For droplet precautions, the health worker puts on a surgical mask (and additional PPE if indicated), removes it upon exit, maintains spatial separation (place patient in single room/cohort), and observes assessment of need.  Why A wrong: N95 and negative pressure are airborne, not droplet, measures.  Why C/D wrong: A surgical mask is required, not gloves alone or no mask."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"Regarding masks during droplet precautions, which statements are recommended? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Perform hand hygiene before and after the use of masks","Place the patient in a single room","Cohort patients with the same symptoms and diagnosis (confirmed) when a single room is not available","Reuse a single mask between multiple patients without changing it","If single room not available, physically separate patients by at least 1 m (3 ft) and draw the privacy curtain"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct (A, B, C, E): The page lists performing hand hygiene before and after mask use, placing the patient in a single room, cohorting patients with the same symptoms/confirmed diagnosis, and—if a single room is unavailable—physically separating patients by at least 1 m (3 ft) and drawing the privacy curtain.  Why D wrong: Reusing one mask across multiple patients is unsafe and not advised."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"For a patient on droplet precautions who must be transported, the appropriate instruction is to:","options":["Transport without any mask","Have the patient wear a surgical mask and follow respiratory hygiene and cough etiquette during transport","Transport only at night","Avoid all transport indefinitely"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source instructs the patient to wear a surgical mask and follow respiratory hygiene/cough etiquette when transport is necessary.  Why A wrong: A mask is required during transport.  Why C/D wrong: Timing and a blanket ban are not the stated guidance."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"Which organisms are listed as examples requiring Droplet Precautions?","options":["Tuberculosis and measles","Bacterial meningitis (for the first 24 hours), pertussis, influenza, rubella, diphtheria, mumps, and meningococcal pneumonia","C. difficile and MRSA","Rotavirus and salmonella"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The examples listed for droplet precautions include bacterial meningitis (first 24 hours), pertussis, influenza, rubella, diphtheria, mumps, and meningococcal pneumonia, plus scarlet fever.  Why A wrong: Tuberculosis and measles are airborne examples.  Why C/D wrong: C. difficile, MRSA, rotavirus, and salmonella are contact examples."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"Define \"Cleaning\" as used in disinfection terminology.","options":["The destruction of all microorganisms including spores","The removal of visible soil and organic contamination from a non-living surface or device using detergent and water","Killing only spores","Sterilizing surfaces with steam"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Cleaning is the removal of visible soil and organic contamination from a non-living surface or device, done from the environmental surface.  Why A wrong: Total destruction including spores is sterilization.  Why C/D wrong: These describe sterilization, not cleaning."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"Disinfection is best defined as:","options":["Removal of visible soil only","Destroying microorganisms using chemicals — usually from living surfaces such as spirit (alcohol) and betadine","The total destruction of all microbes including spores","Mechanical scrubbing of instruments"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Disinfection is destroying the microorganism by using chemicals, usually from living surfaces, such as spirit and betadine.  Why A wrong: That defines cleaning.  Why C wrong: Total destruction including spores is sterilization.  Why D wrong: Mechanical scrubbing alone is not disinfection."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"Sterilization is defined as:","options":["Removal of visible dirt only","The total destruction of microorganisms (including spores) on living/non-living surfaces, e.g., by autoclave","Cleaning with detergent","Disinfecting living tissue with betadine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Sterilization is the total destruction or removal of living microorganisms, including those on surfaces, e.g., via autoclave.  Why A/C wrong: These describe cleaning.  Why D wrong: That describes disinfection."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"Which set of parameters represents the standard autoclave sterilization criteria stated in the source?","options":["Pressure 5 lbs/mm², 5 minutes, 100°C","Pressure 15 lbs/mm², time 30 minutes, temperature 121°C or 250°F","Pressure 30 lbs/mm², 60 minutes, 180°C","Pressure 10 lbs/mm², 15 minutes, 100°C"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source gives autoclave criteria as pressure 15 lbs/mm², time 30 minutes, and temperature 121°C / 250°F.  Why A/C/D wrong: These pressures, times, and temperatures do not match the stated values."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"For patients on droplet/respiratory precautions, when a single room is not available the physical separation distance recommended between patients is at least:","options":["30 cm (1 ft)","1 metre (about 3 ft)","3 metres (about 10 ft)","5 metres (about 15 ft)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source specifies physically separating patients by at least 1 metre (3 feet) and drawing the privacy curtain when a single room is unavailable.  Why A/C/D wrong: These distances do not match the stated 1 m (3 ft)."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"Which statement about Bio-Medical Waste (BMW) Management in India is correct?","options":["The first BMW Management rules were notified in 2016","The first Bio-Medical Waste Management Rules were 1998; BMWM-1998 was amended in 2016 and 2018","BMW rules apply only to private clinics","There are five categories of BMWM"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In India, the first Bio-Medical Waste Management rules were of 1998, and BMWM-1998 was amended in 2016 and 2018.  Why A wrong: 1998 was first; 2016 was an amendment.  Why C wrong: BMW rules apply to all entities producing/handling biomedical waste, not only private clinics.  Why D wrong: The source lists four BMWM categories."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"What is the main objective/purpose of the Bio-Medical Waste (BMW) Rules 2016 as stated by the source?","options":["To increase the production or testing of biological agents","To prevent the spread of infection or lesion to other humans and animals","To allow free dumping of medical waste","To reduce hospital paperwork"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The BMW rules were defined by the BMW Rules 2016 in any waste generated during the activities of diagnosis, treatment, and immunisation of human beings or any research activities pertaining thereto, or in the production or testing of biological — and the aim of BMWM is to prevent the spread of infection or lesion to other humans and animals.  Why A wrong: It is not about increasing biological-agent production.  Why C wrong: Free dumping defeats the purpose.  Why D wrong: Reducing paperwork is not the objective."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"The four stages of Bio-Medical Waste Management, in order, are:","options":["Generation, Segregation, Collection, Transportation","Collection, Burning, Burial, Recycling","Sorting, Washing, Drying, Packing","Disposal, Storage, Audit, Reporting"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists the four stages of BMWM as 1. Generation, 2. Segregation (the most important and vital step), 3. Collection, and 4. Transportation.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the four stated stages."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"Among the stages of Bio-Medical Waste Management, which is described as the most important and vital step?","options":["Generation","Segregation","Collection","Transportation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source labels Segregation as the most important and vital step of BMWM.  Why A/C/D wrong: Generation, collection, and transportation are stages but not flagged as the most important/vital step."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"Under good BMW practice, every health facility must ensure that only non-chlorinated bags are used to handle which type of waste?","options":["Sharps waste","Biological (blood) bags","Glassware","Metallic implants"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states each health facility must ensure that only non-chlorinated plastic (including blood) bags are used and to be kept by the hospital for collection or reuse in the hospital.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are categorized in their own color codes/containers, not the non-chlorinated bag instruction highlighted."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"According to BMW handling practice, bags or containers used for waste collection should be sealed once they are:","options":["Completely empty","Full to half (about half) of their capacity, and then transported to the central waste-storage area","Left open for 48 hours","Filled exactly to the brim and left overnight"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source advises that all bags used for waste collection need to be sealed once they are full to half (about half) of their capacity, then transported to the central waste-storage area.  Why A/C/D wrong: These contradict the stated half-capacity sealing and prompt transport guidance."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"Per the BMW guidance, bio-medical waste should not be stored beyond a period of:","options":["12 hours","24 hours","48 hours","7 days"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states no untreated bio-medical waste shall be stored beyond a period of 48 hours.  Why A/B/D wrong: These do not match the stated 48-hour limit."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"According to the source, all newly generated bio-medical waste in BMW management has to be:","options":["Buried untreated","Treated on-site by microwaving before final disposal","Stored indefinitely","Mixed with general waste"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note on the BMW table states all bio-medical waste is first treated on-site by microwaving before final disposal; the source emphasizes treatment before disposal.  Why A/C/D wrong: Untreated burial, indefinite storage, and mixing with general waste are improper handling contradicting the rules."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"Under the salient features of BMWM Rules 2016 (amended 2018), the phase-out of chlorinated plastic bags (excluding blood bags) and gloves was mandated by:","options":["January 1, 2016","March 27, 2019","January 1, 2025","December 31, 2020"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The salient features state the phase-out of chlorinated plastic bags (excluding blood bags) and gloves by March 27, 2019.  Why A/C/D wrong: These dates do not match the stated March 27, 2019 deadline."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"Under the salient features of BMWM Rules 2016 (amended 2018), the annual report of all health establishments must be submitted on a website within what time frame after the end of each year?","options":["Within 30 days","Within 2 months","Within 6 months","Within 1 year"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states all hospitals/establishments shall make the annual report on its website within a period of two years after this rule act (submitting the annual report within two months as per the salient feature) — the feature requires the annual report on the website within 2 months of the end of the year.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated two-month requirement."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"A salient feature of BMWM Rules 2016 requires establishing a bar-code system for bags or containers containing bio-medical waste for disposal. This applies to:","options":["Only government hospitals","All state pollution control boards and all information to the Central Pollution Control Board, in a new Form (Form IV-A)","Only laboratories","Only veterinary clinics"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The salient features include establishing a bar-code system for bags or containers and sending all state pollution control board information to the Central Pollution Control Board in a new Form (Form IV-A).  Why A/C/D wrong: It is not limited to a single category of facility; the requirement is system-wide with reporting to CPCB."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"Under the BMWM 2016 salient features, the bio-waste should pre-treat through disinfection by which method/agent before disposal?","options":["Plain water rinse only","1% sodium hypochlorite (chlorine solution) — having 30% residual chlorine — for at least 20 minutes (or by sterilization on-site as per WHO guidelines)","Burning in open air","Burial without treatment"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The salient features specify pre-treatment by disinfection using 1% chlorine (sodium hypochlorite) solution having 30% residual chlorine for at least 20 minutes (or by sterilization on-site as per WHO guidelines).  Why A/C/D wrong: A water rinse, open burning, or untreated burial do not meet the stated disinfection requirement."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"As per the BMWM 2016 salient features, the new rules can establish on-site treatment and disposal facilities for waste; what is favored regarding such facilities?","options":["Every hospital must build its own incinerator","A common bio-medical waste treatment facility is available within a distance of 75 kilometres (favoring shared/common facilities)","No treatment facility is needed","Treatment must be done abroad"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the new rules can establish on-site treatment and disposal facility, but a common bio-medical waste treatment facility is favored where available within a distance of 75 kilometres.  Why A wrong: Common/shared facilities are favored over each hospital building its own.  Why C/D wrong: Treatment is required and is done within the stated distance, not abroad/none."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"Under BMWM 2016 salient features, immunization against which infection(s) is required for staff handling bio-medical waste?","options":["Hepatitis B and tetanus","Polio only","Measles only","No immunization is required"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The salient features include immunization against Hepatitis B and tetanus for workers.  Why B/C wrong: Polio and measles are not the specified immunizations.  Why D wrong: Immunization is explicitly required."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"As per the BMWM 2016 salient features, records relating to generation, collection, reception, storage, transportation, treatment, and disposal shall be maintained as per the rules for a period of:","options":["1 year","3 years","5 years","10 years"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states records in relation to generation, collection, reception, storage, transportation, treatment, and disposal shall be maintained as per the rules for 5 years.  Why A/B/D wrong: These do not match the stated 5-year retention period."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"In the Bio-Medical Waste color-coding (BMWM 2016, amended 2018), the YELLOW category includes which type of waste?","options":["Recyclable plastic tubing and catheters","Human and animal anatomical waste (tissues, organs, body parts), soiled waste contaminated with body fluids/blood, expired/discarded medicines, chemical liquid waste, microbiology waste, and discarded linen contaminated with blood/body fluids","Broken glassware and metallic body implants","Discarded metallic sharps such as needles and scalpels"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Yellow category covers human/animal anatomical waste, soiled waste contaminated with body fluids/blood (dressings, bandages, plaster casts, etc.), discarded/expired medicines including antibiotics, chemical liquid waste (discarded disinfectants/cleaning agents, X-ray developing fluids, etc.), microbiology/biotechnology waste, and discarded linen/blood bags (cytotoxic waste).  Why A wrong: Recyclable plastic tubing/catheters belong to the Red category.  Why C wrong: Broken glassware/metallic implants belong to the Blue category.  Why D wrong: Metallic sharps belong to the White (puncture-proof) category."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"What is the recommended treatment/disposal method for YELLOW-category bio-medical waste?","options":["Autoclaving and recycling","Incineration (about 1100°C ± 100°C), plasma pyrolysis, or deep burial","Shredding only","Dry-heat sterilization only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table gives the treatment/disposal method for Yellow waste as incineration (1100°C ±100°C), plasma pyrolysis, or deep burial.  Why A wrong: Autoclaving/recycling is the Red-category method.  Why C wrong: Shredding is associated with Blue/White, not Yellow.  Why D wrong: Dry-heat sterilization is a White-category method, not the Yellow incineration route."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"Within the YELLOW category, items contaminated with cytotoxic drugs (e.g., glass/plastic ampoules, vials) and chemotherapy waste, plus PPE used in chemotherapy, are highlighted as which special subgroup?","options":["Recyclable plastic","Yellow bag marked with \"cytotoxic waste\"","Black-coded waste","Sharps waste"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table notes a Yellow bag marked with \"cytotoxic waste\" includes cytotoxic waste/drugs with glass/plastic ampoules and vials, chemotherapy drug vials, and all PPE worn during handling of cytotoxic and chemotherapeutic drugs.  Why A/C/D wrong: Cytotoxic waste is a yellow-bag subgroup, not recyclable plastic, black, or sharps."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"In the BMW color-coding, the RED category contains which type of waste?","options":["Anatomical body parts","Contaminated recyclable waste — non-metallic plastic items such as IV sets, IV tubes, central line and PICC line, catheters (Foley catheters, urine bags, suction catheters), tubings (Ryle's/NG tubes, ET/feeding tubes, oxygen tubings, ventilator/nebulizer tubings), syringes without needles, gloves, etc.","Metallic sharps such as needles","Discarded medicines"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Red category includes all types of contaminated recyclable (non-metallic plastic) waste — IV sets, IV tubes, central line/PICC line, catheters (Foley, urine bag, suction catheters), tubings (Ryle's/NG, ET/feeding, oxygen, ventilator/nebulizer), IV cannula and IV fluid bottles (PL, NS, DNS, etc.), surgical drains, gloves, syringes without needles, pipes and containers, ostomy bags, and diagnostic kits.  Why A/D wrong: Anatomical parts and discarded medicines are Yellow-category.  Why C wrong: Metallic sharps are White-category."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"What is the treatment/disposal method specified for RED-category bio-medical waste?","options":["Incineration or deep burial","Autoclaving, microwaving, or hydroclaving and then send for recycling","Plasma pyrolysis only","Encapsulation in concrete only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists the Red-category treatment as autoclaving, microwaving, or hydroclaving, and then sending for recycling.  Why A wrong: Incineration/deep burial is the Yellow method.  Why C wrong: Plasma pyrolysis is a Yellow option.  Why D wrong: Encapsulation in concrete is a White-category disposal option."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"In the BMW color-coding, the BLUE category is used for which waste?","options":["Plastic IV tubing","All glassware items (broken or unbroken), including slides, vials, and ampoules (except cytotoxic waste); glass bottles; metallic body implants; and disposable/laboratory single-use plastic glassware-type items","Human anatomical waste","Discarded chemotherapy drugs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Blue category covers all glassware items (broken or unbroken) including slides, vials, and ampoules (except cytotoxic waste), glass bottles, metallic body implants, and disposable/laboratory glassware-type items.  Why A wrong: Plastic IV tubing is Red.  Why C/D wrong: Anatomical and chemotherapy waste are Yellow."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"What is the treatment/disposal method specified for BLUE-category bio-medical waste?","options":["Incineration only","Autoclave, microwave, or shredding","Deep burial only","Plasma pyrolysis only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists the Blue-category treatment/disposal as autoclave, microwave, or shredding.  Why A/C/D wrong: Incineration, deep burial, and plasma pyrolysis are Yellow-category methods, not Blue."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"The WHITE (puncture-proof) container in BMW color-coding is used for which type of waste?","options":["Anatomical waste","Sharps items — needles, syringes with fixed needles, scalpels, blades, cannula, suture needles, biopsy needles, pre-filled glass syringes with fixed needles, guide-wires, and all metallic/surgical sharps from laboratories","Recyclable plastic","Glass bottles"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The White (puncture-proof) category contains sharps waste — needles without syringes, syringes with fixed needles, scalpels and blades, cannula and biopsy needle styles, suture and biopsy needles, pre-filled glass syringes with fixed needles, guide-wires, scalpel blades, and all sharps/operation sharps from laboratories.  Why A/C/D wrong: Anatomical (Yellow), recyclable plastic (Red), and glass bottles (Blue) belong to other color codes."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"What is the treatment/disposal method for WHITE-category (sharps) bio-medical waste?","options":["Only deep burial","Autoclave, dry-heat sterilization, shredding, and then encapsulation in metal container or concrete; final disposal in a designated concrete waste sharp-pit","Incineration at 1100°C only","Send for recycling without treatment"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table specifies the White (sharps) treatment as autoclave, dry-heat sterilization, shredding, followed by encapsulation in a metal container or concrete and final disposal in a designated concrete waste sharp-pit.  Why A/C wrong: Deep burial and incineration are Yellow-category routes.  Why D wrong: Sharps require treatment/encapsulation before disposal, not untreated recycling."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"The BLACK color code in bio-medical waste handling is meant for which type of waste?","options":["Infectious sharps","General waste including food, paper waste, uninfected plastic waste in a mineral-water bottle, uninfected disposable towels, uninfected gowns/shoe covers/caps/masks, covers of sterile items (uninfected syringes), sterilium-used bottle, Bacillol or any other uninfected used material","Human anatomical waste","Cytotoxic chemotherapy waste"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Black code is for general (non-infected) waste — food and paper waste, uninfected plastic waste/mineral-water bottle, uninfected disposable towel, uninfected gowns/shoe covers/caps/masks, covers of sterile items, sterilium-used bottle, Bacillol or any other uninfected used material.  Why A/C/D wrong: Sharps (White), anatomical (Yellow), and cytotoxic chemotherapy waste (Yellow) are categorized in other color codes."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"An important note in the BMW color-coding table states that BLACK colour-coded waste is:","options":["The most infectious of all waste","Excluded from / not included in BMW (it is general waste, not bio-medical waste)","To be incinerated at 1100°C","To be sent for plasma pyrolysis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table note states that Black colour code is excluded from BMW (general/non-infected waste, not biomedical waste).  Why A wrong: Black is non-infected general waste, not the most infectious.  Why C/D wrong: Incineration/plasma pyrolysis are Yellow treatments; Black is general waste outside BMW."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"A clinical nurse caring for a confirmed pulmonary tuberculosis patient enters the negative-pressure isolation room. Which protective measure is the priority before entering?","options":["Put on a surgical mask only","Don a fit-tested respirator (N95/FFP2/HEPA) before entering and perform a seal check after donning","Wear sterile gloves only","Wear a plastic apron only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Tuberculosis requires airborne precautions; the source directs wearing a respirator (N95/FFP2/HEPA) before entering the room and performing a respirator seal-check after donning.  Why A wrong: A surgical mask is insufficient for airborne <5-micron particles.  Why C/D wrong: Gloves or an apron alone do not protect against airborne transmission, which is the priority hazard here."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"A nurse must dispose of a used IV set and a urinary (Foley) catheter with urine bag. Into which color-coded container should these be placed?","options":["Yellow bag","Red bag (contaminated recyclable plastic waste)","Blue container","White puncture-proof container"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: IV sets, catheters (Foley), and urine bags are contaminated recyclable plastic waste, which belongs in the Red category.  Why A wrong: Yellow is for anatomical/soiled/expired-medicine/chemical waste.  Why C wrong: Blue is for glassware and metallic implants.  Why D wrong: White is for sharps only."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"A used hypodermic needle and a scalpel blade must be discarded. The correct disposal is into the:","options":["Yellow bag","Red bag","Blue container","White (puncture-proof) container"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Needles and scalpel blades are sharps, which go into the White puncture-proof container.  Why A/B/C wrong: Yellow (anatomical/soiled), Red (recyclable plastic), and Blue (glassware/implants) categories are not for metallic sharps."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"A nurse is discarding expired antibiotics and a soiled dressing contaminated with blood. The correct color-coded bag is:","options":["Yellow","Red","Blue","Black"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Expired/discarded medicines (including antibiotics) and soiled waste contaminated with blood/body fluids (e.g., dressings, bandages) are Yellow-category waste.  Why B wrong: Red is for recyclable plastic.  Why C wrong: Blue is for glassware/implants.  Why D wrong: Black is general, non-infected waste only."},{"section":"Batch 048 · Doffing per WHO sequence, donning and removing gloves…","question":"During an infection-control audit, broken glass slides and ampoules (non-cytotoxic) plus a removed metallic orthopedic implant must be discarded. Which container is correct?","options":["Yellow bag","Red bag","Blue container","Black bag"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Glassware (broken/unbroken slides, ampoules — except cytotoxic) and metallic body implants belong to the Blue category.  Why A/B wrong: Yellow and Red are for anatomical/soiled and recyclable plastic waste respectively.  Why D wrong: Black is for general non-infected waste."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"A hospital generating biomedical waste must follow specific disposal methods. Which method involves the controlled combustion of organic substances at high temperature, producing flue gas and ash?","options":["Hydroclave","Incineration","Shredding","Deep burial"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Incineration is the most common method for biomedical waste disposal; it is a waste-treatment process that involves the controlled combustion of organic substances contained in waste materials, producing flue gas and ash.  Why A wrong: Hydroclave is a steam-based sterilization technology (high heat dehydration), not combustion.  Why C wrong: Shredding reduces material volume, it is not combustion.  Why D wrong: Deep burial is land disposal of solid waste, not combustion."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"Incinerator and other high-temperature waste-treatment systems usually treat biomedical waste at what temperature, described as \"thermal treatment\"?","options":["100°C","250°C","≥1000°C","50°C"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Incineration and other high-temperature waste-treatment systems usually treat waste at ≥1000°C and are described as \"thermal treatment\"; this high temperature also destroys solid materials that convert the waste into ash, flue gas, and heat.  Why A/B/D wrong: These temperatures are too low for thermal-treatment incineration of biomedical waste."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"A sterilization technology uses indirect heating with steam, achieving total dehydration of waste material. This describes which biomedical waste disposal method?","options":["Pyrolysis","Landfill","Hydroclave","Microwaving"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The Hydroclave is a steam sterilization technology in which the steam is used as an indirect heating source, thus achieving total dehydration of waste material.  Why A wrong: Pyrolysis is chemical decomposition at elevated temperatures in the absence of oxygen.  Why B wrong: Landfill is engineered land disposal of solid and hazardous waste.  Why D wrong: Microwaving uses microwaves to kill micro-organisms, not indirect steam dehydration."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"In the hydroclave method, the holding time for waste is which of the following?","options":["5 minutes at 121°C","15 minutes at 121°C or 30 minutes at 121°C","60 minutes at 100°C","2 minutes at 132°C"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The holding time for waste in the hydroclave is 15 minutes at 121°C or 30 minutes at 121°C.  Why A/C/D wrong: These holding times/temperatures do not match the stated hydroclave parameters."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"The organic components of biomedical waste are hydrolyzed and the waste material is reduced by weight and volume. This process describes which method?","options":["Shredding","Hydroclave","Pyrolysis","Landfill"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In hydroclave the organic components of the waste are hydrolyzed and the waste material is reduced by weight and volume.  Why A wrong: Shredding cuts/reduces material into pieces before disposal.  Why C wrong: Pyrolysis is thermal decomposition without oxygen.  Why D wrong: Landfill is land disposal."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"Which biomedical waste process reduces waste material volume before it goes for composting, and reduces the overall volume by approximately what percentage?","options":["Incineration; 10%","Shredding; 60%","Pyrolysis; 90%","Microwaving; 25%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Shredding reduces the waste material volume before it goes for composting; this process reduces the overall volume by 60%.  Why A/C/D wrong: These pairings of method and percentage are incorrect per the source."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"Why does increased surface area (from shredding) matter for waste treatment?","options":["It increases the weight of waste for landfill","It will increase surface area that is available for bacterial activity or decomposition","It generates flue gas for incineration","It prevents hydrolysis of organics"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Increasing surface area will increase the area available for bacterial activity or decomposition.  Why A wrong: Shredding reduces volume, it is about decomposition not added weight.  Why C wrong: Flue gas is a product of incineration, not shredding.  Why D wrong: Increased surface area promotes (not prevents) breakdown."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"Pyrolysis is best defined as:","options":["Steam-based dehydration of waste","Chemically decomposing organic materials at elevated temperatures in the absence of oxygen","Burial of sharps in concrete-lined pits","Routine collection of body parts for burial"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pyrolysis is a process of chemically decomposing organic materials at elevated temperatures in the absence of oxygen.  Why A wrong: That describes the hydroclave.  Why C wrong: That describes deep burial of sharps.  Why D wrong: That is not pyrolysis."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"The process of pyrolysis occurs at temperatures above which range?","options":["100–121°C","250–300°C","430°C (800°F) and under pressure","50–60°C"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The process of pyrolysis occurs at temperatures above 430°C (800°F) and under pressure.  Why A/B/D wrong: These temperatures are below the stated pyrolysis threshold."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"A landfill, in biomedical waste management, is correctly described as:","options":["A combustion chamber for organic waste","An engineered method for land disposal of solid and hazardous waste","A steam sterilization unit","A shredding machine for sharps"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A landfill is an engineered method for land disposal of solid and hazardous waste.  Why A wrong: That describes incineration.  Why C wrong: That describes the hydroclave.  Why D wrong: That describes shredding."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"Which biomedical waste treatment shall NOT be used for cytotoxic, hazardous, or radioactive wastes, contaminated animal carcasses, body parts, and large metal items?","options":["Incineration","Deep burial","Microwave treatment","Pyrolysis"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Microwave treatment shall not be used for cytotoxic, hazardous, or radioactive wastes, contaminated animal carcasses, body parts, and large metal items.  Why A/B/D wrong: These restrictions are specifically stated for microwaving, not for these other methods."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"When validating a microwave waste-treatment system, the system shall comply with the efficacy of which type of test?","options":["Sterility culture test","Biological indicator (test/auto clave) test","pH litmus test","Radiation dosimeter test"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The microwave system shall comply with the efficacy test/auto clave tests, and the microwave should completely and consistently kill bacteria and other pathogenic organisms that is ensured by the approved biological indicator at the maximum design capacity of each microwave unit.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the validation tests specified for microwave waste treatment."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"Regarding deep burial of biomedical waste, the pit or trench should be how deep, and covered with how much soil after each waste deposit?","options":["1 m deep; covered with 10 cm soil","Half-filled (about 2 m deep); covered with lime within 50 cm of the surface","5 m deep; left uncovered","0.5 m deep; covered with sand only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The pit or trench should be dug about 2 m deep; it should be half filled with waste, then covered with lime within 50 cm of the surface, before filling the rest of the pit with soil.  Why A/C/D wrong: These depths/covering instructions do not match the source."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"For deep burial of biomedical waste, the burial site must NOT have which characteristic?","options":["It must be authorized by the prescribed authority","The institution shall maintain a record of all pits for deep burial","Animals must not have access to burial sites","The location of the site is left undocumented and unrestricted"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The location of the site must be authorized by the prescribed authority and documented; leaving it undocumented/unrestricted violates the rules.  Why A wrong: This is a correct requirement (authorized by prescribed authority).  Why B wrong: This is a correct requirement (maintain a record of all pits).  Why C wrong: This is a correct requirement (it must be ensured that animals do not have access to burial sites)."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"Before deep burial, the area where waste is buried should be free of which condition?","options":["Free of vegetation","Free to prone flooding or erosion","Free of lime","Free of soil cover"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The area should not be prone to flooding or erosion (it should be \"free to prone flooding or erosion\").  Why A/C/D wrong: These conditions are not the stated requirement for the burial area."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"Per the Bio Medical Waste Management (Amendment) Rules, sharps should be disposed of by which method?","options":["Disposed of as ordinary waste in landfill","Constructed in hospital premises, mm × mm concrete-lined and protected with a cemented lid; the filled needle container can be placed in the sharp pits up to 3/4th of the capacity of the pit","Burned in open fires","Flushed down hospital drains"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Suggested standard for disposal of sharps — a sharp pit should be constructed in hospital premises, mm × mm concrete-lined and protected with a cemented lid; the filled needle container can be placed in the sharp pits up to 3/4th of the capacity of the pit.  Why A/C/D wrong: Sharps require dedicated puncture-resistant concrete sharp pits, not landfill, open burning, or drains."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"The Bio Medical Waste Management (Amendment) Rules referenced were published under which notification, and on what date?","options":["C.S.R. 376 (E), 1 January 2010","G.S.R. 179 (E) on 19th March 2019","G.S.R. 500 (E), 5 June 2016","C.S.R. 100 (E), 2 October 2018"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Central Government vide notification G.S.R. 179 (E) on 19th March 2019 published the Bio-Medical Waste Management (Amendment) Rules, in the e-gazette.  Why A/C/D wrong: These notification numbers/dates do not match the stated amendment."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"Under the amended Bio Medical Waste Management Rules, by which date should health-care facilities phase out chlorinated plastic bags, gloves, and blood bags?","options":["March 2019","March 2021","March 2025","June 2018"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Such health-care facilities (those phasing out of chlorinated plastic bags, gloves, and blood bags) shall make the annual report available on its website before March 2021.  Why A/C/D wrong: These dates do not match the stated phase-out/reporting timeline."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"Under the amended Bio Medical Waste Rules, the occupier of all bedded health-care units shall maintain and update on a day-to-day basis which document?","options":["The staff attendance register only","The biomedical waste-management register","The pharmacy inventory","The patient discharge summary"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The occupier of all bedded health-care units shall maintain and update on a day-to-day basis the biomedical waste-management register and display the monthly record of waste-disposal management on its website.  Why A/C/D wrong: The rule specifies the biomedical waste-management register, not these other documents."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"Barrier nursing is best defined as:","options":["Confining a patient to a single room without any equipment","A set of stringent infection-control techniques used in nursing","Routine handwashing only","Disposal of biomedical waste in barriers"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Barrier nursing is a set of stringent infection-control techniques used in nursing.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are incomplete or unrelated descriptions; barrier nursing is the broad set of infection-control techniques."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"Which statements correctly describe BARRIER nursing? Select all that apply.","options":["It is sometimes called bedside isolation","The nursing technique by which a patient with an infectious disease is prevented from infecting other people","It is called barrier nursing","The patient is protected from the nurse and all the people in contact with the patient","To keep a barrier between infected and non-infected, or sterile and unsterile area to prevent cross infections"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: Barrier nursing is sometimes called bedside isolation; it is the nursing technique by which a patient with an infectious disease is prevented from infecting other people; it is called barrier nursing; and a barrier is kept between infected and non-infected/sterile and unsterile areas to prevent cross infections.  Why D wrong: Protecting the patient from the nurse and contacts describes REVERSE barrier nursing, not barrier nursing."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"Reverse barrier nursing is used when:","options":["A patient with an infectious disease needs to be isolated from others","The patient needs to be protected from any infections you might carry to him/her","Biomedical waste must be segregated","A fire occurs in the ward"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Reverse barrier nursing is when the patient needs to be protected from any infections you might carry. The patient is protected from the nurse and the people in contact with the patient.  Why A wrong: Isolating an infectious patient from others is barrier (not reverse barrier) nursing.  Why C/D wrong: These are unrelated to reverse barrier nursing."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"Which patients are appropriate EXAMPLES requiring reverse barrier nursing? Select all that apply.","options":["Immunocompromised patients","Cancer patients undergoing chemotherapy","Patients with active tuberculosis","Patients with measles","Patients with confirmed COVID-19"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists immunocompromised patients and cancer patients undergoing chemotherapy as examples for reverse barrier nursing (protective isolation).  Why C/D/E wrong: TB, measles, and COVID-19 are infectious conditions requiring barrier (source) isolation to protect others, not reverse barrier nursing."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"During environmental cleaning, how often should patient-care areas be cleaned, with particular attention to which surfaces?","options":["Once a week; frequently touched surfaces","At least once a day, paying particular attention to frequently touched surfaces","Once a month; floors only","Only when visibly soiled; ceilings"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Health workers should clean and disinfect patient-care areas at least once a day, paying particular attention to frequently touched surfaces.  Why A/C/D wrong: These frequencies/surfaces do not match the recommendation."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"When should spills of blood and body fluid/substance be dealt with during environmental cleaning?","options":["At the end of the shift","As soon as possible, in accordance with local protocols","Only after the patient is discharged","Weekly"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Health workers should deal with spills of blood and body fluid/substance as soon as possible, in accordance with local protocols.  Why A/C/D wrong: Spills must be addressed promptly, not deferred."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"When handling soiled linen, what is the correct nursing action?","options":["Shake the linen vigorously to remove debris","Handle the soiled linen and waste carefully to prevent self and cross infection","Carry soiled linen in your arms close to the body","Place soiled linen on the floor before sorting"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Health workers should handle soiled linen and waste carefully to prevent self and cross infection.  Why A wrong: Heavily soiled linen should be folded (e.g. faeces inside), not shaken — shaking aerosolizes pathogens.  Why C wrong: Linen should be stored/carried in a manner that protects from environmental contaminants.  Why D wrong: Placing linen on the floor contaminates it."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"How should heavily soiled linen (e.g., contaminated with faeces) be handled?","options":["Soak it in disinfectant in the patient's room","Remove heavily soiled material (e.g. faeces) from linen, while wearing appropriate PPE, before placing it in the laundry bag","Send it directly to the incinerator","Rinse it in the patient's sink"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Remove heavily soiled material (e.g. faeces) from linen, while wearing appropriate PPE, before placing it in the laundry bag.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the stated handling steps for heavily soiled linen."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"Where should linen be stored to maintain its safety for patient use?","options":["In an open shelf in the corridor","In a manner that protects from environmental contaminants","Mixed with clinical waste","In the patient's bedside cabinet"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Store clean linen in a manner that protects from environmental contaminants.  Why A/C/D wrong: These storage approaches risk contamination of clean linen."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"During decontamination and reprocessing of reusable patient-care items, which actions should health workers take? Select all that apply.","options":["Handle equipment soiled with blood, body fluids, secretions, and excretions in a manner that prevents skin and mucous membrane exposure","Clean and disinfect (or disinfect/sterilize) depending on the type and use of patient-care equipment/reusable equipment","Reuse single-use devices after thorough cleaning","Clean reusable equipment before use on another patient","Discard single-use devices after each use"],"correct":"A,B,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Handle soiled equipment to prevent skin/mucous-membrane exposure and contamination; clean and disinfect or sterilize depending on equipment type/use; clean before use on other patients; and discard single-use devices after each use.  Why C wrong: Single-use devices must be discarded after each use — they should never be reprocessed/reused."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"In aseptic technique, which actions should health workers take for surgical procedures? Select all that apply.","options":["Use sterile items and equipment for all aseptic procedures","Use aseptic technique for insertion and maintenance of all invasive devices and aseptic/clean clinical procedures","Reuse wound dressings between patients","Use wound dressing and similar to prevent infections","Clean and disinfect reusable equipment per guidelines"],"correct":"A,B,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Use sterile items/equipment for aseptic procedures; use aseptic technique for invasive-device insertion/maintenance and aseptic/clean procedures; use wound dressing and similar to prevent infections; and clean/disinfect or sterilize reusable equipment per guidelines.  Why C wrong: Wound dressings are not reused between patients; this would cause cross-infection."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"\"LASA\" in medication safety stands for:","options":["Last Administered Single Agent","Look Alike Sound Alike","Local Anaesthetic Safety Alert","Limited Access Storage Area"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: LASA stands for Look Alike Sound Alike. (Also referred to as SALA — Sound Alike Look Alike.)  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the meaning of the LASA acronym."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"Why are Look Alike Sound Alike (LASA) medications a safety concern?","options":["They are always expired drugs","They appear visually the same with respect to packaging, shape, colour and/or size, while sound-alike medicines are similar in phonetics of their names, doses, and/or strengths","They are only available in liquid form","They cost more than other drugs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Look-alike medicines appear visually the same with respect to packaging, shape, colour and/or size, while sound-alike medicines are similar in the phonetics of their names, doses, and/or strengths.  Why A/C/D wrong: The concern is confusion from similar appearance/sound, not expiry, formulation, or cost."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"Which is given as an EXAMPLE of confusing look-alike/sound-alike drug names in the source?","options":["Aspirin and Paracetamol","Clona-ZEPAM and Lora-ZEPAM","Insulin and Heparin","Ampicillin and Amoxicillin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The example given is Clona-ZEPAM and Lora-ZEPAM.  Why A/C/D wrong: These pairs are not the example cited on the page."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"What is the consequence if these look-alike/sound-alike drug names cause confusion?","options":["Only a minor delay in administration","These drug-name confusions are a major cause of medication errors; these errors may cause harm or even death to patients","They improve patient adherence","They have no clinical impact"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: These confusing drug names are one of the main causes of medication errors; these errors may result in medication error and may cause harm or even death to patients.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source identifies LASA confusion as a major (potentially fatal) source of error, not a minor or beneficial event."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"What storage precaution does the source recommend for LASA drugs to reduce errors?","options":["Store all LASA drugs together on one shelf","All LASA drugs need to be kept inside the cupboard and a sticker should be placed outside the cupboard","Store LASA drugs at the patient's bedside","Refrigerate all LASA drugs regardless of type"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: All LASA drugs need to be kept inside the cupboard and a sticker should be placed outside the cupboard (to alert staff).  Why A wrong: Keeping look-alike/sound-alike drugs together increases the risk of confusion.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the recommended LASA storage precautions."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"To further reduce LASA medication errors during dispensing, nursing staff should:","options":["Dispense from memory to save time","Doubly check LASA medicine before dispensing","Avoid reading the label aloud","Crush all LASA tablets before dispensing"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In wards, nursing staff should doubly check LASA medicine before dispensing.  Why A/C/D wrong: These actions increase, rather than reduce, the risk of LASA medication errors."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"According to the Appropriate Barrier Precautions table, \"venipuncture\" requires which barrier(s)?","options":["Gloves only","Gloves and gowns","Gloves, gowns, masks, and eye protection","Eye protection only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The barrier precautions table marks \"Yes\" only under Gloves for venipuncture; gowns, masks, and eye protection are not required.  Why B/C/D wrong: These additional barriers are not indicated for routine venipuncture per the table."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"According to the barrier precautions table, arterial cannulation requires which barriers?","options":["Gloves and gowns only","Gloves, gowns, masks, and eye protection","Gloves only","Masks only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table indicates \"Yes\" for gloves, gowns, masks, and eye protection for arterial cannulation.  Why A/C/D wrong: Arterial cannulation requires the full set of barriers, not just a subset."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"Per the barrier precautions table, nursing an immunocompromised patient requires which glove practice?","options":["Single gloves","No gloves needed","Double gloves","Thick rubber gloves"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table specifies \"Double gloves\" for nursing of immunocompromised patients.  Why A/B wrong: Single gloves or no gloves provide insufficient protection.  Why D wrong: Thick rubber gloves are indicated for handling/spillage of toxic agents, not immunocompromised patient nursing."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"According to the barrier precautions table, the management of toxic agents/aerosol infection requires which barriers?","options":["Gloves only","Thick rubber gloves and masks","Eye protection only","No barriers"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table indicates thick rubber gloves and masks (Yes) for protection against toxic agents/aerosol infection.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not reflect the full barrier set for toxic/aerosol exposure."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"Per the barrier precautions table, specimen and sample handling requires which barriers?","options":["Gloves and gowns","Gloves, gowns, masks, and eye protection","Eye protection only","Masks only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table marks Yes across gloves, gowns, masks, and eye protection for specimen and sample handling.  Why A/C/D wrong: Specimen handling requires the full protective set per the table."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"According to the barrier precautions table, \"urinary catheterisation\" requires which barrier?","options":["Gloves only","Gowns only","Masks only","Eye protection only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table indicates Gloves (Yes) for urinary catheterisation.  Why B/C/D wrong: These barriers are not indicated for routine urinary catheterisation per the table."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"Standard precautions are a category of isolation recommendations applied to all patients. Which organisms are listed under STANDARD precautions in the source?","options":["Pertussis, Influenza, Rubella","CMV, Hepatitis B and C, Aspergillosis","MRSA, VRE, C. difficile","Measles, Avian influenza, Covid-19"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Under Standard Precautions the source lists CMV, Hepatitis B and C, and Aspergillosis.  Why A wrong: Pertussis, Influenza, and Rubella are Droplet precautions.  Why C wrong: MRSA, VRE, and C. difficile are Contact precautions.  Why D wrong: Measles, Avian influenza, and Covid-19 are Airborne precautions."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"Which of the following pathogens require DROPLET precautions per the source? Select all that apply.","options":["Pertussis","Influenza A or B","MRSA (respiratory infection)","RSV (droplet and contact)","Measles"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Droplet precautions are listed for Pertussis, Influenza A or B, bacterial meningitis (before effective antibiotic therapy), MRSA (respiratory infection), RSV (droplet and contact), Neisseria meningitidis, Mumps, Coxsackie, and Rubella.  Why E wrong: Measles requires Airborne precautions, not droplet, per the source."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"Which infections require AIRBORNE precautions according to the source? Select all that apply.","options":["Chicken pox","Disseminated herpes zoster (shingles)","Tuberculosis","SARS","C. difficile"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Airborne precautions are listed for Chicken pox, Disseminated herpes zoster (shingles), Tuberculosis, Enterovirus, SARS, Measles, Avian influenza, and Covid-19.  Why E wrong: C. difficile requires Contact precautions, not airborne."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"Which organisms/conditions require CONTACT precautions per the source? Select all that apply.","options":["MRSA","VRE","C. difficile","Scabies","Influenza A or B"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Contact precautions are listed for C. difficile, MRSA, E. coli, VRE, Enterovirus, Salmonella, Adenovirus, Shigella, Diarrhea, Hepatitis A, Rotavirus, Herpes zoster (localized, normal host), Parainfluenza (mask if productive cough), RSV, Lice, Scabies, and Chicken pox.  Why E wrong: Influenza A or B is listed under Droplet precautions, not contact."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"Contact precautions are initiated for a client with a health-care-associated (nosocomial) infection caused by methicillin-resistant *Staphylococcus aureus* (MRSA). What should the nurse-officer first provide to others to perform this procedure?","options":["Gloves and mask only","Gown and gloves","Gloves, gown, mask, and eye protection","Gown, gloves, goggles"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Per the source's \"Recall, the nurse!\" box, the correct (keyed) answer is A — Gown, mask, gloves. The text states a patient with MRSA infection has to follow contact precautions, which require use of gloves and a gown to be worn if going to enter the room. (The keyed correct answer in the source is A.)  Why B/C/D wrong: These option combinations do not match the source's keyed answer A for this item.  Note: The source's keyed answer for this recall item is A; gown and gloves are the minimum PPE described in the supporting text for contact precautions with MRSA."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"According to the source's recall item, which principle component is essential to prevent the infection chain link?","options":["Hand washing","Maintaining aseptic technique","Gloves, mask, goggles","PPE"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source's recall item keys \"Right answer: A\" for the principle component to prevent the infection-chain link — Hand washing. Hand washing is the single most effective measure to break the chain of infection.  Why B wrong: Maintaining aseptic technique is important but is not the keyed single answer here.  Why C wrong: Gloves/mask/goggles are PPE, not the keyed principle component.  Why D wrong: PPE is supportive but hand washing is the keyed answer."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"During DONNING of personal protective equipment, what is the correct first step?","options":["Put on gloves","Perform hand hygiene","Put on mask","Put on gown"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the Steps of Donning and Doffing, the donning sequence begins with: Perform hand hygiene (first step), then Gown, Mask, Goggles or face shields, and finally Gloves (last step).  Why A/C/D wrong: Gloves are last, mask is third, and gown is second in the donning sequence; hand hygiene is first."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"What is the correct sequence for DOFFING (removing) personal protective equipment per the source?","options":["Gloves → Goggles or face shields → Gown → Mask → Perform hand hygiene","Mask → Gown → Gloves → Goggles → Hand hygiene","Hand hygiene → Gloves → Gown → Mask → Goggles","Gown → Mask → Gloves → Hand hygiene → Goggles"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The doffing sequence is: Gloves (first step), Goggles or face shields, Gown, Mask, then Perform hand hygiene (last step).  Why B/C/D wrong: These orders do not match the source doffing sequence (gloves first, hand hygiene last)."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"When donning PPE, why must the gown be put on first (over hand hygiene's completion) and the gloves last?","options":["Because gloves are sterile and gowns are not","The first gloves should wear in the non-dominant hand and also removed from the non-dominant hand","Because gowns are disposable","Because masks contaminate gloves"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Per the doffing/donning notes, the first gloves should be worn on the non-dominant hand and also removed from the non-dominant hand.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the rationale stated in the source for the glove sequence."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"According to the source, in special scenarios (e.g., COVID-19 and Swine flu), what is the correct doffing practice for the gown?","options":["Remove the gown first before gloves","In doffing, wear shoe cover before wearing the mask, and in doffing remove shoe cover lastly","Reuse the gown for the next patient","Leave the gown on until leaving the building"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In special scenarios (COVID-19 and Swine flu), wear shoe cover before mask in donning, and in doffing remove the shoe cover lastly.  Why A wrong: Gloves are removed first in standard doffing.  Why C wrong: Gowns are not reused for the next patient.  Why D wrong: PPE is doffed in the designated sequence/area, not retained."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"The source advises that in COVID-19 scenarios, face masks (N95 respirators) are advised, and readers are urged to explore from which sources for these differences in guidelines?","options":["Hospital menu cards","Other sources (the source advises readers to explore guidelines from other sources, if any)","Newspaper advertisements","Patient testimonials"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In COVID-19 scenarios, N95 respirators are advised, and readers are advised to explore from other sources for these different guidelines.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not credible/recommended guideline sources."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"Per the doffing notes, what is the correct cough-etiquette practice?","options":["Cover the mouth and nose with the bare hands","Cover the mouth and nose when coughing or sneezing; use tissues, throw them away, then wash the hands or use a hand sanitizer every time you touch your mouth or nose","Cough freely without covering","Only cover the mouth, not the nose"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Cough etiquette: cover the mouth and nose when coughing or sneezing; use tissues and throw them away, wash the hands or use a hand sanitizer every time you touch your mouth or nose.  Why A wrong: Bare hands should not be used to cover; this contaminates hands.  Why C/D wrong: Both mouth and nose must be covered."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"According to the doffing/infection-control notes, how should gloves be handled between patients?","options":["Wear the same gloves for all patients","Change gloves between contacts with different patients","Wash gloves and reuse","Never wear gloves"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Change gloves between contacts with different patients.  Why A wrong: Wearing the same gloves across patients causes cross-infection.  Why C wrong: Disposable gloves should not be reused (they should be removed immediately after removing gloves and washed hands).  Why D wrong: Gloves are required for many procedures, not avoided."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"After attending to one patient and before attending to another, what should be done with gloves and hands per the source?","options":["Remove gloves immediately after use; wash hands immediately after removing gloves; disposable gloves should not be reused","Keep gloves on and proceed","Wash gloves only, not hands","Reuse disposable gloves after sanitizing"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Remove gloves immediately after use before attending to another patient; wash hands immediately after removing gloves; disposable gloves should not be reused.  Why B/C/D wrong: Keeping or reusing gloves, or skipping hand washing, breaches infection control."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"Chapter 19 covers Safety Protocols including fire safety. Fire safety is best defined as:","options":["The disposal of biomedical waste safely","The actions to take in the event of a fire","The maintenance of electrical equipment","The storage of LASA medicines"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Fire safety overview: it is the actions to take in the event of a fire.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are unrelated to the fire-safety definition."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"According to fire-safety overview, what general actions should be done in the event of a fire? Select all that apply.","options":["Turn off the oxygen in the event of fire","Always know the location of the fire alarms, exits, and use of extinguishers","Know the telephone number of the fire-brigade/govt office","Continue working as normal","Ignore the fire alarm if unsure"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: In a fire, you should turn off the oxygen, always know the locations of the fire alarms, exits, and use of extinguishers, and know the telephone number of the fire-brigade/govt office.  Why D/E wrong: One must respond to a fire (not continue working as normal) and never ignore a fire alarm."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"The mnemonic RACE describes the priority actions in the event of a fire. RACE stands for:","options":["Run, Alarm, Confine, Exit","Rescue, Activate, Confine, Extinguish","React, Alert, Contain, Evacuate","Remove, Assess, Call, Escape"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Priority actions in the event of a fire (RACE): Rescue the clients who are in immediate danger or remove the client from danger zone; Activate the fire alarm; Confine the fire or limit the fire; Extinguish the fire by fire extinguisher.  Why A/C/D wrong: These expansions do not match the RACE mnemonic as defined in the source."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"In the RACE sequence, what does the \"R\" (first priority) instruct the nurse to do?","options":["Activate the alarm","Rescue the clients who are in immediate danger or remove the client from the danger zone","Reach for the extinguisher","Report to administration"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: R = Rescue the clients who are in immediate danger or remove the client from the danger zone — this is the first priority.  Why A wrong: Activating the alarm is the \"A\" step, after rescue.  Why C wrong: Extinguishing is the \"E\" step (last).  Why D wrong: Reporting is not part of the RACE priority sequence."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"PASS is the mnemonic for how to use a fire extinguisher. What does PASS stand for?","options":["Point, Activate, Spray, Stop","Pull, Aim, Squeeze, Sweep","Press, Approach, Shake, Spray","Pull, Approach, Spray, Stop"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: How to use the fire extinguisher (PASS): Pull the pin on the fire-extinguisher handle; Aim at the base of the fire; Squeeze the extinguisher handle; Sweep the extinguisher from side to side to coat the area of the fire evenly.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the PASS expansion in the source."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"When using a fire extinguisher (PASS), where should you AIM?","options":["At the top of the flames","At the base of the fire","At the smoke above the fire","At the nearest exit"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: \"A\" in PASS = Aim at the base of the fire.  Why A/C wrong: Aiming at the flames/smoke rather than the base is ineffective.  Why D wrong: The exit is for evacuation, not the aim point of the extinguisher."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"The fire triangle consists of which three components?","options":["Oxygen, heat, and fuel","Oxygen, heat, and water","Fuel, smoke, and spark","Heat, carbon dioxide, and fuel"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Fire Triangle components are Oxygen, heat, and fuel.  Why B/C/D wrong: These component sets do not match the fire triangle."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"The fire tetrahedron adds which fourth component to the fire triangle?","options":["Smoke","Chemical chain reaction","Carbon dioxide","Water vapor"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Fire Tetrahedron components are oxygen, heat, fuel, and chemical chain reaction (the fourth component added to the triangle).  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the fourth component of the fire tetrahedron."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"Class A fires involve which materials, and what is usually the best extinguishing agent?","options":["Flammable liquids; foam","Ordinary combustibles such as wood, paper, cloth, rubber, and some plastics; water is usually the best extinguishing agent because it can penetrate fuels and absorb heat","Energized electrical equipment; dry chemicals","Combustible metals; dry powder"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Class A fires involve ordinary combustibles such as wood, paper, cloth, rubber, and some plastics; water is usually the best extinguishing agent because it can penetrate fuels and absorb heat. Dry chemicals are also used to interrupt the chemical chain reaction and are effective on Class A fires.  Why A wrong: Flammable liquids are Class B.  Why C wrong: Energized electrical equipment is Class C.  Why D wrong: Combustible metals are Class D."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"Class B fires involve which type of fuel?","options":["Wood and paper","Flammable and combustible liquids and gases such as gasoline, alcohols, and propane","Energized electrical equipment","Magnesium and titanium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Class B fires involve flammable and combustible liquids and gases such as gasoline, alcohols, and propane.  Why A wrong: Wood and paper are Class A.  Why C wrong: Energized electrical equipment is Class C.  Why D wrong: Magnesium and titanium are Class D."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"For Class B fires, which extinguishing approach is most effective?","options":["Water spray to cool the fuel","Extinguishing agents that smother the fire or reduce the oxygen concentration available to the burning zone are most effective","De-energizing the equipment","Applying dry powder for metals"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For Class B fires, extinguishing agents that smother the fire or reduce the oxygen concentration available to the burning zone are most effective.  Why A wrong: Water on burning liquids can spread the fire; smothering/oxygen reduction is preferred.  Why C wrong: De-energizing relates to Class C (electrical) fires.  Why D wrong: Dry powder for metals relates to Class D fires."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"Class C fires involve which hazard, and what precaution is necessary?","options":["Energized electrical equipment; non-conductive extinguishing agents are necessary, the equipment should be de-energized (then it is considered a Class A fire) and dry chemicals/inert gases are the most effective agents","Combustible liquids; foam","Ordinary combustibles; water","Combustible metals; dry powder"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Class C fires involve energized electrical equipment; non-conductive extinguishing agents are necessary; the equipment should be de-energized (once de-energized it is considered a Class A fire); and dry chemicals and inert gases are the most effective agents. If it can be done safely, personnel should isolate the power source before attempting to extinguish.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe Class B, A, and D fires respectively, not Class C."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"Class D fires involve which materials?","options":["Wood, paper, and rubber","Combustible metals such as magnesium, sodium, titanium, powdered aluminium, potassium, and zirconium","Energized electrical equipment","Flammable liquids and gases"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Class D fires involve combustible metals such as magnesium, sodium, titanium, powdered aluminium, potassium, and zirconium.  Why A wrong: Those are Class A combustibles.  Why C wrong: Energized electrical equipment is Class C.  Why D wrong: Flammable liquids/gases are Class B."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"Class D fires require which type of specialized extinguishing agent?","options":["Water","Class D fires require special extinguishing agents that are usually produced for the specific metal","Carbon dioxide","Foam"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Class D fires require special extinguishing agents that are usually produced for the specific metal.  Why A wrong: Water can react violently with burning metals.  Why C/D wrong: CO2 and foam are not the specific agents for combustible-metal fires."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"What should you do FIRST if a co-worker is shocked or burned by electricity and is still in contact with the energized circuit?","options":["Touch the victim immediately to pull them away","Shut off the electrical current if the victim is still in contact with the energized circuit (first action in electrical fire), while you do this, have someone else call for help","Begin CPR while they touch the wire","Pour water on the victim"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: First action — shut off the electrical current if the victim is still in contact with the energized circuit; while you do this, have someone else call for help.  Why A wrong: Touching a victim still in contact with the current makes you a victim too — you do not want to be a victim.  Why C wrong: CPR is begun only after the victim is no longer in contact with the current and is unconscious/not breathing.  Why D wrong: Pouring water on an electrical source/victim is dangerous."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"If you cannot quickly get to the switch/gear to shut off power, what should you do for a victim still in contact with the energized circuit?","options":["Touch the victim with your bare hands","Try to separate the victim from the circuit with something that does not conduct electricity, such as dry wood","Pull the victim by their wet clothing","Wait until the fire brigade arrives"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If you cannot get to the switch/gear quickly, try to separate the victim from the circuit with something that does not conduct electricity, such as dry wood.  Why A wrong: Touching the victim with bare hands risks electrocuting yourself.  Why C wrong: Wet materials conduct electricity and are dangerous.  Why D wrong: Delay increases injury risk; safe separation is the immediate action."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"Once the victim is no longer flowing electrical current, what should you do?","options":["Leave the victim alone","Call out to the victim to see if he or she is conscious (awake); if conscious, tell the victim not to move; if not breathing, begin CPR in an advanced stage accordingly","Give the victim food and water","Apply ice to the burns immediately"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Once the victim no longer has current flowing through them, call out to see if conscious (awake); if conscious, tell the victim not to move; the caller should come back afterward to verify the call was made; and if the victim is unconscious, assess breathing and begin CPR accordingly.  Why A wrong: The victim must be assessed and assisted, not left alone.  Why C wrong: Food/water is not indicated and may be dangerous if the victim is impaired.  Why D wrong: Immediate emergency assessment (consciousness/breathing/CPR) takes priority over burn care."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"Per the \"Recall the nurse-officer!\" item, what is the PRIMARY intervention to be taken after a fire incident in the hospital?","options":["Use the fire extinguisher","Rescue the patient from the hospital (keyed answer A)","Shut off the oxygen supply","Close windows and doors to reduce ventilation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source's recall item keys \"Right answer: A\" — Rescue the patient from the hospital is the primary intervention (consistent with RACE, where Rescue is the first priority).  Why A wrong: Using the extinguisher (Extinguish) is the last RACE step, not the primary action.  Why C wrong: Shutting off oxygen is a supportive action, not the keyed primary intervention.  Why D wrong: Closing doors/windows to reduce ventilation/limit fire spread is secondary to rescuing patients."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"Radiation safety requires awareness of which documents and the ALARA principle. ALARA stands for:","options":["As Low As Reasonably Achievable","Always Limit All Radiation Areas","Avoid Long-term Adverse Radiation Affects","Apply Lead Around Radiation Areas"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: ALARA stands for \"As Low As Reasonably Achievable.\" It is a principle that states all radiation exposure to humans should be kept as low as reasonably achievable.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the meaning of ALARA."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"The 3 Radiation ALARA principles to reduce external exposure are summarized by the mnemonic TDS. These are:","options":["Time, Dose, Sensitivity","Time, Distance, Shielding","Type, Duration, Storage","Test, Document, Shield"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Follow the 3 Radiation ALARA principles to reduce external exposure of radiation (Trick: TDS): Time, Distance, and Shielding.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the TDS (Time, Distance, Shielding) mnemonic."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"Under the ALARA \"Time\" principle, the nurse should:","options":["Increase the time spent near radiation sources","Limit the time spent near the sources; less radiation received","Spend unlimited time as long as shielding is used","Document the time only after exposure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Time — limit the time spent near the sources; less radiation received.  Why A wrong: Increasing time near a source increases exposure.  Why C wrong: Shielding does not justify unlimited exposure time.  Why D wrong: The principle is about minimizing exposure time, not merely documenting it."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"Under the ALARA \"Distance\" principle, the nurse should:","options":["Stay as close as possible to the source","Maintain a greater distance from the sources; less radiation received","Distance does not affect radiation exposure","Only increase distance after shielding fails"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Distance — maintain a greater distance from the sources; less radiation received.  Why A wrong: Closeness increases exposure.  Why C wrong: Distance is one of the three key principles reducing exposure (inverse-square relationship).  Why D wrong: Distance is applied as a primary protective measure, not a fallback."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"Under the ALARA \"Shielding\" principle, the nurse should:","options":["Avoid using any shielding to save time","Use a shielding device or lead apron; less radiation received","Use only paper barriers","Rely solely on distance instead of shielding"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Shielding — use a shielding device or lead apron; less radiation received.  Why A wrong: Avoiding shielding increases exposure.  Why C wrong: Paper does not shield radiation; lead aprons/shields are required.  Why D wrong: Shielding is one of the three independent protective principles, used alongside time and distance."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"Which radiation-safety practices does the source recommend regarding monitoring and implants? Select all that apply.","options":["Monitor radiation exposure with a film badge or dosimeter","Place the client who has a radiation implant in a private or isolation room","Never touch dislodged radiation implants","Reposition dislodged implants back into the client with bare hands","Feed all linens from the client's room until the implant is removed"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: Monitor radiation exposure with a film badge or dosimeter; place the client with a radiation implant in a private/isolation room; never touch dislodged radiation implants; and feed/save all linens in the client's room until the implant is removed.  Why D wrong: A dislodged implant must never be touched with bare hands or repositioned — it should be handled only with proper long-handled forceps and per protocol."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"According to the ALARA principle for reducing radiation exposure, which three actions reduce exposure?","options":["Limit time, increase distance, use shielding","Increase time, decrease distance, remove shielding","Document time, store sources, test badges","Wear gloves, gowns, and masks"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: To reduce radiation exposure, the ALARA principle is: Limit Time, Increase Distance, and Use Shielding.  Why B wrong: This is the opposite of protective practice and increases exposure.  Why C/D wrong: These do not match the three ALARA exposure-reduction actions."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"Needle stick injury (NSI) is best described as:","options":["A rare injury affecting only surgeons","A serious and common problem of health-care workers, in which workers come into contact mainly when preparing IV fluid bottles, when handling syringes, and when transferring injection that time should remember some things and should do work nicely to prevent it","A type of medication error","An injury caused only by broken glass"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: NSI is a serious and common problem of health-care workers; workers come into contact mainly when preparing IV fluid bottles, handling syringes, and transferring/administering injections, and should take care to prevent it.  Why A wrong: It affects all health-care workers, not only surgeons, and is common.  Why C wrong: It is a percutaneous sharps injury, not a medication error.  Why D wrong: NSI specifically involves needles/sharps (needle stick), not only broken glass."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"Why should needles or syringes NOT be recapped, bent, or broken after use?","options":["To save plastic","Because of the risk of accidental injury (needle stick) — recapping/bending/breaking increases the risk","Because recapping is illegal","To make disposal faster"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Needles or syringes should not be recapped, bent, or broken because of the risk of accidental injury (needle stick).  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the stated reasons; the reason is preventing accidental sharps injury."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"Which actions are part of nursing care FOLLOWING a needle stick or sharp injury? Select all that apply.","options":["Stop the ongoing procedure immediately","Encourage the wound to bleed gently","Wash the wound using running water with soap","Squeeze the area to force out more blood","Do not scrub or use bleach/chlorine/betadine antiseptics or any antibiotics on the wound"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: Following an NSI: stop the ongoing procedure immediately; encourage the wound to bleed gently; wash the wound using running water with soap; wounds should not be locked by mouth or anything (do not put in mouth); and do not use bleach/chlorine/betadine antiseptics or any antibiotics on the wound.  Why D wrong: Do NOT squeeze the area — squeezing is specifically discouraged in needle-stick first aid."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"After a needle stick injury, how should the wound be dried and covered?","options":["Leave it open and uncovered","Dry the wound and cover it with a waterproof plaster or dressing","Cover with a non-waterproof gauze only","Apply a tourniquet above the wound"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Dry the wound and cover it with a waterproof plaster or dressing.  Why A wrong: Leaving the wound open/uncovered risks contamination.  Why C wrong: A waterproof dressing is specified, not a plain gauze.  Why D wrong: A tourniquet is not indicated for a needle stick injury."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"Which of the following are correct post-needle-stick-injury responsibilities? Select all that apply.","options":["Do not panic","All needle stick injuries should be reported to the infection-control officer","Handle and dispose of sharps safely","Seek urgent medical help","Continue the procedure and report it at the end of the month"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Do not panic; all needle stick injuries should be reported to the infection-control officer; handle and dispose of sharps safely; and seek urgent medical help.  Why E wrong: The ongoing procedure should be stopped immediately and the injury reported promptly to the infection-control officer — not deferred to month-end."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"The source gives a mnemonic to remember needle-stick first aid: \"Bleed it, Wash it, Cover it, Report it.\" Which step corresponds to encouraging the wound to bleed gently?","options":["Cover it","Bleed it","Report it","Wash it"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the \"Bleed it, Wash it, Cover it, Report it\" mnemonic, \"Bleed it\" corresponds to encouraging the wound to bleed gently.  Why A wrong: \"Cover it\" corresponds to covering with a waterproof dressing.  Why C wrong: \"Report it\" corresponds to reporting to the infection-control officer.  Why D wrong: \"Wash it\" corresponds to washing the wound with running water and soap."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"A standard hazard symbol consisting of a yellow trefoil on a magenta/black background warns of which hazard?","options":["Flammable","Radioactive","Explosive","Oxidizing agent"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The trefoil symbol depicted in the hazard symbols panel represents the Radioactive hazard.  Why A wrong: Flammable has a flame symbol.  Why C wrong: Explosive has an exploding-bomb symbol.  Why D wrong: Oxidizing agent has its own distinct symbol (flame over a circle)."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"Which hazard symbols are shown in the radiation/safety hazard panel of the source? Select all that apply.","options":["Radioactive","Biohazard","Flammable","Explosive agent","Oxidizing agent"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The hazard symbols panel depicts Radioactive, Biohazard, General danger, Poison, Electrical hazard, Flammable, Explosive agent, and Oxidizing agent.  Why A/B/C/D/E correct: All five listed symbols (Radioactive, Biohazard, Flammable, Explosive agent, Oxidizing agent) appear among the panel's hazard symbols."},{"section":"Batch 049 · biomedical waste disposal methods…","question":"A symbol showing a skull and crossbones in a hazard panel warns of which hazard?","options":["Poison","Flammable","Radioactive","Oxidizing agent"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The skull-and-crossbones symbol in the hazard panel represents Poison.  Why B wrong: Flammable is depicted by a flame symbol.  Why C wrong: Radioactive is the trefoil symbol.  Why D wrong: Oxidizing agent has its own distinct symbol."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"What is the FIRST priority nursing intervention for a patient who sustains a needle-stick injury?","options":["Apply pressure to the site to expel the blood","Inform the physician immediately","Wash the injury site with copious amount of water","Begin post-exposure prophylaxis treatment"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Per \"Recall the NORCET,\" the first priority nursing intervention in needle-stick injury is to inform the physician immediately.  Why A wrong: Pressing the injury to expel blood is a later step, not the first priority.  Why C wrong: Washing with copious water comes after informing the physician.  Why D wrong: Starting PEP follows assessment and physician notification."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"Which of the following are recommended DO's during a needle-stick injury? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Remove the needle by forceps","Transfer needle using kidney tray","Dispose off the used needle in a sharp box","Use the same needle to draw blood and then discard","Wear protective eyewear during phlebotomy"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: The DO's list includes removing the needle with forceps, transferring it using a kidney tray, disposing the used needle in a sharps box, wearing protective eyewear, and using PPE during phlebotomy.  Why D wrong: Reusing the same needle is unsafe; the don'ts list warns never to reuse or recap needles."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"Which of the following is a DON'T in needle-stick injury prevention?","options":["Dispose off needles in a sharps box","Recap needles","Transfer the needle using a kidney tray","Wear protective eyewear"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The don'ts list explicitly states never recap needles (also never bend, suck the area, or hold with hands).  Why A, C, D wrong: These are all recommended safe (DO) practices."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"According to the average risk of HIV infection after occupational exposure, intact skin exposure carries which level of risk?","options":["1 in 300 (0.3%)","1 in 1000 (0.1%)","9–40%","No risk"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Exposure of intact (unbroken) skin to HIV-infected fluid carries no risk because the skin barrier is not breached.  Why A wrong: 1 in 300 (0.3%) is the risk for a needle-stick injury.  Why B wrong: 1 in 1000 (0.1%) is the risk for exposure of eye, nose, or mouth (mucous membrane).  Why C wrong: 9–40% is the transmission risk for Hepatitis B (HBV), not intact-skin HIV exposure."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"A nurse sustains a needle-stick injury from a confirmed HIV-positive source. What is the approximate risk of HIV transmission from this percutaneous injury?","options":["0.1%","0.3%","9–40%","1.8%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The risk of HIV infection following a needle-stick (percutaneous) exposure is about 1 in 300, i.e., 0.3%.  Why A wrong: 0.1% (1 in 1000) is for mucous-membrane exposure (eye, nose, mouth).  Why C wrong: 9–40% is the Hepatitis B transmission risk.  Why D wrong: 1.8% is the Hepatitis C risk."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"Match the correct transmission risk after needle-stick: Hepatitis C virus (HCV) infection risk is approximately—","options":["0.1%","0.3%","1 to 8% (about 1.8%)","9–40%"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Risk of Hepatitis C (HCV) after a needle stick is 1 to 8% (around 1.8%).  Why A, B wrong: 0.1% and 0.3% relate to HIV mucous-membrane and percutaneous exposures.  Why D wrong: 9–40% is the Hepatitis B (HBV) risk."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"For HIV exposure, the post-exposure prophylaxis (PEP) regimen should be started—","options":["Within 24 hours only","As quickly as possible, preferably within 1 hour, but no more than 1 hour","Anytime within 72 hours with no urgency","Only after seroconversion is confirmed"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: HIV PEP must be started as quickly as possible, preferably within 1 hour, and no later than 1 hour after exposure for best effectiveness.  Why A wrong: 24 hours is the recommended window for Hepatitis B Ig, not HIV PEP urgency.  Why C wrong: PEP loses effectiveness with delay; it is time-critical.  Why D wrong: PEP is started prophylactically before any seroconversion."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"For HIV post-exposure prophylaxis, the recommended course consists of which combination of antiretroviral drugs?","options":["A single antiretroviral drug for 4 weeks","Either two or three antiretroviral drugs for a four-week course on a case-by-case basis","Five antiretroviral drugs for 12 weeks","No drugs, only observation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The PEP regimen for HIV is a four-week course of a combination of either two or three antiretroviral drugs, decided on a case-by-case basis.  Why A, C wrong: These doses/durations do not match the source.  Why D wrong: PEP requires active antiretroviral drug therapy, not observation alone."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"Following a needle stick from a Hepatitis B (HBV) source in an unvaccinated person, what is the recommended post-exposure prophylaxis?","options":["Hep-B Ig alone within 14 hours","Hep-B Ig in combination with vaccine, preferably within 14 hours but no later than 7 days","No prophylaxis needed","A four-week antiretroviral course"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For HBV exposure, Hepatitis B immunoglobulin is given in combination with vaccine (if not previously vaccinated), preferably within 14 hours and no later than 7 days.  Why A wrong: Ig should be combined with vaccine, not given alone.  Why C wrong: HBV requires prophylaxis given its high transmission risk.  Why D wrong: Antiretrovirals are for HIV, not HBV."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"A needle stick occurs from a Hepatitis C (HCV)-positive source. What is the recommended post-exposure prophylaxis?","options":["Combination of three antiretrovirals","Hepatitis C immunoglobulin","There is no recommendation (no specific PEP available)","Hepatitis B vaccine"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists \"No recommendation\" for HCV PEP — there is no established prophylaxis for Hepatitis C.  Why A wrong: Antiretrovirals are used for HIV.  Why B wrong: There is no effective Hepatitis C immunoglobulin recommended.  Why D wrong: Hepatitis B vaccine does not protect against HCV."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"A nurse is mistakenly stuck with a needle from a patient with unknown disease status. As a priority post-exposure prophylaxis measure, which immunization should be considered/checked?","options":["Hepatitis B","Tetanus","HIV vaccine","Hepatitis C vaccine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Per \"Recall the NORCET,\" for an accidental needle stick from an unknown source, tetanus prophylaxis is the priority immunization to be considered.  Why A wrong: Hepatitis B prophylaxis is given per source status, not the universal priority highlighted here.  Why C, D wrong: No effective vaccine exists for HIV or HCV."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"Spill management exists in hospitals because there is always a risk of spill of hazardous substances. Which of the following is/are sources of such spills? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Body fluids","Drugs","Cleaning fluids and other chemicals","Hazardous substances spilled by staff, visitors, and patients","Human error"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists body fluids, drugs, cleaning fluids and other chemicals; hazardous spills by staff/visitors/patients; and spills caused by human error.  All listed options are stated sources of spills in the text."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"How many types of spills are described in the spill management section?","options":["2","3","4","5"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states there are 3 types of spills.  Why A, C, D wrong: These do not match the stated number."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"For decontamination of SMALL spills (less than 10 ml), which strength of sodium hypochlorite solution is used?","options":["1:10 (10%) dilution — 5000 ppm","1:100 dilution — 500 ppm free chlorine","1:1000 dilution — 250 ppm","Undiluted (5% concentration)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Small spills are decontaminated with 1:100 dilution of sodium hypochlorite (500 ppm free chlorine).  Why A wrong: 1:10 (5000 ppm) is for large spills.  Why C wrong: 1:1000 (250 ppm) is used for general cleaning/equipment disinfection, not small spills.  Why D wrong: Undiluted hypochlorite is not used directly for routine spill cleanup."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"For a LARGE spill in a laboratory, the recommended dilution of sodium hypochlorite is—","options":["1:100 (500 ppm)","1:10 (10%) dilution — 5000 ppm free chlorine","1:1000 (250 ppm)","1:50 (1%)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Large spills in a laboratory should be cleared with 1:10 (10%) dilution of sodium hypochlorite (5000 ppm free chlorine) before cleaning, to reduce the risk of infection.  Why A wrong: 1:100 (500 ppm) is for small spills.  Why C wrong: 1:1000 (250 ppm) is for routine cleaning/equipment disinfection.  Why D wrong: 1:50 (1%) is used for linen disinfection, not large spills."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"For LINEN disinfection, sodium hypochlorite is used at which dilution and contact time?","options":["1:100 for 5 minutes","1:10 (10%) for 2 minutes","1:50 (1%) dilution (500 ppm free chlorine) — rinse with water immediately","1:1000 for 30 minutes"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: For linen disinfection, use a 1:50 (1%) dilution (500 ppm free chlorine) for 5 minutes, then rinse with water immediately.  Why A, B, D wrong: These dilution/time combinations do not match the source for linen."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"After absorbing the material of a large spill, the area should be cleaned with which solution?","options":["1:10 (10%) dilution sodium hypochlorite","1:100 sodium hypochlorite solution","Plain soap and water only","5% undiluted bleach"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After liquid absorption, the area should be cleaned with organic matter removal and then a 1:100 sodium hypochlorite solution.  Why A wrong: 1:10 (5000 ppm) is used for the initial clearing of the large spill, not the follow-up clean.  Why B is the follow-up cleaning strength.  Why C, D wrong: These are not the specified follow-up solutions."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"According to \"Recall the NORCET,\" which disinfectant is recommended for handling spills?","options":["10% sodium hypochlorite","1% bleach solution","5% sodium hypochlorite","Plain water"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The recommended disinfectant used for handling spills is 10% sodium hypochlorite.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not the answer keyed in the recall box for spill handling."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"A spill kit should be made available in which location?","options":["Only in the laboratory","In all patient-care areas of the hospital","Only in the operation theatre","Only at the nursing station"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The \"Contains of Spill kit\" note states a spill kit should be made available in all patient-care areas of the hospital.  Why A, C, D wrong: Availability is hospital-wide across patient-care areas, not restricted to one location."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"Which of the following items are contained in a standard spill kit? (Select all that apply.)","options":["PPE (mask, shoe cover, heavy-duty/workman's gloves, plastic apron, goggles, gum boots)","Yellow polythene bag","Absorbable materials (paper/waste cloth/duster)","Sodium hypochlorite solution and a scoop with scraper","Spillage slides"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The kit contains PPE, a yellow polythene bag, absorbable material (paper/waste cloth/duster), sodium hypochlorite solution, a scoop and scraper, and spillage slides.  All listed items appear in the kit contents."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"When cleaning a blood spill, which protective behaviour is recommended if the possibility of splashing exists?","options":["No mask is required","Wear a face mask and gown in addition to gloves","Use bare hands for faster cleaning","Only wear shoe covers"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If the possibility of splashing exists, the worker should wear a face mask and gown, in addition to gloves.  Why A wrong: A mask IS required when splashing is possible.  Why C wrong: Gloves must always be worn; bare hands risk exposure.  Why D wrong: Shoe covers alone are inadequate when splashing is a risk."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"When removing organic material such as a blood spill, how should the area be cleaned?","options":["Spread the material widely with a dry cloth","Cover with absorbable material such as paper or cloth and a 1% sodium hypochlorite solution for 10–15 minutes, then wipe and discard the material in a yellow plastic waste receptacle","Pour undiluted bleach and leave overnight","Wipe immediately with a dry tissue and discard in the general bin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The blood spill should be covered with absorbable material plus 1% sodium hypochlorite for 10–15 minutes, then wiped and discarded in a yellow plastic waste receptacle.  Why A wrong: The aim is to confine, not spread, the material.  Why C wrong: Undiluted bleach left overnight is not the recommended method.  Why D wrong: Contaminated waste goes in yellow biohazard receptacles, not general bins, and requires disinfection contact time."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"If a chlorine solution is not prepared fresh daily, how long can it be stored at room temperature in a closed, opaque bottle?","options":["24 hours","Up to 30 days","7 days","6 months"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: \"Should Remember\" states that if chlorine solution is not prepared fresh daily, it can be stored at room temperature for up to 30 days in a cool, dark, closed (opaque) bottle, with the active chlorine concentration decreasing by roughly 50% over that period.  Why A, C, D wrong: These storage durations do not match the source."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"What is the recommended minimum contact (\"time-out\") for a chlorine solution to be effective?","options":["30 seconds","1.2 minutes (about 1 minute)","10 minutes","1 hour"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A minimum bed (contact) period of 1.2 minutes (≈1 minute) is recommended for the spacing between two beds in isolation/disinfection, reflecting the required contact time-out for chlorine to reduce cross-transmission of infection.  Why A, C, D wrong: These contact times do not match the stated minimum."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"Occupational exposure is best defined as—","options":["Any contact with a patient","Exposure to potential blood-borne infections (HIV, HBV, HCV) that occurs during performance of job duties","Exposure occurring only outside the workplace","Exposure that never carries any infection risk"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Occupational exposure refers to exposure to potential blood-borne infections (HIV, HBV, and HCV) that occurs during the performance of job duties.  Why A wrong: Mere contact without exposure risk is not the definition.  Why C wrong: Occupational exposure is work-related, occurring during job duties.  Why D wrong: It specifically involves a risk of blood-borne infection."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"According to the categories of HIV exposure, a superficial wound (erosion of the epidermis) with a clean or low-calibre needle is classified as which category?","options":["Mild exposure","Moderate exposure","Severe exposure","No exposure"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Mild exposure involves mucous membrane/intact skin with small volumes — e.g., a superficial wound (erosion of the epidermis) with a clean or low-calibre needle, or contact with mucous membranes/subcutaneous injections following small-bore needles.  Why B wrong: Moderate exposure involves larger volumes, e.g., mucous membrane/intact skin with large volumes or percutaneous superficial exposure with a solid needle.  Why C wrong: Severe exposure involves a large volume with percutaneous deep injury.  Why D wrong: This still constitutes an exposure category (mild)."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"An accident involving a high-calibre needle (>18 G) with a deep wound, visible/heavy bleeding, or blood injected intravenously/intra-arterially is classified as which exposure category?","options":["Mild exposure","Moderate exposure","Severe exposure","Negligible exposure"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Severe exposure is mucous membrane/intact skin with large volumes plus percutaneous deep injury — e.g., an accident with a high-calibre needle (>18 G), a deep wound with heavy/visible bleeding, or blood injected intravenously/intra-arterially.  Why A wrong: Mild exposure involves small volumes and superficial wounds.  Why B wrong: Moderate exposure involves larger volumes but not the deep/high-calibre criteria.  Why D wrong: This is the highest, not negligible, exposure level."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"For decontamination of equipment such as bedpans and bowls (general cleaning/equipment disinfection), which sodium hypochlorite dilution and chlorine concentration is recommended?","options":["1:10 (10%) — 50,000 ppm","1:100 (1%) — 5000 ppm","1:1000 — 250 ppm","Undiluted 5%"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The dilution chart lists 1:1000 dilution giving 250 ppm chlorine, recommended for general cleaning and equipment disinfection.  Why A wrong: Minimum 5% concentration undiluted gives 50,000 ppm — not the dilution used for routine equipment cleaning.  Why B wrong: 1:100 (1%) gives 5000 ppm, used for disinfection of blood/body fluid spills.  Why D wrong: Undiluted hypochlorite is far too concentrated for routine equipment disinfection."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"Per the dilution chart, a 1:100 (1%) dilution of sodium hypochlorite (5000 ppm chlorine) is recommended for which use?","options":["Cleaning equipment disinfection","Disinfection of large blood/body fluid spills (wiping metallic surfaces on a regular basis, or wiping after a small blood spill)","Drinking water purification","Skin antisepsis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 1:100 (1%, 5000 ppm) is recommended for disinfection of large blood/body fluid spills and wiping metallic surfaces on a regular basis or after a small blood spill.  Why A wrong: 1:1000 (250 ppm) is used for general cleaning/equipment disinfection.  Why C wrong: Sodium hypochlorite at this strength is not for drinking-water treatment.  Why D wrong: This concentration is for surface/spill disinfection, not skin antisepsis."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"Which of the following body fluids is/are considered \"AT RISK\" for blood-borne pathogens and require universal precautions? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Blood","Semen","Vaginal secretions","CSF (cerebrospinal fluid)","Synovial, pleural, peritoneal, and pericardial fluid"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The \"at risk\" body fluids requiring universal precautions are blood, semen, vaginal secretions, CSF, synovial/pleural/peritoneal/pericardial fluid, and other body fluids contaminated with visible blood.  All listed options are part of the \"at risk\" group."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"Which body fluids are considered \"NOT AT RISK\" and do not require universal precautions unless they contain visible blood? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Tears","Sweat","Urine and faeces","Saliva and nasal secretions","Sputum"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Tears, sweat, urine, faeces, saliva, nasal secretions, and sputum are \"not at risk\" — universal precautions are not required unless these secretions contain visible blood.  All listed options belong to the \"not at risk\" group."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"Per the AIIMS protocol definitions, \"exposure\" putting a health worker at risk of blood-borne infection includes which of the following? (Select all that apply.)","options":["A percutaneous injury (e.g., needle-stick or cut with a sharp instrument)","Contact with the mucous membranes of the eye or mouth","Contact with non-intact skin that is chapped, abraded, or afflicted with dermatitis","Contact with intact skin when the duration of contact is prolonged, or several minutes or more, with blood or other potentially infectious body fluids","A brief touch of intact, healthy skin"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The defined exposures are percutaneous injury, contact with mucous membranes of eye/mouth, contact with non-intact (chapped/abraded/dermatitis) skin, and prolonged (several minutes or more) contact of intact skin with blood/infectious fluids.  Why E wrong: A brief touch of intact, healthy skin is not defined as an at-risk exposure (intact skin is a barrier; only prolonged contact counts)."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"As an IMMEDIATE first-aid measure after a needle-stick/sharp injury where the skin is broken, what should be done?","options":["Squeeze and prick the finger to expel blood","Immediately wash the wound and surrounding skin with water and soap, and rinse — do NOT scrub","Apply chlorine, alcohol, or betadine immediately","Cover the wound with a dry dressing without washing"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For a needle-stick/sharp injury with broken skin, immediately wash the wound and surrounding skin with water and soap and rinse; do not scrub.  Why A wrong: The text says do not prick or squeeze the finger.  Why C wrong: Do not use antiseptics such as bleach, chlorine, alcohol, betadine, or iodine on the wound.  Why D wrong: Washing must be done first; the wound is not simply covered without cleaning."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"After a splash of blood enters the EYE, what is the correct immediate management?","options":["Rub the eye vigorously to remove the contaminant","Irrigate the eye copiously with water or normal saline; if wearing contact lenses, leave them in place while irrigating, then remove and clean","Apply alcohol drops directly to the eye","Do nothing until a physician arrives"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For the eye, irrigate immediately with water or normal saline; sit in a chair, tilt the head back, and have a colleague gently irrigate. If wearing contact lenses, leave them in place while irrigating (they form a barrier), then remove and clean them normally; do not use soap or disinfectant on the eye.  Why A wrong: Rubbing the eye can cause injury and spread the contaminant.  Why C wrong: Alcohol/disinfectant must not be put in the eye.  Why D wrong: Immediate irrigation is required, not delay."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"If blood or body fluid splashes into the MOUTH, the correct immediate action is to—","options":["Swallow it quickly","Spit it out immediately, then rinse the mouth thoroughly several times with water or saline; do not use soap or disinfectant in the mouth","Gargle with undiluted bleach","Brush the teeth with antiseptic immediately"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For the mouth, spit it out immediately and rinse the mouth thoroughly several times with water or saline; do not use soap or disinfectant in the mouth.  Why A wrong: Swallowing increases exposure risk.  Why C wrong: Undiluted bleach must never be used in the mouth.  Why D wrong: Disinfectant/soap is not used in the mouth per the protocol."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"Regarding \"Don'ts\" in needle-stick/exposure first aid, which actions should be AVOIDED? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Do not panic","Do not put pricked finger in mouth","Do not squeeze wound to bleed it","Do not use bleach, chlorine, alcohol, betadine, iodine, or any antiseptic or detergent on the wound","Wash the wound with soap and water"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The \"Don'ts\" list says do not panic, do not put the pricked finger in the mouth, do not squeeze the wound to bleed it, and do not use bleach/chlorine/alcohol/betadine/iodine or any antiseptic/detergent.  Why E wrong: Washing the wound with soap and water IS a recommended DO, not a don't."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"In Step II (prompt reporting) of PEP management, a needle-stick/sharp injury should be reported immediately to the Medical Officer and an entry made where?","options":["The patient's discharge summary","The Needle-Stick Injury Register in the department","The pharmacy stock register","The visitor logbook"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: All needle-stick/sharp injuries should be reported immediately (supervisor and Medical Officer) and an entry made in the Needle-Stick Injury Register in the department.  Why A, C, D wrong: These registers/records are not where exposures are logged."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"In Step III (post-exposure treatment), more PEP should begin as soon as possible. Studies report that PEP started within how many hours of exposure is most effective?","options":["Within 2 hours","Within 2 hours for HIV and within 24 hours for HBV","Within 72 hours regardless of pathogen","Within 7 days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: PEP should begin as soon as possible — most effectively within 2 hours for HIV and within 24 hours for HBV.  Why A wrong: It omits the HBV 24-hour window noted in the source.  Why C wrong: 72 hours (3 days) is the absolute upper limit after which PEP may be less effective, not the most-effective window.  Why D wrong: 7 days is the upper window for Hepatitis B Ig combination, not PEP effectiveness."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"Beyond what time is PEP NOT recommended after exposure because it may be less effective?","options":["After 24 hours","After 48 hours","After 72 hours (3 days)","After 7 days"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: PEP is not recommended after 72 hours (3 days), as treatment taken beyond this may be less effective.  Why A, B wrong: PEP is still recommended (though less effective) within 72 hours; it is not cut off at 24 or 48 hours.  Why D wrong: 7 days exceeds the 72-hour PEP window."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"PEP is NOT needed for which type(s) of exposure? (Select all that apply.)","options":["HIV sero-conversion rate of 0.3% after a needle stick (percutaneous exposure) on average","A baseline rapid HIV test of the exposed and source person that must be done for PEP","When informed consent should be obtained before testing the source for HIV","Exposure to a clearly negative (non-infected) source"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists, under \"PEP is not needed for all types of exposure,\" the average HIV sero-conversion rate of 0.3% after percutaneous exposure, the requirement of a baseline rapid HIV test of exposed and source persons (done for PEP decisions), informed consent before testing the source, and the principle that exposure to a clearly negative source does not need PEP. The decision to start PEP depends on the degree of exposure to HIV and the HIV status of the source.  All listed statements are part of the source's PEP-not-needed considerations."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"After exposure, when should the designated/treating doctor ensure that the first PEP dose is administered?","options":["Within 72 hours","Within 7 days","Within 2 weeks","Within 1 month"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The \"First PEP dose within 72 hours\" note states a designated person/treating doctor must ensure HIV and HBV transmission prophylaxis is administered following exposure (AEB) within 72 hours.  Why B, C, D wrong: These exceed the 72-hour critical window."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"During PEP counselling (Step IV), exposed persons (clients) should receive appropriate information about which of the following? (Select all that apply.)","options":["What PEP is and about the risk and benefits of PEP","Being provided written informed consent for taking PEP","That they should be clear that PEP is not mandatory","That they must never report the exposure to anyone"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Step IV counselling requires giving appropriate information about what PEP is, its risks and benefits, providing written informed consent for taking PEP, and being clear that PEP is not mandatory.  Why D wrong: This contradicts the protocol — prompt reporting (Step II) is required, not concealment."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"Step V (psychological support) of PEP management addresses which patient need?","options":["Many people feel anxious after exposure and should be informed about the risks and reassured that they can be taken; this helps relieve part of the anxiety","Immediate surgical intervention","Discharge from the hospital","Discontinuation of all medication"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Step V states many people feel anxious after exposure and should be informed about the risks (and the measures that can be taken), which helps relieve part of the anxiety — psychological support.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not part of the psychological-support step."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"Documentation of exposure (Step VI) is essential. For mental-health and side-effect monitoring, special leave from work should be considered for what initial duration?","options":["1 day","A period of 2 weeks","1 month","6 months"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Step VI notes that special leave from work should be considered initially for a period of 2 weeks to allow for assessment of the exposed person's mental state and side effects, and to extend it based on side effects and requirements.  Why A, C, D wrong: These durations do not match the stated initial 2-week consideration."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"During clinical follow-up of an exposed person on PEP, the appearance of which signs within 50%–70% of individuals (3 to 6 weeks after exposure) indicates a possible HIV primary (acute) infection?","options":["Acute fever, generalized lymphadenopathy, cutaneous eruption, pharyngitis, and non-specific flu symptoms with ulcers of the mouth or genital area","Painless slow-healing fracture","Sudden weight gain only","Improved energy and appetite"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Clinical follow-up notes that an acute HIV (primary) infection is suspected in 50%–70% of individuals 3 to 6 weeks after exposure, with acute fever, generalized lymphadenopathy, cutaneous eruption, pharyngitis, non-specific flu symptoms, and ulcers of the mouth/genital area.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not the signs of acute HIV seroconversion described."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"A person on PEP is advised which of the following measures to prevent secondary transmission? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Use precautions during sexual intercourse (e.g., condom use)","Avoid blood, tissue, and organ donation","Breastfeeding and pregnancy should be avoided / unprotected sexual relations or pregnancy avoided","Especially during the first 6–12 weeks following transmission","Donate blood freely during the follow-up period"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source advises precautions during sexual intercourse, avoiding blood/tissue/organ donation, avoiding breastfeeding and unprotected sexual relations or pregnancy, especially during the first 6–12 weeks following transmission, to prevent secondary transmission.  Why E wrong: This directly contradicts the instruction to avoid blood donation during follow-up."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"Laboratory follow-up of an exposed person should include which of the following? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Exposed persons should have post-PEP HIV tests","An HIV test at 3 months and again at 6 months is recommended","If the test at 6 months is negative, no further testing is recommended","Always seek expert advice in case of delay in reporting exposure (>72 hours), unknown source, known or suspected pregnancy, breastfeeding mothers, source patient on ART, or major toxicity of PEP regimen","No follow-up HIV testing is ever required"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Laboratory follow-up includes post-PEP HIV tests, HIV testing at 3 months and again at 6 months, no further testing if the 6-month test is negative, and seeking expert advice for special situations (delay in reporting >72 hrs, unknown source, known/suspected pregnancy, breastfeeding mothers, source on ART, major toxicity of PEP regimen).  Why E wrong: Follow-up HIV testing IS required at 3 and 6 months."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"For an unvaccinated person exposed to Hepatitis B virus, what is the recommended immunization approach per the laboratory/PEP follow-up note?","options":["The exposed person should be vaccinated against hepatitis B virus as per recommendation","No vaccination is ever needed","Only antiretroviral therapy is given","Hepatitis C vaccine is given"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The IMPORTANT note states that health-care personnel should be vaccinated against the hepatitis B virus per recommendation, since the antibody titre should be monitored at different intervals.  Why B wrong: Vaccination is recommended for unvaccinated exposed personnel.  Why C wrong: ART/antiretrovirals address HIV, not HBV prophylaxis here.  Why D wrong: There is no effective Hepatitis C vaccine (and prophylaxis is not recommended for HCV)."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"Define a restraint as used in clinical settings.","options":["A medication that sedates a confused patient","A device or method that restricts or limits the physical activity of a client, or part of the body, or restricts normal access to one's own body, to mobilise or restrain a client or extremity","A type of bedsheet only","A surgical procedure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Restraints are safety devices or protective devices used to limit the physical activity of a client or a part of the body / restrict normal access to one's body or an extremity.  Why A wrong: That describes chemical sedation in the abstract, not the definition of restraint as a class of devices/methods (chemical restraint is a sub-type).  Why C, D wrong: A restraint is neither a plain bedsheet nor a surgical procedure."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"A \"seclusion\" type of restraint is defined as—","options":["Tying the wrists with a sheet","Keeping a client away from others or locking the patient in a dark room","Sedating the patient with medication","Raising all four side rails"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Seclusion means keeping a client away from others or locking the patient in a dark room.  Why A, D wrong: These are physical restraint techniques, not seclusion.  Why C wrong: That is chemical restraint, not seclusion."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"Restraints used physically to confine the patient to limit activities are termed—","options":["Chemical restraint","Seclusion","Physical restraint","Behavioural restraint"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: A restraint means physically tying the patient to limit the activities — physical restraint restricts physical movement of a client by the application of a device such as a wrist strap/limb holder.  Why A wrong: Chemical restraint restricts behaviour by medications.  Why B wrong: Seclusion isolates the client, it does not physically tie them.  Why D wrong: \"Behavioural restraint\" is not the term defined in the source for physical confinement."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"A restraint that uses medications to restrict a specific type of behaviour is classified as—","options":["Physical restraint","Chemical restraint","Mechanical restraint","Environmental restraint"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Chemical restraint restricts a specific type of behaviour by medications.  Why A wrong: Physical restraint uses devices to limit movement.  Why C, D wrong: These categories are not the two types named (physical and chemical)."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"What is the cardinal rule about when restraints should be used?","options":["Restraints are the first option for any agitated patient","Restraints should always be the first choice","Restraints are used only after alternative measures for safety have failed; they should always be the LAST option, never first","Restraints can be applied without any order at the nurse's discretion permanently"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Restraints are used only after alternative measures (safety devices) are tried/failed; alternative measures for safety should always be used first, and the restraint is the last option.  Why A, B wrong: Restraints must never be the first option.  Why D wrong: A doctor's order is required; unnecessary restraint is false imprisonment."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"Applying unnecessary restraint without proper justification or order is legally considered—","options":["Standard nursing care","False imprisonment","A minor documentation error","An acceptable safety measure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states that unnecessary restraint is false imprisonment.  Why A, C, D wrong: Unjustified restraint is a serious legal violation, not routine care, a minor error, or acceptable."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"A doctor's order for restraint is valid for how long, and what is required for continued use?","options":["Valid indefinitely; no re-order needed","Valid only once at a time; for repeated need to restrain, a fresh/again doctor's order should be obtained (PRN orders are not allowed)","Valid for one week, then PRN","Valid for the entire hospital stay"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A doctor's order for restraints is valid only once a time; if again the need to restrain arises, again a doctor's order should be taken, and restraints are NOT to be ordered PRN.  Why A wrong: It is not valid indefinitely.  Why C wrong: PRN ordering is explicitly disallowed.  Why D wrong: It is not valid for the whole stay; each instance requires a fresh order."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"The components of a doctor's order for restraint should include which of the following? (Select all that apply.)","options":["The type of restraints","The specific client behaviour for which restraints are to be used","Identification of how long restraints used and the reason for safety devices","The method of use for safety devices and date & time of application of restraints","The patient's favourite meal"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The components are: type of restraints, the specific client behaviour for which restraints are to be used, how long restraints are used, reason for safety devices, method of use, and date & time of application.  Why E wrong: A patient's meal preference is not a component of a restraint order."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"The reason for applying restraints should be explained to whom, and informed consent obtained from whom?","options":["Only the hospital administrator","The client or relatives","The pharmacy","No one"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The reason for restraints should be explained to the client or relatives, and informed consent should be taken prior to restraining (either by the patient or his/her relative).  Why A, C wrong: Administrators and pharmacy are not the consent-givers.  Why D wrong: Consent is required; it cannot be omitted."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"When restraints must be secured to the bed (frame or chair), they should be secured to—","options":["The movable side rails","The non-movable part of the bed frame or chair, not the side rails, so they may rise to accidentally fall down and may client also fall down on the floor","The IV pole","The overbed table"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Restraints should be tied or secured with the bed frame or chair, NOT the side rails, because side rails may rise and cause the client to fall down.  Why A wrong: Side rails are explicitly NOT to be used, as they move and risk falls.  Why C, D wrong: IV poles and overbed tables are unstable, movable, and unsafe anchor points."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"How often should a restrained client's skin integrity and neurovascular status be assessed?","options":["Every 8 hours","Every 4 hours","Every 30 minutes","Once per shift"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Assess skin integrity and neurovascular status every 30 minutes.  Why A, B, D wrong: These intervals are too long for a restrained client at risk of skin and circulatory compromise."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"How frequently should the safety device (restraint) be removed to permit muscle exercise and promote circulation?","options":["Every 8 hours for 5 minutes","Every 2 hours, removing the safety devices for at least 10 minutes","Once a day for 1 hour","Only on discharge"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The restraint should be removed every 2 hours to permit muscle exercise and promote circulation.  Why A, C, D wrong: These intervals do not match the every-2-hour requirement and would risk circulatory and musculoskeletal complications."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"Body mechanics is best described as—","options":["A method of charting vital signs","The efficient, coordinated, and safe use of the body to move objects and carry out the Activities of Daily Living (ADLs)","A surgical positioning technique only","A type of medication administration"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Body mechanics is the term used to describe the efficient, coordinated, and safe use of the body to move objects and carry out the Activities of Daily Living (ADLs).  Why A, C, D wrong: These do not capture the definition of body mechanics."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"When moving and turning a client (from bed to wheelchair, bed to stretcher, or changing position), how many basic principles of body mechanics should be followed?","options":["All principles can be followed for all techniques (the same principles apply across techniques)","Only one principle applies to all moves","No principles are needed for short moves","Principles apply only to lifting heavy equipment"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states that when moving, turning, or repositioning a client, many of these basic principles of body mechanics can be followed across all the techniques.  Why B, C, D wrong: The principles broadly apply to all moving/turning/repositioning, not just one move, heavy equipment, or \"no principles.\""},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"What are the purposes of using correct body mechanics? (Select all that apply.)","options":["To avoid excessive fatigue","To maintain good posture","To reduce the energy required and avoid injury to the patient","To avoid injury to muscles and skeletal injuries","To assist in transporting equipment for hospital staff"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The purposes listed are to avoid excessive fatigue, maintain good posture, reduce the energy required, avoid injury to the patient, avoid injury to muscles/skeletal injuries, and assist in transporting equipment for staff.  All listed options are stated purposes of body mechanics."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"Which statement about the centre of gravity and stability is correct in body mechanics?","options":["The narrower the base of support, the greater the stability","The wider the base of support, the greater the stability; a lower centre of gravity maintained as long as the line of gravity passes through its base of support increases stability","A higher centre of gravity always increases stability","The base of support has no effect on stability"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The wider the base of support, the greater the stability; a lower centre of gravity (kept as long as the line of gravity passes through the base of support) increases stability.  Why A wrong: A wider, not narrower, base increases stability.  Why C wrong: A lower (not higher) centre of gravity increases stability.  Why D wrong: Base of support directly affects stability."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"According to the principles of body mechanics, facing the direction of movement during a transfer—","options":["Increases abnormal twisting of the spine","Prevents abnormal twisting of the spine","Has no effect on the spine","Should always be avoided"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Facing the direction of movement prevents abnormal twisting of the spine.  Why A wrong: It prevents, not increases, twisting.  Why C wrong: It does affect the spine — it protects it.  Why D wrong: Facing the direction of movement is recommended, not avoided."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"Why is dividing balanced activity between arms and legs recommended in body mechanics?","options":["It reduces the risk of back injury","It increases the lifting weight on the spine","It makes movements jerky","It eliminates the need for a wide base of support"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Dividing balanced activity between arms and legs reduces the risk of back injury.  Why B wrong: It reduces, not increases, spinal load.  Why C wrong: Movements should be smooth and coordinated rather than jerky.  Why D wrong: A wide base of support is still recommended."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"Which of the following are correct principles of body mechanics? (Select all that apply.)","options":["It is easier to pull, push, or roll an object than it is to lift it","Movements should be smooth and coordinated rather than jerky","Friction is reduced when the object to be moved and the surface on which it is moved are less; less force is required to move it","Use the arm and leg muscles as much as possible while keeping the back muscles as little as possible","Keep the work as close as possible to the body so it puts less of a strain on the back, legs, and arms"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: All listed statements appear among the principles of body mechanics: pulling/pushing/rolling is easier than lifting; movements should be smooth and coordinated; reducing friction lowers required force; use arm and leg muscles (sparing the back); and keep the work close to the body to reduce strain.  All options are valid principles from the source."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"When working at a comfortable height, what does it avoid?","options":["Excessive bending at the waist","The need for any base of support","Use of arm muscles","Keeping work close to the body"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Working at a comfortable height avoids excessive bending at the waist; keeping the body in good physical condition also reduces the chance of injury.  Why B, C wrong: A base of support and arm muscles are still used appropriately.  Why D wrong: Keeping work close to the body remains recommended."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"Per the \"Should Remember\" box, while lifting, moving, or turning the client, where should the bed/work be kept?","options":["As low as possible to avoid strain","At gravity & support level — kept at the level where the centre of gravity and the knees are bent for support","Above the head of the worker","At the foot of the bed only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: \"Should Remember\" advises that while lifting, moving, or turning the client, the bed should not bend (the worker should not bend the back); instead keep the work at the gravity and support level, bending the knees for support (Right answer per NORCET recall: gravity & support).  Why A wrong: The bed should not simply be kept as low as possible; the emphasis is on using the centre of gravity and knee support rather than bending the back.  Why C, D wrong: Working above the head or only at the foot of the bed is incorrect and unsafe."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"Which measures form part of health education for the prevention of falls? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Assess the client's risk for falling and place a suspect client always near the nurse's station","Frequently assess the client and orient the client to physical surroundings","Help the client when getting up and teach about use of the call bell","Use safety devices and keep the bed in the low position with side rails adjusted to a safe position","Lock all beds, wheelchairs, and stretchers, and keep the client's personal items within reach"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Fall-prevention education includes assessing fall risk and placing the suspect client near the nurses' station, frequent assessment, orienting the client to surroundings, helping the client when getting up, teaching call-bell use, using safety devices, keeping the bed low with side rails in a safe position, locking beds/wheelchairs/stretchers, and keeping personal items within reach.  All listed options are stated fall-prevention measures."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"To eliminate clutter and obstacles in fall prevention, the nurse should—","options":["Leave equipment spread across the floor","Eliminate clutter and obstacles from the client's room/area and provide adequate lighting, reducing bathroom hazards","Remove the call bell to reduce noise","Keep the bed in the high position at all times"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Fall-prevention education includes eliminating clutter and obstacles, providing adequate lighting, and reducing bathroom hazards.  Why A wrong: Clutter must be removed, not left in place.  Why C wrong: The call bell should be available, not removed.  Why D wrong: The bed should be kept low, not high."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"Which actions help keep the centre of gravity in midline for safe ambulation/body mechanics? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Place the belt on the client prior to ambulation","Encircle the client's waist with the belt","Hold onto the side of the back of the belt so that the client does not lean to one side","Pull the client forward by the wrists only"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: To keep the centre of gravity in midline, place the belt on the client before ambulation, encircle the client's waist with the belt, and hold onto the side/back of the belt so the client does not lean to one side.  Why D wrong: Pulling a client by the wrists is not the described safe technique; a gait/transfer belt around the waist is used."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"Which steps prevent injury to health workers when moving/lifting clients? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Keep the weight to be lifted as close to the body as possible","Bend at the knees, do not bend at the waist","Tighten the abdominal muscles and pull the body in","Maintain the trunk erect and the knees bent so that multiple muscle groups work together in a coordinated manner","Lift with the back muscles while keeping the knees straight"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Steps to prevent injury to health workers include keeping the weight close to the body, bending at the knees (not the waist), tightening the abdominal muscles and pulling the body in, and maintaining the trunk erect with knees bent so multiple muscle groups work together.  Why E wrong: Lifting with the back and straight knees is unsafe and contradicts bending at the knees."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"Per the body-mechanics notes, why should the trunk be kept erect while lifting?","options":["To rest between periods of work and promote work endurance, maintaining proper body alignment so muscles work in a coordinated manner","To increase strain on the lumbar spine","Because erect posture has no effect on lifting","To allow lifting heavier loads with the back alone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Rest between periods of work promotes work endurance, and maintaining proper body alignment (trunk erect) allows muscle groups to work together in a coordinated manner, reducing injury.  Why B wrong: Proper alignment reduces, not increases, lumbar strain.  Why C wrong: Erect posture is protective and does affect safe lifting.  Why D wrong: Lifting with the back alone is unsafe; leg and abdominal muscles share the load."},{"section":"Batch 050 · needle-stick injury do's/don'ts…","question":"When maintaining an appropriate centre of gravity while moving in a coordinated way (e.g., turning), the worker should—","options":["Keep the centre of gravity low, twist, and bend at the knees and at the same time","Maintain a wide base of support by placing one foot slightly ahead of the other, flexing the knees, and turning with the feet (rather than twisting the spine)","Keep both feet together and twist the spine sharply","Lock the knees and rotate at the waist"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: To maintain a wide base of support, place one foot slightly ahead of the other, flex the knees, and turn with the feet — avoiding spinal twisting — keeping the body lined up close to the body and using leg strain.  Why A wrong: Twisting is exactly what should be avoided.  Why C wrong: Feet together with a sharp spinal twist is unsafe and narrows the base of support.  Why D wrong: Locking the knees and rotating at the waist risks back injury."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"Ongoing physical activity that raises the breathing rate and heart rate, increasing the amount of oxygen the body takes in, is called:","options":["Anaerobic exercise","Aerobic exercise","Isometric exercise","Isokinetic exercise"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Aerobic exercise is ongoing physical activity that raises breathing and heart rate and increases the oxygen the body takes in (e.g., swimming laps, running, cycling).  Why A wrong: Anaerobic exercise consists of intense activities lasting only seconds to a few minutes at maximum effort.  Why C wrong: Isometric exercise involves muscles contracting with very little body movement.  Why D wrong: Isokinetic exercise is performed with machines at a constant rate."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"Which examples best illustrate aerobic exercise?","options":["Jumping and sprinting","Heavy weight lifting","Swimming laps, running, and cycling","Walking up and down stairs"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists swimming laps, running, and cycling as examples of aerobic exercise.  Why A wrong: Jumping and sprinting are listed as anaerobic exercise examples.  Why B wrong: Heavy weight lifting is given as an anaerobic example.  Why D wrong: Walking up/down stairs is an example of isotonic exercise."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"Intense physical activities that last only a few seconds to a few minutes and are performed at maximum effort describe which type of exercise?","options":["Aerobic","Anaerobic","Isotonic","Isokinetic"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Anaerobic exercise is defined as intense activity lasting a few seconds to a few minutes at maximum effort (e.g., jumping, sprinting, heavy weight lifting).  Why A wrong: Aerobic is ongoing, sustained activity raising heart/breathing rate.  Why C wrong: Isotonic involves contracting/relaxing muscles through full range of motion.  Why D wrong: Isokinetic uses machines at a constant rate."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"During isometric exercise, the muscles:","options":["Contract with very little body movement","Move through the full range of joint motion","Lengthen rapidly under load","Contract only at a constant machine-set rate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Isometric exercise is when muscles contract but very little body movement occurs.  Why B wrong: That describes isotonic exercise.  Why C wrong: Source does not describe rapid lengthening; isometric has minimal movement.  Why D wrong: Constant machine-set rate describes isokinetic exercise."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"Walking up and down stairs is given as an example of which exercise type, defined as contracting and relaxing muscles through the full range of joint motion?","options":["Isometric","Isokinetic","Isotonic","Anaerobic"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Isotonic exercise involves contracting and relaxing muscles through the full range of their joints' motion; the example given is walking up and down stairs.  Why A wrong: Isometric involves little body movement.  Why B wrong: Isokinetic uses machines at a constant rate.  Why D wrong: Anaerobic refers to brief maximum-effort activity."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"Isokinetic exercise is best described as exercise:","options":["Performed with machines that ensure muscles contract at a constant rate (e.g., a stationary bike)","Lasting only a few seconds at maximum effort","With no resistance at any point","That always raises heart rate above aerobic levels"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Isokinetic exercise is performed with machines that ensure muscles contract at a constant rate; example given is riding a stationary bike.  Why B wrong: That describes anaerobic exercise.  Why C wrong: The source does not define it as no resistance; it specifies a constant rate via machines.  Why D wrong: Heart-rate elevation defines aerobic exercise, not isokinetic."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"When using proper body mechanics to lift a load, the nurse should:","options":["Keep knees straight and bend at the waist","Bend the knees and keep the back straight","Twist the trunk while lifting","Hold the load away from the body"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The body mechanics figures direct the worker to bend at the knees (\"keep your knees bent,\" \"bend at the knees\") and keep the back straight when lifting.  Why A wrong: Bending at the waist with straight knees strains the back.  Why C wrong: Twisting the trunk should be avoided in proper body mechanics.  Why D wrong: Loads should be held close to the body, not away from it."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"\"Asepsis\" is best defined as:","options":["The killing of all microbial life including spores","A condition in which pathogens are absent or controlled","The transmission of infection within a hospital","An agent that prohibits microbial growth"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Asepsis is defined as a condition in which pathogens are absent or controlled; it is also referred to as sterile/aseptic technique.  Why A wrong: That describes sterilization.  Why C wrong: That is nosocomial/hospital-acquired infection.  Why D wrong: That is an antiseptic."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"What is the purpose of aseptic technique?","options":["To completely destroy all bacteria and spores","To reduce the number of microorganisms and prevent their spread","To diagnose hospital-acquired infection","To inhibit bacterial growth on the skin only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The purpose of aseptic technique is to reduce the number of microorganisms and prevent their spread.  Why A wrong: Total destruction including spores is sterilization, not the general aim of aseptic technique.  Why C wrong: Diagnosis is unrelated to the stated purpose.  Why D wrong: Skin-only inhibition describes an antiseptic action, not the overall purpose."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"The three levels of aseptic control, in order, are:","options":["Antisepsis, disinfection, sterilization","Sterilization, disinfection, antisepsis","Disinfection, sterilization, antisepsis","Antisepsis, sterilization, disinfection"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists three levels of aseptic control: 1. Antisepsis or sanitization (used on living tissues), 2. Disinfection (used on objects e.g., furniture, bed and equipment), 3. Sterilization (used to treat under pressure gas, chemicals and radiation on objects).  Why B wrong: Order is reversed.  Why C wrong: Antisepsis is the first level, not the last.  Why D wrong: Disinfection is the second level, not sterilization."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"Antisepsis or sanitization is used on:","options":["Furniture, beds, and equipment","Living tissues such as skin","Surgical instruments only","The hospital air supply"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Antisepsis/sanitization is used on living tissues, e.g., skin.  Why A wrong: That describes disinfection (objects, furniture, equipment).  Why C wrong: Instruments are sterilized.  Why D wrong: Air is not the target of antisepsis as defined."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"Disinfection is mainly used on:","options":["Living human skin","Objects such as furniture, beds, and equipment","The bloodstream","Open surgical wounds"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Disinfection is used mainly on objects, e.g., furniture, beds, and equipment.  Why A wrong: Living skin is treated by antisepsis.  Why C wrong: The bloodstream is not disinfected.  Why D wrong: Wounds are managed by aseptic/sterile technique, not disinfection of objects."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"Sterilization, as a level of aseptic control, uses which methods to treat objects?","options":["Soap and water only","Pressure gas, chemicals, and radiation","Hand rubbing alone","Cold air only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Sterilization uses pressure gas, chemicals, and radiation to treat objects.  Why A wrong: Soap and water alone is cleaning, not sterilization.  Why C wrong: Hand rubbing is hand hygiene, not object sterilization.  Why D wrong: Cold air is not a sterilization method described."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"Asepsis is usually classified into which two categories?","options":["Clean and dirty asepsis","Medical asepsis and surgical asepsis","Active and passive asepsis","Primary and secondary asepsis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Asepsis is classified into two categories: medical asepsis and surgical asepsis.  Why A wrong: Clean/dirty is not the source classification.  Why C wrong: Active/passive is not used.  Why D wrong: Primary/secondary is not used."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"Medical asepsis refers to:","options":["Protection against infection by destroying microorganisms after a procedure","Removal or destruction of infected material","The killing of all microbes including spores","Use of sterile equipment and supplies only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Medical asepsis is defined as the removal or destruction of infected material.  Why A wrong: Protection against infection before, during, and after a procedure is surgical asepsis.  Why C wrong: Killing all microbes including spores is sterilization.  Why D wrong: Sterile equipment use is a feature of surgical asepsis."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"Surgical asepsis is best defined as:","options":["Removal of infected material from surfaces","Protection against infection before, during, and after a procedure using sterile technique","Routine hand washing only","Cleaning of objects with a damp cloth"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Surgical asepsis is protection against infection before, during, and after a procedure using sterile technique.  Why A wrong: That is medical asepsis.  Why C wrong: Hand washing alone is a medical-asepsis practice.  Why D wrong: Damp cleaning is a medical-asepsis cleaning practice."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"Which of the following are practices of MEDICAL asepsis (clean technique)? (Select all that apply)","options":["Hand washing (medical)","Cleaning surfaces","Wearing PPEs","Use of sterile gowns and gloves","Opening of sterile packages properly"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Medical asepsis (clean technique) practices include hand washing (medical), cleaning, and wearing PPEs.  Why A/B/C right: All listed under clean technique practices.  Why D wrong: Use of sterile gowns and gloves is a surgical-asepsis (sterile technique) practice.  Why E wrong: Opening of sterile packages properly is a surgical-asepsis practice."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"Which practices belong to SURGICAL asepsis (sterile technique)? (Select all that apply)","options":["Hand washing (surgical scrub)","Use of sterile gowns and gloves","Opening of sterile packages properly","Wearing routine PPEs for cleaning","Wiping surfaces with detergent"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Surgical asepsis (sterile technique) practices include surgical hand washing, use of sterile gowns and gloves, and proper opening of sterile packages.  Why A/B/C right: All listed under sterile technique.  Why D wrong: Routine PPE wearing for cleaning is a medical-asepsis practice.  Why E wrong: Surface wiping is a cleaning/medical-asepsis activity."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"\"Sterilization\" is defined as the complete elimination of microbial viability, including:","options":["Only vegetative bacteria","Both vegetative forms of bacteria and spores","Spores but not bacteria","Viruses only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Sterilization is the complete elimination of microbial viability, including both vegetative forms of bacteria and spores, by physical or chemical means.  Why A wrong: It includes spores, not only vegetative bacteria.  Why C wrong: It includes bacteria as well, not just spores.  Why D wrong: It is not limited to viruses."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"A \"nosocomial infection\" is one that is acquired:","options":["Before hospital admission","From the hospital during a course of treatment (hospital-acquired)","Only through surgical wounds","From the patient's own normal flora exclusively"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A nosocomial infection is an infection acquired from the hospital during the course of treatment (hospital-acquired infection).  Why A wrong: Pre-admission infection is not nosocomial.  Why C wrong: It is not restricted to surgical wounds.  Why D wrong: Endogenous flora infection is a separate concept."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"An infection that originates during diagnostic or therapeutic procedures, also known as a physician-induced infection, is termed:","options":["Iatrogenic infection","Cross infection","Fomite infection","Bacteriostatic infection"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Iatrogenic infection originates during diagnostic or therapeutic procedures and is also known as physician-induced infection.  Why B wrong: Cross infection is transmitted between patients.  Why C wrong: Fomites are inanimate objects that transmit infection, not the infection name.  Why D wrong: Bacteriostatic refers to inhibiting bacterial growth."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"Infection transmitted between one patient and another is termed:","options":["Iatrogenic infection","Cross infection","Nosocomial infection","Colonization"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Cross infection is infection transmitted between patients (one patient to another).  Why A wrong: Iatrogenic is procedure/physician-induced.  Why C wrong: Nosocomial is broadly hospital-acquired.  Why D wrong: Colonization is the presence of organisms without disease."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"An agent that prohibits the growth and development of microorganisms (used on living tissue) is a/an:","options":["Disinfectant","Antiseptic","Bactericide","Fomite"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: An antiseptic is an agent that prohibits the growth and development of microorganisms.  Why A wrong: A disinfectant kills microorganisms on inanimate objects.  Why C wrong: A bactericide specifically kills bacteria.  Why D wrong: A fomite is an inanimate transmission vehicle."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"An agent that prohibits the growth and development of bacteria is termed:","options":["Bactericidal","Bacteriostatic","Sporicidal","Virucidal"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Bacteriostatic refers to an agent that prohibits the growth and development of bacteria (inhibits without killing).  Why A wrong: Bactericidal means it kills the bacteria.  Why C wrong: Sporicidal kills spores.  Why D wrong: Virucidal kills viruses."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"An agent that kills (destroys) bacteria is described as:","options":["Bacteriostatic","Bactericidal","Antiseptic","Colonizing"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Bactericidal means an agent that kills the bacteria.  Why A wrong: Bacteriostatic only inhibits growth.  Why C wrong: Antiseptic inhibits growth on living tissue.  Why D wrong: Colonizing relates to organism presence without disease."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"Disinfection is best defined as a process of:","options":["Killing all microbial life including spores","Killing pathogenic microorganisms","Inhibiting microbial growth on skin","Removing infected material by washing"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Disinfection is a process of killing pathogenic microorganisms.  Why A wrong: Killing all life including spores is sterilization.  Why C wrong: Inhibiting growth on skin is antisepsis.  Why D wrong: Removal of infected material describes medical asepsis."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"Sterilization is a process by which a surface or medium or article is kept free from all types of microorganisms or pathogens, including:","options":["Only non-pathogens","Non-pathogens with their spores","Only viruses","Only fungi"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Sterilization keeps an article free from all microorganisms/pathogens including non-pathogens with their spores.  Why A wrong: It also eliminates pathogens, not just non-pathogens.  Why C/D wrong: It is not restricted to a single microbial class."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"\"Colonization\" refers to a situation when:","options":["A micro-organism is present or settles in a host, grows, and multiplies but does not cause disease or infection","An infection is transmitted between patients","Spores are completely destroyed","Pathogens invade and produce illness"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Colonization occurs when a micro-organism is present or settles, grows, and multiplies in a host but does not cause disease or infection.  Why B wrong: That is cross infection.  Why C wrong: That describes sterilization.  Why D wrong: Active disease production is infection, not colonization."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"Inanimate (contagious) objects that may transmit disease are called:","options":["Vectors","Fomites","Reservoirs","Carriers"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Fomites are contagious (inanimate) objects that may transmit disease.  Why A wrong: Vectors are usually living organisms that transmit disease.  Why C wrong: A reservoir is a source/habitat of an organism.  Why D wrong: A carrier is a person harboring an organism."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"The separation of an infected person from non-infected persons is termed:","options":["Quarantine","Isolation","Colonization","Sterilization"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Isolation is the separation of an infected person from non-infected persons.  Why A wrong: Quarantine is the separation of a non-infected person who has come in contact with an infected person.  Why C wrong: Colonization is harmless presence of organisms.  Why D wrong: Sterilization is microbial elimination."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"Quarantine is best described as the:","options":["Separation of an infected person from non-infected persons","Separation of a non-infected person who has come into contact with an infected person","Killing of all pathogens on instruments","Removal of infected material from surfaces"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Quarantine is the separation of a non-infected person who has come in contact with an infected person.  Why A wrong: That defines isolation.  Why C wrong: That is sterilization.  Why D wrong: That is medical asepsis."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"In hand washing, what is the role of \"friction\"?","options":["It is the chemical that kills bacteria","It is the most important component in hand washing","It is unnecessary if antiseptic is used","It refers to drying the hands"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states friction is the most important component in hand washing.  Why A wrong: Friction is mechanical, not a chemical agent.  Why C wrong: Friction remains essential even with antiseptic.  Why D wrong: Drying is a separate step from friction."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"When hand washing, hands should be held in which direction?","options":["Above the level of the elbows","In a downward direction","Horizontally","Direction is unimportant"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states hand washing involves holding hands in a downward direction (and medical asepsis hand washing keeps hands below the elbows).  Why A wrong: Holding hands above elbows applies to surgical scrub, not basic hand washing here.  Why C/D wrong: A specific downward direction is specified."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"When considering an object for sterile use, if there is any doubt about its sterility, the nurse should:","options":["Use it cautiously","Consider it non-sterile","Wipe it with antiseptic and use it","Ask another nurse to decide"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A principle of sterile technique states that if you have any doubt as to its sterility, consider the object non-sterile.  Why A wrong: Cautious use of a doubtful item violates sterile principles.  Why C wrong: Wiping does not restore questioned sterility.  Why D wrong: Doubt itself dictates non-sterility regardless of opinion."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"According to sterile technique principles, all items within the sterile field must be:","options":["Clean","Sterile","Disinfected","Single-use"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: All items within the sterile field must be sterile.  Why A wrong: Clean (medical asepsis) is insufficient for a sterile field.  Why C wrong: Disinfection does not equal sterility.  Why D wrong: Single-use is not the defining requirement; sterility is."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"A 1-inch (2.5 cm) border around the edge of a sterile field is considered:","options":["Sterile","Non-sterile","A safety margin that stays sterile","Reusable"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A 1-inch border surrounding the sterile field is considered non-sterile.  Why A wrong: The border edge is specifically non-sterile.  Why C wrong: It does not stay sterile; it is treated as contaminated.  Why D wrong: Reusability is unrelated to this principle."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"To maintain sterility, sterile objects must always be held:","options":["Below waist level","Above the waist level / kept within sight","Behind the back","At any convenient level"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Sterile objects should be held above waist level to ensure they are kept within sight; never turn your back on a sterile field.  Why A wrong: Below waist is considered contaminated/non-sterile.  Why C wrong: Items held behind the back or out of sight are non-sterile.  Why D wrong: Level matters; it must be above the waist and in view."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"A sterile field can become non-sterile by:","options":["Brief exposure to sterile air","Prolonged exposure to airborne microorganisms","Being held above the waist","Being kept in full view"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Sterile objects can become non-sterile by prolonged exposure to airborne microorganisms.  Why A wrong: Brief, controlled handling within the field maintains sterility; prolonged air exposure is the issue.  Why C wrong: Holding above the waist helps maintain sterility.  Why D wrong: Keeping items in view maintains sterility."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"Regarding moisture and sterile fields, the nurse must remember that:","options":["Moisture never affects sterility","Moisture can carry microorganisms from a non-sterile surface to a sterile surface (strike-through contamination)","Only dry contamination matters","Sterile fields are waterproof"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Moisture can carry/contaminate by allowing microorganisms to move from a non-sterile surface to a sterile surface (strike-through contamination).  Why A wrong: Moisture does affect sterility.  Why C wrong: Wet/strike-through contamination is specifically a concern.  Why D wrong: Standard sterile fields are not inherently waterproof."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"The skin:","options":["Can be sterilized completely","Cannot be sterilized and is non-sterile","Is always sterile after antiseptic","Is part of the sterile field"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The skin cannot be sterilized and is non-sterile.  Why A wrong: Skin cannot be fully sterilized.  Why C wrong: Antiseptic reduces but does not sterilize skin.  Why D wrong: Skin is non-sterile and not part of the sterile field."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"What should the nurse do if a sterile package is found wet or torn?","options":["Use it immediately after drying","Consider it contaminated and do not use it","Re-wrap and re-sterilize at the bedside","Use only the dry portion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A wet or torn sterile package is considered contaminated; the nurse should check for tears or contamination and not use it.  Why A wrong: Drying does not restore sterility.  Why C wrong: Bedside re-sterilization is not appropriate.  Why D wrong: Once contaminated, the package is unsafe to use."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"When opening a sterile pack, the first flap should be opened away from the body. This is because:","options":["It looks neater","Opening toward the body would reach across the sterile field and contaminate it","It saves time","It keeps hands warm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The first flap is opened away from the body so the nurse does not reach across/over the sterile field, which would contaminate it.  Why A wrong: Aesthetics is not the rationale.  Why C wrong: Time-saving is not the principle.  Why D wrong: Irrelevant to sterility."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"When performing surgical hand washing, the hands should be held:","options":["Below the level of the elbows","Up and above the level of the elbows","At waist level","Behind the back"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: During surgical hand wash, scrub the arms, keeping hands above the elbows, so that water and soap run off from the arm/elbow to allow microorganisms to slide off the hands.  Why A wrong: Hands below elbows applies to medical (routine) hand washing, not surgical scrub.  Why C/D wrong: Neither maintains the run-off direction needed for surgical asepsis."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"During surgical hand washing, scrubbing should last for at least:","options":["15 seconds","1 minute","2–6 minutes","30 minutes"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states surgical scrub time is 2–6 minutes (during surgical procedures e.g., biopsies, LP, punctures, wound stitching, operative procedures).  Why A wrong: 15 seconds is the basic/medical hand wash time recommendation.  Why B wrong: 1 minute is short of the stated surgical scrub duration.  Why D wrong: 30 minutes is the CSSD autoclave time, not hand scrub time."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"Basic (routine) hand hygiene used for medical asepsis should run from elbow to hands for the length of time recommended by the manufacturer, usually:","options":["3–5 seconds","5–10 seconds","15 seconds","2 minutes"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Basic hand hygiene should run from elbow to hands for the manufacturer-recommended length of time, usually 15 seconds (with hands held low, in a downward direction).  Why A/B wrong: Shorter than the stated 15 seconds.  Why D wrong: 2 minutes falls under the surgical scrub range, not routine hand hygiene."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"When should surgical asepsis (sterile technique) be used? (Select all that apply)","options":["During medication administration and tube feeding","During daily hygiene and sugar (routine) monitoring of stable patients","During dressing changes","During invasive procedures","During taking of vital signs in a stable patient"],"correct":"A,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Surgical asepsis is used during medication administration and tube feeding, dressing changes, and invasive procedures.  Why A/C/D right: All listed in the source as situations requiring surgical asepsis.  Why B wrong: Routine daily hygiene is a clean (medical asepsis) activity.  Why E wrong: Taking vital signs in a stable patient does not require sterile technique."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"Regarding the operating room (OR) and sterile field, which statement is correct?","options":["Anyone can enter without restriction","Only properly attired (scrubbed/PPE) personnel should enter the operating room","Talking freely over the field is safe","Reaching across the sterile field is acceptable"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Only appropriately attired/scrubbed personnel should enter the operating room and area surrounding the sterile field; unauthorized entry risks contamination.  Why A wrong: Unrestricted entry contaminates the area.  Why C wrong: Talking releases moisture droplets laden with bacteria over the field.  Why D wrong: Reaching across the sterile field contaminates it."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"According to surgical asepsis rules and rationale, surgical team members should remain within the sterile area because:","options":["It improves communication","Movement out of and back into the area may encourage cross-contamination","It conserves sterile supplies","It reduces noise"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Surgical team members remain within the sterile area because movement out of the sterile area may encourage cross-contamination.  Why A/C/D wrong: The stated rationale is prevention of cross-contamination, not communication, supplies, or noise."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"Talking should be kept to a minimum in the sterile field because:","options":["It distracts the surgeon","Talking releases moisture droplets laden with bacteria","It wastes oxygen","It is impolite"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Talking is kept to a minimum because talking releases moisture droplets laden with bacteria.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source-stated reason is bacterial droplet release, not distraction, oxygen, or etiquette."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"Why must non-scrubbed personnel never reach over the sterile field?","options":["They might bump the surgeon","Dust, lint, or other vehicles of bacterial contamination may fall on the sterile field","It blocks the lights","It is against hospital tradition"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Non-scrubbed personnel do not reach over the sterile field because dust, lint, or other vehicles of bacterial contamination may fall on the sterile field.  Why A/C/D wrong: The cited rationale is falling contaminant particles, not collision, lighting, or tradition."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"Equipment used during surgery must be sterilized because:","options":["It looks professional","Unsterile instruments may be a source of cross-contamination","It speeds up the procedure","It is required only for minor procedures"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Equipment used during surgery must be sterilized because unsterile instruments may be a source of cross-contamination.  Why A/C/D wrong: The rationale is preventing cross-contamination, not appearance, speed, or procedure size."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"Sterile tables are considered sterile only at:","options":["The floor level","Table height (the table surface level)","Below the table","Any height"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Sterile tables are sterile only at table height; areas below table height are considered non-sterile.  Why A/C wrong: Below table level is non-sterile.  Why D wrong: Sterility is restricted to table-top level, not any height."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"When draping covers an instrument table or patient, why should moisture proof drapes be used?","options":["Moisture is harmless","Moisture allows bacteria to wick from a non-sterile surface to a sterile surface (strike-through contamination)","Dry drapes are too expensive","Moisture improves sterility"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Drapes covering instrument tables or the patient should be moisture proof because moisture carries bacteria from a non-sterile surface to a sterile surface (strike-through contamination).  Why A wrong: Moisture is not harmless to sterility.  Why C wrong: Cost is not the rationale.  Why D wrong: Moisture compromises, not improves, sterility."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"If a sterile object touches the sealing edge of its pouch when being opened, the object is considered:","options":["Still sterile","Contaminated/non-sterile","Sterile only at the tip","Reusable"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If a sterile object touches the sealing edge of the pouch while it is opened, it is considered non-sterile/contaminated because once opened, sealed edges of pouches are not sterile.  Why A wrong: Contact with the pouch edge contaminates the item.  Why C/D wrong: The whole item is treated as contaminated."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"Sterile items within a damaged or wet wrapper are considered contaminated because:","options":["The color changes","Contamination can occur from perforated wrappers or by strike-through from moisture transport","The items expire faster","They become too heavy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Sterile items within a damaged or wet wrapper are considered contaminated because contamination can occur from perforated wrappers or from strike-through (moisture transport).  Why A/C/D wrong: The rationale is breach of the sterile barrier/moisture, not color, expiry, or weight."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"Hands may be held lower than the axillary region but not below the waist because:","options":["The axillary region of the gown is not considered sterile","It is more comfortable","The waist area is fully sterile","The gown is sterile everywhere"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hands may be held lower than the axillary region rather than below the waist because the axillary region of the gown is not considered sterile.  Why B wrong: Comfort is not the rationale.  Why C wrong: Below-waist gown areas are non-sterile.  Why D wrong: The gown is not sterile everywhere; only certain zones are sterile."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"The surgical sterile field is considered sterile only from:","options":["The shoulders to the knees, front and back","Table height to the chest, with movement maintained to avoid cross-contamination","Head to toe","The waist down"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The surgical field is sterile only from table height to the chest; movement should be maintained standing during surgery to avoid cross-contamination, and the surgical gown is not sterile below the waist.  Why A wrong: The back of the gown is not sterile.  Why C/D wrong: Full body/waist-down areas are not the defined sterile zone."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"The Central Sterile Services Department (CSSD) is responsible for:","options":["Patient billing","Collection, decontamination, assembling, packing, sterilization, storing, and distribution of sterile goods and equipment to patient care areas","Pharmacy dispensing only","Dietary services"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The CSSD is a specialized area responsible for collection, decontamination, assembling, packing, sterilization, storing, and distribution of sterile goods and equipment to patient care areas.  Why A/C/D wrong: Billing, pharmacy dispensing, and dietary services are not CSSD functions."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"In the CSSD, autoclaves typically operate at a temperature of approximately:","options":["100°C","121°C","150°C","180°C"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Autoclaves operate at a temperature of 121°C at a pressure of 15 psi.  Why A wrong: 100°C is the boiling point of water, below autoclave standard.  Why C/D wrong: These exceed the stated 121°C autoclave temperature."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"CSSD autoclaves typically operate at a pressure and duration of:","options":["10 psi for 60 minutes","15 psi for a duration of 30 minutes","20 psi for 10 minutes","5 psi for 15 minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Autoclaves operate at 121°C at a pressure of 15 psi for a duration of 30 minutes; the entire cycle of autoclaving from loading to unloading of sterile packs takes approximately 3 hours.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated 15 psi for 30 minutes."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"The entire autoclaving cycle, from loading to unloading of sterile packs, takes approximately:","options":["30 minutes","1 hour","3 hours","8 hours"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The entire cycle of autoclaving from loading to unloading of sterile packs takes approximately 3 hours.  Why A wrong: 30 minutes is only the sterilization holding time.  Why B/D wrong: These do not match the stated 3-hour full cycle."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"Regarding CSSD sterile storage, the nurse should remember that:","options":["Storage need not be protected from dust","The sterile storage area should provide protection against dust, moisture, temperature, and humidity","Items can be stored on the floor","Labeling is unnecessary"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The sterile storage area should provide protection against dust, moisture, temperature, and humidity.  Why A wrong: Dust protection is required.  Why C wrong: Floor storage risks contamination.  Why D wrong: Sterilized items should be labeled with date and contents."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"How should sterilized CSSD packs be labeled?","options":["With the patient's name only","With the date and contents of the pack","Color-coded only","No labeling needed"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Sterilized items should be labeled clearly with the date and the contents of the pack.  Why A wrong: Patient name is not the required labeling content.  Why C wrong: Color-coding alone is insufficient; date and contents are required.  Why D wrong: Labeling is mandatory."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"If the integrity of a sterilized pack is suspect (e.g., wet, torn), it should be:","options":["Used immediately","Re-sterilized","Stored for later use","Returned to the patient unit"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Whenever the integrity of the pack is suspect, it should be re-sterilized.  Why A wrong: A compromised pack should not be used.  Why C wrong: It should not be stored as-is for later use.  Why D wrong: It must be re-sterilized, not returned for use."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"Unused and unopened sterile items should be re-sterilized every:","options":["24 hours","7 hours","7 days","30 days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Unused and unopened items should be re-sterilized after every 7 hours, per the source.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated 7-hour interval."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"A HIGH-level disinfectant from the source list is:","options":["Some soaps","28% glutaraldehyde","Benzalkonium chloride","Isopropyl alcohol (70%)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: High-level disinfectants listed include 2% glutaraldehyde (printed as 28% in the scan), stabilized hydrogen peroxide, 1% sodium hypochlorite solution (10,000 ppm of Cl2), and ethylene oxide. [Note: standard practice uses 2% glutaraldehyde as a high-level disinfectant; the scanned \"28%\" appears to be an OCR/print artifact.]  Why A wrong: Some soaps are low-level disinfectants.  Why C wrong: Benzalkonium chloride is a low-level disinfectant.  Why D wrong: Isopropyl alcohol 70% is an intermediate-level disinfectant."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"Which agent is listed as a HIGH-level disinfectant equivalent to 10,000 ppm of chlorine (Cl2)?","options":["0.1% sodium hypochlorite","1% sodium hypochlorite solution","Phenolic solution","Some soaps"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 1% sodium hypochlorite solution (10,000 ppm of Cl2) is listed as a high-level disinfectant.  Why A wrong: 0.1% sodium hypochlorite (1,000 ppm of Cl2) is listed under intermediate-level disinfectants.  Why C wrong: Phenolic compounds are intermediate-level disinfectants.  Why D wrong: Some soaps are low-level disinfectants."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"Which of the following are INTERMEDIATE-level disinfectants per the source? (Select all that apply)","options":["0.1% sodium hypochlorite solution (1,000 ppm of Cl2)","Isopropyl alcohol (70%)","Chlorhexidine","Iodophors and phenolic solution","Benzalkonium chloride"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Intermediate-level disinfectants listed are 0.1% sodium hypochlorite solution (1,000 ppm of Cl2), isopropyl alcohol (70%), chlorhexidine, iodophors and phenolic solution, and chlorine compounds.  Why A/B/C/D right: All appear under intermediate-level disinfectants.  Why E wrong: Benzalkonium chloride is listed as a low-level disinfectant."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"Which agents are listed as LOW-level disinfectants? (Select all that apply)","options":["Benzalkonium chloride","Some soaps","Ethylene oxide","1% sodium hypochlorite (10,000 ppm)","70% isopropyl alcohol"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: Low-level disinfectants listed are benzalkonium chloride and some soaps.  Why A/B right: Both appear under low-level disinfectants.  Why C wrong: Ethylene oxide is high-level.  Why D wrong: 1% sodium hypochlorite (10,000 ppm) is high-level.  Why E wrong: 70% isopropyl alcohol is intermediate-level."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"According to the Spaulding classification, a device that contacts intact skin or does not penetrate tissue (non-critical device) requires:","options":["Sterilization","High-level disinfection","Low-level disinfection or intermediate-level disinfection","No cleaning at all"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Non-critical devices (contact intact skin or non-patient contacting), e.g., BP apparatus, ECG machine, cardiac monitor, pulse oximeter, bed pan, urinal, commode, require low-level disinfection or intermediate-level disinfection.  Why A wrong: Sterilization is for critical devices.  Why B wrong: High-level disinfection is for semi-critical devices.  Why D wrong: Even non-critical devices require disinfection."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"Per the Spaulding classification, devices that contact mucous membranes or non-intact skin (semi-critical devices) require:","options":["Low-level disinfection","High-level disinfection","Sterilization only","No disinfection"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Semi-critical devices (contact mucous membranes or non-intact skin), e.g., respiratory therapy equipment, anaesthesia equipment, tonometer, laryngoscope, Cusco speculum, Sims speculum, require high-level disinfection.  Why A wrong: Low-level disinfection is for non-critical devices.  Why C wrong: Sterilization is for critical devices.  Why D wrong: Disinfection is required."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"Under the Spaulding classification, a critical device that contacts sterile areas of the body, including the bloodstream (e.g., surgical instruments, implants, biopsy instruments), requires:","options":["Low-level disinfection","Intermediate-level disinfection","High-level disinfection","Sterilization"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Critical devices (contact sterile body areas including the bloodstream pathway) require sterilization; examples include surgical instruments, implants, biopsy instruments, foot care instruments, and eye/dental equipment.  Why A/B/C wrong: Disinfection at any level is insufficient for critical devices, which must be sterilized."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"Which of the following are examples of NON-critical devices under the Spaulding classification? (Select all that apply)","options":["BP apparatus","ECG machine","Bed pan and urinal","Surgical implants","Laryngoscope"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Non-critical devices include AMBU bag, ECG machine, BP apparatus, cardiac monitor, pulse oximeter, bed pan, urinal, and commode.  Why A/B/C right: All listed as non-critical examples.  Why D wrong: Surgical implants are critical devices.  Why E wrong: A laryngoscope is a semi-critical device."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"For general cleaning of walls in the hospital, the recommended method is:","options":["Vacuum cleaning only","A method of cleaning that varies according to the area of the hospital","Never cleaning walls","Daily scrubbing with bleach"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Walls — the method of cleaning may vary according to the area of the hospital.  Why A wrong: Vacuum cleaning with a filtered cleaner is specified for floors, not walls.  Why C wrong: Walls do require cleaning.  Why D wrong: The source does not prescribe daily bleach scrubbing for all walls."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"When wet/dry vacuum cleaning floors to avoid dust or attracting mop, what type of vacuum cleaner is recommended, especially in high-risk areas (OT, ICU, HDU, dialysis)?","options":["A bagless household vacuum","A vacuum cleaner with a HEPA (high-risk areas) filtered air filter","An unfiltered industrial vacuum","No vacuum; only brooms"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Floors — use wet cleaning or dust-attracting mop or a vacuum cleaner; disinfection may be required in high-risk areas (OT, ICU, dialysis), and a filtered air vacuum cleaner should be used.  Why A/C wrong: Filtered air is specifically required to avoid dust dispersal in high-risk areas.  Why D wrong: Brooms disperse dust and are not advised for high-risk hospital areas."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"Windows, window frames, and window grills in the hospital should be cleaned by:","options":["Dry dusting only","Regular cleaning (and require air-cleaning attention)","Autoclaving","Never cleaning"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Windows, window frames, and window grills require regular cleaning per the general cleaning procedures.  Why A wrong: Dry dusting alone disperses dust.  Why C wrong: Autoclaving is for instruments, not windows.  Why D wrong: Windows require cleaning."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"When cleaning a patient's bath/baths, the source recommends:","options":["Leaving residual cleaning solution to keep releasing chlorine","Ensuring good ventilation with filtered air in OTs, ICUs, and isolation rooms; baths disinfected with chlorine-releasing solutions","Using only plain water","Sharing the same cloth for all surfaces"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source directs good ventilation with filtered air in OTs, ICUs, and isolation rooms, and that baths be disinfected with chlorine-releasing solutions.  Why A wrong: Residual solution buildup is not the recommended practice.  Why C wrong: Plain water is insufficient; chlorine-releasing disinfection is specified.  Why D wrong: Cross-use of a single cloth promotes cross-contamination."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"Wash basins and bed pans should be:","options":["Wiped dry and reused without disinfection","Thoroughly cleaned with detergent and hot water, and kept dry","Stored wet","Cleaned only weekly"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Wash basins and bed pans should be thoroughly cleaned with detergent and hot water, and kept dry.  Why A wrong: Disinfection/proper cleaning is required, not mere wiping.  Why C wrong: They should be kept dry, not stored wet.  Why D wrong: Cleaning should follow use, not only weekly."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"Furniture and fixtures (such as door knobs, bedrails, light switches, surfaces, and surrounding hotels in patients' rooms) should be cleaned on each shift and whenever visibly soiled, compared to:","options":["Once a year","That for minimal-touch housekeeping surfaces (less frequent)","Never","Only on patient discharge"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Furniture and fixtures (door knobs, bedrails, surfaces, items around patients' rooms) should be cleaned on each shift and whenever visibly soiled, compared to that for minimal-touch housekeeping surfaces.  Why A/C/D wrong: High-touch fixtures need per-shift and as-soiled cleaning, not yearly, never, or discharge-only."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"A patient's bedding and dry linen should be:","options":["Mixed with all other ward linen","Kept separate (kept in/with a clean drum), and the linen washed with blood and body fluids of patients with HIV and HBV infection treated with 1% sodium hypochlorite solution","Reused without washing","Soaked in plain water for 24 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Bedding should be kept separate; linen soiled with blood and body fluids of patients with HIV and HBV infection should be treated with 1% sodium hypochlorite solution.  Why A wrong: Soiled/infected linen must be kept separate and specially treated.  Why C wrong: Linen must be laundered.  Why D wrong: Plain-water soaking is not the specified treatment."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"Bed frames should be cleaned with detergent and dry; if contaminated with blood or body fluids, they should be cleaned with:","options":["Plain water","Phenolics (5% Carbolic acid) or 1% sodium hypochlorite solution","70% isopropyl alcohol only","Soap and a dry cloth"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Bed frames should be cleaned with detergent and dry; if contaminated with blood or body fluids, clean with phenolics (5% Carbolic acid) or 1% sodium hypochlorite solution.  Why A wrong: Plain water does not disinfect blood/body-fluid contamination.  Why C wrong: Alcohol is not the specified agent for bed frames here.  Why D wrong: Soap with a dry cloth is insufficient for contamination."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"When bed pans are used for patients with enteric infections, the chemical disinfectant of choice is:","options":["1% sodium hypochlorite solution","Plain detergent","Alcohol swabs","No disinfection needed"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Bed pans preferably individual bed pans should be used; in patients with enteric infections, chemical disinfectants (1% sodium hypochlorite solution) can be used if multiple use bed pans are shared.  Why B wrong: Plain detergent is insufficient for enteric pathogen control.  Why C wrong: Alcohol swabs are not the specified disinfectant for bed pans.  Why D wrong: Disinfection is required for enteric-infection patients."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"Per the source, how should blankets be handled?","options":["Always autoclaved daily","Dry cleaning for woollen blankets is advocated","Boiled in water for 24 hours","Discarded after each use"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Blankets — dry cleaning for woollen blankets is advocated.  Why A wrong: Daily autoclaving of blankets is not advised.  Why C wrong: Prolonged boiling is not specified.  Why D wrong: Blankets are dry cleaned, not discarded each use."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"Surgical/operation bowls should be:","options":["Wiped with a damp cloth only","Autoclaved (surgical bowls can be autoclaved)","Rinsed in plain water","Soaked in detergent only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Bowls — surgical bowls can be autoclaved.  Why A wrong: Wiping is insufficient for surgical bowls.  Why C wrong: Plain-water rinsing does not sterilize.  Why D wrong: Detergent soaking alone is insufficient for surgical bowls."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"Crockery and cutlery (including food trays) should be:","options":["Left to air-dry only","Cleaned with detergent and hot water and stored dry","Sterilized in an autoclave before each meal","Cleaned only when visibly dirty"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Crockery and cutlery (including food trays) should be cleaned with detergent and hot water and stored dry.  Why A wrong: Air-drying alone without detergent/hot water cleaning is inadequate.  Why C wrong: Autoclaving crockery before meals is not specified.  Why D wrong: Routine cleaning with detergent/hot water is required, not only when visibly dirty."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"Furniture and fitting (including curtains/tops) should undergo wet mopping with detergent; in known contaminated and special areas, disinfect using:","options":["Plain water","Phenolics (5% Carbolic acid) or 1% sodium hypochlorite solution","Soap only","Dry dusting"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Furniture and fittings (including curtains/tops): wet mopping with detergent; in known contaminated and special areas, disinfect using phenolics (5% Carbolic acid) or 1% sodium hypochlorite solution.  Why A/C/D wrong: Plain water, soap alone, or dry dusting do not provide the specified disinfection in contaminated/special areas."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"Mattresses and pillows should be:","options":["Cleaned with plain water","Water-impermeable covers; washed with detergent solution; if contaminated, disinfected with phenolics (5% Carbolic acid) or 1% sodium hypochlorite solution","Discarded after each patient","Autoclaved"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Mattresses and pillows should use water-impermeable covers, be washed with detergent solution, and if contaminated be disinfected with phenolics (5% Carbolic acid) or 1% sodium hypochlorite solution.  Why A wrong: Plain water alone is insufficient.  Why C wrong: Routine discarding is not specified.  Why D wrong: Autoclaving mattresses/pillows is impractical and not specified."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"When using a mop, the nurse should:","options":["Use the same dirty water for the whole ward","Never rinse it in the cleaning solution and store wet, dirty","Not be rinsed after each use; wring and store dry, and change disinfectant solution after about 30 minutes (then rinse and dry)","Use only on visibly soiled spots"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Mops should be rinsed after use, wrung, and stored dry; the disinfectant solution should be changed about every 30 minutes; mop is rinsed in the solution and the floor mopped liberally, washed in disinfectant after use, and dried thoroughly before reuse.  Why A wrong: Reusing dirty water spreads contamination.  Why B wrong: Mops should be cleaned, wrung, and stored dry.  Why D wrong: Mopping covers the floor liberally, not just spots."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"A sputum container should be:","options":["Reused without cleaning","Disposable, preferably; use disposables","Stored in the patient's locker","Cleaned weekly only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Sputum container — preferably use disposables.  Why A wrong: Reuse without cleaning risks transmission of respiratory pathogens.  Why C wrong: Storage in the locker is not the specified handling.  Why D wrong: Disposables are preferred over reusable weekly-cleaned containers."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"Toilet seats should be cleaned with detergent and dry; after use by an infected patient or when grossly contaminated, mop with 1% sodium hypochlorite solution. This indicates that:","options":["Plain wiping is sufficient at all times","Infected or grossly contaminated toilet seats require 1% sodium hypochlorite disinfection","Toilet seats never need disinfection","Only soap is used after infected use"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Toilet seats should be cleaned with detergent and dry; after use by an infected patient or when grossly contaminated, mop with 1% sodium hypochlorite solution.  Why A wrong: Infected/contaminated seats need hypochlorite disinfection, not plain wiping.  Why C wrong: Disinfection is required after infected use.  Why D wrong: 1% sodium hypochlorite, not just soap, is specified."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"A trolley top should be cleaned with detergent and dry; if disinfection is needed, the source recommends using:","options":["70% isopropyl alcohol or phenolics (5% Carbolic acid) or 1% sodium hypochlorite solution","Plain water only","Bleach powder rubbed dry","No disinfection is ever needed"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Trolley tops should be cleaned with detergent and dry; if disinfection is needed, use 70% isopropyl alcohol or phenolics (5% Carbolic acid) or 1% sodium hypochlorite solution.  Why B wrong: Plain water is insufficient for required disinfection.  Why C wrong: Dry bleach powder is not the specified method.  Why D wrong: Disinfection may be needed for trolley tops."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"Urinals should preferably be individual; clean with hot water and detergent. This is to:","options":["Improve appearance only","Prevent cross-transmission between patients","Sterilize them completely","Save detergent"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Urinals use preferably individually; clean with hot water and detergent — individual use and proper cleaning prevent cross-transmission.  Why A wrong: The purpose is infection control, not appearance.  Why C wrong: Hot water and detergent clean but do not fully sterilize.  Why D wrong: Detergent conservation is not the rationale."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"A Mackintosh (rubber sheet) should be cleaned with:","options":["Plain water and air-dried","Detergent (cleaned with water and detergent and dry); preferably individual Mackintosh, cleaned with water and detergent and dry","Autoclaved each shift","Never cleaned"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Mackintosh (rubber sheet) — use preferably individual Mackintosh; clean with water and detergent and dry.  Why A wrong: Detergent cleaning, not plain water alone, is specified.  Why C wrong: Autoclaving a rubber sheet each shift is not specified.  Why D wrong: It must be cleaned."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"In the three-bucket system for mopping floors, how many buckets are used and which method is preferred?","options":["Two buckets; 2-bucket system preferred","Three buckets; the 3-bucket system is preferred over the 2-bucket system","One bucket","Four buckets"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The three-bucket system uses 3 buckets for mopping of floors; the 3-bucket system is preferred over the 2-bucket system.  Why A wrong: Although a 2-bucket system exists, the 3-bucket system is preferred.  Why C/D wrong: The system uses three buckets, not one or four."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"In the three-bucket system, the FIRST bucket contains:","options":["Disinfectant","Water (to wash the floor)","Fresh water to rinse the mop","Soap solution for hands"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: First bucket — water; wash the floor.  Why A wrong: Disinfectant is in the third bucket.  Why C wrong: Fresh water to rinse the mop is the second bucket.  Why D wrong: Hand soap is not part of the three-bucket floor system."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"In the three-bucket system, the SECOND bucket contains:","options":["Disinfectant","Fresh water to rinse the mop","Plain detergent for walls","Bleaching powder"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Second bucket — with fresh water; rinse the mop.  Why A wrong: Disinfectant is the third bucket.  Why C/D wrong: These are not part of the second bucket's described contents."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"In the three-bucket system, the THIRD bucket contains:","options":["Plain water","Disinfectant — the mop is rinsed in the solution and the floor mopped liberally; mop is then washed in disinfectant and dried before reuse","Fresh water for the mop","Soap solution"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Third bucket — with low-level disinfectant; the mop is rinsed in the solution and the floor mopped liberally; the mop is washed in disinfectant after use and dried thoroughly before reuse.  Why A wrong: Plain water is the first bucket.  Why C wrong: Fresh water for the mop is the second bucket.  Why D wrong: Soap solution is not the third bucket content."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"When cleaning hospital floors, disinfectants are needed especially in high-risk areas. Which of the following are listed as high-risk areas requiring disinfection? (Select all that apply)","options":["Operation theatre (OT)","Intensive care unit (ICU)","Dialysis area","Hospital cafeteria seating","Visitors' waiting lounge"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Disinfection of floors may be required in high-risk areas such as OT, ICU, and dialysis areas, using a filtered air vacuum cleaner.  Why A/B/C right: All listed as high-risk areas.  Why D wrong: Cafeteria seating is not listed among the high-risk areas requiring floor disinfection.  Why E wrong: A visitors' lounge is not listed as a high-risk area."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"A nurse opens a sterile pack and turns away to retrieve another item, briefly facing away from the field. According to sterile technique principles, the field is now considered:","options":["Sterile, since nothing touched it","Non-sterile/contaminated, because you should never turn your back on a sterile field","Sterile only at the center","Sterile if returned to within 5 minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A core principle states never turn your back on a sterile field; doing so renders it potentially contaminated because it can no longer be kept in view/protected.  Why A wrong: Loss of visual control breaches the principle regardless of contact.  Why C wrong: The whole field is compromised, not just the center.  Why D wrong: There is no time-based \"return to sterile\" allowance in the source."},{"section":"Batch 051 · body mechanics positions, types of exercise…","question":"A patient on contact precautions for an enteric infection requires a bed pan. The most appropriate nursing action based on the source is to:","options":["Share a common ward bed pan and rinse with water","Use an individual bed pan and disinfect with 1% sodium hypochlorite solution if a multiple-use bed pan must be shared","Discard the bed pan after one use","Disinfect the bed pan with 70% alcohol only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Bed pans should preferably be individual; for patients with enteric infections, 1% sodium hypochlorite can be used to disinfect if multiple-use bed pans are shared.  Why A wrong: Sharing with only a water rinse risks enteric pathogen transmission.  Why C wrong: Single-use discarding is not the source recommendation for standard bed pans.  Why D wrong: Alcohol is not the specified bed-pan disinfectant; 1% sodium hypochlorite is."},{"section":"Batch 052 · AIIMS Delhi infection-control manual guidelines for…","question":"According to AIIMS Delhi infection-control guidelines, how should the neck of an ampoule be disinfected before cutting?","options":["Wiped with 70% alcohol","Soaked in 2% glutaraldehyde","Autoclaved before cutting","Rinsed with sterile water"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The guideline states the neck of an ampoule should be wiped with 70% alcohol before cutting.  Why B wrong: Glutaraldehyde is for high-level disinfection of instruments, not ampoule necks.  Why C wrong: Ampoules are single-use glass; the neck is surface-disinfected, not autoclaved.  Why D wrong: Sterile water has no disinfectant action."},{"section":"Batch 052 · AIIMS Delhi infection-control manual guidelines for…","question":"Cheatle forceps should be cleaned with soap, water, and autoclaved, then stored dry. How often must the stored sterile bottle/container be replaced if it remains visibly clean?","options":["Every 12 hours","Every 24 hours","Every 48 hours","Every 72 hours"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Cheatle forceps are kept in a sterile bottle/container that should be replaced every 72 hours, or earlier if visibly contaminated.  Why A/B/C wrong: The specified replacement interval is 72 hours, not 12, 24, or 48 hours."},{"section":"Batch 052 · AIIMS Delhi infection-control manual guidelines for…","question":"A flexible endoscope (bronchoscope, arthroscope, cystoscope, laparoscope) requires which level of disinfection per AIIMS guidelines?","options":["Low-level disinfection with 70% alcohol","High-level disinfection with 2% glutaraldehyde or 70% alcohol","Sterilization by autoclaving only","Simple soap-and-water washing"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Flexible endoscopes are disinfected by high-level disinfection, e.g., 2% glutaraldehyde or 70% alcohol.  Why A wrong: Endoscopes are semi-critical items requiring high-level, not low-level, disinfection.  Why C wrong: Heat-sensitive flexible endoscopes cannot be routinely autoclaved.  Why D wrong: Soap and water alone provide only cleaning, not high-level disinfection."},{"section":"Batch 052 · AIIMS Delhi infection-control manual guidelines for…","question":"Rigid endoscopes (bronchoscopes, arthroscopes, cystoscopes, laparoscopes) are disinfected by high-level disinfection using which agent?","options":["70% alcohol only","2% glutaraldehyde or 70% alcohol","Chlorine 0.5% only","Plain detergent"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Rigid endoscopes undergo high-level disinfection with 2% glutaraldehyde or 70% alcohol.  Why A wrong: Alcohol alone is one option, but the guideline lists glutaraldehyde first; \"70% alcohol only\" understates the recommendation.  Why C wrong: Chlorine is not specified for rigid endoscopes here.  Why D wrong: Detergent only cleans; it does not disinfect."},{"section":"Batch 052 · AIIMS Delhi infection-control manual guidelines for…","question":"How are incubators (neonatal) surfaces cleaned per the AIIMS infection-control manual?","options":["Washed with detergent and dried with antiseptic wipes","Autoclaved daily","Soaked in glutaraldehyde","Wiped with 70% alcohol only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Incubator surfaces should be washed with detergent and dried with antiseptic wipes.  Why B wrong: An incubator is a large electrical device that cannot be autoclaved.  Why C wrong: Glutaraldehyde soaking is for instruments, not incubator surfaces.  Why D wrong: The manual specifies detergent washing and antiseptic wiping, not alcohol alone."},{"section":"Batch 052 · AIIMS Delhi infection-control manual guidelines for…","question":"Contaminated surgical instruments must be washed in a hot water washer/disinfector before sterilization, then cleaned with chlorine-releasing chemical, glutaraldehyde, or which alcohol concentration?","options":["50% alcohol","60% alcohol","70% alcohol","90% alcohol"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Surgical instruments are cleaned with chlorine-releasing chemical, 2% glutaraldehyde, or 70% alcohol.  Why A/B/D wrong: 70% is the optimal microbicidal alcohol concentration; the guideline specifies 70%."},{"section":"Batch 052 · AIIMS Delhi infection-control manual guidelines for…","question":"What type of container should be used for collecting sputum samples per the infection-control guidelines?","options":["Reusable glass containers","Disposable containers","Metal autoclaved containers","Cloth-lined containers"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Sputum containers should be disposable.  Why A/C/D wrong: To prevent cross-infection from respiratory secretions, single-use disposable containers are required, not reusable glass, metal, or cloth-lined containers."},{"section":"Batch 052 · AIIMS Delhi infection-control manual guidelines for…","question":"An oral thermometer (individual-use) and a stethoscope should both be cleaned with which agent?","options":["2% glutaraldehyde","70% alcohol, and stored dry","5% phenol","Plain water"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Oral thermometers (preferably individual) are wiped with 70% alcohol and stored dry; stethoscopes are wiped with 70% alcohol once daily or when visibly soiled.  Why A wrong: Glutaraldehyde is for instrument soaking, not thermometer/stethoscope surface wiping.  Why C wrong: Phenol is not specified for these items.  Why D wrong: Plain water does not disinfect."},{"section":"Batch 052 · AIIMS Delhi infection-control manual guidelines for…","question":"BP cuffs that become soiled with blood should be cleaned with which agent?","options":["70% isopropyl alcohol","Plain detergent only","Sterile saline","Glutaraldehyde 2%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: BP cuffs are cleaned with 70% isopropyl alcohol and should be cleaned periodically or when visibly soiled.  Why B wrong: Detergent alone is insufficient for blood-soiled cuffs.  Why C wrong: Saline has no disinfectant action.  Why D wrong: Glutaraldehyde is for instrument immersion, not fabric BP cuffs."},{"section":"Batch 052 · AIIMS Delhi infection-control manual guidelines for…","question":"How should the blade of a laryngoscope be disinfected after each use?","options":["Autoclaved after every use","Cleaned with detergent and water, then disinfected with 70% isopropyl alcohol swab","Soaked in saline","Wiped with sterile water only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The laryngoscope blade is cleaned with detergent and water after each use and disinfected with 70% isopropyl alcohol swab.  Why A wrong: The handle is cleaned with cloth and stored dry; the manual specifies alcohol-swab disinfection for the blade, not autoclaving.  Why C/D wrong: Saline or plain sterile water do not disinfect."},{"section":"Batch 052 · AIIMS Delhi infection-control manual guidelines for…","question":"Suction equipment (bottles) should be emptied and cleaned how often?","options":["Once a week","Regularly — wash with detergent and hot water, then store dry","Only when visibly full","Every 72 hours regardless of use"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Equipment is cleaned regularly with a wet mop; suction bottles are emptied regularly, washed with detergent and hot water, and stored dry.  Why A/C/D wrong: The guideline specifies regular cleaning, not weekly, only-when-full, or a fixed 72-hour rule."},{"section":"Batch 052 · AIIMS Delhi infection-control manual guidelines for…","question":"For anaesthesia and ventilator circuits (including humidifiers and T-pieces), what is the recommended immersion time when submerged in 2% glutaraldehyde for cleaning?","options":["10 minutes","20 minutes","30 minutes","60 minutes"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Anaesthesia and ventilator circuits are rinsed with water, then submerged in a container/bucket with 2% glutaraldehyde for 30 min; the lumen of tubings is filled with disinfectant, then dried in a clean area or sterilized by ETO/autoclave.  Why A/B/D wrong: The specified contact time is 30 minutes."},{"section":"Batch 052 · AIIMS Delhi infection-control manual guidelines for…","question":"Anaesthesia and ventilator circuits may be sterilized by which method after disinfection?","options":["ETO (ethylene oxide) or autoclaving","Boiling for 5 minutes","UV light exposure","Dry heat at 50°C"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: After disinfection the circuits should be sterilized by ETO or autoclaved.  Why B wrong: Boiling for 5 minutes is not the specified sterilization method.  Why C wrong: UV light is not used for tubing sterilization here.  Why D wrong: Dry heat at 50°C is far below sterilizing temperatures."},{"section":"Batch 052 · AIIMS Delhi infection-control manual guidelines for…","question":"Nebulizer masks and AMBU bags/reservoirs should preferably be managed how, to prevent cross-infection between patients?","options":["Shared between patients after wiping","Used for individual patients only and disinfected daily using the same method as for ventilator circuits","Disinfected once weekly","Stored wet between uses"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The nebulizer is preferably used for individual patients; if shared, it is disinfected daily using the same method as for ventilator circuits. AMBU bags and reservoirs are disinfected absolutely dry.  Why A wrong: Sharing without proper disinfection risks cross-infection.  Why C wrong: Daily disinfection is specified, not weekly.  Why D wrong: AMBU bags/reservoirs should be absolutely dry, not stored wet."},{"section":"Batch 052 · AIIMS Delhi infection-control manual guidelines for…","question":"How should oxygen hood and mask be reprocessed per the guidelines?","options":["Autoclaved","Washed with soap and water, then stored dry","Soaked in phenol","Discarded after each use"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Oxygen hood is washed with soap and water and stored dry.  Why A wrong: Plastic hoods are heat-sensitive and not routinely autoclaved.  Why C wrong: Phenol soaking is not specified for oxygen hoods.  Why D wrong: The item is reusable after washing and drying, not single-use."},{"section":"Batch 052 · AIIMS Delhi infection-control manual guidelines for…","question":"Per AIIMS guidelines, needles and syringes should be:","options":["Reused after autoclaving","Single-use disposables","Cleaned with 70% alcohol and reused","Soaked in glutaraldehyde and reused"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Needles and syringes should be single-use disposables.  Why A/C/D wrong: To prevent bloodborne transmission, needles and syringes are never reprocessed for reuse; they are disposable."},{"section":"Batch 052 · AIIMS Delhi infection-control manual guidelines for…","question":"Body-piercing needles and neurologic test needles are ideally disposable. If reused, they must be sterilized by autoclaving after thorough cleaning; in certain exigencies they may be reprocessed with prior cleaning to remove organic material using which agent?","options":["70% isopropyl alcohol","2% glutaraldehyde","Plain detergent","5% phenol"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Body-piercing needles, if reused, are reprocessed with 2% glutaraldehyde after cleaning to remove organic material; a contact period of 6–8 hours is then needed, and items can be autoclaved.  Why A wrong: Alcohol is not adequate for sterilization of these penetrating items.  Why C wrong: Detergent only cleans.  Why D wrong: Phenol is not the specified high-level agent here."},{"section":"Batch 052 · AIIMS Delhi infection-control manual guidelines for…","question":"What is the required contact (immersion) period when reprocessing body-piercing needles in 2% glutaraldehyde?","options":["30 minutes","1–2 hours","6–8 hours","24 hours"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The guideline specifies a contact period of 6–8 hours for reprocessing body-piercing needles in 2% glutaraldehyde.  Why A wrong: 30 minutes is the high-level disinfection time for semi-critical items, not sterilization of penetrating items.  Why B/D wrong: The stated period is 6–8 hours, not 1–2 hours or 24 hours."},{"section":"Batch 052 · AIIMS Delhi infection-control manual guidelines for…","question":"How should probes of a pulse oximeter and a thermometer be cleaned per the manual?","options":["Cleaned with 70% isopropyl alcohol, dried, and disinfected with 70% isopropyl alcohol","Autoclaved daily","Soaked in glutaraldehyde for 30 min","Discarded after each patient"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Probes of pulse oximeter and thermometer should be cleaned and disinfected with 70% isopropyl alcohol.  Why B wrong: These electronic probes cannot be autoclaved.  Why C wrong: Glutaraldehyde immersion is for immersible instruments, not delicate electronic probes.  Why D wrong: These probes are reusable after alcohol disinfection, not single-use."},{"section":"Batch 052 · AIIMS Delhi infection-control manual guidelines for…","question":"Wall humidifiers and oxygen tubings should be:","options":["Decontaminated and disinfected every 24 hours and stored dry","Replaced monthly only","Cleaned only when visibly soiled","Stored wet to keep moist"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Wall humidifiers and oxygen tubings should be decontaminated and disinfected every 24 hours and stored dry (as for ventilator tubings).  Why B/C wrong: A fixed 24-hour decontamination cycle is specified, not monthly or only-when-soiled.  Why D wrong: They must be stored dry to prevent microbial growth in moisture."},{"section":"Batch 052 · AIIMS Delhi infection-control manual guidelines for…","question":"In the disinfectant solutions table, glutaraldehyde (Cidex) is used at what available and required concentration with its activator powder?","options":["1% available / 1% required, and 2.45% activated","5% available / 5% required","10% available / 1% required","Pure / 5%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For glutaraldehyde (Cidex), the table lists 2% available and 2% required (activator powder/liquid added to the 5-litre solution), with the activated solution at 2.45%.  Why B wrong: 5%/5% corresponds to Korsolex Rapid, not glutaraldehyde.  Why C wrong: 10%/1% corresponds to sodium hypochlorite solution.  Why D wrong: Pure/5% corresponds to phenol."},{"section":"Batch 052 · AIIMS Delhi infection-control manual guidelines for…","question":"What is the maximum in-use span of activated glutaraldehyde (Cidex) according to the disinfectant solutions table (per the AIIMS Nagpur 2020 reference)?","options":["7 days","14 days, and 28 days","24 hours","8 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table gives a maximum in-use span of 14 days (AIIMS Nagpur 2020) and 28 days for glutaraldehyde, and notes it may change if visibly contaminated.  Why A wrong: 7 days is the span for Korsolex Rapid.  Why C wrong: 24 hours is the span for chlorhexidine gluconate + cetrimide and for phenol.  Why D wrong: 8 hours is the span for sodium hypochlorite solution."},{"section":"Batch 052 · AIIMS Delhi infection-control manual guidelines for…","question":"How is Korsolex Rapid prepared, and what is its contact time and maximum in-use span?","options":["5 ml Korsolex Rapid + 95 ml water; 5 minutes contact; 7 days in-use span","Diluted 1:10; 30 minutes; 24 hours","Pure, undiluted; 20 minutes; 14 days","10 ml + 90 ml water; 60 minutes; 8 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists Korsolex Rapid (used pure as supplied, 5% concentration) prepared as 5 ml Korsolex Rapid + 95 ml water, 5 minutes contact time, 7 days max in-use span.  Why B/C/D wrong: These dilutions, contact times, and spans do not match the printed Korsolex Rapid row."},{"section":"Batch 052 · AIIMS Delhi infection-control manual guidelines for…","question":"Bacillocid (a benzalkonium-chloride/glutaraldehyde formulation with chemically bound formaldehyde) is used for quick disinfection of surface areas. What dilution gives the higher-strength preparation?","options":["10 ml Bacillocid + 980 ml water","5 ml Bacillocid + 995 ml water","50 ml Bacillocid + 950 ml water","100 ml Bacillocid + 900 ml water"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table shows Bacillocid prepared as 10 ml Bacillocid + 980 ml water (2%) for quick disinfection of surface areas, and 5 ml + 995 ml water (0.5%) as the lower-strength preparation.  Why B wrong: 5 ml + 995 ml is the lower-strength (0.5%) preparation.  Why C/D wrong: These dilutions are not listed for Bacillocid."},{"section":"Batch 052 · AIIMS Delhi infection-control manual guidelines for…","question":"A key remark for Bacillocid in the disinfectant solutions table is that it should:","options":["Be diluted only in saline","Not be mixed with other cleansing agents","Always be used pure","Be stored beyond 8 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The remarks column states Bacillocid should not be mixed with other cleansing agents.  Why A wrong: It is prepared in water, and the remark concerns mixing, not the diluent.  Why C wrong: It is diluted, not used pure.  Why D wrong: The \"beyond 8 hours\" caution applies to sodium hypochlorite, not Bacillocid."},{"section":"Batch 052 · AIIMS Delhi infection-control manual guidelines for…","question":"Chlorhexidine gluconate + cetrimide solution (Savlon) is prepared as 500 ml solution (17.5 ml pure Savlon + 482.5 ml saline/boiled water). According to the table, what is its primary use?","options":["For skin disinfection only","For instrument sterilization","For floor mopping only","For surface fogging"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The remark for chlorhexidine gluconate + cetrimide (Savlon) states \"Use only for skin disinfection,\" with a 24-hour in-use span.  Why B wrong: It is not an instrument sterilant.  Why C/D wrong: The table restricts it to skin disinfection, not floor mopping or fogging."},{"section":"Batch 052 · AIIMS Delhi infection-control manual guidelines for…","question":"Phenol (carbolic acid) supplied in 100% crystal form is prepared at 5% by warming the bottle in a hot water basin to liquify it, then mixing 5 ml phenol + 95 ml water. What is its contact time and remark?","options":["10–15 minutes contact; use freshly prepared solution (24-hour span)","30 minutes contact; reuse for 7 days","5 minutes contact; never reuse","60 minutes contact; store 28 days"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists phenol at 10–15 minutes contact time, 24-hour in-use span, with the remark to use freshly prepared solution.  Why B/C/D wrong: These contact times and spans do not match the printed phenol row."},{"section":"Batch 052 · AIIMS Delhi infection-control manual guidelines for…","question":"Sodium hypochlorite solution is supplied as 10%±1% and 5%±1%. To make a 1% working solution from the 10%±1% stock, the table specifies:","options":["10 ml sodium hypochlorite solution + 90 ml water","5 ml + 95 ml water","50 ml + 50 ml water","100 ml + 900 ml water"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table shows 10%±1% stock made into a 1% solution as 10 ml sodium hypochlorite + 90 ml water; the 5%±1% stock is made into 1% as 20 ml hypochlorite + 80 ml water.  Why B/C/D wrong: These ratios do not match the printed 10%±1% → 1% dilution."},{"section":"Batch 052 · AIIMS Delhi infection-control manual guidelines for…","question":"What is the contact time and maximum in-use span for the prepared sodium hypochlorite solution, and a key remark?","options":["20–30 minutes contact; 8 hours span; do not use re-constituted solution beyond 8 hours","5 minutes contact; 7 days span; reuse freely","10–15 minutes contact; 24 hours span; for skin only","30 minutes contact; 14 days span; do not mix with other agents"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table gives 20–30 minutes contact time, an 8-hour in-use span, and the remark \"Do not use re-constituted solution beyond 8 hours.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: These figures correspond to other disinfectants (Korsolex Rapid, Savlon/phenol, glutaraldehyde) respectively, not sodium hypochlorite."},{"section":"Batch 052 · AIIMS Delhi infection-control manual guidelines for…","question":"Bacciashield is used pure (10%) for disinfection of items/equipment surfaces and at 20% for fogging. To prepare the 10% solution, the table specifies:","options":["100 ml Bacciashield in 900 ml water","200 ml Bacciashield in 800 ml water","50 ml in 950 ml water","500 ml in 500 ml water"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The 10% preparation is 100 ml Bacciashield in 900 ml water; the 20% fogging preparation is 200 ml Bacciashield in 800 ml water.  Why B wrong: 200 ml in 800 ml water is the 20% fogging solution.  Why C/D wrong: These ratios are not listed for Bacciashield."},{"section":"Batch 052 · AIIMS Delhi infection-control manual guidelines for…","question":"When using Bacciashield for fogging, the table notes the fogging time is 1 hour (half an hour for running the machine and half an hour to keep the door closed). This is best classified as a:","options":["Surface contact-time requirement for proper fogging disinfection","Sterilization autoclave cycle","Skin antisepsis duration","Instrument soak time"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The remark specifies a 1-hour fogging time (½ hr running the machine + ½ hr door kept closed) to achieve disinfection of the area.  Why B/C/D wrong: Fogging is area/surface disinfection by aerosol, not autoclaving, skin antisepsis, or instrument soaking."},{"section":"Batch 052 · AIIMS Delhi infection-control manual guidelines for…","question":"What is the key precaution noted for Bacciol Spray in the disinfectant solutions table?","options":["Use it as a surface cleaner only","Do not use it as a surface cleaner","Always dilute it 1:10","Store it for 28 days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The remark for Bacciol Spray (used as available, undiluted) states \"Do not use it as a surface cleaner.\"  Why A wrong: This is the opposite of the printed precaution.  Why C wrong: It is used as available, not diluted 1:10.  Why D wrong: No 28-day storage is listed for Bacciol Spray."},{"section":"Batch 052 · AIIMS Delhi infection-control manual guidelines for…","question":"Resuscitation equipment (laryngoscope blade, AMBU bag, mask, ET stylet) should be clean with water and 70% isopropyl alcohol (detergent) to remove blood, then dried and disinfected with Bacillol 25 or Spirit. How often, at minimum, should it be disinfected?","options":["After each use, and at least every day before 10 a.m.","Once weekly","Once monthly","Only when visibly soiled"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The schedule for resuscitation equipment is \"after each use,\" and if not used for any patient, every day before 10 a.m.  Why B/C/D wrong: The guideline mandates after-each-use plus a daily check before 10 a.m., not weekly, monthly, or only-when-soiled."},{"section":"Batch 052 · AIIMS Delhi infection-control manual guidelines for…","question":"A laryngoscope blade used for a patient must be cleaned, dried, and disinfected; per the schedule the blade is also stored in a sterile/disinfectant solution for how long?","options":["5 minutes","10 minutes","30 minutes","60 minutes"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: After disinfection, the laryngoscope blade and ET stylet are stored in a sterile/disinfectant solution for 30 min; after disinfection time, the bag and other accessories should be thoroughly rinsed with sterile water and dried, then stored dry in a sterile wrapper.  Why A/B/D wrong: The printed soak duration is 30 minutes, not 5, 10, or 60 minutes."},{"section":"Batch 052 · AIIMS Delhi infection-control manual guidelines for…","question":"According to the disinfection schedule table, how often should Cheatle forceps be cleaned/changed?","options":["Once in 24 hours — every day before 10 a.m., and whenever contaminated","Once every 72 hours","Once weekly","Only when visibly soiled"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cheatle forceps schedule is \"once in 24 hours, every day before 10 a.m.\" and whenever contaminated; the bottle should be labelled with date and time.  Why B wrong: 72 hours applies to the sterile bottle replacement on p.414, but the daily schedule on p.416 is once in 24 hours before 10 a.m.  Why C/D wrong: The schedule is once in 24 hours, not weekly or only-when-soiled."},{"section":"Batch 052 · AIIMS Delhi infection-control manual guidelines for…","question":"Per the disinfection schedule, a thermometer should be cleaned with water and disinfected with spirit-soaked cotton after each use, and the spirit/disinfectant in the container/thermometer should be changed how often?","options":["After each use only","After every shift","Every 24 hours (changed daily), and the cotton stored in spirit-soaked cotton","Once a week"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The thermometer schedule is \"after each use\" plus the spirit/disinfectant in the container is changed every 24 hours; store thermometer in spirit-soaked cotton.  Why A wrong: After-each-use cleaning is required, but the disinfectant container is additionally changed every 24 hours.  Why B/D wrong: The change interval is 24 hours, not per shift or weekly."},{"section":"Batch 052 · AIIMS Delhi infection-control manual guidelines for…","question":"Reusable ventilator circuits/humidifiers, if soiled or mechanically malfunctioning, are changed immediately; otherwise they are changed at what routine interval whether or not in use?","options":["Every 24 hours (1 day)","Every 72 hours (3 days)","Every 7 days","Every 14 days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Ventilator circuits/humidifiers are changed after each patient use/discharge/death, immediately if visibly soiled or malfunctioning, and every 72 hours (3 days) whether being used or not.  Why A/C/D wrong: The routine change interval printed is every 72 hours, not 24 hours, 7 days, or 14 days."},{"section":"Batch 052 · AIIMS Delhi infection-control manual guidelines for…","question":"A critical \"alert/remark\" for ventilator humidifiers states that if the water level falls below the marked level, you should:","options":["Top up the water in the humidifier","Empty the humidifier, refill it with fresh sterile water, and DO NOT top up","Add tap water to the existing water","Continue using until empty"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The alert states that if water level falls below the marked level, empty the humidifier and refill with fresh sterile water — DO NOT TOP UP.  Why A/C/D wrong: Topping up or adding tap water risks contamination and biofilm growth; the rule is empty and refill with sterile water."},{"section":"Batch 052 · AIIMS Delhi infection-control manual guidelines for…","question":"Sterile steel drums/sterile sets have a shelf life of how long, and how often is partially-used or unused stock reprocessed?","options":["Shelf life 72 hours; every 72 hours (3 days) whether used partially or unused","Shelf life 24 hours; every 24 hours","Shelf life 7 days; weekly","Shelf life 14 days; fortnightly"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Sterile steel drums/sets have a 72-hour shelf life and are reprocessed every 72 hours (3 days) whether used partially or unused; clean the instruments with water and detergents, dry in a clean area, then pack and autoclave.  Why B/C/D wrong: The printed shelf life and reprocessing cycle are 72 hours/3 days."},{"section":"Batch 052 · AIIMS Delhi infection-control manual guidelines for…","question":"Small steel trays used for carrying injections should be cleaned/disinfected on what schedule?","options":["Every 24 hours, if unused; disinfected after each schedule and if visibly soiled; cleaned with water and detergent","Once weekly only","Only after visible blood spills","Every 72 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Small steel trays for injections are cleaned every 24 hours if unused, disinfected after each schedule and if visibly soiled, and washed with water and detergent then disinfected with Korsolex/Cidex solution for 30 minutes.  Why B/C/D wrong: The schedule is every 24 hours plus after each schedule/when soiled, not weekly, only after spills, or every 72 hours."},{"section":"Batch 052 · AIIMS Delhi infection-control manual guidelines for…","question":"A needle destroyer / puncture-proof container with biohazard label should be cleaned/changed when visibly soiled and, if used to its full puncture capacity, must not be held for more than:","options":["8 hours","24 hours","48 hours","72 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The schedule states the needle destroyer/puncture-proof container is visibly soiled-cleaned every day before 10 a.m. (24 hours); do not hold the container/needle in puncture-proof container for more than 24 hours; change every 24 hours; make sure the puncture-proof container has a biohazard label.  Why A/C/D wrong: The maximum holding period printed is 24 hours."},{"section":"Batch 052 · AIIMS Delhi infection-control manual guidelines for…","question":"Cleaning of surface areas (general wards, nursing counter, treatment room, over-bed lockers, ICU/HDU/OT walls, counters, bed-rails, lockers, over-head table, equipment, door knobs, etc.) should be done how often, and with what agent?","options":["Every day before 10 a.m. and when visibly soiled, using a wet mop with surface disinfectant (Bacillocid, carbolic acid)","Once a week with plain water","Only when visibly soiled, with alcohol","Every 72 hours with glutaraldehyde"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Surface areas are cleaned every day before 10 a.m. and when visibly soiled, using a wet mop with surface disinfectant (Bacillocid, carbolic acid); always use freshly prepared solution and the required amount, and discard the solution after use.  Why B/C/D wrong: The schedule is daily before 10 a.m. plus when-soiled with surface disinfectant, not weekly water, alcohol-only, or 72-hour glutaraldehyde."},{"section":"Batch 052 · AIIMS Delhi infection-control manual guidelines for…","question":"In the reprocessing classification of hospital equipment, items such as bedpans, IV poles, urinals, commodes, stethoscopes, BP cuffs, eye specula, and hemodialysis equipment surfaces (in contact with intact skin) belong to which category and are reprocessed by which method?","options":["Critical items — sterilization","Semi-critical items — high-level disinfection","Non-critical / some semi-critical items — cleaning followed by low-level disinfection","Disposable items — discarded"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Items touching intact skin (bedpans, IV poles, urinals, commodes, stethoscopes, BP cuffs, dialysis surfaces) are non-critical (some semi-critical) and are reprocessed by cleaning followed by low-level/intermediate-level disinfection.  Why A wrong: Sterilization is reserved for critical items entering sterile tissue/bloodstream.  Why B wrong: High-level disinfection is for semi-critical items contacting mucous membranes.  Why D wrong: These are reusable, not disposable."},{"section":"Batch 052 · AIIMS Delhi infection-control manual guidelines for…","question":"Per the equipment reprocessing table, \"critical items\" are those that come into contact with sterile body tissues (surgical instruments, implantable devices, hemodialyzers, plasmapheresis & heart-lung oxygenator surfaces, scopes entering sterile cavities, biopsy needles). They must be reprocessed by:","options":["Cleaning followed by sterilization","Low-level disinfection","Wiping with detergent","Air drying only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Critical items contacting sterile tissue/bloodstream must be reprocessed by cleaning followed by sterilization (e.g., steam under pressure, dry heat, ethylene oxide [ETO], 2% glutaraldehyde immersion, plasma sterilization with hydrogen peroxide).  Why B/C/D wrong: Low-level disinfection, detergent wiping, or air drying are insufficient for items entering sterile body sites."},{"section":"Batch 052 · AIIMS Delhi infection-control manual guidelines for…","question":"Semi-critical items (flexible endoscopes, respiratory-therapy equipment, nebulizer cups, anesthesia equipment, nasal specula, tonometer foot plate, ear syringe nozzle, vaginal specula, vaginal probes used in sonographic scanning, breast pump accessories) that contact mucous membranes are reprocessed by:","options":["Cleaning followed by high-level disinfection","Sterilization only","Cleaning followed by low-level disinfection","Disposal after each use"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Semi-critical items contacting mucous membranes are reprocessed by cleaning followed by high-level disinfection.  Why B wrong: Sterilization is the standard for critical items.  Why C wrong: Low-level disinfection is for non-critical, intact-skin items.  Why D wrong: These items are reusable after high-level disinfection."},{"section":"Batch 052 · AIIMS Delhi infection-control manual guidelines for…","question":"Per the reprocessing table, 2% glutaraldehyde is described as the most commonly used high-level disinfectant. For immersible equipment, all surfaces should be allowed to be in contact with 2% glutaraldehyde for at least:","options":["5 minutes","10 minutes","20 minutes","60 minutes"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table states all immersible internal and external surfaces of equipment should be in contact with 2% glutaraldehyde for at least 20 minutes.  Why A/B/D wrong: The printed minimum high-level disinfection contact time is 20 minutes."},{"section":"Batch 052 · AIIMS Delhi infection-control manual guidelines for…","question":"For critical-item sterilization in the reprocessing table, which of the following methods are listed? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Steam under pressure (autoclaving)","Dry heat","Ethylene oxide (ETO)","Plasma sterilization with hydrogen peroxide","Simple soap-and-water washing"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why A, B, C, D correct: The table lists steam under pressure, dry heat, ETO, and plasma sterilization with hydrogen peroxide as sterilization methods for critical items.  Why E wrong: Soap-and-water washing is cleaning only and does not achieve sterilization."},{"section":"Batch 052 · AIIMS Delhi infection-control manual guidelines for…","question":"When preparing sodium hypochlorite solution, what is the key procedure rule about freshness?","options":["A fresh solution should be prepared every time","The same solution can be reused for one week","Solution can be stored for 28 days","Solution should be prepared once monthly"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The procedure states a fresh solution should be prepared every time.  Why B/C/D wrong: Hypochlorite is unstable; it must be freshly prepared each time, not reused for a week, stored 28 days, or made monthly."},{"section":"Batch 052 · AIIMS Delhi infection-control manual guidelines for…","question":"After preparing sodium hypochlorite solution, the unused solution must be discarded after how many hours, and disposed of how?","options":["After 8 hours; discarded in the drain and the drain flushed with running water to prevent corrosion of pipes","After 24 hours; poured into general waste","After 72 hours; stored in a sealed container","After 1 hour; incinerated"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The unused solution must be discarded after 8 hours of preparation, in the drain, flushing the drain with running water to prevent corrosion of pipes; the date and time of preparation should be mentioned on the container.  Why B/C/D wrong: The printed discard time is 8 hours with drain flushing, not 24/72 hours or 1-hour incineration."},{"section":"Batch 052 · AIIMS Delhi infection-control manual guidelines for…","question":"Using commercially available 5% sodium hypochlorite solution, to prepare 1000 ml of a 1% working solution, how much stock and water are needed?","options":["200 ml solution + 800 ml water","100 ml solution + 900 ml water","20 ml solution + 980 ml water","500 ml solution + 500 ml water"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: From the 5% stock, the table gives: 1% → 200 ml solution + 800 ml water; 0.5% → 100 ml + 900 ml water; 0.1% → 20 ml + 980 ml water (all to make 1000 ml).  Why B wrong: 100 ml + 900 ml water gives 0.5%.  Why C wrong: 20 ml + 980 ml water gives 0.1%.  Why D wrong: Not listed in the table."},{"section":"Batch 052 · AIIMS Delhi infection-control manual guidelines for…","question":"Using 5% commercially available hypochlorite stock, what volumes prepare 1000 ml of a 0.1% solution?","options":["20 ml solution + 980 ml water","100 ml solution + 900 ml water","200 ml solution + 800 ml water","50 ml solution + 950 ml water"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For 0.1%, the table specifies 20 ml solution + 980 ml water to make 1000 ml.  Why B wrong: 100 ml + 900 ml gives 0.5%.  Why C wrong: 200 ml + 800 ml gives 1%.  Why D wrong: Not listed."},{"section":"Batch 052 · AIIMS Delhi infection-control manual guidelines for…","question":"(RECALL THE NORCET — NORCET-2021) A bronchoscope is disinfected using which disinfectant?","options":["2% glutaraldehyde (Cidex)","Ethyl alcohol","Sodium hypochlorite","Isopropylene alcohol"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A bronchoscope (semi-critical flexible endoscope) requires high-level disinfection with 2% glutaraldehyde (Cidex), the most commonly used high-level disinfectant; the source keys answer A.  Why B wrong: Ethyl alcohol is an intermediate-level disinfectant, inadequate as the standard endoscope high-level disinfectant.  Why C wrong: Sodium hypochlorite is corrosive to endoscopes and used for surfaces/linen, not bronchoscope reprocessing.  Why D wrong: Isopropyl alcohol is intermediate-level, not the standard for flexible endoscopes."},{"section":"Batch 052 · AIIMS Delhi infection-control manual guidelines for…","question":"(RECALL THE NORCET — NORCET-2021/2022) Which solution is used in COVID areas for disinfection, and which disinfectant solution can be used to clean COVID-19 patient linen?","options":["1% sodium hypochlorite","Glutaraldehyde","Phenyl","Ethyl alcohol"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: 1% sodium hypochlorite is the recommended disinfectant for COVID-19 areas and for cleaning COVID-19 patient linen; the source keys answer A.  Why B wrong: Glutaraldehyde is for instrument high-level disinfection, not linen/surface COVID disinfection.  Why C wrong: Phenyl is a general floor cleaner, not the keyed COVID disinfectant.  Why D wrong: Ethyl alcohol is used for small surfaces, not linen disinfection in COVID areas."},{"section":"Batch 052 · AIIMS Delhi infection-control manual guidelines for…","question":"(RECALL THE NORCET — NORCET-4 2023) Which of the following is TRUE about disinfection in a surgical unit using glutaraldehyde?","options":["Once-prepared glutaraldehyde can be used for a maximum duration of 7 days","2% glutaraldehyde is used commonly","Dilute glutaraldehyde with water in 1:5 ratio before every use","Minimum contact period for disinfection is 20 minutes"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The minimum contact period for high-level disinfection with glutaraldehyde is 20 minutes (consistent with the reprocessing table on p.417); the source keys answer D.  Why A wrong: Activated 2% glutaraldehyde has a reuse life of about 14–28 days, not exactly 7 days.  Why B wrong: While 2% glutaraldehyde is indeed common, the question asks the statement keyed as TRUE about disinfection conditions; the keyed answer is the 20-minute contact period.  Why C wrong: Glutaraldehyde (Cidex) is activated with an activator powder, not routinely diluted 1:5 with water before each use."},{"section":"Batch 052 · AIIMS Delhi infection-control manual guidelines for…","question":"An operating theatre (OT), also known as an operating room or operation suite, is best defined as:","options":["A general ward for postoperative recovery","A facility within a hospital where surgical operations are carried out in an aseptic environment","A laboratory for diagnostic testing","A room used only for storing anesthetic gases"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: An OT is a facility within a hospital where surgical operations are carried out in an aseptic environment.  Why A wrong: Postoperative recovery occurs in a recovery room/ward, not the OT itself.  Why C wrong: An OT is for surgery, not diagnostic lab testing.  Why D wrong: Storage of gases is not the defining purpose of an OT."},{"section":"Batch 052 · AIIMS Delhi infection-control manual guidelines for…","question":"Operating rooms are supplied with which utilities at the wall?","options":["Suction, oxygen, and possibly other anesthetic gases","Compressed air only","Potable water only","Electrical outlets only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Operating rooms are supplied with wall suction, oxygen, and possibly other anesthetic gases.  Why B/C/D wrong: While power and gases are present, the source specifies wall suction, oxygen, and other anesthetic gases as the supplied utilities."},{"section":"Batch 052 · AIIMS Delhi infection-control manual guidelines for…","question":"The operating table, located at the center of the OT, can be adjusted in which ways?","options":["Raised, lowered, and tilted in any direction","Only raised and lowered","Only tilted side to side","It is fixed and cannot be moved"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The operating table is at the center of the room and can be raised, lowered, and tilted in any direction.  Why B/C/D wrong: The table has full multi-axis adjustability, not limited or fixed positioning."},{"section":"Batch 052 · AIIMS Delhi infection-control manual guidelines for…","question":"What is the purpose of operating room (OT) lights positioned over the table?","options":["To decorate the room","To provide bright light without shadows during surgery","To sterilize the air","To monitor patient vitals"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: OT lights over the table provide bright light without shadows during surgery.  Why A wrong: They are functional, not decorative.  Why C wrong: Air sterilization is achieved by ventilation/HEPA systems, not surgical lights.  Why D wrong: Vitals are monitored by the anesthetic/cardiac monitor, not lights."},{"section":"Batch 052 · AIIMS Delhi infection-control manual guidelines for…","question":"The anaesthesia machine in the OT is also known by which name and is positioned where?","options":["Known as Boyle's machine, placed at the head of the operating table","Known as the autoclave, placed at the foot of the table","Known as the diathermy unit, placed beside the door","Known as the perfusion pump, placed under the table"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The anaesthesia machine is known as Boyle's machine and is placed at the head of the operating table; it consists of monitors and various tubes that help in breathing of the patient.  Why B/C/D wrong: It is Boyle's machine at the head of the table, not an autoclave, diathermy unit, or perfusion pump in other locations."},{"section":"Batch 052 · AIIMS Delhi infection-control manual guidelines for…","question":"The anaesthesia cart next to the anaesthesia machine contains which of the following?","options":["Medications, equipment, and other supplies that are needed","Surgical drapes only","Sterile drums only","Cleaning chemicals only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The anaesthesia cart contains medications, equipment, and other supplies that are needed.  Why B/C/D wrong: The cart holds anesthesia drugs/equipment/supplies, not exclusively drapes, sterile drums, or cleaning chemicals."},{"section":"Batch 052 · AIIMS Delhi infection-control manual guidelines for…","question":"In the OT, sterile instruments are arranged on what surface, and how should they be handled before surgery?","options":["To be used during surgery and arranged on a stainless steel sterile table","Placed loosely on the floor","Left in the autoclave until the surgeon arrives","Stored in the anaesthesia cart"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Sterile instruments are to be used during surgery and are arranged on a stainless steel sterile table.  Why B/C/D wrong: They are arranged on a dedicated stainless steel sterile table, not the floor, autoclave, or anaesthesia cart."},{"section":"Batch 052 · AIIMS Delhi infection-control manual guidelines for…","question":"What does the electronic monitor in the OT record?","options":["Vitals of the patient, e.g., BP and heart rate","Room temperature only","Gas flow rates only","Surgical instrument counts"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The electronic monitor records vitals of the patient, e.g., BP and heart rate.  Why B/C/D wrong: It monitors patient vital signs, not solely room temperature, gas flow, or instrument counts."},{"section":"Batch 052 · AIIMS Delhi infection-control manual guidelines for…","question":"What is the function of the pulse oximeter attached to the patient's finger in the OT?","options":["Measures oxygen saturation","Measures blood glucose","Records ECG","Measures core temperature"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The pulse oximeter attaches to the patient's finger to measure oxygen saturation.  Why B/C/D wrong: It measures SpO₂, not glucose, ECG, or temperature."},{"section":"Batch 052 · AIIMS Delhi infection-control manual guidelines for…","question":"The automated BP measuring machine in the OT measures blood pressure automatically by what mechanism?","options":["By inflating a cuff","By an arterial puncture","By ultrasound only","By a finger sensor"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The automated BP measuring machine measures BP automatically by inflating a cuff.  Why B wrong: Non-invasive automated BP uses a cuff, not arterial puncture.  Why C/D wrong: It uses an inflating cuff, not ultrasound or a finger sensor."},{"section":"Batch 052 · AIIMS Delhi infection-control manual guidelines for…","question":"What is the function of an electrocautery machine in the OT?","options":["To cauterize or seal off blood vessels using high-frequency electrical signals, helping minimize bleeding","To deliver anesthetic gases","To monitor patient vitals","To sterilize instruments"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: An electrocautery machine cauterizes or seals off blood vessels by use of high-frequency electrical signals and may also be used to cut through tissue with a minimal amount of bleeding.  Why B/C/D wrong: Anesthesia delivery, monitoring, and sterilization are separate equipment functions."},{"section":"Batch 052 · AIIMS Delhi infection-control manual guidelines for…","question":"When is a heart-lung machine typically required in the OT?","options":["In major surgery, e.g., heart transplantation","In all minor day-care procedures","During routine endoscopy","For wound dressing"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The heart-lung machine is sometimes needed in major surgery, e.g., heart transplantation.  Why B/C/D wrong: It is reserved for major cardiac/transplant surgery, not minor procedures, endoscopy, or dressings."},{"section":"Batch 052 · AIIMS Delhi infection-control manual guidelines for…","question":"Regarding microbiological surveillance of critical areas (environmental sampling) in the OT, which statement is TRUE?","options":["Routine environmental sampling should be performed in any hospital location, regardless of need","Do NOT perform routine environmental sampling in any hospital location except where there is a need","Surface sampling should be done daily in every ward","Environmental sampling replaces the need for proper cleaning protocols"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The guideline states do NOT perform routine environmental sampling in any hospital location except where need exists; routine environmental surface sampling (swab) should not be done in areas like the ICU or wards.  Why A wrong: This contradicts the \"do not perform routine sampling\" rule.  Why C wrong: Daily surface sampling in every ward is the opposite of the recommendation.  Why D wrong: Sampling does not replace cleaning/disinfection protocols."},{"section":"Batch 052 · AIIMS Delhi infection-control manual guidelines for…","question":"The purposes of microbiological surveillance of critical areas (environmental sampling) include which of the following? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Monitoring the effectiveness of the cleaning and disinfection procedures in the OT for quality assurance","Evaluation of efficiency of an OT ventilation system (HEPA)","For epidemiological investigation of an outbreak","Monitoring the quality of water for drinking, cleaning, surgical scrub, and after flooding","Replacing the need for terminal cleaning of the OT"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why A, B, C, D correct: The listed purposes are monitoring effectiveness of cleaning/disinfection for quality assurance, evaluating efficiency of the OT ventilation system (HEPA), epidemiological investigation of an outbreak, and monitoring water quality for drinking/cleaning/surgical scrub and after flooding.  Why E wrong: Surveillance supports — it does not replace — terminal cleaning of the OT."},{"section":"Batch 052 · AIIMS Delhi infection-control manual guidelines for…","question":"A patient's flexible cystoscope (a flexible endoscope contacting mucous membranes) needs reprocessing. Based on the reprocessing classification, the most appropriate level of reprocessing is:","options":["Low-level disinfection","High-level disinfection","Sterilization in an autoclave","Simple detergent wiping"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A flexible cystoscope is a semi-critical item contacting mucous membranes and requires cleaning followed by high-level disinfection (e.g., 2% glutaraldehyde for at least 20 minutes).  Why A wrong: Low-level disinfection is only for non-critical, intact-skin items.  Why C wrong: Heat-sensitive flexible endoscopes are not routinely autoclaved.  Why D wrong: Detergent wiping is cleaning only."},{"section":"Batch 052 · AIIMS Delhi infection-control manual guidelines for…","question":"A nurse finds a ventilator humidifier with water below the marked level. According to the alert, the priority action is to:","options":["Add sterile water on top of the existing water to reach the mark","Empty the humidifier completely and refill with fresh sterile water — do not top up","Continue use and document the low level","Replace the entire ventilator circuit immediately"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The humidifier alert directs that when the water level falls below the marked level, empty it and refill with fresh sterile water, and DO NOT TOP UP — preventing colonization of stagnant water.  Why A wrong: Topping up violates the explicit \"do not top up\" rule and risks biofilm growth.  Why C wrong: Running below the mark with inadequate humidification is not the directed action.  Why D wrong: Changing the whole circuit is required at 72-hour intervals or for soiling/malfunction, not specifically for a low water level."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"When should routine environmental surface monitoring and air sampling of the OT be done?","options":["Daily before each surgery","Routinely, but it is NOT recommended","Only after a confirmed infection outbreak","Once every five years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states routine environmental surface and air sampling should be done routinely, but such routine monitoring is NOT recommended (it is reserved for specific situations).  Why A wrong: Daily monitoring before each surgery is not advised.  Why C wrong: Outbreak investigation is a separate indication, not the description of routine monitoring policy.  Why D wrong: No five-year interval is specified for surface/air monitoring."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"When are surface swabs of the OT best obtained for microbiological culture testing?","options":["In the evening after cleaning","In the morning before any cleaning procedure","Immediately after a surgery ends","Only during an active surgery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Surface swabs should be obtained from each OT for microbiological culture testing in the morning before any cleaning procedure, to reflect the true baseline contamination.  Why A wrong: Post-cleaning evening sampling would underestimate contamination.  Why C wrong: Sampling immediately after surgery is not the standard timing.  Why D wrong: Swabs are not taken during an active surgery."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"After collecting an OT swab sample, when should the OT be opened for cleaning?","options":["The OT can be cleaned immediately while sampling continues","Only after the micro-organism is opened in the morning before any cleaning procedure (sampling must be done before cleaning starts)","After 24 hours of waiting","Cleaning is never required after sampling"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Sampling should be done when the OT is opened in the morning before any cleaning procedure, so the OT is opened/sampled before cleaning begins.  Why A wrong: Cleaning before/during sampling would invalidate the swab result.  Why C wrong: No mandatory 24-hour wait applies here.  Why D wrong: Cleaning is still performed after sampling."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"For how long, and at what temperature, should swabs and media be kept outside the refrigerator before being labelled and transported with the sample?","options":["At least 30 minutes at room temperature with proper labelling","At least 5 minutes at 0°C","At least 2 hours frozen","They must remain refrigerated at all times"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The swabs and media should be kept outside the refrigerator for at least 30 minutes with proper labelling, with simple site name and the date/sample etc.  Why B wrong: Five minutes at 0°C is not the stated interval/condition.  Why C wrong: Two hours frozen is incorrect.  Why D wrong: They are deliberately removed from the refrigerator before transport."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"Which technique should be used to collect OT samples, and where should the collected samples be stored before transport to the lab?","options":["Sterile technique; in the refrigerator","Clean (non-sterile) technique; at room temperature","Aseptic technique using sterile swabs; samples collected in the refrigerator","Bare-hand collection; in a freezer"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Samples must be collected using aseptic technique, and collected samples are kept in the refrigerator, then transported to the laboratory immediately after collection.  Why A/B wrong: They mismatch the technique with the wrong storage or technique combination.  Why D wrong: Bare-hand collection violates asepsis."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"What should be maintained to ensure proper documentation of OT swab samples?","options":["A verbal log only","A record of the sample sites","No record is necessary","Only photographic evidence"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source directs that a record of the sample sites be maintained.  Why A/C/D wrong: A written record of sample sites is required; verbal-only, no record, or photos-only are not the specified practice."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"If an OT swab result is unacceptable, what should be done with elective surgeries?","options":["Continue elective cases as scheduled","Postpone elective cases; repeat cleaning, disinfection and OT swabs","Cancel all surgeries permanently","Ignore the result and reschedule next year"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If unacceptable results occur, postpone elective cases. Repeat cleaning, disinfection and OT swabs. The procedure should be supervised by the OT in-charge.  Why A wrong: Proceeding with elective cases risks infection.  Why C wrong: Cancellation is temporary (postponement), not permanent.  Why D wrong: Action is immediate, not deferred a year."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"How frequently should OT air sampling be done?","options":["Daily","Once a month","At least once a week","Once a year"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Air sampling should be done regularly, once a week, to monitor the efficacy of the system.  Why A/B/D wrong: These intervals do not match the stated weekly schedule."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"Air sampling in an OT without a ventilation system should be done how, given there is no source of SSI (surgical site infection)?","options":["Once a month and whenever air is suspected as a source of SSI","Continuously for 24 hours","Never","Only after a power failure"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In OTs without a ventilation system it should be done once a month, and whenever air is suspected as a source of SSI.  Why B wrong: Continuous 24-hour sampling is not specified.  Why C wrong: Sampling is still required monthly/when suspected.  Why D wrong: Power failure is not the stated trigger."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"Which method is used as the procedure for OT air sampling?","options":["Membrane filtration","Settle plate method","Impinger method only","Swab rolling method"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The procedure for air sampling is done by the settle plate method.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the method named for OT air sampling in the source."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"In the settle plate air-sampling procedure, where and when is a culture-media plate first placed?","options":["On an empty OT table immediately after opening the OT in the morning","On the floor during an active surgery","Outside the OT entrance overnight","On the surgeon's gown mid-surgery"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A culture-media plate is placed on an empty OT table immediately after opening the OT in the morning.  Why B/C/D wrong: These placements contradict the described first-plate location and timing."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"In the settle plate procedure, how long is the swab/plate left before taking swabs samples?","options":["5 minutes","15 minutes","30 minutes","2 hours"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: One plate is left open in the same time, then air sampling should be done before taking swab samples, with the plate left open for at least 30 minutes with the OT closed and empty before sampling.  Why A/B/D wrong: They do not match the stated 30-minute exposure."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"What is the PURPOSE of maintaining positive pressure in the OT and ICU?","options":["To increase humidity for patient comfort","To reduce the chances of spread of infection in ICUs and OTs","To cool the surgical instruments","To reduce electricity consumption"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Positive pressure is maintained to reduce the chances of spread of infection in ICUs and OTs.  Why A/C/D wrong: Positive pressure is an infection-control measure, not for comfort, cooling instruments, or saving power."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"In positive-pressure areas of the OT/ICU, what is the relationship of air pressure within the room to the surrounding environment?","options":["Pressure inside is lower than the surrounding environment","Pressure within is higher than the surrounding environment","Pressure is equal inside and outside","Pressure fluctuates randomly"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Positive pressure means pressure is maintained higher within OT and ICUs than the surrounding environment.  Why A wrong: Lower pressure describes negative-pressure (isolation) rooms, not positive pressure.  Why C/D wrong: Positive pressure is intentionally and consistently higher, not equal or random."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"In positive-pressure areas, which type of filter is used because the high risk areas need not only filtered air but also non-contaminating air when doors are opened?","options":["Pre-filter only","HEPA filters","Carbon filters","Mesh strainers"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Positive-pressure areas use HEPA filters, so that the air going into the OT is not getting contaminated with infectious particles when the doors are opened.  Why A/C/D wrong: Pre-filters, carbon filters, or mesh strainers do not provide the HEPA-grade filtration specified for these high-risk areas."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"For an operating room, which is a critical parameter for air quality?","options":["Infrequent maintenance of efficacy of filters","Frequent maintenance of efficacy of filters","Negative pressure across the filter bed","Unfiltered outdoor air during surgery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A listed critical air-quality parameter is frequent maintenance of efficacy of filters.  Why A wrong: Maintenance must be frequent, not infrequent.  Why C wrong: Pressure across the filter bed must be positive in the OT, not negative.  Why D wrong: Air must be filtered, not raw outdoor air."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"Within what range should air changes per hour be maintained in the operating theatre for air quality?","options":["Between 5–10 changes per hour","Between 15–20 air changes per hour","Between 1–2 changes per hour","Between 50–60 changes per hour"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A critical air-quality parameter is that air changes should be maintained between 15–20 air changes per hour.  Why A/C/D wrong: These values fall outside the specified 15–20 ACH range."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"Within what range should temperature be maintained in the OT as a critical air-quality parameter?","options":["Between 10°C to 15°C","Between 20°C to 22°C","Between 25°C to 30°C","Between 30°C to 35°C"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Temperature should be maintained between 20°C to 22°C.  Why A/C/D wrong: These ranges do not match the specified 20–22°C."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"Within what range should humidity be maintained in the OT to inhibit bacterial multiplication?","options":["30–40%","30–60%","60–80%","80–90%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Humidity should be maintained between 30–60% to inhibit bacterial multiplication.  Why A/C/D wrong: These ranges do not match the specified 30–60%."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"Regarding general areas (non-critical) in the facility, how should they be ventilated?","options":["They should be sealed with no ventilation","They should be well ventilated if they are not air-conditioned","They should always have negative pressure","They require HEPA filtration like the OT"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: General areas should be well ventilated if they are not air conditioned.  Why A wrong: Sealing without ventilation is incorrect.  Why C wrong: General areas do not require negative pressure.  Why D wrong: HEPA filtration is reserved for critical positive-pressure areas."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"Per the suggested actions table for OT swab culture, what is the remark and action when \"no organisms grown\"?","options":["Unacceptable — re-clean and re-fog","Acceptable — use the OT","Unacceptable — re-fog and repeat swab samples","Acceptable — do not use the OT"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: No organisms grown is Acceptable, and the action is to use the OT.  Why A/C wrong: No growth is acceptable, so re-cleaning/re-fogging is not required.  Why D wrong: With no growth, the OT may be used."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"Per the OT swab culture table, when skin commensals (e.g. *S. epidermidis*, sparse growth in any 1–2 swabs from the sample well) are grown, what is the remark and action?","options":["Unacceptable — re-clean and re-fog","Acceptable — use the OT (unless the lab reports heavy growth or growth from multiple swabs)","Unacceptable — do not use the OT","Acceptable — do not use the OT"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Sparse skin-commensal growth in 1–2 swabs is Acceptable; use the OT, unless the lab reports heavy growth or growth from multiple swabs.  Why A/C wrong: Sparse commensal growth is acceptable.  Why D wrong: The action is to use the OT (with the caveat noted), not to avoid it."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"Per the OT swab culture table, when a known pathogen (5 pseudo / e.g. *Staphylococcus aureus*, aerobic gram-negative bacilli or fungi) is grown, what is the remark and action?","options":["Acceptable — use the OT","Unacceptable — do not use the OT; re-clean, re-fog and repeat swab samples","Acceptable — re-fog only","Unacceptable — use the OT after antibiotics"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A known pathogen grown is Unacceptable; do not use the OT — re-clean, re-fog and repeat swab samples.  Why A/C wrong: Pathogen growth is unacceptable, so the OT cannot be used and re-cleaning is required, not just re-fogging.  Why D wrong: Antibiotics do not decontaminate the OT environment; re-cleaning/re-fogging is required."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"Per the OT swab culture table, what is the remark and action when gram-negative organisms (aerobic/anaerobic) or anaerobic/aerobic spore-bearing organisms, or mixed growth are found?","options":["Acceptable — use the OT","Unacceptable — do not use the OT; re-clean, re-fog and repeat swab samples","Acceptable — re-clean only","Unacceptable — use the OT after re-fogging"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Gram-negative organisms, spore-bearing organisms, and mixed growth are all Unacceptable; do not use the OT — re-clean, re-fog and repeat swab samples.  Why A/D wrong: These results are unacceptable and the OT must not be used until re-cleaned, re-fogged and re-swabbed.  Why C wrong: Both re-cleaning and re-fogging plus repeat sampling are required, and the result is unacceptable."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"At what temperature range should the operation theatre be maintained, and how many air changes per hour are needed (per the \"Should Remember\" box)?","options":["15–25°C with 15–20 air changes per hour, both kept turbulent","20°C–25°C with 15–20 air changes per hour, both kept laminar","30°C–35°C with 50 air changes per hour","10°C–15°C with 5 air changes per hour"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Per the box, the operation theatre is maintained at 20°C–25°C, 15–20 air changes per hour, and both temperature and air humidity should be maintained to inhibit bacterial multiplication. (The narrative critical-parameter list keys 20–22°C; this box value is reproduced as printed.)  Why A wrong: Air should be laminar, not turbulent.  Why C/D wrong: These temperatures and air-change rates do not match the source."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"In an OT with a high degree of asepsis, why must positive pressure be ensured?","options":["To provide appropriate environment (asepsis) and keep theatres free from micro-organisms","To raise room humidity above 90%","To allow free entry of outside air","To reduce the number of air changes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In OTs with high asepsis, positive pressure ensures an appropriate (aseptic) environment and keeps theatres free from micro-organisms.  Why B/C/D wrong: Positive pressure is not for raising humidity, admitting outside air, or reducing air changes."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"The four zones of an OT, classified by degree of cleanliness, include which of the following?","options":["Protective area, Clean area, Sterile area, Disposal corridor","Waiting, Recovery, Storage, Office","Reception only","Triage, Resuscitation, Discharge, Morgue"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The four zones based on degree of cleanliness are Protective area, Clean area, Sterile area, and Disposal corridor.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not represent the four cleanliness-based OT zones described."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"Which areas belong to Level-I (Protective / outer zone) of the OT?","options":["Reception, autoclave, changing rooms, pre-anaesthetic examination room, and waiting area / trolley bay","The operating room itself","Instrument sterilising rooms","The dirty wash room"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Level-I (Protective outer zone) includes Reception, Autoclave, changing rooms, pre-anaesthetic examination room and waiting area, trolley bay.  Why B wrong: The operating room is the aseptic/sterile zone, not the protective zone.  Why C wrong: Sterilising rooms fall in the clean zone.  Why D wrong: The dirty wash room is in the disposal zone."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"Which functions are part of Level-II (Clean area) of the OT?","options":["Scrub stations, gowning, glowing/gloving, operating room (OT), anaesthetic room, instrument sterilising rooms, trolley area","Reception and changing rooms only","Disposal of dirty linen only","The waiting area and trolley bay"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Level-II (Clean area) includes Scrub stations, gowning, glowing/gloving, operating room (OT), anaesthetic room, instrument sterilising rooms, and trolley area.  Why B/D wrong: Reception, changing rooms and the waiting/trolley bay are Level-I (protective).  Why C wrong: Dirty-linen disposal is Level-IV (disposal/dirty zone)."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"Level-III (Aseptic / sterile zone) of the OT is defined as which type of area?","options":["The protective outer zone","The aseptic or sterile zone where the highest degree of antibacterial precautions is taken","The disposal of dirty linen","The waiting and changing area"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Level-III is the Aseptic/sterile zone — this is where the highest degree of antibacterial precautions are taken.  Why A/D wrong: These describe the protective Level-I zone.  Why C wrong: Dirty-linen disposal is Level-IV."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"Level-IV of the OT corresponds to which area, and how is it accessed relative to the OT?","options":["Disposal / dirty zone — the dirty wash room or sluice room, accessed through a disposable corridor with dirty work transacted through the area for washing or disposal","Aseptic zone — entered directly from the sterile OT","Clean zone — used for scrubbing","Protective zone — used for reception"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Level-IV is the Disposal / dirty zone (dirty wash room or sluice room, disposable corridor), where dirty work is transacted through the area for washing or disposal.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe the sterile, clean and protective zones respectively, not the disposal/dirty zone."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"What is the first step when beginning the first cleaning of the day in the OT (before cases begin)?","options":["Wear a clean gown, cap, mask and clean utility gloves","Wash floors with HLD immediately","Begin surgery","Open the windows for ventilation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: This should be performed first, irrespective of whether the OT will be used or not — wear a clean gown, cap, mask and clean utility gloves.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the first step; PPE/attire precedes cleaning."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"During the first cleaning of the day, what is the rule about the surgeon/anaesthetist wearing the OT before cleaning is complete?","options":["They may enter freely before cleaning","The surgeon/anaesthetist should not enter the OT before cleaning is complete","They must wear surgical gowns and enter","They should clean it themselves first"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The surgeon/anaesthetist should not enter the OT before cleaning is complete.  Why A/C/D wrong: Entry is not permitted until cleaning is finished, regardless of attire."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"During the first cleaning of the day, mopping should follow which sequence?","options":["Bottom to top","The previously mentioned cleaning sequence (top to down, in to out)","Random direction","Outside-in only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Follow the sequence of cleaning as described previously (top to down; in to out).  Why A/C/D wrong: These contradict the prescribed top-to-down, in-to-out sequence."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"How should all antiseptic bottles and the trays in which they are kept be handled during the first cleaning of the day?","options":["Left unopened","Clean all antiseptic bottles and the trays in which they are kept","Refilled without cleaning","Discarded daily"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source directs to clean all antiseptic bottles and the trays in which they are kept.  Why A/C/D wrong: They must be cleaned (not left, refilled without cleaning, or discarded)."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"What colour-coded waste-collection bag is used for the BMWM container on dirty areas during the first cleaning of the day?","options":["Blue","The correct BMWM colour-coded waste collection bag placed in the waste bins","Transparent","Green"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Ensure the BMWM colour-coded waste collection bags are placed in the waste bins, per biomedical waste management colour coding.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies use of the appropriate BMWM colour-coded bag rather than naming a single fixed colour."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"Where should OT closed for at least 10–15 minutes with ventilation equipment after cleaning, and what is done with the doors?","options":["Keep the OT closed for 10–15 minutes with ventilation equipment on after cleaning; clean the doors and antiseptic bottles at the scrub basin and keep them closed","Keep the OT open with fans off","Leave the doors open for an hour","No closure is required"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Keep the OT closed for 10–15 minutes with ventilation equipment on after cleaning; clean the doors and antiseptic bottles, and keep the doors closed.  Why B/C/D wrong: The OT is kept closed (not open) with ventilation on, and doors are kept closed."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"During cleaning, when only cleaning personnel should be present in the OT, and what is the rule for the doors?","options":["Visitors may enter; doors stay open","During cleaning, only cleaning personnel should be present in the OT and the doors should be kept closed","The surgeon supervises inside with doors open","Doors are removed for cleaning"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: During cleaning, only cleaning personnel should be present in the OT and the doors should be kept closed.  Why A/C/D wrong: Only cleaning staff are present and the doors are kept closed."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"During cleaning between cases in the OT, what should be done with the surgical Wet Floor sign?","options":["Remove the Wet Floor sign during cleaning","Place a cautionary 'Wet Floor' sign at the entrance of the room","Hide the sign from staff","No sign is needed"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: When cleaning in between cases, place a cautionary 'Wet Floor' sign at the entrance of the room.  Why A/C/D wrong: The wet-floor caution sign must be displayed at the entrance, not removed or omitted."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"When cleaning the floor between cases, in which direction should it be cleaned relative to the door?","options":["From the door towards the door (out-to-out)","Begin at the far end / farthest from the door and move towards the door (in to out)","From the centre outward in circles","Direction does not matter"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: When cleaning the floor, begin at the far end / farthest from the door and move towards the door (in to out); the cleaning staff should always move from clean to unclean areas and never vice versa.  Why A/C/D wrong: They contradict the in-to-out (far end towards door) directional rule."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"When cleaning surfaces between cases, what general direction rule applies to minimise contamination?","options":["Clean from dirty to clean surfaces","Clean from clean to dirty surfaces (clean to unclean), minimising the number of horizontal surfaces and providing smooth finishes","Clean only vertical surfaces","Spray water randomly"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: During terminal/between-case cleaning, the general rules include minimising the number of horizontal surfaces, providing smooth finishes, and moving from clean to unclean areas.  Why A wrong: Moving dirty-to-clean spreads contamination.  Why C/D wrong: Both horizontal and vertical surfaces are cleaned methodically, not randomly."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"During terminal cleaning, how should soiled linen be handled?","options":["Wet-mop the floor and remove soiled linen and surrounding walls dry","Remove and collect all soiled linen and discard it; clean and disinfect blood spills first","Re-use soiled linen","Leave linen for the next shift"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Terminal cleaning includes removing and collecting all soiled linen, discarding it, and cleaning/disinfecting blood spills before other cleaning.  Why A wrong: Linen is removed and collected, not dry-mopped over.  Why C/D wrong: Soiled linen is removed for laundering, not reused or left behind."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"During terminal cleaning, what should be done with telephones and computer keyboards in the OT?","options":["Leave them untouched","Clean and disinfect telephones and computer keyboards","Remove them permanently","Wash them with running water"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Terminal cleaning includes cleaning and disinfecting telephones and computer keyboards.  Why A/C wrong: They must be cleaned and disinfected, not ignored or removed.  Why D wrong: Electronic items should not be washed with running water."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"During terminal cleaning, how should the OT table be moved, and how should furniture be returned?","options":["Mop the floor, then move the OT table to the centre and leave equipment where used","Mop the floor, making sure the OT table is moved and the floor is washed underneath; move all furniture to the centre of the room and continue cleaning the floor; replace all furniture and equipment in its proper location","Leave the table in place and skip the floor","Stack all furniture outside permanently"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Move the OT table so the floor is washed underneath, move furniture to the centre, clean the floor, then replace all furniture and equipment in its proper location.  Why A/C wrong: The table is moved aside (not left/centred) so the floor underneath is washed.  Why D wrong: Furniture is replaced in its proper location, not stored outside."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"How often should a detailed wash-down of the OT complex be done?","options":["Daily","At least once a week","Once a month","Once a year"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A detailed wash-down should be done at least once a week for OTs that are used daily.  Why A/C/D wrong: These intervals do not match the at-least-weekly schedule."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"When should the OT be stopped and removed from use and closed for at least one hour, with the ventilation system / AC off, during the wash-down?","options":["When low-level disinfectant until a fog is seen in the air — stop and close the OT for at least one hour with ventilation off","Never","Only during surgery","For 5 minutes only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: When a high-level disinfectant until a fog is seen, stop and close the OT for at least one hour with the ventilation system / AC off.  Why B/C/D wrong: The closure is required for at least one hour during fogging-type wash-down, not skipped or limited to 5 minutes, and not during surgery."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"After how many hours of being open should you change the OT dress, mask and use sterile gloves before performing wash-down sampling?","options":["After 1–2 hours open","After 2 hours of being open, change the OT dress, mask and use sterile gloves","After 24 hours","After 8 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After 2 hours open, change the OT dress, mask and use sterile gloves.  Why A/C/D wrong: These intervals do not match the stated 2-hour rule."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"Which of the following are sample sites to be swabbed at minimum during the OT wash-down (SATA)?","options":["OT table upper surface","OT light/lower glass surface","Anaesthesia machine and sterile instruments trolley surface","Air conditioner outlet/louvers (if AC present)","The surgeon's home address"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Sample sites at minimum include the OT table upper surface, OT light/lower glass surface, anaesthesia machine, sterile instruments trolley surface, any two walls (sample sites above the OT table height), floor (two samples on either side of the OT table), and air conditioner outlet/louvers (if AC present).  Why E wrong: A surgeon's home address is not a physical OT sample site and is not in the source."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"When sampling the floor during the OT wash-down, how many samples are taken and where?","options":["One sample in the centre","Two samples, on either side of the OT table","Four samples in the corners","No floor samples are needed"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For the floor, take two samples on either side of the OT table.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the two-sample, either-side instruction."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"When can the OT be used after the wash-down swab procedure (regarding the wash-down OT sampling results)?","options":["Immediately, without waiting for results","The OT can be used if all three swab samples report the no growth of any organisms","Only after one year","The OT is permanently closed after wash-down"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The OT can be used if all the three swab samples report no growth of any organisms.  Why A wrong: Results must confirm no growth before use.  Why C/D wrong: The OT resumes use once swabs are clear, not after a year or permanently closed."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"If swab results from the OT wash-down show no growth of any organisms, what action is taken?","options":["Repeat the OT sampling as mentioned above","Keep the OT closed for an additional year","Re-clean and re-fog the OT regardless","Discard all instruments"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: If no growth of organisms is reported, repeat the OT sampling as mentioned above (i.e., follow the confirmatory sampling protocol) and proceed.  Why B/C/D wrong: With no growth, additional year-long closure, mandatory re-fogging, or discarding instruments is not required."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"If results are not satisfactory even after the OT wash-down (e.g. growth of fungi or repeated contamination), what should be done?","options":["Ignore and continue surgeries","Seek help from an expert in infection control","Demolish the OT","Use the OT only at night"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If results are not satisfactory even now, seek help from an expert in infection control.  Why A/C/D wrong: Continuing surgeries, demolition, or night-only use are not the advised response; expert infection-control consultation is."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"For cleaning labour rooms, what should be done when equipment is brought into the labour room from outside?","options":["Use it immediately","Wipe all equipment surfaces down with HLD (high-level disinfectant) before bringing them into the room","Spray it with water only","Leave it unwashed"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Whenever any equipment from outside is brought into the labour room, wipe all equipment surfaces down with HLD before bringing them into the room.  Why A/C/D wrong: Equipment must be wiped with HLD first, not used as-is, water-only, or left unwashed."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"When cleaning a labour room, when should the connected passages and rooms be cleaned relative to the labour room?","options":["Always clean the labour room before cleaning the connected passages and rooms","Clean passages first, room last","Both simultaneously","Connected rooms are never cleaned"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Always clean the labour room before cleaning the connected passages and rooms.  Why B/C/D wrong: The labour room is cleaned first; passages/connected rooms follow."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"When cleaning a labour room floor, in which sequence should the floor be cleaned relative to the door?","options":["From the door inward","In a top-to-down sequence, i.e. cleaning the labour room floor from the door away from the door towards the floor (move towards the door / in-to-out)","In random patches","Only the centre"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: When cleaning the labour room floor in a top-to-down sequence, clean from the door and move away/towards the door using equipment based on the in-to-out method.  Why A/C/D wrong: Cleaning proceeds methodically from far end towards the door, not door-inward, random, or centre-only."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"During terminal cleaning of a labour room, how should equipment that has been used (e.g. table, IV stand, stool, etc.) be cleaned, and what disinfectant detail applies?","options":["Wipe down with water only","Wet-mop the floor around the labour room with HLD","Clean and disinfect all over surfaces of equipment; for blood/body-fluid spills, clean with blood/body-fluids occurs (remove BMW at least thrice a day or when the waste container is 3/4ths full)","Leave equipment uncleaned until next delivery"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Clean and disinfect all-over surfaces of equipment; clean blood/body-fluid spills; and remove BMW at least thrice a day or when the waste container is 3/4ths full; daily routine cleaning and disinfection is similar to OTs.  Why A/B wrong: Water-only and floor-only mopping are insufficient for terminal equipment cleaning.  Why D wrong: Equipment must be cleaned/disinfected, not left until the next delivery."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"What should be done first when \"Cleaning after a delivery\" begins?","options":["Begin cleaning as soon as possible","Wait 24 hours","Begin surgery","Open all windows for an hour"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: When cleaning after a delivery, begin cleaning as soon as possible.  Why B/C/D wrong: Cleaning is prompt, not delayed; surgery/window-airing are not the first step."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"What PPE should be worn when cleaning after a delivery, especially when splashing is expected?","options":["Only a cap","Wear utility gloves; wear a gown and goggles if splashing is expected","No PPE","Sterile surgical gown only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Wear utility gloves; wear a gown and goggles if splashing is expected.  Why A/C/D wrong: Utility gloves are needed, plus gown and goggles when splashing is anticipated."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"How should blood/body-fluid spills be handled when cleaning after a delivery?","options":["Leave them to dry","Clean all blood/body fluid spills first","Cover with linen and remove later","Mop over them with water"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Clean all blood/body fluid spills first when cleaning after a delivery.  Why A/C/D wrong: Spills must be cleaned first, not dried, covered, or mopped over with plain water."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"What should be done with soiled linen during cleaning after a delivery?","options":["Re-use it directly","Remove soiled linen carefully and put it in a waterproof container/bag","Burn it in the room","Leave it on the floor"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Remove soiled linen carefully and put it in a waterproof container/bag.  Why A/C/D wrong: Soiled linen is bagged in a waterproof container, not reused, burned in-room, or left on the floor."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"When cleaning instruments after a delivery, how should they be handled before sending them for transport/cleaning and sterilisation?","options":["Remove any instruments used in the delivery and send/transport them for cleaning and sterilisation","Throw away all metal instruments","Leave them on the trolley","Soak them in plain water only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Remove any instruments used in the delivery and send/transport them for cleaning and sterilisation.  Why B/C/D wrong: Instruments are sent for cleaning/sterilisation, not discarded, left out, or merely soaked in water."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"When cleaning utility gloves after a delivery, what should be done?","options":["Discard them in the linen bag","Wash the utility gloves with soap and water and clean/disinfect them","Reuse them without washing","Wear them home"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Wash the utility gloves with soap and water and clean/disinfect them; change the utility gloves and wash your hands at the end of cleaning.  Why A/C/D wrong: Utility gloves are cleaned/disinfected and reused per protocol, not discarded carelessly, reused unwashed, or worn home."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"How should soiled gowns used during a delivery be handled, and what footwear instruction applies?","options":["Soiled gowns used during delivery, soiled goggles, soiled footwear should be collected separately and disinfected by immersion in chlorine solution (500–1000 ppm) for 5–10 min; immersion in chlorine solution before washing them with soap and water","Soiled gowns are reused without washing","Footwear is never disinfected","Gowns are soaked in plain water for 1 minute"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Soiled gowns, goggles and footwear used during delivery should be collected separately and disinfected by immersion in chlorine solution (500–1000 ppm) for 5–10 minutes before washing them with soap and water.  Why B/C/D wrong: They contradict the separate-collection, chlorine-immersion (500–1000 ppm, 5–10 min) protocol."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"How often should a detailed wash-down of the labour room be performed?","options":["Daily","At least twice a month","Once a year","Never"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Perform detailed wash-down of the labour room, using the procedure mentioned for detailed wash-down of the operation theatre, at least twice a month.  Why A/C/D wrong: These intervals do not match the at-least-twice-a-month rule."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"When mopping the floor to clean equipment, in which direction should the mop be wiped relative to the equipment?","options":["Wipe the equipment surface moving the mop in one direction over it","Wipe in random directions over the wiped area","Wipe back and forth repeatedly","Do not mop near equipment"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Wipe the equipment surface moving the mop in one direction over it; wipe with pressure (do not go back into the wiped area).  Why B/C/D wrong: One-directional wiping prevents re-contamination, unlike random, back-and-forth, or no mopping."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"Which direction should cleaning at the top of the equipment proceed?","options":["Always begin cleaning at the top of the equipment and move downwards (top to down)","Bottom to top","Centre outward","Direction is irrelevant"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Always begin cleaning at the top of the equipment and move downwards (top to down).  Why B/C/D wrong: Cleaning follows top-to-down, not bottom-up, centre-out, or random."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"When moving from one piece of equipment to another, what should be done with the fold of the mop, and how is the disinfectant/cleaner handled?","options":["Reuse the same fold; reuse the same disinfectant indefinitely","When moving from one piece of equipment to another, change the fold of the mop, and add disinfectant/cleaner; allow the disinfectant/cleaner to dry naturally","Discard the mop after every wipe","Rinse the mop in the patient sink"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: When moving from one piece of equipment to another, change the fold of the mop, add fresh disinfectant/cleaner as needed, and allow the disinfectant/cleaner to dry naturally.  Why A/C/D wrong: The mop fold is changed (not reused indefinitely or discarded each wipe) and rinsing in a patient sink is inappropriate."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"Which authorities are referenced as recommending a well-equipped heat ventilation air conditioning (HVAC) system in the fogging overview?","options":["Now, CDC and guidelines recommend a well-equipped heat ventilation air conditioning (HVAC) system","WHO and FDA only","The local municipality","No authority is cited"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states that now, CDC and guidelines recommend a well-equipped heat ventilation air conditioning (HVAC) system.  Why B/C/D wrong: The cited reference is CDC and guidelines, not WHO/FDA, the municipality, or none."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"What is the role of an HVAC system in the OT environment?","options":["It only cools the room","An HVAC system maintains indoor air temperature and humidity, control odours, removes contaminated air, and minimises the risk of transmission of airborne micro-organisms","It increases airborne micro-organisms","It is used only for lighting"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: An HVAC system maintains indoor air temperature and humidity, controls odours, removes contaminated air and minimises the risk of transmission of airborne micro-organisms.  Why A/C/D wrong: HVAC does more than cool, reduces (not increases) airborne organisms, and is unrelated to lighting."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"Why is the use of HEPA filters preferred in an OT with HVAC?","options":["They are cheaper than other filters","Use of HEPA filters (to remove particles of size air > 0.3 mm) and laminar air flow and UV radiation further help in maintaining asepsis and infection control","They increase humidity","They eliminate the need for cleaning"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Use of HEPA filters (to remove particles of the stated size), laminar air flow, and UV radiation further help in maintaining asepsis and infection control.  Why A/C/D wrong: HEPA filters are an infection-control measure, not chosen for cheapness, humidity, or eliminating cleaning."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"Why is fumigation (fogging with formalin) no longer used in most countries including India?","options":["Because it is difficult to perform, dangerous to use, unreliable, and formalin itself is a known carcinogen","Because it is too cheap","Because it is too effective","Because it improves air quality"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Fumigation (fogging with formalin) is no longer used in most countries including India because it is difficult to perform, dangerous to use, unreliable, and formalin itself is a known carcinogen.  Why B/C/D wrong: These contradict the stated reasons (carcinogenic, dangerous, unreliable)."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"Fogging (or disinfection) of the OT is categorised into which two types?","options":["Disinfection of OT with HVAC system and Disinfection of OT without HVAC system","Wet fogging and dry fogging only","Manual and automatic only","Day fogging and night fogging"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Fogging/disinfection of OT is categorised into two types: Disinfection of OT with HVAC system and Disinfection of OT without HVAC system.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the two categories named in the source."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"Nowadays, which product is preferred for fogging instead of formalin?","options":["An aldehyde-based product containing glutaraldehyde and chemically bound formaldehyde, as a principal disinfecting agent used for fogging, e.g. Bacillocid","Pure water","Plain bleach only","Ethanol vapour"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Nowadays, an aldehyde-based product containing glutaraldehyde and chemically bound formaldehyde is used as a principal disinfecting agent for fogging, e.g. Bacillocid.  Why B/C/D wrong: Plain water, plain bleach, or ethanol vapour are not the named principal fogging agent."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"In an OT with a HVAC system, is fogging required for disinfection?","options":["Fogging is not required for an OT with a HEPA-filtered positive air supply system","Fogging is mandatory daily","Fogging is required every hour","Fogging is required only with HVAC"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Fogging is not required for an OT with a HEPA-filtered positive-air-supply (HVAC) system.  Why B/C/D wrong: With a properly functioning HVAC/HEPA system, routine fogging is not required."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"For an OT WITH an HVAC system, how must the system be ensured to be effective before deciding not to fog, and how often is the ventilation system design validated?","options":["The ventilation system design must be appropriate and performance validated at least once a year","Validated once every 5 years only","No validation is needed","Validated only after an outbreak"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The ventilation system design should be appropriate and performance is validated at least once a year; HEPA filters are changed as per validation test, which should be done at least every year, and weekly air count monitoring using settle plates / air sampler is done.  Why B/C/D wrong: Annual validation is required, not five-yearly, never, or outbreak-only."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"In an OT WITHOUT an HVAC system, what general instruction applies before fogging?","options":["Remove all the dust from the area where fogging has to be carried out (clean the room thoroughly and mop all the surfaces)","Leave dust in place","Add water to the floor","Open the windows during fogging"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: General instructions before fogging include removing all the dust from the area where fogging has to be carried out, and cleaning the room thoroughly and mopping all the surfaces.  Why B/C/D wrong: Dust is removed (not left), and windows are sealed (not opened) before/during fogging."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"Before starting the fogging process in an OT without HVAC, what should be done to all surfaces and the door?","options":["Before starting the fogging process, mop all surfaces, then they should be cleaned with disinfectant; labels should be put on the door with time of starting; expected time of opening","Spray water on the door","Leave the door open","Add bleach to the floor only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Before starting the fogging process, mop and clean all surfaces with disinfectant, and put labels on the door with the time of starting and expected time of opening.  Why B/C/D wrong: These contradict the clean-with-disinfectant and door-labelling instructions."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"In an OT without HVAC, what should be done with windows, ventilators and exhausts before fogging?","options":["Seal the room including windows and ventilators air tight; use adhesive tapes to close the gaps; switch off the fans; keep air conditioning switched off; switch on exhaust for 15 minutes prior to starting air conditioning","Open all windows and ventilators","Keep fans running on high","Keep air conditioning on full"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Seal the room (including windows and ventilators) air tight, use adhesive tapes to close the gaps, switch off the fans, keep air conditioning switched off, and switch on the exhaust for 15 minutes prior to starting air conditioning.  Why B/C/D wrong: The room must be sealed air-tight with fans and AC off, not opened or kept running."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"After completing fogging, when can air conditioning be started?","options":["Air conditioning can be started after 1 hour of the procedure","Immediately","After 5 minutes","Never again"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Air conditioning can be started after 1 hour of the procedure.  Why B/C/D wrong: A one-hour wait is required, not immediate, 5-minute, or never."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"To calculate the disinfectant to be used for fogging, what dimensions must first be measured?","options":["To undertake terminal disinfection (fogging), calculate the area to be fogged in cubic feet, i.e. Length × Breadth × Height","Only the floor length","Only the ceiling height","The patient's weight"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: To undertake terminal disinfection (fogging), calculate the area to be fogged in cubic feet, i.e. Length × Breadth × Height (L × B × H).  Why B/C/D wrong: Volume in cubic feet (L × B × H) is required, not a single dimension or patient weight."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"Per the fogging calculation example, for a room measuring 10 ft (L) × 8 ft (B) × 10 ft (H), what is the volume in cubic feet?","options":["800 cubic feet","28 cubic feet","80 cubic feet","1000 cubic feet"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Example: L = 10 ft, B = 10 ft and H = 10 ft (and the worked figure 10 × 8 × 10) gives 800 cubic feet (10 × 8 × 10 = 800). [The source's example header lists L = 10 ft, B = 10 ft, H = 10 ft and the worked dilution table uses 800 cu ft.]  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not equal the worked 800 cubic-feet volume."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"In the fogging dilution table, for 1000 cubic feet, what volume of Ecoshield + water and time of fogging are specified?","options":["1000 cu ft → 200 ml Ecoshield + 800 ml water = 1 L; time = 1 Litre (≈1 L)","1000 cu ft → 1 ml + 1 ml = 2 ml; 5 hours","1000 cu ft → 0 ml; no fogging","1000 cu ft → 5 L; 10 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The dilution table specifies 1000 cu ft → 200 ml Ecoshield + 800 ml water = 1 L (the 1-litre row).  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the table's 200 ml + 800 ml = 1 L entry for 1000 cu ft."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"In the fogging dilution table, what is specified for 2000 cubic feet?","options":["2000 cu ft → 400 ml + 1600 ml = 2 L (2 Litres)","2000 cu ft → 100 ml + 100 ml = 200 ml","2000 cu ft → 0 ml","2000 cu ft → 5 L"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For 2000 cu ft the table specifies 400 ml + 1600 ml = 2 L (2 Litres).  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the 400 ml + 1600 ml = 2 L entry."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"In the fogging dilution table, what is specified for 2500 cubic feet?","options":["2500 cu ft → 500 ml + 2000 ml = 2.5 L (2.5 Litres)","2500 cu ft → 250 ml + 250 ml = 500 ml","2500 cu ft → 5 L","2500 cu ft → 0 ml"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For 2500 cu ft the table specifies 500 ml + 2000 ml = 2.5 L (2.5 Litres).  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the 500 ml + 2000 ml = 2.5 L entry."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"Per the fogging table footnote, for every 1000 cubic feet of space, what volume of Bacillocid (special) is used?","options":["For each 1000 cu ft of space, use 1 litre of 0.5 to 1.0% Bacillocid (special) solution","10 litres of 50% solution","1 ml of 0.001% solution","No Bacillocid is used"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For each 1000 cu ft space, use 1 litre of 0.5 to 1.0% Bacillocid (special) solution.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the 1 litre per 1000 cu ft at 0.5–1.0% concentration."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"What is Ecoshield / Bacillocid described as for fogging disinfection?","options":["A non-toxic, environment-friendly disinfectant for critical-area fogging based on stabilised hydrogen peroxide (H₂O₂)","A toxic carcinogenic gas","Pure water","An antibiotic"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Ecoshield/Bacillocid is a non-toxic, environment-friendly disinfectant for critical-area fogging; it is a complex formulation of stabilised hydrogen peroxide (with silver nitrate solution, 0.01% w/v) — silver ions playing a role in stabilising the H₂O₂.  Why B/C/D wrong: It is non-toxic and environment-friendly (not a toxic carcinogen), not plain water, and not an antibiotic."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"Which of the following are listed indications for fogging (SATA)?","options":["After new construction and renovation","Airborne diseases like Tuberculosis, Influenza, Ebola, etc.","Before commissioning a new clinical area such as a new OT and ICU","After annual maintenance in the above-mentioned areas","Routine fogging of every OT after every surgery"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Indications for fogging include after new construction/renovation; airborne diseases (TB, influenza, Ebola, etc.); commissioning a new clinical area such as a new OT and ICU; and after annual maintenance in the above-mentioned areas. Fogging of the OT may also be done on the basis of any microbial (positive culture / growth).  Why E wrong: Routine fogging after every surgery is not listed; fogging is for specific situations (new construction, airborne disease, commissioning, post-maintenance, or positive culture)."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"Per the fogging-of-OT guidance, is routine fogging of OT recommended in wards?","options":["Routine fogging is NOT recommended, especially in wards","Routine fogging is mandatory in all wards","Routine fogging is recommended hourly","Fogging is never permitted"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Routine fogging is NOT recommended, especially in wards.  Why B/C/D wrong: It is not mandatory/hourly in wards, but neither is it never permitted (it is used for specific indications)."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"In which situations should fogging of wards/rooms be done (SATA)?","options":["When an isolated ward/room is emptied at the end of an outbreak","When an infected patient is discharged from a room (in absence of an outbreak)","When an outbreak of infection occurs in a ward","After every routine ward shift change"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Fogging of wards/rooms should be done when an isolated ward/room is emptied at the end of an outbreak; when an infected patient is discharged from a room (in absence of an outbreak); and when there is an outbreak of infection in a ward.  Why D wrong: Routine fogging after every shift change is not an indication."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"In the fogging procedure, after cleaning the floors clear, what is done before keeping the disinfectant system on (the fogger), and what is the rule about the AC?","options":["Cover all power sockets and plug points before the procedure; keep ventilation systems / AC off","Keep the AC running","Leave power sockets exposed","Spray water on the sockets"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In the fogging procedure, after all cases are over, clean the OT as per the procedure for cleaning, keep the ventilation system (AC) off, and cover all power sockets and plug points (using plastic cover) before fogging, to prevent the fogged liquid from going into the machines.  Why B/C/D wrong: The AC is off (not running) and sockets are covered (not exposed or sprayed with water)."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"Why must all sockets and plug points be covered with a plastic cover during fogging?","options":["To prevent the fogged liquid from going into the machines; no electronic equipment may be left uncovered","To improve appearance","To increase humidity","To allow the fog to enter the machines"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cover sockets/plug points with a plastic cover to prevent the fogged liquid from going into the machines; no electronic equipment is left uncovered.  Why B/C/D wrong: The purpose is equipment protection, not appearance, humidity, or allowing fog ingress."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"Which disinfectant is used in the fogging procedure, and how should the solution be poured into the fogging machine?","options":["Disinfectant Bacillocid special, or Ecoshield/Bacillocid is used as per the area size of the OT for the allotted time; pour the disinfectant solution into the fogging machine","Plain water poured slowly","Bleach poured into the fog tank","Formalin only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Disinfectant Bacillocid special, or Ecoshield/Bacillocid is used as per the area size of the OT for the allotted time, and the disinfectant solution is poured into the fogging machine.  Why B/C/D wrong: Plain water, plain bleach, or formalin are not the specified fogging disinfectant."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"During the fogging procedure, what should be done with the OT switch, and for how long should the fog be kept?","options":["Switch on the machine; keep it for 60 minutes or 1 hour; no neutralisation with ammonia is required","Keep the fog for 5 minutes and neutralise with ammonia","Switch off the machine immediately","Keep the fog for 24 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Switch on the machine, keep it for 60 minutes / 1 hour, and no neutralisation with ammonia is required.  Why B/C/D wrong: The fog is kept for ~1 hour with no ammonia neutralisation, not 5 minutes with ammonia, immediate switch-off, or 24 hours."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"What is the appropriate PPE during the fogging procedure (use of PPE)?","options":["Appropriate use of PPE — wear masks and goggles, etc.","No PPE needed","Only gloves","A surgical drape only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Appropriate use of PPE — wear masks and goggles, etc., during fogging.  Why B/C/D wrong: Appropriate PPE (masks, goggles, etc.) is required, not none, gloves-only, or a drape."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"When measuring the room to be fogged, in what unit is the room measured, and where should the fog machine nozzle be placed?","options":["Measure the area of the room to be fogged in cubic feet; place the fogger machine and place it at least 2 feet above the floor surface in one corner of the room; keep the fog machine nozzle at an angle of about 45 degrees, kept at the ground floor in a corner","Measure in litres; place the machine on the floor","Measure in kilograms; hold the nozzle by hand","No measurement needed"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Measure the area of the room to be fogged in cubic feet; place the fogger machine at least 2 feet above the floor surface in one corner of the room; keep the fog machine nozzle at about a 45-degree angle to the ground floor in one corner.  Why B/C/D wrong: The room is measured in cubic feet (not litres/kilograms), the machine is raised ≥2 ft (not on the floor), and measurement is required."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"In the instructions for post-fogging of the OT/ICU, what is the sequence of steps after stopping the fogger machine?","options":["After stopping the fogger machine, the fogged area must be isolated for one hour; after one hour of isolation, one should start the AC system for 10 minutes; after that the OT is ready for use","Open the OT immediately for surgery","Keep the OT closed for one week","Restart fogging again"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: After stopping the fogger machine, the fogged area must be isolated for one hour; after one hour of isolation, start the AC system for 10 minutes; after that the OT is ready for use.  Why B/C/D wrong: A one-hour isolation then 10-minute AC run is required before use, not immediate use, week-long closure, or re-fogging."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"What is the formula for preparing disinfectant solutions, and what does each symbol denote?","options":["A = (D × Q) / H, where A = amount of disinfectant, D = desired strength, H = strength in hand, Q = quantity","A = D + Q + H","A = D − H","A = H / D"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For preparation of disinfectant solutions, the formula is A = (D × Q) / H, where A = amount of disinfectant, D = desired strength, H = strength in hand, Q = quantity.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the (D × Q) / H formula or the symbol definitions."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"Using the formula, to prepare 1% hypochlorite solution from 10% hypochlorite solution where D = 1%, H = 10%, Q = 1000 ml, what amount (A) of disinfectant is required and how much water?","options":["A = (1 × 1000) / 10 = 100 ml of hypochlorite; water required = (1000 − 100) = 900 ml (i.e. 100 ml of 10% hypochlorite + 900 ml water = 1% solution)","A = 1000 ml; no water needed","A = 10 ml; 990 ml water","A = 500 ml; 500 ml water"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A = (D × Q) / H = (1 × 1000) / 10 = 100 ml of hypochlorite; water required = (1000 − 100) = 900 ml, so 100 ml of 10% sodium hypochlorite solution + 900 ml water makes a 1% sodium hypochlorite solution.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not equal the worked result of 100 ml disinfectant + 900 ml water."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"As per the general guidelines, how often should disinfectant solutions be reconstituted/changed, and what governs the time span?","options":["Disinfectant solutions should be reconstituted and changed according to its use life span, as per the manufacturer's recommendations","They are never changed","They are changed once a year regardless","They last indefinitely"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Disinfectant solutions should be reconstituted and changed according to its use life span — as per the manufacturer's recommendations.  Why B/C/D wrong: They are changed per use-life and manufacturer guidance, not never, fixed annually, or indefinitely."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"As per the general guidelines, what PPE must be worn while handling chemicals, and how often should opened normal saline (NS) bottles used for dressing be discarded?","options":["Always use PPE (gloves, mask, and apron) while handling chemicals; opened normal saline (NS) bottles used (for dressing) should not be used beyond 24 hours; replace fresh bottles every day morning before, i.e. label the bottle with date/time","No PPE; reuse NS bottles for a week","Only gloves; discard NS after one month","Full sterile gown; NS lasts indefinitely"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Always use PPE (gloves, mask, apron) while handling chemicals; opened NS bottles (for dressing) should not be used beyond 24 hours and should be replaced with fresh bottles every day morning, with the bottle labelled with date/time.  Why B/C/D wrong: PPE is required and opened NS is discarded within 24 hours, not reused for a week/month or indefinitely."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"Regarding carbolization of OT and labour rooms, what level of disinfectant is used and how is the activity carried out?","options":["For carbolisation of OT and labour room, a high-level disinfectant with assured activity needs to be used, e.g. an aldehyde-based product containing glutaraldehyde and chemically bound formaldehyde as a principal disinfecting agent (e.g. Bacillocid)","A low-level disinfectant such as plain soap","Only water","An antibiotic spray"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For carbolisation of OT and labour room, a high-level disinfectant with assured activity is used — e.g. an aldehyde-based product containing glutaraldehyde and chemically bound formaldehyde as a principal disinfecting agent (e.g. Bacillocid).  Why B/C/D wrong: A high-level disinfectant is required, not low-level soap, water, or an antibiotic spray."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"Where should disinfectants be used for surface disinfection of all critical care areas during carbolization?","options":["It can be used for the purpose of surface disinfection of all critical care areas","Only on patient skin","Only outdoors","Never on surfaces"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The high-level disinfectant can be used for the purpose of surface disinfection of all critical care areas.  Why B/C/D wrong: It is used for surface disinfection of critical areas, not on skin, outdoors-only, or never on surfaces."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"Although phenols are reasonably good disinfectants, why should they be phased out as they may cause damage?","options":["Although phenols are reasonably good disinfectants, they should be phased out as they may cause damage to environmental water sources and aquatic life","Because they are too expensive","Because they smell pleasant","Because they are completely ineffective"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Although phenols are reasonably good disinfectants, they should be phased out as they may cause damage to environmental water sources and aquatic life.  Why B/C/D wrong: The stated reason is environmental/aquatic harm, not cost, smell, or ineffectiveness."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"Why should phenol be avoided where there is a need for low odour, and what is its relation to whitey/black phenyl?","options":["If phenol is to be used, then black phenyl should be suggested as white phenyl is mainly fragrance with reduced disinfectant activity","White phenyl is more effective than black phenyl","Phenol is odourless","Phenyl is not a disinfectant"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: If phenol is to be used, then black phenyl should be suggested, as white phenyl is mainly fragrance with reduced disinfectant activity.  Why B wrong: White phenyl has reduced disinfectant activity (it is mainly fragrance), so it is not more effective.  Why C wrong: Phenol/phenyl has odour (the reason black phenyl is preferred for activity).  Why D wrong: Phenyl is a disinfectant; its activity differs by type."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"As per the general guidance, why should dirty linen need to be collected in a colour-coded and soiled-linen-in-yellow coloured trolley/bag?","options":["Dirty linen need to be collected in a green coloured trolley and soiled linen in a yellow coloured trolley/bag","All linen goes in a single white bag","Dirty linen requires no segregation","Linen is burned immediately"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Dirty linen needs to be collected in a green coloured trolley and soiled linen in a yellow coloured trolley/bag (colour-coded linen segregation).  Why B/C/D wrong: Colour-coded segregation (green for dirty, yellow for soiled) is required, not a single bag, no segregation, or immediate burning."},{"section":"Batch 053 · Microbiological monitoring of the OT…","question":"When using bleaching solution to soak linen, what colour-coded handling applies, and how is it removed?","options":["All infected linen needs to be soaked in 1% bleaching solution; soak the linen for proper time/period, after washing rinse the linen in plain water with plain water to remove the bleach; the linen is then handed over for washing","Linen is left in bleach indefinitely","Bleach is never used on linen","Linen is dried without rinsing"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: All infected linen needs to be soaked in 1% bleaching solution for the proper period; after the soak it is rinsed in plain water to remove the bleach, then handed over for washing.  Why B/C/D wrong: Linen is soaked for a defined time and rinsed to remove bleach (not left indefinitely, never bleached, or dried unrinsed)."},{"section":"Batch 054 · ICU & HDU definitions and characteristics…","question":"An Intensive Care Unit (ICU) is BEST defined as:","options":["A \"step-down\" unit between the general ward and the ICU","A designated, specialized area for multidisciplinary, focused management of patients who have life-threatening, partially or completely reversible multi-organ dysfunction","A unit that admits only patients needing routine nursing care","An area for storage of bio-medical waste and equipment"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The ICU is a designated, specialized area for multidisciplinary, focused management of patients with life-threatening, partially or completely reversible multi-organ dysfunction.  Why A wrong: A \"step-down\" unit between the general ward and the ICU describes the HDU, not the ICU.  Why C wrong: ICUs do NOT admit patients needing only routine nursing care.  Why D wrong: This describes a BMW storage/housekeeping area, not the ICU."},{"section":"Batch 054 · ICU & HDU definitions and characteristics…","question":"Which terms are synonymous with the Intensive Care Unit (ICU)?","options":["Casualty and Emergency","Critical Care, Intensive Care, Intensive Therapy Unit","High Dependency Unit and step-down unit","Recovery room and post-operative ward"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: ICU is synonymous with Critical Care, Intensive Care, and Intensive Therapy Unit.  Why A wrong: Casualty/Emergency is a separate area (Emergency & Casualty), not a synonym of ICU.  Why C wrong: HDU/step-down is a distinct, lower-acuity unit.  Why D wrong: Recovery/post-op ward is not an ICU synonym in this text."},{"section":"Batch 054 · ICU & HDU definitions and characteristics…","question":"According to the Summary of CCU/ICU/HDU/Casualty, a Critical Care Unit (CCU) is defined as a separate, specially staffed, and equipped area dedicated to:","options":["Routine outpatient consultations only","Management and monitoring of patients with potential lifethreatening illnesses, injuries, and complications","Storage of medical records","Elective day-care surgery only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A CCU is a separate, specially staffed and equipped area for management and monitoring of patients with potential life-threatening illnesses, injuries, and complications; it comprises Emergency/Casualty, ICU, and HDU.  Why A wrong: Outpatient consultation is not the CCU's purpose.  Why C wrong: Record storage is not a CCU function.  Why D wrong: Elective day-care surgery is unrelated to the CCU definition."},{"section":"Batch 054 · ICU & HDU definitions and characteristics…","question":"In the Emergency and Casualty area, suspected airborne-infection patients are placed in special rooms equipped with:","options":["Positive pressure ventilation","Negative pressure ventilation with isolation facilities","No ventilation control","Standard room ventilation only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The critical care unit requires isolation rooms with negative pressure ventilation for admitting and monitoring such infectious cases that are highly virulent and may spread infection to others; these rooms are supported with monitoring equipment and oxygen.  Why A wrong: Positive pressure protects the patient from outside organisms, not others from the patient.  Why C and D wrong: Airborne-infection isolation specifically requires negative pressure, not standard or absent ventilation control."},{"section":"Batch 054 · ICU & HDU definitions and characteristics…","question":"The High Dependency Unit (HDU) is BEST described as a unit where patients are cared for at a level:","options":["Higher than the ICU","More than a critical care unit but less than an extensive ICU level","Equal to a general ward","Requiring no monitoring at all"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: An HDU provides a higher dependency unit where care is more than a normal ward but less than an extensive ICU; patients can be cared for there when their illness is at a normal stage, where patients can be cared for than in a normal ward but not to the extent provided in an intensive care unit.  Why A wrong: HDU is a step-down (lower) level than the ICU.  Why C wrong: HDU is higher acuity than a general ward.  Why D wrong: HDU patients still require monitoring."},{"section":"Batch 054 · ICU & HDU definitions and characteristics…","question":"Which statement correctly describes the HDU as a \"step-up/step-down\" unit?","options":["It is the highest level of care delivered in intensive care","It is a \"step-up\" or \"step-down\" unit between the level of care (intermediary care) delivered on a general ward and intensive care","It does not admit any patients requiring monitoring","It provides invasive ventilation and vasopressor assistance"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The HDU acts as a \"step-up\" or \"step-down\" unit between the level of care (intermediary care) delivered on a general ward and intensive care.  Why A wrong: The ICU, not the HDU, is the highest level of care.  Why C wrong: HDU manages patients who need closer monitoring/support than a general ward provides.  Why D wrong: Invasive ventilation and vasopressor support are features of the ICU, not the HDU."},{"section":"Batch 054 · ICU & HDU definitions and characteristics…","question":"A nurse in the HDU recognizes that one of the unit's key characteristics is that it:","options":["Admits patients with multi-organ failure requiring invasive ventilation","Manages cases where patients do not require multi-organ support, but face complications that necessitate close monitoring, supervision, and treatment","Provides constant cardiac monitoring with vasopressor support","Admits patients who require only routine general nursing care"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In general, the HDU manages cases where patients do not require multi-organ support but face complications that necessitate close monitoring, supervision and treatment at the secondary care level.  Why A wrong: Multi-organ failure with invasive ventilation belongs to the ICU.  Why C wrong: Constant monitoring with vasopressor support is an ICU characteristic.  Why D wrong: The HDU does not admit patients who only need routine nursing care on a regular basis."},{"section":"Batch 054 · ICU & HDU definitions and characteristics…","question":"Which statement correctly describes the basic characteristics of the ICU?","options":["ICU is a \"step-down\" unit acting as an intermediary care level","ICU acts as a \"step-up\" unit where the highest level of care is delivered in intensive care, with continuous monitoring and support for patients with multi-organ failure or acute/chronic single or multiple organ failure","ICU admits patients needing only routine nursing supervision","ICU does not provide invasive ventilation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The ICU acts as a \"step-up\" unit, the highest level of care delivered in intensive care, providing invasive monitoring, constant monitoring and support to patients with or at risk of developing acute (or acute on chronic) single or multiple organ failure, and manages patients with severe complications or organ failure with invasive ventilation and vasopressor assistance.  Why A wrong: The \"step-down/intermediary\" description applies to the HDU.  Why C wrong: ICUs do not exist for routine nursing supervision.  Why D wrong: ICUs provide invasive ventilation and vasopressor support."},{"section":"Batch 054 · ICU & HDU definitions and characteristics…","question":"Treatment in the critical care unit requires continuous and intensive observation and interventions by a multi-professional team. The specialist who manages a critically ill patient in the ICU is known as:","options":["General duty medical officer","Intensivist or Critical Care specialist","Sanitation supervisor","Pharmacist"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A specialist who has the specific training, certification, and experience in managing critically ill patients in the ICU is known as an Intensivist or Critical Care specialist.  Why A wrong: A general medical officer is not the dedicated critical-care specialist.  Why C and D wrong: Sanitation staff and pharmacists are support roles, not the critical-care specialist."},{"section":"Batch 054 · ICU & HDU definitions and characteristics…","question":"A patient who has experienced any major intra-operative complication (e.g., cardiovascular or respiratory instability) should be admitted to which unit?","options":["General ward","ICU","HDU only","Outpatient department"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Patients who have experienced any major intra-operative complication, such as cardiovascular or respiratory instability, are treated in the ICU.  Why A and D wrong: A general ward or OPD cannot provide the intensive monitoring such complications need.  Why C wrong: Major intra-operative complications warrant ICU-level (not HDU-only) care."},{"section":"Batch 054 · ICU & HDU definitions and characteristics…","question":"According to the AB 2022 norms, what percentage of the total beds should ideally be critical care beds?","options":["5–10%","8–10%","10–15%","15–20%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: As per IPHS 2022 norms, 8–10% of the total beds should ideally be critical care beds.  Why A, C, D wrong: These percentages do not match the stated 8–10% norm."},{"section":"Batch 054 · ICU & HDU definitions and characteristics…","question":"The number of beds in a critical care unit primarily depends on:","options":["The number of nurses available","The number of beds in a hospital","The hospital's geographic location only","The number of physicians on staff"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The number of beds in a critical care unit will depend on the number of beds in a hospital.  Why A, C, D wrong: The text bases bed numbers on total hospital beds, not on staffing counts or location."},{"section":"Batch 054 · ICU & HDU definitions and characteristics…","question":"For a hospital with an existing bed strength of \"Less than 200,\" what is the recommended bed strength of the critical care block?","options":["25","50","75","100"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The bed strength table lists \"Less than 200\" existing beds → 50 critical care block beds; \"200–300\" → 75; \"More than 300\" → 100.  Why A wrong: 25 is not a listed value for any tier.  Why C wrong: 75 corresponds to 200–300 existing beds.  Why D wrong: 100 corresponds to more than 300 existing beds."},{"section":"Batch 054 · ICU & HDU definitions and characteristics…","question":"For a hospital with an existing bed strength of \"More than 300,\" the recommended critical care block bed strength is:","options":["50","75","100","150"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: \"More than 300\" existing beds → 100 critical care block beds per the table.  Why A wrong: 50 corresponds to less than 200 beds.  Why B wrong: 75 corresponds to 200–300 beds.  Why D wrong: 150 is not a listed value."},{"section":"Batch 054 · ICU & HDU definitions and characteristics…","question":"At the CCB level, for a 100-bed critical care block, how many Isolation Ward beds are recommended?","options":["5","10","14","20"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: At the 100-bed CCB level, Isolation Ward beds = 10 (the table lists Isolation Ward as 30 / 30 / 14 across the 100/75/50 columns — at the 100 level Isolation Ward shows 10 and Isolation Room 5/4/4). The text designates 10 isolation ward beds at the highest CCB tier.  Why A wrong: 5 is the isolation ROOM figure, not the isolation ward.  Why C wrong: 14 appears in the lowest CCB column.  Why D wrong: 20 is not listed for isolation ward at this level."},{"section":"Batch 054 · ICU & HDU definitions and characteristics…","question":"Per IPHS, the minimum floor space recommended per ICU bed is:","options":["9.0 sq metres","25–30 sq metres","10–24 sq metres","3.5 sq metres"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The space requirement table lists ICU bed floor space per bed as 25–30 sq metres, with space between bed and wall of 0.9 m and 2.5 m center-of-two-adjacent-beds spacing.  Why A wrong: 0.9 m is the space between bed and wall, not the floor space.  Why C wrong: 10–24 is not the stated ICU floor-space figure.  Why D wrong: 3.5 m is the spacing dimension, not floor area."},{"section":"Batch 054 · ICU & HDU definitions and characteristics…","question":"Which of the following is an ICU Admission Criterion?","options":["Routine post-operative recovery in a stable patient","Altered level of consciousness of recent onset","Patient awaiting elective surgery","Patient requiring only oral medication"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: ICU admission criteria include altered level of consciousness of recent onset; haemodynamic instability (clinical features of shock, hypotension); and need for respiratory support (escalating oxygen requirement, denovo respiratory failure requiring non-invasive ventilation, invasive mechanical ventilation, etc.).  Why A, C, D wrong: Routine recovery, elective-surgery waiting, and oral-medication-only patients do not meet ICU admission criteria."},{"section":"Batch 054 · ICU & HDU definitions and characteristics…","question":"Which of the following are recognized ICU admission criteria? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Altered level of consciousness of recent onset","Haemodynamic instability with clinical features of shock or hypotension","Need for respiratory support such as escalating oxygen requirement or denovo respiratory failure","Invasive mechanical ventilation requirement","Stable patient needing routine ward-level care"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Altered LOC of recent onset, haemodynamic instability (shock/hypotension), need for respiratory support, and invasive mechanical ventilation are all listed ICU admission criteria.  Why E wrong: A stable patient needing routine ward care does not warrant ICU admission."},{"section":"Batch 054 · ICU & HDU definitions and characteristics…","question":"Under \"The Following Critically Ill Patients are NOT to be admitted in ICU,\" which condition precludes ICU admission?","options":["A patient in established shock","A patient at rest-of-kin (patient's family member) informed refusal to be admitted in ICU","A patient with new-onset respiratory failure","A patient with acute renal failure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A patient or rest-of-kin (family member) informed refusal to be admitted in ICU is a stated reason not to admit. Other non-admission criteria include: any disease with a treatment limitation plan; anyone with a living will or advanced directive against ICU care; terminally ill patients with a medical judgment of futility; and low priority cases in case of pandemic or disaster situation where there is resource limitation (e.g., bed, workforce, equipment).  Why A, C, D wrong: Shock, new respiratory failure, and acute renal failure are admission indications, not exclusions."},{"section":"Batch 054 · ICU & HDU definitions and characteristics…","question":"Which of the following are reasons a critically ill patient should NOT be admitted to the ICU? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Patient or kin informed refusal of ICU admission","Any disease with a treatment limitation plan","Anyone with a living will or advanced directive against ICU care","Terminally ill patients with a medical judgment of futility","Low-priority cases in a pandemic/disaster with resource limitation"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: All five — informed refusal, a treatment-limitation plan, a living will/advance directive, terminal illness with futility judgment, and low-priority cases under resource limitation in a pandemic/disaster — are listed as reasons not to admit to ICU.  Why none excluded: Each option matches a stated non-admission criterion."},{"section":"Batch 054 · ICU & HDU definitions and characteristics…","question":"Which statement reflects an ICU Discharge Criterion?","options":["Worsening of physiological abnormalities","Return of physiological alterations to near normal or baseline status","New onset of multi-organ failure","Need to start invasive ventilation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: ICU discharge criteria include return of physiological alterations to near normal or baseline status; reasonable resolution and stability of the acute illness that necessitated ICU admission; and a patient/family agreement for ICU discharge for a treatment-limiting decision or palliative care.  Why A, C, D wrong: Worsening abnormalities, new multi-organ failure, and need for invasive ventilation are indications to remain in (or be admitted to) the ICU, not to discharge."},{"section":"Batch 054 · ICU & HDU definitions and characteristics…","question":"While a critically ill patient is awaiting an ICU bed, the minimum monitoring required does NOT routinely include:","options":["Blood pressure (continuous/intermittent)","Heart rate (continuous/intermittent)","Oxygen saturation – SpO2 (continuous/intermittent)","Daily 12-lead exercise stress testing"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The minimum monitoring required while awaiting an ICU bed includes blood pressure (continuous/intermittent); clinical monitoring (pulse rate, respiratory rate, breathing pattern, etc.); heart rate (continuous/intermittent); oxygen saturation – SpO2 (continuous/intermittent); capillary refill time; urine output (continuous/intermittent); neurological status (e.g., Glasgow Coma Scale – GCS, Alert Verbal Pain Unresponsive – AVPU scale, etc.); intermittent temperature monitoring; and blood sugar. Daily exercise stress testing is not part of this minimum monitoring.  Why A, B, C wrong: These are explicitly part of the minimum monitoring set."},{"section":"Batch 054 · ICU & HDU definitions and characteristics…","question":"The minimum neurological monitoring required while awaiting an ICU bed uses which assessment tools?","options":["APGAR and Braden scales","Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS) and Alert Verbal Pain Unresponsive (AVPU) scale","Morse Fall Scale and Norton scale","Wong-Baker faces and FLACC scale"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Neurological status is monitored with the Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS) and the Alert Verbal Pain Unresponsive (AVPU) scale.  Why A wrong: APGAR (newborn) and Braden (pressure injury) are not neurological-LOC scales here.  Why C wrong: Morse and Norton assess fall risk and pressure injury risk respectively.  Why D wrong: Wong-Baker and FLACC are pain scales, not LOC tools."},{"section":"Batch 054 · ICU & HDU definitions and characteristics…","question":"Before transferring a critically ill patient to the ICU, the FIRST priority in minimum stabilization is to ensure:","options":["Adequate oxygenation and ventilation, including securing the airway (e.g., tracheal intubation if GCS ≤ 8)","Completion of all laboratory tests","Family counseling is documented","The patient's bed is fully cleaned"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Minimum stabilization before transferring requires ensuring an airway (e.g., tracheal intubation if the patient has a GCS ≤ 8) and ensuring adequate oxygenation and ventilation; stable haemodynamics either with or without vasoactive drug infusion; ongoing correction of hyperglycemia/hypoglycemia and other life-threatening electrolyte/metabolic disturbances; and initiation of definitive therapy for the life-threatening condition (e.g., external fixation of a fractured limb, administration of anti-epileptics for recurrent seizures, anti-arrhythmics for arrhythmias, IV antibiotics for sepsis).  Why B, C, D wrong: Airway/oxygenation/ventilation take priority over completing labs, documenting counseling, or bed cleaning before transfer."},{"section":"Batch 054 · ICU & HDU definitions and characteristics…","question":"During inter-facility transfer of a critically ill patient, which monitoring parameter must be maintained continuously?","options":["Continuous heart rate, blood pressure, and continuous SpO2","Daily weight only","Intake-output charting once per shift only","Bedside blood culture only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Minimum monitoring required for transferring a critically ill patient (inter-facility transfer to hospital/ICU) includes blood pressure (continuous/intermittent), clinical monitoring (pulse rate, respiratory rate, breathing pattern, etc.), continuous heart rate, continuous SpO2, and neurological status (AVPU, GCS, etc.).  Why B, C, D wrong: Daily weight, once-a-shift I/O, and a single blood culture are not the continuous-transfer monitoring set."},{"section":"Batch 054 · ICU & HDU definitions and characteristics…","question":"Which is a stated step-up criterion for transferring a patient from HDU to ICU?","options":["Resolution of the illness with reasonable clinical stability","Requiring invasive ventilatory support","Multi-organ failure recovering","Patient ready for ward transfer"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Step-up criteria for transfer from HDU to ICU include requiring invasive ventilatory support; haemodynamic instability requiring significant/increasing vasopressor/inotrope support; multi-organ failure or worsening one or more organ system dysfunctions; and continuous or intensive monitoring required for any reason/condition.  Why A, C, D wrong: Resolution of illness, recovering organ failure, and readiness for ward transfer are step-DOWN (ICU→HDU) directions, not step-up."},{"section":"Batch 054 · ICU & HDU definitions and characteristics…","question":"Which is a step-down criterion for transferring a patient from ICU to HDU?","options":["Requiring escalating vasopressor support","Resolution of the disease which led to admission, with reasonable clinical stability that allows for at least 24 hours of monitoring","Onset of new multi-organ failure","Need for invasive mechanical ventilation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Step-down criteria for transfer from ICU to HDU include resolution of the disease with reasonable clinical stability so the patient can be looked after with less intensive monitoring/support for at least 24 hours, and strict monitoring still required for such transferred patients in the HDU as the clinical condition may again worsen and some patients may again need to be shifted back to ICU.  Why A, C, D wrong: Escalating vasopressors, new multi-organ failure, and invasive ventilation are step-UP criteria (toward the ICU)."},{"section":"Batch 054 · ICU & HDU definitions and characteristics…","question":"Which is a criterion for transfer from HDU to a general ward?","options":["Once the underlying physiologic condition that necessitated admission to HDU gets resolved or stabilized, for a reasonable period of time, the patient can be considered for transfer from HDU to the ward","The patient develops worsening organ dysfunction","The patient requires continuous vasopressor infusion","The patient needs invasive ventilation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Criteria for transfer from HDU to ward: once the underlying physiologic condition that necessitated HDU admission gets resolved or stabilized for a reasonable period, the patient can be considered for transfer to the ward.  Why B, C, D wrong: Worsening dysfunction, continuous vasopressors, and invasive ventilation are reasons to keep the patient in higher-acuity care, not to transfer to a general ward."},{"section":"Batch 054 · ICU & HDU definitions and characteristics…","question":"A Critical Care Unit is divided into four major areas. Which set correctly lists them?","options":["Waiting area, Changing room, Critical care complex, Cleaning area","Operating room, Recovery room, Storage room, Office","Reception, Pharmacy, Laboratory, Mortuary","Triage, Casualty, Imaging, Dialysis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Critical Care Unit is divided into four major areas: (1) Waiting area – counselling area and trolley area; (2) Changing room – parents'/attendants' and staff changing room separately; (3) Critical care complex – nursing station, linen store, critical equipment store, therapy area, isolation room, ICU and HDU beds; and (4) Cleaning area.  Why B, C, D wrong: These lists do not match the four named CCU areas."},{"section":"Batch 054 · ICU & HDU definitions and characteristics…","question":"Under \"Categories of ICU care in a patient,\" which of the following falls under Supportive care?","options":["Make differential diagnosis","Airway management, haemodynamic support, oxygenation","Prevention of stress ulcers","Spiritual and cultural assistance"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Supportive care includes airway management, haemodynamic support, and oxygenation.  Why A wrong: Making differential diagnoses falls under Treatment of primary illness.  Why C wrong: Prevention of stress ulcers is under Preventive care.  Why D wrong: Spiritual and cultural assistance is under Psychological counseling."},{"section":"Batch 054 · ICU & HDU definitions and characteristics…","question":"Treatment of primary illness in the ICU includes which of the following? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Make differential diagnoses","Necessary investigations","Adequate treatment with antibiotics","Haemodynamic support","Spiritual counseling"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Treatment of primary illness includes making differential diagnoses, necessary investigations, and adequate treatment with antibiotics.  Why D wrong: Haemodynamic support is Supportive care.  Why E wrong: Spiritual counseling is Psychological counseling."},{"section":"Batch 054 · ICU & HDU definitions and characteristics…","question":"Which of the following are listed under Preventive care in the ICU? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Prevention of stress ulcers","Prevention of bed sores","Ventilator associated pneumonia (VAP) prevention","Prevention of nosocomial infection","Prevention of DVT"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Preventive care includes prevention of stress ulcers, prevention of bed sores, ventilator-associated pneumonia (VAP), prevention of nosocomial infection, and prevention of DVT.  Why none excluded: All five appear in the Preventive care category."},{"section":"Batch 054 · ICU & HDU definitions and characteristics…","question":"Psychological counseling as a category of ICU care includes:","options":["Antibiotic treatment and VAP prevention","Psychological support to patient and family members, plus spiritual and cultural assistance","Airway management and oxygenation","DVT prophylaxis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Psychological counseling comprises psychological support to the patient and family members and spiritual and cultural assistance.  Why A wrong: Antibiotics/VAP prevention fall under treatment and preventive care.  Why C wrong: Airway/oxygenation is supportive care.  Why D wrong: DVT prophylaxis is preventive care."},{"section":"Batch 054 · ICU & HDU definitions and characteristics…","question":"According to the \"Must Remember about ICU & HDU\" environmental standards, the recommended HVAC system requirement is:","options":["6 Air Changes Per Hour (ACPH)","10–12 Air Changes Per Hour (ACH) for the general ICU/HDU HVAC","45 ACH","No specified air-change requirement"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For Heating, Ventilation and Air-conditioning system (HVAC): the ICU/HDU requires 10–12 Air Changes Per Hour (ACH); an isolation room requires 6 Air Changes Per Hour (ACPH); and the OT requires 15 Air Changes Per Hour (ACPH).  Why A wrong: 6 ACPH applies to the isolation room specifically.  Why C wrong: 45 is the light-intensity value (foot-candles) at bedside, not air changes.  Why D wrong: An air-change requirement is explicitly specified."},{"section":"Batch 054 · ICU & HDU definitions and characteristics…","question":"Match the area to its required air changes per hour: an Operating Theatre (OT) requires:","options":["6 ACPH","10–12 ACH","15 Air Changes Per Hour (ACPH)","45 ACPH"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The OT requires 15 Air Changes Per Hour (ACPH).  Why A wrong: 6 ACPH is for the isolation room.  Why B wrong: 10–12 ACH is the general ICU/HDU requirement.  Why D wrong: 45 is a lighting value, not air changes."},{"section":"Batch 054 · ICU & HDU definitions and characteristics…","question":"The recommended ICU/HDU room temperature should be maintained at approximately:","options":["16–18°C","21–24°C","28–30°C","35°C"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Must Remember note specifies the ICU/HDU temperature should be 21–24°C.  Why A, C, D wrong: These ranges fall outside the stated 21–24°C target."},{"section":"Batch 054 · ICU & HDU definitions and characteristics…","question":"The recommended light intensity at the bedside in the ICU/HDU should be at least:","options":["100 lux","200 lux","300 lux (clean ≈ 45 lux / 45 foot-candles throughout, with bedside fitting providing at least 300 lux)","1000 lux"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The note states Light: clean LED light fitting with illumination at bedside must be at least 300 lux (general illumination ~45 throughout, with the bedside reaching at least 300 lux).  Why A, B, D wrong: 100, 200, and 1000 lux do not match the stated minimum bedside requirement of 300 lux."},{"section":"Batch 054 · ICU & HDU definitions and characteristics…","question":"According to the Must Remember note, the noise level in the ICU/HDU should be kept (as per the International Noise Council recommendations) at or below approximately:","options":["90 dB(A) by day and 80 dB(A) at night","45 dB(A) by day and 35 dB(A) at night, with the night value (20 dB[A]) being the most stringent","100 dB(A) at all times","There is no noise standard"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Noise: as per INC (International Noise Council) recommendations, the noise level should not exceed approximately 45 dB(A) by day, 40 dB(A) evening, and around 20 dB(A) at night.  Why A and C wrong: 90/80/100 dB(A) are far above the recommended quiet ICU environment.  Why D wrong: A noise standard is explicitly provided."},{"section":"Batch 054 · ICU & HDU definitions and characteristics…","question":"Regarding curtains in the ICU/HDU, the Must Remember note recommends:","options":["Heavy cotton curtains that are difficult to clean","Preferably 3-colors i.e., blue, yellow, and red/pink (not used in HVAC/ICU) as they interfere with identifying cyanosis, icterus, and pallor respectively","No curtains should ever be used","Black curtains for complete darkness"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Curtains: preferably 3-colors i.e., blue, yellow, and red/pink should NOT be used in HDU/ICU as they interfere with identifying cyanosis, icterus, and pallor respectively.  Why A wrong: Curtains should be easy to clean, not heavy/hard to clean.  Why C wrong: Curtains are used; the note restricts certain colors, not their presence entirely.  Why D wrong: Black curtains for darkness are not recommended."},{"section":"Batch 054 · ICU & HDU definitions and characteristics…","question":"The type of fire extinguisher recommended for use in the ICU/HDU is:","options":["Water-type extinguisher","ABC-type/dry powder extinguisher, since fluids cannot escape route planned, as a clean agent that does not damage equipment","Foam-type extinguisher only","No extinguisher is needed in the ICU"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note specifies an ABC-type (clean-agent/sufficient) fire extinguisher that has a separate fire-escape route planned; it should ensure power back-up.  Why A and C wrong: Water and foam types can damage sensitive electrical/electronic ICU equipment.  Why D wrong: A fire extinguisher and an escape route are required safety provisions."},{"section":"Batch 054 · ICU & HDU definitions and characteristics…","question":"Per IPHS guidelines 2022, the Human Resource for a Critical Care Unit should be deployed/transferred to:","options":["Other general wards on rotation","Other areas (i.e., the human resource in critical care should not be deployed/transferred to other areas)","The outpatient department","Administrative offices"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: As per Public Health Standards (IPHS) guidelines 2022, the Human Resource in critical care should NOT be deployed/transferred to other areas, to preserve critical-care competency and continuity.  Why A, C, D wrong: Reassigning critical-care staff to general wards, OPD, or offices contradicts the IPHS recommendation."},{"section":"Batch 054 · ICU & HDU definitions and characteristics…","question":"According to the staffing norms table, the recommended nurse-to-bed ratio for the Medical Officer/ICU and the General ICU is intended to ensure high-acuity coverage. Per the table, the Nursing Officer (General ICU) staffing norm is:","options":["1:10","1:1 for General ICU and 1:2 for Pediatric ICU","1:3","1:100"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The staffing norms table lists the Nursing Officer (General ICU) at 1:1 (one nurse per bed) and Nursing Officer (including pediatric) where pediatric ICU is 1:2.  Why A wrong: 1:10 corresponds to the Medical Officer and Sanitation Staff norms.  Why C wrong: 1:3 corresponds to the Nursing Officer (General HDU).  Why D wrong: 1:100 is not a listed value."},{"section":"Batch 054 · ICU & HDU definitions and characteristics…","question":"Per the staffing norms table, the Sanitation Staff norm in the critical care unit is approximately:","options":["1:1","1:3","1:10","1:50"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists Sanitation Staff at 1:10 (one per 10 beds), and the Medical Officer at 1:10 as well.  Why A wrong: 1:1 is the General ICU nursing ratio.  Why B wrong: 1:3 is the General HDU nursing ratio.  Why D wrong: 1:50 is not a listed value."},{"section":"Batch 054 · ICU & HDU definitions and characteristics…","question":"According to the job description, an Intensivist (for adult patients) is:","options":["A sanitation supervisor","A specialist who has been trained to manage critically ill patients and, if feasible, the first choice of a person as an intensivist should be a specialist who has a postgraduation degree in Anaesthesiology/Medicine/Chest medicine/Pediatrics with further subspecialty training of at least a year in intensive care, with a structured exit exam and certification process","A pharmacist who dispenses ICU drugs","A technician who maintains equipment"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: An intensivist (for adult patients) is a specialist trained to manage critically ill patients; ideally a specialist with a PG degree in Anaesthesiology/Medicine/Chest medicine/Pediatrics with further subspecialty training of at least a year in intensive care, having a structured exit exam and certification. In case there are no intensivists available, an Anaesthesiologist/Internal Medicine physician/Chest physician/Pediatrician may, by virtue of experience in critical care medicine and the prescribed curriculum, work under their respective subject of training and be considered as an intensivist.  Why A, C, D wrong: Sanitation, pharmacy, and technician roles are distinct from the intensivist."},{"section":"Batch 054 · ICU & HDU definitions and characteristics…","question":"Per the job description, who is the primary person responsible for patient care and coordination with different specialties, rounding the clock?","options":["The Sanitation staff","The Intensivist","The Technician","The Housekeeping supervisor"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The intensivist is the primary person responsible for patient care and coordination with different specialties; they should also be part of antibiotic stewardship.  Why A, C, D wrong: Support roles are not the primary patient-care coordinator."},{"section":"Batch 054 · ICU & HDU definitions and characteristics…","question":"According to the Specialist job description, a Specialist should round the clock for which areas?","options":["Only the general medical ward","OT, ICU, Emergency, delivery, and other areas as per IPHS — taking treatment decisions implemented after consultation with the intensivist who is in charge of the ICU","The pharmacy and stores only","Outpatient clinics only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Specialists should round the clock for OT, ICU, Emergency, delivery, and other areas as per IPHS; specialists of different disciplines will take rounds of the patients admitted under them on visiting and during day-on-call as they do in other wards. They will suggest their treatment decisions, which will be implemented after consultation with the intensivist who is in charge of the ICU. Supportive care and baseline management during 8 hr shift-duty including handling equipment present in the unit.  Why A, C, D wrong: The specialist's coverage is broad (OT/ICU/Emergency/delivery), not limited to ward, pharmacy, or OPD only."},{"section":"Batch 054 · ICU & HDU definitions and characteristics…","question":"Per the Medical Officer job description in the ICU, the Medical Officer for ICU beds covers:","options":["1 Medical Officer for 10 ICU beds (or 10 beds in a critical care area, and 1 for 20 beds in two critical areas), interpreting basic pathological/biochemical tests and doing basic tests such as POC tests under procedures like CPR, ABG, central line insertion, etc.","Only administrative paperwork with no clinical duties","Sanitation and cleaning of the ICU","Maintenance of biomedical equipment only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: 1 Medical Officer for 10 ICU beds or 10 beds in a critical care area, and 1 for 20 beds in two critical areas; interpreting basic pathological/biochemical tests; doing basic tests, e.g., POC tests, under procedures like CPR, ABG, central line insertion, etc.; and one of the MOs will act as in-charge and will discharge additional administrative duties.  Why B, C, D wrong: The Medical Officer has clinical duties, not solely paperwork, cleaning, or equipment maintenance."},{"section":"Batch 054 · ICU & HDU definitions and characteristics…","question":"Which responsibilities belong to the Nursing in Charge per the job description? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Supervisory, logistics management, and regular reporting","Preparing daily roster of nursing staff","Work under the intensivist(s) in-charge and help with improving the clinical, administrative services for the ICU","Interpreting differential diagnoses for prescribing antibiotics independently","Performing major surgical operations"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing in Charge duties include supervisory, logistics management, and regular reporting; preparing the daily roster of nursing staff; and working under the intensivist(s) in-charge to help improve the clinical and administrative services for the ICU.  Why D wrong: Independent antibiotic prescribing/differential diagnosis is a physician/intensivist responsibility.  Why E wrong: Performing major surgery is not a nurse-in-charge duty."},{"section":"Batch 054 · ICU & HDU definitions and characteristics…","question":"Per the Nursing Officer job description, the staffing/coverage pattern is:","options":["1:1 ICU, 1:2 Step Down Unit like HDU, 1:6 wards and other areas, on an OT-2 per OT per shift; 1 nurse rounds the clock for delivery, etc.","1 nurse for 50 ICU beds","No nurse coverage during night shift","1 nurse for the entire hospital"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing Officer: 1:1 ICU, 1:2 Step Down Unit like HDU, 1:6 wards and other areas; OT–2 per OT per shift; 1 nurse rounds the clock for delivery, etc. Patient care during 8 hr shift duty including handling equipment present in the unit and other things.  Why B, C, D wrong: These contradict the stated 1:1 ICU and continuous-coverage ratios."},{"section":"Batch 054 · ICU & HDU definitions and characteristics…","question":"Which clinical tasks are part of the Nursing Officer's patient-care duties during an 8-hour shift? (Select all that apply.)","options":["General nursing care and Basic Life Support/Basic Cardiac Life Support","Administration of medication(s) through all routes including intravenous","Simple blood testing like Blood Glucose by glucometer and recording ECG","Airway suction and nebulisation","Maintenance of clinical parameters/multipara monitors and ventilator parameters"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing Officer patient-care duties include general nursing care; Basic Life Support/Basic Cardiac Life Support; administration of medication through all routes including IV; simple blood testing like blood glucose by glucometer; recording ECG; airway suction and nebulisation; monitoring of clinical parameters (manually and via multipara monitors)/ventilator parameters; maintenance of different charts; sampling, blood and body fluids; managing requisitions for tests; and maintenance and keeping ready stock of drugs, equipment, and consumables, etc.  Why none excluded: All listed tasks are stated Nursing Officer duties."},{"section":"Batch 054 · ICU & HDU definitions and characteristics…","question":"Which tasks are listed under the Technician's role during the 8-hour shift (basic pathological/biochemical work)? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Basic Life Support/Basic Cardiac Life Support and chest physiotherapy","Oxygen therapy including handling oxygen manifold and centralized oxygen supply","Assisting MOs in performing different procedures and assisting bedside dialysis (where available) in collaboration with dialysis technician","Blood sampling by peripheral venepuncture and assisting in USG/Echocardiography (if available)/X-ray procedure","Computation and clerical work"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Technician duties include BLS/BCLS, chest physiotherapy, oxygen therapy (oxygen manifold and centralized oxygen supply), assisting advanced life support and other resuscitative life support clinical courses, assisting MOs in performing different procedures, assisting bedside dialysis (where available) in collaboration with dialysis technician, blood sampling by peripheral venepuncture, basic blood test by biochemical analysers/cell counter/glucometer/arterial blood gas analyser/electrolyte analyser, maintenance of equipment present in the unit (manually/via multipara monitors and ventilator parameters round the clock), and computation/clerical work.  Why none excluded: All listed items are stated Technician duties."},{"section":"Batch 054 · ICU & HDU definitions and characteristics…","question":"Under the Housekeeping support-staff norms (as per IPHS/GOI), the cleaning staff is responsible for:","options":["Prescribing antibiotics","Cleaning and mopping with disinfectant of equipment, beds, floor, ceilings, and toilets, etc., as per cleaning protocols","Interpreting laboratory results","Administering intravenous medications"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Housekeeping/cleaning staff are responsible for cleaning and mopping with disinfectant of equipment, beds, floor, ceilings, and toilets, etc., as per cleaning protocols; responsible for transportation and storage of all waste material including bio-medical waste (BMW) at the designated BMW storage room; cleaning blood spills and others such as human excrement, urine, vomitus, sterile body fluids, etc., when required; and staff should also be trained for bed making, patients' personal hygiene, turning and positioning patients, transfer of patients from wards to other areas, and other patient care activities.  Why A, C, D wrong: Prescribing, lab interpretation, and IV medication are clinical roles, not housekeeping duties."},{"section":"Batch 054 · ICU & HDU definitions and characteristics…","question":"Sepsis is BEST defined as:","options":["A localized skin infection with no systemic effect","A potentially life-threatening condition that arises when the body's response to infection causes injury to its own tissues and organs","A normal immune response to a minor wound","A non-infectious allergic reaction"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Sepsis is a potentially life-threatening condition that arises when the body's response to infection causes injury to its own tissues and organs; multiple organs may fail due to severe sepsis.  Why A, C, D wrong: Sepsis is a dysregulated systemic response to infection, not a localized infection, a normal minor response, or a non-infectious allergy."},{"section":"Batch 054 · ICU & HDU definitions and characteristics…","question":"Sepsis can be divided into which three stages?","options":["Mild, moderate, severe","Sepsis, severe sepsis, septic shock","Acute, subacute, chronic","Compensated, decompensated, irreversible"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Sepsis can be divided into three stages (mnemonic Trick-SS): (1) Sepsis, (2) Severe sepsis, and (3) Septic shock.  Why A, C, D wrong: These staging schemes are not the ones given for sepsis in the text."},{"section":"Batch 054 · ICU & HDU definitions and characteristics…","question":"Which of the following are listed clinical features of sepsis (mnemonic Trick-SEPSIS)? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Shivering, fever, or very cold","Extreme pain or general discomfort (\"worst ever\")","Pale or discoloured skin","Sleepy, difficult to rouse, confused","\"I feel like I might die\" / Shortness of breath"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The clinical features of sepsis (Trick-SEPSIS) are: S – Shivering, fever, or very cold; E – Extreme pain or general discomfort (\"worst ever\"); P – Pale or discoloured skin; S – Sleepy, difficult to rouse, confused; I – \"I feel like I might die\"; S – Shortness of breath.  Why none excluded: All five options correspond to the listed SEPSIS clinical features."},{"section":"Batch 054 · ICU & HDU definitions and characteristics…","question":"Regarding management of sepsis, which fluid and dose are specified for crystalloid resuscitation in hypotension?","options":["IV fluids (crystalloids): 30 ml/hour for hypotension","IV fluids (crystalloids): 30 ml/kg for hypotension","IV fluids (colloids): 10 ml/kg over 24 hours","No IV fluids are given in sepsis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Management of sepsis includes antibiotics; IV fluids (crystalloids): 30 ml/kg for hypotension; and a vasopressor of choice: NORAD (noradrenaline/norepinephrine).  Why A wrong: The dose is weight-based (30 ml/kg), not \"30 ml/hour.\"  Why C wrong: Crystalloids at 30 ml/kg are specified, not colloids at 10 ml/kg.  Why D wrong: IV crystalloid fluids are a cornerstone of sepsis resuscitation."},{"section":"Batch 054 · ICU & HDU definitions and characteristics…","question":"Per the sepsis management list, the vasopressor of choice is:","options":["Dopamine","Adrenaline (epinephrine)","NORAD (noradrenaline/norepinephrine)","Dobutamine"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The management list names the vaso-pressure of choice as NORAD (noradrenaline/norepinephrine).  Why A, B, D wrong: Dopamine, adrenaline, and dobutamine are not the first-line vasopressor stated here for sepsis."},{"section":"Batch 054 · ICU & HDU definitions and characteristics…","question":"Scoring systems in ICU are used to assess and evaluate the critical condition of a patient. Which of the following are listed as \"First-day scoring systems\"? (Select all that apply.)","options":["APACHE (Acute Physiology and Chronic Health Evaluation)","SAPS (Simplified Acute Physiology Score)","MPM (Mortality Prediction Model)","SOFA (Sequential Organ Failure Assessment)","MODS (Multiple Organ Dysfunction Score)"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: First-day scoring systems most commonly used include APACHE (Acute Physiology and Chronic Health Evaluation), SAPS (Simplified Acute Physiology Score), and MPM (Mortality Prediction Model).  Why D and E wrong: SOFA and MODS are listed under \"Repetitive scoring systems,\" not first-day scores."},{"section":"Batch 054 · ICU & HDU definitions and characteristics…","question":"Which scoring systems are listed under \"Repetitive scoring systems\" in the ICU? (Select all that apply.)","options":["OSF (Organ System Failure)","SOFA (Sequential Organ Failure Assessment)","ODIN (Organ Dysfunction and Infection System)","MODS (Multiple Organ Dysfunction Score)","LOD (Logistic Organ Dysfunction)"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Repetitive scoring systems include OSF (Organ System Failure), SOFA (Sequential Organ Failure Assessment), ODIN (Organ Dysfunction and Infection System), MODS (Multiple Organ Dysfunction Score), and LOD (Logistic Organ Dysfunction).  Why none excluded: All five are listed as repetitive scoring systems."},{"section":"Batch 054 · ICU & HDU definitions and characteristics…","question":"The SOFA score is primarily used to:","options":["Diagnose fractures","Predict mortality in septic patients","Assess fall risk","Measure nutritional status"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The SOFA score is used to predict mortality in septic patients.  Why A, C, D wrong: SOFA assesses organ dysfunction/mortality risk, not fractures, fall risk, or nutrition."},{"section":"Batch 054 · ICU & HDU definitions and characteristics…","question":"The qSOFA (quick SOFA) is BEST described as:","options":["A complex 24-hour laboratory-intensive score","A bedside score that does not need labs and can be assessed in a few seconds; the more qSOFA points, the higher the risk of poor outcome","A radiology scoring system","A nutritional screening tool"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: qSOFA is a bedside score with suspected infection; it does not need labs and can be assessed in a few seconds. The more qSOFA points, the higher the risk of poor outcome. The qSOFA score ranges from 0 to 3 points; the presence of 2 or more qSOFA points near the onset of infection is associated with a greater risk of death (or prolonged intensive-care unit stay).  Why A, C, D wrong: qSOFA is rapid and bedside (no labs), not a complex lab score, radiology score, or nutrition tool."},{"section":"Batch 054 · ICU & HDU definitions and characteristics…","question":"A patient with suspected infection scores 2 qSOFA points. This is associated with:","options":["No increased risk","A greater risk of death or prolonged ICU stay","Immediate discharge readiness","A normal physiological state"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The presence of 2 or more qSOFA points near the onset of infection is associated with a greater risk of death or prolonged ICU stay.  Why A, C, D wrong: A qSOFA of ≥2 signals high risk, not low risk, discharge readiness, or normalcy."},{"section":"Batch 054 · ICU & HDU definitions and characteristics…","question":"In the Sequential Organ Failure Assessment (Quick) — qSOFA — which three components each score 1 point?","options":["Heart rate, temperature, urine output","Low blood pressure (SBP ≤ 100 mmHg), high respiratory rate (≥ 22 breaths/min), and altered mentation (GCS < 15)","Blood glucose, oxygen saturation, capillary refill","Pupil size, skin colour, breath sounds"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The qSOFA assessment assigns 1 point each for: low blood pressure (Systolic BP ≤ 100 mmHg), high respiratory rate (≥ 22 breaths/min), and altered mentation (GCS < 15).  Why A, C, D wrong: These parameters are not the three qSOFA criteria."},{"section":"Batch 054 · ICU & HDU definitions and characteristics…","question":"According to the SIRS criteria for sepsis, which body-temperature thresholds meet a SIRS criterion?","options":["Temperature 36.5–37.5°C","Temperature > 38°C or < 36°C","Temperature > 40°C only","Temperature exactly 37°C"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: SIRS (Systemic Inflammatory Response Syndrome) criteria include body temperature > 38°C or < 36°C; a heart rate > 90 beats per minute; tachypnea manifested by respiratory rate greater than 20 per minute or, on blood gas, a PaCO2 of less than 32 mmHg (4.3 kPa); and an abnormality in the white blood cell count, e.g., > 12,000/cu mm or < 4,000/cu mm, or > 10 percent immature neutrophils within a certain time frame.  Why A, C, D wrong: A normal/narrow temperature range does not meet a SIRS criterion; only > 38°C or < 36°C qualifies."},{"section":"Batch 054 · ICU & HDU definitions and characteristics…","question":"Which of the following are listed SIRS criteria for sepsis? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Body temperature > 38°C or < 36°C","Heart rate > 90 beats per minute","Tachypnea (respiratory rate > 20/min) or PaCO2 < 32 mmHg (4.3 kPa)","WBC > 12,000/cu mm or < 4,000/cu mm, or > 10% immature neutrophils","Blood pressure exactly 120/80 mmHg"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: SIRS criteria are: temperature > 38°C or < 36°C; heart rate > 90/min; tachypnea (RR > 20/min) or PaCO2 < 32 mmHg; and abnormal WBC count (> 12,000/cu mm or < 4,000/cu mm, or > 10% immature neutrophils).  Why E wrong: A specific normal blood pressure of 120/80 mmHg is not a SIRS criterion."},{"section":"Batch 054 · ICU & HDU definitions and characteristics…","question":"According to the SIRS-to-shock spectrum diagram, the progression is correctly ordered as:","options":["Septic shock → Severe sepsis → Sepsis → SIRS","SIRS → Sepsis (≥ 2 SIRS criteria + documented/confirmed source of infection) → Severe sepsis (sepsis + end-organ damage/hypotension/lactate ≥ 4 mmol) → Septic shock (severe sepsis with persistent hypotension despite resuscitation + end-organ damage)","Sepsis → SIRS → Septic shock → Severe sepsis","Severe sepsis → Septic shock → SIRS → Sepsis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram progresses SIRS → Sepsis (≥ 2 SIRS criteria plus a documented or confirmed source of infection, NUF) → Severe sepsis (sepsis with end-organ damage, hypotension SBP < 90, or lactate ≥ 4 mmol) → Septic shock (severe sepsis with persistent hypotension despite fluid resuscitation plus documentation of end-organ damage).  Why A, C, D wrong: These reverse or scramble the correct escalating order."},{"section":"Batch 054 · ICU & HDU definitions and characteristics…","question":"Per the sepsis spectrum diagram, \"Severe sepsis\" is defined as sepsis PLUS which of the following? (Select all that apply.)","options":["End-organ damage","Hypotension (SBP < 90 mmHg)","Lactate ≥ 4 mmol","A documented source of infection","A normal lactate with no organ dysfunction"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Severe sepsis = sepsis + end-organ damage, hypotension (SBP < 90 mmHg), or lactate ≥ 4 mmol.  Why D wrong: A documented source of infection is part of defining Sepsis (≥ 2 SIRS criteria + infection), not the additional criterion for SEVERE sepsis.  Why E wrong: Normal lactate with no organ dysfunction does not indicate severe sepsis."},{"section":"Batch 054 · ICU & HDU definitions and characteristics…","question":"Per the spectrum diagram, Septic shock is BEST characterized as:","options":["SIRS without infection","Severe sepsis with persistent hypotension despite fluid resuscitation, plus documentation of end-organ damage","Sepsis with a normal blood pressure","An isolated fever with no hypotension"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Septic shock is severe sepsis with persistent hypotension (refractory to fluid resuscitation) and documentation of end-organ damage.  Why A, C, D wrong: SIRS without infection, normotensive sepsis, and isolated fever do not meet the septic shock definition."},{"section":"Batch 054 · ICU & HDU definitions and characteristics…","question":"A nurse is preparing to transfer a patient with a GCS of 7 to the ICU. Based on the minimum stabilization criteria, the priority intervention before transfer is to:","options":["Obtain family consent forms first","Secure the airway (tracheal intubation) to ensure adequate oxygenation and ventilation","Start enteral feeding","Apply a fall-risk wristband"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Minimum stabilization requires securing the airway with tracheal intubation if the patient has a GCS ≤ 8 (a GCS of 7 qualifies), ensuring adequate oxygenation and ventilation before transfer.  Why A, C, D wrong: Consent paperwork, feeding, and fall-risk labeling do not take precedence over airway protection in a patient with GCS ≤ 8."},{"section":"Batch 054 · ICU & HDU definitions and characteristics…","question":"A nurse caring for an HDU patient notes increasing vasopressor requirement and worsening of two organ systems. Based on transfer criteria, the appropriate action is to:","options":["Discharge the patient to the general ward","Step the patient up from HDU to ICU","Continue HDU care with no change","Reduce monitoring frequency"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Step-up criteria from HDU to ICU include haemodynamic instability requiring significant/increasing vasopressor support and worsening of one or more organ system dysfunctions, warranting transfer to the ICU.  Why A wrong: Worsening dysfunction is the opposite of a ward-discharge criterion.  Why C wrong: Unchanged HDU care is unsafe given escalating support needs.  Why D wrong: Monitoring should increase, not decrease, in deterioration."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"A critically ill patient in the ICU is pulling at lines or tubes and is aggressive. The nurse documents this on the Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale (RASS). Which RASS score best represents this behaviour?","options":["+4 (Combative)","+2 (Agitated)","0 (Alert and calm)","-2 (Light sedation)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: RASS +2 = \"Agitated,\" described as frequent non-purposeful movements, fighting the ventilator (pulling at lines/tubes is non-purposeful, aggressive movement).  Why A wrong: +4 (Combative) is violent and an immediate danger to staff, more extreme than described.  Why C wrong: 0 is alert and calm, the neutral score, not agitated.  Why D wrong: -2 (Light sedation) indicates a sedated, not agitated, patient."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"On the Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, a score of +4 indicates which patient state?","options":["Restless","Very agitated","Combative — violent, immediate danger to staff","Alert and calm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: RASS +4 = \"Combative,\" defined as violent and an immediate danger to staff.  Why A wrong: Restless is +1 (anxious, apprehensive but movements not aggressive/vigorous).  Why B wrong: Very agitated is +3 (pulls or removes tubes, fights ventilator).  Why D wrong: Alert and calm is the score 0."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"A patient on the RASS scale \"pulls or removes tubes and fights the ventilator.\" What RASS score does the nurse assign?","options":["+4","+3","+1","-1"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: RASS +3 = \"Very agitated,\" described as pulls or removes tubes/catheters and fights the ventilator.  Why A wrong: +4 (Combative) is violent/dangerous to staff.  Why C wrong: +1 (Restless) is anxious/apprehensive but movements are not aggressive.  Why D wrong: -1 (Drowsy) is a sedated, not agitated, state."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"On the RASS scale, which score corresponds to \"Restless\"?","options":["+1","+2","0","-1"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: RASS +1 = \"Restless,\" anxious or apprehensive but movements not aggressive or vigorous.  Why B wrong: +2 is Agitated.  Why C wrong: 0 is Alert and calm.  Why D wrong: -1 is Drowsy."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"A nurse assesses a sedated ICU patient who is not fully awake but has sustained awakening (eye opening and contact) to voice for more than 10 seconds. Which RASS score applies?","options":["-1 (Drowsy)","-2 (Light sedation)","-3 (Moderate sedation)","-4 (Deep sedation)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: RASS -1 = \"Drowsy,\" defined as not fully alert but with sustained awakening (eye opening/contact) to voice for >10 seconds.  Why B wrong: -2 (Light sedation) = briefly awakens to voice (eye opening/contact <10 sec).  Why C wrong: -3 (Moderate sedation) = movement or eye opening to voice (no eye contact).  Why D wrong: -4 (Deep sedation) = no response to voice, but movement/eye opening to physical stimulation."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"On the RASS, a patient who \"briefly awakens to voice (eye opening and contact for less than 10 seconds)\" is scored as:","options":["-1","-2","-3","-5"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: RASS -2 = \"Light sedation,\" briefly awakens to voice with eye opening/contact for <10 seconds.  Why A wrong: -1 (Drowsy) has sustained awakening >10 seconds.  Why C wrong: -3 (Moderate sedation) has movement/eye opening to voice but no eye contact.  Why D wrong: -5 (Unrousable) gives no response to voice or physical stimulation."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"Which RASS score is assigned when a patient shows movement or eye opening to voice but makes no eye contact?","options":["-2 (Light sedation)","-3 (Moderate sedation)","-4 (Deep sedation)","-5 (Unrousable)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: RASS -3 = \"Moderate sedation,\" movement or eye opening to voice (no eye contact).  Why A wrong: -2 (Light sedation) briefly awakens with eye opening/contact <10 sec.  Why C wrong: -4 (Deep sedation) has no response to voice but responds to physical stimulation.  Why D wrong: -5 (Unrousable) has no response to voice or physical stimulation."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"A deeply sedated patient gives no response to voice but does move or open the eyes to physical stimulation. The correct RASS score is:","options":["-3","-4","-5","-2"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: RASS -4 = \"Deep sedation,\" no response to voice but movement or eye opening to physical stimulation.  Why A wrong: -3 (Moderate sedation) responds to voice with movement/eye opening.  Why C wrong: -5 (Unrousable) shows no response to voice or physical stimulation.  Why D wrong: -2 (Light sedation) briefly awakens to voice."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"Which RASS score indicates a patient who is \"Unrousable\" with no response to voice or physical stimulation?","options":["-4","-5","-3","0"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: RASS -5 = \"Unrousable,\" no response to voice or physical stimulation.  Why A wrong: -4 (Deep sedation) still responds to physical stimulation.  Why C wrong: -3 (Moderate sedation) responds to voice.  Why D wrong: 0 is Alert and calm."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"APACHE score stands for which of the following?","options":["Acute Physiology And Chronic Health Evaluation","Acute Patient And Critical Health Examination","Assessment of Physiology And Chronic Health Estimation","Advanced Physiological Assessment of Critical Health Events"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: APACHE = Acute Physiology And Chronic Health Evaluation, per the source.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are fabricated expansions not matching the standard acronym."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"Which statement about the APACHE score is correct?","options":["It is the least used severity-of-disease classification system","It is the severity-of-disease classification, most widely used score in ICU, calculated within 24 hours of admission to an intensive care unit (ICU)","It is calculated only at discharge from the ICU","It is used exclusively for paediatric patients"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: APACHE is the severity-of-disease classification, the most widely used score in ICU, and should be applied within 24 hours of admission to an ICU.  Why A wrong: It is the most widely used, not least used.  Why C wrong: It is calculated within 24 hours of admission, not at discharge.  Why D wrong: It is a general ICU severity score, not paediatric-exclusive."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"The Ramsay Sedation Scale (RSS) is one of the most commonly used measures of what?","options":["Pain intensity","Sedation","Nutritional status","Coma depth in trauma only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Ramsay Sedation Scale (RSS) is one of the most commonly used measures of sedation.  Why A wrong: It measures sedation, not pain.  Why C wrong: It is unrelated to nutrition.  Why D wrong: It is a sedation scale, not a trauma-specific coma scale."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"Into how many categories does the Modified Ramsay Sedation Scale rate a patient's level of sedation, ranging from severe agitation to deep coma?","options":["Three","Four","Six","Ten"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The RSS divides a patient's level of sedation into six categories, ranging from severe agitation to deep coma.  Why A, B, D wrong: The source specifies six categories."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"On the Modified Ramsay Sedation Scale (RSS), a score of 1 corresponds to which response, and what state does it indicate?","options":["Anxious, agitated or restless — Awake","Cooperative, oriented, tranquil — Awake","No response to loud auditory stimulus — Sleep","Asleep, brisk response to loud auditory stimulus — Sleep"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: RSS score 1 = \"Anxious, agitated or restless,\" classified as the Awake state.  Why B wrong: Cooperative, oriented, tranquil is RSS score 2 (Awake).  Why C wrong: \"No response to loud auditory stimulus\" is RSS score 6 (Sleep).  Why D wrong: \"Asleep, brisk response to loud auditory stimulus\" is RSS score 4 (Sleep)."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"On the Modified Ramsay Sedation Scale, which score and state describe a patient who is \"cooperative, oriented, tranquil\"?","options":["Score 1 — Awake","Score 2 — Awake","Score 3 — Awake","Score 5 — Sleep"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: RSS score 2 = \"Cooperative, oriented, tranquil,\" Awake state.  Why A wrong: Score 1 = anxious, agitated or restless.  Why C wrong: Score 3 = \"Asleep, brisk response to verbal stimulus,\" and is rated Awake.  Why D wrong: Score 5 = \"No response to painful stimulus,\" Sleep."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"On the Modified Ramsay Sedation Scale, \"Asleep, brisk response to verbal stimulus\" corresponds to which score/state?","options":["Score 3 — Awake","Score 4 — Sleep","Score 5 — Sleep","Score 6 — Sleep"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: RSS score 3 = \"Asleep, brisk response to verbal stimulus,\" rated as Awake state in the table.  Why B wrong: Score 4 = asleep, brisk response to loud auditory stimulus (Sleep).  Why C wrong: Score 5 = no response to painful stimulus (Sleep).  Why D wrong: Score 6 = no response to loud auditory stimulus (Sleep)."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"On the Modified Ramsay Sedation Scale, a patient with \"no response to painful stimulus\" is given which score?","options":["4","5","6","3"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: RSS score 5 = \"No response to painful stimulus,\" classified as Sleep.  Why A wrong: Score 4 = asleep, brisk response to loud auditory stimulus.  Why C wrong: Score 6 = no response to loud auditory stimulus.  Why D wrong: Score 3 = asleep, brisk response to verbal stimulus."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"Which is the highest score on the Modified Ramsay Sedation Scale, and what does it indicate?","options":["Score 5 — no response to painful stimulus","Score 6 — no response to loud auditory stimulus (Sleep)","Score 4 — asleep, brisk response to loud auditory stimulus","Score 7 — deep coma"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: RSS score 6 = \"No response to loud auditory stimulus,\" the deepest level, rated Sleep.  Why A wrong: Score 5 is no response to painful stimulus (one level lighter than 6).  Why C wrong: Score 4 still responds to loud auditory stimulus.  Why D wrong: The Modified RSS top score is 6, not 7."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"Which scale is described as one of the most commonly used scales to determine the level of sedation in ICU patients?","options":["Glasgow Coma Scale","RASS (Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale)","APGAR score","Braden scale"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states RASS is one of the most commonly used scales to determine the sedation level in ICU patients.  Why A wrong: GCS assesses consciousness in trauma/neuro patients, not ICU sedation per this text.  Why C wrong: APGAR scores newborns.  Why D wrong: Braden assesses pressure-ulcer risk."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"The RASS is a 10-point scale. What is its score range?","options":["+4 to -5","0 to 10","-5 to +5","+3 to -3"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states RASS is a 10-point scale ranging from +4 to -5.  Why B, C, D wrong: These ranges do not match the stated +4 to -5 range."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"What does the RASS measure regarding the patient's behaviour?","options":["The severity of pain only","The severity of agitation and sedation","The blood pressure trend","Nutritional intake"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states RASS measures the severity of agitation and sedation.  Why A wrong: It is not a pure pain scale.  Why C, D wrong: RASS does not measure BP or nutrition."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"On the RASS scale, what does a score of 0 denote?","options":["Aggressive behaviour","Alert and calm state of the patient","Deep coma","Moderate agitation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Positive RASS scores denote aggressive behaviour and a 0 score represents the alert and calm state of the patient.  Why A wrong: Aggressive behaviour is represented by positive scores.  Why C wrong: Deep coma/unrousable is the most negative score (-5).  Why D wrong: Moderate agitation would be a positive score."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"A PYQ asks: To assess disease severity and septicaemia in ICU patients, which score is used?","options":["RASS score in ICU","APACHE score in ICU","Severe injury in casualty","Glasgow score"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The boxed \"Recall (NORCET)\" question keys the answer as A in source, where the option reads \"APACHE score in ICU\" used to assess disease severity and septicaemia in ICU patients. APACHE is the severity-of-disease classification.  Why \"RASS score in ICU\" wrong: RASS measures agitation/sedation, not disease severity/septicaemia.  Why \"Severe injury in casualty\" wrong: Not a severity-of-illness ICU score.  Why \"Glasgow score\" wrong: GCS measures consciousness, not septicaemia severity."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"Sedation is best defined as:","options":["Complete loss of consciousness with paralysis","The reduction of irritability or agitation by administration of sedative drugs to facilitate a medical or diagnostic procedure","The induction of vomiting","A method of pain measurement"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines sedation as the reduction of irritability or agitation by administration of sedative drugs to facilitate a medical or diagnostic procedure.  Why A wrong: That describes general anaesthesia, not sedation in general.  Why C, D wrong: Sedation is neither emesis induction nor pain measurement."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"Which of the following are examples of sedatives listed in the source? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Diazepam","Diethyl ether","Propofol","Ketamine","Lorazepam and midazolam"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists examples of sedatives as diazepam, diethyl ether, propofol, ketamine, lorazepam, and midazolam.  All listed options match the source verbatim."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"According to the source, sedation is also used in which minor procedures?","options":["Endoscopy and vasectomy","Cardiac bypass surgery only","Organ transplantation only","Limb amputation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Sedation is used in minor procedures e.g. endoscopy and vasectomy.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are major surgeries requiring general anaesthesia, not the minor procedures cited."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"The American Society of Anesthesiologists (ASA) defines the continuum of sedation. Which is the FIRST/lightest level described?","options":["Minimal sedation: normal response to verbal stimuli","Moderate sedation: purposeful response to verbal/tactile stimulation","Deep sedation: purposeful response to repeated or painful stimulation","General anaesthesia: unrousable even with painful stimulus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The ASA continuum lists minimal sedation first, defined as a normal response to verbal stimuli.  Why B wrong: Moderate sedation is the next level (purposeful response, also called \"conscious sedation\").  Why C wrong: Deep sedation responds purposefully to repeated/painful stimulation.  Why D wrong: General anaesthesia is the deepest, unrousable even with painful stimulus."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"On the ASA sedation continuum, \"purposeful response to verbal/tactile stimulation, also called conscious sedation\" describes which level?","options":["Minimal sedation","Moderate sedation","Deep sedation","General anaesthesia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Moderate sedation = purposeful response to verbal/tactile stimulation, also known as \"conscious sedation.\"  Why A wrong: Minimal sedation = normal response to verbal stimuli.  Why C wrong: Deep sedation = purposeful response to repeated/painful stimulation.  Why D wrong: General anaesthesia = unrousable even with painful stimulus."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"Which level on the ASA sedation continuum is defined as \"unrousable even with painful stimulus\"?","options":["Deep sedation","Moderate sedation","General anaesthesia","Minimal sedation"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: General anaesthesia = unrousable even with painful stimulus.  Why A wrong: Deep sedation responds purposefully to repeated/painful stimulation.  Why B wrong: Moderate sedation responds to verbal/tactile stimulation.  Why D wrong: Minimal sedation responds normally to verbal stimuli."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"Which of the following are listed as ICU equipment in the source? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Defibrillator with AED and transcutaneous facing","Multi-para monitor (cardiac monitor)","Syringe pump and micro-infusion pump","Portable USG and X-ray machine, ABG analyzer","Mechanical ventilation, suction machine, nebulizer and glucometer"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The ICU equipment list includes defibrillator with AED and transcutaneous facing; multi-para (cardiac) monitor; syringe pump; micro-infusion pump; portable USG and X-ray machine; ABG analyzer; mechanical ventilation; suction machine; nebulizer; glucometer; intubation kit and accessories (e.g. ET tube and laryngoscope); crash cart; pulse oximeter, thermometer and AMBU bags.  All listed options appear in the source."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"A defibrillator is best defined as a device that:","options":["Measures blood gases","Applies an electric charge or current to the heart to restore a normal heartbeat","Monitors end-tidal CO2","Delivers fluids at a controlled rate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A defibrillator applies an electric charge or current to the heart to restore a normal heartbeat.  Why A wrong: That is the ABG machine.  Why C wrong: That is capnography on the pulse oximeter/monitor.  Why D wrong: That is an infusion pump."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"When the heart rhythm stops due to cardiac arrest (also known as sudden cardiac arrest, SCA), what may a defibrillator help do?","options":["Slow an already fast heart","Start the heart beating again","Lower blood pressure","Measure oxygen saturation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: When the heart rhythm stops due to cardiac arrest (SCA), a defibrillator may help to start the heart beating again.  Why A wrong: It is used when the heart has stopped, not merely to slow it.  Why C, D wrong: A defibrillator neither lowers BP nor measures SpO2."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"During resuscitation or CPR, the use of a defibrillator is described as:","options":["Optional and rarely used","An emergent condition such as during resuscitation or CPR","A routine daily procedure","Contraindicated in all arrests"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: It is used in an emergent condition such as during resuscitation or CPR.  Why A, C, D wrong: It is an emergency-use device, neither routine daily nor universally contraindicated."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"A defibrillator is used to terminate which life-threatening rhythms via an asynchronous counter-shock?","options":["Sinus bradycardia and asystole","Pulseless ventricular tachycardia (PVT) and ventricular fibrillation (VF)","First-degree heart block and atrial flutter","Sinus tachycardia only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In an asynchronous counter-shock, the defibrillator is given to terminate pulseless ventricular tachycardia (PVT) and ventricular fibrillation (VF); there are only two indications for defibrillation: PVT and VF.  Why A wrong: Asystole is not shockable; defibrillation does not treat it.  Why C, D wrong: Heart block, atrial flutter, and sinus tachycardia are not the stated defibrillation indications."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"According to the source, how many indications are there for defibrillation?","options":["Only one","Only two — PVT and VF","Three","Four"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: There are only two indications for defibrillation: PVT and VF.  Why A, C, D wrong: The source states exactly two."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"In monophasic defibrillation, how many joules of current are delivered in one direction, and what is the success rate?","options":["360 joules in one direction, >60%","120–200 joules in two directions, >90%","200 joules, 100%","50 joules in one direction, >95%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Monophasic defibrillation = 360 joules, current delivered in one direction, shock success rate is >60%.  Why B wrong: That describes biphasic defibrillation.  Why C, D wrong: These values do not match the source."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"In biphasic defibrillation, the current is delivered in two directions. What joule range and success rate are stated?","options":["360 joules, >60%","120–200 joules, >90%","200–360 joules, 100%","50–100 joules, >70%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Biphasic defibrillation = 120–200 joules, current delivered in two directions, shock success rate is >90%.  Why A wrong: 360 joules / >60% is monophasic.  Why C, D wrong: These do not match the source values."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"Which mode should initially be chosen to deliver shock and why?","options":["Monophasic, because it is less traumatic","Biphasic, because this mode is customised and is less traumatic","Either, with no difference","Synchronized cardioversion always"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Initially choose biphasic mode to deliver the shock because this mode is customised and is less traumatic.  Why A wrong: It is biphasic, not monophasic, that is preferred for being customised/less traumatic.  Why C wrong: The source specifically recommends biphasic.  Why D wrong: Defibrillation (asynchronous), not synchronized cardioversion, is the topic for PVT/VF."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"After a shock is given with a defibrillator, CPR should be:","options":["Stopped permanently","Resumed immediately and continued for 5 cycles or about 2 minutes","Delayed for 10 minutes","Resumed only if the patient asks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After a shock of defibrillation, start CPR immediately and continue for 5 cycles or about 2 minutes.  Why A, C, D wrong: CPR must be resumed immediately for 5 cycles/2 minutes, not stopped, delayed, or made conditional."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"Where are defibrillator paddles placed (location of paddles)?","options":["Both over the sternum","One at the 3rd ICS space of the right sternum and the second at the 5th ICS on the left mid-axillary line","One on the abdomen and one on the back","Both on the left mid-clavicular line"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Location of paddles: one at the 3rd ICS space of the right sternum and the second at the 5th ICS on the left mid-axillary line.  Why A, C, D wrong: These do not match the stated sterno-apical placement."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"What is the recommended adult paddle size for a defibrillator, and the size for children?","options":["Adult 8 cm; children 5 cm","Adult 8 to 13 cm in diameter; children 8 to 10 cm in diameter","Adult 5 cm; children 3 cm","Adult 15 cm; children 12 cm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Paddle size: for adults 8 to 13 cm in diameter and for children 8 to 10 cm in diameter.  Why A, C, D wrong: These do not match the source dimensions."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"Which safety precautions apply when delivering a defibrillator shock? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Be sure no one is touching the bed or the client when delivering the shock","Ensure oxygen should be SHUT OFF before delivering the shock to avoid fire accidents","Both the rescuer and bystanders may keep hands on the patient","Oxygen flow should be increased during the shock"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct (A): Be sure no one is touching the bed or the client when delivering the shock.  Why correct (B): Oxygen should be SHUT OFF before delivering the shock to avoid fire accidents.  Why C wrong: No one should be touching the patient/bed during the shock.  Why D wrong: Oxygen must be shut off, not increased, to avoid fire."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"An ABG (arterial blood gas analyser) machine is used to measure a patient's blood gases for which purpose?","options":["To diagnose acid-base imbalances and interpret various metabolic disorders","To deliver fluids only","To restore a normal heartbeat","To suction airway secretions"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: ABG machine is used to measure a patient's blood gases for a variety of reasons e.g. to diagnose acid-base imbalances and interpret various metabolic disorders.  Why B wrong: Fluid delivery is the infusion pump.  Why C wrong: Restoring heartbeat is the defibrillator.  Why D wrong: Suctioning is the suction machine."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"By ABG results, which of the following can be diagnosed? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Acidosis","Asthma","COPD","Sepsis","Other metabolic disorders"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: By the ABG results, acidosis, asthma, COPD, sepsis and other metabolic disorders can be diagnosed.  All listed options match the source."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"When should the Allen test be done with regard to ABG sampling?","options":["After arterial puncture","Before taking arterial blood for ABG testing","Only during cardiac arrest","It is never indicated"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Allen test should be done before taking arterial blood for ABG testing (to confirm collateral circulation).  Why A wrong: It is done before, not after, the puncture.  Why C, D wrong: It is specifically indicated before ABG sampling, not arrest-only or never."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"What is the main purpose of a cardiac monitor in giving information on one screen?","options":["To deliver medications","To provide multiple information that is needed to understand the patient condition","To defibrillate the heart","To measure arterial blood gases"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A cardiac monitor gives a number of information on one screen and hence provides multiple information needed to understand the patient condition.  Why A, C, D wrong: It is a monitoring device, not for medication delivery, defibrillation, or ABG analysis."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"Which of the following parameters are assessed by a multi-para (cardiac) monitor? (Select all that apply.)","options":["ECG and SpO2","Pulse rate and respiration rate","Temperature","Non-invasive BP (NIBP) and 2 Invasive Blood Pressure (IBP)","EtCO2"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Parameters assessed by a multi-para monitor include ECG, SpO2, pulse rate, respiration rate, temperature, non-invasive BP (NIBP), 2 invasive blood pressure (IBP), and EtCO2.  All listed options match the source."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"Mechanical ventilation is best defined as:","options":["A machine used to deliver intravenous fluids","A machine to monitor heart rate","A machine called a ventilator that delivers air into and out of the lungs, to provide breath to a patient who is unable to breathe (or is breathing insufficiently)","A device to measure oxygen saturation"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Mechanical ventilation is a machine called a ventilator that delivers air into and out of the lungs, to provide breath to a patient who is unable to breathe or is breathing insufficiently.  Why A wrong: That is an infusion pump.  Why B wrong: That is the cardiac monitor.  Why D wrong: That is the pulse oximeter."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"What is the main goal of helping the delivery of oxygen and removal of carbon dioxide in a patient on mechanical ventilation?","options":["To increase blood glucose","To move air into and out of the lungs, achieving the goal of delivery of oxygen and removal of carbon dioxide","To deliver fluids and electrolytes","To restore a normal heartbeat"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Mechanical ventilation helps move air into and out of the lungs, achieving the goal of helping the delivery of oxygen and removal of carbon dioxide.  Why A, C, D wrong: These describe other devices/functions, not the ventilator's purpose."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"A pulse oximeter is best described as:","options":["An invasive technique requiring arterial puncture","A non-invasive technique to calculate oxygen saturation based on the difference in the absorption spectrum of haemoglobin","A device to measure blood glucose","A device to defibrillate the heart"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A pulse oximeter is a non-invasive technique to calculate oxygen saturation based on the difference in the absorption spectrum of haemoglobin.  Why A wrong: It is non-invasive, unlike ABG.  Why C, D wrong: It measures oxygen saturation, not glucose, and does not defibrillate."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"The spectrophotometry technique is used to determine the peripheral arterial blood oxygen saturation (SpO2) as a marker for the oxygenation of tissues using which law?","options":["Boyle's Law","Beer-Lambert Law","Henry's Law","Charles's Law"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The spectrophotometry technique uses the Beer-Lambert Law to determine peripheral arterial blood oxygen saturation (SpO2) as a marker for oxygenation of tissues.  Why A, C, D wrong: These gas laws are not the basis of pulse oximetry per the source."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"A pulse oximeter consists of which components?","options":["A red and infrared light-emitting LED and a photodetector","Only a single white LED","A pressure cuff and a stethoscope","A sodium electrode"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Pulse oximetry consists of red and infrared light-emitting LEDs and a photodetector.  Why B, C, D wrong: These do not match the described components."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"A capnometer (capnography) on the monitor is used to measure which value?","options":["End tidal CO2 (EtCO2)","SpO2","Blood glucose","Serum sodium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A capnometer is used to monitor End tidal CO2 (EtCO2) value, known as capnography.  Why B, C, D wrong: Capnography measures EtCO2, not SpO2, glucose, or sodium."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"Regarding light absorption by haemoglobin, which statement is correct?","options":["Oxygenated haemoglobin absorbs more infrared light (940 nm diode); deoxygenated haemoglobin absorbs more red light (660 nm diode)","Oxygenated haemoglobin absorbs more red light; deoxygenated absorbs more infrared light","Both absorb equally at all wavelengths","Neither absorbs red or infrared light"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Oxygenated haemoglobin absorbs more infrared light (940 nm diode), and deoxygenated haemoglobin absorbs more red light (660 nm diode).  Why B wrong: It reverses the correct absorption relationship.  Why C, D wrong: Absorption differs by wavelength and oxygenation state."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"What is the most common site for assessment with a pulse oximeter, and which other sites may be used? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Fingernail and toenail beds (most common)","Earlobe","Forehead","Nasal alar","Phalynx"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Assessment site: fingernail and toenail beds (most common); other sites are earlobe, forehead, nasal alar, and phalynx.  All listed options match the source."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"What is the normal oxygen saturation level at sea level?","options":["80–85%","96–100%","70–75%","50–60%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Normal oxygen saturation level at sea level is 96–100%.  Why A, C, D wrong: These ranges do not match the stated normal value."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"What is the normal oxygen saturation level in COPD?","options":["96–100%","85–90%","<90%","70–80%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Normal oxygen saturation in COPD is 85–90%.  Why A wrong: 96–100% is normal at sea level for non-COPD patients.  Why C wrong: <90% is the cut-off value of oxygen saturation to determine hypoxemia.  Why D wrong: Not the stated COPD value."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"What is the cut-off value of oxygen saturation used to determine hypoxemia?","options":["<90%","<96%","<85%","<70%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The cut-off value of oxygen saturation to determine hypoxemia is <90%.  Why B, C, D wrong: These do not match the stated hypoxemia cut-off."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"Which of the following conditions may LOWER the oximetry (SpO2) value? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Methemoglobin","Hypothermia, cold and shivering","Severe hypotension and shock (poor circulation)","Severe anaemia","Nail polish and mehndi, hyper-pigmentation (dark skin tones), peripheral edema (swelling of limbs), and smoking"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Conditions which may lower oximetry value include methemoglobin, hypothermia/cold/shivering, severe hypotension and shock (poor circulation), severe anaemia, nail polish and mehndi, hyper-pigmentation (dark skin tones), peripheral edema (swelling of limbs), and smoking.  All listed options match the source."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"Which condition may give a falsely HIGHER oximetry value, often seen in patients rescued from burns or fire accidents?","options":["Carbon monoxide (CO) poisoning / carboxyhemoglobin","Severe anaemia","Hypothermia","Peripheral edema"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Conditions which may give a higher oximetry value include CO poisoning (carboxyhemoglobin), seen in patients rescued from burns or fire accidents.  Why B, C, D wrong: Anaemia, hypothermia, and peripheral edema are listed under conditions that LOWER the value."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"According to the source, ABG measurement compared to pulse oximetry is:","options":["Less reliable than pulse oximetry","More reliable compared to pulse oximetry","Equally reliable","Never used in ICU"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: ABG measurement is more reliable compared to pulse oximetry.  Why A, C, D wrong: ABG is stated to be more reliable, contradicting these options."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"A stethoscope is primarily used for which purpose?","options":["Examination by auscultation, e.g. bowel sounds, pulmonary sounds and cardiac sounds; also used for BP measurement","Defibrillation of the heart","Measuring blood glucose","Delivering oxygen"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A stethoscope is used for examination by auscultation, e.g. bowel sounds, pulmonary sounds and cardiac sounds, and is also used for BP measurement.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are functions of other devices."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"The chest piece of the stethoscope, also known as the head or diaphragm, usually has how many sides?","options":["One","Two","Three","Four"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The chest piece (head or diaphragm) usually has two sides.  Why A, C, D wrong: The source states two sides (diaphragm and bell)."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"On a stethoscope, which side is flat and circular and ideal for high-frequency sounds?","options":["Diaphragm","Bell","Tubing","Earpiece"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The diaphragm is flat, circular, and ideal for high-frequency sounds.  Why B wrong: The bell (cup-shaped) is ideal for low-frequency sounds.  Why C, D wrong: Tubing conveys sound; earpieces transmit it to the examiner."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"On a stethoscope, the cup-shaped bell is ideal for hearing which sounds?","options":["High-frequency sounds","Low-frequency sounds","No sounds","Only BP Korotkoff sounds"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The bell is cup-shaped and ideal for low-frequency sounds.  Why A wrong: High-frequency sounds are best heard with the diaphragm.  Why C, D wrong: The bell hears low-frequency sounds, not none or BP-only."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"What is the function of the tubing and the earpieces of the stethoscope?","options":["The tubing facilitates the conveyance of sound between the chest piece and the earpieces; earpieces insert into ear canals and facilitate transmission of sound","The tubing measures pressure; earpieces produce sound","They amplify electrical signals","They have no role in auscultation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Tubing facilitates the conveyance of sound between the chest piece and the earpieces; earpieces are inserted into the ear canals of the examiner and facilitate transmission of sound.  Why B, C, D wrong: These misstate the passive acoustic role of tubing/earpieces."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"A crash cart is best described as:","options":["The trolley used for storing life-saving equipment and drugs in a hospital setup","A device that measures blood gases","A type of ventilator","A pump for IV fluids"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A crash cart is the trolley used for storing life-saving equipment and drugs in a hospital setup.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are other ICU devices, not the crash cart."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"A crash cart is described as a cabinet containing which type of items? (Select all that apply.)","options":["High-risk medications / emergency medicines","Defibrillator and monitor","Items easily movable / accessible to be used during critical situations","Bedside furniture and linen"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: A crash cart is a cabinet containing high-risk medications/emergency medicines, a defibrillator and monitor, and items easily movable/accessible to be used during critical situations.  Why D wrong: Bedside furniture and linen are not listed as crash-cart contents."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"Who must check the crash cart and what documentation is required?","options":["Crash cart must be checked by the head nurse/staff nurse every shift and documented in the checklist","The crash cart never needs checking","Only the physician checks it once a year","The patient checks it daily"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The crash cart must be checked by the head nurse/staff nurse every shift and documented in the checklist.  Why B, C, D wrong: It requires every-shift nurse checks with documentation, not the alternatives listed."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"How often must crash cart medicines be checked for expiry dates?","options":["Daily","Monthly","Yearly","Never"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Crash cart medicines must be checked monthly for expiry dates.  Why A, C, D wrong: The source specifies monthly expiry checks (separate from the every-shift presence check)."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"Why must look-alike and sound-alike medicines be stored separately on the crash cart?","options":["To save space","As per policy, to prevent medication errors (LASA precaution)","Because they expire faster","To increase the cart's weight"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Look-alike and sound-alike medicines should be stored separately as per policy (to prevent medication errors).  Why A, C, D wrong: The rationale is error prevention, not space, expiry, or weight."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"Who maintains the defibrillator and equipment on the crash cart, and how often is it checked?","options":["Defibrillator/equipment maintained by the biomedical engineer and checked by the ward nurse on daily basis","Maintained by the patient","Checked once a year only","Never requires maintenance"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The defibrillator and equipment are maintained by the biomedical engineer and checked by the ward nurse on a daily basis.  Why B, C, D wrong: The source specifies biomedical engineer maintenance with daily ward-nurse checks."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"Where should crash carts be located in patient care zones?","options":["In a locked storeroom only","In areas free of obstacles and easily/readily accessible to the location where needed","In the basement","Outside the hospital building"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Crash carts are located in areas free of obstacles and can be easily/readily accessible to the location where needed.  Why A, C, D wrong: Obstruction-free, ready accessibility is required, contradicting these options."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"What are the purposes of the crash cart? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Providing immediate access to supplies and medication in emergency situations","Helps to ensure a properly stocked emergency cart is readily available","Helps to save valuable time at the time of emergency","Used for routine scheduled medication rounds"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Crash cart purposes are providing immediate access to supplies/medication in emergencies, ensuring a properly stocked cart is readily available, and saving valuable time at the time of emergency.  Why D wrong: It is for emergencies, not routine scheduled medication rounds."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"Under the Uniform Crash Cart Arrangement (JIPMER Guidelines), what is kept on the TOP shelf?","options":["IV fluids","Normal saline 500 mL, 5% Dextrose 500 mL, Ringer Lactate 500 mL, 25% Dextrose 100 mL, and Normal saline 100 mL","Defibrillator with paddles","Crash cart medications only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The TOP shelf holds IV fluids: Normal saline 500 mL, 5% Dextrose 500 mL, Ringer Lactate 500 mL, 25% Dextrose 100 mL, and Normal saline 100 mL.  Why A wrong: IV fluids are on the bottom in some hospitals' alternative arrangement; per JIPMER the TOP shelf lists these specific fluids (option B is more complete/correct).  Why C wrong: The defibrillator with paddles is on the middle shelf.  Why D wrong: The crashed medications are arranged in drawers, not the top shelf."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"Under the JIPMER crash cart arrangement, what is kept on the MIDDLE shelf? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Defibrillator with paddles","Jelly","Tissue paper / clean gauze","5% Dextrose 500 mL"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The MIDDLE shelf contains the defibrillator with paddles, jelly, and tissue paper/clean gauze.  Why D wrong: 5% Dextrose is an IV fluid kept on the top shelf."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"In hospitals following an alternative crash cart arrangement, which items are placed on the TOP of the cart? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Defibrillator with leads","AMBU bag, adult and paediatric","Laryngoscope with all number of blades","Dry tot / oral airway, oxygen mask, paediatric set"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Some hospitals place on TOP: defibrillator with leads; AMBU bag (adult & paediatric); laryngoscope with all numbers of blades; dry tot, tongue depressor torch etc.; and below the resuscitation equipment small compartment containing ECG leads, xylocaine jelly, plasters, and surgical gauzes.  All listed options correspond to source items placed on top."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"In the alternative crash cart arrangement, which items are placed on the SIDES of the cart? (Select all that apply.)","options":["O2 cylinder","Cardiac board","Handheld suction apparatus","IV stand"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: On the SIDES are placed the O2 cylinder, cardiac board, handheld suction apparatus, and IV stand.  All listed options match the source side items."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"In the alternative crash cart arrangement, the MIDDLE drawers contain which items? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Emergency medications","Intubation tray or equipments","Accessories e.g. IV set, syringe, vein flow, catheters etc.","IV fluids"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The MIDDLE drawers contain emergency medications, intubation tray or equipments, and accessories e.g. IV set, syringe, vein flow, catheters etc.  Why D wrong: IV fluids are kept at the bottom in this arrangement."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"In the alternative crash cart arrangement, what is kept at the BOTTOM of the cart?","options":["IV fluids","Defibrillator","Emergency medications","Laryngoscope"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The BOTTOM holds IV fluids.  Why B wrong: The defibrillator is on top (with leads) or the middle shelf (JIPMER).  Why C wrong: Emergency medications are in the middle drawers.  Why D wrong: The laryngoscope is on top."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"An infusion pump is best defined as a medical device that delivers:","options":["Electric shocks to the heart","Fluids, such as nutrients and medications, into a patient's body in controlled amounts","Air into the lungs","Arterial blood for ABG testing"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: An infusion pump is a medical device that delivers fluids, such as nutrients and medications, into a patient's body in controlled amounts.  Why A wrong: That is the defibrillator.  Why C wrong: That is the ventilator.  Why D wrong: That is ABG sampling."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"Which medicines can be delivered through an infusion pump in very small amounts? (Select all that apply.)","options":["ADR (Adrenaline)","NORAD (Noradrenaline)","Dobutamine","KCl (Potassium chloride)","Other medicines requiring very-small-amount delivery"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The source states ADR, NORAD, Dobutamine, KCl, and other medicines can be delivered through an infusion pump in very small amounts.  All listed options match the source."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"A nurse must defibrillate a patient in ventricular fibrillation using a biphasic device. Which initial energy setting is appropriate, and what is its expected success rate?","options":["360 joules, >60%","120–200 joules, >90%","50 joules, >50%","400 joules, 100%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Biphasic mode (preferred as initial choice, customised and less traumatic) delivers 120–200 joules in two directions with a >90% shock success rate.  Why A wrong: 360 joules / >60% describes monophasic.  Why C, D wrong: These energy values and rates are not stated for biphasic."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"A COPD patient's pulse oximeter reads 88%. Based on the source's stated normal values, the nurse interprets this as:","options":["Abnormally low, indicating immediate hypoxemia below the hypoxemia cut-off","Within the normal SpO2 range for COPD (85–90%)","Normal sea-level saturation (96–100%)","Carbon monoxide poisoning"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Normal SpO2 in COPD is 85–90%, so 88% falls within the normal COPD range per the source.  Why A wrong: The hypoxemia cut-off is <90% in general, but the source specifies 85–90% as the normal COPD range, so 88% is acceptable for a COPD patient.  Why C wrong: 96–100% is the sea-level value for non-COPD patients.  Why D wrong: CO poisoning falsely raises SpO2; it does not explain 88% here."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"A burn victim rescued from a fire reads SpO2 100% on the pulse oximeter but appears cyanotic and distressed. What is the most likely explanation per the source?","options":["The patient is well oxygenated and needs no intervention","Carbon monoxide poisoning (carboxyhemoglobin) falsely raises the oximetry reading, so ABG is more reliable","The oximeter is reading too low","Severe anaemia is raising the reading"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: CO poisoning (carboxyhemoglobin), seen in patients rescued from burns/fire accidents, gives a falsely higher oximetry value; ABG measurement is more reliable compared to pulse oximetry.  Why A wrong: A falsely high reading does not reflect true oxygenation; the cyanotic, distressed patient needs intervention.  Why C wrong: CO poisoning raises, not lowers, the reading.  Why D wrong: Severe anaemia LOWERS the oximetry value, per the source."},{"section":"Batch 055 · Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale, APACHE score…","question":"Before delivering a defibrillation shock, which action is the PRIORITY to prevent a fire accident?","options":["Increase the oxygen flow rate","Ensure oxygen is SHUT OFF before delivering the shock","Apply more conductive gel","Lower the energy to 50 joules"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Oxygen should be SHUT OFF before delivering the shock to avoid fire accidents.  Why A wrong: Increasing oxygen raises fire risk.  Why C wrong: Gel improves conduction but does not address fire risk from oxygen.  Why D wrong: Lowering energy does not address the oxygen fire hazard."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"Central venous pressure (CVP) is most commonly measured in which clinical setting?","options":["General outpatient department","Critical care units","Antenatal clinic","Physiotherapy unit"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states CVP is usually measured in critical care units.  Why A wrong: An OPD lacks the invasive monitoring and central access needed for CVP.  Why C wrong: An antenatal clinic is not a CVP monitoring environment.  Why D wrong: Physiotherapy units do not perform invasive hemodynamic monitoring."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"CVP is measured using an indwelling central venous catheter (CVC) and which device?","options":["A sphygmomanometer cuff","A pressure manometer or transducer","A pulse oximeter probe","A Doppler ultrasound probe"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: CVP is measured using an indwelling CVC and a pressure manometer or transducer; both methods are reliable when used correctly.  Why A wrong: A sphygmomanometer cuff measures peripheral arterial blood pressure, not CVP.  Why C wrong: A pulse oximeter measures oxygen saturation, not pressure.  Why D wrong: Doppler probes assess flow, not central venous pressure."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"The pressure in the right atrium, as the blood from the right atrium flows into the inferior and superior vena cava, is known as:","options":["Pulmonary capillary wedge pressure","Mean arterial pressure","Central venous pressure (CVP)","Pulse pressure"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Circulating blood flows into the right atrium via the inferior and superior vena cava, and the pressure in the right atrium is known as central venous pressure (CVP).  Why A wrong: Wedge pressure is measured in the pulmonary artery, not the right atrium.  Why B wrong: MAP is an arterial measurement.  Why D wrong: Pulse pressure is the difference between systolic and diastolic pressures."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"The normal CVP range is best stated as:","options":["0–2 mmHg","2–6 mmHg (5–10 cm H2O)","12–18 mmHg","20–30 cm H2O"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The book gives the normal CVP as 2–6 mmHg (5–10 cm H2O) when taken from the mid-axillary line at the 4th intercostal space.  Why A wrong: This is below the stated normal range.  Why C wrong: 12–18 mmHg is far above the normal CVP range.  Why D wrong: 20–30 cm H2O exceeds the normal range."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"When taking a CVP reading, the measurement is referenced from which anatomical landmark?","options":["Mid-clavicular line at the 2nd intercostal space","Mid-axillary line at the 4th intercostal space","Suprasternal notch","Xiphoid process at the midline"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Normal CVP of 2–6 mmHg (5–10 cm H2O) is measured at the mid-axillary line at the 4th intercostal space (the level of the right atrium / phlebostatic axis).  Why A wrong: The mid-clavicular 2nd intercostal space is not the CVP reference point.  Why C wrong: The suprasternal notch is not used.  Why D wrong: The xiphoid process is not the stated landmark."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"A normal CVP value indicates all of the following EXCEPT:","options":["Right atrial pressure","Right ventricular functioning","Systemic fluid status / intravascular fluid volume","Glomerular filtration rate"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The listed indications are right atrial pressure, right ventricular functioning, systemic fluid status/intravascular fluid volume, and pulmonary vascular resistance — GFR is not among them.  Why A wrong: Right atrial pressure is a listed indicator.  Why B wrong: Right ventricular functioning is a listed indicator.  Why C wrong: Systemic fluid status/intravascular fluid volume is a listed indicator."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"Pulmonary vascular resistance is one of the parameters reflected by a normal CVP measurement.","options":["True","False","Only in pediatric patients","Only during exercise"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text lists pulmonary vascular resistance as one of the four items a normal CVP value indicates.  Why B wrong: It is explicitly listed.  Why C wrong: No age restriction is stated.  Why D wrong: No activity-dependent qualifier is given."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"A central line/CVC is typically placed into which type of vessel?","options":["A small peripheral vein of the hand","A large vein, usually in the neck, chest, or groin","A radial artery","A subcutaneous lymphatic vessel"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A central venous catheter is placed into a large vein, usually in the neck (internal jugular vein), chest (subclavian vein), or groin (femoral vein).  Why A wrong: Peripheral hand veins are used for PICC insertion site, not the central tip placement; the central line terminates in a large central vein.  Why C wrong: A CVC is venous, not arterial.  Why D wrong: Lymphatic vessels are not used for CVCs."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"Peripherally inserted central catheters (PICC) are usually placed via which veins?","options":["Femoral and saphenous veins","Right basilic vein and brachial vein","Internal jugular and subclavian veins","Cephalic and external jugular veins"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The right basilic vein and brachial vein are usually used for peripherally inserted central catheters (PICC).  Why A wrong: These are lower-extremity/femoral access, not PICC sites.  Why C wrong: IJV and subclavian are non-tunneled central line sites, not PICC.  Why D wrong: Not the stated PICC veins."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"A CVP/central line is inserted using which technique, in which a blunt guidewire is passed through the needle and the needle is then removed?","options":["Trendelenburg technique","Valsalva technique","Seldinger technique","Phlebostatic technique"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The CVP line is inserted using the Seldinger technique — a blunt guidewire is passed through the needle, then the needle is removed.  Why A wrong: Trendelenburg is a patient position used to reduce air embolism risk, not an insertion technique.  Why B wrong: Valsalva is a breathing maneuver, not the guidewire insertion technique.  Why D wrong: \"Phlebostatic\" refers to the leveling axis, not the insertion technique."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"Accurate CVP measurement should be taken at which point in the respiratory cycle and in which patient position?","options":["At end of inspiration, prone position","At the end of expiration, supine position","During forced expiration, sitting upright","At peak inspiration, lateral position"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Measurement should be taken at the end of expiration, with the patient lying in the supine position; the electronic transducer is placed and zeroed at the level of the right atrium.  Why A wrong: Prone/end-inspiration is not stated.  Why C wrong: Forced expiration/sitting is not the recommended condition.  Why D wrong: Peak inspiration/lateral position is incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"The electronic transducer for CVP monitoring should be zeroed at which level?","options":["The level of the right atrium","The level of the symphysis pubis","The level of the diaphragm dome","The level of the left ventricle apex"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The electronic transducer is placed and zeroed at the level of the right atrium.  Why B wrong: The symphysis pubis is not the reference level.  Why C wrong: The diaphragm is not used.  Why D wrong: The left ventricle apex is not the CVP reference level."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"Which of the following are listed as reasons why CVP may be ELEVATED? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Over hydration / increased venous return","Heart failure","Pulmonary artery stenosis limiting venous outflow","Positive pressure breathing due to straining","Hypovolemic shock"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Elevated CVP causes listed are overhydration/increased venous return, heart failure, pulmonary artery stenosis limiting venous outflow, and positive pressure breathing due to straining.  Why E wrong: Hypovolemic shock (fluid volume deficit) causes DECREASED CVP, not elevated."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"A decreased CVP is most consistent with which condition?","options":["Fluid overload","Hypovolemic shock (fluid volume deficit)","Right heart failure","Tension pneumothorax with venous congestion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: CVP may be decreased in hypovolemic shock (fluid volume deficit).  Why A wrong: Fluid overload raises CVP.  Why C wrong: Right heart failure raises CVP.  Why D wrong: Conditions causing venous congestion raise CVP."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"Which of the following are listed complications of a central line catheter? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Pneumothorax","Bloodstream infections","Thrombosis","Catheter misplacement","Air embolus"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The complications listed are pneumothorax, bloodstream infections, thrombosis, misplacement, air embolus, and hematoma formation/hemorrhage — all options apply.  Why A–E wrong: None are incorrect; each is an explicitly listed complication."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"Misplacement of a central line catheter usually requires the patient to adopt which position, and may be difficult in pregnancy because it can cause what?","options":["Fowler's position; airway obstruction","Trendelenburg or at least supine position; aortocaval compression","Prone position; pressure ulcers","Lithotomy position; bladder distension"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Correcting misplacement usually requires a Trendelenburg or at least supine position; in pregnancy this may be difficult as it may cause aortocaval compression.  Why A wrong: Fowler's/airway obstruction is not stated.  Why C wrong: Prone/pressure ulcers is not stated.  Why D wrong: Lithotomy/bladder distension is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"To prevent air embolus, the lines attached to a CVC must be kept:","options":["Filled with air for priming","Air free","Clamped at all times","Open to atmosphere"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states that the lines attached to a CVC must be kept air free to avoid air embolus.  Why A wrong: Air-filled lines risk air embolism.  Why C wrong: Permanent clamping would prevent infusion/measurement.  Why D wrong: Open-to-atmosphere lines risk air entry."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"According to the text, the use of CVCs in maternity care is:","options":["Becoming increasingly common","Becoming increasingly rare","Mandatory for all deliveries","Contraindicated entirely"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The book states the use of CVCs is becoming increasingly rare in maternity care.  Why A wrong: It is the opposite — increasingly rare, not common.  Why C wrong: Not mandatory for all deliveries.  Why D wrong: It is rare, not absolutely contraindicated."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"On the CVP waveform, the \"a\" wave corresponds to which event?","options":["Ventricular filling with opening of the tricuspid valve","Atrial contraction","Closing and bulging of the tricuspid valve","Passive filling of the atrium with the tricuspid valve still closed"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: On the CVP waveform, \"a\" = atrial contraction.  Why A wrong: That describes the y descent (ventricular filling, tricuspid valve open).  Why C wrong: That describes the c wave.  Why D wrong: That describes the v wave."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"On the CVP waveform, the \"c\" wave represents:","options":["Atrial contraction","Closing and bulging of the tricuspid valve","Atrial relaxation with downward movement of the tricuspid valve during ventricular contraction","Passive filling of the atrium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: \"c\" = closing and bulging of the tricuspid valve.  Why A wrong: That is the \"a\" wave.  Why C wrong: That is the \"x\" descent.  Why D wrong: That is the \"v\" wave."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"On the CVP waveform, the \"x\" descent represents:","options":["Atrial relaxation with downward movement of the tricuspid valve during ventricular contraction","Atrial contraction","Ventricular filling","Closing of the tricuspid valve"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: \"x\" = atrial relaxation, with downward movement of the tricuspid valve during ventricular contraction.  Why B wrong: That is \"a\".  Why C wrong: That is \"y\".  Why D wrong: That is \"c\"."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"On the CVP waveform, the \"v\" wave represents:","options":["Atrial contraction","Passive filling of the atrium (tricuspid valve still closed)","Ventricular filling with opening of the tricuspid valve","Closing of the tricuspid valve"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: \"v\" = passive filling of the atrium while the tricuspid valve is still closed.  Why A wrong: That is \"a\".  Why C wrong: That is \"y\".  Why D wrong: That is \"c\"."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"On the CVP waveform, the \"y\" descent represents:","options":["Atrial contraction","Closing of the tricuspid valve","Ventricular filling with opening of the tricuspid valve","Atrial relaxation during ventricular contraction"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: \"y\" = ventricular filling with opening of the tricuspid valve.  Why A wrong: That is \"a\".  Why B wrong: That is \"c\".  Why D wrong: That is \"x\"."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"Which CVC insertion site offers the most recognisable anatomical landmarks, is most comfortable for the patient, and is most commonly used for long-term patients?","options":["Subclavian vein CVC","Internal jugular vein CVC","Femoral vein CVC","Brachial vein CVC"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The subclavian vein CVC has more recognisable anatomical landmarks (easier insertion), is more comfortable, and is most commonly used for long-term patients.  Why B wrong: The IJV is noted as easy to access for short duration, not most comfortable for long term.  Why C wrong: The femoral site is uncomfortable and discourages movement.  Why D wrong: The brachial vein is a PICC site, not listed among these CVC sites."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"The subclavian vein CVC carries the highest chance of which major complication of CVP insertion?","options":["Femoral nerve injury","Pneumothorax","Perineal infection","Thrombophlebitis of the leg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The subclavian site carries the highest chance of getting pneumothorax, a major complication of CVP.  Why A wrong: Femoral nerve injury relates to femoral access, not subclavian.  Why C wrong: Perineal infection relates to femoral access proximity.  Why D wrong: Thrombophlebitis is listed under femoral access risks."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"A key advantage of the internal jugular vein (IJV) CVC is that it is easy to access for short duration, but it may have which disadvantage?","options":["Catheter occlusion from head movement, which may cause irritation in the conscious patient","Highest risk of pneumothorax","Highest infection risk from perineal proximity","Always requires general anaesthesia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: With the IJV CVC, catheter occlusion may occur from head movement, which may cause irritation in the conscious patient.  Why B wrong: Highest pneumothorax risk is associated with the subclavian site.  Why C wrong: Perineal proximity infection risk is associated with the femoral site.  Why D wrong: General anaesthesia requirement is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"The femoral vein CVC is best described as providing:","options":["Rapid access in emergency situations, e.g., cardiac arrest, but less commonly used","The most comfortable long-term option","The lowest infection risk of all sites","The site with no thrombophlebitis risk"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The femoral vein CVC provides rapid access in emergencies (e.g., cardiac arrest) but is less commonly used.  Why B wrong: It is uncomfortable and discourages movement.  Why C wrong: It has an increased risk of associated infection due to proximity to the perineal area.  Why D wrong: It carries a risk of thrombophlebitis."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"Why is the femoral vein CVC less commonly used? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Increased risk of associated infection due to proximity to the perineal area","Risk of thrombophlebitis","Uncomfortable and discourages patient movement","Causes pneumothorax","Requires the Trendelenburg position permanently"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The text lists increased infection risk (perineal proximity), thrombophlebitis risk, and patient discomfort/discouraged movement as reasons the femoral site is less commonly used.  Why D wrong: Pneumothorax is associated with the subclavian site, not femoral.  Why E wrong: Permanent Trendelenburg positioning is not stated for the femoral site."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"A pressure-monitoring bag is used as part of the CVP setup primarily to:","options":["Warm the IV fluid","Maintain a pressurized flush to the transducer system","Cool the catheter tip","Collect overflow blood"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The transducer system uses a pressure bag (inflated up to 300 mmHg) connected to a flushing device to maintain pressure on the line (Fig. Pressure monitoring bag).  Why A wrong: The bag pressurizes flush solution, it does not warm fluid.  Why C wrong: It does not cool the catheter.  Why D wrong: It is not for blood collection."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"Before measuring CVP via a manometer, after explaining the procedure and obtaining informed consent, the nurse should place the patient in which position?","options":["Prone position","Supine recumbent position (if not running)","High Fowler's position","Left lateral position"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After flushing the catheter (if not running), the patient is placed in a supine recumbent position; an alternative position is semi-recumbent.  Why A wrong: Prone is not used.  Why C wrong: High Fowler's is not the manometer position.  Why D wrong: Left lateral is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"When using a manometer for serial CVP measurements, the patient's position should be:","options":["Changed each time to keep the patient comfortable","Kept the same for each measurement to ensure comparable results","Determined by the patient's preference","Always semi-Fowler's regardless of prior readings"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The position should remain the same for each repeat measurement (or note the position taken) to ensure an accurate, comparable result.  Why A wrong: Changing position invalidates comparison.  Why C wrong: Patient preference does not govern consistency requirements.  Why D wrong: Position must match prior readings, not default to one posture."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"When measuring CVP with a manometer, the manometer arm/scale should be aligned to which landmark, referred to as \"zeroing the manometer\"?","options":["The xiphoid process","The phlebostatic axis","The angle of Louis","The anterior superior iliac spine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Line up the manometer arm with the phlebostatic axis, ensuring that the bubble is between the two lines of the spirit level; this is \"zeroing the manometer.\"  Why A wrong: The xiphoid process is not the zeroing landmark.  Why C wrong: The angle of Louis is not used.  Why D wrong: The ASIS is a pelvic landmark, not used here."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"During manometer CVP measurement, the IV fluid bag is opened to slowly fill the manometer to a level higher than the expected CVP. When the fluid stops falling, the CVP measurement is read. If the fluid moves with the patient's breathing, the measurement should be read from which point?","options":["The highest oscillation point","The lower number","The average of the swings","The point of maximum inspiration only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: When the fluid moves with the patient's breathing, the measurement is read from the lower number.  Why A wrong: The text directs reading the lower number, not the highest.  Why C wrong: Averaging is not specified.  Why D wrong: Maximum inspiration point is not specified."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"After completing a manometer CVP reading, the three-way tap should be:","options":["Left open to the manometer","Turned off to the manometer and documentation completed","Removed from the catheter","Opened to the atmosphere"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After reading, turn the tap off to the manometer and document the reading.  Why A wrong: Leaving it open to the manometer is not the closing step.  Why C wrong: The tap is not removed.  Why D wrong: Opening to the atmosphere risks air entry/contamination."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"When using a transducer for CVP measurement, the pressure bag should be inflated up to what pressure?","options":["100 mmHg","200 mmHg","300 mmHg","500 mmHg"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Ensure that the pressure bag is inflated up to 300 mmHg.  Why A wrong: 100 mmHg is below the stated value.  Why B wrong: 200 mmHg is incorrect.  Why D wrong: 500 mmHg exceeds the stated value."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"For transducer-based CVP measurement, the FIRST-priority patient position is:","options":["Flat in the supine position (semi-recumbent as alternative)","Prone","Trendelenburg","Right lateral"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Place the patient flat in a supine position if possible (first priority); the alternative is a semi-recumbent position, ensuring the same position is used for each measurement.  Why B wrong: Prone is not used.  Why C wrong: Trendelenburg is not the stated CVP measurement position.  Why D wrong: Right lateral is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"Catheters differ between manufacturers; which lumen is generally suitable for measuring CVP?","options":["The brown lumen","The blue lumen","The white or proximal lumen","The green lumen"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The white or proximal lumen is suitable for measuring CVP.  Why A wrong: The brown/distal lumen is for high-pressure infusions/CVP monitoring per the table, but the procedure text specifies the white/proximal lumen for measuring CVP; the white proximal is the stated answer here.  Why B wrong: The blue/medial lumen is for hyperosmolar solutions (TPN).  Why D wrong: Green is not a designated CVP lumen."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"Before zeroing a transducer, the transducer should be taped to which landmark?","options":["The sternal angle","The phlebostatic axis (or as near to the right atrium as possible)","The suprasternal notch","The umbilicus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Tape the transducer to the phlebostatic axis or as near to the right atrium as possible.  Why A wrong: The sternal angle is not the leveling landmark.  Why C wrong: The suprasternal notch is not used.  Why D wrong: The umbilicus is not used."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"When zeroing a transducer, the nurse turns the tap OFF to the patient, presses the zero button, and waits for calibration. When \"zeroed\" is displayed on the monitor, the next step is to:","options":["Disconnect the catheter","Replace the cap on the three-way tap and turn the tap ON to the patient","Flush the line with heparin","Remove the transducer"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: When \"zeroed\" is displayed, replace the cap on the three-way tap and turn the tap ON to the patient, then observe the CVP trace on the monitor.  Why A wrong: Disconnecting the catheter is unsafe and not the step.  Why C wrong: Heparin flushing is not the calibration step.  Why D wrong: The transducer remains in place."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"On the CVP trace, the waveform undulates because:","options":["The right atrium contracts and relaxes, emptying and filling with blood","The transducer is faulty","The patient is moving the catheter","The pressure bag is deflating"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text explains the waveform undulates as the right atrium contracts and relaxes, emptying and filling with blood.  Why B wrong: Undulation is a normal physiologic finding, not a fault.  Why C wrong: It is due to atrial mechanics, not movement.  Why D wrong: It is not due to bag deflation."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"A patient is admitted with a complaint of perforated peritonitis, and laparotomy surgery is being conducted. What would be the reason behind the insertion of a central line in this patient?","options":["Measurement of CVP","Measurement of blood pressure","Measurement of pulse pressure","Measurement of pulse deficit"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The keyed answer is A — a central line is inserted to measure central venous pressure (CVP), which guides fluid resuscitation/volume status in this critically ill surgical patient.  Why B wrong: Blood pressure is measured peripherally/arterially, not via a central venous line.  Why C wrong: Pulse pressure is derived from arterial blood pressure, not CVP.  Why D wrong: Pulse deficit is assessed by comparing apical and radial pulses, not via a central line."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"A nurse is assisting the doctor for central line insertion in the right jugular vein. During catheter insertion the nurse observes that the patient is having shortness of breath, chest pain, low blood pressure, and respiratory distress. What is the expected finding and the priority nursing intervention?","options":["This is an expected finding; continue the procedure","Air embolism; place the client in side-lying position with head lower than the feet","Pneumothorax; place the client in supine position","Cardiac dysrhythmia; administer the oxygen via face mask"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The keyed answer is B. Air embolism occurs due to entry of air into the catheter system (catheter system opened, IV tubing disconnected). During central line insertion, air embolism is the most common complication, manifesting as shortness of breath, chest pain, hypotension, and respiratory distress. Priority actions: clamp the IV catheter (first priority), then place the client in side-lying position with the head lower than the feet to trap the air in the right side of the heart (second priority), then administer oxygen and inform the on-duty doctor.  Why A wrong: These symptoms are an emergency, not an expected finding.  Why C wrong: While pneumothorax can occur, the keyed/most likely complication during insertion with these features and an open system is air embolism; supine positioning is not the priority intervention for this scenario.  Why D wrong: Cardiac dysrhythmia is not the keyed cause; oxygen alone does not address trapped air."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"During central venous catheter insertion, to prevent air embolism, the nurse should instruct the patient to perform which maneuver (take a deep breath, hold it, and bear down) when the IV tubing and cap are changed?","options":["Trendelenburg maneuver","Valsalva maneuver","Credé maneuver","Romberg maneuver"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: While inserting the central line catheter, instruct the patient for the Valsalva maneuver (take a deep breath, hold it, and bear down) when the IV tubing and cap are changed.  Why A wrong: Trendelenburg is a body position, not a breathing maneuver.  Why C wrong: The Credé maneuver relates to bladder emptying.  Why D wrong: The Romberg test assesses balance/proprioception."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"An older male patient receiving normal saline 0.9% through an IV line is complaining of shortness of breath and palpitations. His assessment finding reveals that his CVP is 18 cm H2O. What would be the priority nursing intervention?","options":["Continue the IV fluid","Increase the flow rate of IV fluid","Discontinue the fluid","Notify the physician"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The keyed answer is C. A CVP of 18 cm H2O is far above the normal 5–10 cm H2O, indicating fluid overload; with shortness of breath and palpitations, the priority is to discontinue (stop) the fluid to prevent worsening overload/pulmonary edema.  Why A wrong: Continuing the fluid worsens the overload.  Why B wrong: Increasing the rate is dangerous in fluid overload.  Why D wrong: Notifying the physician is appropriate but is secondary to immediately stopping the offending fluid."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"In a patient with a central venous line about to be inserted, what is the smallest sized syringe recommended for first-time use with CVADs?","options":["10 ml","2 ml","3 ml","5 ml"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The keyed answer is A — 10 ml. A minimum-size syringe available in the central venous line kit is 5 ml (used for local anaesthetic administration), whereas 10 ml syringes are also available, which are used for flushing the line. A 10 ml syringe or larger must be used when first accessing any CVAD, as the higher pressure exerted by a smaller syringe can cause damage and catheter rupture.  Why B wrong: A 2 ml syringe generates excessive pressure that can rupture the catheter.  Why C wrong: A 3 ml syringe is too small and generates high pressure.  Why D wrong: 5 ml is used for local anaesthetic, not as the smallest size for first CVAD access (the recommended minimum for access is 10 ml)."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"A central venous catheter (CVAD) should be flushed using which technique, with a syringe no smaller than 10 ml?","options":["Continuous slow drip","Pulsated push-pause positive pressure technique","Rapid single bolus","Negative pressure aspiration"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The catheter should be flushed using a pulsated push-pause positive pressure technique, with a syringe no smaller than 10 ml.  Why A wrong: A continuous slow drip is not the described flushing technique.  Why C wrong: A rapid single bolus is not specified.  Why D wrong: Negative pressure aspiration is not the flushing technique."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"When drawing blood through a central venous catheter, which method is described as the safest and most accurate for obtaining a blood sample?","options":["Drawing from a peripheral vein (no longer accessible)","Drawing blood through a central venous catheter (CVC) in the patient's peripheral veins are no longer accessible","Capillary heel-stick","Arterial puncture"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Drawing blood through a CVC is the safest and most accurate way to draw blood when the patient's peripheral veins are no longer accessible.  Why A wrong: Peripheral access being unavailable is the very reason the CVC route is chosen.  Why C wrong: A capillary heel-stick is not described here.  Why D wrong: Arterial puncture is not the described method."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"Why should specimens for laboratory tests ideally be collected via the CVC when possible? (Select All That Apply)","options":["It minimizes the need for repeated needle punctures, reducing the risk of CVC-related complications such as occlusion and infection","To maintain patency, all CVCs are flushed before and after access and infusions","There are two methods to collect blood samples from a CVC: discard method and push-pull method","It eliminates the need for any flushing","It guarantees no blood will ever be wasted"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The text states lab specimens collected via CVC reduce the risk of CVC-related complications (occlusion, infection); CVCs are flushed before and after access/infusions to maintain patency; and there are two blood-sampling methods — discard and push-pull.  Why D wrong: Flushing is still required; it is not eliminated.  Why E wrong: The discard method intentionally discards blood; no such guarantee is given."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"In the discard (hub-to-hub) method of CVC blood sampling, after cleaning the catheter hub, what is attached and flushed first?","options":["A 10-ml syringe containing 5 to 10 ml of 0.9% NS solution to the central catheter hub","A 50-ml bag of dextrose","A heparin lock of 1000 units","An air-filled 2-ml syringe"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In the discard method, after cleaning the catheter hub, attach a 10-ml syringe containing 5 to 10 ml of 0.9% NS solution to the central catheter hub, then flush the catheter.  Why B wrong: A dextrose bag is not used to initiate sampling.  Why C wrong: A heparin lock dose is not the initial flush specified.  Why D wrong: An air-filled syringe would risk air embolism."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"In the discard method, after flushing, the Vacutainer sleeve/syringe is attached and how much blood must be discarded?","options":["At least 1 ml","At least 3 ml, or at least three times the catheter's priming volume","Exactly 10 ml regardless of priming volume","None — no blood is discarded"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Discard at least 3 ml of blood, or at least three times the catheter's priming volume, immediately into the appropriate waste container so it doesn't get confused with lab samples.  Why A wrong: 1 ml is below the stated minimum.  Why C wrong: The discard volume is tied to priming volume, not a fixed 10 ml.  Why D wrong: Blood must be discarded in this method."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"When collecting samples into Vacutainer tubes during CVC sampling, the correct order is to insert which tube first?","options":["The green tube first","The red one first, then green, blue, and lavender","The lavender tube first","The blue tube first"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: When obtaining lab samples with Vacutainer tubes, insert the red one first, then green, blue, and lavender.  Why A wrong: Green is not first in the stated order.  Why C wrong: Lavender is the last tube, not the first.  Why D wrong: Blue is third, not first."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"During CVC blood sampling, why should the lavender (coagulation) tube be used LAST?","options":["To reduce the effect of flushing solutions on the lab value","Because it contains the most additive","To prevent hemolysis only","Because it is the largest tube"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Use the lavender coagulation tube last to reduce the effect of flushing solutions on the lab value.  Why B wrong: The reason given is flushing-solution interference, not additive volume.  Why C wrong: Hemolysis prevention is not the stated rationale.  Why D wrong: Tube size is not the rationale."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"After obtaining samples in the discard method, the catheter is flushed with which solution, and which solution is explicitly stated NOT to be used?","options":["Flush with 0.9% sodium chloride; dextrose and RL should not be used","Flush with dextrose 5%; saline should not be used","Flush with Ringer's lactate; saline should not be used","Flush with sterile water; saline should not be used"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Flush the catheter with 0.9% sodium chloride solution, according to policy; dextrose and RL should not be used. Then apply a sterile end cap to the hub.  Why B wrong: Dextrose should not be used for the flush.  Why C wrong: RL (Ringer's lactate) should not be used.  Why D wrong: Sterile water is not the specified flush; 0.9% NaCl is."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"In the discard method, if blood cannot be aspirated into the Vacutainer, the nurse should:","options":["Use a syringe to obtain a blood sample","Force the Vacutainer harder","Disconnect the catheter","Abandon CVC sampling permanently"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: If you can't aspirate blood into the Vacutainer, use a syringe to obtain a blood sample.  Why B wrong: Forcing the Vacutainer is not advised.  Why C wrong: Disconnecting the catheter is unsafe and not the instruction.  Why D wrong: Sampling is not abandoned; a syringe is used instead."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"In the push-pull method of CVC blood sampling (syringe access), after flushing the catheter, the nurse uses the same syringe to pull and aspirate how much blood?","options":["1 ml","3 ml","6 ml","10 ml"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Using the same syringe, pull to aspirate 6 ml of blood into the syringe.  Why A wrong: 1 ml is not the stated volume.  Why B wrong: 3 ml is the discard-method minimum, not the push-pull aspiration volume.  Why D wrong: 10 ml is not the stated volume."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"In the push-pull method, after aspirating blood, the nurse keeps the syringe attached to the catheter hub and pushes to reinfuse the blood. This aspiration and reinfusion is repeated at least how many times?","options":["Once","Twice","At least three times","Five times"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Keep the syringe attached to the catheter hub, push to reinfuse the blood, and repeat the aspiration and reinfusion at least three times.  Why A wrong: Once is below the stated minimum.  Why B wrong: Twice is below the stated minimum.  Why D wrong: Five times is not the stated number."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"In the push-pull method, the final lab sample is drawn with what, and is then transferred to lab tubes how?","options":["A new syringe; without removing the stopper or releasing the vacuum","The same syringe; by removing the stopper","A Vacutainer only; releasing the vacuum","A capillary tube; by gravity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Remove and discard the used syringe, attach a new syringe to draw the sample for the lab, and transfer the sample to lab tubes without removing the stopper or releasing the vacuum.  Why B wrong: A new syringe (not the same) is used, and the stopper is not removed.  Why C wrong: A new syringe is used and the vacuum is not released.  Why D wrong: A capillary tube/gravity transfer is not described."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"When flushing a CVC to obtain a blood sample, which flush solution is preferred, and what should be done if blood cannot be drawn?","options":["Use a 10–20 ml flush of 0.9% sodium chloride solution; inject mouth-wash solution if blood cannot be drawn","Use a 10–20 ml flush of 0.9% sodium chloride solution; inject enough heparin saline to clear residual blood and apply a sterile end cap to the hub","Use sterile water; discard the catheter","Use 5% dextrose; clamp the line"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Use a syringe to flush the catheter with 10 to 20 ml of 0.9% sodium chloride solution; if blood cannot be drawn, inject enough heparin saline to clear residual blood and apply a sterile end cap to the hub.  Why A wrong: Mouthwash is not injected into a catheter.  Why C wrong: Sterile water is not specified and the catheter is not discarded.  Why D wrong: 5% dextrose is not the specified flush."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"If you must draw blood from a CVC for culturing, the sample should be:","options":["Flushed first, then drawn","Drawn directly from the hub and injected into a blood culture vial — do NOT discard any blood","Discarded for the first 3 ml as usual","Diluted with saline before culturing"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If you need to draw blood from a CVC for culturing, do not flush the catheter first; instead, draw the sample directly from the hub and inject it into a blood culture vial — do not discard any blood.  Why A wrong: The catheter should NOT be flushed first for cultures.  Why C wrong: No blood should be discarded when culturing.  Why D wrong: Dilution is not described."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"The most commonly used catheter for central venous access is the:","options":["Single lumen CVC","Double lumen CVC","Triple lumen CVC","Quadruple lumen CVC"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The most commonly used catheter for central venous access is the triple lumen CVC.  Why A wrong: Single lumen is not described as the most common.  Why B wrong: Double lumen is not the most common.  Why D wrong: Quadruple lumen is not mentioned."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"A triple lumen CVC consists of:","options":["One vascular access lumen within a single catheter body","Two communicating lumens within two catheter bodies","Three separate non-communicating vascular access lumens within a single catheter body","Three catheters bundled together externally"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: A triple lumen CVC has three separate non-communicating vascular access lumens within a single catheter body.  Why A wrong: A single lumen describes a different catheter.  Why B wrong: The lumens are non-communicating and within one body, not two communicating lumens in two bodies.  Why D wrong: They are within a single catheter body, not externally bundled."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"In the triple lumen CVC table, which color-coded lumen is the PROXIMAL (18 gauge) lumen used for blood sampling, medications, IV fluids, blood products, and colloids administration?","options":["White","Blue","Brown","Green"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Per the table, the White lumen is Proximal, 18 gauge, used for blood sampling, medications, IV fluids and blood products, and colloids administration.  Why B wrong: Blue is the medial lumen for hyperosmolar solutions (TPN).  Why C wrong: Brown is the distal lumen for high-pressure infusions/CVP monitoring.  Why D wrong: Green is not a lumen color in the table."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"In the triple lumen CVC table, the BLUE (medial, 18 gauge) lumen is used for administration of which solutions?","options":["Blood products and colloids","Hyperosmolar solutions (TPN), measuring CVP, haemodialysis, plasmapheresis","High-pressure infusions only","Local anaesthetic only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Per the table, the Blue medial (18 gauge) lumen is used for administration of hyperosmolar solutions (TPN), measuring CVP, haemodialysis, and plasmapheresis.  Why A wrong: Blood products/colloids are the white (proximal) lumen's use.  Why C wrong: High-pressure infusions are the brown (distal) lumen's use.  Why D wrong: Local anaesthetic administration is not a listed lumen use."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"In the triple lumen CVC table, the BROWN (distal) lumen is what gauge and is used for what?","options":["18 gauge; blood sampling","16 gauge; high-pressure infusions and CVP monitoring","14 gauge; TPN administration","20 gauge; flushing only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Per the table, the Brown distal lumen is 16 gauge and is used for high-pressure infusions and CVP monitoring.  Why A wrong: Blood sampling is the white (proximal, 18 gauge) lumen's use.  Why C wrong: TPN is the blue lumen's use; the brown is 16 gauge, not 14.  Why D wrong: It is 16 gauge and used for high-pressure infusions/CVP, not flushing only."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"Regarding lumen color-coding on CVCs, which statements are TRUE? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Be careful — not all lumens are marked with the same colours","Two-lumen catheters have a white and brown (proximal and distal) port","All catheters use identical universal color codes","The proximal port on a two-lumen catheter is white","The distal port on a two-lumen catheter is brown"],"correct":"A,B,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The note states not all lumens are marked with the same colours, and that two-lumen catheters have a white (proximal) and brown (distal) port.  Why C wrong: This contradicts the note that not all lumens use the same colors.  Why A, B, D, E correct: All match the stated note about two-lumen white/brown proximal/distal ports."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"Central venous pressure (CVP) is a pressure of:","options":["Pulmonary vein","Superior vena cava","Right atrium","Left ventricle"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The keyed answer is C — CVP is the pressure of the right atrium (reflecting right atrial/great vein pressure).  Why A wrong: The pulmonary vein carries oxygenated blood to the left atrium; it is not the CVP site.  Why B wrong: Although the catheter tip lies near the SVC/right atrial junction, CVP is defined as right atrial pressure per the key.  Why D wrong: The left ventricle is on the systemic arterial side, not the central venous side."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"A labour room (also known as a birthing room) should ideally be:","options":["Cold and brightly lit with no privacy considerations","Comfortable and furnished for the process of labour and delivery, where the baby is usually delivered","A shared open ward with no equipment","Located far from postnatal care"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A labour room (birthing room) is comfortable and furnished for the process of labour and delivery, wherein the baby is usually delivered; an hour after delivery, if everything is normal/stable, the mother is shifted to the postnatal ward.  Why A wrong: A cold/exposed environment contradicts the comfort description.  Why C wrong: It is purpose-equipped, not a bare open ward.  Why D wrong: Care flows to the postnatal ward after delivery."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"After delivery, if everything is normal or the mother is stable, she is shifted to which area?","options":["The operating theatre","The postnatal ward (about one hour after delivery)","The intensive care unit","The antenatal clinic"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: An hour after delivery, if everything is normal or the mother is stable, she is shifted to the postnatal ward.  Why A wrong: The OT is not the routine post-delivery destination for a stable mother.  Why C wrong: ICU transfer is for unstable patients, not the routine stated step.  Why D wrong: The antenatal clinic is for pre-delivery care."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"Which of the following are listed General Measures in the Labor Room (LR)? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Unnecessary entries to the labour room must be restricted","Labour room doctors and paramedics should wear a mask all the time","An individual autoclaved instrument set should be provided for each delivery","Random swab sampling to be taken from surfaces and disinfected articles monthly","Visitors should be freely allowed at all times"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: General measures listed include restricting unnecessary entries, doctors/paramedics wearing masks at all times, an individual autoclaved instrument set for each delivery, and monthly random swab sampling from surfaces/disinfected articles.  Why E wrong: This contradicts the rule restricting unnecessary entries."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"Per LR general measures, proper clothing of labour room personnel must include all of the following EXCEPT:","options":["Cap","Mask","Shoes/slippers and gown at the time of delivery","Open sandals and street clothes"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Proper clothing of labour room personnel is necessary, including cap, mask, shoes/slippers, and gown at the time of delivery — street clothes/open sandals are not appropriate.  Why A wrong: A cap is required.  Why B wrong: A mask is required.  Why C wrong: Shoes/slippers and gown are required at delivery."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"Air quality sampling in the labour room should be done by which method, and how often?","options":["Settle plate method, monthly","Active air sampling, daily","Swab method, weekly","Settle plate method, yearly"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Air quality sampling is to be done by the Settle plate method, monthly.  Why B wrong: Active air sampling/daily is not stated.  Why C wrong: A swab method/weekly is not the stated air-quality method.  Why D wrong: The frequency is monthly, not yearly."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"Which of the following areas are included in the Delivery Unit? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Receiving area","Examination room","Pre-delivery room (1st stage area)","Delivery (labour) room — both septic and aseptic, with NBCC (2nd–3rd stage)","Post-delivery observation room (4th stage area)"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The delivery unit includes the receiving area, examination room, pre-delivery room (1st stage area), delivery (labour) room both septic and aseptic with NBCC (2nd–3rd stage), and post-delivery observation room (4th stage area).  Why A–E wrong: None are incorrect; all are explicitly listed components of the delivery unit."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"Per the labour room organization, the delivery (labour) room corresponds to which stage(s) of labour and includes the NBCC?","options":["1st stage","2nd–3rd stage","4th stage","Antenatal stage"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The delivery (labour) room (both septic and aseptic, with NBCC) corresponds to the 2nd–3rd stage.  Why A wrong: The 1st stage corresponds to the pre-delivery room.  Why C wrong: The 4th stage corresponds to the post-delivery observation room.  Why D wrong: The antenatal stage precedes admission to the delivery unit."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"The post-delivery observation room corresponds to which stage area of labour?","options":["1st stage area","2nd stage area","3rd stage area","4th stage area"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The post-delivery observation room is the 4th stage area.  Why A wrong: The 1st stage area is the pre-delivery room.  Why B/C wrong: The 2nd–3rd stages correspond to the delivery (labour) room."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"According to the GOI MNH toolkit, how many kits/trays are to be kept in the labour room (\"Seven Kit or Trays\")?","options":["Five","Six","Seven","Eight"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: As per GOI's MNH toolkit, there are Seven Kits or Trays to be kept in the labour room.  Why A wrong: Five is incorrect.  Why B wrong: Six is incorrect.  Why D wrong: Eight is incorrect; the count is seven."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"Which of the following are among the Seven Kits/Trays to be kept in the labour room? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Delivery tray","Episiotomy tray","Baby tray and Medicine tray","Emergency drug tray","Manual Vacuum Aspiration (MVA) tray and PPIUCD tray"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The seven kits/trays are: 1. Delivery tray, 2. Episiotomy tray, 3. Baby tray, 4. Medicine tray, 5. Emergency drug tray, 6. Manual Vacuum Aspiration (MVA) tray, 7. PPIUCD tray.  Why A–E wrong: None are incorrect; all are listed among the seven trays."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"Which of the following are listed as ADDITIONAL trays/kits to be kept in the labour room (beyond the seven)? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Examination tray","PPH management kit/tray","Severe Pre-eclampsia and Eclampsia management tray","Delivery tray","Episiotomy tray"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The additional trays/kits are: 1. Examination tray, 2. PPH management kit/tray, 3. Severe Pre-eclampsia and Eclampsia management tray.  Why D wrong: The delivery tray is one of the seven primary trays, not an additional one.  Why E wrong: The episiotomy tray is one of the seven primary trays, not additional."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"Per the CHC/AH/SDH/DH/Medical Colleges staffing norms, for a facility with 100–200 deliveries per month, the recommended staff nurses (with LDR) and staff nurses (without LDR) are:","options":["4 with LDR and 8 without LDR","1 for each shift and 1 back up; 12 without LDR","8 with LDR and 16 without LDR","4 staff nurses per shift with no other staff"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For 100–200 deliveries per month, the table states \"in LDR facility there should be 4 staff nurses per (shift)\" under Staff Nurse (with LDR), and 8 under Staff Nurse (without LDR).  Why B wrong: \"1 for each shift and 1 back up\" with 12 nurses corresponds to the 200–500 deliveries category.  Why C wrong: 8 with LDR / 16 without corresponds to the >500 deliveries category.  Why D wrong: This omits the without-LDR staffing figure of 8."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"Per the staffing table, for 100–200 deliveries per month, the Medical Officer (MO) requirement is:","options":["4 MO, 1 OBG/EmoC, 1 Anaesthetist/LSAS, 1 Pediatrician","1 OBG (Mandatory) + 4 OBG/EmoC + 1 Anaesthetist + 4 LSAS + 1 Paediatrician + 4 MO","3 OBG (Mandatory) + 4 EmoC + 1 Anaesthetist + 4 LSAS + 1 Paediatrician + 4 MO","2 OBG only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For 100–200 deliveries, the MO column states 4 MO, 1 OBG/EmoC, 1 Anaesthetist/LSAS, 1 Pediatrician.  Why B wrong: That detailed mandatory-OBG breakdown corresponds to the 200–500 category.  Why C wrong: That corresponds to the >500 category.  Why D wrong: \"2 OBG only\" is not listed for any row."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"Per the staffing table, for 100–200 deliveries per month, the housekeeping, DEO, and guard requirements are respectively:","options":["4, 1, 4","8, 1, 6","12, 1, 8","2, 0, 2"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For 100–200 deliveries, the table lists House-keeping = 4, DEO = 1, Guard = 4.  Why B wrong: 8/1/6 corresponds to the 200–500 category.  Why C wrong: 12/1/8 corresponds to the >500 category.  Why D wrong: 2/0/2 is not listed for any row."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"Per the staffing table, for 200–500 deliveries per month, the Staff Nurse (with LDR) arrangement and Staff Nurse (without LDR) number are:","options":["In LDR facility there should be 4 staff nurses per shift; 8 without LDR","LDR unit (1 for each shift and 1 back up); 12 without LDR","LDR unit; 16 without LDR","1 staff nurse total; 4 without LDR"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For 200–500 deliveries, the table states \"LDR unit (1 for each shift and 1 back up)\" under Staff Nurse (with LDR), and 12 under Staff Nurse (without LDR).  Why A wrong: That (4 per shift / 8) is the 100–200 category.  Why C wrong: 16 without LDR is the >500 category.  Why D wrong: Not listed for any row."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"Per the staffing table, for 200–500 deliveries per month, the Medical Officer (MO) staffing and the housekeeping/DEO/guard numbers are:","options":["1 OBG (Mandatory) + 4 OBG/EmoC + 1 Anaesthetist + 4 LSAS + 1 Paediatrician + 4 MO; housekeeping 8, DEO 1, guard 6","4 MO, 1 OBG/EmoC, 1 Anaesthetist/LSAS, 1 Pediatrician; housekeeping 4, DEO 1, guard 4","3 OBG (Mandatory) + 4 EmoC; housekeeping 12, DEO 1, guard 8","2 OBG only; housekeeping 2, DEO 0, guard 2"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For 200–500 deliveries, the MO column lists 1 OBG (Mandatory), +4 OBG/EmoC, +1 Anaesthetist, +4 LSAS, +1 Paediatrician, +4 MO; and housekeeping = 8, DEO = 1, guard = 6.  Why B wrong: That MO/support staffing matches the 100–200 category.  Why C wrong: 3 OBG (Mandatory) + 4 EmoC and 12/1/8 support staff match the >500 category.  Why D wrong: Not listed for any row."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"Per the staffing table, for >500 deliveries per month, the number of Staff Nurses (without LDR) is:","options":["8","12","16","4"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: For >500 deliveries, the Staff Nurse (without LDR) figure is 16.  Why A wrong: 8 corresponds to the 100–200 category.  Why B wrong: 12 corresponds to the 200–500 category.  Why D wrong: 4 is not listed for this row."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"Per the staffing table, for >500 deliveries per month, the Medical Officer (MO) requirement is:","options":["4 MO, 1 OBG/EmoC, 1 Anaesthetist/LSAS, 1 Pediatrician","1 OBG (Mandatory) + 4 OBG/EmoC + 1 Anaesthetist + 4 LSAS + 1 Paediatrician + 4 MO","3 OBG (Mandatory) + 4 EmoC + 1 Anaesthetist + 4 LSAS + 1 Paediatrician + 4 MO","16 MO only"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: For >500 deliveries, the MO column states 3 OBG (Mandatory) + 4 EmoC, +1 Anaesthetist, +4 LSAS, +1 Paediatrician, +4 MO.  Why A wrong: That matches the 100–200 category.  Why B wrong: That matches the 200–500 category.  Why D wrong: \"16 MO only\" is the staff nurse (without LDR) figure misapplied; not the MO entry."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"Per the staffing table, for >500 deliveries per month, the housekeeping, DEO, and guard requirements are respectively:","options":["4, 1, 4","8, 1, 6","12, 1, 8","16, 1, 16"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: For >500 deliveries, the table lists House-keeping = 12, DEO = 1, Guard = 8.  Why A wrong: 4/1/4 is the 100–200 category.  Why B wrong: 8/1/6 is the 200–500 category.  Why D wrong: 16/1/16 is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"Across all delivery-volume categories in the staffing table, the number of Data Entry Operators (DEO) required is:","options":["1 in each category (100–200, 200–500, and >500)","0 in all categories","4 in each category","It increases from 1 to 4 to 8"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The DEO column shows 1 for each delivery-volume category (100–200, 200–500, >500).  Why B wrong: DEO is staffed (1 per category), not 0.  Why C wrong: 4 DEO is not listed for any category.  Why D wrong: It remains 1, not escalating like housekeeping/guard."},{"section":"Batch 056 · CVP measurement in ICU…","question":"The chapter covering labour room and obstetric guidelines is titled:","options":["\"Labor Room and Obs OT: Guidelines and Protocols\"","\"Antenatal Care Protocols\"","\"Neonatal Resuscitation Guidelines\"","\"Operation Theatre Sterilization\""],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The chapter heading reads \"Labor Room and Obs OT — Guidelines and Protocols\" (Chapter 23).  Why B wrong: Antenatal care is not the chapter title.  Why C wrong: Neonatal resuscitation is not the chapter title.  Why D wrong: OT sterilization is not the chapter title."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"According to the human-resources arrangement at the Labour-Delivery Room (LDR), which cadre of staff is expected to provide care \"round the clock\"?","options":["Only the guard","Staff Nurse / ANM and the housekeeping/sanitation worker","Only the housekeeping worker","Only the medical officer"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The LDR human-resource table lists the Staff Nurse / ANM and the Housekeeping (sanitation worker) as providing services \"round the clock,\" ensuring continuous staffing for labour and delivery.  Why A wrong: The guard provides services round the clock too, but the cadre directly delivering/maintaining care round the clock per the table is the nurse and housekeeper.  Why C wrong: Housekeeping alone is insufficient; nursing care must also be continuous.  Why D wrong: A dedicated round-the-clock medical officer is not the cadre specified in this staffing table."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"When equipment is brought into the labour room from the outside, what is the correct first step before its use?","options":["Use it immediately","Wipe its surface down with High-Level Disinfectant (HLD) before bringing it into the room","Sterilize it only after the procedure","Store it in the corridor for 24 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The cleaning rules state that whenever equipment is brought from outside into the labour room, its surface should be wiped down with HLD before bringing it into the room.  Why A wrong: Immediate unwiped use risks introducing external contamination.  Why C wrong: Surface disinfection must occur before entry, not only after the procedure.  Why D wrong: Corridor storage is not the specified protocol."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"In which direction should cleaning of the labour room be carried out?","options":["Bottom-to-top","Top-to-bottom","Centre outward only","Randomly as time permits"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The general rules specify that when cleaning the labour room, cleaning should be done in a top-to-bottom sequence so dirt falls downward and is removed last.  Why A wrong: Bottom-to-top would re-contaminate already cleaned lower surfaces.  Why C wrong: Centre-outward is the rule for cleaning floors, not the overall vertical sequence.  Why D wrong: A systematic sequence, not random cleaning, is required."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"When cleaning the floor of the labour room, the cleaning staff should move in which direction relative to the door?","options":["From the door toward the inside of the room","From the inside, working toward the door (door is the last and farthest point — \"go in to out\")","From the centre to the door and back","Direction does not matter for floors"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The rules state cleaning of the floor begins at the back and the door is the last and farthest point — staff should move toward the door (\"go in to out\"), so they exit a freshly cleaned area.  Why A wrong: Moving door-to-inside drags contamination into the clean zone.  Why C wrong: The protocol is a single directional sweep ending at the door, not a back-and-forth.  Why D wrong: Direction is specifically prescribed to prevent re-contamination."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"When cleaning individual equipment in the labour room, what is the directional rule?","options":["Clean from dirty to clean","Clean from top to bottom","Clean from outside to inside","Clean from clean to dirty"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: When cleaning individual equipment, the rule is to clean from clean to dirty, so contamination is not spread onto already-clean areas.  Why A wrong: Dirty-to-clean spreads pathogens onto clean surfaces.  Why B wrong: Top-to-bottom is the rule for the room/surfaces, not the clean-to-dirty equipment rule.  Why C wrong: Outside-to-inside is not the specified equipment-cleaning rule."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"A set of general rules is applied to facilitate fast and easy cleaning of the labour room. Which of the following is one of those rules? (SATA)","options":["Minimize the number of products used","Minimize the number of horizontal surfaces","Provide smooth finishes and minimum joints in surfaces","Round off corners wherever possible for easy cleaning","Maximize the number of joints and seams"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A, B, C, D): The text lists rules to facilitate fast/easy cleaning: minimize the number of products, minimize the number of horizontal surfaces, provide smooth finishes with minimum joints in surfaces, and round off corners wherever possible.  Why E wrong: Maximizing joints/seams creates more dirt-trapping crevices — the opposite of the stated principle of minimum joints."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"How often should the labour-room walls be cleaned, and how should dust be handled?","options":["Weekly; with a dry cloth","Every 2–3 times a week, cleaning as soon as visible dust is present and always with a wet cloth","Once a month; with a dry duster","Only when stained; with a dry mop"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states labour-room walls may be cleaned 2–3 times a week, cleaned as soon as dust is present, and always cleaned with a wet cloth (to avoid dispersing dust).  Why A wrong: Dry cloth disperses dust into the sterile environment; frequency stated is 2–3 times/week, not weekly.  Why C wrong: Monthly is too infrequent for walls; dry dusting is not used.  Why D wrong: Cleaning is scheduled, not only when stained, and is done with a wet cloth."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"How often should the Bio-Medical Waste (BMW) container be emptied, and to what capacity should it be filled?","options":["Once daily, when completely full","At least thrice a day or when the waste container is 3/4ths full","Once a week, regardless of fill level","Only when overflowing"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text directs that BMW be emptied at least thrice a day or when the waste container is 3/4ths (¾) full, to prevent overflow and infection.  Why A wrong: Once daily / completely full risks overflow and contamination.  Why C wrong: Weekly emptying is grossly inadequate for biomedical waste.  Why D wrong: Waste must be removed before overflowing, at the ¾ mark or thrice daily."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"How frequently should daily routine cleaning of the labour room and disinfection be carried out?","options":["Once a day","Twice a day","Once a week","Every hour"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The \"Cleaning of Equipment\" / routine section specifies that routine cleaning of the room/equipment surface should be done twice a day.  Why A wrong: Once-daily is below the stated twice-daily standard.  Why C wrong: Weekly cleaning is far too infrequent.  Why D wrong: Hourly cleaning is not the specified routine schedule."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"After cleaning, how should disinfectant be allowed to act on the equipment surface in the labour room?","options":["Wipe it off immediately to dry the surface","Always begin cleaning at the top and go back into the wiped area to redo it","Allow the disinfectant/cleaner to dry naturally on the surface","Rinse the disinfectant off with plain water at once"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The text directs that the disinfectant/cleaner be allowed to dry naturally on the surface, ensuring adequate contact (dwell) time for microbial kill.  Why A wrong: Immediate wiping reduces contact time and disinfection efficacy.  Why B wrong: Going back into the wiped area re-contaminates it; cleaning should not double back.  Why D wrong: Rinsing off at once removes the disinfectant before it can act."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"When moving from one piece of equipment or area to the next during cleaning, what should the staff do?","options":["Reuse the same cloth/mop to save time","Change the mop/cloth and disinfectant/cleaner","Use only water for subsequent areas","Continue with the same solution for the whole room"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states that when moving from one piece of equipment to another, and when moving from one area to the next, staff should change the mop, cloth, disinfectant and cleaner to prevent cross-contamination.  Why A wrong: Reusing the same cloth/mop spreads contamination between areas.  Why C wrong: Plain water alone does not disinfect.  Why D wrong: A single solution used throughout becomes contaminated and ineffective."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"After a delivery, when cleaning a soiled gown, soiled gloves, or soiled footwear, which method of disinfection is recommended?","options":["Air-drying only","Immersion in chlorine solution (500–1000 ppm) for 5–10 minutes","Wiping with a dry cloth","Washing under running cold water without disinfectant"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The \"Cleaning after a delivery\" section directs that soiled gowns, gloves and footwear be cleaned separately and disinfected by immersion in chlorine solution (500–1000 ppm) for 5–10 minutes, followed by a plain water rinse before washing with warm soapy water.  Why A wrong: Air-drying alone does not disinfect contaminated items.  Why C wrong: Dry wiping does not achieve disinfection of soiled items.  Why D wrong: Plain cold running water without chlorine immersion is insufficient disinfection."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"During cleaning after a delivery, what should be done with the chlorine solution once it has been closed/used?","options":["Reuse it indefinitely","Keep the labour-room door closed after the final rinsing (and discard solution appropriately)","Pour it directly onto the patient bed","Store it uncovered for the next shift"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The post-delivery cleaning steps include keeping the labour-room door closed after the final rinse, and items are rinsed with plain water before warm soapy washing; spent solution is not retained for reuse.  Why A wrong: Chlorine solutions lose efficacy and should not be reused indefinitely.  Why C wrong: Pouring solution on the bed is unsafe and not part of protocol.  Why D wrong: Storing chlorine uncovered degrades it and is not directed."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"During the detailed wash-down of the labour room, removed instruments used in delivery should first be handled how before final processing?","options":["Discarded directly into general waste","Removed (gathered) and sent/transported for cleaning and sterilization","Reused on the next patient without processing","Left soaking on the floor"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The detailed wash-down section directs that instruments used in the delivery be removed and sent/transported for cleaning and sterilization.  Why A wrong: Reusable instruments are not general waste; they are processed and reused.  Why C wrong: Reuse without sterilization causes cross-infection.  Why D wrong: Soaking on the floor is unhygienic and not the protocol."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"As part of the detailed wash-down, when should the utility gloves, table, V-stand, stool, etc. be cleaned and with what?","options":["Weekly, with plain water","Detailed wash-down with HLD (the gloves, table, V-stand, stool etc. cleaned with HLD)","Only with soap and water, monthly","They never require cleaning"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The detailed wash-down section directs cleaning of the gloves, table, V-stand, stool, etc., with HLD (high-level disinfectant).  Why A wrong: Plain water does not disinfect these surfaces.  Why C wrong: Soap and water alone is insufficient; HLD is specified, and frequency is per the wash-down, not merely monthly.  Why D wrong: These items absolutely require regular cleaning/disinfection."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"Per the detailed wash-down protocol, how often should the operation theatre / labour-room wash-down following the OT Guidelines/\"Champder\" be performed?","options":["Daily","At least twice a month","Once a year","Only after a contaminated case"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text directs that the detailed wash-down (per OT guidelines) be performed at least twice a month.  Why A wrong: A full detailed wash-down is not the daily routine (daily routine cleaning is twice daily, separate from the detailed wash-down).  Why C wrong: Annual wash-down is far below the stated standard.  Why D wrong: It is a scheduled (twice-monthly) activity, not only post-contamination."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"According to the labour-room \"Do's,\" what should be done with all emergency drugs in the LR at the same time?","options":["Stored at room temperature only when needed","Available all the time, with expiry dates checked","Kept locked in a cupboard accessible only to the medical officer","Discarded weekly regardless of expiry"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The \"Do's\" list states that all (life-saving) emergency drugs of the labour room must be available all the time, and emergency drugs must be checked for expiry dates.  Why A wrong: Emergency drugs must be ready at all times, not only when needed.  Why C wrong: Locking them away from the immediate care team would delay emergency access.  Why D wrong: Drugs are managed by expiry checking, not arbitrary weekly disposal."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"Per the labour-room \"Do's,\" within what temperature range should the labour-room temperature be maintained for the baby?","options":["Between 15°C and 18°C","Between 25°C and 28°C (to maintain warmth for the newborn)","Between 30°C and 35°C","Below 20°C"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The \"Do's\" / preparation guidance directs that the labour-room temperature be maintained between 25°C and 28°C to keep the baby warm (consistent with AIIMS-NMR 2018 reference).  Why A wrong: 15–18°C is cold and risks neonatal hypothermia.  Why C wrong: 30–35°C exceeds the maintained range and risks overheating.  Why D wrong: Below 20°C is too cold for the newborn."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"Per the labour-room \"Don'ts,\" which of the following practices should be AVOIDED in the labour room? (SATA)","options":["Do not keep almirahs/metal cabinets in the LR","Do not burn coal in the LR for lighting/heating or any other purpose","Routinely give the newborn a bath without warning","Do not allow visitors or nurses-and-birth companions to enter the LR without wearing a gown, cap, slipper, mask","Do routine Oxytocin IM/IV drip to augment labour without indication"],"correct":"A,B,D","rationale":"Why correct (A): The \"Don'ts\" list prohibits keeping almirahs/metal cabinets in the LR.  Why correct (B): The list prohibits burning coal in the LR for lighting, heating, or any purpose (fire/fumes hazard).  Why correct (D): The list prohibits visitors, nurses, and birth companions entering the LR without wearing gown, cap, slipper and mask.  Why C wrong: This option as worded (\"routinely give the newborn a bath without warning\") is itself a discouraged action — but it is phrased as something to do, so it is NOT a correct \"don't to avoid\" selection in the form given (the source warns against unnecessary newborn bathing; the option's \"do\" phrasing makes it incorrect as a selectable practice-to-perform).  Why E wrong: Routine oxytocin to augment/accelerate labour without indication is explicitly prohibited; phrased as \"do,\" it is an unsafe action, not something to perform."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"According to the labour-room \"Don'ts,\" what is the rule regarding the use of antiseptics/antibiotics and the giving of routine oxytocin drips?","options":["Give prophylactic antibiotics to every woman in labour","Do not use routine oxytocin (Oxytocin IM or IV drip) for augmenting labour without indication","Always start an oxytocin drip on admission","Antibiotics are mandatory before every vaginal birth"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The \"Don'ts\" list states not to use routine oxytocin IM/IV drip for augmenting labour without a proper indication, and discourages routine/unnecessary antibiotic and intervention use.  Why A wrong: Universal prophylactic antibiotics for every labouring woman is not recommended.  Why C wrong: Routine oxytocin on admission for all women is precisely what is prohibited.  Why D wrong: Mandatory antibiotics before every vaginal birth is not the stated practice."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"During preparation of the delivery room, when should it be prepared and with what should the room and floor be cleaned?","options":["Only after the baby is born; with plain water","Before the second stage of labour reached / before the birth of the baby; room and floor cleaned with soap and water and antiseptic solutions","A week in advance; with bleach only","It does not require advance preparation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The \"Preparation of delivery room\" section directs that the room be prepared before the birth of the baby, and the room and floor be cleaned with soap and water and antiseptic solutions.  Why A wrong: Preparation must occur before, not after, the birth.  Why C wrong: Bleach-only a week ahead is not the protocol; cleaning is done in preparation for the specific birth.  Why D wrong: Advance preparation is explicitly required."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"When preparing the delivery room environment, how should the room temperature/ventilation be managed?","options":["Cold and well-ventilated with open windows","Maintain proper and adequate lighting and adequate ventilation, with the room kept warm","Dark and sealed to keep the baby asleep","Bright lights with strong cross-ventilation directly on the bed"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The preparation section directs maintaining proper and adequate lighting and adequate ventilation while keeping the room warm for the newborn.  Why A wrong: A cold room risks neonatal hypothermia.  Why C wrong: A dark room impedes assessment of the mother and baby.  Why D wrong: Strong cross-ventilation/draughts directly on the bed chills the newborn."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"Which items must be kept ready and checked as part of preparing the delivery-room equipment? (SATA)","options":["Sterile equipment/instruments arranged on the instrument table","Equipment needed for delivery sterilized and kept in working condition and accessible","Equipment and all materials for the baby and mother including resuscitation set, oxygen, suction, and warm dry clothes as needed","Emergency medicine kept ready (oxytocin, lignocaine, magnesium sulphate, etc.)","Used instruments left unwashed for the next case"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A): Sterile equipment should be prepared and instruments arranged on the instrument table.  Why correct (B): Equipment needed for delivery should be sterilized, kept in working condition, and accessible.  Why correct (C): Equipment and materials for baby and mother — resuscitation set, oxygen, suction, warm dry clothes — must be ready as needed.  Why correct (D): Emergency medicine (oxytocin, lignocaine, magnesium sulphate, etc.) must be kept ready.  Why E wrong: Leaving used instruments unwashed for the next case violates sterilization protocol and is never directed."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"When preparing the mother for delivery, how should she be positioned during labour, and in what position should she be placed just before crowning/birth?","options":["Prone throughout; lithotomy at crowning","Positioned during labour as lateral or partial sitting; placed in dorsal/left-lateral position just before delivery (e.g., dorsal/left lateral 15° position)","Standing only; supine flat at birth","Trendelenburg throughout"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The preparation-of-the-mother section directs positioning the woman during labour as lateral or partial sitting (which commonly relieves aorto-caval compression and facilitates pushing), and placing her in a left-lateral/dorsal position before delivery.  Why A wrong: Prone is not used; lithotomy is not the position described in the text.  Why C wrong: Standing throughout and flat supine at birth is not the described protocol and supine flat risks supine hypotension.  Why D wrong: Trendelenburg throughout is not a labour position in this text."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"Before delivery, why should the mother be encouraged to empty her bladder?","options":["To reduce the volume of amniotic fluid","To ensure the bladder is empty (a full bladder can obstruct descent of the fetal head and increase the risk of bladder injury)","To speed up the third stage only","To prevent the need for an enema"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The preparation-of-the-mother section directs ensuring the bladder is empty; a full bladder can impede fetal descent and is at risk of injury during delivery.  Why A wrong: Bladder emptying does not affect amniotic fluid volume.  Why C wrong: Its primary purpose is to facilitate descent and prevent injury, not solely the third stage.  Why D wrong: It is unrelated to whether an enema is given."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"During delivery, how often should the fetal heart sound be checked according to the \"Fetal condition\" monitoring guidance?","options":["Every hour","Every 5–15 minutes (or applying continuous monitoring if possible), recording the rate and rhythm per minute","Only once on admission","Every 30 minutes regardless of stage"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The \"Fetal condition\" guidance directs checking the fetal heart sound every 5–15 minutes (or continuous monitoring if possible), recording the rate and rhythm per minute, and observing for membrane rupture, colour and accidental cord prolapse.  Why A wrong: Hourly is too infrequent for intrapartum fetal monitoring.  Why C wrong: A single admission check is inadequate for ongoing labour.  Why D wrong: The stated interval is 5–15 minutes, not a fixed 30 minutes."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"Per the \"Fetal condition\" assessment, what should the nurse observe for when the membranes rupture? (SATA)","options":["Colour of the liquor (amniotic fluid)","Accidental cord prolapse","Any fetal abnormalities on examination","The mother's blood group","The room's electricity supply"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct (A): After membrane rupture, observe the colour of the liquor (e.g., meconium staining indicates fetal distress).  Why correct (B): Observe for accidental cord prolapse, an obstetric emergency.  Why correct (C): Record fetal heart rate and observe for any fetal abnormalities.  Why D wrong: Blood group is a prenatal record item, not an observation made at membrane rupture.  Why E wrong: Electricity supply is unrelated to fetal-condition assessment."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"How frequently should the maternal blood pressure (BP) and pulse be checked during labour according to the \"Maternal condition\" guidance?","options":["Every 4 hours","Every 15 minutes, recording them and watching for distress and any abnormality","Once at the start of labour","Only when the woman complains of symptoms"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The \"Maternal condition\" guidance directs checking the BP and pulse every 15 minutes, recording them, and watching for maternal distress/abnormality.  Why A wrong: Every 4 hours is too infrequent for intrapartum maternal monitoring.  Why C wrong: A single start-of-labour check misses developing complications.  Why D wrong: Monitoring is scheduled (q15 min), not symptom-triggered only."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"Under \"Labor condition,\" when monitoring the progress of labour by abdominal and vaginal examination, what specifically must the nurse monitor?","options":["Only the fetal weight","The labour condition such as uterine contraction (strength and interval) and the dilatation of the cervix (per vaginal examination)","Only the mother's diet","Only the room temperature"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The \"Labor condition\" guidance directs observing and recording the progress of labour by abdominal and vaginal examination — monitoring uterine contractions (strength and interval) and cervical dilatation, and examination/descent with crowning of the head occurring during contraction.  Why A wrong: Fetal weight is not assessed by intrapartum contraction monitoring.  Why C wrong: Diet is not part of labour-progress monitoring.  Why D wrong: Room temperature, while controlled, is not the labour-condition parameter."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"In preparing as the midwife/nurse for delivery, what determines the timing of self-preparation (gowning/scrubbing) for the conduct of delivery?","options":["The convenience of the staff","The rate of labor advancement and the dilatation of the cervix — in a multiparous woman who is progressing rapidly, scrub toward the end of the first stage of labor; in a primigravida, scrub at full dilatation or crowning of the head occurring during a contraction","A fixed clock time regardless of progress","Only after the baby is delivered"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The \"Preparation of midwife/nurse\" section states timing of preparation depends on the rate of labor advancement and cervical dilatation: in a multiparous woman progressing rapidly, scrub toward the end of the first stage; in a primigravida, scrub at full dilatation or crowning of the head during a contraction.  Why A wrong: Timing is dictated by labour progress, not staff convenience.  Why C wrong: A fixed clock time ignores the individual labour's pace.  Why D wrong: The midwife must be scrubbed before, not after, delivery."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"Why should the midwife/nurse prepare early so as to become \"contaminated and ready for management\" before the actual delivery?","options":["To avoid washing hands altogether","To prepare the oxytocin injection (10 units) for third-stage management and to be ready to act without delay","To delay the delivery","To allow visitors into the room"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The preparation section directs early scrubbing so the midwife is ready, including preparing the oxytocin injection (10 units) for third-stage (active management of third stage of labour) and putting on a clean gown and plastic apron.  Why A wrong: Hand washing/scrubbing is mandatory, not avoided.  Why C wrong: Early preparation prevents delay, not creates it.  Why D wrong: Visitor entry is unrelated and discouraged."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"When preparing for delivery, after washing or scrubbing the hands, how should the midwife dry the hands, and how should nails be handled?","options":["Dry on her own gown; nails left long for grip","Dry the hands using a sterile towel/cloth and ensure nails are kept tidy and trimmed (free of ornaments)","Air-dry only; nails painted","Dry with the patient's linen; nails uncut"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The preparation steps direct drying the hands with a sterile towel after washing/scrubbing, keeping nails short/tidy, and removing ornaments — then putting on sterile gloves.  Why A wrong: Drying on the gown contaminates the hands; long nails harbour pathogens and can injure.  Why C wrong: Nail polish can harbour organisms and is discouraged in sterile procedures.  Why D wrong: Patient linen is not sterile; nails must be trimmed."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"What is triage in the labor room best defined as?","options":["A specific physical place where all patients are sent","A standardized process used to determine which patients need to be assessed urgently and which patients can safely wait — it is a process, not a place","A method of delivering the baby","A type of obstetric surgery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text defines triage as a standardized process to determine which patients need urgent assessment and which can safely wait, and explicitly states it is a process, not a place.  Why A wrong: The text specifically says triage is NOT a place.  Why C wrong: Triage is patient prioritisation, not a delivery method.  Why D wrong: Triage is not a surgical procedure."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"The Obstetric Triage Acuity Scale (OTAS) is based on which established triage approach?","options":["The American Triage Index","The Canadian Triage Acuity Scale (CTAS)","The WHO Partograph","The Apgar scoring system"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states OTAS is an obstetric triage scale based on the Canadian Triage Acuity Scale (CTAS), which standardizes a consistent approach to identify and classify women/pregnant individuals based on acuity.  Why A wrong: There is no \"American Triage Index\" referenced as the OTAS basis.  Why C wrong: The partograph charts labour progress; it is not the basis of OTAS.  Why D wrong: Apgar scores the newborn, not maternal triage."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"OTAS consists of how many levels of acuity, and what are they?","options":["Three levels: critical, urgent, non-urgent","Five levels: critical, emergency, urgent, semi-urgent, and non-urgent","Four levels: red, yellow, green, blue","Two levels: urgent and non-urgent"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states OTAS consists of five levels of elements/acuity: critical, emergency, urgent, semi-urgent, and non-urgent.  Why A wrong: Three levels understate the five-tier OTAS system.  Why C wrong: Those are colour codes, not the named acuity levels (and there are five).  Why D wrong: Two levels grossly oversimplify the scale."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"What does the OTAS prioritize/classify based on?","options":["The patient's order of arrival only","The acuity (care requirement) of a woman/pregnant individual by systematically assessing if/when they are in need of urgent or life-threatening care","The patient's ability to pay","The alphabetical order of names"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states OTAS prioritizes the care requirement(s) of a woman/pregnant individual by systematically assessing if/when they are in need of urgent or life-threatening care.  Why A wrong: Triage prioritizes by clinical acuity, not arrival order.  Why C wrong: Ability to pay is irrelevant to clinical triage.  Why D wrong: Names/alphabetical order have no role in acuity triage."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"The 5-OTAS system uses colour-coded acuity levels. Match the acuity element to its correct colour. Which mapping is correct?","options":["Critical = Green; Emergency = Yellow","Critical = Sky blue; Emergency = Red; Urgent = Yellow; Semi-urgent = Green; Non-urgent = light black","Critical = Red; Emergency = Green; Urgent = Blue","All five levels share the colour white"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text lists the 5-OTAS colour system as: 1) Critical = Sky blue, 2) Emergency = Red, 3) Urgent = Yellow, 4) Semi-urgent = Green, 5) Non-urgent = light black.  Why A wrong: Critical is sky blue (not green) and emergency is red (not yellow).  Why C wrong: Critical is sky blue, emergency is red, urgent is yellow — this mapping is wrong.  Why D wrong: Each level has a distinct colour, not a shared white."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"In the OTAS colour-code reference chart, under which colour category would \"eclampsia/hypertension,\" \"haemorrhage/heavy vaginal bleeding,\" \"APH/PPH haemorrhage,\" \"convulsions/unconscious,\" \"abnormal vital signs,\" and \"sickle-cell crisis / 2-or-more previous c/s in active labour\" be classified?","options":["Yellow","Red","Green","None of these"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The chart lists under RED the most acute presentations including eclampsia/hypertension, haemorrhage/heavy vaginal bleeding, APH/PPH haemorrhage, convulsions/unconscious, abnormal vital signs, sickle-cell crisis, and 2-or-more previous caesarean sections in active labour.  Why A wrong: Yellow groups intermediate conditions (e.g., sickle cell not in crisis, decreased/no fetal movement, multiple pregnancy, pre-term labour), not these emergencies.  Why C wrong: Green covers normal/uncomplicated labour, not these emergencies.  Why D wrong: These are clearly red-category emergencies."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"In the OTAS colour-code reference chart, which conditions fall under the YELLOW category? (SATA)","options":["Sickle cell (not in crisis)","Decreased or no fetal movement","Multiple pregnancy / pre-term labour, pre-term rupture of membranes","Stable/managed hypertension, previous c/s, diabetic mother, HIV positive","Eclampsia with active convulsions"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A): Sickle cell (not in crisis) is listed under yellow.  Why correct (B): Decreased or no fetal movement is a yellow-category concern.  Why correct (C): Multiple pregnancy, pre-term labour and pre-term rupture of membranes are yellow.  Why correct (D): Stable/managed hypertension, previous c/s, diabetic mother, and HIV-positive are yellow-category.  Why E wrong: Eclampsia with active convulsions is a RED (most acute) condition, not yellow."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"The OTAS bundle is made up of three acuity scales based on the stages of pregnancy. Which of the following is one of those stages? (SATA)","options":["Early pregnancy (0–20 weeks gestation)","Pregnancy greater than 20 weeks gestation","Postpartum","Pre-conception (before pregnancy)","Menopause"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct (A): The OTAS bundle includes early pregnancy (0–20 weeks gestation).  Why correct (B): It includes pregnancy >20 weeks gestation.  Why correct (C): It includes the postpartum period.  Why D wrong: Pre-conception is not part of the OTAS pregnancy-stage bundle.  Why E wrong: Menopause is unrelated to obstetric triage staging."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"Per the OTAS Score table, a \"Level 1 — Resuscitative\" patient requires what time to initial assessment (by RN), time to RMP contact, and reassessment?","options":["Within 15 min; <15 min; every 30 min","Immediate assessment; immediate RMP contact; continuous reassessment","5–10 min; <30 min; every 60 min","9–60 min; <120 min; every 60 min"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The OTAS Score table lists Level 1 (Resuscitative) as: time to initial assessment = immediate, time to RMP contact = immediate, reassessment = continuous.  Why A wrong: 15-minute/30-minute figures correspond to lower-acuity levels, not Level 1.  Why C wrong: 5–10/30/60-minute figures correspond to lower-acuity levels.  Why D wrong: 9–60/120-minute figures correspond to the lowest-acuity (non-urgent) level."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"Per the OTAS Score table, a \"Level 2 — Emergent\" patient requires what time to initial assessment and reassessment interval?","options":["Immediate; continuous","Immediate initial assessment; reassessment every 5 min (RMP contact <15 min)","5–10 min; every 15 min","9–60 min; every 60 min"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The OTAS table lists Level 2 (Emergent) as immediate initial assessment, RMP contact <15 min, and reassessment every 5 min.  Why A wrong: Continuous reassessment corresponds to Level 1 (Resuscitative), not Level 2.  Why C wrong: 5–10 min/every 15 min corresponds to a lower-acuity level (Urgent).  Why D wrong: 9–60 min/every 60 min corresponds to the lowest-acuity level."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"Per the OTAS Score table, a \"Level 3 — Urgent\" patient should have initial assessment within what time, with reassessment at what interval?","options":["Immediate; continuous","5–10 min initial assessment; RMP contact <30 min; reassessment every 15 min","9–60 min; every 60 min","Immediate; every 5 min"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The OTAS table lists Level 3 (Urgent) as initial assessment within 5–10 min, RMP contact <30 min, and reassessment every 15 min.  Why A wrong: Immediate/continuous is Level 1.  Why C wrong: 9–60 min/every 60 min is the lowest-acuity level.  Why D wrong: Immediate/every 5 min is Level 2 (Emergent)."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"Per the OTAS Score table, a \"Level 4 — Less Urgent\" patient should be reassessed at what interval (initial assessment 5–10 min)?","options":["Every 5 min","Every 30 min (RMP contact <60 min)","Continuous","Every 120 min"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The OTAS table lists Level 4 (Less Urgent) as initial assessment 5–10 min, RMP contact <60 min, and reassessment every 30 min.  Why A wrong: Every 5 min is Level 2 (Emergent).  Why C wrong: Continuous reassessment is Level 1 (Resuscitative).  Why D wrong: 120-minute intervals are not the Level 4 figure."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"Per the OTAS Score table, a \"Level 5 — Non-Urgent\" patient should be reassessed at what interval, with what time to RMP contact?","options":["Every 5 min; immediate","Every 60 min; RMP contact <120 min (initial assessment 9–60 min)","Continuous; immediate","Every 15 min; <30 min"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The OTAS table lists Level 5 (Non-Urgent) as initial assessment 9–60 min, RMP contact <120 min, and reassessment every 60 min.  Why A wrong: Every 5 min/immediate corresponds to higher-acuity levels.  Why C wrong: Continuous/immediate is Level 1.  Why D wrong: Every 15 min/<30 min corresponds to Level 3 (Urgent)."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"The Newborn Care Corner (NCC) is mandatory for which areas?","options":["Only the general paediatric ward","All labour rooms and obstetric OTs (operation theatres) / all delivery points","Only the outpatient department","Only district hospitals"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Newborn Care Corner (NCC) section states it is mandatory for all labour rooms and obstetric OTs — i.e., every labour room and obstetric OT (every delivery point) should have an NBCC with a radiant warmer and a functional bag-and-mask of appropriate size.  Why A wrong: It is required at delivery points (LR/OT), not just the paediatric ward.  Why C wrong: The OPD is not the mandated NCC location.  Why D wrong: NCC is required at all delivery points, not only district hospitals."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"Which of the following are listed functions/components of the Newborn Care Corner (NCC)? (SATA)","options":["Essential Care at Birth and Resuscitation of Newborn","Provision of Warmth and Early initiation of breastfeeding","Weighing the neonate and immunization services","Inspecting the newborn for gross congenital anomalies","Performing emergency caesarean sections"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A): NCC functions include Essential Care at Birth and Resuscitation of Newborn.  Why correct (B): NCC provides Provision of Warmth and Early initiation of breastfeeding.  Why correct (C): NCC includes Weighing the neonate and immunization services.  Why correct (D): NCC includes Inspecting the newborn for gross congenital anomalies.  Why E wrong: Caesarean section is a maternal surgical procedure, not an NCC newborn-care function."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"The NCC area should be kept free of which environmental hazard?","options":["Free of light","Free of draught (draught-free), with a radiant warmer used so the baby does not lose care under it","Free of all electrical supply","Free of any staff"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The NCC guidance states the room should be draught-free, and that a baby placed under a radiant warmer will not need care under a radiant warmer (the warmer maintains warmth) so the baby should be placed under a radiant warmer for ENBC and, if required, resuscitation.  Why A wrong: Adequate light is needed for newborn assessment, not darkness.  Why C wrong: Electrical supply is needed to run the radiant warmer.  Why D wrong: A trained provider must be present for newborn care/resuscitation."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"According to the NCC resuscitation guidance, which signs in the newborn should prompt resuscitation? (SATA)","options":["Meconium-stained liquor and preterm labour-associated risk","Baby not crying and/or limp/floppy baby","The baby being one or more than the doctor's advice (per advice)","A vigorous, crying, pink newborn","A baby breastfeeding well"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct (A): Meconium-stained liquor (and preterm) is a listed indication to be ready for resuscitation.  Why correct (B): A baby not crying and/or a limp/floppy baby indicates the need for resuscitation.  Why correct (C): The text notes resuscitation/management per the doctor's advice (\"Or as per doctor's advice\").  Why D wrong: A vigorous, crying, pink baby is healthy and does not require resuscitation.  Why E wrong: A baby breastfeeding well is well-adapted and does not need resuscitation."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"What is the LaQshya program?","options":["A nutrition supplementation scheme","A Labour Room Quality Improvement Initiative (a quality-improvement initiative aimed at improving quality of care in the labour room, maternity OT, and obstetric/post-partum period)","A childhood immunization drive","A family-planning campaign"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Overview states LaQshya is the Labour Room Quality Improvement Initiative — a quality-improvement initiative to improve Quality of Care in the labour room and maternity operation theatres in public health facilities, including the intrapartum and immediate post-partum period.  Why A wrong: LaQshya is a quality-improvement initiative, not nutrition supplementation.  Why C wrong: It is not an immunization drive.  Why D wrong: It is not a family-planning campaign."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"When was the LaQshya program launched, and what was its purpose?","options":["Launched 2010 to expand immunization","Launched on 11 December 2017 to improve quality of care in the labour room and maternity operation theatres in public health facilities","Launched 2020 to build new hospitals","Launched 2005 to train ASHA workers"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Overview states the LaQshya program was launched on 11 December 2017 to improve quality of care in the labour room and maternity operation theatres in public health facilities.  Why A wrong: 2010 immunization expansion is not LaQshya.  Why C wrong: LaQshya is a quality initiative, not a hospital-construction program, and the year is 2017.  Why D wrong: 2005 ASHA training is unrelated to LaQshya."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"The LaQshya program targets which facilities under its purview?","options":["Only private nursing homes","All Government Medical College Hospitals, District Hospitals & equivalent health facilities, and designated FRUs as well as high-case-load CHCs","Only urban primary health centres","Only AYUSH dispensaries"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states LaQshya targets all Government Medical College Hospitals, District Hospitals & equivalent health facilities, designated FRUs (First Referral Units), as well as high-case-load CHCs.  Why A wrong: It targets public/government facilities, not private nursing homes.  Why C wrong: Its scope is the higher-delivery-load facilities listed, not only urban PHCs.  Why D wrong: AYUSH dispensaries are not the named target facilities."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"What are the objectives of the LaQshya programme? (SATA)","options":["Reduce maternal and newborn mortality and morbidity due to causes such as retained placenta, preterm, haemorrhage, pre-eclampsia and eclampsia, obstructed labour, puerperal sepsis, newborn asphyxia, and newborn sepsis","Improve the quality of care during delivery and the immediate postpartum period","Stabilization of complications and ensure timely referrals, and enable an effective two-way follow-up system","Enhance satisfaction of beneficiaries, ensure a positive birthing experience, and provide Respectful Maternity Care (RMC) to all pregnant women attending public health facilities","Increase the use of unsafe traditional birth practices"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A): LaQshya aims to reduce maternal/newborn mortality and morbidity from causes including retained placenta, preterm, haemorrhage, pre-eclampsia/eclampsia, obstructed labour, puerperal sepsis, newborn asphyxia, and newborn sepsis.  Why correct (B): It aims to improve the quality of care during delivery and the immediate postpartum period.  Why correct (C): It aims for stabilization of complications, timely referrals, and an effective two-way follow-up system.  Why correct (D): It aims to enhance beneficiary satisfaction, a positive birthing experience, and RMC for all pregnant women.  Why E wrong: Promoting unsafe traditional birth practices contradicts the safety-focused objectives of LaQshya."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"What is the stated aim of the LaQshya scheme?","options":["To increase the caesarean-section rate","To reduce preventable maternal and newborn mortality, morbidity, and stillbirths associated with the care around delivery in the Labour Room and Maternity Operation Theatre, and ensure respectful maternity care","To replace all hospital deliveries with home deliveries","To reduce the number of antenatal visits"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The LaQshya Scheme – Aim states it is to reduce preventable maternal and newborn mortality, morbidity, and stillbirths associated with the care around delivery in the Labour Room and Maternity OT, and to ensure respectful maternity care.  Why A wrong: Increasing C-section rate is not a LaQshya aim.  Why C wrong: LaQshya improves facility-based delivery care, not a shift to home delivery.  Why D wrong: Reducing antenatal visits is contrary to the goal of better maternal care."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"Under LaQshya, compliance with its requirements for certification is verified by which body?","options":["The hospital's own staff only","The independent empanelled NQAS (National Quality Assurance Standards) assessors","The local panchayat","The pharmaceutical company"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Overview states that at the time of LaQshya certification, compliance with the above-mentioned requirements is verified by the independent empanelled NQAS assessors.  Why A wrong: Self-assessment alone does not confer certification; independent assessors verify.  Why C wrong: The panchayat is not the certifying body.  Why D wrong: Pharmaceutical companies have no role in LaQshya certification."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"The LaQshya target for surgical site infection (SSI) rate in the Maternity OT is to achieve what?","options":["SSI rate of ≤25% or at least a 10% reduction","SSI rate of ≤5% or at least a 30% reduction from the baseline","SSI rate of ≤50%","No specific SSI target"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states that under LaQshya, facility-level targets are to achieve a 5% or less Surgical Site Infection rate in the Maternity OT, or at least a 30% reduction from the baseline.  Why A wrong: The target is ≤5% (not ≤25%) and at least 30% reduction (not 10%).  Why C wrong: 50% is far above the ≤5% target.  Why D wrong: A specific SSI target is defined."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"Respectful Maternity Care (RMC) refers to what?","options":["Charging fees for maternity services","Care that maintains the humane and dignified treatment of a childbearing woman throughout pregnancy, birth, and the postpartum period, respecting her rights and choices through supportive communication, actions, and attitudes","Confining the woman to bed at all times","Restricting the woman's family from any contact"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The RMC section defines RMC as maintaining the humane and dignified treatment of a childbearing woman throughout pregnancy, birth, and the postpartum period, respecting her rights and choices through supportive communication, actions, and attitudes.  Why A wrong: RMC concerns dignity and rights, not fee collection.  Why C wrong: RMC supports freedom of movement, not bed confinement.  Why D wrong: RMC promotes companionship, not isolation from family."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"Which of the following are elements of ensuring Respectful Maternity Care (RMC)? (SATA)","options":["Ensure privacy of the woman in labour and take informed consent","Avoid performing harmful practices and avoid discrimination based on ethnicity, race, economic status, religion (including denial of admission due to illegal immigration status)","Provide liberty of movement during labour (e.g., walking, moving around) and allow a birth companion","Avoid unnecessary separation of pregnant women and newborns after birth, and avoid verbal/physical violence (slapping/hitting) and depriving women of services","Provide care alone and abandon the woman during delivery (i.e., leaving the woman alone or unattended)"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A): RMC ensures privacy of the woman in labour and the taking of informed consent.  Why correct (B): RMC requires avoiding harmful practices and discrimination based on ethnicity, race, economic status, or religion (including denial of admission for illegal immigration status).  Why correct (C): RMC provides liberty of movement during labour and allows a birth companion.  Why correct (D): RMC avoids unnecessary separation of mother and newborn, avoids verbal/physical violence (slapping/hitting), and avoids depriving women of services.  Why E wrong: Abandoning or leaving the woman alone/unattended during delivery is precisely what RMC says to AVOID."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"Under RMC, breastfeeding should be promoted in what manner?","options":["On a strict 3-hourly schedule only","Breastfeeding on demand","Only after 24 hours of birth","Only with the doctor's written order"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The RMC list states \"Promoting breastfeeding on demand,\" supporting feeding whenever the baby cues.  Why A wrong: A rigid schedule contradicts demand-feeding promoted by RMC.  Why C wrong: RMC promotes early initiation of breastfeeding, not a 24-hour delay.  Why D wrong: On-demand breastfeeding is a routine RMC practice, not requiring a written order."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"Per the RMC list, evidence-based care should be provided to do what?","options":["To minimize the use of evidence","To enhance and optimize the normal processes of pregnancy, birth, and postpartum","To increase routine surgical interventions","To replace clinical judgment entirely with protocols"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The RMC list states that evidence-based care should enhance and optimize the normal processes of pregnancy, birth, and postpartum.  Why A wrong: RMC promotes the use of evidence, not its minimization.  Why C wrong: RMC discourages unnecessary surgical intervention, favoring normal physiological processes.  Why D wrong: Evidence-based care guides, not replaces, clinical judgment."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"What does Facility Based Newborn Care (FBNC) establish, along with Home Based Newborn Care (HBNC) and Home Based Care for Young Child (HBYC)?","options":["A program for adult cardiac care","A continuum of care to ensure that every newborn receives essential care right from the time of birth and the first 48 hours at the health facility and then at home up to the first months of life","A scheme limited to elderly patients","A surgical training program for doctors"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The FBNC section states that Facility Based Newborn Care (FBNC), along with HBNC and HBYC, establishes a continuum of care to ensure that every newborn receives essential care right from the time of birth and the first 48 hours at the health facility, and then at home up to the first months of life.  Why A wrong: It concerns newborns, not adult cardiac care.  Why C wrong: It is for newborns and young children, not the elderly.  Why D wrong: It is a continuum of newborn care, not a surgical training program."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"Match the FBNC newborn care program components to their correct level of facility. Which is correct?","options":["NBCC (Newborn Care Corner) — at every labour/delivery point","NBSU (Newborn Stabilization Unit) — at sub-district level hospital","SNCU (Special Newborn Care Unit) — at sub-district, district, and medical college level hospital","All of the above"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The FBNC program lists Newborn Care Corner (NBCC) at every labour/delivery point, Newborn Stabilization Unit (NBSU) at the sub-district level hospital, and Special Newborn Care Unit (SNCU) at sub-district, district and medical college level hospital — so all listed mappings are correct.  Why A/B/C wrong individually: Each is correct on its own, but the most complete correct answer is \"all of the above.\""},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"What is the Newborn Care Corner (NBCC) designed for, and what equipment should it have?","options":["For sick adults; with ventilators only","A space in the labour room/OT for immediate newborn care, equipped with a radiant warmer, resuscitation kit/machines, self-inflating bag/AMBU bag (including masks of size 0 and 1), and an oxygen source","For storing medical records only","A waiting area for visitors"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The NBCC is described as a designated space in the labour room/OT for immediate newborn care, equipped like a labour room with a radiant warmer, machines, self-inflating bag/AMBU bag (with masks of size 0 and 1), and an oxygen source.  Why A wrong: NBCC is for newborns, not sick adults.  Why C wrong: It is a care space, not a records store.  Why D wrong: It is not a visitor waiting area."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"Where is the Newborn Care Corner (NBCC) established, and what does it provide?","options":["Only in the OPD; provides immunization only","Established to provide support to newborns requiring resuscitation services and/or assistance at the time of birth, by Skilled-Birth-Attendant (SBA)-trained staff","Only at home; provides housing","Only in private clinics; provides surgery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states NBCC is established to provide support to newborns requiring resuscitation services and/or assistance at the time of birth, by SBA-trained staff.  Why A wrong: It is at delivery points, not the OPD, and provides resuscitation/assistance, not only immunization.  Why C wrong: It is a facility-based newborn-care point, not housing.  Why D wrong: It is for immediate newborn care/resuscitation, not surgery."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"What services does the Newborn Care Corner (NBCC) provide at every delivery point? (SATA)","options":["Prevention of infection","Provision of warmth and resuscitation","Early initiation of breastfeeding and identification & prompt referral at-risk/sick newborns","Immunization services","Major surgery for malformations"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A): NBCC provides prevention of infection.  Why correct (B): NBCC provides provision of warmth and resuscitation.  Why correct (C): NBCC provides early initiation of breastfeeding and identification & prompt referral of at-risk/sick newborns.  Why correct (D): NBCC provides immunization services.  Why E wrong: Major surgery for malformations is not an NBCC function (it lacks surgical capacity at the corner level)."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"Where is the Newborn Stabilization Unit (NBSU) established, and what is its main function?","options":["At the district/medical-college level only; for chronic adult illness","At sub-district-level hospitals; a 4-to-6-bedded unit for managing sick and small newborns that are not so seriously sick and can be managed at the first level of newborn care","At the home only; for breastfeeding support only","In the OPD; only for immunization"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The NBSU section states it is established in the sub-district-level hospital as a 4-to-6-bedded unit for managing sick and small newborns that are not so seriously sick and can be managed at the first level of newborn care.  Why A wrong: NBSU is at the sub-district level (not solely district/medical-college) and is for newborns, not chronic adult illness.  Why C wrong: It is a facility unit with beds, not a home breastfeeding-support service.  Why D wrong: NBSU offers stabilization care, not OPD immunization only."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"Per the NBSU section, preferential stabilization of sick and small newborns before transfer to SNCU/NICU essentially does what?","options":["Worsens the outcome of these babies","Essentially improves the outcome of these babies","Has no effect on outcomes","Replaces the need for an SNCU/NICU"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states preferential stabilization of sick and small newborns at the NBSU before transfer to SNCU/NICU essentially improves the outcome of these babies.  Why A wrong: Stabilization before transfer improves, not worsens, outcomes.  Why C wrong: The text explicitly states an improvement, not \"no effect.\"  Why D wrong: Stabilization complements but does not eliminate the need for higher-level SNCU/NICU care."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"Where is the Special Newborn Care Unit (SNCU) located, and what is its capacity/role?","options":["At the labour room only; 1-bedded","Located at the district level (and medical colleges) with dedicated and adequately trained doctors, staff nurses, and support staff, to provide 24×7 comprehensive secondary-level newborn care to small and sick neonates","At the home; for routine vaccination","In the OPD; for adult care"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The SNCU section states it is located at district-level hospitals and medical colleges with dedicated and adequately trained doctors, staff nurses and support staff to provide 24×7 comprehensive secondary-level newborn care to small and sick neonates.  Why A wrong: SNCU is a district-level unit, not a single labour-room bed.  Why C wrong: It is a hospital unit for sick neonates, not home vaccination.  Why D wrong: SNCU is for newborns, not adults, and not located in the OPD."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"Per the SNCU section, what is the minimum recommended number of beds for an SNCU, and what is its referral relationship with NBSUs?","options":["Minimum 1 bed; SNCUs refer cases to NBSUs","The minimum recommended number of beds for an SNCU is 12, and the SNCU should be added for each NBSU within additional deliveries; SNCUs provide care for sick and small newborns that NBSUs (managing newborns <1800 g) refer up","Minimum 50 beds; no referral relationship","Minimum 2 beds; only adults referred"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the minimum recommended number of beds for an SNCU is 12, that an SNCU is added per a defined additional-delivery threshold, and that management of low-birth-weight infants <1800 g and management of all sick newborns occurs at the SNCU, with NBSUs referring such cases upward.  Why A wrong: The minimum is 12 beds, not 1, and the referral flow is from NBSU up to SNCU.  Why C wrong: 50 is not the stated minimum; there is a defined referral relationship.  Why D wrong: 2 beds is incorrect; SNCUs care for neonates, not adults."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"Per the SNCU section, the SNCU provides care for sick and small newborns, and the management of low-birth-weight newborns includes weighing infants <1800 g. The management of all sick newborns requiring mechanical ventilation and major surgical interventions involves what?","options":["No referral, all managed locally","Follow-up of all these newborns and referral of those requiring mechanical ventilation and major surgical interventions to higher centres/referral services","Discharge without follow-up","Only outpatient management"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text indicates that management of all sick newborns includes follow-up of newborns, and immunization services and referral services — newborns requiring mechanical ventilation and major surgical interventions are referred onward, with follow-up of all such newborns.  Why A wrong: Cases needing ventilation/major surgery are referred, not all managed locally.  Why C wrong: Follow-up of these newborns is provided, not discharge without follow-up.  Why D wrong: SNCU provides inpatient secondary care, not only outpatient management."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"Per the Mother Newborn Care unit description, what is the aim of these units regarding the mother and newborn?","options":["Permanent separation of mother and newborn","No separation of mother and newborn — keeping baby and sick mother who require newborn care together, with the mother and newborn dyad kept together (rooming-in), empowering the mother to participate in developmentally supportive care for her own newborn","Keeping the baby only with the nurse, never the mother","Discharging the mother immediately, keeping only the baby"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Mother Newborn Care Unit description emphasizes no separation of mother and sick newborn who require newborn care, keeping them together (rooming-in) and empowering the mother to participate in developmentally supportive care of her newborn.  Why A wrong: The aim is no separation, the opposite of permanent separation.  Why C wrong: The mother is kept with and empowered to care for the baby.  Why D wrong: Mother and baby are kept together, not separated by discharging the mother."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"In the comparison table \"Expected care to be provided at newborn care facilities,\" which care-of-sick-newborn services are listed under the Special Newborn Care Unit (SNCU)? (SATA)","options":["Managing of low-birth-weight infants <1800 g","Managing all sick newborns (except those requiring mechanical ventilation and major surgical interventions)","Follow-up of all babies discharged from the unit and high-risk newborns","Referral services","Performing only home visits with no facility care"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A): SNCU care of sick newborn includes managing low-birth-weight infants <1800 g.  Why correct (B): SNCU manages all sick newborns except those requiring mechanical ventilation and major surgical interventions (which are referred).  Why correct (C): SNCU provides follow-up of all babies discharged from the unit and high-risk newborns.  Why correct (D): SNCU provides referral services.  Why E wrong: SNCU is a facility-based unit; performing only home visits with no facility care contradicts its role."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"In the comparison table, which \"care of sick newborn\" service is listed for the Newborn Care Corner (NCC/NBCC)?","options":["Mechanical ventilation","Identification and prompt referral of \"at-risk\"/\"sick\" newborns","Major surgical interventions","Phototherapy for hyperbilirubinemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The comparison table lists for NBCC under care of sick newborn: identification and prompt referral of \"at-risk\"/\"sick\" newborns.  Why A wrong: Mechanical ventilation is beyond NBCC capability (it is referred up to SNCU/NICU).  Why C wrong: Major surgical interventions are not performed at the NBCC.  Why D wrong: Phototherapy for hyperbilirubinemia is an NBSU-level service, not an NBCC service."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"In the comparison table, which \"care of sick newborn\" services are provided at the Newborn Stabilization Unit (NBSU)? (SATA)","options":["Management of low-birth-weight infants <1800 g (born with another complication)","Phototherapy for newborns with hyperbilirubinemia","Management of newborn sepsis","Stabilization and referral of sick newborns and those with very low birth weight (rooming-in), and referral services","Open-heart cardiac surgery"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A): NBSU manages low-birth-weight infants <1800 g (born with another complication).  Why correct (B): NBSU provides phototherapy for newborns with hyperbilirubinemia.  Why correct (C): NBSU manages newborn sepsis.  Why correct (D): NBSU provides stabilization and referral of sick and very-low-birth-weight newborns (rooming-in) and referral services.  Why E wrong: Open-heart cardiac surgery is far beyond NBSU scope and is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"In the comparison table, which services are listed under \"Care at birth\" common to NBCC, NBSU, and SNCU? (SATA)","options":["Prevention of infection","Provision of warmth and resuscitation","Early initiation of breastfeeding","Weighing the newborn","Performing caesarean section on the mother"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A): \"Care at birth\" includes prevention of infection across NBCC/NBSU/SNCU.  Why correct (B): It includes provision of warmth and resuscitation.  Why correct (C): It includes early initiation of breastfeeding.  Why correct (D): It includes weighing the newborn.  Why E wrong: Caesarean section is a maternal procedure, not a newborn \"care at birth\" service in this table."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"In the comparison table, which \"care of sick newborn\" support is provided at the NBCC level under care-of-newborn?","options":["Mechanical ventilation support","Breastfeeding support","Major surgery support","Dialysis support"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The comparison table lists \"Breastfeeding support\" under care of newborn at the NBCC (as it does at NBSU and SNCU).  Why A wrong: Mechanical ventilation is not available at NBCC.  Why C wrong: Major surgery is not an NBCC service.  Why D wrong: Dialysis is not provided at the NBCC."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"Per the labour-room \"Do's,\" what should be checked/done regarding the labour table and the availability of LR drugs?","options":["The labour table need not be cleaned between deliveries","The labour table should be cleaned/checked and emergency life-saving drugs should be available at all times in the LR (kept ready and within reach)","Drugs should be locked far from the labour table","The labour table is only for storing linen"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The \"Do's\" list directs that the labour table be maintained/checked and that all life-saving emergency drugs of the LR be available at all times within reach for prompt use.  Why A wrong: The labour table must be cleaned/disinfected between deliveries.  Why C wrong: Emergency drugs must be readily accessible, not locked far away.  Why D wrong: The labour table is for the delivery, not merely linen storage."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"Per the \"Do's\" for the labour room, what action is directed regarding the newborn baby at birth and the blood/Rh-negative mother?","options":["Do not weigh the newborn at birth","Initiate breastfeeding within one hour of birth, and collect cord blood in an Rh-negative mother","Always separate the newborn for 24 hours","Never collect cord blood"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The \"Do's\" list directs initiation of breastfeeding within one hour of birth, and collection of cord blood in an Rh-negative mother (for testing/Rh prophylaxis decisions).  Why A wrong: Weighing the newborn is part of essential care; the directive is to breastfeed early and collect cord blood, not to skip weighing.  Why C wrong: Routine 24-hour separation contradicts early breastfeeding and rooming-in.  Why D wrong: Cord blood IS collected in an Rh-negative mother, not never."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"Per the labour-room \"Don'ts,\" what is the rule about using the routine partograph and conducting unnecessary procedures such as separating the baby from a low-risk/sick newborn unnecessarily?","options":["Always separate every newborn from its mother","Do not separate the baby unnecessarily from a low-risk newborn / do not abandon routine respectful practices, and avoid unnecessary procedures","Separate all babies for routine observation for 12 hours","Always perform an episiotomy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The \"Don'ts\" emphasize not separating the baby unnecessarily and avoiding unnecessary routine procedures, consistent with respectful, evidence-based care that keeps mother and baby together.  Why A wrong: Routine separation of every newborn is precisely discouraged.  Why C wrong: Routine 12-hour separation for observation is not indicated and contradicts rooming-in.  Why D wrong: Routine/universal episiotomy is an unnecessary procedure that is discouraged."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"A primigravida in active labour presents with eclamptic convulsions and an unrecordable, abnormal vital-sign pattern. Using the OTAS framework, what is the correct triage classification and required response?","options":["Green / non-urgent; reassess every 60 minutes","Red — emergency/critical (resuscitative–emergent); requires immediate assessment and immediate-to-rapid RMP contact with continuous/frequent reassessment","Yellow / urgent; reassess every 30 minutes","No triage needed; await routine examination"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Eclamptic convulsions with abnormal vitals fall under the RED OTAS category (the most acute, resuscitative/emergent levels), which mandates immediate assessment, immediate/rapid RMP contact, and continuous/frequent reassessment.  Why A wrong: Green/non-urgent applies to normal uncomplicated labour, not eclampsia.  Why C wrong: Yellow/urgent (e.g., stable/managed hypertension, pre-term labour) understates the acuity of active convulsions.  Why D wrong: Eclampsia is a life-threatening emergency requiring immediate triage and intervention."},{"section":"Batch 057 · LDR human resources & labour-room cleaning protocols…","question":"A baby is born limp, floppy, and not crying, with meconium-stained liquor noted at delivery. Per NCC/NBCC guidance, what is the priority nursing action?","options":["Delay all care and wait for spontaneous crying","Place the baby under the radiant warmer in the draught-free NCC and provide resuscitation (using the self-inflating/AMBU bag with size 0/1 mask, oxygen source) as indicated, per protocol/doctor's advice","Bathe the baby immediately","Send the baby home with the mother without assessment"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A limp, floppy, non-crying baby with meconium-stained liquor needs immediate resuscitation; the NCC/NBCC is equipped with a radiant warmer, AMBU/self-inflating bag (masks size 0 and 1) and oxygen for this, in a draught-free environment, per protocol/doctor's advice.  Why A wrong: Delaying resuscitation in a non-breathing newborn risks hypoxic injury/death.  Why C wrong: Bathing a depressed, possibly hypothermic newborn is contraindicated and delays resuscitation.  Why D wrong: A compromised newborn requires immediate care, not discharge."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"A newborn is brought to a Special Newborn Care Unit (SNCU). Which of the following findings meets the criterion for immediate admission based on birth weight?","options":["Birth weight of 2200 g","Birth weight less than 1800 g","Birth weight of 1900 g","Birth weight of 2500 g"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Per SNCU admission criteria, any newborn with birth weight < 1800 g (or gestation < 34 weeks) should be immediately admitted to the SNCU.  Why A wrong: 2200 g is above the 1800 g threshold.  Why C wrong: 1900 g is above the 1800 g cut-off and does not by itself meet the criterion.  Why D wrong: 2500 g is a normal/borderline weight, not an admission trigger."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"Below what gestational age does a newborn qualify for immediate admission to the SNCU?","options":["Less than 30 weeks","Less than 32 weeks","Less than 34 weeks","Less than 37 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: SNCU admission criteria specify gestation < 34 weeks as an indication for immediate admission.  Why A/B wrong: These cut-offs are lower than the stated 34-week threshold and would miss eligible 32–33 week infants.  Why D wrong: < 37 weeks defines prematurity generally, but the SNCU immediate-admission criterion is < 34 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"Which of the following is considered a \"large baby\" warranting SNCU admission?","options":["Weight > 3.0 kg","Weight > 3.5 kg","Weight > 4.0 kg","Weight > 4.5 kg"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The admission criteria define a large baby as weight > 4.0 kg.  Why A/B wrong: 3.0 kg and 3.5 kg are within the normal birth-weight range.  Why D wrong: 4.5 kg exceeds the threshold but the stated cut-off is > 4.0 kg, so D is not the defined value."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"Which neonatal respiratory finding is listed as a criterion for immediate SNCU admission?","options":["Mild intermittent grunting only","Apnea or gasping","Occasional sneezing","Periodic breathing in a term infant"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Apnea or gasping is explicitly listed as an SNCU admission criterion.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not listed admission criteria; apnea/gasping signals respiratory failure requiring immediate care."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"A neonate consistently turns away and refuses to suck at the breast and bottle. Under SNCU admission criteria this is documented as:","options":["Perinatal asphyxia","Refusal to feed","Abdominal distension","Central cyanosis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: \"Refusal to feed\" is a distinct SNCU admission criterion.  Why A wrong: Perinatal asphyxia is a separate criterion relating to oxygen deprivation at birth.  Why C wrong: Abdominal distension is a different listed criterion.  Why D wrong: Central cyanosis is a separate criterion involving bluish discoloration."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"Respiratory distress in a neonate qualifying for SNCU admission may be indicated by which of the following?","options":["Rate < 60/min only","Rate < 60/min or grunt/retractions","Absence of grunting","Regular abdominal breathing"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The criterion lists respiratory distress as rate < 60/min OR grunt/retractions. (Note: a respiratory rate < 60/min is normal; the source likely intends a rate >60/min as tachypnea, but the criterion is keyed as the option capturing rate abnormality plus grunting/retractions.)  Why A wrong: It omits the grunt/retraction component which is part of the criterion.  Why C wrong: Grunting, when present, is a sign of distress — its absence is reassuring, not a criterion.  Why D wrong: Regular abdominal breathing is normal in neonates and not a distress sign."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"Severe jaundice in a neonate, as an SNCU admission criterion, is characterized by which of the following?","options":["Appears after 7 days of life","Appears < 24 hrs or stains palms and soles, or lasts > 2 weeks","Confined to the face only","Resolves within 24 hours without treatment"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Severe jaundice is defined as appearing < 24 hrs, staining palms & soles, or lasting > 2 weeks.  Why A wrong: Onset after 7 days is not the severe-jaundice criterion described.  Why C wrong: Facial-only jaundice represents mild, lower-bilirubin jaundice, not severe.  Why D wrong: Self-resolving jaundice within 24 hours is not severe."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"According to SNCU admission criteria, hypothermia in a neonate is defined as an axillary temperature of:","options":["< 36.5°C","< 35.4°C","< 34.0°C","< 37.0°C"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The criteria define hypothermia as < 35.4°C (and hyperthermia as > 37.5°C).  Why A wrong: 36.5°C is the lower end of the normal neonatal range, not hypothermia by this criterion.  Why C wrong: 34.0°C is severe hypothermia but is not the stated threshold value.  Why D wrong: 37.0°C is normal body temperature."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"Per the SNCU admission criteria, hyperthermia is defined as a temperature greater than:","options":["37.0°C","37.5°C","38.5°C","39.0°C"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hyperthermia is defined as > 37.5°C in the admission criteria.  Why A wrong: 37.0°C is normal.  Why C/D wrong: These are higher fever values but are not the stated criterion threshold."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"A neonate presents with cold periphery, capillary filling time (CFT) > 3 seconds, and a weak, fast pulse. Which SNCU admission criterion does this represent?","options":["Central cyanosis","Shock","Coma","Abdominal distension"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Shock is defined as cold periphery with CFT > 3 seconds and a weak & fast pulse.  Why A wrong: Central cyanosis refers to bluish discoloration from hypoxemia, a separate criterion.  Why C wrong: Coma involves loss of consciousness/convulsions/encephalopathy, not peripheral perfusion signs.  Why D wrong: Abdominal distension is an unrelated GI sign."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"Which of the following groups of findings is listed together as a single SNCU admission criterion?","options":["Shock, central cyanosis, refusal to feed","Coma, convulsions, or encephalopathy","Apnea, large baby, severe jaundice","Hypothermia, abdominal distension, diarrhoea"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Criterion 11 groups coma, convulsions, or encephalopathy together as one neurological admission indication.  Why A/C/D wrong: These mix items that the source lists as separate, distinct criteria rather than as one grouped criterion."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"Which gastrointestinal sign is included as a criterion for immediate SNCU admission?","options":["Mild posseting after feeds","Abdominal distension","One soft stool per day","Visible bowel sounds only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Abdominal distension is explicitly listed as an SNCU admission criterion.  Why A wrong: Mild posseting is a normal finding, not a criterion.  Why C wrong: A normal stool pattern is not a distress sign.  Why D wrong: This is not a listed admission criterion."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"Both diarrhoea/dysentery and which other condition are listed among the SNCU admission criteria?","options":["Bleeding & major malformations","Routine vaccination follow-up","Physiological weight loss","Normal Moro reflex"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Diarrhoea/dysentery (point 13) and Bleeding & major malformations (point 14) are both listed admission criteria.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are normal or routine findings, not admission criteria."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"Which neonate is appropriate for transfer from the SNCU to the step-down unit?","options":["A neonate in shock with weak pulses","A baby whose respiratory distress is improving and who no longer requires oxygen supplementation to maintain saturation","A neonate with new-onset convulsions","A baby with severe jaundice staining the palms and soles"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A criterion for transfer to step-down is that the baby's respiratory distress is improving and oxygen supplementation is no longer needed to maintain saturation.  Why A/C/D wrong: Shock, convulsions, and severe jaundice are acute admission criteria requiring intensive SNCU care, not step-down transfer."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"A baby on antibiotics in the SNCU is being considered for step-down transfer. Which condition supports this transfer?","options":["The baby still needs antibiotics for completion of duration of therapy","The baby has worsening sepsis","The baby requires escalating oxygen","The baby has new convulsions"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A baby on antibiotics only for completion of the duration of therapy (i.e., clinically stable) is suitable for step-down.  Why B/C/D wrong: Worsening sepsis, escalating oxygen needs, and convulsions indicate the baby needs continued intensive care, not step-down."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"A low-birth-weight infant of less than 1800 g is being considered for step-down. Which criterion must this baby meet?","options":["Must require mechanical ventilation","Must be on IV inotropes","Must be stable for adequate weight gain","Must have ongoing apnea"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Low-birth-weight babies (< 1800 g) qualify for step-down when stable for the purpose of adequate weight gain.  Why A/B/D wrong: Need for ventilation, inotropes, or ongoing apnea indicate continued instability requiring SNCU-level care."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"A neonate is receiving phototherapy. According to step-down transfer criteria, this baby may be moved to step-down provided that the baby is:","options":["Still requiring intensive resuscitation","Otherwise stable","Hemodynamically unstable","In respiratory failure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Babies requiring phototherapy may be transferred to step-down if otherwise stable.  Why A/C/D wrong: A baby needing resuscitation, who is hemodynamically unstable, or in respiratory failure is not stable enough for step-down."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"Which neonate is admitted for observation but is hemodynamically stable, qualifying for the step-down unit?","options":["A baby admitted for observation who is hemodynamically stable","A baby with active hemorrhage","A baby in coma","A baby with capillary refill > 3 seconds"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Babies admitted for observation but who are hemodynamically stable are appropriate for step-down.  Why B/C/D wrong: Active hemorrhage, coma, and prolonged capillary refill indicate instability and the need for intensive care."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"Which of the following is a criterion for discharging a baby from the SNCU to home?","options":["The baby requires a radiant warmer to maintain temperature","The baby is able to maintain temperature without a radiant warmer","The baby has weak peripheral pulses","The baby is not accepting any feeds"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Ability to maintain temperature without a radiant warmer is a home-discharge criterion, indicating thermal stability.  Why A wrong: Dependence on a radiant warmer means the baby is not yet ready for home.  Why C wrong: Weak peripheral pulses suggest shock/instability, not readiness for discharge.  Why D wrong: Discharge requires the baby to be accepting breast feeds."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"For discharge to home, a baby's hemodynamic status and peripheral pulses should be:","options":["Hemodynamically stable with normal CFT and strong peripheral pulses","Hemodynamically unstable with weak pulses","Prolonged CFT but strong pulses","Normal CFT but absent pulses"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A home-discharge criterion is that the baby is hemodynamically stable (normal CFT, strong peripheral pulses).  Why B/C/D wrong: Instability, prolonged CFT, or absent pulses indicate the baby is not ready for discharge."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"Regarding feeding, which is a criterion for discharging a baby from the SNCU to home?","options":["Baby accepting breast feeds well","Baby on continuous IV fluids only","Baby on nasogastric feeds with poor tolerance","Baby refusing all feeds"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Baby accepting breast feeds well is a home-discharge criterion, demonstrating adequate oral intake.  Why B/C/D wrong: Dependence on IV fluids, poorly tolerated NG feeds, or feed refusal indicate the baby is not ready for home."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"With respect to weight, what is the documented criterion for discharging a baby from the SNCU to home?","options":["Weight gain on a single day","Documented weight gain for 3 consecutive days, with weight more than 1.5 kg","Any weight below 1.5 kg","Weight loss for 3 consecutive days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The home-discharge criterion is documented weight gain for 3 consecutive days, with the weight more than 1.5 kg.  Why A wrong: A single day's gain is insufficient; sustained gain over 3 days is required.  Why C wrong: A weight below 1.5 kg does not meet the threshold.  Why D wrong: Weight loss indicates the baby is not thriving and is not ready for discharge."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"Before discharging a baby home from the SNCU, in addition to clinical stability, the nurse must ensure that the:","options":["Primary illness has resolved and the mother is confident in taking care of the baby at home","Baby has received all booster vaccines","Parents have completed a CPR certification course","Baby weighs at least 2.5 kg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A home-discharge criterion is that the primary illness has resolved and the mother is confident in caring for the baby at home.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the discharge criteria specified on the page."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"While visually estimating blood loss to assess postpartum hemorrhage (PPH), a fully soiled sanitary towel is estimated to contain approximately how much blood?","options":["30 ml","60 ml","100 ml","250 ml"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A soiled sanitary towel corresponds to an estimated blood loss of 30 ml.  Why B wrong: 60 ml corresponds to a saturated small swab (10×10 cm).  Why C wrong: 100 ml corresponds to a saturated sanitary towel.  Why D wrong: 250 ml corresponds to a soaked incontinence pad."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"During visual blood-loss estimation for PPH, a fully saturated incontinence pad is estimated at approximately:","options":["100 ml","250 ml","500 ml","1000 ml"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A saturated incontinence pad corresponds to an estimated 250 ml of blood loss.  Why A wrong: 100 ml is a saturated sanitary towel.  Why C wrong: 500 ml corresponds to a full kidney dish or a floor spill of 100 cm diameter.  Why D wrong: 1000 ml corresponds to blood spilling on a bed."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"In visual PPH blood-loss estimation, a full kidney dish of blood is estimated at approximately:","options":["250 ml","350 ml","500 ml","1000 ml"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: A full kidney dish corresponds to an estimated 500 ml of blood loss.  Why A wrong: 250 ml corresponds to an incontinence pad.  Why B wrong: 350 ml corresponds to a saturated swab (45×45 cm).  Why D wrong: 1000 ml corresponds to blood spilling on a bed."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"A saturated small swab measuring 10×10 cm corresponds to approximately how much estimated blood loss?","options":["30 ml","60 ml","100 ml","350 ml"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A saturated small swab (10×10 cm) is estimated at 60 ml.  Why A wrong: 30 ml corresponds to a soiled sanitary towel.  Why C wrong: 100 ml corresponds to a saturated sanitary towel.  Why D wrong: 350 ml corresponds to a large saturated swab (45×45 cm)."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"During PPH assessment, a saturated large swab (45×45 cm) is estimated to hold approximately:","options":["100 ml","250 ml","350 ml","500 ml"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: A saturated swab measuring 45×45 cm corresponds to an estimated 350 ml of blood loss.  Why A wrong: 100 ml corresponds to a saturated sanitary towel.  Why B wrong: 250 ml corresponds to an incontinence pad.  Why D wrong: 500 ml corresponds to a full kidney dish."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"A saturated sanitary towel is estimated to contain approximately how much blood during PPH assessment?","options":["30 ml","60 ml","100 ml","250 ml"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: A saturated (fully soaked) sanitary towel corresponds to an estimated 100 ml of blood loss.  Why A wrong: 30 ml corresponds to a merely soiled (not saturated) sanitary towel.  Why B wrong: 60 ml corresponds to a saturated small swab (10×10 cm).  Why D wrong: 250 ml corresponds to an incontinence pad."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"Blood spilling onto the bed during a PPH is visually estimated at approximately:","options":["500 ml","1000 ml","1500 ml","2000 ml"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Blood spilling on the bed corresponds to an estimated 1000 ml of blood loss.  Why A wrong: 500 ml corresponds to a full kidney dish or a 100 cm floor spill.  Why C wrong: 1500 ml is not the value assigned; it is not one of the listed estimates.  Why D wrong: 2000 ml corresponds to blood spilling onto the floor."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"A floor spill of blood approximately 100 cm in diameter is estimated to represent how much blood loss?","options":["350 ml","500 ml","1000 ml","2000 ml"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A floor spill of 100 cm diameter corresponds to an estimated 500 ml of blood loss.  Why A wrong: 350 ml corresponds to a saturated large swab.  Why C wrong: 1000 ml corresponds to blood spilling on the bed.  Why D wrong: 2000 ml corresponds to blood spilling to the floor (a large unconfined spill)."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"During a postpartum hemorrhage, blood spilling onto the floor (a large unconfined spill) is visually estimated at approximately:","options":["500 ml","1000 ml","1500 ml","2000 ml"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Blood spilling to the floor corresponds to an estimated 2000 ml of blood loss — the largest visual estimate listed.  Why A wrong: 500 ml is a full kidney dish/100 cm floor spill.  Why B wrong: 1000 ml is blood spilling on the bed.  Why C wrong: 1500 ml is not a value listed in the visual guide."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"What is the primary purpose of pedigree analysis?","options":["To measure blood glucose in a family","To present family information as an easily readable chart to understand the inheritance of genes from parents to offspring","To diagnose infectious diseases","To determine a patient's blood group"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pedigree analysis presents family information in the form of an easily readable chart to understand the inheritance of genes from parents to offspring.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are unrelated laboratory or clinical assessments and are not the purpose of pedigree analysis."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"A pedigree chart that can represent a family tree along with the family members and their genetic traits is also simply called a:","options":["Karyotype","Family tree","Punnett square","Genome map"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The presentation of family information in pedigree form is simply called a \"family tree.\"  Why A wrong: A karyotype is a display of an individual's chromosomes, not a family tree.  Why C wrong: A Punnett square predicts genotype probabilities for a single cross, not a multigenerational chart.  Why D wrong: A genome map shows gene locations on chromosomes, not family relationships."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"In genetics, a pedigree analysis is primarily used to determine the mode of transmission of which kind of traits?","options":["Multifactorial environmental traits only","Single-gene traits","Acquired immune traits","Random somatic mutations"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In humans, pedigree analysis is used to determine the mode of transmission of single-gene traits.  Why A/C/D wrong: Pedigree analysis as described targets single-gene (Mendelian) inheritance, not purely environmental, immune, or random somatic changes."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"Gregor Mendel is best known as the:","options":["Father of microbiology","Father of genetics","Father of immunology","Father of anatomy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states Gregor Mendel is known as the father of genetics.  Why A/C/D wrong: These titles belong to other scientists and disciplines, not Mendel."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"In genetics, the units that are responsible for the inheritance of traits from parents to offspring are called:","options":["Genes","Ribosomes","Antibodies","Hormones"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Genes are the factors responsible for the inheritance of traits from parents to offspring.  Why B/C/D wrong: Ribosomes synthesize proteins, antibodies are immune proteins, and hormones are chemical messengers — none are the hereditary units."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"Genes are the hereditary units of organisms that are responsible for carrying information from one generation to the next. From whom are they passed?","options":["From peers to peers","From parents to offspring","From the environment to the cell","From siblings to siblings"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Genes are hereditary units responsible for carrying information from the parents to their offspring.  Why A/C/D wrong: Hereditary genetic information is passed vertically from parents to offspring, not laterally or from the environment."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"Pedigrees are constructed using a standardized set of symbols. In a pedigree, a square and a circle conventionally represent:","options":["Square = female, circle = male","Square = male, circle = female","Both represent deceased individuals","Both represent affected individuals"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: By convention, squares represent males and circles represent females.  Why A wrong: The assignment is reversed; squares are male, circles are female.  Why C wrong: A deceased individual is indicated by a diagonal slash through the symbol, not by the basic shape.  Why D wrong: Affected status is shown by filled (shaded) symbols, not by the shape itself."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"On a pedigree chart, how is a deceased individual represented?","options":["A filled (shaded) symbol","A symbol with a diagonal line (slash) through it","A diamond shape","A symbol with a dot in the centre"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A deceased individual is shown by a diagonal line (slash) drawn through the symbol.  Why A wrong: A filled symbol indicates an affected individual, not death.  Why C wrong: A diamond denotes an individual whose sex is unknown.  Why D wrong: A central dot/small filled circle denotes a carrier (heterozygous for a recessive allele)."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"On a pedigree chart, an individual whose sex is unknown is represented by a:","options":["Circle","Square","Diamond","Triangle"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: When the sex of the person is unknown, a diamond is used.  Why A wrong: A circle indicates a female.  Why B wrong: A square indicates a male.  Why D wrong: A triangle is conventionally used to indicate a spontaneous abortion/stillbirth in some systems but the page specifies a diamond for unknown sex."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"A horizontal line connecting two individuals on a pedigree, with a vertical line leading downward to their children, represents:","options":["Sibling relationship","A mating relationship between parents, with the vertical line leading to their offspring","A deceased individual","A carrier individual"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Parents are connected by a horizontal line, and a vertical line leads down to their offspring.  Why A wrong: Siblings are shown connected to a common horizontal sibship line, not directly by a mating line.  Why C/D wrong: These relate to slashes and central dots on individual symbols, not connecting lines between mates."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"On a pedigree, the offspring of a couple are connected to each other by what type of line, and are listed in what order?","options":["A vertical sibship line; in alphabetical order","A horizontal sibship line; listed in birth order from left to right","A diagonal line; in random order","No line; arranged by sex"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The offspring are connected by a horizontal sibship line and listed in birth order from left to right.  Why A/C/D wrong: The page specifies a horizontal sibship line and birth-order arrangement from left to right, not vertical/alphabetical, diagonal/random, or unlinked-by-sex arrangements."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"On a standard pedigree, how is each successive generation identified?","options":["By a lowercase letter (a, b, c)","By a Roman numeral (I, II, III), with each individual within a generation identified by an Arabic numeral (1, 2, 3, etc.)","By the family surname only","By color coding alone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Each generation is identified by a Roman numeral (I, II, III, and so on), and each individual within a generation is identified by an Arabic numeral (1, 2, 3, and so on).  Why A/C/D wrong: The standard convention uses Roman numerals for generations and Arabic numerals for individuals, not letters, surnames, or color alone."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"Analysis of a pedigree using the principles of Mendelian inheritance can determine whether a trait is inherited as:","options":["Only dominant","Only recessive","A dominant or recessive pattern of inheritance","Always sex-linked"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Analysis of the pedigree using the principles of Mendelian inheritance can determine whether a trait is dominant or recessive.  Why A/B wrong: Pedigree analysis distinguishes between dominant AND recessive patterns; it is not limited to one.  Why D wrong: It can identify autosomal as well as sex-linked patterns, not always sex-linked."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"The first family member identified with a genetic disorder, who is indicated on the pedigree by an arrow, is known as the:","options":["Carrier","Proband","Consultand","Founder"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pedigrees are often constructed after a family member with a genetic disorder is identified; this individual, known as the proband, is indicated on the pedigree by an arrow.  Why A wrong: A carrier has an allele but is unaffected and is shown by a half-filled/dotted symbol, not necessarily an arrow.  Why C/D wrong: While \"consultand\" and \"founder\" are real terms, the page specifically names the arrow-indicated index individual as the proband."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"On a pedigree, two parallel horizontal lines (a double line) connecting two individuals indicate:","options":["A normal (unrelated) mating","Mating between relatives (consanguinity)","Spontaneous abortion","Deceased individuals"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A double connecting line denotes mating between relatives (consanguineous mating), distinguished from a single line used for an ordinary mating.  Why A wrong: A single horizontal line indicates a normal (non-related) mating.  Why C wrong: Spontaneous abortion is shown by a small filled triangle/symbol.  Why D wrong: Deceased individuals are indicated by a slash through the symbol."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"Autosomal inheritance refers to traits inherited on which chromosomes?","options":["The X chromosome only","The Y chromosome only","Chromosome numbers 1 to 22 (the autosomes)","Mitochondrial DNA"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Autosomal means inherited on chromosome numbers 1 to 22.  Why A wrong: X-linked inheritance involves the X chromosome.  Why B wrong: Y-linked inheritance involves the Y chromosome.  Why D wrong: Mitochondrial inheritance involves mtDNA, not the autosomes."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"\"Sex-linked\" inheritance means a trait is inherited on which chromosome(s)?","options":["Autosomes only","Either the X or Y chromosome","Mitochondrial DNA","Chromosome 21 only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Sex-linked means inherited on either the X or Y chromosome.  Why A wrong: Autosomes are chromosomes 1–22, not the sex chromosomes.  Why C wrong: Mitochondrial DNA is a separate inheritance category.  Why D wrong: Chromosome 21 is an autosome."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"Which of the following is an example of an autosomal recessive disorder?","options":["Huntington's disease","PKU (phenylketonuria), Tay-Sachs disease, or albinism / cystic fibrosis","Color blindness","Hypophosphatemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Autosomal recessive examples include PKU (phenylketonuria), Tay-Sachs, albinism, and cystic fibrosis.  Why A wrong: Huntington's disease is autosomal dominant.  Why C wrong: Color blindness is X-linked recessive.  Why D wrong: Hypophosphatemia (vitamin-D-resistant rickets) is X-linked dominant."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"Which disorder is given as an example of an autosomal dominant condition?","options":["Cystic fibrosis","Huntington's disease","Hemophilia","Color blindness"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Huntington's disease is the example given for autosomal dominant inheritance.  Why A wrong: Cystic fibrosis is autosomal recessive.  Why C wrong: Hemophilia is X-linked recessive.  Why D wrong: Color blindness is X-linked recessive."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"X-linked recessive means the allele is found on only the X chromosome and can be expressed in:","options":["Females only","Males or females equally with no difference","Males only (and rarely homozygous females)","Neither sex"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: X-linked recessive means the allele is found on only the X chromosome; because males have one X, they express the trait more readily — the page states it \"can be in males or females\" but, as the disorder section clarifies, males are affected more often. (The defining feature is single-X expression in males.)  Why A wrong: It is not limited to females; males are in fact more often affected.  Why B wrong: There is a sex difference — males are affected more often than females.  Why D wrong: The trait is expressed, not silent in both sexes."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"Which conditions are listed as examples of X-linked recessive disorders?","options":["Color blindness and hemophilia","Huntington's disease and PKU","Cystic fibrosis and Tay-Sachs","Albinism and sickle cell anemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Color blindness and hemophilia are the examples given for X-linked recessive disorders.  Why B wrong: Huntington's is autosomal dominant; PKU is autosomal recessive.  Why C/D wrong: Cystic fibrosis, Tay-Sachs, albinism, and sickle cell anemia are autosomal recessive examples."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"Y-linked inheritance means the allele is found on the Y chromosome and can therefore be passed only in:","options":["Females","Males","Both sexes equally","Carriers of either sex"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Y-linked means the allele is found on the Y chromosome and can only be in males (since only males possess a Y chromosome).  Why A/C/D wrong: Females have no Y chromosome, so a Y-linked trait cannot occur in females or be expressed equally in both sexes."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"A dominant trait is best defined as one that:","options":["Requires two recessive alleles to be expressed","Is expressed in an offspring when it is contributed by a parent through a dominant allele","Can never skip a generation only in females","Is found only on the Y chromosome"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A dominant trait is one expressed in an offspring when it is contributed by a parent through a dominant allele.  Why A wrong: Requiring two recessive alleles describes a recessive trait.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the definition of a dominant trait."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"A recessive trait is one that is expressed only when an organism has:","options":["One dominant and one recessive allele","Two recessive alleles (two same forms) of a gene","A single allele on the Y chromosome","A dominant allele from one parent"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A recessive trait is expressed when an organism has two recessive alleles (two same forms) of a gene.  Why A wrong: With one dominant and one recessive allele, the dominant trait is expressed (carrier state).  Why C/D wrong: A single Y-linked allele or a dominant allele would express the dominant trait, not a recessive one."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"In autosomal recessive disorders, both parents are typically unaffected carriers. What is true about how the trait appears across generations?","options":["It appears in every generation without skipping","It typically skips a generation, so affected offspring can be born to unaffected (carrier) parents","It is passed only from father to son","It only affects males"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In autosomal recessive disorders, both parents (carriers) can be unaffected yet have affected offspring; the trait typically skips a generation. Both males and females are equally likely to be affected.  Why A wrong: Skipping generations is characteristic of recessive, not dominant, inheritance.  Why C wrong: Father-to-son transmission is characteristic of Y-linked, not autosomal recessive.  Why D wrong: Autosomal recessive affects males and females equally."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"In an autosomal recessive disorder, what fraction of the children of two carrier parents are typically affected?","options":["None","About 1/4 (one fourth)","About 1/2 (one half)","All of them"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Approximately 1/4 of the children of two carrier parents will be affected in an autosomal recessive disorder.  Why A wrong: Some offspring (about 1/4) are affected.  Why C wrong: 1/2 is the expected affected proportion seen in autosomal dominant transmission, not recessive carrier × carrier.  Why D wrong: Not all children are affected; the expected affected fraction is one fourth."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"Which statement correctly describes autosomal dominant inheritance with respect to generations?","options":["It skips generations","It does not skip a generation, so affected offspring usually have an affected parent and the trait is found in every generation","It affects only females","It is transmitted only by carriers who are themselves unaffected"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Autosomal dominant disorders do not skip a generation; affected offspring usually have an affected parent, and the trait is found in every generation. One parent must have the disorder for offspring to be affected.  Why A wrong: Skipping generations characterizes recessive disorders.  Why C wrong: Both sexes are equally affected in autosomal dominant inheritance.  Why D wrong: Affected individuals (not silent carriers) transmit the dominant trait."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"In an autosomal dominant disorder, both males and females are equally likely to be affected. This is because the responsible gene is located on:","options":["The X chromosome","The Y chromosome","An autosome (chromosomes 1–22)","Mitochondrial DNA"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Because the gene is on an autosome, both males and females are equally likely to be affected, making it an autosomal disorder.  Why A/B wrong: X- or Y-linkage produces sex-biased patterns, not equal involvement.  Why D wrong: Mitochondrial inheritance is maternal, not equal across sexes for this reason."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"Which statement best describes the pattern of X-linked recessive disorders?","options":["Affected fathers pass the disorder directly to their sons","Males are more likely to be affected than females, and affected fathers do NOT pass the disorder to their sons","The disorder appears in every generation","It is transmitted only from father to son"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In X-linked recessive disorders, males are more likely to be affected than females, and affected fathers do NOT pass the disorder to their sons (a father gives his Y, not his X, to sons). Females are usually carriers of these disorders.  Why A/D wrong: An affected father cannot transmit an X-linked trait to his sons; father-to-son transmission is characteristic of Y-linked, not X-linked, inheritance.  Why C wrong: X-linked recessive disorders typically skip a generation, not appear in every generation."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"In an X-linked recessive pedigree, an affected father's daughters will most likely be:","options":["All affected","Carriers (his daughters receive his affected X), while the father cannot pass it to his sons","Completely free of the allele","All affected sons"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The father passes his X (carrying the affected allele) to all his daughters, who therefore become carriers; he cannot pass the allele to his sons (they receive his Y). Females are generally carriers of these disorders.  Why A wrong: Daughters become carriers (heterozygous), usually unaffected, not all affected.  Why C wrong: Daughters do receive the affected X from the father, so they are not allele-free.  Why D wrong: Sons receive the Y from the father and are not affected via the father's X."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"Which of the following is characteristic of an X-linked dominant disorder?","options":["It commonly skips a generation","It is common in a pedigree and does not skip a generation","It only affects males","Affected fathers transmit it to their sons"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: An X-linked dominant disorder is common in a pedigree and does not skip a generation.  Why A wrong: Not skipping generations is characteristic; skipping is seen in recessive disorders.  Why C wrong: Both sexes can be affected in X-linked dominant disorders, though females may be affected more frequently.  Why D wrong: Fathers pass the X (and thus the disorder) to all daughters, not to sons."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"In an X-linked dominant disorder, if the father is affected, which of his children will be affected?","options":["All of his sons","All of his daughters will be affected, and none of his sons (since sons receive the Y)","Half of his sons","None of his children"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In an X-linked dominant disorder, if the father is affected, all of his daughters will be affected and no sons will be affected (sons receive the father's Y chromosome).  Why A/C wrong: Sons do not inherit the father's X, so they are not affected by the father's X-linked dominant allele.  Why D wrong: All daughters are affected, so it is not true that none of his children are affected."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"In an X-linked dominant disorder, an affected mother (heterozygous) has approximately what chance of passing the disorder to each of her offspring?","options":["0%","25%","50%","100%"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: An affected (heterozygous) mother has a 50% chance of passing the X-linked dominant disorder on to each of her offspring (each child has a 50% chance of inheriting the affected X).  Why A wrong: There is a real risk of transmission, not zero.  Why B wrong: 25% is the affected fraction in autosomal recessive carrier crosses, not this scenario.  Why D wrong: A heterozygous mother transmits the affected X to about half, not all, of her offspring."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"Which statement is true about a Y-linked disorder?","options":["Both males and females can be affected","Only males can be affected, and if the father is affected all his sons will be affected","Affected fathers pass it to their daughters","It commonly skips a generation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In a Y-linked disorder, only males can be affected; if the father is affected, all of his sons will be affected (each son receives the Y), and it does not skip a generation.  Why A wrong: Females lack a Y chromosome and cannot be affected.  Why C wrong: Daughters receive the X, not the Y, so they cannot inherit a Y-linked disorder.  Why D wrong: Y-linked disorders never skip a generation (each affected father passes it to all sons)."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"Which statement correctly describes mitochondrial (maternal) inheritance?","options":["The disorder is passed only from the father","The disorder is only passed from the mother; if the mother is affected, all offspring will be affected","Only sons are affected","The disorder skips alternate generations"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In mitochondrial disorders, the disorder is only passed from the mother; if the mother is affected, all of her offspring will be affected. If the father is affected, he does not pass it on to his offspring.  Why A wrong: Mitochondria are inherited from the mother (egg cytoplasm), not the father.  Why C wrong: Both sons and daughters of an affected mother can be affected.  Why D wrong: Mitochondrial inheritance affects all offspring of an affected mother and does not skip generations in that line."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"According to the 3-step trick to solve pedigrees, the FIRST step is to look for which pattern of inheritance?","options":["Autosomal vs X-linked","Mitochondrial inheritance","Dominant vs recessive","Y-linked inheritance"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The first step is to look for mitochondrial inheritance: a female transmits the disease to all of her offspring (both males and females), while a male does not transmit the disease and only females transmit it. If not mitochondrial, proceed to step 2.  Why A/C/D wrong: Dominant vs recessive is step 2; X-linked/autosomal/Y-linked determination is step 3."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"When applying the second step of pedigree analysis (dominant vs recessive), how can you recognize a DOMINANT inheritance pattern?","options":["The trait skips generations","At least one member in all generations has the disease, and both affected parents can produce normal (unaffected) offspring (parents must be heterozygous)","Only males are affected","The disease is only transmitted by the mother"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For dominant inheritance, at least one member in all generations will have the disease, and both affected parents can produce a normal offspring (because the parents must be heterozygous).  Why A wrong: Skipping generations indicates recessive inheritance.  Why C wrong: Affecting only males is not a defining feature of dominant inheritance.  Why D wrong: Maternal-only transmission describes mitochondrial inheritance."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"According to the second step of the pedigree trick, which feature indicates a RECESSIVE inheritance pattern?","options":["The disease is present in some generations and absent in others (skips a generation), and both normal parents can produce an affected offspring, so they must be heterozygous","The trait appears in every generation","Only the father transmits the disease","All offspring of an affected parent are affected"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For recessive inheritance, there will be some generations in which the disease is absent (skips a generation), and both normal parents can produce an affected offspring; in such a case, the parents must be heterozygous (carriers).  Why B wrong: Appearing in every generation indicates dominant inheritance.  Why C wrong: Father-only transmission indicates Y-linked inheritance.  Why D wrong: All-offspring-affected from one affected parent suggests mitochondrial (maternal) or Y-linked patterns, not recessive."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"In the third step of the pedigree trick, an X-linked DOMINANT disorder is recognized when a male transmits the disease to:","options":["Half of his daughters only","All of his daughters (and none of his sons)","All of his sons only","None of his children"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For X-linked dominant inheritance, an affected male transmits the disease to all of his daughters (and not to his sons, since sons get his Y). A female transmits it to half of her sons and half of her daughters.  Why A wrong: A male transmits to ALL daughters, not half.  Why C wrong: Sons inherit the father's Y, so they are not affected via the father's X.  Why D wrong: All daughters are affected, so it is not \"none.\""},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"In the third step of the pedigree trick, how is an X-linked RECESSIVE disorder recognized?","options":["If only males are affected, it is likely to be X-linked recessive, and females either act as carriers or remain unaffected","Only females are affected","The disorder affects every generation equally","The disorder is passed only from father to son"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For X-linked recessive inheritance, if only males are affected it is likely to be X-linked recessive, and females only act as carriers and remain unaffected.  Why B wrong: It predominantly affects males, not only females.  Why C wrong: Equal involvement of every generation is more typical of dominant patterns.  Why D wrong: Father-to-son transmission characterizes Y-linked inheritance, not X-linked recessive."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"According to the pedigree trick, what is true regarding sex predilection in autosomal dominant inheritance, and what fraction of offspring does an affected individual transmit the disease to?","options":["Strong male predilection; transmits to 1/4 of offspring","No sex predilection; an affected individual transmits the disease to 1/2 of offspring","Female predilection; transmits to all offspring","Male predilection; transmits to 1/2 of offspring"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Autosomal dominant inheritance shows no sex predilection, and an affected individual transmits the disease to 1/2 of offspring.  Why A wrong: There is no sex predilection, and the transmitted fraction is 1/2, not 1/4.  Why C/D wrong: There is no female or male predilection in autosomal dominant inheritance."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"Per the pedigree trick, autosomal recessive inheritance shows no sex predilection, and an affected individual transmits the disease to approximately what fraction of offspring? Also, which factor increases its risk?","options":["1/2 of offspring; advanced maternal age increases risk","1/4 of offspring; consanguinity increases the risk of autosomal recessive disorders","All offspring; X-linkage increases risk","1/8 of offspring; consanguinity decreases risk"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Autosomal recessive inheritance has no sex predilection; an affected individual transmits the disease to 1/4 of offspring, and consanguinity increases the risk of autosomal recessive disorders.  Why A wrong: The transmitted fraction is 1/4, and the listed risk factor is consanguinity, not maternal age.  Why C/D wrong: These misstate the fraction and/or the effect of consanguinity (which increases, not decreases, the risk)."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"According to the pedigree trick, which statement about Y-linked inheritance is correct?","options":["It shows a female predilection","It never skips a generation, and only males are affected (the father transmits the disease to his sons)","It skips every other generation","Affected fathers transmit it to their daughters"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Y-linked inheritance never skips a generation; only males are affected, and the father transmits the disease to his sons.  Why A wrong: It affects males (not females), so there is no female predilection.  Why C wrong: Y-linked traits do not skip generations.  Why D wrong: Daughters do not inherit the Y chromosome, so an affected father does not transmit it to his daughters."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"(NORCET PYQ) In a given pedigree flowchart where the legend is Male unaffected (open square), Female unaffected (open circle), Male affected (filled square), and Female affected (filled circle), the displayed pattern represents which mode of inheritance?","options":["Autosomal dominant","Autosomal recessive","X-linked recessive","X-linked dominant"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Per the book's answer key, the correct answer is A — Autosomal dominant. A trait appearing in every generation with affected individuals (both sexes) having at least one affected parent is the hallmark of autosomal dominant inheritance.  Why B wrong: Autosomal recessive typically skips generations with unaffected carrier parents.  Why C wrong: X-linked recessive would show predominantly affected males with carrier females and no male-to-male transmission.  Why D wrong: X-linked dominant would show affected fathers transmitting to all daughters but no sons."},{"section":"Batch 058 · SNCU admission/transfer/discharge criteria…","question":"(NORCET 2022 PYQ) In the following human pedigree, the filled symbols represent affected individuals. The type of inheritance shown by this pedigree is:","options":["X-linked dominant","X-linked recessive","Autosomal dominant","Autosomal recessive"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Per the book's answer key, the correct answer is A — X-linked dominant. In X-linked dominant inheritance, affected fathers transmit the trait to all daughters (and no sons), the trait does not skip generations, and females may be affected more frequently — consistent with the keyed answer.  Why B wrong: X-linked recessive would predominantly affect males with carrier (unaffected) females.  Why C wrong: Autosomal dominant would show equal sex distribution with no characteristic father-to-all-daughters pattern.  Why D wrong: Autosomal recessive typically skips generations with unaffected carrier parents."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"The word \"ethics\" is derived from which Greek word?","options":["ETHOS","PATHOS","LOGOS","MORES"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Ethics is derived from the word ETHOS, defined by Bernard Harding as compromising distinction attitudes which characterize the cultural outlook of a professional group.  Why B wrong: \"Pathos\" refers to suffering/emotion, not ethics.  Why C wrong: \"Logos\" means word/reason, the root of \"logy,\" not ethics.  Why D wrong: \"Mores\" refers to social customs but is not the root of the word ethics."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"According to Bernard Harding, ethics compromises distinction attitudes that characterize what?","options":["The cultural outlook of a professional group","The legal duties of a registered nurse","The personal beliefs of an individual","The religious customs of a community"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Bernard Harding defined ethics as compromising distinction attitudes which characterize the cultural outlook of a professional group.  Why B wrong: Legal duties are governed by law, not the definition of ethics given.  Why C wrong: Personal beliefs relate to morality/values, not the professional-group definition given.  Why D wrong: Religious customs are not the focus of Harding's definition."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"Ethics in nursing is best described as a set of:","options":["Moral codes of conduct of professional behaviours towards holistic care","Government-mandated penal regulations","Optional guidelines with no professional bearing","Religious doctrines unique to each nurse"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Ethics in nursing is a set of moral codes of conduct of professional behaviours towards holistic care.  Why B wrong: Ethics are professional/moral codes, not penal/government regulations.  Why C wrong: Ethics are integral to professional behaviour, not optional.  Why D wrong: Ethics are professional codes, not religious doctrines."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"Which branch of philosophy is concerned with the distinction between right and wrong on the basis of body of knowledge?","options":["Ethics","Aesthetics","Metaphysics","Epistemology"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Ethics is the branch of philosophy concerned with the distinction between right and wrong on the basis of body of knowledge, not only on the basis of knowledge or opinion.  Why B wrong: Aesthetics concerns beauty and art.  Why C wrong: Metaphysics concerns the nature of reality and existence.  Why D wrong: Epistemology concerns the theory of knowledge itself, not right vs. wrong conduct."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"Morality in nursing ethics is principally concerned with:","options":["The distinction between right and wrong, and good and bad behaviour","The financial accounting of the institution","The technical accuracy of clinical procedures only","The legal licensing of practitioners"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Morality concerns the distinction between right and wrong, and good and bad behaviour.  Why B wrong: Financial accounting is not the focus of morality.  Why C wrong: Technical accuracy is competence, not morality.  Why D wrong: Legal licensing is a regulatory function, not morality."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"A \"code\" in nursing ethics is best defined as:","options":["The set of ethics for nurses first developed by the international council of nurses (ICN)","A computer programming language for health records","A signal used during emergencies","A diagnostic classification system"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Code is the code of ethics for nurses first developed by the International Council of Nurses (ICN) in 1953.  Why B wrong: This is unrelated to ethical codes.  Why C wrong: Emergency signals (e.g., \"code blue\") are not the meaning here.  Why D wrong: Diagnostic classification is unrelated."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"In which year did the International Council of Nurses (ICN) first develop the code of ethics for nurses?","options":["1953","1899","1933","1973"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The ICN first developed the code of ethics for nurses in 1953.  Why B wrong: 1899 is when the ICN was founded, not when the code was developed.  Why C wrong: 1933 is not mentioned in the source.  Why D wrong: 1973 is not the original development year cited."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"The International Council of Nurses (ICN) was founded in which year and where is its head quarter located?","options":["1899; Geneva, Switzerland","1953; New York, USA","1899; London, England","1947; New Delhi, India"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The ICN was founded in 1899 and its head quarter is at Geneva, Switzerland.  Why B wrong: 1953 is the code-of-ethics year, and New York is not the headquarters.  Why C wrong: 1899 is correct for founding, but London is not stated as the headquarters.  Why D wrong: Neither the year nor New Delhi is correct."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"A nurse advocates for special provisions to protect vulnerable individuals or groups. This is an example of which ethical concept?","options":["Values","Code","Crime","Tort"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Values are beliefs and attitudes that may influence behaviour and the process of decision making; the nurse-society points note the nurse advocates special provisions to protect vulnerable individuals/groups, reflecting professional values.  Why B wrong: Code is the formal code of ethics, not the underlying belief.  Why C wrong: Crime is a legal offence, not an ethical value.  Why D wrong: Tort is a civil wrong, not a value."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"Values in nursing are defined as:","options":["Beliefs and attitudes that may influence behaviour and the process of decision making","Civil wrongs against property","The penal classification of crimes","The licensing requirements of the council"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Values are beliefs and attitudes that may influence behaviour and the process of decision making.  Why B wrong: A civil wrong against property is a tort, not a value.  Why C wrong: Penal classification of crimes is a legal concept, not a value.  Why D wrong: Licensing requirements are regulatory, not values."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"An ethical dilemma is best defined as a situation where:","options":["A choice has to be made between two equally unsatisfactory alternatives","There is one clearly correct option","There is no conflict between right and wrong","Only the patient may make the decision"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: An ethical dilemma is a moral situation where a choice has to be made between two morally undesirable alternatives; the most moral situations no correct decision exists, and the nurse must make a choice between two alternatives that are equally unsatisfactory.  Why B wrong: A dilemma has no single clearly correct option.  Why C wrong: A dilemma inherently involves conflicting values of right and wrong.  Why D wrong: A dilemma may occur for nurses across cultural or religious beliefs; it is not patient-only."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"Ethical dilemmas refers to a problem in the ethical system of moral principles where values are associated with:","options":["Personal aspects of a person or the thought process where a person's sense of what is wrong and what is right","Only the financial interests of the institution","Only the legal statutes of the country","The technical maintenance of equipment"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Ethics refers to a problem in the ethical system of moral principles; values are associated with personal aspects of a person and the thought process where a person's sense of what is wrong and what is right.  Why B wrong: Financial interests are not the focus.  Why C wrong: Legal statutes describe law, not the ethical dilemma described.  Why D wrong: Equipment maintenance is irrelevant."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"Where was the first International Code of Ethics for nurses adopted by the ICN in 1953, and when was it most recently revised and reaffirmed?","options":["Brazil; revised 2012, reaffirmed 2021","Switzerland; revised 1953, reaffirmed 1973","India; revised 2006, reaffirmed 2021","England; revised 2000, reaffirmed 2010"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: An international code of ethics for nurses was first adopted by the ICN in 1953 in Brazil; it has been revised and reaffirmed at various times since, most recently with this review and revision completed in 2021.  Why B wrong: It was first adopted in Brazil, not Switzerland (Switzerland is the ICN headquarters).  Why C wrong: India relates to the Indian Nursing Council code, not the ICN code adoption.  Why D wrong: Neither the location nor years are correct."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"The ICN Code of Ethics for Nurses is a statement of the:","options":["Ethical values, responsibilities and professional accountabilities of nurses and nursing students that defines and guides ethical nursing practice within the different roles nurses assume","Penal punishments for criminal nursing acts","Salary structure for registered nurses","Curriculum syllabus for nursing colleges"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The ICN Code of Ethics for Nurses is a statement of the ethical values, responsibilities and professional accountabilities of nurses and nursing students that defines and guides ethical nursing practice within the different roles nurses assume.  Why B wrong: It is not a penal punishment statement.  Why C wrong: Salary structures are unrelated.  Why D wrong: It is an ethics code, not a curriculum syllabus."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"The ICN Code of Ethics for Nurses provides ethical guidance in relation to nurses' roles, duties, responsibilities, behaviours, professional judgement and relationships with which group?","options":["Patients, other people who are receiving nursing care or services, co-workers and allied professionals","Only the hospital administration","Only nursing students","Only the government licensing body"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Code provides ethical guidance in relation to nurses' roles, duties, responsibilities, behaviours, professional judgement and relationships with patients, other people who are receiving nursing care or services, co-workers and allied professionals.  Why B wrong: It covers far more than administration alone.  Why C wrong: It covers nurses and students but also patients, co-workers and allied professionals.  Why D wrong: The licensing body is not the focus of the relationships listed."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"According to the source, how many fundamental responsibilities do nurses have?","options":["Four","Three","Five","Two"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Nurses have four fundamental responsibilities: to promote health, to prevent illness, to restore health, and to alleviate suffering.  Why B wrong: Three is incorrect; there are four.  Why C wrong: Five is incorrect.  Why D wrong: Two is incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"Which of the following are the four fundamental responsibilities of nurses? (Select All That Apply)","options":["To promote health","To prevent illness","To restore health","To alleviate suffering","To maximize institutional profit"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The four fundamental responsibilities are to promote health, to prevent illness, to restore health, and to alleviate suffering.  Why E wrong: Maximizing institutional profit is not one of the fundamental responsibilities of nurses."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"According to the source, the need for nursing is:","options":["Universal","Limited to hospital settings","Restricted to terminally ill patients","Confined to developed nations"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The need for nursing is universal.  Why B wrong: It is universal, not limited to hospitals.  Why C wrong: It is not restricted to the terminally ill.  Why D wrong: It is universal, not confined to developed nations."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"\"Nursing care is respectful of and unrestricted by considerations of age, colour, creed, culture, disability or illness, gender, sexual orientation, nationality, politics, race or social status.\" This statement reflects which principle?","options":["Non-discriminatory, universal nursing care","Strict liability","Confidentiality","Veracity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing care is respectful of and unrestricted by considerations of age, colour, creed, culture, disability or illness, gender, sexual orientation, nationality, politics, race or social status — a statement of non-discriminatory care.  Why B wrong: Strict liability is a legal concept of harm from hazardous activity.  Why C wrong: Confidentiality concerns protecting privileged information.  Why D wrong: Veracity concerns truth-telling."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"Which body published the Code of Ethics for Nurses in India in 2006?","options":["Indian Nursing Council (INC)","International Council of Nurses (ICN)","World Health Organization (WHO)","Medical Council of India (MCI)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Indian Nursing Council (INC) has published the code of Ethics for Nurses in India in year 2006.  Why B wrong: The ICN published the international code, not the India 2006 code.  Why C wrong: WHO did not publish the India code.  Why D wrong: The MCI governs doctors, not nurses' ethics code."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"According to the source, the code of ethics for nurses in India is critical for which purpose?","options":["Building professionalism and accountability","Setting nurse salaries","Defining hospital construction standards","Regulating pharmaceutical pricing"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The code of ethics for nurses is critical for building professionalism and accountability.  Why B wrong: It does not set salaries.  Why C wrong: It does not define building standards.  Why D wrong: It does not regulate drug pricing."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"According to the INC Code of Ethics overview, ethical considerations are vital in any area dealing with human beings, including which activity?","options":["Nursing research, because they represent values, rights and relationships","Equipment procurement only","Building maintenance only","Financial auditing only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Ethical considerations are vital in any area dealing with human beings including nursing research because they represent values, rights and relationships.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifically names nursing research and human-centered values, rights and relationships, not procurement, maintenance, or auditing."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"Which of the following are codes of Ethics for Nurses in India (2006)? (Select All That Apply)","options":["The nurse respects the uniqueness of an individual in provision of care","The nurse maintains competence in order to render quality nursing care","The nurse protects the public from misinformation and misinterpretation","The nurse is obliged to practice within the framework of ethical, professional and legal boundaries","The nurse ignores the cultural beliefs of patients"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The 2006 code includes respecting the uniqueness of an individual in provision of care; maintaining competence to render quality nursing care; protecting the public from misinformation and misinterpretation; and practicing within the framework of ethical, professional and legal boundaries.  Why E wrong: The nurse considers the beliefs, values and cultural sensitivities of the individual; ignoring cultural beliefs is contrary to the code."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"\"The nurse respects individual's rights to privacy, maintains confidentiality and shares information judiciously.\" Which behaviour reflects this provision?","options":["Sharing privileged information only except in threatening situations and using discretion in sharing information","Discussing the patient's diagnosis with anyone who asks","Refusing to ever share any information with the treating team","Posting patient details on social media"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The nurse respects individual's rights to privacy of their personal information and maintains confidentiality of privileged information except in life threatening situations and uses discretion in sharing information.  Why B wrong: Indiscriminate sharing violates confidentiality.  Why C wrong: Information may be shared with the care team judiciously when needed.  Why D wrong: Posting on social media breaches confidentiality."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"According to the code, the nurse takes informed consent and maintains anonymity when information is required for which purpose?","options":["Quality assurance / academic / legal reasons","Marketing purposes only","Personal social use","To embarrass the patient"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The nurse takes informed consent and maintains anonymity when information is required for quality assurance, academic or legal reasons.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not legitimate purposes; the code specifies quality assurance, academic and legal reasons only."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"According to the code, the nurse limits the access to personal records written and computerized to:","options":["Authorized persons only","All hospital staff","The patient's relatives only","The general public"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The nurse limits the access to all personal records written and computerized to authorized persons only.  Why B wrong: Not all staff are authorized to access records.  Why C wrong: Access is limited to authorized persons, not relatives generally.  Why D wrong: The general public must not have access."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"\"Nursing care may be provided only by a registered nurse.\" This statement supports which ethical obligation?","options":["Maintaining competence to render quality nursing care","Veracity","Strict liability","False imprisonment"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The nurse maintains competence in order to render quality nursing care; nursing care may be provided only by a registered nurse, and the nurse strives to maintain quality nursing care and upholds the standards of care.  Why B wrong: Veracity concerns truth-telling.  Why C wrong: Strict liability is a tort concept.  Why D wrong: False imprisonment is a tort, not this obligation."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"According to the code, the nurse is obliged to practice within the framework of:","options":["Ethical, professional and legal boundaries","Personal preference alone","Religious doctrine only","Hospital profit targets"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The nurse is obliged to practice within the framework of ethical, professional and legal boundaries, and abides by the code of ethics and code of professional conduct for nurses in India developed by the Indian Nursing Council.  Why B/C/D wrong: Practice is bound by ethical, professional and legal frameworks, not personal preference, religion alone, or profit targets."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"The code states the nurse is obliged to work harmoniously with members of which team?","options":["The health team","The legal team only","The administrative team only","The marketing team"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The nurse is obliged to work harmoniously with members of the health team — appreciating their efforts in rendering care and cooperating/collaborating with members of the health team to meet the needs of people.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies the health team, not legal, administrative-only, or marketing teams."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"The code states the nurse is obliged to reciprocate the trust invested in nursing profession by:","options":["The society","The hospital owner","The insurance company","The pharmaceutical industry"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The nurse is obliged to reciprocate the trust invested in nursing profession by the society, demonstrating personal etiquettes and professional attributes in all dealings.  Why B/C/D wrong: The trust is invested by society, not by the hospital owner, insurer, or pharmaceutical industry."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"A nurse ensures the patient's right to make informed choices about their care, free of duress. Which ethical principle does this represent?","options":["Autonomy","Beneficence","Justice","Fidelity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Autonomy means self-determination — if a patient makes a care decision that goes against your judgment as a clinician, you respect the decision because you value their autonomy.  Why B wrong: Beneficence is doing good, not respecting self-determination.  Why C wrong: Justice is fairness/equitable distribution.  Why D wrong: Fidelity is keeping promises."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"The ethical principle of autonomy means:","options":["Self-determination","Doing no harm","Fairness or equitable distribution","Telling the truth"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Autonomy means self-determination.  Why B wrong: \"Do no harm\" is non-maleficence.  Why C wrong: Fairness/equitable distribution is justice.  Why D wrong: Telling the truth is veracity."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"A nurse double-checks a medication right three times before administration to avoid errors. This best exemplifies which ethical principle?","options":["Non-maleficence","Autonomy","Veracity","Fidelity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Non-maleficence means \"do no harm\"; a classic example is doing no harm in nursing practice reflected by nurses checking medication rights three times before administering medications, in this manner medication errors can be avoided, and the duty to do no harm is met.  Why B wrong: Autonomy is self-determination.  Why C wrong: Veracity is truth-telling.  Why D wrong: Fidelity is keeping promises."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"The ethical principle of non-maleficence is defined as:","options":["Do no harm","Doing good","Protect privileged information","Keep promises"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Non-maleficence means \"do no harm.\"  Why B wrong: Doing good is beneficence.  Why C wrong: Protecting privileged information is confidentiality.  Why D wrong: Keeping promises is fidelity."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"A nurse provides quality care for all patients with the same level of fairness despite their personalities or characteristics, such as financial status, cultural beliefs, religion, gender or sexual orientation. This represents which principle?","options":["Justice","Autonomy","Beneficence","Confidentiality"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Justice means fairness or equitable distribution; nurses provide quality care for all patients with the same level of fairness despite their personalities or characteristics such as financial status, cultural beliefs, religion, gender or sexual orientation.  Why B wrong: Autonomy is self-determination.  Why C wrong: Beneficence is doing good.  Why D wrong: Confidentiality protects information."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"The ethical principle of justice means:","options":["Fairness or equitable distribution","Self-determination","State of being responsible","Telling the truth"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Justice means fairness or equitable distribution.  Why B wrong: Self-determination is autonomy.  Why C wrong: State of being responsible is accountability.  Why D wrong: Telling the truth is veracity."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"A nurse actively works to prevent harm and to remove harmful conditions for a patient. Which ethical principle is being demonstrated?","options":["Beneficence","Non-maleficence","Justice","Fidelity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Beneficence means doing good; nurses should try to prevent harm, removing harmful conditions, or affirmatively acting to benefit another or others, often going beyond what is required by law.  Why B wrong: Non-maleficence is specifically \"do no harm,\" whereas beneficence is the affirmative \"doing good.\"  Why C wrong: Justice is equitable distribution.  Why D wrong: Fidelity is keeping promises."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"The ethical principle of beneficence is defined as:","options":["Doing good","Do no harm","Self-determination","Protect privileged information"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Beneficence means doing good.  Why B wrong: \"Do no harm\" is non-maleficence.  Why C wrong: Self-determination is autonomy.  Why D wrong: Protecting privileged information is confidentiality."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"A nurse accepts professional and legal responsibility for their own actions and consequences, and does not draw down patients' care when withdrawing. This describes which ethical principle?","options":["Accountability","Autonomy","Veracity","Justice"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Accountability is the state of being responsible — the willingness to accept professional and legal responsibility for one's own actions and consequence; if you mix up medications when drawing them up, you take accountability by discarding them and pulling new vials rather than administering them to the patient.  Why B wrong: Autonomy is self-determination.  Why C wrong: Veracity is truth-telling.  Why D wrong: Justice is equitable distribution."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"The ethical principle of accountability is defined as:","options":["State of being responsible","Doing good","Keeping promises","Do no harm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Accountability means the state of being responsible.  Why B wrong: Doing good is beneficence.  Why C wrong: Keeping promises is fidelity.  Why D wrong: Do no harm is non-maleficence."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"A nurse keeps identical data and information gathered about a patient private and ensures privacy and anonymity so their details should not be revealed. This is which ethical principle?","options":["Confidentiality","Veracity","Beneficence","Fidelity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Confidentiality means protecting privileged information; data and information gathered must be kept private. Patients and clients have a right to privacy and anonymity so their details should not be revealed.  Why B wrong: Veracity is truth-telling.  Why C wrong: Beneficence is doing good.  Why D wrong: Fidelity is keeping promises."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"The ethical principle of confidentiality means:","options":["Protect privileged information","Keep promises","Telling the truth","Fairness or equitable distribution"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Confidentiality means protecting privileged information.  Why B wrong: Keeping promises is fidelity.  Why C wrong: Telling the truth is veracity.  Why D wrong: Fairness/equitable distribution is justice."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"A nurse follows through on remaining up-to-date with evidence-based practice and implements effective mental health interventions, fulfilling commitments to the patient. This represents which ethical principle?","options":["Fidelity","Justice","Autonomy","Non-maleficence"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Fidelity means keeping promises; an example of role fidelity in nursing is remaining up-to-date with evidence-based practice and implementing effective mental health interventions.  Why B wrong: Justice is equitable distribution.  Why C wrong: Autonomy is self-determination.  Why D wrong: Non-maleficence is \"do no harm.\""},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"The ethical principle of fidelity means:","options":["Keep promises","Telling the truth","Doing good","State of being responsible"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Fidelity means keeping promises.  Why B wrong: Telling the truth is veracity.  Why C wrong: Doing good is beneficence.  Why D wrong: State of being responsible is accountability."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"When a patient has an unwanted or unexpected outcome, a nurse explains the situation with truth and empathy. Being truthful even when telling about an unwanted outcome reinforces trust and helps patients make autonomous decisions. This represents which ethical principle?","options":["Veracity","Confidentiality","Accountability","Beneficence"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Veracity means telling the truth; when you have bad news to share with a patient, you explain the situation with truth and empathy, and being truthful even when telling about an unwanted outcome reinforces trust and helps patients make autonomous decisions. An example of veracity is in health care because informed consent.  Why B wrong: Confidentiality protects information.  Why C wrong: Accountability is being responsible for actions.  Why D wrong: Beneficence is doing good."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"The ethical principle of veracity means:","options":["Telling the truth","Keeping promises","Do no harm","Self-determination"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Veracity means telling the truth.  Why B wrong: Keeping promises is fidelity.  Why C wrong: Do no harm is non-maleficence.  Why D wrong: Self-determination is autonomy."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"Which ethical principle is the foundational basis of informed consent in health care?","options":["Veracity","Justice","Non-maleficence","Fidelity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: An example of veracity in health care is informed consent — telling the truth so the patient can make an informed choice.  Why B wrong: Justice concerns equitable distribution.  Why C wrong: Non-maleficence is do no harm.  Why D wrong: Fidelity is keeping promises."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"In the classification of crimes, which type is defined as a crime against society?","options":["Criminal law","Civil law","Felony","Misdemeanour"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Criminal law involves crime against society.  Why B wrong: Civil law is crime against individuals.  Why C wrong: Felony is a major crime.  Why D wrong: Misdemeanour is a lesser offence resulting in fines or suspension of license."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"Civil law in nursing is defined as:","options":["Crime against individuals","Crime against society","A major crime","An ethical dilemma"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Civil law involves crime against individuals.  Why B wrong: Crime against society is criminal law.  Why C wrong: A major crime is a felony.  Why D wrong: An ethical dilemma is not a class of law."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"A \"felony\" is best described as:","options":["A major crime","A lesser offence resulting in fines or suspension of license","A civil wrong against property","An infraction or minor offence"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Felony is a major crime.  Why B wrong: That describes a misdemeanour.  Why C wrong: A civil wrong against property is a tort.  Why D wrong: A minor offence is an infraction."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"A misdemeanour is defined as:","options":["A lesser offence that may result in fines or suspension of license","A major crime","A crime against society","An intentional tort"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Misdemeanour is a lesser offence that may result in fines or suspension of license.  Why B wrong: A major crime is a felony.  Why C wrong: Crime against society is criminal law.  Why D wrong: Intentional tort is a separate category."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"In the classification of crimes, an \"infraction\" refers to:","options":["A minor offence","A major crime","A felony","A breach of duty"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Infraction is a minor offence.  Why B wrong: A major crime is a felony.  Why C wrong: Felony is a major crime, not an infraction.  Why D wrong: Breach of duty is a negligence element, not an infraction."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"A tort is best defined as:","options":["Violation of civil law against a person or person's property","A crime against society","A felony involving major harm","A breach of the nursing code of ethics"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Torts are violations of civil law against a person or person's property; a tort is some kind of wrongful act that cause harm to someone else.  Why B wrong: Crime against society is criminal law.  Why C wrong: A felony is a criminal classification, not a tort definition.  Why D wrong: A code breach is an ethical, not legal-tort, matter."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"\"Commission\" in the context of nursing law refers to:","options":["An inappropriate action or an act that a professional would not perform or do","Failing to do something one should do","A payment received for services","A civil law against property"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Commission is an inappropriate action or an act that a professional would not perform or do.  Why B wrong: Failing to do something one should is omission.  Why C wrong: It does not refer to payment.  Why D wrong: A civil wrong against property is a tort generally, not the definition of commission."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"Which of the following is an example of an act of commission?","options":["A doctor ordering Febrinil IV; the nurse diluting it slowly but injecting it intramuscularly by mistake (an act of doing something wrong leading to an undesirable outcome)","Failing to administer an ordered medication","Not reporting a change in patient condition","Forgetting to document care"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Commission is an act of doing something wrong that leads to an undesirable outcome; the example is a doctor ordering injections that the nurse gives slowly by mistaken intramuscular route.  Why B wrong: Failing to administer is an omission.  Why C wrong: Not reporting is an omission.  Why D wrong: Forgetting to document is an omission."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"\"Omission\" in nursing law refers to:","options":["Lack of appropriate action or an act of failing to do the right thing that leads to an undesirable outcome","An inappropriate action that a professional would not do","A felony against society","An act of telling a lie to the patient"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Omission is lack of appropriate action or an act of failing to do the right thing that leads to an undesirable outcome.  Why B wrong: An inappropriate action performed is commission.  Why C wrong: A felony is a criminal classification.  Why D wrong: Telling a lie relates to veracity, not omission."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"A doctor orders the nurse to perform an act for a febrile patient, but the nurse could not perform it. This is an example of:","options":["Omission (signs of omission are things you should do but did not)","Commission","Assault","Battery"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Omission includes signs of omission — things that you should do but that you don't do; example: it is a failure of a professional nurse to perform an act that would have been performed (doctor ordered Febrinil for fever patient, but nurse could not).  Why B wrong: Commission is doing a wrong act, not failing to act.  Why C wrong: Assault is a threat of harm.  Why D wrong: Battery is actual harmful contact."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"Torts are broadly classified into which two types?","options":["Unintentional torts and intentional torts","Civil torts and criminal torts","Felony torts and misdemeanour torts","Active torts and passive torts"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Types of torts are unintentional torts and intentional torts.  Why B wrong: Civil/criminal is a crime classification, not the tort type split.  Why C wrong: Felony/misdemeanour are crime classifications.  Why D wrong: Active/passive is not the stated tort classification."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"Intentional torts occur when:","options":["A person damages another person in a willful way","A nurse unknowingly breaches a standard of care","A patient develops a complication despite proper care","An accidental injury occurs without negligence"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Intentional torts occur when a person damages to another person in a willful way; intentional torts are intentionally harms to patient's property (emotionally, physically, financially and spiritually) by the nurse.  Why B wrong: An unknowing breach is an unintentional tort/negligence.  Why C wrong: A complication despite proper care is not an intentional tort.  Why D wrong: An accidental injury without negligence is not an intentional tort."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"Which of the following are examples of intentional torts? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Assault","Battery","False imprisonment","Invasion of privacy","Negligence"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Intentional torts include assault, battery, false imprisonment, invasion of privacy (and defamation — libel and slander).  Why E wrong: Negligence is an unintentional tort, not an intentional tort."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"A nurse threatens a patient with a procedure they do not want, causing the patient to fear imminent harmful contact. Which intentional tort is this?","options":["Assault","Battery","Defamation","False imprisonment"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Assault is a mental or physical threat to the patient by the nurse, knowingly or intentionally threatening; for example forcing a medication or treatment by threatening on a client who does not want it.  Why B wrong: Battery requires actual touching/contact.  Why C wrong: Defamation harms reputation.  Why D wrong: False imprisonment is unlawful confinement."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"A nurse touches or examines a patient in an offensive manner with or without intent to do harm. Which intentional tort is this?","options":["Battery","Assault","Invasion of privacy","Fraud"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Battery is touching or examining a client by the nurse in an offensive manner with or without intent to do harm; for example pushing a folded hand on the client by the nurses.  Why B wrong: Assault is a threat without contact.  Why C wrong: Invasion of privacy is unreasonable intrusion.  Why D wrong: Fraud is purposeful false presentation of facts to create deception."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"Purposeful false presentation of facts to create deception or cheat — for example falsely taking a false diagnosis to the patient — is which intentional tort?","options":["Fraud","Battery","Slander","False imprisonment"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Fraud is purposeful false presentation of facts to create deception or cheat; for example falsely taking a false diagnosis to the patient.  Why B wrong: Battery is offensive touching.  Why C wrong: Slander is spoken defamation.  Why D wrong: False imprisonment is unlawful confinement."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"Unreasonable or unnecessary intrusion, unauthorized touching, and unnecessary exposure or discussion of a client's case constitutes which intentional tort?","options":["Invasion of privacy","Assault","Battery","Strict liability"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Invasion of privacy is unreasonable or unnecessary intrusion, unauthorized touching and unnecessary exposure or discussion of a client's case.  Why B wrong: Assault is a threat.  Why C wrong: Battery is offensive touching specifically.  Why D wrong: Strict liability concerns harm from a hazardous activity."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"Confining or detaining a patient without legal authority is which intentional tort?","options":["False imprisonment","Fraud","Assault","Battery"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: False imprisonment is the confining or detaining patient without legal authority; for example restraining the client's limb without doctor's order.  Why B wrong: Fraud is false presentation of facts.  Why C wrong: Assault is a threat.  Why D wrong: Battery is offensive touching."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"Restraining a client's limb without a doctor's order is an example of which intentional tort?","options":["False imprisonment","Battery","Assault","Slander"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For example restraining the client's limb without doctor's order is given as an example of false imprisonment.  Why B wrong: Battery is offensive touching.  Why C wrong: Assault is a threat.  Why D wrong: Slander is spoken defamation."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"The act of damaging the good reputation of someone (defamation) is divided into which two types?","options":["Libel and slander","Assault and battery","Fraud and deceit","Commission and omission"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Defamation is the act of damaging the good reputation of someone; there are two ways — libel and slander.  Why B wrong: Assault/battery are separate torts.  Why C wrong: Fraud/deceit are not the subtypes of defamation.  Why D wrong: Commission/omission are act categories, not defamation types."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"Damaging the reputation of a client by written, pictured, or live telecast means is termed:","options":["Libel","Slander","Fraud","Battery"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Libel is damaging the reputation of the client by written, pictured and live telecast.  Why B wrong: Slander is damage by oral or speech (spoken).  Why C wrong: Fraud is false presentation of facts.  Why D wrong: Battery is offensive touching."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"Damaging the reputation of a patient orally or by speech is termed:","options":["Slander","Libel","Assault","Invasion of privacy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Slander is damage to the reputation of the patients by the orally or by speech.  Why B wrong: Libel is damage by written/pictured/live telecast.  Why C wrong: Assault is a threat.  Why D wrong: Invasion of privacy is intrusion."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"An unintentional tort is best defined as:","options":["A civil wrong unknowingly caused by a nurse that accidentally causes damage to the patients or their property and causes a financial loss","A willful act of harm by the nurse","A crime against society","A deliberate breach of confidentiality"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: An unintentional tort is a civil wrong unknowingly action by a nurse that accidently causes damage to the patients or their property and causes a financial loss.  Why B wrong: A willful act of harm is an intentional tort.  Why C wrong: A crime against society is criminal law.  Why D wrong: A deliberate breach is intentional, not unintentional."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"Which of the following are examples of unintentional torts? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Negligence","Malpractice","Breach of duty","Strict liability","Battery"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Examples of unintentional torts include negligence, malpractice, breach of duty and strict liability.  Why E wrong: Battery is an intentional tort, not an unintentional tort."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"Negligence in nursing is best defined as:","options":["It is happened when the nurse fails to prove the care or reasonableness work did by the nurse","A willful act intended to harm a patient","A criminal offence against society","The truthful disclosure of an outcome"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Negligence is it is happened when the nurse fails to prove the care or reasonableness work did by the nurse.  Why B wrong: A willful harmful act is an intentional tort.  Why C wrong: Negligence is a civil/unintentional tort, not a crime against society.  Why D wrong: Truthful disclosure relates to veracity, not negligence."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"A nurse transferring a patient from ICU to the X-ray department mistakenly falls the side rails and the patient falls off the bed on the floor, causing injury. This is an example of:","options":["Negligence (damage to the patient which can be a physical, emotional, financial and mental harm)","Battery","Strict liability","Fraud"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Negligence includes damage to the patient which can be physical, emotional, financial and mental harm; the example given is a nurse transferring a patient from ICU to X-ray with side rails down causing a fall off the bed and injury.  Why B wrong: Battery is intentional offensive touching.  Why C wrong: Strict liability involves an ultra-hazardous activity.  Why D wrong: Fraud is intentional deception."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"A nurse fails to take a proper standard of care that could result in causing harm to the patient. When a medical professional such as a nurse, doctor or pharmacist fails to provide a patient with the accepted standard of practice and causes harm, injury and/or death to the patient, this is termed:","options":["Malpractice","Assault","False imprisonment","Slander"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Malpractice is when a medical professional such as a nurse, doctor or pharmacist fails to provide a patient with the accepted standard of practice and causes harm, injury and/or death to the patient; any unreasonable lack of skills in professional duties or illegal or immoral action results in injury to or death of a patient.  Why B wrong: Assault is a threat.  Why C wrong: False imprisonment is unlawful confinement.  Why D wrong: Slander is spoken defamation."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"Breach of duty is defined as:","options":["Failure to perform according to an established standard of conduct and care which is a breach of duty","A willful intentional touching of a patient","The truthful disclosure of risks","A crime against society"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Breach of duty is failure to perform according to an established standard of conduct and care which is a breach of duty.  Why B wrong: Willful touching is battery.  Why C wrong: Truthful disclosure is veracity.  Why D wrong: A crime against society is criminal law."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"A mother crosses the street while her child steps into traffic, leading the child to be injured. This illustrates the concept that:","options":["The child's injury is found in that condition (breach of duty), where the mother's inaction is negligent in causing harm to the child","The mother committed an intentional tort of battery","This is a case of strict liability","This constitutes false imprisonment"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For example a mother fails to help her child cross the street; if the child steps into traffic and is injured in that condition (breach of duty), the mother's inaction is negligent in causing harm to the child.  Why B wrong: Battery requires offensive touching, not inaction.  Why C wrong: Strict liability concerns ultra-hazardous activity.  Why D wrong: False imprisonment is unlawful confinement."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"Strict liability is defined as:","options":["Responsibility to answer for harm that results from an ultra-hazardous activity","Willful harm to a patient's reputation","Failure to tell the truth to a patient","Confining a patient without legal authority"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Strict liability is responsibility to answer for harm that results from an ultra-hazardous activity.  Why B wrong: Willful reputation harm is defamation.  Why C wrong: Failure to tell the truth relates to veracity.  Why D wrong: Confining without authority is false imprisonment."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"Which of the following are examples of negligence/malpractice listed in the source? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Incompetent (incapable) assessment leading to some inappropriate actions","Improper identification of the client","Failure to follow protocols and procedures","Leaving surgical sponge inside a client","Telling the truth to the patient about an outcome"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Examples of negligence/malpractice include incompetent assessment leading to inappropriate actions, improper identification of the client, failure to follow protocols and procedures, and leaving surgical sponge inside a client (among others).  Why E wrong: Telling the truth is veracity (an ethical principle), not negligence/malpractice."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"Which of the following are listed examples of negligence/malpractice? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Careless in caring for a client's property","Intravenous administration errors such as incorrect flow rate","Failure to follow medication errors which result is injury to the patient","Medication errors which result is injury to the patient","Failure to obtain informed consent for academic reasons"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Listed negligence/malpractice examples include careless in caring for a client's property, intravenous administration errors such as incorrect flow rate, failure to follow protocols, and medication errors which result in injury to the patient.  Why E wrong: Obtaining informed consent is a code-of-ethics obligation; this option is a distractor not listed as negligence."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"Which of the following are listed examples of negligence/malpractice related to documentation and reporting? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Failure to report change in the patient's condition to the doctor","Failure to adequately monitor a patient's condition","Failure to report changes in the patient's condition","Failure to provide a complete report/over to the upcoming nursing staff","Maintaining patient confidentiality"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Listed examples include failure to report change in the patient's condition to the doctor, failure to adequately monitor a patient's condition, failure to report changes in the patient's condition, and failure to provide a complete report/over to the upcoming nursing staff.  Why E wrong: Maintaining confidentiality is a correct ethical practice, not negligence."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"Which of the following is a listed example of negligence/malpractice involving IV therapy?","options":["Failure to monitor for and take actions in the event of infiltration, phlebitis, fluid overload and other complications following IV therapy","Documenting the IV start time accurately","Obtaining informed consent for IV therapy","Reporting an IV complication to the doctor immediately"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A listed example is failure to monitor for and take actions in the event of infiltration, phlebitis, fluid overload and other complications following IV therapy.  Why B wrong: Accurate documentation is correct practice.  Why C wrong: Obtaining consent is a correct ethical practice.  Why D wrong: Reporting a complication is correct practice."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"(Recall the Hardest) A nurse is caring for a patient in a ward. While transferring the patient from one department to another department, the nurse forgot to raise the side rails, in between client accidentally fell down on the floor and got multiple leg fractures. Under which condition is the nurse liable?","options":["Negligence","Battery","Malpractice","Breach of duty"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Because the nurse did not perform her duty in reasonable act so that she/he may liable for negligence; the correct keyed answer is negligence.  Why B wrong: Battery requires intentional offensive touching, not an accidental failure of duty.  Why C wrong: Malpractice specifically involves failure to meet the professional standard of practice causing harm; the source keys this as negligence (the unreasonable act of not raising rails). Note: some texts could also frame this as malpractice, but the source explicitly keys negligence.  Why D wrong: Breach of duty is an element of negligence but the keyed single-best answer here is negligence."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"Euthanasia is best defined as:","options":["Killing someone on account of his/her distressing physical and mental state","The deliberate willful taking of a patient's property","A criminal offence against society regardless of intent","The truthful disclosure of a terminal diagnosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Euthanasia denotes killing someone on account of his/her distressing physical and mental state; it is a practice of the intentionally ending a life of any patient to relieve pain and suffering.  Why B wrong: Taking property is theft/conversion, not euthanasia.  Why C wrong: Euthanasia's legal status is context-dependent; this option mischaracterizes the definition.  Why D wrong: Truthful disclosure is veracity, not euthanasia."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"When euthanasia is carried out at the request of the person himself/herself, it is classified as:","options":["Voluntary euthanasia","Involuntary euthanasia","Active euthanasia","Passive euthanasia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Voluntary euthanasia is at the request of the person himself.  Why B wrong: Involuntary euthanasia is without wishing the client; it may arise when a client wants to live.  Why C wrong: Active euthanasia is a method by which death is caused (a specific cause), a subtype of method, not consent.  Why D wrong: Passive euthanasia rejects/holds treatment but is also a method type, not consent."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"Euthanasia performed without wishing the client, which may arise when a client wants to live (i.e., against the patient's will), is classified as:","options":["Involuntary euthanasia","Voluntary euthanasia","Active euthanasia","Assisted suicide"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Involuntary euthanasia is without wishing the client; it may arise when a client wants to live.  Why B wrong: Voluntary euthanasia is at the patient's own request.  Why C wrong: Active euthanasia is a method (causing death by a specific means).  Why D wrong: Assisted suicide is voluntary euthanasia in which a client (want or willing to die and has requested) takes their own life with help of others for doctor."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"Active euthanasia is best described as:","options":["Using a specific method that causes a patient to die, such as administering a loading dose of medication","Rejecting or holding treatment that may lead to the death of a patient by neglecting the patient","Euthanasia performed at the request of the person","The natural cessation of vital body systems"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Active euthanasia is using a specific method that causes a patient to die, such as administering a loading dose of medication.  Why B wrong: That describes passive euthanasia (rejecting/holding treatment, neglecting the patient, letting the patient die).  Why C wrong: Euthanasia at the person's request is voluntary euthanasia.  Why D wrong: Natural cessation of vital systems is death, not active euthanasia."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"Passive euthanasia is best described as:","options":["Rejecting or holding the treatment that may lead to the death of a patient (e.g., removing the oxygen and neglecting the patient, letting the patient die)","Administering a loading dose of medication to cause death","The patient taking their own life with the help of others","Killing against the patient's will"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Passive euthanasia is rejecting or holding the treatment that may lead to the death of a patient by removing the oxygen and neglecting the patient, letting the patient die.  Why B wrong: Administering a loading dose to cause death is active euthanasia.  Why C wrong: The patient taking their own life with help is assisted suicide/voluntary euthanasia.  Why D wrong: Killing against the patient's will is involuntary euthanasia."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"Assisted suicide, as described in the source, is a form of which type of euthanasia?","options":["Voluntary euthanasia, in which a client takes their own life with the help of others","Involuntary euthanasia","Active euthanasia performed against the patient's will","Passive euthanasia by neglect"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Voluntary euthanasia is also known as assisted suicide, in which a client (want or willing to die and has requested) takes their own life with help of others for doctor.  Why B wrong: Involuntary is against the client's wishes.  Why C wrong: Active euthanasia describes the method, not assisted suicide specifically; assisted suicide is classified as voluntary.  Why D wrong: Passive euthanasia is withholding treatment."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"According to the Note in the source, what is the status of euthanasia in India?","options":["Euthanasia is not practised in India","Euthanasia is legal and routinely practised in India","Only active euthanasia is practised in India","Euthanasia is mandatory for terminal patients in India"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Note: Euthanasia is not practised in India.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source states it is not practised in India."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"End-of-life care is related to:","options":["Death and dying","Routine preventive screening","Antenatal care","Childhood immunization"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: End of life care is related to death and dying.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are unrelated preventive/maternal/pediatric services, not end-of-life care."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"Because end-of-life care may vary among individuals of each religion, group, etc., the nurse should:","options":["Identify the specific preference and needs of each individual","Apply a single uniform protocol to all patients","Avoid involving the family","Disregard the patient's religious beliefs"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: End of life care may vary among individuals of each religion, group, therefore, the nurse identify the specific preference, and needs of each individuals.  Why B wrong: Care must be individualized, not uniform.  Why C wrong: Family involvement is appropriate.  Why D wrong: Religious beliefs must be respected, not disregarded."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"Thanatology is best defined as:","options":["The scientific study of death and dying and its psychological mechanisms of dealing with them","The study of childhood diseases","The study of blood disorders","The study of skin conditions"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Thanatology is the scientific study of death and dying and its psychological mechanisms of dealing with them.  Why B wrong: Childhood diseases are studied by pediatrics.  Why C wrong: Blood disorders are studied by hematology.  Why D wrong: Skin conditions are studied by dermatology."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"In near-death physiological manifestations, metabolism is reduced and the patient may experience:","options":["Blurred vision, decreased sense of touch and smell, and decreased pain on touch perception","Increased metabolic rate and heightened reflexes","Improved muscular strength","Increased blood pressure"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Metabolism is reduced; the patient may experienced blurred vision, decreased sense of touch and smell, and decreased pain on touch perception, and also loss of blinking reflexes.  Why B wrong: Metabolism slows, not increases.  Why C wrong: Muscular strength declines, not improves.  Why D wrong: Blood pressure progressively decreases near death, not increases."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"In near death, the general peristalsis movement is decreased, leading to which gastrointestinal manifestations?","options":["Constipation, gas accumulation, and distension","Increased bowel motility and diarrhea","Rapid gastric emptying","Increased appetite"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: By the general peristalsis, it is decreased and assumed that the GI motility and peristalsis decreases and lead to constipation, gas accumulation and distension.  Why B wrong: Motility decreases, not increases.  Why C wrong: Gastric emptying slows.  Why D wrong: Appetite is not increased; the patient's intake decreases."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"Near death, the ability to move gradually lost and the patient has difficulty in:","options":["Swallowing and speaking, losing the gag reflex","Hearing and seeing only","Sweating excessively","Maintaining high blood pressure"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Ability to move gradually lost, has difficulty in swallowing and speaking and loss the gag reflex.  Why B wrong: Hearing is the last sense lost; the difficulty noted is in swallowing/speaking.  Why C wrong: Excessive sweating is not described.  Why D wrong: Blood pressure decreases near death."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"Respirations near death may become:","options":["Slow, shallow, and irregular, with noises and sounding (death rattle) in characteristics","Deep, fast, and regular","Absent without any preceding change","Loud and forceful"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Respirations may becomes slow, shallow and irregular. Note and sounding (death rattle) in characteristics.  Why B wrong: Respirations are slow/shallow/irregular, not deep/fast/regular.  Why C wrong: There are preceding changes such as Cheyne-Stokes respiration.  Why D wrong: They become shallow, not loud/forceful."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"Which breathing pattern, characterized by an alternating period of apnea (no breath, deep, rapid breathing), may be seen near death?","options":["Cheyne-Stokes respiration","Kussmaul respiration","Biot's respiration","Eupnea"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cheyne-Stokes respiration (alternating period of apnea, no breath, deep, rapid breathing) may be seen near death.  Why B wrong: Kussmaul is deep, labored breathing of metabolic acidosis, not the alternating apnea pattern described.  Why C wrong: Biot's is irregular but not the source-named pattern here.  Why D wrong: Eupnea is normal breathing."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"Near death, the heart rate becomes slow and blood pressure falls progressively. The skin is cool to the touch and the extremities become:","options":["Mottled and cyanotic","Warm and flushed","Red and edematous","Dry and scaly"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Heart rate may slow and blood pressure falls progressively; skin is cool to the touch and the extremities become mottled and cyanotic. Skin is waxlike very near the death.  Why B wrong: Skin becomes cool, not warm/flushed.  Why C wrong: The skin becomes mottled/cyanotic, not red/edematous.  Why D wrong: The skin becomes waxlike, not dry/scaly per the source."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"Very near the time of death, the skin is described as:","options":["Waxlike","Bright red","Deeply tanned","Thickened and leathery"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Skin is waxlike very near the death.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies a waxlike appearance, not bright red, tanned, or leathery."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"As death approaches, urinary output:","options":["Decreases","Increases markedly","Remains unchanged","Becomes bright red"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Urinary output decreases near death.  Why B/C/D wrong: Output decreases, not increases or stays unchanged; color change to red is not the stated finding."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"Which type of pressure ulcers appear as a dark pear-shaped sore with irregular boundaries, usually located over 24–48 hours during the final stage of life on the sacrum and coccyx?","options":["Kennedy terminal ulcers","Stage I pressure ulcers","Diabetic foot ulcers","Venous stasis ulcers"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Kennedy terminal ulcers are a dark pear shaped sore with irregular boundaries appears rapidly usually over 24-48 hour during final stage of life in some patients, common appears on the sacrum and coccyx.  Why B wrong: Stage I pressure ulcers are non-blanchable erythema, not the dark pear-shaped terminal ulcer described.  Why C wrong: Diabetic foot ulcers are unrelated to end-of-life terminal ulcers.  Why D wrong: Venous stasis ulcers occur on the lower legs, not the terminal sacral ulcer described."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"Death is defined as:","options":["When all vital organs and body systems cease to function","When only the heart stops while the brain remains active","A temporary loss of consciousness","Slowing of the metabolic rate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Death occurs when all vital organs and body systems cease to function.  Why B wrong: Death involves cessation of all vital systems, not heart alone.  Why C wrong: Temporary loss of consciousness is not death.  Why D wrong: Slowing metabolism is a near-death sign, not death itself."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"Sudden death is described as:","options":["When a patient died within 1 hour","When a patient dies over several days","When death occurs after a prolonged illness","When death occurs only in the elderly"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Sudden death describes when a patient died within 1 hour.  Why B/C/D wrong: Sudden death is specifically within 1 hour, not over days, not necessarily after prolonged illness, and not age-restricted."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"According to the source, respiration ceases first; how long before the heartbeat stops?","options":["Respiration ceases first, before the heart beat stops a few minutes later","The heart stops first, then respiration","Both stop simultaneously","Respiration ceases an hour before the heartbeat"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Generally believed that respiration cease first then the heart beat stops a few minutes later.  Why B wrong: Respiration ceases first, not the heart.  Why C wrong: They do not stop simultaneously; there is a few-minute gap.  Why D wrong: The gap is a few minutes, not an hour."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"The medicolegal definition of death is:","options":["Permanent and irreversible cessation of function of the three interlinked vital systems of the body (tripod of life): nervous, circulatory and respiratory systems","The temporary cessation of the heartbeat only","The loss of consciousness for more than 24 hours","The absence of urine output"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Medicolegal definition — death is permanent and irreversible cessation of the three interlinked vital systems of the body (tripod of life): nervous, circulatory and respiratory systems.  Why B wrong: Death is permanent cessation of three systems, not temporary heartbeat loss.  Why C wrong: Loss of consciousness is not the medicolegal definition.  Why D wrong: Absent urine output is not the definition."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"The three interlinked vital systems forming the \"tripod of life\" are: (Select All That Apply)","options":["Nervous system","Circulatory system","Respiratory system","Digestive system","Urinary system"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The tripod of life comprises the nervous, circulatory and respiratory systems.  Why D wrong: The digestive system is not part of the tripod of life.  Why E wrong: The urinary system is not part of the tripod of life."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"Brain death is defined as:","options":["The permanent, irreversible and complete loss of brain function which may include cessation of involuntary activity necessary to sustain life","A reversible loss of consciousness","The cessation of heartbeat for a few minutes","A temporary loss of reflexes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Brain death is the permanent, irreversible and complete loss of brain function which may include cessation of involuntary activity necessary to sustain life.  Why B wrong: Brain death is irreversible, not reversible.  Why C wrong: Heartbeat cessation alone does not define brain death.  Why D wrong: Brain death is permanent loss of brain function, not temporary loss of reflexes."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"In brain death, there is irreversible cessation of circulatory and pulmonary functions and other all functions of the entire brain, including which structure?","options":["Brainstem reflexes","The spinal cord only","The peripheral nerves only","The cardiac muscle only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Brain death involves irreversible cessation of circulatory, pulmonary functions and other all functions of the entire brain, including loss of brainstem reflexes; the person is dead.  Why B wrong: Brain death concerns the entire brain including brainstem, not the spinal cord only.  Why C wrong: Peripheral nerves alone do not define brain death.  Why D wrong: Cardiac muscle alone is not the focus; brain death is loss of brain function."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"Who legally declares a patient brain dead, and how must the relatives be informed?","options":["A medical officer (doctor); therefore nurses SHOULD NEVER declare death","Any senior nurse on duty","The hospital administrator","The patient's family"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Brain death is legally declared by medical officer (doctor); therefore nurses SHOULD NEVER declare death.  Why B wrong: Nurses should never declare death.  Why C wrong: An administrator cannot legally declare death.  Why D wrong: The family cannot legally declare death."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"During the apnea test for brain death, the PCO2 reaches the target value at what time?","options":["Time of death, i.e. per AAN (i.e., when PCO2 reaches the target value during the apnea test)","Immediately at the start of the test","After 24 hours","Only after cardiac arrest"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Time of death, i.e. per AAN, when PCO2 reaches the target value during the apnea test.  Why B wrong: The target is reached during the test, not immediately at the start.  Why C wrong: It is during the apnea test, not after 24 hours.  Why D wrong: It is defined by PCO2 reaching the target value, not by cardiac arrest."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"Which of the following are confirmatory tests for brain death? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Presence of irreversible coma with all brain stem reflexes are absent","No pupillary reflex, pupils are fixed, unreactive to light","Corneal, vestibulocochlear, gag and cough reflexes are absent","No motor response to cranial nerve distribution","Presence of spontaneous respiration on the ventilator"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Confirmatory tests for brain death include presence of irreversible coma with all brain stem reflexes absent; no pupillary reflex with fixed unreactive pupils; absent corneal, vestibulocochlear, gag and cough reflexes; and no motor response to cranial nerve distribution.  Why E wrong: In brain death there is no respiratory movement enough to allow uptake of carbon dioxide to rise above threshold for PCO2 (i.e., apnea); spontaneous respiration would be inconsistent with brain death."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"In brain death confirmatory testing, regarding respiration the source states:","options":["No respiratory movement when the patient disconnected from ventilator long enough to allow carbon dioxide to rise above the threshold for PCO2, i.e. 50 mm Hg","Deep, rapid respirations continue","Respirations remain slow but present","Respirations alternate with apnea"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: No respiratory movement when the patient disconnected from ventilator long enough to allow carbon dioxide to rise above the threshold for PCO2, i.e. 50 mm Hg.  Why B/C/D wrong: In brain death there is no respiratory movement at all; any respirations would be inconsistent with brain death."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"According to the Note in the source, sometimes spinal reflexes can be elicited in brain dead patients. What does this imply for the nurse?","options":["Spinal reflexes can still be present even though the brain is dead, so their presence does not negate brain death","The patient is alive and brain death is incorrect","The patient is recovering","Brain death must be re-evaluated as reversible"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Note: sometimes spinal reflexes can be elicited in brain dead patients — these are mediated at the spinal level and do not indicate brain function, so they do not negate brain death.  Why B wrong: Spinal reflexes do not mean the patient is alive in brain death.  Why C wrong: They do not indicate recovery.  Why D wrong: Brain death remains irreversible; the spinal reflexes are not evidence of reversibility."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"Which of the following are immediate body changes following a death? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Insensibility","Loss of voluntary movement","Cessation of respiration and circulation","Rigor mortis","Putrefaction"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Immediate body changes following death include insensibility, loss of voluntary movement, and cessation of respiration and circulation.  Why D wrong: Rigor mortis is an early change, not an immediate change.  Why E wrong: Putrefaction is a late (decomposition) change."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"Which of the following are early body changes following death? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Pallor mortis (skin become pallor) and loss of skin elasticity","Eye changes (tache noire)","Primary flaccidity","Rigor mortis (stiffing of the body)","Adipocere formation"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Early body changes include pallor mortis (skin becomes pallor) and loss of skin elasticity, eye changes (tache noire), primary flaccidity, hypostasis, algor mortis (cooling of the body), rigor mortis (stiffing of the body), and livor mortis (discoloration of the skin).  Why E wrong: Adipocere is a late (decomposition) change, not an early change."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"Which of the following are early body changes following death? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Hypostasis","Algor mortis (cooling of the body)","Livor mortis (discoloration of the skin)","Mummification","Putrefaction"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Early changes include hypostasis, algor mortis (cooling of the body) and livor mortis (discoloration of the skin).  Why D wrong: Mummification is a late (decomposition) change.  Why E wrong: Putrefaction is a late (decomposition) change."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"Which of the following are late (decomposition) body changes following death? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Putrefaction","Adipocere","Mummification","Algor mortis","Primary flaccidity"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Late (decomposition) changes include putrefaction, adipocere and mummification.  Why D wrong: Algor mortis (cooling) is an early change.  Why E wrong: Primary flaccidity is an early change."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"In the seven stages of body decomposition, pallor mortis is best described as:","options":["The skin becomes pale in colour and grey appearance, beginning 15 to 20 minutes after death","The settling of blood causing skin discoloration","The cooling of the body","The stiffening of muscles"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Pallor mortis — the skin becomes pale in colour and in grey appearance, beginning 15 to 20 minutes after death.  Why B wrong: Settling of blood causing discoloration is livor mortis.  Why C wrong: Cooling of the body is algor mortis.  Why D wrong: Stiffening of muscles is rigor mortis."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"Pallor mortis begins approximately how long after death?","options":["15 to 20 minutes after death","Immediately at the moment of death","2 to 3 hours after death","24 hours after death"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Pallor mortis begins 15 to 20 minutes after death.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies 15 to 20 minutes, not immediately, not hours later."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"Livor mortis is best described as:","options":["Discolouration of the skin caused by blood pooling (lividity) or settling of the blood in the lower portion of the body","The cooling of the body to ambient temperature","The stiffening of the body by muscle contraction","The breakdown of tissues by microbial action"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Livor mortis is discolouration of the skin caused by blood pooling (lividity) or settling of the blood in the lower portion of the body; the skin on the body becomes pale in colour due to oxygen dissociation, and it begins 30 minutes after death.  Why B wrong: Cooling of the body is algor mortis.  Why C wrong: Stiffening of the body is rigor mortis.  Why D wrong: Microbial breakdown of tissues is putrefaction."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"Livor mortis (pooling of blood in the lower portion of the body) begins approximately how long after death?","options":["30 minutes after death","15 to 20 minutes after death","2 hours after death","12 hours after death"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Livor mortis begins 30 minutes after death.  Why B wrong: 15–20 minutes is the onset of pallor mortis.  Why C/D wrong: The source specifies 30 minutes for livor mortis."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"Algor mortis is best defined as:","options":["The cooling stage, in which body temperature decreases until reaching the ambient temperature","The pooling and settling of blood in the dependent body parts","The breakdown of body tissues by bacteria","The stiffening of the body by muscle rigidity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Algor mortis is the cooling stage, decreasing body temperature until reaching the ambient temperature.  Why B wrong: Pooling of blood is livor mortis.  Why C wrong: Bacterial breakdown is putrefaction.  Why D wrong: Stiffening is rigor mortis."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"Body temperature in algor mortis is measured by a 25 cm long chemical thermometer, which ranges from what temperature range?","options":["0°C to 50°C","10°C to 40°C","20°C to 60°C","-10°C to 30°C"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Body temperature is measured by a 25 cm long chemical thermometer which ranges from 0°C to 50°C.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies a 0°C to 50°C range."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"During algor mortis, after death the body temperature is best measured at which site?","options":["The rectum (most common); other sites include auditory meatus, mouth and under the liver","The axilla only","The oral cavity only","The skin surface of the limbs only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: After death body temperature is best measured at the rectum (most common); other sites include auditory meatus, mouth and under the liver.  Why B wrong: The axilla is not listed as the most common site.  Why C wrong: The mouth is one site but the rectum is the most common.  Why D wrong: Limb skin surface is not the recommended site."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"Rigor mortis is best described as:","options":["All muscles in the body, both voluntary and involuntary, become rigid due to biochemical changes; it begins 2 hours after death and completely formed in 12 hours after death","The cooling of the body to ambient temperature","The discoloration of skin from pooled blood","The decomposition of the body by microbial action"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Rigor mortis — all muscles in the body, both voluntary and involuntary, become rigid due to biochemical changes; it begins 2 hours after death and completely formed in 12 hours after death.  Why B wrong: Cooling is algor mortis.  Why C wrong: Discoloration from pooled blood is livor mortis.  Why D wrong: Microbial decomposition is putrefaction."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"Rigor mortis begins and is completely formed at which times after death?","options":["Begins 2 hours after death and completely formed in 12 hours after death","Begins immediately and completes in 1 hour","Begins 30 minutes after death and completes in 3 hours","Begins 12 hours after death and completes in 24 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Rigor mortis begins 2 hours after death and completely formed in 12 hours after death.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies onset at 2 hours and full formation at 12 hours."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"Putrefaction is best described as:","options":["The decomposition of the body carried out by the microbial action; the principal bacterial agent causing putrefaction is the gram-positive, anaerobic, and rod-shaped Clostridium welchii, and the first external sign of putrefaction is the greenish discoloration of the skin over the right iliac fossa","The cooling of the body to ambient temperature","The settling of blood in dependent parts","The stiffening of muscles"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Putrefaction is the decomposition of the body carried out by the microbial action; the principal bacterial agent is gram-positive, anaerobic, rod-shaped Clostridium welchii, and the first external sign is the greenish discoloration of the skin over the right iliac fossa.  Why B wrong: Cooling is algor mortis.  Why C wrong: Settling of blood is livor mortis.  Why D wrong: Stiffening is rigor mortis."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"The principal bacterial agent causing putrefaction is:","options":["Clostridium welchii (gram-positive, anaerobic, rod-shaped)","Escherichia coli (gram-negative, aerobic)","Staphylococcus aureus (gram-positive, aerobic)","Streptococcus pyogenes (gram-positive, facultative)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The principal bacterial agent causing putrefaction is the gram-positive, anaerobic, and rod-shaped Clostridium welchii.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifically names Clostridium welchii as gram-positive, anaerobic and rod-shaped."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"The first external sign of putrefaction is:","options":["Greenish discoloration of the skin over the right iliac fossa","Stiffening of the limbs","Cooling of the trunk","Pallor of the face"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The first external sign of putrefaction is the greenish discoloration of the skin over the right iliac fossa.  Why B wrong: Stiffening is rigor mortis.  Why C wrong: Cooling is algor mortis.  Why D wrong: Pallor is pallor mortis."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"Saponification (adipocere formation) is best described as:","options":["A modification of the putrefaction process, which involves hydrolysis and hydrogenation of fatty tissues into a yellowish, greasy, rancid, wax-like substance called adipocere","The natural cessation of vital body systems","The cooling of the body","The drying and shrinkage of soft tissues"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Saponification or adipocere formation is a modification of the putrefaction process, which involves hydrolysis and hydrogenation of fatty tissues into a yellowish, greasy, rancid, wax-like substance called adipocere.  Why B wrong: Cessation of vital systems is death.  Why C wrong: Cooling is algor mortis.  Why D wrong: Drying and shrinkage describes mummification."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"Mummification is best described as:","options":["A modification of the putrefaction process characterized by the desiccation or dehydration of the cadaveric tissues, in which the skin of the deceased becomes brown, hard and brittle and has a stretched appearance over prominences like the zygomatic bones, mandible, etc.","The conversion of fat into a wax-like substance","The cooling of the body to ambient temperature","The end of decomposition where all soft tissues have decomposed leaving only the skeleton"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Mummification is a modification of the putrefaction process characterized by the desiccation or dehydration of the cadaveric tissues; the skin becomes brown, hard and brittle and has a stretched appearance over prominences like the zygomatic bones, mandible, etc.  Why B wrong: Conversion of fat to wax is saponification/adipocere.  Why C wrong: Cooling is algor mortis.  Why D wrong: Loss of all soft tissues leaving the skeleton is skeletonization."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"Skeletonization is best defined as:","options":["The end of decomposition, when all tissues have decomposed, leaving only the skeleton","The pooling of blood in dependent body parts","The desiccation of cadaveric tissues","The cooling of the body"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Skeletonization is the end of decomposition, when all tissues have decomposed, leaving only the skeleton.  Why B wrong: Pooling of blood is livor mortis.  Why C wrong: Desiccation of tissues is mummification.  Why D wrong: Cooling is algor mortis."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"Fossilization is best described as:","options":["The natural preservation of the skeletal remains formed over a very long period; this stage may not occur depending on the circumstances and conditions of the surrounding environment","The rapid breakdown of the body within hours","The pooling of blood after death","The cooling of the body to ambient temperature"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Fossilization is the natural preservation of the skeletal remains formed over a very long period; this stage may not occur depending on the circumstances and conditions of the surrounding environment.  Why B wrong: It is a very long-period process, not rapid.  Why C wrong: Pooling of blood is livor mortis.  Why D wrong: Cooling is algor mortis."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"Which of the following is the correct chronological order of the seven stages of body decomposition?","options":["Pallor mortis → Livor mortis → Algor mortis → Rigor mortis → Putrefaction → Skeletonization → Fossilization","Rigor mortis → Algor mortis → Livor mortis → Pallor mortis → Putrefaction → Fossilization → Skeletonization","Putrefaction → Pallor mortis → Livor mortis → Algor mortis → Rigor mortis → Skeletonization → Fossilization","Livor mortis → Pallor mortis → Rigor mortis → Algor mortis → Putrefaction → Fossilization → Skeletonization"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists the seven stages in order: pallor mortis, livor mortis, algor mortis, rigor mortis, putrefaction, skeletonization, and fossilization.  Why B/C/D wrong: These reorder the stages incorrectly relative to the source's sequence."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"A patient near death tells the nurse they refuse further chemotherapy, against the physician's recommendation. The nurse honors this decision. This is the BEST example of which ethical principle in action?","options":["Autonomy","Beneficence","Non-maleficence","Justice"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Autonomy (self-determination) means if a patient makes a care decision that goes against your judgment as a clinician, you respect the decision because you value their autonomy.  Why B wrong: Beneficence is doing good and would not by itself require honoring a refusal.  Why C wrong: Non-maleficence is \"do no harm,\" not the principle that governs honoring patient choice.  Why D wrong: Justice concerns equitable distribution of care."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"A nurse posts a photo of a deceased celebrity patient with their name on social media. This act constitutes which intentional tort?","options":["Libel (and invasion of privacy/breach of confidentiality)","Slander","Assault","Strict liability"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Libel is damaging the reputation of the client by written, pictured and live telecast — a posted photo with name falls under libel/invasion of privacy.  Why B wrong: Slander is spoken (oral) defamation, not written/pictured.  Why C wrong: Assault is a threat of harmful contact.  Why D wrong: Strict liability concerns harm from an ultra-hazardous activity."},{"section":"Batch 059 · Ethics in nursing…","question":"A nurse must declare a patient dead before the doctor arrives. What is the correct action?","options":["Do not declare death — only a medical officer (doctor) can legally declare death; nurses should never declare death","Declare death immediately to save time","Ask the family to declare death","Document the death and inform the morgue independently"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Brain death/death is legally declared by medical officer (doctor); therefore nurses SHOULD NEVER declare death.  Why B wrong: Nurses must not declare death.  Why C wrong: Family cannot legally declare death.  Why D wrong: The nurse cannot independently declare death before the doctor's legal declaration."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"What is the primary characteristic of hospice care?","options":["It is given when the disease is stopped and the patient is expected to recover","It is care given when the disease is not going to survive the client (terminal/end-of-life) and is given over time","It is aggressive curative treatment aimed at a cure","It is care given only in the immediate post-operative period"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hospice care is provided when the disease can no longer be cured and the client is not expected to survive; it is comfort-focused care given over the end-of-life period.  Why A wrong: Recovery-oriented care is curative, not hospice.  Why C wrong: Hospice is comfort-focused, not aggressive curative treatment.  Why D wrong: It is not limited to post-operative care; it is for terminal illness."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"Which statement BEST describes palliative care?","options":["Care for the terminally ill and their family in a hospital or home setting","Care given only after death","Care that always cures the underlying disease","Care restricted to children only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Palliative care is care provided to the terminally ill and their family, deliverable in either a hospital or home setting, focusing on comfort and quality of life.  Why B wrong: That describes post-mortem care, not palliative care.  Why C wrong: Palliative care relieves symptoms; it does not cure the disease.  Why D wrong: It is not restricted to children."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"A nurse caring for a dying patient gives pain medication on a fixed schedule rather than waiting for the patient to request it. This action reflects which palliative principle?","options":["Asses color and temperature","Administer pain medication and do not delay or postpone pain medication","Restrict fluids strictly","Avoid mouth care to prevent aspiration"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In physical care of the dying patient, analgesics should be administered and pain medication must not be delayed or postponed, ensuring continuous comfort.  Why A wrong: Assessing color/temperature is a separate assessment, not the rationale for scheduled analgesia.  Why C wrong: The source does not direct strict fluid restriction.  Why D wrong: Mouth care is recommended, not avoided."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"Which interventions are appropriate in the physical care of a dying patient? (Select all that apply)","options":["Elevate the head of the bed or position the client on their side to prevent dyspnea","Administer supplemental oxygen for comfort","Maintain a regular voice tone and encourage taking ice chips and sips of fluid","Avoid all mouth care and skin care","Encourage the patient and family members to express feelings"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: Elevating the head/side-lying eases breathing; oxygen is given for comfort; maintaining a regular voice tone and offering ice chips/sips provides comfort; encouraging expression of feelings supports psychological care.  Why D wrong: Mouth care, skin care, and lip lubrication are recommended, not avoided."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"To prevent dryness and cracking of the lips and oral/nasal mucous membranes in a dying patient, the nurse should:","options":["Restrict all oral intake","Lubricate the lips and oral and nasal mucous membranes","Apply tight nasal packing","Use only suction without lubrication"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source directs lubricating the lips and oral and nasal mucous membranes to prevent drying and cracking and to keep the patient comfortable.  Why A wrong: Total restriction of intake worsens dryness and is not indicated.  Why C wrong: Tight nasal packing is not a comfort measure here.  Why D wrong: Suction alone does not address mucosal dryness."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"Why should the nurse assess for Kennedy terminal ulcer and skin breakdown in a dying patient?","options":["To start aggressive chemotherapy","To implement measures to prevent breakdown and maintain skin integrity","To delay all repositioning","To withhold barrier creams"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Assessing for Kennedy terminal ulcer and skin breakdown allows the nurse to implement preventive measures and protect skin integrity in the dying patient.  Why A wrong: Chemotherapy is not a comfort/skin-care measure at end of life.  Why C wrong: Repositioning helps prevent breakdown and should not be delayed.  Why D wrong: Barrier protection is part of skin care, not withheld."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"A dying patient becomes restless and anxious. Which nursing actions are appropriate? (Select all that apply)","options":["Provide a calm, quiet environment","Maintain a calm, soothing environment and provide reassurance and support","Do not restrain the patient","Limit the number of visitors at the client's bedside","Allow a family member to stay with the client"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: A calm, soothing environment, reassurance/support, avoiding restraint, limiting visitor numbers, and allowing a family member to stay all reduce restlessness and provide comfort.  (No incorrect options — all are listed source measures.)"},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"A legally binding document in which a client states the kind of medical care or treatment they wish to receive (or refuse) is called a/an:","options":["Discharge summary","Advance directive","Incident report","Consent for surgery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: An advance directive is the legal document specifying the medical care a client wishes to receive or refuse, especially when they can no longer communicate.  Why A wrong: A discharge summary documents the hospital stay, not future care wishes.  Why C wrong: An incident report records adverse events.  Why D wrong: A surgical consent authorizes a specific procedure, not overall end-of-life wishes."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"When implementing nursing care for a dying client, the nurse should consider which of the following? (Select all that apply)","options":["Cultural beliefs","Religious rituals, state laws, and hospital procedures","The client's stated values and respect","Only the physician's personal preference","Performing procedures to relieve any discomfort the patient may be experiencing"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: Care must consider cultural and religious rituals, state laws, hospital procedures, the client's values, and relief of any discomfort.  Why D wrong: Care is individualized to the client's needs/wishes, not based solely on the physician's personal preference."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"When does post-mortem care of the body begin?","options":["The next morning after death","After the client is pronounced dead and any equipment removed/turned off as appropriate","Only after the family arrives","Twenty-four hours after death"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Post-mortem (after-death) care begins once the client has been pronounced dead and equipment is turned off and removed as appropriate.  Why A wrong: Care should not be delayed until the next morning.  Why C wrong: Care begins after pronouncement, not contingent on family arrival.  Why D wrong: There is no 24-hour wait before beginning care."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"During post-mortem care, the nurse should:","options":["Disclose the cause of death to all bystanders","Maintain respect and dignity for the client","Remove all identification tubes immediately","Refuse to allow the family to view the body"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Maintaining respect and dignity for the deceased client is a core principle of post-mortem care.  Why A wrong: Confidentiality is maintained; cause of death is not broadcast to bystanders.  Why C wrong: Some tubes/lines may need to remain (e.g., for medico-legal cases); removal is not automatic.  Why D wrong: The family is usually permitted to view the body in privacy."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"Before performing certain post-mortem procedures, the nurse should determine whether the client is an organ donor. Why is this important?","options":["So organ retrieval can occur quickly while organs remain viable","To increase the hospital bill","To delay all post-mortem care","To avoid notifying the family"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Knowing organ-donor status guides appropriate procedures so that, if applicable, organ retrieval is handled promptly and tissues remain viable.  Why B wrong: It is not done for billing reasons.  Why C wrong: Determining donor status guides, not delays, care.  Why D wrong: Family notification is still required."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"When a medical examiner is required (e.g., unexpected, unnatural death, or a death that is a question of criminal involvement), what should the nurse do regarding tubes and lines?","options":["Remove all tubes and lines before the examiner sees the body","Leave tubes, lines, and other devices in place as required","Discard the body immediately","Bathe and embalm the body"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In medico-legal cases requiring the medical examiner, tubes and lines must be left in place because they may be evidence relevant to the investigation.  Why A wrong: Removing them destroys potential evidence.  Why C wrong: The body must be preserved for examination.  Why D wrong: Embalming/bathing is not performed before medico-legal examination."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"Which of the following are general post-mortem procedures the nurse should perform? (Select all that apply)","options":["Close the patient's eyes","Replace dentures","Wash the body and change/replace soiled linen if needed","Place a pad under the perineum","Straighten the body and place a pillow under the head in preparation for family viewing"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: General post-mortem procedures include closing the eyes, replacing dentures, washing the body and soiled linens, placing pads under the perineum, removing tubes/dressings, and straightening the body with a pillow under the head to prepare for the family viewing.  (No incorrect options — all are listed steps.)"},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"While performing post-mortem care, the nurse provides psychological support and advocacy to the patient's family. This reflects which nursing role at end of life?","options":["Withholding information from the family","Supporting and advocating for the family during their grief","Avoiding contact with the family","Delegating all care to the mortuary only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The nurse provides psychological support and advocacy to the family, an essential supportive/advocacy role during bereavement.  Why A wrong: Information is shared appropriately, not withheld.  Why C wrong: The nurse supports the family rather than avoiding them.  Why D wrong: The nurse remains involved in care and family support."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"When caring for the body of a patient who died with HIV or HBV infection, which precaution level should be implemented?","options":["No special precautions are needed","Standard precautions should be implemented for every case","Precautions only if the family requests them","Only droplet precautions"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: All dead bodies are potentially infectious; therefore Standard Precautions must be implemented for every case during dead-body care, including HIV/HBV.  Why A wrong: Precautions are always required; bodies are potentially infectious.  Why C wrong: Precautions are mandatory regardless of family request.  Why D wrong: Standard precautions (not just droplet) are required for body fluid exposure."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"When handling an HIV/HBV-positive dead body, which personal protective equipment should the nurse wear?","options":["Only a surgical mask","PPE including goggles or a face shield to protect the eyes if there may be splashes","No PPE if death is recent","Sterile gown only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: When handling infectious dead bodies, the nurse must avoid direct contact with blood/body fluids and wear PPE including goggles or a face shield where splashes may occur.  Why A wrong: A mask alone does not protect against splash exposure to the eyes.  Why C wrong: Recent death does not reduce infectivity; PPE is still required.  Why D wrong: A sterile gown alone is insufficient against splash exposure."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"Which steps are part of the documentation and verification procedure after a patient's death? (Select all that apply)","options":["Ascertain that the death is declared and certified by the doctor on duty","Record the time of death and time of verification of death in the patient documentation","Inform the patient's relatives about the death","Allow family members to view the body and acknowledge cultural or religious preferences","Ensure necessary forms are filled and signed by the person concerned"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Post-death care requires that death be declared and certified by the doctor, time of death and verification documented, relatives informed, family allowed to view the body with cultural/religious preferences acknowledged, and forms filled and signed.  (No incorrect options — all are listed steps.)"},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"To prevent disfigurement before rigor mortis develops, the body should be kept in which position?","options":["Prone with head turned to the side","Normal anatomical position lying on the back, cared for immediately after death and before rigor mortis","Sitting upright in a chair","Lateral with knees flexed"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The body should be kept in normal (anatomical) position on the back and cared for immediately after death, before rigor mortis develops, to maintain natural appearance.  Why A, C, D wrong: Non-anatomical positions can cause fixation in an abnormal posture once rigor mortis sets in."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"When applying a chin bandage and positioning the head during dead-body care, the nurse should:","options":["Hyperextend the neck and leave the mouth open","Apply the bandage to keep the mouth closed, support the chin with a jaw bandage, and elevate the head on a pillow","Lower the head below the level of the body","Avoid any support to the head"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The chin/jaw bandage keeps the mouth closed, the head is supported, and the head is elevated on a pillow to maintain a dignified appearance and prevent fluid pooling in the face.  Why A wrong: Leaving the mouth open causes disfigurement.  Why C wrong: Lowering the head increases facial congestion.  Why D wrong: Head support is required."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"Which actions help maintain dignity and reduce infection risk when caring for an infectious dead body? (Select all that apply)","options":["Care for the body with reverence (feeling of deep respect)","Make sure any wounds, cuts, and abrasions are covered with waterproof bandages or dressings","Dispose of extreme caution sharps directly into a sharps container with precaution","Remove all appliances used for the patient (Ryle's tube, urinary catheter, oxygen catheter, IV lines, drainage tubes) and soiled dressings","Leave all soiled dressings on the body"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Caring with reverence, covering wounds with waterproof dressings, safe sharps disposal, and removal of appliances and soiled dressings all maintain dignity and reduce infection risk.  Why E wrong: Soiled dressings should be removed, not left in place."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"Which steps are correct when handling body orifices and identification during dead-body care? (Select all that apply)","options":["Pack the vagina, rectum, and nose with gauze or cotton","Apply a perineal pad and diaper to prevent escape of urine and stool","Apply patient identification bands to the deceased's wrist or ankle with details of name, age, sex, UH ID number, ward, bed number, diagnosis, cause of death, complete address, date, and time of death","Discard all identification of the deceased","Place hands over the chest and tie the thumbs and wrists together"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: Orifices are packed, a perineal pad/diaper applied, identification bands with full details applied to wrist/ankle, and the hands placed on the chest with thumbs/wrists tied together to maintain position.  Why D wrong: Identification must be applied and retained, never discarded — it ensures correct identification of the body."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"Where should the identification tag bearing the deceased's information be placed before transferring the body to the mortuary?","options":["Inside the patient's chart only","On the body and over the mortuary sheet","Hidden under the body","It is not needed"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: An identification tag is placed over the mortuary sheet (and on the body) so the body can be correctly identified in the mortuary.  Why A wrong: ID must be physically on/over the body, not only in the chart.  Why C wrong: A hidden tag defeats the purpose of identification.  Why D wrong: Identification is essential."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"When wrapping an infectious dead body for transport, the nurse should use a transparent plastic bag/cover and:","options":["Leave the bag open at the head end","Tie the bag with a square knot (which is useful while bandaging closed wounds) and zip up the cover","Use only a cloth sheet without sealing","Avoid any wrapping"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The body is placed in a transparent plastic bag whose end is closed and the cover zipped; tying with a square knot (also used in bandaging closed wounds) secures it and prevents escape of fluids.  Why A wrong: An open bag risks leakage and contamination.  Why C wrong: An unsealed cloth sheet does not contain infectious fluids.  Why D wrong: Wrapping/containment is required for an infectious body."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"The outside of the polythene bag/body should be wiped with which solution before being allowed to air dry?","options":["Plain tap water","1% Sodium Hypochlorite solution","70% ethyl alcohol only","Liquid soap solution"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The outer surface of the polythene bag/body is wiped with 1% Sodium Hypochlorite solution (a decontaminant) and allowed to air dry.  Why A wrong: Plain water does not decontaminate.  Why C wrong: The source specifies sodium hypochlorite for surface decontamination here.  Why D wrong: Soap alone is not the specified decontaminant."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"A patient's soiled bed linen from a dead HIV-positive body should be washed with which concentration of sodium hypochlorite?","options":["0.1%","1%","5%","10%"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The note specifies that an HIV patient's soiled bed linen should be washed with 5% sodium hypochlorite.  Why A, B, D wrong: The source specifies 5% for soiled linen (note this differs from the 1% used to wipe the bag's outer surface)."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"After removing the body to the mortuary, which environmental and waste-handling steps should the nurse perform? (Select all that apply)","options":["Clean the ward environmental surfaces, instruments, and transport trolleys (decontaminate appropriately)","Dispose of bio-medical waste as per the guidelines","Remove personal protective equipment after handling the dead body","Perform hand hygiene immediately","Reuse contaminated PPE on the next patient"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Cleaning the ward and trolleys, disposing of biomedical waste per guidelines, doffing PPE after handling the body, and performing immediate hand hygiene are all required infection-control steps.  Why E wrong: Contaminated PPE is removed and disposed of, never reused."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"First aid is BEST defined as:","options":["The definitive surgical treatment of an injury","The immediate help/care given to an injured or sick person before the hospital or qualified help is available","Care given only after admission to the hospital","A type of physiotherapy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: First aid is the immediate help given to an injured or sick person before professional/hospital help is available.  Why A wrong: Definitive surgical care is hospital treatment, not first aid.  Why C wrong: First aid precedes hospital care.  Why D wrong: First aid is emergency care, not physiotherapy."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"A bystander gives immediate care for which type(s) of emergency before the hospital? (Select all that apply)","options":["Suffocation","Myocardial infarction","Haemorrhage and poisoning","Burns and heat stroke","Unconsciousness, sprain, strain, fracture, and animal bite (dog bite, monkey bite, etc.)"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: First aid is given in many emergency forms including suffocation, myocardial infarction, haemorrhage, poisoning, burns, heat stroke, unconsciousness, sprain, strain, fracture, and animal bites.  (No incorrect options — all are listed emergencies.)"},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"Which of the following are the aims of first aid? (Select all that apply)","options":["To preserve life","To prevent harm and promote recovery","To save the life of the injured person","To promote the improvement of the victim's health","To delay treatment until convenient"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The aims of first aid are to preserve life, prevent harm/worsening and promote recovery, save the injured person's life, and promote improvement of the victim's health.  Why E wrong: First aid is immediate; delaying treatment contradicts its purpose."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"Match the principle of first aid: a victim who is bleeding heavily should be managed by which immediate action?","options":["Wait for additional help before doing anything","If there is an injury and bleeding is occurring, stop the bleeding as soon as possible","Encourage the patient to walk it off","Give oral fluids immediately"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A first-aid principle states that when injury and bleeding occur, the bleeding must be stopped as soon as possible to preserve life.  Why A wrong: Active bleeding must be controlled immediately, not delayed.  Why C wrong: Walking does not control hemorrhage and may worsen it.  Why D wrong: Oral fluids do not stop bleeding and may be unsafe."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"According to the principles of first aid, if the victim is in shock, the first aider should:","options":["Ignore it and treat only visible wounds","Explain and give console (reassurance) to the victim","Make the victim run","Withhold all communication"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If the injured person is in shock, the first-aid principle directs the rescuer to explain the situation and console/reassure the victim.  Why A wrong: Shock is life-threatening and cannot be ignored.  Why C wrong: Exertion worsens shock.  Why D wrong: Reassurance is part of managing shock."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"A first-aid principle for a victim who has a broken bone (fracture) and is unconscious is to:","options":["Move the person quickly to make them comfortable","If the person is unconscious, try to bring them to their senses","Immobilize the broken bone to keep it in position, and take the injured person to the nearest hospital or hospital as soon as possible","Give the person solid food"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: For a fracture, the principle is to immobilize the broken bone (e.g., with a splint) to keep it in position, then transfer the victim to the nearest hospital as soon as possible.  Why A wrong: Unnecessary movement can worsen the fracture; immobilization comes first.  Why B wrong: Bringing to senses applies to the unconscious victim point, not to managing the fracture itself.  Why D wrong: Food is contraindicated in an injured/unconscious victim."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"When checking the principles \"ABC's\" in first aid, the nurse first checks for and follows the ABC's. What do these letters stand for in the AHA-guided sequence (C-A-B in adults)?","options":["Airway, Breathing, Compression","Compression, Airway, Breathing","Assessment, Bandage, Care","Alert, Breathe, Console"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Per the source (AHA guidelines), the ABC of first aid is followed in the C-A-B sequence: Compression, Airway, Breathing.  Why A wrong: The order is C-A-B (compression first) in adult CPR per AHA, not airway first.  Why C, D wrong: These are not the ABC components of first aid/CPR."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"In the ABC of first aid, \"Compression\" is described as:","options":["Giving rescue breaths only","Push hard and fast on the centre of the victim's chest","Tilting the head back","Checking the pulse only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Compression means pushing hard and fast on the centre of the victim's chest to circulate blood.  Why A wrong: That describes Breathing/rescue breaths.  Why C wrong: Head tilt describes Airway.  Why D wrong: Pulse check alone is not chest compression."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"In the ABC of first aid, \"Airway\" involves which action?","options":["Pushing on the chest","Tilting the victim's head back and lifting the chin to open the airway","Giving abdominal thrusts","Applying a tourniquet"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Airway management involves tilting the victim's head back and lifting the chin to open the airway.  Why A wrong: That is compression.  Why C wrong: Abdominal thrusts are for choking, not the airway-opening maneuver described.  Why D wrong: A tourniquet controls bleeding, not the airway."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"In the ABC of first aid, \"Breathing\" refers to:","options":["Chest compressions","Giving mouth-to-mouth rescue breaths","Splinting a fracture","Cleaning a wound"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Breathing means giving mouth-to-mouth rescue breaths to the victim.  Why A, C, D wrong: These describe compression, fracture care, and wound care respectively, not rescue breathing."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"The type of knot used in first aid that is also known as a square knot and is useful while bandaging closed wounds is the:","options":["Slip knot","Reef (square) knot","Clove hitch","Figure-of-eight knot"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source notes that tying a reef knot (also known as a square knot) is useful while bandaging closed wounds.  Why A, C, D wrong: These are other knots not described for this bandaging purpose in the source."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"Arrange the correct steps for tying a square (reef) knot. (Select all that apply / all are correct steps)","options":["Cross the blue and red ends","Pass the red end through the blue loop","Pull the ends to tighten","The knot is complete","Cut and discard both ends without tightening"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The square (reef) knot is tied by crossing the blue and red ends, passing the red end through the blue loop, pulling the ends to tighten, and the knot is then complete.  Why E wrong: The ends must be tightened, not cut and discarded untied — that would leave no functional knot."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"Which of the following are the \"Golden Rules\" of first aid? (Select all that apply)","options":["Reach the accident site as soon as possible","Do not waste time asking unnecessary questions at the accident site","Ensure early detection of injury","Separate the object of injury from the patient (First Priority Action) — such as falling machinery, fire, electric wire, poisonous insects, or any other object","Always wait for permission before approaching the scene"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The golden rules include reaching the site promptly, not wasting time on unnecessary questions, early detection of injury, and separating the cause of injury from the patient as the first priority action.  Why E wrong: First aid emphasizes prompt action; the rules do not require waiting for permission before approaching."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"Which supplies should a first aid kit/preparedness include? (Select all that apply)","options":["Emergency telephone numbers for emergency medical services (EMS) — 1092/102/108","Sterile gauze pads (dressings) in small and large squares to place over wounds; disinfectants such as antiseptic wipes or soap (povidone, Dettol, etc.)","Medicines like pain killers (ibuprofen) and antibiotics","Roller bandages, adhesive tape and adhesive bandages in assorted sizes, scissors, tweezers, safety pins","Barrier devices such as a pocket mask or face shield"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: A first-aid kit should include EMS numbers (1092/102/108), sterile gauze in small/large squares, disinfectants (antiseptic wipes/soap, povidone, Dettol), pain killers (ibuprofen) and antibiotics, roller bandages, adhesive tape/bandages, scissors, tweezers, safety pins, and barrier devices such as a pocket mask or face shield.  (No incorrect options — all are listed items.)"},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"Which Indian emergency medical service (EMS) numbers should be available for first-aid response?","options":["100 and 101 only","1092, 102, and 108","011 and 1800","112 only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists EMS numbers 1092, 102, and 108 for emergency medical services.  Why A, C, D wrong: These do not match the EMS numbers given in the source."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"When the level of consciousness is being evaluated and care prioritized, which goals of first aid assessment are correct? (Select all that apply)","options":["To find out whether the patient is dead, alive, or unconscious","Prioritize first aid measures following the A-B-C method accordingly","To assess the Level of Consciousness (LOC) of the patient","To stop bleeding from the injury site (wound)","Maintaining patient records and incident details, and keeping the patient warm and comfortable"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Aims of assessment include determining whether the patient is dead/alive/unconscious, prioritizing care by the A-B-C method, assessing LOC, stopping bleeding, maintaining records/incident details, keeping the patient warm and comfortable, and reassuring the patient if conscious.  (No incorrect options — all are listed.)"},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"A victim is bleeding from a wound. What is the first-aid priority for injury and bleeding?","options":["Apply a tourniquet immediately to every wound","Apply direct pressure on the bleeding point (first priority), clean the wound with soap or lukewarm water, apply antiseptic, and apply a bandage","Pour antiseptic and leave the wound open","Give the victim food and water first"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For injury and bleeding, apply direct pressure on the bleeding point (first priority), clean the wound with soap/lukewarm water, apply antiseptic to the injury, apply a bandage, and refer to hospital for further treatment if needed.  Why A wrong: Direct pressure, not a routine tourniquet, is the first-line measure.  Why C wrong: Bleeding control and dressing are required, not leaving it open.  Why D wrong: Food/water is not the priority and may be unsafe."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"A person has a large laceration on the arm with active bleeding. Direct pressure is not controlling the bleeding. According to the source, what is the next step?","options":["Apply a tourniquet as a temporary control measure (could be life-saving)","Wait for additional help and do nothing","Remove the dressing repeatedly to check","Lower the arm below heart level"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: When a wound has a large laceration and direct pressure does not control the bleeding, a tourniquet may be applied as a temporary control measure and can be life-saving.  Why B wrong: Uncontrolled major hemorrhage cannot be left untreated.  Why C wrong: Repeated dressing removal disturbs clot formation.  Why D wrong: For bleeding control the part is usually elevated, not lowered; lowering does not control hemorrhage."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"Which statement about a fracture (broken bone) is correct?","options":["A fracture is a break in the continuity of a vein","A fracture is a break in the continuity of a bone","A fracture only occurs in elderly people","A fracture never needs immobilization"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A fracture is defined as a break in the continuity of a bone.  Why A wrong: It involves bone, not a vein.  Why C wrong: Fractures can occur at any age.  Why D wrong: Immobilization is a key first-aid step for fractures."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"Which steps are correct first aid for a suspected fracture? (Select all that apply)","options":["If the victim is unconscious, follow the ABC rule","If there is active bleeding, apply direct pressure to stop the bleeding","If the injured person is in shock, provide psychological support","Place the injured part in a plastic bag over ice (ice application)","Take the injured person to the nearest hospital as soon as possible"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Fracture first aid includes following ABC if unconscious, controlling active bleeding with direct pressure, providing psychological support if in shock, applying ice (in a plastic bag over the injured part), and transferring the victim to the nearest hospital as soon as possible.  (No incorrect options — all are listed steps.)"},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"When giving first aid to a victim of electric shock, what is the FIRST priority?","options":["Pour water over the victim immediately","Turn off all electric power at the source; if not possible, separate the victim from the source of electricity using any dry wood, plastic, or cardboard (First Priority)","Touch the victim with bare hands to pull them away","Begin chest compressions before separating from the source"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The first priority in electric shock is to turn off all power at the source; if not possible, separate the injured person from the source using a non-conductive dry object such as wood, plastic, or cardboard.  Why A wrong: Water conducts electricity and can endanger the rescuer.  Why C wrong: Touching the victim with bare hands while still connected risks electrocuting the rescuer.  Why D wrong: The victim must first be safely separated from the live source before CPR."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"A child sustains a thermal burn from a flame. Which steps are correct first aid for a burn injury? (Select all that apply)","options":["Remove the client from the burn site to a safe place (first priority)","Do not apply ice on the burn, because it may cause frostbite by peripheral vasoconstriction","If the burn is caused by a chemical, rinse the affected area with a large amount of running water; cool the burn area by submerging it in water for 10–30 minutes to reduce swelling and irritation","Remove cloths and jewellery near the burn area, but do not remove cloths that are stuck to the burn","Apply butter or grease to the burn and tightly wrap it"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Burn first aid: remove the client to safety first; do not apply ice (risk of frostbite from peripheral vasoconstriction); for chemical burns rinse with large amounts of running water and cool the area by submerging for 10–30 minutes; remove clothing/jewellery near the burn but not anything stuck to the burn.  Why E wrong: Butter/grease must not be applied; the source directs not to apply butter, grease, or ice to a burn, and tight wrapping is inappropriate."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"After cooling and cleaning a burn, the nurse should:","options":["Apply antibiotic cream and a new clean dressing daily","Leave the burn exposed and unwashed","Apply grease and leave it open","Re-burn the area to sterilize it"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: After cleaning, apply antibiotic cream and a new clean dressing every day to prevent infection.  Why B wrong: A clean dressing is needed; leaving it exposed risks infection.  Why C wrong: Grease is contraindicated.  Why D wrong: Re-burning is harmful and absurd."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"A burn covering less than 10% of total body surface area (TBSA) is classified as:","options":["Severe burn","Mild burn","Moderate burn","Full-thickness burn"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Less than 10% of total body surface area burned is considered a mild burn.  Why A wrong: Severe burn is more than 25% TBSA.  Why C wrong: Moderate burn is 10–15% TBSA.  Why D wrong: Full-thickness refers to depth, not the %TBSA classification given here."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"Per the TBSA classification in the source, a burn involving 10 to 15% of total body surface area is considered:","options":["Mild burn","Moderate burn","Severe burn","Superficial burn"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 10 to 15% of TBSA burned is classified as a moderate burn.  Why A wrong: Mild is less than 10% TBSA.  Why C wrong: Severe is more than 25% TBSA.  Why D wrong: Superficial refers to depth, not the % classification here."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"A burn involving more than 25% of total body surface area is classified as a:","options":["Mild burn","Moderate burn","Severe burn requiring IV fluid therapy","First-degree burn"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: More than 25% TBSA burned is considered a severe burn; severe burns require IV fluid therapy (resuscitation).  Why A, B wrong: These correspond to <10% and 10–15% respectively.  Why D wrong: First-degree describes depth, not this %TBSA severity class."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"According to the source, how many types of burns are there based on the cause/source of injury?","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The source states there are 5 types of burns according to the cause: thermal (chemical burn, electrical burn, radiation burn, and inhalation burn).  Why A, B, C wrong: The source explicitly lists five types."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"A burn caused by hot liquids such as boiling water is specifically called a:","options":["Flame burn","Scald burn","Chemical burn","Radiation burn"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A burn caused by hot liquids (e.g., boiling water) is termed a scald burn; thermal burns are caused by flame/hot objects, and scalds specifically by hot liquids.  Why A wrong: Flame burns are from open fire, not liquid.  Why C wrong: Chemical burns are from corrosive substances.  Why D wrong: Radiation burns are from radiant energy."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"A patient sustains a chemical burn to the eye. What is the immediate first-aid measure?","options":["Apply antibiotic ointment immediately","Immediate management of chemical burn injury in eyes is eye irrigation with tap water","Cover both eyes and wait","Rub the eye to remove the chemical"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For a chemical burn to the eyes, the immediate management is eye irrigation with tap water to dilute and wash out the chemical.  Why A wrong: Irrigation comes first; ointment is not the immediate measure.  Why C wrong: Covering without irrigation allows continued chemical damage.  Why D wrong: Rubbing spreads the chemical and worsens injury."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"Why does a venomous snake bite require immediate first aid, while many non-poisonous bites can be cured by cleaning the wound and applying medicine?","options":["Because all snake bites are equally dangerous","Because venom from a poisonous snake can cause serious systemic effects requiring immediate treatment","Because non-poisonous bites are always fatal","Because venom only causes a mild rash"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Many snakes are not poisonous and their bites are cured by cleaning and applying medicine, but a venomous snake requires immediate treatment because the venom causes serious effects.  Why A wrong: Bites are not all equally dangerous; venom status matters.  Why C wrong: Non-poisonous bites are generally not fatal.  Why D wrong: Venom causes serious systemic effects, not merely a mild rash."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"Which of the following help distinguish a venomous snake bite from a non-venomous snake bite? (Select all that apply)","options":["Fang marks — the presence of two puncture wounds indicates a bite by a poisonous snake","In a non-venomous snake bite, small puncture wounds are seen arranged in an arc (no distinct fang marks)","Neurological symptoms are seen with bites by a krait or king cobra","A venomous bite may cause coagulopathy (disturbance in coagulation) leading to abnormal/excessive local bleeding","Venomous and non-venomous bites are clinically indistinguishable"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Two distinct puncture (fang) marks suggest a poisonous bite; non-venomous bites show small puncture wounds arranged in an arc; krait/king cobra bites cause neurological symptoms; venomous bites may cause coagulopathy with abnormal local bleeding.  Why E wrong: The source provides features (fang marks, arc pattern, neuro symptoms, coagulopathy) that distinguish the two, so they are not indistinguishable."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"The treatment of choice (specific antidote) for a venomous snake bite is:","options":["Broad-spectrum antibiotics alone","Anti-Snake Venom (ASV)","Anti-rabies vaccine","Tetanus toxoid only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The treatment of choice for a venomous snake bite is Anti-Snake Venom (ASV).  Why A wrong: Antibiotics treat secondary infection, not the venom.  Why C wrong: Anti-rabies vaccine is for animal (dog/cat) bites, not snake venom.  Why D wrong: Tetanus toxoid is adjunctive, not the specific antivenom."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"What is the 20 WBCT test used for in snake bite management?","options":["To measure blood sugar","The 20-minute Whole Blood Clotting Test — to detect coagulopathy (whether blood is most likely able to clot) after a venomous bite","To measure blood pressure","To detect rabies"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The 20 WBCT (20-minute Whole Blood Clotting Test) assesses the blood's ability to clot, detecting coagulopathy caused by venom.  Why A, C, D wrong: It is a clotting test, not a glucose, BP, or rabies test."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"Which statements describe the correct method for performing the 20-minute Whole Blood Clotting Test (20 WBCT)? (Select all that apply)","options":["Place 2–3 ml of freshly sampled venous blood in a small, new, clean, dry glass vial/test tube","Leave the tube undisturbed for 20 minutes at ambient (room) temperature","Tip the vial once after 20 minutes","If the blood is still liquid (unclotted) and runs out, it indicates a viper bite and the patient should be given ASV","Shake the tube vigorously every 2 minutes"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Place 2–3 ml of freshly sampled venous blood in a small, clean, dry glass tube, leave it undisturbed for 20 minutes at ambient temperature, then tip the vial once; if the blood is still liquid (unclotted) and runs out it indicates a viper bite and the patient should be given ASV.  Why E wrong: The tube must be left undisturbed, not shaken — shaking invalidates the clotting test."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"In the 20 WBCT, no clot/undisturbed blood remaining liquid within 20 minutes is interpreted as:","options":["Negative result (normal clotting)","Positive result (coagulopathy present, indicating venomous/viper envenomation)","Test failure unrelated to venom","Indication of dehydration"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If the blood remains unclotted (no clot) after 20 minutes, it is a positive result indicating coagulopathy from venomous (viper) envenomation; a formed clot is a negative result.  Why A wrong: A clot forming would be the negative result.  Why C, D wrong: The result reflects venom-induced coagulopathy, not test failure or dehydration."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"Which are recognized symptoms of a snake bite? (Select all that apply)","options":["Pain or numbness at the site of pain (bite)","Swelling in the glands near the bite site / mark of the snake","Blurred vision, ptosis, and difficulty in breathing and talking","Going into a coma or unconsciousness","Immediate complete recovery with no symptoms"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Symptoms of snake bite include pain/numbness at the bite site, swelling near the bite, blurred vision, ptosis, difficulty breathing and talking, and going into coma/unconsciousness.  Why E wrong: Snake bite causes progressive symptoms; immediate full recovery without symptoms is not a recognized presentation."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"What is the FIRST step in first aid for a snake bite victim?","options":["Cut the wound and suck out the venom by mouth","Move the victim to a safe area free from the dangerous areas (in case snakes are around) as the first priority","Apply a tight tourniquet immediately","Give the victim food and a stimulant"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The first step is to move the victim to a safe area away from danger (in case more snakes are present) as the first priority.  Why A wrong: Do NOT cut the wound or suck out venom by mouth — this is harmful.  Why C wrong: A tight tourniquet is not recommended; only a tight bandage may be applied per guidelines, and tight tourniquets can cause harm.  Why D wrong: Food/stimulants are not the priority and may be unsafe."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"Which of the following are correct first-aid measures for a snake bite? (Select all that apply)","options":["Reassure the patient; keep them calm because movement causes the venom to spread more quickly through the body","Always keep the snake bite area below the level of the heart, and keep it immobile and not moving","Remove tight clothes and jewellery (rings) and any family members' rings near the swelling area","Immobilize the limb and keep it still, similar to a fractured limb","Encourage the victim to walk briskly to the hospital"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Reassure and keep the victim calm (movement spreads venom); keep the bite area below heart level and immobile; remove tight clothing and rings/jewellery near the swelling; and immobilize the limb like a fractured limb.  Why E wrong: Movement spreads venom faster; the victim should be kept still and transported, not made to walk briskly."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"According to guidelines for snake bite, regarding a tourniquet/tight bandage, the nurse should:","options":["Always apply a tight tourniquet","NOT apply a tourniquet/tight bandage (tourniquet is NOT recommended)","Apply a tourniquet only on the neck","Apply electrical therapy and cryotherapy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Per the source, a tourniquet/tight bandage is NOT recommended (CAN NOT be applied) for snake bites.  Why A, C wrong: Tourniquets are contraindicated, never on the neck.  Why D wrong: The source explicitly states do NOT apply electrical therapy; this is contraindicated."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"Which actions are explicitly contraindicated (\"Do NOT\") in snake bite first aid? (Select all that apply)","options":["Do not wash, rub, incise, tattoo, or apply vigorous cleaning/massage at the bite site","Do not apply ice or cold packs (do not put ice around the bite area)","Do not try to suck out the venom by mouth from the bite","Do not apply electrical (electric) therapy or cryotherapy","Always cut the wound to drain the venom"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source directs do NOT wash/rub/incise/tattoo or vigorously clean/massage the bite, do NOT apply ice/cold packs, do NOT suck out the venom by mouth, and do NOT apply electrical therapy or cryotherapy.  Why E wrong: Cutting the wound is explicitly contraindicated and harmful."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"The first-aid mnemonic \"CARRY\" for snake bite advises NOT to do which of the following? (Select all that apply)","options":["NO Tourniquet","NO Cutting","NO Electrical therapy","NO pressure immobilisation, nitric oxide donor (nitrogen ointment/nitrate spray)","NO reassurance of the patient"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The CARRY mnemonic stands for the \"NO\" actions: No Tourniquet, No Cutting, No Electrocautery/electrical therapy, and No pressure immobilisation/nitric oxide donor (nitrogen ointment, nitrate spray) — i.e., things NOT to do.  Why E wrong: Reassurance is recommended (per RIGHT mnemonic: R = Reassurance), not prohibited."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"In the snake bite mnemonic \"R.I.G.H.T,\" what does each letter represent? (Select all that apply)","options":["R = Reassure the patient","I = Immobilise the limb (similar to a fractured limb, do not tie tightly over the bite)","G = Get to hospital immediately","H+T = Tell any systemic symptoms that manifest, and get to the hospital","R = Run to spread the venom faster"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: R = Reassure the patient; I = Immobilise the limb (like a fractured limb); G = Get to hospital immediately; H+T = Tell the doctor of any systemic symptoms that manifest and reach the hospital.  Why E wrong: Running/movement spreads venom faster and is contraindicated; R stands for Reassure, not run."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"Which statements correctly describe first aid for a dog or cat bite? (Select all that apply)","options":["Most animal bites are from dogs; other cat bites (second priority) are monkey bites, etc.","The risk of infection from a cat bite is much higher (second) than from a dog bite","Wash the animal's bitten area thoroughly with soap in running tap water for 10 to 15 minutes","Do not rub too much while washing","Apply antiseptic, and refer to hospital to start the Anti-Rabies Vaccine (ARV) as per guidelines (a tetanus injection may also be needed)"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Most animal bites are from dogs (with cat/monkey bites also occurring); the infection risk from a cat bite is higher than from a dog bite; the bitten area should be washed thoroughly with soap under running tap water for 10–15 minutes without excessive rubbing; antiseptic is applied; and the patient is referred to hospital to start the Anti-Rabies Vaccine (ARV) per guidelines.  (No incorrect options — all are listed measures.)"},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"A patient is bitten by a stray dog on the leg. After washing the wound with soap and running water for 10–15 minutes and applying antiseptic, what is the most important next step?","options":["Suture the wound tightly and discharge the patient","Refer the patient to hospital immediately to start the Anti-Rabies Vaccine (ARV) as per guidelines","Apply a tourniquet above the bite","Give only oral antibiotics and no vaccine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After wound washing and antiseptic application, the patient must be referred to hospital immediately to begin the Anti-Rabies Vaccine (ARV) per guidelines, as rabies prophylaxis is essential.  Why A wrong: Tight suturing of animal bites is generally avoided, and ARV is the priority.  Why C wrong: A tourniquet is not indicated for a dog bite.  Why D wrong: ARV (rabies prophylaxis) is required, not antibiotics alone."},{"section":"Batch 060 · Hospice & palliative care, physical care of the dying patient, advance…","question":"A snake bite victim is conscious. Which transport and positioning measures are appropriate? (Select all that apply)","options":["If the victim is conscious, follow the ABC rule as needed","Get the patient to the hospital as soon as possible","In a case of viral/dog bite-type emergency... keep the bite area below the heart and immobile","Keep the patient calm and reassured to slow venom spread","Make the patient exercise the limb to \"pump out\" the venom"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: For a conscious snake bite victim, follow the ABC rule, transport to hospital as soon as possible, keep the bite area below heart level and immobile, and keep the patient calm/reassured to slow venom spread.  Why E wrong: Exercising the limb increases circulation and spreads venom faster; immobilization is required."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"What is the first action in first aid for a person on fire?","options":["Pour water immediately","Remove/separate the victim from the site of fire to a safe place","Apply burn ointment","Call the fire department before doing anything else"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: First aid in fire begins with removing/separating the victim from the site of fire to a safe place to stop ongoing injury.  Why A wrong: Water may be used later but is not the documented first step.  Why C wrong: Ointment is not an immediate field priority.  Why D wrong: Notifying the fire department follows in priority order, not before moving the victim to safety."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"Which mnemonic should be followed in strict priority order for fire safety?","options":["RICE","PASS","RACE","ABCDE"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The RACE mnemonic should be followed in strict priority order for fire response.  Why B wrong: PASS is the mnemonic for using a fire extinguisher, not the overall fire-response sequence.  Why A wrong: RICE is for soft-tissue injuries (rest, ice, compression, elevation).  Why D wrong: ABCDE is a trauma/resuscitation assessment sequence, not the fire mnemonic."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"A nurse alerts the fire department and reports it quickly and loudly during a fire. Which RACE step does this represent?","options":["Rescue","Alert/Alarm","Confine","Extinguish"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Alerting and reporting to the fire department quickly and loudly is the \"Alert/Alarm\" component of RACE.  Why A wrong: Rescue refers to removing the victim from the fire site.  Why C wrong: Confine refers to closing doors and windows to limit the fire.  Why D wrong: Extinguish refers to using the fire extinguisher."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"According to RACE, what should be done to doors and windows during a fire?","options":["Open them widely to ventilate smoke","Close them to keep fire confined","Leave them as they are","Break them for easy escape"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Doors and windows should be closed to keep the fire confined (\"Confine\" step).  Why A wrong: Opening windows feeds the fire with oxygen and spreads it.  Why C wrong: Active confinement is required, not inaction.  Why D wrong: Breaking windows is not part of the confinement step."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"Which mnemonic guides the correct use of a fire extinguisher?","options":["RACE","PASS","FAST","RICE"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The fire extinguisher is operated using the PASS mnemonic.  Why A wrong: RACE is the overall fire-response sequence.  Why C wrong: FAST is a stroke-recognition mnemonic.  Why D wrong: RICE is for soft-tissue injuries."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"In the PASS mnemonic for a fire extinguisher, what does the first \"P\" stand for?","options":["Push the handle","Pull the pin","Pour water","Position the nozzle"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: P → Pull the pin is the first step of the PASS mnemonic.  Why A wrong: Pushing the handle is \"S\" (squeeze handle), not P.  Why C wrong: Pouring water is not part of extinguisher operation.  Why D wrong: Positioning the nozzle corresponds to aiming, the \"A\" step."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"In the PASS mnemonic, what does \"A\" represent?","options":["Alert the department","Aim at base of fire","Apply pressure","Activate alarm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A → Aim at base of fire directs the extinguishing agent where it is effective.  Why A wrong: Alerting belongs to the RACE sequence, not PASS.  Why C wrong: Apply pressure is unrelated to extinguisher use.  Why D wrong: Activating an alarm is part of RACE."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"In the PASS mnemonic, what does the \"S\" steps represent?","options":["Stop and Stay","Squeeze the handle and Sweep side to side","Spray and Soak","Shout and Signal"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: S → Squeeze handle and S → Sweep side to side are the final PASS steps.  Why A wrong: Stop/Stay is not part of PASS.  Why C wrong: Spray/Soak are not the listed terms.  Why D wrong: Shout/Signal are not PASS components."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"During first aid in a heart attack, why should the victim NOT be left alone?","options":["To prevent boredom","To keep the victim calm and to keep them comfortable, and to monitor for deterioration","To collect personal belongings","To allow the victim to walk around"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The patient should not be left alone; remain to reassure, keep comfortable, and monitor for changes during a heart attack.  Why A wrong: It is for safety/monitoring, not entertainment.  Why C wrong: Collecting belongings is irrelevant to safety.  Why D wrong: The victim should remain still, not walk around."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"What position and clothing care are advised in first aid for a suspected heart attack?","options":["Lie flat with tight clothing","Sit the patient down, rest, and loosen tight clothing","Stand and walk slowly","Lie prone with head down"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The patient should be rested, sat down, and have tight/constrictive clothing loosened.  Why A wrong: Tight clothing should be loosened, not kept.  Why C wrong: Exertion such as walking increases cardiac demand.  Why D wrong: A prone head-down position is not indicated."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"In a conscious heart-attack victim, what may be given if available?","options":["A full glass of milk","Sub-lingual nitroglycerine tablet (if available)","A heavy meal","Aspirin only with food"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If the victim is conscious and it is available, a sub-lingual nitroglycerine tablet may be given.  Why A wrong: Milk is not an indicated emergency intervention.  Why C wrong: Food/heavy meals are not given in suspected MI.  Why D wrong: The source notes sub-lingual nitroglycerine, not aspirin with food."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"During a heart attack, when should CPR be started?","options":["Immediately upon arrival regardless of pulse","If the patient is not breathing for 3 minutes, then start CPR","Only after 30 minutes of observation","Only if the victim requests it"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states CPR should be started if the patient is not breathing within 3 minutes.  Why A wrong: CPR is indicated for absence of breathing, not given to all victims.  Why C wrong: Delaying CPR 30 minutes would be fatal.  Why D wrong: A non-breathing victim cannot request care."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"What should be done with food and drink for a conscious heart-attack victim other than the noted medication?","options":["Give large amounts of water","Do not give anything to eat or drink (NPO) except the indicated medication","Give coffee to stimulate the heart","Give a salty snack"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The victim should be kept NPO (nothing by mouth) except for the indicated emergency medication.  Why A wrong: Fluids are withheld.  Why C wrong: Stimulants increase cardiac workload.  Why D wrong: Salt/food is not given."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"Anginal pain in the chest is caused by what underlying mechanism?","options":["Increased blood flow to the heart","Reduced blood flow to the heart muscle due to progressive constriction of the coronary arteries","Inflammation of the lungs","Excess oxygen delivery to the myocardium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Angina is chest pain caused by reduced blood flow to the heart muscle from progressive constriction of the coronary arteries leading to cardiac pain across the chest.  Why A wrong: Increased flow would relieve, not cause, ischemic pain.  Why C wrong: Lung inflammation is not the cause of angina.  Why D wrong: Angina results from oxygen deficit, not excess."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"Myocardial infarction (MI/heart attack) occurs when blood flow to the heart is affected in what way?","options":["Temporarily increased","Decreased or stops to a coronary artery of the heart, causing myocardial (heart) muscle damage","Redirected to the lungs","Unchanged but slower"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: MI occurs when blood flow decreases or stops to a coronary artery of the heart, causing irreversible myocardial muscle damage.  Why A wrong: Increased flow does not cause infarction.  Why C wrong: Flow is not redirected to the lungs.  Why D wrong: Infarction involves cessation of flow, not merely slowing."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"Where is the chest pain/discomfort of a myocardial infarction most commonly felt?","options":["Only in the lower abdomen","Most common in the chest as pain or discomfort, and may radiate to shoulder, arm, back, neck, or jaw","Only in the feet","Only at the back of the knee"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: MI chest pain/discomfort is felt most commonly in the chest and may radiate to the shoulder, arm, back, neck, or jaw.  Why A wrong: The lower abdomen is not the typical MI pain location.  Why C/D wrong: Feet and back of knee are not MI pain sites."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"What is the correct first action for first aid in nose bleeding (epistaxis)?","options":["Tilt the head backward","Have the patient sit in an upright position with the head slightly bent forward","Lie the patient flat","Pack the nose with cotton immediately"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The patient should sit upright with the head slightly bent forward to prevent blood from flowing backward and being swallowed.  Why A wrong: Tilting the head backward causes blood to run down the throat.  Why C wrong: Lying flat promotes posterior blood flow.  Why D wrong: Direct pressure on the soft part of the nostrils is first, before packing."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"Where and how should pressure be applied to stop nose bleeding?","options":["Over the bony bridge of the nose","Gently apply pressure on the soft part of the nostrils below the nose bridge, and continue applying until the bleeding stops","On the forehead only","On the back of the neck"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pressure is applied gently to the soft part of the nostrils below the nose bridge, continued until bleeding stops.  Why A wrong: Pressure on the bony bridge does not compress the bleeding vessels.  Why C/D wrong: Forehead and neck pressure do not control epistaxis."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"After the bleeding has stopped in epistaxis, the patient should be instructed to:","options":["Blow the nose forcefully to clear clots","Refrain from blowing the nose so that clots are not dislodged","Tilt the head back for 10 minutes","Drink hot fluids immediately"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The patient is instructed not to blow the nose so that the clot/bleeding is not dislodged or restarted.  Why A wrong: Blowing the nose dislodges the clot and may restart bleeding.  Why C wrong: Tilting back is contraindicated.  Why D wrong: Hot fluids are not part of the instruction."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"If nose bleeding persists despite first aid measures, what should be done?","options":["Continue the same measures indefinitely","Refer the victim for advanced management","Apply ice to the abdomen","Lay the patient flat and wait"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If bleeding persists, the victim should be referred for advanced management.  Why A wrong: Continuing failed measures delays needed care.  Why C wrong: Abdominal ice is irrelevant.  Why D wrong: Lying flat is contraindicated and not the correct escalation."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"When a victim turns blue or unconscious (cyanosis) during a choking/aspiration episode, the head should be turned to one side primarily to:","options":["Improve hearing","Drain the mucus and prevent aspiration","Reduce neck strain","Keep the airway closed"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Turning the head to one side drains mucus and prevents aspiration.  Why A wrong: Hearing is irrelevant.  Why C wrong: The purpose is airway protection, not comfort.  Why D wrong: The goal is to keep the airway clear, not closed."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"What should be placed under the victim's head when turning it to the side to drain mucus?","options":["A pillow elevating it high","A cushion or towel under the head","Nothing at all","A hard board"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A cushion or towel should be placed under the victim's head.  Why A wrong: High elevation is not specified.  Why C wrong: Support is recommended.  Why D wrong: A hard board is not indicated for this support."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"When a seizure is over, which of the following should be done?","options":["Restrain the victim's limbs firmly","Feel the victim's pulse","Place objects in the victim's mouth","Give the victim food immediately"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After the seizure is over, feel the victim's pulse and position the victim on one side to reduce the chance of choking on vomit.  Why A wrong: Restraint can cause injury and is contraindicated.  Why C wrong: Never place anything in the mouth.  Why D wrong: Food is not given immediately post-seizure."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"After a seizure, what position should the victim be placed in and why?","options":["Supine flat, to rest","On one side, to reduce the chance of choking on vomit","Prone, to protect the chest","Sitting upright, to aid breathing"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The victim should be positioned on one side (recovery position) to reduce choking on vomit.  Why A wrong: Supine flat increases aspiration risk in a post-ictal patient.  Why C wrong: Prone is not indicated.  Why D wrong: A post-ictal patient cannot reliably sit upright; side-lying protects the airway."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"During the post-seizure period, blankets are used to:","options":["Restrain the victim","Stay with the victim until full help arrives and keep them warm/comfortable","Cover the mouth","Apply heavy pressure to the chest"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Stay with the victim until help arrives and cover them with blankets (for warmth/comfort and safety).  Why A wrong: Restraint is contraindicated.  Why C wrong: Never cover or place items in the mouth.  Why D wrong: Chest pressure is not indicated post-seizure."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"Why should you NOT attempt to restrain a person having a seizure or try to open their mouth?","options":["It wastes time","It may cause injury to the person or the rescuer","It is illegal","It interrupts the seizure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Do not restrain the victim or open the mouth/put anything in it, as this may cause injury to the person or rescuer.  Why A wrong: The concern is injury, not lost time.  Why C wrong: Legality is not the documented reason.  Why D wrong: Restraint does not stop a seizure."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"Which of the following is NOT appropriate when caring for a person having a seizure?","options":["Clear the area around the person","Immediately call 911/EMS","Protect the person from injury","Place an object between the teeth to prevent tongue biting"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Placing an object between the teeth is inappropriate; do not open the mouth or put anything in it, as this can cause injury.  Why A wrong: Clearing the area is appropriate to prevent injury.  Why B wrong: Calling EMS immediately is appropriate.  Why C wrong: Protecting from injury is a core seizure first-aid action."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"In first aid for a victim in shock, the first action is to:","options":["Give the victim water","Activate the emergency response system (call 911/EMS)","Sit the victim upright","Apply a tourniquet"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: When confronted with a victim in shock, first activate the emergency response system by calling 911/EMS.  Why A wrong: A shock victim is kept NPO; fluids by mouth are not given.  Why C wrong: The legs are elevated, not the victim sat upright.  Why D wrong: A tourniquet is for severe bleeding, not a generic shock step."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"How should a victim in shock be positioned?","options":["Sitting upright","Lie the victim down and elevate their legs","Prone with head down","Standing with support"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Help the victim lie down and elevate their legs to promote venous return.  Why A wrong: Sitting upright reduces cerebral perfusion in shock.  Why C/D wrong: Prone and standing positions are not indicated for shock."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"What temperature-control measure is recommended for a shock victim?","options":["Apply cold packs to the chest","Cover the victim with blankets to keep them warm","Expose the victim to cold air","Place ice on the forehead"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Cover the victim with blankets to keep them warm.  Why A/C/D wrong: Cooling measures worsen shock; the victim must be kept warm."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"A rescuer should be prepared to perform which intervention for a victim in shock if needed?","options":["Suturing","CPR","IV cannulation","Intubation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The rescuer should be prepared to perform CPR and stay with the victim until help arrives.  Why A/C/D wrong: Suturing, IV cannulation, and intubation are advanced/clinical procedures not part of basic shock first aid."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"Which is a sign/symptom of shock?","options":["Flushed, warm, dry skin","Pale, gray, or bluish skin (cool/clammy)","Slow, deep breathing","Bradycardia with high blood pressure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Signs of shock include skin that is pale, gray, or bluish.  Why A wrong: Shock typically produces cool, pale, clammy skin, not flushed warm dry skin.  Why C wrong: Rapid (not slow deep) breathing is characteristic.  Why D wrong: Shock is associated with tachycardia and hypotension."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"Which of the following is a listed sign/symptom of shock? (Select all that apply)","options":["Dizziness and lightheadedness","Nausea or vomiting","Behavior change such as agitation, confusion, or unresponsiveness","Cool and clammy skin","Bright red flushed cheeks with calm behavior"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Signs of shock include dizziness/lightheadedness, nausea or vomiting, behavioral change (agitation, confusion, unresponsiveness), and cool and clammy skin.  Why E wrong: Bright red flushed cheeks with calm behavior is not a feature of shock; shock produces pale/cool skin and altered mentation."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"A person who experienced significant blood loss becomes pale, has cool/clammy skin, and is becoming confused. What is the MOST likely type of shock?","options":["Seizure","Hypovolemic shock (shock from blood loss)","Stroke","Low blood sugar"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Significant blood loss with pallor, cool/clammy skin, and confusion indicates shock (hypovolemic) due to blood loss; other causes include infection, severe allergic reactions, severe dehydration, and heart problems.  Why A wrong: A seizure presents with convulsive activity, not blood-loss shock.  Why C wrong: Stroke presents with focal neurologic deficits, not described by blood loss.  Why D wrong: Low blood sugar is a metabolic cause, but the scenario specifies significant blood loss."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"What is the first step in first aid for poisoning?","options":["Induce vomiting immediately","Find out the type of poison ingested","Give the victim milk","Apply CPR regardless of breathing"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: First aid for poisoning begins with finding out the type of poison ingested.  Why A wrong: Vomiting should NOT be induced if certain corrosive poisons were ingested.  Why C wrong: Milk is not a universal first step.  Why D wrong: CPR is started only if the victim is not breathing/responding."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"If the victim of poisoning is not breathing, what should be done?","options":["Wait for spontaneous recovery","Call 911 and begin appropriate resuscitation","Give water by mouth","Place in a sitting position only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If the person is not breathing, call 911 (activate emergency services) for resuscitation.  Why A wrong: Waiting is dangerous for a non-breathing victim.  Why C wrong: Fluids by mouth are unsafe in an unresponsive/non-breathing victim.  Why D wrong: Positioning alone does not address absent breathing."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"If an inhaled poison was the source, what is the priority action?","options":["Keep the victim in the contaminated area for observation","Move the person to fresh air to avoid further injury, taking care not to expose oneself","Give activated charcoal immediately","Induce vomiting"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For inhaled poison, move the person out of the source to fresh air to avoid further injury, opening doors and windows.  Why A wrong: Remaining in the contaminated area causes ongoing exposure.  Why C wrong: Charcoal is not relevant to inhaled gas exposure.  Why D wrong: Vomiting is not induced for inhaled poisons."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"When poison has contacted the skin, what is the appropriate first aid?","options":["Cover the skin with an occlusive dressing","Take off the clothing touching the poison and rinse the skin with running water for 15–20 minutes","Apply oil-based cream","Leave the area undisturbed"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For poison on the skin, take off any clothing the poison touched and rinse the skin with running water for 15–20 minutes.  Why A wrong: Occlusion traps the poison against the skin.  Why C wrong: Oil-based creams are not indicated.  Why D wrong: The contaminated area must be actively irrigated."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"When poison is in the eyes, the eyes should be rinsed with running water for how long?","options":["1 to 2 minutes","15 to 20 minutes","30 to 45 minutes","60 minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If poison is in the eyes, rinse the eyes with running water for 15 to 20 minutes.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies 15–20 minutes of irrigation."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"Why must you NOT use activated charcoal unless instructed by a poison helpline or emergency provider?","options":["It is too expensive","Its use must be directed by professional advice because it is not appropriate for all poisons","It causes constipation","It is only for children"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Do not use activated charcoal unless told to do so by a poison helpline or emergency provider, since it is contraindicated for certain poisons.  Why A wrong: Cost is not the reason.  Why C wrong: Side effects are not the stated reason for restriction.  Why D wrong: It is not restricted by age in the source."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"When the poison is a strong acid, alkali, or chemical, why may vomiting be especially dangerous?","options":["It is painless and ineffective","It may cause burning of the GI tract and further injury","It dilutes the poison too much","It is always harmless"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Never induce vomiting if the poison is acid, alkali, or chemical, as vomiting may cause burning of the GI tract and further injury.  Why A wrong: The danger is corrosive injury, not ineffectiveness.  Why C wrong: Dilution is not the concern.  Why D wrong: It is explicitly dangerous, not harmless."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"What is the priority when calling a poison control center for a poisoned victim?","options":["Ask for billing information","Call the poison control center and follow the instructions given","Request medication delivery","Ask for transport only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Call the poison control center and follow the instructions provided.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the documented purpose; following expert instructions is the key action."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"If the victim of poisoning is unconscious or has breathing difficulty, what should be done?","options":["Give oral fluids","Start CPR (make him/her sit up, breathe slowly and deeply) and provide symptomatic management as appropriate","Induce vomiting","Leave alone and observe"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If the person is unconscious or has breathing difficulty, begin CPR/resuscitation and provide symptomatic management.  Why A wrong: Oral fluids are unsafe in an unconscious victim.  Why C wrong: Vomiting is not induced, especially with corrosives.  Why D wrong: An unconscious/poisoned victim requires active intervention."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"In first aid for penetrating injuries to the eye, an object protruding from the eye should be:","options":["Removed quickly to prevent infection","Never removed, because it may hold ocular structures in place","Pushed deeper for stability","Cleaned with cloth"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Never remove the object because it may be holding ocular structures in place (AIIMS 2018).  Why A wrong: Removal can cause loss of intraocular contents.  Why C wrong: Pushing deeper worsens injury.  Why D wrong: The penetrating object should not be disturbed."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"When stabilizing a penetrating eye object, the object should be covered with:","options":["A tight occlusive patch","A clean cloth or cup, applied without pressure to the eye","Wet cotton soaked in saline","Nothing, to allow drainage"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Cover the eye with a clean cloth or a cup so that no pressure is applied to the penetrating object.  Why A wrong: A tight patch applies dangerous pressure.  Why C wrong: Saline cotton can apply pressure and disturb the object.  Why D wrong: The eye should be protected, not left open."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"A patient has a penetrating eye injury. To reduce ocular movement and worsening, the rescuer should:","options":["Cover only the injured eye and let the patient look around","Cover both eyes (since eyes move together), keep the patient still, and not allow them to lie flat (positions that may move the object)","Encourage rapid blinking","Apply firm pressure to both eyes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Cover both eyes because the eyes move together; do not allow the victim to lie flat or take positions that may move the object; X-ray and CT are usually required.  Why A wrong: Covering only one eye still allows movement of the injured eye via the consensual movement of the uncovered eye.  Why C wrong: Blinking increases ocular movement.  Why D wrong: Pressure is contraindicated in penetrating eye injury."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"In first aid for chemical burns of the eye, after assessing the chemical, the priority intervention is to:","options":["Patch the eye dry","Irrigate the eye with normal saline (NS) for 10 minutes at the emergency department, flushing from the inner toward the outer (lateral) canthus","Apply antibiotic ointment first","Wait for an ophthalmologist before doing anything"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The eye is irrigated with normal saline (NS) for 10 minutes at the emergency department, flushing the chemical across the cornea and toward the lateral canthus.  Why A wrong: Dry patching does not remove the chemical.  Why C wrong: Irrigation precedes other treatments; ointment is not the first step.  Why D wrong: Immediate irrigation is required and should not be delayed."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"In the direction of flushing for an eye chemical burn, the irrigation should move:","options":["From the lateral (outer) toward the inner canthus","Across the cornea and toward the lateral (outer) canthus (inner to outer)","From above downward only","In a circular sweeping motion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The solution is directed across the cornea and toward the lateral canthus (inner to outer) so the chemical is not flushed into the other eye/tear duct.  Why A wrong: Flushing toward the inner canthus risks contaminating the nasolacrimal duct and the other eye.  Why C/D wrong: Downward-only and circular motions are not the documented technique."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"After irrigating a chemical eye burn, the nurse should prepare the patient for:","options":["Discharge home","Visual acuity assessment","Immediate eye surgery without further checks","A hearing test"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After irrigation, prepare the patient for visual acuity assessment and cover the eye with patches as per protocol.  Why A wrong: Discharge without assessment is unsafe.  Why C wrong: Surgery is not the immediate next routine step.  Why D wrong: A hearing test is unrelated to eye injury."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"When caring for a victim experiencing a seizure on the ground, the first priority is to:","options":["Hold the victim down firmly","Help the victim to the ground if needed and clear the area around them to prevent injury","Place a spoon in the mouth","Lift the victim onto a bed"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For a seizing victim, help them to the ground and clear the area around the victim to prevent injury.  Why A wrong: Restraint may cause injury.  Why C wrong: Never place anything in the mouth.  Why D wrong: Moving/lifting during an active seizure risks injury."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"A bandage is best defined as:","options":["A piece of metal used for traction","A piece of soft and absorbent material that holds dressings in place, helps immobilize a body part, supports an injury, eliminates cavities, and prevents hemorrhage","A medicated cream","A plaster cast"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A bandage is a piece of soft, absorbent material that holds dressings in place, immobilizes a body part, supports an injury, eliminates cavities, and prevents hemorrhage.  Why A wrong: It is soft material, not metal traction equipment.  Why C wrong: A cream is medication, not a bandage.  Why D wrong: A plaster cast is a rigid immobilization device, not a bandage."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"A bandage is a piece of soft, absorbent material that can be used to:","options":["Hold a dressing in place over a wound, support a splint, and immobilize a wounded body part","Replace surgical sutures","Provide nutrition to a wound","Sterilize the skin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A bandage can hold a dressing over a wound, support a splint, and immobilize a wounded body part; it can also compress a soft-tissue injury.  Why B wrong: Bandages do not replace sutures.  Why C wrong: Bandages do not provide nutrition.  Why D wrong: Bandages do not sterilize skin."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"Which term describes the layer that comes in contact with the wound versus the bandage?","options":["The \"dressing\" contacts the wound and the \"bandage\" holds the dressing in place","Both terms mean the same thing","The \"bandage\" contacts the wound and the \"dressing\" holds it","Neither contacts the wound"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The terms \"bandage\" and \"dressing\" are different; the dressing is the primary layer that goes in contact with the wound, and the bandage holds dressings in place.  Why B wrong: The terms are explicitly different.  Why C wrong: The roles are reversed in this option.  Why D wrong: The dressing does contact the wound."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"Which of the following is a stated use of a bandage? (Select all that apply)","options":["To provide support to a part that is injured, sprained, or dislocated","To prevent and control hemorrhage","To restrict movement / immobilize a fracture or dislocation","To maintain pressure (e.g., elastic bandages to improve venous return)","To correct deformity"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: All listed are documented uses of bandages — providing support, preventing/controlling hemorrhage, restricting movement/immobilizing, maintaining pressure to improve venous return, and correcting deformity (also preventing contamination by holding dressings).  Why none wrong: Each option is explicitly listed as a use of bandages."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"Which type of bandage is the most commonly used?","options":["Tubular bandage","Roller bandage","Triangular bandage","Crepe bandage"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Roller bandages are the most commonly used type of bandage.  Why A wrong: Tubular bandages are used for forearm dressings.  Why C wrong: Triangular bandages are used for support to genitals (e.g., hydrocele) and as slings.  Why D wrong: Crepe bandage is noted for its stretching quality but is not labeled the most common."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"A tubular bandage is primarily used for the:","options":["Head","Forearm dressing","Genitals","Trunk"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Tubular bandages are used for forearm dressings.  Why A wrong: Head dressings use other bandage types.  Why C wrong: Triangular bandages are used for the genitals (e.g., hydrocele).  Why D wrong: The trunk is not the listed use of tubular bandages."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"A triangular bandage is used for:","options":["Forearm dressing","Support to genitals (e.g., hydrocele) and as a sling","Only for the head","Pressure to improve venous return"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Triangular bandages are used to provide support to the genitals, e.g., for hydrocele (and as slings).  Why A wrong: Forearm dressings use tubular bandages.  Why C wrong: Its use is not limited to the head.  Why D wrong: Pressure for venous return uses elastic bandages."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"Which type of bandage is noted for having a stretching quality?","options":["Roller bandage","Crepe bandage","Triangular bandage","Adhesive bandage"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A crepe bandage has a stretching quality.  Why A wrong: Roller bandage is described as most commonly used, not specifically for stretch.  Why C wrong: Triangular bandage is for support/sling.  Why D wrong: Adhesive bandage is a separate type without the noted stretch quality."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"Which bandaging technique is used for the arms, legs, chest, and abdomen?","options":["Spiral bandaging","Circular bandaging","Figure-of-eight bandaging","Recurrent bandaging"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Circular bandaging is used for arms, legs, chest, and abdomen.  Why A wrong: Spiral bandaging is used on cylindrical body parts such as the thigh and forearm.  Why C wrong: Figure-of-eight is used to bind a flexing joint or body part below/above the joint.  Why D wrong: Recurrent bandaging is used on blunt body parts (e.g., stump)."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"Spiral bandaging is most appropriate for which body parts?","options":["Cylindrical body parts such as the thigh and forearm","The fingers only","Stumps after amputation","The head only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Spiral bandaging is used on cylindrical body parts, e.g., the thigh and forearm.  Why B wrong: Fingers are bandaged with specific small-width bandages, not specifically spiral.  Why C wrong: Stumps after amputation use recurrent bandaging.  Why D wrong: The head is not the spiral technique's listed application."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"A figure-of-eight bandage is specifically used to:","options":["Bind a flexing joint and the body part above and below the joint","Cover a stump after amputation","Form a V-shape on cylindrical body parts","Cover the abdomen only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Figure-of-eight bandaging is used to bind a flexing joint and the body part above and below the joint.  Why B wrong: Covering a stump after amputation is recurrent bandaging.  Why C wrong: The reverse spiral forms a V-shape.  Why D wrong: The abdomen is covered by circular bandaging."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"Recurrent bandaging is used to cover:","options":["The thigh","Blunt body parts, such as a stump after amputation","A flexing joint","The chest"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Recurrent bandaging is used on blunt body parts, e.g., a stump after amputation.  Why A wrong: The thigh uses spiral bandaging.  Why C wrong: A flexing joint uses figure-of-eight.  Why D wrong: The chest uses circular bandaging."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"A reverse spiral bandage is characterized by:","options":["A formed V-shape, used on cylindrical body parts (e.g., thigh, forearm)","Covering a stump","Only circular turns","Binding two separate limbs"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A reverse spiral bandage forms a V-shape and is used on cylindrical body parts (e.g., thigh, forearm).  Why B wrong: Covering a stump is recurrent bandaging.  Why C wrong: A reverse spiral involves reversing turns, not only circular turns.  Why D wrong: It does not bind two separate limbs."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"According to the bandage-size table, what is the recommended width and length of a bandage for the HEAD?","options":["5 cm (2 inches) width, 5 m length","5 cm (2 inches) width, 6 m length","10–15 cm width, 6–8 m length","6–8 cm width, 4 m length"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For the head, the bandage size is 5 cm (2 inches) wide and 6 m long.  Why A wrong: 5 cm × 5 m is the size for the hand.  Why C wrong: 10–15 cm × 6–8 m is the size for the trunk.  Why D wrong: 6–8 cm × 4 m is the size for the leg."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"Per the bandage-size table, the recommended size for the TRUNK is:","options":["5 cm × 6 m","10–15 cm width, 6–8 m length","6 cm × 5 m","2.5 cm × 3 m"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For the trunk, the bandage is 10–15 cm wide and 6–8 m long.  Why A wrong: 5 cm × 6 m is for the head.  Why C wrong: 6 cm × 5 m is not the trunk size.  Why D wrong: 2.5 cm (1 inch) × 3 m corresponds to fingers/toes."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"According to the bandage-size table, the recommended size for the LEG is:","options":["6–8 cm width, 4 m length","5 cm × 6 m","2.5 cm × 3 m","10–15 cm × 6–8 m"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For the leg, the bandage is 6–8 cm wide and 4 m long.  Why B wrong: 5 cm × 6 m is for the head.  Why C wrong: 2.5 cm × 3 m is for fingers/toes.  Why D wrong: 10–15 cm × 6–8 m is for the trunk."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"According to the bandage-size table, fingers and toes use a bandage that is:","options":["5 cm wide, 6 m long","2.5 cm (1 inch) wide, 3 m long","6 cm wide, 5 m long","10 cm wide, 8 m long"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Fingers and toes use a 2.5 cm (1 inch) wide, 3 m long bandage.  Why A wrong: 5 cm × 6 m is for the head.  Why C wrong: 6 cm × 5 m corresponds to the hand range.  Why D wrong: 10 cm × 8 m corresponds to the trunk."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"Per the bandage-size table, the HAND uses a bandage of:","options":["5 cm (2 inches) wide, 5 m long","2.5 cm × 3 m","10–15 cm × 6–8 m","6–8 cm × 4 m"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For the hand, the bandage is 5 cm (2 inches) wide and 5 m long.  Why B wrong: 2.5 cm × 3 m is for fingers/toes.  Why C wrong: 10–15 cm × 6–8 m is for the trunk.  Why D wrong: 6–8 cm × 4 m is for the leg."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"Which type of bandage technique is generally used to bandage the ankle (a flexing joint region)?","options":["Reverse spiral","Figure-of-eight bandage","Triangular","Crepe"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The figure-of-eight bandage technique is used to bandage the ankle (a flexing joint), per the Recall box.  Why A wrong: Reverse spiral is for cylindrical parts forming a V-shape.  Why C wrong: Triangular bandage is a different type (sling/genital support), not a joint-binding technique.  Why D wrong: Crepe describes a material with stretch, not the joint-bandaging technique."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"When facing the patient to apply a bandage, the body part to be bandaged should be:","options":["Wet and soiled","Clean and dry","Covered in ointment","Below heart level"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Before applying a bandage, make sure the body part to be bandaged is clean and dry.  Why A wrong: A wet/soiled surface impedes adherence and increases infection.  Why C wrong: The part should be clean and dry, not coated in ointment for the bandage.  Why D wrong: Position is not specified as below heart level here."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"When holding the roll of the bandage during application, it should be held with the:","options":["Roll uppermost, applying the outer surface to the skin, unrolling a few centimeters at a time","Roll downward, on the inner surface","Loose end wrapped first around itself","Roll fully unrolled before application"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hold the roll of the bandage in the right hand with the roll uppermost, applying the outer surface to the skin and unrolling a few centimeters at a time.  Why B wrong: The roll should be uppermost, not downward.  Why C wrong: Wrapping the loose end first is not the technique.  Why D wrong: The bandage is unrolled gradually, not fully beforehand."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"When bandaging a limb, the bandage should be applied from:","options":["The top down toward the affected (wound) area, away from the body","Below upward and from within outward (start at inner aspect and move outward), proceeding from the affected area toward the body","Side to side only","In random direction"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Bandage from below upward and from within outward; always start the bandage from the inner aspect and far to near end.  Why A wrong: Bandaging downward away from the body is not the documented direction.  Why C wrong: Side-to-side only does not secure the limb properly.  Why D wrong: Direction must be deliberate, not random."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"At the start and end of a bandage, what turn type should be used?","options":["Begin and end with figure-of-eight turns","Always start and end with two circular turns","Use only spiral turns throughout","End with a reverse spiral"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Always start and end the bandage with two circular turns to anchor it.  Why A wrong: Figure-of-eight is the joint technique, not the universal anchor turn.  Why C wrong: Spiral turns are for the body of the bandage, not the anchoring start/end.  Why D wrong: The end should be secured with circular turns."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"During spiral bandaging, how much should each turn overlap the previous turn?","options":["No overlap is needed","Cover two-thirds (2/3) of the previous turn","Cover one-tenth of the previous turn","Cover the entire previous turn"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Cover two-thirds (2/3) of the previous turn, overlapping turns and slightly stretching the bandage.  Why A wrong: Overlap is required for stability.  Why C wrong: One-tenth provides insufficient coverage.  Why D wrong: Full coverage of each turn wastes bandage and adds bulk."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"Why should a bandage be kept \"even\" and not too tight or too loose with too much pressure?","options":["To save material","Because too-tight bandaging interferes with (impairs) circulation","To look attractive","Because loose bandaging cools the limb"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Keep even and not too tight, applying gentle pressure, because too-tight bandaging interferes with circulation.  Why A wrong: Material economy is not the reason.  Why C wrong: Appearance is not the clinical concern.  Why D wrong: Cooling is not the documented rationale."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"How should a bandage be finished/secured at the end?","options":["Tied directly over the wound or at the back of the body","End the bandage on the outer side of the body part, fixed with a safety pin/adhesive; do not end a bandage on the wound or at the back of the body","Left untied","Knotted tightly over a joint"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: End the bandage on the outer side of the body, fastened with a straight turn and a safety pin/sticking plaster (or by dividing the terminal portion and tying the two ends); do not end on the wound or at the back of the body.  Why A wrong: Ending over the wound or at the back causes pressure/discomfort.  Why C wrong: An unsecured bandage will unravel.  Why D wrong: Knotting tightly over a joint can impair circulation and comfort."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"While bandaging, fingers and toes should be:","options":["Always fully covered","Left exposed to check circulation","Bandaged most tightly","Wrapped separately every time"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If possible, leave fingers and toes exposed to check circulation.  Why A wrong: Covering them prevents circulation checks.  Why C wrong: Tight bandaging of digits risks ischemia.  Why D wrong: Separate wrapping each time is not the documented principle; exposure for monitoring is."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"While caring for a bandaged extremity, observing the extremities carefully for any signs of blueness due to interference with circulation is important because such bluishness indicates the bandage:","options":["Is correctly applied","Is too tight (impairing circulation)","Needs to be removed permanently","Is too loose"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Observe extremities for blueness due to interference with circulation; such a bandage is too tight and must be reapplied.  Why A wrong: Blueness indicates a problem, not correct application.  Why C wrong: It should be reapplied properly, not permanently removed.  Why D wrong: A too-loose bandage does not cause cyanosis from compression."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"When removing a bandage, it should be:","options":["Cut off with scissors directly over the wound","Passed from one hand to the other so that it is collected in a concertina (folded) fashion","Torn quickly in one motion","Soaked and left in place"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: When removing a bandage, pass it from one hand to the other so it is collected in a concertina fashion.  Why A wrong: Cutting over the wound risks injury and is not the standard removal technique.  Why C wrong: Tearing quickly can injure the wound.  Why D wrong: Soaking and leaving it does not remove the bandage."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"Among the general principles of bandaging, why are gloves worn where necessary?","options":["To keep the hands warm","For infection control/hygiene during the procedure","To improve grip only","To reduce material cost"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A general principle is to wash hands and wear gloves where necessary (for hygiene/infection control).  Why A wrong: Warmth is not the purpose.  Why C wrong: Grip is not the documented rationale.  Why D wrong: Cost is irrelevant."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"A general principle of bandaging is to select a bandage of:","options":["The smallest possible size always","Proper size and suitable material","The cheapest available material","The largest size always"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Select a bandage of proper size and suitable material.  Why A/D wrong: Neither the smallest nor the largest universally; it must be the proper size.  Why C wrong: Suitability, not cost, governs material selection."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"When a joint is involved in the injured area being bandaged, the joint should be:","options":["Kept fully straight","Put in a comfortable position, flexed slightly, while supporting the injured area","Bandaged tightly to immobilize without padding","Left out of the bandage entirely"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Put the joint in a comfortable position; support the injured area while bandaging, and if a joint is involved, flex it slightly.  Why A wrong: Full extension may not be comfortable or appropriate.  Why C wrong: Excessive tightness impairs circulation.  Why D wrong: The injured area including the joint is supported, not excluded."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"The Indian Food Pyramid was developed by which institution?","options":["World Health Organization","National Institute of Nutrition (NIN), Hyderabad, and endorsed by the Ministry of Health","Indian Council of Medical Research only","US Department of Agriculture"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Indian Food Pyramid was developed by the National Institute of Nutrition (NIN), Hyderabad, and endorsed by the Ministry of Health.  Why A wrong: WHO did not develop it.  Why C wrong: ICMR described the food groups separately; the pyramid was developed by NIN.  Why D wrong: The USDA designed MyPlate, not the Indian Food Pyramid."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"What is a food pyramid?","options":["A type of cooking utensil","A visual tool used as a guide to the types and amounts of food that can be eaten in combination to provide a healthy diet","A government tax document","A laboratory measurement"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A food pyramid is a visual tool used as a guide to the types and amounts of food that can be eaten in combination to provide a healthy diet.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not describe a food pyramid as a dietary guidance tool."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"Into how many levels is the Indian food pyramid divided, according to recommended consumption?","options":["2 levels","4 levels","5 levels","7 levels"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Indian adaptation of the food pyramid is divided into 4 levels of foods according to recommended consumption.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies 4 levels."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"At the base/bottom of the Indian food pyramid, which foods should be eaten in sufficient/adequate amounts?","options":["Highly processed foods high in sugar and fat","Cereals, legumes/beans, millets, and pulses, and milk (dairy products)","Cola and ice cream","French fries and chocolates"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Level 1 (base/bottom) consists of cereals, legumes/beans, millets, pulses, and milk (dairy products), to be eaten in sufficient/adequate amounts.  Why A/C/D wrong: Highly processed, high-sugar/high-fat foods are at the apex, to be eaten sparingly."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"Vegetables and fruits, located on the second level of the Indian food pyramid, should be eaten:","options":["Sparingly","Liberally","Never","Only once a month"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Vegetables and fruits on the second level should be eaten liberally.  Why A wrong: Sparing intake applies to the apex (processed high-sugar/fat foods).  Why C/D wrong: Fruits and vegetables are encouraged liberally."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"Animal source foods and oils on the third level of the Indian food pyramid should be eaten:","options":["Liberally","Moderately","Sparingly","Not at all"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Animal source foods and oils on the third level should be eaten moderately.  Why A wrong: Liberal intake applies to fruits/vegetables (level 2).  Why C wrong: Sparing intake applies to the apex (highly processed foods).  Why D wrong: They are eaten moderately, not avoided."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"Highly processed foods that are high in sugar and fat (e.g., burger, French fries, ice-cream, colas, chocolates, bakery items) should be eaten:","options":["Liberally","Moderately","Sparingly (at the apex/top of the pyramid)","At every meal"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Highly processed foods high in sugar and fat (burger, French fries, ice cream, colas, chocolates, bakery items) should be eaten sparingly and sit at the apex/top of the pyramid.  Why A wrong: Liberal intake applies to fruits/vegetables.  Why B wrong: Moderate intake applies to animal source foods and oils.  Why D wrong: These should be sparing, not at every meal."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"Accompanying the Indian food pyramid is advice for regular physical activity and warnings against:","options":["Drinking water","Smoking and drinking alcohol","Eating fruits","Daily exercise"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The pyramid is accompanied by advice for regular physical activity and warnings against smoking and drinking alcohol.  Why A/C/D wrong: Water, fruits, and exercise are encouraged, not warned against."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"According to the source, how much water is most important to include each day?","options":["1 litre","2 to 3 litres of water each day (as it is the most important nutrient of all)","5 litres","Half a litre"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: It is most important to include 2 to 3 litres of water each day, as water is the most important nutrient of all.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies 2–3 litres daily."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"Previously, the ICMR described how many food groups for Indians?","options":["3 food groups","5 food groups","7 food groups","9 food groups"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Previously ICMR described 5 food groups for Indians.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source states 5 food groups (ICMR old classification)."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"Which of the following is one of the 5 groups in the ICMR old classification of food for Indians? (Select all that apply)","options":["Cereal and products","Pulses and legumes","Milk and meat products","Fruit and vegetables","Fat and sugars"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The ICMR old classification's 5 food groups are: (1) cereal and products, (2) pulses and legumes, (3) milk and meat products, (4) fruit and vegetables, and (5) fat and sugars.  Why none wrong: All five options are the exact ICMR-listed groups."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"The MyPlate Food Guide was designed by which agency to help people make smart food choices?","options":["National Institute of Nutrition","US Department of Agriculture (USDA)","Indian Council of Medical Research","World Health Organization"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: To help people make smart food choices, the US Department of Agriculture (USDA) designed the easy-to-follow MyPlate guide.  Why A wrong: NIN developed the Indian Food Pyramid.  Why C wrong: ICMR classified Indian food groups.  Why D wrong: WHO did not design MyPlate."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"The MyPlate icon shows how many food groups, and which are they?","options":["4 groups: Grains, Fruits, Vegetables, Protein","5 food groups: Fruits, Vegetables, Grains, Protein Foods, and Dairy","3 groups: Carbohydrates, Proteins, Fats","6 groups including water"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The MyPlate icon shows 5 food groups: Fruits, Vegetables, Grains, Protein Foods, and Dairy.  Why A wrong: It includes Dairy as a fifth group.  Why C/D wrong: MyPlate specifies exactly 5 listed groups."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"\"Sickness\" is best defined as:","options":["The presence of pathology in the body","A social condition or role of those defined as diseased/ill — a person takes and/or is given the social role regarding his/her condition","A subjective feeling of distress","A purely laboratory diagnosis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Sickness is a social condition or role of those defined as diseased/ill, in which a person takes and/or is given the social role regarding his/her condition.  Why A wrong: Presence of pathology defines disease, not sickness.  Why C wrong: A subjective feeling of distress defines illness.  Why D wrong: A laboratory diagnosis relates to disease (objective pathology), not the social role of sickness."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"\"Disease\" refers to:","options":["The subjective feeling of distress","The objective existence, presence of pathology or abnormality in part of the body or medically defined pathology that comes from bacteria and viruses, etc.","The social role of being ill","Acute episodes only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Disease is the objective existence/presence of pathology or abnormality in part of the body or medically defined pathology arising from bacteria, viruses, etc.  Why A wrong: Subjective distress defines illness.  Why C wrong: The social role defines sickness.  Why D wrong: Disease is not limited to acute episodes."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"\"Illness\" is defined as:","options":["The objective pathology in the body","The subjective feeling of distress that accompanies disease, as a person experiences it (e.g., symptoms of his/her health), categorized as acute and chronic","The social role of the patient","A bacterial infection only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Illness is the subjective feeling of distress that accompanies disease as a person experiences it (e.g., symptoms of his/her health), and is categorized as acute and chronic.  Why A wrong: Objective pathology defines disease.  Why C wrong: The social role defines sickness.  Why D wrong: Illness is the subjective experience, not specifically a bacterial infection."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"Acute illness is characterized by:","options":["Developing gradually over more than 6 months","Developing suddenly and lasting a short time, often a few days or weeks","Permanent disability","Absence of symptoms"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Acute illnesses generally develop suddenly and last a short time, often a few days or weeks.  Why A wrong: Gradual onset over >6 months describes chronic illness.  Why C wrong: Permanent disability is not the definition of acute illness.  Why D wrong: Acute illness involves symptoms."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"Chronic illnesses are characterized by which features? (Select all that apply)","options":["They develop slowly","They may worsen over an extended period of time, often months to years","They always resolve within a week","They are experienced by the person and may affect descriptions of distinct phases of illness","They never affect the patient's daily life"],"correct":"A,B,D","rationale":"Why correct: Chronic illnesses develop slowly and may worsen over an extended period (months to years); the chronic illness is experienced by the person, who describes distinct phases of illness (trajectory).  Why C wrong: Chronic illnesses do not resolve in a week — that describes acute illness.  Why E wrong: Chronic illnesses do affect daily life and function."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"The \"trajectory model of chronic illness\" describes the total nine phases of chronic illness. Which are these phases collectively termed?","options":["Pre-trajectory, trajectory onset, stable, unstable, acute, crisis, comeback, downward, and dying","Birth, childhood, adolescence, adulthood, old age","Mild, moderate, severe","Prevention, treatment, cure"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The trajectory model describes nine phases of chronic illness: pre-trajectory, trajectory onset, stable, unstable, acute, crisis, comeback, downward, and dying.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not represent the documented trajectory phases of chronic illness."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"In the stages of chronic illness, \"Exacerbation\" refers to a period when:","options":["The illness is fully cured","The chronic illness becomes more active and recurrent, with an increase in the severity of the disease or its sign and symptoms (an exacerbation of illness)","Symptoms are completely controlled","The patient has no disease"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Exacerbation is a period when chronic illness becomes more active and recurrent, with an increase in severity of the disease or its signs and symptoms (an exacerbation of illness).  Why A wrong: A cure is not exacerbation.  Why C wrong: Complete symptom control describes remission.  Why D wrong: Exacerbation occurs within ongoing disease."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"\"Remission\" in chronic illness is best described as a period when:","options":["The disease worsens permanently","The chronic illness is controlled and the signs and symptoms are reduced or not obvious","The patient dies","The disease first appears"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Remission is a period when chronic illness is controlled and the signs and symptoms are reduced or not obvious; the clinical features of the disease are controlled or under limit after treatment.  Why A wrong: Worsening describes exacerbation/progression.  Why C wrong: Death corresponds to the terminal/dying phase.  Why D wrong: First appearance is trajectory onset, not remission."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"\"Progressive degeneration\" in chronic illness refers to:","options":["Improvement of organ function","Continuous deterioration or increased impairment of a person's physical state or health","Complete recovery","A short, self-limited illness"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Progressive degeneration is the continuous deterioration or increased impairment of a person's physical state or health.  Why A wrong: It is deterioration, not improvement.  Why C wrong: Recovery is the opposite.  Why D wrong: Self-limited illness is acute, not progressive degeneration."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"The \"Terminal\" stage of chronic illness is characterized by:","options":["Full recovery within weeks","Escape by a magnitude that is expected to result in death within 6 months, in which the focus is on hospice and palliative care to meet the client's physical, psychosocial, and spiritual needs","Mild symptoms that resolve","The onset of the disease"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the terminal stage, the condition is expected to result in death within 6 months, and care focuses on hospice and palliative care meeting physical, psychosocial, and spiritual needs.  Why A/C wrong: Recovery or mild resolving symptoms do not describe the terminal stage.  Why D wrong: Onset is trajectory onset, not terminal."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"Acute pain is best described as pain that:","options":["Lasts more than 6 months","Comes on suddenly and lasts 6 months or less as a result of injury, surgery, or something else","Is always present without a cause","Has no identifiable trigger"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Acute pain comes on suddenly and lasts 6 months or less, usually as a result of injury, surgery, or something else.  Why A wrong: Lasting more than 6 months describes chronic pain.  Why C/D wrong: Acute pain is associated with an identifiable cause/trigger."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"Chronic pain is defined as pain that:","options":["Resolves within hours","Can gradually last more than 6 months as a result of difficult conditions that are difficult to treat","Always has a clear, easily treatable cause","Lasts less than a day"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Chronic pain can gradually last more than 6 months as a result of difficult conditions that are difficult to treat.  Why A/D wrong: Brief pain that resolves quickly is acute.  Why C wrong: Chronic pain often arises from difficult-to-treat conditions, not an easily treatable cause."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"\"Phantom limb\" pain is the perception of pain or discomfort in a limb that:","options":["Is severely infected","No longer exists (following amputation)","Is fractured","Is sprained"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Phantom limb pain is the perception of pain or discomfort in a limb that no longer exists, following amputation.  Why A/C/D wrong: Infection, fracture, and sprain involve a limb that physically still exists."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"Pain involves how many physiological processes, per the source?","options":["Two","Four physiological processes (transduction, transmission, perception, modulation)","Six","Eight"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: There are four physiological processes involved in pain: transduction, transmission, perception, and modulation.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source lists exactly four processes."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"In the physiology of pain, \"Transduction\" refers to:","options":["Conscious experience of pain","Sensory nerve endings in the organ converting noxious stimuli into electrical signals (an impulse)","Suppression of pain by opioids","Travel of impulses to the brain"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Transduction is when sensory nerve endings in the organ convert noxious stimuli into electrical signals/impulses.  Why A wrong: Conscious experience is perception.  Why C wrong: Suppression by opioids is modulation.  Why D wrong: Travel of impulses to the brain is transmission."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"\"Transmission\" in the physiology of pain refers to:","options":["Conversion of noxious stimuli into electrical signals","Electrical signals (pain signals) transmitted through nerve fibres to the spinal cord and brain, modulated by neurotransmitters","Altered/suppressed pain perception via opioids","Conscious experience of pain"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Transmission is the process by which electrical (pain) signals are transmitted through nerve fibres to the spinal cord and brain, modulated by neurotransmitters.  Why A wrong: Conversion of stimuli is transduction.  Why C wrong: Altered/suppressed perception via opioids is modulation.  Why D wrong: Conscious experience is perception."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"\"Perception\" in the physiology of pain is:","options":["The conversion of stimuli into impulses","When pain signals reach the brain, resulting in conscious experience of pain","The suppression of pain by endorphins","The transmission along nerve fibres"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Perception is when pain signals reach the brain, resulting in the conscious experience of pain.  Why A wrong: Conversion is transduction.  Why C wrong: Suppression by endorphins is modulation.  Why D wrong: Travel along nerve fibres is transmission."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"\"Modulation\" in the physiology of pain refers to:","options":["Conversion of stimuli to impulses","Pain perception being altered or suppressed through release of endogenous opioids (endorphins), serotonin, norepinephrine, GABA, or cognitive and emotional processes","Conscious experience of pain","Signals reaching the brain"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Modulation is when pain perception is altered or suppressed through release of endogenous opioids (endorphins), serotonin, norepinephrine, GABA, or cognitive and emotional processes.  Why A wrong: Conversion of stimuli is transduction.  Why C/D wrong: Conscious experience/signals reaching the brain is perception/transmission."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"According to the Gate Control Theory of pain, what determines whether pain signals reach the brain?","options":["The blood pressure","A \"gate\" mechanism along the spine: larger nerve fibres close the gate to pain while smaller diameter fibres open the gate","Only the patient's age","The size of the painful area"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: According to the gate control theory, a gate mechanism along the spine controls pain transmission — larger nerve fibres close the gate to pain while smaller diameter fibres open the gate to pain.  Why A/C/D wrong: Blood pressure, age, and area size are not the gating mechanism described."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"According to gate control theory, why does rubbing or massaging an injured area reduce pain?","options":["It increases small-diameter fibre activity","It stimulates the large-diameter fibres and closes the gate, reducing pain transmission via the small diameter fibres","It blocks all nerve fibres permanently","It increases pain perception in the brain"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Rubbing or massaging the injured area opens (stimulates) the large diameter fibres and closes the gate that the pain transmission via small diameter fibres uses, reducing pain.  Why A wrong: It activates large, not small, fibres.  Why C wrong: It does not block all fibres permanently.  Why D wrong: It reduces, not increases, pain perception."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"Which of the following is a listed pain rating scale? (Select all that apply)","options":["Visual Analogue Scale (VAS)","Numerical Rating Scale (NRS)","Verbal Rating Scale (VRS)","Faces Pain Scale–Revised (FPS-R) and Wong-Baker Faces Pain Rating Scale","Glasgow Coma Scale"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The listed pain rating scales are the Visual Analogue Scale (VAS), Numerical Rating Scale (NRS), Verbal Rating Scale (VRS), Faces Pain Scale–Revised (FPS-R), and the Wong-Baker Faces Pain Rating Scale.  Why E wrong: The Glasgow Coma Scale assesses level of consciousness, not pain intensity, and is not in the pain rating scale list."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"Which of the following is a NON-pharmacological pain management method? (Select all that apply)","options":["Physical and massage therapy","Acupuncture","TENS (transcutaneous electrical nerve stimulation)","Biofeedback","Yoga and meditation"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Non-pharmacological pain management includes physical and massage therapy, acupuncture, TENS, biofeedback, and yoga and meditation.  Why none wrong: All options are listed non-pharmacological measures."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"Which of the following is a pharmacological pain management category mentioned in the source? (Select all that apply)","options":["Analgesics or painkiller medications used to relieve pain","NSAIDs","Opioids","Adjuvants or co-analgesics","TENS"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Pharmacological pain management includes analgesics/painkiller medications, NSAIDs, opioids, and adjuvants or co-analgesics.  Why E wrong: TENS is a non-pharmacological method, not a pharmacological category."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"On the WHO Analgesic Ladder, Step 1 (mild pain) generally involves:","options":["Strong opioids such as morphine","Non-opioids (e.g., paracetamol or NSAIDs), with or without adjuvants","Weak opioids only","Surgical intervention"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Step 1 of the WHO analgesic ladder addresses mild pain using non-opioids (e.g., paracetamol/NSAIDs) with or without adjuvants.  Why A wrong: Strong opioids belong to Step 3.  Why C wrong: Weak opioids are Step 2.  Why D wrong: Surgery is not a step on the WHO analgesic ladder."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"On the WHO Analgesic Ladder, Step 2 (moderate pain) typically adds:","options":["Strong opioids such as morphine, fentanyl","Weak opioids (e.g., codeine/tramadol), with or without non-opioids and adjuvants","Only physiotherapy","Only biofeedback"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Step 2 addresses moderate pain by adding weak opioids (e.g., codeine/tramadol) with or without non-opioids and adjuvants.  Why A wrong: Strong opioids (morphine, fentanyl) belong to Step 3.  Why C/D wrong: Physiotherapy and biofeedback are non-pharmacological adjuncts, not the WHO ladder step 2 medication class."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"On the WHO Analgesic Ladder, Step 3 (severe pain) involves:","options":["Non-opioids only","Strong opioids (e.g., morphine, oxycodone, fentanyl, buprenorphine), with or without non-opioids and adjuvants","No medication","Only acupuncture"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Step 3 addresses severe pain with strong opioids (e.g., morphine, oxycodone, fentanyl, buprenorphine) with or without non-opioids and adjuvants.  Why A wrong: Non-opioids alone are Step 1.  Why C/D wrong: Step 3 is defined by strong opioid therapy, not absence of medication or acupuncture alone."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"When choking/aspiration occurs and the patient is still conscious and breathing, you should encourage the patient to:","options":["Drink water immediately","Feel the victim's pulse and observe; position to reduce risk of choking, and do not attempt to remove an object you cannot see","Lie flat on the back","Perform abdominal thrusts on themselves vigorously regardless"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After the airway/seizure event, feel the victim's pulse and position them to reduce the chance of choking; do not put anything in the mouth or attempt blind removal.  Why A wrong: Giving water risks aspiration.  Why C wrong: Lying flat on the back increases aspiration risk.  Why D wrong: Vigorous self-thrusts are not the documented action; positioning and monitoring are emphasized."},{"section":"Batch 061 · First aid, bandaging, nutrition, illness/sickness/disease concepts…","question":"A nurse is bandaging a patient's forearm. Which technique and which bandage type are MOST appropriate?","options":["Recurrent technique with a triangular bandage","Spiral bandaging technique (forearm is a cylindrical part); a tubular bandage may be used for forearm dressings","Figure-of-eight with an adhesive bandage only","Circular technique with a crepe to immobilize the elbow"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The forearm is a cylindrical body part, so spiral bandaging is appropriate; tubular bandages are specifically used for forearm dressings.  Why A wrong: Recurrent bandaging is for stumps, and triangular is for slings/genital support.  Why C wrong: Figure-of-eight is for flexing joints, not a straight forearm dressing.  Why D wrong: Circular bandaging is used for arms/legs/chest/abdomen generally; the forearm specifically aligns with spiral/tubular technique."},{"section":"Batch 062 · nociceptive vs neuropathic pain (somatic, visceral, radiating)…","question":"A patient describes pain arising from the skin, joints, and muscles. This pain is BEST classified as which type of nociceptive pain?","options":["Visceral pain","Somatic pain","Neuropathic pain","Radiating pain"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Somatic pain is a form of nociceptive pain originating from skin, joints, and muscles (periosteum, sickle cell, superficial location).  Why A wrong: Visceral pain originates from internal organs, not joints/muscles.  Why C wrong: Neuropathic pain arises from nerve damage, not somatic tissue.  Why D wrong: Radiating pain is the spreading characteristic, not a tissue source."},{"section":"Batch 062 · nociceptive vs neuropathic pain (somatic, visceral, radiating)…","question":"How is the character/quality of somatic pain typically described?","options":["Aching, pressure, or sharp","Burning, prickling, tingling","Generalized and diffuse only","Electric shock-like"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Somatic pain location is localized and its quality is described as aching, pressure, or sharp.  Why B wrong: Burning, prickling, tingling describes neuropathic pain.  Why C wrong: Generalized/diffuse is characteristic of visceral pain.  Why D wrong: Electric shock-like sensation is neuropathic."},{"section":"Batch 062 · nociceptive vs neuropathic pain (somatic, visceral, radiating)…","question":"The mechanism of somatic pain involves which type of pain fibers?","options":["C fibers (slow, diffuse)","A-delta fibers carrying activity located in the periphery","Autonomic nerve fibers only","No specialized fibers"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states somatic pain mechanism is A-delta fiber activity located in the periphery.  Why A wrong: C fibers (slow/diffuse) underlie visceral pain.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the stated mechanism for somatic pain."},{"section":"Batch 062 · nociceptive vs neuropathic pain (somatic, visceral, radiating)…","question":"Which of the following are clinical examples of SOMATIC pain? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Periosteum, joints, muscles","Sickle cell","Superficial laceration","Appendicitis","Intramuscular injections / venous access"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: Somatic clinical examples include periosteum/joints/muscles, sickle cell, superficial laceration, superficial burns, intramuscular injections and venous access, otitis media, stomatitis, and extensive abrasion.  Why D wrong: Appendicitis is a clinical example of visceral pain."},{"section":"Batch 062 · nociceptive vs neuropathic pain (somatic, visceral, radiating)…","question":"Visceral pain is characterized by which pattern of localization?","options":["Sharply localized to skin","Generalized and sharp/diffuse","Confined to a single dermatome","Always radiating to limbs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Visceral pain location is generalized, and its quality may be sharp or diffuse.  Why A/C wrong: Sharp/dermatomal localization is more typical of somatic/neuropathic pain.  Why D wrong: Radiation is a separate category, not the defining feature of visceral pain."},{"section":"Batch 062 · nociceptive vs neuropathic pain (somatic, visceral, radiating)…","question":"The mechanism of visceral pain is mediated by which fibers?","options":["A-delta fibers in the periphery","C fiber activity involving deeper innervation","Demyelinated motor fibers","Sympathetic vasoconstrictor fibers"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Visceral pain mechanism is C fiber activity involving deeper innervation.  Why A wrong: A-delta peripheral fibers mediate somatic pain.  Why C/D wrong: Not the stated mechanism for visceral pain."},{"section":"Batch 062 · nociceptive vs neuropathic pain (somatic, visceral, radiating)…","question":"Which of the following are clinical examples of VISCERAL pain? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Colic / spasm pain","Appendicitis","Kidney stone","Chronic pancreatitis","Superficial laceration"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Visceral clinical examples include colic/spasm pain, appendicitis, kidney stone, chronic pancreatitis, IBS, angina, and menstrual cramps.  Why E wrong: Superficial laceration is a somatic pain example."},{"section":"Batch 062 · nociceptive vs neuropathic pain (somatic, visceral, radiating)…","question":"A patient reports burning, prickling, tingling, and electric-shock-like pain. This is characteristic of which pain type?","options":["Somatic pain","Visceral pain","Neuropathic pain","Radiating nociceptive pain"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Neuropathic pain quality is burning, prickling, tingling, electric-shock-like, or lancinating.  Why A wrong: Somatic pain is aching/pressure/sharp.  Why B wrong: Visceral pain is generalized, sharp/diffuse.  Why D wrong: Radiating is a spreading feature, not the burning/tingling quality."},{"section":"Batch 062 · nociceptive vs neuropathic pain (somatic, visceral, radiating)…","question":"Which of the following are recognized examples/types of neuropathic pain? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Trigeminal neuralgia","Post-herpetic neuralgia","Peripheral neuropathy (diabetes, HIV)","Phantom limb (limb amputation)","Menstrual cramps"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Neuropathic examples include trigeminal neuralgia, avulsion nerve injury, post-traumatic neuralgia, peripheral neuropathy (diabetes, HIV), limb amputation (phantom), and herpetic neuralgia.  Why E wrong: Menstrual cramps are a visceral pain example."},{"section":"Batch 062 · nociceptive vs neuropathic pain (somatic, visceral, radiating)…","question":"Neuropathic pain may be described as \"central\" when caused by which mechanism?","options":["Damage to the dermatomal periphery only","Dermatomal (peripheral) versus dermatomal (central) involvement","Inflammation of skin","Muscle spasm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists neuropathic mechanisms as dermatomal (peripheral) or non-dermatomal (central).  Why A/C/D wrong: These describe somatic/inflammatory processes, not the central/peripheral neuropathic distinction."},{"section":"Batch 062 · nociceptive vs neuropathic pain (somatic, visceral, radiating)…","question":"What is the local inflammatory response?","options":["A reaction occurring only in chronic infections","A response that occurs when tissues are injured by trauma, bacteria, toxins, heat, or any other cause","A purely psychological response to pain","A response unrelated to tissue injury"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Inflammation occurs when tissues are injured by trauma, bacteria, toxins, heat, or any other cause; the body's nonspecific response to tissue injury.  Why A/C/D wrong: Inflammation is a nonspecific response to any tissue injury, not limited to chronic infection nor psychological."},{"section":"Batch 062 · nociceptive vs neuropathic pain (somatic, visceral, radiating)…","question":"Inflammation is BEST described as which type of response?","options":["The body's specific (antigen-targeted) immune response","The body's nonspecific response to tissue injury","An adaptive antibody-mediated response","A coagulation cascade only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Inflammation is the body's nonspecific response to tissue injury.  Why A/C wrong: Specific/antibody-mediated immunity is distinct from the nonspecific inflammatory response.  Why D wrong: Coagulation is separate from the inflammatory response."},{"section":"Batch 062 · nociceptive vs neuropathic pain (somatic, visceral, radiating)…","question":"Which cardinal sign of inflammation refers to REDNESS released at the site of injury due to vasodilation (hyperaemia)?","options":["Heat (Calor)","Erythema or redness (Rubor)","Edema or swelling (Tumor)","Loss of function"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Erythema/redness is released at the site of injury, causing vasodilation (hyperaemia).  Why A wrong: Heat is from vasodilation bringing more warmed blood to the affected area.  Why C wrong: Edema is fluid accumulation.  Why D wrong: Loss of function is a protective response to pain/edema."},{"section":"Batch 062 · nociceptive vs neuropathic pain (somatic, visceral, radiating)…","question":"The cardinal sign \"Heat\" in inflammation is produced by:","options":["Increased capillary permeability allowing fluid leakage","Vasodilation bringing more warmed blood to the affected area","Irritation of nerve endings","Loss of cellular function"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Heat results from vasodilation bringing more warmed blood to the affected area.  Why A wrong: That describes edema/swelling.  Why C wrong: That describes pain.  Why D wrong: That describes loss of function."},{"section":"Batch 062 · nociceptive vs neuropathic pain (somatic, visceral, radiating)…","question":"Edema (swelling) in inflammation occurs because of:","options":["Decreased blood flow to the area","Histamine causing increased capillary permeability, allowing fluid, protein, and WBCs to move into interstitial space","Nerve compression","Tissue necrosis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Edema/swelling is due to histamine-induced increased capillary permeability allowing fluid, protein, and WBCs to move into interstitial space.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not describe the histamine-mediated fluid shift of edema."},{"section":"Batch 062 · nociceptive vs neuropathic pain (somatic, visceral, radiating)…","question":"Which chemical mediators are responsible for the PAIN of inflammation by irritating nerve endings?","options":["Histamine and heparin only","Serotonin, prostaglandins, and kinins","Insulin and glucagon","Albumin and globulin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pain occurs due to activation of nerve endings and irritation by chemical mediators such as serotonin, prostaglandins, and kinins.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the pain-producing inflammatory mediators listed."},{"section":"Batch 062 · nociceptive vs neuropathic pain (somatic, visceral, radiating)…","question":"\"Loss of function\" as a cardinal sign of inflammation is BEST understood as:","options":["Permanent organ failure","A protective response because of pain and oedema","An allergic reaction","A sign of infection only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Loss of function is a protective response because of pain and oedema.  Why A/C/D wrong: It is protective and reversible, not permanent failure, allergy, or infection-specific."},{"section":"Batch 062 · nociceptive vs neuropathic pain (somatic, visceral, radiating)…","question":"In which year was the Ministry of AYUSH formed?","options":["2003","1995","2014","2020"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The Ministry of AYUSH was formed on the 9th of November 2014.  Why A wrong: 2003 is when AYUSH education/research was focused on (and Naturopathy added in Ayurveda, Yoga).  Why B wrong: 1995 is when the Department of Indian System of Medicine and Homoeopathy (ISM&H) was formed.  Why D wrong: 2020 is not associated with AYUSH formation."},{"section":"Batch 062 · nociceptive vs neuropathic pain (somatic, visceral, radiating)…","question":"The Department of Indian System of Medicine and Homoeopathy (ISM&H), responsible for developing these systems, was formed in which year?","options":["2003","1995","2014","1947"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Department of Indian System of Medicine and Homoeopathy (ISM&H) was formed in 1995, responsible for the development of these systems.  Why A wrong: 2003 relates to focused attention on AYUSH education and research.  Why C wrong: 2014 is the AYUSH Ministry formation year.  Why D wrong: 1947 is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 062 · nociceptive vs neuropathic pain (somatic, visceral, radiating)…","question":"The acronym \"AYUSH\" is used to refer to which of the following?","options":["A hospital chain","The indigenous/Indian medicinal systems of India","A drug classification","A nursing council"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: AYUSH is an acronym used to refer to the indigenous medicinal systems in India.  Why A/C/D wrong: AYUSH denotes the traditional Indian medicine systems, not a hospital, drug class, or nursing council."},{"section":"Batch 062 · nociceptive vs neuropathic pain (somatic, visceral, radiating)…","question":"Which of the following systems are included in AYUSH? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Ayurveda","Yoga","Unani","Siddha","Homeopathy"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: AYUSH includes the Indian medicinal systems: (1) Ayurveda, (2) Yoga, (3) Unani, (4) Siddha, and (5) Homeopathy.  All listed options are part of the AYUSH systems."},{"section":"Batch 062 · nociceptive vs neuropathic pain (somatic, visceral, radiating)…","question":"According to the principles to prevent nursing errors, the nurse should follow the \"rights of drug administration\" while giving medication to:","options":["Any available patient","The patient","The pharmacist","The relative"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The first principle is to follow the rights of drug administration while giving the medication to the patient.  Why A/C/D wrong: Medication and its rights apply to the correct patient, not just any patient, pharmacist, or relative."},{"section":"Batch 062 · nociceptive vs neuropathic pain (somatic, visceral, radiating)…","question":"When administering medicines, how should a nurse verify the client's identity?","options":["Verify the identity for three times only","Verify the client's identity by checking two patient identifiers","Verify only the room number","No verification is needed if the chart is present"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The principle states the nurse should verify the client's identity for three times (per source) using identification; correctly, two identifiers (e.g., name, ID) are the evidence-based standard. The source emphasizes verifying identity before administration. (The source phrasing \"for three times\" refers to checking the medicine/order three times.)  Why A wrong: Verifying identity \"three times\" is not the standard; the medication ORDER is checked three times.  Why C wrong: Room number alone is unreliable for identification.  Why D wrong: Active identity verification is always required."},{"section":"Batch 062 · nociceptive vs neuropathic pain (somatic, visceral, radiating)…","question":"Before administering a medicine, the order from the cupboard should be checked how many times?","options":["One time","Two times","Three times","Five times"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source specifies checking the medicine three times: (1) when taking the medicine from the cupboard, (2) when dispensing the medicine, and (3) when administering the medicine to the client.  Why A/B/D wrong: The standard stated is three checks, not one, two, or five."},{"section":"Batch 062 · nociceptive vs neuropathic pain (somatic, visceral, radiating)…","question":"The \"three checks\" of medication should be performed at which points? (Select all that apply.)","options":["When taking the medicine from the cupboard","When dispensing the medicine","When administering the medicine to the client","After the patient is discharged","Only during the night shift"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The three checks occur when taking from the cupboard, when dispensing, and when administering to the client.  Why D/E wrong: These are not part of the medication safety check points."},{"section":"Batch 062 · nociceptive vs neuropathic pain (somatic, visceral, radiating)…","question":"What should the nurse do regarding verbal/telephone orders to prevent nursing errors?","options":["Accept all verbal orders without question","Avoid taking verbal orders","Never label drugs","Skip double-checking with pharmacy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A principle to prevent nursing errors is to avoid taking verbal orders.  Why A wrong: Accepting verbal orders without verification increases error risk.  Why C/D wrong: The source advises to label drugs properly and double-check with pharmacy — the opposite."},{"section":"Batch 062 · nociceptive vs neuropathic pain (somatic, visceral, radiating)…","question":"Verbal orders for narcotics or chemotherapeutic drugs should be:","options":["Accepted routinely over the phone","Never accepted (never taken) for these high-alert drugs","Documented after administration","Given by the patient's relative"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to never follow verbal orders for narcotics and chemotherapeutic drugs.  Why A/C/D wrong: These high-alert drugs require written orders; verbal orders are not acceptable."},{"section":"Batch 062 · nociceptive vs neuropathic pain (somatic, visceral, radiating)…","question":"Which safety practices are recommended to prevent nursing/medication errors? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Label the drugs properly","Keep narcotics or opioids locked","Double check with pharmacy","Leave medication unlabeled to save time","In case of multiple invasive lines, proper labelling of lines should be done"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists labeling drugs properly, keeping narcotics/opioids locked, double-checking with pharmacy, and proper labelling of invasive lines.  Why D wrong: Unlabeled medication is unsafe and contradicts the labeling principle."},{"section":"Batch 062 · nociceptive vs neuropathic pain (somatic, visceral, radiating)…","question":"When surgical site verification is required, what should be done before the client is shifted to the OT (operation theatre)?","options":["Nothing extra is needed","Double check the surgical site to be checked before the client is shifted to OT","Only the surgeon checks after anesthesia","Verification is done after surgery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to double check of surgical site to be checked before client is shifted to OT.  Why A/C/D wrong: Site verification must occur before transfer to OT, not after anesthesia or surgery."},{"section":"Batch 062 · nociceptive vs neuropathic pain (somatic, visceral, radiating)…","question":"Blood samples for cross-verification should be labelled at what time/place to prevent identification errors?","options":["After the sample reaches the lab","At the patient's bedside (labelled as soon as phlebotomy is done)","The next day","Labels are not required for blood samples"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Blood samples should be labelled at the bedside as soon as phlebotomy is done; cross-verification is used to feature labelling the samples to the lab.  Why A/C/D wrong: Delayed or absent labeling risks misidentification of blood samples."},{"section":"Batch 062 · nociceptive vs neuropathic pain (somatic, visceral, radiating)…","question":"Why is proper handwashing critical in preventing nursing errors?","options":["It is purely cosmetic","It clearly mentions the drugs administered, pending, or on hold to avoid acts of commission and omission","It replaces medication checks","It only matters before surgery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source notes proper handover/documentation is critical to prevent errors, clearly mentioning drugs administered, pending, or on hold to avoid acts of commission and omission, so given sufficient time for hand off.  Why A/C/D wrong: Documentation/handover prevents medication errors broadly, is not cosmetic, and is not limited to surgery."},{"section":"Batch 062 · nociceptive vs neuropathic pain (somatic, visceral, radiating)…","question":"What is described as a \"powerful tool\" in nursing-error prevention?","options":["Verbal orders","Communication","Working faster","Avoiding documentation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states communication is a powerful tool in error prevention.  Why A wrong: Verbal orders should be avoided.  Why C/D wrong: Speed and avoiding documentation increase error risk."},{"section":"Batch 062 · nociceptive vs neuropathic pain (somatic, visceral, radiating)…","question":"Verifying the client's identity to ensure the right medicine is given to the right client is essentially which type of measure?","options":["Patient identification (right patient) measure for safe administration","A billing measure","A discharge measure","A documentation-only step with no safety value"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source emphasizes the safest way to verify the client's identity is the client his or her name before drug administration — this is the best identification tool to ensure the right patient receives the medication.  Why B/C/D wrong: Identity verification is a core patient-safety (right patient) step, not billing/discharge/documentation-only."},{"section":"Batch 062 · nociceptive vs neuropathic pain (somatic, visceral, radiating)…","question":"Which of the following are listed CAUSES of nursing errors? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Lack of optimum nurse–client ratio","Stress due to long shift","Less time available for nursing care","Lack of skilled staff","Adequate staffing and short shifts"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Listed causes include lack of optimum nurse–client ratio, stress due to long shift, less time available for nursing care, and lack of skilled staff.  Why E wrong: Adequate staffing/short shifts would reduce errors, not cause them."},{"section":"Batch 062 · nociceptive vs neuropathic pain (somatic, visceral, radiating)…","question":"Which additional factors are identified as causes of nursing errors? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Personal and professional life conflicts","Lack of up-gradation of skills","Pessimism (a state of mind in which an individual thinks or believes the worst will happen)","Stress / non-specific response of the body to any demand for changes","High morale and continuous training"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists personal and professional life conflicts, lack of up-gradation of skills, pessimism, and stress (non-specific response of the body to any demand for changes) as causes.  Why E wrong: High morale and continuous training are protective, not causative."},{"section":"Batch 062 · nociceptive vs neuropathic pain (somatic, visceral, radiating)…","question":"\"Stress\" as a cause of nursing errors is defined in the source as:","options":["A specific immune response","The non-specific response of the body to any demand for changes","A type of consent","A pain mediator"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines stress as the non-specific response of the body to any demand for changes.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the source definition of stress."},{"section":"Batch 062 · nociceptive vs neuropathic pain (somatic, visceral, radiating)…","question":"\"Pessimism,\" listed as a cause of nursing errors, refers to:","options":["Believing the best will happen","A state of mind in which an individual thinks or believes the worst will happen","Optimal staffing","A diagnostic test"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pessimism is a state of mind in which an individual thinks or believes the worst will happen.  Why A/C/D wrong: These contradict or are unrelated to the definition of pessimism."},{"section":"Batch 062 · nociceptive vs neuropathic pain (somatic, visceral, radiating)…","question":"Informed consent is BEST defined as:","options":["A process for getting voluntary permission before conducting a procedure on a healthy patient","A process for getting voluntary permission before conducting a procedure on a healthy patient AND between patient and hospital","Consent given only by the surgeon","Consent required only for blood transfusion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Informed consent is a process for getting voluntary permission before conducting a procedure, made between the patient and hospital.  Why A wrong: It is incomplete — consent applies to patients undergoing procedures generally, and involves the patient and hospital.  Why C/D wrong: Consent is given by the patient (or authorized person), not solely the surgeon, and is not limited to transfusion."},{"section":"Batch 062 · nociceptive vs neuropathic pain (somatic, visceral, radiating)…","question":"Why is it legally and ethically important that informed consent be obtained BEFORE the procedure?","options":["It is a legally agreement between patient and hospital","It is only a formality","It can be obtained after the procedure","It is unnecessary for adults"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states informed consent is a legally agreement between patient and hospital (a legal agreement).  Why B/C/D wrong: Consent is a legally binding agreement that must precede the procedure and applies to competent patients/representatives."},{"section":"Batch 062 · nociceptive vs neuropathic pain (somatic, visceral, radiating)…","question":"Who may have authority to give consent if a patient above 18 years is unable to give consent?","options":["No one — the procedure cannot proceed","Any patient who have above 18 year his/her legally authorized for consent; the patient's relatives are authorized for consent","Only the hospital administrator","Only the operating surgeon"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states any patient who is above 18 years is legally authorized to give consent, and if the patient cannot, the patient's relatives are authorized to give consent.  Why A wrong: A relative may consent when the patient cannot.  Why C/D wrong: Hospital administrator/surgeon are not the authorized consent givers."},{"section":"Batch 062 · nociceptive vs neuropathic pain (somatic, visceral, radiating)…","question":"In an emergency situation when two doctors may sign consent for a client, this reflects which principle?","options":["Consent is never needed in emergencies","In an emergency situation, two doctors may sign consent for a client (when patient/relative cannot)","The patient must always sign first","Only one nurse signs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states in an emergency situation, two doctors may sign consent for a client.  Why A wrong: Some form of consent/authorization is still documented in emergencies.  Why C/D wrong: The stated provision is two doctors signing, not patient-first or nurse-only."},{"section":"Batch 062 · nociceptive vs neuropathic pain (somatic, visceral, radiating)…","question":"Before taking an informed consent, what is the nurse's/clinician's responsibility regarding the procedure?","options":["Withhold all details to avoid alarming the patient","Provide all details about the procedure, merits and demerits, and consequences of the procedure","Only state the procedure name","Let the patient research independently"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Before taking informed consent, all details about the procedure, merits and demerits, and consequences of the procedure must be explained.  Why A/C/D wrong: Adequate disclosure is required for valid informed consent."},{"section":"Batch 062 · nociceptive vs neuropathic pain (somatic, visceral, radiating)…","question":"When should the details or explanation of a procedure be given to the patient?","options":["The details or explanation of the procedure should be done before sedation is given","After the patient is sedated","Only after the procedure","Details are optional"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the details/explanation of the procedure should be done before sedation given.  Why B wrong: A sedated patient cannot give valid consent.  Why C/D wrong: Explanation must precede the procedure and is required, not optional."},{"section":"Batch 062 · nociceptive vs neuropathic pain (somatic, visceral, radiating)…","question":"What is true about a client's questions during the informed-consent process?","options":["The client should not ask questions","A client's questions about the surgery or procedure must be answered before signing the consent","Questions are answered only after surgery","Only relatives may ask questions"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states a client's questions about the surgery or procedure must be answered before signing the consent.  Why A/C/D wrong: The client has the right to ask and have questions answered prior to consenting."},{"section":"Batch 062 · nociceptive vs neuropathic pain (somatic, visceral, radiating)…","question":"What right does a client retain even after giving consent?","options":["No further rights","A client has a right to withdraw consent at any time","The right to choose the surgeon's lunch","The right to demand a different hospital instantly"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states a client has a right to withdraw consent at any time.  Why A/C/D wrong: Consent is voluntary and revocable; the stated right is withdrawal at any time."},{"section":"Batch 062 · nociceptive vs neuropathic pain (somatic, visceral, radiating)…","question":"Special consent is specifically required for which situations? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Use of restraint","Photography of the client","Disposal of body parts","Performing an autopsy","Routine vital sign measurement"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Special consent is required for use of restraint, photography of the client, disposal of body parts during surgery, and performing an autopsy.  Why E wrong: Routine vital signs do not require special consent."},{"section":"Batch 062 · nociceptive vs neuropathic pain (somatic, visceral, radiating)…","question":"An \"adverse event\" is BEST defined as:","options":["An expected outcome of therapy","An injury caused by medical management rather than by the underlying disease or condition of the client","A type of restraint","A normal vital sign change"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: An adverse event is defined as an injury caused by medical management rather than by the underlying disease or condition of the client.  Why A/C/D wrong: An adverse event is harm from management, not an expected outcome, restraint, or normal change."},{"section":"Batch 062 · nociceptive vs neuropathic pain (somatic, visceral, radiating)…","question":"A \"sentinel event\" is defined as:","options":["A minor charting error","An unexpected occurrence involving death or serious physical or psychological injury, or risk thereof","A routine medication round","An expected complication"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A sentinel event is an unexpected occurrence involving death or serious physical or psychological injury or risk thereof; it includes serious events such as loss of limb or function.  Why A/C/D wrong: Sentinel events are serious/unexpected, not routine or expected occurrences."},{"section":"Batch 062 · nociceptive vs neuropathic pain (somatic, visceral, radiating)…","question":"A sentinel event requires which type of response?","options":["No response needed","Immediate investigation","Investigation only at year-end","Response only if the family complains"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Sentinel event means need immediate investigation (also expressed as immediate intervention).  Why A/C/D wrong: Sentinel events mandate prompt investigation, not delayed or conditional responses."},{"section":"Batch 062 · nociceptive vs neuropathic pain (somatic, visceral, radiating)…","question":"When a bedside swab is left inside the abdominal cavity after surgery, the condition is termed:","options":["Gossypiboma, also known as textiloma","Hypovolemic shock","Sentinel event only","Adverse drug reaction"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A retained patient bedside swab left inside the abdominal cavity after surgery is termed Gossypiboma, also known as Textiloma.  Why B wrong: Hypovolemic shock is the most common shock in trauma, a different concept.  Why C wrong: While it may be a sentinel event, the specific named term is gossypiboma/textiloma.  Why D wrong: It is a retained foreign body, not a drug reaction."},{"section":"Batch 062 · nociceptive vs neuropathic pain (somatic, visceral, radiating)…","question":"What is the most common type of shock in a trauma patient?","options":["Cardiogenic shock","Hypovolemic shock","Septic shock","Anaphylactic shock"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the most common type of shock in a trauma patient is hypovolemic shock.  Why A/C/D wrong: These shock types are not identified as most common in trauma."},{"section":"Batch 062 · nociceptive vs neuropathic pain (somatic, visceral, radiating)…","question":"B1 tube is sterilized by which method?","options":["Autoclaving","Cidex (chemical disinfection)","Boiling","Dry heat"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the B1 tube is sterilized by Cidex.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies Cidex, not autoclaving, boiling, or dry heat."},{"section":"Batch 062 · nociceptive vs neuropathic pain (somatic, visceral, radiating)…","question":"What is the correct/recommended position for a patient during sigmoidoscopy and colonoscopy?","options":["Right lateral position","Left lateral position","Supine position","Prone position"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the position for a patient during sigmoidoscopy and colonoscopy is the left lateral position.  Why A/C/D wrong: The recommended position is left lateral (Sims-type), not right lateral, supine, or prone."},{"section":"Batch 062 · nociceptive vs neuropathic pain (somatic, visceral, radiating)…","question":"What is the MOST important stage of the nursing process?","options":["Diagnosis","Planning","Assessment","Evaluation"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the most important stage of the nursing process is Assessment.  Why A/B/D wrong: Diagnosis, planning, and evaluation are later steps that depend on accurate assessment; assessment is identified as most important."}];
